\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for June 2, 2026, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Ali Velshi, Jacob Soboroff \par \uc2 8,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2026\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MS NOW HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think he`s losing it. I don`t -- I think he`s too old.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Voters head to the polls in Iowa, New Jersey, California, beyond. Tonight, Jacob Soboroff on the big stakes in the Golden State and Ali Velshi at the Big Board. Then --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: we happen to have the head of \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2 . And where is Mr. Pulte right here? Pulte? He`s done a great job.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  on the growing alarm over Trump`s choice for Director Of National Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 BILL PULTE, DIRECTOR OF U.S. FEDERAL HOUSING: This lady signed these documents or she didn`t. She committed mortgage fraud or she didn`t.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Plus, Senator Tim Kaine on the president`s boredom with Iran and about that slush fund.\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE, ACTING U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The reasons for the fund I think were it remain as important as they were before but we are not moving forward with the fund.\par \par \uc2 REP. GRACE MENG (D-NY): Not moving forward ever.\par \par \uc2 BLANCHE: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): What we know about what the president is still getting.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): You just gave the president and his family a tax immunity to the tune of about $100 million.\par \par \uc2 BLANCHE: Not true.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. It is a Tuesday during a Midterm Election year, which means it is election night in America once again. Tonight, the marquee race, of course, is in California. That`s where dozens upon dozens of candidates are running in the open nonpartisan primary to replace the term-limited governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , who clearly has thoughts about what he wants to do next.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the top two Democrats in polling are progressive philanthropist \uc2 Tom Steyer\uc2  and former Biden Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 . Neither candidate was pegged as a front-runner for much of this race. Becerra in particular was pulling in single digits as recently as March, but of course, he surged in recent weeks following the implosion of Congressman Eric Swalwell`s campaign amid allegations of sexual misconduct, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 Steyer, for his part, is running as a left populist candidate despite himself being a billionaire, framing himself as a self-proclaimed class traitor in the mold of FDR. Meanwhile, the Republican front-runner is a former Fox News host, a guy named Steve Hilton. Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  urged his supporters to turn out for him rather than \uc2 Riverside County\uc2  Sheriff Chad Bianco, who is the other major Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember how elections work in California, which is a little different than other places. Everyone runs together in one nonpartisan primary, which means it`s entirely possible that the two nominees for the general election will both be Democrats. It`s even possible they would both be Republicans if a large enough Democratic field split the vote enough. As of right now, the likeliest option will be one Democrat and one Republican.\par \par \uc2 We`re also watching a crowded race to replace retiring Speaker Emerita \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in San Francisco. That`s been a really interesting one. Of course, in Los Angeles, incumbent mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  is up for re-election. She`s facing a primary challenge from her left, while former reality TV personality Spencer Pratt is running as a conservative Independent.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have much more on those California races later tonight. In fact, I`m going to join Rachel Maddow, the rest of the team at the big table for some late-night special coverage.\par \par \uc2 There are some other interesting races to watch tonight, though, because in New Jersey, where polls just closed moments ago at 8:00 p.m., Democrats are aggressively competing in a bunch of swing districts that will determine control of the House, including perhaps the most important and the most tantalizing for the Democrats in terms of a pickup. There is currently a four-way primary in the district, currently represented by Republican Tom Kean. That`s the state`s seventh district.\par \par \uc2 Now, the reason this is a particularly interesting race is it`s a district that even before this year was eyed as one that would be very, very competitive. It`s a very, very centrist district. It moves back and forth. But the other reason that this is now a very contested seat is that the congressman representing it, Tom Kean Jr., has not been seen publicly for almost three months. He`s missed more than 100 votes in the House. His aids have said he has been absent due to a personal medical issue and will return soon. He is still trading stocks though according to stock disclosures.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:12]\par \par \uc2 Yesterday Kean received Trump`s "complete and total endorsement." adding that Tom is working tirelessly and will never let you down.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, Democrats Josh Turek and Zach Wahls are running in a primary to replace the retiring incumbent Republican Senator Joni Ernst. There will be no surprise in the Democratic gubernatorial primary in that state. That`s because Rob Sand, who we just had on the program last week, I believe, is running unopposed. And Mr. Sand has a real shot at flipping the governor`s mansion come November. He`s the only statewide elected Democrat in that state.\par \par \uc2 Nearly across the board, the message from Democratic candidates is a promise to work on affordability and to hold the Trump administration accountable, to push back on the way things are going, to make a wrong track of the country into a right track. Because right now the Trump administration is governing as though accountability simply does not exist. In fact, more than ever, the lower the polling gets, the more they behave that way.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just today, another just totally jaw-dropping, gob-smacking, leave you speechless kind of news alert when it was announced that Donald Trump was announcing a flagrantly, almost impossibly unqualified candidate to replace Tulsi Gabbard as the Director of National Intelligence. It`s the man you see there, his guy named Bill Pulte who`s spent the last year digging into the mortgage records of the president`s critics to hound them with malicious prosecutions. That guy, Trump`s attack dog.\par \par \uc2 That guy who, by the way, has zero intelligence or national security experience whatsoever. Trump wants to put him in charge of the entirety of the American intelligence apparatus, all 11 agencies, all the spies, all the wiretaps, all the satellites, all the human into that guy. All of it. We`ll have more on that later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Today, we also learned that Trump`s Acting Attorney General, Todd Blanche, who`s very clearly auditioning for a permanent role as the attorney general, told Congress that the $ 1.8 billion slush fund for insurrectionists is apparently no more. They appear to be withdrawing the current proposal, although it remains to be seen what this White House puts forward instead.\par \par \uc2 It`s very clear the public backlash was more significant the administration expected. And it`s exactly why, and this is something we come back to again and again on this program, why public opinion, the basics of it, persuasion, convincing people of your views, arguing about things, right? Public opinion and small democratic still matter. In fact, they matter more than they have ever mattered in the decades I`ve been covering politics. And it`s also why Democrats right now feel very bullish about their chances come November.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, MS NOW Chief Data Reporter, Ali Velshi, at the board. Ali, you want to just start with the latest in New Jersey and then take us through what this night is going to look like.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MS NOW CHIEF DATA REPORTER: Yes, tonight we`re going to get numbers from New Jersey. We`re looking at Iowa. We`re looking at South Dakota, New Mexico, Montana, and California. California is not colored in by the way because it`s not a partisan primary as you just explained.\par \par \uc2 But let`s look at the -- at the primaries in New Jersey to start with. I`m going to start with the Republican primary because you talked about the seventh district is uncontested. Tom Kean is the incumbent there. And as you said, he hasn`t been seen so nobody knows where he is but there`s a -- there`s a good contest to compete with him.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Bennett is one of the candidates here. Tina Shah was an assistant to -- an adviser to Andy Kim. We`re going to be watching this one very, very closely. But as of now we do not have any numbers in from New Jersey just yet.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at Iowa for a second. This is interesting because as you said there are two candidates running here. Josh Turek and Zach Wahls you`ll remember from 2011 got up and made that speech at a public hearing about how he`s got two mothers. He`s been involved in politics in the state for some time. Josh Turek is polling a little higher. He`s in the lead at the moment, but again, no numbers and we won`t have numbers for a little while there. But that`s interesting because the idea that we`re actually talking about Iowa as a place where a U.S. Senate candidate might be in contention once again is an interesting story.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go again to -- I`m going to press on open primaries which means nonpartisan and we go to California where you discussed what happens there. You end up with a situation where people can run -- you can -- you can end up with two Democrats or two Republicans in a given -- in a given district. We`re going to be -- we`re looking at a lot of districts here. Why? Because five of them were changed as a result of what happened to Texas. In theory, there are five districts where Democrats can win. So, what you`re looking at here is to see whether the two candidates, the two top vote getters in the five different districts that we`re looking at in California end up being Democrats, which would mean that one of them will be a Democrat when we -- when we see the election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:08]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And then finally, the gubernatorial race, which is remarkable, and I`ll show you why. There are 60 people in this race for governor. Now, look, it`s not going to come down to 60 people. It`s come down to about three or four people. I`m trying to find the one. You know, there`s some really interesting candidates here, including one whose name is, for the love of God or something like that. I guess that`s his actual there Living for God and Country DeMott.\par \par \uc2 But most importantly, we`re looking at the front of the race here and Xavier Becerra, who has his claim to fame is he sued the administration 122 times as the attorney general. He`s probably polling in the top spot. Steve Hilton, former British citizen, is in the number two spot, very MAGA. And then Tom Steyer, the billionaire, who was sort of in politics and a big climate guy before being a billionaire was a sort of an albatross around your neck.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a really interesting conversation that progressives are having in in California because what they`d like to do is come out of the night with Becerra and Tom Steyer at the top of the ticket, which means come November, a Democrat will be the governor of California.\par \par \uc2 Again, I should warn you and everybody else, it`s going to be a late night. Last time around when there were primaries, it was 6:00 in the morning Eastern and only 52 percent of the vote had come in. Because Californians like to vote by mail. They`re really good at it. But in this particular case, because the top vote getter, the person who was at the top of the polls, Eric Swalwell, pulled out, a lot of people just held their ballots back. They were having trouble deciding which of these two candidates they`re most interested in on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 A lot of progressive groups are saying support -- basically hold your nose and support Tom Steyer. Yes, he`s a billionaire, but you want these two guys in the front of the Democratic race.\par \par \uc2 As you said, it`s entirely likely that it`s one of these guys and Steve Hilton, but it`ll be very interesting for Democrats if the two top vote getters in the Democratic race for governor end up both being Democrats. So, lots and lots and lots to look at over the next many, many hours together.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Ali Velshi, we`re going to be checking back with you throughout the night later in this show and then later in the evening. Thank you. That was great.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Soboroff of course of course is the host of "CONNECT" here on MS NOW. That`s starting June 13th. He joins me now from Los Angeles. Jacob, first, just tell me where you are and what you`re hearing.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, MS NOW REPORTER: Chris, I`m in -- I got to do a little bit of a golf announcer voice for a second until I walk outside. But this is the heart of downtown Los Angeles. I am in a polling place at the intersection of Grand Avenue and Fifth Street, really smack in the middle of Downtown LA, which is which is pretty neat actually because about a year ago, you and I were live on this very network as the first spontaneous protest broke out just blocks away from here to those ICE raids here in L.A.\par \par \uc2 And over the course of the last year, we have seen literally hundreds of thousands of people come to the streets of downtown Los Angeles in a stand against this administration, which I`ll talk a little bit more about once we get outside. But check it out. I mean, the vibes are strong here right now. And I can show you through these windows, actually. The line stretches all the way around the block from Grand Avenue down to down Fifth Street. This is the end of the line, and people are stretching around here as people are voting inside.\par \par \uc2 And you can see folks up here. Here in California, as our dear friend Ali Velshi said, we have a great early voting system here. I`m so so