r loyalty to Trump and willingness to break the law. That`s going to endanger our country. This is a president that looks not for experience but for people that will extend his corruption, his chaos, and his cruelty.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I just to -- stay on this for a second. I mean, there`s two aspects of this but to start on the first one which you note here is this is a serious and important job. We`re currently in a war although there`s ostensible ceasefire although there`s arms -- there`s you know, bombings being exchanged, missile fire being exchanged today. And first and foremost, it does seem a real problem to put someone who has no business doing the job in in charge of the coordination of the intelligence agencies.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes, look, we have professionals that keep us safe every day, that are not political, that do their job. The thought that somebody who`s already shown their willingness to use their position to drum up charges on the head of the Fed or senators themselves, this guy has shown that his sole purpose is to try to go after Trump`s enemies. Well, imagine giving them the apparatus of our national intelligence agencies. This is scary and terrifying, but it`s also showing that you don`t take the threats to our country seriously, and you`re willing to sacrifice our security in order to have a loyalist who`s going to go after your perceived enemies. This is crazy and corrupt and very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, he`s being appointed under the -- I think the vacancy reform act, meaning like as an acting, right? He`s -- they`re going to try to sort of sidestep any Senate confirmation. I think they understand that it would be uphill battle. Senator John Cornyn expressing some doubt.\par \par \uc2 I do wonder -- this seems to me in the same category as the -- as the -- as a sort of seditionist slush fund, like so out past the frontier of the colorably defensible that that maybe it won`t work. And on that note, I want to ask a