 in 2024, 50 to almost 49. Cedar Rapids is in Linn County, again about an eight-point lead there. And then Johnson County, Iowa City where it was a very, very substantial lead.\par \par \uc2 So, when we get the numbers out of Iowa, we`re going to be paying very, very close attention to it. I want to go back to the Democratic primaries in New Jersey right now because we`ve got a lot of results in right now. The one we`re looking most closely at Tom Kean`s district is District 7 in New Jersey. 35 percent of the vote in. Rebecca Bennett is the lead candidate right now in a very, very comfortable lead she`s got over Tina Shah and Brian Varela. So, we`re watching that closely to see when that gets determined.\par \par \uc2 In District 11, this is the one that had that very contentious primary some time ago. Tom Malinowski was thought to be in the lead and then AIPAC threw money into another candidate ended up defeating Tom Malinowski and getting an Analilia Mejia who definitely is not the candidate AIPAC wanted. She`s now going to be moving ahead. She`s got -- I mean this is decided, 43 percent of the vote in. She`s at 82 percent there.\par \par \uc2 And we`re watching some other races in here. Rob Menendez, of course, is -- has been determined to be the candidate in District 8. And again, we`re watching District 12 very, very carefully. Adam Hamawy, who was credited as the doctor who helped Senator Tammy Duckworth when she was injured, he`s 47 percent of the vote in. He`s got a slight lead over Brad Cohen. It`s not a very, very substantial lead at the moment. So, we`re going to watching this one very closely.\par \par \uc2 But, that`s the stuff that we`ve got in from New Jersey right now. We`ll watch Iowa and of course you and I are going to be up very late into the night and early tomorrow morning watching California.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And that Iowa race, I mean, are we expecting -- obviously California, we know we`re going to be up very late. The Iowa race, my recollection is that we`re going to get -- probably get results tonight, right, in that primary. They`re going to be taking on Ashley Hinton, who`s the going to be the -- almost certainly going to be the nominee on the Republican side for that Joni Ernst seat.\par \par \uc2 And that`s -- you know, because it`s a -- you don`t have a recumbent, you don`t have an incumbent, you know it`s low on the tier in terms of the most likely to flip but it`s in that kind of Ohio, Texas, Iowa --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- possible stretch.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, I would say this. If on November 3rd Democrats are having a good night across the country, Iowa certainly in play. It`s -- nobody -- it`s not the thing people are thinking is the first choice on the play to getting the four seats that the Democrats need to take over the Senate. But the fact that we`re discussing it is intriguing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Zach Wahls, as you noted before with that incredible viral speech, he`s been -- he`s been a state legislator and politician. Josh Turek, people should Google during this commercial break. Google his first ad --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- for the primary because it is a great ad and we`ll teach you something about Josh Turek, who`s a Paralympian among many others.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s right. It`s an -- this is a very interesting race.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. All right, Ali, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, Senator Tim Kaine on Donald Trump`s boredom with the war he started next.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Donald Trump`s war against Iran is now entering its fourth month. There is no end in sight despite the news alerts you may be getting about an impending deal. There is also quite importantly no authorization for war from Congress. And today, as Secretary of State Marco Rubio testified on Capitol Hill for the first time since the war started, one senator also reminded him the White House hasn`t even shared its legal justification, just shared it for the war with Congress yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): By not sharing the legal opinion with the Article One oversight branch, you give us the opinion that there`s something in there you don`t want us to see. Could you use your influence as the president`s chief national security adviser, Secretary of State, NSA to get the administration to share the Iran war legal opinion with Congress?\par \par \uc2 [20:50:06]\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, look, first of all, obviously, the sec -- the Department of State does not develop the legal opinion. That`s legal the legal counsel.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: You`re the national -- you`re the chief national -- you`re the chief --\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: So, I think what you`re asking is why haven`t the Office of Legal Counsel provided it? They don`t work for the National Security Counsel. They don`t work for me.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I`m actually asking --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Democrat Tim Kaine of Virginia sits on the Senate Armed Services and Foreign Relations Committee and he joins me now. Well, it`s very funny when this administration tries to kind of give a little bit of like a bureaucratic answer about whose turf is whose on the day that they appointed Bill Pulte to run the intelligence apparatus of the entire United States. I take you were not very satisfied with that answer from the Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: No, Chris, that`s fair. I mean, Marco Rubio is the first person since Henry Kissinger to be both Secretary of State and National Security Adviser. And for him to say, well, I don`t really have any control over what these other guys do. That was sort of a feckless response.\par \par \uc2 The administration shared with us the legal rationale for the boat strikes in the Pacific and Caribbean. I found them deeply, deeply unsatisfactory, but at least they shared them with us and classified. And they shared with us the legal rationale for the Maduro removal operation. Again, I didn`t find it persuasive, but they shared with us.\par \par \uc2 But on the Iran war, 92 days in, they have refused to share with any members of Congress. So what are they hiding? Either there`s some equivocation about the legal justification, or maybe some qualification like you can do this, but don`t bomb schools and bridges and civilian infrastructure. Or maybe there`s some factual assertions like this war will be over in two days, or the Iranians will never try to close the Straits of Hormuz. They`re clearly hiding something. And the fact that they won`t share it with Congress and the Republicans seem fine with that. That shouldn`t be fine with the American public.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You just mentioned the boat strikes in the Caribbean which is a story that we`ve covered here but has sort of gone on I think in the background frankly. And there`s reporting we`ve just crossed the threshold of 200 individuals that we have killed. I think lots of independent legal observers would say that we`ve -- the U.S. government has murdered them. They were -- they were -- they were civilians who were killed not in self- defense, under no plausible account of war.\par \par \uc2 And you raised something today in the hearing I thought was interesting that your understanding is there the targeting criteria does not even require the presence of narcotics on the boats. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes, Chris. I`ve been briefed on the targeting criteria and sadly I can`t describe it here because it`s all been classified. But I think I can describe things that aren`t targeting criteria. And Senator Paul, a Republican and I today in the hearing pointed out that to strike these boats and kill all on board, the U.S. military does not have to have any evidence that there`s narcotics on the boats. And they do not have to have any evidence that anyone on the boats is armed.\par \par \uc2 So, for some reason, the presence of narcotics and whether the folks on board are armed is irrelevant to the question of whether we can sink the boats and kill everybody on board. And the Secretary of State did not challenge me on that. And he couldn`t because I`ve been briefed consistently on this since September 2nd. Again, I`m sorry that I can`t describe what the targeting criteria are. They`ve only done this in classified to try to put a gag rule on senators.\par \par \uc2 But I think I`m not prohibited from describing my shock and surprise about what the targeting criteria are not. The administration could say, we`ll only strike boats if we have some proof that there`s narcotics on board. We`ll only strike boats if these folks are armed. They intentionally chose not to include those as targeting criteria and that raises massive human rights and legal questions.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As we speak tonight, we have reports of American, I believe naval forces stopping or targeting ships around Iran in the Strait of Hormuz. We also have reports of Iran retaliating with fire aimed at Kuwaiti targets. This is not the first time obviously this sort of thing has happened amidst a ceasefire. Donald Trump says he doesn`t care if there`s a deal and also that he`s kind of bored of the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve kind of thrown my hands up at trying to cover where we are because every -- it`s like Groundhog Day. Do you have a sense of where we are? Like are you privy to any information that would make more sense of where things are than we are?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:07]\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, first, Secretary Rubio, the thing he said today that might have been the most surprising is, oh, no, we`re not still at war. That almost got him laughed out of the room. We have U.S. troops deployed there. There`s firing between the U.S. and Iran and U.S. positions in the region and other nations in the region and commercial shipping. But they`re trying to pretend that it`s not a war.\par \par \uc2 There`s a ceasefire that is holding to some degree. At least we`re not in a maximum bombardment campaign. But here`s where we are, Chris. The ceasefire that`s partial has to be made complete. We have to complete it, extend it, and then President Trump has to find an offramp for this illegal, unnecessary, and unwise war that`s costing American troops, and it`s punishing American families with gas prices, food prices, and so much else.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia, thank you for your time tonight, sir.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this big Tuesday night. The election coverage continues all night long. I`ll be back at 10:00 p.m. Eastern with Rachel and the whole crew for more of our special MS NOW election coverage. First, "THE BRIEFING" with Jen Psaki starts right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2026 Versant Inc.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020260603em6200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour With Stephanie Ruhle For November 28, 2025, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Philip Bump, David Noriega, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 8,327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2025\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:02:02]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MS NOW HOST: Good evening. I am Stephanie Ruhle. It is 7:00 p.m. on the East Coast and 4:00 p.m. in the West.\par \par \uc2 The man accused of shooting two National Guard members in Washington, D.C., is facing multiple charges, including first-degree murder; 20-year-old Specialist Sarah Beckstrom was killed; 24-year-old staff sergeant Andrew Wolfe is in critical condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is a 29-year-old Afghan national who worked with the CIA during the war in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in MS NOW national correspondent David Noriega. He joins us live from Bellingham, Washington, where the suspected shooter lives.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 What more do we know about this man at this point? Who is he? Why was he living there in Washington?\par \par \uc2 DAVID NORIEGA, MS NOW CORRESPONDENT: Well, Stephanie, there is a small, but growing community of Afghan nationals who have settled here in Bellingham, Washington, a much larger community of many thousands further south in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these people came to this part of the country around 2021 after Afghanistan fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Now, the suspected shooter in this case came into the country in 2021, but was granted asylum this year in April, which means he came in under the Biden administration, but won asylum under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The work for the CIA that you mentioned, this person belonged to a so- called Zero Unit, which are essentially CIA-backed covert paramilitary strike forces in Afghanistan that were designed to go after \uc2 Taliban\uc2  or suspected \uc2 Taliban\uc2  insurgents.\par \par \uc2 Human rights groups have described these Zero Units as essentially death squads. They have been accused on multiple occasions of committing human rights violations like extrajudicial killings and enforced disappearances.\par \par \uc2 But the important thing here, Steph, is that this person basically worked for the CIA, came to the United States and was granted asylum. So we`re going to hear and we have been hearing a lot around the question of whether this person was vetted or whether people from Afghanistan have been vetted. This person worked for the CIA and successfully got asylum.\par \par \uc2 That essentially means that he was about as vetted as it`s possible to be in this context,Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How is the community there responding to this?\par \par \uc2 NORIEGA: Well, I just got off the phone with an Afghan man who lives here in Washington state, has lived here for several years. He asked me to please not use his name on air because he is afraid.\par \par \uc2 And he told me that not only -- it`s not only his family, but everyone that he knows in the Afghan community here in Washington state that is worried that this incident is going to have repercussions for the community at large. He made a point of telling me that he considers this crime committed, this attack on the National Guard troops in Washington to be a heinous crime.\par \par \uc2 He said that he considered it heartbreaking. But he`s worried that American people are going to see the actions of this one individual and consider it representative of the Afghan community at large.\par \par \uc2 He also told me, he asked me to convey to our viewers that not just he and his family, but every other Afghan person who is living in the United States in this moment, feels profound gratitude towards the United States, towards -- and feels profoundly grateful for the ability to live here in this country safely, to send their girls, their daughters to school in this country, and that they truly hope that their ability to stay here is not jeopardized as a result of this one person`s crime, Stefanik.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:13]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: David, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the president says he is not pulling the National Guard out of Washington, D.C. In fact, he wants to send hundreds more. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, just I want to have more. As you know, we`re sending 500 more. We had very little crime in Washington now for many months, record-setting low crime, and this happened. And I don`t know how you put this on the scale of that.\par \par \uc2 This is sort of different. This is a terrorism threat, and this was done because they`re so effective. If they weren`t effective, you probably wouldn`t have had this done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports that D.C. police will now patrol with the National Guard, at least for the time being. That is according to two D.C. police officials in an e-mail that MS NOW has not reviewed.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there are a lot of questions about the decision to deploy the National Guard there and other cities around the country. "The Atlantic" points out that before the shooting -- quote -- "Military commanders had warned that their deployment represented an easy target of opportunity for grievance-based violence."\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel this hour. Philip Bump is here, MS NOW columnist and contributor. Democratic strategist Joel Payne, he was also a senior aide for Hillary for America and press secretary for former Senator Harry Reid. And Mark McKinnon joins us, former adviser to both \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 John McCain\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, Philip, what do you make of how Trump and his team have responded to this?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BUMP, MS NOW COLUMNIST AND CONTRIBUTOR: I mean, it`s hard not to see this as them seizing an opportunity, right?\par \par \uc2 In the same way that they seized an opportunity when they first deployed the National Guard to D.C. by -- there was this incident in which someone who was associated sort of loosely with the administration had been assaulted, and they sort of used that as a pretext to send in the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 It`s very obvious that for a long time they had wanted to take a severe action on immigration, I mean, going back to Donald Trump`s first campaign in December 2015, I believe it was, when he called for a ban on anyone who`s immigrating from any country that`s predominantly Muslim, right?\par \par \uc2 This is a pattern that he`s had for a long time. It`s been the case for a long time that they have sought to crack down on the number of people arriving in the country from countries that they don`t like for whatever reason. One can read into it however they want.\par \par \uc2 But this seems very pretextual. I mean, a lot of the conversation that you hear is this is a person who did a thing. It is not a community who did a thing. It is not a country who did a thing. It is a person who did a thing, and that to have the response to that be to double down on a policy that was already dubious, I think it suggests that the policy was something they`d planned to do anyway.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mark, but why would you send more National Guard troops into D.C.? What does that solve, besides this sort of dramatic show of force?\par \par \uc2 MARK MCKINNON, FORMER MEDIA ADVISER TO PRESIDENT \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 : I think it`s a reflex, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Carville mantra, as you know, it`s the economy, stupid. The Trump mantra, is, it`s immigration stupid. I mean, it started when it came down the elevator and it`s been as reflex all along. He believes it`s the strongest issue. And I think everybody around him thinks, as long as we`re talking about immigration, we`re talking about an issue that we have strength on.\par \par \uc2 And it`s just -- it`s amazing to me to watch as a moment that calls for national unity, that Trump`s reflex when asked if he was going to go to the funeral was to talk about how he ran the numbers up in West Virginia in the last election. So it`s always about him. How does it serve his interests? How does it serve him politically?\par \par \uc2 And when asked also -- in the wake of the Charlie Kirk shooting, he was asked whether or not he had any obligation to bring the country together. And he said, I could care less. And so that`s his approach to these things. He could care less.\par \par \uc2 He just sees it as an opportunity politically. And that`s all.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joel, what was your reaction to the report that the president was warned that something like this could happen?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, Steph, my reaction to Sarah Beckstrom`s murder was just deep anger and grief, right?\par \par \uc2 My reaction to Donald Trump`s -- the information that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was warned about this is, unfortunately, that`s -- that is something that you could predict. I mean, you could predict that these young people who sign up for service, who sign up to serve their communities, right, to help their communities back home in West Virginia if there`s a flood, if there`s a natural disaster, if there`s a local event that requires surge support -- they didn`t sign up to stand on a street corner in Washington, D.C., frankly, as a part of Donald Trump`s political show.\par \par \uc2 And I deeply aggrieved that this young woman lost her life as a part of this. I also think there`s righteous anger that people are feeling about this. The problem is, we got a president that takes that anger and that turns it back towards us and tells us to turn it against each other.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:05]\par \par \uc2 And that`s unhealthy and that`s unsustainable. And that`s the type of thing that breaks not just the democracy, but that breaks communities and that breaks a covenant that we have as Americans with each other.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, now the president is talking about a new immigration crackdown.\par \par \uc2 We know, Philip, that this president believes he is winning whenever he`s talking about immigration. When immigration is the focus, it`s a win for him. Is this another example of that?\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes. I mean, first of all, I don`t think that`s even true anymore, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I hate even saying it. What a horrible tragedy it`s centered around.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes, yes, I mean, look, I don`t think it`s the case that this is the winning issue that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think, sincerely believes.\par \par \uc2 And there`s two aspects to that. The first is, we have seen how his immigration numbers have gone down. We have seen how they have gone down in response to conversations about the ways in which his immigration policies are negatively affecting people. We saw with the Kilmar Abrego Garcia situation earlier this year that people looked that -- looked upon what he was doing and saw that negatively.\par \par \uc2 And his numbers went down there. And I also think that he does this in part because I think they think it`s a political issue that will succeed for them, which I don`t think it is. But I think it`s in part because he has these visceral feelings about immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Like, we can`t escape the fact that he has strongly hostile negative opinions of immigrants, and particularly immigrants of color. Like, let`s just call it what it is, right? And so I think, to some extent, yes, they think that this is what they got elected for, which is, I think, true more of 2016 than 2024.\par \par \uc2 But I think that they think that this is what they`re supposed to be doing. But I think this is also what they want to be doing. I think Trump, I think \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , in particular, I think these people want to have this crackdown, want to see a more pure American state than what we have right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s driving it probably at least as much as these political ideas.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s the thing. That`s not going to distract people from the affordability crisis, Mark.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Sure.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Having this new renewed immigration crackdown isn`t going to change another reality that`s the truth for the American people and not good politically for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: That trumps everything, to use Trump`s name directly there, because, whether it`s immigration, whether it`s the war in Ukraine, at the end of the day, the thing that people are going to vote on more than anything is something that they feel personally that affects them, that affects their quality of life.\par \par \uc2 And, right now, as we saw in the last election, across the board, it`s the issue of affordability. So they can try and distract with immigration, but what they should really be doing is figuring out a way to talk about it and effect policies that will affect it in a way that they can talk about it with some meaning that has some impact on their lives, because just distracting with immigration isn`t going to cut it.\par \par \uc2 Because, at the end of the day, people will feel the effects of -- everybody will feel the effect of the economy, whereas everybody doesn`t feel the effect of immigration.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joel, former Congressman, former -- formerly Republican Congressman, was he? Yes.\par \par \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  points out, in 2021, almost every Republican attacked the Biden administration and the State Department because they were too slow in approving Afghanistan`s special visas as Afghanistan was falling. Now they`re attacking Biden for doing what they asked him to do. What is your take on that?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, they`re attacking Biden for doing what they asked him to do. They`re also attacking Biden when they know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his administration, is the one that actually rubber-stamped this suspect remaining in the country under this program.\par \par \uc2 And what does that mean, Stephanie? Does it mean that the program is garbage because someone slipped through the cracks and someone abused the system and took advantage of it? No. And does it mean that it`s a bad idea to welcome people in who didn`t originate from this country?\par \par \uc2 No, of course not. And this is the tragedy of the Trump era. I mean, there are many tragedies of the Trump era. But one of the outstanding tragedies of the Trump era is that moments that call for us to come together, be solutions-oriented, think about ways to fix a system that`s a good idea, that`s a little bit broken, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no interest in that.\par \par \uc2 He`s only got interest in using everything he can as a political weapon to divide us. And he`s going to keep doing it more and more. And he`s going to keep raising the temperature on this as his presidency continues to drain the toilet, because his presidency is in the toilet.\par \par \uc2 This economy is in the toilet. This agenda is in the toilet. And as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to get more desperate, he is going to continue to lash out. Yes, it was this horrible tragedy with this young woman in Washington, D.C., today. It`ll be something else next week. And he`s going to reach and he`s going to overreach until he forces a climax moment where the country can`t stay together anymore.\par \par \uc2 He wants this country to combust, when we desperately need a leader to keep us together.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, last night, a reporter tried to point out that it was the Trump administration that vouched for the vetting of Afghans that were brought into this country. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:09]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Your DOJ I.G. just reported this year that there was thorough vetting by DHS and by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  of these Afghans who were brought into the U.S., so why do you blame the Biden administration?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because they let him in. Are you stupid? Are you a stupid person? Because they came in to -- on a plane, along with thousands of other people that shouldn`t be here, and you`re just asking questions because you`re a stupid person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: "Are you a stupid person?" last night on Thanksgiving. Your reaction, Philip?\par \par \uc2 BUMP: No, what do you say, right? I mean, what do you say? Like, this is the president of the United States. For him to say that in any context is bizarre for a president to say that. For him to say that in that particular context, I think is, sadly, not surprising.\par \par \uc2 I was just thinking it was nine years ago this month after Thanksgiving in 2016 that I wrote a piece for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " talking about how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t understand unity beyond people agreeing with him. That`s the unity he`s always believed in.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things we have seen in his second term in office is, he has built the White House and built the executive branch to be that, to be people who agree with him, who aren`t contradicting him, who aren`t challenging him, who aren`t necessarily presenting him with alternate opinions.\par \par \uc2 And I think we see it moments like that, when he sits there, and he is not used to people confronting him. He interacts with the press a lot and the press asks him questions, but he is used to people surrounding him who are just agreeing with him and telling him what he wants to hear.\par \par \uc2 And he gets viscerally angry when he encounters something that`s different. And he has built around him this bubble that he wanted to build. But the effect of that is that he is further dividing the country, as was just said, and it results in these moments in which he`s just sort of lashing out because this is something he`s not accustomed to.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But when you divide, divide, divide, you make the pot smaller and smaller.\par \par \uc2 Mark, why doesn`t the president ever make any attempt to unify the country, especially in times of tragedy, when we should come together?\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: It`s just not in his DNA, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think he thinks he won his first election, I think he thinks he won his second election by attacking and dividing the country, and that he has a core base of supporters who won`t leave him whether or not he shoots somebody on Fifth Avenue or not. So that`s just the way he`s wired, and I don`t think anything`s ever going to undo that.\par \par \uc2 No national tragedy that`s going to happen that`s going to make him think that he should react in a way that any other president would to bring the country together. So, I just don`t think we`re ever going to see that as long as Donald Trump`s president.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, well, then back to the politics of immigration, do Democrats need to connect the dots for people and explain that Trump`s immigration policy is feeding into the affordability crisis, Joel?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, of course it is, and I think some art Democrats are finding creative ways to do that.\par \par \uc2 But let`s also just, like, fan out here a little bit. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent a lot of time in his last campaign talking about, as he would put it, illegal immigration. It turns out it`s actually not about -- quote -- "people who are undocumented or in the country illegally."\par \par \uc2 It`s actually to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about changing the makeup of people who are here legally, because, if you will notice, in his posts over the last few days, he`s talked about going after the migration system, going after people who are already naturalized, talking about denaturalizing people.\par \par \uc2 Essentially, he and his board of sycophants and his oligarchs get to choose who`s in the country. That is the dream world that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. And smart Democrats will start framing the issue of immigration around that and, yes, connecting that to instability in the country, instability in our communities and instability in our economy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mark, Trump and \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  may see this as justification for mass deportation and a massive reduction in immigration, but will the American voter agree?\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Well, already they`re not, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 And then back to your point about immigration as a connection to affordability, well, look, ask farmers across the country now what they`re thinking about and how their -- what -- how their economy is doing. And this is particularly true in red states around the country.\par \par \uc2 So -- and because it`s those kind of operations that more than anybody in this country need immigrant labor. So, I -- and the -- this administration is hiding government statistics. I think that`s one of the reasons why, is that it`s going to show that a lot of the labor shortage and labor problems are directly related to immigrants.\par \par \uc2 And that directly feeds into the affordability problem. And I think Democrats will definitely be talking about that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Philip, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  posted on social media a call for something I had not heard before, remigration now. That is a term tied to white nationalists.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:09]\par \par \uc2 I`d like you to walk our audience through it and what you make of our \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  now amplifying it in their -- on their official page account.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes, I mean, I think it`s important that people understand that this is not a novel occurrence for that account. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 `s account and many people that work for or are voices of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  have embraced this sort of white ethno- nationalism language and rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 There have been a lot of posts which have sort of seized upon this idea of the sort of the triumphant white worker prevailing over the immigrant hordes, right? This rhetoric, it`s just, like, sort of astonishing to see. You ask any historian, if you point to it, and any historian will just tell you there are echoes in history of when we have seen this sort of language before.\par \par \uc2 The idea here is that people are fundamentally unwelcome in the United States and ought to go back to the countries from which they came, which is a sharp break just by itself from the sense that we have, I think, all had for a long time of what the United States was and what immigration meant to the United States, which is that this was a refuge for people who needed somewhere to go, the people who had been kicked out of their own countries, the people who weren`t able to stay there, the people who were -- who had need and were looking for a country that would actually bless them with opportunity.\par \par \uc2 That`s what the United States was meant to be. But now we`re seeing this sort of rhetoric where it`s like, go home, get out of here, we don`t want you, coming from these official voices and vehicles and using a language that has these really, really shocking historical echoes.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- it is exactly what it looks like. And it is the sort of thing that I sincerely did not believe we would ever see in the United States. And now we`re seeing it, and it`s seeing -- we`re seeing it so often that we tend to tune it out. And we certainly can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we`re not going to tune it out here.\par \par \uc2 Mark, I -- as soon as -- for the last 24 hours, I was actually thinking about you, because the president, he now could target other Afghan nationals, those who helped our country, our country, during the war there. You are someone who advised \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Well, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the ironies just keep stacking up that -- the points that`s been made earlier in the program, that those Afghanis that came here, including this -- the suspect, are people who were defending America, were fighting the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And Trump`s calling a reporter stupid because she`s asking about the policy that Republicans called for to bring back -- they criticized Biden for not bringing back more Afghanis, who were freedom fighters.\par \par \uc2 They were fighting for America. I mean, they were in every sense of the word of patriots for us. So, I mean, it`s just -- the logic stream, as usual, of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just doesn`t add up. But it`s particularly true as you think about -- I mean, that`s a long, complicated conflict in Afghanistan that goes back many, many administrations.\par \par \uc2 But it`s a pretty clear point and not too complicated to say that these people were fighting for America, and that they were real patriots when they were doing that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, gentlemen, I want you to stay with us. We have got more to cover, but we`re going to take a quick break.\par \par \uc2 When we return: Health care premiums are about to skyrocket unless Republicans negotiate on the \uc2 ACA\uc2  subsidies. There`s only one problem. They can`t seem to get on the same page.\par \par \uc2 And later: how dinner with strangers has helped behavioral scientist Jon Levy uncover what it takes to be a great leader. I sat down with him to talk all about it.\par \par \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR just getting under way on a very special Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:28:31]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Time is running out for lawmakers to extend health care subsidies, and Republicans, well, they`re the ones in charge, and here`s the problem. They`re not sure what to do about it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Americans are finding out how much more expensive health insurance is about to get. According to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , next year, \uc2 ACA\uc2  insurers are raising premiums 26 percent on average, subsidy or not. But if the tax credit expires, enrollees could be paying -- are you ready for this number? Sit down, swallow your drink. I don`t want you to choke or spit it out -- 114 percent more.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is still here.\par \par \uc2 Mark, let`s talk about this issue, because Republicans are trapped between conservative philosophy and political reality. What do you think is going to happen here?\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: The same thing has happened for over a decade that the Republicans have been arguing to take away Obamacare. The problem is, they don`t have an alternative. And they haven`t for over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 The former Speaker of the House \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  has said that Republicans will never be able to agree on a plan. So the fact is, they`re arguing against something, but they don`t have an alternative. And they never have had. Have we seen a blueprint? Have we seen a draft? We haven`t seen anything.\par \par \uc2 And, as I said, it`s been over a decade. So the notion that they`re suddenly going to come up with one -- and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was trying to, and Mike Johnson stepped in and said, please don`t bring it up because we can`t agree on anything.\par \par \uc2 So it`s simply not going to happen. And this is -- they`re going to pay the piper here soon, because people aren`t going to be able to pay those subsidies that you just talked about, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 [19:30:07]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So that`s Republicans who are not on the same page.\par \par \uc2 Joel, Democrats obviously did not secure the tax credit extension during the shutdown. So where do they stand on this issue?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, one thing you can say about Democrats is, whether it was on purpose or by happenstance, they actually did manage to elevate this issue to the national consciousness.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think one of the side effects of the former shutdown that lasted about 40 days a couple of weeks ago, doesn`t it seem like time is flying? No one remembers that.\par \par \uc2 But that shutdown elevated this issue, and it brought it to the forefront, and it reinforced that Democrats historically fight for more health care support for the American people, and Republicans fight any efforts to expand health care access for the American people. And that is a feeling that already exists within the electorate. So it`s an easy feeling to reinforce.\par \par \uc2 Two other thoughts about this, Stephanie, really quickly. One, Republicans have a coalition now that they need to represent that they are not comfortable representing them in their full needs. They have a coalition of people who are dependent upon government services to support their health care needs, to make health care more affordable.\par \par \uc2 They chased that coalition in 2024. They are not in a position to actually represent those folks, one. Two, I had something to do with the Affordable Care Act some 15 years ago. I worked in Harry Reid`s leadership office in the first two years of Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s presidency.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans fought tooth and nail to prevent the \uc2 ACA\uc2  from becoming law back then because they knew, as soon as the American people saw that there was a reasonable solution to lower the cost of health care and to make health care more affordable and accessible, that the American people would never turn their back on it.\par \par \uc2 This is a thing that they knew that the American people needed and would ultimately want. And I think it`s interesting that proof positive as we stand here today that Republicans find themselves fighting political rhetoric that they have been propagating for the last two decades that the American people fully do not support.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Philip, overall health care is getting more and more expensive even if you get it through an employer. Is the current path that we`re on even sustainable?\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Well, that`s a whole different question.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I mean, one of the issues that we`re having here is the fact that the population is getting older. As you get older, your health care costs tend to increase, for obvious reasons. So there are external factors that are playing a role here.\par \par \uc2 I do think it`s sort of amazing. And I think the point that was just made about how the passage of the \uc2 ACA\uc2  was meant to establish something that was concrete and that people would rally around and now have. It took a while. Democrats were sort of waiting for that to occur.\par \par \uc2 But I think the other important thing, to the first question you asked, is that this was also itself a compromise piece of legislation. The Democrats in 2004 into 2008 were advocating for Medicare for all, were advocating for something much more sweeping.\par \par \uc2 This was actually a right-leaning idea proposed by \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and advocated by \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and others like that was meant to be sort of the compromise in the middle ground that ended up getting passed. So part of the reason that Republicans don`t have an answer is that this was sort of cribbed from the Republicans 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 This is something that was taken from across the aisle to an extent. And that`s part of the challenge. And I think that Democrats are well- positioned. The challenge, of course, that they have got and that we saw during the shutdown fight is that they don`t have the power in government.\par \par \uc2 And without the power in government, then it means these things are at risk, that it could be nibbled at. But as it`s nibbled at, those become points, exactly the point you just heard, where the Democrats can then attack the Republicans as being the ones who are doing -- the ones that are tearing it away.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mark, I see you nodding.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Yes, I just -- I actually felt I`d forgotten about that very, very salient point, which is basically the plan that was adopted was the plan that was initially proposed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So it really was a compromise on the Democrats` part. But once that became law, there was no longer a Republican position, because there`s nothing -- nothing else is defensible. And that`s where Republicans have found themselves in this cul-de-sac that they can`t get out of.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s political dynamite. I really think -- and it`s obvious that they knew that, and that`s why they put off the subsidies going away until after the mid -- until after the election. So it is -- it is fraught. It is fraught. And there is no solution in sight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, we have got a new topic.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " published an article that raised questions about President Trump`s age and his stamina. This was not a surprise. The president was unhappy. He called it a hit piece.\par \par \uc2 Mark, what do you think? I mean, of course, he`s not going to like it.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Deja vu, man. Where have we heard this before?\par \par \uc2 This just goes back to points that we were making in the last election, which is that there`s a reason that, in the United States, you can`t be 66 years old and be a park ranger or a commercial airline pilot.\par \par \uc2 [19:35:08]\par \par \uc2 As you get older, things get worse. And I don`t care how good you are. You`re not going to be as good at 80 as you were at 65. And that`s just inevitable. That`s the physics of health and the physics of aging. And Trump -- and we`re seeing it with Trump now.\par \par \uc2 So everybody who was screaming about Biden, I hope that you`re -- it`s coming back on you now, because let`s see the MRIs of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, we know we had them, but we don`t know what the results are.\par \par \uc2 Joel, in case people forgot, right, President Biden, he left office at 82. He was forced out of the presidential race because of age concerns after the debate. What are your thoughts now that we`re having those same conversations and it`s about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, Stephanie, I have been doing television panels like this for 10 years. And I`m sure, if you go back and look at me 10 years ago, when I started, you see these gray hairs now, you see the less hair that I have on top of my head. That`s aging.\par \par \uc2 Age comes for all of us. And so it was always silly that Republicans were kind of selling themselves on this idea that, like, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to be beyond the grasp of aging and beyond what the effects of the office is.\par \par \uc2 Even if you are doing the presidency at the, let`s just say, remedial level that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing it -- I don`t think any of us think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is necessarily doing this job at the level that some of our other presidents have done it in the past -- the job ages you.\par \par \uc2 You have to fly five time zones. You have to be on the phone with world leaders all the time. You have to deal with complicated, very consequential issues. That weighs on you. Every single one of our presidents look like they have aged 25 years.\par \par \uc2 And so what Republicans have now, along with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being deeply unpopular, is, they are tied to a leader who sounds more and more each passing day like someone who sounds like yesterday and doesn`t sound like tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And, in politics, that is a place that you do not want to be in. You do not want to be in a place where you are rallying around someone who sounds like what was, as opposed to what is. And that`s a bigger problem that Republicans have right now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but it`s not just the Republican Party, Philip. And it`s not just the president, right?\par \par \uc2 Congress, members of Congress, lots of them are really old, and they`re still really powerful. And when you try to bring it up, people go off and say, it`s ageist, it`s inappropriate, though, to Mark`s point, you can`t fly a plane, you can`t be a park ranger, you can`t sit on a corporate board after a certain age, OK?\par \par \uc2 Both parties seem to have struggled to foster the next generation. What`s going to change that? Because we have people who we would not want to drive on a highway or pick up a heavy box as leading our government.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, and you know this. I had a book come out in 2023 that looked at the Baby Boom and how the Baby Boom had now passed into the age where they were of retirement age and older. This is what is happening, is part of the reason America is getting older is that there are all these people born in the `40s and `50s and into the `60s who are now older people, who are now retirement age and senior citizens.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of those -- some of those people are members of Congress. And there are -- some of those people are the president of the United States, who was born the first year of the Baby Boom, right? So this is demography fundamentally.\par \par \uc2 And I think to Joel`s point that where this will be judged is in algorithms, that I think this debate over what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was doing and what Trump was doing, it all comes down to what they actually did as president. And that will be where the political -- the politics comes in.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, then.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you for joining me this evening.\par \par \uc2 When we return: dinner with a purpose -- how my next guest throws dinner parties to learn the secret to being a great leader.\par \par \uc2 My conversation with Jon Levy is next. You`re going to want to see this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:43:37]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: These days, it can be hard to find leaders you can look up to.\par \par \uc2 I recently sat down with someone who has hosted literally thousands of dinner parties with leaders across the industry to answer one important question: What makes a great leader?\par \par \uc2 As he explains in his new book, the answer may surprise you. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And joining me now, Jon Levy, CEO and founder of The Influencers and author of the new book "Team Intelligence: How Brilliant Leaders Unlock Collective Genius."\par \par \uc2 I just need to know. I`m so glad you`re here. I need to understand. You started these dinners. You`re now doing them 15 cities all around the world, but you started them in your apartment on the Upper West Side.\par \par \uc2 JON LEVY, AUTHOR, "TEAM INTELLIGENCE: HOW BRILLIANT LEADERS UNLOCK COLLECTIVE GENIUS": Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Who the hell wanted to come over? How did you get them to do this?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: So what I ended up doing was realizing that, if I want to figure out how to meet you, I need to create something that makes so much curiosity that makes you want to leave your house and say, I want to go there.\par \par \uc2 So I had to have the most interesting people. I`d have a design that made you think, holy cow, this is -- I have never heard of anything like this. And so the guests actually, when they arrive, they`re not allowed to talk about what they do or even give their last name.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But even before that, how did you get anyone to say yes, right? I don`t even want to go to my sister`s house for dinner.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: That`s so funny.\par \par \uc2 The answer is that people came because they thought other fancy people were coming. And so, in the early days, it wasn`t as impressive. Nowadays, it`s Nobel laureates, Olympians and astronauts, sometimes a princess or a, I don`t know, head of state.\par \par \uc2 [19:45:06]\par \par \uc2 But in the early days, it was much simpler. And I just kept doing it and doing it. And people would show up. And then I realized something completely insane. Most really successful people`s e-mail addresses are publicly available.\par \par \uc2 And so I just started e-mailing them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re kidding?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: No, no.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. And so, tell me, what have you learned over these years?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Well, the first thing we kind of realized is that...\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And you -- hold on. You would just say, hey, StephanieRuhle.com...\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: ... I`m pretending I`m your special person?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Feel like coming over for dinner?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: I`d say: I host a dinner party where the guests are not allowed to talk about what they do or even give their last name. We`re going to cook a meal together.\par \par \uc2 And the food is going to be mediocre at best. And when we sit down to eat, we`re going to play a game to guess what everybody does. And then you`re going to find out that you`re going to meet the most interesting people you have ever had a conversation with.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Who`s the most interesting person who`s come to your house for dinner?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Oh, my God. I have hosted 4,000 people. So...\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, great. Who`s your wow?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: My wow?\par \par \uc2 I`d say Emily Rafferty, who used to run The Met. She`s one of the most extraordinary women I have ever met in my life.\par \par \uc2 Julie Gerberding, the former director of the CDC, I have never met a more loving human being in my life.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And I thought he was going to say Emily Ratajkowski. And I would have said, my husband is looking to have dinner with her too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, the most -- they were the most -- and what have you learned through this?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: So what`s amazing is that we have this image of leaders as these ultra-charismatic visionary people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: And I have to be honest, most of the people that come to my dinners are wildly famous or they`re wildly effective, but they don`t have any of those characteristics.\par \par \uc2 They tend to have just different skills. And what we discovered was what the real defining characteristic of a leader was. And it`s none of the things we thought. The actual thing has to do with how you felt when you were in high school on a Sunday at 6:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember the Sunday scaries?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I was going to say, filled with anxiety every single Sunday. And guess what? I still have it.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Yes. And most of us still do.\par \par \uc2 But on Friday at 1:00 p.m., we get so excited. We -- even though we`re still at work.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s the freaking weekend.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 And so, notice, human beings don`t relate to the present. We relate to the future that we think we have. And that means the reason that I follow you as a leader is because, when I interact with you, you make me feel that there will be a new and better future.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want you to think about this. It means that I don`t necessarily need to like the person, right? They don`t need to be qualified. It`s an emotional response. And, in fact, I want you to think about leaders around the world. We will ignore and forgive their transgressions and failures as long as they make us feel that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LEVY: I`m talking about any and every leader.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What -- no, no, no, but when you`re making that description, right, you don`t have to like the person, the person doesn`t have to be qualified,but they`re making you feel like they are going to make America great again, they`re going to offer you something better in the future, when you were describing that, were you saying I`m sending you the message that this is Trump?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: I`m saying that, if we look at any leader that people follow, whether it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right -- I`m, frankly, not a very political person.\par \par \uc2 But you -- after the last election, people said, why did people vote for him, right? Oh, he may have broken these laws. He may have done all these things. How could that be the case? And when you look at the options that people had, they were either a candidate who said, I will hold strong to whatever we have been doing and we will keep doing it...\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And people said, I don`t like what I have right now.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 They said essentially that the contract of America, where every generation does better than the previous one, has been broken. So why would I want more of this? Meanwhile, another candidate said something else.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But the book isn`t just -- your book isn`t just about single leaders. It`s team intelligence.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 So here`s what`s wild. When the research actually looked at it, a singular leader, just because we follow them doesn`t mean they`re going to be effective. The smallest unit of effectiveness is a team. And so what actually predicts an effective team? It`s none of the things we have been told.\par \par \uc2 So it doesn`t matter how smart the smartest person is on the team, average intelligence or even for people to like each other. The single greatest predictor is this, adding you to the team does more than adding me to the team. And what do I mean by that? It`s the number of women on the team.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Yes. So the reason isn`t chromosomes, right? So I don`t want your male listeners to be like, oh, my God, I`m out of a job and nobody`s going to want me.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s emotional intelligence?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: My God, I might be your next featured dinner guest.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LEVY: I`d love to have you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s about emotional intelligence.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Yes, because when you go from a single person activity, like if it`s golf or tennis, then it`s pure talent. It`s I.Q., right?\par \par \uc2 But when you`re a team, it`s more about passing than it is about shooting. At that point...\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s about feeling and connecting and understanding where the holes are and filling them in.\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you sound like you could have written the book.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow. My goodness.\par \par \uc2 Jon, congratulations. By the way, I can`t wait to come to dinner.\par \par \uc2 [19:50:00]\par \par \uc2 LEVY: It would be an honor. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Before we go, I want to take just a moment to talk about the victims of the horrific National Guard shooting that took place earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-year-old specialist Sarah Beckstrom joined the West Virginia National Guard in 2023, the very same day she graduated from high school. She was assigned to the Military Police Company and deployed to D.C. in August. According to a friend, she had hopes of becoming an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent.\par \par \uc2 [19:55:06]\par \par \uc2 Beckstrom also spent her time working for a community health center that serves people with mental illness and substance abuse disorders. Loved ones describe her as having a heart of gold who was hard not to love. She passed away on Thanksgiving evening with her family right by her side.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-one -- excuse me -- 24-year-old Andrew Wolfe, he joined the West Virginia National Guard in 2019. Over the years, he rose to the rank of staff sergeant and earned multiple awards. A friend described Wolfe as one of the strongest, determined and God-fearing men she knows. He remains in critical condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 Our hearts go out to the victims and their families, as we pray for Staff Sergeant Andrew Wolfe`s full, full recovery.\par \par \uc2 We need a deep breath tonight, for sure.\par \par \uc2 On that note, I wish you all a very safe and a very good night. From all of us here at MS NOW, thanks for staying up late with me. I will see you at the end of Monday.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2025 Versant Inc.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020251129elbs00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Last Word With Lawrence O`Donnell for November 17, 2025, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Ken Dilanian \par \uc2 7,491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 November 2025\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:36]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MS NOW HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Your coverage around the country about what`s happening in this kind of resistance that that we`ve never needed before and never seen before is really inspiring to see people out there just taking this situation as it comes unpredictably and innovating and in this collective way to protect other people, to protect their neighbors. Really remarkable to watch.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MS NOW HOST, "TRMS": Yeah, it`s -- what`s interesting to me now is that it`s we`re now seeing enough of a pattern from the Trump administration that we`re seeing cities learning from each other. Best practices being passed on, people learning what works both legally but also practically which I think is going to be really important moving forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, and, Rachel, a certain amount of that learning is actually happening I believe in your hour. I think people around the country are seeing what you`re presenting, other people are doing and showing this is - - okay, this is what`s happening here. Here`s what`s going on in Charlotte. Here`s what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 And other -- other places in the country are watching that. They`re taking notes. And when it comes their way, uh they are learning about how to rise up to this.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, it`s going to be interesting, Lawrence, to see later this week, we are expecting a protest in Washington. And all the other protests have not centered on Washington thus far. The 50501 protest, the hands-off protest, the "No Kings" protests, but this weekend, this Saturday -- noon on Saturday at the Lincoln Memorial is going to be a protest in Washington, preceded by a bunch of people lobbying their members of Congress on Thursday. And it`s going to be really interesting to see if the tenor and the feel that we`ve got right now in the country manifests in Washington with a big day of protest like I think we`re going to see.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, there`s also that big groundswell of apparently Republican votes for releasing the Epstein files in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL; And now also very clearly in the Senate. So, there`s these two giant waves now coming into Washington, Donald Trump`s not going to like either one of them.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s exactly right. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, as air force one was landing last night after another taxpayer-funded weekend trip to Florida, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted the most dramatic and important reversal of his political career. Even more important than his admission in September of 2016 that he had been lying about President Obama`s birth certificate since 2011. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began his reversal last night in writing with a lie, saying, quote, "As I said on Friday night aboard air force one to the fake news media, House Republicans should vote to release the Epstein files."\par \par \uc2 He never said that on Friday night. That was a lie. And so, in the biggest reversal of Donald Trump`s political career, he had to pretend that he had already reversed himself. And that wasn`t a reversal at 9:15 p.m. last night, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted out those words, "House Republicans should vote to release the Epstein files."\par \par \uc2 What did it take? What did it take to force him? And you know, he was forced to force him to tweet those words. Fifty-one years ago, three Republicans went to the White House to talk to Republican President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  to tell him the worst news of his political career. The most famous Republican senator at the time along with the Republican Senate minority leader and the Republican house minority leader sat in the oval office with the president and they told him he was going to lose the biggest vote of his political career in the House of Representatives and in the Senate and it was going to be a massive vote more than two-thirds in both bodies.\par \par \uc2 Those Republican congressional leaders told their Republican president that he was going to be impeached by the Democratic controlled House of Representatives with a massive Republican vote. And he was going to be convicted in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , controlled by Democrats, but with a massive Republican vote.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:01]\par \par \uc2 And here is what they told reporters gathered in the White House driveway. The first speaker you`re about to hear is Senator Barry Goldwater, who had been the Republican nominee for president nine years earlier. The second speaker you will hear is John Rhodes, then the minority leader of the House, followed by the Senate Minority leader, Hugh Scott, who reveals the most to those reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THEN-SEN. BARRY GOLDWATER (R-AZ): We made no suggestions. We were merely there to offer what we see as a condition on both floors.\par \par \uc2 Now, Congressman John?\par \par \uc2 THEN-REP. JOHN RHODES (R-AZ): Well, actually, I have nothing to add except to say that the president did seem to be in good spirits and good health and as four old friends, we talked over a situation which of course is very painful for all of us.\par \par \uc2 THEN-SEN. HUGH SCOTT (R-VA): We have told him that the situation is very gloomy on Capitol Hill and that it is a very distressing situation and we gave him further evaluations which I think ought to remain between ourselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Very gloomy on Capitol Hill, very distressing situation. The day after Hugh Scott described that very distressing situation, \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  announced he was resigning the presidency. Is \uc2 John Thune\uc2  the new Hugh Scott?\par \par \uc2 Senator John Thun has Hugh Scott`s old job in the Senate as the Republican leader. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  already knew, as we were reporting last week, that there was probably going to be more than enough votes in the House of Representatives to vote to release the Epstein files. The number built toward the end of the week to indicate from reliable sources that there could be a hundred Republican votes in the House of Representatives in addition to the 214 Democrats to release the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 That kind of vote in the House of Representatives would create enormous pressure in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , pressure that \uc2 John Thune\uc2  must have decided he would not be able to sidestep. \uc2 John Thune\uc2  must have calculated two things. First, if the Democrats asked for a vote in the Senate on releasing the Epstein files, they could easily pass the vote threshold required to force that vote in the Senate. \uc2 John Thune\uc2  had to calculate what the final vote count would be.\par \par \uc2 And there is a very strong chance that John Thune`s vote count went well over 90 and possibly close to a hundred if not exactly, including \uc2 John Thune\uc2  himself. And that meant that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could not count on the Senate blocking a vote to release the Epstein files. That meant that the bill to release the Epstein files would pass the House, pass the senate, and would end up on Donald Trump`s desk, where he would have the decision to sign or veto a bill that would probably have landed on his desk with a veto proof majority already in both the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Mike Johnson, the weak Republican speaker of the House, could not deliver that news to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . He could not speak for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  . Only \uc2 John Thune\uc2  could.\par \par \uc2 And so, the question is something the history books will answer for us or probably other investigative reporting much sooner than that. The question is who told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  what was going to happen in the House and more importantly in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , especially the Senate. Who was the Hugh Scott this time? Who was the Barry Goldwater?\par \par \uc2 And did the Republicans dare tell this directly to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ? Or was it communicated just to Donald Trump`s White House chief of staff or someone else who could have the pleasure of then delivering the bad news to the bad president? If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thought \uc2 John Thune\uc2  could block a vote on the Epstein files, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could have held on to the position he`s held on to every day of his presidency that the Epstein files should never be released.\par \par \uc2 The Trump legal team, formerly known as the Justice Department, announced that the case was officially closed on July 7th of this year in an unsigned written statement saying, quote, "The Department of Justice and the federal bureau of investigation have conducted an exhaustive review of investigative holdings relating to \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  . We did not uncover evidence that could predicate an investigation against uncharged third parties. It is the determination of the Department of Justice and the federal bureau of investigation that no further disclosure would be appropriate or warranted."\par \par \uc2 [22:10:10]\par \par \uc2 The reason it was impossible for any previous administration to release the Epstein files is that the criminal investigation and prosecution of \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  and the criminal investigation of his convicted co-conspirator, Ghislaine Maxwell, were underway during those previous administration. Ghislaine Maxwell`s appeal to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  was still pending through the end of the Biden administration into the Trump administration. And so, it would have been inappropriate to release the Epstein files until Ghislaine Maxwell`s appeal was rejected by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  and until the Trump legal team announced that they were formally closing the case on July 7th.\par \par \uc2 That made that case closed announcement on July 7th at the very same time that they announced that they would not ever under any circumstances release a page of the Epstein files. That was the July 7th position that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his legal team at the justice department believed that they could hold for the rest of the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidency and beyond. That position lasted exactly 132 days. And then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  completely reversed his position last night, saying that every Republican in the House should vote to release the Epstein files, which means logically that every Republican in the senate should vote to release the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 And then today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was asked if he would sign a bill delivered to him after passage in the House and the Senate to release the Epstein files. And he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: You urged House Republicans to vote in favor of this Epstein release bill they`re going to vote on tomorrow. I just want to be super clear on your position. Do you want to see that pass the Senate? Would you sign that bill if it gets to your desk?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I do want to say -- here`s what I want. We have nothing to do with Epstein. The Democrats do. All of his friends were Democrats. Some of the people that we mentioned are being looked at very seriously for their relationship to \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  , but they were with him all the time. I wasn`t -- I wasn`t at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Reality note, just have to interrupt here. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent more time with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  than any other political person mentioned in the Epstein files. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only person, the only politician in the Epstein files who we`ve seen, as we are seeing right now, him captured on video and photographs, constantly partying with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  said he was Donald Trump`s closest friend for 10 years. And he said that on tape. So, no, the people \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to pretend were with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  all the time, as he put it, were not with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  nearly as much as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  at the peak of Jeffrey Epstein`s criminal conduct before \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  was ever charged with the crime.\par \par \uc2 Here is how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  finished that answer about would he sign the legislation ordering the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : I`m for any -- I don`t -- they can do whatever they want. We`ll give them everything. Sure, I would. Let the -- let the Senate look at it. Let anybody look at it, but don`t talk about it too much because honestly, I don`t want to take it away from us.\par \par \uc2 It`s really a Democrat problem. The Democrats were Epstein`s friends.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In a tweet, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threw Bill Clinton`s name on a list of men and told this lie. Records show that these men and many others spent large portions of their life with Epstein. And on his island, stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  himself in writing said \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  never set foot on Jeffrey Epstein`s island. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  has said he never set foot on Jeffrey Epstein`s island. And the records we have now of Bill Clinton`s flights on Jeffrey Epstein`s plane do not indicate that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  spent more than two trips to Europe and Africa on the Epstein plane with a number of stops on each of those trips in Europe and Africa. That looks like a lot of flights, but they are really just one trip with multiple stops. Those trips were less than two weeks long.\par \par \uc2 And so, the Epstein files are still really about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . No matter what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries to say. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  proves that he thinks the Epstein files are about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he has decided that his otherwise flawless ally, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , is now what he calls a traitor because she was one of the original Republicans in the House supporting a vote on the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:03]\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in tweets now, even after saying Republicans should vote to release the Epstein files, calls \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  wacky Marjorie traitor brown and a traitor to our country.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  says those attacks by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are putting her life in danger. And now, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is joining me in calling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a liar, something I`ve been doing since 2011.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  tweeted, "President Trump just attacked me and lied about me. It`s astonishing really how hard he`s fighting to stop the Epstein files from coming out. And he actually goes to this level. But I don`t worship or serve \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Most Republicans in the House and Senate have and do worship and serve \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ."\par \par \uc2 But if they see one or two like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  not just survive by defying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , but beat him, win in their defiance of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , beat him publicly, then Donald Trump`s grip on this government is looser tonight than it was yesterday.\par \par \uc2 There`s going to be a massive rebellion against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the House of Representatives when that vote is conducted tomorrow. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that. He knew that that vote was coming. And so, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been trying to label it as something other than a rebellion. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to label it as now they`re following his orders to vote to release the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Mmassie has correctly identified what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is really up to. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  revealed that in a tweet on Friday when he said, quote, "I will be asking AG Pam Bondi and the department of justice together with our great patriots at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to investigate Jeffrey Epstein`s involvement and relationship with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  , \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  , Reid Hoffman, \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  , and many other people and institutions to determine what was going on with them and him."\par \par \uc2 And so, there`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying Pam Bondi was wrong on July 7th. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  was wrong. the Justice Department was wrong on July 7th when they put in writing that in their case closed statement, quote, "We did not uncover evidence that could predicate an investigation against uncharged third parties."\par \par \uc2 And now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has decided that uncharged third parties like \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  must be investigated even though the Pam Bondi Justice Department and the Kashyap Patel FBI issued a written statement saying there`s absolutely no evidence to do that. There is no predicate at all for investigating \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  or any other names in the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why it`s not the Justice Department anymore. That`s why it`s the Trump revenge department. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will order an investigation of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and any other name in the Epstein files that he doesn`t like. And that investigation ordered by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will once again seal off the Epstein files, no matter what the vote in the house or Senate is.\par \par \uc2 And that is the scheme correctly identified by Republican Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  , who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been attacking since the day \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  launched his crusade to release the Epstein files. And \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  , and this is a lesson to all Republicans, \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  is still standing. \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Here is Congressman Massie yesterday before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told all Republicans to vote for the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): I think we could have a deluge of Republicans. There could be a hundred or more. I`m hoping to get a veto proof majority on this legislation when it comes up for a vote. And you know, the president`s been saying this is a hoax. He`s been saying that for months.\par \par \uc2 Well, he`s just now decided to investigate a hoax if it`s a hoax. And I have another concern about these investigations that he`s announced. If they have ongoing investigations in certain areas, those documents can`t be released. So, this might be a big smoke screen, these investigations, to open a bunch of them to as a last-ditch effort to prevent the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And tonight, \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  , who continues to prosecute his case, added to that observation about the smokescreen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will try to create to block the release of the Epstein files, no matter how big the vote is in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, I`m concerned that now he`s opening a flurry of investigations and I believe they may be trying to use those investigations as a predicate for not releasing the files.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:03]\par \par \uc2 That`s my concern.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: You think they`ll try to block it?\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, they will. I`m afraid they`re going to try to use a provision of the law that um allows you not to release these materials if they`re subject of an ongoing investigation would harm and the release of which would harm the ongoing investigation. If they`re serious about it, they should release them right now. I do believe they`ll try to stop it somewhere else and that`s going to backfire on them too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is Democratic Congressman Robert Garcia of California. He`s the top Democrat on the House Oversight Committee leading the pursuit of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Garcia, thank you very much for joining us tonight. And I just want to pick up where Congressman Massie just left off where he`s identified what he believes is the scheme here to try to put suddenly start an investigation of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and other people even though the Justice Department has already said there`s nothing there. There`s absolutely nothing to investigate. No reason to do it.\par \par \uc2 But that investigation involving a grand jury possibly would then lock up these documents legally. But Congressman Massie says he believes you can continue to fight even through that smokescreen to try to get them.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBERT GARCIA (D-CA): I think I lost you there.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, I`m just wondering, Congressman, if you agree with \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  , that there`s a way of continuing to push for these Epstein files, even if Donald Trump`s trying to create a legal smokescreen to block their release even after the vote.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Yeah. Well, let me just start by saying that I agree with \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  . I think this is absolutely what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to do. When you have someone that`s been obsessed from day one in trying to cover up to deflect -- to campaign first, he wants him released then he doesn`t his actions over the last week have been outrageous, what he did with \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  bringing her into the Situation Room, this is clearly somebody now that began to panic.\par \par \uc2 And so, this last flip-flop is clearly devised as a strategy to continue to cover up the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 Now, we think through our work, through the subpoena work we`ve done through the oversight committee, we`ve got other legal avenues in front of us, we have a plan in place to try to ensure that we get those files regardless of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries to do in this investigation. But it`s clear he is setting up another chapter to deflect and to try to ensure that survivors don`t get any justice.\par \par \uc2 And so, I got to hand it to \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  . He`s been a true partner and hero, but he`s absolutely right here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to ask you a question that I would not have asked you last week. Is it possible you`re going to get a unanimous vote in the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: It is possible. Look, we had a goal of trying to get first we thought if we can get 25 Republicans that would be a huge win. Then we thought maybe we can get a hundred, and it was looking like we could last week.\par \par \uc2 We were going to get 100 Republicans likely before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began to panic and now it`s looking like there`s a strong possibility we`re going to get a unanimous vote and if that`s the case, it compels \uc2 John Thune\uc2  in a very big way to actually take that vote.\par \par \uc2 So we think it`s very possible.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, it`s -- there`s no way the Senate can ignore a massive vote like this coming out of the House. And that`s something that it seems like someone already communicated to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that you won`t be able to stop a vote in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: That`s exactly right. I mean, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he even said that he would sign it. What people need to remember with all of this back and forth is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right now today has the power to release the files. He doesn`t even need a vote of the Congress. There`s a subpoena in place from a committee.\par \par \uc2 He can release all the files right now, but he chooses not to because he`s leading this cover up. Pam Bondi is involved with the cover up. Mike Johnson is complicit in this cover up. And so, Donald Trump`s going to do whatever he can to protect himself, protect his wealthy friends, and to ensure that the truth doesn`t come out.\par \par \uc2 But what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needs to remember, and I`ve said this also to the DOJ, is there are there are hundreds of hardworking, good civil servants, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, law enforcement officials that have worked to put these files together. There are many people that know what`s in these files and who are not going to allow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from hiding, destroying, or not letting us get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Robert Garcia, thank you very much for starting off our coverage tonight.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a new report in "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2  " interviewing Justice Department officials who have been fired or forced to resign by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  contains this quote from former Justice Department official Dena Robinson. Quote, "I wouldn`t even call it the Justice Department anymore. It has become Trump`s personal law firm."\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here`s what Republican Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  said tonight now that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has told all Republicans to vote for the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: Well, he got tired of me winning, so he joined our side. I have no animosity toward him. I regret that it got personal for some folks, but it never has. For me, it`s always been about the survivors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  of Minnesota. She serves on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and the Senate Commerce Committee. Senator, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The -- it -- it`s hard for me to imagine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agreeing to this, unless he knew the Senate --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Completely.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- could not stop this for him. Absolutely couldn`t stop it for him.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Yes. I listened to your opening words, and I -- you couldn`t be more accurate. He was dead set against this, lobbying people, of course, calling them as recently as a week or two ago. And suddenly the change of course.\par \par \uc2 We know the votes were overwhelming in the House. I agree with your last guest, the congressman said, as well as Congressman Massie. But what`s going on in the Senate? Well, you can`t have a big vote in the House without also, I believe, having a strong vote in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 That means it would be veto-proof when it went over to the president. And even if he refused to sign it into law, as you know well, it would become law. So I think he had no choice. And he decided to say, hey, I don`t care at all.\par \par \uc2 What really worries me when he is starting this faux investigation, which he claims now trying to shift this over to democrats is they`ll use that as an excuse to actually refuse to release some of the documents, when what we need here for the victims is transparency.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When this comes to the Senate, and we now know that it absolutely will come to the Senate, procedurally, getting to that across that 60-vote threshold seems pretty obvious. I just asked Congressman Garcia a question I never expected to ask last week, and that was, could the vote in the House be unanimous? Could the vote in the Senate on this be unanimous?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Easily. Because I haven`t heard anyone come out strongly on the other side. The Senate`s been kind of quiet, a little busy with a few other things lately. And I would think when you start having conservatives, I was adding it up this weekend and I thought, who is going to be on the other side of this if there`s an overwhelming vote in the House?\par \par \uc2 So I think the president saw the writing on the wall, and I think it`s time to release all the files. And he -- he doesn`t have to do any more investigations, let`s just get all the files out there.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. I mean, he`s in possession of them right now. They -- they could release them tomorrow. They could just be on --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Completely.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- everyone`s desk tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Exactly. As they said from the very beginning, and then wouldn`t release them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So as we -- as we go forward, we`re -- we`re -- you`re dealing with the Justice Department exposed by "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2  " and by kind of the obvious turning over there that is really working for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , its really what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 And Todd Blanche, his former criminal defense lawyer is there. And -- and the idea that -- that they won`t continue to try to protect him in the Epstein files seems impossible and --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Impossible to believe.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- what -- with this investigation that they`re planning to start, if they empanel a grand jury and they just submit the entire Epstein file to the grand jury, doesn`t that seal off that material then as long as that grand jury is pending?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: You know, I don`t know if that is their dastardly plan, but they could try to do something like that. I found it interesting that you asked that question today when a magistrate judge actually came out in the Comey case and said, enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 This is so corrupt how you`ve done this, I`m putting words in his mouth. But he basically said that they made major errors there. Their -- the White House aide that was put in there to do a first grand jury case, major areas, including telling the grand jury that they didn`t even really have everything in front of them.\par \par \uc2 That there were other reasons that they should indict instead of just what the evidence was that was in front of them. That`s what she said. So I just think you have a Justice Department that has basically decided they`re going to do whatever he wants snap of his fingers.\par \par \uc2 You know, indict certain people despite what career prosecutors said, they say, how high can we jump? Prosecute people that he doesn`t like, how high can we jump? Protect his friends and pardon his friends, how high can we jump?\par \par \uc2 I was struck when the "Saturday Night Live" did the skit of the Senate Judiciary hearing, which I`m sure you saw, where Amy Poehler played Pam Bondi, as, of course, Pam Bondi hurled insults at every single member -- democratic member of the committee.\par \par \uc2 I realized at that moment that I trusted Amy Poehler as attorney general of the United States more than Pam Bondi, Lawrence. The point is, is that this has been corrupt.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:11]\par \par \uc2 There are still good work going on in some of the field offices, I got to tell you that doing their jobs, and I hope some of those good career people stay in their jobs. But what`s going on in Washington is appalling. as "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " story pointed out.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, the most incompetent \uc2 FBI\uc2  director in history, Kashyap Patel doesn`t just use an \uc2 FBI\uc2  private jet for date night, he`s also ordered a massive \uc2 FBI\uc2  security detail to accompany his Nashville girlfriend wherever she goes.\par \par \uc2 The first \uc2 FBI\uc2  director in history to order \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents to basically function as assistants for his girlfriend. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:54]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:40:25]\par \par \uc2 The most incompetent \uc2 FBI\uc2  director in history, Kashyap Patel, is not just using his \uc2 FBI\uc2  private jet, $60 million jet for date night. According to new reporting from MS NOW`s Ken Dilanian and Carol Leonnig, "\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel`s country music performer girlfriend currently enjoys the protection of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  security detail to shield her from potential threats."\par \par \uc2 "Prompting concerns that the arrangement may delay law enforcement from responding to other incidents. Two people with direct knowledge tell MS. NOW,. Alexis Wilkins, Patel`s girlfriend, has a security detail made up of elite \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, usually assigned to a SWAT team in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office in Nashville, where Wilkins spends time for her work."\par \par \uc2 "People familiar with \uc2 FBI\uc2  security protocols said they were unaware of any instance in which the girlfriends of top \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials received government staffed security details. SWAT team agents on a detail with Wilkins would presumably be unable to respond or delayed in responding to crises that might develop in their territory, such as mass shootings or terror attacks."\par \par \uc2 "According to the sources, who spoke on condition of anonymity to discuss sensitive security matters." And joining us now is Ken Dilanian, MS NOW Justice and Intelligence Reporter. Ken, this is really stunning material. The idea of a girlfriend getting an \uc2 FBI\uc2  security detail is one thing, but a SWAT team, that is a -- an astonishing diversion of resources.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MS NOW JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE REPORTER: Yes, Lawrence. And as you can imagine, since the story published this morning, I`ve gotten a lot of messages from current and former \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, who are absolutely appalled at what they are calling a waste of resources and an abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did not talk to us for this story, they didn`t respond to our request for comment. But today, they put out a statement saying that the reason Alexis Wilkins has his security detail is that she`s been targeted by what they call hundreds of credible death threats, but they declined to describe those threats or say anything else about them. And so we`re left with that explanation.\par \par \uc2 Look, nobody should be -- should be subjected to death threats, but there`s a lot of concern because this is a detail that is from the Nashville field office, which is not a huge field office. And many -- many of the members on this detail are coming from the SWAT team.\par \par \uc2 And FBI SWAT is kind of a rotation. You serve in it for a certain period of time, and you`re also doing other cases while you`re on the SWAT team and you`re investigating threats. And so obviously that is not happening if you were protecting Kash Patel`s 27-year-old girlfriend, you`re not available to -- to work on cases.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s a diversion of resources at a time when, you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is down personnel. And as you said, it`s never happened before because we -- I mean, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  directors tend to be married, the last couple were, and so they -- they had their own security detail.\par \par \uc2 And their wives traveled with them and they were protected. \uc2 Louis Freeh\uc2  was not married, and I`m not aware -- no one I`ve talked to is aware of any security detail for any significant other of his. And so this is just unprecedented. And, again, people are calling it an -- an abuse of power, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There`s also reporting in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " that the sort of ethics lawyer at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  wanted to find out ways to allow Kash Patel to take -- accept gifts that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director is not allowed to accept. And so this seems to be part of a pattern with this \uc2 FBI\uc2  director.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Yes. There are a lot of questions. We -- we`ve obviously seen Kash Patel appearing at sporting events, sitting in owners boxes. And we`ve asked, is he paying for these tickets? How -- how is he taking these trips?\par \par \uc2 You know, there was reporting that he was at this -- this luxury hunting lodge in Texas called The Boondoggle Ranch. Is he paying his own way? They`re not answering those questions. And so it does absolutely raise concerns about whether the ethics rules are being followed. We`re just not getting any explanation, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ken Dilanian, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, Virginia`s Governor-elect, Democrat \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  , won her election by 15 points in Virginia by winning the argument on affordability. Donald Trump`s new argument on affordability is to fight against the minimum wage, making people`s lives more affordable. That`s next with Governor-elect \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 [22:35:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:39]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s so-called messaging on affordability does not include increasing workers` incomes to help them afford what they need to buy. Today, while speaking to an audience of McDonald`s franchise owners, which includes some of the richest franchise owners in the world, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told them they`re going to have to fight the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: The minimum wage thing you have to be talking about, you`re going to have to fight.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:11]\par \par \uc2 We were talking about California. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  from California.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : And I knew he`s laying siege on the minimum wage. And you people probably know because that`s a very complex subject. And you people probably know better than anybody the impact one way or the other, good or bad. And you`re going to let your local congressmen, your senators know about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now for her first MS NOW interview since winning the Virginia`s governor-election is Governor-elect \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  of Virginia. She`s a former democratic congresswoman and CIA officer.\par \par \uc2 Governor-elect, thank you very much for joining us tonight. It is really great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): Thank you so much for having me. I`m glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So Virginia is one of those states that has a higher minimum wage than the federal minimum wage, significantly higher, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to think is a very bad idea. Telling people running McDonald`s franchises, you`re going to have to fight the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: I mean, what -- what we continue to hear from -- from this president is -- is just out of step with what, frankly, I heard on the ground over the past nearly two years of my campaign for governor.\par \par \uc2 People are struggling, people are really having a hard time making ends meet, whether it`s costs at the grocery store, the pharmacy counter, when they get their energy bill, when they sign their kids up for daycare, if they can find a spot, and housing prices continue to go up, whether you`re a renter or buyer.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is that life is just continues to be difficult. And to hear this president kind of so flippantly talk about something such as the minimum wage, not addressing real issues within the housing market, real issues within, you know, across communities where we see energy prices going up, you know, real issues that affect people in -- in -- in -- chiefly.\par \par \uc2 We will see increased healthcare costs continue to go up, in part because of the so-called one big beautiful bill. So he is wreaking havoc on our economy and on people, and ultimately not in any way being responsive.\par \par \uc2 And I -- I think we saw that in the results of the Virginia election, people want a governor who is going to be responsive to the challenges that they are facing on the ground in -- in their day-to-day lives.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If a -- if a democrat wins a state like Virginia by five points, other democrats want to know, how did you do it? What do we need to know? What do we need to know nationally to win by 15? You must --\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- have something that national democrats need to know about.\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: Well, I will say it`s the largest democratic margin of victory in Virginia since the Kennedy Administration. And the way that we did it, frankly, not just winning our three statewide seats, but also flipping 13 seats in the House of delegates, where we had tremendous candidates who want to represent their communities.\par \par \uc2 For me, it was all about traveling all across our state, listening to people, which sounds, you know, such a -- a simple thing. But -- but the reality is that far too often, people will get prescriptive about what it is that we need to do policy-wise.\par \par \uc2 But if you are on the ground, if you are listening, if you are mobilizing people, around the concerns that they have for themselves, for their kids, for their community, for their local economy people, people want a government that is responsive.\par \par \uc2 And -- and certainly whether it`s issues of lowering costs, issues of strengthening our schools or, you know, overall safety and security within our communities, people were really responsive to the campaigning that I did in every corner of our commonwealth, focused on really straightforward policies that are going to make an impact on the things that are, you know, front of mind for them.\par \par \uc2 The last thing you think about before you lay your head down at night, and the first thing you think about in the morning.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you talk about every corner of the commonwealth, I think of the corner that goes way down into Tennessee, shares the border with Tennessee, Tazewell, towns like that down there. And I looked at those voting returns, and you performed better in those places, which are very, very strongly republican places than any democrat I`ve seen running in that kind of region.\par \par \uc2 So you have found a way to talk to Trump voters and earn their vote. Is there -- is there something that you were -- that was specifically targeted at Trump voters and what you were presenting?\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: I think -- and -- and you`re talking about the corner of Virginia that is known as the place where Virginia begins. And -- and, you know, if you look at a map, it`s actually west of Detroit. Virginia is a --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: -- large state. And -- and in going to communities that don`t typically vote for democrats, that is admittedly the case, I -- I think what was important for me was to demonstrate interest and true desire to get to know people, to be present, to -- to demonstrate respect.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:16]\par \par \uc2 Because, you know, in -- in every part of Virginia, whether they`re historically republican voting or democratic voting or they`re -- they`re toss up communities, whether they`re, you know, down the street from my home, or they`re hours and hours away, people -- people want to feel rooted.\par \par \uc2 And people want to feel that their concerns matter and that their voices matter, that their communities matter, you know? And -- and I moved back home to Virginia 11 years ago to be closer to family. And -- and for me, I grew up in the suburbs of Richmond, I was able to move back home.\par \par \uc2 My husband was able to, you know, have his job remote, it -- it worked for us. But in so many parts of Virginia, that`s just not an option.\par \par \uc2 And so to even be able to speak to what it is to, you know, have that -- have that feeling of being rooted in a community that you love, a place where you want to raise your kids, that -- that`s not a -- that`s not a possibility for so many people who are getting either priced out of the communities they grew up in, or where there`s not enough economic opportunity for their kids to come back to.\par \par \uc2 Then you add on to that the challenges of child care deserts, and you add on to that, you know, changing economic frictions, the attack on the federal workforce we`re seeing coming out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 The fact that, no -- you know, no fewer than six rural hospitals across Virginia will close. You know, these -- these are serious challenges that - - that, you know, I intend to address and certainly heard about during my time on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  , governor-elect of Virginia, thank you very much for joining us here tonight.\par \par \uc2 SPANBERGER: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:55]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:27]\par \par \uc2 Virginia`s Govern-elect \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  gets tonight`s "Last Word." "The 11th Hour" with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2025 Versant Inc. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein  are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020251118elbh00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Last Word With Lawrence O`Donnell for September 05, 2025, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jen Psaki, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 8,597 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:01]\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Look, no further than October 11th when I and many of my amazing colleagues will be at MSNBC Live 25 in New York City, a day focused on you, all of our loyal viewers, we`re so excited about it. We`ll bring all the discussions you see on T.V. to life in an in-person experience. Nicolle Wallace will have Martin Sheen with her at the morning session, and I will be a part of the evening session with Rachel, Lawrence, Steph, Ari, and the great Ali Velshi.\par \par \uc2 Tickets are still available at MSNBC.com/live25, or you can scan the Q.R. code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me tonight from Chicago. THE LAST WORD is next with Ali Velshi in for Lawrence. Hey, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: What a great show you had. I thought the conversation with governor Pritzker was great. You know, I didn`t want to really dignify this topic, but earlier this week, Ted Cruz took a swipe at \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  on social media. I don`t know if you saw this. It was beneath the dignity of a U.S. senator. He said, I wouldn`t want to get between \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  and a Domino`s Pizza line, which is a wait joke. And then Pritzker came back at him and said, I wouldn`t want to get between Ted Cruz and a trip to Cancun when Texas is in an emergency, which I thought was --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Touche. Touche, Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That was a clap back, if I had ever seen one. But that was a really important interview. And I`m looking for this October 11th to being with a lot of our viewers and hearing directly from them. So, I look forward to being with you there as well.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It`s the best part. And since Lawrence is going to dance, I think, because you also do the 10:00 P.M., maybe that means you`re dancing too.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m going to do something. I`m going to be with Stephanie. Stephanie and I are going to do a Velshi rule thing, which will be --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Perfect. We love it. I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- which will be fun. I`m looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Have yourself a great weekend. I`ll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, I want to talk about the economy. How bad is the economy? So bad that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is now trying to distract from the economy and the jobs numbers that he got this morning by talking about \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posted at 9:56 A.M. after no doubt watching 90 minutes of coverage of the abysmal jobs report that the Epstein investigation, quote, is merely another Democrat hoax, just like Russia, Russia, Russia. The Department of Justice has done its job. They`ve given everything requested of them. It`s time to end the Democrat Epstein hoax and give the Republicans credit for the great even legendary job that they are doing, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Wow, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does everything possible not to talk about Epstein, but he needed you not to talk about the jobs numbers this morning. Here`s the result of the great, even legendary job that Trump and his rubberstamp Republicans are doing with the American economy. It created negative jobs.\par \par \uc2 Now, this almost never happens without a recession. The last time it happened was under Trump during the height of the pandemic in December of 2020. The jobs report today revised the June jobs number. Normally, it`s September, so we get the August numbers, but it revised some prior numbers. They revised the June jobs number to negative.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the economy created an anemic 22,000 jobs. That`s about a third of what economists had expected. The Wall Street Journal put it, quote, U.S. job growth continued to slow down in August with just 22,000 new jobs, a sign that the labor market is deteriorating markedly. Friday`s job report adds to a summer of slow hiring and points to a stagnant job market that has lengthened job searches, shut young people out of employment and increased unemployment for black workers.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department`s report also highlighted weaknesses from earlier in the year. The government revised its numbers from previous months and said that the economy lost a net 13,000 jobs in June. That was the first such decline since December of 2020. The losses in August were widespread with the reports showing drops in employment across sectors including manufacturing, finance, unemployment -- and finance, unemployment rose from 4.2 percent to 4.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, I just want to be clear, this is kind of the worst position that an economy can be in. Sometimes there`s things you can pull out of a jobs report that are good. There`s nothing in this jobs report that was good. There were no silver linings there. There were healthcare jobs created, but there have been healthcare jobs created in every single month for the last many, many, many, many years. So, we always create healthcare jobs. This is emergency territory.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  now probably has to cut rates in September, which will goose spending, and it has to do that while it`s still struggling to keep inflation under control. In some, the near future looks to hold fewer jobs and lower income because fewer jobs means workers will get fewer raises, fewer opportunities for promotion or moving to a new job for better pay, all while we still have higher prices.\par \par \uc2 Inflation and slow job growth is a terrible, terrible combination. It is an almost economically unsolvable problem. And this one is Donald Trump`s fault. Trump got the results he asked for with his DOGE cuts and his tariffs.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 There`s no part of this that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can spin. He did say, by the way, that this will all improve in a year or two. That`s what he said today.\par \par \uc2 And he tried to remember, he fired the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  after last month`s terrible jobs report, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . That`s the job counter. That`s the ref. Trump`s new nominee isn`t on the job yet. In fact, Trump only got around to officially nominating his nominee for the head of the \uc2 BLS\uc2  on Wednesday, so his Senate confirmation process hasn`t started.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  notes how the Trump deportations are keeping the unemployment rate down even as job creation stalls, quote, very slow job growth has been offset for now by Mr. Trump`s aggressive campaign to expel immigrants from the country. With fewer people available to work, the unemployment rate has remained stable in what \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair, recently called a curious kind of balance. And to no one`s surprise, Trump attacked Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  again today with one of his weird social media posts.\par \par \uc2 It`s not Jerome Powell`s fault that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is wrecking the economy. Even less surprising members of Trump`s sycophant cabinet who deal with economic policy parroted Trump`s attacks on the Fed chair because their biggest job right now seems to be appearing -- appeasing the dear leader rather than advising him on how to fix the economy that he`s breaking. Trump could actually improve this situation immediately if he dropped his appeal to the Supreme Court and ended his illegal tariff nonsense. But, no, instead he`s escalating his attacks on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair and against the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2  member, Lisa Cook, who he has no power to fire and who`s suing him to keep her seat.\par \par \uc2 Unrelated to Lisa Cook, there is another open seat on the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2 . And Trump`s nominee for that open seat sea of the Stephen Miran, who chairs the Trump`s Council of Economic Advisers, wants to keep his White House job while serving on the independent \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . That, of course, has never happened before due to the obvious conflict of interest that it would create.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the former treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , told me on Sunday about how Trump`s attempts to control the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could end up hurting Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY: This is a thought that many heads of state have had and thought, let`s pressure our central bank to do exactly that, lower interest rates, it juices the economy, gets down interest expense. And this is the basic recipe for high inflation or hyperinflation.\par \par \uc2 What independence means is that the technocrats at the \uc2 Central Bank\uc2  use economic reasoning data to try to figure out what are the best settings of monetary policy to achieve these goals free of political interference. And that is what President Trump is putting at risk and there will be a cost to every American that could be profound if this is allowed to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, joining me now is \uc2 Kenneth Rogoff\uc2 . He`s a professor of economics at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 . He`s the former chief economist at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . He`s the author of Our Dollar, Your Problem, an Insider`s View of Seven Turbulent Decades of Global Finance and the Road Ahead. He`s also an expert on recessions, which I hope we don`t have to call on him for.\par \par \uc2 Ken, great to see you. Great to have you here in person, it`s a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 Couple things going on. One is let`s just talk about the Fed and the politicization of the Fed. There`s a million examples of this. Some of them are historic, some of them happened in America. One of them happened very recently in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  makes the point that the being a Fed official or a Fed employee is some wonky work. It`s charts and projections and things like that. It is best that this is left to people who do the wonky work and who like it as opposed to politicians who would like low interest and high growth all the time.\par \par \uc2 KENNETH ROGOFF\uc2 , PROFESSOR OF ECONOMICS, HARVARD UNIVERSITY: No, exactly. I mean, the Fed, by resisting, always pulling down the short-term interest rate, that`s the one they control, prevents the long-term interest rates, which affect most of us, mortgages, car loans, student loans from going up. Because if you push short rates down too much, you goose inflation and lenders are going to want to get compensated for that. So, it has worked to have the Fed be independent.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t always have that. In the 1970s, we had sky-high inflation all over the world and having an independent central bank, there were things to complain about. They`re not perfect. They got some things wrong. But, overall, it`s worked great a lot.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right. Independent doesn`t mean perfect, it just means independent of political interference. We`ve watched in Turkey where Erdogan decided he wanted to get --\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: He`s firing the one after another.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. And they had inflation in very high double digits.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It was very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Inflation`s harder to catch than a recession.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Recessions, we have some experience with. You can flood the zone with money. You can bring interest rates down. You can goose the economy. You end up with what`s called creative destruction, you know, some jobs go away, but new ones get created, new industries get created. Getting hold of inflation can be much harder and can take much longer.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: Yes, because people lose faith. They were saving their money. And we just experienced it in the pandemic, and suddenly it`s worth 10 percent less.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: And I think in the pandemic, they looked at the independent Fed, and so far it stood up. But if we do that again anytime soon, they`re going to lose faith. It`s -- the in low inflation`s part of our trust in the dollar in the government.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Explain to me why this combination is uniquely bad, lower job growth, which may or may not, we`ll have to see what the GDP numbers are, but which may be correlating to lower economic growth, and inflation. It`s not terribly high, but inflation that`s not exactly yet where the Fed wants it to be. The things you have to do to fix inflation and the things you have to do to fix lower unemployment are opposite to what?\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: Well, I first say the economy might be growing but the labor jobs are not. I mean, it seemed like just yesterday you would look at the jobs numbers and if it was below 150,000, we think that was terrible, disastrous.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: This was 22,000. June was negative. And they`re not good numbers. And to answer your question, if the economy is strong and inflation`s high, you can kind of rein in, spend less, raise interest rates. If the economy`s weak and you`re not worried about inflation, you can goose it up. But when the economy`s weak and inflation`s high, there`s no simple demand management kind of thing. You have to do the tough work of, you know, making the economy work better.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, here`s my one question. It is tough work and it`s hard for central banks, even if they have full independence, to do the right things. That`s monetary policy. You have to do things on the fiscal policy side, meaning, you know, Congress and the president have to do things. There is something the president can do right now. There`s great deal of uncertainty about these tariffs. It`s putting pressure on bonds. It`s putting pressure on the U.S. dollar. People are just uncertain about what`s going to go on.\par \par \uc2 It would fix things to some degree if we got some finality and predictability around these tariffs and probably some pullbacks on them.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: I think the market and jobs and everything would be overjoyed if he just said, well, the Supreme Court told me I couldn`t, they`re terrible. They`re terrible people, but I can`t do it. Of course, that would be fantastic. That`s the single most powerful thing he could do is undo the signature policy.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Are you worried yet? I mean, you`re the guy I call when there`s any signs of anything that looks like a recession, does it a little slow down in the jobs number worry you yet?\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: Yes. I mean, it`s been noticeable over the summer. I mean, the report tried to put a spin of saying it`s stalled. It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: We`re treading water. But I`d say a lot of things he`s doing are pushing us out to sea.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: I mean, he`s undermining all our institutions. You talked about it.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m very nervous about things. I don`t know how it`ll end, but I don`t think it`s a good situation for the long-term.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kenneth, it`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 ROGOFF: Thank you for having me. Thank you for coming in person.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: \uc2 Kenneth Rogoff\uc2  is a professor of economics at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 `s, the former chief economist at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . And he is the author of the a new important book called Our Dollar, Your Problem, an Insider`s View of Seven Turbulent Decades of Global Finance and the Road Ahead.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, Donald Trump`s deportation raids are also disrupting the economy. What do you think happens to produce prices when the person who picks the fruit disappears? Yesterday`s federal -- yesterday, federal agents carried out the largest single site enforcement operation in Homeland Security history. They lined up hundreds of workers outside a Hyundai plant in Georgia, arresting 475 people, most of them South Korean nationals.\par \par \uc2 The special agent in charge called it the result of a multi-month criminal investigation, but we haven`t seen any criminal charges. And \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports that some U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents had been detained and were being released.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the city of Chicago is bracing for Donald Trump`s threats to expand ICE raids in Chicago, potentially deploying the National Guard, as he has in other Democratic cities, like Los Angeles and Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Democratic congresswoman, \uc2 Lauren Underwood\uc2 , of Illinois. She`s a member of the \uc2 House Appropriations Committee\uc2  and the ranking member of the Homeland Security Committee. Congressman Underwood, thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD\uc2  (D-IL): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We just had a conversation that that \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  had with the governor of Illinois about the preparations underway for what`s going on in Chicago. What`s your latest information right now? Because Governor Pritzker said there`s no direct conversation with the Trump administration on this, but they`re hearing things from people that you all know in Homeland Security or in ICE.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:07]\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: One of the hallmarks of this administration has been a real departure from norms and expectations, including Congressional notifications. So, like the governor, we haven`t had direct conversations with the department or with ICE regarding this planned invasion, deployment, whatever word you want to use, this is unlawful, it`s un- American, and they are not invited into the city of Chicago or state of Illinois, and that flies in the face of federal law.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let me ask you about this, because there`s a separate discussion going on here about the misdirection, the misinformation about violent crime in our major cities, about where that violent crime is, about the way it`s been trending. But, fundamentally, no one will argue that there are issues in Chicago that have to be dealt with specifically as they relate to gun crime, but no experts in this suggests that militarization of police, federalization of the National Guard is actually the way you fight crime in a place like Chicago. There are underlying issues that need to be dealt with that you all have been struggling with for a good amount of time. This is just not that.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Well, without a doubt. Chicago Mayor Brandon Johnson and Governor Pritzker have been in close coordination with their federal law enforcement partners as they direct the Illinois State Police and the \uc2 Chicago Police Department\uc2 , right? There are ongoing joint investigations and partnerships that have been longstanding, and so this type of escalation, using crime as an excuse. Well, Chicagoans, Illinoisans, Americans know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has used a number of excuses. He used homelessness as an excuse in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And it is just unacceptable to see this kind of escalation, using fear tactics and authoritarian whims from the president. It just really exceeds the type of real serious approach that our elected leaders in our state have taken to addressing this epidemic of gun violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: When we saw it in Los Angeles and we saw it in Washington, D.C., and arguably when we saw it in New York when the federal government made some sort of deal with our mayor to get involved in cooperating with immigration enforcement, it looked to me not like a New York-specific or Washington-specific, or L.A.-specific or Chicago-specific thing. It looked like the soft underbelly of being able to militarize crossing the \uc2 Rubicon\uc2 , if you will, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being able to do something that we have generally expected not be done by a president, the militarization, the federalization of National Guard and the militarization of policing in our streets.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Yes. And just as he`s trying to exceed his powers and renaming the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , right the Department of War, they think it`s a game. This is not a game. We are talking about the American people. And in this moment, we have to stand together, united as we are here in Illinois. In tough times, what we do, we come together, we protect one another, especially the most vulnerable in our communities. And that`s what all elected leaders are doing across our state right now.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, it`s impressive to watch not just what you`re doing in Illinois about this situation but what you were doing in Illinois with respect to the Texas members of the legislature who came out there and were able to get refuge while they made that a national issue that now other states are taking up.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you on the show, thank you, Illinois Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Underwood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has inflicted yet another humiliation on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and, of course, Donald Trump`s response is to do him another solid. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: This week, the world is commemorating the end of World War II, September 2nd, 1945. That war ended with more than 70 million people dead, and entire nations had to rebuild themselves around the idea of never again, because war destroys people, it destroys economies, it destroys nations.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t understand that history or the dividends that post-war institutions, like \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  have paid. On Wednesday, China staged a massive military parade of the sort that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once wanted for himself on the National Mall on his birthday, which instead was an awkward thing that was full of empty seats and people that Trump obviously forced to attend.\par \par \uc2 China`s show of power featured the leader of the most populous country on Earth, India, as well as the Russian president, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , look at them all, chumming it up. It was a strong men`s show of global dominance, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loves and craves. And Trump had to watch from the sidelines. In fact, when asked about the parade on Wednesday, standing with Poland`s president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I thought it was very, very impressive, but I understood the reason they were doing it, and they were hoping I was watching, and I was watching.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: He`s not entirely wrong. China`s Xi Jinping has stolen the world stage. Part of that is a long-term effort by China, but a lot of it is because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to try to burn the world stage down. Trump literally rolled out the red carpet for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and then let him dominate the truncated summit`s press conference, a spectacle which continues to deliver nothing but humiliation for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not only are there zero talks between Putin and Zelenskyy, which is what Trump supposedly went to Alaska to procure, but when Putin was being welcomed back to the world stage in China this week, Putin said he might have to end the war in Ukraine that he started by using force.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 He told reporters in China, quote, let`s see how the situation develops. If not, then we will have to resolve all the tasks before us by force of arms, end quote.\par \par \uc2 How much clearer does Putin have to make it? Like that`s about as clear a rejection of the Trump theory that he can woo Putin into giving him a win. Putin`s already rejected western security guarantees for Ukraine. And even though the Russian economy is suffering from severe inflation and the Russian military has failed now for three years in its objective to take much of Ukraine, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is helping Putin yet again by cutting U.S. aid to the Baltic States, some of NATO`s most vulnerable members, they sit on Russia`s border and they have large Russian-speaking populations.\par \par \uc2 America is watching the rise of strong men while our president aspires to be one.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the retired lieutenant colonel, Alexander Vindman. He served as the director for European Affairs at the National Security Council during the Trump administration. Also with us, Tom Nichols, professor emeritus at the U.S. Naval War College and staff writer at The Atlantic. Gentlemen, good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Tom, let me start with you and I -- because I really want to be clear here. I think China`s an adversary. I think China`s dangerous. I think China needs to be dealt with. But, boy, that was a demonstration, not just the parade, in which you had tens of thousands of goose-stepping soldiers all walking in unison with, and a whole bunch of weaponry, but the audience the Kim Jong-Un, the \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 , the \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , leaders of some democratic countries, all there standing next to a guy who looked like he represented stability and power.\par \par \uc2 No, I think you`re there. You`re muted, Tom.\par \par \uc2 TOM NICHOLS, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: Sorry about that. In the midst of all this chaos, that`s the game. I mean, China is putting itself forward, and it`s been doing this for a while to say, you know, we are a member -- we`re not, you know, crazy, this isn`t the cultural revolution anymore, we`re not a crazy revolutionary power. But especially with Trump just turning the world upside down for no reason, you know, 50 percent tariffs and, you know, trying to bail on the Europeans and so on, the Chinese have kind of stepped forward and said, you may not like us, you may not like our regime, but we are calm, we are stable, we respect the status quo, we`re open for business, we`re predictable and we won`t judge you. We won`t judge what kind of regime you are. We`re here to talk to anybody.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, somehow Trump thinks that he really can just kind of talk all these guys into being his friends and letting him into the cool kids club just by meeting with them. It`s childlike but dangerous, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Meanwhile, the countries in the world whom we should be wooing and trying to work with, particularly the democracies of the world we`re kind of ignoring, we`re fighting with Canada, we`re fighting with the Europeans, we`re fighting with the Indians, we`re fighting with all these folks.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Colonel, I will say Vladimir Putin`s had a great month. I mean, this guy has gone from being a global pariah into being welcomed on American soil, pulling one over on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  yet again and now chumming it up with everybody in China.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. ALEXANDER VINDMAN (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : It`s pretty clear to me that the there has been a consolidation, a collaboration coordination amongst authoritarian regimes to challenge the established world order, this idea that there are rules to the way states interact together, with each other, that aggression from large states, strong states against weak states, is no longer tolerated. This is something that we fought two wars in this 20th century for hundreds of thousands of Americans, millions of souls lost to set these rules.\par \par \uc2 And Trump is creating an enormous amount of opportunity for authoritarian regimes to fill the vacuum of our absence. If we don`t build a team of strong allies to counterweight the convergence of China, Russia, North Korea, Iran, and other authoritarian-curious (ph) regimes, it`s a vacuum for them to cast an image, as Tom pointed out, that they are the force to be dealt with and that the U.S. is no longer liable. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has given gifts to both Xi and Putin over the course of this past month and certainly since his tenure and second tenure.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: An entirely unnecessary gift. It`s an unnecessary vacuum. America could actually be there. A lot of these countries would be very happy to do more business with America, but we`re sort of pushing them away.\par \par \uc2 Tom, the thing about you two guys, Colonel Vindman has a great deal of experience in Russia and Asia and understanding that stuff, and you have a great deal of experience in the military. I`m not sure there`s a strategy here. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been talking about his weave, how, you know, he likes to change topics, and it`s sort of an artful thing, but it seems disastrous lately when he was talking to the head of the E.U. the other day in Scotland.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:12]\par \par \uc2 It was -- it was -- it was dramatically stupid. And then he had the Polish president in the other day talking about these seven wars that he`s ended. He makes a reference to Congo and Rwanda. And it`s very clear that he`s either -- he`s either very misinformed, knows nothing about history, or we need to start talking about the 25th Amendment. Let`s listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: The tariffs are vital to the success of this country. When I say I ended seven wars, at least half of those wars were ended because of the power of tariffs. I was able to say, well, we`re not doing any business with you, or if you`re going to war, we`re putting a big tariff on you.\par \par \uc2 And all of a sudden they announced that the war was settled. I would settle -- I settled seven different wars, one going 31 years, one -- look at the Congo and Rwanda, look at that. Thirty-one years, 10 million people dead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m not going to get into the fact that he -- he`s -- he`s conflating very different things. The -- the -- the Rwanda genocide where the estimate is that 800,000 to two million people were killed, was a -- was a very different thing than he just said that he settled.\par \par \uc2 He also said he settled three pre-wars, Tom. I don`t know what a pre-war is. And -- and that`s why he should -- all his staff keep telling him he should get something called the Nobel Prize for -- for Peace. I -- I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Is it -- does he not know what he`s talking about or what -- what is this - -\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: No.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- all about?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: No. He doesn`t know what he`s talking about. I mean, let`s start with the obvious problem that goes back a long way, which is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not a very intelligent person. I -- I would argue he`s probably the least intelligent human being ever to hold the Oval Office, maybe one of the least intelligent people ever to be set loose in American public life.\par \par \uc2 Although, you know, there are some members of Congress over the past 100 years who could give them a good run. But certainly at this level of seniority at the -- in the White House, to have somebody like this who just doesn`t know what he`s talking about,\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t understand the international system, he still doesn`t understand how tariffs work even though he`s been in love with them for 50 years. But I think there`s also an encroaching problem here that as he`s getting older he`s getting weirder.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he -- you know, he says things and I -- you know, I -- I hate to do this game of what if another president did it? Because it`s so close to what about ism? But considering the amount of fire that the republicans directed at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for mumbling or forgetting somebody`s name.\par \par \uc2 You know, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had sounded like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , there would have been hearings, there would have been impeachment, there --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: -- would have been demands for the 25th Amendment. And I think the really unfortunate thing is the republicans and -- and the rest of the American electorate has to shoulder some of this burden. Millions of Americans have decided that this is just how he is.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t make any sense. He doesn`t know what he`s talking about. He talks -- he talks crazy about, you know, sharks and electrocution and his uncle and blah, blah. But, you know, we`ve just decided to accept that a emotionally disordered, deeply ignorant person is the commander-in-chief of the united. States Armed Forces with his finger on the button of 1,800 -- 1,700 strategic nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In -- in a --\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: With some (INAUDIBLE) to accept that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- dangerous world. Yes, in a dangerous world. Colonel, I want to ask you one quick question, what is this thing about aid or -- or -- or -- or some military assistance that we`ve -- we`ve ceased to some Baltic nations? What`s the implication of that?\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: In the big scheme of things, it`s not a huge amount of money. We`re talking about some hundreds of millions over the course of years, seven or eight years. But symbolically, it`s important. It shows another withdrawal of our support.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re talking about Baltic states that are spending significant portions of their GDP to help float the Ukrainian war effort. They`re transferring enormous amounts of material. And they were counting on these training programs to -- to continue to sustain their own defense efforts.\par \par \uc2 So -- I mean, there`s a lot of things that don`t make sense. You know, this whole nonsense about the Department of War, something that we fought World War II and pivoted to the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  because we were trying to cast a different future for ourselves, for the whole globe.\par \par \uc2 They`re reversing course somehow. We`re woke since `47 --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: -- when that name changed. I mean, these are absurd notions. He ended these wars like he ended the Ukraine-Russia war in 24 hours. It`s all made up, it`s all make believe, it doesn`t make any sense. But he says it. And, you know, to some people it`s acceptable. To most of us we just kind of brush it off our shoulders, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: And we`re going to subject ourselves to this for, you know, almost another three and a half years.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Guys, thanks for joining us. I appreciate it. Lt. Col. Aexander Vindman and Tom Nichols. All right. Coming up, the former "Fox" weekend host that`s running the Pentagon said today he wants maximum lethality, not tepid legality, violent effect, not politically correct. We`re going to raise up warriors, not just defenders.\par \par \uc2 The most ridiculous part of that statement was that he said it next to President Bone Spurs. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:10]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:39:42]\par \par \uc2 On November 6th, 2024, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said this in his reelection speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had no wars. For years we had no wars. They said, he will start a war. I`m not going to start a war. I`m going to stop wars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Which makes it all the more confusing that the supposed peace loving antiwar president, who, of course, avoided the draft himself because of bone spurs in his feet during the Vietnam War, decided to sign an executive order today, essentially asking people to call the Department of Defense the Department of War.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, officially changing the name of the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  would require an act of Congress and 60 votes in the Senate, which Trump could not get.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:21]\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , the former "Fox Weekend" host serving as Trump`s Defense Secretary, said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, UNITED STATES DEFENSE SECRETARY: The War Department is going to fight decisively, not endless conflicts. It`s going to fight to win, not -- not to lose. We`re going to go on offense, not just on defense. Maximum lethality, not tepid legality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Maximum lethality, not tepid legality. What does that mean? Seriously? Does it mean kill people? A lot of people? The maximum number of people? Possibly in contravention of the law? Is that just a cosplay expression from a clownish defense secretary, or is that an actual statement of policy?\par \par \uc2 Could be the latter. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports, "The Trump Administration declared the start of a new and potentially violent campaign against Venezuelan cartels on Wednesday, defending a deadly U.S. military strike on a boat that officials said was carrying drugs even as specialists in the law of war questioned the legality of the attack."\par \par \uc2 "The Trump Administration has not offered any legal rationale. But Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said in an appearance on "Fox and Friends" on Wednesday that administration officials knew exactly who was in that boat and exactly what they were doing, although he offered no evidence."\par \par \uc2 Video of the incident posted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on social media. But look at this, it appears to show a long, multi-engine speedboat traveling at sea when a bright flash of light bursts right over the aircraft. The boat is then briefly seen covered in flames.\par \par \uc2 "NBC News" notes, "The video, which is largely in black and white, is not clear enough to see if the craft is carrying as many as 11 people. The video also didn`t show any large or clear stashes of drugs inside the boat."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  of Washington. He`s the top Ranking Democrat of the Armed Services Committee. Congressman, good to see you. Thank you for being with us. I mean, part of it, forgetting about the legality of it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s not something we want to forget about because that`s important, is that this week, if you were looking at militaries and militaries and what they were doing, China was showing you how strong they were, and we blew up a boat outside of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): Yes. Look, I mean, that was one of the most disturbing press conferences I`ve seen in some time because Trump basically is doing two things. One, he is weakening the United States and placing us in greater danger in a thousand different ways.\par \par \uc2 And then he really thinks he can just lie his way out of it. The number of lies told in that press conference, including all these wars he supposedly has stopped. I mean, let`s just take the India-Pakistan War, which he said he threatened tariffs and that`s how the war ended.\par \par \uc2 Modi has made it quite clear that President Trump had nothing to do with stopping --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Correct.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- the conflict between India and Pakistan. And then Trump put the 50 percent tariff on India. So if the threat of terrorists was what was supposed to have stopped the war, you put the tariffs on and the people involved said you had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And believe me, we could talk for hours about all the lies that were contained in that press conference. So let`s go from the lies to reality. The reality is the war in Ukraine is worse than ever because of the weakness that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has shown to Putin.\par \par \uc2 Giving Putin a green light in Ukraine to keep fighting in a thousand different ways. The war in Gaza is worse because President Trump is not trying to, you know, put any pressure whatsoever on \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  or do anything to stop that war.\par \par \uc2 And now he wants to start wars with every drug cartel in the world. That is an enormous expansion. And the idea that if we just drop enough bombs and kill enough people, the world will bend to our will. Did it look like China and Russia were bending to our will yesterday?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. Does not what --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It did not.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: They did not look like that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He`s pushing our allies away, making us weaker, and placing us in danger and trying to get away with it by saying, I`m just going to lie to you. I`m just going to create whatever --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- reality I want to create. And we sit there and we listen to it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, the lies are endless. Let`s focus on the reality. And the reality is Trump has placed our country at greater danger, he has weakened us. he has alienated our allies, and strengthened our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. He start -- well, he posted this morning or yesterday he said, we`ve lost India because -- because Modi is in Shanghai doing this stuff. You -- you know why we lost India? India is a democracy. They would like to trade with us.\par \par \uc2 They -- they do buy Russian oil, I get it. I`d rather they didn`t. But -- but they -- they in China have energy shortages. But we can -- we could have done this a different way. Instead we put 50 percent tariffs on them.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right. Well -- and -- and why did we book 50 percent tariffs on them? We booked 50 percent tariffs on them because Trump was offended that Modi wouldn`t give him credit for a war -- for ending a war, that Trump didn`t --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- deserve credit for.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This notion that he did it because India buys Russian oil. Trump won`t even enforce our sanctions on Russia. He`s not putting a tariff on India because of that concern, he blew off an ally because the ally refused to lie for him personally. I mean, it`s just placing us in so much greater danger.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:12]\par \par \uc2 And then the idea that, oh, but we`ve solved the problem by changing the name of the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  to the Department of War. That`s going to send a power -- it`s ridiculous. And the policies he`s implementing are incredibly harmful to us. Focus on the policies, not the lies.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And -- and we`ll leave aside all that stuff that \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said about why -- legality and lethality, and it was just gibberish. Congressman, good to see you as always. Thank you for being with us. Armed Services Ranking Member --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- Congressman Adam Schiff -- \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2 , I`m sorry. Coming up, another day, another way that Trump is screwing over regular people. He gets a Qatari plane for himself and he cancels airline refunds for you. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:56]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:50:28]\par \par \uc2 If you find yourself stranded at the airport because your flight was delayed or -- or worse, canceled, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has just made sure you will not get compensated. The Trump Administration is doing away with a Biden era proposal requiring airlines to pay passengers stranded by canceled or significantly delayed flight, as well as to foot the bill for meals, hotels and rebookings in cases where the airline was at fault.\par \par \uc2 This, of course, comes after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  formally accepted a $400 billion \uc2 Boeing\uc2  747 from Qatar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I could be a stupid person and say, oh no, we don`t want a free plane. We give free things out, we`ll take one too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Trump may call the jet from Qatar a free plane, but American taxpayers are the ones footing the more than $1 billion bill to retrofit, upgrade, and secure that free plane for Trump`s use. So while you`re stuck wandering the terminal halls eating food at extortionary prices, your tax dollars are paying for Trump`s free plane.\par \par \uc2 A passenger rights organization told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " "The Trump change is unfortunate and ultimately self-defeating. Without a delay compensation rule, airlines do not have the incentive needed to make their systems reliable, noting that currently the cost of delays the cost of the flight brownouts is borne by the passenger."\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Steve Cohen\uc2 , who authored and helped enact protections for air travelers, said, "These rules exist because the airlines, left to their own devices, repeatedly fail their customers. The flying public deserves the reforms we enacted, especially the automatic refunds for canceled or delayed flights."\par \par \uc2 "The elimination of fees for seating children with their parents, the treatment of mobility devices like wheelchairs, and several other improvements to the flying experience, the airline lobby is trying to eliminate."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Democratic Congressman \uc2 Steve Cohen\uc2  of Tennessee. He`s a former member of the House Transportation Committee, where he served as the top democrat on the Aviation Subcommittee. Congressman, great to see you again. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Couple things. First of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made some reference to airline fares being $2 or something like that. Ge hasn`t paid for an airline fare in quite -- many decades. I don`t think he understands any of this. But you remember when there used to be all those flight delays, and then they -- they imposed a rule that said, if you delay this many people for this long, it`ll be this much of a fine per passenger.\par \par \uc2 All of a sudden, those delays where you sat on the Tarmac for three plus hours disappeared. Like, that`s just how it works. You -- if you don`t make a rule, the airlines are not going to give you any money for -- they`re not going to give you anything back. They`re not going to change their behavior.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEVE COHEN\uc2  (D-TN), FMR. AVIATION SUBCOMMITTEE RANKING MEMBER: No. They`re not Santa Claus.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: They don`t come in on a sleigh.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk about this particular thing. What`s the motivation here? Why are we not doing something that`s not about costing the government money, not about costing the taxpayer money, it`s about saying to the airlines, behave properly, you -- you will pay compensation for things you do wrong.\par \par \uc2 Like every other business I do deal with has to pay money for things they do when they do something wrong. What`s the motivation?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Well, almost. every country in the world there are consumer protections for airline travel. And we tried to even up those in the aviation bill that we passed. Last year it was a really good bill. And the things that are in law, they can`t change.\par \par \uc2 But some of them were done by regulation and with our encouragement but \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  went a little further on giving more consumer protections. And those they can change, unfortunately. And they will. There`s not been a republican administration that cared for consumers over the -- the corporate inch, corporate structures.\par \par \uc2 And this administration has given unbelievable tax breaks to the wealthiest people in America. And then they also give all kinds of benefits to the biggest corporations in America. And whether it`s the fossil fuels industry or whether it`s the airline industry or every industry, that`s where they`re at.\par \par \uc2 That`s who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is about. So don`t ever think as an individual you`re going to get anything out of this administration that benefits you as a consumer.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. By the way, the -- the Lobby Association Airlines for America, for the airlines has put out a statement. "We`re encouraged by this Department of Transportation reviewing unnecessary and burdensome regulations that exceed its authority and don`t solve issues important to our customers."\par \par \uc2 I -- I fly probably on average once a week. It`s very important to me. Like --\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Very important to all of us.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- I pay a fee, we schedule things, we expect to get to a place. And I get that things go wrong, but I would like incentives in place for them to go right. And if they`re not, that -- that I get compensated. I paid money for a flight that departs at a particular time and lands at a particular time.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: We should expect that. And we should be able to plan for that. So when we land, people know where we`re going to land, and we shouldn`t have to give up time when we could be doing families or doing business or whatever one has to do when they land.\par \par \uc2 And they`re inconvenienced often sometimes it`s because they don`t have enough crew to make the flight go.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:13]\par \par \uc2 They don`t have either flight attendants or pilots, and then they messed up their staffing. That the airline should be responsible for. Acts of God, bad weather, et cetera, that`s something else. But when it`s the flu -- when it`s the crew, they should be responsible. And almost every country in the world makes them responsible.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: But, you know, they -- they don`t want to have regulations. They -- they -- they -- they -- if it`s in the law, they said they`ll follow it. But right now -- but they -- in the law they have the lobbyists. That`s where they have more influence than anyplace else. Well, now, apparently, with Secretary Duffy, they`ve got a lot of power, too.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Secretary Duffy, who after he left Congress, became an airline industry lobbyist. Surprise, surprise. Congressman, good to see you. Thank you for joining us this evening, Tennessee Congressman \uc2 Steve Cohen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Before we go, by the way, I was talking about that jet that the Qataris gave Trump. It was -- I said, $400 billion. The -- the jet was $400 million. Tonight`s "Last Word" is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:11]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:11]\par \par \uc2 All right. Tomorrow on "The Velshi Banned Book Club," I will be speaking to an author whose contribution to American literature simply cannot be overstated. Stephen King, who has written modern classics like "The Shining, "The Stand," "The Green Mile," "Shawshank Redemption," "It."\par \par \uc2 King has published more than 65 novels and over 200 short stories, including a brand new adaptation of "Hansel and Gretel." Do not miss my conversation with the one and only Stephen King, America`s best expert on exploring our society through some very scary stories.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s "Last Word." The 11th Hour" with Stephanie Ruhle begins now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2025 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.  Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020250906el9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 5, 2025, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My plan will rapidly defeat inflation, quickly bring down prices, and reignite explosive economic growth.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Donald Trump`s plan is not working.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Make no mistake, this is a weak jobs report.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These revisions are pretty brutal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The more you drill down, the uglier it gets.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Tonight, big flashing warning signs for the Trump economy and what it means for you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Our big year won`t be really next year. I think it will be the year after.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Then, is the United States preparing for war on Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: Maximum lethality, not tepid legality. Violent effect, not politically correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): Plus, is the President trying to slink away from RFK after yesterday`s Senate hearing?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Look, you have vaccines that work. They just pure and simple work. They`re not controversial at all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voice-over): When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes.\par \par \uc2 Well, today is Jobs day. It`s the Friday -- last Friday of the month when the government posts the country`s hiring numbers the previous month. It`s also usually the day when the pundits on Fox try to spin bad economic news as a plus for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which has become sort of a monthly ritual here in the second term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, HOST, \uc2 FOX BUSINESS NETWORK\uc2 : This is just -- it just proves that we`re -- we have an economy that all the experts continue to underestimate.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX BUSINESS NETWORK\uc2 : Absolutely better than expected even though April was revised downward. You got 139,000 jobs created in the month of May. Yes, a decline of government workers. That`s exactly what President Trump has been trying to do. The Nasdaq down about 200 going -- 73,000 jobs. 73,000 jobs created in the month of July. The number just coming out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that`s the total.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We do see a softening market. Now, there are jobs out there. I know you did ask me about that. There`s still opportunities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You can see it was getting harder as it progressed to spin, but none of that even happened today because there was just no way to spin the news. The United States barely created any new jobs this summer. The Bureau of Labor Standards reporting the U.S. economy added only 22,000 jobs in August, less than a third of the figure most analysts expected.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest in a series of increasingly grim jobs reports since Trump took office. And the deeper you look, the worse it gets, because today, the government also revised its nu from previous months. They said rather than gaining 14,000 jobs in June, the economy actually lost 13,000 jobs that month. It was the first such decline since, you guessed it, December 2020, the end of the first Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 In fact, The Journal says, outside of 2020 when the pandemic hit, this is the weakest year for job creation in America since 2009, the height of the great recession in the Wall Street bailout. What is even wilder is that this is the first jobs report to come out of \uc2 BLS\uc2  since Trump fired the commissioner last month, complaining when he got a bad number that the number were rigged to make him look bad.\par \par \uc2 What was the administration`s response today? Don`t trust our numbers.\par \par \uc2 (B