 And that is Donald Trump`s greatest political weakness, bar none.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:21]\par \par \uc2 I mean, the core Trump brand, right, throughout his first campaign in 2016 and his first term as president and his campaign last year was that he is a great businessman. He`s a billionaire CEO who would be good for the economy. And yes, that always seemed ridiculous to a lot of us. He governs like a man who went bankrupt running a casino somehow, because that`s who he is. Even as he ran on the economy last year against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , promising to bring down prices on consumers on day one, he was also vowing to raise prices on everybody with a tariff plan that experts warned would lead to room. And so, now here we are.\par \par \uc2 There is no question in anyone`s mind this is Donald Trump`s economy. He has made sure of that. He has unilaterally and almost certainly unlawfully slapped hundreds of billions of dollars of new taxes on everyday working Americans, hiking prices on the stuff they buy every day. He brags about this tax hike every chance he gets, puts up graphics about how much he is taxing you.\par \par \uc2 He has completely thrown us from the top to the bottom of the global trading order by raising tariffs to the highest level since, yes, the great depression. And what we are seeing as a result is an economy screeching to a halt, with still elevated unemployment and prices still above target. Republican pollsters know it`s hurting them. Anyone who has seen a special election or a town hall in recent months know it`s hurting him -- anyone who`s read a poll. But we also know Trump will not change course.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he could. He could just abandon the whole tariff gambit. But no, he has already fired the head of the government agency that produces the statistics who he blamed for bad economic news. He`s trying to get one of his own cronies in there now. He`s actively trying to take over the independent \uc2 Central Bank\uc2 , an unprecedented move in the 100-plus year history of the institution so he can, like other dictators he admires, mess with the interest rates, so he can insulate him further from the realities of the economy that he is breaking as that same reality slaps more Americans in the face.\par \par \uc2 Senator Angela Alsobrooks, Democrat of Maryland, serves on the Senate Finance Committee and she joins me now. Senator, your response to reaction to today`s jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ANGELA ALSOBROOKS (D-MD): Well, you know what? The truth -- it`s hard to escape the truth. Sooner or later, the truth will find you. That`s what we know. And the truth is finding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his administration and it is that his economy is failing. The jobs numbers today, in spite of having fired, the person who was responsible for the numbers last month show us what we all knew is that the policies of this administration would eventually hurt the very people he promised to help.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing not only that, we only added 22,000 jobs. We were expecting about 75,000. But we are seeing that everywhere you look, this economy is failing and it`s hurting people. It`s not only that we`re not only not seeing the jobs, we know as well that you don`t even have to look at the numbers to understand it in your real life, that the cost of groceries are now five percent higher this year. We know as well that the cost of goods as a result of these erratic tariffs, that the cost of goods will be going up, that your rent or mortgages are not going down, that the cost of housing has increased.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we heard on the first day was an absolute lie, that he would somehow not only cause the cost of groceries to go down on day one, but that he would improve our lives. We are now not only poorer, but we`re sicker as a result of what we also see with this Secretary of \uc2 Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, the jobs numbers again, it`s hard to run from the truth. The truth will eventually find you and the truth is rising to the top. This is a failing economy, and it`s nothing to celebrate, because he`s hurting everyday hardworking Americans. And you know what? It`s only getting worse by the moment.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To the extent there`s a coherent theory of the economics of mass deportation -- and I`m not sure there is, but to the extent there is one, it`s you know, kicking out immigrant workers will provide employment boost for those in America who are sort of at the most marginal parts of the labor market. Instead, what we`re seeing is a spike in youth unemployment, a spike in African-American unemployment, a particularly bad spike in unemployment for black women.\par \par \uc2 What does it signal to you that these groups are seeing these higher levels of unemployment about what it means what`s coming next?\par \par \uc2 ALSOBROOKS: Well, you know what? I mean, I think it`s a very sad story. Again that the people who are hurting -- this -- there`s nothing to celebrate in today`s numbers. This failing economy is hurting so many people. And it`s not just the groups of people that you have mentioned. As you mentioned, of course, African Americans. We know that younger Americans. When I think about the job market, I think about my 20 year old daughter, who will be soon as she graduates from college facing a job market that is frightening for so many of them. And what it translates to, the ones who are afraid that they won`t even be able to live as well as their parents. That the average age of first time homebuyers is now somewhere closer to 40 years old rather than 20 when my parents were buying a home.\par \par \uc2 So, these are real numbers. They`re real lives that are being impacted. And you know what? All of -- all of the distractions. You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he was interested in immigration reform, he and his party had an opportunity to do that with comprehensive immigration reform, and he wouldn`t do it. But so now, the distractions, all of these distractions still won`t take us from the truth. He`s wrecked our economy. He`s cruel. And what we know as well is that we`re now going to also be sicker.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:50]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You sit on the committee that will be evaluating the nominee to replace the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  Commissioner he fired. A lot of people feel like this is an attempt to essentially capture the agency, to be able to cook the books and massage them. Some new reporting about the individual who has been nominated for that post, President Trump` nominee to lead the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  this coming from CNN`s K Files, operated a since deleted account that features sexually degrading attacks on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , derogatory remarks about gay people, conspiracy theories, and crude insults aimed at critics of Trump. NBC News reported and CNN confirmed he`s a bystander at the U.S. Capitol on January 6th, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Do you -- what do you need to see from this nominee? Do you have confidence this is the right person for the job?\par \par \uc2 ALSOBROOKS: Let me tell you, I don`t need to see any more from this bad movie. I have been in the room when these confirmation hearings have happened, and honestly, it`s like a Saturday Night Live episode. These people who are overwhelmingly unqualified for the jobs that they`ve been put forward for. His nominee, who`s -- who will be before the committee, is much like many of the others that I`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 He`s not only unqualified, the only qualification that matters to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that these people will rubber stamp what he does. Think about the person who`s now going to now be CDC Director is a venture capitalist. That`s the person who`s supposed to replace a medical doctor. So, these are unqualified people the only criteria is to cook the books. What we`ve seen is chaos, increasing cost and corruption. And these nominees