r-in-Chief.\par \par \uc2 Now, U.S. law on armed conflict does not allow the president to just kill almost a dozen people traveling in international waters, thousands or even hundreds of miles away from the country`s territory. But U.S. warships are taking -- are taking up station in the Caribbean, with at least one being buzzed by Venezuelan jets, while almost a dozen American F-35s head to Puerto Rico, along with U.S. Marines carrying out military exercises. All of it looking like a build up to armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 Helene Cooper is a national security reporter for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , has been reporting on the strike and the U.S. military buildup in the Caribbean. And she joins me now. Helene, I wonder if you can first sort of walk us through what your reporting indicates about the legal justification for this strike and whether the normal channels that one would go through before killing 11 people, which is, you know, there`s lawyers at the DOD and lawyers in the White House, like, do they go through those channels? It felt very much like that was something they were doing after but I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, NATIONAL SECURITY REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Hi, Chris, thanks for having me. The legal thing has caught the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , had them up in stitches for the past month. Ever since President Trump first late July signed this military authorization that he was going to announce -- decided that he was going to deploy the military against what he calls --is now calling narco terrorists, these drug smugglers from Venezuela, primarily he`s really going after Venezuela and but also the Cartel del Soles.\par \par \uc2 So, he has gone after -- he announced that he was going to go after these guys, that he was going to use the American military in this manner. As you know, for the entirety of the second term of President Trump`s, he has been deploying the military at all manner -- you know, all manner of things that they normally would not be used for. So, now he`s using the military to go after drug smugglers in the Caribbean who he says are bringing fentanyl to the United States.\par \par \uc2 And the legal -- but for about a month, ever since he signed this order, the Pentagon has been trying to figure out what the legal justification would be for this. As of yesterday, they still hadn`t .I mean, that self- defense letter that you just saw, yes, he can say that. Whether that`ll hold up in court is a different matter altogether, because you had 11 people who \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  came out and said were on their way to Puerto -- were headed toward Trinidad, which is not the United States. And then President Trump said, actually they were coming to the United States. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  changed -- you know, they -- the administration narrative about this has continued to shift.\par \par \uc2 So, whether that holds up is a different story. While I was at the Pentagon yesterday, several of the officials that I talked to said -- expressed some frustration that they still hadn`t been able to articulate any clear legal justification for this. You mentioned the AUMF, the Authorization for the Use of Military Force, that goes back to September 11th, and that was meant in retaliation for the September 11th attacks. It had nothing to do with drug smuggling.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s like that law has already been stretched by every administration since then, including President Obama, President Bush, the first Trump Administration, even President Biden. But this is going, you know -- you know, they`ve been using that for Houthis in Yemen and for all sorts of military exercises and military strikes. But this one, you know, in the Caribbean against a boat of what President Trump said were 11 drug smugglers, even that is not entirely sure.\par \par \uc2 At the Pentagon, several officials expressed skepticism that you would have 11 people in a boat that size that was smuggling drugs. Why would a drug smuggler, instead of putting drugs in the boat, pack it with 11 people when you could have drugs in there instead? So, that is like -- the whole thing is just is really -- has been -- has been really interesting to cover just because of the way they`ve been doing it, and because even at the Pentagon, there`s been a lot of doubt cast about what the mission is and what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Let me read from some of your reporting here that some officials, the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  privately expressed concern on Wednesday about the administration shifting narratives, including where the vessel is headed, which you just mentioned. Rubio said on Tuesday it was going to Trinidad, Trump said the United States. On Wednesday, Rubio changed his version, saying the drug laden boat was bound for the United States.\par \par \uc2 Now, on top of this one strike, I mean, what becomes clear here, I think, is there is a massive deployment happening to that region. The veteran Pentagon reporter at Fox News, Jennifer Griffin, I think, said something today like, it sure looks a lot like we`re going to war there. I mean, what is your understanding? There`s one strike here. They`re saying there`s going to be more. You know, there`s this famous quote from Secretary of State John Hay back in 1896, I think, about the Spanish civil war, in which he called it a "splendid little war." There`s a little bit of a feeling here that they`re looking for something more than striking boats.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:34]\par \par \uc2 COOPER: They certainly are. They would like -- they hate Nicolas Maduro, the president of Venezuela. They have said that he is a drug smuggler himself, that he is the leader of some of these organizations and they -- I don`t think that they are going as far as regime change, but who knows? I don`t think the United States would invade Venezuela with the number of people we have. We don`t have. We have not assembled an invasion force for Venezuela. You`ve got 4500 sailors in the Iwo Jima amphibious ready group that has been sent down there. You have 2200 Marines that are down there. There are ten warships. That`s a huge number.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s not nothing.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: But that`s not the kind of ground invasion force that you would need to go into somewhere like Venezuela. But you could do strikes and you could strike Venezuela and that`s what we don`t know. I mean, you -- they`ve built up an armada now in the southern Caribbean. And the biggest problem with this is, yes -- well, a couple of the Pentagon people I was talking to yesterday, they said this is a show of force, and maybe, you know, this will give these drug smugglers, smugglers pause when they`re thinking about coming in. They`ll be afraid of being blown up. But then there`s also the -- it raises the possibility of something happening.\par \par \uc2 You saw maduro sent his two F-16s to buzz one of the American warships. And now we`ve sent our F-35s to Puerto Rico. And at some point something happens and it trips up. And then you might end up in an armed conflict, which I don`t know whether so much of what this administration does sometimes appears to be performative and then they pull back. But we`re in dangerous waters here, pardon the pun, because anything could happen when you bring that kind of military hardware to the region. And this is right on our back door.\par \par \uc2 Helene Cooper of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, as the man in charge of our health care system does everything he can to make Americans less safe, Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , on what he is doing to protect residents of Colorado, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:32:25]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , the dangerous crank in charge of our country`s health severely restricted who can get COVID vaccines last week, and then