ernment`s.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That decision made by the federal government was made by, it seems, RFK Jr., although, you know he said it was a recommendation from below, but it`s fairly evident that he`s manipulated the people below him to come to that conclusion.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been critical of RFK Jr. in the past, but you`ve also been quite laudatory when he was nominated. He said, I`m excited by the news president-elect will appoint RFK Jr. to \uc2 HHS\uc2  gov. You also said some other positive things about him.\par \par \uc2 I remember reading that time and being like, oh, that`s interesting. I`m not excited about him being nominated, but I think the people not excited are looking like they were right more than the people who were excited. Do you still feel excited?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I put in that very first statement that I was hoping he would, you know, go towards vaccine choice and not restrict vaccines. That was always the fear I had, the fear so many people had, and it seems like it`s coming true.\par \par \uc2 My hopes and is he doing some of this? Yes, I wish he did more focus on health, diet, nutrition, reducing chronic disease. We can reduce chronic conditions like diabetes by simple dietary changes. He`s taken some action on artificial diets, totally for that stuff.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:15]\par \par \uc2 But frankly, that can be eclipsed if he`s making it harder to get lifesaving vaccines. We`re seeing it with COVID now, and frankly, I`m very worried about other important vaccines, like measles and others that are absolutely critical for people of all ages to help eradicate these deadly, deadly plagues from society.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have said before that your view of -- you`re prochoice, basically, on vaccines, and you -- and you`re sort of skeptical of mandates.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Florida just announced that they`ll be getting rid of all vaccine mandates for their public school children. Do you think that`s a good idea?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: We don`t have mandates here in Colorado for kids, we have a simple opt out form. We strive to get the highest vaccination rate we can. We have goals and educating parents, educating kids.\par \par \uc2 People should want the vaccines against measles, against lots of different conditions, because it`s good for your kids. I vaccinate my kids. I encourage everybody to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, Colorado has had no mandates as far as I know ever, and I oppose efforts to do that, because you have to win over people`s hearts and minds to be able to empower them with the ability to make the health care decisions that are in their own best interest. And that`s been our path on Colorado, and we`ve been able to increase the vaccination rate through that work.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But aren`t you guys -- you guys are trending down, right, in your vaccination rates, like the rest of the country?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: You know, the whole -- the whole country after COVID-19 and some of the propaganda against vaccines, primarily from the political right, led to small decreases in vaccination rates, so we`re working hard to get those back up.\par \par \uc2 I think that, frankly, when you have governors going after vaccines or federal officials, it casts even more doubt on the minds of the public people who might like what that politician is doing in another area, and saying, hey, so and so is saying maybe the vaccine isn`t good.\par \par \uc2 So, I think look at the science, look at the data. I always lead with the data. Vaccines` safe, highly effective, great way to protect your kids, and if you get the COVID vaccine and flu vaccine this fall, great way to increase your own protection.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I guess the question now right is, as we have seen national vaccination rates come down, we`ve seen polling show that people are more skeptical of vaccines, and we`ve had the largest measles outbreak in 30 years, including the death of two children, and I believe one adult, if I`m not mistaken.\par \par \uc2 What happens if those trends continue, particularly if the single most important and authoritative voice in the federal government`s health infrastructure is sort of encouraging that skepticism?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I hope that more states follow what Colorado did. I think I saw that a couple others have now to first of all, make the COVID vaccine easily available to anyone who wants it.\par \par \uc2 And we need to remove politics from health care decisions. These are decisions about our health. You know, whether you`re a Republican, Democrat, liberal, conservative, you care about your aging parents, you care about the health of your kids, you care about your own health, we need to make sure that people are empowered with the information they need and the ability to choose to make the right choice to protect themselves and get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Obviously, many Senate Democrats have called for the resignation of \uc2 Robert Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , along with a bunch of public health officials, including former directors of CDC across a variety of administrations, both Republican and Democrat, various professional bodies. Do you think he should resign?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, again, I think the people calling for his resignation, the senators, etcetera, didn`t, didn`t vote for him in the first place. So, and I obviously supported \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when he was running against RFK for President. Proud of that. And then obviously RFK switched over to Trump. I supported \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, you know, again, I think Democrats in general have a very different direction on health, to empower people with the ability to make your own choices, remove barriers to advance medicine to vaccines, reform the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , to speed the approval process for life saving drugs already available in Europe. Not afraid to take on big pharma and negotiate better rates for Americans and yes, to make vaccines easy, accessible and low cost or free, so people are better armed to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to take as a no but Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, a flailing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries again to change the subject, but the Epstein scandal isn`t done with him yet. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:44:13]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues his hand-fisted attempts to meddle in and influence the election for the next mayor of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, we got reporting that disgraced former New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  told donors at a private fundraiser in the Hamptons that Trump would wade into the race for New York City Mayor and help clear a path for his election.\par \par \uc2 Then earlier this week, we got reporting that people close to Trump were thought to be working on an administration job offer to get the incumbent Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  to drop out in a bid to help clear the field for Cuomo, who`s running as an independent.\par \par \uc2 So, it`d just be Cuomo and Mamdani and then the Republican Curtis Sliwa. Now that same day, we learned about the possible job offer, a job at the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That night, on Wednesday, I had democratic mayoral nominee Zohran Mamdani on this program to get his reaction to the president of the United States meddling in New York City mayoral election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:07]\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D), MAYORAL NOMINEE FOR NEW YORK CITY: I think it`s outrageous. And I think oftentimes, in a race such as this, it`s tempting to think of this solely in the question of, how does this impact the race?\par \par \uc2 But this is an affront to our city`s democracy. This is an affront to the values that make so many of us proud to be Americans, that we could choose our own leaders, not that our leaders pick themselves or their friends.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing in this time when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has taken snap from Hungry New Yorkers has passed legislation that would throw millions of New Yorkers off their health insurance that we have Democrats in \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  and \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  working with this administration to try and subvert the will of Democratic primary voters right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, as a New Yorker, as a guy who has followed the career of \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  had another program probably dozens of times, including when he was charged by the Southern District of New York with bribery and various campaign finance offenses. I remember when I read the political article the other day, I thought, I just don`t think that a regional HUD administrator job is going to be enough to get Adams out of the race.\par \par \uc2 And it looks like the Trump folks thought the same thing, because earlier today, \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported the role now being discussed for Adams is as the U.S. ambassador to Saudi Arabia, which is just so perfectly corrupt, it`s almost impressive.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is not just some admin job, no, far from it. I mean, this is Ambassador which you get to be called for life. It`s a posting to one of the richest, most powerful Arab countries we have strong relations with, and where you can network with lots of other rich, powerful folks.\par \par \uc2 But it appears as though Trump`s attempt to clear the path did not work, at least not yet, because this afternoon, Adams came out with a big announcement, speaking at the mayor`s residence, saying, I`m not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: There has been so much speculation, communications, announcements of what I`m doing, no matter what I have stated over and over again publicly. So, I want to be clear with you, \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is a snake and a liar. I am in this race, and I`m the only one that can beat Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 I committed myself and dedicated myself to a city that I love, and I`m going to continue to do that. This polo shirt that I`m wearing that says \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , Mayor of the City of New York, I`m going to wear that for another four years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, he could wear it for another four years, but I`m not sure he`s going to be reelected. So, there you have it.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, the gig Trump was planning to give \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  was just not enough, or we`re watching the negotiation play out in real time. To be honest, I suspect we have yet to reach the conclusion of this saga.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:52:17]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For weeks, even months, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been trying to distract attention from the Epstein issue by talking and posting about virtually anything else.\par \par \uc2 But today`s job numbers are so brutal that Trump seemed desperate to divert attention from that by posting about Epstein. He gave his usual spiel of nothing to see here, writing, the Department of Justice has done its job. They`ve given everything requested to them. It`s time to end the Democrat Epstein hoax and give the Republicans credit for the great, even legendary job that they are doing, doesn`t seem like that`s going to work.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Goldberg is an opinion columnist \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , where her latest piece is how Epstein won`t break Trump`s coalition, but the fissures are starting to show. Jennifer Welch is cohost of the great podcast, I`ve Had It and they join me now.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, I was -- I was -- it was funny to me to see him post about Epstein, because one of the sort of signature features of life in the Trump age is constantly being like, he`s distractive. Now, now we`re back to Epstein not to talk about the job`s numbers.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, OPINION COLUMNIST, NEW YORK TIMES: I`m actually not even sure that any of this with him is strategic as much as it is. You know, he`s impulsive. He saw someone talking about this press conference on T.V. and was mad.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I wonder too, Jennifer, like, do you -- do you think much of this is strategic? And do you think the kind of -- this sort of obsessive returning to the scene of the crime by like talking about Epstein and about how no one should be talking about Epstein is effective?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER WELCH, CO-HOST, "I`VE HAD IT": Well, I mean, those job numbers were abysmal, and so when you have your whole administration fighting the public nonstop. This is the golden age. It`s an incredible economy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, desperate people do desperate things. And I don`t think he`s all there all the time, and I agree with Michelle that he probably is. You know, it`s just an arbitrary decision to do this, but we have to remember that his supporters are kind of primed to be OK with this type of stuff.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you think about all of the things that Trump has done to support sexual abusers, we forget about him endorsing \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2  for Senate in Alabama and Trump (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 So, there`s you can just go down the laundry list of this type of supporting of sexual abuse, and it`s just rampant with MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I`m glad you brought that up, because it does seem -- I said this last night as I was talking to Jess McIntosh about this.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was, quite famously, in 2017 the sort of Me Too movement, which really was a remarkable sort of social upheaval.\par \par \uc2 And then it really felt like the 2024 and 2025 particularly Trump campaign was like the anti Me Too movement. It was like --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right and then the