was he going to tell the grand jury? And what about this additional documentation and evidence that he was supposed to turn over?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Chris, Corcoran`s testimony came after a federal judge ruled that the Department of Justice has successfully shown that there was evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed a crime through his attorneys and that therefore, what`s known as the crime fraud, exemption -- exception to attorney-client privilege applies here.\par \par \uc2 And so, you had this remarkable situation where the president`s attorney is in the grand jury in that courthouse behind me testifying in this case about classified documents. And what we think he was testifying about in part is this question of this document that Trump`s lawyers executed and signed and gave to the Justice Department back in June where they said that the Trump team had done a diligent search and had found no additional classified documents at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 That was false because three months later, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched Mar-a-Lago found more than 100 classified documents, including some very significant ones in Donald Trump`s office. So, the question has always been, who lied to the Justice Department about classified documents? Was it Donald Trump`s lawyers? Evan Corcoran is a former federal prosecutor. Or was it \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Did he cause that misleading or false statement to be made?\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we believe that Evan Corcoran is testifying about. And also, he was required to bring in some documents. Now there`s one report that he had a recording or transcripts of recordings, there`s been a lot of speculation. We don`t have reporting about what that is. Obviously, there`s a history of \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , another Trump lawyer recorded one of his conversations with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If there are recordings between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers, that would be a dramatic and significant development. But the big picture here, Chris, is that this development really underscores that this \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  documents case is not just about mishandling classified documents. It`s more importantly about obstruction of justice. And that`s what makes it different from the cases involving \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the classified documents found in the residence of those men. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, I want to read from some reporting in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . This is about the original ruling that ordered Corcoran to testify. In a lengthy memorandum of law that accompany the rolling, judge Howell, according to two people briefed on the matter, laid out damning assertions made by prosecutors that Mr. Trump knowingly deceive the government and cause Mr. Corcoran to misstate to prosecutors where the documents were being held at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 So, some people might see that and say, from a non-legal perspective, hey, there`s evidence of your crime and therefore Trump should be charged. But is it that simple?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Of course, we know it`s never that simple. Because prosecutors have to have proof beyond a reasonable doubt. They have to prove all the elements that make up a crime set out in the written documents in our rules of law. That`s why getting this testimony was so critical for Jack Smith. You`ve got to talk to Evan Corker, the attorney and ask him what were you told?\par \par \uc2 Who told you who gave you the information that went into this document that you drafted before it was sent to DOJ certifying that everything had been returned? Corcoran is a former federal prosecutor. He`s not naive. He understands that there is a crime called obstruction of justice. The events that we`re seeing today suggests that it`s entirely possible the former president was not truthful with his lawyers.\par \par \uc2 And now prosecutors are trying to get the details to determine whether they can bring charges in that regard.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, also according to the Times, another Trump attorney has also been ordered to testify. A woman by the name of Jennifer Little. Who is she and why might her testimony be significant?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: We don`t know a lot about her connection to the classified documents case. She`s an Atlanta area lawyer. The original assumption was that she was perhaps involved in the Fulton County team. But this suggests that there are additional reasons that prosecutors have established in front of the court that they have a good faith basis for putting her in front of the grand jury as well.\par \par \uc2 And it`s worth noting, Chris, that as we came on the air there was reporting that the court has also ordered in the January 6th probe, a slew of additional witnesses testify over assertions of executive privilege. People including former Trump Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . So, it looks at this point as though prosecutors are successfully convincing the courts that as in any other case they`re entitled to get testimony from fact witnesses here.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There is a pattern that may be developing. Ken Dilanian, thank you. Joyce, you`re going to stay with me. We have exclusive new details on that deadly drone attack on Americans at a military base in Syria. Why one of the radars used to detect incoming threats was down.\par \par \uc2 A groundbreaking ruling. What the parents of a school shooter will face when they stand trial for involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 And new signs of Russian aggression in the Arctic. The evidence. Norway is sharing exclusively with NBC News ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are waiting to get a live update from the Pentagon following a series of deadly exchanges in Syria between U.S. forces and Iran-backed militants. The U.S. launched airstrikes overnight on targets in Syria after an American contractor was killed in a suicide drone attack. Five U.S. service members and another contractor were also wounded. The White House today revealed that a second base used by U.S. military was targeted by missile attacks this morning,\par \par \uc2 Possibly in retaliation for those airstrikes. No one was hurt in that attack. NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell joins us now from the White House. I know we are expecting to learn more that Pentagon briefing at the top of the next hour. But what do we know right now, Kelly?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:01]\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, as we await, Chris, getting whatever new details we are able to provide through that briefing. There are some important things that we have already learned. Part of that is that President Biden authorized the U.S. military action while he was aboard Air Force One heading to Ottawa, Canada for the meetings with \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 , that our colleagues are covering right now.\par \par \uc2 So, it gives you a sense of some of the behind the scenes work that was happening as the president was dealing with this urgent issue while they had other foreign policy matters on his plate. And part of what we`ve also learned from our team at the Pentagon, is that part of the radar apparatus was down for maintenance at this installation in Syria, where U.S. personnel were based.\par \par \uc2 And there is a question about whether that added to the vulnerability of this Iranian drone being able to have the attack capability that it showed, could that be a part of it. So, those are questions that need to be pursued. Also, earlier, John Kirby, the spokesperson for the National Security Council was speaking with Andrea Mitchell about some of how the U.S. has assessed what has gone on here.\par \par \uc2 For people who`ve been thinking so much about the war in Ukraine, they 