side of his office. So those are important questions to ask.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jake Sherman, Tim Miller, guys, good to see you. Hope you have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 Two world leaders grappling with major political crises but unbending in the face of mass anti-government protests. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:28]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today members of the Florida state legislature are preparing to vote on gun legislation that allow concealed weapons to be carried without a permit or training. It comes on top of a different bill that would reduce the age requirement for buying a rifle from 21 to 18.\par \par \uc2 And at an intense hearing on guns on Capitol Hill yesterday, the father of one of Parkland victims was arrested after this exchange with Republican Congressman Pat Fallon. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT FALLON (R-TX): Is this an insurrection? So will they be held to the same -- I don`t want another January 6th, do we?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they`re trying to overthrow the government they ought to be held to the same standard but I think they`re trying to express their --\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Whoa, whoa, whoa. Is the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  not doing their jobs? What in the hell is going on?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Manuel Oliver, father of Parkland victim Joaquin Oliver joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much for being here. What were you trying to say? What did you want members of that committee to hear in the room?\par \par \uc2 MANUEL OLIVER, FATHER OF PARKLAND VICTIM: Well, what happened yesterday is that these members were lying for more than an hour. Every chance they`ve had to speak for five minutes was packed with lies. Everything was a big fight and putting the ATF as the main problem and not talking about the real problem, which is gun violence itself.\par \par \uc2 You can see these guys joking, laughing, and smiling, comparing me with the insurrection. So that says a lot of our leaders. And if I had two options, one is raise my kid`s voice, and other one is getting arrested, I`ll go with number two.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, you weren`t just taken out of the room. As you said, you were arrested, and Congressman Maxwell Frost tweeted this video. I looked at it again a couple of hours ago and it had 1.6 million views. So would you do that over again just to try to get your message out, to draw some attention to what happened in that hearing?\par \par \uc2 OLIVER: Well, let me be very clear in that my plan was not to get arrested. I did not receive a warning from the chairman. My wife Patricia did not receive a warning from the chairman. We were expelled from the room, and immediately after that, I was arrested, I was thrown to the floor. The videos show really what happened. The statement from the police, \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  is a false statement when they say that I tried to get back in the room again.\par \par \uc2 So I think the story will continue, but again, I`m not the victim here. We have 45,000 victims from gun violence, and that`s what really matters.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We mentioned those two bills in the Florida legislature, one of them reversing a legislative win that came out of the March for Our Lives group`s advocacy following the death of your son, 17 total classmates in Parkland back in 2018. What`s your message to those lawmakers in Florida who are voting today?\par \par \uc2 OLIVER: Well, that my state and we have been working really hard on trying to have comprehensive gun laws. Actually we were able, after the pain and terrible situation that we where they kill our loved ones, we were able to raise the age. That was a bill that we started in Parkland, and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  was able to sign it. And now we`re going back. We`re moving backwards. So shame on the state.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a very evident way of thinking and putting priorities together. And victims of gun violence are not part of that list. So I hope that we can make things happen before it gets even rose.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: 119 mass shootings so far this year, according to the Gun Violence Archives.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Oliver, I know you`ve been fighting this in memory of your son. Thank you so much for taking the time. We really appreciate you being with us today.\par \par \uc2 OLIVER: Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now in Denver all students in the public school system are off for a mental health day. It comes after a shooting at a high school there earlier this week that left two staff members injured. The suspected teen shooter dead. The superintendent says in a statement the day is meant to, quote, "allow us all to take a moment to pause and process the challenging events this year."\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 Are skyscrapers in San Francisco safe? The warning from city officials that shoving down blocks in the city. I`ll talk about the dangers with the city`s Board of Supervisors` president. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Two world leaders are embroiled in domestic political crises centered on the limits of executive power. France`s Emmanuel Macron and Israel`s \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  both remaining defiant in the face of widespread and at times violent demonstrations at home.\par \par \uc2 For Macron, the decision to sidestep parliament and force through an unpopular pension reform plan unleashed a furious outcry across France, though he survived two no confidence votes. For Netanyahu, already the focus of an ongoing corruption probe, a plan to overhaul the Israeli judicial system has sparked constitutional crisis and weeks of protests that show no signs of letting up.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in David Ignatius, foreign columnist and associate editor for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 David, let`s start in France where it`s estimated a million people took to the streets of Paris yesterday alone, and they`ve reached far from the capital. This was the scene in Bordeaux overnight when protesters apparently lit city hall on fire.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 Macron is portraying himself as the adult, making the hard choice. Yesterday he said this reform isn`t a luxury, it`s a necessity. But do you think he can keep this up?\par \par \uc2 DAVID IGNATIUS, THE WASHINGTON POST FOREIGN COLUMNIST AND ASSOCIATE EDITOR: Well, I have sympathy for Macron in trying to deal with the demographic realities in France. But I`m struck both in France and in Israel by the way in which democratically elected leaders are trying to change what`s seen as the social contract. Change fundamentals of how people live, how the laws work.\par \par \uc2 In France, this retirement issue, it goes to the heart of what people think of as the social deal they have with the government, and they`re furious. So whether it`s necessary or not to align France long term with demographic realities is a separate question, I think, from the way it`s being done and the degree of public anger, which is extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And it is in Israel as well. Israel`s attorney general said today that Netanyahu broke the law with his involvement in overhauling the judiciary. So we`ve seen these weeks of protest, escalating violence with the Palestinians. We`ve even had a rare diplomatic rift with the U.S., of course its close ally over settlements on the West Bank. How tenuous do you think Bibi Netanyahu`s position is right now, and what might the implications be?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: Well, what makes it tenuous in my mind is the protests in Israel have now spread widely among the community that we think of as being part of Israeli military and intelligence life. Reservists are refusing to serve their terms. Some of the people out on the streets in Tel Aviv, the hundreds of thousands of people have been -- people who from the heart of the Israeli national security elite.\par \par \uc2 So I think that`s really a warning sign to Netanyahu. The essential strength of Israel is in some ways implicated in these protests. People have been expecting him to back down, expecting a compromise. I was told by a senior Israeli official. One was coming a week ago but they`re not there yet, and, again, I think Netanyahu feels that his ability to govern with his coalition is at stake. Otherwise I think he would have made a deal a week ago.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, we haven`t even mentioned India, David, where there`s been something of a political earthquake. \uc2 Rahul Gandhi\uc2 , leader of the political opposition to Prime Minister Modi, has been disqualified as a member of parliament. Do you think there`s a legitimate worry that some people have been voicing about the future of India`s democracy?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: So I`m troubled to see three great democracies, India, Israel and France, all suffering from fundamental problems in terms of the social cohesion, the social contract as I said earlier. I think under Prime Minister Modi, India has had some rocky moments that I think Modi needs to pay more attention to keeping the fabric of consensual aspect of Indian democracy solid. But it is striking, isn`t it, that in these three countries around the world, we`re seeing kind of fundamental challenges to democratic stability.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are indeed. David Ignatius, always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 New claims today that Russian military threats are growing in the Arctic. The new evidence being shared exclusively with NBC News, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:56:47]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New claims from Norway to tell you about. A growing Russian threat in the Arctic. The \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally now boosting its naval patrol and releasing a trove of videos exclusively to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In the forbidding cold of the North Sea, \uc2 NATO\uc2  is fully armed and on watch. For three days and two nights, we joined a crew of the Norwegian Navy Coast Guard ship Sortland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: With this lighthouse, we`re cutting west and headed out to the oil fields.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: A new \uc2 NATO\uc2  mission, standing guard over the massive oil and gas fields here that keep Europe and Britain warm. It`s a high priority. This exclusive video from the Norwegian Air Force shows Russian nuclear subs, ships, and fighter jets moving through and over these highly vulnerable waters between Norway and Scotland.\par \par \uc2 Adrian Valanger (PH) is the ship`s officer.\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN VALANGER (PH), NORWEGIAN SHIP OFFICER: What do we see out here is that there are Russian vessels that -- having weird sailing patterns that makes me suspicious.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And something else that Norwegian fast boats are now chasing down.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What makes \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Norway awfully suspicious right now is the number of drones that have been flying over Norway`s oil and gas platforms, even its refineries. If it is Russia, they have spent years surveying the landscape and they know precisely where all of Norway`s vulnerabilities might be.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They come and they just disappear and don`t necessarily head back home to a mothership.\par \par \uc2 VALANGER (PH): NATO Warship Foxtrot 333, this is Whiskey 342, over.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: We were there as \uc2 NATO\uc2  moved German, Spanish and Dutch warships into Norway`s largest gas fields. Norwegian foreign minister \uc2 Anniken Huitfeldt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): How critical is Norway to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance?\par \par \uc2 ANNIKEN HUITFELDT\uc2 , NORWEGIAN FOREIGN MINISTER: I think Norway is \uc2 NATO\uc2  in the north, but in case of a military attack against Norway, we are absolutely dependent on reinforcement from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Six months after someone blew up the Nord Stream gas pipeline that ran from Russia to Germany, there are plenty of theories about who did it. Russia, Ukrainian sympathizers, Britain, the U.S., even Norway.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Did Norway have any role at all in the attack on Nord Stream?\par \par \uc2 HUITFELDT: No, not at all. That`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has accused the U.S. and the West of the attack, claims the U.S. has dismissed. Back on our Norwegian patrol ship, heightened vigilance.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): It`s not just the oil and gas platforms, it is also the undersea pipeline that carry oil and gas, and internet, data and telecom lines stretching all the way across the Atlantic, and all of it potentially vulnerable to sabotage.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Here on the North Sea, the global economy vulnerable to attack.\par \par \uc2 Tom Costello, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have a lot to cover in our second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, more air strikes in Ukraine as new reporting pulls back the curtain on what types of weaponry Russia is sending to the front lines and what it tells us about the state of the war.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230325ej3o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 7, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Frank Figliuzzi, Ken Dilanian, Morgan Chesky, Brian  Cheung, Ali Vitali, Tom Costello, Eugene Robinson, Mike Memoli, Step  Twitty, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 8,100 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 A shocking and sickening end to the brazen kidnapping of four Americans in Mexico. Two had been found dead, a third is injured. Some lawmakers were already calling for military action against drug cartels before this tragedy. So, what is the administration going to do now? We may get some early answers when the White House briefing starts just a few minutes from now. And we have some brand-new information about the two Americans who survived.\par \par \uc2 Plus, one Republican lawmaker calling BS on Tucker Carlson`s narrative that January 6 was a peaceful protest. Another Republican calling it flat-out a lie. \uc2 The Capitol Police\uc2  are going further insisting it invites the prospect of more anti-government violence.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden`s big pitch for 2024 that he`s the only one who can save Medicare. How it fits with his larger message to voters and contrasts with MAGA Republicans? He says are intent on making things worse.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with that breaking news. Two of the four Americans kidnapped in Mexico just a few days ago had been murdered. Two others are alive. And we just learned minutes ago that they are now back across the border to the U.S., one of them though injured. A State Department briefing is scheduled next hour with key questions unanswered, who exactly is responsible and what will the U.S. do about it? One of the biggest clues is this disturbing video of the initial kidnapping. The gunman seen here wearing a bulletproof vest drags people into a white pickup truck in what the U.S. officials say is a case of mistaken identity.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Ken Dilanian who just got new reaction from the Attorney General. Also, with us, Frank Figliuzzi, former Assistant Director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . Ken, what do we know at this hour about the investigation and the whereabouts of those two survivors?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Chris, the best information we`re getting so far is from Mexican officials. The Attorney General of the State of Tamaulipas saying that in fact, the two Americans who survived had been transferred to U.S. custody. We are seeing photos of an ambulance from Brownsville, Texas going across the Mexico border back to Texas. We have not yet confirmed whether that`s related.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials steadfastly unwilling to comment on any of this, presumably because they want to wait and talk to the victims` families once they know the full story. But I have been told by law enforcement sources that what happened here, as you said, a case of mistaken identity. These four Americans were crossing the border because one of them was on route for a cosmetic surgical procedure in Mexico where it`s much cheaper to do that, and they were fired upon by cartel gunmen. And one working theory about what happened here is that the cartel thought that these were Haitian people smugglers encroaching on their turf.\par \par \uc2 But regardless of the motivation, we saw from that video, some of those Americans were grievously wounded right away. We saw a cartel gunman dragging them into a white pickup truck. And they were taken. Presumably, the cartel quickly realized their mistake and dropped them off. And the Mexican authorities have now picked them up.\par \par \uc2 Now, I was able to ask Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  about this. He wouldn`t comment on the status of the Americans but he did talk about the ongoing battle between the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and the Mexican drug cartels. Take a listen, Chris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: On the general problem of the cartels, look, the cartels are responsible for the deaths of Americans that we are fighting as hard as possible. The DEA and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are doing everything possible to dismantle and disrupt and ultimately prosecute the leaders of the cartels and the entire networks that they depend on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: And, Chris, of course, this incident is putting a spotlight on just how much of Mexican territory is really open to the cartels who seem to be operating with impunity, and raising questions about what the Mexican government is doing about it, and what the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is doing to help. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much. I appreciate that. And I know you`re going to continue to let us know if you get more information. Frank, in the meantime, you`ve been involved in investigations like this. What are U.S. and law enforcement officials doing right now to get those answers and what resources do they have in place?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: So, first, Ken already referenced the complete debriefing that`s going to occur between U.S. law enforcement particularly led by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and these returning victims. They will glean a lot of intelligence so that -- (AUDIO GAP) who, what, where, and when, and that will become important pieces of a puzzle that they`ll put together with Mexican law enforcement partners. But, you know, part of the challenge in working to mitigate the threat in Mexico has been the lack of trusted partners amongst various law enforcement agencies in Mexico. The U.S. works hard to develop trust with certain people that they can go to in the Federal Police and the various state authorities, but it is a challenge because of the influence of the drug cartels.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s all about the money and the power and sometimes they own the very police officials that the U.S. enforcement agencies are trying to work with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, what are the chances that the families get answers that the American people get answers that we just have any understanding of what really happened here? We think this is a case of mistaken identity. There`s nothing in the background of these folks to suggest anything otherwise. But what are the chances we find out what really happened here?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Well, I can tell you. This mistaken identity theory or assertion actually is consistent with the kinds of things that we have seen happen in various hotspots in Mexico. Meaning that the cartel violence really doesn`t want any part of harming Americans, but rather, is engaged in such regular battle street gunfights that inevitably, Americans get caught up in it. So, I think it`s a good working theory. The problem is what to do about it.\par \par \uc2 And here is where I think an increased presence with the agreement and concurrence of the Mexican government of U.S. law enforcement, even as some are proposing potential advisors from the U.S. military with alongside Mexican military could help here. But with regards to this investigation, Chris, you`re going to see this go quiet as they debrief the victims and they use sensitive intelligence methods, sources, techniques, to really pinpoint who did this and where they are. And you`re -- likely the next news you`re going to hear is the possibility that we`ll just hear that they`ve been taken down and the threats been mitigated.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How much, Frank, electronic surveillance is there? Here, we`re so used to it in the United States, and anybody who watches a crime show knows you essentially follow a car, right from point A to its endpoint. But does any of that exist in Mexico, particularly in this region?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So sensitive topic, of course. But I can tell you this, you know, and it`s going to sound cryptic but that`s what I can tell you is, there is joint cooperation agreements with regard to sensitive agencies, three letter agencies, and the sharing of intelligence with trusted partners. And yes, there are certain forms and techniques of sensitive electronic intercepts but the problem is, who you`re dealing with and whether it leaks, and whether the people who did this have enough juice power influence in the right places to get tipped off that they need to get out of dodge. They likely are already in a panic knowing about the international attention and have already changed locations. So, that makes it even more challenging.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, there was a $50,000 reward that was offered by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , as I`m sure you know. I don`t know whether or not that had anything to do with where we are fairly quickly after this disappearance. But having said that, when you talk about Mexican law enforcement partners and how they can be bought off by the drug cartel, is there a financial incentive that can and would be used perhaps by the Mexican government to get more information?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So, I -- it`s interesting. I view that reward, which by the way, was fast and high with regard to my experience in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . So, that`s interesting because it`s an acknowledgment that money plays a role here. But I believe the reward was really aimed at Mexican citizens who may have seen or heard something but not the cartel members or organization members because in reality in their life, 50 U.S. dollar -- 50,000 U.S. dollars versus execution or incurring the wrath of your cartel leader is not worth it. So, I think the reward was aimed at citizens who might have heard or seen something and would discreetly share it with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, I want to bring in NBC`s Morgan Chesky, who was at the U.S. border. Right now, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  tells Americans flat out, do not travel to the region where this happens. It`s one of the six regions they say to avoid because of crime or kidnapping or both, Morgan. Remind us what these Americans were doing there. Have you seen any visible security in the area? Bring us up to date.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Chris, according to family members of these victims, this group of four originated in South Carolina. They rented that white van we`ve seen in that horrific video with North Carolina plates. And then according to family members, Chris, they made that long journey hundreds of miles here to South Texas to Brownsville, Texas where on Friday, officials say they crossed over one of the international bridges here.\par \par \uc2 And it was just shortly thereafter, Chris, that federal authorities say they fell under heavy gunfire from a group of armed men. From there, we pick it up with this video that we`ve seen now where you can see these armed individuals walking around in broad daylight, taking these individuals, loading them into the back of that pickup truck. Chris, according to family members, the only -- one of the primary reasons they made this drive was so that a woman in this group of four could undergo a cosmetic procedure just across the river here in Matamoros.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as you mentioned, despite a level four travel advisory being in place, not just in the city of Matamoros but in the state of Tamaulipas since October 2022. And yet, despite the -- that is urging from State Department officials do not travel there, they did show. And unfortunately, we are seeing the horrific consequences of that play out in real-time here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So many people who go over the border to get much less expensive medical procedures, dental procedures, in addition to medications. Morgan Chesky, thank you. Frank Figliuzzi, you`re going to stay with me.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, there`s another big story we`re following, and it`s a fair warning from Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  . Expect more interest rate hikes and faster, which means that if you`re thinking about buying a house or a car or you`ve got credit card debt, interest rates are heading even higher than policymakers expected. It`s a sign the battle against inflation isn`t done yet. And the back and forth over Fed policy in front of the Senate Banking Committee got a little bit intense when Elizabeth Warren cited the Fed`s own study that this move will cost jobs and lots of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): Chair Powell, if you could speak directly to the two million hardworking people who have decent jobs today, who you`re planning to get fired over the next year, what would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2  , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: I would explain to people more broadly that that inflation is extremely high, and it`s hurting the working people of this country badly. All of them, not just two million of them.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: And putting two million people out of work is just part of the cost and they just have to bear it?\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Well, working people be better off if we just walk away from our jobs and inflation remains five to six percent.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Well, let me --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC News business and data reporter Brian Cheung is here on set with us. Look, I mean, this is complicated stuff. None of us at least, I`m not -- most of people watching are not economists. We look to the Fed but so far, a lot of people are looking at their 401(Ks), they`re looking at food prices, they`re looking at their everyday lives and saying whatever you guys think you`re doing, it`s not working, and why should we believe that this time is going to be any better? Give us sort of the lay of the land, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, MSNBC BUSINESS & DATA REPORTER: Yes. Chris, the real difficulty here is that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  , which is the steward of this economy is essentially between a rock and a hard place, right? The rock of high inflation, high prices at the store, but also the hard place of potentially increasing the amount of job loss that we have in this country if you try to take inflation down by raising interest rates, which the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has done, by the way over the last year, at the fastest pace that we`ve seen since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 The idea is you make those borrowing costs higher. And that`s going to take the steam out of this economy, which should hopefully lower prices. But do businesses then start to lay people off because of the slowing economy? Isn`t happening yet because the unemployment rate as the Fed chairman was telling the Senate Banking Committee this morning is still at lows that we haven`t seen since the 1950s.\par \par \uc2 But look, at the end of the day, the story here is that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is raising interest rates. And the suggestion from the Fed chairman just this morning is they might have to get even more aggressive. They could actually increase the size of their interest rate hikes in two weeks. We`ll have to see.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I don`t know if we have a board ready to go but I was checking to see what was going on, cnbc.com --\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Markets did not like it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Here we go. Look at this. Down 472. Not a surprise right given what he had to say?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes. Well -- I mean, but the thing is, it was a surprise to markets what he did say when we - when we talk about how leading up to this testimony this morning. We hadn`t heard anything explicit from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that said, look, they were kind of slowing the pace of their price -- or their interest rate hikes over the last few meetings, but maybe they could re-accelerate that again.\par \par \uc2 We`ve really heard from the Fed Chairman that essentially it is on the table for them to re-increase the amount of interest rate hikes that they do at a single meeting which could be on the table for two weeks now. Either way, the translation for Americans is that your mortgage rates, your credit card rates, your auto loan rates are going to remain high as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  continues to try to get inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Our crack control room, you see how quickly they got those numbers out?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes, that was quick.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It wasn`t planned. Thank you so much, Brian.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s always good to see you. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 New, a Republican senator just called BS on \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  for his interpretation of a video from January 6, and new warnings that it could spawn dangerous conspiracy theories. Plus, if you can believe it, another major air scare, this time, someone tried to open a door mid-flight and stab a flight attendant, the big step the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is now taking to drill down on safety. And later, the bloody battle for Bakhmut reaches an inflection point, inside the fight that has Russian soldiers resorting to using shovels as weapons. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Stark warnings from \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  today concerned that Tucker Carlson`s airing of a selectively edited video of January 6 will spawn dangerous conspiracy theories and potentially fuel more violence. In a letter just obtained by NBC News, the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  Chief went even further, blasting the segment as being "filled with offensive and misleading conclusions about the January 6 attack." Carlson`s version of the insurrection is that it was nothing more than as he put it, peaceful chaos. To support that, he showed this video, protesters mostly milling around the Capitol, describing it as proof it was neither an insurrection nor deadly. But it was pulled from 44,000 hours of security camera video, and we don`t know how it was edited, and frankly, neither do security officials.\par \par \uc2 Although Carlson claimed they`d run all the video by the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  , a source tells NBC News they only saw one clip. Carlson, meanwhile, downplayed images like this, describing it as the work of a small number of hooligans and calling majority of protesters meek and orderly. The violence that left 140 officers injured, contributed to the deaths of several others, and had lawmakers hiding and running for their lives was barely mentioned.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill. And Frank Figliuzzi is back with me. Ali, I understand, tons of reaction is coming in. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Chris, the reaction is coming in but it depends which Republican you`re asking and, frankly, which side of Congress you`re asking those questions on because in my conversations with House Republicans in recent days, they`ve been eager for this footage to come out. In fact, part of the reason why \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has it is because people on the rightmost flank of the Republican Party in the House pressured speaker Kevin McCarthy during the speakership race to release this footage in the first place. So, that`s on the House side of this the way that most Republicans are receiving this footage.\par \par \uc2 On the Senate side, though, much more quick to criticize. And it sounds a little bit like this. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2  , (R-ND): I think that breaking through glass windows and doors to get into the United States Capitol against the orders of police is a crime. To somehow put that in the same category is on, you know, permitted peaceful protest is just a lie.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS, (R-NC): I think it`s (INAUDIBLE). I was down there and I saw maybe a few tourists, a few people who got caught up and things. But when you see police barricades breached, when you see police officers assaulted, all of that are you had to be in close proximity to it. If you were just a tourist, you should have probably lined up at the Visitor Center and come in on an orderly basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And then, of course, there`s the reaction, Chris, that we`re getting not just from elected lawmakers but from \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and rank and file here themselves. We`ve seen on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in recent days and recent hours officers who were injured on January 6 showing what happened to them after that day. And then of course, a letter from Tom Manger, the head of the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  , to his officers at one point saying the program last night conveniently cherry-picked from the calmer moments of our 41,000 hours of video. He goes on to say the commentary fails to provide context about the chaos and violence that happened before or during these less tense moments. TV commentary will not record the truth for our history books. The justice system will. The truth and justice are on our side.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we know that those hundreds of cases are still working their way through the Department of Justice. But certainly, the agreement that we had heard about and what I knew was happening during the January 6 hearings was that when this kind of security footage was going to be used, there was coordination between the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and at that point, the January 6 committee. I talked about this recently with then-chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  about how they used to do that. And there were portions where \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  said to them, we don`t feel comfortable with you using that and the committee just didn`t use it. Clearly, that`s not the kind of coordination that happened with Tucker`s show.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And, Frank, Carlson told his audience last night that this you know, so-called insurrection, these are lies about January 6, he says, are being used as a pretext "to strip their civil liberties and freedoms and to allow donate some of our core human rights."\par \par \uc2 What`s your level of concern with this kind of messaging to folks who already believe 2020 was stolen, and who supported maybe what happened on January 6?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: You know, we`ve heard the term big lie so often, Chris, now with regard to the claims of fraudulent -- presidential election, but this is the next big lie that January 6 didn`t happen. It wasn`t violent. It was fabricated, ginned up fabricated by the government, that people are political prisoners. Now, I want to point out a couple of things out there.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it`s dangerous because it could lead to yet another attack. But there`s also a macro strategy here, which is, you know, we have a former president who`s under investigation for his role in inciting the very violence that happened on January 6. And what Fox News seems to be implying are headed toward is, hey, any prosecution of a former president or high-ranking officials for that kind of violence is bogus because that never happened, right? So, what we`re seeing here in selective editing is not just a calm before a storm, but a calm minus the storm.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Dan Goldman had a great quote on this, which was, if you videotaped the four planes that were hijacked on 9/11, everything would look really peaceful until of course, they were violently hijacked by terrorists and crashed. So, we`re seeing the peaceful part, but it appears we`ll never see the violent part, at least on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, what would you say, big picture, if someone told you they believed Tucker Carlson`s version of events?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  wants people to believe in their rabbit hole with him that this was a largely non-violent protest that the people charged we`re up to officially one thousand arrests now, Chris. And by the way, the guy that was featured so prominently with Tucker last night, the QAnon Shaman, he pled guilty to the charges against him.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is calling for him to have a new trial. He didn`t have a trial because he conceded that he did what he was charged with, and he regrets doing it. So, the notion that`s out there is that the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  let everybody in. There`s no idea that they committed any crime and this is all a sham. The reality is six \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officers have been dismissed for their conduct that day. And many of the officers were trying to survive by not doing battle with a greatly larger crowd than the police officers could match.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Franklin Figliuzzi, thank you, Ali Vitali, always great to have you on the program. So, what could the release of this January 6 video mean for as Frank just pointed out a thousand-plus cases that the DOJ is investigating connected to that day? I`ll ask a former prosecutor and legal expert ahead in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 A man accused of trying to stab a flight attendant adding to the growing concerns surrounding airline safety including close calls and turbulence so severe, it was deadly. The troubling details, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s been another terrifying mid-air scene. This time on board a United flight bound for Boston where a jumped to his feet after flight attendants believed he`d tried to pull the latch on an emergency door.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m taking over to this plane.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m telling you right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The suspect ranted and yelled and allegedly tried to stab a flight attendant in the neck with a broken spoon. Passengers ultimately tackled 33-year-old who was arrested and charged as soon as the flight safely landed.\par \par \uc2 That high altitude showdown follows a spate of other recent airline scares including an emergency landing in Cuba when birds struck the engine of a Southwest flight, causing the cabin to fill with smoke. It`s a pattern the Senate plans to investigate starting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, let`s start with one of these cases. NBC reached out to the lawyer for the man charged in the Boston air scare. We haven`t gotten any comment back thus far. But what else do we know about that? It looks like it had to be absolutely terrifying to be on that flight.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I just moments ago got done interviewing the woman who shot that home video that we`ve been showing there, that cell phone video, and she says it was terrifying. She was on board with her husband and her 17-year-old daughter. And that man she says was clearly having some sort of a psychological issue and when he then started allegedly stabbing the flight attendant, male flight attendant, with a broken spoon, you saw there multiple male passengers jumped up, tackled him, right there near the exit on the left-hand side, and between the exit and the galley, and kept him down for 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 He continued to struggle, she said, until they landed in Boston, immediately met by \uc2 Massachusetts State Police\uc2  who put him in cuffs and took him into 