NSING: Mike Memoli, Eugene Robinson, great talking to both of you, guys. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 New details about a fierce battle in an eastern Ukraine city, why Russia is sending convicted criminals, some fighting with shovels.\par \par \uc2 And can parents be held criminally liable for their kids` crimes? What happened today in a hearing that may provide an answer ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s go to the White House briefing. They`re talking about the kidnapped Americans, two of them who died. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: -- senior members of the White House have also been engaged. We extend our deepest condolences to their families and friends, for the sake of privacy and out of respect to the families, we are going to refrain from further comment about those circumstances at this time. I can confirm that U.S. officials are in touch with the families of the individuals, but, again, we will respect their privacy regarding our conversations with them.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate the hard work of the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , DHS and DEA for their swift response to this awful incident and for their continued collaboration with Mexican authorities. These U.S. agencies remain in close touch with their counterparts and we expect that they will share more as they can.\par \par \uc2 Attacks on U.S. citizens are unacceptable. No matter where or under what circumstances they happen. We will continue to work closely with the Mexican government to ensure justice is done in this case. Since day one of this administration, we have been focused on disrupting transnational criminal organizations including Mexican drug cartels and human strugglers -- smugglers, pardon me.\par \par \uc2 In the past few months, President Biden signed an executive order giving the Department of Treasury expanded authorities to penalize cartel organizations and those who control or enable them. And we have imposed powerful new sanctions against cartel organizations in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 We remain committed to applying the full weight of our efforts and resources to counter them. Right now our immediate concerns are for the safe return of our citizens, the health and well-being of those who survived this attack, and the support which must be rendered to the families of those who need it. Now, I want to move on to the news that we put out this morning.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And so that`s \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  , the White House press secretary, on the brazen kidnapping in broad daylight of four Americans, two of them killed, the other two have been brought back to the United States, one of them injured.\par \par \uc2 We are expecting to get more information from the State Department at a briefing about 10 minutes from now. That`s when it`s scheduled to start in any case. But we`ll keep our ear to that White House press briefing to see if there is anything else that is newsworthy and bring it to you.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, at this hour in Ukraine, officials are vowing to find the Russian soldiers they say killed an unarmed prisoner of war. That death was apparently captured in a viral video released by the Ukrainian military too gruesome to show you here.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 But what you are seeing is a screen shot of the soldier just seconds before he was shot according to Ukrainian officials. His last words, we`re told, "Glory to Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 NBC News has not been able to independently verify the video and the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  did not immediately respond to request for comment. The Ukrainian military says the prisoner had been missing around the city of Bakhmut where Russian forces are closing in. Bakhmut has little strategic importance but massive symbolic value to both sides.\par \par \uc2 Joining us, retired Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, the former deputy commander for U.S. European Command.\par \par \uc2 It`s always good to have you on the program. I want to talk about first the large number of mercenaries who are fighting now for Russia. The leader of the infamous Wagner Group says they`re being deprived of ammo, we`ve had reports of hand-to-hand combat in Bakhmut, Russian soldiers using shovels to fight after running out of bullets.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " says that many of the mercenaries are prisoners who were being sent, and they use this phrase, certain death. What`s going on there, General?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY (RET), NBC NEWS MILITARY ANALYST: So, there is a couple of things. Number one, we`ve been fighting or the Ukrainians and the Russians have been fighting for Bakhmut for about eight months now and the Wagner Group is the lead organization, mercenaries, that are fighting to seize Bakhmut for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 They have a combination of two type of forces that are fighting. Number one, they have the convicts that you just talked about, and number two, they have some really well-trained forces that are seasoned from Africa and Syria. While the ones that are the convicts are the ones that`s leading the fight, they are very ill-trained. They are not well-equipped. They`re fighting in human waves and they don`t know what they`re doing and the Ukrainians are just mowing them down.\par \par \uc2 In many cases they`re not given proper ammunition and there hence the shovels. And so you`re going to see this continue with the Russians using these convicts to try and advance into Bakhmut. Currently it is working. To be quite honest with you, they`ve been able to pick up the south portion of Bakhmut. They`ve been able to pick up the west and the northern portion. So there`s one round in for the Ukrainians right now that`s in the east. But it`s technique one I wouldn`t advise any other military to use.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. The Ukrainian president, obviously, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  , says he met with his top generals yesterday and they urged him not to withdraw from Bakhmut. I mean, there is a strong case to be made that those resources could be better used elsewhere, perhaps even allow Ukraine to go on the offensive, but on the other hand, we talked about just how much this means in terms of momentum, in terms of morale, to both sides.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of the decision to keep fighting overall?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Well, I think the reason why the decision was made, I go back to eight months of fighting, and so to every one Ukrainian that`s been lost, there have been seven reportedly Russians that have been lost. And there have been a lot of lives lost in this fight for Bakhmut that is really not a significant or a strategic objective. And so I think it all goes down to the fact that someone wants to win this fight, and the Ukrainians don`t want the Russians to win because it would be a symbolic victory for them and a symbolic loss for the Ukrainians. So I think at the end of the day, that`s what this is all about at this point.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Retired Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, it`s always good to have you on the program. Thank you so much for your time, sir.\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Teacher turnover. Schools across the nation now seeing a record number of resignations. Why is this happening? We`ll hear from some teachers who have resigned, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:56]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s no sign today of a slowdown in the nationwide teacher shortage after new numbers show resignations are at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Stephanie Gosk has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Across the country, fed-up teachers, many tired of long hours and low pay, are calling it quits. In one Virginia county they are hoping a $3,000 bonus will keep teachers from leaving the job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our teachers work hard, our staff works hard. And they need to be fairly compensated.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: But they are down more than 150 teachers. It`s a similar story in school districts across the country. New numbers from eight states show how tough things are getting. Last school year Maryland and Louisiana saw the highest number of resignations in a decade. In Washington state it was the highest number in three decades.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA TILLSON, FORMER TEACHER: When everything shut down and COVID kind of took full swing, that was my first year in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Angela Tillson (PH) resigned from her teaching job in Massachusetts last December.\par \par \uc2 TILLSON: COVID is still very much affecting the education system. The gaps are striking, and there`s only so much that we can do as one person in the overload of work.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on-camera): It sounds like you reached a real breaking point?\par \par \uc2 TILLSON: Yes. I did.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Like many, she shared her story online. There are a mix of reasons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am not going to miss unreasonable class sizes. I am not going to miss 23-minute lunch breaks.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Children are struggling academically. Teacher shortages mean more work and then there`s the pay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Today was my last day as a teacher. I`d like to think it`s just a pause, but I honestly don`t know right now.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In the fall Sayreville, New Jersey, sent out flyers hoping to inspire people to help. Three retired teachers returned to the job, but the district still has 12 vacancies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We were starting to experience a little bit of the shortage last year, but it seems that we hit the pinnacle this year.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on-camera): Did it get worse?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Without a doubt.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): The key, educators say, is getting young college students to choose the profession.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our message to them is you couldn`t ask for a more noble field. You know, if you really want to make an impact on this world, this is where it`s to be done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thanks to Stephanie Gosk for that report, and we`ve got a lot to cover on our second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230308ej3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 6, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Vaughn Hillyard, Matthew Dowd, Jesse Kirsch, Dana  Griffin, Bill Karins, Ali Vitali, Courtney Kube, Matt Bradley, Joyce Vance,  Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,229 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Brutal and bitter nomination fights in modern history. After taking veiled shots at each other from across the country, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are both gearing up for their debuts in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Plus, take a look at these extraordinary images. Another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train flying off the tracks less than 200 miles from that derailment in East Palestine a month ago. With the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  set to arrive, there are today, there are no reports of any toxic chemical spills. But lots of questions about how in the world this happened again.\par \par \uc2 And what started out as a wild weather story has quickly turned into a life-threatening disaster. Scores of California residents hemmed in by literal walls of snow, more than six feet high. They`re trapped and they`re running out of food. We`ll have a live report on the rescue operations that are underway right now.\par \par \uc2 Today, the battle lines for the 2024 Republican nomination are being drawn more clearly than we`ve seen to date, setting up what may be a long and nasty fight for the Republican nomination. We heard it in Donald Trump`s borderline apocalyptic address to a less than robust MAGA crowd at CPAC, while the still undeclared \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  amped up his attacks on liberals in his speech to a packed house at the Reagan Library, portraying himself as the antidote to Trump-style chaos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): In for years, you didn`t see our administration leaking like a sieve. You didn`t see a lot of drama or palace intrigue. What you saw was surgical precision execution day after day after day. And because we did that, we beat the left day after day after day.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know battle. They know it. I know it. You know it. Everybody knows that. This is it. Either they win or we win. And if they win, we no longer have a country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now both Trump and DeSantis are headed to Iowa in the coming days as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports that the former president is digging in for an ugly and protracted battle. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard spent the weekend at CPAC. He`s back now and joins me on set. Susan Page is the Washington bureau chief for USA Today. And Matthew Dowd is MSNBC`s senior political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Oh, Vaughn, what fun you had this weekend at CPAC. Look, Trump is reportedly gearing up, right? He hasn`t been doing much now. He`s reportedly not just going to go out and do rallies, but apparently, if reporting is right, policy speeches as well. What is this campaign look like going forward? Do we know?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. The campaign advisors I`ve talked to, they intend for this should be a very deliberate campaign from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making the case that he over his four years, five-four conservative policies. If we go back to 2015 -- 2016, remember the questions we were having? It was, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually, right a pro- life? Is he actually a fiscal conservative, right? This is --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is he serious about running? Because this is what I recall.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He`s serious about running. Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Do they -- what type of a Republican would he actually be in the White House? That`s not a question this time around. We all lived through those four years. And so, for now, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he`s going and making the case to conservatives around the country, stick with me that after those four years that we could do this again.\par \par \uc2 Next week, a week from now he`s going to Iowa. And then focus there is this education proposal that he put forward in which one of the proposals is to slash federal funding for "any school or program pushing critical race theory, gender ideology or other inappropriate racial, sexual or political content onto our children." And another proposal --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But when you`re at CPAC, are people talking about policies when they talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Chris, let`s be very straightforward about this. for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , a lot of this is about grievance politics, right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He is an aggrieved individual. He says I am going to essentially do what I can to take down the deep state, the Democratic Party. At the same time there are conservative -- or policies today -- modern-day conservative policies that were being discussed at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 One of those being banning gender-affirming care for minors. We heard that over and over from speakers on stage. The border, really -- you know, fentanyl. We -- there are very much issues that propel and compel Republicans to politics. Let`s be very clear. A lot of these are so-called --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That frankly, he used to get elected the first time and to keep the troops together since then.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Correct. And what did you also hear from him this weekend? The Social Security.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He said there`s other Republicans out there taking a shot at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: There`s other -- there are others that want to reform entitlement. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said I am here to protect them. And so, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he is intending to try to make a -- there`s a lot of folks that are going to take the question with the actual policies that he is presenting and putting forward but they are issues that he intends to actually run on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A lot of people are talking, Matt, about the differences between these two men. Let me talk to you, at least on the surface, they`re both running on the same sort of platform, which is look, what I`ve done.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , it`s look. What I did as president. Now we`re seeing with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , look what I`ve accomplished as governor, that`s going to tell you what kind of president I`m going to be. But do you see differences? What differentiates them or might in reality in the eyes of a Republican voter?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, to me, the big difference is one is Trump and one`s not Trump. And that`s the big -- that`s the big difference that there -- he`s going to try to push in this. And I think he`ll try to highlight one is a loser, which is what DeSantis campaign will try to brand \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as in, you can`t win with this guy. And I`m a winner. I just won in a landslide, the largest victory for a Republican in Florida in modern -- in modern times.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s that`s more of it. It`s not going to be an issue. I don`t think they`ll ever be an issue debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . You never get into an issue debate. He doesn`t care about issues.\par \par \uc2 I think the interesting things are these are probably the two biggest practitioners of grievance politics that we`ve seen in our times in politics. And so that strive for the top of the ticket will actually be a search for the bottom is who can go and who can be the cruelest, who can be the meanest, who can take on what their view is enemies of the America they want and that`s what those strive for. But one will try to do it as I`m not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . You know me. I won. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost a series of elections. You don`t want to go with him again.\par \par \uc2 That, to me, will be the decisional point. And the vote -- and the -- and the -- and the pivot upon which the voters in the Republican primary, they liked both of these men. In fact, they liked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more now than they did at the beginning of the process in 2015. So, they liked both of these folks. They liked them both for the hateful politics they push. But one wants to be say I`m a winner, the other one he wants to say he`s a loser.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I think there`s another similarity, Susan, I`ll posit this that they both are in many ways backward-looking not just in running on their record because that`s always to some extent, right looking back, but it tells you what they`re going to do going forward. But look at DeSantis. He`s pitching Florida as a model for the country. But in doing so, for example, he reignited or tried to reignite the debate over COVID. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: When the world went mad when common sense suddenly became an uncommon virtue, Florida stood as a refuge of sanity, a citadel of freedom for people throughout the United States, and indeed, throughout the world. We refused to let our state descend into some type of Fauci and dystopia where people`s rights were curtailed and their livelihoods were destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But to Matt`s point of grievance politics, Susan, he`s also pushing conservative policies like banning diversity programs at public universities, expanding gun rights, restricting abortion rights, how does that help him beat Trump or does it?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2  : Well, I think it helps him beat Trump in the Republican primary. You know, one of the interesting things we`re seeing is it`s possible we are not going to see a replay of the Republican primaries from 2016 when he had a big field and that helped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  win the nomination. I mean, at the moment, it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with no one else really getting a toehold so far. That`s a different landscape for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to face.\par \par \uc2 And in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , he faces someone with whom he has few ideological differences. The differences are stylistic. And this, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a veto-proof majority in the Florida Legislature. So, we should expect them in the next few months to pass his agenda, which will enable him to say look, look at what I can get done and contrast that also with for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s one thing we haven`t said yet, Vaughn, and I don`t think you saw this at CPAC. But there is the exhaustion factor. It`s hard to be exhausted by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  because frankly, a lot of the country is just getting to know who he is. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a completely different story.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: To your point of we all know where we`ve been. We all know what he did for the four years he was president -- the four years since. But is your sense that the exhaustion factor is significant enough to help \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: I think that a conversation actually had with two operatives just this morning centered on the question of how many Republicans who used to be actively engaged in Republican primaries had become disenchanted with the party, maybe even changed your party registration to independent or Democrat so they could vote another party`s primary. You know, how many of those folks that are maybe more tuned with the likes of a \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  , a \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  , a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  , and \uc2 Martha McSally\uc2  ? How many Republican voters are out there? We`re talking 15 percent, 20 percent, 25 percent that maybe -- I`m not meeting out on the road as much here. They`re a little quieter over the last eight years.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But folks that want to be engaged are frustrated by what -- when I say conservatism, what that means today. It doesn`t mean the same thing as 12 years ago, right? And so how many of those folks are going to reengage in this Republican primary presidential contest? They couldn`t do that four years ago, but how many of them are going to be in Iowa and New Hampshire, that maybe I`m not running into on the side of the road or at CPAC or at, you know, the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  event in Cedar Rapids that I was at two weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 But folks that were quietly re-engaged within the Republican Party and tried to take hold that event, that is frankly, an answer that I don`t have, but I`m interested to see. And that may not be in Ron DeSantis`s vote, it may not even be a Nikki Haley vote, right, but it may be for somebody like a \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  or a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So, we`re going to talk about that a little later on in our next hour. Right? There -- a moderate could possibly win the Republican nomination. But, Matt, there`s another interesting development from CPAC, I`m curious to hear your take on. Kari Lake won the vice presidential poll of people like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Nikki Haley. Is that a name-recognition thing or does it tell us something about the MAGA wing of the party?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think it definitely tells us something about the MAGA wing. But I wouldn`t call it the MAGA wing. I would call it the base of the Republican Party in this. You know what`s fascinating is I watched you show that clip of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , which he was at Simi Valley, which is obviously the home of the Reagan Library is that if \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  showed up at this CPAC convention, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  , their hero of past -- of the past would be booed because of where he stood on bridging divides where he stood on various issues. He would be booed today at that CPAC convention in the course of the stat shows you --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Would \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  himself, boo? Right -- he`s not -- he wasn`t the kind of person who would do that. But would he has -- his son`s suggestion -- suggested, I think Michael Reagan suggested this, be unhappy with what he heard in his library from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think if \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  had shown up and saw Kari Lake, he would have thought he was in one of the movies in the 1940s. One of the be movies that he starred in that this is like this can`t be real, right? This is not real in the course of this.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, to me, is so typical of where the Republican Party is. She`s loved by the base. She says the craziest things that they cheer her when she says the craziest thing. She`s still denying the election, that she obviously lives in a state now governed by a Democrat there in Arizona. And I said I think the fact that we`re saying that \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  would be booed and would be out of sync and Kari Lake was a hero, tells you everything you want to know about where the Republican Party is today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Oh, that`s right. Michael Reagan was actually talking about Kari Lake. He wasn`t talking about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . All right, confusing my weakened speeches. Susan, there`s no modern precedent for a defeated former president competing in a primary like this. So, what are you going to be watching for going forward? We just heard was one of the things that Vaughn is going to be looking for after talking to his sources, but what are you going to be looking for to gauge political strength -- political weaknesses, sort of how this race is going to unfold going forward?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, it`s not only unprecedented in modern times because we have a former president who was defeated for re-election running again, but it`s also impressive because we could have a nominee -- a candidate who has been criminally indicted and is still running for president. Former President Trump said this weekend he would run even if he is indicted, which is clearly a possibility in several of the investigations against him.\par \par \uc2 I think that thing you look for is whether \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can consolidate the vote that is not Trump and convince some of the Trump voters that it is OK to vote for him. And that`s going to be a harder task than you think because Trump is a great hero to a significant number of Republicans. He is the face of this party, and he is not likely to go quietly away if \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seems to be gaining ground or even winning the nomination.\par \par \uc2 Would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  support \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  if he wins the Republican nomination?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I`m not sure I know the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I have one more question even though we`re out of time, \uc2 Matt Dowd\uc2  . And that is, again, since he answered a question about it. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to be indicted, does that hurt him or does it just energize his forces?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- I -- it`s not often that we`re able to pick out at something that he says is truthful and probably accurate, which is, as he said in the course of that, that the -- he actually thought an indictment would help him. I actually agree with that because it feeds into they`re all against us. Everyone is against us. I`m fighting for you. And their -- for their taking me on but they`re really taking you on, which is basically what he said.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And so not only do I think he said I would run even if indicted, I think he`s running because he might be indicted. That`s, I think, pined behind his strategy. I actually think among the Republican base before you get to a general election, it would actually help him.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Such interesting stuff. Matthew Dowd, Susan Page, Vaughn Hillyard, who will be on the road for what the next 18 months or so along occasionally with the rest of us, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: I`ll bring you along for some of it, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, you`ve got to see this heart-stopping clip out of Ohio. Take a look. The view from one Springfield Ohio driver, it came -- do we have that? At that exact moment. Another -- there it is, another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train ran off the tracks. The driver is actually slowly backing the car away from that dangerous scene.\par \par \uc2 Officials report no injuries or threats to public health. The railway company confirms there were no hazardous material onboard the 28 cards that derailed. But this is \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  `s second derailment in the state in just weeks. The fourth in less than five months in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Jesse Kirsch who was on the ground there for us in Springfield. Jesse, officials from the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  are now investigating, as I understand at the site where you are, what more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, Chris, two things. One, you can see this is still a blocked-off roadway. We`ve got a cleanup ongoing here. But the trains are moving again. We see multiple trains coming by on these tracks, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  trains.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, this company has now had four derailments in this state in less than five months. Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  , calling that on acceptable making another push for legislative reform, something we know that has bipartisan support in Congress right now. And of course, the CEO of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  is expected to be testifying on Capitol Hill later this week.\par \par \uc2 And the ramifications of what happened roughly a month ago in East Palestinian are clear, even here, more than 200 miles away. I spoke with a gentleman earlier. And I pointed out to him at the \uc2 Ohio Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  says that nothing hazardous leaked into the soil, into the air, or into the water here. Despite that, he says he is still skeptical of what he`s hearing. And what comes to mind is what happened in East Palestine. And of course, residents there are still concerned about their health, about their financial well-being, about the future of that small community after what happened there about a month ago.\par \par \uc2 Back here, this is obviously a fresh derailment. You mentioned federal investigators are now looking into this incident. We have no official word on cause -- we still don`t have an official word on cause of what happened in East Palestine. And one of the things I asked of Norfolk Southern Railroad yesterday is what they would say to people who question whether this railroad should be allowed to operate right now, considering what is going on. The railroad says that safety is its number one priority.\par \par \uc2 They`ve actually just unveiled today a new safety plan in the aftermath of the East Palestine incident. That safety plan includes a push for improving -- expanding their network of sensors, which tracks the temperature of the wheel bearings. One of the things that \uc2 NTSB\uc2  we know is looking at from that -- from that derailment in East Palestine. So, they continue to emphasize safety as something they`re focused on and say that after each -- and each derailment, they do an investigation. And if there are takeaways to improve safety, they will implement those.\par \par \uc2 So still to be determined what comes out of this scene. But we do know now the government is independently investigating separately from the railroad. However, as we heard from a resident here earlier and what we`ve heard from people in the East Palestine, that doesn`t necessarily take away some of the skepticism in communities based on what`s been going on with the railroad.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, they`ve got a long, long climb to get credibility back. Jesse Kirsch, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A nightmare situation in California. Unusual snowstorms have left thousands without power, but others are completely trapped. Now, some people are running out of food. We`ve got a live report from the ground next.\par \par \uc2 The strict new anti-abortion measures and how changing the definition of life-threatening is being used by conservative lawmakers. And the stark warning from Iran`s Supreme Leader about the ongoing suspected poisonings of schoolgirls is ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The effects of wild and dangerous weather are being felt from coast to coast. Right now, more than 120,000 households in Kentucky don`t have power after hurricane-force winds killed at least five people there. Another 25,000 without electricity after heavy snow in Michigan. 20,000 also in the dark in California where officials say some areas could be snowed in for a week as historic blizzards ground traveled to a standstill. Already people in the mountains near LA are pleading for more resources. They`re writing to help us in the snow. You see it right there.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Dana Griffin is live from those mountains in Crestline, California. And NBC News Meteorologist Bill Karins is here with the very latest for us. Dana, what is it like on the ground today, and how complicated and dangerous it is to try to get resources to people who are snowed in?\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, first of all, good afternoon. It is very dangerous and very complicated. And the best way to show you that is literally just to kind of illustrate it for you here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 I want you to take a look behind me. This is the only grocery store in this town. If you look through the windows, this is not a reflection of the outside. You`re actually looking inside Goodwin & Sons Market where so much snow and debris has fallen when that roof caved in on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But the beautiful thing about the story is how much the community has come together. So many items have been donated, including 50 of these shovels from the Rancho Cucamonga Lowes. This is the last one. And they say that they used shovels like this because the plastic ones have been breaking because the snow is just so heavy and dense and compact, really icy.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this distribution center that has formed outside of this now-shuttered grocery store. There are protein shakes, formula, diapers, food, so many items that people really could use right now. We`re going to walk over to the distribution lines. You can see still this line stretches down to the end of the parking lot.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at some of the people. There are a little kids here. Parents bringing their children. One of the new items that we`ve seen here is DiGiorno Pizza, obviously something that you can freeze and pop into an oven if you know you`re a resident that still has heat. There are still hundreds of people without heat.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And the big concern now is how many people are at those higher elevations that are still stuck inside their homes. One woman who was standing inside this line commented that we`re the lucky ones. We could actually leave our homes. A lot of them we`re having to hike out of feet of snow to try to get down here.\par \par \uc2 But this is just the beauty of this community of how much they have come together to help each other out. They`re even giving out dog food and bird seed because people have animals that they`re also trying to keep alive during this really frustrating time for them. And the concern now is come the end of the week on Friday, when more snow is expected in this community, how much more is that going to affect these community -- this community. So, everyone`s trying to stay optimistic, but they`re really looking for more help, for plows to reach their streets so that more of them can get out, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dana Griffin, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Bill, I mean, honestly, when she first -- that -- the camera person, like went into the grocery store, I was having trouble getting oriented that the huge snow pile was actually inside that grocery store. Give us a sense of just how bad it is there.\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, MSNBC BROADCAST METEOROLOGIST: And how bad it`s going to get?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: I mean I hate it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m not sure I want to know.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: So many times, we have these big events and your -- you know biggest question is, oh, what`s next. And a lot of times, it`s not much or it`s something small. I`m fearful of what`s coming Thursday into Friday, and then possibly Tuesday to Wednesday, the week after that. We`re in a very active weather pattern for the West.\par \par \uc2 And what`s going to change is that these next couple storms are going to be what we call atmospheric rivers and they`re going to be warmer. So, all of a sudden, we`re going to get rain on top of these roofs that a lot of snow, it`s just going to soak it all up, and then we`re going to have -- dealing with roof collapses, we`re going to have flooding issues. I mean, California is going to be a mess for at least the next two weeks. And we may not have even seen the worst of it.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got a couple of baby storms. We`ll call these as little storms off the coast right now. Mostly it`s only cruises problems in the northern half of the Sierra Mountain chain. So, we still have these winter storm warnings from Yosemite up to Kirkwood. Mammoth Lakes is not in this one -- Mount Shasta. So, this is northern half of California so we`re giving southern half of California a break.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the timing goes, it`s just going to be a persistent flow coming off the ocean. So, the orthographic lifts of the mountains when the air rises up the mountains, it produces snow. So, they will get 12, 18 maybe in some cases, two feet of snow over the next couple days.\par \par \uc2 But it`s kind of spread out a little bit. Temperatures are fairly warm, so the snow levels are pretty high. We get a little heavier blast coming in Wednesday. And that`s when we should see additional problems, especially for anyone that`s still trying to dig out in this area.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, it`s just amazing. You know if you told me six months ago, we`d be talking about too much rain or too much snow in California, I wouldn`t have believed you. I mean, this has been an incredible winter for snow and rain. And it looks like it`s going to continue through March.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Bill Karins, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you. And stay with us. A live update on where our search and rescue efforts stemmed from the Sheriff of Madeira County, California. That`s coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 Police are now interviewing victims, security officials, and attendees searching for a cause of a stampede that killed one person and injured eight more at the end of a concert in Rochester, New York. The mayor promising accountability this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MALIK EVANS, MAYOR OF ROCHESTER, NEW YORK: This is a tragedy of epic proportions. What I saw last night in the aftermath of what happened at this concert breaks my heart and it is totally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As it had just wrapped up in a downtown venue when the Rochester police chief says the crowd of thousands began to surge toward the exits. Two of the eight surviving victims are said to be in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 New information about the classified documents taken from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , a top Democrat revealing what the Gang of Eight has been told. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:50]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A rare occurrence on Capitol Hill today. Top lawmakers from both parties agreeing on one thing, the need for intelligence officials to share more information about what`s in those classified documents found on the Biden, Trump and Pence properties. Two members of the Gang of Eight spoke with our Chuck Todd on "MEET THE PRESS."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R), OHIO: In the things that we do know, one of the things we know is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is not being forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): Let`s just say that neither one of us are satisfied that we got enough information. We got a flavor for what was there. And I won`t speak for Mike, but I will tell you having been given a flavor, this is a very serious issue. This wasn`t stuff that we can say clearly does not matter. It matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Ali. I mean, there have been widespread bipartisan calls for more details. So where does this go from here?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and Chris, think about it. Even just to get to last week`s briefing, you had to have Democrats and Republicans on both sides of Congress asking for months since those documents that were found at Mar-a-Lago became public. That`s the result of months of asking, is a meeting that they had last week with the Gang of Eight that you see on your screen there where they got some information.\par \par \uc2 But even walking into that meeting, people like Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  , the top Republican on the Senate Intel Committee, didn`t have high hopes for a high degree of transparency. Instead, their view walking in was that this was going to be sort of like the opening salvo, the first conversation that they`re having about what could potentially be in these documents.\par \par \uc2 But you listen to the two top -- the Republican and Democrat on the House side of the Intel Committee, and it`s clear what they said on "MEET THE PRESS." They`re not satisfied with what they`ve gotten so far. They`re still not sure that sources and methods weren`t compromised by these classified documents being found out of their usual chain of command, and they want more information.\par \par \uc2 Now, how they get that information is sort of the question of the day up here on Capitol Hill. Clearly we`re going to hear more pressuring from all of these key senators and congresspeople on this issue, but, again, they might continue running into the same problem, which is that the director of National Intelligence and other entities have said that while this risk assessment that they`re doing because Congress asked them to is something that they could get briefed on.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 They are running into the problem of those special counsel investigations and not wanting to compromise those. So a careful balancing act on the part of the administration here. But, again, lawmakers just want more information about what was specifically in these documents.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, I know you`ll keep us posted. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 U.N . members have reached an historic agreement to protect the world`s oceans. It`s a unified treaty and it`s been in the works for nearly 20 years to protect biodiversity on the high seas. Translation, they`re creating a new body which is supposed to manage conservation of ocean life and set up areas to protect marine life, dolphins, whales, sea turtles and many fish that travel long distances across international waters.\par \par \uc2 Two Ukrainian pilots are in the U.S. How it may help them get into the cockpits of fighter jets. The Pentagon reporter who broke that story will join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:03]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For the first time in eight months, pro-Putin fighters are close to taking a Ukrainian city, but Ukraine`s ability to respond long term is getting a major boost from the U.S. Military.\par \par \uc2 Today two Ukrainian pilots are on American soil working with U.S. Military pilots for the first time on the use of attack aircraft including F-16s. And additional Ukrainian pilots could be joining them soon.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Pentagon correspondent Courtney Kube broke the news and she joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Courtney, they`re calling this a training assessment, not a training program. What`s the difference and what more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Right. They`re actually calling this a familiarization event. But when you look at what familiarization means, some synonym or in the thesaurus, and synonyms are to educator to train.\par \par \uc2 What the Defense officials that we`ve spoken with over the last couple of weeks about this are saying is that, number one, these pilots are here to assess their skills, to see how long it could take them to train on other platforms beside what the Ukrainian air force is already using. And that includes both flying platforms and employing things like munitions from other platforms.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to that, they are also familiarizing them with things that are -- with aircraft that they do not use already in the Ukrainian inventory. It`s important to point out these pilots, these two pilots who are already here have been here for a couple of weeks at a U.S. Military base in Tucson, Arizona. And as many as 10 more may be joining them in the coming days as part of this larger event.\par \par \uc2 Now during the reporting on this, Chris, our colleague Carol Lee and I spoke with a number of administration officials and Defense officials about this. And they insist that despite this event that`s occurring at a U.S. Military base in Arizona, the administration still is resisting the calls from Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and other Ukrainian officials to provide attack aircraft like F-16s to the Ukrainians. They continue to say that they have not made that decision.\par \par \uc2 We actually heard some specifics about that for the first time from the Pentagon`s top policy official Dr. Collin Cole last week before the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  . He said that it would take as much as 18 months to train some of these Ukrainian pilots on aircraft like the F-16. But we spoke with Defense officials after that, and part of this assessment will determine whether that actually is the case.\par \par \uc2 If you have pilots who have experience on other attack aircraft like MIGs that the Ukrainians fly, it`s possible they could cut that training time down to as low as six months. And we have to say, you know, since this war started, since Russia invaded Ukraine just over a year ago now, the Ukrainian military has repeatedly been able to surpass expectations about how quickly they can train on some of these more difficult and advanced systems that the U.S. and others have been providing them, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It is fascinating stuff. Courtney Kube, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s top general told President Zelenskyy today that they don`t want to give up on the key eastern city of Bakhmut even as Russian forces are closing in.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Matt Bradley is following the story for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Matt, I had heard the speculation Ukraine was ready to retreat after all that fierce fighting in Bakhmut. Well, what are officials there saying now?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, now we`re hearing, as you just mentioned, from President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  saying that it looks as though the generals -- and we know that President Zelenskyy doesn`t run the day-to-day of this war, it`s the generals who do so. They say that they want to stay in Bakhmut, that they want to continue defending this city.\par \par \uc2 Now as Courtney Kube just mentioned a moment ago, this has been going on for a year ever since Russia`s invasion happened in February 24th back last year. This battle for Bakhmut has been going on almost as long as that. It is by far the longest battle that Ukraine has endured since the beginning of this fight. And it`s arguably the most lethal and the most damaging.\par \par \uc2 But we`re seeing here two sides pit against each other that seem to be almost entirely equal in terms of strength, even though the Ukrainians seem to be having the upper hand in a lot of different areas throughout the country. In Bakhmut, they seemed very evenly matched. Much of the fighting has been led by the Wagner Group on the Russian side, which is headed by \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  who`s an oligarch, who`s very close to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 He`s been leading the fight, not the Russian regular military, for most of this time. But, you know, the fact that there`s been so much damage on both sides for this entire battle, and that there`s been so much death on both sides this entire battle, it really has baffled Western military analysts who`ve been watching this, who`ve been advising the Ukrainians. They wondered why the Ukrainians don`t withdraw and why the Russians themselves don`t withdraw from this city and focus on other places.\par \par \uc2 Because the fact is that Bakhmut, while it does have some strategic value, is really not worth the amount of material and lives that both sides have been spending. It is, as \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  , the U.S. Defense Department secretary said just today, that it would be a symbolic victory for Russia to take Bakhmut, not really a strategic victory. But really the fight for Bakhmut has also opened up a lot of fissures within the military leadership in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 While you just mentioned how leaders in Ukraine seem to be a bit more on side, they all are together with Zelenskyy offering to defend Bakhmut, to continue their stand against the Russian invasion there, the Russian attack has really been so damaging to the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:12]\par \par \uc2 And that`s because, as I mentioned, this man Prigozhin, who leads the Wagner Group, this is a private military -- a private army. He has been repeatedly accusing the Kremlin, not necessarily Putin himself, but the leaders of the Ministry of Defense there in Russia of not providing them with the weapons they need in order to succeed in Bakhmut. So as this battle rages on the Russian side, despite all the advice, despite all of the conventional military thinking, it really has done a lot of damage to the unity of the Kremlin and the Russian war effort. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Once again, Ukrainians who are often outperforming what a lot of people think that they can.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 The moves that threaten reproductive rights across the U.S., the states taking on abortion and the intricate strategies some lawmakers are using.\par \par \uc2 And millions of Americans now left without the means to put food on the table. I`ll ask Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  of Massachusetts if Congress will do anything about it. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now two Republican governors have bills on their desks that would make it harder for people to access abortions in their states. In Utah, there`s a measure that would effectively ban abortion clinics from operating, pushing all abortions medical or surgical in that state to hospitals. In Wyoming, two bills that would ban abortions with narrow exceptions and criminalize the use of medications for abortions.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in former U.S. attorney and professor at the University of Alabama Law School, Joyce Vance.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, let me just start with an example. Abortion was long considered protected by the Wyoming state constitution, which guarantees adults the right to make their own health care decisions. So lawmakers wrote this bill saying abortions are not health care.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is there any limit, essentially, to what states can legislate or I guess in this case define?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: So there really aren`t any limits on states any longer. Before the Supreme Court sort of ripped all of the protections that in previously accompanied abortion off, states had limits on what they could do. But in Dobb`s last term, the Supreme Court said there is no constitutional right to get an abortion. That means states can set up whatever legal regime they want. It can be completely permissive or it can ban abortion outright, and that`s why we`re seeing states like Utah and Wyoming pass these very restrictive measures. There`s no longer any concern that it`s not constitutional.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So does it include this? Another example, Joyce. Exemptions from that law include a pregnancy that would endanger the woman`s life or health, but they specifically say that does not cover being suicidal. They do not consider being suicidal a threat to a woman`s life. Could these rewrites be the basis for a legal challenge or simply, again, because they`re state legislatures and they`re writing laws, they can define anything any way they want to?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So there will be challenges. I feel certain of that. The problem of that is that with the Supreme Court, this very conservative Supreme Court, is the ultimate decider on abortion issues. It`s tough to feel optimistic, and so the example that you identify, Chris, you know, this law that has very limited exceptions for the health of the mother, will doctors still perform those procedures at the risk of falling under criminal suspicion?\par \par \uc2 So you know that that will disincentivize them from performing or providing services in virtually all circumstances, and then as you say, even that bill has no carve out whatsoever for the mother`s emotional health or mental health. So these are not protections for women. These are simply efforts for these bills to survive scrutiny in the courts if they get there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about a bill sort of on the other end of the spectrum. In Washington state where abortion is legal, state lawmakers are proposing laws to protect women who are getting an abortion. One would block subpoenas for information from other states where it`s illegal. So in other words, a woman who lives in a state, let`s say Texas where there are restrictions, decides she wants to come to Washington state to get an abortion, that would block anyone in that state from sending that information back to Texas or any other state where it might be used to prosecute her, right?\par \par \uc2 I know, for example, there are countries that won`t share information with the U.S. or don`t have extradition treaties with the U.S. Maybe that`s a bad comparison, but would this be a new area of state law, this blocking, any sharing of information?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So I think the extradition agreements are actually a really good example because there are some countries, the United Kingdom is one of them, that says, look, we don`t agree with the United States policy on the death penalty, so we won`t submit folks in our care to your jurisdiction. That`s essentially what`s going to happen with this litigation over whether one state that chooses to outlaw abortion can impose its belief in other states.\par \par \uc2 For instance, when citizens of Texas travel elsewhere to obtain medical services, look, this is the next frontier in litigation. Five years ago, we would have all said there`s no chance that Texas can interfere in Washington, but now I think we have to be honest and say we just don`t know what the courts will rule.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ll be talking about this continuously.\par \par \uc2 Joyce Vance, it`s always good to see you, my friend. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, the latest scare in the sky. Why one Florida bound train had to make a rare emergency landing. Wait until you hear those details, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The plane just shook and then it dropped. It was -- very terrifying because a lot of kids and a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today we`re talking about new aviation concerns because there have been more scary incidents in the sky, including a rare emergency landing and severe turbulence that left one passenger dead.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Tom Costello has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): High drama on the runway at Havana`s airport on Sunday. A \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  flight, departing for Fort Lauderdale, forced to make an emergency landing. The airline says an apparent bird strike hit the plane`s engine and its nose shortly after takeoff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chaos. Complete chaos. Talking about dropping really quick and the fuse getting filled up, and nobody could breathe, and everybody`s screaming.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile, a separate instance of severe turbulence in the air turned deadly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Requesting medical assistance on the runway.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: A private CL-30 jet carrying five people on its way to Virginia from New Hampshire encountered severe turbulence on Friday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is the tower with a medical emergency, landing runway 6.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The pilot diverted the jet from its original route to make an emergency landing in Connecticut. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  and \uc2 NTSB\uc2  confirming the extreme turbulence caused fatal injuries to a passenger on board.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not rare to have serious injuries, broken bones, people being jostled about, but to have someone actually die means that something traumatic really must have happened.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating and the manufacturer of the jet says it will fully support and provide assistance to all authorities as needed.\par \par \uc2 It comes after yet another midair incident last week.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230307ej3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for February 28, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Charlie Sykes, Ali Vitali, Lindsey Reiser, Jonathan  Lemire, Ellison Barber, Lisa Rubin, Philip Rucker, Ryan Nobles, Tom Winter,  Dasha Burns \par \uc2 8,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Making stops at a number of critical early primary states in the coming weeks. And with two big gatherings of conservatives coming, the split in the party is on full display, including the GOP event where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t welcome.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the incredibly high stakes Supreme Court hearing on the Biden administration`s attempt to wipe out nearly half a trillion dollars worth of student loans. One justice pressing the solicitor general on why that`s not an abuse of presidential power.\par \par \uc2 And the White House using federal dollars as the Biden administration tries to solve a problem facing millions of moms out there, a lack of affordable childcare. How businesses in need of workers may be part of the solution? Coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with the battle for the soul of the Republican Party amid new signs of momentum for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . His book hitting number one just hours after going on sale. But also new signs of the challenge he and the rest of the potential Republican field face with a poll out today showing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  holding a nearly 20-point lead.\par \par \uc2 And all of that coming ahead of two conservative events that will demonstrate the choice the party has ahead of it. One, for CPAC, an organization bound tightly to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA crowd. The other, the Club for Growth, which has mostly severed ties with the former president in its search for new blood.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at the guest list. While Trump will headline at CPAC in DC, nearly every other Republican hopeful will be at the Club for Growth`s donor retreat in Florida. That includes \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , Tim Scott. Trump, very pointedly, wasn`t even invited there.\par \par \uc2 But anyone ready to declare the end of the Trump era needs to take a look at this new morning console poll. It shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with 48 percent. That`s more than DeSantis, Pence, and Haley combined.\par \par \uc2 So, lots to talk about. I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali. And \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2  is the editor at large of the Bulwark and an MSNBC political analyst. So, Charlie, you wrote quite colorfully, I might add --\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES\uc2  , MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- that when CPAC convenes, you`ll sooner find a golden statue of Trump than a conservative principle. So, I ask you in that context, are we just seeing Republicans shift away from CPAC because of say the sexual assault allegations against its leader or is this a larger shift away from Trump and the MAGA crowd in general?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, it`s clearly a split between the crazies and the normies in the Republican Party, but I think it`s more of a separation than a complete divorce. We`ll have to see where it goes. I think everybody needs to be reminded that the future of the Republican Party is not going to be decided by the donors or by these organizations. It`s going to be decided by the Republican primary voters, and your poll just showed where they are at the moment.\par \par \uc2 But you know, CPAC is clearly been trending in this direction for some time. And it`s been you know notoriously known as the Star Wars bar scene of the conservative movement. And under \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  , it has become more extreme, more part of the entertainment wing, and less substantive.\par \par \uc2 But this is -- this is a rather dramatic contrast between what`s happening at CPAC and what`s happening at the Club for Growth. And it wasn`t that long ago that it would have been inconceivable for someone like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to simply say no, I`m not going to CPAC. So, something`s going on. What it is, is not exactly clear at the moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s kind of tough to top, Ali, when somebody throws out the Star Wars bar scene, but I know you`re going to give it the old college try. Look. DeSantis is looking more and more like a candidate. Talk about what we`re just learning that he`s up to.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Look, he`s doing the things that we would expect candidates at this point to do. And largely that means that he`s meeting with donors, huddling with them over the weekend. He`s plotting trips across the country. Most recently, he was in places like New York. He will also go to Chicago. And then also follow that up with some closed-door stops with Republican state parties in Texas and Alabama before then going to California.\par \par \uc2 That`s part of his book tour. And there`s going to be other stops associated with that too. But I think what really caught my attention is that he`s got this book out about a blueprint for Florida -- a Florida blueprint for America. That`s why his advisors and frankly DeSantis himself says he`s so focused on the upcoming Florida legislative session because it allows him to continue to bolster his credentials, bolster his foundations, and allow that to be what he ultimately runs on for president if he makes that decision.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other thing that caught my attention, and you mentioned this, our new reporting that a source familiar confirms to me that the DeSantis team is mulling trips to states that most people who want to be president tend to go to. That`s Iowa, New Hampshire, Nevada, maybe even South Carolina. So, clearly, this is coming together in a very unofficial capacity, even as something official probably comes more toward the summer, the month I keep hearing is June, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And yet, Charlie --\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2  : Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- this poll shows us that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still has a firm grasp on the Republican electorate, right? I mean, maybe, let`s say even beyond firm.\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2  : Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And for years, we talked about how Republican leaders waited to see what the voters would do, and maybe they follow them.\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2  : Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Are the voters speaking or is it way, way, way too early to talk like that?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, it is early but I think that you are seeing the dynamic. Look, I mean, one of the key things to understand is that if you had a Venn diagram of Trump supporters and DeSantis supporters, there`s a lot of overlap. There have been surveys that have asked Trump supporters who is your acceptable second choice, and they largely say DeSantis, same thing with DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 This is why this is the race to watch. This is why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been attacking DeSantis because he understands that DeSantis poses the greatest threat to him. Because as DeSantis rises, it is likely by taking votes away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And so, you are seeing \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  taking all of these steps that look like he`s running for president.\par \par \uc2 What we don`t know, two things. Number one, how does he scale up? How does \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  go from Governor of Florida to a presidential candidate? And how will he react to Donald Trump`s attacks? Does he have a glass jaw? And that there are some Republicans that somehow think that you can defeat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  without ever saying his name, without ever answering him back, and I think that`s naive.\par \par \uc2 So, what we don`t know is what`s going to happen. What happens when these two engage with one another? When \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , if he`s ever willing to fire back at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , what will the reaction of the base be? And that`s a -- that is a complete question mark at the moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I think it`s the key question, Ali. And when I was reading the fantastic sort of summary that you and other folks in our very late-night DC unit wrote about what`s in this book --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- one line struck me in particular, and it`s this -- speaking of DeSantis. It`s a talented student ready to take on the mantle however reluctant his mentor is to give it to him. So, so far, it`s sort of been a one-sided fight at least verbally but where`s the expectation of where this might be going?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. Chris, and when you talk about what I wrote in our note overnight, that`s my sense after reading this book because nowhere in the 288 pages is there an outright criticism of Trump. There are a lot of mentions of him more than a hundred of them throughout those roughly 288 or so pages. But never does DeSantis take him on directly.\par \par \uc2 And I will attempt to match Charlie for Star Wars. I don`t know if you want to call it Yoda and Skywalker or Skywalker and Vader, but at a certain point, only one of them is going to be able to continue. It`s maybe more Voldemort and Harry Potter, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, I think that Charlie is right because you have to be the last person standing. You can`t be the person standing alongside Trump. That`s not how you win the nomination. At a certain point, they are going to have to go at them.\par \par \uc2 But what I think this book does is answer the question of how DeSantis might try to do that without alienating the MAGA base. And by making himself look like he`s his own governor, he`s his own policy maker in the state of Florida, at various points in this book, he details ways that he was able to get more aid for Floridians in a faster way because of his relationship with Trump. He`s really trying to show I think that he is an adept political student who previously has benefited from Trumpism and sees why this happened in the first place in 2016 but also making it seem that almost by accident and almost cold to this moment, he is now the best person to lead.\par \par \uc2 And you contrast that with the way that former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley is trying to make this a generational argument and then \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in an interview with me making it more of a stylistic leadership argument. So, each of them are trying to do something that`s kind of the same, same but different, which is going at Trump but not directly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2  , love having you both on the program. Love all of those references. Appreciate you. I`m a Baby Yoda fan myself, but that`s for another day.\par \par \uc2 Right now, another huge story we`re following very closely. And that`s the Supreme Court hearing those arguments on two critical cases challenging President Biden`s campaign promise to forgive some crippling student loan debt. In Biden v. Nebraska, six Republican-led states are suing the Obama - - the Biden administration, arguing that the president doesn`t have the authority to erase school debt, that it`s up to Congress. And in Department of Education v. Brown, two student loan borrowers who weren`t eligible for relief say the government broke the rules when they rolled out the program by not giving them a chance to comment.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC political analyst and Politico`s White House bureau chief Jonathan Lemire is going to break down the political implications for us in just a moment. But first, Lindsey Reiser is here with me in the studio.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So many millions literally of people on pins and needles --\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY REISER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- waiting to see what the decision is here. Help us understand how we got here and what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 REISER: So, this is who qualifies. Borrowers of federal loans would get forgiven $10,000 -- $20,000 if you`re a Pell Grant recipient. They show financial need. But here are the income requirements. This is for individuals making up to $125,000 or for households making up to $250,000.\par \par \uc2 Chris, this was first proposed on the campaign trail in 2019. Senator Elizabeth Warren, a candidate then, proposed forgiving up to $50,000 of student loan debt. Candidate Biden then trailed after that about a year later, suggesting a minimum of $10,000 forgiveness. Then we had the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And part of the Cares Act that President Trump signed pause student loan repayments, paused interest accrual. It became an official plan in 2022. Last August, if you remember from President Biden, then the lawsuits start coming in. That leads us to today with the Supreme Court arguments.\par \par \uc2 Chris, this is a look here at the national debt and how it`s broken up among families. We know mortgages makes up the lion`s share. But look at number two, student loans.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Can I just say this is incredible? I don`t think most people would know. We know that mortgage -- the biggest amount of money anybody ever spends is usually out of house, right? But student loans?\par \par \uc2 REISER: This is how ubiquitous it is. And then we look at the student loan debt and how it has increased over the years. 2007, the amount of student loan debt in this country was half a trillion, and now it is 1.634 trillion. This is why the White House, Chris, says this is needed.\par \par \uc2 This is the graph right now showing the trajectory of the cost of a four- year public university. This is how Pell Grant values have relatively remained stagnant. And then we look at what real people are saying about this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have Lisa Brown of North Carolina, I`m going to struggle until the day I die with this debt. Another one, Brianna in Connecticut, how am I supposed to plan on having kids and pay for their future? But then, Chris, we have the opposition.\par \par \uc2 For example, Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  saying not one penny of this debt is being canceled or forgiven. It`s being transferred from the 13 percent of people who don`t have a debt to the 87 percent of the country that does not. That`s the state of the art in that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. You got people -- you have people who are arguing I paid off my debt, why should somebody else get it forgiven, right?\par \par \uc2 REISER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And that`s part of the argument for the Republicans here. We`re just going to have to wait and see. Fascinating stuff, Lindsey.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, I want to talk about the implications of this ruling for the 2024 race. Let me start on the Democratic side. What would a win mean for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- for his administration? And if the court rules against this, could it energize the Democratic base that`s concerned for a lot of other reasons, frankly, about a conservative Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. The White House is, of course, watching today`s hearing arguments with extreme interest. And there`s been some reporting that the judges voiced a lot of skepticism about the legality of the administration`s plan. So, we shouldn`t rush to conclusions but that may not be a good sign for the west wing.\par \par \uc2 But you are right. They think this is a winning political issue. Now, of course, they also will say they just believe in it on the merits. They think this is the right thing to do. They think this is a good thing for Americans, particularly the working class who would stand to benefit the most from this.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, there`s a political upside. If they were able to get this through. If this does become law, you will see the number of people -- the number of voters across the Democratic Party be energized, be excited, perhaps that in 2024, want to reward the Democratic Party and President Biden with more votes. And conversely, as you just hinted, if it goes the other way and if this has struck down and the president is ruled not to have the legal ability to do this, it could inflame voters that much further.\par \par \uc2 Might there be some anger at the Biden administration for not keeping a campaign promise? Yes, it could. Aids tell me their little concern about that. But they think it will be outweighed by a frustration with the Supreme Court that they feel the American people believe is out of step with American values. And that was on display.\par \par \uc2 They say, per -- that -- would polling suggests how the American people views the right to choose versus what the Supreme Court did by striking down Roe v. Wade last year. And this would be yet another example of that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And I wonder if it works again the way that you know, it has in the past that the Supreme Court has suddenly become such a flashpoint in these elections. Because conversely, on the Republican side -- and I`m looking at these great quotes that Lindsey picked out, look at Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  , if we were truly canceling student loans as President Biden continues to claim, we the people would not be paying for it. Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  calls the student loan socialism. Slap in the face to every family who sacrifices to save for college. Did they think they have a winning issue here as well?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: I think there are some Americans who do feel that if they were to not benefit from this, they look around and see that others had portions of their debt forgiven and they don`t. Sure. I think that there would be perhaps some resentment and anger there.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 But I think there would be others, of course, who would feel exactly the opposite who say, look, this is you know the debt is crippling, the college payment system is -- it`s out of whack right now with what your average American can afford, and a little bit of a help up, particularly among people who have not benefited much in the past would be appreciated. So, this is a tricky one. It does play down party lines, to be clear, but polling does suggest that on the whole, it`s more popular with Americans. And that`s what the Biden White House is banking on.\par \par \uc2 And also, of course, just bigger picture. Also, on the issue of executive power here. Another thing up for debate, you know, whether the United -- the president of the United States, who we`re seeing here get off Marine One about to head to Virginia for an event on health care and other efforts to help out the working class, that if the president wheezing -- voting in the pandemic as emergency, if he`s found to not have the ability to do this, well, then that would limit the ability for this president and future presidents going forward.\par \par \uc2 And as we know, Chris, every presidential election, at least somewhat is about the ability to appoint future justices to the Supreme Court. So far, this president only has one. But we`re here to get another four years, certainly, the odds are will be he`d have chances for more.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan Lemire and good timing with this live picture of the president, as you say, heading off Marine One getting on to Air Force One, thank you. Lindsey Reiser, absolutely fascinating stuff. We`re going to keep a close eye on what`s happening at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, it`s been another emotional day at the Alex Murdaugh murder trial as the prosecution calls new witnesses to the stand. We`re at the courthouse. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  strips \uc2 Disney\uc2  of its self-governing status and taking on big corporations -- is taking on big corporations and culture wars, a smart strategy or a risky one? And the \uc2 U.S. Marshals Service\uc2  suffers a major security breach, the sensitive information that was compromised. Ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: More fireworks on day 25 of the highly closely watched Alex Murdaugh double murder trial. The defense intensely questioning a prosecution rebuttal witness, former Murdaugh law partner, and close friend Ronnie Crosby. He testified Murdaugh stole millions from his own firm.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICK HARPOOTLIAN, MURDAUGH`S DEFENSE ATTORNEY: Are you angry at him for stealing your money?\par \par \uc2 RONNIE CROSBY, WITNESS: I have no feeling one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: You don`t have any feelings about Alex Murdaugh betraying you and stealing your money? You`re -- I admire you. I don`t know that I can look beyond that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Objection, Your Honor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ellison Barber is outside the courthouse. And we`re joined by MSNBC legal analyst Lisa Rubin. Ellison, talk to us a little bit more about what happened there as the prosecution is calling its final witnesses.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. We`ve heard from a few witnesses today but let`s go a little further on that witness who you just played a soundbite from Ronnie Crosby. This is someone who says he has known Alex Murdaugh since the `90s. He was a law partner of his. They have worked together very closely.\par \par \uc2 There was a really tense exchange between Crosby and the defense attorney Dick Harpootlian where Harpootlian kept pressing him saying, aren`t you angry? You have to be so angry that Alex Murdaugh stole all of this money from you, that he looked at you and lied for so many years. Ronnie Crosby repeatedly said he had experienced anger but said he did not have it anymore. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: You are not angry with Alex Murdaugh?\par \par \uc2 CROSBY: I have had anger with him -- extreme anger, Mr. Harpootlian because of what he did to my law firm, my partners, my client, his clients, our clients, what he did to his family, what he did to so many people. Yes, I experienced a lot of anger. But you can`t walk around with anger. You have to find a way to deal with it and move forward. And I have done that. And if you suggest -- you are dead wrong if you think I`ve come in here and told this jury something because of money when we were talking about two people who were brutally murdered, then you`re headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELLISON: So, you hear just some of the emotion in his voice. He was frustrated by the fact that the defense was seeming to imply that he was testifying against Alex Murdaugh because he was so angry that he had stolen money from him for so many years -- in light rather, for so many years. You`ve heard him there maintaining that that wasn`t what motivated him. Instead of saying that he forgave him which he did not say he said he simply felt nothing when it comes to Alex Murdaugh, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lisa, that exchange. I don`t know, does it make the -- let`s just say we`re seeing it out of context there, but he certainly seems like a believable witness.\par \par \uc2 LISA RUBIN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: He does.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He wasn`t having any of it.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: He does. And Harpootlian who represents Alex Murdaugh was definitely trying to shake Ronnie Crosby, right? He was trying to get that flash of anger from him, that in some respects, the prosecution got out of Murdaugh himself last week. And he didn`t get it. He got maybe exactly what the prosecution wanted, which is enough emotion to make him seem credible and human, but not so much that he seemed like a hot-headed person motivated solely by anger with his former law partner as opposed to just wanting the truth to come out.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ellison, we also heard from the doctor who performed the original autopsy. She disputed the findings from the defense. Tell us a little bit about what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Yes. So, when you have the defense pathologist take the stand the other day, he questioned a lot of the findings in the original autopsy. Questioning blood spatter, questioning trajectory, claiming that what the original autopsy and the state pathologist said was an exit wound was actually a contact wound, which would imply that a shooter was in a different position than the prosecution had argued prior. She pushed back very aggressively on that and defended her work repeatedly saying that she stands by her original report saying that the gunshot wound on Paul Murdaugh`s head was an exit wound. She said that is because of the wounds that are on his neck and shoulder. She also, in very graphic detail, told the jury that in her view, there is no way that a gunshot wound on the back of his head could be a contact wound from a gunshot.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 She said I know what you have seen is horrific. But if that was true, if what the defense is claiming was true that that was the contact wound the damage to Paul Murdaugh`s face and skull, it would have been so much worse, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Lisa, we`ve got about 50 more minutes before they come back from a lunch break that they are indicating maybe one more witness. Look, that`s what everybody is waiting for is for the jurors to go to the scene of the crime. What are you going to be watching for?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Maybe we watching to see how carefully jurors are sort of looking at the distances between two places, right? The prosecution has established a timeline where we know that at 8:44, Murdaugh took a cell phone video, perhaps accidentally for about 50 seconds. The murders were allegedly committed, according to the prosecution, minutes later. Murdaugh says he was back in his bed. He then rose. He went to go visit his mother. And so, the jurors are going to be looking sort of how much distance is there between all of these things on the property.\par \par \uc2 One of the complicating factors, Chris, is that there are trees that are higher and bigger and denser than they were at the time of the crime. And that may impact how the jury sees it as well. But I`m reminded of the O.J. Simpson trial where jurors very famously also took a field trip. And changes in the crime scene can be ones that impact how the jurors see a crime. So, we shall see.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s a risk. Lisa Rubin, Ellison Barber, thanks to both of you. And in fact, in our next hour, I`m going to be talking to a defense attorney who actually took a jury to a crime scene in another very high- profile case. We`ve got a lot to talk to him about. That`s coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 The White House plan to get companies to provide affordable childcare. We`ll talk about it with someone working inside the CHIPS program. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:41]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got breaking news out of the White House. Announcing just moments ago that President Biden will nominate Julie Su to be the next labor secretary. Su is currently deputy labor secretary and would replace \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  who`s set to leave to head up the NHL`s Players Union.\par \par \uc2 The White House called Su a, quote, "tested and experienced leader" and a champion for workers. Assuming she`s confirmed Su would be the second Asian-American woman to lead the Labor Department following \uc2 Elaine Chao\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 And today for the White House, a kind of let`s kill two birds with one stone approach. Making a whopping $40 billion available to the makers of computer chips, but if those businesses want that cash they`ll have to provide childcare to employees. Something that a lot of business leaders have been saying anyway that`s necessary if they`re going to hire the employees they need.\par \par \uc2 According to a McKenzie report, 45 percent of mothers with young children who left the workforce during the COVID-19 pandemic cited childcare as a major reason for their departure. 35 percent of childcare centers that shut down during the pandemic are still closed. Supporters of the move say it`s a powerful way to leverage the funds included in the bipartisan CHIPS Act to empower workers.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about it with Adrienne Elrod, director of External Affairs for the Commerce Department`s CHIPS program.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you on the program, Adrienne. So how is this new requirement going to work and how will it help address what everybody knows -- we`ve talked about this a lot on this program -- is a serious childcare problem in this country?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, COMMERCE DEPARTMENT CHIPS PROGRAM DIRECTOR OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS: Yes, Chris, thank you so much for having me on today. It`s great to be back with you. As you noted, first of all, the CHIPS Act we just released the notice of funding opportunity today which means that leading edge and (INAUDIBLE) companies were open for business, you can apply for the funds, but as part of that, Chris, we wanted to make sure that childcare components was part of this, and that means that if you are receiving over $150 million whether you are a large-scale fab or a smaller supplier from this funding you have to provide some sort of childcare component.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean? We know that this is not a one-size-fits-all. There are some communities that have robust childcare facilities so maybe it`s something as small as reimbursing for mileage or providing a stipend to your employees. In some communities there are not enough childcare facilities. In fact, as you noted, that is the case in many places across the United States.\par \par \uc2 We feel that we are not being good stewards of taxpayer dollars here at the Commerce Department if we do not make sure that we can fill these jobs, fill these fabs with workers which means we need to knock down one of the most important barriers to getting people back in the workforce, and that is providing childcare access.\par \par \uc2 So, again, we realize this is not a one-size-fits-all policy. That some companies may decide to provide childcare on side access. Some companies may decide to do this in a different way. But we want to work with these companies to figure this out and we also want these companies to work with their local communities to also determine the best strategy going forward for their employees.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me give one example since you brought it up. It isn`t one-size-fits-all. But your colleagues at Commerce gave us this. In the Syracuse area where Micron announced $100 billion chipmaking investment last year, the need for slots in childcare facilities is nearly three times the size of the actual childcare capacity in that region.\par \par \uc2 So how far could this go to alleviating the problem? How fast might these remedies take effect? Could it, for example, be used to actually build a facility?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes, absolutely. Again, Chris, we are working with companies and we will be working with companies and applicants to help them get to this place. We`re also going to be releasing a workforce development guide probably within the next week to 10 days that will lay out some ideas and sort of some best practices that current companies are doing right now in the United States.\par \par \uc2 I`ll also add, Chris, that \uc2 TSMC\uc2  , which is one of the largest fab producers in the world based in Taiwan, they`re also building a facility in Arizona, they have been providing on-site childcare access in to their employees in Taiwan for quite some time and they`re doing this because they have to get workers in these jobs. A lot of these jobs require odd hours, a lot of these jobs, you know, are very technical and they simply cannot get the employees in those jobs to do that unless they provide childcare access.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there`s a lot of net benefits to come out of this. Getting more women in the workforce I think is always a good thing but one of the large reasons of why we`re doing this is because we are not being good stewards of taxpayer dollars if we can`t fill these jobs in the fabs that we`re putting all this money toward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Adrienne Elrod, I`m sure there are other folks who work for other companies who want me to ask questions about when it`s going to come to the business near them but we`ll save that for another day. Thanks for being on the program. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: DeSantis versus \uc2 Disney\uc2  . Can taking on big corporations and culture wars separate the Florida governor from other Republican candidates? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  isn`t shy about putting the culture war center stage in Florida, doing it again on Monday by signing a bill to revoke \uc2 Disney\uc2  `s special self-governing status.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:00]\par \par \uc2 And while he insists the decision was all about fairness, he has also made it clear that the company`s so-called woke agenda and their opposition to his "Don`t Say Gay" bill did play a part.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: You clearly had a movement within the corporation itself, of course, Burbank, California, based elements of it that said it`s their job or it`s their goal to inject a lot of this sexuality into the programming for young kids.\par \par \uc2 Those are not the values that we want to promote in the state of Florida. We want to promote the safety of our students and the rights of our parents. We believe being joined at the hip with this one California-based company was not something that was justifiable or sustainable, and so we said we`re going to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Phil Rucker, deputy national editor for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s talk about the risk-reward factor. What`s the calculation that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is making here?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, Governor DeSantis over the last couple of years and especially in the last six months or so has tried to pick some of these cultural battles in Florida as a way to communicate his own values to a national Republican audience as he thinks about running for president, so this fight with \uc2 Disney\uc2  is exhibit A but you can also look at his agenda on schools, on education, on the AP African- American studies course.\par \par \uc2 It`s been one issue after another where he`s tried to stake out sort of ground, a position on the conservative right as a way to appeal to a national audience of sort of hard-right Republican voters who he knows are going to be so influential in a presidential primary next year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Without a doubt, but clearly as he touts this record in Florida, he`s done it in his book, he`s been traveling, he`s going to be traveling some more. I mean, if he got the nomination, then what? I mean, how does that set of facts help him win a presidency?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Well, look, I mean, it would be very difficult, I think, for him to win over more mainstream independent voters purely on this cultural agenda but I assume that he`s going to be looking to play a more palatable aspects of his governing record in Florida that point to the economy in the state of Florida, that point to the way he`s handled other aspects, you know, on his record as governor, and really try to draw a contrast with President Biden assuming Biden seeks re-election.\par \par \uc2 That`s a contrast that I know the DeSantis people and the Republican Party generally are eager to try to frame for voters, but the challenge for DeSantis is going to try to make himself appeal to the general election audience as more than just a cultural warrior.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and challenge for many Republicans.\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Phil Rucker, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This is just in from Capitol Hill. Some lawmakers bragging about carrying guns in the Capitol complex. The new letter that asks them to stop. Ryan Nobles has that exclusive report for us, next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the U.S. Marshal Service suffering a major security breach compromising sensitive information. We`ve got the details on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 And could a ban on TikTok, one of the world`s most popular pastimes, soon be on the way? That`s coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got some exclusive new NBC reporting for you. As Republicans defend their right to carry guns in the Capitol, in a new letter obtained by NBC, Democrats are calling on the president to help them stop that.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ryan Nobles joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So many questions. What do we know about Republicans carrying guns at the Capitol? What do Democrats want to happen here?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so, Chris, let`s first talk about the policy here on Capitol Hill. Basically all 535 members of both the House and Senate, whether they`re Republican or Democrat, are not required to go through any kind of security screening in order to get into the Capitol complex. They can just walk by there, wave by \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 In fact, even the people that they are traveling with in their party are often not required to go through these security screenings. Now, it is the rule on Capitol Hill that you cannot have a gun anywhere other than inside the personal office of a member, but because there is a lack of security screening there`s no way to enforce that rule.\par \par \uc2 And we talked to several Republicans that have said that they believe they have their armed members of Congress right now that are carrying guns on Capitol Hill, Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  in particular told me that he actually feels safer knowing that his fellow Republican members or other members in general are carrying guns.\par \par \uc2 Well, not everyone feels that way including some Democrats like California Congressman Jared Hufman who has sent a letter to President Biden asking that the next architect of the Capitol vote on the Capitol Police Board to essentially close this loophole, to require every member of Congress to walk through a security screening to make sure that they`re not carrying a firearm and complying with this rule that strictly states that they cannot carry guns.\par \par \uc2 Now this is a back and forth debate between Republicans and Democrats. There was a pretty animated conversation that took place in a congressional hearing not too long ago, where Democrats attempted to keep in an amendment that said that firearms were not allowed inside these committee hearings and Republicans pushed back on that.\par \par \uc2 Now even though this debate goes on, it really is a moot point, Chris, unless this loophole is closed and we talked to former \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officers who say this just makes their job more difficult. Not knowing specifically whether or not anyone who passes by these security screenings without having to go through the metal detectors is carrying a weapon and it just makes their job more difficult.\par \par \uc2 So this is a debate that will continue on now as we wait to see who President Biden appoints as the next architect of the Capitol, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. For folks who don`t work on Capitol Hill and who don`t understand architects like -- it sounds like the person who built the Capitol, right? Has long since gone to that great architecture place in the sky.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02\par \par \uc2 But what is the architect and what does he do? Does he have that kind of authority?\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Right. So the architect to the Capitol basically serves as the administrative head of everything that happens here on Capitol Hill. The way the buildings are laid out. The way that people are able to go from one part of the complex to another. And the architect to the Capitol serves on a body called the Capitol Police Board along with the two sergeant-of-arms who serve -- from both the Senate and the House.\par \par \uc2 Now that Capitol Police Board is the body that essentially votes for and puts into place all the security protocols on Capitol Hill. And right now, the sergeant-at-arms on the Senate is appointed by a Democrat. The sergeant-of-arms at the House, appointed by a Republican. This architect to the Capitol, the new one, will be appointed by President Biden and could be the deciding vote if this policy were to come up again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan Nobles, as always, thank you so much for that breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there`s been a pretty stunning revelation from a newly unsealed federal complaint. A convicted felon tried to board a flight from Newark to Ft. Lauderdale but not with a gun. Instead, with an AR-15 rifle, a taser, and a fake \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  badge. TSA agents were able to stop him at the departure gate. The man who is a convicted felon, again, has been charged.\par \par \uc2 It happened back in December but it`s just being made public and it comes as the TSA has been intercepting a record number of firearms at airport checkpoints all across the country.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning about a major security breach at the U.S. Marshal Service. Multiple senior law enforcement officials tell NBC News that sensitive data was taken from one of their systems just a little more than a week ago. An investigation is now underway.\par \par \uc2 Now notably, the incident did not involve the database that has the Witness Protection Program in it, but here with more, NBC News investigative correspondent, Tom Winter.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, what sensitive data was there?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT: Right. So the information according to the U.S. Marshal Service and law enforcement that we`ve spoken to, Chris, is focused on the investigative files of the Marshal Service. So the types of information that might be out there are if you`re looking for somebody, you want to know their family members. You want to know places where they`ve hung out in the past. Prior addresses.\par \par \uc2 All the type of information that can help the Marshals Service say, you know what, we`re looking for Tom Winter. These are the best places to look for him. So that information is in there as our understanding. On top of that, information about \uc2 U.S. Marshals Service\uc2  employees and staff and the people that work for the Marshals Service may also be in those files.\par \par \uc2 Now it can be helpful to know what they know about you if you`re a criminal. It can be helpful to know what they don`t know about you. So perhaps that could further elude detection. So that`s something that is going to -- we haven`t seen the data yet. It hasn`t been published anywhere online. It may not be published online but that`s something that could potentially be advantageous to whoever may have taken this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, yes. Do we know who`s responsible? Or is there any kind of like buzz about who it might be? Who are the likely suspects?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Right now there are no indications. I mean, this could be, and we`ve seen instances in the past where we`ve had teenagers get access to law enforcement database to try and figure out if they`re under investigation for some small drug offense. I`ve seen that example.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. Seriously this could be like a 16-year-old kid?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: It could be. It could also be a nation state trying to determine what the Marshals Service knows about people that are wanted in this country. I`m talking about the people that have been involved with ransomware attacks in the country. Some were involved with ransomware attacks in the past. China, Iran, North Korea. Russia, obviously. So these are all places that could have a hand in it.\par \par \uc2 There are complex criminal gangs, organized criminal groups that might have a hand in it. It`s just too soon to say and dependent on how sophisticated this ransomware breach is. It could take some time to eventually determine who`s responsible for it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom Winter, always great to have you here on set. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 It took all winter, but today, the New York area woke up to a slushy morning after its first real snowfall of the season began overnight. Snow is still expected to continue throughout the day in the northeast. Experts predicting that parts of New Jersey and Connecticut could get up to eight inches of snow. It all comes from that vast system that`s wreaked havoc on at least 35 states from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 Pandemic-era SNAP benefits that have kept food on the table for millions of Americans set to expire tomorrow. The dire consequences this could have on families that are still struggling. We have those details, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:55]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tremendous amount of worry, even panic right now because essential benefits that have helped put food on the table for millions of Americans since the pandemic began are set to expire tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here`s NBC`s Dasha Burns with our report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA RAMOS, SINGLE MOTHER OF SIX, RECEIVING SNAP BENEFITS: Are we ready?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Jessica Ramos is a single mom. Feeding her six kids has always been a struggle.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What do you think about when you go to bed at night?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: If we`re going to be OK for the next week.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Her family is among more than 42 million Americans who receive SNAP benefits, what used to be known as food stamps. During the pandemic, the government boosted those benefits. Jessica received an extra $300 a month.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: They came in handy. They really did. But prices are going so high that we need them.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: These benefits kept 4.2 million people out of poverty and reduced child poverty by 14 percent according to the Urban Institute. But this month marks the end for those expanded benefits because of a provision in last year`s Omnibus Spending Bill.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: The scary part for the next month, how are we going to live? How -- what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 BURNS (on-camera): What are the sort of decisions you`re going to have to make to keep feeding your kids?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: It was so hard. Either pay a bill or go buy some groceries. That`s - -\par \par \uc2 BURNS: That`s where you`re at.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Jessica is not alone. Food banks across the country are preparing for a rise in food insecurity.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MATYSIK, SHARE FOOD PROGRAM EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: We have people calling our organization every day looking for more food resources.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: George Matysik is the executive director of Share Food Program, one of the largest food banks in Philadelphia. He says they`ve already seen a 70 percent increase in demand over the last year as food prices have skyrocketed.\par \par \uc2 MATYSIK: For organizations like ours, that means having to work round the clock to make sure we`re sourcing more food to serve more people.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The typical SNAP household will see monthly allotments drop by at least $95. For older Americans and big families like Jessica`s, the difference will be hundreds of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): The people in Washington who are making these decisions, what do you want them to understand about what life is like for you?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: For them to put themselves in our shoes. That we really are struggling out here.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230301ej2s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for February 3, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Courtney Kube, Jake Sherman, Bill Karins, George  Solis, Jonathan Allen, Robert Gibbs, Brian Cheung, Raf Sanchez \par \uc2 8,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Suddenly thrown off course by of all things, a surveillance balloon. We`ll get the latest on that and the briefing from the Pentagon that we just watched live in the last hour.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the bitter, brutal deep freeze that could shatter records all over New England in one spot. Windshields expected to drop so low that could cause frostbite in 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 And a potentially huge shift in the presidential primary calendar sparking an intraparty battle ahead of the DNC`s big meeting in Philadelphia. I`ll talk to the head of the DNC, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  , about how he thinks things will shake out. That`s a little later in this hour. But we start with a dramatic move that could have huge implications for the already fraught relationship between the U.S. and China.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration pulling the plug on Secretary of State Blinken`s planned trip to China, just hours after the Pentagon revealed it had detected a Chinese surveillance balloon over Montana. So right now, the video -- this video was taken in Billings, Montana on Thursday, although it`s not clear if this is the balloon in question. Obviously, there`s the balloon. That was Secretary Blinken who was supposed to leave tomorrow, that`s now on hold.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has decided to monitor the balloon instead of shooting it down, even though it`s apparently still gathering intelligence as we speak, a decision not sitting well with some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): This is the first time in my lifetime I`ve ever seen anything like this. And it`s very disturbing to know that it`s still up there in the air near our middle of military installations. The time is now, let`s get this thing out of the air.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Courtney Kube. She broke this story. Jake Sherman, cofounder of Punchbowl news and an MSNBC political contributor. So, Courtney, I know this came up at the Pentagon briefing that wrapped up just a few minutes ago. Tell us what did we learn but what are also still the outstanding questions?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, it dominated the Pentagon briefing, that`s for sure. One of the things, one of the big questions that I`ve been asking is, where is this balloon? We know, as you mentioned, that it was spotted over Billings, Montana. The Pentagon has acknowledged that. But yesterday when I was asking about it, no one would talk about the exact location.\par \par \uc2 So, we learned a little bit more about that from Brigadier General Pat Ryder, the Pentagon spokesperson at the briefing. Here`s what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PAT RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Well, we won`t get into specifics in regard to the exact location, I can tell you that the balloon continues to move eastward, and is currently over the center of the continental United States. Again, we currently assess that the balloon does not present a military or physical threat to people on the ground at this time, and we`ll continue to review -- excuse me, continue to monitor and review options.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, why that`s important is we know that it is still over the continental U.S. And in fact, it seems as if it`s moved south and it`s moving east, so it may actually be more in the center of the U.S. than it was when it was up in Montana. So, one of the questions answered -- the big question, though, that that opens up is where is it headed? And that`s something that the Pentagon still does not want to talk to us about what it -- where it could be headed.\par \par \uc2 We do know that if it traverses across eastward across the United States, it has the potential to move across several very important locations. When it was in Montana, it was near \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  base that hosts the intercontinental ballistic missiles. A strategic base for the U.S. military. But if it traverses across the United States of moving east, it has the potential to move past many more bases like that.\par \par \uc2 Now, a senior defense official I spoke with yesterday, when I asked what can the U.S. do, as they monitor it minute to minute, what can they do if they see that it is potentially going to a location that the U.S. doesn`t want it to be surveilling? And obviously, they maintain the right to bring this balloon down. But in addition to that, they are taking some steps on the ground. Some things as simple as just moving, look, moving things that out of the sight of this surveillance drone.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Chris, China has satellites that are already seeing a lot of these things. There`s commercial satellites that can see some of these things anyway. But the defense officials we`ve been speaking to about it says that the collection capabilities of this balloon are not that much more mature or significant than what China already has. But again, as it moves, if the Pentagon, if the military sees that I think they will continue to assess any potential options and actions that they may take.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, how much more fraught, how much more of an escalation, Courtney, would it be to even really come close, let alone decide to bring it down?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: I think that one -- what people have to remember is not only is this flying at a very, very high altitude about 60,000 feet. So higher than commercial aviation, but it`s believed to have a helium component to it and it`s enormous. So, we can see it from the ground and it`s flying that high. Think about how big it actually is, in reality. That`s one of the concerns here is because it`s so large, it could create this big debris field if it`s blown up, if it comes down.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s one of the major reasons. According to the defense officials who we`re speaking to, that`s one of the major reasons that they`re concerned about bringing it down.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:03]\par \par \uc2 But as you also mentioned there`s the enormous geopolitical implications of the U.S. shooting down or bringing down a Chinese asset no matter where it is.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you, Jake, about a couple of aspects to this. One, obviously, she said the geopolitical implications. Look, Blinken would have been the highest-ranking member of this administration to go to China. I think, first Secretary of State in what, five years to postpone it. What are you hearing and just how big a deal is it?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Huge deal here on Capitol Hill for Secretary of State to go into six years, Chris, which is -- would have been a big statement. A few things worth noting. Number one, members of Congress were caught off guard by this. Meaning there were briefings this week in front of Senate Intelligence and in front of the Armed Services Committee where this was not mentioned and members of Congress like nothing less than finding things out from the news media and from reports which they did from Courtney`s great reporting. That`s number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, China is the only thing perhaps that people on that Republicans and Democrats on Capitol Hill agree on. They -- Kevin McCarthy, the Speaker of the House just formed a China Select Committee. Got 146 Democratic votes in the House of Representatives. So, we already know that China is a punching bag for members of Congress on both sides of the aisle. Number three, McCarthy has now asked for a Gang of Eight briefing on this balloon and everything about it.\par \par \uc2 The Gang of Eight are the top lawmakers in the majority and minority the leaders 