y it`s about two to four minutes before you get frostbite on your skin. So, you know, notice we`re at negative 41 in Maine already. Burlington, Vermont, we mentioned you a negative 35. Saranac Lake negative 35.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s many areas now that we`re getting into that dangerous category. That`s why they canceled school for a lot of people, Chris. They didn`t want people walking home as this arctic blast is, you know, has moved in. It`s here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. This is just not right. This is not -- weather is just not right. George, we know cities are typically stocked up for snowstorms, they`re used to snow storms. But what do you do to prepare for cold like this? The first maybe foremost concern is what if there are power outages? I mean, people in houses were outside, the wind is blowing and it`s 10, 20, 30 below?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chris. Right now, one of the key things is possibly think warm thoughts. Just hearing some of those wind chills, especially as they`re supposed to drop into tonight, into tomorrow morning. It is brutal. Right now, it actually feels pretty balmy compared to other parts of the country where the temperature continues to drop. We`re somewhere in the 20-degree range right now.\par \par \uc2 By tomorrow morning. It`ll feel what minus seven, six degrees in New York City. That is absolutely brutal. But of course, the key thing from officials here is to -- if you are going to step foot outside to make sure you bundle up where layers of course limit that time outside and in major cities. Yes, they are keeping an eye on things like the power grid to make sure it can withstand this bitter glass.\par \par \uc2 And cities like Boston, they`ve actually taken preventative measures today and canceled classes because of the cold snap. And of course, other cities, including here in New York, we`re looking at shelters and warming centers to make sure people can withstand this bitter 