kin wasn`t or was exposed, rather. And so, really, it`s critical that we`re keeping an eye on each other up here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, help us who are non-scientists and non-meteorologists to understand why it`s important you stay up there the kind of scientific work you do while you`re there up on Mount Washington.\par \par \uc2 TARASIEWICZ: Absolutely. So, there`s many answers to that question. But really, an event like this shows the importance of our observations. We`re getting a good sample of the mid-tropospheric temperatures. And so, whether that`s occurring above most of our heads, but that`s where a lot of our air masses originate from, as they travel around the country. And so, now Washington gives us a perspective into the middle atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 Unlike, you know, weather balloons are only able to watch those twice a day. Whereas up here on the summit, we`re 24/7/365 operation. And so, we`re really able to get a high-resolution perspective of mid tropospheric temperatures.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know for a lot of people, they`re listening to you. And they`re thinking this is insane. What is the attraction? I mean, scientifically, personally to decide, yes, I`m going to be the guy who`s up on top of Mount Washington knowing that these records get set.\par \par \uc2 TARASIEWICZ: I think insanity is a little bit of a part of it. But a lot of it is an interest that goes back to when I was quite young. I used to always sort of enjoy the arctic blast we would get. In fact, so my first weather memories are the nor`easters in the early 2000s that we had across New England. Those really captured my attention. And so yes and you just really have to appreciate Mother Nature and you have to be ready to get humbled every second of the day.\par \par \uc2 Even right now, one of the doors to the outside is flexing, like I`ve never seen it flex before. And so --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Really?\par \par \uc2 TARASIEWICZ: -- really just -- yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s just the winds are that high.\par \par \uc2 TARASIEWICZ: They are. They`re coming right from the west. And so, they`re blowing right on one of our thicker doors. We`ve got about a two-inch thick steel door strapped down with several bungee cords. And so, snow and wind is actually starting to blow through that and make quite a bit of noise.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m glad you`re laughing and you have a sense of perspective about that. We`re looking from the outside at the building that you`re in. Thank you so much for taking the time. Thank you for the scientific work you do. I also want to say I have been there, Francis, in much nicer weather. There`s a beautiful Cog Railway, but I don`t recommend it in the winter. Thank you so much for taking the time to talk to us. We appreciate it. Stay safe there.\par \par \uc2 TARASIEWICZ: Thank you. Thanks for having us as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well contrast what`s happening in the Northeast with temperatures finally getting above freezing down in Texas. That`s critical because nearly a quarter million people there are still without electricity after ice storms brought down power lines all over the state and it has been a nightmare on the roads. According to one official in the Dallas, Fort Worth area, an overnight freeze there contributed to 50 crashes. Five-O, with injuries between six last night and five this morning.\par \par \uc2 OK. Ready for competition? How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is using familiar playbooks to try to knock his 2024 Republican challengers out of the race before they jump in.\par \par \uc2 Plus, moving on. I`ll talk to DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  about a major decision in the Democratic Party. They`re about to make it. Will they skip Iowa to kick off 2024?\par \par \uc2 And bulletproof vests for six-year-olds. The desperate race to make them available to children who need to get out of areas in Ukraine targeted by Russia. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:27:59]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, as his first official challenger is about to announce \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going knives out on his potential rivals, calling one former member of his administration overly ambitious and accusing another of taking more credit than he should have. And those are downright nice compared to the things he said about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . Also, potentially really troubling for the party.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s refusal to say he endorsed anyone who beat him if someone else got the nomination. Let`s talk about it with NBC Senior National Politics Reporter Jonathan Allen. And Robert Gibbs, former White House press secretary and MSNBC political analyst. So, Jonathan, I want to play for you what \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SALEM RADIO NETWORK HOST: If you`re not the nominee, will you support whoever the GOP nominee is?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It would depend. I would -- I would give you the same answer I gave in 2016 during the debate. The first question I was asked by Bret Baier as to rather -- I was asked to rather interesting questions, it would have to depend on who the nominee was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, we can`t get ahead of how the primaries may play out. They`re a long way away. But why does that kind of statement make Republicans nervous?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: Well, it`s a pretty open threat that if somebody else were to win the nomination, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would take his ball and his base and go home. And that could deprive Republicans of the opportunity to win the next election. So, he`s basically saying either nominate me, where there`s a potential that I`m going to sink whoever the nominee is.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The way they put it, Robert, in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  , is that Trump is exposing a potential quagmire for Republicans. Here`s exactly what they said. In 2020 for example, about nine percent of Republicans voted for someone other than Mr. Trump compared with just three percent of Democrats who voted for someone other than their nominee, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . Look, nobody`s particularly surprised but fair to say maybe best-case scenario for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , assuming he is the nominee is a divided GOP.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Oh, absolutely. I mean, I think, you know, to collide a couple of things together. First of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is helped immeasurably by a large Republican field. It`s interesting that he wants to poke them and essentially go negative on him as they get in. But in reality, a big field helps him because it divides the not-Trump vote. And eventually you get into primaries where it`s winner take all.\par \par \uc2 And you can see Trump do what he did in 2016 which is get 35, 40 or 45 percent of the vote and walk away with all the delegates. Secondly, if he were to mount some sort of campaign and get his name on the ballot or to keep say, 10 percent of the -- of his voter`s home, you would be talking about a democratic landslide because all of those swing states would no longer be swing states. So, I think this is a lot more bark than bite from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . But if you`re the Republican Party, it certainly works.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, look at this list, I guess, potential Trump rivals right now. There`s a whole bunch of them. But Trump is claiming Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  cried, begging him for his endorsement when he ran for governor. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : He had nothing. He was dead. He was leaving the race. He came over and he begged me, begged me for an endorsement, he was getting ready to drop out. He said, if you endorsed me, I`ll win. And there were tears coming down from his eyes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tears coming down from his eyes, Robert. Just -- and it so far hasn`t taken the bait this time or anytime, really. But how long can that last? And what`s he waiting for? I mean, at some point he has to engage, right?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Probably. But look, I would say this, I think, you know, I read this somewhere. So, it`s not an original thought. But it`s one worth repeating that in 2015 and 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was punching up. He started at very little and he was punching up in the Republican primaries. It`s really quick in this race and he seems to be punching down. And I think he`s really letting people know as the polls have that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a serious competitor and somebody who is either equal to or just behind him, even in some cases ahead of him in polling.\par \par \uc2 I think this is a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that we`re not used to seeing, somebody who realizes that they used to be president were king of the party for a long time. They may not be the lead dog anymore.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Maybe not. But Jonathan, how quickly do you think once Nikki Haley gets in, we might start to see some other people following suit, or might they just wait it out through the summer?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I think we`re going to see a lot of people wading through the summer, particularly, you know, in the case of DeSantis, the people around him say that he`s going to wait for the legislative session in Florida to end. So, you`re probably be looking at, you know, sometime next summer for him to make an announcement. He may get engaged, otherwise, he may decide that he has to start punching back.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, some of the governors, Youngkin might also wait for the Virginia legislative session. I think we`re likely to see Pence, Pompeo make decisions a little sooner. And I think we`re likely to see Trump ramp 