ocess had already taken place.\par \par \uc2 So what we see now is that the Democratic Party has evolved a lot since the 1972 race when saw Iowa and New Hampshire. And so now we are making sure that the diversity in our party is reflected in terms of the diversity of the selection of the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about another argument I`ve heard, Jaime, which is that South Carolina already has a lot of power, that you have Iowa and New Hampshire winnowing the pool, and then South Carolina becomes the kingmaker or maybe someday the queenmaker because, as you say, every nominee since `92 except \uc2 John Edwards\uc2  has won the state. They say the biggest change is that being first in the nation brings travel and advertising dollars more than influence. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, the thing that I say, if being first doesn`t mean as much, why are people fighting so hard to have it, right? Listen, it`s important. It is really important to make sure that the most loyal constituencies of the Democratic Party, African-Americans we see, Latino Americans, that they get an opportunity, just like everybody else, to have a say in terms -- and an early say in terms of determining who`s going to be the most powerful person on the face of this planet.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:09]\par \par \uc2 And that`s what this does. It resets the table to make sure, and at the same time, we still see New Hampshire retaining its same place that it`s always had. You know, it`s always been the second contest. There`s always Iowa, and then you go to New Hampshire, and now it will be South Carolina and you go to New Hampshire, so that does not change. So -- but we`re proud of this calendar because it really reflects not only the diversity of the Democratic Party, but the diversity of this great nation of ours.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As you well know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has entered the 2024 race. Other Republicans are poised to jump in. But President Biden has not officially announced, and I wonder if you can give us a little indication of what the discussions are within the party about when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should announce. Are there arguments for waiting as opposed to doing it sooner?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, listen, I mean, the president will make a determination when he wants to officially announce, but he has given all of us his intentions, that he is going to run again, and so we at the DNC are doing all that we can to build up for that moment, to make sure that not only when he makes that announcement that we can run, we run hard and that we win.\par \par \uc2 There`s a clown show going on over on the other side. They are trying to out extreme each other. We see it with Nikki Haley, we see it with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . We see it with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And so I will have my popcorn ready, willing and watching them while we make sure that we are building the organization on the ground in all 50 states to win this election once again and to continue to deliver for the American people.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  , thank you for taking the time. Have a good meeting. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Well, you may have seen the headline after headline, frankly, after headline about those massive layoffs within the tech industry, which is part of the reason that the new job numbers out today were so stunning. They defied all predictions.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC business and data reporter Brian Cheung.\par \par \uc2 What do these numbers, which were so different than what we thought they were going to be, tell us about the economy overall?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA CORRESPONDENT: Yes, absolute gang busters, right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: When we take a look at the numbers over 500,000 jobs added just in the month of January. For reference, by the way, analysts were only expecting about 187,000 jobs added in the month, and the unemployment rate dipped down to 3.4 percent. That matches levels that we saw in 1969. You would have to rewind to 1953 to see an unemployment rate that was lower than that, 3.1 percent at the time.\par \par \uc2 So where do we see the job gains then? It`s not in the tech sector where we`re hearing about layoffs. It`s happening in leisure and hospitality. It`s happening in professional business services, in the government and also in health care, but it`s important to note that this is not surprising. If you`ve been listening to businesses in those industries, they`ve been saying we`re aggressively hiring.\par \par \uc2 You look at the airline industry and you even look at Chipotle, for example. They`re adding 15,000 jobs ahead of -- I kid you not -- it`s called burrito season. More people go out and buy burritos apparently between March and May. I had a chance --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes. I had a chance to speak with the chief restaurant officer about exactly that. Like why is that? Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BOATWRIGHT\uc2  , CHIPOTLE CHIEF RESTAURANT OFFICER: We`ll go through a heavy campaign here leading into burrito season to bring in as many high caliber, high quality people as we possibly can. But look, there`s an evergreen approach to our hiring strategy. And we`ll continue to hire throughout the summer months as the volumes continue to grow year-over- year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: So not at all recessionary types of, you know, dynamics that you hear there, and again, \uc2 Scott Boatwright\uc2  , the chief restaurant officer there, also saying that they`re trying to expand the amount of restaurants that Chipotle has from 3,000 to 7,000 because that many people want Chipotle burritos.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: They have to staff up for all of that. So it shows the demand for labor in this very weird economy, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I always learn something from you, Brian. Now it`s burrito season. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Stay warm. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The path to police reform. I`ll talk to two city leaders who ran for office to change the criminal justice system. Will the death of Tyre Nichols help them bring about meaningful change at the local level now. And fitting children with bulletproof vests. The race to safely get kids out of a war zone in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For a devastating example of just how bad the situation is in parts of Ukraine, there`s this. A rush is on to get children still living in hard-hit cities kid-sized bulletproof vests just as Russia is expected to launch a new offensive in the south and east.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Raf Sanchez has more from Kyiv. This is like a gut punch thinking of little kids getting fitted for bulletproof vests. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, bulletproof vests for kids. It`s almost a phrase you can`t believe you have to say, but it has become a wartime necessity here in Ukraine. They are made in the west of this country by a group called the Lviv Defense Cluster, and they`re making them for kids as young as 4 years old.\par \par \uc2 The idea is that these vests are for those crucial seconds when you are evacuating a small child from a house or an apartment building and getting them into a car, but when they`re in the open, when they`re exposed and vulnerable potentially to Russian fire. One of the places that they are being used right now is the besieged city of Bakhmut. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ (voice-over): In Bakhmut, police say fewer than 200 children are left in what was once a city of 70,000 people. To get them out, they`re using these kid-sized bulletproof vests. They`re made by the Lviv Defense Cluster, a team that usually produces armor for \uc2 NATO\uc2  special forces now designing it for children like 9-year-old Angelica Csehunov, whose family was forced to flee their home in Kharkiv at the start of the war.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This vest is for children from 4 until 6 years old.\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ (on-camera): Four to 6 years old.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): A pocket for parents` phone numbers and a strap to carry kids to safety. My bulletproof vest a little less colorful than Angelica`s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Feels good.\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ (on-camera): It feels good? Good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: And Chris, the Lviv Defense Cluster has made about 550 of those vests so far. They`re raising funds so that they can make more. They cost about $700 each, and the fear here in Ukraine is that they are going to be ever more in demand as Russia prepares for what the Ukrainian government fears is going to be this long-awaited new offensive. We don`t know yet where. We don`t know when, but President Zelenskyy is saying it could happen any day now.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian government says Russia has mobilized half a million new troops and that they are preparing to strike either in the east around the fighting in Bakhmut or down in the south near Kherson. Now, in just the last hour or so, the U.S. has announced yet another military aid package for Ukraine. This one is about $2 billion. Crucially it includes more of those HIMARS, those long-range missiles that the Ukrainians have been using so effectively.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, as you said, a real gut punch that children in this country being fitted for bulletproof vests.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Raf Sanchez, thank you as always for your reporting and please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Activists turned politicians. How our next two guests are mobilizing for change from inside city hall. And Tyre Nichols loved capturing beautiful moments. We`re going to take an intimate look at the world through his unique lens.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:39]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: President Biden and members of the Congressional Black Caucus now say they have a plan to move forward on police reform after the death of Tyre Nichols. It does include a legislative strategy, but with Congress divided, officials acknowledge the plan can`t just address change at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS, OFFICE OF PUBLIC ENGAGEMENT SENIOR ADVISER AND DIRECTOR: We know that there`s only so much that can be done from the White House and so much of this has to do with what happens at the state and local level.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in two activists turned city council members, now working to make change in their communities. New York City Councilman Chi Ossie who was inspired to run after the murder of George Floyd and Birmingham Councilwoman LaTonya Tate who started a career in criminal justice after her own son was incarcerated.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Councilman, you`ve led marches after the murder of George Floyd, after Breonna Taylor was shot and killed. Now we have a photo of you, I think it was just last weekend, leading a protest following the death of Tyre Nichols. Is this time going to be any different?\par \par \uc2 CHI OSSE, NEW \uc2 YORK CITY COUNCIL\uc2  MEMBER: It depends on our elected officials. You know, something that we were advocating for since 2020 as a protester and now as an elected official is police accountability. We`ve seen a backlash on that conversation, not only of course from the Republican Party but most especially within the Democratic Party when it came to the conversation of police accountability.\par \par \uc2 I think many Democrats were scared of talking about that subject because during the midterms and general election they would be labeled as socialists or radicals but it`s still --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Or soft on crime because a lot of people were concerned about that issue, the polls showed that, right?\par \par \uc2 OSSE: Exactly. Exactly. But I think it distracted us from doing what`s right or at least distracted some of our most powerful Democrats from doing what`s right, and that`s continuing to advocate and legislate for police accountability. It`s something that the black community has been wanting to see since police and violence has impacted our communities. It`s something I continue to push and advocate for as a local city council member here in New York. Now we just need the courage from our mayor and other top Democratic officials to really make the changes that organizers and elected officials like myself have been pushing for.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Councilwoman, you`re a former parole officer. We`ve heard police chiefs around the country calling Tyre Nichols` death outrageous, inhumane, sickening. It doesn`t help their efforts, right, to be respected in the community. What are conversations you`re having like with the people you know within the system, including police?\par \par \uc2 LATONYA TATE, BIRMINGHAM, ALABAMA COUNCIL MEMBER: Well, thank you for having me, and let me just extend my condolences to the Nichols` family and the political leaders and the leaders and the activists and the people on the ground in Memphis, so my heart goes out to them. But the conversations that I have been striking up and -- with our mayor is we have to look at police different, and so I echo what my councilman in New York is saying that this is what the black community and the community has been wanting.\par \par \uc2 And we have to reimagine what policing looks like in this time. You know, the Tyre Nichols murder was not a pursuit of public safety. So you`re hearing that word, public safety, being tossed around. But are we really, you know, doing what public safety looks like. And so being a former PO, I know what I witnessed and what I saw watching that video. Those are my training efforts that we were trained when I went through the academy to, you know, pursue a person and hunt them down, and, you know, to their death.\par \par \uc2 So I have not had an opportunity to really sit down with our chief. I do plan to do that in the coming days. But I have been listening to community`s idea. I did meet last week with about 20 organizations that are doing the work on the ground, and so I can`t, you know, highlight our mayor, Mayor Randall Wolf, and he`s making those investments. So right before I got elected, my colleagues had been in talks.\par \par \uc2 And we do have the Hospital Balance Innovation Program in place with the health department. The mayor made a significant investment in mental health in the schools and the heat program, which is the rehabilitation, empowerment therapy in our elementary and high schools. So we are making those investments, just trying to build that ecosystem out, but I`m looking at policing in a different lens and hoping that we can push, as the councilman said, our state legislatures along with my colleagues, on reimagining what policing looks like in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:12]\par \par \uc2 Now being the first black female to chair public safety in the city of Birmingham is just history all by itself.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I wish we had more time to talk, but I hope you both will come back. I would love to know in weeks or a month or so if you`re starting to see movement, if you`re feeling that this activism that a lot of people are feeling after this latest death is fading away. Will you both come back? I`d love to have you both here. LaTonya Tate, Chi Osse, thank you for being with me. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, we very much want to share with you the world through Tyre Nichols` lens. Our studio is covered with his colorful light-filled images of sunsets, people and nature. The beautiful moments that he captured because they stuck out to him. He said photography helped him express himself in, quote, "ways I cannot write down for people."\par \par \uc2 So as Tyre Nichols put it in his personal photography Web site, quote, "Welcome to the world through my eyes." So we now show it to you, accompanied by some words from the people who loved him best.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He set his own path, you know, he made his own light. He seen the world way different than I`ve ever seen it before.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He was 8 and already knew who he was. Like, I`m Tyre, this is me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ll never forget my brother. I`ll never forget my Gemini twin.\par \par \uc2 ROWVAUGHN WELLS, TYRE NICHOLS` MOTHER: I really truly believe my son was sent here on an assignment from God. And I guess now his assignment is done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230204ej2300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for February 1, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Antonia Hylton, Eddie Glaude, Charles Coleman, Guad  Venegas, Kristen Welker, Ashley Parker, Ali Vitali, Brian Cheung, Garrett  Haake, Mike Memoli \par \uc2 7,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We are watching along with you the grief and pain evident on the faces of mourners who are now arriving at the church in Memphis for Tyre Nichols` funeral. His mother speaks of hope. What can possibly make his sacrifice worth the agony?\par \par \uc2 Also, this hour, shocking numbers from our new NBC poll that could throw the presidential race into chaos before it even gets going. Two-thirds of the country don`t want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run. Plus, more than 12 million people are facing ice storm warnings as the deep south gets hit with a deep freeze. Hundreds of flights canceled, roads covered with ice, it`s made traveling dangerous and it`s contributed to at least six deaths.\par \par \uc2 But we begin in Memphis, where starting an hour from now the funeral for Tyre Nichols will attempt to take the focus away from the horrific images of his brutal death and instead honor his life, and the man his mother called a "beautiful soul." Mourners have begun to arrive right now at Mississippi Boulevard Christian Church less than 20 miles from where the 29-year-old lived and died.\par \par \uc2 His death, the latest rallying cry for our nation sickened by watching the same images play out on police body cameras again and again. The fact that George Floyd`s brother and Breonna Taylor`s mother will attend today`s funeral underscoring the sad truth that past calls for change have gone unfulfilled. Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  who will deliver the eulogy for Nichols was asked earlier today what comfort he can give the family at a time like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, FOUNDER, NATIONAL ACTION NETWORK: The only comfort that I can offer them is that we will stand with them and fight in the name of Tyre and others to try and change the legal and legislative structure that deals with policing in this country. This is not about a sprint movement. It`s about a marathon. You run until you break the ribbon, and we`re going to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We saw just about 90 minutes ago the casket carrying Tyre Nichols` body being wheeled into the church. We are expecting roughly 2500 people to attend the funeral. And along with the chance for loved ones to grieve, cry, and as the pastor put it simply be together, today`s service will also put a bold exclamation point on the demands being shouted from streets all over the country to those in power. What are you doing to stop this from happening again?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Antonia Hylton in Memphis. Eddie Glaude is Princeton`s Chair of the Department of African American Studies and an MSNBC contributor. Charles Coleman is a former Brooklyn prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst. Thank you all for being here. Antonia, set the scene for us.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, it`s a heavy and somber morning. And where I am right now, people are starting to fill in. The parking lot is almost full. And they expect that there`s likely going to be overflow, some people who aren`t going to be able to physically attend the funeral that`s set to start in about an hour here.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned this a bit but for anyone who hasn`t been to a church like this one, a historic black church, you are going to hear a focus on Tyre Nichols` life, a celebration of who he was as a person, a son, a father, someone who loves to skateboarding and photography, had friends all over this country. But you`re also going to be given a message. A message that connects Tyre to the history, the gravity of the city of Memphis.\par \par \uc2 I`ve had the chance to talk to the senior pastor, Turner, here over the last several days. And I know that you`re going to hear several people connect Tyre`s story to the assassination of Dr. King to all the activism, the organizing that has happened in this city for generations now. And you`re going to hear very specific calls for action and calls for reform. And so, it`s a celebration of his life, but also a desire to make his death you know, spur action, take on meaning, really send a message to the rest of this country.\par \par \uc2 And it comes at a really important moment as this investigation continues to unfold as we hear from the DA that additional charges could be brought not just to the five officers at the center of all this, but to additional people, officers, EMTs, others who were responsible for the police reports that night.\par \par \uc2 And we got news from our affiliate reporting just last night, they got access to the personnel file, so the five officers involved here. And four of the five officers have a record that shows their own infractions. And you know, I have to mention one because it really struck me. One of the officers involved in the beating of Tyre Nichols that resulted in his death has himself a history of reckless driving, the accusation that began this traffic stop in the first place.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 He was put on paid -- unpaid leave for two days and sent to remedial driving school because while rushing to the scene of a call, he caused a three-car crash. And so, that -- and this is just one of the many examples. Others involve failure to fill out important paperwork or use of force issues. And so, it`s raising a lot of questions as I talk to residents here in this city about what people in this department may have known about the history of these five officers, what kind of culture has been allowed to fester there, and what kind of additional calls for accountability or reform might be necessary?\par \par \uc2 And everyone that I talked to here, thinks that this extends beyond just these five. And you`re going to hear some of that message today. You know, it`s a church and it`s focused on Tyre`s life, but they want Tyre`s life to take on a critical meaning and real action now, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There is, Eddie, for this funeral a lot, right? The pastor of the church, Dr. Lawrence Turner, said we get so zeroed in on how Tyre Nichols died, we don`t get a chance to recognize that he lived before that moment. I mean, it`s a sad fact. We`ve talked about these names before. \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , Eric Garner, Freddie Gray, Daunte Wright. We know them because of how they died, not how they lived. Talk to us about why it`s so important to remember the life of Tyre Nichols in this moment.\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Chris, it`s important because the way they died was a result of their dehumanization, the fact that they were treated as less than were examples of a population of folk who are devalued. So, it`s important in these moments with any life to tell the full story of the life lived. That the life is not just simply a kind of result of a tragic circumstance or cruel outcome of barbaric behavior but there was love, there were joys, there were triumphs, there were desires, there were brokenhearted moments, there were actual moments of elation, to tell the story of the full human being.\par \par \uc2 Because oftentimes, we don`t see the full human being, were either these kinds of objects of scorn, that is to say, people who are prone to crime, Chris, or were they objects of pity, people who need our -- you know, our concern, as opposed to being the full human beings that we are. So, eulogies are important in these moments to give us a sense of the full life lived because the life that was taken from us was dehumanized, was denied dignity and stand.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Charles, as Antonia was pointing out, we know that the police brutality that led to his death will be a part of today`s funeral. Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  is giving the eulogy. He predicted that Nichols will be one of those people that forces the country to deal with questions of police abuse. I want to play a little bit of what he said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are here to establish and proclaim that we are going to continue to fight this fight around police brutality and killing until we get federal laws changed. What happened to Tyre is a disgrace to this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve heard that before. And I -- and I know, Charles, that Rev would be the first person to acknowledge that. So, why should this time, how could this time be any different?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, one of the themes underlying today`s entire conversation as you look at the funeral of Tyre Nichols is the notion of legacy. Funerals are a celebration of one`s legacy and one`s life. And in part, when you`re thinking about legacy as it relates to Tyre Nichols. In the past, the question of whether the country had the moral fortitude necessary to enact the appropriate reform around law enforcement has been met with a resounding no.\par \par \uc2 But it`s important to remember that at one point, people sincerely doubted our country`s fortitude in terms of being able to abolish slavery. They doubted the country`s moral fortitude around being able to do away with segregation. And so, this is another example of how the legacy of so many is critical in moving the needle forward and helping the country establish the moral fortitude necessary to do what is right. And in this case, doing what is right means addressing at the policy level, at the federal level, the broken policing system in America.\par \par \uc2 We have to shift to a public safety model. We have to shift to a model that is rooted in service and protection rather than in violence and aggression. And we have to address the notion that black bodies are not dealt with the same way as people of other races, colors, and ethnicities. And that is something that I think the legacy of Tyre Nichols plays a part in moving the conversation forward.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We did not have a \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Police Act before \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . Even though it died on the Senate floor, there is now an opportunity for Senate Democrats to revive that measure and see if they are willing to fight. Do they have the moral fortitude to fight for what is right with respect to police reform? And so that is the question that Tyre Nichols` legacy helps to answer.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`ve been thinking a lot about that big-picture question, Eddie, of willingness to change. And so, last night, I went back again to James Baldwin`s deeply thought-provoking essay, Nothing Personal, on isolation in police brutality. And how you pointed out in that new publication, I guess a year or so ago, that he wrote it under the shadow of Medgar Evers` assassination in 1963. And you called that essay a eulogy of sorts. So, I wonder, would your eulogy today for Tyre Nichols come to a different conclusion than Baldwin did? Is the call to action the same?\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Well, would it be different? No. There at the root of it, Chris, is this refusal of the country to come to terms with who and what it is. Baldwin not only wrote about the police. Generally, he wrote about black police and how race shapes the way in which they inhabit that culture. They live that culture.\par \par \uc2 And I was thinking about this -- I mean, it`s interesting that you brought that up. I mean, we could talk about the framing of policing in this country from the 1968 streets act -- Safe Streets Act to the war on crime, and to how all of that has increased the kind of incarcerated -- mass incarceration led to the kind of militarization of the police, the way in which communities are over-policed and under -- and over surveilled and under protected. Baldwin was talking about a general framework of a community that had to be contained, a community that wasn`t thought to be a few -- a kind of critical feature of American life. So, the short answer to your question, Chris, is that the conclusion would be the same because the country still has some growing up. We still refuse to acknowledge who we are, that diversity is not some additive feature of American life is constitutive of who we are until we admit that, and then police accordingly. We will find ourselves here over and over again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I hope you`ll come back because I keep -- again, going back to a couple of words that he used and you used in your afterward, which were terror and love. I hope we have a chance to talk about that again. Eddie Glaude, always great to have you, and Charles Coleman as well. Antonia Hylton, thank you for your fantastic reporting. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Tyre Nichols` funeral is expected to start by the end of this hour after a serious delay earlier today due to weather and travel. NBC`s Guad Venegas is covering the storm there in Memphis for us. Guad, at this hour, 25 million people across the country are under winter weather advisories. What`s going on out there?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, that is correct. We know that the main cities where this alert is in place, you know, Dallas, Oklahoma City, Austin, and Little Rock, Arkansas, and here in Memphis, authorities are asking people to avoid travel if possible. I have some icicles from the buildings around us. This is what we have. And pretty much everything behind me is frozen. The streets are frozen here in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 Now, the temperature did rise a little bit. It`s about 30 degrees, so when I last checked a few minutes ago. So, it`s getting a little more wet but it`s even slippier -- slippery than it is -- than it was before. We`ve seen a lot of people fall crossing the street. You know, some vehicles have been making their way through Memphis, but it`s very dangerous to drive in these conditions. And that`s why authorities are asking people to avoid travel.\par \par \uc2 We also know that in Texas, state authorities are now reporting seven people dead as a result from accidents that are related to the weather. And as we`ve read the information that comes from Texas authorities, it`s once again all of these reports indicating individuals traveling in a vehicle hit a patch of an icy road and lost control. And that`s what happens. A lot of the vehicles are driving down the road. They feel like they have control and they hit one patch of ice and then lose control. So, seven people reported that as of now in the state of Texas. And the roads here, as I mentioned, are basically frozen.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been saying this all morning, we had ice skates. You could ice skate up and down the streets here in Memphis. This ice storm alert is in place today through the evening and into tomorrow. So, throughout the night, they`re asking people to stay home if they can.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Guad Venegas, you stay safe out there. We thank you for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news in the classified documents scandal. New details just out in the DOJ`s search of President Biden`s vacation home. And yet another Republican now expected to challenge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024. Are the floodgates opening? Plus, George Santos on the record in a TV interview, the big question, did he do himself any favors? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now, to the breaking news on that \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of President Biden`s beach house. His personal attorney says no documents with classified markings were found. Three sources familiar with the situation tell NBC News that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducted a search this morning of the president`s residence in Rehoboth Beach, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Kristen Welker at the White House for us. Kristen, when did \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents get there, how long were they there, and did they find anything at all?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Let me walk you, Chris, through the statement that we just got from Bob Bauer, who`s the president`s personal attorney, and that helps to answer some of your questions, but I`ll do some analysis into that on the other side. He says the DOJ`s planned search of the president`s Rehoboth residence is conducted in coordination and cooperation with the president`s attorneys, has concluded this search was conducted from 8:30 a.m. to noon, no documents with classified markings were found.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Consistent with the process in Wilmington, the DOJ took further review of some materials and handwritten notes that appear to relate to his time as vice president. That last line is significant, Chris, because they wouldn`t have taken these notebooks and other items if they weren`t of some interest. So, we`ll have to see what if anything comes of that.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, broadly speaking, this comes against the backdrop of the special counsel investigation into President Biden`s handling of classified documents after materials with classified markings were first found at his Washington, DC think tank in November before the midterm elections. We know that that office has now been searched. His Wilmington home has been searched where more classified documents were found.\par \par \uc2 The White House has been very careful to try and draw a sharp distinction between President Biden`s handling of classified documents and former President Trump`s handling of classified documents. Of course, his Mar-a- Lago estate was searched when he refused to turn them over. That was under a subpoena. In this instance, this search today of President Biden`s Rehoboth Beach property was not under subpoena. This was in cooperation with the president`s legal team.\par \par \uc2 Broadly speaking, though, Chris, this is yet another data point -- yet another development on what has become a real political headwind for this President. Of course, next week, he`s got a State of the Union address. Sometime thereafter, we expect he`ll likely announce his bid for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Polling Though, Chris, shows this is just kind of neutralized in the minds of Americans. A majority of them think both President Biden and former President Trump has mishandled classified documents, so it`s not clear what the political impact will be in the long term here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kristen Welker, thank you so much. But there are some other new poll numbers that show the 2024 front runners up against a daunting reality. Two-thirds of voters think both \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should not run for reelection. The NBC News poll reveals that more than 60 percent of voters either have reservations or are very uncomfortable with Biden and Trump running for the White House again. Can those numbers be overcome, or will they encourage challengers to enter the race?\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill. Ashley Parker is a national political correspondent for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC political analyst. Ashley, these are devastating numbers for both Biden and Trump. I mean, is there a strategy to overcome them? Are you hearing any sense of concern from either camp of supporters?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: The numbers are devastating, as you said. It`s a fascinating new poll. I will say they are in line somewhat with other polls we`ve seen and there`s -- and a lot of these other polls. There`s a second number that is similarly interesting. It`s that for as many Republicans who would prefer Trump not to be the nominee and for as many Democrats who would prefer Biden not to be the nominee, if they are asked, OK, well, let`s say this worst-case scenario happens. You`re a Democrat, would you vote for Biden? You`re a Republican, would you vote for Trump?\par \par \uc2 An overwhelming number -- not all, but an overwhelming number say that yes if they are the nominee, they would vote for them. So, in some ways, the best thing for both of these candidates to do to overcome these numbers is to win because there`s a lot of Democrats who might prefer someone other than Biden but will support him if he`s their party`s nominee. And that`s the exact same for a number of Republicans when it comes to Former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Isn`t this interesting? I mean, you and I have seen each other out there on the campaign trail at various times, Ali. It does seem at least on the Republican side -- I don`t know if you just saw somebody run past you - - behind you. You tried to hurry to get --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: It`s busy somewhere here on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But speaking of hurrying, there do seem to be some Republicans who seem to be hurrying to get in the race.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. Everyone is running, apparently, quite literally here in Congress, and apparently in South Carolina as well. Because what we`re seeing in these poll numbers is -- Parker`s right, she makes it sound easy. But if you win the primary, voters tend to stay pretty tribal in terms of sticking with the Republican nominee if they`re Republicans, sticking with the Democratic nominee if they`re Democrats. But just winning out of a primary might be easier for someone like Biden if he decides to run because he`s more likely it seems like to freeze out anybody else in the field as the sitting president, compared to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is now likely officially in the next two weeks to get at least one other challenger in former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 I`ve spoken with three sources who were familiar with her rollout and it has played out exactly as they told me it would overnight. Today, she sent invitations to would-be supporters and backers, inviting them to an event for a special announcement in their words in Charleston, South Carolina, that`s going to be in the middle of the month, February 15. So, it looks like at least there, Trump is going to be getting his first official challenger, and it`s someone from within his own administration`s ranks.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Someone who has national security and foreign policy experience as a former ambassador to the \uc2 UN\uc2  in the Trump administration. As someone who has been a successful two-term governor of a critically important state in South Carolina. So, there will be at least one. Though, there are multiple names in the mix right now, on the Republican side. Many of the sources that I`ve been talking to say that there are at least six to eight candidates who are stepping up and actively exploring bids.\par \par \uc2 So, that conversation is getting louder now, especially as we`ve seen Trump have a kind of muted response on the campaign trail in terms of the lack of events that he`s doing. And on the fundraising front, our colleague Jonathan Allen reporting on the way that they`re trying to revamp that operation to serve them better in the long haul for this very lengthy primary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ashley, I want to also talk about something that was in your paper today, which is the \uc2 RNC\uc2  plan to kind of ramp up their focus on false disproven election fraud claims. The report "reveals the degree to which Republicans continue to trade on former President Donald Trump`s false claims that Democrats and their allies rigged his defeat in 2020." The report also acknowledges that the GOP`s obsession with election fraud has cost the party. So, how do you balance those two things?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Well, there`s something fascinating you`re seeing now among the Republican base, which is that if you just look back historically with maybe the exception of Goldwater, normally when a candidate loses, Democratic or Republican, they, A., accept the loss, but they also sort of slink away, right? Hillary spending a lot of time hiking in her -- by her house in New York. Even someone like Senator Romney after his defeat in 2012, kind of quietly taking some time before running for Senate with little fanfare.\par \par \uc2 But now you`re seeing with former President Trump as case study number one. But I could point you to Kari Lake, for instance, who my colleague just reported is on her way to DC right now to appear at the National Prayer Breakfast to just scheduled as Vaughn Hillyard on your team just reported, to events in Iowa. These people who have lost and who have refused to accept their defeat are now being lionized in the party. It is rare to see people who lose number one, and who refuse to accept a defeat, number two, being the sorts of people that a traditional political party wants to headline conventions or appear at prayer breakfast, but that is a dynamic that is going on right now in the Republican base.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s going to be fascinating because we went from will anybody challenge Trump to Trump, Haley, Hogan, Pence, DeSantis, maybe Lake, and that`s just for starters. Ali Vitali, Ashley Parker, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2 . Remember her? Made her name navigating big personalities and papering over PR disasters for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, she`ll try to do the same for a controversial name in New York sports, \uc2 Knicks\uc2  and Rangers owner \uc2 James Dolan\uc2 . According to The New York Post. Dolan recently hired the former White House communications director after he was ripped by fans and lawmakers alike for using facial recognition technology to ban some of his critics from \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . No word on how long the two have been working together.\par \par \uc2 In less than an hour, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is set to hike rates again. What will their decision tell us about our economy through 2023? Plus, the White House meeting everyone in DC will be watching, President Biden and Speaker McCarthy readying for a showdown. That`s next on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re just minutes away from a big move by the Fed, expected to hike interest rates for the eighth time in a year, but this time by less than it has before. It`s a sign the Fed`s masterplan to bring down inflation is working, even though other parts of the economy like consumer spending and retail sales are slipping.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Brian Cheung following all of this for us.\par \par \uc2 So, if we put it all together, Brian, and the Fed only raises as expected a quarter point, does that mean the worst is over?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA CORRESPONDENT: No, and the reason why is because inflation as of the last reading was still 6.5 percent, and where the Fed really wants to see that is 2 percent. And even the decline that we`ve seen from the 9 percent in the middle of last year, to 6.5 percent, Americans really aren`t observing that at the store yet. They`re not feeling that relief yet.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is not done yet. For what it`s worth, what we have seen from the Federal Reserve`s action side of things is that they are creating this off-ramp to at some point later this year stop raising interest rates entirely. But keep in mind the campaign that they`ve been embarking on over the last year or so has been ratcheting up borrowing costs, the fastest pace that we`ve seen in decades to try to deliberately slow this economy.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lag impact of that. When they raise interest rates tomorrow, we`re not going to feel that until maybe six months down the line. So hopefully inflation continues to go down but the mission is far from over.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, the good news is that the stock market was strong in January, right, and I think it was Nasdaq`s best month since July.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: It was, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is the momentum going to continue?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Well, look, with the stock markets, I don`t have a crystal ball.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Who knows?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: I wish I can tell you if it was going to be green or red on any given day but --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Why are we down almost 300 right now?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Well, interesting, there was some data this morning that showed that job openings were actually a lot larger than economists had expected in the month of December. Now that`s kind of concerning because when you think about what the Fed is trying to do here, well, they`re hoping that this economy is going to slow and that, you know, employers are perhaps going to pull back on how aggressively they`re trying to hire.\par \par \uc2 That hasn`t happened based off the data that we`ve looked at. But, look, by and large, the overall story here is that it`s a very weird economy where we have 3.5 percent unemployment, that matches in over 50-year low. But inflation is really high, and as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  ratchets these interest rates up, they`re worried that they`re going to actually have to give up some of those employment gains.\par \par \uc2 People might lose their jobs. We`re seeing that in the tech sector already. Does that escalate as they continue to raise interest rates, something we`ll have to watch this year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brian Cheung, always great to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Refusing to budge. President Biden`s first meeting with Speaker McCarthy. The speaker`s reaction before he heads into the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: After weeks of dodging questions, George Santos sat down for his first TV interview since stepping back from his committee assignments. In what appeared to be a scripted and edited conversation with conservative outlet OAN, Santos denied he lives in a fantasy world, admitting simply to poor judgment. He claims to have learned his lesson, but says he, quote, "doesn`t know what looking sorry looks like."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): I know that a lot of people want to create this narrative that I faked my way to Congress which is absolutely categorically false. I have worked hard, I have built ground up a career through experience and through knowledge and through self-education. And, you know, I think it`s amazing that I have to sit here and be spoken down to on a regular basis yet again by the media.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s bring in NBC`s Garrett Haake on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 It`s a familiar voice, right, to go after the media when things aren`t going well for you. Having said that, I was wondering what he hoped to accomplish with this. You know, did he think that he could help himself? How closely the folks on the Hill who are in the Republican caucus are paying attention to it? What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, yes, Chris, look, I mean Santos did not have to dodge any tough questions in that 13-minute interview because he wasn`t asked any. I mean, this was about as friendly of a venue as he could have possibly hoped for.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, the view from the Santos campaign or the Santos office may be this. He`s got to get out of here and start telling his story at some point. You probably want to start with the most friendly outlet you possibly can and one that`s going to speak to your base voters. If Santos can sort of solidify things for his base supporters, solidify things for people who may not have followed his associated scandals as closely as, you know, viewers of other channels and people who might be more skeptical of him, he can at least keep the bottom from falling out any farther.\par \par \uc2 It`s possible this interview, you know, made some progress in that regard here. He, you know, repeated complaints about the media that I have heard from every single politician I have ever covered in my entire career. No politician thinks they`re covered fairly by the media. Santos clearly doesn`t think he is either. But in terms of actually getting, you know, lessening the heat around him and answering substantiative questions about the various things we know that he has lied about, it`s done nothing to get the rest of us from backing off. He`s still facing difficult questions every time he leaves his office, and, you know, for the time being he`s still off of his committees until he says he is cleared of any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 And Chris, that could be quite some time given that we know various investigations including criminal investigations are still ongoing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Garrett, stay with me because at this hour high stakes, but low expectations for the first meeting between President Biden and Kevin McCarthy since he became speaker. The conversation is expected to focus largely on the debt ceiling where frankly they`re worlds apart. Though McCarthy told "Punchbowl," quote, "We`re eventually going to have to dance together, so when do you want to play the music, now or later?"\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC White House correspondent Mike Memoli along with Garrett.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, what is the president`s strategy here?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, the president is going into this discussion today to speaker McCarthy as it relates to the debt ceiling, knowing full well what happened when he was vice president about 13 years ago, 12 years ago, with the last time we came close to a debt default. It`s this idea that before the president wants to negotiate with Republicans, he thinks Republicans need to negotiate with Republicans. As the president has been saying repeatedly over the last few days, show me your budget and I`ll show you mine.\par \par \uc2 The White House committing as the president is required to do, by the way, to release their budget proposal in the coming weeks, and they want to see what Republicans are going to put together to try to match the rhetoric. They say they want to cut spending, well, how are they going to do it? Is it going to be on programs like Social Security and Medicare? Is it going to be defense spending? Is it going to be other potentially popular areas where the budget cuts might be unpopular with the American people?\par \par \uc2 That`s really what the president is trying to do here to draw the Republicans out. But the speaker is holding the high ground in this respect. He says this has to be a negotiation. I think if you gave some truth serum to the president, he knows ultimately this will be something that`s negotiated. But for now, they are saying that the full faith and credit of the United States should not be held hostage to some political demands of the moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So to the point of negotiation, Garrett, and if he`s right and at some point they`re going to have to dance together, will the music start playing before the U.S. is on the edge of default?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, if past is prologue when it comes to dealing with Congress, the answer is probably not. I mean, look, to extend your dancing metaphor a little bit further here, I think both of these men are waiting for the other to lead, and that`s the bottom line here. Biden doesn`t see any reason to come off his position and Speaker McCarthy has yet to come forward with any specific cuts.\par \par \uc2 The challenge that McCarthy faces right now is one we all saw play out when he was elected speaker in the first place. He has a fairly unruly caucus and they don`t agree even amongst themselves on what they`d like to see cut or how they`d like to try to meet their ambitious goals of cutting the deficit and cutting federal spending.\par \par \uc2 There are conversations going on even now between all the different caucuses and committees, and subgroups within the Republican conference to try to come up with some of that agreement because ultimately they`re going to have to take that first to the rest of their fellow Republicans, and then ultimately to the White House if they`re going to have some kind of agreement. But the idea that this is going to get done well in advance of any particular fiscal deadlines without the markets freaking out, it tends to fly in the face of really all of our experience covering Congress when it comes to these kinds of fiscal deadlines.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s talk real deadlines because people often want to know, so when does the rubber meet the road, when do we start doing, you know, 24/7, we got to get this done.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:10]\par \par \uc2 What`s the drop dead date, I guess, is essentially what I`m asking you, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, the Treasury Department says right now they think it`s sometime in June, but as we know that date can sometimes be extended. To me, if tax receipts come in well this year, and the Treasury pushes it back, what you could see is a natural deadline could be the start of the August recess, for example. Congress tends to get most focused when their own time away from the Capitol comes into question.\par \par \uc2 If the next X date from Treasury slides that far, that would be the date I`d be looking at here. But again they haven`t even begun the budgeting process that they would have to go through under normal circumstances yet in this Congress. We`re just a very long way from real substantiative discussions and answers on these topics.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Garrett Haake, Mike Memoli, thank you, guys. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big programming note for you. Tonight, White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is sitting down with Lawrence O`Donnell for one of his final interviews before leaving office. Watch the "LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL," tonight, 10:00 p.m. Eastern only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 In minutes we`ll go back to Memphis, where mourners are gathering to honor the life of Tyre Nichols. His mother convinced some good must come from her son`s death. How advocates hope to make her plea a reality.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:25]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are just a few minutes from the start of Tyre Nichols funeral in Memphis, Tennessee, at the Mississippi Boulevard Christian Church. We will show it to you from beginning to end, but now we want to continue the important conversation about whether this life, this time, will lead to change.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Keith Mayes, associate professor of African-American and African Studies at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 . He is author of an upcoming book about race and special education called "The Unteachables."\par \par \uc2 Keith, it`s good to have you here. Another black man is dead at the hands of police, and again, you have people saying this must be the time that things change, and I wonder if you`re hopeful?\par \par \uc2 KEITH MAYES, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MINNESOTA\uc2  ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR OF AFRICAN- AMERICAN AND AFRICAN STUDIES: You know, Chris, I actually am hopeful this time around. I think when I gave a few interviews when the tragic death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  occurred, I was very unhopeful of the situation because I kept seeing that no matter how many black lives are lost at the hands of the police, it seems like we didn`t have the political will to make the kind of change that was necessary to see reform.\par \par \uc2 But I think this time around with the officers being African-American who were involved with the tragic death of Tyre Nichols, I think that it changes the dynamics of the conversation because this is about -- this is not about black and white. This may be more about black and blue, and I don`t mean in the adversarial sense of the black community being against police officers, but it does demonstrate that the issues that are inside the police departments and law enforcement across this country are systemic problems as we have been saying all along.\par \par \uc2 So I think that this time there could be an opportunity here to make real change because of the racial dynamics.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, we should say it`s not as if nothing has been done over the years. We have seen much broader use of body cameras. Cities are investing, many cities and communities, training around the use of force has changed. Just one example, New York City ended qualified immunity for police. But what`s the next realistic step?\par \par \uc2 MAYES: The next realistic step is changes in the federal law. And I think that oftentimes federal law follows on the heels of what takes place legislatively and that the policy at the local levels. So it`s great to see that New York City changed its qualified immunity policies, but I want to see that happen across the country in different cities in the United States.\par \par \uc2 I would like to see states pass laws for police reform, and then maybe that will galvanize Congress to do something, but, again, I think that if we continue to keep the focused attention on what happens inside precincts and less on what`s wrong with black folks, particularly young black males, I think that we have an opportunity to make real substantiative change.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I`m wondering where the conversation goes? Because I don`t think there`s any doubt about the outrage that is out there, the outrage we saw through \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  and something did happen, right? I mean, at least a proposal was made, and so now President Biden wants to go back to the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Policing Act. But does public sentiment, does public outrage turn into action, or how do you turn it into action maybe is the better question.\par \par \uc2 MAYES: I think we continue to create momentum at the grassroots level. I think we use these moments, as tragic as they are, as educational campaigns and moments to galvanize and garner more support from people who may have been indifferent or even hostile to the issue of police reform. I think that because these tragedies are so often, I think we have the opportunity to educate generations of young black, white and brown folks about the issues.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 I`m old enough to remember the Rodney King incident, but it seems as though the social media and with this focused hyper attention on police community relations, it appears as though these tragedies are happening more often, but it`s just that we have our focused attention on them, and we may have a camera on them oftentimes, so I think that we continue to build momentum and educate the public to demand that Washington and statehouses across the country do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 Body cameras are an important step. Changing qualified immunity policies are an important first step, but we need to do so much more, and I`m very hopeful, even more so than I was with the tragedy -- after the tragedy of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  here in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Keith Mayes, it is good to end this hour on a hopeful note. Thank you so much for taking the time to be with us.\par \par \uc2 I do want to note as we wait for the funeral to begin, that we just learned that in spite of some weather delays, Vice President Harris has arrived in Memphis, and in fact, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 , who is of course one of the family`s attorneys, said that Tyre Nichols` mom got a call from the vice president and it helped her. In fact he says Harris managed to get a smile out of her, a woman who has been remarkably positive and hopeful and determined that her son`s death will not be in vain.\par \par \uc2 That is going to do it for us this hour. The church in Memphis filling up for the celebration of the life of Tyre Nichols. Those services, again, set to begin shortly, so stay with MSNBC for full coverage from Memphis. We will be there as soon as it starts. "KATIE TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230202ej2100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 30, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ellison Barber, Kelly Cobiella, Steve Kornacki,  Matthew Dowd, Vaughn Hillyard, Dasha Burns, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 7,810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 A sixth officer has now been relieved of duty in Memphis as heartbreaking new details are shedding light on both the horrific beating that cost Tyre Nichols`s life and the minutes that passed when something might have been done to save him. We`ll dig deeper into the decisions made by police and first responders and the decisions officials are faced with right now that could stop the next attack from happening.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our new NBC News poll showing Americans increasingly grim about the country`s future saying it`s a disaster. The warning signs for Joe Biden`s expected reelection bid. How can he turn things around?\par \par \uc2 And no golden elevator no wild crowds. Donald Trump`s 2024 campaign kicks off with what one publication called a sleepy event in New Hampshire. Just 400 people in a high school auditorium. Does that kind of kickoff open the door even wider for other Republicans to jump into the race?\par \par \uc2 So, a lot to talk about today but at this hour, the country is struggling to come to grips with the sheer brutality and inhumanity of the death of Tyre Nichols, put into devastating focus by today`s \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis. It concludes that officers shouted at least 71 commands in 13 minutes, many of them orders described as impossible for Nichols to follow. And all of that before reporting he was in custody. And as we learned just an hour ago, a sixth officer who was involved, Preston Hemphill, has now been relieved of duty.\par \par \uc2 The details from the video are so brutal. We won`t show it again. But they bring us to a critical moment for this country as local state and federal officials go back to work this Monday with the voices of thousands of protesters demanding answers to a central question. What are you going to do to stop this from happening again? With astonishing grace and forgiveness, Tyre`s mother just this morning found reason to hope.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWVAUGHN WELLS, TYRE NICHOLS MOTHER: I feel like my son will sacrifice for the greater good and it`s going to be a lot of good that comes out of this. Hopefully, we can help another kid and another families not go through something like this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ellison Barber is live in Memphis. Harry Litman is a former U.S. attorney and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General. Kirk Burkhalter is a retired New York police detective and director of the 21st Century Policing Project at \uc2 New York Law School\uc2 . Good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, what more do we know about this sixth officer who was relieved of duty, and could there be even more officers facing some level of punishment?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, there certainly could be that`s something that the DA for Shelby County has been very clear on particularly in the last 48 hours saying that this investigation is ongoing. And just because there are five officers facing charges, that does not mean that more indictments and additional charges could perhaps happen down the road. As far as the sixth officer, we are still learning a lot of details here. But we do have his name right now. According to a spokesperson for the Memphis Police Department, officer Preston Hemphill has been relieved of duty. He is, as you said, the sixth officer to be relieved of duty following the detainment and death of Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 They are not commenting right now on the extent of his involvement that night, but they are telling NBC News that this officer was relieved of duty with the other five officers shortly after the traffic stop on January 7. But they say his name was not originally released to the public because he was not fired. The spokesperson says as it relates to this particular officer, the investigation is still ongoing. When asked by NBC News, the spokesperson declined to say whether or not this officer is on paid or unpaid leave, but she did say that additional information when it is available, will be updated and provided through their social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 This officer, again, his name, Preston Hemphill, was hired in 2018 There`s been some chatter about this officer online. Actually, local community activist noticing in an early affidavit that his name was mentioned and there was some sort of reference to him using a taser that night. Those activists as well as a local newspaper, the Tennessee Holler, started raising questions particularly online, saying, hey, what`s the deal here? Who is this person that was named? Is there anything that has happened here?\par \par \uc2 And then really, just in the last hour, we`re getting confirmation, though not a lot of details from the Memphis Police Department that this individual who was being talked about, particularly amongst community activist groups here, that he has, in fact, been relieved from duty but again, they`re not commenting on what exactly his involvement that night was and they are not saying whether or not he is on paid or unpaid leave, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you, Ellison. We appreciate that update. Kirk, it`s still, even days later almost impossible to get your head around what happened during this traffic stuff. We don`t even know why Tyre Nichols was pulled over. But what we do know from this \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis about what the police were yelling at him is this.\par \par \uc2 "The orders were often simultaneous and contradictory. Officers commanded Mr. Nichols to show his hands even as they were holding his hands. They told him to get on the ground even when he was on the ground. And they ordered him to reposition himself, even when they had control of his body."\par \par \uc2 Is that bad training? Is it the heat of the moment? Why do we see so many traffic stops turning into deadly encounters?\par \par \uc2 KIRK BURKHALTER, LAW PROFESSOR, \uc2 NEW YORK LAW SCHOOL\uc2 : Well, that`s an excellent question. There are several reasons. But starting with the information that you just set forth, this is not necessarily bad training. It`s panic. And it`s ineffective policing. We don`t know if it`s bad training or not.\par \par \uc2 When someone`s presented with multiple commands, the brain goes into overload. And quite often, the person -- the recipient of those commands is unsure which to follow. And not following any of those commands can have consequential -- can have depth-related consequences, as was the case here.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like if you watch the video, the traffic stop emanated from a perceived slight. And I don`t know that for a fact. But if you watch the video, the opposite talks about the fact that he felt Tyres Nichol -- Tyre Nichols` vehicle almost struck his and they were angry. That, we do know. The officers were angry from the moment they encountered Mr. Nichols, and I think that played a lot into their behavior.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Harry, I mentioned that we`re not even sure if Nichols was lawfully stopped. The chief says the investigation hasn`t confirmed whether he was driving recklessly. If indeed, it was a case where one of those officers felt he almost got into an accident with him. But where does that tell you about that part -- what does that tell you about that part of the investigation, and how easy or difficult or important will it be to find out what happened as a motivating factor?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: So, even if they had some justification initially in your -- which happens in other cases, it`s comp -- nevertheless, it devolves so quickly into essentially an unlawful free for all just savage pummeling where he is remarkably free. He`s saying, guys, you`re doing so much, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 And the point in a -- policing 101 with respect is you must give clear and specific commands. He`s getting -- it`s literally hellish. He`s getting it from every direction, then he`s getting pepper sprayed, and he`s getting tased. He doesn`t know what`s going on. And then you know, they stand him up, and to absolutely deliver crushing blows.\par \par \uc2 So, the whole thing is not simply brutal, but also a complete sort of bedlam and confusion. That is -- that paints a crime scene whether or not he -- and it does seem like, maybe he dissed them, oh my, we`ll and -- you know, has to do with showing who`s in control of the streets. But if he had -- if he is driving on the wrong side of the street or whatever, it doesn`t begin to justify almost any part. I worked on the Rodney King case, and the theory was it was legitimate at first, but then maybe a minute and it got illegitimate. As best I can tell, the entire encounter here and the beating from all directions was from the beginning without justification.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kirk, there are also questions about the medical response. And I want to go through a pretty generic but to pointed timeline of how this unfolded. 8:37, Nichols is handcuffed and he`s immobile. 8:41, medics arrived on the scene but it`s not until 8:57 that they begin to provide aid. Then 9:02, the ambulance arrives. 9:18, Nichols is taken to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 This seems like a really long time. Although we don`t know again, what police were telling the first responders about what was happening. But what are the protocols in a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 BURKHALTER: Well, the police are obligated. Obligated, arguably constitutionally to provide medical aid. We see this all the time. We see police officers engaged in gun battles with the suspects. And even after that battle is over, even if police injured a person, they immediately respond and provide medical aid. And this is because to do otherwise, actually crosses over into the punishment phase. And that`s what our courts are here for. So, certainly the police are obligated to provide medical aid.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We didn`t see that certainly in the video. As a matter of fact, police officers telling Mr. Nichols to sit up. They are walking around recounting the incident while he was there, basically bleeding to death. So, this was such an -- that part of this interaction is clear to be obvious official misconduct in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`d like to ask you both the same question before we have to go. And if you could keep your answers fairly brief. And I know it`s a more complicated question than that.\par \par \uc2 And, Harry, you mentioned that you worked on the federal prosecution of police officers in the Rodney King case. Look, in these high-profile cases, we have seen convictions but it doesn`t seem like they`re having an effect to tell police you can`t do this anymore. Am I off base here? Are they not sending a message with some of these sentences that they`re putting out there now?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: I think most of the police --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Harry, can I ask you first?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: I think most police experts would tell you there has been a change in culture. But one of the most disconcerting elements, all of these guys, all six of them are hired between 2017 and 2020. They`re the newer officers, and yet they`re from the older, take no prisoners kind of culture. That`s really troubling.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What do you make of it, Kirk? Do you think that there is a change, at least in the mindset of how these things are being handled?\par \par \uc2 BURKHALTER: I think there -- we see-- it`s both. We see a slight change in the mindset. But also keep in mind, what we saw here goes beyond the simple violation of police procedure. This was sadistic behavior. And the question is whether these officers should have even been police officers, or whether through some form of extensive monitoring whether they should have been surveyed off the police department prior to this incident.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m being told I`m out of time but I have to ask you this question because I was just reminded of it. I was flying back and I was sitting next to a young African American male on the plane. And I noticed that he was watching on a Saturday Night, a documentary on another network about police stops. And, Kirk, so I asked him, I said, I was surprised that he was watching that. And he said, why would anybody question why we run? And I didn`t have an answer for him, do you?\par \par \uc2 BURKHALTER: You know, it`s damned if you do damn if you don`t. And that`s the point we`re getting to in the minds of the civilian population. Why would we question -- I would just encourage folks that that`s never the answer. But I understand. How can you explain to a civilian anything different after seeing this type of video?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kirk Burkhalter and Harry Litman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We will have further conversations. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up later this hour. Val Demings has seen this problem from both sides as a former police chief and a member of Congress. We`ll talk about her concerns with police culture and whether changing the rules can change behavior.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there`s a huge diplomatic test underway for the Biden administration at this hour. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  trying to help tamp down violence that has broken out between Israelis and Palestinians, including an attack on a synagogue that left seven Israelis dead. It`s the worst attack in 15 years.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kelly Cobiella is in Jerusalem for us. Kelly, Blinken did not travel there because of this violence. He had the trip scheduled for a while now. But what is the potential impact of this trip during this unrest, and what are folks there looking for?\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, it puts the -- push this issue right up to the top of the agenda for America`s top diplomat. \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  having to address this just after stepping off the plane as he landed here in Israel today, saying that it was pertinent for both sides. Both sides needed to take steps to stop what he called a rising tide of violence. And he addressed it again in those joint statements -- in that joint statement with the Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  warning against taking any action that could jeopardize that long-term goal of a two-state solution. Take a listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Anything that moves us away from that vision is in our judgment detrimental to Israel`s long-term security and its long-term identity as a Jewish and democratic state. That`s why we`re urging all sides now to take urgent steps to restore calm, to de- escalate, we want to make sure that there`s an environment in which we can -- I hope, at some point create the conditions where we can start to restore a sense of security for Israelis and Palestinians alike, which of course, is sorely lacking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: And there were some really big issues on the agenda still are for this trip, namely, Iran.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken is saying that both the U.S. and Israel agree that Iran could never acquire a nuclear weapon but what is the plan B if Iran does not rejoin the nuclear accord, and also keeping Iran`s military ambitions in check? The U.S. has accused Iran of supplying Russia with Iranian-made - - Iranian-made drones to use in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And then just over the weekend, we saw a drone attack on an Iranian military facility. The Iranians have blamed Israel and the U.S. The CIA hasn`t had a comment. Neither has the \uc2 Israeli Defense Ministry\uc2 . But this is something a tactic that Israel has used in the past, these drone attacks on Iran and again, Iran`s ambition something that both countries have in common, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kelly Cobiella, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Also oversees the site of the latest target of ruthless Russian shelling, a kindergarten. It`s adding to Ukrainian President Zelenskyy`s argument that his country needs even more critical weapons after allies pledged to send dozens of battle tanks to Ukraine. But since it could be months before they get there, Zelenskyy said, we have to make time for our weapon. He`s also asking for fighter jets with talks over getting them to Ukraine reportedly on the fast track, including a list of Ukrainians ready to train on those F-16s, but no commitment from the Pentagon or European allies.\par \par \uc2 Front runner frustration. Steve Kornacki at the big board with the new NBC poll, where Joe Biden`s support seems to be slipping and what can he do about it? Plus, Donald Trump`s message to the growing number of Republicans who say they want someone else at the top of the ticket. Also, no snow, the record we just set this winter, and how it`s hurting some key businesses across the Northeast. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: 645 days out from the 2024 election, and about a year out from the projected start of the primaries. And today, we now have an early but very tangible look at both the mood of the country and the strategies of the front runners. A new NBC News poll shows that while President Biden`s approval rating hasn`t changed, several key indicators have. And the movement is negative.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Steve Kornacki. He is with us at the big board. Good to see you, Steve. Also joining us is Matthew Dowd, MSNBC senior political analyst and former chief strategist for the Bush-Cheney 2004 campaign.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know why I was so surprised. But the sheer pessimism and the record-setting level of pessimism really is sort of a standout in this poll that I think is a warning maybe for both sides.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I think -- it`s really striking, I think, Chris. We`re talking about the first NBC poll of 2023. You`ve had the Republicans take over the House, some big changes in Washington. But the overall political climate, as you say, you look at Joe Biden`s approval rating. In our poll, he is still underwater. 45 percent approved. 50 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 You just look at all the numbers from last year heading into the midterm. I mean, he`s still right where he was last year. We were saying last year, not a particularly popular president, that remains the case heading into 2023.\par \par \uc2 You talk about pessimism here asking folks, you know, their income compared to the cost of living. And look at this. Again, we were talking about this all last year. Two-thirds basically of voters don`t feel that their income is keeping up with the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes,\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: That fear of inflation, very palpable out there still, that economic pessimism. And then we asked about what you`re seeing here is just a long list here of traits and attributes. We`re asking folks about whether they feel \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has them or not, whether they feel positively or negatively about Biden --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This is incredible.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, that`s it. You see, he scores well on easygoing and likable. But look at some of the areas where he`s upside down. Honest and trustworthy, competent and effective, mental and physical health, something too, obviously the oldest president that we`ve had.\par \par \uc2 So, you look at this. We were saying this -- we were telling this story in the run for 2022, an unpopular president, economic pessimism-specific concerns about Biden, you did see Democrats do a little defying of political gravity in 2022. These numbers tell you, at least at the beginning of this 2024 journey, if Biden`s the Democratic candidate in `24, they may have to try to defy that gravity again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Matthew Dowd, he`s certainly not the first president who at the middle of his first term, looks like he`s in some trouble when you look at the polls. But what are these numbers, these big drops and things like honest and trustworthy, competent and effective, tell you about a Biden 2024 campaign?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: It tells me that like 2020 and like 2016, it`s going to be another referendum on the president, it`s going to be a choice election because as you started this segment, and as Steve knows, the country is very sour on everybody. The very country is very sour on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the country is very sour on the -- on the Democrats in Congress, the country is very sour on the Republicans in Congress, and so like 2022, we`re going to see it`s going to be a choice election. Democrats were successful in arguing that choice in 2022, and I`m sure if Biden runs, he`s going to argue don`t make it a referendum on me, make it a choice between who I am and who the Republican nominee is.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, that doesn`t say that you`re anticipating or that they should sit around and wait to see an economic turnaround because the first thing you think of easy, easy "if the economy turns around, his fortunes turnaround."\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, yes, but I don`t think he has much control over that. So, if I were them, I would worry too much about it. I mean, I would continue if I were them making the contrast between what they`re doing and what the Republicans in the House of Representatives are doing. I think that contrast is it benefits them. But the economy, if it goes great, there`ll be benefited. If it doesn`t, they`re going to be harmed by it.\par \par \uc2 But let`s keep in mind that the economy wasn`t doing well in 2022. And Democrats and the president smartly made it a choice and made it about a bigger issue, like the -- like democracy and like the future of our country. So, the economy is sitting there. But I -- you know, it`s one of the things is the economy is the most important issue in every election. The question becomes, what do you run at?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Very -- two very different questions. Steve Kornacki, thank you so much. Fascinating stuff. \uc2 Matt Dowd\uc2 , stay with me because, after the break, former President Trump swings through too early primary states, why he says he`s more committed than ever but someone on his party says he`s not really bringing the fire?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing a presidential landscape dramatically changed from when he won in 2016, even when he ran and 2020 frankly. Smaller venues, much smaller crowds, what Axios is calling a sleepy start to his 2024 campaign in the early primary states of New Hampshire and South Carolina. But Trump has a different take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They said he`s not doing rallies, he`s not campaigning, maybe he`s lost that step. But we didn`t. I`m more angry now and I`m more committed now than I ever was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: More angry and more committed. But the jabs are also beginning to fly. Trump taking shots at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  while Nikki Haley continues to tease a run of her own.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is in South Carolina. NBC`s Dasha Burns is in Pennsylvania. Matthew Dowd is back with me. So, Vaughn, in New Hampshire, the crowd as we said small about 400.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 You`re in South Carolina. Give me a sense of what you see as the enthusiasm or lack thereof there, and not just from the crowds but I also want to know about all important elected officials and other prominent Republicans whose support is important in primaries.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right. The Trump campaign is trying to do something a little different here, Chris. This is the first time on the road since he made his announcement there at Mar-a-Lago in the middle of November after the midterm elections. And here in South Carolina, it was under the dome of the South Carolina statehouse. And it wasn`t a matter of trying to turn out thousands of folks for a typical rally we`ve been accustomed to. But instead, it was to lock down support among elected officials and party activists.\par \par \uc2 You know, in New Hampshire, it was at the annual meeting of the New Hampshire GOP where he announced the outgoing New Hampshire chairman was coming on to his campaign team as New Hampshire senior adviser. Then here in South Carolina, he was flanked by the likes of individuals who he claimed to be part of his leadership team. Those individuals included the governor, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , the senior senator, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , as well as three of the six sitting Republican congressmen here from the state of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you go back six years ago when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was announcing and running that first go-around, it was tough for him to find folks at the party activist level or elected officials who would willingly stand by him. But you saw the likes of, you know, the Republican Women`s Club here from South Carolina that drove to Columbia to come for this event. And he is going to them and making the direct appeal to stand with him for another term.\par \par \uc2 Before anybody else gets into this race, he is laying and attempting to lay the foundation to build off from there as these other Republicans will be jumping into the race, too, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Dasha, some potential challengers, as I mentioned, are talking, South Carolina`s Nikki Haley sounds like she`s going to run. She`s making the argument it`s time for a new generation to lead. New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  made an appearance on Sunday morning. He`s considering a run.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been talking to Republican voters in Lucerne County. That was blue, now it`s deep red. And I wonder if they`re telling you they think on the Republican side it`s time for a change.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, Chris, here at the Avenue Diner in Lucerne County there is a lot of frustration right now. People are talking about the high prices for groceries, their electric bills, small business owners talking about how they can`t take out loans because of the interest rates, right? Not surprising to hear that people here in Lucerne County which as you mentioned is deep red now in the post-Trump era.\par \par \uc2 Not surprising that they`re frustrated with this administration and there`s a lot of sort of nostalgia for the bygone Trump era. But just because they miss President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president does not mean that they want to see him as president again. It`s interesting. One thing that we hear a lot about both Trump and Biden when we talk to voters, the age factor. But the other factor that I`ve been hearing a lot when it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from voters that voted for him twice, by the way, is the baggage element, that there is so much drama surrounding him.\par \par \uc2 Although these voters don`t believe he did anything wrong, they say they think he was a great president, they`re still frustrated with how much talk there is about him and about the various controversies. For example, I want you to hear from a gentleman named Allen, he`s a small business owner in Lucerne County. Here`s what he told me earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Would you vote for Trump over some other Republicans? Are you interested in seeing some new blood?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN WOOTON, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: It depends. I wouldn`t mind seeing somebody new, you know, somebody that`s not as, you know, has so many followers that are radical. You know? If he didn`t have such radical followers, he`d be a great president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: By the way, I also talked to a few Democratic voters here who also aren`t so thrilled about seeing Biden run again. I mean, you were talking a little bit to Steve Kornacki and Matthew Dowd about this earlier. Not a lot of optimism on either side. The outlook feels pretty bleak when you talk to voters right now, both because of the economic climate right now and also when they`re looking at their options.\par \par \uc2 I will say, though, that the Republican voters I have been talking to, I asked them about the new GOP-controlled House, how they`re feeling about that. They said they really want to see Republicans work with Democrats to just get something done. The stalemate that they`ve been watching for years now is really starting to wear on them, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. So, Matthew, Trump`s main rival, at least in the very early polls, is Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who hasn`t even announced whether he`s running. But a New Hampshire poll shows him leading Trump by 12 points. Trump`s answer when, you know, he talks about DeSantis is less like I`m the better choice and more grievance. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Ron would not be governor if it wasn`t for me. And that`s OK. Then when I hear he might run, you know, I consider that very disloyal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Disloyal. How at this very early stage do you see this race shaping up, even if it`s the four candidates we just mentioned, Trump, DeSantis, Haley, Sununu? Give me your take, again understanding it`s very early in the game.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: When I hear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think of that toy where you remember you pointed it at the goat and then you pull the lever, and it said the thing, and if you keep pointing it, it`s saying every single response he has when somebody does something he likes, it`s grievance. It`s never substantive. It`s always some level of grievance.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is vulnerable. Vulnerable to a run, but I think the problem is, the king of the Republican Party right now is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In order to be the king, you have to beat the king. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today, I`ll remind people, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is more popular among Republicans than he was when he ran and won in 2016. If you look at his numbers in 2016 running in the Republican primary, he`s more popular today.\par \par \uc2 And I think Nikki Haley is probably between a rock and a hard place in this because she`s criticized \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so she has that baggage. And then she tries to claim loyalty over loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So she has that baggage. And I saw a poll this weekend that shows Nikki Haley, who was governor of South Carolina, in fourth place in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And so while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has vulnerabilities, he`s still immensely popular among the Republican base. And as he did in 2016, there`s a lot of candidates that surfaced and say, wow, they`re going to be a great candidate. Jeb Bush, he`s going to be a great candidate. Scott Walker, he`s going to be a great candidate. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , he`s going to be a great candidate. All of them fell by the wayside in Donald Trump`s wake. So we`ll have to wait and see. But right now he`s vulnerable, but he`s still the king of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I do not know that toy, but it sounds like something that I`ve missed from my childhood. So I`m going to have to deal with that in addition to everything else today.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Dowd, Vaughn Hillyard, Dasha Burns, thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 The push for police reform. Black lawmakers demanding a meeting with the president more than a year after congressional talks broke down over the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act. Former police chief and congresswoman Val Demings on where changes need to come from.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got breaking news for you. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting that the Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  will begin presenting evidence to a grand jury surrounding allegations that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  paid hush money to Stormy Daniels during the 2016 presidential campaign. NBC News has not confirmed the report.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring back former U.S. attorney Harry Litman.\par \par \uc2 Can I read part of what "The Times" said. They said, "Mr. Bragg`s decision to impanel a grand jury focused on the hush money, super charging the longest running criminal investigation into Mr. Trump, represents dramatic escalation in an inquiry that once appeared to have reached a dead-end."\par \par \uc2 What does this tell you? And by the way, they`re suggesting this might not be a regular grand jury, but a special grand jury, Harry.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Right. So, first, it stands to reason that it is a special grand jury that will meet for six months to consider these charges. As "The Times" says this has had quite a tortuous history. Bragg`s predecessor, \uc2 Cyrus Vance Jr\uc2 ., wanted to bring a case about this and other charges. Bragg himself decided not to. But now it looks like he wants to go forward.\par \par \uc2 The core here, Stormy Daniels basically is getting hush money and that payment seems to have been characterized on the books as a legal fee to \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  which it wasn`t. And that`s the New York crime, although there are different ways they could try to characterize it. They have some evidence. Cohen talked by phone to Trump twice the day before he wires the payment. Today, it looks as if the testimony, Chris, has begun today with David Pecker, the head of the "National Enquirer" who was agreeing to kill the story.\par \par \uc2 But all in all, it`s that lurid hush money, but the actual crime in New York has to do with mischaracterization of the payments. That looks as what they`re going after.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So give me a bigger picture understanding of what it would mean or how it would operate if it`s a special grand jury versus a regular grand jury.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Sure answer is no different. It`s not like Fulton County, Georgia. They can bring charges. But instead of a regular grand jury that`s just there twice a week to hear whatever robbery or embezzlement comes through, they`re focused on this. So it`s a complicated tale that they`re going to weave, and they`re bringing it in front of them specifically. They`re specialists on what will be the Stormy Daniels hush money case.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re going to keep an eye on it. Harry Litman, thank you once again for popping back up for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Up next, the calls for police reform after Tyre Nichols` death reverberating in local communities and the halls of Congress. The latest details from D.C. and from a former congresswoman, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:12]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At this hour the Congressional Black Caucus is waiting to hear from the White House pushing for a meeting with President Biden this week over negotiations on, quote, "national reforms needed to our justice system," specifically the actions and conduct of our law enforcement following the death of Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 The numbers alone are alarming. The A.P. reports that nationwide police have killed roughly three people per day consistently since 2020 according to academics and advocates for police reform who track such deaths. But what amounts to real change? Here is \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  professor Eddie Glaude this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, PRINCETON UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: Oftentimes we think representation satisfies the question of justice. So all we need to do is to add people of color, add women, you know, diversify, and somehow that`s supposed to change the very substance of an institution. Just putting black faces in front doesn`t necessarily change the institution. You could have a black face dropping -- you know, being the face of a military dropping unjustified bombs on innocent people. That doesn`t change the fact that it`s evil, right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 Ali, the president is calling on Congress to pass the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act. What are the chances it or any kind of reform is going to pass, and by the way, is there a date on the books between Biden and the \uc2 CBC\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. No date on the books yet. But of course the Congressional Black Caucus has asked for that meeting in the aftermath of this latest moment of police violence. And then in terms of the way that the pressure could manifest in this building, it exists. But it`s not clear that the pressure has anywhere to go. The realities here in this building have become in some ways even more difficult for the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act than it was in 2021 when those talks fell apart in the fall of that year.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 Still, we`re seeing some of the key lawmakers on the Senate side speak to their ongoing negotiations on this topic. For example, you`ve got Senator Cory booker saying that he`s never going to walk away from the negotiating table on this. He`s renewing his legislative efforts. His top Republican partner in this was Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina. An aide close to Scott told NBC News that he wasn`t the one who left the negotiating table and he still encourages his colleagues on the other side to join him in these efforts.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, when you see people sort of still playing the blame game over how these negotiations fell apart in the first place, not exactly heartening to seeing this compromise come to fruition down the line. And then you have to look to the House realities, too, which have significantly changed from the time that these negotiations were last revived. Now, Republicans hold control of this chamber, and if you look at what people like Congressman Jim Jordan said, the head of the Judiciary Committee, where this legislation will have to originate, it`s clear on "MEET THE PRESS" over the weekend, he said that while this was a tragic moment, it`s not one where he thinks that there`s a federal role to play in rule making.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 And here with me now, former Congresswoman Val Demings, who was also police chief in Orlando, Florida, from 2007 to 2011.\par \par \uc2 It`s always good to talk to you, and as we look at how can we fix this, and I know we`ve had these conversations so many times before. Can I start with asking you to put on your congressional hat? Is there at least partially a legislative answer, and where is the best place for it? Is it national, state laws? Tell us what laws can do.\par \par \uc2 VAL DEMINGS, FORMER ORLANDO POLICE CHIEF: Well, Chris, it`s good to be back with you, and certainly there is usually a legislative role to be played here. You know, I was disappointed that the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act fell by the wayside. No, it was not a perfect law, of course not. But it was a major step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 I`m also disappointed to hear of the blame game that`s coming out of Congress right now. We`ve had a young 29-year-old man killed. His mother, his family, his friends, his community grieving. Let`s get back to the table and get something done. That`s what the communities deserve and that`s what the American people deserve.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is, though, we know that Congress doesn`t even have much of an appetite to come up with rules and policies and legislation to protect its own members who were under threat, and so that`s why I`ve said that police executives, sheriffs and chiefs cannot wait. Union leadership cannot wait for the legislative fix. While they`re hashing it out at the state and federal level, police chiefs and sheriffs need to come to the table and come up with best practices.\par \par \uc2 Look internally and modify those policies that need to be modified that will help protect communities but also protect the officers themselves.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: John Kasich, who was governor of Ohio when 12-year-old Tamir Rice was killed by Cleaveland officers, talked about this this weekend and he said his state then formed a collaborative with activists, police, lawyers, civil leaders, and their goal was to address real reform. Here`s part of what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KASICH (R), FORMER OHIO GOVERNOR: We had the most significant reforms in the country come out of that collaborative, and it`s made a difference in Ohio. There are many ways in which you can stay on top of it. It`s not just enough to get somebody in there and to train them. It has to be consistently monitored and if you have a bad actor in there, if you have somebody that is not a good policeman, you`ve got to get rid of them. You have to take the action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is he right, and how hard is it to pull off a monitoring system? Is that a realistic model for the rest of the country?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You know, Chris, I think that we can do anything that we really want to do. I just did some work with the two years, actually, worth of work with the National Policing Institute on the Council of Race and Policing where we looked at evidence-based solutions. The bottom line is we have to reimagine, if you will, public safety. That is not just the police role.\par \par \uc2 The police is the community, and the community is the police, and it`s going to take all of us and our respective places to come together and develop our rules, our laws, hold our elected officials accountable, our policies and procedures, and the proper oversight and accountability to get us to a place where we need to be. Every responsible person ought to look at what happened to Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 Every good law enforcement officer ought to look at what happened to Tyre Nichols and let`s come to the table and develop what we need to, to make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We only have a very short time left, but I want to ask you about what we just heard from Eddie Glaude, which is, if you`re going to begin to change culture, changing the face of police does not necessarily change the institution. So what does?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:11]\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You know, we can -- now, look, I am a strong advocate because I believe hiring is a major part of it. We`ve got to get the right men and women in the profession doing the job in the first place who have the right temperament. So I do believe in diversity that departments, just like every other institution should reflect the diversity of the communities in which they solve or serve. But we do know that there is not one fix.\par \par \uc2 Through the work of the national policing institute, we found out this evidence-based research that there is not one culture in policing. And invite me back, we can talk more about that. But what you may find is different agencies might have the right rules, regulations, policies and procedures in place, but there is a culture that is toxic in a particular unit, and I think that is exactly what we`ve seen in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I agree with the professor on the one hand is that there`s not one answer to fix all, that is going to take a collaborative effort to come together and get this done, but I believe, Chris, that we can do it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I hope you will come back because there`s so much more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Former Congresswoman Val Demings, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, President Biden landed in Maryland. It was met by the mayor of Baltimore, the governor of Maryland. The president`s remarks live from Baltimore on infrastructure coming up in the next hour.\par \par \uc2 Katy Tur will pick up our coverage after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230131ej1u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 16, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Glenn Kirschner, Monica Alba, David Jolly, Ali  Vitali, Dana Griffin, Carol Lee, Eddie Glaude, Matt Bradley, Susan Page,  Trymaine Lee \par \uc2 7,754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: They performed. Again, thank you. And that does it --\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPHEN TWITTY (RET), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  : Yes. Thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thanks so much, General. That does it for this edition of ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS. Remember, follow us online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at @mitchellreports. Chris Jansing be here right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 One revelation at a time. The political and ethical problems facing Democrats and Republicans are growing deeper by the day, more pages of classified material found at the president`s home, more head-scratching reports about Republican Congressman George Santos`s questionably behavior as his party looks the other way, where does all of it lead? Plus, President Biden honoring the legacy of \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  ., touting economic advances for underserved communities, expanded voting rights, and a robust defense of democracy, but how much can he really get done? And finally, we`ll look at how the massive infrastructure legislation passed last year may help restore one historic black and brown neighborhood in New Orleans to its former glory, not by adding anything, but by taking something away. We`ll have that story later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the ethical dilemmas staring Republicans and Democrats in the face, and the growing frustration within both parties over what some see as self-inflicted wounds. For Democrats, yet another call for information on the Biden documents controversy, this one from the Republican head of the House Oversight Committee. He wants the visitors` log from Biden`s home in Wilmington after Biden`s attorney said five more pages of classified documents were found there earlier in the week. A spokesman for the Secret Service said those logs don`t exist but Republicans insist that just adds to the list of unanswered questions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): How obnoxious are they really? The truth is that they suppress the information before the election so that it would not have an impact during the election. That sounds like a little bit of engineering from my perspective. And the second thing is the stench of hypocrisy coming from this administration is making the American people sick. It`s one of the reasons why we have so little confidence in this administration and in government overall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And for Republicans already tired of being asked about Congressman George Santos and his lies, two new reports raising yet more questions about his background came out this weekend. One connects him to accompany the SEC says defrauded its investors. The second links them to the cousin of a Russian oligarch. And the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  says some of this was known last year when a background study on Santos found "fraudulent academic degrees involvement in a firm accused of a Ponzi scheme, multiple evictions, and a suspended driver`s license. All of that was in the report which also said that Mr. Santos who is openly gay had been married to a woman."\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vital. NBC`s Monica Alba is at the White House for us. Glenn Kirschner is a former federal prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst. And David Jolly is a former Republican congressman from Florida and an NBC -- MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, we`ve pointed out repeatedly how the Biden and Trump document inquiries. The actual facts of the cases are very different. But I want to play what Jim Comer, who`s the head of the Oversight Committee said just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER, (R-KY): Well, my concern is that the special counsel was called for but yet hours after that, we still had the president`s personal attorneys who have no security clearance still rummaging around the president`s residence, looking for things. I mean, that would essentially be a crime scene, so to speak, after the appointment of a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Glenn, if you aren`t looking for classified material, the Republicans ask why would you send lawyers who don`t have a security clearance? And does all of it just allow Republicans to raise more questions here?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, I think that observation, Chris, has some superficial appeal, let`s only get people looking for documents that have the absolute highest security clearance. I don`t know that that comports with reality. I handled classified documents when I was an Army jag handling an espionage case, and I had a top-secret SCI clearance.\par \par \uc2 And the documents that you`re dealing with are marked, they`re clearly marked, many of them are in a folder that is clearly marked, so I don`t -- you can argue that perhaps a best practice is, but I don`t know that it`s a problem -- an investigative problem having President Biden`s attorneys look at things. And if they come upon a folder that is marked top secret, they stop, they alert the Department of Justice, and then a -- you know proper procedures are followed. So, I think this sounds good on the surface. I don`t know if that is necessary or practical.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Monica, I think Glenn just got to the heart of what is the problem right for the White House, which is that even if they did things by the book, even if everything they did was legal, the questions being raised caused so many problems, so many headlines. And I`m wondering -- well, my understanding is you have some new reporting from the White House about not just their reaction to all of these things, but also, are they going to cooperate with these two House investigations?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: You`re absolutely right, Chris, this just adds to the growing list of questions, even though the White House did come out and say, look, the moment we located these classified documents, we alerted the proper authorities, we handed them over, we did do things by the book. But these other ongoing questions surrounding why this happened in the first place, how long it took the White House to make this public after media reports? That continues to really dog this White House. So, it says much about the communication strategy and the public optics here.\par \par \uc2 So, speaking of publicly, the president hasn`t answered questions from reporters now for the fourth straight day. He`s been ignoring those. But privately, I`m told that he is frustrated overall with the ongoing backlash to this controversy, feeling that they did try to do everything in the manner that was appropriate but that because some of the news was bungled in the last couple of days in terms of transparency that the White House feels they are not going to get the credit they would like on that front. And they are concerned here about some Americans conflating the two very different cases, as we`ve been talking about, with former President Trump and the more than 300 classified records that were seized at Mar-a-Lago by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after months of not cooperating. So, the White House wants to make that distinction very clear.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, right now, they`re not just dealing with this special counsel probe but House Republicans are launching their own investigations. And so, you did have Chairman James Comer talking about wanting to request these visitor logs from Wilmington, Delaware, a private home, and the White House is saying very pointedly today, well, they don`t exist and that`s because the Secret Service says it is a personal residence and they don`t maintain that. They do security checks on who comes and goes. And, of course, anyone who interacts with the First Family is screened in that manner but they don`t keep hold of any of that information. So that`s not something they`re going to be providing. But it just shows you that Republicans are going to keep asking for a lot of things as the White House says they will continue to cooperate with the Department of Justice review, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Glenn, the point that we haven`t heard anything from the president in four days now. Legally, is there a limit to what he can say or what frankly, either party can say about these cases that are under investigation while a special counsel was involved?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes. I think it depends on your perspective, Chris. If I put my American citizen hat on, I want all of the details in real-time. If I put my prosecutor hat on, I don`t think the Biden administration should be saying anything.\par \par \uc2 There is presently a special counsel investigation up and running. And the last thing any prosecutor wants to do is have information sort of tumbling out into the public square that could negatively impact the ongoing investigation. If witnesses hear that information, they could either nefariously or innocently, maybe even subconsciously mold their future testimony. So, I think legally speaking the right answer, as unsatisfying as it may be is that the administration should not be talking about this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. In fact, Congressman, the White House, and Democrats have been claiming transparency saying, this is so much different than the situation with Trump. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DANIEL GOLDMAN, (D-WA): President Biden and his team have reached out to the archives. They`ve reached out to the Department of Justice. They have done everything they can to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2  , (D-MD): That is a very different posture than what we saw with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  where he was fighting for a period of more than eight months to not turn over hundreds of missing documents that the archives was asking about.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): There was no effort to hold on to them, no effort to conceal them, no effort to obstruct the Justice Department`s investigation. All of that is a very sharp contrast to Donald Trump`s handling of the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nevertheless, do you think that each new revelation makes it harder for Democrats to so vocally defend the president?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, Senator Stabenow admitting that Biden`s criticism of Trump then back when all of those allegations and revelations were made is embarrassing, or I think she -- the word she used was certainly embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Well, look, context is important, although that narrative you just played is exactly right. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  based on what we know now, and that`s an important caveat, is an example of how a president or elected official should handle a matter in which they discover they`re in possession of documents, in contrast to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , who, by all -- by all appearances, looks like he engaged with criminal intent and the -- in the hiding of documents.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, I think the failure by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was this, Chris, it was simply communications 101. You can`t have three different discoveries because that shows you`re not actually taking it seriously. When they made the first discovery the first week in November, the president should have ordered a wholesale review of every document, every box, and then issued one statement and said we got them all, I responded, we did it correctly, and there won`t be a further comment. So, from a PR standpoint, yes, this is very bad.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you. Republicans will continue to hit Biden, but they also are going to hit the Department of Justice. Because recall, one of their central thesis is that the government is favoring Democrats. And so, they`re going to go after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- or after DOJ just as hard as they are on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . And going after DOJ might actually have more legs with his story for Republicans than going after Biden himself.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, let`s look at the other story. George Santos and the number of accusations, revelations that keep piling up. The Wall Street Journal says he had ties to a company that operated like a Ponzi scheme. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports, he may have had a business relationship with the cousin of a sanctioned Russian oligarch. You even have that sound making the rounds over the weekend where he does a radio interview and he says, you know, he blew up both of his knees when he was playing championship volleyball in college, a college by the way that he now acknowledges he never attended. How long can they let this escalate Republicans, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Well, I think they will let this continue to go until there are credible criminal charges against Santos, which, you know, two weeks ago, we didn`t think this would end up in a criminal space but it very well appears it might because of sources of money. Otherwise, here`s what Republicans in the House will do. They will quietly recruit a Republican to run in that district in two years. They don`t want to just give away the seat to the Democrats, but they will give no campaign support to Santos. You`ll see them taking an arm`s length approach, not help him, not hurt him unless there are criminal charges, and then they`ll move to remove him from the House.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Kevin McCarthy, as you know, Ali, says Santos should face the Ethics Committee. Is that happening? How long might that take? Where do you see this going in terms of internally within the House?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, they are notorious though, for doing that behind closed doors and very secretively. Though I do think there`s a little bit of location irony and that our producers have been sticking out Santos all week note that his office is just one floor directly above where the Ethics Committee meets. So, that might be a notable location up here on Capitol Hill. But look, these kinds of investigations are slow-moving, and we don`t typically get many details on them as they are ongoing.\par \par \uc2 I think the important thing here too, is that while that investigation is happening, you are not hearing a clamoring from leadership to say that they want this guy to resign. Usually, you would see House leadership here in Washington echo what the home state party is saying. In this case, that`s not what we`re seeing at all. And in fact, it`s probably in part because of what Congressman Jolly was just saying.\par \par \uc2 The idea that while there are the legal concerns here, the optics, of course, are horrible for Republicans that they have this man in his ranks after he lied about his entire resume. But then there`s also the politics concerns, which is that they already have a very slim and tenuous majority here in the House. Republicans would not be eager to open up another special election that would likely be a referendum on Santos lastly favoring Democrats that could -- that could impact even just one seat in their majority. And Republicans need the numbers to stay where they are right now, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, from a legal standpoint, Glenn, what is the most likely risk for him? Is it those filings with the \uc2 FEC\uc2  questioning some of the campaign finance stuff? Do you see a legal opening here?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes. You know, lying to your constituents is probably not a crime, but lying on certain forms and documents that you fill out as a candidate, particularly if they`re filled out under the penalties of perjury, that could be a crime. And, of course, campaign finance charges could also be in the offing.\par \par \uc2 I have to say, Chris, it was pretty eye-popping when I saw the reporting that he was saying he was earning a $55,000 salary in 2020. And then in 2021, he loaned his own campaign $700,000. So, I`m not a mathematician, but seems he would have had to have saved every penny of his salary for 13 years to then loan his own campaign. $700,000, feels like there may be some campaign finance problems there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Glenn Kirschner, Ali Vitali, Monica Alba, thank you. David Jolly, you`re sticking around.\par \par \uc2 In California, too long weeks of devastating and relentless rainstorms continue to bring misery to millions, including roads crumbling right before their eyes.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When it seems down -- oh, it`s right here. Oh my God go away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And it`s not over yet, more than seven million Californians at risk of widespread floodwaters as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports from the Monterey Peninsula near San Francisco, the Santa Cruz Mountains, Lake Tahoe, the Sierra Nevada, Merced County to Los Angeles County. NBC`s Dana Griffin is in Capitola, California in the middle of it all. Dana, how much longer there you wonder can people hang on? Tell us what the situation is right now and what it looks like over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. As far as hanging on, Chris, you know, people are very fatigued. This is the 10th storm that has hit this region since Christmas. The good news is it`s likely going to fizzle out. As you can see, we`re -- we`ve got some light rain.\par \par \uc2 But take a look behind me. You can see the creek that is now feeding into the ocean. This is moving so swiftly right now. And in the distance, you can see those high waves just crashing in. And also, the distance there. This is the Capitola wharf. And you see that huge chunk there, that was missing two weeks ago when 20-foot waves started crashing toward it.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is some of the things that we are seeing here in this area, a lot of flooding, a lot of high waves, we`ve got the winds here, and a lot of businesses have their areas boarded up to try to keep the water out but the businesses that we are going to be passing here just a moment now, they`ve all been boarded up for days because it`s just not safe. A lot of the windows have been broken and it`s just a mess, really. People are trying to clean up now.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to show you just a little bit here. This is the famed cell does if you come to Capitola ever visited here, this is where people like to bring their families come to have a glass of wine. We took a look inside, a piece of that wharf that we just showed you is now sitting in the dining room inside this restaurant.\par \par \uc2 And come take a look over here. This is some of the debris. This is indicative of the debris that is washing ashore, this huge log here. We`re seeing this up and down littered across Capitola Beach. This trashcan here, this dumpster is filled with debris. There`s another one just feet away.\par \par \uc2 And as you can see, this entire area has been roped off because it`s just too dangerous for people to come here. It`s unclear exactly how long these businesses will be shuttered. But the thing that people are really hoping for is for the rain to stop so that California can dry out. And that`s the good news. The remainder of January for the most part is expected to be dry and that`s going to help these businesses try to rebuild, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dana Griffin, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 President Biden puts voting rights back in the spotlight but any hope of delivering on one of his biggest campaign promises faces a major obstacle, a Republican-led House. Massive search and rescue efforts underway for Ukrainians trapped in the rubble following Russia`s deadliest civilian strike in months. We`ll get the latest from Kyiv. And the highway now runs through one of America`s oldest black neighborhoods and the birthplace of Jazz, why communities like this may finally have a chance to rebuild. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING Reports only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For the second day in a row, President Biden is urging Americans to remember Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  .`s fight for justice and racial equality, as he and Vice President Harris sound a clarion call.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We face another inflection point in our nation`s history. Well, that`s going to determine what this country looks like several decades from now. You know this is a time for choosing. When we choose democracy over autocracy or community over chaos, love or hate, these are the questions of our time that I ran for president and tried to help answer that Dr. King`s life and legacy in my view, show us the way forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That was just moments ago. The president joining the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  at the National Action network`s MLK breakfast after becoming the first sitting president to speak during a Sunday service at Kings Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta yesterday. But with a GOP-controlled House, is there any chance of fulfilling his campaign promise to protect to expand voting rights? Let`s bring in NBC`s Carol Lee, and Edie Glaude, the chair of the African American Studies at \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  and an NBC News contributor. Good to have both of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Carol, President Biden did call for Congress to pass the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act in his speech today, but we all know what the political realities are. Talk about the White House strategy, and frankly, their realistic expectations.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Look, Chris, once the Republicans took control of the House, the idea of getting voting rights passed was even dimmer than it was when Democrats had control of the House. And there was a 50-50 Senate with the Democrats being in control there. This is something that the president a year ago really stepped to the plate and gave a full- throated endorsement of.\par \par \uc2 He said he would support abolishing the filibuster in the Senate to get voting rights legislation passed, it didn`t happen. And that was with the Democratic-controlled Congress. And so, what you`re seeing this year, a year later, is the president making that pitch, saying it`s something he still supports, and he still wants to see done, and he`ll continue to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 But it wasn`t really the centerpiece of what we heard the president talk about in the past two days. Those themes were largely focused on what you might think he would talk about it, a 2024 reelection campaign. He talked about this battle for the soul of the nation. That`s been his theme. The president has been hitting since running for the presidency in 2020.\par \par \uc2 He also then talked about today at a length of his -- for about his economic policies and how he and his view they have benefited black Americans talking about low unemployment rate among black Americans, efforts to bring about equity and housing, talking about his nomination of \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And so, what you heard from the president is that he wants voting rights passed. I think he`ll -- this the White House is very realistic about that not going -- that`s not going to happen. And so, the argument he`s -- that he`s making, particularly in his studio audience today is, hey, I told you, if you had my back, I would have yours. Here`s all the way that I -- ways that I have now can stick with me here, and this is something voting rights and other civil rights legislation that we can try to pursue in the future.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol, thank you for that. You know, Edie, the intersection of race and democracy has always been complicated. And there are a couple of things that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said yesterday and today that I want to get your take.\par \par \uc2 I mean, one is, you just heard him say we`re at an inflection point. I think we`ve been saying that for years. If we`ve been saying it for years, is it truly an inflection point? But he also said, there`s always hope. And I wonder if you agree with that, and if so, where do you find it?\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I find -- first of all, it`s just wonderful to see you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You too.\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: I find hope in us that everyday ordinary human beings could find the courage and the will to figure out how to live together differently, how to feel -- live together, how to -- how to be otherwise. But you know, we`ve always been in an inflection point when it comes to race in the country. You know you can go back to Federalist 54, I spoke as Madison was trying to justify the three-fifths clause in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 We can think about what happened in the context of the Civil War and radical reconstruction, and the ways in which we betrayed the promise of a multiracial democracy. And we see, look at this now, just 50 years after the passage of the Voting Rights Act in 1965, here we are battling the -- around the issue of voting once again, which is an echo of the battle around the 15th Amendment, which was an echo around the battle of how do we account for these people who are at once human beings uncharted. So, it is -- it is the contradiction that runs through -- as the throughlines, a contradiction, and there`s a throughline of this fragile experiment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, when you think about if you have hope and -- look, a lot of people when you ask, so what can the average American do who is concerned about this issue, you`re often told, you know, you vote. But beyond that, you know, I look to people like Shaye Moss, right, and her mom, Ruby Freeman, who stood up for what is important that what happened in there -- at her voting site, the way that her mom, what she endured, all of those things, serve as an example. But for the average American, who cares about this issue beyond voting? What can they do, Eddie? What can they do?\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: It`s such a hard question. And, you know, I was -- I was thinking about a speech that Dr. King delivered at Vanderbilt law school in 1962, and it was entitled the ethics of desegregate -- the ethics of integration -- the ethics of integration. And what he does in his speech, Chris, he makes a distinction between desegregation and integration. And he says that desegregation is a short-term -- is a negative formulation. It`s a short- term goal. Integration is the full recognition of the security of personality, the dignity and standing of every human being where we live a life fully together.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, we are walking mysteries to each other. And the intimacies of our hatred, right in those segregated spaces that allow hatred and fear, and grievance to metastasize. King said in the speech, he says, we are -- we have physical proximity without spiritual affinity. We`re physically desegregated but spiritually segregated. And he says, if we don`t fundamentally integrate this society, we will find ourselves over and over again, dealing with this contradiction.\par \par \uc2 So, what do we do in our everyday lives? We have to figure out how to be together differently. We got to commit ourselves to just -- we Have to commit ourselves to justice, broadly but we also have to break down the barriers that keep us as mysteries to one another, Chris. And I think if we live up to Dr. King`s legacy, in this moment, we addressed the structural barriers, but we also have to address these spiritual barriers that continue to produce right the hatred that over --that threatens to overwhelm our country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All of us with different kinds of marching orders on this day when we remember \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  . and the many ways he marched and change this country. Edie Glaude, as always, always an honor to have you on the program. Come back soon, will you?\par \par \uc2 GLAUDE: Indeed. It`s great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Russian missiles raining down in Dnipro, killing dozens. We`ve got the latest on the desperate search for survivors.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:04]\par \par \uc2 And an update on what`s now being called the deadliest plane crash in 30 years. All of that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Forty people including three children are dead, and a massive search and rescue operation is under way. People locally say 550 people are involved in that rescue after an apartment building was hit by a Russian missile strike on Saturday. By Ukraine`s count 72 apartments destroyed, 164 others damaged from a missile known as an aircraft carrier killer.\par \par \uc2 Once again, Russia faces accusations of war crimes, but President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  told Russian TV everything is going according to plan.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Matt Bradley is in Kyiv for us.\par \par \uc2 What`s the latest you`re seeing and hearing on the ground? What`s the word on those search and rescue efforts, Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, I mean, what we`re actually hearing which surprised me is that this search and rescue effort is actually still ongoing, more than 48 hours after this missile struck. So the last time we actually saw someone being pulled out alive from this horrific scene was yesterday.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 But it seems as though there`s still a sliver of hope, and rescue workers are still trying their best even though we`ve heard from some of them saying they really don`t believe that anybody could still be left alive in that building. So, still, they`re trying to rescue people. It`s not just a search. It`s an actually rescue effort that is still going on.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, you know, this is going to rank among probably one of the most lethal Russian air strikes against the civilian population here in Ukraine since this war began nearly 11 months ago. And that`s why, you know, as you mentioned, the Kremlin spoke, they addressed this issue. They actually said that this wasn`t their doing. They said that they didn`t target this building. And we heard from -- I think it was Dmitry Peskov implied that this was a missile that was intercepted by Ukrainian air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 Now I`ve been around here in the capital and elsewhere in the country, and I`m seeing some of the (INAUDIBLE) that`s fallen from intercepted missiles. And really it`s very small. It really is kind of like trash falling from the sky. And that can hurt property and it can kill people. But when you look at that wreckage, it really calls into question what the Kremlin is saying. They almost certainly launched a missile right into that apartment building. This was not the falling debris from a missile that was intercepted. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Matt Bradley, thank you for that update. We do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 After 30 years on the run, Italy`s most-wanted man, mafia boss Matteo Messina Denaro was arrested this morning at a private health clinic in Sicily. Police say the 60-year-old ordered some of that nation`s most brutal killings including of two prosecutors and is thought to be the leader of the notorious Cosa Nostra organized crime syndicate.\par \par \uc2 Officials believe he continued to run the organization even while in hiding all of those years. When caught, police say, he was in line for a COVID shot and briefly and unsuccessfully tried to flee.\par \par \uc2 New warnings from the U.S. Treasury as Congress prepares for what could be a messy battle over the looming debt ceiling. How this could affect every American from savings to mortgage payments. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:14]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Officials have now recovered the black boxes from the plane that crashed in Nepal just moments before it was supposed to land with no sign of any survivors among the 72 people onboard. A national day of mourning has been declared in the country in the wake of the worst airplane crash in three decades. The government has set up a panel to investigate the cause. The weather was good, the pilot did not report anything untoward as the plane approached the airport.\par \par \uc2 Meantime here at home, Congress is preparing for what is expected to be a major and bitter battle over the debt ceiling. As Treasury Secretary Yellen warns, the country is just days away from hitting the debt limit and that defaulting could cause irreparable harm to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 But what exactly happens? Well, researchers at a think tank Third Way estimated last month the U.S. could lose roughly three million jobs. Individual Americans could lose thousands in 401(k) savings and those looking for a new 30-year mortgage could end up paying an additional $130,000.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in "USA Today" Washington bureau chief Susan Page. David Jolly is back with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Congressman, I have a suspicion the debt ceiling is not high on Americans` list of what keeps them up at night. In fact I often get asked what the heck is it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY (R), FORMER FLORIDA REPRESENTATIVE: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But we just went over a few of the ways that potential inaction could hurt the American people. How serious could the consequences be?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: It could be very serious if Republicans in the House decide they`re willing to rattle world markets to score some type of political point. So very importantly, Chris, you know, we all know government shutdowns. That`s a failure to fund the government on an annual basis. This is different. The debt limit increase is something that Congress has to pass by law to allow us to take on more debt and pay our bills. It is akin to paying your credit card company.\par \par \uc2 So if you fail to do that, our government stays open but we begin to default on the debt we owe to other people, and ultimately it rattles world markets, it jeopardizes the credit worthiness of the United States. Lenders start charging us more money and you see economic implications you just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy promised this hard-right caucus that he was not going to agree to simply lifting the debt limit. The problem is, he really can`t honor that promise. He only controls the House. Democrats control the Senate and the White House. And there are those even in McCarthy`s caucus who just want to have a clean debt limit lift and not rattle world markets. So Kevin McCarthy very likely will have to break one of his early promises to the Freedom Caucus.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But, and I think you just answered it, but let me play devil`s advocate here and say what conservative Republicans -- it`s not just conservative Republicans. They say at some point we have to get the spending under control. At some point we just have to stop this. So what`s the answer to it?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Stop giving away tax cuts and don`t spend so much money during the Trump administration. I mean, look, both sides of the aisle like to spend money and Republicans have every bit as much a culpability as Democrats do. There is no more conservative position in the country, in your household or in government than paying your bills, than paying the money that you said you would. That is a point of government integrity and it should be a conservative position.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are going to misrepresent the dynamics of this and they also ultimately will declare victory even though they`re going to suffer defeat in this debate. So I don`t think they`ll tell the American people that they didn`t get done what they wanted to get done, but they`re not going to get done what they say they`re going to get done.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, your paper, Susan, "USA Today" is out with that new poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  . It found that 58 percent of Americans say it seems unlikely that Republicans will do any compromising with Democrats in the next two and a half years.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:08]\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. Will this debt limit fight be any different just because of the enormity of what would happen if it doesn`t get done? Are the stakes just too high not to find compromise? Talk to me about that poll and how it might play in here?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2  WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF: Well, we found very little expectation of things getting done in Congress over the next two years based on what Americans saw with the long battle over just the first step of electing a speaker. Not usually the hardest thing Congress has to do. So I think this does not bode well for even such political action as raising the debt limit because it does involve compromise.\par \par \uc2 You know, we think it`s not hard to see the White House and the Democratic- controlled Senate agreeing on raising the debt limit but you have to have the House go along as well, and that the House is under narrow Republican control with an empowered Freedom Caucus that has vowed not to allow the debt ceiling to be raised unless there are steps taken to control spending. So somebody is -- that is an area that where it`s hard to reach compromise and the fact is, neither side is willing to give much to the other side.\par \par \uc2 And we saw, you know, just in 2011, when we approached the debt ceiling crisis how it rattled the market, caused real economic consequences, and in that case we didn`t even breach the debt. We may go further than that this time around.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You think so? You think it`s possible it could go beyond then?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I think it`s possible, sure. I mean, they`ve got -- the extraordinary measures start with the Treasury Department, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said Thursday. She`ll have some week where she can just manipulate the numbers to avoid a crisis but the time will come maybe in early June when the rubber hits the road, and given that it took 15 ballots to elect a speaker, I don`t know why we would expect this to be a smooth process to raise the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I mean, that does raise a good point, David. I mean, we called it at the top. A major and bitter battle. Is there any doubt that it`s going to be that?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: No. And Republicans are going to take this all the way to the end because, again, they`re kind of playing a losing hand. They don`t have the ability to get everything they want, but they can try to squeeze out some concessions. The question is, are there more than five House Republicans that are going to blink, and say, look, Kevin, you cannot take this all the way to the brink and risk defaulting on our debt.\par \par \uc2 I know there are more than five House Republicans that just want to lift the debt limit. Do they have the courage to take on the speaker on this? That would mean some type of Democratic-Republican coalition to get it done. Ultimately this probably has Republican and Democratic votes at the end because it has to, but it`s going to be a painful process. Is that pain all internal in terms of American politics or does it get externalized into world markets? That`s the risk, that`s the question we don`t know the answer to yet.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Susan Page, David Jolly, thank you both. I feel like we had this conversation year after year, and yet here we are again. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 All right. We`re talking big numbers, we`re talking money. There`s a new report out from Oxfam. It says -- I want you to listen to this. The richest 1 percent of people, the richest 1 percent, have accumulated almost two- thirds of the $42 trillion in new wealth created over the last two years. So here`s another way to put it. Crisp $1, which I was handed. For every $1 of new global wealth earned by a person in the bottom 90 percent, a billionaire gained roughly $1.7 million. $1 to $1.7 million.\par \par \uc2 The historically black Treme community of New Orleans is one of hundreds across the country intentionally destroyed by a highway project. Now this community may finally have a way to build back some of what they lost. We`ve got that story for you, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So there was no effort to maintain. How are we going to keep this community vibrant? How are we going to keep it going? How can we put it on life support?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Destruction is destruction, whether you use a bomb or whether you use cement, you come out with the same end result.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:10]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The trillion-dollar infrastructure bill could be a source of vindication for historically black New Orleans communities.\par \par \uc2 Take an aerial view, look at it right now, of the Treme neighborhood. For decades it thrived with black music, black food, black businesses. But in the `60s, Treme and thousands of other black and brown communities across the country were intentionally targeted and uprooted for a highway project.\par \par \uc2 For Treme, that meant physically breaking apart the vibrant spaces along iconic Claiborne Avenue. As "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " puts it, quote, "The elevated stretch of Interstate 10 has been cast as the villain that robbed the historic African-American community." In fact, locals nicknamed it the monster.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Trymaine Lee from New Orleans` Treme neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you. I know you know this neighborhood well. What could the infrastructure bill do to help build back the Treme community, and frankly what might it all also not be able to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know what, Chris? That indeed is the billion-dollar question. What could be done to monstrosities like this. About that big trillion-dollar infrastructure bill, more than a billion is going to cities to help them reconnect, right. To help communities that have been divided by infrastructure or racism, help them reconnect.\par \par \uc2 But here in New Orleans, generations of New Orleanians here in Treme, a very historic black neighborhood have dealt with this symbol of what many believe is structural racism. Now there are people in this community who hope that some money could go to tearing this down. Others say at least beautify it. But one thing is clear for the folks I spoke with, something has to be done. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRED JOHNSON, NEIGHBORHOOD DEVELOPMENT ASSOCIATION CEO: It affects the psyche and the behavior of those who of us that`s got to engage with it day in and day out, whether you walk in it or whether you are driving by it.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 It affects your psyche. You know what I mean? And for someone like me, who is born and reared in this area, and saw the glaringly difference, it`s painful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: The psychological weight of bearing witness to this monstrosity year after year, there finally is some hope, but it still is a bit of a contentious issue here on the ground. Folks have been coming together, though, as a community that takes some agency in trying to determine the future of not just the I-10 but their communities.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Trymaine Lee, thank you very much. And really, that`s just sort of a little tease because tomorrow, Trymaine, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and Chris Hayes will host "NATIONAL DAY OF RACIAL HEALING," an MSNBC town hall live from New Orleans. They`ll discuss the recent rise in hate speech across America and how we can make strides toward becoming a more just and equitable society. Tomorrow it airs at 7:00 p.m. on Telemundo, then at 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC. You can also watch it streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time, right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 The day was January 9th, 2023. President Biden woke up to continuing headlines about a Republican . with a speaker who could barely get the gavel giving everything up but his shoes --\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230117ej1g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 10, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Mike Memoli, Ken Dilanian, Cristobal Alex, Marissa  Parra, Ali Vitali, Mark Leibovich, Tom Winter, Ryan Nobles, Brendan Buck,  Catie Beck \par \uc2 8,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Today, as President Biden meets with world leaders abroad, he has something of a new headache at home, a Justice Department investigation into classified material found in the office connected to the president. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has assigned the case to a U.S. attorney appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but that didn`t stop the former president from asking when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  plans to raid the White House.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a major legislative victory for the new Speaker of the House. But can Kevin McCarthy corral his GOP caucus into actually governing? And the emotional vigil for a heroic teacher who got her kids out of the classroom after she was shot by a student. We`ll have the good news from her doctors.\par \par \uc2 But let`s go live to Mexico, which is where we see President Biden, he is meeting along with his wife with President \uc2 Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2  and his wife. There, you see the two first ladies who are walking that -- shot freezing a little bit, but Mike Memoli is there traveling with the president in Mexico City. We also have NBC News justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian, Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney, and former deputy campaign -- former Deputy Assistant Attorney General, and Cristobal Alex, a former senior adviser to the Biden campaign who worked on the Biden-Harris transition. He is also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 As we watch them go up the stairs, Mike, tell us exactly what we`re seeing. And I assume we just missed what is often, you know, the formal photo.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chris. Literally, just in the minutes before we came on the air here, we saw the Presidential motorcade arrive at the Palacio Nacional here in downtown Mexico City, you can actually see over my right shoulder the white tent, which the beast pulled up to where President Biden entered, and then quickly assembled with the president of Mexico and the Prime Minister of Canada for that family photo. Now, over the next few hours, these three leaders and their teams will be hunkered down for this 10th North American Leaders Summit. This is the first time a sitting president as we know has come to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 This summit was something of a hiatus during the Trump administration but President Biden has tried to revive the summit, in part, as we`ve seen over the last week now, to use it as a forum to address what has been one of the main political liabilities for him, which is the migrant surge at the southern border where these three leaders are going to be announcing today some ramped up efforts to coordinate to try to deal with some of these root causes including the launching of a virtual portal, they`ll call it, which is going to be a sort of one shop clearinghouse for migrants who want to come to Mexico to the United States to Canada from other parts of the hemisphere here to lay out what are the legal pathways for them to do that. This has really been part of an effort on the part of the U.S. in particular to try to stem the flow to the border by making it clear the consequences of coming here through legal means versus illegal means of what the process is for claiming asylum and the like.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, overshadowing now this summit is this revelation, of course, of the classified documents that were found at what was then the former vice president`s office in Washington. We have a new statement from the White House in which they sort of speak to I think the fact that there`s very limited information that they`ve been able to or willing to confirm so far. And I`ll read part of it to you now. It`s from Ian Sams, a spokesperson who says that this is an ongoing process under review by the Justice Department, and therefore, we are limited in what we can say about it. But we are committed to doing this the right way, and we will provide further details when and as appropriate.\par \par \uc2 So, Chris, it stands to reason that the other two leaders here, of course, understand -- seen press reports about this. We know that some of the many senior officials on the part of the White House who are traveling with him likely have been distracted somewhat from the business of the summit here as they`ve been preparing the president for a moment later today, where we may see him asked and have to respond for the first time on camera to these questions about what exactly he was learned of this discovery, and what more he might be able to say about what those documents are and how they were in his possession still years after he had left the White House as vice president. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much for that. So, Ken, we just heard Mike mentioned that the Justice Department is examining this small number of documents found at a Biden thing take. What more do we know about them, what they are exactly, when and exactly where they were found, and how the Justice Department is responding?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Chris, the White House just has not been transparent about the nature of these documents, the level of classification, which I think are very important questions. And by the way, there`s no reason they can`t talk about this, despite the fact that the Justice Department is reviewing it. They`re choosing not to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But what is true is that Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has referred this matter to the U.S. attorney in Chicago, who is a Trump appointee. And that was explicitly done because he`s a Trump appointee to sort of maintain public confidence in the independence of this review. It`s a review. It`s not a criminal investigation. He`s trying to determine whether a criminal investigation needs to be undertaken. And if that determination is made, then presumably we would see the appointment of a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no reason to think that that would happen in this case if the White House account is correct, which essentially is that these documents were packed accidentally and inadvertently, and were turned over as soon as they were discovered back six days before the midterm. Of course, that doesn`t answer the question of why the White House sat on this information. And President Biden himself who was informed sat on this information for two months. And we`re only hearing about it now. But the Justice Department is telling us that they are continuing this review and that no conclusions have been reached in, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Cristobal, you are a member of the Biden-Harris transition team. And I guess a lot of people have this idea, and I think it may be a reasonable idea that classified materials are handled very carefully. How does something like this happen?\par \par \uc2 CRISTOBAL ALEX, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right now, it`s unclear how it happened. Certainly, there was a mistake made it appears. And you`re exactly right, during transition, but especially during the White House days that I was there, this was something that was taken with the utmost seriousness. Of course, we`re seeing a very big difference in what the Biden White House is doing around this and what Trump has done, right?\par \par \uc2 From what we know right now, there were fewer than a dozen documents found at the Pen Center, which is a beautiful facility in Washington, without a lot of public access to. It very much unlike a hotel, where you have hundreds, if not thousands of people coming through. And I commend the administration for very quickly, ensuring that a Trump holdover, the lawyer from the Northern District of Illinois, who`s going to be handling this and reviewing the matters, so I think they`re going to be very transparent about it. It`ll be very unlike the way the Trump team is handling it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Harry, there are two aspects to something like this, right, all the time. One of them is the public perception. And we`ve already heard some Republicans and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in particular coming out and making all kinds of accusations. But from a legal standpoint, help us with a comparison between the Biden and the Trump situations. From a legal standpoint, how is this different from the classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Night and day. You have the inadvertent -- we don`t even know if Biden had anything to do with it, retention of some documents, and from that point, they do everything by the book. And by the way, I really want to take down the temperature of this DOJ investigation. It just happens routinely you find these docs, you`re supposed to, Trump didn`t, but Biden did and tell the National Archives, they take possession, they inform the DOJ, and Garland puts a Trump U.S. attorney on it.\par \par \uc2 From -- by contrast, of course, Trump is involved in taking them in the first place. They don`t tell the archives until the Archives discovered it. And then there`s a whole series of both deception and bobbing and weaving.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the potential crime here has to do with obstruction that is with not responding to an investigation and trying to obscure it. That`s the exact opposite of what Biden`s done. So, it`s a talking point, basically, for the intellectually dishonest. They`re going to keep making it for sure because of the surface similarity, but as a matter of law, night and day, not a chance in the world that Biden will see criminal charges here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So -- yes, as a matter of law moving forward, what`s going to happen, Harry?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Right. So, the Chicago U.S. attorney will look into it and try to figure out if is there any possible basis for a criminal charge. That wouldn`t mean, for example, they purposely took it knowing they weren`t supposed to, or they -- well, there`s nothing you can say, of course, about trying to obstruct an investigation, they turned it over right away. And then in due course, having looked look through it, they the Chicago U.S. attorney will report to the attorney general, almost certainly to say there`s nothing more to be done here. And that`ll be the end of the matter.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know how long it`ll take. And we don`t know when or if Garland will right away make that public. But I just want to emphasize, nobody of any party here would think there`s any kind of whiff of a crime unless there`s something we really don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In fact, we`ve heard from some high-profile Republicans saying they don`t think that there`s necessarily anything wrong here. But what they`re looking at is President Biden being treated differently than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and we`ll leave that to the Harry Litman`s and to the legal folks. But I want to go back to the -- to sort of the central question. What`s it like, let`s say in the middle of a transition, is it organized canopy frenetic? I still trying to figure out why this ended up. Not implying anything bad, just saying, how could this happen?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Yes, it`s a great question. And from my -- from my understanding, this happens sometime between 2017 and 2018, not during the presidential transition itself. Big difference there. But here`s the other piece of this, which is, this White House takes security and confidentiality to an extreme. If you want to review these sorts of documents in the White House, you`re going into a skiff, a secure room, you`re not taking your electronic devices with you, and it`s something that they pay very close attention to.\par \par \uc2 And as the guest just said, there seems to be a night and day difference here between the way Trump handled this and really ignored subpoenas, really hid documents, versus what this White House is doing, which is a very transparent approach to it. And, you know, unfortunately, the other side is going to make as much hay out of this as they possibly can until it fades away. And I assume \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is already doing whatever he can on social media and elsewhere to distract from his own problems and putting them on the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Mike, if you want to look at differences in that statement that you read from the White House, there was no suggestion that those documents belong to him as we saw with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . But let me ask you beyond that, is there anything you`re hearing from the folks traveling with the president, on his staff that shows you there`s a level of concern about how this is playing politically?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, I think the first and foremost concern of the White House right now is that this story is covered with the sort of specificity and nuance that it requires. And we`re certainly doing our best to do that. This is the efforts that are underway beyond the ability of them to answer questions about the specific documents here is largely about trying to make sure it`s presented in the full context.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, Chris, we are sort of looking back now at a period over the last few weeks in which President Biden had arguably, some of the best weeks of his presidency coming off that better than expected midterm elections, the trip he took overseas to the G 20 Summit, to the dysfunction that we`ve seen on the part of House Republicans in efforts to elect a speaker, and what we saw last week with President Biden appearing with the leader of Senate Republicans touting some of his legislative wins. And so, in that respect, I think the White House and some of the people I`ve been speaking with are very frustrated about the idea that this is now throwing them off what had been probably one of their best periods of the presidency so far.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Cristobal, that brings us back to the border, right? I mean, we saw -- earlier, I was listening to the comments made by President Biden and by Macron and -- I`m sorry, by the -- by Canadian`s Trudeau -- Canada`s Trudeau, talking about their relationship. Trudeau calling him Joe, the friendship, sort of the picture of here`s what international relationship should look like, right? But the problem at the border, realistically, is there anything in these conversations that can help with what absolutely is a political problem for this president on the Mexican border?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Absolutely, there is. And I was heartened to see the president landing in Air Force One in my hometown of El Paso on his way to Mexico City. Of course, El Paso is a city of immigrants. It`s a city -- it`s a binational city. And unfortunately, right now, they`re under a lot of pressure with the migrant surge that we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 And so, the new policy that the president has released together with Homeland Security is an important one. And the combination that they`re having now, really dealing with regional security, stabilization, root causes of migration, all of that will be absolutely critical to stemming that flow. And importantly, as the president has laid out, once Title 42 is lifted, which will eventually happen, there will be increased pathways to legal migration for these asylum seekers and others. So, it`s an important time there. This is the first time a U.S. president has been in Mexico in nine years. So, a lot on the line here at very high stakes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But can I ask you -- we`re really out of time, but as somebody who knows El Paso so well, what goes through your mind when you see those pictures of folks sleeping on the streets, kids slipping on the streets?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: It`s heartbreaking. It really is. And I also remember when Jill Biden, Dr. Biden, the wife of the president, went to the border, crossed the border, and fed those migrants Christmas dinner. So, there`s an empathy and understanding in this administration.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: First of all, Alex, thank you for being here. Mike, Memoli, Ken Dilanian, Harry Litman, thank you all so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Well, before leaving for Mexico, President Biden approved an emergency declaration for California where now at least 14 people are dead and a five-year-old is missing from those intense storms and cyclones. Officials rescued the little boy`s mother from a creek bed, but have called off the search for him because of the extreme weather. Thousands of people have been forced to flee their homes. And with another dangerous storm system on the way, more than 32 million in Los Angeles remain under flash flood warnings.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Marissa Parra is in Aptos, California. So, Marissa, I`ll tell you, the pictures we`re seeing are unbelievable. What are you seeing on the ground, and how concerned are the people you`re talking to?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MARISS PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, first, I will say you`re seeing probably some sunshine, and it looks like just another beautiful sunny day here in California. But when you zoom out, you get a fuller picture here. We`re standing in the middle of a very much flooded street here. This is Aptos, California, as you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the reasons why you don`t really want to be going through the flood water as much as possible. There`s a lot of gross things that are floating around here. So, we`re trying to stay above the floodwaters as much as possible, hard to do when most of the streets are flooded out.\par \par \uc2 So, you have residents, you have businesses around here that have been damaged as a result. And when it comes to how people are feeling, I think it`s somewhere between a mixture of this despair, for one. There`s a lot of people in despair right now. But then there are people who say, well, we`ve been through this before, we`ve been through natural disasters, many of them telling me about times where they went through an earthquake or a wildfire or a different flooding at a different time. And so, there is some level of acceptance with that too.\par \par \uc2 Just to give you a little perspective, this is the Pacific Ocean right here behind me. We were told that at one point during the surge at the storms worse, that water was just cascading over this brick wall over here. And then, of course, leading to the very flooded streets over here that we were just standing in front of.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned that five-year-old boy that was swept away in the floodwaters. I mean, when it comes to the search and rescue efforts and recovery efforts, we do know that it depends on where you are in California. So, it is sunny here right now but as our meteorologists have been saying, this is going to be a situation where it`s sunny one minute and then raining another. So, it may be fine right now, but that rain might be coming within an hour. So, this could look very different.\par \par \uc2 We know that in other parts of California, especially further north in the Bay Area, it is currently thunder storming pretty hard right now. So, that is making things very difficult. People are trying to clean up. We`ve seen people trying to put sandbags in place and trying to sweep away or maybe scoop up the mud. But as they`re doing so, it rains about half an hour later. We saw one guy doing that in the middle of the rain.\par \par \uc2 And so, on top of that, you have 200,000 Californians without power. These crews -- these emergency crews, and then furthermore, these crews who work on restoring power, tree removal services have been working around the clock to try to restore power as people try their hardest to get back to normal life while we no more storms are on the way. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa, you`ve been doing such great reporting out there. Thank you so much. We appreciate you and your team.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy scores his first legislative win as a speaker, but where does his slim and fractured majority go from here? Plus, former Trump org CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  will likely be headed to prison, what we expect at that sentencing hearing that set to begin just one hour from now. And new details about the heroic teacher who led her students to safety after being shot by a six-year-old. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: California Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  , who just won her third term in the House announcing today she intends to run for the state`s 2024 Senate seat. Here`s part of the new video she posted to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2 , (D-CA): California needs a warrior in Washington to win these fights. It`s time for new leadership in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, sitting Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  who is 89, put out a statement saying everyone is, of course, welcome to throw their hat in the ring, and I will make an announcement concerning my plans for 2024 at the appropriate time.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Kevin McCarthy scored a major legislative victory last night. With only four Republican votes to spare, the House did vote along party lines to approve the new rules package. Here`s how the AP put it. It was an effective start to what could otherwise be a new era of potentially crisis governing after "last week`s raucous speaker`s race."\par \par \uc2 The GOP House Majority also passed its first bill. This one, tied to rescinding more than $70 billion in new \uc2 IRS\uc2  funding. It has virtually no chance in the Senate, but Senate Majority Leader Schumer says he is not rolling out other opportunities to work across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  , (D-NY): I intend to sit down with him and with Leader McConnell because we want to get things done in a bipartisan way to help the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, staff writer at The Atlantic, Mark Leibovich, he`s also the author of the book, Thank You For Your Servitude, Donald Trump`s Washington and The Price Of Submission. Also with us, the former aide to speakers, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  , Brendan Buck.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, very early this morning, our friend, Jonathan Lemire, "WAY TOO EARLY," predicted we should get ready "for a lot of Republican-on- Republican political violence."\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Where does the majority -- the new majority go from here?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Look, it`s probably not way too early to make that kind of a prediction here, Chris, because that`s what this speaker fight has shown us is that we`re probably in for way more of what it took to just constitute this Congress. Because again, electing a speaker, getting onto the business of putting committee chairmen and women in place, that`s supposed to be the easy part of the beginning of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 And we know that McCarthy made concessions during this process that are likely going to make it more difficult for him to govern. And power was taken away from the Speaker`s office and given more to the rank and file. That was the entire point for many of these hardline conservatives. Though, if you listen to some of the McCarthy allies who are in leadership who brokered the deal, that`s not what they`re putting out there today. Listen to \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER, (R-MN): This will arguably not make Kevin McCarthy a weaker speaker. This will make Kevin McCarthy perhaps the strongest speaker in modern times, this agreement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Now, look, I`m sure Brendan has his own take on this too, knowing what it takes to be a speaker and what McCarthy gave away. But look, the broad view here on Capitol Hill from folks that I talked to is McCarthy made his job more difficult here by showing these hardliners he will capitulate to the things that they want. Chief among them this idea that it takes one member to now motion to vacate the speakership.\par \par \uc2 On its face, yes, that is something that`s important, but also the fact that that was a red line that McCarthy said he wasn`t going to cross that he crossed it once by lowering that threshold to five, then ultimately, he went to exactly where the Conservatives where, said that only one member would be able to do that. So, again, McCarthy was able to negotiate and get the speakership, but the question is going to be at what cost?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Brendan, Ali has teed you up. Look, we all know that where you start as a speaker doesn`t have to be and often isn`t where you end up. And that can go both ways, right? Up and down. But what is this early start telling you about McCarthy`s strength or lack thereof?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER CHIEF COMMUNICATIONS ADVISER TO FORMER SPEAKER PAUL RYAN: Yes. I think what \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  is getting to is that McCarthy has effectively empowered the rest of the conference. He has said this is going to be a bottom-up process, I`m going to let you offer your amendments, I`m going to let you decide what we`re bringing up. And that works well if you have a conference that is interested in legislating, it is interested in compromise. And that`s where this thing is going to run in trouble because Republican politics right now is purely performative. So, there`s not a whole lot of opportunity for them to be thoughtful policymakers and get things done.\par \par \uc2 The thing that Kevin McCarthy has going for him right now is low expectations and time before they have to actually do anything meaningful. So, it was really important that right before the holidays, Congress passed a long-term spending bill. Kevin McCarthy doesn`t have to worry about funding the government for several months. He doesn`t have to worry about a debt limit increase for several months.\par \par \uc2 So, what he can do now is put up bills like he did this week, purely messaging bills, getting rid of \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, plays with the base, easy, every Republican vote for it. I think you`re going to see a lot of those things over the next several months where he is just stacking up wins, easy things that are going nowhere in the Senate, but allow him to tell his members, look, we`re doing things that are based ones. Because eventually, he`s going to have to cut a deal with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, he`s going to have to find some way to fund that government, do a debt limit increase, and that`s when all this trouble is going to come. That`s when the motion to vacate is probably going to become a problem for him. So, for now, low expectations, not a whole lot he has to do to keep people happy, keep giving them what they want, eventually, that price is going to be paid when you have to do this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He`s worried, of course, Mark. I mean, the first bill from Republicans is based on a widely debunked lie about the \uc2 IRS\uc2  . I think the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  fact-checker just made it four Pinocchios. And, you know, the Senate`s not going to pass legislation eviscerating the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , as we heard from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  earlier today. But can he make that turn? Is the turn possible when you look at, frankly, what the core base originally, the 20 people who are against Kevin McCarthy, the kind of power they yielded against another 200?\par \par \uc2 MARK LEIBOVICH, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. I mean, I think it could clearly the expectation game is I guess for a very brief period of time, Kevin McCarthy`s friend. I think after last week, he -- it was sort of a big screaming reminder to everyone in the caucus of how bad of a look this is if it continues at least for the time being. So, I think in some ways, McCarthy had kind of a built-in argument for it like, let`s not continue this into next week, let`s get the rules thing, at least off the decks, and you know, do some pretty safe and easy base plays, as Brendan said, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , you know, a bill that`s obviously not going anywhere. But it`s a much -- you know, I think a lot more of the Republican base cares a lot about, you know, the annoyance of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  than they do about Washington Post Pinocchios at this point.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I mean, Kevin McCarthy is a solicitous speaker. I mean, there are iron-fisted speakers, there are solicited speakers, and Kevin McCarthy, for a lot of reasons, is going to solicit whatever it takes to get his very, very narrow majority to sort of stand by him and keep him the job in the same way that he was very solicitous of President Trump and very famous and infamous kind of ways involving candy, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 So, look, this is how he`s going to approach it. It`s going to be kind of a week-to-week thing or day-to-day thing. And it looks like at least for the first two days of this week, he`s going to survive. And as Brendan said, again -- I mean, it`s going to be -- it`s going to get a lot harder, you know, fairly soon, but at least he`ll have a little bit of breathing room first.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Mark, but when you look at the House Republicans` promises, they`re voting today on a resolution to create a special Judiciary Subcommittee on what they call the weaponization of the federal government that could include reviewing investigations into former President Trump. So, I mean, does other stuff get done if you`re doing so much of that?\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are folks on the Hill and you could not if you`re -- if you`re among them, any of you that this is going to occupy a huge amount of time of what gets done there, Mark?\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Yes, absolutely. And again, the keyword is performative. I mean, this is a very large critical mass of the Republican caucus right now does not care about the respect of their colleagues, does not care about the committee assignments, does not care about actually passing bills. They care about things that can actually kind of demagogue issues around the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , around, you know, weaponization of the Justice Department, things that the base is extremely passionate about.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I mean, getting things done is relative and also you have to define it in terms of, is getting things done, you know, quite to member X, Y and Z getting on \uc2 FOX\uc2  , or is getting things done actually meaning passing a bill? I think in much -- you know, in ways that we might not fully appreciate yet, I think it much more is defined by the former.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Mark Leibovich, thank you so much. And Brendan, you`re sticking around.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got sentencing in just about 45 minutes or so for former Trump Org CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  in the company`s 15-year tax fraud scheme. We`ll be live at the New York City courthouse where he`s likely to face a prison sentence, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got breaking news from Capitol Hill. Senate Intel Committee chairman \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  is calling for his committee to be briefed on the classified documents found in an office used by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali. If I`m remembering right, this is consistent. He wanted a briefing like this on Mar-a-Lago, too, right?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s exactly right, Chris. And he didn`t end up getting that, whether it be for scheduling issues or because other members on his committee weren`t prioritizing it as much. But, look, Warner has been urging members to get a briefing on what happened with the Mar-a-Lago documents. Now he`s saying he wants a similar briefing about the nature of the documents found in an office that President Biden once used.\par \par \uc2 And look, I`ll read a portion of this statement to you because what Warner says is -- this is a quote, "From what we know so far, the latter is about finding documents with markings and turning them over, which is certainly different from a month`s long effort to retain material actively sought by the federal government." So, again, drawing the distinction between what Biden did which is finding documents and turning them over versus what Trump allegedly did, which is keeping the documents and only turning them over when the feds effectively had to go get them.\par \par \uc2 But here we`re seeing an instance where consistency is key. And the Senate`s first reaction on this, even as they`re not in the building working, clearly hoping to be apprised of this situation further, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I think they`re not back until the end -- near the end of January.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But just to be clear, maybe stating the obvious, this absolutely is within the purview of this committee.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Definitely, in the same way that Mar-a-Lago`s documents were in the purview of this committee, these documents from President Biden`s office are also within that committee`s purview. And again, it`s why we`re seeing these lawmakers, at least \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  , the first of the Democrats, saying, all right, I requested a briefing on Mar-a-Lago. I want a briefing on this, too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, thank you so much for getting us this breaking news.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got other breaking news because in the next 45 minutes or so, former \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  will be sentenced for his part in a 15-yearlong tax fraud scheme at the company. As part of a plea deal, he testified against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  , which was found guilty of tax fraud last month.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigative correspondent Tom Winter is live outside the courthouse. He`s been covering this case throughout.\par \par \uc2 What kind of sentence are prosecutors asking for here, Tom?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT: Right. So prosecutors here have already asked for and really the judge has already granted their wishes up until a point. So initially they asked for six months. And now they say that they`re at a point where the judge has already said that he`s in a five-month sentence for Weisselberg. So that`s what the judge said back when he pleaded guilty in August. And that`s likely what he`ll get here today.\par \par \uc2 We have no indication from any sort of public filings or any sort of other announcements by the district attorney`s office, for instance, that they`re going to consider any accounts of perjury or that Weisselberg didn`t live up to his obligation to testify truthfully in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  trial. So really this is kind of a forgone conclusion here today in this five- month jail sentence. Five years of probation.\par \par \uc2 And he`s also going to have to pay fines and penalties -- excuse me -- on the money that he did not pay as a result of his unreported income. So that`s where this case stands. It`s different from federal court. And really the only question that appears to be in the air today is whether or not in fact Weisselberg goes to Rikers immediately following the conclusion of this sentencing or if he`s given a date to record in some later time.\par \par \uc2 But basically this boils down to approximately 100 days in jail when you consider time off for good behavior, which he`ll likely get.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom Winter at the courthouse, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 A growing laundry list of investigations into lies told by Congressman George Santos. So will the GOP-led House do anything about it? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:59]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The investigations examining George Santos` actions and comments are growing fast. Take a look at this. Here`s Congressman Dan Goldman and Richie Torres hand-delivering a complaint they just filed with the House Ethics Committee to his door. Already Santos` comments are being investigated by the Nassau County district attorney, the New York state attorney general`s office, federal prosecutors in the Eastern District of New York.\par \par \uc2 And just yesterday an ethics complaint from the \uc2 Campaign Legal Center\uc2  was filed to the Federal Elections Commission. They alleged that \uc2 Santos\uc2  violated federal campaign finance laws by claiming he loaned $705,000 to his campaign, but that it actually came from unknown individuals or corporations.\par \par \uc2 His team has declined to comment to NBC News. And we have yet to hear a response from his lawyer as well. But when will we hear more from Santos ? Here`s what he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Why won`t you answer our questions?\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): I will be addressing the media soon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: When? When is soon?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: On my time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Your time? You`re representing the people of New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles. Brendan Buck is back with us.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, is GOP leadership showing any sign of concern or any sign they might take action, even maybe not giving him committee assignments, or is McCarthy waiting for someone else to take action?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: It`s a great question, Chris. It seems as though George Santos is taking incoming from all different directions. Local prosecutors, the Ethics Committees, the Federal Elections Commission, all of them are looking into his inconsistent background. But the one group that has remained relatively quiet about it is his leaders in the Republican Party, and they`re the ones that have the ability to take the most direction action in the quickest amount of time.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what the new majority leader Steve Scalise said about the situation earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): This is something that`s being handled internally. Obviously there were concerns about what we had heard and so we`re going to have to sit down is and talk to him about it. And that`s something that we`re going to deal with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: So what`s remarkable about Scalise`s comments is that they are concerned about it and they are addressing it, but he also says that they haven`t even sat down to talk to Santos about this. And also pretty remarkably, Kevin McCarthy, the now speaker of the House, has yet to say a single word about the George Santos situation.\par \par \uc2 So to your point, Chris, you know, it`s going to take time for these investigations to play themselves out. Kevin McCarthy could right now, today, say that George Santos is not getting any committee assignments until all this stuff hashes itself out. He has yet to say that at this particular point.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brendan, do you see a point? At what point might Kevin McCarthy see Santos as frankly a not-worth-it distraction?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: Yes. I give him a bit of a break for what he had to get through the speaker vote to resolve this. I do imagine he`s going to have to bring him in relatively soon, have a pretty serious conversation. Look, there has to be some consequences for doing this. You can`t just lie your way into Congress and there`d be no accountability for it.\par \par \uc2 But, look, we know the reality. The majority is very slim. Kevin McCarthy is not going to be interested in ditching one of his pressure few four-seat majority votes. I imagine what he`s going to end up doing is saying this is a matter of the House Ethics Committee to investigate. And he`ll probably reserve taking any action until they come to some conclusion. Now that`s not a great place for George Santos to be either. It`s a bit of a good news, bad news situation.\par \par \uc2 The House Ethics Committee I am certainly will want to look into this, but they tend to take a backseat if they know that there are federal investigations, if DOJ is looking into something. They tend to hit the pause button. So while ethics may be slow to act, that probably means that he`s in more of a criminal situation that he has to be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 I do think the Ethics Committee is probably going to be what pushes him out. They can levy huge fines against him, which we don`t think he may actually have the money to be able to pay. So there`s a lot of ways this could go. I imagine Kevin McCarthy is going to punt a little bit on wanting some of these due process stuff to work out. But we`ve got to remember he`s not going to be willing to throw somebody overboard really quickly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I also want to ask you about the current state of U.S. politics vis-a-vis the fact that there are dozens of members of Congress who are election deniers and if you see a connection to the horrific attack on Brazil`s government buildings by supporters of former President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  . We know President Lula is investigating the attack. 1500 arrests have been made.\par \par \uc2 But Eugene Robinson wrote this in the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  ," quote, "Instead of serving as a model of democracy, the United States has given the world lessons in denying election results and stoking popular disappointment into nihilistic rage." Where does this go from here?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes, you know, it was one of the things that I thought about on that day. You know, it was a building that I worked in for a long time. It`s not just a place where we do work. It`s a symbol not just for this country, but for the rest of the world of how things are supposed to work, how peaceful transfer of power is supposed to work.\par \par \uc2 Now I`m sure there`s a lot of nuances to what happened in Brazil that maybe different than here, but as a country, we lose the moral authority to tell other places that this type of violence and attempts at insurrection are unacceptable when we had it happen on our own Capitol. And so few people were willing to say anything about it. You know, I hate that now we can be in part blamed for things happening in countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 It`s shameful and I think it reminds us how important we need to take seriously what we do because the rest of the world is closely watching us at all times.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Without a doubt. Brendan Buck, thank you. Ryan Nobles, always great to have you on the program as well.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, Arkansas has its first female governor. \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  , Donald Trump`s former White House press secretary, is also the first Arkansas governor in more than 40 years with no experience in elected office. Sanders is following in the footsteps of her father \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  , who was governor for more than a decade and ran for president. She says her focus now is the state, but she`s widely seen as having national political ambitions of her own.\par \par \uc2 New details on the heroic teacher who walked her students to safety benefit after being shot by a 6-year-old student. We`re live in Virginia with those details, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To have something like this, a safe place where kids are supposed to run with their friends, where they`re supposed to pass notes in school, that`s the kind of darkness they shouldn`t have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:23]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Police are hailing a Virginia first grade teacher as a hero after she bravely escorted her kids out of the classroom after being shot by one of her students last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF STEVE DREW, NEWPORT NEWS POLICE: She suffered a gunshot wound but she was still able to get all of her students out of that classroom. Mrs. Zwerner was the last person to leave that class.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You can hear the emotion in his voice. Police say a 6-year-old student shot the teacher Abigail Zwerner through the hand. The bullet went into her chest while she was giving a lesson, adding that the shot was intentional and the gun was legally purchased by the child`s mother. Zwerner is now awake and listed in stable condition.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Catie Beck is in Newport News, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 So many questions. What more do we know about that amazing, quick reaction? What are you hearing from community members and the key question, could the parents face charges?\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, authorities were pressed on that yesterday in the press conference. Will this mother face charges for the action of her 6-year-old? And the police chief said at this point, the investigation is still ongoing. They still have to do more interviews, collect more facts and then review the law and see where those two intersect and if they do, there will be charges. He`s certainly not taking them off the table, but has not given them any indication on when or if there will be charges for this mother. Certainly something they`re considering.\par \par \uc2 As for the teacher, there has just been an outpouring of love and support over the last 24 hours for Abigail Zwerner. She is someone that they described as selfless, as a ray of sunshine, someone who came in to see these first graders every day with the same positive spirit and energy.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 The police chief sharing an anecdote from visiting her in the hospital yesterday. He said her first question to him, right away, was how are my kids, referring to her students. Wondering how they are. Concerned about their well-being. Have to take into account, you know, these are 6- and 7- year-olds who witnessed an incredibly violent act and are going to be asked to walk back through the doors behind me and go to school.\par \par \uc2 Today, parents were here collecting the belongings of students. The school is closed for the remainder of the week, but they are allowing parents to come and retrieve backpacks and coats and water bottles left behind in all of the chaos from Friday.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been able to speak to some of those parents who express a great fear, a great reserve about letting their child go back and having to explain this to their child. How do you explain that this building is now safe? So one of the things the superintendent brought up yesterday, he said quite honestly, we were unprepared. We never thought a 6-year-old could bring a gun into school so maybe we do need to go back to the drawing board and think about metal detectors at the door or other safety protocols for the lower schools. We use them for our upper schools. Maybe we should put them in place here as well. So this sort of unprecedented action from such a young child is certainly sparking a lot of conversation. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Who could anticipate that? But can we also say how time after time after time, the most horrible of circumstances, teachers are selfless. They`re brave. They put their students first. Remarkable.\par \par \uc2 Catie Beck, thank you for covering this for us.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230111ej1a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 9, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ryan Nobles, Anna Palmer, David Jolly, Guad  Venegas, Blayne Alexander, Paul Butler, Gabe Gutierrez, Mike Memoli, Maria  Teresa Kumar, Marissa Parra \par \uc2 8,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Today, House Republicans finally get their chance to be in charge, but the majority and new speaker Kevin McCarthy will be tested right out of the gate. The latest on the rules package and the secret deals that could derail the new majority before it even gets started. Plus, the Fulton County grand jury investigating former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has wrapped up its work and delivered its final report to a judge. So, what happens now and when will we learn if any charges will be filed? We`ve got a live report for you in just a minute. And the latest on \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  . His new claims about his fight with his brother, his relationship with his father, the latest in a seemingly never-ending parade of bombshells he`s dropping on the royal family.\par \par \uc2 But we started in the House where Republicans are hoping to put the extraordinary spectacle of last week`s drawn-out speaker fight behind them and finally get to work. But the very first order of business which is voting on what`s known as the rules package could fracture the party all over again. That`s because it includes several pages of secret deals that Kevin McCarthy cut with far-right Republicans in order to get the job of a speaker in the first place. And we will find out until tonight whether all of the rest of the Republican rank-and-file are on board. One Republican, Tony Gonzales, has already said he will not vote for the package tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES, (R-TX): This rules package vote isn`t just about the rules. It`s what is the next week look like? What is next month look like? And you know what? I do think there`s an opportunity in the House for rank-and-file members to lead. The American public are counting on House Republicans to be the one savior in this whole. If we can`t get this right, it`s going to be -- it`s going to be a nightmare after nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles. David Jolly is a former Republican congressman from Florida and an MSNBC political analyst. And Anna Palmer is a co-founder of Punchbowl news and an MSNBC contributor. So, Ryan, you have some new reporting on details of Republicans gearing up for intense oversight of the Biden administration. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, it`s no secret that there aren`t many that believe much of the legislation that comes out of the House of Representatives over the next two years will ultimately become law because Democrats control the Senate and, of course, they control the White House. So, the one thing that House Republicans have at their disposal is the ability to use their oversight responsibility. And they plan to use it in many different ways. Almost every committee that the House of Representatives has will be engaged in some level of oversight, and much of it will be directly aimed at the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 You know, as soon as the end of this month, we could see hearings begin on a wide range of things, including the origins of the COVID virus, look at the withdrawal from Afghanistan, the role that China is playing on the global stage, and a variety of other things. And, you know, we`re also waiting to see exactly how this Republican majority uses its subpoena power.\par \par \uc2 For instance, the Oversight Committee, which James Comer will become the head of, has the power for their chair to issue a subpoena with only the approval of the speaker`s office without the approval of the other members of the committee. So that could mean that they bring potential subpoena targets in front of the committee in rapid succession. And, of course, we know that Comer in the Oversight Committee has specifically said that they`re interested in looking into the Biden family`s business dealings and whether or not there was any conflict of interest with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  either as president or vice president.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just an example of what the next two years are going to be like. And it`s part of why they want to get this rules package done as quickly as possible because it will then set the stage to empower these committees to begin their investigations.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Anna, let`s talk about the rules package, and it`s a key part of Punchbowl`s reporting today. It says it includes "a secret three-page addendum that McCarthy and his allies hashed out during several days of grueling negotiations with the House Freedom Caucus. This pact includes the most controversial concessions McCarthy made in order to become speaker, three seats on the Rules Committee for conservatives, freezing spending at the fiscal year 2022 levels, a debt ceiling strategy, coveted committee assignments, and more." And you suggest there`s even more coming. What can you tell us about this and the implications for the House functioning frankly?\par \par \uc2 ANNA PALMER, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Hey, it`s pretty wild when you consider the fact that part of this entire rules package is going to get voted on tonight is not public. And there is -- our expectation that this remains a secret as long as possible that this is really part of that delicate dance that we saw. Now, speaker Kevin McCarthy engaged in all of last week, but it just showcases I think how tenuous his leadership is and how much power these conservatives have exerted.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And the fact they are going to have their tentacles in all different facets from investigations to rules, and the Rules Committee, you know, decides everything about how and what is going to get on the House floor to these coveted committee assignments, it just showcases you how much McCarthy had to give up in order to get this speakership.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. So, David, what does that mean for moderate Republicans, and again, what does this mean for the functioning of Congress?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. Chris, the question around moderate Republicans I think was answered last week. There`s one group within the Republican caucus we really don`t talk about much, and it`s the 200 members who sat there and got rolled as well, as Kevin McCarthy got rolled. Any one of them could have objected last week, but they agreed to go along with this. And I think that`s a very telling posture. You know, maybe we see some people speak out against the rules package --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What this tells us, David, specifically -- what does it tell us?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: It tells us that the caucus is under such stress, if you will, in terms of fracturing, that they cast their lot and said we`re just going to stick with McCarthy as he gives away the store to the conservatives. I mean, if Matt Gates said last week something probably most telling of all, this rules package, these concessions by Kevin McCarthy now make the chair of the House Freedom Caucus more powerful than the Speaker of the House. And 200 members of Congress that started out with Kevin allowed Kevin to elevate the Freedom Caucus Chair to that level of stature. So, I think it`s very telling.\par \par \uc2 Now, to Anna`s point, everything now lives on this margin of about five votes within the caucus, so you know, you -- Kevin`s got to find the Goldilocks temperature on absolutely everything. And he`s just not going to be able to do that. On some hard questions he`s going to have to work with Democrats on questions he refuses to, the caucus will just see the inability to move forward on some of their priorities.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Anna, even if moderates go along, right with the rules package, but then you have all these other things that were pushed hard by the far right. They include, according to your reporting, tightening abortion restrictions, cutting defense spending, social programs. First of all, isn`t even clear that there`s a point at which the Republicans will break what looks really iffy there? And is there any sign frankly, that the Republicans are setting themselves up to work with Democrats on anything?\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Well, I -- certainly, you`re not going to see Kevin McCarthy lead with the fact that he`s going to have to work with Democrats on some of these things.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALMER: But to Ryan`s point, a lot of the focus has been on the conservatives, right, in terms of investigations, what they want to do. Well, you already starting to see some of the moderates upset with the fact that the -- in some of these early bills, three of them are going to be around abortion. And of some of this, this is not what moderate Republicans want to be talking about. I think the bigger question is, as much as these priorities are -- you know, Republican priorities, a lot of this is going to maybe pass in the House if it does pass at all, but it`s not going anywhere in a democratically controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you look at that, this is where the kind of just the blocking and tackling of keeping the lights on becomes very difficult for Kevin McCarthy. That includes how do you kind of figure out just keeping the government running. How do you figure out the debt ceiling? All of these things are going to be really controversial.\par \par \uc2 And particularly, we`ve already started seeing a lot of disagreement potentially around even defense spending, something that kind of Republicans historically have been very supportive of increasing or at least keeping up those levels. There`s an increasing skepticism of funding that`s going to Ukraine, for instance. And so, as pressure to cut spending happens, it`s not just going to be on discretionary programs, there`s only so much money that they can cut there. And so, what does that look like for the defense? I think it`s a big question for Republicans and a very hard one that Kevin McCarthy is going to have to figure out how to thread the needle on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And threading the needle, I -- it`s hard to imagine a needle that could be threaded any more thinly than this, David. And yes, to listen to a bunch of Republicans over the weekend, they just want to put all of this in the rearview mirror, and then everything`s going to work out somehow. Let me play a little of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN BISHOP, (R-NC): It`s positive, not negative. It`s not weakening, Kevin McCarthy is providing the speaker with the tools he needs for Republicans to go to the mat for the American people.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH): Sometimes democracy is messy but I would argue that`s exactly how the Founders intended it. They wanted a real debate, real input from all people, and then you get a decision.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): Let`s remember that a little temporary conflict is necessary in this town in order to stop this town from rolling over the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, the deal was made because of real input, David. They want to believe what happened last week was actually a good thing and will ultimately strengthen the Republican Party. Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: No, there`s a little counter-messaging going on, Chris. It`s similar when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announces he got more votes than any incumbent president in history, but he leaves out the context that he lost the election. Last week was chaos. There`s no covering it up. Perhaps that are now more cohesive because they`ve been in battle together.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But here`s the danger in what -- in what McCarthy agreed to. It`s not that more members got empowered. That is a good thing, actually. The House has had a chokehold on allowing backbenchers and middle benchers to have more say. Kevin gave them that.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, who constitutes those backbenchers and middle benchers, are the same people that aided and abetted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the interaction and the narrative of trying to steal the election. That is almost a qualitative problem that Kevin has created. Because under \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  , those speakers kept that caucus in check. And that was long before the danger of January 6, and the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 What Kevin McCarthy did is elevate and empower a caucus of insurrectionists. So, they are right to say the House is better when more members have power. The problem is we`re in a unique era where those members present a unique danger to the Republic. That`s the danger in what Kevin gave away last week.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan Nobles, Anna Palmer, thank you both. David Jolly, you`re going to stay with me. I mean, I want to go to Brazil now because the parallels to our own January 6 insurrection are inescapable. Check out these images showing thousands of people breaking into buildings, smashing windows, destroying equipment, ransacking offices. Claims of election fraud, fueled by far-right extremists paved the way for what you`re seeing, the worst and most chaotic demonstrations the country has seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 Today, authorities there are clearing a tent city belonging to anti- democracy protesters, arresting hundreds, and trying to lock down the Capitol. President \uc2 Lula Da Silva\uc2  held an emergency cabinet meeting inside the Presidential Palace today, one day after supporters of his predecessor, far-right former president \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  stormed the palace and other branches of government. President Lula -- for fueling anti-government anger by claiming the election was rigged.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Guad Venegas is following the story for us from Miami. Former Congressman David Jolly is back. So, what is the situation there right now in Brazil`s capital, Guad?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, we saw the images today, President Lula back in the capital. We first saw those crowds on Sunday when the protesters came in and went into these government buildings, Congress and Supreme Court, and the presidential palace at the time the president wasn`t in Brasilia, in the capital. He was traveling, assessing some damage that had been caused by the rain. Since then, the military has come in. They`ve detained hundreds of protesters, and they will remain as this investigation continue, now -- continues.\par \par \uc2 Lula da Silva\uc2  has claimed that \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  , the outgoing president shares some of the responsibility because of his actions. We know that Bolsonaro left the country before the transition of power to \uc2 Lula da Silva\uc2  , which happened on January 1. And now it`s going to be an investigation to look into the cause.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Lula da Silva\uc2  also said yesterday he believed there was financers behind those protesters. And as of now, we know that the Supreme court has ordered the military, as you shown in those images, to clear these camps and essentially clear any protesters around Brazil that were thinking of joining these Bolsonaro supporters.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Congressman, I mentioned there are obvious parallels to January 6.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Even though some of the players are the same, including \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  , who cheered on the protesters --\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He called them freedom fighters, patriots, I`m talking about the Brazilian folks, former president Bolsonaro`s son posted this from Bannon back in November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2  , FORMER WHITE HOUSE STRATEGIST: Look in the streets of Brazil, look at the great patriots in Brazil, that add a lot of danger to themselves, have come forward in the streets of Brazil. This was the people saying no, you didn`t follow the Constitution, use these machines, use the judiciary to shut us down in the media and we`re not going to tolerate it. It`s going to be very interesting to see how that plays out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He has not shied away from his words or his actions involving January 6, and he seems to actively be trying to do the same elsewhere. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has been one of the leading voices acknowledging that the Trump movement in today`s Republican Party is part of a larger international movement, creating instability around the globe and trying to topple governments. He has been honest about that. And that`s the danger, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there`s danger and violence when it`s injected into our politics. There`s an injury to our republic and democracies around the world when people are disenfranchised. But the goal of the movement is often instability. The tampering by Russia in the U.S. election first and foremost was about creating instability. And that`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 The throughline to what we saw last week, this new committee House Republicans have about the weaponization of the federal government is about stoking instability domestically in the United States. That`s the dangerous thread between what we saw in Brazil, what we`re seeing in the U.S., and around the globe.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Jolly, Guad Venegas, thank you, guys. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And while the legal process for those protesters is just getting started, the consequences for January 6 rioters in this country continue including a trial just now getting underway for the man seen in one of the most famous images from that day. Jury selection for 61-year-old Richard Barnett of Arkansas, he`s the man here with his boots up on \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  his desk, is beginning. Opening statements are as expected as soon as tomorrow. Barnett is facing eight charges including obstruction of an official proceeding, theft of government property, and entering and remaining in a restricted building or grounds with a deadly or dangerous weapon. Prosecutors say he carries a stun gun.\par \par \uc2 The special Grand Jury investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others for their attempts to overturn the 2020 election in Georgia is done with its work. So, when will we learn what they found? That`s next. And President Biden trying to tackle immigration issues in Mexico. What`s at stake for today`s big meeting with Mexico`s president and \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  ? And Prince Harry`s bombshell memoir about the hit the -- that hit the shelves after revealing he`s had no contact with his brother or his father for a while. We`ll dig into drama with a royal expert ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have more now on that breaking news out of Georgia. The Fulton County grand jury just tied up an eight-month-long criminal investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . The question is whether the former president and his allies made coordinated efforts to interfere with the state`s 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 And the grand jury also heard from a lot of people inside Trump`s inner circle, names like \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  , his former personal attorney, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  , and Trump`s former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  . Georgia state elected officials testified as well, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  . The grand jury submitted its charging recommendations to the DA who will ultimately decide how to proceed. A spokesperson for the DA has declined to comment to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Blayne Alexander from Atlanta and former federal prosecutor Paul Butler. So, Blayne, what more can you tell us about this, and can we expect this report to be made public?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, that`s certainly the big question, Chris. And that`s something we`re hoping to find out in about two weeks time. There`s been a hearing set for January 24 to address that very question of whether or not this will be made public and whether it`ll be made public in its entirety or redacted or portions of it. There`s going to be a -- certainly, a lot of interest in kind of arguing as to whether or not there should be made public. We know that the DA`s office is going to make her case, also members of the media can make cases as well. The AJC, the newspaper of record here in Atlanta has already made it clear that they plan to fight for this to be made public.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, what I think is important to mention as well, the special grand jury itself voted. They said that they believe that it should be made public. So, that`s what`s going to be argued in a hearing in about two weeks or so.\par \par \uc2 But as for what happens next here, it is important to remind our viewers why this special grand jury was convened in the first place. This was convened to investigate. This is not a grand jury that has the power to issue indictments but rather, what we`re going to see in this report really is going to be a summary of this more than eight months` worth of testimony -- of sworn testimony that they`ve heard. And we`re also going to likely see some sort of recommendations as to whether there should be charges filed and who should receive those charges as well, Chris.\par \par \uc2 I also want to point out that remember, it was that phone call, that now infamous phone call from then-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the secretary of state demanding that he find enough votes to overturn the election -- overturn the election results here in Georgia. But since then, we have seen this investigation widen far beyond that. And we also know that there have been dozens of people who have come forward and testified under oath. So, certainly, a lot of people are going to be watching very closely to see what is in that report, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Paul, what in your mind is the heart of this case? What would the grand jury have to believe likely happen for this to mean possible charges? And then ultimately, if there is to be an indictment, what do you think they would have a need to have found?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, Chris, in Georgia, it`s a crime to interfere with a state election. And based on everything that we know from what`s been reported, and also from the January 6 House panel, it seems clear that the former president and people like \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  actively tried to shift the vote in Georgia to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , even though he lost the election. And so, a lot of what might become public from the grand jury testimony, we already know from other sources. So really, this is a decision for \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  , whether she wants to move forward with what would be a historic first prosecution of a former president.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, no legal precedent, Paul, at all a county grand jury, a former President. So, what are the next steps for the DA? What do you imagine she`s going to be looking at? What are the things she needs to take into consideration? You obviously don`t want to go into a case like this unless you feel pretty darn sure that you can win it.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: You have to win, Chris. And the problem is, there`s no guarantee that you would. Now, of course, a jury in Atlanta would be different from a jury in other jurisdictions that might be more favorable to the president or give him credit like the judge in Florida tried to do because he was president. But still, this would be an unprecedented case. And so, the judge -- I`m sorry, the district attorney will want to make sure that she has all of the evidence that she needs to persuade a jury beyond a reasonable doubt if she decides to bring the charges.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that there will be a whole lot of coordination with a federal investigation, including with the Special Counsel, Jack Smith. I think that they`ll probably talk but I think especially the feds will be reluctant to reveal their cards to the local District Attorney.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Paul Butler, Blayne Alexander, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 There`s a critical meeting with Mexico`s president today after President Biden`s first trip to the border. The stakes and what he hopes to accomplish, next.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At the U.S.-Mexico border right now, pleas for help from local and state officials, hours after President Biden`s visit to El Paso and that mounting pressure to get the humanitarian crisis there under control. Without a doubt, one of the biggest challenges of Biden`s presidency. He`s now in Mexico City for a critical meeting with the Mexican president. But as the immigration crisis expands, the chances for meaningful reform appear to be shrinking with a new Republican-controlled Congress unlikely to make any moves toward compromise and Democrats with their own internal disagreements.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez is in El Paso. Mike Memoli is with the president in Mexico City. Also joining us, Maria Teresa Kumar, President, and CEO of Voto Latino and an MSNBC political analyst. So, Gabe, we know there`s been a surge in migrants, there have been new records that have been set for migrants in El Paso sleeping on the streets. What`s the reaction been to the president`s visit? And are you hearing any hopefulness that something can be resolved and things can get better?\par \par \uc2 [13:30:02]\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, there is a lot of skepticism that something could be done, at least in the short term. But we have spoken with people that were glad that President Biden visited the border for the first time since taking office. But at the same time we spoke with other local officials that this is too little too late. And of course Texas Governor Greg Abbott, the Republican, greeted President Biden on the tarmac yesterday, and said essentially that, that the president should have come here much earlier.\par \par \uc2 But besides politicians, we have spoken with volunteers here as well as a former city official as well who is so frustrated at what has transpired over the last couple of months, this record number of migrants that have arrived here in El Paso, although the White House says that that number of illegal border crossings in El Paso has dropped by some 70 percent since mid-December. Still, we spoke with some of those local volunteers and officials. Here was their reactions to the president`s visit yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA TIRRES, EL PASO VOLUNTEER: I see the way that people are living in the streets. I see the fear that they have. And, you know, we are a civilized society. We need to do more.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA RODRIGUEZ, FORMER EL PASO COUNCIL MEMBER: I`ve never seen this before. This is unprecedented for the city of El Paso. They have done zero to prevent this. I think that they are trying to cover bullet wounds with band-aids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And of course immigration is such a complex issue that has stymied administrations from both political parties. But what you see behind me, Chris, is something that, you know, really is the face of this humanitarian crisis. There are still migrants that are sleeping here in the streets and have been for quite some time. Again, even though those numbers are less than they were in mid-December, there is a lot of skepticism here about any potential solution to this humanitarian crisis anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Mike, in just a couple of hours President Biden is scheduled to meet with Mexican President \uc2 Andres Manuel Lopez-Obrador\uc2  , and later then with Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  . Realistically, what does the president hope he can achieve?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, I think it`s interesting first to look at what we`ve been seeing over these last few days because for this White House immigration really did feel like a backburner issue for most of the last two years so far in the president`s term. But now starting with the announcement of some of these new policies the president rolled out at the White House Thursday, yesterday`s visit to El Paso, seeing the border firsthand for the first time as president, and then now his meetings here in Mexico, the White House is both hoping to both illustrate and inform both the challenges and potential solutions to this migration issue that they`re facing.\par \par \uc2 And we know the president got a kickstart on some of the conversations with the Mexican president last night when AMLO, as he`s called here, joined him in the motor motorcade ride from Air Force One into downtown. The White House noting that as part of the policies the president rolled out on Thursday, the Mexican government is stepping to the plate here agreeing to take in some of those 30,000 migrants a month from Haiti, Nicaragua, Cuba, that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is expelling under the new policies the president rolled out. They say this is the groundwork potentially for future agreements in which Mexico can do even more.\par \par \uc2 Now it`s also worth noting that Canada, we don`t think of them obviously as having such a role in migration, but the White House has emphasized repeatedly that this is an issue that really spans the hemisphere and that all the leaders as the president gathered them in June in Los Angeles have a role to play here. So, for instance, Canada might be able to play a role in helping to deal with some of the Haitian migrants. They have a French speaking population of course in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Now the other thing, Chris, though, the White House often points on out when the president meets with his Mexican counterpart that this relationship is not just about migration. There are some other thorny issues that they`re going to be discussing, from trade, building on the USMCA agreement that President Trump brought into force, but also some of these fallout effects from the Inflation Reduction Act as it relates to clean energy incentives. And there`s obviously a lot more to come as it relates to fentanyl which is a related issue to the border crisis but one that the White House is hoping to impress upon the Mexican government to do more to crack down on that spread over the course of the meetings here in the next couple of days, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike and Gabe, thank you guys so much.\par \par \uc2 So, Maria Teresa, I feel like we`ve been talking about this, you and I, for decades. Maybe we have. First, what is your reaction to seeing President Biden make the trip finally for a lot of people, finally, to the border, but how concerning do you see the situation right now on the ground in El Paso?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You know, you nailed it, Chris. This is a humanitarian crisis. This is not an immigration issue. This is a humanitarian crisis. It was good that the president goes there. It`s more to pacify, I`ll be honest, the Republicans and the progressives, and that`s why he`s going. But is it the right timing and is it for the right purpose? We don`t need a photo-op, we need solutions.\par \par \uc2 And I do think that it comes to a point in our American policy where we need to decouple issue of the immigration reform that the original gang of eight led by McCain, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  was part of those conversations, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was part of the conversations, to deal with the 11 million people living within our border.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:12]\par \par \uc2 That conversations have been sidelined to what we`re seeing and witnessing right now at the border that is a consequence, a fallout of neglect in the western hemisphere. For the last 30 years, we haven`t had a serious conversation of what are we going to do in Latin America.\par \par \uc2 The last time we had this conversation, Chris, was really around \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  and that had a whole bunch of underpinnings. But what we`re seeing at the border is a result of a combination of narcoviolence, environmental crisis and the fallout from COVID. It is the least reported news that the COVID pandemic hit Latin America hardest. After almost 30 years of progress, all of a sudden close to 16 million middle class Latin Americans were found below the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 And that is the consequence we`re seeing. And so, as I see the president having conversations with Trudeau and the president of Mexico, what he is emphasizing that this is a North American issue, it absolutely is because if we have instability that we`re seeing in Brazil, that we`re seeing Peru, and other parts, it is going to be at the end of the day a fault line between the border crisis and much more.\par \par \uc2 So this is the beginning of a bigger wave if we do not figure out what is our strategy for the western hemisphere. And my hope is coming out of these conversations with the president that he says, one, we have to decouple the immigration crisis with what we are actually witnesses, and this is the residual effects of the COVID pandemic along with what I mentioned before, the narcotrafficking and sadly the climate crisis that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I wonder, though, how you get that done. I mean, Democrats are not all united. A lot of what they`re conversations have been about, right, has been about the use of Title 42, there is disagreement about that. You have a new Republican leadership coming in on the House. What realistically can \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  do to start at least, to start to address this problem which again has plagued Republican and Democratic administrations for a very long time?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: So I think the biggest challenge, and this is where I will be controversial, is that I think we need to decouple Title 42 from the 11 million undocumented immigrants where the original conversation started. We have to decouple foreign and domestic issues. And -- that`s number one. Number two, I think, having a conversation that Biden did very well when we were in -- when we decided to support Ukraine with the Russian invasion, he communicated exactly why there`s going to be investment, why we needed to make sure that we were protecting that democracy.\par \par \uc2 He does a wonderful job understanding and explaining that to the American people. And he needs to do the exact same thing right now with what exactly is happening in Latin America because what Americans now, when we talk about immigration reform, all we think of is the crisis at the border and that`s actually the end result of a larger problem.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Maria Teresa Kumar, always great to have you on the program. Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 California now bracing for relentless bomb cyclones. Hundreds of thousands of people remain without power. The widespread damage experts warn could be on the way. This is a live picture you`re seeing right now from Capitola.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:39]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now 7,000 nurses at two of New York City`s biggest hospitals are on strike over pay and staffing. The nurses rejected a roughly 19 percent pay increase ignoring calls from New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  to avoid the strike. It`s causing major headaches as the hospitals have to move patients, divert ambulances and scale back some other services at a time when many hospitals post-pandemic are already understaffed.\par \par \uc2 We`ll keep our eye on that one. Meantime, storm-batten communities in California are bracing for a parade of cyclones starting today after torrential downpours and high winds knocked out power to more than 100,000 people over the weekend. And these communities are in for a one-two punch, heavy rain today and tomorrow with a scary risk of flooding and mudslides.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Marissa Parra is in Capitola, California. Marissa, what are you seeing right now on what`s about to come?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so, I mean, in terms of what the wind and the rain can do to these communities, I just want to show you what it has already done. This is someone`s backyard. You look at this fence, not only bent, but there`s kelp stuck in the middle of it. So clearly this water, this rain has created such high levels that it has already brought water to where it doesn`t normally go to.\par \par \uc2 And I want to show you one of the biggest concerns as we pan around over here. So Capitola Beach normally right behind me, instead littered with debris. This is all logs, these are trees that have been fallen, carried out to sea. And this is posing a big problem when it comes to the rivers around here that are already cresting.\par \par \uc2 Before I get to that, I want to take you to the pier over there. This is a wharf that has been partially washed away. That was from the last bomb cyclone. That is so important to remember because these are communities that have already been hit so hard in the last week. These businesses over here, some of them had logs go straight into their window because the water was so high that it carried the logs straight into their businesses.\par \par \uc2 And on top of that, they were flooded. We just saw how high that water level got. But then you have places, residents that are not around here but that are in serious danger right now because the next big concern aside from what you expect with water, right, you have downed trees, downed power lines, but we are in an area that is vulnerable right now. It`s not used to this much rain.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 And on top of that, we saw those wildfires that created a situation where now we`re going to see more mudslides because those areas were cleared of the trees and the vegetation that might have made that a little more difficult than it is right now. So around us, we have several different highways that are closed off because of mudslides and because of downed trees. But there are areas around here where there is a concern that we could see more of that.\par \par \uc2 So when we talked about the debris, what we`re seeing in some parts of various rivers around here, there is a bridge not that far away from here. There are crews that are on stand by and they have the machines and the tools in place to take apart the dam that might arise as a result of the debris like this. But there are evacuation orders and warnings in different parts of the Santa Cruz Mountains.\par \par \uc2 And that really just depends on where you are. We`re in a warning where we are. But there are parts that are being evacuated as a result of an order. So right now it depends on where you are, but in terms of the threat here, all of the things that come with wind and rain especially in a place like California, the real concerns are going to be flooding and the landslides and mudslides that come as a result.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa Parra, you and your crew, stay safe. Thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 Prince Harry`s explosive book about to hit bookstore shelves. The revealing interviews already making waves. I`ll speak with a royal expert who reports Prince William is, quote, "burning with anger" over the revelations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  , DUKE OF SUSSEX: None of anything that I`ve written or anything that I`ve included is ever intended to hurt my family, but it does give a full picture of the situation as we were growing up. And also squashes this idea that somehow my wife was the one that destroyed the relationship between these two brothers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:43]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Prince Harry`s bombshell tell-all book "Spare" is set to drop in less than 24 hours but the Duke of Sussex is already talking about the explosive details about private moments between him and his family.\par \par \uc2 Here he is on "60 Minutes."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, HOST: Do you speak to William now? Do you text?\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  : Currently no. But I look forward to -- I look forward to us being able to find peace.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: How long has it been since you spoke?\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  : A while.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Do you speak to your dad?\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY: We haven`t spoken for quite a while. Not recently.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: In the book, you call this a full scale rupture. Can it be healed?\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  : Yes. The ball is very much in their court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In that interview, Harry also makes shocking allegations of a physical altercation back in 2019 saying his brother William threw him to the ground. Both Kensington Palace and Buckingham Palace declined to comment on the allegations in the book to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in "Sunday Times" royal editor Roya Nikkhah.\par \par \uc2 Roya, lots to talk about. Look, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  was on "GMA" this morning and he said his mother would be heartbroken about where he and his brother have ended up. I wonder what you`re hearing about Prince William`s reaction to this book, and do we have any other clues about what`s going on behind the scenes right now with the royal family?\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH, THE SUNDAY TIMES ROYAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi there. I mean, the royal family I think is slightly flabbergasted by everything that`s come out in the last few days in terms of the book and also the interviews. I know from writing a piece about it over the weekend and speaking with some of William`s closest best friends that really devastated him and pretty hurt him, and quite angry about it. But his feeling is that -- is for the time being going to -- he`s not going to retaliate.\par \par \uc2 He`s going to stay silent. I think that`s the view of the king as well. They feel the best way to deal with this and absorb this all is to not comment on it, and to not come out fighting. And I think potentially down the line one would hope that might be the better way to try and effect some sort of reconciliation. A royal reconciliation I think it will be very, very tough.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There are lots of conversations that Harry is having. Let me play a little bit more. He opened up on "60 Minutes" about the death of his mother.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  : For a long time, I just refused to accept that she was gone. Part of, you know, she would never do this to us, but also a part of maybe this is all part of a plan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Telling \uc2 CBS\uc2  the plan he hoped was maybe that his mother was somehow hiding, still alive. Eight years later, he says he asked to see the police files, he wanted proof she was actually in the car. He wanted proof that she was injured. I wonder if there is sympathy for Harry among the U.K. public, or even within the family.\par \par \uc2 NIKKHAH: Enormous sympathy for Harry over the death of his mother and the trauma that has inflicted, a lifelong trauma. It is impossible to read the book as I have been and watch those interviews and hear him talk about the impact that had on him from the age of 12 to the present day, and still feeling, as you mentioned in there, when he was asked to see those files, he still doesn`t feel he`s got all the answers that he needs around the circumstances surrounding the death of his mother.\par \par \uc2 There is huge sympathy for him here, in the U.K., around all of that. And in the royal family. But what I think people in the U.K. have a little bit more problem with is the level of extremely intimate and personal details, family details that Harry has chosen to put out there. And you know, of course, it is his story, he talks about he wants to own his story. People understand that, too, but it`s when you go into that much detail and you know that your family can`t and won`t respond, it doesn`t feel like such as sort of even fight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re almost out of town but I do want to ask you about what he had to say about Camilla, famously remembering the moment his mother called her the third person in the marriage. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  : She was the villain. She was the third person in the marriage. She needed to rehabilitate her marriage. With her on the way to being queen consort, there was going to be people or bodies left in the street because of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Bodies left in the street. A big part of this tour, of conversations and the book, is saying that the royal family went after him and they went after his wife, right?\par \par \uc2 NIKKHAH: That`s what Harry says, and he says, you know, he claims that Camilla leaks information about conversations with William and meetings in order to rehabilitate her image. That`s a very, very different chain in events that`s been factually out there for a long time here on that. It was not Camilla that personally leaked that. But he very much feels that, you know, in order to rehabilitate her own image, she did that almost. He describes it as that, you know, his own PR also just took, you know, she didn`t really care who she hurt in the family and I think that will -- that`s Harry`s version of events. That would be very difficult for the king to hear and read.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Roya Nikkhah, just touching based on a few of the things in the book. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, there is good news out there. Great news actually. It comes in the form of the view from the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  Damar Hamlin`s hospital bed. Hamlin tweeting his photo ahead of the Bills` first game since he suffered those life-threatening injuries on the field. That tweet read simply, "Game time." But what a game. His team responding in storybook fashion. With a kickoff return for a touchdown on the first play. Afterward, quarterback Josh Allen got emotional while sporting Hamlin`s jersey number 3.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH ALLEN, BUFFALO BILLS QUARTERBACK: It has been three years and three months since the last kickoff return. So pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Hamlin`s response to fans posting their love for him, a powerful three-word post, "Love you more."\par \par \uc2 "KATY TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230110ej1900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for December 29, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Cori Coffin, Niala Charles, Marissa Parra, Sam Brock, Matt  Bradley, Raf Sanchez, Christopher White, Dylan Dreyer \par \uc2 7,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC HOST: Thank you for staying with us, I`m Cori Coffin, at MSNBC headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 More than 2000 additional flight cancellations for \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  today as the company`s reset in response to a disastrous holiday weekend continues to come at the expense of their customers. Also, this hour, we`ll check on the next big holiday storm systems as Buffalo residents begin to dig out and get back on the road and hopefully avoid flooding in their communities. And an update from officials as doctors continue around the clock`s supervision of Pope Benedict -- Pope Emeritus Benedict from his bedside in a Vatican City monastery.\par \par \uc2 Some new developments in the \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  debacle just in the last few minutes. Southwest is now announcing plans to "return to normal operations with minimal disruptions by tomorrow." But still today, thousands more flights have been canceled. Thousands of fliers still struggling to figure out exactly how they`re going to make it to their final destinations. Other airlines like American, Delta, United, and Alaska have even offered to try to help by putting a cap on flight prices between certain cities to give stranded customers more options. We`re unclear whether or not that`s actually working.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC News correspondent, Niala Charles for us at LAX. And now what -- Niala, what`s the latest from Los Angeles? What you`ve been seeing there? And flyers trying to get to their final destination, what`s their -- I guess their strategy there from LAX?\par \par \uc2 NIALA CHARLES, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So, Cori, usually LAX during the holiday season is one of the most busiest airports in the country. We`re at the Southwest terminal here and there was hardly anyone here. It`s practically deserted. And that`s because, over the past couple of days, Southwest has canceled a majority of its flights, only operating a third of its scheduled flights. And that`s why there aren`t that many people here.\par \par \uc2 And that new announcement now from Southwest that there`ll be resuming normal operations tomorrow is a relief for people. However, there`s many people in the meantime that are still dealing with what has been a chaotic travel holiday season because of the Southwest fiasco. Behind me, you can see a sea of baggage here. And that`s because although Southwest has flown baggage to the final destinations, in many cases, the people themselves have not made it. So, now it`s that stressful process of people trying to get their bags whether or not they`re able to get to their destinations.\par \par \uc2 Southwest now in the process of trying to reunite baggage with people. They have a form online that people can fill out so that people can get their luggage delivered to them. But we`ve seen many people who have been stranded the last several days have been left to leave without the essential items in their bags because, in many cases, their flights have been canceled or delayed after they have checked in their bags. So, we`ve seen a lot of people having to buy new clothes because they have clothing in here, and also medications. A lot of people didn`t expect this to happen, so they have important medication in these bags they haven`t been able to get to as well.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen car seat checked in here that have been waiting here for days. We`ve also seen motorized chairs. So, that just gives you an idea of how important the things here have been to many people. And at this point, Southwest is releasing a statement saying that they will be refunding people for their canceled flights but also giving money back for reasonable accommodations that had been -- had to be made because of the unexpected extend in the travel. That could be hotel fees and transportation fees, and also food. Some people we`ve seen have spent thousands of dollars because of these delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 However, although it seems that Southwest is making an attempt to give people some money back here, we`ve spoken to families who say that frankly just isn`t enough because these holiday times that they expected to spend with their family have been priceless. And now, they`ve been ruined, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: The ripple effects that will come out of this debacle will not be known for months. Niala Charles for us in Los Angeles. Thanks, Niala.\par \par \uc2 For more on what travelers can do, or at least try to deal, let`s bring in the Managing Editor of The Points Guy, Chief Clint Henderson. Welcome in, Clint, thank you for being here. All right. My first question is what options do travelers have now to try to get to their final destination, and what about their lost luggage?\par \par \uc2 CLINT HENDERSON, MANAGING EDITOR, THE POINTS GUY: So, what we`re telling people to please guys go ahead and rebook yourself, if at all possible, on a competing airline. We have one family friend in Montana whose flight was canceled for tomorrow even though operations are supposed to be resuming, and so I told her to go ahead and rebook herself on Delta, save every receipt, so save receipts for rental cars, housing, hotels, food, Ubers, whatever you spend, and save those receipts and do request refunds from Southwest. I have a feeling their feet are going to be so in the fire in the wake of this that they`re going to make right by passengers to try to restore some of the reputations.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: But really, how long is that going to take, Clint? I mean, that`s going to be -- it seems like thousands upon thousands of customers asking for reimbursements. The daunting task that the airline has of not only getting their operations back, but also giving people money back, and then other monetary compensation on top of that, how long did that take? What else do you think customers need to be made whole?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: So, I think it`s going to take weeks or months to refund everyone, their all their charges. And, you know, Southwest has given itself a lot of wiggle room with the word reasonable. So, we don`t know exactly what that means. But it`s going to be up to Congress, the media, and all of us to hold them accountable and make sure customers are eventually paid back. Now, even in the best of times, it takes weeks, months to get repaid. So, hopefully, because of so much political pressure Southwest is going to make that a quicker process. That remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Is there anything else people can do in the future to avoid getting caught up in something like this?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Oh my god, we have so many great tips of The Points Guy. First of all, use a credit card that has travel insurance built in, so Chase Sapphire Preferred and American Express Platinum, these cards come with trip interruption, trip delay trip cancellation insurance. So, they -- even if it`s a weather thing, which normally the airlines don`t have to pay you back for, even if it`s a weather issue, these credit cards will reimburse you.\par \par \uc2 Also, book a nonstop flight if you can. Don`t try to build in stops. If there`s a direct flight, make sure you`re taking that direct flight. Have the airline app installed on your phone, so you can rebook yourself in the event of irregular operations. Don`t check luggage. I know that`s really tough for families and during the holidays. But if you can avoid checked luggage, that`s going to save you so much. You see Southwest is not even beginning to resume the process of restoring baggage services to people. So, those are just a couple of tips. There`s a lot more at thepointsguy.com.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: All right. So, Clint, do -- you have said that the airline needs to invest in better technology and needs to have more and better-trained employees, would you say that those two key things need to be in place before consumers can trust booking another ticket on Southwest?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: So, I think that is the most important thing Southwest has to do right now. They have to pay their customers back first of all, and then they need to like invest in the technology that was so sorely lacking. Basically, all their systems failed because of some storms. That shouldn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 Their scheduling software, their ramp agents, it was a mess from place to place to place, and they`ve got to invest the resources to get their employees properly trained. They`ve got a lot of new employees, but also in that technology so that the software is not failing when a flight attendant needs to find out where she`s supposed to go next for the next flight.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Clint Henderson, managing editor at The Point Guy, thanks, Clint. We appreciate all that.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: And as crews keep working to clear the roads in Buffalo, in that area, after last week`s record-setting snow storm, concerns are shifting out to potential flooding. The temperature is expected to jump into the 50s on Friday causing all that snow to melt. And then over the weekend, the forecast is calling for rain which could make flooding issues even worse. Joining me now is NBC`s Marissa Parra in Buffalo. Marissa, what`s the plan?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, what I can say is that the plan does look different today than it was yesterday because, in every way, there is such a stark contrast between the conditions today and what we saw when we were reporting on the same story yesterday. So, people here in Buffalo are seeing the green of the grass, seeing the black of asphalt for the first time in almost a week. Those temperatures are creating that snow melt that you mentioned that there were some flooding concerns about. I`ll get to that in just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But what I will say is even though the travel ban has been lifted, now a travel advisory instead, the highways back open, the airport also back open, even though in many ways Buffalo is moving forward and reopening, I will say that there`s still a lot of clean up to be done in a lot of ways. They have 600 pieces of snow removal equipment on the roads in some capacity in the greater Buffalo area. And so, we`ve been hearing from various officials asking for people to still take it easy, be cautious on the roads because that equipment is still out there.\par \par \uc2 But then the other thing. When we talk about the plan to try to mitigate that accelerated snow melt. Well, we spoke to the Lackawanna City mayor. Lackawanna is a city just south of Buffalo. They also saw a lot of snow. And the mayor was describing how they use a tactic when it comes to pushing the snow into areas to try to mitigate flooding concerns. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNETTE IAFALLO, MAYOR OF LACKAWANNA, NEW YORK: This is what they`re pulling off of the streets and trying to get it opened up so that people can start moving around again. We dump it here because there`s nothing here and when it melts, it`s not going to damage anything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARRA: So, the idea is not just to move the snow off of the roads but to pull it into areas that aren`t going to create a flooding havoc.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And so, another thing that we were understanding from officials is that removal crews are working really hard now, not just to remove snow from areas like fire hydrants, right? That`s a safety concern. But also, outside of drains so that as the snow starts to melt, it has a place to go down as it turns into water. Now, another thing here Governor Hochul did say she was sending state aid and resources just to get ahead of those flooding concerns, 800,000 sandbags, generators, pumps.\par \par \uc2 Now the weather is warming up. We mentioned it`s in the 40s now approaching the 50s later this weekend. But you know, I will say that things are looking up in several regards. We did just get this stat from Erie County officials -- National Guard who was helping out make dialysis runs. They did 80 yesterday. Today, they only got two calls. So, in many ways, things are improving here. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: A little bit of silver lining there. Marissa Parra in Buffalo, thanks, Marissa.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court`s decision to keep Title 42 in place while legal challenges move through the courts is a frustrating and painful reality for thousands of migrants seeking asylum through the winter months along the southern border. NBC`s Sam Brock has been speaking with those migrants in El Paso throughout the week and filed this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: The streets of El Paso continue to be a representation of human heartache playing out before our very eyes. Obviously, El Paso has always been a part of the immigration crisis for a long time. But this is a level of acuity, according to the local congressman that represents the city that they have never seen before. All these folks here, most of them anyway, were sleeping overnight in encampments. Now, that`s been cleaned up, you`ll see all the blankets and the supplies.\par \par \uc2 But they`re in this limbo status at the moment because what are your options if you`re from Venezuela or from Honduras, or any of the countries that are blocked by Title 42 from seeking asylum? And they really only have a couple at this point. One of them would be to go to another city, get on a bus, and try to get out of here.\par \par \uc2 But I spoke with the gentleman who showed me his ticket. They`re scared to do that because there are internal U.S. checkpoints within 100 miles of the border, and they could still be stopped and sent back to Mexico, or they can present themselves to border protection. But the problem with that is, unless there`s some sort of exception for them or they meet an exemption, they`re likely going to get expelled under that scenario as well, which leaves them in limbo in the streets here, trying to survive and figured out what their next move will be.\par \par \uc2 The bus behind me right now right over there, that is for warmth. These folks come overnight. And as they`re sleeping on the streets, bundle up in blankets, as it dips into the 30s, they`re able to go there for a brief period of just respite from what they`re trying to experience right now. And it`s just so heartbreaking, so hard to see this, but there are no signs at this point that that status quo is going to change because now we`re talking about another six months to find out any sort of resolution on Title 42.\par \par \uc2 And perhaps the hardest part about all of this is that many of these folks don`t understand the specifics, the complexities of Title 42. Many of us are having a hard time trying to grasp it. So, the local Congresswoman here is trying to set up a forum right now to have conversations with all of these folks about what their options are. But again, it`s pretty limited as this crisis continues. There are roughly a thousand encounters a day in the El Paso area, and that is with Title 42 in place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Our thanks to Sam Brock for that reporting.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. A new wave of Russian airstrikes has knocked out power to residents all across Ukraine, including almost half of all houses in Kyiv. How people are coping with the temperatures dropping there? Plus, Benjamin Netanyahu`s new government officially sworn in today in Israel, how his far-right coalition came together, and the new push for more West Bank settlements.\par \par \uc2 But first, hospitals are bracing for a new wave of COVID and flu cases after the holidays. I`ll speak with the head pediatrician at \uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2  about what they`re expecting and his message to parents.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Hospitals across the country are feeling the strain amid a rise in respiratory viruses including COVID, RSV, and the flu. And with people gathering for the holidays, public health officials are preparing for yet another potential COVID surge as the U.S. records 100 million COVID cases.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Dr. \uc2 Jim Versalovic\uc2 , pathologist-in-chief at \uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2 . Doctor, welcome in. With so many people gathering this week, what could this so-called tripledemic of respiratory viruses look like come January?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 JIM VERSALOVIC\uc2 , PATHOLOGIST-IN-CHIEF, TEXAS CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: Well, we need to continue to stay vigilant. I know we`ve been saying this every winter. This is the third consecutive winter here in the United States where we`re dealing with COVID-19 in addition to influenza, RSV, and other respiratory viruses. So, these are airborne viruses, readily transmissible, contagious among children and adults.\par \par \uc2 We continue to see children hospitalized. Our in-patient numbers that is hospitalized children have doubled approximately in the past two weeks here at Texas Children`s, the largest pediatric hospital in the United States. And additionally, our positivity is now double-digit again, just in the last several days. We`ve seen this double-digit positivity steadily increasing here.\par \par \uc2 So, children are being infected. Children are being hospitalized. We know this is going on with adults as well across the country. So, we just need to again continue to use common sense with masking, distance while gathering, staying at home, and avoiding others when ill, and continuing to promote vaccination as the primary means for disease prevention.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Yes. You spoke about -- a lot about how this is affecting children right now, specifically. How can parents prepare as they send their children back to school next week?\par \par \uc2 VERSALOVIC: Well, first and foremost, vaccination both for flu or influenza and COVID. We know these vaccines work. They`ve been approved for children six months of age and above. Both the annual flu shot, the quadrivalent flu vaccine is an effective match this year for the flu strains that we`re seeing nationally for CDC data.\par \par \uc2 And additionally, for COVID, the bivalent vaccine, which includes Omicron coverage has now been approved for children six months of age and above. So, it`s very important that we promote vaccines that we continue to think about children with underlying risk factors, cardiopulmonary disease, diabetes, down syndrome, among others, that could put children still at risk for severe COVID, even though as a whole Omicron has been milder overall, we still see severe disease, particularly in those children with underlying risk factors.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 So, parents need to talk with their pediatrician as they`re concerned as their children get ready to go back to school in the new year. And masking, common sense around masking particularly in crowded settings, indoors and even outdoors. It`s important for parents to keep in mind ways in which they can protect their children. And to keep also in mind that opinions may be out there about vaccines and COVID, still misinformation, but we need to be careful. Parents, of course, have opinions but we need to ask ourselves, are we doing the right thing and the best thing to protect our children this winter?\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: On that note, a big reason we keep seeing these surges, especially in pediatric cases, is due to a drop in vaccination rates and not just COVID. A New York Times piece found that "within the first year of the pandemic, the nation`s children miss 9 million vaccine doses for diseases like polio and measles." How troubling are these numbers to you, Doctor?\par \par \uc2 VERSALOVIC: Well, they are troubling. You know, we are now nearly 70 years in since the introduction of the Salk vaccine to prevent the ongoing disease of polio -- transmission of polio across the United States, the polio epidemic in the 1950s. And now, nearly 70 years later, we`re still grappling with the importance of communicating. Disease prevention through vaccination, immunization, it`s so important. These are fundamental principles in medicine that have -- are well known now for decades.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing disturbing measles outbreaks in states such as Ohio, Minnesota here in 2022. We have seen that also with a concomitant drop in vaccination rates nationally. For measles, pertussis, influenza, with COVID worse, we still have a long ways to go in terms of getting boosters to many children. So, we have work to do, and it never ends.\par \par \uc2 I think this is important. Every year, we have new flu vaccines to keep up with the evolving strains of influenza viruses continue to evolve rapidly. We`ve seen this in the year of Omicron, 2022 with new subvariants, and then need to continue to refine vaccines and get these newer vaccines to children and to not forget the tried-and-true lessons of prevention of measles and other infectious diseases in children through vaccination. So, it is troubling, but we need to continue to do our job in communicating to the public and sharing all the information about these viruses.\par \par \uc2 They continue to evolve rapidly. We need to stay ahead of the game. We`re doing that and trying to do our best as medical professionals, but we -- parents it`s a team effort, all of us to protect children.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Dr. \uc2 Jim Versalovic\uc2 , we really appreciate your time today. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VERSALOVIC: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Coming up. \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is back a year and a half after he was pushed out of the office, a longtime Israeli Prime Minister`s new cabinet was sworn in again today. We`ll take a look at what`s likely shaking up -- shaping up to be the most right-wing government in Israel`s history. But first, a new barrage of missile strikes targeting critical infrastructure across Ukraine. The push to evacuate dangerous parts of the east, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: As Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  rejects Ukraine`s most recent peace plan, a series of massive Russian missile attacks are targeting civilian infrastructure across the country. In the southeast region of Kherson, people are forced to flee, while huge parts of major cities like Kyiv and Lviv are in the dark.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in NBC`s Matt Bradley live in Kyiv for us. Matt, give us the latest on the situation with the Russian attacks and the situation on the eastern front with the Donbass region.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Well, you`ll hear from the government that actually there -- the Ukrainians` defenses were quite successful. They had taken down 54 of the 69 missiles that the Russians launched across this country. Now, that sounds like a remarkable rate. That`s like 75 to 80 percent of those missiles shot down. But that`s actually consistent with what Ukrainians have been doing for the past several months.\par \par \uc2 But you know, you mentioned that Eastern Donbass region and that`s a really critical and very important part of this. The Russians have not been making any advances on the battlefield. The Ukrainians haven`t made many advances either for the past several weeks.\par \par \uc2 But the Russians haven`t done anything, made any victories since about June, and that`s one of the reasons why when we hear from the Ukrainian government, they say that these missile barrages that have been coming regularly, up until now, but they`re really attributed to the fact that the Russians are frustrated on the front lines. And that`s another thing that we`re seeing in Bakhmut, and also nearby in Kreminna. That`s another city that`s just north of Bakhmut.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Russians have been making some advances in Bakhmut. If you remember, that`s where \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  just visited right before he went to Washington, just last week. So, you know, that is the heart of the fighting in the eastern Donbass region. The Ukrainians have been losing a little bit of ground, but now it looks like they`re making up for that just north in the city of Kreminna. And that means that they`re within striking distance of actually taking that city.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, the Russians reported recently that they had actually withdrawn their sort of central base in Kreminna to another nearby town. So, it looks as though after some very, very fierce fighting over the last several weeks and months, we could start to see a gradual shift in the frontlines in the eastern part of the country. Now, if Kreminna falls to the Ukrainians, the Ukrainians say, that means that they`re within striking distance of taking several other towns nearby, like Lysychansk, and Severodonetsk.\par \par \uc2 They say, the Ukrainians, that if the Russians lose Bakhmut -- lose Kreminna, that their whole frontline could fall. Now that sounds a little bit like hyperbole, but at the end of the day, it looks like the Russians could lose some major ground once again, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Matt Bradley reporting out of Kyiv, the city that`s got about 40 percent knocked out of power right now with those strikes happening on the nation`s power grid.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, President Biden releasing a statement saying he is looking forward to working with \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  following his return to power in Israel. But the prime minister`s new hard-right political allies will be expecting policies that push their agendas and may frustrate world leaders.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Raf Sanchez filed this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: After just a year and a half out of power, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is back as Israel`s prime minister. He was already the longest serving leader in Israeli history, and he will now build on his 15 previous years in office.\par \par \uc2 The government he formed today is the most right-wing and the most religious in Israeli history. It includes several figures from the far right and we saw large protests today as this new government was formed in the parliament, the Knesset.\par \par \uc2 The most controversial figure here is a man named Itamar Ben-Gvir. He`s the new national security minister. This is a man from the far-right. He has a string of criminal convictions, including inciting racism and supporting a Jewish terrorist organization, and yet he is going to be in charge of the Israeli police under this new government.\par \par \uc2 Now Netanyahu says Ben-Gvir has changed since those convictions, which were several years ago, but we`re hearing a lot of fear from Palestinians both inside Israel and in the occupied West Bank about what this new government is going to mean for them. We`re also hearing concern from the LGBTQ community. Some of these more religious ministers in this new government have a long history of homophobic behavior.\par \par \uc2 Now Netanyahu says this government will represent all Israelis, whether they voted for him or not. The government also says one of its top priorities will be the expansion of Israeli settlements in the West Bank. And that really puts them on a collision course with the Biden administration which says that settlements are an obstacle to peace, that they are a detriment to a two-state solution between Israel and the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 We`re also likely to see disagreements between this new Israeli government and the White House about the way forward on Iran. President Biden would like to see a diplomatic solution to curb Iran`s nuclear ambitions. This new Israeli government likely to take a much more hawkish line.\par \par \uc2 Now President Biden, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , they disagree on a number of policy areas. But they have known each other for decades. They are personally friendly, and we are likely to see the president call the new prime minister at some point in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: NBC`s Raf Sanchez reporting.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  renewing his call for prayers today for Pope Emeritus Benedict XVI. Today the Vatican says the retired Pope`s condition remains serious, but he is stable.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, "National Catholic" Vatican reporter Christopher White.\par \par \uc2 Chris, what information do you have about Pope Emeritus Benedict`s condition this hour?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WHITE, NATIONAL CATHOLIC REPORTER VATICAN CORRESPONDENT: Well, the news out of Rome here seems to be a slight shift from yesterday when there was, you know, quite a bit of mystery surrounding the retired Pope`s condition. Today of course as you said we`re hearing that he`s still in serious condition, but stable. I think yesterday most people thought perhaps it was the beginning of the end. But I think around Rome there is a sense that perhaps not just yet.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Now you reported that a mass will be held for Benedict tomorrow. What can we expect from that? And what other public support have we seen so far?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes, this is a mass that was announced by the \uc2 Diocese of Rome\uc2 , you know, the head of the Rome diocese, and this is going to be, you know, I think a closely watched event. Of course, it will be primarily a spiritual event, but I think we`ll be watching his words closely to try to get a sense of what they know in terms of how the Pope may -- the retired Pope may be doing.\par \par \uc2 Across the world, religious leaders both Catholic, protestants and Jewish leaders have echoed their prayers for the retired Pope. But I think we`re all just waiting to get a bit more further information on how he`s doing and if he may indeed recover.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Now, Chris, this is the first time we have had a Pope emeritus and a current Pope at the same time. Now I want to be sensitive here, but we have never seen what happens if the Pope emeritus passes. Talk to us about this moment in time.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes, we`re in a bit of uncharted territory here. You know, should Benedict die, when he does die, he most likely will be buried by \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . It will be the first time a living Pope has buried his predecessor. And I think in terms of what to expect, there`s still some mystery there. Pope Benedict has not released his final wishes yet as to how he would like to be buried. The Vatican has not commented on that either.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 So we suspect there will be something like a state funeral where he will lie in state for some time, but we don`t know, you know, will there be dignitaries from around the world, at what level, you know, how long, you know, between death and the funeral when that time comes. We just don`t know. And so we`re all eager for more information, but so far the Vatican has remained tight lipped about this.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: A little bit of welcome news there about the Pope emeritus`s condition being serious but stable for the moment.\par \par \uc2 Christopher White reporting for us. Thank you, Christopher.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, most kids are home from school this week, but when they are back, lots of their schools will be struggling to get teachers into the classrooms. Up next, we`ll take a look at why there`s such a shortage of educators across the U.S. and what can be done to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Well, there`s good news and there`s bad news. The good news is that all 22 million jobs lost during the coronavirus pandemic have been fully recovered.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 The bad news is that those workers didn`t all go back to their old jobs. One of the sectors struggling to rebound the most is K-12 public education. And it`s more than teachers, it`s bus drivers, custodians and other support staff. My next guest writes that the solution to getting them back is to pay all of them more.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " economic columnist Heather Long.\par \par \uc2 Heather, welcome in. Teachers have left the profession for a slew of different reasons. Is the answer really that simple?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER LONG, WASHINGTON POST ECONOMIC COLUMNIST: Well, it would certainly help. Obviously, it`s economics 101 that if you raise the pay, more people are going to apply. And we`ve certainly seen that the hardest hit sector, the one struggling to recover the most is the hospitality sector. And that`s exactly what they`ve done. They keep increasing the pay to try to get more applicants.\par \par \uc2 But in the second hardest hit sector, public education, we have not seen that. This job used to be a pretty good job in the `70s, `80s, and `90s relative to other college graduates, plus of course when you go into education, you`re helping a lot of people and you feel that great sense of pride. We all can talk about teachers we love who helped shaped our lives. But it`s no longer the case that this is a great profession anymore.\par \par \uc2 You have huge burnout among teachers after the pandemic and the online Zoom classes. Now we have the culture wars and all the scrutiny over book banning, and how we`re teaching history and other subjects. And you put all that together and teachers are now paying way worse than most other college graduates are. And that`s why we`re seeing teachers leave the profession and we`re seeing a lot of young people not want to become teachers.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: OK. Among everything you just noted, I think that is -- those items are key to showing where the problems are in the sector. Is there anywhere that`s getting it right?\par \par \uc2 LONG: Yes. I`m happy to say we`re starting to see some pretty innovative solutions. I think one of the best is what we`re seeing out of the state of Hawaii. For the past several years, they have been giving a bonus to teachers, a pretty significant one, a $10,000 for special education, which is one of the hardest positions to fill across the country.\par \par \uc2 They have also been giving bonuses of an extra $3,000, $5,000 or $88,000 to teachers willing to go to the schools that are usually the hardest to staff. That`s usually higher poverty schools or schools in more rural or some of the urban areas. These schools have tended to have teacher shortages for years. But it really went on steroids during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And to see that kind of impact that a program, even just $10,000 has made a huge impact for the state of Hawaii in being able to attract and retain special education teachers.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Right now, schools are sitting on a massive infusion of cash from the federal pandemic relief aid. That must be spent by 2024. So what is stopping school districts from funneling that money directly into paying people more, recruitment and retention?\par \par \uc2 LONG: It seems so obvious. I wish that is what more districts would start doing. We`re starting to see pockets of that across the country. Washington state has a bill, for example, to do that next year where some of its teachers and hardest to fill position. But unfortunately, there seems to be this demonization of teachers across the country and that they are asking for too much or that they are teaching in the wrong ways or, you know, teaching the wrong books.\par \par \uc2 And it`s just we`ve gotten sidetracked from the fundamental basics here that we need to be educating the next generation and we want -- we can either do two things. We can either lower the standards for teachers or we could raise the pay. I certainly know which one of those I would choose.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: It`s an easy choice. I`d like to know your thoughts on the bill that was introduced by Congresswoman Frederica Wilson to raise the national minimum salary for K-12 public school teachers to $60,000 minimum. Would it be enough? Do you think more is needed?\par \par \uc2 LONG: It would certainly be a game changer. $60,000 is roughly the median salary for teachers. But of course, starting teachers are often paid far less. A lot of those salaries are around the $40,000 to $45,000 mark when teachers are first starting out. Many of course have student loans and debts to pay off. So that would be a pretty big game changer.\par \par \uc2 But again, I would say let`s start with addressing the biggest problems. And for years, the biggest problems continue to be high poverty schools and fields like special education, science, foreign language. If we could raise those salaries, it would go a long way.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Heather Long for us this afternoon. Please check out her piece in the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." Heather, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the recovery from this week`s massive snowstorm in western New York is just getting underway. That`s just one of many weather disasters that hit the U.S. this year. How climate change contributed to everything from snowstorms to wildfires to tornadoes in 2022 and what experts say we could see next year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:56]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: The deadly Christmas storm that has the city of Buffalo still reeling was only the latest example in a year of extreme climate events here at home and across the globe. From record flooding and heat to wildfires, snowstorms and hurricanes, 2022 had it all.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Dylan Dreyer takes a look at how climate change made a wild weather year for us and the swings this year as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC HOST (voice-over): 2022, a violent year of climate and weather extremes. From heat waves and drought to catastrophic flooding and hurricanes, the impacts reverberating around the globe. So far this year, $15 billion weather disasters hit the U.S. According to \uc2 NOAA\uc2 , the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 , the final count yet to be tallied.\par \par \uc2 The first billion-dollar disaster came in the spring when warmer temperatures fueled three deadly tornado outbreaks in as many weeks. Spawning more than 200 reported tornadoes across more than a dozen states.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s very devastating. You know, like it`s all your hard work is just gone up in the air.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 DREYER: In the west sections of Yellowstone National Park were devastated by destructive flash floods. Roads were washed away. And the park closed to the public for the first time in three decades.\par \par \uc2 Climate change causing the atmosphere to be warmer and wetter. Making conditions ripe for these types of events.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was very surprising how quickly it came.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: This summer was a story of extremes. Europe had its most intense heat wave in recorded history. London set a historic all-time high of 104 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Back in the U.S., six 1,000-year floods occurred in a span of weeks in July and August, places like St. Louis, Dallas, and eastern Kentucky, deluged with eight to 15 inches of rain in just 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Death Valley received its entire years` worth of rain, in just three hours. The footprint of climate change leaving its mark across nearly the entire U.S. Parts of the west baked in the most severe heat ever recorded in the month of September.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve never dealt with something this hot before.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Nearly 300 weather stations hitting their hottest temperatures. In places like Salt Lake City, Reno, and Sacramento. Meanwhile, already extreme drought conditions in the region worsened. Lake Mead`s water level plunged to the lowest yet. The \uc2 Bureau of Reclamation\uc2  claiming a tier two water shortage for the first time ever hoping to avert a water crisis.\par \par \uc2 A slow start to the hurricane season, category four Ian roared ashore southwest Florida with winds topping 150 miles per hour, time for the fifth strongest hurricane ever to strike the United States. Neighborhoods in Fort Myers and Naples left in ruin.\par \par \uc2 Warmer waters are acting as jet fuel, causing the storms to rapidly intensify. Just as the season was coming to a close, category one Nicole became the first November hurricane to make land fall in nearly 40 years. Scientists attribute this to warmer sea surface temperatures in the Atlantic Ocean. This year, running up to 3.5 degrees above average.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Nicole is the nail in the colin for Daytona beach shores. Ian came in and did all this damage and now Nicole is just putting this away.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: In upstate New York, winter arrived early when a massive November snowstorm re-wrote the history books, burying neighborhoods and even the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  stadium under 80 inches of snow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s nothing in the store. We got there, you can`t get a loaf of bread or anything.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: With eerie similarity to last year, a series of deadly cool season tornadoes ripped through the south. More than 100 reported over two weeks across seven states between Thanksgiving and Christmas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is my house right here. Yes, my house is gone.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: The December twister is part of a massive week-long, cross-country storm that dropped four feet of snow in the west, brought blinding blizzard conditions to the upper Midwest and dozens of tornadoes to the south.\par \par \uc2 Then a historic and deadly bomb cyclone crippled half the country over the holidays. Bringing with it plummeting temperatures, blizzard conditions lasting nearly 40 hours. And 50 inches of snow. The storm pushed the city of Buffalo into the record books, it`s the city`s snowiest start to the season ever. At least 100 inches and counting.\par \par \uc2 Dylan Dreyer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Our thanks to NBC`s Dylan Dreyer for that reporting.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Michael Mann, he`s the director of the University of Pennsylvania`s Center for Science, Sustainability and the Media, and he`s also the author of "The New Climate War."\par \par \uc2 Michael, welcome in. In a year that featured so many extreme climate events what stood out to you as being the most significant?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN, UNIVERSITY OF PENNSYLVANIA CENTER FOR SCIENCE, SUSTAINABILITY AND THE MEDIA: Yes, thanks. It`s great to be with you. And, you know, we saw a little bit of everything. This past year, we saw pretty much everything that climate change has to throw at us in the form of these amplified extreme weather events, whether it`s the heat and the drought and the wildfires that we`ve seen out west, the unprecedented flooding events that we`ve seen around the country, and even these winter storms.\par \par \uc2 Some people think that it`s somehow inconsistent that we see these winter snowfalls as a result of climate change and global warming but it is entirely consistent with the underlying physics. When you warm up the oceans and lakes like the Great Lakes, that means that when these storms travel over them, they`re able to evaporate more moisture into the atmosphere. And that moisture is available to produce those record snowfalls that we`ve seen with nor`easter events, along the east coast of the U.S., that impact the city of Philadelphia where I live, and especially in the Great Lakes, where we see these massive lake effect snows that are enhanced by human-caused warming, by climate change.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: I want to point out a "TIME" magazine headline that struck me this week. I`m going to read it here, it`s lengthy. "2022 was almost a disaster for climate change action.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Instead, there was hope. That refers to the passage of the climate provisions contained in the Inflation Reduction Act which opened up billions of dollars of funding to help the country turn away from CO2- emitting fossil fuels and toward renewable energy and battery-powered vehicles, heat humps and other climate-friendly technologies. So even though we see a lot of these disasters, is there a reason to be hopeful in 2023?\par \par \uc2 MANN: Yes, like I like to say, there is urgency, and we see the urgency in the form of these devastating and deadly and costly extreme weather events, and the inundation of our coasts from sea level rise and melting ice. There is urgency but there`s also agency. There is still time to avert the wort impacts of climate change. And we have to be able to hold two seemingly incongruous thoughts in our minds at the same time.\par \par \uc2 It can be possible that we`re seeing substantial progress while there isn`t yet enough progress. And that`s what we saw, for example, in the most recent \uc2 U.N\uc2 . climate conference in Sharm El-Sheik just a little more than a month ago, where we didn`t see the sorts of commitments that we might have wanted to see, to rapidly phasing out coal and other fossil fuels. At the same time, we saw substantial progress when it comes to dealing with the, you know, the assistance to the developing world and countries that don`t have the sort of infrastructure that we have in dealing with the impacts that they`re already feeling.\par \par \uc2 And we saw the most aggressive climate bill ever signed into law here in the United States, you know, the climate provisions of the Inflation Reduction Act which are going to make a real difference in bringing the United States back to the lead when it comes to global climate action.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Michael Mann with us this afternoon, the author of "The New Climate War." Michael, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for me at this busy hour. Thanks for watching. Yamiche Alcindor picks up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221230eict00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for December 28, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Witt, Blayne Alexander, Marissa Parra, Michelle Grossman, Raf  Sanchez, Ryan Nobles, Philip Rucker, Donna Edwards, Susan Del Percio, Matt  Bradley, Steph Twitty, Tom Costello \par \uc2 7,998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WITT, MSNBC HOST: Thank you all for staying with us. I`m Alex Witt here at MSNBC World Headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 This hour, we`re going to have an update on the \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  cancellation crisis that is frustrating customers and flight crews trying to get passengers back home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The pilots did show up, we are there to work, we`re ready and willing and able, Southwest just can`t match us to the aircraft. There is a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WITT: Also, this hour, a closer look at the political strategy for both President Biden and former President Trump as both men plot their approaches to 2024. And hopes for a peace agreement to end the war in Ukraine are weakening as Russian officials dismissed President Zelenskyy`s latest plan after major disagreements over who controls four key regions.\par \par \uc2 But we begin this hour with two big stories weather and travel. It is the same story for \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  customers today as they see another wave of cancellations across terminal departure boards. And in Buffalo, where the weather is still preventing tens of thousands of residents from leaving their homes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now are NBC`s Blayne Alexander at Hartsfield Jackson airport in Atlanta, NBC`s Marisa Parra in Buffalo, and NBC meteorologist Michelle Grossman. Welcome to all.\par \par \uc2 So, Blayne, we`ll start with you as we`re hovering around the same cancellation numbers from Southwest that we saw this time yesterday. So, I guess that`s something of a silver lining because it`s not getting worse but it`s clearly still unacceptable for all travelers.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: And, Alex, it doesn`t look like it`s going to be getting better until sometime next week. That`s according to the CEO. They believe that it will be likely sometime next week before they are able to get back on track with the flights. But until then, yes, we are going to see a significant cancellation number, a significant slashing of flights, something to the tune of 2500 or so planned for tomorrow as well. So, now we`re looking ahead to Friday to see what the schedule is going to look like.\par \par \uc2 I`m looking at the Southwest counter right here in Atlanta. Keep in mind, this is not one of the most -- the hardest hit areas, those two would be Denver and Chicago Midway, two big hubs there. But even here, I`m seeing this kind of line that`s been an almost constant throughout the duration of this travel issue. So, that`s what`s happening right now when it comes to Southwest.\par \par \uc2 But also, when you talk about what Southwest is going to be giving to these stranded customers -- these stranded travelers, the airline says today on their website that they`re going to be offering reasonable reimbursements for people who had to come out of pocket for things like hotel, things like food, things like taxi and \uc2 Uber\uc2 . All of these expenses that they had to suddenly incur based on being stranded, they`re going to make reasonable reimbursements there because transportation secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  says that his department is looking into this to see whether or not these are issues within their control. And if so, he really is going to be pushing for those reimbursements.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that we know is that the Southwest CEO has been in contact with Secretary Buttigieg. This is something that, you know, from by all accounts has gone beyond just weather delays, and now into something that you know, the airline needs to do to kind of rectify the situation and see what they can do to keep this from happening again, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Are you getting a sense that passengers on Southwest that have been so displaced and inconvenienced here will continue to travel on Southwest? Have you heard people say, oh, I wish I`d gone to another airline, or I`m trying to at least get on another airline?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I hear that almost every hour that I will never fly southwest again.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I hear people say. You know, I was standing and doing the live shot yesterday and getting ready, two people literally walked by and shouted, I hate Southwest. So, that -- I mean, you can tell that people are very frustrated, people are upset and their patients are run out.\par \par \uc2 But I have spoken with some people, and this really is the silver lining in all of this, people who say listen, I understand like I`m just like everybody else trying to get home, the ticket agents, the people at the counter, they are doing the best that they can. They are extremely task. And I`ve talked with a number of travelers who really want to acknowledge that every time they`ve come up with people that they`ve tried to -- you know, employees saying that they`re doing the very best that they can and that they are just doing a fantastic job trying to rebook. But again, they don`t have many resources, their hand is tied behind their back, and it just is a difficult situation for everybody, Chris.\par \par \uc2 WITT: I tell you. You know, we hear all the time, pack your patience during the holidays, but this is just ridiculous. Unbelievable. OK, Blayne, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Marissa, to you now, as we focus on the weather, and specifically Buffalo because there are dozens of weather-related deaths and so many residents still stuck waiting for conditions to ease enough to make the road safe there. What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think that there`s a lot of layers to this, right? I want to talk first about the mood here in Buffalo because we know that this is an area that knows snow. In fact, we were just here, ourselves about a month ago for that latest winter storm. And this one was so much more deadly. And I want to talk about the reasons why before I get into how people are feeling in kind of the aftermath of that.\par \par \uc2 So, when we compare the two snow storms, this one saw less snow, however -- the reason one of the main factors, in fact, is that there was so much colder temperatures. So, at its peak, during this blizzard, what we were seeing due to the lake effect is negative 20-degree wind chills with gusts upwards of 70 miles per hour. So, you`ve got to imagine what people were up against as they were trying to survive that.\par \par \uc2 This time around, because it was so much colder if someone was outside unable to get inside, or maybe they were trapped inside of their car, or worse. In fact, we saw a lot of people without power, and what we saw was they went without power for several days, and so, unlike last time, what you have are unsurvivable conditions, if someone is not able to get to a warm place or is not able to get to a warming shelter. So, there is a lot of shock around here. I will say.\par \par \uc2 We`ve spoken to a number of people on the street, just even outside of the hotel here, there`s a lot of sadness, there`s a lot of heaviness in the air as we talk to people because they know what a loss this was. And there`s a lot of frustration. I think one of the next steps here is going to be trying to figure out where things went wrong, and if could things have been done better. That is something that will be answered in the coming days, weeks, and months, of course, like any catastrophe, where you see dozens of people die from a big weather event.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of how things stand here on the ground, you just heard the rail go past me. So, the rail system back up and running. The buses, there are several routes that are back up and running. The airport back open as of 11:00 this morning. We know that there were flights scheduled to arrive and to take off around the noon hour. So that is a big change. They`re hoping that Buffalo can be reopened by this weekend.\par \par \uc2 But what you`ll also notice is the driving ban. That is still in effect, even though you`ll see people driving around me. Now, we don`t know if they might be essential personnel. They might be part of the cleanup crews trying to get things done here. Now, that might be confusing to people. They might be saying, well look how good the roads look behind me.\par \par \uc2 But what you have to remember, there are parts of Buffalo that still have not yet been cleared. And there are parts that -- Route 33 is a great example. We know an Erie County Executive was talking about how they see snow drifts upwards of 30 feet. So, they`re in the process of trying to clear other parts of Buffalo that are seeing immense snow totals, much worse than, of course, what we`re seeing here behind me. And that leads me to my next point in terms of the next steps.\par \par \uc2 Well, of course, we`re seeing a big warm-up here. You can tell by the fact that if you turn over this way, it almost looks like it just rains here. I mean, there`s 450 pieces of equipment out here on the roads trying to clear the snow before it creates flooding conditions, which is happening very quickly that snow melts. We`re upwards of maybe approaching 40 degrees here, expected to climb even higher to the 50s this weekend. And on top of that, we`re expecting some rain. So, that is the next step here.\par \par \uc2 Next step is, of course, making sure everyone is taken care of, everyone has their power restored. We understand it`s a little maybe hovering around 800 power outages reported. But this next step is trying to get all of these roads cleared, get Buffalo back up and running, and try to avoid a flooding catastrophe after what everyone just went through here.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Listen, a comprehensive report. To your point earlier, you said there are multi-layers to this story. Thank you for touching on them all. Appreciate that, Marissa.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, so we got that feel for how it`s going in Buffalo today. What about the residents there? What can they expect moving forward? Talking about the flooding, is it going to heat up quickly, and so that would cause a problem? And also, other storms we need to be watching out for.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GROSSMAN, MSNBC METEOROLOGIST: Hey there, Alex, great to see you. Well, we do expect it to be slow. We`re not expecting extreme, expensive flooding but still could see those creeks rising with that snowmelt very fast and also the rain that we`re expecting Friday, Saturday, Sunday. So, it was a few days where we`re expecting some showers. We could see some ice jams too. Some of this water could end up in people`s basements. We`re going to be watching that very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 But taking a look at the national radar, we`re pretty quiet in the eastern part of the nation even in the middle of the country. It`s the West that we`re focused on over the next several days. We have a powerful storm system that is bringing snow -- feet of snow in many spots. We`re looking at double-digit rainfall totals. Heavy rain falling in the southwest right now.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just today. We have a series of storms that will be moving through not only today but through Sunday. So, this is a big deal over the next several days. Great news for the snowpack, great news for the drought, but it`s fast and it`s furious and it`s coming in a short amount of time.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So, for the forecast for today, that`s what we`re focusing on, that high mountain snow, the heavy rain also winds gusting to 70 miles per hour, that`s going to create some travel troubles in the air, also on the roadways. Look at the middle of the country warming up. That is good news compared to the temperatures we had. But a lot of times when you get to warm up this time of year, it comes with rain, you pay a price for it, so we`re looking at the chance for strong storms later on tomorrow on the southern plains. We`ll touch upon that in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 Sunny and cool stump portions of the Mid-Atlantic into the southeast. but we`re going to start to warm up there too starting tomorrow. And then into the weekend, we`re near 60 degrees in some spots. That were in the single digits last weekend.\par \par \uc2 This is what it looks like in terms of your travel impacts. We have that area of low pressure, it`s bringing that heavy snow to the Rockies, the four corners. and also bringing those really gusty winds, Alex. So, we`re going to focus on that. And then as we go throughout the next several days, it is a parade of storms bringing really strong weather to the west. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 WITT: OK. Michelle Grossman, thank you for the heads-up on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to the breaking news from overseas as there are new concerns today over the health of Pope Emeritus Benedict, with the 95-year-old under medical supervision, as his condition worsens. NBC`s Raf Sanchez is following the story from our London Bureau force. So, Raf, we all woke up to these new statements from Vatican City. What have you learned since?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, Alex, we`ve learned that the current pope, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , actually went to visit Benedict earlier today at the monastery where he`s been living since his retirement back in 2013. That is a sign I think of how serious things are that the pope felt it was important to go make that visit today, possibly to say a final goodbye.\par \par \uc2 Alex, as you said, our first indication that pope -- former Pope Benedict`s health was failing came from \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  who went off script during an audience at the Vatican today, and he asked for people all over the world to pray for the Pope Emeritus`s health. Right after that, he went to make this visit. This is not a common occurrence. The last time these two men were together was four months ago at a ceremony to install new Cardinals.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican is not giving precise details about Pope Benedict`s condition. They are saying he`s being monitored around the clock by doctors. And they do say that the worsening in his health is associated with old age. He turns 95 back in April.\par \par \uc2 And, Alex, this is kind of an unprecedented situation when a sitting pope passes away, it triggers centuries-old rituals and traditions. We know what the funeral of a pope looks like. And we know, of course, that it culminates with a cardinal conclave to elect a new pope.\par \par \uc2 What we do not know is what happens when a former pope passes away. Because Pope Benedict, of course, is the first pope in 600 years to step away from his position. They had to create this new title, Pope Emeritus, for him. And so, we are likely to see if and when Pope Benedict does pass away.\par \par \uc2 Some history is being made. We have not seen a funeral like this happen before. The last papal funeral was in 2005 when Pope \uc2 John Paul II\uc2  passed away. We saw dozens of heads of states attending that funeral, including then-President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  as well as presidents Clinton and President George H. W. Bush. But right now, I think Catholics all over the world heeding that call from \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , sending up their prayers for Pope Benedict, and the whole world very closely watching for any updates from the Vatican, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WITT: We are, indeed. But you make a very good point, several of them in fact talking about how unique this will be if and when he does pass. Thank you, Raf Sanchez.\par \par \uc2 Tax day for Trump. After years of delays, the public will see six years of Donald Trump`s redacted tax returns. That will happen Friday. What they could reveal about his business practices and why it matters?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 WITT: He tried to hide them for years but the American public will finally get their first glimpse at former President Donald Trump`s official tax returns. The House Ways and Means Committee is set to make Trump`s records public this Friday, according to a committee spokesperson. But the journey to this public release has not been easy. Last month, the issue went all the way to the Supreme Court finally granting the committee access to the records. And then last week, the committee voted on the issue and a party- line decision, which was followed by a delay in the release due to redactions of sensitive personal information.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles. Ryan, another welcome. So, when can we expect to see Trump`s tax returns, this Friday for sure, and how exactly will they be released?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: So, we expect them to be released a Friday around noon time Eastern, that`s when the House will gavel in during a pro forma session, and that`s when they will be made an official part of the Congressional Record. And the Ways and Means Committee expect to put out a press release with the information associated with that filing. So, that`s the first time we`re going to get to see these records in totality.\par \par \uc2 But it`s unlikely that it`s going to reveal a ton of new information. The committee already releasing a pretty lengthy report that was put together by the Joint Committee on Taxation that went into the concerns that the committee had about the information that they found in his tax returns. In particular, the fact that a mandatory auditing system that was put in place at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  was not utilized during a big section of the Trump administration while he was president.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we could still find out some details about his charitable tax donations -- or his charitable donations that he claimed on his tax returns and things along those lines. But, you know, kind of the top line, a big piece of information about the former president and his taxes is something we`ve already known that he has often, you know, claimed major business losses and as a result is not paid as much in federal income tax as one might expect for someone who`s described himself as being as wealthy as he is.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 But still, a lot of times, the devil is in the details when it comes to forms like this, and there are certainly going to be a lot of people poring over them once they get into our possession later this week.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Yes, 100 percent. How about lawmakers? How are they reacting to all of this? And is it distracting from other policy issues?\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yes, it`s a good question, Alex. And, you know, Democrats insist that the reason that they want the former president`s tax returns is because they believe that there needs to be reform to this policy at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  that requires the auditing of presidential taxes. Right now, it`s just the policy of the agency, it`s not required under statute, so, there`s really no penalty for the fact that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  did not engage in this practice for a good part of the Trump presidency. They would like to make that law under statute now.\par \par \uc2 And then on the other side, Republicans are arguing that this is a -- you know, something that`s going to set a very dangerous precedent that, you know, making a situation where private tax returns could become public is something that could be used as a political tool down the road. But Democrats argue this is a unique case. So, a lot of back and forth on this issue as you might imagine, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Indeed. Thank you so much, Ryan Nobles, for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, let`s bring in national columnist for the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , Philip Bump. That last point we`re talking about with Ryan and how the \uc2 IRS\uc2  really got hammered in this report, I`m going to get to that in a second. But regarding Trump`s tax records, they are expected to include the information from 2015 to 2021, the years Trump was in office, what kind of information are you, Philip, expecting to see?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BUMP, NATIONAL COLUMNIST, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, I think that -- I, like a lot of people, I`m not a tax expert and so I think one of the things we can expect here is a level of intricacy that most of us are not used to. Allow -- you know, all of these LLCs and all this layering, in order to be able to shield profits and to be able to claim debts. And, you know, all of these machinations that really, really rich people always go through when they`re filing their taxes, which I think will look bad to any, you know, regular person, like myself, just because we don`t understand like how this works in all of these various loopholes.\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has long been trying to avoid is this level of scrutiny because he understands he`s -- you know, he`s actually bragged at times about how he uses the tax code to his advantage. But that doesn`t look good. And it certainly doesn`t reinforce what he`s always claimed just as these he -- when -- you know, back in 2011, even he was saying, I want to release my taxes, good. I`ll show how rich I am. But instead, what we`re going to see is that these big losses are sort of obscured through these weird technical machinations. And I think that`s just going to reflect very poorly on him, which is why you never want to release them.\par \par \uc2 WITT: So, on your piece for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , you called Trump`s promise to release his tax returns, his oldest political lie. I mean, it`s been something he`s been asked about and avoided, in fact, since 2011. So, walk us through how this went all the way to a forced release of the records and why has Trump avoided it for all these years. What do you think he`s afraid of revealing?\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Well, I think it fundamentally comes down to the same reason he pushed 