ar \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes. That were the excuse you could use to avoid an audit. I think a real lot of Americans feel pretty relieved right now, right? I mean, look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  very clearly has a sophisticated layer of accountants and lawyers who look at these things and do their best to limit his tax liabilities. That`s -- again, not uncommon for super-rich people. But for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who both presents himself as a common guy and presents himself as being this huge wealthy success, that he has these losses which he claims repeatedly in order to avoid having to pay taxes and this obfuscation through these various corporate entities. All those things undermine his core argument which is I am rich and strong and successful, and I always win.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s not what -- you know you would expect then your tax return shows you billions of dollars each year and you`re paying all this money in taxes, and that`s not what these returns show. And I think that`s why particularly in 2016 and moving forward, he had no interest in making the boat.\par \par \uc2 WITT: OK. Thank you for that rundown on all of it, Philip Bump. Good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Meantime, ready to run, President Biden hunkering down with his family on vacation amid growing pressure rather for him to announce his 2024 intention. So, will there be a repeat of the 2020 Biden-Trump match- up?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WITT: With the approach of a new year and a new Congress, President Biden is taking some time off to vacation with family in the U.S. Virgin Islands, where he could be mulling over a final decision on whether to mount a 2024 reelection bid. But all signs indicate he is readying for the fight. And the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is highlighting a White House strategy we can expect to see a lot more of, aggressive pushback against volatile remarks made by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 For more on this, let`s bring in Philip Rucker, a deputy national editor for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC political analyst, also co-author of I Alone Can Fix It: Donald J. Trump`s Catastrophic Final Year.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Also with us, former Democratic congresswoman Donna Edwards and Susan Del Percio, a Republican strategist and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good to see all three of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Philip, let`s talk about what your paper is doing which is outlining the Biden strategy of quickly and sharply hitting back. Quote, "The rapid responses coming in the weeks since Democrats outperformed expectations in the November midterms, come as some Democratic strategists see a political advantage in pointedly and frequently drawing a contrast with Trump, the Republican Party, and the Republican lawmakers poised to take over the House of Representatives."\par \par \uc2 Is this a big change for President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST, DEPUTY NATIONAL EDITOR: You know, Alex, it`s not a wholesale change, but it certainly is an escalation of a strategy they`ve had, which is to use the most inflammatory and really flimsy actions and words of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to brand the whole Republican Party. This was the strategy of Democrats and of Biden in particular going into the midterm elections, but we`re seeing it increase in the last couple of weeks as the White House prepares to position against the Republican majority in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 So they`re using some of the fringier statements of people like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , et cetera, to try to sort of cast a pall over the entire Republican Party as they know they`re going to be at logger heads here in Washington and the legislative fights come next week.\par \par \uc2 WITT: And Donna, we have the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," which is adding 