hilip, Donna, and Susan, I`m glad for you guys to balance your conversations to the loud mouths that we get otherwise. Good to see you all. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Civilian targets, Russia once again stepping up attacks on the southern city of Kherson after Ukrainian troops liberated that city only six weeks ago. New reaction to Volodymyr Zelenskyy`s 10-point peace plan, right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:46]\par \par \uc2 WITT: Russia has renewed its brutal attacks on Ukraine unleashing more shelling and mortar attacks on civilian infrastructure in the city of Kherson, weeks after Ukrainian forces liberated that from Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from Kyiv is NBC`s Matt Bradley.\par \par \uc2 Matt, welcome. What is the situation on the ground there right now?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Alex. The city of Kherson has been enduring a week of relentless Russian bombardment, and just overnight we saw about two dozen projectiles by Russia fired at the city and that comes after dozens and dozens of Russian attacks earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY (voice-over): Ukraine`s southern city of Kherson under fire once again. Only weeks ago these civilians were welcoming Ukrainian liberators now do Russian bombardments including the shelling of a maternity ward Tuesday, according to the government, have sent them fleeing. Russian artillery attacks on Christmas eve killed 11 people according to the region`s governor.\par \par \uc2 Tamara`s son is among Kherson`s dead. I urged him to wake up and leave the house but he didn`t and that was it, she said. Our live are ruined.\par \par \uc2 But the fiercest battles are in the east. Like Bakhmut. Among Ukraine`s longest battles so far, a violent crucible for the wider war.\par \par \uc2 Bakhmut, Kreminna and other areas in Donbas require maximum strength and concentration now, Ukraine`s president said. The situation there is difficult and painful.\par \par \uc2 The constant drumbeat of war now a routine rhythm for many.\par \par \uc2 You kind of get used to it, said this man. You don`t even look or listen carefully to what is happening and where. That`s how we are now. But still I want to live a normal life.\par \par \uc2 After five months of fighting, there`s little here left to destroy. Except lives. Like Volodymyr Yeshov (PH), a video game designer who was killed in Bakhmut just before Christmas. He always cared about other people more than himself, said his friend.\par \par \uc2 And diplomacy reached another impasse this week. Russia announcing it will ban oil sales to any country that imposes a U.S.-led cap on what it can charge. The move may limit the West`s ability to punish Moscow. But on Ukraine`s home front, energy is also in short supply. Ukrainian civilians huddled through a cold, dark Christmas amidst electricity outages. War weary but still celebrating as best they can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Yes, and Alex, this war is now in its 11th month. And I just want to, you know, talk about the toll that it has been taken on the civilian population. The United Nations came out with a new report just recently saying that 40 percent of the population, that`s about 18 million people, now rely on assistance. But, you know, they also interviewed some others. They said that 7 percent of the population says they don`t want to leave. Alex?\par \par \uc2 WITT: Absolute extraordinary. All of it. Heartbreaking as well. That mother who told her son to leaf and he didn`t, and lost his life. Pretty tragic. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former deputy commander of the United States European command and MSNBC military analyst, Lieutenant General Steph Twitty.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 General, welcome, sir. Your reaction to these targets. They are civilian structures and critical infrastructure