r their land bridge.\par \par \uc2 WITT: General, you heard Matt`s report that President Putin has signed that decree banning crude oil supply from February 1 to any country that has joined the $60 price cap scheme for Russian sea-born oil, as they`re calling it, a scheme. What is his calculous right now?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, this is an interesting move. So it was the \uc2 G7\uc2 , E.U. and Australia that really backed these sanctions here. And Putin turned around and said, OK, I`ll add my own sanctions here and that`s this decree that we`re talking about with the $60 barrel. And so what I believe Putin is doing is counting on Russia, India, Pakistan and others to be able to continue to buy his oil and it`s not impacted by this $60 decree.\par \par \uc2 So he`s pretty much saying, OK, you can have your decree, G1. E.U., I`ll go to my friends, my other countries who`s been buying oil and therefore I won`t lose any sleep of not providing oil to E.U. countries. And what I will tell you is I foresee the price of oil going up in Europe as well as probably the price of oil in Africa and elsewhere as a result of this. And so he may win on this particular move that he`s doing here.\par \par \uc2 WITT: Absolutely extraordinary. Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, sir, thank you so much for your time and expertise.\par \par \uc2 Out of this world, the incredible advancements we made this year in space exploration. That`s next when MSNBC REPORTS continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:45]\par \par \uc2 WITT: In a year that has brought countless stories from around the world, 2022 had a streak of incredible accomplishments in space, from \uc2 NASA\uc2  launching Artemis 1, putting the U.S. on the path to returning to the moon, and the launch of the James Webb telescope bringing new spectacular images from deep space.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Tom Costello has a look back at the year in space.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Three, two, one, boosters and ignition. And liftoff of Artemis 1.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In a year of triumphs for space exploration, NASA`s Artemis moon mission was the headline- grabbing finale. A 25-day, 1.4 million mile test flight of the new Orion spaceship that will one day carry astronauts back to the moon. And 80-mile high, high resolution flyover of the moon, a long orbit deeper into space.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Orion is right on the money, coming right down the pike.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Then a spectacular reentry with the heat shield hitting 5,000 degrees. That`s half the temperature of the sun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And there it is, high over the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Before a gentle parachute drop into the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Splashdown.\par \par \uc2 BEN NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: It is the beginning of the new beginning, and that is to explore the heavens.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: That new beginning will include astronauts on a similar test flight around the moon in 2024. Then a lunar landing in 2025 or `26 with a crew that includes a woman and a person of color. The first return to the moon since those heady days of \uc2 Apollo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Houston, Tranquility base here. The Eagle has landed.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But \uc2 NASA\uc2  is also leaning heavily on private companies. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  now regularly launches both crew and cargo to the ISS.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): This is the view of earth from the International Space Station and the simulator at \uc2 NASA\uc2  in Houston. Outside the Cupola, the blue richness of earth and the blackness, the deep blackness of space. And right there the Canada arm which has reached out to grab an incoming cargo vessel.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): And \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is now working overtime on its starship that will carry astronauts to the moon, then perhaps mars in the late 2030s.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:11]\par \par \uc2 Another huge success in 2022, NASA`s DART mission.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we have impact.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The spacecraft in timelapse traveling at 14,000 miles per hour, slamming into a small asteroid named Dimorphos seven million miles from earth and pushing it ever so slightly off its orbit. A critical success if \uc2 NASA\uc2  hopes to one day deflect an incoming, planet-killing asteroid away from earth.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Now this is a watershed moment for planetary defense and a watershed moment for humanity.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But the most visual space achievement in 2022 were those spectacular images from the deep space James Webb telescope. Using infrared cameras, we`re now looking at light billions of years old, the creation of the universe, distant stars and galaxies, stunning nebulas, begging the question, are we alone?\par \par \uc2 We could have an answer about whether or not there`s life in the universe, which would change everything, right? It would change our entire understanding of what we were and who we are in the universe.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: It`s big and beckoning to a new generation of explorers.\par \par \uc2 Tom Costello, NBC News, Houston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WITT: Well, that was great. And you can all ring in the new year on MSNBC with Tom Costello`s exclusive documentary "Battlefield Space." Tom is going go inside space force, the newest branch of the U.S. Military. That`s midnight on Saturday on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for us this hour. I`m Alex Witt. Thanks so much for watching. I`ll see you again tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern. Hallie Jackson picks up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221229eics00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC Reports for December 27, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Cori Coffin, Blayne Alexander, Michelle Grossman, Marissa Parra,  Sam Brock, Peter Baker, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, Matthew Dowd, Richard Engel,  Ryan Nobles, Glenn Kirschner, Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,194 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Thank you for staying with us. I`m Cori Coffin at MSNBC headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 This hour, we`ll check the national forecast as the outrage continues to grow among \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 ` customers with thousands of new cancellations. Also coming up. A live report from the southern border as the future of tens of thousands of migrants remains in legal limbo. And Dr. \uc2 Peter Hotez\uc2  joins me to discuss tripledemic fears and realities as we approach the new year.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with the persistent problems at our nation`s airports in the spotlight focused on \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  as tens of thousands of their customers face yet another day of waiting to find when they can finally catch a flight home. Joining me now is NBC`s Blayne Alexander at the Atlanta airport. Blayne, chaos continues for travelers today, mostly due to this attempt by Southwest to reset their schedules and staffing after these nationwide cancellations. What are you seeing today?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, we`re certainly seeing a lot of people online, a lot of people are frustrated, and a lot of people trying to figure out when they can get to their destination, Cori. You know, I think that the numbers really tell the story. You talked about the fact that we`ve seen cancellations across the board, but it`s really important to point out that the lion`s share are from \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 . In fact, yesterday, more than 70 percent of all cancellations came from Southwest. Today alone, Southwest has slashed their flight schedule down to just one-third of the amount of the flights that they normally fly. So, we`re talking about more than 2500 cancellations just from that one airline.\par \par \uc2 So, when we`ve spoken to people here in Atlanta, I`d remind -- and keep in mind, this is not the worst of the areas where the backups are happening but you kind of hear people with different variations of travel stories. One person who told me they got a text at 3:00 a.m. saying that the second leg of their trip had been canceled. Now, they`re trying to figure out how to get from point A to point B. Somebody else who had to take a bus -- a 14-hour bus just for a chance to hopefully get on a better flight or get on some flight in a different city.\par \par \uc2 So, across the board, you`re seeing people who really are dealing with days-long travel issues. The ask for Southwest, they said in a statement that they understand -- they acknowledge that this is unacceptable and they`re apologizing to their customers. But they`re also pointing to weather saying that this massive storm that kind of blanketed the country really is what`s gotten them off course. They say that they did everything on their end in terms of putting personnel and staffing where needed. But it`s really important to point out, Cori, that the flight Attendants Union for Southwest and the Pilots Union strongly disputing that. Now, the DOT says they`re going to investigate this too to see whether or not this was actually in the airline`s control, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: And, Blayne, for those passengers who have had their flights canceled, what exactly are they entitled to?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, I think that it depends for so -- let`s say for people who did already have their flights canceled, Southwest says that they can either get a full refund or they can get a flight credit to use at any time in the future. But the bigger question is whether or not the expenses -- the additional expenses that people had to incur will actually be reimbursed. So, we`re talking about hotel fees, taxis, meals, all the things that people had to pay for out of their own pocket while they were stranded.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is where that DOT inquiry comes in because remember, Southwest is one of several major airlines that agreed to reimburse customers for things like that if the flights were canceled for reasons within their control, as the DOT looks into determining whether or not Southwest could have done more, or whether it was simply the weather or something beyond their control. That really will determine what these travelers are entitled to in the days to come, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Right. Any sort of investigation that could come from this -- what`s happening with this Southwest debacle will be really interesting to see because specifically, you would compare how other airlines are handling it versus how Southwest is handling it. And their claim that they did everything they could, is that really true, and what details will come to light coming up in the future?\par \par \uc2 I also find it interesting that you say that it`s actually not the worst spot in the country dealing with these delays and cancellations where you are in Atlanta, but yet it is absolutely massively packed behind you. Just interesting stuff to keep in mind. And we hope that all of those families stay safe as they`re waiting to finally get back home to loved ones. Blayne Alexander, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn real quick to the weather forecast to see how it`s going to look in the next coming days for anybody trying to fly out or even drive out, really. Hey, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GROSSMAN, MSNBC METEOROLOGIST: Hey there, Cori, great to see you again. Well, we`re looking good in the middle of the country. We`re looking good in the east. So that`s good news. But in the West, we are watching a major system that`s bringing an atmospheric river that`s quite literally a river of moisture that`s moving from the Pacific. It`s bringing very heavy rain from Washington all the way down to California, and some really heavy snowfall as well, up to three feet in some spots.\par \par \uc2 So, this is what we`re expecting in terms of the weather with holiday hub impacts. No problems from Boston to Philly, Charlotte, and Miami, so hopefully, that can help them get back on track, as well as Minneapolis, Chicago, Denver, Dallas, Houston. But look at the West. We have orange. That means because these are moderate impacts in Salt Lake City, Las Vegas, Phoenix. Some major impacts are expected in Seattle, San Francisco, Los Angeles. We have the rain. We have the wind. We even have snow in the higher elevations of the mountains. And that will continue on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Notice a very quiet stall in the middle of the country, Houston and Dallas, and then into the east coast as well. Seattle, Salt Lake City, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Phoenix expecting major delays. We could see some minor delays in Denver.\par \par \uc2 Now by Thursday, we are looking at a system that could bring some stronger thunderstorms in portions of the southern plains. Houston, you`re now into the possibility of moderate delays. Still looking at major impacts from Seattle, Salt Lake City, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, and Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 So, get the idea. You`re going to see that storm system, kind of repeat performances as we go throughout Thursday, even Friday. Thursday looking good along the east coast, also the Ohio Valley, but we`re going to start to introduce some rain into the Ohio Valley by Friday, and then eventually, Saturday, we`re going to see some wet weather along the east coast as well.\par \par \uc2 This is what it looks like for today in terms of your forecast. Snow showers continuing along the Great Lakes. We`re not expecting intense snowfall. That`s starting to weaken. We`re looking at those winds switching which is good news. So, could pick up a few more inches downwind of Lake Ontario but not quite as intense, still chilly.\par \par \uc2 And that story is going to change over the next several days. We`re actually going to be above normal as we near the end of the workweek or the holiday week for some of us. Mountain snow will fall in portions of the Cascades, the Sierra Nevada Mountains where we can see up to three feet of snow and heavy, heavy rain. This is going to be a big story.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we need this rain. Yes, they`re in a drought. But it`s coming fast and it`s coming furious. It`s a lot of rain in a short amount of time. So, double-digit rainfall in portions of the Northwest into Southern California today. That will shift off a bit as we go throughout tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 This is what it looks like on the radar. You can see heavy rain falling right now. That`s where you see the brighter colors, the yellows, the reds, the oranges, that`s indicating where we`re seeing heavy rainfall. So, it`s not just the airlines that are going to be impacted. It`s going to be tough to travel on the roadways as well along with that wind-driven rain because we`re going to see winds gusting pretty high.\par \par \uc2 The highest elevations could see 70-mile-per-hour wind gusts. That`s going to move into the Rockies tomorrow. But where you see the blue, that is your snow falling. There`s pink also, which is really tricky when you travel because that`s where we`re seeing the icy conditions.\par \par \uc2 Winter alerts are posted where you see the white winter weather advisories. Winter storm warnings are in your pink. And we`re going to see these alerts continuing into Wednesday. So, it`s not just today, it`s also tomorrow, that`s going to be a little bit prolonged. And here, your wind alerts I was talking about those winds, anywhere from 30 to 40 miles per hour, but certainly could see some winds gusting up to 60 -- 70 miles per hour. That makes it very difficult to travel in, especially with precipitation falling. 16 million people impacted from the northwest into the southwest, the inner Mountain West, and that will move off to the east as we head throughout the middle of the workweek.\par \par \uc2 Snowfall forecasts up to three feet of snow as possible. That`s great news for the skier, it`s not great news for the drivers. And then by tomorrow into the Rockies, we`re seeing to 10 to 18 inches locally, but we could see up to two feet as well. And we`re going to see some snow into the portions of Southwest and the highest elevations of the mountains as well.\par \par \uc2 Rainfall forecasts we could see anywhere from -- we could see double digits, so one to two to three, even up to six inches in some spots, but higher -- locally higher amounts. And we`re going to see the chance for flash flooding, especially where you see those burned areas where they have those fires because it`s just really hard to absorb any of that water.\par \par \uc2 Here`s some good news. We are still chilly today. Chicago below normal, Raleigh, Little Rock. It`s warm out to the West. I`ll just show you this real quick before we leave you. $But we`re going to start to warm up. And then we`re going to be above normal as we head throughout the later part of this week. Back to you, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: All right, a little bit of a warm-up starting.\par \par \uc2 GROSSMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Those are the golden words we wanted to hear, Michelle, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GROSSMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Michelle Grossman for us.\par \par \uc2 GROSSMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: And Michelle also mentioned that it`s going to be dangerous on the roads for parts of the country, so let`s turn now to the ongoing weather emergency not only in Buffalo where temperatures are still freezing there but also in surrounding areas as federal and state and local officials try to respond to this deadly winter event. NBC`s Marisa Parra joining me now. And, Marissa, you`re driving yourself. As we know, some people are stuck at home under driving bans in some neighborhoods, while others are trying to drive themselves out of maybe difficult situations and some really rough roads. You`re trying to get home. What are you seeing today?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, there`s no two ways around it, right? Traveling right now is a nightmare. Whether you`re trying to travel by air, especially if you`re trying to travel by air or if you`re traveling by road.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Which is probably between the two the preferred method? Just anecdotally speaking, I will say that a lot of rental car facilities are at their max. They were already a little short-staffed because of the pandemic -- in short supply rather. And so, they`re seeing large numbers of people with canceled flights flocking to their facilities, trying to hit the roads like where we are.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve been making our way from Cleveland to Buffalo because obviously, the buffalo airport is not open. It`s not expected to open until tomorrow possibly, to be determined on that one. So, as we`re making our way through Ohio, through Pennsylvania, we`re now in New York about an hour or so away from Buffalo. The roads are looking good where we are. We see a thin dusting and coating of snow but otherwise, I-90 is looking good.\par \par \uc2 We do know, however, as we get closer to Buffalo, that will be a different story. We know that there is a passage, that I-90 Corridor in the Buffalo area that is closed off. There are several reasons for not just having roads closed, but a driving ban in effect. It`s not just for your safety, but that`s also so that crews can do what they need to do.\par \par \uc2 We know that this has been a deadly storm. There have been people who have died as a result of being in their cars -- being stuck in their cars. And in general, we know that at least 20 people have died in Buffalo City alone. Across the counties, overall, in Erie and Niagara County, it`s looking at about 35. And that number is expected to rise.\par \par \uc2 So, crews need to get to where they need to be whether it`s emergency vehicles, getting to people who are having emergency events. And in general, they also need to work on clearing the roads. So, please whatever you`re doing, wherever you are, just obey your local orders. The driving ban is in effect in the Buffalo area. But for here where we are right now, roads are looking good. We`ll continue to keep you updated with what we see. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Marissa Parra for us this afternoon, Marissa, thank you, and drive safe.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead. COVID cases and deaths are up across the country. We`ll talk to a doctor about how best to protect yourself this holiday season. But first, thousands of migrants are stuck in limbo at the border, many sleeping on the streets in freezing conditions. What the Supreme Court could do today that could prolong the crisis?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Tens of thousands of asylum seekers are waiting in freezing temperatures to hear the Supreme Court`s decision on Title 42 today. That`s a Trump-era COVID policy that has been keeping them from entering the United States. Joining me now from El Paso, Texas is NBC`s Sam Brock, also with me is Chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and MSNBC political analyst, Peter Baker. Thank you for both being in today. Sam, let`s begin with you. Tell us what you`re seeing there.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: What I just saw in the last 10 minutes is pretty amazing, and I think illustrative, Cori, of what we`re seeing on the ground here in El Paso. This truck just pulled in. And all of these folks, there was no one here, I`m talking about hundreds of people from all over the world really just came up in line right now. And what are they waiting for? They`re waiting for food.\par \par \uc2 These are folks that have been standing outside, as you said overnight in frigid conditions 20s, and 30s for the last several weeks, and this represents an opportunity right here, at least to be fed for their families, OK? That just gives you a window into the desperation right now. And also, the efforts from local community groups, whether it`s churches, or charities, NGOs, to try to pull their resources together to help people right now who are so direly in need of that assistance.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m just going to give you another glance right now, which is all of the folks who are standing out here right now. And as I have conversations with people, Cori, generally speaking, they tend to be coming from Venezuela and Honduras. One of the reasons for that is the Title 42, the way that it`s applied, those are two countries where there is automatic expulsion if you`re trying to request asylum. In others like Cuba or Nicaragua, for whatever reason, it`s applied inconsistently, those residents can apply for asylum. So, you`re just seeing all of these families piling throughout the streets here in El Paso.\par \par \uc2 And I had a conversation, Cori, a little while ago with a mom from Honduras, and her five kids 8, 5, 3, 2, and 1. I spoke with her and her husband about what this process has been like, and also what they would like to achieve after trekking across thousands of miles just to get here. This is what her husband told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: A roof over their head and a job to be able to provide for their kids. That`s what so many people are explaining as the root cause of why they`re here. And so many of them also know, Cori, there were rumblings about Title 42 being lifted. They have the date in their head of December 25 because that`s when it was supposed to expire. And, of course, the Supreme Court stepped in and issued a stay. And now, we are all waiting to find out whether or not the Supreme Court is going to take any action today or not. So, we`ll find out in a matter of hours, Cori.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Food, warmth, and health for their children, I`m sure is now the immediate need that they have. Sam Brock, thank you very much for your reporting at the border.\par \par \uc2 Now, Peter, does the White House have a plan to minimize the chaos if Title 42 is lifted today?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, they say they do. They say they`ve been preparing for this. And they`ve been trying to move resources to be prepared. But I don`t think anybody could be prepared for the kind of crowd you`re seeing there.\par \par \uc2 That kind of influx is just enormous, obviously. And it`s a humanitarian crisis, one that has not, you know, occupied the major discussion here in Washington. Obviously, it`s used as a partisan wedge between Democrats and Republicans. Republicans criticizing President Biden for allowing this crisis to begin with, in their view. But the truth is, it`s -- you know, it -- for people on the ground there, for people who are waiting for a chance to come into the United States who were sacrificing everything for that opportunity, it`s a humanitarian crisis and one that has not really been fully, you know, realized here, I think in Washington.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: 19 Republican-led states have been fighting the White House on lifting Title 42 for months now. Is the White House at all concerned that this could become a central line of attack for 2024?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Oh, definitely. Well, they know that. They understand that. There are -- their view -- this politics -- the politics of this is that it animates, obviously people on the right. And that the less they talk about it, the less it will influence potentially people in the middle, the ones they want to keep on their side.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right. I mean, it`s a flashpoint. It was obviously a big issue in the last election we just had last month. It was propelled a lot of voters who did vote for Republicans, obviously not as many Republicans voted as people expected. And so, I think the White House feels that there`s some comfort in that regard. They -- that people view this administration as one that`s trying to find a reasonable balance here between the harsh tactics of the Trump administration and the reality on the ground there. But it`s a political tinderbox, no question about it.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Peter Baker, thank you very much for joining us. And once again, thank you to Sam Brock for his reporting in El -- in El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s turn to our panel. Joining me now is the founder of Country Over Party Matthew Dowd, and \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2  columnist and co-host of sisters- in-law podcast, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, thank you both being in -- for being in. They are both MSNBC political analysts. Kimberly, let`s begin with you. What options does the Supreme Court have today?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: So, a couple of things can happen. The Supreme Court can decide that they`re not going to wait into this battle and take this case up, in which case the state would be lifted and the order, Title 42 would also be lifted with those restrictions, or the Supreme Court can decide that they will take this case up, oral arguments will be scheduled in the months ahead, and in the meantime, likely they would to keep everything at status quo, leave this day in place. Either way, it`s not going to solve what is happening at the border. What we`re seeing is that`s really the human tool for what this legal and political battle that is playing out is having on real people in real-time.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: How did we get here, Kimberly? And when the court decides, do you believe that it would be the end of this saga? I think you just alluded to the fact that it would not.\par \par \uc2 STOHR: No, it would not be the end of this saga at all. Because whether or not section -- Title 42 is lifted, you`re still going to see the surge of people who came here fleeing terrible conditions with the promise of something brighter ahead that they thought would meet. It`s not going to deter -- whatever happens at the court is not going to deter that. You`re going to either have this crisis at the border, or you`re going to have an increase in the number of people crossing, coming in, and making asylum claims on an overly already tax system that is been crying out for immigration reform for decades and it just doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a long -- we didn`t just get here overnight. This is the result of the inability to address on a legislative level immigration something that both parties are incentivized to do and should do but it`s become such a political -- a politicized fight that they haven`t been able to. The Supreme Court will not solve it with this one order, but we don`t know exactly what will. And with a divided Congress coming in, there`s a little hope.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Yes. Speaking of that, Matthew, we have seen several escalations in that battle between Republican state officials and the Biden administration over the country`s immigration policy. Most recently, Texas Governor Greg Abbott sending several busloads of migrants to the residence of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Washington, DC on Christmas Eve. Are Republicans on the right side of this issue?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: You mean, are they on the right side of the issue of abandoning people`s humanity and not doing anything for them and using them as political pawns? No.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: When you put it that way.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Now that I say that. I mean, I think what the Republicans -- what we have, and I`m going to carry on to what was Kimberly said in this, is it`s there`s a complete incentive for Republicans not to do anything about this and carry it on as a political football because they know among their base, they`re absolutely engaged on this issue of having no immigration reform and building a border wall. That`s what the Republicans want. So, as long as this issue continues, it`s politically helpful for the Republicans for their base.\par \par \uc2 For the Democrats, I think the problem is, is they have no ability to get Republican votes to do comprehensive immigration reform. And so, if they wait into it with no solution, then it becomes just another thing they can`t get done because the Republicans won`t go along with it. I remember full well -- and I worked for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , as you know, he tried to do comprehensive immigration reform and it was killed by the members of his home -- of his own party in this.\par \par \uc2 And I would just advise people watching this to understand why it`s so not only this humanitarian crisis, as Peter and Kimberly alluded to, but the idea that this country fundamentally needs immigration reform for the economy, for the people here, for the families here that are divided in this, we have 11 million jobs in this country, unfilled today. And the idea that we can`t do immigration reform to solve multiple problems is, to me, such a sign of the dysfunction in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Yes. And on one hand, that is a solution that you can see clearly. And on the other hand, you have these people who are human beings. And to continue to politicize it, and if one party continues to feel that feeding that chaos helps their messaging, it`s just almost unbelievable to realize that these people are being used as pawns.\par \par \uc2 And, Matthew, before we go, I do want to turn now to the question of Congressman George Santos, and if he is still going to be incoming and this resume that wasn`t. What`s your reaction to this? And ultimately, who bears responsibility for vetting or failing to vet him as a candidate? Is it Republicans because he is one of theirs, or is it, Democrats, for failing to do the oppo -- the right oppo research?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, I think it`s all of the above, and different responsibility is on this, definitely there was political malpractice by Democrats involved in the campaign not to have highlighted this and brought this up to voters` attention, absolutely. But in the end, it`s the Republicans and who they nominate and this candidate. I was thinking about this today this morning, and I was thinking when a time in America, we need Mr. Smith goes to Washington, we get the talented Mr. Ripley showing up on January 3, in the course of this.\par \par \uc2 And so, I -- if you -- we had any self-regulation of people -- of Republicans in this, they would self-regulate George Santos would realize I shouldn`t ally, I feel bad about it, I made everything up, I shouldn`t serve, I`m going to resign. That won`t happen because again, there`s a lack of self-regulation. And the Republicans have no political incentive on their own in this immediacy because McCarthy needs every vote he can get. So, the Democrats at this point won`t be able to do anything about it. But my guess is -- I`ll make a prediction. George Santos` tenure in Congress is a short one.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: You know, our panels at the last hour also brought up the question of any sort of legal implications with some of the filings that he had done. So, we`re going to hold you to that prediction, and we`ll see how this plays out and bring you guys back on for more on all of this.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Matthew Dowd and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 The war in Ukraine has been raging on for almost a year now and there`s no end in sight. NBC`s Richard Engel takes a look at how it all started, and the one thing that Putin didn`t expect. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:06]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Doctors across the country are once again sounding the alarm about the worsening tripledemic with COVID-related hospitalizations up nearly 7 percent and COVID-related deaths up 15 percent. As rising rates of COVID, RSV and the flu push hospitals to their breaking point, major pharmacies like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  are being forced to put a purchasing limit on pediatric cold, flu and fever medications.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Dr. \uc2 Peter Hotez\uc2 , co-director of the Center for Vaccine Development at \uc2 Texas Children`s Hospital\uc2  and dean of the National School of Tropical Medical at \uc2 Baylor College of Medicine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hotez, welcome in. How worrying are these new statistics?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 PETER HOTEZ\uc2 , DEAN, NATIONAL SCHOOL OF TROPICAL MEDICAL, BAYLOR COLLEGE OF MEDICINE: Well, we`re kind of seeing a mixed picture actually across the country. The amount of influenza activity is actually starting to now decline. It`s still a pretty high screaming level. For the last few weeks, it has started to go down so potentially that`s good news. Unless it does bump up again in 2023. But because the level is still high, we`re strongly encouraging people to get your influenza vaccination if you haven`t gotten it yet.\par \par \uc2 So, although flu is starting to go down, there`s a commensurate rise in the number of hospitalizations from COVID-19 so we`re starting to see those climb again. And the worry of course is that it could start to resemble something like it did in 2021 with the big Alpha winter wave or 2022 with the big B.A.1 Omicron wave. I`m hoping that doesn`t happen, but here`s something that we have to keep in mind.\par \par \uc2 In the northeast right now about half of those virus isolates from COVID cases are the XBB variant which has a lot of immune escape properties. So the reason you need to know that is the bivalent vaccine may have some cross protective immunity against that XBB variant that you don`t see with the original vaccine. So that`s a long-winded way of saying get your bivalent booster, if you`ve not gotten it yet, because I`m really quite concerned about this XBB subvariant in the northeast, which is now about half the cases.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Among these surges, it`s not too late to get your flu shot and it`s certainly not too late to get your booster. What can parents and family do in general to stay safe, as many of us are still gathering for the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Well, just again, I think we`re under-vaccinated as a population certainly with respect to boosters, so if you look at those who are going into hospitals now, they are not only the unvaccinated, but those who failed to get a recent booster so if you`re more than four, five, six months out of your last booster, you have some vulnerability there. So that`s the number one priority in terms of your COVID booster, get your flu shot.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, we`re doing a pretty poor job at vaccinating your kids against COVID. And we are seeing a lot of pediatric hospitalizations as well, so my advice has been take as many of these illnesses off the table as you can. There`s about seven or eight different virus and bacterial pathogens out there now. Not only including what we call the tri-demic or the triple epidemic, RSV, influenza and COVID, but also metapneumovirus, and rhinovirus and also pneumococcal bacterial pathogens. So -- and strep A. So the more you can take off the table through vaccinations, the better.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: All right. Really quick while we have you, Doctor. Speaking of being under vaccinated, 81 kids in Columbus, Ohio, have tested positive for the measles driven by vaccine hesitancy. How concerning is this to you with much of the country already fighting this trifecta of the COVID, the flu and RSV?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Yes. I`m worried about measles and particularly as we head into late winter, early spring because historically that`s when we would see measles season, a peak in the pre-vaccine era. You know, all of that horrible antivaccine activism that caused so many people to needlessly lose their lives because our refusing COVID-19 vaccine, that`s now spilling over into other childhood vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 So you`re starting to hear the same kind of phony baloney, health freedom propaganda rhetoric now being used around childhood vaccinations. So I am worried that we could see a return of measles or whooping cough or even polio. And so this is going to be a big challenge I think in 2023.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Dr. \uc2 Peter Hotez\uc2 , thank you. It`s sobering numbers. Certainly are. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Nearly nine million Ukrainians are without power according to President Zelenskyy thanks to Russia`s assault.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Richard Engel takes a look back at the war and the fierce resistance by Ukrainians and a warning that some of what you`re about to see may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In the twilight hours of February 24th, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  took to Russian state television to announce a special military operation that aimed to demilitarize and de-Nazify Ukraine. Minutes later, explosions rocked Ukraine`s capital Kyiv. Missiles rained down across the country and Russia`s massive military moved across the border.\par \par \uc2 Putin`s special operation was clearly war. Although Putin had long denied his intentions to invade his neighbor, he`d openly claimed Ukraine was part of Russia and demonized Ukraine`s leaders. Putin had already illegally annexed Ukrainian territory, the Crimean Peninsula. in 2014 with little international backlash. This time condemnation came quickly.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Putin`s choice to make a totally unjustifiable war on Ukraine will have left Russia weaker and the rest of the world stronger.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Wide-ranging sanctions were rolled out, but they did little to deter Russia`s military 10 times the size of Ukraine`s moved swiftly to surround Kyiv, but almost immediately, cracks in the mighty Russian army began to appear. Their advances stalled. The Ukrainians organized and fought back. Civilians banded together. Many took up arms to defend their country.\par \par \uc2 It seemed \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  hadn`t accounted for this, the will of the Ukrainian people led by an unlikely hero, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . The nation rallied behind the comedian turned politician, now wartime leader.\par \par \uc2 Facing resistance and failing to capture Kyiv, Russia focused its attacks on eastern Ukraine. Moscow called it a change of strategy. For the Ukrainians it was a major victory, but any celebrations were short lived. As Russian forces withdrew from around Kyiv, evidence of atrocities and war crimes on a massive scale emerged. First in Bucha, the pattern would continue in other liberated areas.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians officials documented 50,000 alleged Russian war crimes. Russia has repeatedly denied its soldiers are responsible.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Why do you think this is happening that there are now thousands of allegations of Russian war crime here, thousands of them?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: Something was, you know, something was broken with mentality. With them.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voice-over): In the east and in the south, Russia`s bloody campaign continued with Putin`s forces firing missiles and artillery to hammer towns and cities, often indiscriminately. U.S. officials estimate tens of thousands of Ukrainian civilians have been killed and 100,000 Russian troops killed or wounded.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian have soldiered on, backed by international support, including $20 billion in weapons and military assistance from the United States. Ukrainian troops have used it to launch a major counteroffensive, liberating large parts of eastern Ukraine and then the southern city of Kherson, occupied by Moscow for eight months. As the Ukrainian military moved in, soldiers were given a hero`s welcome.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy called the liberation of Kherson the beginning of the end of this war.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): How are you feeling today, Mr. President? How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Very well.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: How is this moment for you?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: The moment is very important. That is the biggest city we lost, occupied since 24th of, you know, February. So that is biggest city. And now it`s free. So Ukraine came so I`m happy.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voice-over): As the conflict nears one year, there`s still no end in sight. Ukrainians fear the world is losing interest in the war as Putin employs a brutal new tactic, mass targeting Ukraine`s infrastructure just as temperatures plummet.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:09]\par \par \uc2 He couldn`t take Ukraine as completely or quickly as he`d hoped. Now it seems he`s trying to starve and freeze the country into submission.\par \par \uc2 Richard Engel, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Our thanks to Richard Engel for that report.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, after years of wrangling, the House Ways and Means Committee is set to release former President Trump`s tax returns, but they might reveal how they may affect the investigations into him.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Donald Trump`s long awaited tax returns could be released by the House Ways and Means Committee at any moment now. This after a vote along party lines last week and after a year`s long battle over the returns that went all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And Ryan, we know the committee was critical of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  for failing to audit the former president. What new information are we hoping to learn from these documents that could be released at any moment?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, there`s so much that could potentially be in these documents that we haven`t been able to comb through. So far all we have is a lengthy report from the Joint Taxation committee that kind of outlined their concerns with what they discovered in the Trump taxes, but, you know, there`s still a lot that we don`t know about charitable donations, how exactly his business paid taxes and filed taxes.\par \par \uc2 These are all complex questions that we`ll have to be pored over to really determine whether or not there`s anything that was inappropriate that was done. Of course we`re still waiting on those tax returns to come out. And the committee hasn`t given us real indication when we might see them.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: All right. Ryan Nobles, very quick reporting on your part, waiting for those documents. We`ll return to you as soon as those are released.\par \par \uc2 We want to go over to Glenn Kirschner now to kind of break all this down. He`s a former federal prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, welcome in. All right. Lots to talk about here. Trump has said that not paying his taxes makes him smart. So is the American tax system part of the problem?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, I don`t think there`s anybody who would say the \uc2 IRS\uc2  code couldn`t use a good going over to see if some loopholes could be closed. But we`ve learned some really disturbing things already, Cori, even though we haven`t seen the tax returns yet. I mean, we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  year after year while in office was saying that he can`t release his tax returns to the American people because he`s under audit.\par \par \uc2 And we have learned based on some reporting that that was not true. That was not accurate. In fact, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  under the, you know, Trump-appointed commissioner Charles Redding declined or refused or neglected to conduct the mandatory annual audits of Donald Trump`s tax return so it turns out that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not being straight with the American people about why he was continuing to hide his tax returns.\par \par \uc2 And I would add that his \uc2 IRS\uc2  commissioner Charles Redding never once corrected the record. And then I think adding insult to the American people`s injury is that, you know, Charles Redding or the \uc2 IRS\uc2  ultimately said, well, we didn`t really have the time or the resources to conduct the mandatory annual audits of Donald Trump`s tax returns, but what we all know is that they have plenty of time and more than enough resources to conduct discretionary audits, nonmandatory audits of some of Donald Trump`s avowed enemies like \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2  and \uc2 Andrew McCabe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So none of this, Cori, really passes the smell test. And I think it will be interesting to see what it is in those tax returns that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was so determined to hide from the American people.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: It is sus, as the kids call it these day. I just learned what that is so I had to use it on air.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, I don`t know if this is a question for you necessarily or if it`s for Ryan Nobles, who we might get back on here in just a moment. But the committee is suggesting changes to \uc2 IRS\uc2  auditing rules. But that would require legislation, no?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, and I think legislation is in order. You know, I actually think there should be some legislation considered by Congress to require some transparency, some disclosures of presidential candidates when it comes to their finances and their tax returns. There are, you know, constitutional purists who will say, well, wait a minute, that`s not one of the requirements in the Constitution for somebody to run for the highest office in the land, but I don`t think that means Congress should walk away from at least an attempt to legislate some transparency, some disclosure provisions, and then see if that legislation can pass constitutional muster.\par \par \uc2 If it does, great. If it doesn`t, what I think Congress should do is learn from the court`s opinion if they get cut back, if they kind of step the toe over the constitutional line. Retool the legislation and try it again because if you don`t try to legislate some transparency, we are forever running the risk of being exactly where we are right now with Donald Trump`s tax returns.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: And the frustration is no matter how much that makes sense in 2023 with the split Congress, it may get exactly nowhere. How about all of these legal troubles and all these investigations that Trump has been in for years? The Trump legal troubles have multiplied over the past few months. So with the documents set to be released, what do you think poses the biggest threat to the former president?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, it`s a great question. Will the release of these tax returns do anything to forward the investigative ball on any of the many criminal investigations that are up and running into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You know, the election crimes investigation in Georgia, probably not the federal investigations of the insurrection or the Mar-a-Lago documents. Probably not. I think the most likely candidate with these tax returns might become relevant is the ongoing investigation being conducted by the Manhattan District Attorney`s office because they just sort of famously won criminal convictions of Allen Weisselberg, Donald Trump`s chief financial officer, for basically what was a tax fraud scheme.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:09]\par \par \uc2 And of course they also convicted, the jury did, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  of similar crimes so it seems like perhaps whatever we see in these tax returns might be relevant to the investigation being conducted by the New York state authorities.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: OK. That could be an interesting update there, and real quick with the last few seconds that we have with you, Glenn, with the changeover in Congress coming, looming really, what do you think the timeline could be for the January 6th Committee to turn over their documents and hope for any sort of outcome here before the Republicans take control of the House?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, it seems like come January 3rd, I think that is when the J6 Committee will likely no longer exist so it seems like we don`t have a lot of time. If they`re going to be releasing additional transcripts, any additional findings or informing the American people in any other way, it looks like they need to get on that right quick because come January, it seems like, you know, we`re not going to be getting anything else from the J6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: All right. We`re coming up right on to the deadline for that.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirschner, we appreciate it. Thank you very much. And we`ll have you on standby as we`re waiting for that breaking news throughout the day here for those Trump tax returns to be released at any moment.\par \par \uc2 And up next, another attack at a power station in the Tacoma area of Washington. What police are now looking for when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:20]\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: Local and federal law enforcement are investigating a series of Christmas Day attacks on electrical power substations in Washington state. They come just weeks after substations in North Carolina were attacked with gunfire. Officials believe it could be the latest in a series of attacks on the nation`s power grid.\par \par \uc2 Here`s NBC`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It happened early Christmas morning. Three power substations in the Tacoma area targeted and vandalized. Another on Christmas night. Arriving officers found blue flames, a perimeter fence cut, and all 14,000 people left without power, Amanda Clark lost electricity and her well-water.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA CLARK, RESIDENT: Scary in our small, little community that something like that would happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re going to be investigating to see if this was coordinated by a specific group or people.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  issued a warning this month after power companies in Oregon and southern Washington were attacked using hand tools, arson, firearms and metal chains possibly in response to an online call for attacks on critical infrastructure. In North Carolina, substations were attacked with gunfire, knocking out power to 45,000 homes and businesses.\par \par \uc2 A Homeland Security terrorism advisory warned in November that domestic actors and foreign terrorist organizations maintain a visible presence online to motivate supporters to conduct attacks in the homeland.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  seems concerned that domestic terrorism is encouraging this online. What`s the motivation?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: They`re looking for chaos and destabilization because they believe the government is oppressive. They believe the government is what keeps everything going and they don`t like the way things are going.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COFFIN: My thanks to Tom Costello for that reporting.\par \par \uc2 And that will do it for us at this hour. I`m Cori Coffin. Hallie Jackson picks us up with more news after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221228eicr00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for December 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Shaquille Brewster, Marissa Parra, Catie Beck,  Bill Karins, Julie Tsirkin, Barbara McQuade, Sahil Kapur, Kevin Cirilli,  Zinhle Essamuah, Dough Heye \par \uc2 8,012 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hey, everybody, good to see you. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian, live for you here from Miami.\par \par \uc2 The blockbuster January 6 report is out. One of the most wide-ranging and highly-anticipated committee investigations in modern history. We know they recommend that Trump face criminal charges but what about the dozens and dozens of government officials, lawyers, and others who helped set the stage for January 6? What happens to them?\par \par \uc2 Plus, powerful snowstorms and life-threatening cold blanketing a huge part of this country. wind chills at 32 below in Minneapolis, just 10 degrees in Houston, Texas. In Denver, it went from 42 to five in an hour. The latest in the massive storm, the cold, and how it`s making a mess of people`s holiday plans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was really chaos. It was back and forth. And one minute, there`s a fight at 8:30 -- or 6:45, and then a minute it`s gone.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And finally, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy is back in the capital city of Kyiv following his trip to Washington. He left with the promise of billions more in aid but with Republicans taking control of the House in just a couple of weeks, that may be a lot tougher to get going forward.\par \par \uc2 I want to go though, begin with this treacherous winter storm causing chaos and disruption all across the country. More than 200 million people are under some kind of weather advisory at this hour. Just take a look at just how little visibility this one driver had on the road in Iowa this morning. Wow. And it`s not just there. In Erie County, New York, it is so dangerous. They just issued a driving ban for a population approaching 1 million.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring an NBC Shaq Brewster from Benton Harbor, Michigan. Marissa Parra is driving on I-95 in northeast Maryland. NBC`s Catie Beck from Reagan National Airport in Virginia. Also, with us NBC News meteorologist Bill Karins as well.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Shaq. Let me just get started with you because it feels like you`re in the most kind of treacherous of positions. You`re in Benton Harbor as I mentioned. The windshield there, it`s cold enough to cause frostbite as we`re learning in just as little as 10 minutes. And after you`re on "THE TODAY SHOW" I believe earlier today you tweeted out. You all, help. Take us there, Shaq.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, I`m still waiting for that help because I`m still here. But I`ll tell you, you have officials are -- when they are warning to -- for folks to stay inside, to stay off the road, it`s very clear why that is. I`m sure you see the wind whipping around me right now. And it`s not just the snow. You know, when you look at the radar, there are periods of time in which it seems that the snow takes a bit of a break. But you can`t tell because the wind is just whipping the snow that is on the ground.\par \par \uc2 You talk about the visibility issues. I`ll have us move over behind me there. That is a road -- that is a road where there have been cars going through every once in a while and you see how low the visibility is. And that`s the concern as people are on the highways, as they see the snow totals and say hey, I`m used to this, they then go on the roads in the -- because of the wind picking up the snow and blowing it around, that`s where the danger lies. So, that`s why officials across this region are saying stay off the roads, stay inside, the worst is still to come.\par \par \uc2 We know winds are expected to pick up even more later this afternoon. We talked about the snow. There`s supposed to be more snow into Christmas Day.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: So, they`re expecting up to two feet in Benton Harbor, more than two feet in some other areas, so they`re being told to hunker down and at a certain point, I`ll start listening to that as well.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Here`s me helping you, Shaq. Get inside. I`ll let you go. Go inside that van of yours. You`re probably standing pretty close -- hopefully standing pretty close to your last shot.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Thank you, Shaq. Please stay safe if you will. Marissa, let me come to you on this one. You`re on the road there, I 95 How are the conditions changing there this afternoon?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, you know, I almost feel guilty but it looks as good as it does right now after watching Shaq out there just outbattling the elements which look pretty severe. So, I want to show you the conditions here because it is sunny. The roads are looking clear, traffic seems to be moving at least where we are at a steady decent clip. But I want to walk you through the last 24 hours because what you are not seeing right now is all of the rain that the Northeast was really just getting pummeled by. I mean, there was definitely some flood advisories. We know Rhode Island was seeing some heavy flooding.\par \par \uc2 But what you missed in the last, I would say, a couple of hours here, we got that arctic front. Now, it did not hit us like we`re seeing in the Midwest. But we did see a couple of things happen.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We watched the temperature drop by about 15 degrees. Very suddenly within the course of one hour, we saw those temperatures dip below freezing. Those you know can, of course, create very less than-desirable conditions on the road. So, thankfully, we did see people taking it slow as they were driving. And we know this is in the middle of a very busy time on the roads. We`re expecting 102 million drivers.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: This is the preferred method of travel for holidays, not just Thanksgiving, but of course, Christmas as well. So, the good news is we haven`t seen anything treacherous like what we`ve seen in the Midwest. People are taking it slow. However, one thing to keep in mind for those of you who are driving, just be careful because we know that this can be deadly.\par \par \uc2 We saw one instance of a deadly situation out of Missouri. So, if you`re driving in the northeast, we know Buffalo is getting hit hard. Just be very careful on those roads, as you`re making your way to your Christmas travel plans.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, Marissa -- yes, Marissa, I`ll tell you are in a better position than Shaq is. However, with those plunging temperatures, of course, and all of that rain that they had just a couple of hours ago, it certainly is still very much a dicey situation out there on the roadways. Thank you for that. I`ll let you go, Marissa.\par \par \uc2 Let me come to you, Catie. Just in the last hour, we have seen more and more cancellations, right? You got a lot of folks wanting to get home for the holidays amidst all this. And this is really mucking up some folks` plans as they`re trying to move around the country here ahead of Christmas. What more are you learning there?\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely -- yes, absolutely. The timing could not be worse. This is the biggest two travel days of the holiday season, yesterday and today. And already this morning, we were aware that more than 5000 flights had been canceled, 500 out of Chicago just this morning. And we were aware of some ground stops happening in major cities this morning too, Denver, Charlotte, Seattle, even Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 Since that time, some of those ground stops have lifted, so it looks like planes are landing and taking off from here again. But that still doesn`t say much in terms of cancellations and delays which are going to happen in almost every city in the country, regardless because of all the connections. So, there is a pretty big log jam out there now.\par \par \uc2 But passengers, believe it or not, when you talk to them, I don`t know if it`s just the holiday spirit or some optimism that they are eventually going to get home. They don`t seem to be too down about it. Here`s what some of them had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re only actually here because our flight that was supposed to be yesterday got canceled yesterday, and it was -- they rebooked us for today. So, it`s --\par \par \uc2 PARRA: But now you`re delayed again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. So, it`s been a few days. So, all in all, it`ll be about three days of travel to go spend five days with family. And it`s family so that in itself means something, craziness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BECK: Yes, so a lot of folks just hoping they can get home, just hoping they can see their family. And there`s still one more day of a buffer here as tomorrow could prove to be a pretty clear travel day. The problem is you`re going to have a lot of people rescheduling those canceled flights and they`re going to be trying to get on already booked flights.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BECK: So, tomorrow could be a bit hairy at the airports as well.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I got to say, I`m not sure that last one really wanted to get home as she talks about her family there. But at least she was being honest. We all have those feelings when it comes to spending time with family around the holidays. There`s always a hiccup here or there, she might enjoy being at the airport a little longer it seems. All right, Catie, thank you. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Right.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Bill Karins, walk us across the entire country because everybody is feeling it from the West Coast all the way over the East Coast. And if you can, Bill, do this on what`s happened in Buffalo and New York, because I`m going to speaking with the mayor there in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, MSNBC METEOROLOGIST: All right. And Buffalo is by far the most extreme of any location that`s going to go through the weather over the next 48 hours. I mean, that`s the bottom line. Even right now, Buffalo has got a wind gust just last hour of 69 miles per hour. That`s heading up their deaths like strong Tropical Storm gusts, and they`re going to go through that for three straight days. It`s snowing hard. They just said at the airport, it`s zero visibility.\par \par \uc2 Usually, like you -- they all give you a report and say a half mile in fog or quarter mile eighth of a mile, it`s very rare to have zero visibility. And somebody telling him shutting down the roads telling everyone to stay where they are. And then maybe through the whole day tomorrow too on Christmas Eve. So, yes, that`s huge disruptions to your life and to the safety if you get trapped.\par \par \uc2 So, the winds are cranking all through the area. The extreme arctic air just went through New York City, the cold front, and now all that cold air is blowing in the middle of a fearful that it rained a lot around New York City, in northern New Jersey, and Connecticut areas. And now the cold air is coming. And you can already see some of that rains changing the snow. So, by the evening rush hour, be careful on those untreated surfaces which will be most of them now -- you know, it`s hard to treat surfaces when it`s raining.\par \par \uc2 And those wind chill temperatures now we`re down to 27 in DC, earlier today we were in the 50s. You see the single digits back through the Great Lakes and now the temperatures are going to crash in New England. That`s why I`m worried about the icy roads there. Everybody else has already frozen solid. Snowfall, it`s not going to be a lot yet to be near Lake Michigan where Shaq was located, near Benton Harbor, or from Cleveland to Erie up to Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And we could see the possibility, especially coming off of Lake Erie and Lake Ontario where we`re going to see snow reports. You know this is -- this is typical Tug Hill Plateau area, three to four feet. And in Buffalo itself and the Southtowns, two to three feet of snow with those strong gusty winds and that`s why it could be one of the worst blizzards in that area since 1985. And that`s a long time ago. They`ve had a lot of storms since then but this one may be one of the worst ever.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: And the wind chills, we finally just dropped some of them here. Let me go all the way down to South Florida almost. Miami`s not in the wind chill advisory, but it`s close. And so, as we go throughout tonight and tomorrow, that cold air is now starting to head even into the sunshine state of all places, Yasmin, so yes. You know, we don`t have a lot of travel issues on the roads and airports in the South. It`s mostly confined to the Great Lakes, in the Northeast, and the northwest today.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, I got to tell you. I`m in Miami right now visiting my in- laws for the holidays, and folks who are a little worried about the plunging temperatures that they`re going to be anticipating or they`re going to be seeing just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: They`re worried about if they can --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: We`re not used to cold --\par \par \uc2 KARINS: They`re worried about their outfits. That`s what they`re worried about. They`re worried about their not having their outfits look good.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Bill Karins, thank you as always, my friend. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, joining me on the phone is Buffalo, New York Mayor Byron Brown. He just issued a state of emergency for his city. Mayor Brown, thanks for joining us on this. We appreciate it. Let`s say you just heard my colleague Bill Karins talking about the situation that you guys were dealing with there, one of the worst weather situations that we`re going to be seeing across this country in the next 48 hours or so. We`re being told you have zero visibility -- zero visibility and you`re encouraging folks not even to get on the roads at this point. What do you want people to know, Mayor?\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN, MAYOR OF BUFFALO, NEW YORK (voiceover): This is not a usual snowfall for buffalo. It is as advertised. These are blizzard conditions. 59 miles per hour winds. In fact, the winds are whipping so hard. People can feel their homes moving.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We oppose -- impose the driving ban in Buffalo at 9:30 a.m. this morning. We do not want people driving all day to day. We don`t want them driving tomorrow, Saturday. We`ve had reports of some people that have gone out not following the guidance and have gotten stuck in their vehicles, that not only puts them at risk, it puts emergency first responders at risk. The wind has brought trees down, which have brought power lines down. In one case, a power line came down and started a house on fire.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: So, these are extreme conditions. And what was forecasted is definitely hitting Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Mayor, you got power outages also happening. And sure, we`ll likely -- there will likely be more to come. What resources at this point do you have available to you, especially considering those frigid temperatures?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We have a close relationship with that power company, \uc2 National Grid\uc2 . They have 2800 personnel working in Buffalo and across Western New York. There were a high of 3600 power outages in the city of Buffalo. That has been reduced by over 600 so far. So, \uc2 National Grid\uc2  is working hard. But all of these in -- all of these people that are out working, emergency first responders, those who are working to restore power, they have to be safe as they do that.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. You guys are just hit with another historic storm. It`s added again. Do you feel prepared for what is to take place over the next few days?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We`re as prepared as you can be. We`re coordinating with the state of New York, the county of Erie, I`ve been in constant communication all day with the state commissioner, of Homeland Security, and Emergency Services, as well as the state commissioner of the Department of Transportation. So, we are prepared. But these are pretty extreme blizzard conditions.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: And even Buffalo is not used to getting this kind of weather.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. We are looking at some images coming out of your area right now, Mayor, and it looks very intense there. We encourage folks to heed the warnings there, stay home, and stay safe during this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Mayor Byron Brown, we appreciate you. And if you will, Sir, please stay in touch with us throughout this thing as things progress. Thank you, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We sure will. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Coming up, everybody. The fallout from the release of the January 6 report today, what we`re learning from the more than 800 pages of findings, and how former President Trump is now reacting to what is inside. Plus, Mitch McConnell`s new warning to president -- former President Trump about 2024. You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, welcome back. The newly released January 6 report pins the blame for the insurrection squarely on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying that he led a "multipart conspiracy to overturn the 2020 election." And in fact, the committee only issued criminal referrals for Trump and Attorney \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  despite the conclusion that "many other players were involved in the effort." Last hour, I asked committee member Adam Schiff, is he -- if he`s concerned that he`s letting those people off the hook.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): No, I don`t think so. I -- you know, we did identify others in addition to the president that we believe have violated federal laws. But we also made it very clear in our criminal referrals that this was not an exhaustive list, either of the individuals that may have committed crimes, or of the crimes themselves that were committed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, let me bring it now NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Julie Tsirkin. And Nicholas Wu is a congressional reporter for Politico as well. So, he`s talking about the others, Julie, right there. And these others, they`re named as kind of co-conspirators in this final report. We`re talking about folks like \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , Jeffrey Clark, as well, and yet the criminal referrals were only issued for, as I mentioned, the former president of the United States, along with his attorney, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 . Do we know the strategy behind this?\par \par \uc2 JULIE TSIRKIN, MSNBC CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Have a look. Committee member, \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , who by the way led this whole effort to identify individuals that the committee would then refer to the DOJ for prosecution. He told us on Monday that there are a lot of actors as he called them involved in this but the committee really only wanted to focus on the masterminds, the ringleaders, in the words of \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 . And that`s exactly what they did by putting out this 800-plus page report, as well.\par \par \uc2 Remember, these referrals don`t carry much legal weight to them but they are -- the committee putting their final act together in the historical record by publishing this report, by issuing these referrals, which they stood for a very long time whether or not to do. They are just sending a message to the Justice Department that these individuals deserve to be prosecuted, \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  even said, and everybody else involved in this. And there were hundreds and hundreds of people not only involved in the plot but also of course, in the interaction itself. That`s up to the Department of Justice, who has the legal authority to actually go ahead and prosecute these individuals.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TSIRKIN: What they wanted to do with that -- with this report, which we didn`t learn necessarily new information from, was to put all of this in an accountable historical record days before they reset to turn over the House chamber control to Republicans, making their final mark in these transcripts that they`re releasing to. And so that was really the goal for them and not to get lost by issuing hundreds or all of these referrals that would then get muddied -- that would then muddy the waters.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Guys, if you will stand by. I want to bring in \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2 , a former U.S. Attorney, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , and an MSNBC legal analyst as well.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, it`s always -- it`s great to talk to you. Take me -- us out to DOJ, if you will, for a moment. When you look at kind of this final report, these 800 plus pages, right, the sole focus really being on the former president of the United States and his culpability and all of this in inciting this insurrection. Does that make it more likely, in fact, at the end of the day that he will face charges? And with that others will not?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE\uc2 , MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: No, I don`t think so. I think that much of that focus was for the public to understand that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the central character in this, that he drove this, that he caused this, and to avoid distraction from others. But I think the Justice Department will take a holistic view of this. They`re going to be more interested in the facts than in the conclusions of the committee.\par \par \uc2 And so, all of those witness transcripts will be useful. The charges they describe will certainly be something that the Justice Department can consider. But at the end of the day, they`re going to make their own independent assessment. So, it`s the facts that matter, not the conclusions.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s quite possible that in addition to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a number of other people could likely be facing criminal charges, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 , Jeffrey Clark, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , Kenneth Chesebro, Ken Klukowski. All of those people are described in the report as having potential criminal culpability. So, if the Justice Department is able to put together sufficient evidence to satisfy themselves, I don`t see any reason they would not charge them just because the committee did not specifically refer them for criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Nicholas, I know that you did a lot about the far-right extremists that showed up at the Capitol. You`ve written about them. And they were mentioned a lot, obviously, in this final report, as well, and their involvement in January 6, not just as a part of the insurrection but as leading it really. Take us inside your reporting.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS WU, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, POLITICO: Well, what we saw in the report, and particularly its eighth chapter was really this examination of how these far-right extremist groups kind of became the \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  at the front of this extremist mob, right, and how they were activated in many ways by President Trump`s tweet, right saying to be at the Capitol, it`d be wild. An infamous tweet nearly two years ago now. And so, this is where we saw some of the newer analysis and new material come forward from the committee in their final report. And, of course, this is something that DOJ is looking closely at as well.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Barbara, we saw nearly 900 people, I believe, who were in the mob that day whose charges were brought against them.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 I think one of the kinds of outstanding questions amidst all of this that we were hoping to get answered was the connection between these individuals that were outside the Capitol to people inside the White House or in the former president`s inner circle. Were there conversations being had? Were there phone calls being made between those two groups? Were there are text message exchanges, and so on and so forth? Do you believe real connections now can be made between those two?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: I think it`s a possibility, Yasmin. You know, it appears that the committee did not make that link. They have a lot of circumstantial evidence about it, and \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  and \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  being seen with some of these groups, Oath Keepers and Proud Boys on January 6 itself and the days leading up to it. But we don`t yet have a direct link where members of Trump`s inner circle were giving orders or planning the attack in advance. And so that link isn`t there.\par \par \uc2 But the Justice Department, I think, can take the material they got from this committee and they can conduct the investigation because they have tools that are different from and superior to the tools that were available to the committee. They can use grand jury subpoenas to compel testimony. They can use witness immunity to override people`s invocation of their Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination. And we see the Fifth Amendment invoked throughout these transcripts. They can use search warrants, another tool unavailable to the committee to obtain physical phones. And we know they`ve already done that for \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  and Jeffrey Clark. And that`s a way to reveal the conversations, which can be a real goldmine.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: So, if there are those connections between the friends of Stone, the chat group that included \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and members of these groups, the Justice Department has more tools and could be able to make that link.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Got it. Nicholas, let me read for you part of Bennie Thompson`s foreword here in the final report saying this. The shortfall of communications, intelligence, and law enforcement around January 6 was much less about what they did or did not know. It was more about what they could not know. The President of the United States inciting a mob to march on the Capitol and impede the work of Congress is not a scenario our intelligence and law enforcement communities envisioned for this country. Prior to January 6, it was unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering if you`re surprised at all, Nicholas, as to how -- it seems the intelligence community kind of got off so easily, right? The brace of security kind of got off so easily. Because I think some would argue that January 6 was, in fact, imaginable after four years of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WU: Well, the idea that there was a failure of imagination is really something. It`s a theme that we`ve heard since the attack, but it`s something that we`ve heard from law enforcement, other folks who have testified to it, and you know it`s something that we did see ultimately spoken to in this final report. And so, I think it`s less a question of whether they`re getting law enforcement get off easily. They devote entire appendices to the response of the National Guard and the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  and other intelligence and security breakdowns.\par \par \uc2 And their recommendations include you know a number of different things that could be done to improve intelligence sharing, and security, right? So, I think at the end of the day, the committee in many ways accomplished its central mission of like trying to connect all the dots here with the attack and it`ll be left ultimately to those individual agencies to figure out how they go forward from here.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Julie Tsirkin, I want to give the last word to you. And we got to switch gears here just a little bit because there`s a vote happening now in the House on the $1.7 trillion omnibus bill. Many of members -- of the members voting right now are voting via proxy, this thing could take a while. What do we know?\par \par \uc2 TSIRKIN: More speedy than we thought. I mean, they`re already in the final vote now, on track to pass the omnibus after some procedural shenanigans. But there are over 250 members who said they`re going to be voting by proxy, each member who`s here and we don`t think -- I mean, as many as less than 50 are in the chamber right now. They`re allowed to vote for up to 10 members by proxy. And we`ve seen that happening all morning on some procedural votes they were taking as well. It does make the process take a little bit longer, but because Republicans -- House Republicans who, of course, are whipping against this bill, the omnibus, the $1.7 trillion to fund the government through next September 30, they didn`t stretch out their time as much as some feared and as much as they have in the past because, of course, the storm is happening, the holiday weekend is upon us, and members really just want to get out of town, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. And this is the very bill that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  encouraged Republican members of Congress to vote no on. I will say - - Julie, I got to say I love that you use the word shenanigans. That`s exactly the word that I tell my kids when we get a babysitter, no shenanigans, and yet you have grown adults using procedural shenanigans to hold up. Julie, those are the words. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TSIRKIN: Yes, kids member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. Nicholas, Ruth, thank you. \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2 , thank you as well.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody, coming up, has former President Trump`s clout among the GOP diminished? At least one top ranking Republican thinks so, this guy, Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , his powerful rebuke of the former president, and the sharp warning if Trump attempts to back more candidates in 2024.\par \par \uc2 You are watching MSNBC. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back. In an exclusive interview with NBC`s Sahil Kapur, Senate Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  delivered rare, pointed criticism of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , saying, quote, this, "I think the former president`s political clout has diminished."\par \par \uc2 Sahil Kapur joins me now, along with Kevin Cirilli, an Atlantic fellow and \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  finance contributor, and Doug Heye, a Republican strategist and a former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director.\par \par \uc2 Sahil, let me just start with you on this one and if you will, take us through more of your conversation.\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Sure, Yasmin. Some unusually sharp criticisms from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  to former President Trump as Trump attempts a comeback in 2024.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:00]\par \par \uc2 And when I sat across from McConnell in his Capitol leadership office, it became abundantly clear that this is a man who holds Trump responsible for the Senate Republicans` disastrous showing in 2022, not only failing to capture the majority, but ultimately losing a seat. And McConnell blames Trump in part for choosing flawed candidates who ended up losing winnable races, as well as Trump`s influence, you know, itself which McConnell argues has tarnished the Republican Party`s image with crucial swing voters and independents.\par \par \uc2 Let`s put a part of what McConnell told me here on the screen. He said, quote, "We lost support that we needed among independents and moderate Republicans primarily related to the view they had of us as a party largely made by the former president that we were sort of nasty and tended toward chaos," unquote. McConnell went on to say, quote, "And oddly enough, even though that subset of voters did not approve of President Biden, they didn`t have enough confidence in us in several instances to give us the majority we needed."\par \par \uc2 Now McConnell mentioned three states specifically where Trump-endorsed candidates ended up winning the primaries and losing the general election that Republicans had hoped they could win. That was Arizona, Georgia and New Hampshire. Now the upshot of all this was less McConnell trying to fire a warning shot toward Trump about the presidential race, it was more McConnell signaling to Trump back off Senate races in 2024.\par \par \uc2 McConnell issued a warning that he intends to actively look for quality candidates in 2024 Senate races and he insists that this diminished clout, as he puts it, of former President Trump is going to enable McConnell and his allies to be more influential than they were last time. McConnell conceded that he feared Trump`s influence was too strong in Republican primaries in 2022 ;for him to do that. He insisted it will be different this time.\par \par \uc2 Last point, Yasmin, I pressed McConnell on one factor to which he is arguably responsible for the Republicans` poor performance in 2022. His role in building the Supreme Court that overturned Roe vs. Wade. McConnell did concede to me that Dobbs ruling generated enthusiasm among Democratic voters, but he expressed no regrets for that decision. He said their main problem was poor candidates. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow. It`s interesting, Kevin, because he doesn`t talk about kind of the former president`s diminished power with regards to inciting an insurrection or lying to the American public about the 2020 election. In fact, it`s because of losing elections for them, for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CIRILLI, YAHOO FINANCE CONTRIBUTOR: Look, I would add to what Sahil said.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What do you make of some of what we learned?\par \par \uc2 CIRILLI: Yes. I would add to what Sahil said in that great interview that he did with Leader McConnell, which is essentially McConnell has always felt that he could do with a little less drama coming from Mar-a-Lago. And if you look at the CNBC all-American economic survey that was released earlier this month, the polls back him up on this. 61 percent of independent voters coupled with 37 percent of Republican voters do not want former President Trump to run for reelection in 2024, or to give it another go.\par \par \uc2 That same poll, according to CNBC, found that 17 percent of Americans right now are worried about losing their job. And so I think for McConnell, who is staring down a 2024 Senate cycle, where he`s got his eyes on Ohio as well as Pennsylvania, he wants to make sure that he`s putting up candidates that are going to be more competitive.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: But here`s the thing with McConnell, Doug, right, and I think back to that historic speech that he made on the Senate floor after the January 6th insurrection. And then subsequently how he kind of backed away from his rhetoric towards the former president in that he wouldn`t say to Sahil that he would not support the former President Trump if he wins the Republican nomination. Right? So he says all of this and yet he will not go on the record and say he won`t support him if he wins the nomination. What do you make of that? Is that significant to you?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, Sahil and Kevin knew very well having interviewed McConnell so many times that what he says one word, it actually means a lot more than just that specific word. It`s either -- he doesn`t give a lot of interviews, but when he does they`re very impactful. And clearly, if Trump is the nominee, most Republican senators and House members are going to support him. But what we`re seeing McConnell doing is standing up and saying this is bad for the party and yes, politicians care about elections.\par \par \uc2 And it`s bad for Republicans winning elections in this past election and moving forward and the less of a role that Trump plays, even if his influence think is outsized the, the two states that Kevin mentioned, Pennsylvania and Ohio, the Trump-endorsed candidate got 32 percent, 33 percent of the vote. Meaning two-thirds of Republicans didn`t support the Trump nominee whether they ultimately won or lost. Trump`s influence is still important. And he won the nomination without winning the majority of delegates. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is obviously very aware of that.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Kevin, in this interview, McConnell basically also said that one of the reasons why the Republicans lost kind of this moderate support was because of the former president.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 How does the Republican Party kind of focus in on these swing voters? CIRILLI: Look, (INAUDIBLE) says, I mean, look at Maryland Governor Hogan. I mean, this is someone who had said to me even said about a year and a half ago that there was open talk within the former administration about folks trying to decide what to do about former president Trump and really if they wanted him to be the nominee back in 2020. Elections are about the future. The biggest issue for the Republican Party is what`s going to be in this primary. Because a crowded Republican primary field helped ascend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016.\par \par \uc2 And so the open question now, and again I go back to that CNBC poll, Jasmin, is there enough open primary to counter act the balance of the base of Trump`s movement and the independents who are never Trumpers.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Sahil Kapur, thank you. Kevin Cirilli, thank you as well. Doug Heye, I`m going to ask you to stick around, so thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, all. Some Americans, they`re spending the holidays with a water supply that they don`t trust enough to drink. Coming up the law that could finally help, and why Biden`s \uc2 EPA\uc2  administrator says racism can`t help from arriving sooner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We know black, brown, tribal communities, low-income communities have seen a lack of investment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:035]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back. The water in Jackson, Mississippi, is officially deemed safe to drink. But many people who live there still don`t trust it. That is because they have dealt with more than 300 boil water notices just the last two years and there are continuous issues with water pressure.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Zinhle Essamuah went to Jackson to learn more. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ZINHLE ESSAMUAH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT (on-camera): The city of Jackson has grappled with an ongoing water crisis for decades, subject to ongoing boil water advisories, brown water and low water pressure. I spoke with residents of Jackson and the head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  to hear what the federal government is doing to intervene.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): 35-year-old Danika Samuel (PH) is a mom of six and lifelong resident of Jackson, Mississippi, a city plagued with a decades long water crisis. Water in the city is deemed safe to drink, but many residents still don`t trust it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You should see my momma do the same thing I do now for my kids. Every morning we come downstairs and we get us some big tall both of water, they put on the tub, they wash their face and they brush their teeth.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: The water crisis trigger triggering an \uc2 NAACP\uc2  complaint in September alleging racist policies by Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  and the state of Mississippi, claiming federal money was allocated to smaller majority white communities instead of Jackson. Governor Reeves previously said his administration is committed to ensuring all federal funds are made available on a race-neutral basis. The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  now investigating if Mississippi violated the Civil Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2 , EPA ADMINISTRATOR: No city in the United States of America should have a fragile system that leaves 190,000 citizens without clean water to drink.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  says the federal government has not adequately invested in community communities.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Why do you think that a city that`s over 80 percent black is facing a decade`s long water crisis?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: The environmental justice is a serious issue in this country, which is why the president has made it a priority. We know black, brown, tribal communities, low-income communities have seen a lack of investment, but also on the front lines of the impacts of these lack of investments and climate change.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voice-over): Jackson was the first city Regan visited following his 2021 appointment.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: I saw port-a-potties lined all along the school, and I thought that was due to construction. But that`s what the students had been using for years because they`ve been dealing with low water pressure.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: The White House says new bipartisan legislation will invest at least $50 billion in the nation`s infrastructure, including expanding access to clean drinking water.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: It`s my hope that the people of Jackson now get the type of relief that they`ve been looking for decades.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: Danika Samuel hopes that relief comes soon.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL: I want my kids to have a wonderful future. I want this to go somewhere so my kids won`t have to worry about clean water.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: Zinhle Essamuah, NBC News, Jackson.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Our thanks to Zinhle for that.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead, everybody, President Zelenskyy is back in Ukraine again after his urgent and emotional plea before both chambers of Congress. Coming up, the Republican response to his continued call for help just two weeks out from taking control of the House.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:47]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Welcome back, Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is back in the capital city of Kyiv today, following his visit to the United States. As Zelenskyy posted on his Telegram account that he is back in his office, and quote, "working towards victory." This is happening as Russia continues its relentless air strikes in the eastern and southern fronts in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the U.S., Congress is set to approve $45 billion in additional aid for Ukraine, part of that $1.7 trillion spending package that passed the Senate just yesterday, the House is voting now on that spending bill.\par \par \uc2 Back with us is Doug Heye, the former communications director for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Doug, good to talk to you once again. Not all Republican lawmakers were on board with Zelenskyy`s visit to Washington. Want to take a listen to Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  and what he had to say about the Zelenskyy address.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): I didn`t go to the speech because I didn`t want to be part of a photo-op asking for more money from the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , where they haven`t given us a single piece of accounting on anything they`ve spent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And then you got Kevin McCarthy who essentially said listen, we support aid to Ukraine, but we certainly don`t support kind of this blank, the blank check. What do you make of the shift in power come January to a Republican-led House, and the potential future aid to Ukraine? What do you anticipate coming?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Well, I still think a majority of Republicans support the Ukrainian effort and Zelenskyy`s speech which was fantastic obviously and that he was able to even do it was significant, you know, it helped solidify some of that support. But there are real challenges in the Republican conference which will be in the majority.\par \par \uc2 And I think for Zelenskyy, you know, this means you have to do a few things differently in your communications. One, and this is sort of what Hawley referenced, you got to talk about not just what you need but what you`ve done with what you`ve already gotten and why that`s been effective, which essentially then helps you make your case for more money. And also I think you have to communicate differently --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Doug, I don`t mean to interject but haven`t we seen what they have already done? I mean I said this over and over again on the air, in that we completely underestimated the success and the power, and the resilience of the Ukrainian military, right? I mean the only reason, one of the reasons they were able to pull off what they had so far is because of the foreign aid that they have been continually receiving. And so haven`t we seen what they have done with that aid?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:08]\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Sure. Sure. Look, we`ve seen the superficial version of that. What Congress looks at, what the appropriate military appropriations subcommittee, what the Armed Services Committees need to look at is more detail and that`s the Ukrainian government it would benefit them to be as specific as possible. Here is what you`ve given us and what we`ve done with it and what`s been effective. That`s not what we see on news reports. These are detailed things, sometimes might be classified that Congress will look at more specifically. And that will help to say with incoming committee chair, Mike McCaul, help make that best case.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing I would recommend is different form, and every time, not every time obviously, but so often when we see Zelenskyy giving speeches, or excuse me, giving interviews, he`s meeting with a celebrity, or he`s sitting down with \uc2 David Letterman\uc2  and these are forums that Republicans not only don`t really view, but might view skeptically. So I would tell them, less ben stiller, more Bret Baier, these ways that he can communicate with a skeptical audience and that`s what he needs to do.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, he`s certainly sat down with those people but he has also sat down and walked with and met with in his own country because he had not left Ukraine until now, with foreign leaders, as well as, along with many Ukrainians on the front line fighting this war day in and day out over the last 11-plus months.\par \par \uc2 Doug Heye, thanks for sticking with us. We appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us, everybody, after a busy two hours. Erin Einhorn picks things up with more news after a very quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221224eicn00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for December 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Ryan Nobles, Symone Sanders-Townsend, Paul  Butler, Clint Watts, Catie Beck, Shaquille Brewster, Josh Lederman, Matt  Bradley, Peter Beinart, Ron Allen, Irwin Redlener \par \uc2 7,816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, everyone, I`m Andrea Mitchell in for Chris Jansing live in Washington.\par \par \uc2 As we speak, senators are trying to push that gigantic $1.7 trillion funding bill over the finish line, just 12 hours or so after it looked like the whole thing might fall apart last night. That could have meant that billions in aid for Ukraine as well as critical legislation like electoral account reform would go by the wayside. But the Senate is not the final word. The bill still has to pass the House before going to President Biden`s desk for his signature, all before government funding runs out tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the full January 6 report is set to be released at any moment, which is two hours ago, we received one of the most widely anticipated parts of the investigation. The full transcript of testimony from \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ` top aide Cassidy Hutchinson, nearly 200 pages in length. We`ll go live to Capitol Hill with more on that in a minute.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll also get the latest on the dangerous weather, the brutal cold impacting within a third of the population. A blizzard could bring whiteout conditions to the Great Lakes and the Northeast, dropping as much as three feet of snow just in time for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 But we began with the January 6 report. 800 pages of detailed information about the insurrection and what led up to it are about to be released to the public on Wednesday. The committee put out dozens of witness transcripts connected to the investigation. And just hours ago, they released a brand new one, we`ve been waiting for this, the transcript of Cassidy Hutchinson`s interview. You`ll remember her bombshell testimony of this laid the groundwork -- the groundwork for that testimony back in June.\par \par \uc2 It`s all happening against the backdrop of some real drama on Capitol Hill today, as the Senate puts the finishing touches they hope on the omnibus government funding bill that includes a critical tool to try to prevent a repeat of January 6, reform of the Electoral Count Act, as well as $45 billion that \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  was pleading for in person just last night.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles. Luke Broadwater is a congressional reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  is an MSNBC national security analyst and distinguished research fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute. Emphasis on distinguished. And Symone Sanders-Townsend, a distinguished former chief spokesperson for Vice President Harris host, of course, of "SYMONE" on MSNBC. As well as Paul Butler, a former federal prosecutor, a law professor at Georgetown and MSNBC legal analyst. Welcome, all.\par \par \uc2 We`re on the verge of the holidays. People are trying to get out of town. And the Senate is in such lockdown, as \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is trying to speed through these amendments, Ryan Nobles, that one of our guests in the last hour, Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  could not leave. They`re basically handcuffed to their desks, virtually at least, because they`re not even allowed to get off and do a quick TV interview.\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: He is not wasting any time.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yes. Well --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You talk to me about funding now.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Well, they wasted a lot of time leading up to this point, Andrea, but now they`re --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yes. Now, they`re at the point where they`re basically up against it. Not just up against the deadline of a government shutdown tomorrow, but up against the backdrop of this terrible storm that`s going to grip a good portion of the country and could potentially strand many of these lawmakers here ahead of the Christmas holiday.\par \par \uc2 But they are speeding through this. They have 18 amendments that they need to vote on before they vote on the final, a $1.7 trillion omnibus package. This was an agreement that was reached this morning after a lengthy debate last night over the extension of Title 42 and getting a vote on that. So, there will be two separate votes on that and probably will amount to a stalemate on that issue. But it should be enough to get this bill out of the upper chamber, send it back to the House to be voted on later this evening, and avoid a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 And as you point out, $1.7 trillion in spending, $45 billion for aid to Ukraine, the reform of the Electoral Count Act, countless other pieces of both government funding and legislation that are massively significant as the Democrats round out their control of both the House and Senate before Republicans can take control of the House in the new year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And of course, Ryan, they did not include what a lot of us had been living hoping for, for our friends, the Afghan -- the Afghan refugees who had worked so closely with U.S. forces, a path to citizenship to green cards so that they could be permanent residents, they could get jobs.\par \par \uc2 They don`t have incomes, they`ve been left hanging, Symone. That`s one of the things that has not happened, but the electoral Count Act did. And that`s now hanging in the balance if they can get these amendments through and get that bill done.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: Absolutely. And you know, the Electoral Count Act, this -- what it really did is close a number of these loopholes, Andrea. So, it included provisions to make sure that hey, electors have to be appointed on election day from the various states.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It clarifies that the vice president`s role in this is purely clerical that there is no ability to object, reject, redefine, and then it also raises the threshold for the number of senators or other members of Congress needed to actually object to anything during the proceedings. So, I think it`s important \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  got on board with it, I do believe yesterday or in the last 24 to 48 hours. And so, this is one of the steps that the January 6 committee report, I`m told, recommends happens legislatively. There are other legislative fixes that will be in there to prevent what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Ryan, let`s also shift to the January 6 report. We have a new transcript this morning from Cassidy Hutchinson has referred to you. Tell us about what it reveals and any indication of when the full report might come out.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: So, to answer your second question first, Andrea, literally any second, I`m refreshing my phone constantly waiting for that report to drop in. I will let you know the second we get it. But to answer your first question about Cassidy Hutchinson, we obviously knew so much about her testimony already because of that bombshell hearing where she answered questions under oath under the gaze of the public eye. But these transcripts are revealing even more information about her experience, extensive conversation she had with her boss \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , where Meadows admits countless times that he believes that the former president lost the election and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he lost the election.\par \par \uc2 But then there`s also this. Somewhat of a kind -- of a veiled pressure campaign against associates close to the former president trying to kind of color her testimony to a certain extent. And it`s part of what led her to change counsel. Let me read to you this part of her transcript. She said this about Stefan Passantino, who is a former Trump lawyer, and Stefan at that point was representing her and he had said something to the effect of the committee doesn`t know what you can and can`t recall. So, we want to be able to use that as much as we can, unless you really, really remember something very clearly. And that`s when you give a short, sweet response.\par \par \uc2 She then goes on to tell the committee, I want to make this clear to you, Stefan never told me to lie. But he specifically told me -- he specifically told me I don`t want you to perjure yourself, but I don`t recall isn`t perjury. They don`t know what you can and can`t recall.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this is a very, very, at the very least suspicious interaction between Hutchinson and her former attorney. And I remember back at the time reporting on the fact that she had changed counsel and what that meant toward her testimony, this is it in black and white, that basically the fact that she changed counsel led her to be a lot more open with the committee. And when you read this transcript, she goes into great detail about the things that she recalled. That likely would not have happened if she had kept that same counsel, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Paul Butler, you know, you`re the law professor, let`s talk about the line between advising your client and witness tampering or suborning perjury because there was also another White House official who said to Cassidy Hutchinson, according to another part of the transcript, you know, Mark, her boss knows that you`re family -- something to the effect, I`m paraphrasing, you`re family, you`re a member of the team, and so don`t let you know Mark and, you know, the boss \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  down. So, there`s a lot of pressure on Cassidy Hutchinson and this is why there was a tale from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  at the end of that bombshell hearing that there was a lot more to be said and that they were going to be looking at other people.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, witness tampering is trying to improperly influence or alter the testimony of a witness in a civil or criminal case. Hutchinson said she only went with this lawyer the first lawyer Passantino because Trump folks were paying for him and she knew she needed a lawyer, and couldn`t afford one on her own. If this lawyer actually dangled job offers and told her she would be taken care of, that`s grounds for disbarment and potential criminal prosecution for witness tampering.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers like this give attorneys a bad name, and judges in Bar Association take these kinds of allegations very seriously. Lawyers are supposed to be officers of the court and trying to get a lawyer to -- try to get a witness to lie in court is the opposite of the oath that we take as members of the bar. So, now we have this witness, Cassidy Hutchinson, as a fact witness to the crime to January 6, and now a fact witness also to a potential cover- up.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Luke, let`s talk about, while we wait for the report, a batch of 34 witness transcripts as we say. You dug through them you pointed out many of the witnesses simply plead the fifth over and over and over again and we could show you some of them because according to your account, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  pleaded the fifth more than 150 times. He, of course, is another Former Trump lawyer.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Former National Security Adviser \uc2 Mike Flynn\uc2  pleading the fifth to virtually every question he was asked. And Arizona Republican Party chair \uc2 Kelli Ward\uc2  even pleading the fifth when asked about her phone number, and where she went to college. What does this tell us about cooperation?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Right. Well, the committee is going to be releasing hundreds of transcripts over the next few days. This was the first batch that came out last night, 34 of them. The common theme among all these were -- these were people who are invoking their fifth amendment right against self-incrimination. They -- you only do that if you believe that there`s a possibility that your answer to the question could expose you to criminal liability. And so, witness after witness did this.\par \par \uc2 Now, the important thing here is that this was one way some witnesses were able to block parts of the committee`s investigation. And the committee said the other day that they probably could have made even more criminal referrals had so many people not invoked the Fifth Amendment. But if you do read through these transcripts, you can see more so from the questions than the answers some of the evidence that the committee was amassing, things about the fake elector scheme, about who transmitted those documents to Capitol Hill to try to further the fake electoral scheme about, calls made to local elections officials telling -- encourage them to stop the count of the -- of the votes for President Biden. So, there is evidence in there but you have to dig through them to see the text messages and the e-mails that were obtained through a subpoena and we`re in the questioning from the investigators.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And in an earlier segment, we were discussing John Ratcliffe, the former head of National Intelligence, who was a Trump loyalist. He testified that the president knew very well that he had lost the election, but then would backslide the next day. But that he did know early on before January 6 that he had lost the election, which is an important aspect in here, right?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Wait, I think you`re seeing that in Cassidy -- sorry, in Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony today. There`s -- yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Go ahead, Luke.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: There`s a -- there`s a good in there about her conversation with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . And \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  says to her after the call -- the famous call with \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows he`s lost, I`m still going to try to fight for him, but he knows it`s over. And there`s another time when he`s going to, you know, arrange a net, a presidential archive for the -- for the post-presidency. And he asked her not to tell \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that because he`ll be mad because he wants me to keep fighting this even though, you know, we all know he lost. So, there`s an acknowledgment or Meadows` part and he says there`s an acknowledgment on Trump`s part that he knows the true outcome here is that he lost but that he wants to keep trying to find ways to overturn the election anyway.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And separately, on another transcript, there was one from John Ratcliffe as well. \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , when you think about all the people from law enforcement who suffered on January 6, the officers were attacked, we know some of them you know very well after all this time. What do you think when they see so many of these witnesses simply refusing to participate in the investigation and taking the fifth, which is their right?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Yes. Andrea, I just think it shows the balance of any sort of Congressional investigation as opposed to these law enforcement investigations that we`ve had. Just compare this to what we`ve seen from the Oath Keepers, the sedition charges, the indictment, and in terms of that trial, we just learned a lot more in terms of what the connections were and what the goals were of that plan.\par \par \uc2 I think that really shows you what a law enforcement investigation can do versus just a congressional investigation, which, while it`s necessary, it is great to do, it doesn`t get you all the way because they know two things, one, pleading the fifth that allows you to dodge answering those tough questions. And the second part is, you`re not really under any sort of reinforcing to other pieces of evidence to the same degree that you might get from law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 We compare this to the Oath Keepers of the Proud Boys investigations. There`s very clearly some text messages that have been surfaced through waffle, you know, subpoenas, through investigative inquiries and search warrants, you`re seeing a lot of that data show up that really puts the pieces together. And I think that`s where we`re going to end at the end of this investigation today, which is what was the relationship? What did people believe when they went to the Capitol? Was there a coordination and communication between the White House and those people that descended on the Capitol that day? And was it a pre-planned event?\par \par \uc2 I just worried that we went through all of this and we won`t really get to those toughest questions that were out there. I think it might be elusive in the end just because we don`t have much coming out of these transcripts that have been shown so far.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Symone, you actually spoke to committee chair \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  about the investigation and the cooperation between the committee and the Department of Justice. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Yes. In my conversation with Chairman Thompson yesterday, I asked him first if he has confidence in the Justice Department investigation. He said he`s more comfortable now that Jack Smith is on the case as special counsel. And he went on to detail actually that the work of the committee has been very substantively helpful to the Justice Department. I want to play for you all what he said right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2 , (D-MS): There were people that we deposed, that Justice had not deposed. There were electors in various states that Justice couldn`t find, we found.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: And you found them.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: We found them. We deposed them. And we -- so, we had a lot of information but now, we move -- make that all that information available to them. And if they come back and want to interview staff or any members as to any additional information that we know, we`d be more than happy to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Paul, Chairman Thompson suggests there are people that the committee found that the DOJ could not find. How do you think this cooperation is going to work going forward?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So, the DOJ`s investigation is relatively early, but I`m certain that prosecutors will be scouring. The final report of the executive summary is 150 pages. Imagine how long the actual report will be. Prosecutors hope it will be detailed enough to give them leads, but not so detailed as to compromise the criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ali Vitali -- Ryan Nobles I should say, Luke Broadwater, \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , Paul Butler, and Symone Sanders-Townsend, thanks to all of you. Happy holidays.\par \par \uc2 And the Winter Storm Warning, millions right now bracing for what could be a pre-holiday nightmare on the roads at the airport. A little cold also at home. What you need to know? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: President Biden issued a stark warning to the public this morning as 124 million Americans are now in the path of what could be one of the worst peak Christmas storms in decades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Please heed the local warnings. We`ve contacted -- we`ve tried to contact 26 governors so far in affected regions. Please take this storm extremely seriously. And I don`t know whether your boss will let you but if you all have travel plans, leave now. Not a joke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Eight states and counting are now under blizzard warnings from Montana to Western New York. Blizzard conditions also from Chicago to Buffalo are expected to be so severe it will make travel nearly impossible for the next few days. Airports across the country are being thrown into chaos. And like the president, experts and airlines are urging travelers get out earlier.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Catie Beck from Reagan National in Arlington, Virginia, and Shaq Brewster driving around hard-hit Illinois in an SUV. So, Catie, what are you seeing and hearing from travelers?\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Andrea, the chaos hasn`t struck Washington, DC just yet. I think most of the passengers we`ve seen today have walked up to that departure board and felt some relief. Most of those flights are on time, very few are canceled or delayed at this point.\par \par \uc2 Will that last? Probably not. The storm is now making its way into the Midwest and that is going to cause those airports to really clamp down on flights from hubs like DC and Atlanta. They`re expecting to have you know, millions of people delayed as a result of this massive storm that is hitting on the two biggest travel days of the holiday season which are today and tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So far here at Reagan, some folks have been heeding the warning to book early and get out ahead of the storm. We did speak to some folks who said they move their flights up to try and beat those holiday headaches.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BECK: Were you guys super nervous this morning?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We already changed our flight from tomorrow which is going to be really snowed out, so hopefully we`re going to beat it but we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BECK: Now, Chicago where Shaq is, has already preemptively canceled 500 flights today. We do see some flights on the board to Chicago right now that actually are on time. The passengers we spoke to on those flights, they -- their fingers are crossed that they`re -- they`ve packed extra clothes and snacks for the day in anticipation that they might be waiting. But the airlines are certainly asking everybody to check those updates in the statuses and have waived the fees for those wanting to change their flights and get ahead of the storm. So, here at Reagan, not chaotic yet but we haven`t gotten into the thick of the storm yet either, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Catie, thank you. And, Shaq, you could talk to me about the weather conditions you`ve been driving around since we last spoke in the hour -- the hour -- the previous hour.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, it --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: What`s it look like?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Well, since last hour, you know when we talked last hour, we talked about how the flurries we`re talking -- we`re turning into a consistent snowfall. And that`s what we`re seeing right now. I`ll let you take a look in front of our vehicle where it`s -- you see the road conditions not too bad right now. We do notice and have noticed that those snow plows that were pre-positioned along the entrance ramps and exit ramps up the highway have now shifted to on -- to going on the highway. We saw them going around and starting to drop that salt and sand mix so that drivers can be safe.\par \par \uc2 Officials are telling people and really pleading with people at this point to take it slow and avoid the roads altogether. In Illinois, for example, the State Highway Patrol saying if you can avoid going on highways, stay off the highways. If you look at what you saw in Indiana, they`ve pre- positioned the National Guard along the highways and activated to mobilize the National Guard along highways to help protect stranded drivers. And the reason that is is because of those pictures that you`re looking at right now. The concern is that as the snow continues to fall, as those temperatures continue to plunge, we`ve noticed about a drop in about 15 degrees in the past couple of hours where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 That will turn into icy conditions and these roads will become treacherous especially as the wind picks up and those whiteout conditions start to appear. So, right now, things are smooth. We do know downtown Chicago -- in the suburbs right now in downtown Chicago, they have started to get some of those first flus -- first few flurries and first few snowflakes. But right now, things are OK. The concern and the pleas that you`re hearing from officials is that folks, stay off the roads so that it stays that way, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Shaq, Bill Karins in the last hour said that the real threat to driving is the dropping temperatures. It`s that icing it`s not the amount of snow.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So, just be careful. Thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thanks to Catie. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 As Russia`s war in Ukraine rages on, will the next Congress vote to provide that additional aid after Zelenskyy`s -- Volodymyr Zelenskyy`s emotional historic speech last night? That`s next. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: It was a hero`s welcome here in Washington last night for Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . He met with the president first at the White House, he gave an historic speech to a joint meeting of Congress last night with a dire assessment of these next few months saying, quote, "Let the world see that the United States is here."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: We defeated Russia in the battle for minds of the world, and next year will be a turning point. I know it. The point when Ukrainian courage and American resolve must guarantee the future of our common freedom.\par \par \uc2 Your money is not charity. It`s an investment in the global security and democracy that we handle in the most responsible way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: There was applause, but it was not universal.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Josh Lederman from the White House. NBC`s Matt Bradley in Kyiv for us. Also with us \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 , MSNBC analyst Peter Beinart.\par \par \uc2 So, Josh, let`s talk about this because there were some Republicans, not including the Senate leader, but some other Republicans certainly on the House side, who were not on board with this money for Ukraine. And we now are waiting for the Omnibus to get through because that money is stuck in there, the $45 billion.\par \par \uc2 But how did the historic trip lay the foundation for what Ukraine needs and wants from the U.S.? They didn`t get everything they wanted in terms of weapons.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They didn`t get everything they wanted, but they got more than they had gotten in the past, at least in terms of this first Patriot missile battery. And the goal really was to try to show to President Putin that if he thinks that he`s going to be able to play a waiting game basically and that Western support for Ukraine will erode as we head into the second year of this war in February, that that is not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 And today, Secretary Blinken, as he was having his closing news conference for the year saying the U.S. is taking the ball on this issue and running with it. And even this announcement we saw today, Andrea, from the National Security Council is part and parcel with that, with the White House saying that they have now declassified information about North Korea transferring arms, lethal weaponry to the Wagner Group, which the U.S. now estimates has 50,000 people fighting in Ukraine, 40,000 of them conflicts.\par \par \uc2 That is a strategy of declassifying intelligence trying to blow up the Russian spot as they say that the U.S. has used from the very beginning of this war. In fact even before the war had commenced. I think that is an indication the U.S. plans to continue with the very similar strategy of trying to put the Russians on their heels through those kinds of actions, going forward into next year, and certainly that $45 billion in the Omnibus, more frankly than the White House had even been asking for, will go a long way to making sure that President Biden is able to deliver on that long-term commitment that he pledged to President Zelenskyy during their visit yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So, Matt Bradley, there you are in Kyiv. There have been rolling blackouts. We`ve seen it ourselves. We had that experience with you over the last hour. President Zelenskyy said last night his land is soaked in blood, that the Russian tactics are primitive. You`re there today, emergency power outages as I said continuing there.\par \par \uc2 The importance of his trip here is, though, showing the world the solidarity with the U.S. and having a face to face meeting with President Biden to clear up any misconceptions and to make the appeal for -- which has been making for the tanks, the offensive weapons, the artillery, the ATACMs, the jets. He`s not getting them yet. And maybe he won`t. But as Josh was just saying, he did get a lot.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: He did indeed get quite a lot. And you know, they broke barriers, and not just on the battlefield. And they have been having a lot of success on the battlefield. But as Josh mentioned, those Patriot missiles, that`s a big deal not just in terms of how they`re going to be able to use them, to stop Russian aerial bombardment because of course this is a surface-to-air missile, it`s a defensive weapon. And that`s a big deal for Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, this visit by Zelenskyy you can say it was sort of two parts gratitude, one part further requests. And, you know, when we talk with my conversations with Ukrainian officials and Ukrainian politicians, they are very, very thankful to the United States for providing so much money and material, but they still want more. And they always say when they say thank you, they remind you that the fact is the United States is giving all of this money, but it`s the Ukrainians who are doing the fighting and the dying.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke with one Ukrainian member of parliament, who is actually a leading member of Volodymyr Zelenskyy`s political party here, here`s what she had to say about American largess.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA MEZENTSEVA, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER, SENATE OF THE PEOPLE PARTY: From my heart and from the people of Ukraine, I highly appreciate every penny, every cent that has been spent on your side. Every humanitarian aid you`re sending by mail, every designer thing you`re doing to sell, every cookie you baked to collect money, this is all so touching.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:11]\par \par \uc2 And we highly appreciate it. Here we pay with the highest price. It`s our lives. And they can`t be measured with budget figures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And as that parliamentarian mentioned, you know, they are very, very grateful, but they want more. And what they really want, this Patriot missile system is fundamentally a defensive weapon. And she mentioned, you know, it was only a couple of months ago that Ukraine asked for that Patriot missile system. This was even before the invasion started they were requesting this. And they got a hard no from Washington. But now, look, they`re receiving their Patriot missiles.\par \par \uc2 And so that means that not only have they broken barriers on the battlefield, but they`re breaking barriers with Washington. They believe that if they can bring Washington to yes on the Patriots, then they can get more offensive weapons. And what they really want are these offensive artillery weapons like JDAMs, the ATACMs, which are surface-to-surface. And that means they`ll be able to fire weapons into Russia even, they`ll have that capability, or even into Crimea.\par \par \uc2 That`s something that Washington really doesn`t want to see the Ukrainians do, at least not with American-made weapons because they believe that could possibly escalate the war and maybe even bring \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to do more disastrous things like using nuclear weapons, which he has routinely threatened to use. But at same time when you speak to Ukrainians, they always say there`s really no such thing as a weapon that is purely offensive when it comes to, you know, high-technology because they believe that any offensive capability that they have is fundamentally defensive because they`re the ones who are defending their homeland and they`re the ones who are using American weapons in order to do it.\par \par \uc2 So for them, this is just another step forward and it means that they are going to be asking for more. As long as they feel like they are winning and as long as they feel like Washington will oblige them, the Ukrainian government officials that we`ve been speaking to say, they`re going to be making further requests and they`re going to be asking for the kinds of weapons that might make Washington a little bit uncomfortable. Andrea?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: More than a little bit because they`ve got a hard know on those ATACMs and on the jets again. And for precisely the reasons why the fear in Washington is that it would enlarge and extend the war.\par \par \uc2 Peter, this was a dramatic show of confidence, though, from the Ukrainian leader to come, to leave his country for the first time in 300 days. It showed courage and confidence in the security that \uc2 NATO\uc2  did afford him with an escort, and to (INAUDIBLE) on this trip to give the speech on U.S. soil. He made a point to make it in English and it was brilliantly delivered. He referenced critical comparisons between Ukraine and our own history as well as the fact it was resonant of Winston Churchill in some ways, you know, we will never surrender. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Just like the brave American soldiers which held their lines and fought back Hitler`s forces during the Christmas of 1944, brave Ukrainian soldiers are doing the same to Putin`s forces this Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You know, speaking of our revolutionary war history, the Battle of the Saratoga, but they are the Battle of the Bulge, and comparing Ukrainian forces up against Russia as the American forces that horrible winter of `44 up against the Nazi onslaught.\par \par \uc2 PETER BEINART, CITY UNIVERSITY OF NEW YORK PROFESSOR: Yes. I mean, one of the interesting things about this conflict has been this fight over the meaning of World War II because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , again, has continually invoked Russia as, you know, playing the role of the anti-Nazi force calling, absurdly, but calling Zelenskyy and his regime Nazis. So you see how Zelenskyy is trying to take this analogy, which remains the most powerful analogy, you know, in the United States when it comes to a just war.\par \par \uc2 World War II, in turning that, and saying, no, no, in fact, we represent the moral legacy of the fight that the allies waged against fascism and Vladimir Putin`s Russia is now in the position that the Nazis were in, essentially as an aggressive anti-democratic, illiberal authoritarian power.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Peter, this speech could not have come at a more critical point. First of all, right before the Republicans take over the House in January with again Kevin McCarthy last night talking about no blank checks. Despite the warm reception from Congress. Steve Scalise telling "Politico" that House Republicans, quote, "express an interest in making sure the money is going to be scrutinized," which both the White House and Zelenskyy say is happening.\par \par \uc2 And also militarily, as "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " is reporting a new analysis -- intelligence analysis clearly that it is stalemated, and there is great concern by Zelenskyy about a new Russian offensive as soon as the permafrost, you know, freezes over and those tanks can start rolling again.\par \par \uc2 BEINART: Yes. I think what Zelenskyy is trying to do is put time on the clock essentially to shore up American support, which is of course critical for overall \uc2 NATO\uc2  support, to give the Ukrainians more time, in order to show even more gain. They`ve already shown a significant number this year. More gains, which then actually becomes a kind of virtual cycle that gives people more reason to support.\par \par \uc2 The problem is he`s facing the winter where things will probably, the pace of Ukrainian gains may well slow. Plus as you said, the possibility of a significant Russian counterattack, so the danger for him is that Russia shows it`s going to be in this fight for a long time and that strengthens the forces in the Republican Party and also in parts of Europe like Germany that say we need to push the Ukrainians towards a quick peace.\par \par \uc2 He`s trying to I think buttress the politics here in Washington against that dangerous prospect.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Josh Lederman, Matt Bradley, and Peter Beinart, it`s great to see all of you. Happy holidays, all. And thanks for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 And we move on to the crypto collapse. Disgraced former FTX CEO \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  appearing right now in a New York City courtroom. He was extradited after waiving extradition. His opposition to extradition. So he`s back from the Bahamas and in court. We`ll talk about that, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:51]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Disgraced former FTX CEO \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  appearing right now in a New York City courtroom after he agreed to be extradited back to the States from the Bahamas. He`s facing charges of wire fraud and money laundering stemming from the sudden collapse of his crypto currency, change platform.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Ron Allen outside the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Ron, I understand that the judge just announced, and I`m trying to get my head around this, that Bankman-Fried will be released on bond? This is after the Consumer Commodities Trading Commission estimated that there was an alleged $8 billion, with a B, fraud of his customers?\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And a million customers, victims potentially in this whole thing. Yes, the bond is going to be $250 million, which the DOJ prosecutors said was the largest pre-trial bond ever that they could think of. And it was proposed by the government. And the government said that they were offering this proposal in large part because he cooperated and he waived extradition and came voluntarily -- well, came to the United States last night and didn`t fight the extradition process. So that was something that worked in his favor.\par \par \uc2 He has to surrender his passport. He`s going to move out to California, to Palo Alto, where his parents live. He has to live in his parent`s home. He has to report there by 10:00 a.m. tomorrow morning to be fitted with an electronic monitoring device on his ankle. And the judge says that he will be -- there will be strict pretrial supervision during this entire process. And that was the only time that Bankman-Fried was heard to say anything in court when the judge asked him if he understood the consequences potentially if he didn`t appear for his next court appearance and potentially if he jumped bail. And Bankman-Fried basically just said, yes.\par \par \uc2 We also understand that his parents were in the room for the proceeding. At the end of it they were holding hands and they had a look on their face that seemed to suggest relief because, as you pointed out earlier, there was a lot of questions about whether he would be granted bail at all. But he has. And now we`re waiting to see if he can make that bail and if in fact we see him leaving the court sometime today. Andrea?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ron, let me fill in a little gap here, according to extensive profile in "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." Because, you know, we`ve talked about what the Consumer Financial Trading Commission has said about him. His parents are two Stanford law professors dating back to the 1980s, veteran law professors, very popular law professors. And his father was part of creating this company. He was involved in the original, you know, financing of it.\par \par \uc2 He was with him in the Bahamas. They had an apartment in the Bahamas. They are an integral part of this company, according to at least the practice. So this is not just release someone to their parents. This is a very strange decision all around.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Exactly. Yes, and the other point -- well, perhaps the other point to make, Andrea, too, the other big development overnight was that two of Bankman-Fried`s closest business partners pled guilty -- agreed to plead guilty to conspiracy and fraud charges and presumably to testify against him. So the pressure really ramped up. And the government`s case became even stronger overnight. But yes, it is striking. And it`s going to be very upsetting, I would think, to the many victims of Bankman-Fried`s alleged schemes that he is being set free.\par \par \uc2 But the judge and the prosecutors seem to agree that he was of marginal risk to the community, and that the real risk was a potential flight, of fleeing. But an ankle monitoring and being confined to Northern California apparently the judge seems to think that this is restrictive enough and that justice will be done. And again we`ll see if he`s able to actually leave here today at some point.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ron Allen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And the humanitarian crisis in Ukraine, Ukrainian are being killed, injured, large groups of people are freezing, they`re without power throughout the country, millions are displaced. We will speak to someone who has seen the crisis firsthand about what needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Just in the last 24 hours, since President Zelenskyy visited Washington, Ukraine recorded one civilian death, another 14 injuries, including two children.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, founder of the Ukraine Children`s Action Project, Dr. Irwin Redlener.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Redlener, so good to see you. First of all, your reaction to the address, and what more should people be doing for the children, the children of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 DR. IRWIN REDLENER, MSNBC PUBLIC HEALTH ANALYST: : Well, the address was totally moving. It was inspiring. I think to me, and I`ve been around a long time working with complicated issues of adversity for children, but I mean I think this was an historic speech to Congress. But the thing about that I`m most worried about, so when Karen and I started the Ukraine Children`s Action Project in May, we were concerned initially about two big things.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 One was the enormous amount of psychological trauma experienced by children. Remembering that there was 7.5 million kids in Ukraine prior to the war beginning, this phase of the war, in February, of those 7.5 million kids, 2.5 million of them were displaced outside the country, and became refugees. And another 2.5 million children are displaced to the west, which had been considered a safe zone within Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Everything changed on October 10th, when the war actually became a terror campaign mounted against the people, the civilians, the society in Ukraine, and it`s a disaster for children, Andrea. They are -- kids are at extreme risk now from the freezing cold temperatures. There is at least 15 million Ukrainians who are not getting heat and electricity, and the winter is brutal. And we have been now shifting some of our focus to providing, buying generators, and wood-burning stoves and winter clothing for children, which is so desperately needed right now, and in great volume.\par \par \uc2 And fortunately, we have a couple of UCAP volunteers working in collaboration with a lot of very reliable not-for-profits there and our guys are actually shopping for generators in Poland. And we`re going to get them shipped in there. They`re on the way as we speak. Hundreds of them. And we`re going to do what we can. But I`m worried about the winter, plus COVID for people of Ukraine especially the kids.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: What can people do? Have the Christmas spirit, the holiday spirit, what can people do to help?\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Look, I mean, this boils down to people being as generous as they can around this time. It`s not great to actually ship items. We can`t be, for example shipping generators and winter clothes from the U.S., the logistics are overwhelming, but people can contribute to organizations, especially I must say like the Ukraine Children`s Action Project, which is, the Web site is Ukrainecap.org. And you see it there on the screen.\par \par \uc2 But if people do that, the mass majority of money will go directly to buying what`s needed over there and it`s tax exempt and people should think about donating in this holiday season. But really throughout the winter. This is going to be brutal for the children of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thank you, Dr. Redlener, for something you always do. We`ll have it up on the screen and will put it out on social media as well. Thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Great. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And this does it for us this hour. And I`m going to take some time off so wishing everyone the very happiest holiday, healthy, safe, safe especially for those of you traveling and a wonderful new year. Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Reiser picks it up after this short message.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221223eicm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic <Byline: Yasmin Vossoughian, Garrette Haake, Jake Sherman, Marissa Parra,  Kristina Partsinevelos, Ken Dilanian, Paul Butler, Kathy Park, Vin Gupta,  Courtney Kube, Ellison Barber\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for December 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 8,294 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:30]\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: Hey everybody, good to see you. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian in for Chris Jansing live here at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. An echo chamber of lies. That is how outgoing Republican \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  described the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2 . His warning for republicans and democrats coming just days before his January 6 Committee drops its final report and the criminal referrals that will come with it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, if you haven`t gotten sick lately, you`re lucky everything from flu to strep to COVID. One doctor says it`s like a "bomb of viruses going off all at once."\par \par \uc2 And then finally, civil rights icon, the late \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  honor this week with a brand-new host himself. We`re going to talk to the man who took the portrait and asked him about Louis`s legacy later on in the show. We start though on Capitol Hill where lawmakers are trying to squeeze in some major pieces of business in the final few days in the 117th. Congress. That includes passing a massive and I mean massive, $1.7 trillion government funding bill and they have less than a week to get that done.\par \par \uc2 And then for the January 6 Committee, it means holding its final public hearing on Monday potentially with new never before seen evidence. It may also be the last time that we see committee member \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , who`s leaving Congress all together next month. But not before issuing this stark warning to his republican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): The once great party of Lincoln, Roosevelt and Reagan has turned its back on the ideals of liberty and self-governance. Instead, it has embraced lies and deceit. The Republican Party used to believe in a big tent. Now, we shelter the ignorant, the racist who only stoke anger and hatred to those who are different than us. Unfortunately, we now live in a world where lies Trump truth, where democracy is being challenged by authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 If we America`s elected leaders do not search within ourselves for a way out, I fear that this great experiment will fall into the ash heap of history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Let`s get into it guys if we can. I want to bring in NBC`s Garrett Haake standing by for us on Capitol Hill. Jake Sherman is cofounder of Punchbowl news and an MSNBC political contributor. \uc2 Ashley Pratte\uc2  Oates is a political strategist and a board member of the group Republican Women for progress. Welcome to you all, guys. Thanks for joining us on this. Garrett, let me just start with you on this one and ask you if Kinzinger`s words are really resonating with Republicans there on the Hill overall at all could kind of move the needle for them saying they want to separate their legacy really from the former president?\par \par \uc2 GARRETTE HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: No, Yasmin, I don`t think they are. I mean, \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  gave that speech yesterday to an empty chamber hours after the House was done for the week. I doubt many of his fellow republicans even saw it or read it. I think Kinzinger`s style of going directly at former President Trump and his autocratic tendencies is not something that`s been widely embraced, obviously, by his fellow republicans.\par \par \uc2 And the only other Republican in the House who had a similar style as Cheney. Met a similar fate both lost in primaries and will be going home. I do think to the degree that there are those who agree with \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , within the republican conference, they`re trying to find their opposition a little more strategically. We`ve heard it a lot in these last couple of weeks, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said things about, you know, throwing out the constitution or he`s had these dinners with antisemites and so forth.\par \par \uc2 Where we`ve seen criticism of the activity, but not of the man, whether there is kind of the more full-throated criticism and try to return to a different set of principles guiding the Republican Party in the future as Kinzinger lays out, we`re not there yet.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ashely, there was also a lot of reporting in the lead up to the midterm elections about democrats essentially funding kind of these far-right MAGA candidates, right? Kind of, in a way work against them so that their democratic candidates could have eventually went out, right? Kinzinger also addressed that tactic. Let`s play a little bit of that, and then we`ll talk on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Many of you have asked me where all the good republicans. Over the past two years democratic leadership had the opportunity to stand above the fray. Instead, they poured millions of dollars into the campaigns of MAGA Republicans, the same candidates, President Biden called a national security threat to ensure these good republicans did not make it out of their respective primaries. This is no longer politics as usual.\par \par \uc2 This is not a game. If you keep stoking the fire you can`t point the fingers when our great experiment goes up in flames.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What do you make of it, Ashley? Did you think democrats deserves some blame here as well?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:02]\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PRATTE\uc2  OATES, POLITICAL STRATEGIST: For sure. I mean, he`s correct on this. Good republicans are exiting congress for that very reason. when we`re propping up candidates who are extreme, and we`re pointing fingers at that they need to be looking at the fact that they`re funding these candidates, because they know they have better chance at winning. And they`re, you know, elections later on in the general.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that this was a great point brought up by Kinzinger. And hopefully everyone will do some soul searching in the days and weeks ahead of his leaving congress.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Jake, let`s talk about one place where we could see some bipartisanship ahead of the Christmas holiday. And that is this major spending bill, right? They got a week to get this thing off across the finish line. But according to what you wrote this morning in Punchbowl, they got to get it all right in order to get it done.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: They have to get it together. And your -- hopefully Yasmin about getting it done before Christmas. Garrett and I have been talking about the prospects of that recent days in the always the Capitol. This is going to be a 4000 page, $1.7 trillion bill that is going to be dropped. Let`s call it Sunday or Monday and then voted off in the senate on Wednesday or even earlier.\par \par \uc2 It`s a -- it`s a gigantic, gigantic bill that is going to get bipartisan support, wide bipartisan support because that`s the only way to get it through. But remember, the key here is that any single lawmaker from either party could slow down this bill to a crawl. And with the government running out of money one week from today, that`s going to be an important thing to keep an eye on. Also, furthermore, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has said that he will not vote for a bill if it comes up later than Thursday.\par \par \uc2 So, there is -- there are there`s less than a week to get this done. So yes, it is -- it is widely bipartisan, it will fund government through the end of September 2023. But it`s not without its hiccups and its complications as we try to get toward the end of the year here.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I`ve been watching a heck of a lot of Christmas movies as of late with my kids. Is it bad to hope, Jake for a Christmas miracle? That`s all we want at the end of the year. You`re also talking about Kevin McCarthy, as you wrote this morning, he`s trying to get his members to vote against the spending bill. But you actually want to say this, and I want you to explain it to us if you can. Vote no. But hope yes?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Yes. Well, to your earlier point, Yasmin, this is the face of no hope. But yes, the vote --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Geez.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: The vote no, hope yes idea is that Kevin McCarthy, despite what he says publicly, does not want to shut down threat in January. That`s the other option for them to kick this to the first quarter of next year when Kevin McCarthy is going to either be in his view, hopefully, in his view, a new speaker. He doesn`t want to have to deal with a government shutdown threat on, you know, January 15th or February 15th.\par \par \uc2 That would be an unnecessary distraction for him. So, while he says that he doesn`t want an omnibus that it`s that it`s, you know, overdone and too much spending. He benefits, he probably benefits the most from getting this done. He wouldn`t have to deal with government funding until the end of September 2023.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Actually, Jake just said it right, hopefully wanting to be speaker. The problem is he doesn`t necessarily have the votes as of yet. We`re also learning now that he delayed all the races for committee leadership spots until I believe after speaker election in January. It feels like this is a recipe for chaos in the new congress.\par \par \uc2 OATES: Yes, but isn`t that the Republican Party right now? They`re just in complete shambles. And they`re reeling. I mean, Trump`s candidacy so far for his third bid at president is flailing. Republicans have no idea how to be on message. You know, you`ve got \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  who have been trying to make sure that the Republican Party rebuilds and then you`ve got people like Kevin McCarthy who run to Mar-a-Lago over to Trump to make sure that he`s in good graces with him.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a party that has no idea what direction they`re in because their platform is empty and has been. It`s been the platform of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  since 2016. And until they realign on what their priorities are, this is what we`re going to see. And I don`t know what Kevin McCarthy`s chances are moving forward to being speaker but it`s not a very strategic move what he`s doing right now. He`s just setting it up for more chaos.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Garrett, speaking of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , talk to me about Monday. What can we expect in this final summary hearing from the January 6 Committee?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, look, I think it`s almost certain that the January 6 Committee will refer him to the Department of Justice for prosecution on which exact charges remains to be seen. But the January 6 committee started out with members who had all voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Through the course of their committee, they laid the attack on the Capitol at his feet. And they argued in court findings before federal judges that they thought he was guilty of various crimes.\par \par \uc2 And so, if they do anything short of referring him to the DOJ, I would be shocked. The question is what kind of evidence do they provide beyond what we`ve already seen, both for him and for any other referrals that they might choose to make? And those by the way could run the gamut from other referrals for criminal prosecution, to referrals to the Congressional Ethics Committee or the State Bar Associations.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:03]\par \par \uc2 So, I think Monday is kind of the day of reckoning where the House January 6 Committee turns their investigation into something where they can say, all right, here`s what we`ve come up with. And here`s who we think needs to be held responsible. And Yasmin, it`s going to get totally blown away by the next congress. But remember, this committee was constituted in part to make legislative recommendations for how to prevent another January 6.\par \par \uc2 That`s part of their mandate here. We might get some of that in this report, but it`s likely that none of those recommendations should they be made, will ever see the light of day, certainly not when the republicans take control of the House come January 3rd.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ashley, what are you going to be watching for on Monday?\par \par \uc2 OATES: Oh, man, I think the criminal, you know, counts at this point would be what to focus on here because then, you know, is Trump actually going to be held accountable for what he did? And should he be allowed to run for another presidential bid. The answer there should be no. So, it`ll be interesting to see what happens on Monday and the culmination of, you know, all of these investigations and how this moves forward with republicans controlling congress.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: \uc2 Ashley Pratte\uc2  Oates, thank you. Garrett Haake, thank you. Jake Sherman, happy birthday, friend.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Thank you for joining us on your -- on your birthday. And maybe you can put in a wish for a Christmas miracle. How about that? Just lift some spirits.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I will do -- I will do my best.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Thanks, Jake. Appreciate you guys. All right. By the way, everybody, be sure to join us for special coverage of the final January 6 Committee meeting that starts Monday at noon Eastern right here on MSNBC along with Peacock as well.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody. We have got some breaking news involving Brittney Griner. We learned within the past few hours that she has left Brook Medical Center in Texas and is now home in Arizona for the first time since being released from a Russian prison more than a week ago. I want to bring in NBC correspondent Marissa Parra who`s got more on this for us. Marissa, if you will, I mean, I think a lot of folks are wondering of course about her physical health right now on Brittney as she has been released after being held captive in Russia for so long, but also really her mental health as well on this road to recovery.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Right. Well, I will say this is a big milestone here have one of several, right? Her landing on U.S. soil, her making her way to this facility and now being released, being able to go back home, this is a huge step for her and clearly a good sign and indication of both her mental and physical health because she wouldn`t have been cleared if she wasn`t in a good state with both of those regards.\par \par \uc2 And you`re seeing photos that we`re seeing, for the very first time. She posted these on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that warm embrace that we`re seeing with her and her wife when she first touched down on U.S. soil. We want to show you some of her words that she shared on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Her first post since February 5th, she said "It feels so good to be home. The last 10 months have been a battle at every turn, I dug deep to keep my faith and it was the love from so many of you that helped keep me going. From the bottom of my heart. Thank you to everyone for your help."\par \par \uc2 And she continued to thank everyone who has been a part of this not just of course, the Biden administration but she also thinks everyone at the San Antonio Fort Hood bass, everyone that`s part of the PISA team and PISA of course being the Post Isolation Support Activities. That`s a very carefully guarded process that they go through. Trevor Reed went through the exact same thing when he was released from Russian detention earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of that is the mental side of this, right? The mental decompression from the trauma that one goes through in an experience like this. And as she concluded her post, she made it really clear to say that as part of her next steps, she will be continuing to advocate for the release of Paul Whelan as well as other Americans. We heard the same thing on Sherelle Griner`s Instagram.\par \par \uc2 We know that her wife Sherelle had mentioned that this is something that they both are committed to. As soon as we found out that Brittney Griner was being released, she said that in front of a national audience as part of her thank you and excitement and having her wife home. And then finally she dropped the bombshell, Yasmin. We know that she says an answer to a question so many have had when it comes to her future.\par \par \uc2 What does her future look like with basketball? She said, "I intend to play basketball for WNBA`s Phoenix Mercury this season." We know that this weekend she was able to play basketball for the first time since this all unfolded roughly 10 months ago. Her first move was a dunk that was straight from her agent. And I will end with this too. Her lawyer had told us that while Brittney Griner was optimistic, she would be released.\par \par \uc2 This was a huge surprise that this happened in time for the holidays. So, a big surprise for everyone. I`m sure probably the biggest Christmas surprise and miracle that they could get.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. That is exactly what I was thinking as you were talking there, Marissa in that -- gosh, the timing could just not be better for Brittney and her family to be around each other for the holidays after such a harrowing ordeal. Marissa Parra for us. Thank you. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. A sentencing handed down in just the last couple of hours for one of the first two storm the Capitol on January 6. How long will he serve in prison and what it means for others facing similar charges? And like a bomb of viruses went off. Those words from one pediatrician as hospitals scrambled a treat a massive surge of patients. I`m going to ask a major in the U.S. Medical Corps Reserve what the government can do to help.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:07]\par \par \uc2 And remembering civil rights leader \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 . I`m going to speak to the man behind the late congressman`s iconic portrait. That will be memorialized on a \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  staff. You`re watching MSNBC. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back, everybody. What a day, what a week it`s been when it comes to those markets, right? You`re looking at Wall Street now. Markets are tanking for a third straight day, I believe, down around 500 points or so. 496 to be exact and with these kinds of renewed fears of a impending recession. All three major indicators they`re in the red. The Dow has now fallen more than 1000 points since Tuesdays open.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in CNBC`s Kristina Partsinevelos to talk more about this. I think kind of the overarching question is what`s going on? We know there are these fears, right? Of this impending recession. We`ve been seeing these possible layoffs happening in places like \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Thousands of employees likely to be laid off there and then you had the rate hike yesterday from Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . Talk to us.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:04]\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA PARTSINEVELOS, CNBC NASDAQ REPORTER: Yes. That`s -- precisely, people are just trying to time it out and see how bad this recession is going to be. More specifically, you mentioned \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , maybe some of these job cuts are coming more at the higher level, a lot of tech and banks are laying off. Less so at the lower level, smaller businesses are still doing OK. They actually think it`s a tight labor market for them.\par \par \uc2 But the problem with the market today is the fact that our central bank, you mentioned the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wanting to keep raising interest rates. And this just after they hiked interest rates by half a percentage point on Wednesday. And the Fed isn`t alone. So that`s more of the reaction we`re seeing today, you`ve got the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  that just joined the 50-basis points club, they did the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 And so, markets don`t love it when rates keep increasing because that makes debt more expensive for companies and may cause them to stop expanding and hiring.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: The thing is, Kristina, we are seeing -- we just got these numbers in saying that it seemed like inflation was getting a little bit better, right? Earlier, it seemed then anticipate. So, you think at that point, the reaction to that and markets would be rallying, not 