n was one of the first 10 people to breach the Capitol and one of the most recognizable after footage showed him chasing down \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  Officer Eugene Goodman. You`ve seen this footage many times since January 6.\par \par \uc2 The hero cop lead Jensen and the mob away from the spot where senators and Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in fact were gathered. Narrowly averting a potential catastrophe. NBC`s Ken Dilanian has been following this story for us and brings us more.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Yasmin, the judge really threw the book at Jensen saying he embolden the mob that attacked the Capitol and failed to express remorse for his actions. This 60-month sentence was just short of what prosecutors had asked for. The Iowa construction worker was one of the first rioter standard the Capitol during the January 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 He led what prosecutors called a menacing chase inside of Officer Eugene Goodman who was seen in that now famous video heroically diverting the rioters away from senators. The jury found that Jensen assaulted Goodman and prosecutors noted that he was so intent on getting inside that he scaled a 20-foot wall so that he can be one of the very first rioters to break into the building and disrupt the proceedings in congress.\par \par \uc2 Although there`s some evidence that he thought he was storming the White House. Judge Timothy Kelly said it was a miracle that his conduct didn`t lead to greater injuries or deaths. A jury found Jensen guilty on seven counts including felony charges of civil disorder and assaulting police officers. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officer said Jensen`s actions endanger the entire \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During the trial, Jensen`s attorney described his client as a man who`d fallen deeply down the rabbit hole of QAnon. And the lawyer said that Jensen honestly believed that the 2020 election had been stolen and that he needed to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right that wrong. Now Jensen has already served 20-months in pretrial detention. But that means he faces at least another 40 months behind bars. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ken Dilanian for us, we thank you. I want to bring in Paul Butler now, former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. As always, Paul, it`s great to talk to you about this stuff. So, I want to really just kind of talk about the significance, Paul, if we can have the sentence the judge essentially saying that a significant sentence was in fact, necessary to show A, respect for the law and B, deterrence.\par \par \uc2 Oh, it looks like we lost Paul and he in fact, was frozen. We`re going to try and get Paul Butler back of course. I think it`s important we kind of talk about the significance really of the sentencing especially going forward as we look ahead and of course what factored in of course to the sentencing.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:10]\par \par \uc2 And have Paul back. All right, Paul. So, if we can, let`s try one more time. What is the significance of this sentence?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, Yasmin, Jensesn is still not showing remorse. And that`s why the judge handed down such a tough sentence. Remember, this was a QAnon conspiracy theorists who scaled a 20-foot wall, broke in to the Capitol and hunted down Vice President Pence. Jensen had a knife with a three-inch blade on it. And after he was arrested, he told the \uc2 FBI\uc2  it would have all been worth it if Trump had stayed in power.\par \par \uc2 Even today, he never really expressed regret or apologized for what he did, but he just was sorry about the impact that the criminal prosecution had on his own life. And that`s why the judge said today that Jensen still doesn`t understand what he did was wrong. And that`s why Jensen got sentence near the top of the federal sentencing guidelines.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You talked about his lack of remorse. And I also think it speaks to kind of this extreme indoctrination that he has, because of this violation of his pretrial release, right? July 2021, federal judge agreeing to release Jensen to home detention with instructions and I`m reading this for bait to avoid the internet and political news after his lawyers claimed that he had renounced his conspiratorial beliefs.\par \par \uc2 So, his lawyer said, he had renounced these conspiratorial beliefs. Within a month, Paul, a pretrial services officer caught Jensen watching videos from conspiracy theorist and pillow magnate Mike Lindell on a contraband phone in his garage, and he was sent back to jail. How much do you think something like this played into his sentencing?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So, Yasmin, judges don`t like it when defendants appear to lie to them or appear not to follow their orders. This is a defendant who was very unsympathetic at the sentencing, the police officer testify that the cops didn`t attack the mob, but rather the mob attacked the cops. And prosecutors called Jensen the poster boy of the insurrectionist. He was part of the violence that resulted in lives lost and hundreds of police officer injuries.\par \par \uc2 Very evocative, powerful testimony from police officers about the ways that them -- that Jensen not ruin their lives, what made it change their lives in irreparably.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It`s interesting, Paul, because we`ve seen this video over and over again. I mean, this was one of the first videos I believe that emerged from the January 6 Capitol insurrection and Jensen was front and center of this video, chasing down this police officer that was protecting members inside the Capitol, the former vice president of the United States and protecting his own life trying to protect and save his own life amidst it all, right?\par \par \uc2 How much do you -- do you think videos like this and of course, this kind of lack of remorse that you spoke of a little bit earlier, how much did that play into this whole scenario?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: I think it played into the judge sentencing. It`s such dramatic, horrifying video to watch. The fact that Jensen still isn`t showing remorse is -- means that probably a lot of other people out there don`t really get the harm in what happened. And of course, the other injury of January 6 was to democracy. And there we think of the big lie in the fake elector scheme. People at the top have yet to be held accountable for that, Jasmine.\par \par \uc2 So, the Justice Department has proven that it can go after the little guys, including the very dangerous little guys like Jensen, but now many people are wondering if they will show the same as off to go after the big guys.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. Or the big guy. Yes. Paul Butler. Thank you, as always, we appreciate it. Let`s talk weather everybody because there`s a lot going on right now, right? Millions of people across the northeast are dealing with a nasty mix of snow ice rain as well. But that is nothing compared to the punishing storms that have hit other parts of the country, dumping several feet of snow in some places.\par \par \uc2 Spawning deadly tornadoes and others as well. As we speak, roughly 24 million Americans are under some kind of winter weather alert. NBC`s Kathy Parker reports from hard-hit Syracuse.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, that massive snowstorm that we have been following ever since Tuesday has made its way to the northeast and here in Syracuse, we are tracking several inches of wet snow already. But this is a long duration storm. So, we`re looking at several more inches of snow when the system finally moves on out of here. But the governor of New York \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  activated thousands of pieces of equipment to stay on top of the storm.\par \par \uc2 Crews are also staying busy locally. But this is the same system that created a whole lot of havoc all across the country spawning tornadoes in the south across seven states and hard-hit Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:05]\par \par \uc2 A lot of homes and businesses were completely leveled and destroyed. And also in Duluth, Minnesota, the Great Plains, they were looking at blizzard conditions as well.\par \par \uc2 So a massive storm system and here in Syracuse, we are tracking the system that will last for the next couple of hours, but we`re teetering right around the freezing mark. So we`ll be following the conditions as it goes from snow to a wintery mix, but it`s certainly is a mess as we end the week. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: NBC`s Kathy Park for us, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody. This nightmare scenario, you all know it by now. Hospitals around the country, COVID, RSV, flu as well, and now you got strep filling up emergency rooms before the holidays. A medical expert, a major in the U.S. Air Force Medical Corps Reserve is here to talk about what needs to be done to help. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, overwhelmed hospitals scrambling to treat patients in the middle of a huge viral spike. One pediatrician telling "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," it is, quote, "like a bomb of viruses went off." And we`re seeing numbers skyrocket just as travel is starting to ramp up for the Christmas holiday, for the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 Right now doctors across the country they`re fighting off one of the worst flu seasons in years. Already seen at least 13 million cases. COVID numbers up nearly 40 percent nationwide as well over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Dr. Vin Gupta, pulmonologist and faculty member at the \uc2 Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation\uc2  at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 . He`s also an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gupta, as always, it`s great to talk to you. All right, AAA, 113 million Americans are expected to travel over the holidays, right. If you`re thinking like and it`s looking like this bomb of viruses is going off, people are continuing to travel, no one is really canceling their plans at this point, it seems, right, what are you worried about happening going forward as we all get together?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yasmin, good afternoon. Great to see you. People should keep their plans. Nothing should change. If you`re medically high at risk, for three years, we`ve been talking about what that means. I`m assuming that folks watching know if they are medically high risk, have a treatment plan in place with your medical provider. I have been pushing our fellow colleagues in medicine to do standing prescriptions.\par \par \uc2 If you`re medically high risk, you should have a standing prescription. If you meet criteria for Paxlovid for COVID or Tamiflu for flu, the stuff that we can control, we should make it easy to enable access. So standing prescriptions. Have a treatment plan.\par \par \uc2 But to your question.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: It is well past time, especially because pediatric hospitals across the country are overwhelmed, as you said, that we just need to accept what`s unpopular, and I`ll just say it, that communities across the country, especially with pediatric hospitals that are overwhelmed, which is most, just need to mask up. Schools need to take the leadership here. Public health officials need to accept that --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: And I know that they privately communicate this all the time. We need to have the courage to say this publicly, even if it`s unpopular, that communities, especially with hospitals, children`s hospitals that have little stocking system, need to protect those hospitals. Schools need to lead by example and mask. And that school officials, I`m looking at you, public officials at the local level, it only makes sense. It`s our only buttress here in addition to vaccination, and we need to do it.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So I understand how it`s what makes sense, but then there is, right, reality. The reality on the ground of what we have been dealing with over the last three years. And people`s emotions, and we`ve had this conversation over the years whereas once you ask or allow folks to take those masks off, it`s going to be hard for them to then put them back on. Especially those kids that have been suffering in school, had to stay out for weeks at a time throughout the COVID pandemic have now kind of returned to this, quote-unquote, "normal." And then once again, these administration officials would ask them to put them back on. I think that`s -- it`s a hard sell it seems right now in this country.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Well, Yasmin, I think that`s a small price to pay. Unfortunately, you`re right. I`m not saying that there won`t be some degree of discomfort, inconvenience and sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: What`s the alternative? The point in the military, I say this as a medical reservist on the adult side, won`t work in this case. We don`t have a lot of pediatric providers in the U.S. Military to step in and say, hey, we`re going to save the day. And here`s the cavalry. That doesn`t work here. What we know is that we only have one-tenth the hospital capacity for kids as we do for adults. Adult hospital (INAUDIBLE) this strain, pediatric will be as well. So what`s the alternative here, that we`re just going to let -- at children`s hospitals in particular go overwhelmed?\par \par \uc2 I think that is the greater priority, even if it means unpopular policies. We have to get real here. 8 to 12 weeks, dead of winter, this is probably because we live in an era of respiratory epidemics and pandemics. We have to deal with the fact that this is an expectation. This is a costly lever we may have to pull, and you have to get the public ready for that. The alternative is not going to be pretty.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So essentially if you`re traveling right now, your advice is wear a mask. If you`re getting on a plane, wear a mark. Put masks on your kids. If your kids are going to school, put masks on them as well, even if there`s not necessarily policy in place that mandates that.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: That`s exactly right. Wear a high-quality N-95 like mask. They`re about 50 percent of the price they used to be because few people are buying them right now. So it`s a good time to find deal, if you`re willing to do that. Also, co-benefits of the flu vaccine, you brought up strep throat. Minimal supply of treatments like amoxicillin. Critical for parents out there. Only 46 percent of kiddos have gotten the flu vaccine. That is unacceptably low.\par \par \uc2 Really important for parents out there listening right now. The flu vaccine actually helps protect your child against bacteria like strep A. The less likely they are infected with the virus like flu, the more their immune system is able to fend off other infections like strep throat. And so it`s critical. There are co-benefits to the flu vaccine that we don`t talk about. Vital. If you`re a parent watching this, your kid hasn`t gotten the shot, get them the flu vaccine.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. I`ll tell you. Both my kids had the flu vaccine.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02][\par \par \uc2 Both of them got the flu but literally had a fever for six hours, maybe, and were out of school for two days. And then they were back again. And the doctor said it was because of the fact they got the vaccine that they had it so minimally.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Vin Gupta, as always, it`s great to talk to you about this stuff. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right. An NBC News exclusive, everybody. Revealing U.S. officials believe Ukraine has the military capacity to retake Crimea. Why some expects are concerned about what might happen if they do. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back, everybody. So right now, you see it there. The president just landed back at Joint Base Andrews headed back to Washington, D.C. Moments ago while boarding Air Force One in Delaware, he was asked if the U.S. will send the Patriot air defense missile system to Ukraine. He responded, quote, "You`ll hear in a few minutes." It is not clear at this moment what the president meant by that, in a few minutes. He doesn`t have any public events on his schedule, but we`re going to bring you any updates of course as we have them.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 I do want to turn to turn now, though, to a new NBC News exclusive. A Biden administration official recently telling members of Congress that Ukraine does, indeed, have the military capability to recapture Crimea. That is according to two U.S. officials familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Courtney Kube broke this story for us and is joining us now.\par \par \uc2 Court, as always, it`s great to talk to you. So the question is, they`ve got it seems the necessary supplies to do this. But should they? And what could happen if they did?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: That`s right. So they have the capability. They have the equipment, as you said, that they would potentially need for this kind of offensive which I will say would be an extremely difficult offensive for the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 But as you mentioned, Yasmin, there are some in the Biden administration who we spoke with who are concerned about any kind of future operation, a large-scale invasion or offensive against Crimea because it is such a strategic area for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And some people have even described it as an almost emotional issue. They are worried that if, in fact, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  felt that his hold on Crimea was really threatened that that could be the backing-in-the-corner that we`ve heard about for months that could lead him to really escalate the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Now we should say, at this point -- and the escalation and the concern is it could be something like a dirty bomb or even some sort of a limited low- yield nuclear weapon. Now we should say at this point the U.S. officials we spoke with said there are no indications that Russia is preparing to use either of those inside Ukraine. And in fact, whereas the tensions were pretty high on that front back in October, they`ve really, really decreased in the last several weeks.\par \par \uc2 There`s also no indication at this point that Ukraine is preparing to mount any kind of a large-scale offensive against Crime. In fact they`re actually moving in slightly different directions. They`re pretty dug in in a tough, tough fight up in Bakhmut, up in the east. And they are really in a tough spot down in Kherson where the Russians are really dug in in trenches in the defensive crouch really down in the Kherson area in the south.\par \par \uc2 So at this point, there`s no indications that the Ukrainians are moving in that direction. But it is something that Biden administration are discussing and there`s a point in the future where they are concerned about this may become a reality, Yas.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Got it. All right, Courtney Kube, for us, as always, thank you, Courtney. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right. It was a tense morning, everybody, in Ukraine, in the capital city specifically after a large wave of missile attacks by Russian forces prompted air raid sirens and fear. Kyiv`s military administration said the attack was, quote, "one of the biggest missile attacks since February." The strikes come at the Pentagon says they will begin training 500 Ukrainian troops a month in Germany starting in January.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring in NBC`s Ellison Barber who`s standing by for us in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, as always it`s great to talk to you. Just how devastating was this morning`s missile attack? And what else can we expect in the coming days?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So Ukrainian officials say that their air defense systems intercepted the majority of these missiles. According to the armed forces of Ukraine, Russia fired 76 missiles at Kyiv. They say they intercepted 60 of those, but those that they were un unable to intercept, some of them hit energy infrastructure. Others hit residential areas.\par \par \uc2 In the southern city of (INAUDIBLE) a missile slammed into a residential building. Local officials say at least three people in that strike alone have died. Over a dozen are injured. Air raid sirens started going off at about 8:00 a.m. this morning for hours. People hunkered in shelters waiting for the all clear. Right now, at least five regions are experiencing some form of power outages amid near freezing temperatures.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): This morning Russia launching a new wave of missile strikes across Ukraine clouding the sky over Kyiv and forcing Ukrainians underground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s kind of making you scared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We get used to it.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: The strikes come as Ukrainian officials warn of a renewed push for the capital. The commander of the Armed Forces of Ukraine telling the "Economist," quote, "Russians are preparing some 200,000 fresh troops. I have no doubt they will have another go at Kyiv."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russia offering a new warning of their own. This one directed to the United States. Russia`s Foreign Ministry says if the U.S. provides Ukraine with the Patriot air defense missile system, it will be a provocative move that could entail possible consequences. Three U.S. Defense officials tell NBC News Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  is poised to approve Ukraine`s request for the sophisticated air defense system as soon as this week.\par \par \uc2 But the most intense fighting is still in the east. In Bakhmut yesterday a Ukrainian heavy artillery brigade launched attacks on Russian positions as volunteers like American Brad Henderson tried to rescue civilians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: When Russia carries out these large-scale missile attacks on Ukraine`s energy infrastructure it is not meant to change anything on the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 This is a military doctrine that is designed and focused on making civilians suffer with the hopes that they will get frustrated with the war and that their frustration will then force leaders to capitulate the people impacted.\par \par \uc2 Every time this happens, Yasmin, and we cannot say it enough, it is civilians. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ellison Barber for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  honoring the late congressman and civil rights icon \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 . The man behind this portrait that will be featured on the postage stamp next year joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back, everybody. Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , a civil rights icon, known for making, quote, "good trouble" is being honored with a postage stamp next year. The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  says it, quote, "celebrates the life and legacy" of Lewis, pictured here in this striking portrait.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in now the man behind the camera lens, photographer Marco Grob.\par \par \uc2 Marko, it`s great to talk to you on this. I know that you`ve created portraits as well for former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , Trump and Obama as well. What was it like creating this portrait for \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 MARCO GROB, PHOTOGRAPHER: Well, thank you for having me. Well, it was actually very pleasurable for me, a very pleasurable experience, since Mr. Lewis is one of my icons, and I remember it with -- it`s a very good memory and I`m very fond of that memory.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What was the day like for you, Marco? Take us there if you will, what it`s like, taking a portrait like this of a man like this. What was the day like? What did you do?\par \par \uc2 GROB: It was extremely relaxing. It was one of at a time offices in Washington, D.C., and it was a very different mood than you would normally have with a politician. They don`t particularly like to be photographed because they lose control, and he didn`t -- there was nothing (INAUDIBLE) normally you would have from a politician. So it was very relaxed. We talked a lot actually that day. And I just went on and we spent about two hours.\par \par \uc2 And within talking, I took my pictures, and it was kind of one of those experiences where you don`t even know and don`t even feel that you`re working, it was extremely easy and relaxed. He gave me a very good feeling to work with him.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It`s an incredible portrait, I have to say, Marco. So striking.\par \par \uc2 GROB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And you are obviously very talented as well and we are so thankful that you took this portrait, and of course now able to share it with the world.\par \par \uc2 Marco Grob, for us. Thank you, Marco.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us for this hour, everybody. You can always catch me on "YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN REPORTS" on MSNBC weekends, 2:00 to 4:00 p.m. Eastern right here. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221217eicg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Chris Jansing Reports for December 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ryan Nobles, Charles Coleman, Peter Baker, Kurt  Bardella, Molly Hunter, Morgan Chesky, Ellison Barber, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 7,744 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:02]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 January 6th Committee members fresh off a Sunday meeting, trying to nail down the question of who might be subject to criminal referrals. But that`s just one of a series of crucial choices that need to be made by lawmakers before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the man believed to be behind the 1988 Lockerbie bombing expected to appear in court this afternoon. But has the 34-year long wait for justice made it harder to get a conviction?\par \par \uc2 And a new tool unveiled today to fight hate in one of the country`s largest cities and the stark warning about what happens if we don`t deal with the escalating problem now. I`ll talk with Cynthis Choi, one of the founders of the group Stop AAPI Hate.\par \par \uc2 But we start on Capitol Hill where lawmakers are wrestling with critical decisions as the clock ticks down to the end of the 117th Congress. Three examples, this week to start. Government funding is set to run out on Friday with potential shutdown to follow. And members can`t even agree on a top line number. Republicans can`t seem to agree on how far to go when it comes to investigating the DOJ on Hunter Biden once they take over the House. And one of the most hotly anticipated decisions is coming from the January 6th Committee. How will be named in those criminal referrals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): We`re looking at what is the quantum of evidence that we have against individuals. What is the impact of making a referral? Are we going to create some suggestion by referring some that others there wasn`t sufficient evidence when we don`t know for example what evidence is in the possession of the Justice Department? So if we do make referrals, we want to be careful about how we do them. But I think we`re all certainly in agreement that there is evidence of criminality here and we want to make sure that the Justice Department is aware of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles, Peter Baker, chief White House correspondent for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and an MSNBC political analyst, Kurt Bardella is a Democratic strategist but previously served as House Oversight Committee spokesperson for Republicans, and Charles Coleman is a civil rights attorney, a former prosecutor, and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Charles, look, we know DOJ doesn`t have to do anything with a criminal referral, right, but we do have a committee full of lawyers who understand presumably what would need to go into an indictment. So what would any criminal referrals tell us?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it`s important to understand that there are different types of criminal referrals that can come from Congress. And in this case we actually don`t know which of the two that are germane to what we`re dealing with, with January 6th that they will seek to send over. You have one type of criminal referral that will refer to acts of crime that have been committed against Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, a failure to adhere to a subpoena or contempt of Congress, that could actually be something that is referred to the DOJ vis-a-vis Trump or anyone else who failed to appear after being request to comply and cooperate with the January 6th Committee. You also have another type of criminal referral which can be sent in terms of just general crimes of things that people have committed not necessarily against Congress. There is a litany of activity that we could see from January 6th there.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what we will learn, probably not very much in terms of what`s disclosed as part of a criminal referral. We will likely learn who the referral is made about and possibly the charge. But the information is going to be in most cases nonpublic and they`re not going to disclose that in terms of what they turn over to the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So, Ryan, the committee held that rare Sunday session, we mentioned, yesterday. Do we know anything about what came out of it and are we hearing anything about agreements or disagreements within the committee about where this is going?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, we know they made progress, Chris. That this is still an ongoing debate within the committee about just how many criminal referrals to hand over and the scope of those criminal referrals. And in general, the committee members do appear to be on the same page. It`s just a matter of finalizing exactly what they want to hand over not just to the Department of Justice but potentially other federal agencies as well.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that committee members have said over and over again is that they don`t want this to be a 5-4 vote if they hand over a criminal referral. They want unanimous consent on their final report. And that final report is going to include any referrals if they do determine that. So it`s making sure that every single member, all nine members feel comfortable with the language that eventually gets sent over to any agency within the federal government and the final report in totality.\par \par \uc2 So I`m told that they`re going to take a step back over the next couple of days and review the initial draft of the final report, offer up any potential changes that will need to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:08]\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind that, you know, outside the referrals, we`re talking about a voluminous report that could be as many as eight chapters that include, you know, dispatches from the many different teams that worked on investigating what happened in the days leading up to January 6th and on the day itself. And they`ve now set themselves a deadline. The chairman has said he wants this all wrapped up by December 21st. So the clock is ticking.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Boy, is it. Well, Peter, we know House Republicans have been making a lot of noise about investigating the investigators, DOJ, when they take over in January. But not everybody agrees. People like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , the top Republican on that Judiciary Committee, is more interested, for example, in the Hunter Biden story. Where do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Well, the reason why they would rather focus on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is because they don`t think they can actually defend President Trump, former President Trump on some of the specific allegations being made against him. Other than attacking the investigators, there hasn`t been much of a defense from Trump`s Republican allies on, for instance, the, you know, taking and withholding of classified documents at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 They complained that this search of Mar-a-Lago was somehow an overreach. But they don`t say that the former president had the right to take these documents or had the right to basically stiff the government when the government asked for them back. So they`d rather focus on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . They`d rather focus on other topics where they can, you know, make some headway, they believe, against President Biden and against the Democrats. And because defending former President Trump on the merits is something that leaves them very uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Kurt, Congressman Jim Comer is expected to lead the House Oversight Committee and he was actually on FOX this morning talking about what he plans to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): The Democrats have had the majority in both the House and the Senate. And they`ve completely turned a blind eye on the Biden administration, to their disastrous policies, to their runaway spending and to the elephant in the room, this influence peddling that their family continues to do.\par \par \uc2 We`re basing our investigations on evidence. Everything that we`ve brought forward we have reason to believe is necessary from an investigative or an oversight standpoint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So you wrote an op-ed recently describing this as taxpayer funded witch hunt. So how do you reconcile what he`s saying with what you are describing?\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, FORMER SPOKESPERSON FOR HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: Well, here`s the bottom line. Republicans in Congress are liars. We just saw Jim Jordan, who would be the chairman of the Judiciary Committee, go out there and say in a public hearing that his committee didn`t tweet Kanye, Elon, Trump tweet when they actually did. If they`re willing to lie about fundamental things like that, they`re willing to lie about everything.\par \par \uc2 Never mind the fact Mr. Comber that the Senate Homeland Security Committee led by Republican Ron Johnson did an investigation about the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  stuff already, and guess what, they didn`t find anything. Comer is just making it up.\par \par \uc2 What the Republicans are going to try to do here, Chris, is try to make the accusation first, and then the hope that the investigation that they want to launch will back it up. It`s completely backwards. That`s not how oversight is supposed to work. And that`s why it is a taxpayer funded witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy went out there and said after Benghazi that the goal of the Benghazi committee was political, it was to take down Hillary Clinton. Republicans are drawing back to that playbook hoping that by just the mere appearance of anything they can go after a private citizen, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , who never served in the administration, never served in a federal government, didn`t work in the West Wing unlike \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  and \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , and hoped that they can somehow taint the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not going to work, Chris, because guess what? Voters have no interest in this kind of stuff. And if Republicans, if the lesson they just took away from the midterm elections was to launch investigations and not talk about the issues that affect everyday Americans, they`re going to keep losing election after election.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At the same time, Kurt, you argue that the Senate Democrats should use their oversight power more aggressively in the new Congress. So explain that.\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Well, because we actually have factual information that shows \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  got $2 billion shortly after leaving the White House from Saudi Arabia. We know that Jared and Ivanka worked in the White House in the administration as President Trump and his family reaped the benefits of that financially. We know for a fact that they billed the Secret Service overcharging them while staying at Trump properties that money went right into Donald Trump`s pocket.\par \par \uc2 These things actually happened. There`s documentary evidence to support that. Those are legitimate threats of oversight. What Jim Comer and Jim Jordan, and oversight Republicans are talking about is literally a fishing expedition. They don`t actually have any proof of anything. They just make statements and hope that they can find evidence to back that up.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Peter, let`s talk about what`s going on right now. Kevin McCarthy said last week that any Republican out there who`s trying to work with Democrats right now is wrong. So if they`re going to come up with a deal to fund the government and avoid a shutdown, obviously the two sides have to work together, right? How do you think that is looking right now?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, look, the Democrats can pass, you know, a spending bill by themselves in the House.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:03]\par \par \uc2 The question is the Senate where they do need of course Republican cooperation. And that that`s what they`re looking at. Now there are Republican equities here, basically. They have some reasons to want an omnibus spending bill. They are in favor of increasing defense spending, for instance, a continuing resolution that is to keep everybody at the same level for the next year.\par \par \uc2 Would it be bad for the Pentagon in their view of a lot of Republicans. So they have their own interests in creating a deals even if Kevin McCarthy`s priority right now is emphasizing, you know, partisan conflict because of course he`s trying to satisfy his most conservative members as he`s seeking to win with the speakership with a very, very narrow majority. It`s in his interest to focus on that at the moment.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not where \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has more influence at this point, at least until January 3rd\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles, one of those we know has quite a bit of influence with Speaker-to-be, McCarthy, perhaps, perhaps, speaker to be McCarthy, is \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . She talked about January 6th over the weekend at a dinner Saturday night and has just confirmed that she said this.\par \par \uc2 "I will tell you something, if \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and I had organized that, we would have won. Not to mention, it would have been armed."\par \par \uc2 She claimed to our team today those were sarcastic comments. But do they have any potential legalism indications?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I don`t think they have potential legal indications. But I do think that things like this have to be remembered and have to be addressed very seriously. It is not enough for someone to simply say oh, this was sarcastic or this was joking. Because if, in fact, we find out that someone acts on what she has said under the guise of I was following Congressman Taylor Greene`s speech.\par \par \uc2 That is something that she could potentially find herself in hot water under. And I think it`s very interesting that even as we have seen so much come from the committee where people have said I came to Washington because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told me to, and those people are now facing criminal prosecution, this is an example of where people still haven`t learned their lesson. They aren`t taking it seriously.\par \par \uc2 And so while this actual comment itself may not have criminal implications, it`s not a stretch to believe that someone may follow those instructions and decide that they`re going to take up that mantle, in which case it could be very well linked back to her and that`s something that should be taken extremely seriously.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan Nobles, Kurt Bardella, thank you. Peter Baker, Charles Coleman, you guys are going to stay with me. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 A key suspect in one of the worst terrorist attacks in history will be in court this afternoon in Washington, D.C. It took investigators more than three decades to arrest the alleged bombmaker following the downing of Flight 103 in Lockerbie, Scotland that killed 270 people, including 190 Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY KAY STRATIS, LOST HUSBAND IN ATTACK: It`s taken 34 years to get this far. And so we want this justice fast.\par \par \uc2 KARA WEIPZ, LOST BROTHER IN ATTACK: It`s surreal. It`s overwhelming. It`s - - I don`t even know if I have the exact words at this moment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Molly Hunter is following the story from London for us. Charles Coleman is still with me.\par \par \uc2 Molly, this is a culmination a decades long investigation. What can you tell me about we got here and what the court appearance today is all about?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, this is a huge deal. And I think actually there`s so many unknowns still even after 34 years. You just heard from the victims` family members just there. 34 years is a long time to wait. I think there are basically two big questions that we have had since the DOJ -- excuse me -- announced that Masud would be in court today.\par \par \uc2 First, how did he get into U.S. hands? So we understand that of course the DOJ charged him with two criminal counts two years ago. That was even three years after U.S. officials got their hands on a confession tape that he gave in 2012. That is, you know, a long time even for victims to have to wait and understood that was all happening. There`s been a lot of twists and turns in that. So there has been -- excuse me, that`s the first question is how did he get into U.S. hands.\par \par \uc2 And the second is, is that confession tape going to be admissible? And that will be a legal question of course that we will find the answers to in the next coming kind of weeks and months. Back in 2001, there has been an initial Lockerbie trial. One Libyan operative was convicted but the prosecution`s case, Chris, at the time was that he was not the only one, that there were going to be more coming if they could get the evidence, if they could get the cases together. And that is what victims have really hoped for.\par \par \uc2 I do want to just share a little bit more sound from Paul Hudson, his daughter was one of the victims. Take a listen, Chris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL HUDSON, FATHER OF LOCKERBIE VICTIM MELINA HUDSON: She lost at least three quarters of her life. Her belongings were scattered all over the Lockerbie countryside. We did get her passport and we got her notebook. And the notebook had on cover the quote, "No one dies unless they`re forgotten." And I have tried to live by that.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:04]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: And you try to remember her always.\par \par \uc2 HUDSON: It`s an everyday thing. And of course this time of year with the anniversary, it gets stronger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: The anniversary of course four days before Christmas, Chris. And that we will expect to see the Masud in court, in the arraignment at 3:00 p.m., Chris. Earlier today we expected it to be 1:00 p.m. We do not know why that was delayed until 3:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Charles, the first thing I thought when I heard about this was 34 years is a long time. How much of a complicating factor is that and what about this confession, this alleged confession?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: You know, Chris, looking at this from the standpoint of a former prosecutor, I can honestly tell you that a case like this, trying it is a nightmare. You`re talking about 34 years of evidence that may not necessarily be fresh anymore. You don`t know if you`re going to be able to get all of the witnesses in. And even as Molly talked about with the other case that did generate a conviction, you`re not necessarily guaranteed to be able to transfer all of the evidence that you use in that trial to a trial like this.\par \par \uc2 And so it creates a wealth of different complications for someone who is trying this case. With respect to the actual confession itself, I would expect that defense attorneys are going to fight tooth and nail to try to keep that out because that`s the biggest thing that prosecutors would have working in their favor. Without that they don`t necessarily have a strong a case because of the challenges around how do you get certain pieces of evidence in if you don`t have witnesses to authenticate them and everything else that is required in order for you to try a case of this nature.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s going to rise and fall on the admissibility of that actual confession. We don`t know the circumstances under which law enforcement was able to secure that. So it`s unsure right now as to how admissible it will be. But that`s going to be a key element and factor in terms of how this all plays out.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Charles Coleman, thank you so much for coming in today. Molly Hunter, thanks to you as well.\par \par \uc2 Also in a Washington courtroom today, the Justice Department is launching a new case against members of the Oath Keepers, trying to prove more seditious conspiracy charges in connection with January 6th. This case involves four members that prosecutors say plotted with founder Stewart Rhodes to stop the transfer of power, including two of them who allegedly took part in the insurrection. The case comes less than two weeks after Rhodes and another leader, Kelly Meggs, were convicted of seditious conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Paul Whelan could be home but isn`t. The new revelations about the opportunity the Trump administration had. Plus New York`s new initiative to combat hate crimes and the troubling data we`re seeing out of L.A. And 15 million Americans are under threat of brutal winter weather. Look at these pictures. My goodness. What`s coming next?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:21:54]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New political backlash against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is now admitting he could have freed Paul Whelan but didn`t. The former president writing on Truth Social that he turned down a deal with Russia because he thought releasing Viktor Bout was too dangerous. At the same time Trump also criticizing President Biden for not including former Marine Paul Whelan in the deal he made for Brittney Griner, writing, quote. "He would have been let out for the asking." Whelan`s brother, though, hitting back hard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WHELAN, BROTHER OF PAUL WHELAN: My brother pleaded from his prison for President Trump to tweet about him during President Trump`s term in office, and President Trump didn`t. And for him now to talk about Paul at all is really offensive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s bring in NBC`s Morgan Chesky in San Antonio where Brittney Griner is staying, and Peter Baker is back with us.\par \par \uc2 Peter, President Trump had more than two years to try to free Paul Whelan but former White House National Security official \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2  said he just didn`t have any interest in the case. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: President Trump wasn`t especially interested in engaging in that swap for also Paul Whelan. He was not particularly interested in Paul`s case in the way that one would have thought he would be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Peter, you covered both administrations. You were Moscow bureau chief when you were at the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." What do you see at play here with these different decisions, two different decisions, two different administrations, and why was it a no on Whelan this time? What`s Putin`s play?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, look, these are very hard decisions for presidents to make. No question about it. They`re put in the position of basically trading away something of value, not just the particular prisoner you might give back but also American credibility, the risk that you are telling people that you can be forced into giving something up. That`s a tough tradeoff for any president to make, whether it be President Trump or President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But what you see here of course is former President Trump trying to have it both ways. He is saying, well, President Biden should have gotten Paul Whelan out, he would have gotten out for the asking but in fact he didn`t ask President Putin to get him out when he had a chance for two years. And that`s I think the issue with President Trump where he likes to pretend other people could do things that he himself didn`t or couldn`t have done.\par \par \uc2 So, look, why was Paul Whelan not released this time? The Russians have made Whelan into a spy. And most people will tell you that`s not true, that there`s no basis for that. It`s been denied by the government and by Paul Whelan`s family. But because the Russians have convicted him of espionage they won`t release him for anybody other than somebody seen as an equivalent in some way, an agent of a government.\par \par \uc2 They tried to get this Russian assassin who killed a Chechen in Berlin, and was arrested, and now is serving a life prison in Germany included in the trade, and Germany said no, thanks, we`re not going to be part of that kind of a deal. And therefore they refused to release Paul Whelan since there was nobody they saw they`re getting on the other side as being equivalent.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Morgan, \uc2 ESPN\uc2  is reporting that Griner returned to the basketball court yesterday and her very first move was to dunk, which I think giving us this amazing image of someone who is doing pretty well physically and mentally got in back in the game.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:03]\par \par \uc2 And NBC News also spoke to her lawyer today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA BLAGOVOLINA, BRITTNEY GRINER`S LAWYER: Even Viktor Bout, in his first interview, he said that when they were crossing in Abu Dhabi, you know, from one point to another, Brittney was smiling. She just looked very friendly. That`s Brittney. I think she`s just really great personality. So this really helps her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s another great image of her walking by the man she was exchanged for and smiling. I wonder what you`re hearing now about how she does seem to be doing. Any other information we have about how long, for example, she might be in San Antonio?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. Very encouraging news. We do know that Brittney and her wife are being held at Fort Sam Houston here in San Antonio, not too far from where I`m standing. And according to \uc2 ESPN\uc2 , all reports are good ones. You mentioned that trademark dunk being one of the first moves she pulled out, and was being described as a light workout on the basketball court.\par \par \uc2 Brittney is expected to make a statement later this week. As of right now, though, the decision to play basketball for the Phoenix Mercury is entirely up to her, that`s according to Brittney`s agent. But according to this reporting, we have heard that recovery back home in the United States, here in San Antonio, so far, so good. Family and friends have been visiting her there at Fort Sam Houston. And we do know a special delivery of barbecue from a local San Antonio institution was delivered for Brittney to share with her family.\par \par \uc2 So, so far so good. And of course all eyes go to that statement Brittney is expected to make later this week on whether or not she will return to play in the \uc2 WNBA\uc2 . Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So it is wonderful to hear all of those things. Then that takes us back to what you were talking about, Peter, which is Paul Whelan and what happens there next. And we know you`ve written extensively about what you just mentioned, which is that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  seems to have a particular interest in this assassin. But where do those negotiations go? And I assume that nobody puts much stock in the fact that Putin said earlier a deal could be made.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes. Look, President Biden has vowed to continue pushing for Paul Whelan`s release. But the problem for the Americans at this point is they don`t have somebody in custody who is an equivalent to what Paul Whelan is made out to be by the Russians. They don`t have a Russian spy in American hands to trade. So the question is what then could be done. What kind of an arrangement could be made.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Putin did say that about a trade being possible. But it`s hard to see what it is that the Russian would accept. They wouldn`t, you know, make the trade for Viktor Bout. So it`s a very frustrating situation for Paul Whelan`s family. They`re very, I think, dismayed by the fact that he has been there now for four years on what seemed to be pretty much trumped-up charges. And he has been left behind one more time. This is the second trade that we`ve had with the Russians this year in which Paul Whelan is not part of it. It`s a grim, grim picture for them as they head into this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Morgan Chesky, Peter Baker, thank you both, guys. Appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 The body of American sports journalist Grant Wahl is now back in the United States. Wahl, you may know, died suddenly while covering the World Cup. The State Department says Wahl`s arrives arrived at JFK airport in New York this morning, accompanied by an official from the U.S. embassy in Doha. Wahl made headlines at the games after being detained for wearing a rainbow shirt to express solidarity with the LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 Our sympathies to his wife, Dr. Celine Gounder, who you may recognize from her appearances on this network during the COVID pandemic, and who posted these photos on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Wahl was just 48 years old.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. Olympic and Paralympic Committee says Russian athletes should be allowed to compete in the 2024 Paris Olympics. Their one stipulation, they don`t compete under the Russian flag or in Russian colors. Both the U.S. and International Olympic Committees had earlier opposed the idea of including Russian athletes but reversed course last week. The head of the U.S. Committee`s Board of Directors says the change is important in order to reflect the Olympic ideal of athlete inclusion.\par \par \uc2 Russian attacks rain down on the liberated city of Kherson and other parts of Ukraine, leaving thousands without power in below freezing conditions. We`re on the ground with the latest, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:31]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, a gruesome scene in Iran where the government says it publicly hanged a man arrested in nationwide protests challenging the country`s theocracy. Those protests you know began in September over the death of a 22-year-old woman who was arrested by the country`s morality police. In the three months since, nearly 20,000 people have been detained, nearly 500 have been killed, according to human rights activists in that country.\par \par \uc2 Iranian media says the man who was executed today was allegedly responsible for the fatal stabbing of two security officials.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials say the recent liberated Ukrainian city of Kherson has come under massive fire today. Those increased attacks coming just hours after Ukrainian President Zelenskyy thanked President Biden in a phone call for what he called unprecedented support from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Ellison Barber in Poltava.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, what are you hearing about the attacks in Kherson? And bring us up to date on the parts of Ukraine that I understand are still without power in these freezing conditions.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris. Yes, so the regional governor of Kherson says the city of Kherson was attacked by Russian forces, killing two, injuring at least five. That was an attack that took place this morning.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned that this is a recently liberated city, the one-month anniversary of the liberation of Kherson was yesterday. This is a community that spent months under Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 After Russian forces retreated from the city, they still have continued to shell the city from the other side of the river. For some of the people living there it has just become too much to bear. We met one couple in Kyiv this weekend who have just recently fled from Kherson. They told us their house there, they had no electricity, no heat, no access to running water, and the shelling was just constant.\par \par \uc2 The woman, she told us, that her nerves just couldn`t take it anymore so they decided to leave, head to Kyiv where they could stay in a friend`s apartment. But even in Kyiv, even far from away from the frontlines they say they still do not feel safe. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YURI PETRUSENKO, RESIDENT (through translator): I don`t think we are safe here either. There are 25 regions in Ukraine. All of them are under Russian strike now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And because we have seen this consistent attack, these consistent attacks on Ukraine`s energy infrastructure since early October, you have the situation where we`re talking often about these massive missile strikes and then the massive outages that follow. But because of all of these strikes, there is a situation here where energy officials say about 50 percent of this country`s energy infrastructure is damaged.\par \par \uc2 So even if someone is living in an area where they weren`t under a missile strike and in an immediate blackout, they are still dealing with rolling blackouts because the consistency of these strikes they damage the grid and the infrastructure so much that they have to cut power in different areas at different times so that the system itself can still stay up and running.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this weekend there was a strike in Odessa that left Saturday night 1.5 million people without power in that city alone. But it`s not just Odessa. This is happening every day all across the country regardless of large missile strikes. But those large missile strikes they`ve been happening almost weekly since October. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ellison Barber, thank you so much. Appreciate that update.\par \par \uc2 A rare shooting in Rome has left three women dead. One of them a friend of the Italian prime minister. The Italian newspaper "La Republica" quoted a witness at the scene who said a man entered the coffee shop shouting, I`ll kill you all, before he opened fire. Other customers managed to disarm and hold him until police arrived. Officials say the 57-year-old suspect had been previously reported for making threats against local residents.\par \par \uc2 The community of Moscow, Idaho, is still searching for answers and closure nearly one month after the brutal killing of four college students off campus. A somber winter commencement was held over the weekend where the four victims were remembered with a moment of silence. One of them, Kaylee Goncalves, was supposed to be among the graduates. She had been planning to move to Texas and take a new job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ethan Chapin, Xana Kernodle, Maddie Mogen, and Kaylee Goncalves were taken from us far too soon by a senseless act of violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Officials are still looking for more information regarding a white Hyundai Elantra in the area around the time of the murders. And meantime, they`re warning students to travel in groups.\par \par \uc2 Hate crimes in Los Angeles soar to the highest level in nearly two decades. What`s behind the spike? One of the founders of an organization fighting to stop these attacks joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:0250]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In New York, national and state leaders are meeting to combat the rising antisemitism and growing threats targeting the Jewish community. Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas and New York City Mayor Eric Adams all speaking today about that uptick in violence.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Adams saying hate is being normalized.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D), NEW YORK CITY: Haters are comfortable right now. We made it into a brand. Words start to plant the seeds of action. And I`m truly alarmed and I`m concerned that if we do not grab hold of the comfort of spewing hate, it`s going to continue to turn into action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The governor now launching a new statewide hate and bias prevention unit saying today, quote, "New York state will use every tool at its disposal to eliminate hate and bias from our communities."\par \par \uc2 Now, on the opposite coast, our next guest is part of California`s new Commission on the State of Hate. In Los Angeles, the most populous county in the country, numbers are showing just how dangerous things are getting. According to the county commission on human relations` 2021 hate crimes report reported crimes rose 23 percent. 74 percent of those were violent in nature. The highest percentage in at least 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The biggest jump in motivation behind these attacks? Race, with more than a two-decade-year high in anti-Asian crimes.\par \par \uc2 Here with us now, Cynthia Choi, she`s one of the founders of Stop AAPI Hate and is the co-director of Chinese for Affirmative Action. She`s also part of California`s new Commission on the State of Hate.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia, thank you for being here. What are you seeing and hearing in your community that made you want to be a part of this?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA CHOI, CO-FOUNDER, STOP AAPI HATE: Well, thank you so much for having me. Well, we started Stop AAPI hate when we started to see a rise in incidents, attacks, verbal harassment against community members. And what we did know is that while we asked our government to track this data, we started Stop AAPI Hate so we could understand the nature of the hate and discrimination and bias our communities are experiencing so that we could also address meaningful solutions.\par \par \uc2 And so regrettably I`m not surprised by the latest reports on the rise against hate against our communities and other affected communities because it comports with our data and what we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:12]\par \par \uc2 And for us one of the things that I think is important to note is that law enforcement often tracks incidents that are defined by law as a hate crime. And we do track all incidents of hate. And that`s really important to understand in terms of solutions moving forward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk a little bit about solutions because it`s tough, right? And moments ago my colleague, Jacob Soboroff, spoke to DHS Secretary Mayorkas about the growth of antisemitism and hate in this country. I want to play that for you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Nearly two years ago at your confirmation hearing you said that you had wanted to tackle the issue of antisemitism once and for all. But what I hear you saying today is it`s only gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: I think that`s absolutely right. But our fight has only become more fervent. The resources we have invested in it have become greater. Our dedication to addressing targeted violence against any group has become more and more intent.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Jacob, as you correctly note, the need for it has increased as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So as the need for it grows, as this gets more and more intense, where do you begin if the problem isn`t people are hateful. How do you affect change and protect civil rights if what we know at the heart of the problem is people`s other and very public hatred?\par \par \uc2 CHOI: Well, first of all, I think it`s important that we have lawmakers and community members who have named that this is a problem and that this is an issue that affects so many communities. And so Stop AAPI Hate, from the very beginning, has really called for three solutions moving forward. One is that we have to do a better job making sure that victims and survivors know where to go for help and get that holistic support that`s needed because it has such a traumatic effect on victims and communities at large.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, we have to make sure that people know what their rights are. And, again, where to go for help when an incident occurs. And of course we are focused on prevention, which we believe needs to center on doing education, particularly in our public education systems. And we`re so proud to, in California, have worked with our lawmakers to address hate wherever it happens, which has largely been in public spaces.\par \par \uc2 So whether it`s on transit or people going to the grocery store, no community should be targeted for their race, ethnicity or religion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cynthia Choi, thank you so much for coming on. And I`m sure we`ll be checking back in with you. We really do appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 And you can catch more of Jacob Soboroff`s interview with DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas tonight on "NIGHTLY NEWS" and during the special "EPIDEMIC OF HATE, ANTISEMITISM IN AMERICA," which streams on NBC News Now Wednesday at 10:30 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this moment. Many view it frankly as a picture of progress. The first female vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , swearing in the first female mayor of Los Angeles, Karen Bass. It took 241 years for the nation`s second largest city to elect a female mayor. But Bass has a formidable task ahead, leading a city with a post-pandemic spike in homelessness and violent crime. She says her first official act today will be to declare a state of emergency over homelessness.\par \par \uc2 Heavy snow, torrential downpours and dangerous flooding conditions. The brutal winter weather now moving from west to east. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:59]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Absolutely brutal weather conditions are expected to impact millions of Americans as a relentless storm begins to move from coast to coast. Take a look at this. It`s already painting the Sierra Nevada white, dumping up to four feet of snow and pummeling the Pacific Coast with torrential rains leading to this swift water rescue in Orange County. The fire department there hoisting a man to safety after a vicious flash flood.\par \par \uc2 Now moving its wrath east where blizzard-like road conditions are expected bringing low visibility to places like this in Utah.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Miguel Almaguer is live in Truckee, California. Miguel, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This storm just walloped the West. It was brutal out here, a blanket of storm all across this region. I want to show you what it looks like here in the Sierra Nevada where they were expecting heavy snowfall. That`s exactly what they got. In the span of about 48 hours, they got 48 inches of snow and in some areas, it was even deeper, about five feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 And it wasn`t just the snowfall that really created havoc across this area, whipping winds topped 165 miles per hour. That created zero visibility on the road. Popular I-80 which connects California and Nevada was shut down for several hours. There were motorists who were stranded inside their cars during the brunt of this storm.\par \par \uc2 And at lower elevations in the San Francisco Bay area, and even in Southern California, there were several inches of rain, up to five inches of rain. That created some flash flooding and fears about mudslides and landslides. Fortunately, those widespread concerns did not materialize. But back here, out in the snow, take a look at these conditions.\par \par \uc2 I also want to show you the trees up here. All of that snowfall is hanging on these branches, and it`s been doing so for several days now, and there is concern over the next 24 to 48 hours, some of these branches could come snapping down and actually hit power lines, cutting power to folks in this area. They need that power to stay warm.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 The conditions out here are frigid. About 11 degrees, 12 degrees here in Truckee right now. It`s going to stay cold here, and folks will definitely need their heaters and heat to keep themselves warm here. Now the good news, the brunt of this storm is over, the bad news it`s on the move headed east. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Miguel, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, we can`t miss this moment of history. After a 26-day mission around the moon, NASA`s next generation Orion capsule returning to earth. Seen here, making what they`re calling a, quote, "picture-perfect splashdown." That big splash marking the end of NASA`s Artemis 1 test flight, the crucial first launch and expedition of the agency`s new mega rocket and space capsule for missions to the moon.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator Bill Nelson telling my colleagues at "MORNING JOE," this is just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: This is just the first, this is the beginning of the new beginning, the Artemis generation, not only going back to the moon, but to learn to live, to work, to invent, to create, in order to go further out, and to go to mars by the end of the decade of the 2030s.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just a reminder, no astronauts were on this flight, but future test flights are tentatively scheduled for some time in 2024 with human passengers.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221213eicc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for December 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Vaughn Hillyard, Ryan Nobles, Geoff Bennett, Symone  Sanders-Townsend, Tom Winter, Joyce Vance, Perry Rossum, Claudio Lavanga,  Ellison Barber \par \uc2 7,930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, everyone. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Georgia puts an exclamation point on the 2022 midterms, reshaping the political landscape and delivering some much-needed political breathing room to Senate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): After one year, 10 months, and 17 days of the longest 50-50 Senate in history, 51, a slim majority. That is a great --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Plus, Donald Trump`s family business convicted on tax fraud. The Manhattan DA telling reporters that trial exposes the lies, the greed, and the cheating that characterized a decade-long criminal scheme. But what`s the fallout for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself? And finally, the governor of North Carolina will join me later this hour to discuss two stories with huge implications. The disturbing and deliberate attack that cut power to hundreds of thousands of people, along with the latest on the Supreme Court case that he says could "upend democracy."\par \par \uc2 But we start with Raphael Warnock`s slim albeit decisive win in Georgia, giving Democrats that critical 51 to 49 edge in the Senate and dealing a significant blow to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Warnock`s victory, his fifth in a row, could have huge implications for Democrats. It means an outright majority of the Senate no longer stymied by senators like Joe Manchin or \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . For Republicans, it punctuates what was already an extremely disappointing midterms and represents another loss for one of Donald Trump`s handpicked candidates.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard live in Atlanta. Ryan Nobles joins me from Capitol Hill. Geoff Bennett is the Chief Washington correspondent for the PBS NewsHour and an MSNBC political contributor. And Symone Sanders-Townsend is the former chief spokesperson for Vice President Harris and host of "Simone" here on MSNBC. Great to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 So, Symone, let`s talk with the big takeaway is, both I think, for the political landscape going forward, but also more immediately for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: So, I`ll take your last question first. The biggest takeaway for the Democratic Party and I would all -- I would particularly say the White House and this Democratic Senate is the ability to have more control over committees, right? Judges, something that this White House has prioritized. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has put more black women on the federal bench than any other president in the history of Democratic presidents, OK? So, considerable moving forward is the ability to continue to do that and grow upon that. There will be vacancies on the federal bench.\par \par \uc2 I also think legislation. Everybody remember Build Back Better, Chris. If there were 51 Democratic senators instead of 50, Build Back Better wouldn`t be a talking point. It would be a reality. I think for Democrats across the board more broadly, the electorate, as you talk about politics going forward. The coalition that Reverend Warnock put together was bolstered by the infrastructure in the state. The organizers, the outside groups that really came in and help the work that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  did over the last 10 years to build that infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Now, Georgia is competitive. I don`t think it`s -- I don`t think it`s a blue state, OK? It`s definitely not blue. It`s not purple, Chris, it`s more like light red, it`s pink. And it`s light -- and that pink is going to carry them through and it`s why now Georgia is being talked about as one of the first five states potentially in a Democratic presidential primary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A new color in the political lexicon. OK. So, Ryan, you just heard what Symone had to say. And look, give me what you`re hearing on the Hill. Give me what you`re hearing from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  because what it gives them the ability to do and what they can realistically do may be two different things. How did they see this win?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, building on what Symone said, what it really does is give \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Senate Democrats some breathing room. You know, there were many times that Symone`s old boss the vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had to be rushed up here to break a tie with a 50-50 Senate. That`s no longer going to be necessary. They can push through a lot of these judicial nominees that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would bring to them at a rapid clip. It`s going to make it a lot easier to confirm Cabinet appointments, things along those lines.\par \par \uc2 But also, what could end up happening in this new 51-49 Senate is really empowering committees in a way that they are unable to be empowered right now because all the committees are 50-50. And that means giving Democrats subpoena power to look into investigations on a number of levels that they don`t currently have the ability to do. So, you know, there is a big difference between a 51-49 Senate and a 50-50 Senate. Yes, Democrats still control the way this legislation makes its way through the Senate, but it just gives them breathing room that they didn`t previously have, and it will make it a lot easier for them to tick off a lot of the things on the Biden agenda that are important.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But, of course, it doesn`t eliminate the problem of the Republicans being in control of the House, which is going to stymie a lot of things. But there`s no doubt that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is going to have a lot more flexibility now than he did the previous two years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Vaugh, to Symone`s point of this being a pink state, I want to read part of what the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  wrote this morning, although they didn`t use that exact word. "Georgia, with its storied civil rights history, booming Atlanta suburbs like Marietta, and exploding ethnic diversity, is now officially contested grounds, joining a narrow set of states that will select the next president." I mean, before yesterday, it did seem like an open question whether Georgia would be a true battleground going forward. How would you answer that question? And how do the folks you`re talking to in Georgia answer that question?\par \par \uc2 VAUGH HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. There`s a reality among Georgia Republicans. If you look at Cobb County, for instance, which is that the county around Marietta, just a decade ago, \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  handily won Cobb County. Now, just yesterday, Warnock won it by 19 percentage points. That was three percentage points more than he wanted two years ago. In the struggle, the issue for Republicans is the point in which voter trends become -- more than a trend but become consistent. And now for multiple election cycles in a row, Georgia Republicans and conservative independents have shown a willingness to switch vote -- switch and vote for a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 And in the case of \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , you know, he laughed every time he said it on the campaign trail. He goes, but Georgia, I`m asking you to vote for me one more time. Guess what, you know, a couple of years, \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2  is going to be asking those very Republican voters and conservative independents to vote one more time for him. Six years from now, Warnock just going to ask one more time again here. And yet, that is the question that Republicans have to wrestle with here because \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  won it by eight percentage points.\par \par \uc2 I think as Symone was just suggesting, most folks would still believe this to be a conservative state. But the Republican Party today isn`t unnecessarily conservative in the way that we have viewed it over the last 20 years. And that`s where the Republican Party has to reconcile how they want to proceed here because the Republican Party of \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is a very different Republican Party than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , which has really been predicated on faux outrage, election denialism, and issues like transgender individuals in women`s sports that are intended to rile up a Trump loyal far-right base of support here.\par \par \uc2 That is not what is going to win Georgia and -- or allowed Republicans to win Georgia back into its win column. And yet again, here last night, the greater metropolitan Atlanta area proved exactly what they proved two years ago. And it`s that that year -- that is that they are willing to jump party lines and vote for a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Geoff, I think we can look at this now as sort of the bigger picture for Republicans and maybe the bigger problem for Republicans. Because if we add Georgia to the other states that are widely considered to the battlegrounds, looking at Arizona, Michigan, Nevada, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin. Trump lost all of them two years ago, and Democrats have now won Senate races in four of the five that held them. So, what does this mean for Republicans going forward?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Look, as you will know, Chris, time, money, and energy are finite resources in politics. And now this means that Republicans have to spend more money in Georgia because it`s now competitive, and essentially siphon money away from places like Michigan and Pennsylvania and Nevada and Arizona. It may seem like ancient history now, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won Georgia decisively in 2016. But ever since then, Georgia has been losing a -- losing political playing ground for him.\par \par \uc2 You have Georgia voters rejecting his midterm candidates. They rejected his reelection bid. Beyond that you have his former friends and allies testifying before a special grand jury about Donald Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election. And so, in many ways, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needed \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to win last night and make -- to reset the narrative that he is a drag on the Republican Party. That didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And so, it`s still too early to write off \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the leader-- the de facto leader of the Republican Party. He has a magnetic grip on the grassroots. He has the support of the incoming House majority. But still, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is unable to pull himself out of this downward political spiral that he`s in, you can point to the results last night as being decisive.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And if I can go back to the results of that race because I think both candidates had something or somethings really fascinating to say, Symone, I want to play more from Warnock`s speech. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , (D-GA): I am Georgia. I am -- I am an example and an iteration of its history, of its pain, and its promise of the brutality and the possibility. But because this is America, because we always have a path to make our country greater against unspeakable odds, here we stand together. Thank you, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The whole speech, right? His speech, I think was both a reminder of the deep racism and legacy in the south, but also hopeful and forward- looking. And I wonder how you think, Symone, he navigates that belief, that hopefulness of a more perfect union toward reality, or is that unrealistic that he can?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I don`t think it`s unrealistic at all. The way that Reverend Warnock won reelection last night was that his campaign spoke to and targeted base Democratic voters but also expanded. He really ran up his number in rural counties. He campaigned in places like Forsyth and Cherokee. Those are not Democratic areas.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: You cannot get trounced in the rural parts of a -- of a state and win statewide. It does not work like that. There just are not enough voters, you have to build a coalition.\par \par \uc2 The thing that really struck me about what Reverend Warnock said last night, though, as he said, I am Georgia. And I thought that that was really important because, in the aftermath of the midterm elections, we`ve heard a lot of people saying about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He looked like people in Georgia. I knew people and -- people -- he looked like people in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: But I went to school with people that look like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Well, Malcolm Kenyatta looks like a lot of people in Pennsylvania too. You know, when we look at Wisconsin, Mandela Barnes looks like a lot of people in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And we have to be willing to talk about the fact that across this country, we do not have a lot of people of color who have been elected statewide in place -- into governor`s mansions to states -- to Senate races. And we have to unpack why, in some places, people do not feel like some of these candidates represent them. Well, Reverend Warnock did. I think he`s a model for folks across the country. He is a son of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: He is just as Georgia as \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Geoff, we are literally out of time but I really want to get to this because this is the latest loss for a Trump-back candidate. But even \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  himself seemed to take a shot at the former president last night. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I want to thank all of you as well because we`ve had a tough journey, have we not?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: But one of the things I said is they -- when they call the race, I said enormous, those little -- like they`re going to add up. But one of the things I want to tell all of you is you never stopped dreaming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I don`t want any of you to stop dreaming. I don`t want any of you to stop believing in America. I want you to believe in America and continue to believe in the Constitution and believe in our elected officials most of all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Whatever else you say about this whole process that he`s been through, he conceded last night.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: He did.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He actually conceded, right? Republicans stepped by Trump after he contributed to the loss in Georgia two years ago. Any reason to think they`re going to abandon him now, back to your point earlier? I mean, I`m not sure if any of this has an impact on Trump, or does it? Is there a cumulative effect?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be the de facto head of the Republican Party so long as there is not a potential successor waiting in the wings. There is, of course, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . There`s a \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . Neither of them have been tested at the national level. And it remains to be seen if either of them can connect to the Republican base in the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has. I thought it was particularly interesting in that clip you played from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , his support there, he`s expressed support for the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The core of conservatism, conservative Orthodoxy is that you never speak ill of the Constitution. And here you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just weeks after he hosted an antiSemite and a white supremacist, saying that the Constitution should be shredded in order so that he could seize power once again. So, we`ll have to see how long the leash the Republican Party will give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Geoff, Ryan Vaughn, thank you. Symone, you`re going to stay with me.\par \par \uc2 President Biden called \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  to congratulate him on his win last night and applauded Georgia voters for "standing up for democracy." I want to bring in \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , senior adviser to the President for Public Engagement. And, of course, the former mayor of Atlanta. She knows a little something about that state.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you think that we`re overriding this. Is this more than just a senator winning a race in a single state? Do you see broader implications here?\par \par \uc2 KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS, SENIOR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT FOR PUBLIC ENGAGEMENT: What I see is that elections actually matter. I saw that voters across the state of Georgia showed up because they know that what`s at stake. We understand in the state of Georgia that there are many important issues facing our country, and it matters who`s in the Senate to make decisions and to speak on behalf of the people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And I thought about LL Cool J who was at the White House a few weeks ago along with Shania Twain and Gloria Estefan representing the diversity of America. But LL Cool J has his line in one of his songs, and he says, don`t call it a comeback. I`ve been here for years. I thought about our president. So many people counted out our president. President Biden, the first president since FDR, not to lose a single Senate seat in the midterms. So, it all matter.\par \par \uc2 And I`m so proud of the state of Georgia because oftentimes, we can take elections for granted. But across the state, people showed up. And I was even proud to hear \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  last night. I grew up watching \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  playing at the University of Georgia. He was a hero to so many of us.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what we saw and what we heard from \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was really what we hope to hear from people, no matter the party affiliation. When they run for office and when the election is over, that we should all come together. And last night was a really proud night for all of us.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Gracious in victory and generous in defeat. Now, I want to switch gears just a little bit. We`ve spent a lot of time on this show talking about the rise in hate crimes, including antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 The Anti-Defamation League says antisemitic incidents hit an all-time high last year, the numbers this year are discouraging. So, today, the first gentleman, Doug Emhoff, hosted a roundtable on the issue. And I know you attended it. And it`s important that we talk about these issues. But what can we do beyond that? What can the administration do beyond that?\par \par \uc2 BOTTOMS: Well, it was such an important gathering today with leaders from across the country to hear directly from them on what they are experiencing in their communities, and to give the administration feedback on what we can do to assist in eliminating this hate. And it was a time of -- a very emotional discussion, just hearing some of the examples of what people have experienced and how they are having to mobilize in their respective communities in our respective communities. And so, this is one of many steps.\par \par \uc2 The second gentleman has been very clear that he will not be silent on this issue of hate. And the administration has been very clear that this is of utmost importance. That`s the reason we gathered today. And the conversation will continue. This is just the first of many steps that the administration is taking to make sure that we are hearing directly from those communities that are experiencing this hatred, and that we have the tools that we need to better respond and be better partners.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But what are those steps? We had on the head of the LA Holocaust Museum the other day and she said that they had a 90-year-old woman who was a Holocaust survivor speak to them and she says right now she sees the same signs that she saw leading up to the Holocaust. And I`m wondering what very specific steps the administration is looking at.\par \par \uc2 BOTTOMS: And unfortunately, we got that same feedback during the roundtable today, and it was very disheartening to hear. The administration has obviously put funding in place to help combat this. Also, the president has appointed someone at the ambassador level to be a direct lead on what our response is. And then again, we have the voice and the leadership of the second gentleman of the United States, the first Jewish person in American history to serve in that capacity.\par \par \uc2 And so, we are continuing to work with all of our agencies. We had our agencies represented there today, along with Ambassador Susan Rice, who, you know, heads our domestic policy council. And we listen. And the administration is going to continue to take steps to make sure that we`re not just responsive when there are instances of violence, but that we are really being thoughtful about what our policy initiatives should be and how they can be improved to make sure that we can stop violence before it begins.\par \par \uc2 We had a unity summit of a couple of months ago, where we have people of all nationalities, races, religious affiliations in the White House to talk about hatred in our communities and how we can combat hatred in our communities. And so, this was a very important gathering. And again, it will just be a part of what the administration is doing to make sure that we are bringing attention and we are being attentive to the challenges facing our communities.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , it`s good to have you on the program. Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BOTTOMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 The Trump Organization\uc2  found guilty in a 15-year-long tax fraud scheme. What it means for the future of the company and Trump`s chances in 2024? Plus, a massive counterterrorism operation underway in Germany, why it`s now being called one of the biggest in that nation`s history.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And the high-stakes case over a dispute in North Carolina now before the Supreme Court, why it could totally upend U.S. election laws. North Carolina Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  is here ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Who got the conviction in this case? He just brought in a veteran DOJ official with a history of taking on the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2 , MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: He`s coming in to help us on a broad range of matters.\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC HOST: So, it`s not a Trump thing?\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: Well, I would say without committing exactly to them, we can -- I can say any matter particularly is working on but a broad sweet. So, I think the focus in the last 24 hours has been almost exclusively on Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s bring in NBC`s investigations correspondent Tom Winter, Joyce Vance, a former U.S. attorney and MSNBC legal analyst, Symone Sanders-Townsend is back with us. So, Tom, again, Trump wasn`t on trial in this case, but the Manhattan DA is working on at least two other cases that do involve the former president. Whether he illegally inflated the value of his assets and whether he made a hush money payment to an adult film star who claimed she had an affair with him. Could this conviction impact in any way those ongoing cases?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER: Well, with respect to the hush money payment, I`m still waiting for somebody to be able to tell me what is the violation of New York State law, which are the only charges that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  can bring as District Attorney in regards to a federal election or a federal campaign finance donation.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So, I`m trying to figure out what that`s about because as we know, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  pleaded guilty to campaign finance-related charges and other charges tied to those payments. But that`s a federal election, that was the presidential election so outside of some sort of a charge, which is typically a misdemeanor charge about falsifying corporate records, I`m not quite sure what that could be about. I suppose there is a path to make that a felony, but that would require the rest of the show to potentially explain. I`m not quite sure I`m the person to do it.\par \par \uc2 As far as you know further investigative steps, the grand jury, to the best of our reporting, stopped reporting back in March of this year. So, there hasn`t been a grand jury listening to evidence, at least based on public reports, based on our own reporting. We haven`t heard about any new concrete steps in this investigation for at least eight or nine months now. So, there`s no indication here, that they have materially moved forward.\par \par \uc2 There could certainly be things happening behind the scenes. But there`s just not a sense to me that there`s been actual investigative steps there may have been -- there may be and I believe that there have been discussions of that office. I just don`t know what they`re actually doing to move forward on any potential avenues there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So much of, Joyce, Trump`s success has been around his brand, right? And the fact that he always said he was a very successful businessman and had the names on office buildings, and you know apartment buildings, and so on to prove it. But what could potentially be the impact as you see it on that actual business, and his frankly, longtime CFO, Allen Weisselberg?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, people often have questions about why it makes sense to prosecute a corporation. After all, no one goes to prison when a corporation is convicted and the fines are relatively modest. But I think you identify one of the reasons here. It calls out the fraud and in essence, it devalues the brand if the prosecution results in a conviction.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know exactly what that impact looks like here. It`s possible that there could be a default on some of the Trump organization`s indebtedness, whether entities that hold that debt might, for instance, call the loans. It`s a complicated question that we won`t know the answer to for some time. But certainly, there is reputational damage that is suffered. And it is one way of beginning to deal with someone who`s committed to fraud.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not this might loosen the tongue of former CFO Weisselberg and convince him to talk more about Trump, I think is also up in the air. Up until this point, he has been very steadfast about restricting his ability to talk about Trump personally, although he confessed to the crimes that led to the conviction of the corporation. I think we`ll have to watch Bragg carefully to see if he intends to move forward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Does anybody else look at this, anybody who`s investigating anything around Trump, and think it gives them any more power in terms of people wanting to make a deal, in terms of just the knowledge that complicated cases like this can be won?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think there is a lot of value in that sense. And when prosecutors take on people who were formidable opponents, people who have been seen as Teflon-coated in the way that the former president has been, sometimes it`s that first little hit that you get that convinces people to cooperate, convinces people that that person no longer has the power to damage them if they cooperate. So, yes, there is a little bit of a -- I`d say, a morale value once you see something like this happen. It`s certainly not the entire game, though.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, there`s a lot of possibilities and it`s all speculation, Symone, you know what other things might happen in terms of the other cases that are surrounding Trump, what the impact might be on his business. I`ve heard everything from not very much to it`s over, so something in where --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Somewhere in between.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Somewhere or somewhere between right. But I do wonder if there`s a certain level of chaos that gives Trump an out from 2024 or that Republicans look at and say that`s just too much. Because they really haven`t seemed to reach the level of it`s too much yet.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: My question continues to be what is the line? I do think, though, giving Trump out -- no one asked him to run for president, right? He himself decided to jump into this race and hasn`t held any actual campaign events since he has. And so, I think as long as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to be in the race, he will.\par \par \uc2 I find it very interesting. There`s a disconnect when we talk about Republicans, we are talking about either elected officials or the establishment Republicans, the apparatus of the Republican Party --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: And the grassroots and the people.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 I was in Richmond over the holiday on Thanksgiving and we went down to the Waffle House. You know, I love the Waffle House just like everybody else, Chris. And in the Waffle House, of course you know me, we ended up striking up some conversation and we ended up talking about Trump with all the folks in the section.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Shocking.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: OK. And I was outside --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That never happens.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Yes. And the amount of people who in that Waffle House that I was just at, that were still with the former president, that did not share the sentiment of maybe some of these Republicans who have said, oh, folks are willing to walk away from Trump or he doesn`t have as tough of a grip on the party I thought was quite striking. And to me, we have to make sure we`re looking at the grassroots and the people and the voters juxtaposed with what folks in Washington are doing. And that`s probably why most people. Republican leadership, have not called out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Symone Sanders, thank you. Joyce Vance, always great to have you on the program as well.\par \par \uc2 And there`s new information about a striking new lawsuit surrounding the death of Patrick Lyoya, a black man fatally shot by police in Grand Rapids. The family`s allegations against the city and the officers involved, that`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And just in the last hour, new information about the lawsuit tied to the death of Patrick Lyoya.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 It accuses the city of Grand Rapids and a former police officer of racial profiling and excessive force. Lyoya who was unarmed was fatally shot in the back of the head by police during a traffic stop back in April. Today his family alongside civil rights attorney \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  shared their pain and what they hope to accomplish with the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Perry Rossum.\par \par \uc2 Perry, the officer here has been charged with second-degree murder. He`ll face trial. He has pleaded not guilty. What more can you tell us about this lawsuit and what the family is saying about it?\par \par \uc2 PERRY RUSSOM, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, we have a news conference happening right now in Detroit. We have been monitoring it closely. That`s explaining this lawsuit. But we have a copy of it here that we can walk through right now for you. So this is a lawsuit that was filed today on behalf of Patrick Lyoya. His father was the man that was named on this. It is against Christopher Schurr, the now former police officer from Grand Rapids, and then the city of Grand Rapids as well.\par \par \uc2 There are three different counts that we can walk you through here. Violation one, count one is a violation of civil rights against Officer Schurr. Count two is a violation of civil rights against the city of Grand Rapids. And then count three here is a state law claim of gross negligence and that is against Officer Schurr.\par \par \uc2 In this lawsuit no dollar amount is given, Chris. They want the jury to decide that. But if we can walk you through here, the timeline, this all happened eight months ago in the city of Grand Rapids on April 4th. When Lyoya was pulled over for an issue with his license plate, the officer gets out of the car, walks up to Lyoya, they have a discussion for about a minute. Then Lyoya starts to run. The officer follows.\par \par \uc2 He gets tackled, goes down to the ground. They get back up. They`re scuffling, they put their hands on a taser. Both of their hands are on this taser. The taser is fired twice. They go back down to the ground where they are fighting. Then the officer fires that one shot into the back of Lyoya`s head. Then on June 9th just about two months later, the officer involved here was charged with second-degree murder. And then on June 15th just days later he was fired from the police department.\par \par \uc2 Again this news conference is happening right now. \uc2 Benjamin Crump\uc2  is talking but he spoke just moments ago about what he has to say about this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEN CRUMP, ATTORNEY FOR LYOYA`S FAMILY: Benjamin crump: So when you take driving while black plus excessive force, you end up with Patrick Lyoya being unjustly executed by this Grand Rapids police officer. And that is why we have to continue to speak the truth to power and file this wrongful death lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSSUM: And there`s a criminal side to all of this, Chris. Schurr`s lawyer has said that he was defending himself. Again, this news conference is just happening. This lawsuit was just filed today. So we are working to get comment from now former Officer Schurr`s attorney as well as the city of Grand Rapids, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Perry Rossum, thank you for that update. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Three thousand officers, 130 searches and 25 arrests. The chaotic scene unfolding in Germany that`s now being called one of the biggest counterterrorism operations in that country`s history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:09]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today breaking news out of Germany, where a plot to violently overthrow the government was foiled in an early morning raid. 25 people have now been arrested in the potential far-right coup that has similarities to January 6th insurrection and QAnon.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest counterterrorism operations in that country`s history led to those high-profile suspects, including -- get this -- a nobleman with historic royal titles and a veteran of the nation`s armed forces.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC News foreign correspondent Claudio Lavanga.\par \par \uc2 Claudio, the German prosecutor`s office said the suspects belong to a terrorist group, which aims to install its own leaders through, and I`m quoting here, "forcible elimination of the democratic constitutional state." What more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIO LAVANGA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Chris. Well, the federal prosecutor in Germany said that most of the people who were arrested today belong either they are members or are supporters of the Reichsburger movement in Germany, which essentially doesn`t believe in the legitimacy of the federal government in Germany because they say that there are foreign powers behind it and therefore they want to overthrow it.\par \par \uc2 How did they want to do that? Well, according to the prosecutor, they wanted to overthrow the government with a violent coup using a small armed group. Now among -- and they wanted to replace it with some sort of self- ruled government led by the ringleader who was also arrested.\par \par \uc2 Now a bit of an unlikely ringleader for a terrorist group, the 71-year-old aristocrat that goes by the name Heinrich the 13th. He`s the true descendant of the royal -- the German royal family, the House of Reuss, which used to rule in many parts of Germany up to the beginning of the 20th century. Now he was arrested as well. The prosecutor also said that among those people, there was also a small militia group made up mainly of former members of the armed forces in Germany and this probably why they moved things so quickly. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much, Claudio Lavanga. I know you`ll keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 And this just in, in the next two hours, high-level Cabinet secretaries including the director of National Intelligence and the secretary of Defense are expected to hold a classified briefing with all U.S. senators about what`s going on in Ukraine. That meeting comes hours after Russian president \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  told members of his government that the war in Ukraine could be, quote, "a long process."\par \par \uc2 Russia is dealing with the damage from the deadly drone strikes inside its borders, including one just 125 miles south of Moscow. And it`s escalating its attacks on Ukraine`s infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ellison Barber is live for us once again from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, what does that escalation from Russia look like from the ground where you are?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, as all of that is happening, we are learning more details about two attacks on villages in Donetsk that happened this evening today.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 According to the deputy head of the president`s office, Russian forces hit the central square of a city known as (INAUDIBLE) and the administration building there as well. He says that residents were actually in this square receiving humanitarian aid and claims Russian forces fired cluster munitions. Those are munitions that don`t just explode and hit one target or one area when they explode. They release a bunch of submunitions and can often cause a lot of additional civilian casualties, damage to civilian infrastructure, because they tend to be so indiscriminate.\par \par \uc2 On top of that strike, there was another one this a different village in the same region, Donetsk. This one in Kurakhove. Ukrainian officials say it was attacked with multiple rocket launchers, a bus station was hit, a market, gas stations as well as residential buildings. Right now Ukrainian officials in this region say at least eight civilians are dead, five injured.\par \par \uc2 These two attacks come on the heels of all of the other news you went through at the beginning. It also comes at a time when Ukrainian citizens are trying to have a moment of celebration for the fact that their president, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , and the spirit of Ukraine was named "TIME`s" person of the year.\par \par \uc2 We had a chance earlier today to talk to one of the people featured in that cover image. He is an esteemed chef here in Ukraine when the full-scale invasion started in February. He immediately opened the doors of his restaurant giving thousands of tons of food out to members of the territorial defense. Then he moved to west to do the same for people fleeing Russian attacks. He gave borscht to thousands of people who were internally displaced.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what he told us about receiving that "TIME" award.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IEVGEN KLOPOTENKO, CHEF, 100 YEARS BACK TO THE FUTURE RESTAURANT: The Ukrainian spirit is fighting spirit because all the time we have to fight. It means recognition of what I`m doing. And that gives me a power to go next. To go more. Not to stop. Because sometimes someone has to tell you, you`re doing good. Only sometimes. You haven`t to tell me every day. But once you say, I`m on the right way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Life in Ukraine is often full of contrast and we`re seeing that right now at the same time Ukrainians are trying to celebrate what they consider to be a great honor getting this recognition from "TIME" magazine. They`re also seeing more villages come under attack. More Ukrainian civilians dying. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ellison Barber, thank you so much for that report.\par \par \uc2 A high stakes case now before the Supreme Court with massive implications for U.S. democracy. And new information on those power grid attacks that left tens of thousands without power in North Carolina. The governor there, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , is here to talk both, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:57]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In the last couple of hours, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  heard arguments in a case that could completely upend election law, and have a huge impact on our next presidential race. At the center of the case, Moore v. Harper, is whether the justices will embrace a legal argument called the independent state legislature theory and what it does is it takes power away from state courts to strike down certain election laws passed by state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina Republicans brought this case after a court tossed out their congressional maps calling them unlawful partisan gerrymanders. The governor of North Carolina \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  says this, quote, "fringe claim" before the court would upend democracy. The governor joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for being here. And I should also tell folks, you also served as attorney general, so I wonder from both perspectives, as a governor and a former attorney general, your insights into how you think this would impact not just North Carolina but democracy as a whole.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ROY COOPER\uc2  (D), NORTH CAROLINA: Well, when we think about the foundation of our democracy, it depends on checks and balances. Our state Supreme Court, our governor, who has a veto, and of course, the state legislature. This Republican state legislature in North Carolina is arguing that the legislature and the legislature alone has the authority to set the time, place, and manner of federal elections.\par \par \uc2 When you take that to its extreme, which they are arguing, the state court has nothing to say about it, the governor has nothing to say about it, and you see the potential problems from the actions of this very Republican legislature in the past. Passing voter I.D. laws that even an I.D. at the University of North Carolina doesn`t work. Taking away provisional ballots at polling places. Shortening voting time.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, a lot of their schemes have been overruled by the federal courts. I`ve been able to stop a number of them with gubernatorial veto. But this would allow state legislatures alone, across the country, to set the rules for our federal elections and that`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So do you think, Governor, I just want to -- could this change the landscape of 2024?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Absolutely. You know, taking it to the place where I think many of them would want to go, and if you look at Donald Trump`s plan, being able to ignore the popular vote in a state, and change presidential electors. Depending on what the Supreme Court does here, it could give state legislators unfettered authority to set the rules for federal elections and it could affect the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 And not just with electors, but with the rules on how you operate the voting, restricting the vote for certain populations.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 This Republican legislature has a history of going in and targeting certain populations and making it harder for them to vote. We also see it in diabolical gerrymandering. They put forth a plan back when we had 13 members of Congress, that was 10-3, Republican and Democratic, when asked how could a state that`s pretty even in votes end up that way, they said they didn`t think it was possible to make a plan that was 11-2 Republican.\par \par \uc2 This is their mindset. And this is what we`re trying to stop by fighting this case in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  today. The arguments just ended a couple of hours ago. It was I think three hours long. Tough questions being asked. I hope the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  does the right thing in this case.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I also want to ask you about the latest on what`s happening in your state with the power grid. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  now asking for information tied to the suspects in those targeted attacks on those two sub-stations that knocked out power for thousands. We know it`s going to be restored tonight, sooner than they thought but people are still scared, and I want to play for you very quickly what a mom told us about what this has meant for her and her kids.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HELEN PROBST MILLS, MOORE COUNTY RESIDENT: But there is still is still this sense of not knowing what it was, because it wasn`t a natural disaster, and I think that hanging in the back of your mind is really off footing, very disconcerting to people. It`s been, you know, hard to sleep. Many people have left town because it is really cold.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What`s it been like for your kids?\par \par \uc2 PROBST MILLS: Very deeply unsettling. The idea that somebody would intentionally do this in that way, very frightening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Governor, we only have a minute left but how close are police and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to getting folks like her some answers to exactly what happened here?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, it`s been four rough nights for the people in Moore County. And we know that we have to find who did this. Federal, state, local, are working together, leaving no stone unturned, making sure that we find who did this and why. We also need to look to the future at hardening our electric grid, making sure that it`s more secure.\par \par \uc2 This is unacceptable for someone to be able to intentionally come in and disable a substation, and cut off power for tens of thousands of people for four days. We`ve got to find ways to protect our grid better. We`ve got to find who did this and bring them to justice.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , thank you. I know you have a lot on your plate right now. We appreciate you taking the time, sir.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. "KATY TUR REPORTS" up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221208eic700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for November 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Dan De Luce, Ivo Daalder, Irwin Redlener, Garrett  Haake, Doug Heye, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 9,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIR, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: And all of my counterparts, all the jobs that participated now the senior representatives, for all 50 countries that showed up at this meeting this morning and continue to take part in these discussions which are very, very productive. The mission of the group remains clear, to support Ukraine as they encounter the illegal and unprovoked Russian aggression, and to continue to supply Ukraine with the capabilities necessary to defend their sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 Through these contact group sessions and other close coordinations that I have, and the Secretary has with our counterparts, that I talked to General Zaluzhnyy weekly, and my staff continually talks to his staff. We continue to respond to Ukraine`s battlefield requirements and their need for means of fighting for their freedom.\par \par \uc2 This is a war of choice. It`s a war of choice for Russia. They embarked on a tremendous strategic mistake. They made a choice in February of this year to illegally invade a country that posed no threat to Russia. And making that choice Russia established several objectives. They wanted to overthrow President Zelenskyy and his government. They wanted to secure access to the Black Sea. They wanted to capture Odesa. They wanted to seize all the way to the Dnipro River, pause, and then continue to attack all the way to the Carpathian Mountains.\par \par \uc2 In short, they wanted to overrun all of Ukraine and they lost. They didn`t achieve those objectives. They failed to achieve their strategic objectives and they are now failing to achieve their operational and tactical objectives. Russia changed their war aims in March and beginning of April, there were of choice, then focus on the seizure of the Donbas, the Donetsk, and Luhansk Oblast. That was their operational objectives, and they failed there. Then they changed again and expanded to seize Zaporizhzhia and Kherson.\par \par \uc2 The strategic reframing of their objectives of the illegal invasion have all failed, every single one of them. And we`ve just witnessed last week, Russia`s retreat from Kherson. They retreated across the Dnipro River. They moved to more defensible positions south of the river. Their losses due to Ukrainian success and skill and bravery on the battlefield have been very, very significant. And it`s clear that the Russian will to fight does not match the Ukrainian will to fight.\par \par \uc2 On the battlefield, the Ukrainians` offensive up in Kharkiv has been very successful where they crossed the Oskil River and they have moved the east and are near the town of Svatovi. There is a significant ongoing fight down in Bakhmut right now and in the vicinity of Siversk and Soledar where the Ukrainians are fighting a very, very successful mobile defense. There is limited contact right now in Zaporizhzhia, unlimited contact in and around Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant. And as we already discussed, Kherson`s offensive has been successful.\par \par \uc2 So, across the entire frontline trace of some 900 or so kilometers, the Ukrainians have achieved success after success after success. And the Russians have failed every single time. They`ve lost strategically, they`ve lost operationally, and I repeat they lost tactically. What they`ve tried to do, they failed. They started this war and Russia can end this war. Russia can make another choice. And they can make a choice today to end this war.\par \par \uc2 However, Russia is choosing to use their time to attempt to regroup their forces and they are imposing a campaign of terror, a campaign of maximum suffering on the Ukrainian civilian population in order to defeat Ukrainian morale. The Russians are striking throughout the depth and breadth of all of Ukraine with air launch cruise missiles, with caliber, sea launch cruise missiles, and with other types of munitions. They are striking the Ukrainian civilian infrastructure and has little or no military purpose.\par \par \uc2 While assessments are ongoing, yesterday, strikes looked like they launched at least 60 missiles, and they may have launched upwards of 90 or even perhaps 100. And we`ll have better assessments in the days ahead. But it was likely the largest wave of missiles that we`ve seen since the beginning of the war.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 These missiles -- again, they targeted intentionally and damage civilian power generation facilities to cause unnecessary suffering with the civilian population. We assess now that over a quarter of Ukrainian civilians are without power. The deliberate targeting of the civilian power grid causing excessive collateral damage and unnecessary suffering on the civilian population is a war crime. With the onset of winter, families will be without power, and more importantly, without heat, basic human survival and subsistence are going to be severely impacted and human suffering for the Ukrainian population is going to increase.\par \par \uc2 These strikes will undoubtedly hinder Ukraine`s ability to care for the sick and the elderly. Their hospitals will be partially operational. The elderly are going to be exposed to the elements. In the wake of unrelenting Russian aggression, an incalculable human suffering Ukraine will continue to endure. Ukraine is not going to back down.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian people are hard. They`re tough. And most of all, they`re free and they want to remain free. Ukraine is going to continue to take the fight to the Russians. And I just had a significant conversation with my Ukrainian counterpart and he assures me that that is the future for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 As Ukraine continues to fight, air defense capabilities are becoming critical for their future success. An integrated system, an integrated air defense system -- an integrated air and missile defense system is what is necessary as Ukraine repels Russian aerial attacks. And a significant portion of today`s conversations in today`s meeting with almost 50 countries focused on how we, as a global coalition, can provide the right mix of air defense systems and ammunition for Ukraine to continue its control of the skies and prevent the Russians from achieving air superiority.\par \par \uc2 To combat continued Russian strikes, last Thursday, the United States announced a $400 million in additional commitment to support Ukraine and those capabilities included missiles for the Hawk air defense systems, which is a complement with Spain`s recently committed. There`s other air defense systems included in that 400 million packages along with ground systems such as armored Humvees, grenade launchers, and additional HIMARS ammunition, and lots of other pieces of equipment.\par \par \uc2 Wars are not fought by armies. They`re fought by nations. This war is fought by the Ukrainian people and it`s fought by the Russian people. And this is a war that Russia`s leadership has chosen to put Russia into. They didn`t have to do this but they did and they have violated Ukrainian sovereignty. They have violated the territorial integrity of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It is in complete contradiction to the basic rules that underlined the United Nations Charter established at the end of World War Two. This is one of the most significant attempts to destroy the rules-based order that World War Two was fought all about. And we, the United States, are determined to continue to support Ukraine with the means to defend themselves for as long as it takes. But at the end of the day, Ukraine will retain -- will remain a free and independent country with its territory intact.\par \par \uc2 Russia could end this war today. Russia could put it into it right now. But they won`t. They`re going to continue that fight. They`re going to continue that fight into the winter, as best we can tell. And we, the United States under the direction of the President and the Secretary of Defense, we will continue to support Ukraine for as long as it takes to keep them free, sovereign, and independent with their territory intact.\par \par \uc2 The President of the United States has been very, very clear to us that it`s up to Ukraine to decide how and when or if they negotiate with the Russians. And we will continue to support them as long as it takes. The United States will continue to support Ukraine with the best possible equipment to position them on the battlefield, to give them positions of strength against the Russians, and that is also true of all the other nations that attended the meeting today.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 There is an absolute sense of urgency, an absolute sense of determination on the part of all the member states that attended our meeting today. And I can tell you, the cohesion and coherence of the organization is complete and the resolve is high. Ukrainians are not asking for anyone to fight for them. They don`t want American soldiers or British or German or French or anybody else to fight for them. They will fight for themselves. All Ukraine is asking for is the means to fight. And we are determined to provide that means.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians will do this on their timeline. And until then, we will continue to support all the way for as long as it takes. It is evident to me in the Contact Group today that that is not only a U.S. position, but it is a position of all the nations that were there today. We will be there for as long as it takes to keep Ukraine free. Thank you. And I welcome your questions.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Mr. Secretary, Chairman, thank you very much, gentlemen. The first question will go to the Associated Press, Tara.\par \par \uc2 TARA COPP, ASSOCIATED PRESS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Thank you. Mr. Secretary, President Zelenskyy just denied that it was a Ukrainian air defense missile that landed in Poland. How are you certain that this was possibly a Ukrainian air defense missile and was not a Russian missile?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Thanks, Tara. First of all, the investigation is still ongoing. And Poland is conducting the best investigation. We are assisting in any way we can. We do have some experts on the ground there helping Polish leadership. We have full confidence in Poland`s ability to conduct this investigation in a proper way. And until that`s complete, again, I think it`s -- it`d be premature for anybody to jump to conclusions.\par \par \uc2 And I know that Ukraine has offered to participate and help in any way they can as well. So, we won`t get ahead of you know, what the investigation but you know, our information supports what President Duda said earlier. His preliminary assessment was that this was most likely -- most likely a result of a Ukrainian air defense missile. But we`ll let the investigation play out here.\par \par \uc2 COPP: So, at this point, are you confident in saying that this was not a Russian missile?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: We`re going to let the investigation play out. And then once the results are released, we`ll be confident in everything. But again, we -- our information supports what President Duda has said earlier, Tara.\par \par \uc2 COPP: And then, Chairman Milley, after this strike occurred, did you reach out to your Russian counterparts, or did any other military officer reach out to their Russian counterparts to protect against escalation, and if not, why not?\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: There were -- all right, I do that through my staff to set the calls up. The short answer is yes, some attempts were made, no success. But the Russian counterpart did have. I did talk to my Ukrainian counterpart immediately, General Zaluzhnyy. I talk to him several times, in fact, also a Polish counterpart and several others in charge in Europe. And exactly what the Secretary said, an investigation is ongoing, there`s professionals there to do the forensics, you know, all the debris that`s in and around the impact site and so on, so forth. And very shortly, we`ll know all the facts and we just don`t know what might this second.\par \par \uc2 COPP: So, Russia did not take the call?\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: Right. My staff was unsuccessful in getting me linked up with General Gerasimov. That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: It`s good. Next question, ABC Luis Martinez.\par \par \uc2 LUIS MARTINEZ, ABC NEWS SENIOR PENTAGON REPORTER: For Mr. Secretary -- for Mr. Chairman. Actually, I just like to follow up on Tara`s question initially because, in his remarks, President Zelenskyy cited a conversation with your counterpart, General Zaluzhnyy, saying that he had confirmed to him that it was not a Ukrainian missile. Based on your conversations with him today, was there a disconnect there? And then I`ll follow up on that question.\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: Yes, I`m not going to talk about it. He and I -- our agreement between each other is not talked about the substance of the conversations that we have. We have conversations several times a week. And we acknowledge that we have the conversations but we don`t discuss the substance of the conversation. So, I have to honor that and I`ll continue to honor that.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you that right now, the investigation is ongoing. He`s a professional investigator. There is a debris field there. There`s other forms of data that are going to be available that come from various technical means. And I suspect very shortly, we will have very confirmed data as to what the point of origin is, point of impact, what the -- what the angle of the weapon system was, the flight trajectory, all the details are going to be known in due time but it`s pretty early actually in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 So, we`ll know that and Secretary will know that. President Biden will know that. More get formed here shortly by the investigators. And Poland has put together a team they have led and they put together a team of professional investigators to do that.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Thank you, Sir. Mr. Secretary, yesterday, was kind of the reality of the speculation that has been going on for months about how the \uc2 NATO\uc2  might respond if a Russian missile went into \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory. On the opposite side, the United States has been very careful not to provide weapons systems that might reach into Russia. What about Crimea? If the United States HIMARS is supplied, HIMARS Systems are able to reach inside Crimea regularly, is that a concern given what we saw yesterday?\par \par \uc2 And the follow-up, sir, to your comments about -- further from last week about the possibility of discussions put on by a slowdown in the fighting, let`s say during the winter. It sounds like the comments that you`re making today about the winter are that the Ukrainians are going to continue very strongly. Is -- are you pulling back from your comments from last week that you see an opportunity for negotiations with the Russians?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: No, I think -- I think Ukrainians should keep the pressure on the Russians, you know, to the extent that they militarily can. But winter gets very, very cold. And the natural tendency is for tactical operations are going to naturally probably slow down. And right now, what we`re seeing is the lines from Kharkiv, all the way down to Kherson, for the most part, are beginning to stabilize.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether that means they will be stable throughout the winter or not, nobody knows -- nobody knows for certain. Come January and February, that ground probably will freeze, which could lend itself to offensive operations. So, there could be a lot of activity in the winter. But typically speaking, because of the weather, the tactical operations will slow down a bit. And I think that you know, President Biden and President Zelenskyy himself have said that there`ll be a -- at the end of the day, there`ll be a political solution. So, if there`s a slowdown in the actual tactical fighting, if that happens, then that may become a window, possibly it may not for a political solution, or at least the beginnings of talks to initiate a political solution. So, that`s all I was saying.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: And from you, sir.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Yes. First, let me just agree with what the chairman just said in terms of -- there is a -- there probably will be a slowdown in the fall and going into winter. The fall`s a muddy season, as you know, and so is a spring. So, when the ground hardens, traffic ability will probably improve and then we`ll be -- we may see more activity. But I would remind everyone that this war started in February. So, it -- you know winter does not mean that we`re going to stop fighting or that Ukrainians are going to stop fighting, I certainly like to share my belief that they won`t.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re going to do everything within our power to make sure that they have the means to accomplish their goals and objectives. And along that line, the goals and objectives of this fight are the Ukrainians. They`re not -- they`re not ours and so we won`t be having to prescribe to the Ukrainians what they can and cannot do. And so our focus is to continue to provide them the means to be successful in their endeavors. And so that`s my response to the question on Crimea. Again, Crimea is an issue to be thought through and sorted out by the Ukrainian leadership.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: Let`s go ahead to Helene from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: First for General Austin. So, with winter coming --\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: It`s a bad habit. You keep calling me general, but --\par \par \uc2 COOPER: It`s like ingrained in my head.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Secretary Austin, do you agree then with General Milley`s comments in New York last week that Ukraine cannot achieve a military victory as defined by driving Russia out of all of its territory, including Crimea, and therefore should use winter as an opportunity to negotiate?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: I -- again, having the chairman here, I think it`s fair to allow him to really provide context for his comments. I think -- and you`ve heard me say this before, there are countless numbers of people that have been amazed and astonished by what the Ukrainians have accomplished.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And so I won`t presuppose what`s possible or impossible for them. What I am focused on is just making sure that they have the means to do two things. First is to protect themselves and their civilian population from some of the things that we`ve seen here recently, with the aerial bombardments. The second thing is to enable them to achieve their goals and objectives on the ground as they continue to try to take back their sovereign territory. So, we`re going to continue to support them.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think to this point, we`ve seen them come up with very achievable goals and objectives. We`ve seen a very successful counter- offensive, both in Kharkiv and also in Kherson. And I think they will continue to keep the pressure on the Russians going forward. And in terms of what`s a good time to negotiate. We`ve said repeatedly that the Ukrainians are going to decide that and not us. And we will support them for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 Now, we just spent almost four hours with our colleagues here in Ukraine defense contact group meeting. It was amazing to me how many ministers of defense on their own said we`re going to do this for as long as it takes. And so I continue to see unity, I continue to see resolve, and that`s very, very encouraging. And I think it`s encouraging for Alexei Reznikoff and his team to hear that as well because as you know, they`re in the meeting as well so you know.\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: So, Helene, I`ll make a couple of comments, first on the Russians.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I still have a question for you.\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: OK, but let me -- let me --\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: That`ll be like four questions, I think, Helene.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I`m not asking General Milley --\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: But let me help you out this one first. So, I start with the Russians. Ukraine is a pretty big country. This was not a small piece of turf. And the probability of Russia achieving its strategic objectives of conquering Ukraine, of overrunning Ukraine, the probability of that happening is close to zero. I suppose theoretically, it`s possible, maybe, I guess, but I don`t see it happening militarily. So, I just don`t see that happening.\par \par \uc2 But they do currently occupy about 20 percent of that -- of Ukraine. So, they occupy a piece of ground that`s about 900 kilometers long and I don`t know, probably about 75 or 80 kilometers deep. So it`s not a small piece of ground. And they invaded this country with upwards of 170,000 or 180,000 troops in multiple field armies, combined arms armies. And they have suffered a tremendous amount of casualties. But he`s also done this mobilization, he called up additional people. So, the Russians have reinforced, they have some -- they still have significant Russian combat power inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ukraine`s had great success in the defense. They did a tremendous job in defeating the Russian offensive. It`s incredible what they were able to do. And then they went on the offensive at the beginning of September, and they had great success up in Kharkiv and they`ve had better success even down in Kherson as you just witnessed.\par \par \uc2 But Kherson and Kharkiv, physically and geographically, are relatively small compared to the whole. So, that -- the military task of militarily kicking the Russians physically out of Ukraine is a very difficult task. And it`s not going to happen in the next couple of weeks unless the Russian army completely collapses, which is unlikely. So, in terms of probability, the probability of a Ukrainian military victory defined as kicking the Russians out of all of Ukraine to include what they define, or what they claim is Crimea, to -- the probability of that happening anytime soon, is not high, militarily.\par \par \uc2 Politically, there may be a political solution where politically the Russians withdraw, that`s possible. You want to negotiate from a position of strength. Russia right now is on its back. The Russian military is suffering tremendously. Leaders have been -- you know, the leadership is really hurting bad. They`ve lost a lot of casualties killed and wounded. They`ve lost -- I won`t go over exact numbers because they`re classified, but they`ve lost a tremendous amount of their tanks and their infantry fighting vehicles. They`ve lost a lot of their fourth and fifth-generation fighters, and helicopters, and so on and so forth.\par \par \uc2 The Russian military is really hurting bad. So, you want to negotiate at a time when you`re at your strength and your opponent is at weakness. And it`s possible, maybe, that there`ll be a political solution. All I`m -- all I`m saying is there`s a possibility for it. That`s all I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 RYDER: OK, a good time for just a couple more. I go to \uc2 VOA\uc2 , Carla.\par \par \uc2 CARLA BABB, VOA PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, gentlemen, both for doing this. Mr. Secretary, you stressed that the United States and their allies are committed to Ukraine for as long as it takes. How long do you think Russia can continue this war with its current arsenal and its current personnel? And how much has Iran and North Korea`s weapons extended their ability to wage this war?\par \par \uc2 And, Mr. Chairman, thank you. You`ve thoroughly answered my question that with Luis and Helene, so I`m going to ask you a question on China, if I may. After the meeting with President Biden and President Xi, have you seen any indications that China has changed its ambition to control Taiwan? And, you know, the last time the national defense strategy was rolled out, the Pentagon said America`s military edge was eroding. Now, that this new one has rolled out, is America`s military edge still eroding to China?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: So, thanks, Carla. In terms of how long Russia can sustain their efforts, that`s left to be seen. I think the chairman just gave a very accurate and compelling description of kind of where the Russians are right now. They`re -- they have some problems. They`ve had problems since the very beginning of this trying to sustain their efforts.\par \par \uc2 Those problems have only become more acute. They`ve lost a lot of people. And as important, they`ve lost a lot of important military gear. So, the numbers of tanks that they`ve lost and the numbers of armored personnel carriers, pretty staggering numbers.\par \par \uc2 As important, the numbers of precision-guided munitions that they`ve rifled through in this endeavor is striking. But they won`t be able to reproduce those munitions very quickly because there are trade restrictions on their -- that prevent them from rapidly gaining microchips and other things that are required to produce these kinds of munitions. And so it may take years for them to restock that inventory up to the point that they were before they started this conflict. We`ve seen them struggle with having enough munitions to fight the way that they want to fight, SO they`re reaching out to Iran, they`re reaching out to North Korea. I do think that those countries will probably provide them with some capability. And so for that reason, I don`t think this will be over anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Our -- you know, our goal -- our requirement is to make sure that we continue to provide Ukraine with the means to do what`s necessary to prosecute their campaign. And so they have to continue to keep the pressure on the Russians going forward. And I think, you know, when our fight favors the Ukrainians, we`ve pushed, you know, enormous amounts of winter gear into Ukraine, thanks to countries like Canada, and others who have -- who have really been very, very generous.\par \par \uc2 Russia, on the other hand -- I mean, they`re fighting in a foreign country, the Ukrainians have challenged their supply lines, it will be difficult for them to get the kinds of gear into their troops that they need to be able to fight effectively. And so I think you know the Ukrainians will have the upper hand in this fight as they have right now, but that they`ll continue to maintain that upper hand going into the winter.\par \par \uc2 Just like we saw them operate in February of last year. They know the -- they know the land, they can -- they can pull things from the -- from the local communities, and they`ll be prepared for this -- for this winter weather. And I don`t think the Russians will be as prepared and they will continue to struggle to get -- to get things into their troops using the supply lines that they currently have. And the Russia -- the Ukrainians will continue to pressure those supply lines there.\par \par \uc2 BABB: Thank you. Do you think the Russians can hold out all it takes them the years that you say it would take for them to fully resupply?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: I don`t think the Ukrainians are going to allow them to hold out. I think the Ukrainians are going to continue to pressure them. And so this battlefield dynamic will change -- you know, continue to change. And Ukrainians know that you know, allowing them to rest and refit and rearm is a mistake. That`s a -- that`s a -- an operational mistake. I don`t believe they`re going to make that mistake. And I -- you know, my goal is to make sure that they have the means to do what`s necessary to ensure that they don`t hold up.\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: And you had two questions for the secretary so I hit by on mine. So, I`m looking at on China as quickly as I can say it. China is the pacing threat as we describe it in DOD, is part of the National Defense strategy is defined -- and the previous ones, defined in the current one as the pacing threat. And what do we mean by that? We mean that China is the one country out there that geopolitically has the power potential to be a significant challenge to the United States.\par \par \uc2 And they are. Based on their population, their technology, their economy, and a bunch of other things. China is the greatest geopolitical challenge to the United States. And China is not shy about their goal. They want to be the number one power in the globe by mid-century, by 2049. And they want to do that militarily, diplomatically, informationally, economically, and so on and so forth. So they want to be number one by mid-century.\par \par \uc2 By the 2030s, mid 2030s, they had previously said they want to be number one regionally. So, they want to have a military that outdoes the United States Military regionally by the mid-30s. They had previously said that. And then they advanced that goal to 2027. So they advanced that goal, I think it was two party Congresses ago, one party Congress ago. And what they have said is that they want to be the equal to or superior militarily to the United States. That`s only five years away. So they`re working on that, and they`re working on that very, very hard.\par \par \uc2 But we are not static. And we are working on it. Right now, the United States Military is, without question, despite whatever criticisms people have, the United States Military is the most lethal war-fighting machine on earth, bar none. The United States Military is number one, and we intend to stay number one. And our task -- militaries only have two tasks, single purpose really, which is either to prepare for war or to fight a war.\par \par \uc2 And we are laser focused on that. And we intend to stay number one. China is not going to be a better military than the United States Military is, but they`re going to try. But they`re not going to get there. We will be number one five years from now, 10 years from now, and 50 years from now. We are not going to let China take number one. They have made gains in a wide variety of areas, in cyber, in space, in land, sea, and air.\par \par \uc2 So a peasant army of largely infantry based, you know, when I was commissioned in 1980, that`s kind of what Deng Xiaoping had when he made his reforms. So, he had a very large dismounted infantry, peasant based army, more or less. They had some tanks but not much. And then they got rich. They made a massive amount of money with 10 percent rise over run, dropped down to 7 percent, maybe it`s going to come down to 3 percent or 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 But their GDP allowed them to buy a military. And they believe that it`s their day in the sun. They believe it`s once again time for the middle kingdom to be number one. So that`s what they are shooting for, and we are not going to allow that happen. The United States Military is number one now, and we`re going to be number one five years from now. 2027 is not going to be the date that China becomes number one.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to stay number one the entire time. And as long as we remain number one, then we will deter the war that people worry about, a great power war, between China and the United States. As long as we have the military capability, we have the will to use it, your adversary knows it, then you`ll deter that war. But the key is to have the military capability. And we intend to stay number one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Time for one final question. We`ll go to (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you very much for taking my question. To the secretary first, President Biden and President Xi essentially agreed to maintain the open lines of communications. Do you expect China will resume some of the military channels, military-to-military channels they suspended in August after Speaker Pelosi`s visit to Taiwan? And are you planning to meet with your Chinese counterpart during your visit to Cambodia next week?\par \par \uc2 And to the chairman, also on China, the President Xi consolidated his power in the Chinese Communist Party and he`s now surrounded by his loyal advisers. How much are you concerned that --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The secretary of Defense and the chairman of the Joint Chiefs with their first major remarks about exactly what happened yesterday when there was an explosion in a rural area of Poland not far from the Ukrainian border. The initial concerns were that it may have been a Russian missile. Now we`re hearing from the two leaders there that they believe, with the preliminary assessment from Poland, that it indeed was most likely the result of a Ukrainian air defense missile that unfortunately, as they put it, landed in Poland.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:09]\par \par \uc2 When pressed with the fact that Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy disputed that account, they said, let`s wait for the final assessment. The U.S. is working closely with investigators from Poland. The U.S. is working closely with investigators from Poland. They are on the scene. And they expected that to come shortly.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, it has escalated tensions in terms of, can there be a negotiated settlement? What can happen now at a time when there is concern that there could be an errant missile, that something could happen to actually escalate the situation there?\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Dan De Luce at the Pentagon, former U.S. ambassador to NATO Ivo Daalder, and the founding director of the National Center for Preparedness at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  who has made multiple trips to Ukraine, Dr. Irwin Redlener.\par \par \uc2 Dan, let me start with you. So you have what seems to be a dispute between Zelenskyy and Poland and the United States who believe they have at least a preliminary assessment. What do you make of that and what might it mean when we`re told that shortly we may have some conclusive decision here about what actually happened?\par \par \uc2 DAN DE LUCE, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY AND GLOBAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: That`s right. I think we`ve gone from a very anxious moment yesterday when there seemed to be the validity (PH) that Russia had launched a missile or a missile attack on Poland. And now we`re in a more awkward moment where among allies, among friends, there`s a disagreement here.\par \par \uc2 But from the rest of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , from the \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary general, from -- you just heard the Defense secretary, the Polish president, they all are saying it looks like at the moment that it was this errant, mistaken Ukrainian air defense projectile. And so they`re going to wait for the investigation to look at all that. But it was also -- they were very careful in their language. They weren`t criticizing Ukraine, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And Defense Secretary Austin was making a point saying, you know, really ultimately, Russia does bear responsibility for launching this invasion in the first place and for firing, you know, waves of missiles at Ukraine and then Ukraine has to defend itself. And I think there are some military analysts who will say it`s sort of -- it`s really a surprise in a way that this hasn`t happened before, given the amount of weapons flying through the air.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, an awkward moment right now with Ukraine still maintaining that they don`t think it`s their missile. But there was still a kind of ringing endorsement of continued support for Ukraine. Austin and General Milley saying that there would be no let-up. The U.S. and all of its allies had resolve and determination to provide aid to Ukraine as long as it takes. And he had some really colorful remarks by General Milley where he was saying, Russia`s military is hurting badly and they`ve failed at every moment, at every objective. They failed and they`re now really on the back foot.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There were, Ambassador, many very intense and tense moments yesterday. Many of the world leaders were at the G20 in Bali. They immediately were in conversation. We know \uc2 NATO\uc2  immediately was activated. They have been in conversation. But a lot of the talk today at this press conference was about whether or not this could lead to some sort of political solution, whether or not there was essentially an off-ramp.\par \par \uc2 And when you have these concerns that, OK, this time, this time it was potentially a missile defense system, but there could be another missile that goes awry, there could be more destruction, at the beginning of this war, there were concerns, as you well know, that perhaps could an errant missile hit a nuclear power plant. Does this move that pressure forward? Does it move the ball forward, the possibility of some sort of negotiated settlement or at least sitting down to talks?\par \par \uc2 IVO DAALDER, CHICAGO COUNCIL ON GLOBAL AFFAIRS PRESIDENT: Yes, I really don`t think so. I think -- you think, I think the reaction yesterday was a little over the top everywhere. I think we needed to establish the facts and know the facts. Things like this happen in war, and we don`t want to start a major escalation without knowing exactly what has happened and without making a very deliberate decision that that is the best course of action. So, I think we`re now in the stage of finalizing the fact gathering.\par \par \uc2 And I see \uc2 NATO\uc2 , I see the system as a whole, including the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , trying to go through the motions that are necessary to establish the facts and then move on while perhaps outside there was a lot of churn. At the same time, this does remind everyone that accidents do happen in war, that things go wrong, that nothing goes according to plan, that there is this fog of war.\par \par \uc2 And I hope that President Biden and officials in Bali use the opportunity to remind some of those who have been either on the sidelines or, like China, supportive of President Putin, to remind them that their continued support and their unwillingness to stand up against Russia for its illegal aggression against Ukraine is part of what causes this problem.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:26]\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to resolve this issue through negotiations by having the Ukrainians throw up their hands and say, good, you can take 20 percent of our territory and reward a war of aggression. The only way you`re going to resolve this confrontation is for the Russians to reverse what they did not only just since February but frankly since 2014, and take their forces, and move them back to Russia, and outside of Russia`s territory.\par \par \uc2 It`s that that ultimately caused this conflict, and it is that that ultimately caused the missile, whether Ukrainian or, as it looks like, or even Russian to fall on Polish territory.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But knowing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , do you think that`s a realistic expectation, Ambassador?\par \par \uc2 DAALDER: No, I don`t. And that`s why I think the military effort needs to continue. And I listened very carefully to what General Milley had to say, and I listened very carefully to what Secretary Austin had to say, both of whom indicated that we would do whatever it is necessary for the Ukrainians to continue to advance militarily.\par \par \uc2 They have, for the last six months, been pushing the Russians steadily away from Ukrainian territory, including the major victory over Kherson just this week. And as a result, the Russians are suffering gravely. Dan rightly pointing out that the chairman made very clear that Russia`s military is on its back. So let`s continue to support the Ukrainians with everything they need to defend their cities against these brutal missile attacks and to be able to retake the territory that is rightfully theirs.\par \par \uc2 That`s how you end this war. I don`t think you`re going to end it by having Ukraine surrender or by convincing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to engage in the negotiation that he`s unlikely to enter into.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dr. Redlener, brutal is right. And maybe even that`s an understatement. What we have seen just in one day yesterday, what precipitated the use of the air defense systems was something like 90 missiles that were aimed at Ukraine from Russia. We already knew that there were devastating humanitarian conditions. We heard just now from General Milley. He talked about his own concerns about hospitals being partially operational, about the elderly being exposed to the elements, entering into the winter months.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been there. You`ve worked there in a humanitarian capacity. Tell me what your concerns are. What even went through your mind when you saw the number of missiles and heard of the destruction that was happening yesterday, compounding what we`ve already seen in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 DR. IRWIN REDLENER, FOUNDING DIRECTOR, NATIONAL CENTER FOR DISASTER PREPAREDNESS: Yes, so, Chris, thanks for this opportunity. Well, the problem is that while political and military gains are absolutely happening, that`s fantastic. However, there is a parallel track of civilian suffering that is inescapable. You know, we were there in October. On October 10th, the Russians changed this from a war of territory acquisition, buffers from \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and so on, then it became plain and simple a war of terror.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the reason that the Russians are going after the electrical power grids, the fuel supply lines, et cetera. And we`re now coming into winter and millions upon millions of Ukrainians are going to try to live in bombed out houses and apartments. We saw many of these children and families already in the middle of October who are terrified about what`s coming.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s worth reminding our diplomats and negotiators that in addition to the military supplies, Ukraine, to survive the winter, is going to need a massive amount of wood burning stoves, of backup generators, of winter clothes for children. We`ve been delivering quite a number of winter boots, for example, for children. But I am really fearful, as a public health matter, that this war of terror is going to wreak havoc.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Russians responded to the blowing of the Crimea Russian bridge at one time, which was October 10th, and then a couple of days ago, once they were pushed out of Kherson, they responded with an increasing terrification of what`s happening in that country. It is a tragedy, but we can`t forget the humanitarian needs.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dr. Redlener, Ambassador Daalder, Dan De Luce, thank you all so much. We appreciate you sitting through the news conference with us and sharing your expertise.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running for president again. Will the former president do anything different this time around to get back into the White House?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  launching an historic bid to become the first defeated and impeached former president to try to return to the White House. An extraordinarily early start for the campaigns, 720 days before the election, and with what even many supporters see as poor timing. His poll numbers are down. His most high profile candidates lost. The red wave didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 The setting was new. Lots of gold and American flags at Mar-a-Lago. But the same play book -- same old playbook from 2016 and 2020. Trump exaggerating his accomplishments, making false claims, name calling, airing personal grievances, and painting a disturbingly dark vision of America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: The citizens of our country have not yet realized the full extent and gravity of the pain our nation is going through. And the total effect of the suffering is just starting to take hold. They don`t quite feel it yet, but they will very soon. I have no doubt that by 2024, it will, sadly, be much worse, and they we`ll see much more clearly what happened and what is happening to our country. And the voting will be much different. 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A lot of the familiar faces from past campaigns are gone. No \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , no Kellyanne Conway, not a single U.S. senator. Trump`s own daughter, Ivanka, said she`s out. And as several papers noted, the old energy seems to be missing, too. "Politico" called the speech restrained. Axios called it low energy and said, quote, "Trump has done this shtick so many times before that even he seemed bored by the text."\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Garrett Haake joins me now from West Palm Beach, Florida. Doug Heye served as former communications director for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, guys. Garrett, Einstein said the definition of insanity is doing the same thing over and over again and expecting different results. Are we seeing any evidence Trump is going to do anything different this time than he did when he lost the last time he ran for president?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: The former president is using the same tool kit, but he`s trying to build something slightly different here. And it was an interesting experience to watch him try to walk this tight rope. He is trying to both run as an outsider, like he did in 2016, while also running on elements of his record in the four years that he was president.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to make an argument that you`re an outsider when you were president of the United States. On his prepared text, that`s what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was trying to do last night. But, as is so often is case with him, it is the size and the departures from the prepared text that tell you what he is really thinking. And in those moments we did see last night a lot of the same grievances, a lot of the same concerns about being the victim and about wanting to kind of go after the DOJ or the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or the other sort of, you know, forces that are arrayed against him,\par \par \uc2 [13:50:13]\par \par \uc2 Now last night`s speech was a much more buttoned up affair than what we typically see from the former president, but I don`t expect that to hold. You know, he still, you know, prefers the big rallies, prefers the fly-ins. I think we`re going to see a lot of the same tactics with a different cast of characters behind it as this campaign goes forward in day 719 and beyond.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Doug, it`s a dark vision in many ways what we heard from the former president. Certainly not the most hopeful campaign message. Not just in terms of where he sees this country now, but what he wants to do if he is re-elected because there were a couple of policy ideas he talked about. Well, let me just play them for you and get your response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We`re going to be asking everyone who sells drugs, gets caught selling drugs, to receive the death penalty for their heinous acts because --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It`s the only way to eliminate cheating. I will immediately demand voter ID, same day voting and only paper ballots.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So the death penalty for drug dealers and changing voting laws that are in place in dozens of states across the country. Is there something, Doug, in the polls we`re not seeing that gives you a reason to think Americans will support that? I mean, because it seems like the message from the midterms is that Americans don`t like extremist policies.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, a lot of the things that he talked about, there`s a few things he mentioned on voting, are actually fairly popular. Same-day voter registration, voter I.D. is popular throughout the country, even with Democrats and African-Americans, specifically.\par \par \uc2 But clearly one of the things that was most interesting is what he didn`t talk about when it comes to elections. He didn`t spend really any time on Stop the Steal. And I think he clearly recognizes that that was an absolute albatross for Republican candidates throughout the country. Certainly Arizona is the best example of that probably with Kari Lake`s doomed campaign and Black Masters` as well. And that that`s something that Republicans, and not just independent voters think the Republican Party and whoever the nominee is should stay far, far away from.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Garrett, the "Washington Post`s" David Milbank wrote a column called "The Magic is Gone," and this is what he wrote, in part, "The number of Trump supporters lining the intracoastal waterway or driving back and forth in trucks and motorcycles waving Trump 2024 and let`s go Brandon banners numbered not in the thousands or even the hundreds but the dozens."\par \par \uc2 From home, honestly, it did seem like there was low energy both from the president, and I would say not a tremendous amount of energy from the crowd, but you were there in the room. What did you see and feel, what kind of energy does Trump seem to be launching with?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I would agree with that, Chris. I mean, I`ve covered plenty of Trump rallies and speeches both as a candidate and as a president over the course of these last six years or so I guess now, and it was definitely one of the lower energy performances that you see from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In this case, you know, when he`s reading from prepared remarks, you can always tell and his heart is not usually as in it when he kind of just, you know, gets out there and speaks his mind.\par \par \uc2 The other point that was made in that call, which I think is true, is you don`t see kind of the robust infrastructure around Trump that existed late in his 2016 campaign and certainly existed when he was in the White House. It`s a smaller staff, a more lean crew, and less experienced hands potentially around him. This is going to be a campaign about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what he thinks in any given moment. Much more like 2016 than what we saw in this last election.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Garrett, Doug, we have to cut it short because we`re in that press conference, but thank you, both. Appreciate your time today.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, Senate Republicans voted to keep \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  as their leader. That is despite a challenge from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . And while we have been watching that news conference at the Pentagon, McConnell was taking questions from reporters.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, how did, Ali, this vote for McConell go down and what did he tell you guys in the reporter`s squad?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, it went down the way he said it would which is that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is still the top Republican in the Senate but you can`t talk about this reckoning that they`re having right now both in the Senate and in the House around leadership without thinking about the Trump factor of it all.\par \par \uc2 For someone like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , part of the reason that he even had a challenger is because someone like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been so critical of McConnell-led efforts on the Senate side, but also Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , a close ally of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the man who ultimately challenged McConnell, he got 10 votes, McConnell got 37, far larger number than what he needed to actually keep his role atop of the caucus.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 But this is how he always said it was going to shake out. Still, his first challenge in 15 years that he has been in leadership, so notable on that front, but look, McConnell kind of had the same assessment that everyone else has had, which is that this is the red wave that didn`t materialize, this is part of what he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I never predicted a red wave to begin with, if you will recall. I said all along this is going to be close.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: So McConnell saying there, and sort of airing grievance that he has long had between \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and -- that we`ve long heard between \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , which is they have regularly sparred over issues of candidate quality, which races were actually winnable, where the money and resources should go. All of those things were spats that these two men had along the way of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 The fact that it would come out in a leadership election is again something to take note of here, but McConnell, defiant as he was talking to reporters there, and clear about the message that Republicans need to put forward, making the point that they struggled among voters who may not have loved Biden but who weren`t finding what they needed in the Republican messaging.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, with that breaking news while we were in other breaking news. Thank you so much, Ali. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. But stay tuned. "KATY TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221117eibg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Wilfred Frost, Peter Baker, Ellison Barber, Ali  Vitali, Glenn Kirschner, Carlos Curbelo, Josh Lederman \par \uc2 9,001 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the question is what`s next? After a turbulent six weeks for British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  to resign, making her the shortest-serving PM in history. With one of our closest allies facing a new level of political chaos on top of the worst economic disaster in a generation, what`s at stake for a new prime minister, and what about Britain`s impact on the global economy or its support for Ukraine? We`ll go live to London and check in on reaction here in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a federal judge reveals that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have provided a paper trail leading to crimes of obstruction and conspiracy. Coming up, the lies Trump swore were true even when his attorney John Eastman told him that wasn`t the case, and whether that could prove once and for all that the Big Lie was a crime. And finally, if you`ve stopped worrying about COVID, this winter could come as a rude awakening. We`ll tell you about the new variants out there and whether so-called COVID immunity can stop them.\par \par \uc2 But we start overseas were \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  came into office just 44 days ago, promising to help turn the British economy around and provide some relief for millions who were struggling. That didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, OUTGOING UK PRIME MINISTER: I came into office at a time of great economic and international instability. Our country has been held back for too long by low economic growth. I was elected by the Conservative Party with a mandate to change this. I recognize though, given this situation, I cannot deliver the mandate on which I was elected by the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In fact, her new economic policies backfire. The economic reality is going from bad to worse. Inflation in the UK is at a 40-year high. Food prices alone are up 14 percent. Now a new level of global unease about the world economy is raising questions about what it could mean for the U.S. and maybe even the midterms next month.\par \par \uc2 In a minute, we`ll talk to the New York Times Peter Baker about that. But first, let`s go to London and Sky News anchor and MSNBC contributor Wilfred Frost. Wilf, Truss is out, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  apparently wants his job back. I mean, it`s wild but also incredibly consequential. So, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 WILFRED FROST, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Chris, we did just learn the process of how the next prime minister will be chosen, and three key points to it. The first is that it will be a quick process. A new prime minister in place by next Friday, that`s because the country needs stability, but also because the Conservative Party hopes that MPs will fall behind a single unity candidate, wishful thinking there, of course. The second key point is that the threshold to get on the shortlist is very high. You need one hundred nominations from MPS. There`s only about 370 in total by Monday at 2:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 And the final key point, once down to a final two, it will be the party members across the country, about 200,000 people that will make the final choice. They have to do so online, given the short timescale. The fact that there`s a high initial threshold is bad for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . The fact that the final choice is amongst that 200,000 membership base is good for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . We will see over the course of the weekend who are those final two or three candidates. Nominations close by Monday at 2:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 I think the key point here is that it`s not going to the public. There won`t be a general election. And that is perhaps the only thing delivering unity amongst the Conservative MPs at the moment. They desperately don`t want to go to a general election whilst they are so unpopular in the polls. The bottom line, by next Friday, we will have three prime ministers in less than two months, the latest one with even less of a mandate than her -- his predecessor -- his or her predecessor, excuse me, and with it a diminishing image of the country on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wilfred Frost, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Peter Baker, so the president was asked about this a couple of minutes ago actually, as he was heading out on Air Force One. He said he doesn`t think the political and economic turmoil in the UK will have a big impact on us. But if it adds to global unease, it certainly can help. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it certainly can`t help. And obviously, the United States right now is depending on Britain as an ally in this war in Ukraine that we`re in the middle of against Russia. You know Britain has been a key ally of course in helping to support the Ukrainians, supply the Ukrainians, stand up against the Russians in terms of economic sanctions, they`re about to impose strict -- stricter sanctions on Russian oil in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s something that`s going to be difficult for all of Europe and in which Britain needs to play an important role. So it`s certainly not something welcome at the White House this is uncertainty and instability in Britain, even if \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  would have necessarily been Biden`s personal choice for prime minister there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Peter, stick with me. I mean, Britain is far from alone in its worries about economic uncertainty. And here that feeling may be having a real impact on Democrats` midterm prospects. Here`s how the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  put it. Democrats feared Red October has arrived. Read of it, obviously, for Republicans, candidates who once led in places like Nevada and Wisconsin are now trailing. And of course, there`s this tight race in Georgia for the Senate. That`s where NBC`s Ellison Barber is. She joins us along with Adrienne Elrod, a Democratic strategist and former senior aide with the Biden-Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 So, Peter, I want to go to that Red October story in your paper where John Halpin of the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  ties what we`re seeing in the UK to hear. Let me quote him. "Inflation is a political wrecking ball for incumbent governments around the world. Why should the United States be different?" I mean, look, Democrats are certainly working it in the closing days of the campaign, President Biden heading to Pennsylvania for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  for Georgia gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , but how much has the momentum shifted?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I should say rather strikingly, of course, the Democrats had seemed a little bit wind in their back, as of just a few weeks ago, gas prices were coming down, the energy over the abortion rights issue seemed to be you know, lifting a number of democratic votes. Unemployment remains really low, and that`s something that traditionally has been helpful to an incumbent party. But at this stage, inflation is stubborn. High numbers are really, really biting into a lot of pocketbooks, a lot of American families, and while they may be concerned about other issues, like abortion rights, like immigration, like crime, inflation seems to be number one on the -- on the minds of many voters.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right that there are races where Democrats thought they were comfortably ahead that are suddenly looking more competitive, not just in Nevada, but even in Colorado, even Washington State, a traditionally very safe Democratic seat, you`re hearing some noises there. And so the momentum a few weeks ago is gone and seems to be, you know, heading into a tough, tough slog in these final few weeks, which many, many races are tightening. And really, we don`t know where it`s going to end up, but certainly not looking as good for the Democrats as they had hoped.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which brings us all of those concerns to Ellison This is obviously the first week of early voting in Georgia. Talking to voters, are they telling you that inflation, rising gas prices, those things are impacting the way they`re voting?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, they aren`t. I mean, it`s interesting because we`ve been talking to voters in Georgia on and off for the last year. And in recent weeks, the last month -- month and a half, we`ve increasingly heard voters regardless of their political affiliation, talk about inflation, talk about their concerns as it relates to gas prices. It`s not the only issue that they`re focused on, but it is a very big one. Here`s some of what we`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: An issue because since I live in Guyana and drive all the way to Decatur, you know, it`s a lot of gas, a lot of sitting in traffic, and I think it affects, especially teachers because we really haven`t seen that price adjustment for our cost of living. So it just makes it a little bit more difficult.\par \par \uc2 KAREN GILCHRIST, WALKER SUPPORTER: I`ve already voted, and it did affect the way that I`ve cast my ballot. And it`s very, very important that gas prices along with the inflation and securing the border, those are all very hot topics right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And the candidates know this is an issue on voters` minds, at least in the Georgia Senate race. We`re hearing them talk about it a lot on the campaign trail. We heard incumbent Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  talking about it at an event in Atlanta last night. He talked about it in the context of the work he has done in Washington. He emphasized the need for Washington and his efforts around this to do more to hold oil companies responsible. He said to supporters at that event last night, that those companies are seeing record profits while people are seeing record prices and that something needs to change and that he`s working on it in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 We heard Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  talk about it at an event here in Macon this morning. He says that the cost of gas, the rising prices that people are seeing at the pump and the grocery store are solely because of the policies of the Biden administration, an administration, and a president that he has closely tried to connect Senator Warnock to. And that`s the message that he is trying to deliver home to voters here. Again, both candidates seem to know that this really is something impacting Georgia voters. And we keep hearing them say that it is impacting in factoring into the way they vote, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Adrienne, it`s a harder argument to sell, isn`t it? Well, we`re working on it. Here`s all the stuff that we`ve done. It`s going to get better than -- look, the folks who are in there haven`t done a very good job, oh, and by the way, what you`re going to hear on all the talk radio, all the TV stations is what`s happening in Britain. There`s a sense of global economic unease, which can add to people feeling uneasy, generally. So, what do the Democrats do with the clock ticking?\par \par \uc2 (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Oh, sorry. Sorry about that. I think I was on mute. I think it`s important to run an offensive and defensive policy on this. You know, for one, yes, Democrats have a lot to talk about when it comes to what we`ve done for the economy. The Inflation Reduction Act putting money in people`s pockets. Also, of course, stressing the fact that this has been a once-in-a-lifetime pandemic that we`ve been has caused inflationary numbers, not just to rise here in the United States, but throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, though, Democrats have a lot to talk about when it comes to economic accomplishments. You all just talked about those record-low unemployment rates -- record job growth under President Biden. Again, I realize that those are not always the things that people are feeling at their kitchen table at night. But we have - but we have to, as Democrats stress what we`ve done, the positive accomplishments we -- accomplishments we made.\par \par \uc2 You also, Chris, will look at the closing ads that Democrats are making across the country, a lot of those closing ads are on abortion, which means it`s still an issue. That`s incredibly important to voters, especially those suburban swing voters who will ultimately decide what happens. So I think, again, you kind of sort of run an offensive and defensive strategy on the economy if you`re a Democrat. But you also have to draw the contrast between what life would be like with Republicans having full control of the House and Senate, and where we are now with Democrats having control.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, I don`t think there`s any question about two things. One is that the importance of abortion if you look at the polls is less than it was in the immediate aftermath of Dobbs, right? On the other hand, if you look at the breakdown, it remains very important to Democratic voters, particularly remains very important to women voters.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess it becomes a question of, can that sort of message mobilize voters who might otherwise be on the fence? I know you`ve got your finger on the pulse of a lot of races around the country, what are you hearing about organizing on the ground, especially in these tight races? I know we say it all the time. It`s all about turnout, turnout, turnout. But when you`re looking at polls that were within the margin of error, it`s about turnout.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes. That`s right, Chris. And I think we`re going to see a high turnout on both sides. And it used to be that we lagged turnout in the off- year of "midterm elections." That has changed. That certainly changed in 2018. I think it`s going to change in 2022.\par \par \uc2 And again, yes, the economy is an issue when we talk to most voters. That is something that they cite as a top one or two issues. But the wildcard card here that we have is abortion. And the fact that if you are a swing voter, even a moderate Democrat -- I mean, a moderate Republican, looking at the construct of the Supreme Court, what we have to look forward to in Washington, all you have to look forward to is a six-three Supreme Court for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 So you know that the only way that you can actually drive some sort of change in impact is to vote Democratic in your state. And I do think it`s hard to sort of see that in the polling right now. But I do think ultimately, that motivation is going to be in favor of the Democrats. I don`t know if that means we`re going to hold on to the House and Senate, but I do think it means we`re going to mitigate some of the losses that we might normally have in this type of cycle.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Adrienne Elrod, Peter Baker, Ellison Barber, good to have all of you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  will sit down exclusively with President Biden to talk about the midterm elections, to talk about the economy, much more. That`s Friday on "THE REIDOUT" at 7:00 p.m. Eastern. More of that interview will then air on the Sunday show, Sunday at 10:00 a.m. Eastern only here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 So just how devastating is new evidence against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Evidence in writing, the legal implications of what a judge is now saying. And how could the former president`s legal issues influence potential GOP presidential candidates, as \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  gives new clues about whether he might run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 And the new variants of COVID worrying U.S. and international health immunity experts, it`s all evading these -- all these immunity evading variants that have led to a surge in cases in New York. Might that spread to the rest of the country? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Newly revealed e-mails today show a paper trail that appears to prove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he was lying when he spread claims of voter fraud in Georgia just days before the January 6 attacks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: 10,315 ballots were cast by individuals whose name and date of birth matches a Georgia resident who died in 2020 prior to the election, and your wacky Secretary of State said two people -- two people. Now I don`t know how many people are on that list, but it`s a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So here`s the problem. Those e-mails from former Trump Attorney John Eastman show Trump was informed weeks before that those numbers you just heard him put out there were wrong. And yet, Trump signed legal papers swearing that they were true. Now a federal judge says those e-mails could end up being key evidence that Trump knowingly committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, and former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner. Glenn, we know Trump is famous for not leaving a paper trail, putting his names on things, he`s not an e-mail guy, this looks like an exception, how big a deal could it be?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, it`s a pretty big deal, Chris. Because you have sort of questions about the quantity of evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed crimes, I would argue as a former career prosecutor, there is an abundance of evidence that we`ve seen that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed any number of crimes in his determination to retain the office of the presidency. Then you have the quality of evidence. And I don`t think we have seen evidence of this quality before.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed a certificate and filed it with the court and Judge David Carter found, he ruled that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew it contained false information about allegations of election fraud when he filed it. I can`t say it any better than judge Carter said it. He said that the court finds these e-mails are sufficiently related to and in furtherance of a conspiracy to defraud the United States. That is basically conclusive evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ali, the reason these documents came up was because John Eastman, the lawyer, didn`t want to hand them over to the January 6 committee. Now he`s going to have to. So how does this fit into their work?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, you`ve got to imagine that they want to get their hands on these e-mails as quickly as they can because the committee has been clear about the fact that they`re on the clock. They always have been. But especially now that they`re done with the public phase of their investigative hearings, they`ve turned their attention squarely to writing that final report that they need to put out before the last day of this year.\par \par \uc2 These e-mails, especially for all the reasons that you and Glenn have laid out, speak not just to what Eastman was doing. And of course, they`ve had a lot of focus on him as one of the key lawyers in the false elector schemes, and in other prongs of their investigation into what led to January 6, but also because they speak to things that the former president may have known. Things like the defrauding that he was trying to do, despite the fact that he knew the fraud claims in Georgia that he was touting were fake, that he was doing them anyway. All of those things speak to the mindset of the former president, something that the committee has worked really hard over the course of many hearings, to try to establish publicly his intent, his motive, what he knew, and what he did in spite of what he knew.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In the meantime, Glenn, there`s this article in \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  that says there is a team of prosecutors at the DOJ who believe there is enough evidence to charge Trump but they haven`t made a formal recommendation yet. We`ve speculated a lot that they`ve been waiting for the midterms, but what else might they be waiting for?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, I wouldn`t read too much into this, Chris. I mean, it`s nice to know that prosecutors who are hard at work investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , part of the belief that there is enough evidence to charge him, but you know what, there`s this -- there`s still a long way to go because that will only be a recommendation from the prosecutors, and it will have to go through the Deputy Attorney General Lisa Monaco, and it will ultimately be made that decision by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . And we know he has sort of famously tried to rebuild the credibility and the legitimacy of the Department of Justice. So the one thing I think we can be sure of is we will see no charges before the midterms, but I do believe shortly after the midterms if we don`t begin to see indictments, dropping, that may be an indication that it wasn`t just about the midterms that was causing a delay. It`s more about the quality or quantity of the evidence that they have implicating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in crimes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Interesting. OK, Ali, meantime, we`ve been waiting for the January 6 committee to serve \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a subpoena. And I know you`ve got new reporting on the potential timing of that. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, look, it could be any moment now or the timing could continue to slide. Initially, our expectation was that they were going to issue this subpoena in the early half of this week, obviously, here we are on Thursday, the committee huddling at noon today to try to talk about what the steps forward would be, including trying to hammer out some last details on the date that these records in a deposition could be due. We think that it`s likely to be after the midterms but that`s something that they`re still trying to dot the I`s and cross the T`s on, and actively talking about as we wait for this subpoena to come.\par \par \uc2 But look, there`s also a view by some of the people that I talked to that it doesn`t necessarily even matter because the expectation level that Trump won`t try to draw this out is so low. We`ve often talked about the former president as someone who knows that he has to run out the clock and who is deft at doing that. Certainly, in this case, it`s a moment where if you just look at the weeks on the calendar, it`s not that hard to do.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we`re in mid to late October right now and this committee subpoena runs out at the end of the year, is the fact that we know that Trump could tie this up, could draw this out, but nevertheless, the committee still felt that subpoena hanging it -- subpoenaing him and putting that on the record was important enough that they are moving forward with that. It`s just a matter of them actually officially issuing it at this point, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Glenn Kirschner, great to have both of you here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Department of Justice says it needs millions of dollars to keep the January 6 probe going. And now new NBC News reporting on whether they`ll get the funds they need to "bring the January 6 rioters to justice." Let me bring in NBC`s Sahil Kapur, co-author of that report. And you`re right, Sahil, that that next congressional spending bill might be the last chance for this investigation to continue. What are you hearing on the Hill?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, Chris, it`s not exactly a priority for appropriators at the moment but that seems largely because the issue has not broken through on Capitol Hill. Yes, the Justice Department has said $34 million are critically needed to continue its criminal investigation of January 6 to bring the perpetrators to justice, but a lot of appropriators -- for a lot of them it hasn`t really made an impression.\par \par \uc2 Several senior appropriators in the House in particular have said that funding remains up in the air. Some of them were even surprised when I mentioned that the Justice Department says they need this money to continue the investigation. Some of them didn`t seem to know that the Justice Department was strapped for cash on this.\par \par \uc2 And all eyes at the moment are on that big government funding bill that is expected to pass by the December 16 deadline. Negotiators are expected to return to the drawing board after the election on November 8. And this could be a do-or-die moment for DOJ to get that money because currently, Democrats control the House and the Senate. They are more sympathetic to this. But if Republicans capture control of the House of Representatives, as they are currently favored to do, the chamber would be run by a staunch Trump allies who have been less sympathetic to this investigation. So that adds this element of urgency, you know, to include the funding in this bill.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also worth being clear, Chris, that DOJ is not saying that if it doesn`t get this money, they will have to end the investigation entirely and all the -- you know, the rioters go free. What they`re saying is that this has forced, you know, resources to be moved off of other vital law enforcement activities from U.S. Attorney`s offices all over the country. And what the DOJ is saying to Congress is please don`t put us in that position, don`t force us to choose whether to continue the January 6 investigation or other law enforcement matters, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sahil Kapur, thank you for that renewed reporting. Trump`s former VP opening up about a potential 2024 presidential run. Is all the legal pressure on the former president forcing other GOP people to consider running? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:15]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As Donald Trump`s mounting legal pressure potentially threatens a 2024 comeback, the clock is ticking on any ambitious Republicans who might be waiting in the wings.\par \par \uc2 Will increasing legal jeopardy for Trump mean more GOP presidential candidates?\par \par \uc2 Well, here`s an amused \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  yesterday when asked whether he would support a Trump 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: Well, there might be somebody else I`d prefer more.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: You know, what I can tell you is, I have every confidence that the Republican Party is going to sort out leadership. All my focus has been on the midterm elections, and it will stay that way for the next 20 days. But after that, we`ll be thinking about the future, ours and the nation`s, and I`ll keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For more, let`s bring in \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , a former Republican congressman and an NBC News political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you. So, look, if you`re a Republican who`s thinking about running for president, even if Trump is never charged with anything, does it make him weaker and political opponents more likely to get in the more we hear about these unfolding investigations?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, that`s the interesting thing because as strong as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  remains in the Republican Party, he`s still the dominant force, the most popular figure, a lot of these presidential hopefuls are starting to look past \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And you have people like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who`s clearly positioning himself to run or at least to be a relevant figure in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 You have \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  here in Florida, who is campaigning for re-election without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by his side. So, people are really looking past \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even though he still gets all the attention in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, you make a good point because on one hand you see some of those potential candidates, like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , trying to distance themselves from Trump, at least like physically, right? But then they go out and they campaign for election deniers. Is this a kind of embrace his base but not him strategy?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Yes, this is the careful balance, let`s call it that way to be kind, that a lot of Republicans try to reach these days. And that`s why some people think that we might even see someone who doesn`t come from this paradigm, right, this pro-Trump versus anti-Trump world emerge and become a leading figure. A lot of people think we`ll actually see that in both parties, that the two nominees in 2024 will be people who aren`t even in the headlines today.\par \par \uc2 Another Floridian down here, Miami Mayor \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2 , who`s a Republican, a conservative, but doesn`t fall neatly into this pro-Trump, anti-Trump world. He`s starting to make some noise. So someone like that who can run on a record and isn`t tied to all the noise and the mess of the last three, four, five years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, that leave me to the question, could it be somebody who is not political? I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never ran for anything and he became president. We`ve seen other folks -- well, I mean, obviously you had \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  became governor of California before becoming president. You saw Arnold Schwarzenegger. Could we see a non-politician mount a serious campaign? And if they are thinking about it, do they got to start soon?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Certainly we`re living in times, Chris, where the traditional paths to the presidency really don`t exist anymore. And if they do, there are certainly other paths. And we can certainly see celebrity types like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  try to run for president. We can see business people. You know, obviously \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2  tried to do this in the Democratic Party last time.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 But certainly we could see that dynamic in the Republican lane as well, someone from the business world who says none of these politicians know what they`re doing. We have to reset. There`s really a fertile environment, fertile ground for all these new, different, and creative pathways to the presidency. And I think there`s a good chance something like that could happen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Former Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re also watching live pictures out of Pittsburgh. President Biden is set to speak there next hour. He`s going to be talking about rebuilding America`s infrastructure. He`s there for Democratic Senate candidate, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . At least that he`s among those at the event.\par \par \uc2 Wow, look at that. This is a guy we`re used to seeing in a hoody but obviously the president shows up and he put on a jacket and a tie. So he`s going to have a reception (INAUDIBLE) for Fetterman later tonight in Philadelphia. And we`ll have more on this coming up as we keep our eye on what is happening in Pittsburgh. Obviously Fetterman hoping Biden can help push him over the top there.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, thousands of Ukrainians forced to flee a key region in the south that could very soon become that war`s next battleground. A former U.S. ambassador to Russia joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:22]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thousands of Ukrainians are being forced to flee the Kherson region in southern Ukraine, amid growing fears it will be the next battleground in Russia`s brutal war in Ukraine. People there being ordered to leave by Russian installed leaders.\par \par \uc2 Check out this Telegram post from a pro-Russian leader warning the battle will begin in the very near future.\par \par \uc2 Those evacuations are an apparent reaction to Ukraine`s successful recapturing of villages and farmland in that region. And it comes just hours after Russian President Putin once again tightened his grip, declaring martial law in four illegally annexed regions of Ukraine, including Kherson.\par \par \uc2 Michael McFaul is the former U.S. ambassador to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Always good to have you here. So martial law has now been declared in those regions. You`ve negotiated face to face with Putin. I`m sorry, I apologize. We have to go to Pennsylvania. President Biden, who is there, let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Gov, since you don`t have a coat on, this is going to be painfully short. I`m not going to keep you too long.\par \par \uc2 It`s great -- thanks for that introduction. I really appreciate it, Greg. And, you know -- and thanks to the elected officials here today. Governor Wolf, one of the best governors I`ve ever served with in my life.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One of my best buddies from Scranton, as we say. Bobby Casey, I know his dad and his mom is still around. And Representative Doyle, which -- how about this one? Is this working?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, that`s working.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I tell you what, I knew he`d come here and do this.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I`m joking. Can we get -- there you go. All right.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Lieutenant Governor --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE DOYLE (D-PA): Just as you were saying something nice about me.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I was saying something nice about you. Jay, that`s why I went out. But I`m saying we`re going to try like the devil to keep you from having to -- well, not having to, deciding to leave. I wish you didn`t. And then, John, thank you very much for running. I really do appreciate it. And, Giselle, you`re going to be a great lady in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As I`ve said before that America is the only country in the world that comes out of crises stronger than we went into the crisis. And that`s the story I want to tell here today, briefly. Just nine months ago, January 28th in 2022, I was about to leave the White House on my way to Mill 19 here in Pittsburgh to talk about the resurgence of American manufacturing. And by the way, we`ve created 700,000 manufacturing jobs since then.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Before I left Washington, I got word about what happened earlier in that day. At 6:45 on a snowy day, the bridge behind me collapsed, 100 feet straight down to Fern Hollow. And five cars and a bus were crossing the bridge at the time. Several people were injured. But by the grace of God, the school was delayed that day because of snow and it was just before rush hour, so there was less traffic than usual. It had been a normal -- had it been a normal day, it would`ve been much, much worse.\par \par \uc2 I decided still to come to Pittsburgh. I came straight here from the airport. When I arrived, Mayor Gainey, who is not here right now, was here. And Bobby and Conor and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  were all here, along with the responders. You all told me about the emergency workers who had pulled the survivors to safety. They were heroes that day, and a complete catastrophe was avoided. But it never should`ve come to this.\par \par \uc2 For too long, we talked about building the best economy in the world and the best infrastructure in the world. We talked about asserting America`s leadership. We (INAUDIBLE) about the best and the safest roads, railroads, ports, airports, and so much more. But now, now, we didn`t do it, but we`re finally getting to it. We`re getting it done. We`re finally deciding that instead of being ranked number 13 in the world, 13 in the world -- the United States, 13 in the world in infrastructure, we should be ranked number one.\par \par \uc2 So, instead, instead of Infrastructure Week, which was a punchline for four years under my predecessor, it`s Infrastructure Decade, a headline on my watch. And with the help of your members of Congress here today, I signed into law a once-in-a-generation investment in roads, highways, bridges, railroads, ports, airports, and so much more.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 Over a billion two hundred -- a trillion and two hundred billion dollar. It`s called the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, and it`s the most significant investment in American infrastructure in roads, bridges, et cetera, than Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway system.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania alone has already received $5.2 billion just this year for hundreds of projects across the commonwealth. And that`s just announced another $2.5 billion to fix and upgrade Pennsylvania`s roads and bridges, and there`ll be billions more for other projects.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you why this matters. When I was here in January, I told the governor that we`d help and we`d be help rebuild this bridge behind us as fast as we possibly could. That day, the governor moved and signed an emergency order that let us move separate federal funding as quickly as possible to the project. The reason we could do that is because I had just signed the Infrastructure Law a few months earlier. But had we waited for that money, it would have taken longer.\par \par \uc2 The governor knew he could count on the new law to fund other projects to ensure the projects he already had started for infrastructure in the state would not be slowed down in the process. The result, Pennsylvania has been able to repair Fern Hollow Bridge in less than a year. And by Christmas, God willing, we`ll be walking -- I`m coming back to walk over this sucker. Although my staff said to me, "Do you realize how many times you`ve been to Pittsburgh?" I said, "No." He said, "Nineteen."\par \par \uc2 But, Bobby, don`t tell them in Scranton. Don`t tell them in Scranton. I -- anyway. But we -- it`s being done in record time. Normally you`d be looking at two to five years to build a bridge like this, literally. And the total project costs $25 million. Fully paid for by the federal government. And, by the way, and, by the way, in the process of that, not a single penny of that money has cost anybody making under 400 grand in the nation to pay a penny more in taxes.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, in the process, we also cut the deficit in this process nationally by a trillion four hundred billion dollars just this year.\par \par \uc2 So, Gov, the fact is it wouldn`t have happened without you, Gov. It wouldn`t have -- no, it wouldn`t have happened in this town. That`s not hyperbole. That`s a fact. And you`re working so hard to get it done. I want to thank you. This really matters.\par \par \uc2 Folks, folks in the neighborhood relied on this bridge and the walking path underneath to commute to work, to take their kids to school, to run errands, to stay connected to other neighborhoods like Squirrel Hill and Waterfront and Oakland. And below the bridge, Frick Park is going to be fixed up when this is done. It`s better than it was, hiking paths, new seating areas.\par \par \uc2 This bridge was built initially in 1901. It was rebuilt in 1970. These guys behind me know that. None of them were here then, I don`t think. But the fact of the matter is now it`s a modern bridge of the 21st century with four lanes for vehicle traffic, a path for cyclists and pedestrians. This project has supported over a hundred jobs, good-paying construction, union jobs. Laborers, carpenters, cement mason. So many more.\par \par \uc2 The contractor delivering this project, by the way, has been a company that`s been owned by the same family for four generations, a union company since 1930s that built our roads and our bridges, building during the New Deal, during Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway System, and now the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law. But, you know, this is -- one bridge also tells a broader story. There are nearly 45,000 -- say it again, 45,000 bridges across this country in poor condition. In Pennsylvania alone, there are 3,100 bridges and 7,500 miles of highway in poor condition.\par \par \uc2 And as I was talking to your person, Gov, also, we`re going to get the railroad going, too. I`m a rail guy. Anyway. Pittsburgh is the city of bridges, but too many of them are in poor condition like this bridge behind me before it collapsed. But with the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, we`re doing something about it. This is just one of 2400 bridges across the country that are being repaired just this year because of this law.\par \par \uc2 For example, the Allegheny County work is underway to repair the bridges of the Boulevard of the Allies. Not just bridges, though.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Just outside of Pittsburgh, we`re investing $850 million to replace and expand a 100-year-old Montgomery Locks and Dam that`s critical to the nation`s economy. And I`m -- we`re investing, that investment is going to increase the capacity of this system, the Locks, over 50 percent at a critical choke point in the river.\par \par \uc2 That means we`re going to be able to move an additional 14,000 barges every year, saving the equivalent of having to have one million hauls on tractor trailers to get the same material to where it has to go. These savings are going to allow the locks to be more efficient and move 10 billion tons of goods like coal, petroleum, and steel. As a consequence, we`re lowering the cost of transporting these materials, but just as important, just as important, we`re going to reduce emissions that can come from the trucks and trains hauling it over -- by reducing it by 3.5 million tons of CO2 a year.\par \par \uc2 Three point -- cutting pollution in the air we breathe. This is good for the economy and the environment and public health.\par \par \uc2 And, Bobby, I promised you, he been on my back to get this done for -- since I got elected President. Don`t forget the Locks, Joe. So I just think -- I`m not sure how we`re going to do this, but so long as I -- I couldn`t say no so if we get the money for the Locks projects done, and I think we should call it Casey`s Lock. I don`t know. But, Bobby, you have been -- for a Scranton guy to worry about the Locks in this side -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 In the Hill District of Pittsburgh, we`re providing funding to revitalize the area by making sidewalks and streets safer for pedestrians and drivers, and connecting communities that suffered for decades for lack of investment. And thanks to the Infrastructure Law, Pennsylvanians can get $170 million over five years to build out the electric charging stations that are going to be available to all the electric vehicles that are going to be on the road.\par \par \uc2 And that`s just what we need because half of the new car sales in America are going to be electric by the end of this decade. My administration has recently approved Pennsylvania`s plan so the funds are available now, so the IBEW can start constructing these facilities. We`re putting $20 million into a new 700,000-square-foot terminal at the Pittsburgh Airport to include upgrades to the security screening, baggage check system, so it`s easier and more efficient to catch your flight.\par \par \uc2 No one knows better than the people of Pittsburgh that there are thousands of uncapped orphan wells, oil and gas wells, spewing methane into the air. These abandoned mines scattered throughout this area. It`s a legacy left behind by the mining and oil and gas companies. When the wells and fields went dry, the companies left, and people -- and left it to the people of Pennsylvania to clean it up. Well, guess what? We`re going to clean it up.\par \par \uc2 Now, my Department of Interior is sending Pennsylvania close to -- Conor, I know you`ve worked on this -- close to $350 million this year to cap the wells and reclaim the abandoned mine lands and polluted waterways, creating good-paying jobs in the process for the folks in the same communities that dug the wells in the first place.\par \par \uc2 In addition, we`re investing at least $100 million in Pennsylvania to get high-speed Internet across the state in every part of the state -- urban, suburban, and rural. And no one is going to be left behind. Twenty-one percent of Pennsylvania families don`t have home Internet connections. But never again should a parent have to drive their kid to a McDonald`s parking lot, and sit there to connect to a McDonald`s Internet so they could do their homework. Not a joke. You saw it happening.\par \par \uc2 In addition, there are over 150,000 lead service lines carrying water to homes and schools in Pennsylvania and over 7,000 in Pittsburgh. This impacts children`s brain development. I mean, for real. It`s a hazard to their health. You can`t have this in Pennsylvania. We can`t have this anywhere in America.\par \par \uc2 Now Pennsylvania is going to get $87 million to replace these lead pipes, because every person, every child in this country should have access to clean, safe drinking water. And, by the way, these projects are going to create good-paying, mostly union jobs. That`s because I made sure the overwhelming majority of the funds of this Infrastructure Law are subject to Davis-Bacon prevailing wage requirements.\par \par \uc2 I said when I ran we`re going to build the country from the bottom up and the middle out, not the top down. When the middle does well, the wealthy do very well. So, this law is about more than rebuilding our infrastructure. It`s about rebuilding the middle class -- something John knows a lot about and talks a lot about.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you, I`m a proud Delawarean, but Pennsylvania is my native state.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 It`s in my heart. I can`t tell you how much it means to me to be part of rebuilding this beautiful state. My grandfather Finnegan from Scranton would really be proud of me right now. No, I`m not joking. He would. By the way, he was an All-American football player, John, in Santa Clara.\par \par \uc2 You know, I started my campaign for President here in Pittsburgh because there`s no better place to talk about rebuilding the backbone of America, the middle class. After I accepted my party`s nomination, Pittsburgh was my first major stop. It`s here that I laid out my vision for building a better America with better pay and dignity, dignity for working people, people who built this country.\par \par \uc2 My dad used to say everybody, everybody should be treated with dignity no matter what they`re -- where they`re from. Because here`s what I know: For most of the last century, we led the world by a significant margin because we invested in our people, we invested in ourselves, we invested in our land. But along the way, we stopped doing that. But not anymore. We`re back on track.\par \par \uc2 When you see these projects in your neighborhoods -- the cranes going up, shovels in the ground -- I want you to feel the way I feel. Pride. Pride in what we can do when we work together. And that`s what I mean when I say we`re building a better America.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, let`s get something straight. We managed to get some Republicans to vote for this, 13 in the House and 19 in the Senate. And I`m truly grateful for them. I mean it sincerely. But a whole lot more voted against it but are taking credit for it now. You know, you may have seen the news reported on CNN describing Republicans who voted the Infrastructure Law after attacking me and Democrats for passing what they called socialism.\par \par \uc2 Socialism. Well, now they`re quietly and privately sending me letters. Not a joke. My administration, asking for money, asking for that money, talking about how important the projects in their district are and for America. I`ve got to say, I was surprised to see there are so many socialists in the Republican caucus. But look, and I mean this sincerely, even if they voted against it, I promised when I ran, I`d be a president for all the American people. Everyone.\par \par \uc2 We`re building a better America together, even in districts where congressmen voted against this. They`re going to get the money. And one last thing. This is all being done without raising anyone`s taxes a penny if you make less than 400 grand. And we did it while cutting the deficit in half this year -- in half.\par \par \uc2 Let me close with this. It`s been a rough four or five years in this country. For a lot of families, it`s still kind of tough. But there are bright spots where America is reasserting itself, like here, where the best workers in the world are hard at work building a future, a better future for everybody. We`re proving our best days are ahead of us, not behind us. We just have to keep it going, and we know we can.\par \par \uc2 I have never been more optimistic about America`s future than I am today. And I really mean that. Just remember, we`ve got to remember who we are. We are the United States of America. There`s nothing beyond our capacity if we work together, as this is example of.\par \par \uc2 So God bless you all. And may God protect our troops. And thank you for what you`ve done, particularly all the workers here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: President Biden in Pittsburgh, at the Fern Hollow Bridge which is being reconstructed after collapsing earlier this year, introduced by a union worker.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Josh Lederman who is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Josh, what`s the overall political message of the president here in the state as he pointed out where he has spent his childhood.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. This was not a campaign event, but certainly when you think about what the White House sees as President Biden`s role in these midterm campaigns, nothing captures it better than President Biden standing in front of tractors in a battle ground state that he won, talking about very specific tangible things that Democrats feel that they have delivered on the economy for Americans that can actually be experienced by people, like a bridge going across a river, as they try to focus voters` attention, given the -- all of the importance of the economy on some of those positives, as opposed to on the gas prices, the inflation, the other things they know are top of mind for so many Americans heading into this election. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Josh Lederman, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Again, the president, a little later on today, will be stumping for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who is in a tough race with Dr. Oz. We`ll keep our eyes on that event today.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" is next.\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: And good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221021eiak00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Blayne Alexander, Garrett Haake, David Fahrenthold,  Wilfred Frost, Cal Perry, Steph Twitty, Ryan Reilly, Charles Coleman, Ali  Vitali \par \uc2 8,319 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: "ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS". CHRIS JANSING REPORTS starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With just a few weeks to go in the midterm still too close to call, today, President Biden is putting his time and his energy behind an issue that frankly, most Americans say is not their top priority. You heard it just moments ago he said he would introduce a bill to protect abortion rights if Democrats can hold on to Congress. It`s clearly an effort to motivate voters. His last words were vote, vote, vote. But with economic worries, not abortion, top of mind for most Americans, the question is, will it work?\par \par \uc2 Plus, would you pay more than $1,000 for a hotel room? A new House investigation finds that the Secret Service had to do just that repeatedly because that`s what the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  charged them to stay in their hotels. We`ve got details on this alleged scam, breaking it down with a reporter who knows more about Trump`s finances than almost anyone, The New York Times`s David Farenthold.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of money, nobody likes to pay baggage fees at the airport. It`s even worse if you`re being unfairly charged. But that`s what happened to customers of one major airline, so they fought back and they won. We`ll dig into the details of the $7.5 million settlement by \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , and how you might get a piece of that money.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterms. And as Election Day creeps closer, it seems like we`re getting less clarity and more question marks. Candidates who are supposed to be competitive are struggling. Races that weren`t supposed to be close are neck and neck. And the issue that Democrats thought would propel them to victory just three months ago, abortion, is no longer a top priority for most voters, overwhelmed in the closing weeks of the campaign by economic worries. But today, President Biden is trying to bring abortion back into the national spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote. That`s in why these midterm elections are so critical, elect more Democratic senators to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and more Democrats to keep control of the House of Representatives. And folks, if we do that, here`s the promise I make to you and the American people. The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, the question now is whether this changes how abortion resonates in the Senate races that will decide control of Congress. In Georgia where abortion allegations have dogged Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , polls from September showed just 5 percent of voters even list it as their top issue. And in fact, last night, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and his opponent \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  spent far more time debating the economy than abortion, but they flipped the script we`ve seen another race with the Democrat complaining about the economy and the Republican defending it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2 , (R-GA): Our recovery has been as good as any state in the country. We have had two record years of economic development.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, DEMOCRATIC GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: This is a governor who`s for the last four years has beaten his chest but delivered very little for most Georgians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Blayne Alexander is live in Atlanta, \uc2 Andrew Tobias\uc2  is the politics and Statehouse reporter for The Cleveland Plain Dealer and Alaina Beverly is a former White House aide and served as national deputy director of African American outreach in the 2008 \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden campaign. It`s good to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Blayne, we talked about these unexpected outcomes. The Senate race was expected to be pretty close, but Abrams has underperformed pretty dramatically coming out of last night`s debate. What`s the candidates` strategy for the last three weeks of these campaigns?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, you know, certainly I think it`s good news for both campaigns that voters actually heard from both candidates on the very first day, the very same day that they went to the polls for early voting. You know, these are kind of the things that they want to stay with voters going into this final stretch before Election Day. I think a couple of notable things that we can expect in the last couple of weeks, one, early voting is certainly going to be a key for both of them. It`s something that we know is something that`s strong for Democrats. We know that if the early voters, people come out and vote early, typically, that`s good news for Democrats. And a couple of democratic sources underscored that point for me today, looking at these record-breaking numbers out of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But one thing that`s interesting is that we`re also seeing that same message from Republicans. Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has several times said to his supporters, go out, vote early, we want to show early momentum. It`s something that he said on the campaign trail at events that I`ve been at, and it`s something that he repeated earlier today, even on Fox News. So, when you look at that, and also you look at the fact that Georgia now has about 1.6 million more registered voters than we did back in 2018, it certainly adds a couple of variables in there.\par \par \uc2 Now when it comes to strategy as to where those votes are going to come from. Yes, of course, it matters to Atlanta but both campaigns are really looking to other areas where typically they may not see traditional votes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , it`s the rural areas around Georgia. She`s told me in interviews, and she`s made it clear that she knows she`s not going to take the four counties, but if she can pick up individual votes, that may be enough to make up the difference. For \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , however, he`s going to be looking to the suburbs hoping to flip people over on his side that didn`t vote for him 20 -- in 2018, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And a good reminder that even though we say three weeks away from Election Day for folks there, some of them are already voting. Elena, I want to talk about the president and his strategy. As the leader of his party, obviously, he`s trying to shift the conversation back to abortion. And I get it because when he says, look, remember how you felt that day when Dobbs came down. Because the polls were very different then, but the polls now show people are more worried about the economy. So my question is, is that the speech you should be giving right now?\par \par \uc2 ALAINA BEVERLY, FORMER NATIONAL DEPUTY DIRECTOR OF AFRICAN AMERICAN OUTREACH FOR OBAMA `08: I think that it actually is still motivating. I think that certainly as abortion rights or as the overturn of Roe v. Wade goes farther back in the rearview mirror, people need to be reminded of how important this right is, about how important it is to ensure that we have control of the House and the Senate. Because if the Republicans are to get a majority, that they would have a nationwide ban on the right to abortion and the right tool, a woman`s right to choose. And so, he`s trying to motivate our voters to get to remember how critical these midterm races are going to be.\par \par \uc2 Let`s remember that the Republicans only need one net gain in the Senate in order to have a majority. And people need to remember -- the voters need to be reminded of the fact that their vote matters not just for their local race, and not just for what is happening in their individual communities, but what it means for governance across the country on critical issues like a woman`s right to choose. Certainly, the economy is important and pocketbook issues tend to motivate voters. Just yesterday, my former boss, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was talking about how the Democrats need to be better on messaging on issues of the economy and make sure that we understand what voters are feeling. But voters are also invested in a woman`s right to choose and a woman`s right to bodily integrity. And that`s of course --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I don`t think there`s any doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY: Yes,\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The question is, do they vote on it? And I wonder if there`s a parallel, Alain, to 2010 when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  blamed the economy, for the Democrats quoting him shall lacking in the midterms. He had spent a ton of time focused on health care instead of the economy. He admitted later, it was a mistake. And so the question is. Understanding the upset, understanding how people feel strongly, especially in the Democratic Party about abortion, is Biden making the same mistake?\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY: I know -- I think it`s both end. I think that we need to be talking about the economy and economic harm and economic -- and economic hurt, as well as these critical issues that motivate the base. That will motivate women that will motivate turnout in for suburban women and for individuals who are seeing Republican leadership as being a return, a removal of rights, the stripping of rights as opposed to access to rights. I think it is both end in this instance.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, we are seeing a rebound in Republic -- in the polling for Republicans in the same way that we saw that wave in 2014. But I think that the way that you stymie that wave is by talking -- speaking about what is happening in the pocketbook, as well as what it means for larger issues of governance and the rollback of our rights.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Andrew, let me talk to you about the debate last night in Ohio, Ryan versus Vance, a race too close to call right now. It was kind of fascinating to watch them trying to keep their distance from their party leaders. And I want to show just a little part of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: This guy goes on national TV and says I love \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  that has the audacity of accusing me of kissing anyone`s rear end. It`s pretty rich.\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: I ran against \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , J.D., for leadership. And you have to have the courage to take on your own leaders. These leaders in DC will --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, you surely have some.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: They will -- they will eat you up like a chew toy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Andrew, you wrote about the debate, you said it didn`t break a lot of new ground, but the candidate should have more personal contempt for each other this time around. Do you think this moves the bar at all? What do you make of the overall debate last night, Andrew?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TOBIAS\uc2 , POLITICS & STATEHOUSE REPORTER, CLEVELAND.COM & THE PLAIN DEALER: Yes. So, one of the things I was looking for going into it was that it was being conducted in Youngstown and that`s basically where Tim Ryan is from, he`s representative in Congress for almost 20 years. And so, I was wondering if you might be focusing his message more on a kind of, you know, what he`s done for Youngstown, tried to speak to more like a regional message because it`s a really important political region for the state. But instead, you saw him talking about these national issues. You taught these -- I`m talking about abortion and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And so I think it really depends on you know -- that our brother -- I think that reveals that Ryan was more speaking to people who might catch a clip on social media as opposed to kind of making a presentation to the viewers of the debate itself. And so you saw him go after Vance, saying he`s made a lot out of that comment that former President Trump made about him kissing his rear end, and as J.D. Vance said so politely, and stuff like that. And so, I really think that like I said, it`s -- it speaks to Tim Ryan, focusing on voters who might be motivated by things like abortion, by things like Jan -- the January 6 riots, and things along those lines, as opposed to maybe a broader economic message. And I think that speaks to maybe how this kind of stuff gets consumed through social media and the like.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Few months ago, Ryan was far ahead. Now, as I said, it`s too close to call. What`s your observation on the ground as you talk to voters? What`s going to make the difference in the end?\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I mean, this is a really generic thing to say, but I think it all comes down to turnout.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Turnout, turnout, turnout, yes.\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I mean, though, the polls here have shifted slightly in J.D. Vance`s favor, he`s led in the last two or so that have come out you know, it makes sense to me that President Biden be talking about abortion, trying to mobilize Democratic voters, trying to remind them of that issue, trying to meet the sort of built-in Republican enthusiasm that`s going to be there just because of backlash to you know whether it`s inflation or just generally Democrats controlling the White House. And so -- I mean, it just really depends on who shows up to vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s a lot of kind of, like, chaos in this in terms of how the abortion issue might kind of scramble. People around that might generally vote, you know, maybe they`re -- you know, the so-called, you know, Republican-leaning woman who might, you know, cross over and vote for Democrats definitely who Tim Ryan`s been focusing on heavily in his race. But then, you know, we`ve seen signs of Republican voters. There`s a 25 percent increase in early vote requests in Trumbull County, which President Trump won by 10 points. That`s where Tim Ryan lives. It`s the Youngstown area. And so it`s just really going to depend on who shows up.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So you won`t be so foolish as to predict the outcome of the Senate race. How about a little baseball game between the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and the Guardians at 4:07 p.m.?\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I`m hopeful that we`ll have one today. That don`t -- that didn`t happen last night, so it`s not a given but I`m hopeful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Andrew Tobias\uc2 , Alaina Beverly, I want to ask you for your predictions either. Thank you both so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And now we`ve got another big story that we`ve been following. It`s about threats, finger-pointing, intense pressure, a combination of potentially deadly factors described in detail in the new House investigation, finding that the Trump administration cared more about optics and politics than public health during the darkest days of the pandemic. The report concludes there was a concerted effort to suppress scientific reports and bully top officials at the CDC, all in an attempt to keep the pandemic and thousands of deaths connected to it from reflecting poorly on then-President Trump.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC senior Capitol Hill correspondent Garrett Haake. First of all, Garrett, it`s great to have you back. Welcome. We knew Trump didn`t see eye to eye with a lot of his officials, but this report really digs deep into what was going on behind the scenes. What are your headlines out of this report?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, it`s good to be back. And this report comes with receipts in the forms of transcripts with so many officials up and down \uc2 HHS\uc2 , and CDC, other agencies tasked with not just combating the coronavirus but shaping the public health policy and communications around it.\par \par \uc2 In this report, we hear from officials like Dr. \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2 , then the CDC director who said that putting together reports on the Coronavirus with the kind of oversight they were getting from the Trump White House gave him PTSD. That he says that the agencies like the CDC were effectively muzzled, prevented from giving briefings, forced to go through the White House or the vice president`s office if they were going to give interviews.\par \par \uc2 And then even the integrity of some of the basic scientific releases. These mortality reports that were sent out on a regular basis were compromised by political officials, first, students of the Trump campaigns or the Trump White House. One individual is named here, \uc2 Michael Caputo\uc2 , who was a former longtime Trump political associate who was put in charge of comms at \uc2 HHS\uc2  early on in the pandemic. He`s described as having bully-ish behavior trying to shape all of the communications from the federal government not to be the most scientifically accurate or the most accurate for public health. But this report alleges, meant to be as maximally beneficial to the Trump White House as the pandemic transition into the summer and into the political season in the fall of 2020, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Garrett Haake, back on Capitol Hill for us, good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Millions of your taxpayer dollars spent for hotel rooms, the shocking new findings from a congressional investigation into the profits for former President Trump`s travel. Plus, British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  fighting for her job, we`ll go live to London amid new calls for the new prime minister to take a hike. And the baggage fees you pay at the airport may not have been legit, why one major airline is now paying up big time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A shocking new investigation shows that Donald Trump`s company made a fortune while housing the Secret Service agents protecting him and his family. According to the report released by the White House -- by the House Oversight Committee, \uc2 Trump Hotels\uc2  charge his protective detail more than $1.4 million during his four years in office. \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , who ran the company at the time, responded by saying in part "the Trump family is likely the first family in American history to have not profited off the \uc2 United States government\uc2 ." And back in 2090, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, SON OF FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So, if my father travels, they stay at our properties for free, meaning, it was like you know cost for housekeeping effectively means you actually have to legally charge government something. So everywhere that he goes, if he stays at one of these places, the government actually spends a -- meaningless it saves a fortune because if they were to go to a hotel across the street, they`d be charging $500 a night, whereas, you know, we charge them like, you know, $50.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But it wasn`t $50, in fact, taxpayers were charged as much as $1,185 a night for those rooms, more than five times the recommended government rate.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in a man who won the Pulitzer Prize for his reporting on Donald Trump`s financial scandals. David Fahrenthold, an investigative reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and an NBC News contributor. Always good to see you, David.\par \par \uc2 The House investigation found that Trump visited his own properties well over 500 times during his presidency, and Secret Service agents, of course, are required to stay at those properties because they have to provide protection. You describe it as "an arrangement in which Mr. Trump and his family effectively turn the Secret Service into a captive customer of their business." You`ve been looking into these financial dealings for years, what stands out to you from this House investigation report?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FAHRENTHOLD, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there -- thing that we didn`t know until yesterday was the highest end that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was charging these secret service agents. We had seen that there were charges for that $1,185 or $1,160, but we didn`t know if was that one room, was that five rooms. We know now, conclusively that was just one room.\par \par \uc2 And so that -- I think the clip you played from \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  is really instructive. That`s what the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was saying throughout Trump`s presidency but that they weren`t charging it. It`s free. It`s only $50. It saves the government all this money, but couldn`t be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 Now the records come out, we see they were charging routinely over $500 -- $600 a night for hotel rooms, just because they could and the Secret Service had no other choice. They had to be there. They were a captive customers.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If as the report suggests Trump`s family business leverage the presidency to his financial advantage, is that against the law?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: No. The president is exempt from the conflict-of-interest rules that would apply to almost any other federal government. So if you were a middle manager in the Department of Energy, you certainly couldn`t direct government business to your family business but if you`re the president, you can and so that -- there`s nothing illegal about that. In this case, that what strikes me though, is the real contrast between what they told the public, what they said they were doing, and what they were really doing behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which is something you`ve written about in other instances, as well. You also write this. The records also made clear that \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  provided a misleading account of what his company was charging, to your point. So, could there be any legal implications for family members?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: Out of this, I don`t -- I haven`t heard anybody say you could make a legal case against them for misrepresenting what they were saying to the public. Certainly, in their official dealings with the Secret Service, which were all hidden until these records came out, starting in 2020, they were honest about what they were charging. You know, like, we don`t have very many rooms, there`s a lot of demand, you`re going to -- you`re going to have to pay this rate of more than $1,000 a night. So, you know, person to person in those individual transactions, they weren`t duping the Secret Service, they were just misleading the public and, sadly, I think that`s not going to be a crime. They`re going not to be prosecuted for it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Fahrenthold, always good to have you on the program. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 Her agenda gutted, her popularity plummeting. Today, British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is in serious trouble a little more than a month into her first term. Two new polls show a majority of the British public and her own party wants her out, literally turning her into a joke after the economist compared her unfavorably to a head of lettuce. The Daily Star picked that up, posting a live link to a lettuce cam to see which has the longer shelf life, Truss or the lettuce. There are even betting odds over which will last longer. In an attempt to stay for job, Truss apologized in a BBC interview for her heavily criticized economic policy that sent the markets tumbling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, PRIME MINISTER OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: I recognize we have made mistakes, I`m sorry for those mistakes. But I fix the mistakes. I`ve appointed a new chancellor. We have restored economic stability and fiscal discipline. And what I now want to do is go on and deliver for the public.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It was Truss`s proposed tax cuts combined with a steep drop in the pound and the unsteady market that upset many in Parliament. For more, I want to bring in Sky News Wilfred Frost. Hey, Wilfred, so in spite of this lettuce joke, this is serious stuff, several members of parliament calling for Truss to be gone, including opposition parties who want a general election, and the question, not tonight, I guess is can she survive?\par \par \uc2 WILFRED FROST, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: I think, Chris, now is a question of when she goes and not if. And of course, in the meantime, we just have an unbelievably weak prime minister, particularly when it comes to economic issues highlighted by all of those U-turns you just mentioned. I think it`s unlikely.\par \par \uc2 However, there`ll be a full general election. The next one`s scheduled for December 2024. To bring that forward would require Conservative MPs to vote for it. And they`re not going to do that when they`re quite so far behind in the polls, as they are right now. But she is very much at the mercy of her own MPs. And for them, the timing of all this is really a question of when would be the least humiliating for them as a party having of course just changed their previous prime minister through the same process by getting rid of \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  earlier in the summer.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Just on the very short term, Chris, all eyes tomorrow on the prime minister`s questions in parliament where \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  will have to face the Labour leader and he`s expected to really highlight how weak she is, how many U-turns she`s done, and frankly, the fact that she`s lost her mandate that might crystallize her own MPs to move faster or small chance, it might unify them a little bit. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Small chance, you talk about the fact that they`re going to highlight how weak she is but we`ve all watched from here on the other side of the pond, just how those parliamentary gatherings can play out. What are you expecting to see tomorrow really?\par \par \uc2 FROST: Well, listen, and just yesterday, we did see \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  in Parliament briefly, but she was sitting silently, somewhat somber, somewhat humiliated behind her new finance minister who clearly stole the show and tore up her economic policies. Tomorrow, she`s the one speaking. She`s the one answering the questions.\par \par \uc2 It`s never been seen as one of her strengths, public speaking, throughout all of the hustings over the summer. She has to step up in just an unfathomably strong way to give herself any chance of re-gaining some kind of footing, some kind of respect, not just from her own party, from the opposition, and from the country. And as I mentioned, expectations aren`t particularly high.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Fred Frost, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s brutal attacks on civilians and critical infrastructure, what it means for Ukraine`s fight heading into the winter`s freezing months. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:44]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Russian attacks on critical Ukrainian infrastructure have left the entire country bracing for blackouts. The president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , announced hours ago that recent attacks have already destroyed a third of the country`s power grid leading parts of Kyiv and other cities with no power or water. Just last night another brutal attack in Eastern Kyiv damaged power supplies and killed two people including a pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry in Kyiv, and MSNBC military analyst Lieutenant General Steph Twitty.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Cal, this latest round of attacks looked devastating. What are you seeing on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, deadly as well. At least three people killed in the capital city today alone. Five yesterday. That brings the death toll to at least eight people dead in 24 hours, dozens others wounded. As you said the targets are these energy infrastructure specifically the power generators. You can see the city behind me tonight almost entirely blacked out for two reasons.\par \par \uc2 One, the fear of future airstrikes, two trying to conserve power. Energy officials here saying turn off your lights or start breaking out the candles. They are worried, and as you have alluded to, the winter months are just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 Now as for these drones and these rockets, we are seeing them continued to be fired in waves, that is waves of dozens of rockets and drones at once. It seems clear the intention is to overwhelm the air defense systems here in and around the capital. We can see them as they go off the air defense systems. It`s clear and you can hear the drones, and, again, it is sending them in batches as an attempt to hit these targets.\par \par \uc2 The other thing of note here, and we have continued to see this in what really is an eight-day campaign by Russia to drop as many ordnance as they can on these cities, we`re seeing them miss targets. We know what the energy infrastructure targets they`re trying to hit. They continuously miss these targets and hit civilian areas instead.\par \par \uc2 It`s one more indication of what you hear from officials here which is even if the Russian is in many ways inept or incompetent it doesn`t make them any less deadly, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cal, thank you as always for your reporting there.\par \par \uc2 General Twitty, this lack of power, water, obviously bringing misery, but also danger. And as Cal mentioned the winter months are approaching which could escalate in already desperate situation. So in your estimation how does Ukraine fight back against these relentless attacks and is the West doing enough to help?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY (RET), NBC NEWS MILITARY ANALYST: Yes, so, obviously this is a strange in strategy. The Russians have been unable to get it done on the ground. And so this change in strategy now is to terrorize and break the will of the Ukrainian people. That`s why he`s doing this. To break the will of the Ukrainian people. And so what we have to do now, first of all, the Ukrainians have stepped up to the plate with some pretty good air defense systems. They got the S-300, they got the RST system that came out of Germany.\par \par \uc2 And what we have to do from the West is provide them more systems and the Pentagon is working towards that. \uc2 NATO\uc2  is working towards that. It`s going to take time first to train the Ukrainians and then take time to get these systems in. And so that`s what the Ukrainians will need more advanced air defense systems and radars to be able to counter this significant threat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: General, the Russian draft, meantime, has been chaotic at best. Reports of little or even no training, lack of food, any semblance of organization, and now Moscow, the city, has become the first place to end the unpopular mobilization of troops. They say they have enough people already, but could it be a sign Putin is worried about the growing unrest among his people not just from the draft but, frankly, the number of Russian soldiers coming home in body bags?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, I would agree with you. But it`s also a sign of desperation. And so it is estimated that the Russians have lost somewhere around 80,000 soldiers. That`s either killed or wounded in action. And they`re having a hard time making their numbers, that 300,000 that you know that he mentioned. He`s having a hard time doing it. A mass exodus of males left the country, plus you have moms and dads, they are unwilling to sign their kids up, and so he`s going to have a hard time meeting these numbers.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 And that`s why he`s also upping his ante with all these attacks here because again he can`t get it done on the ground. He can`t get this recruitment (INAUDIBLE), and so he`s still in a bad place now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me ask you about is essentially the reverse of that with Ukraine, and the importance of what`s happening outside of what is clearly a determined Ukrainian army. The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that when the air raid sirens finally stopped on Monday morning many people came out of their underground shelters and simply went to work, and Zelenskyy said the enemy can attack our cities but they won`t be able to break us.\par \par \uc2 How much does the resolve of the Ukrainian people play into the success of the Ukrainian military?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: I got to tell you this is the most fascinating thing that I`ve seen. The will not only of the Ukrainian military but the will of the people. And that provides motivation for the soldiers out there. As you know the Ukrainians have a lot to fight for. First of all, they`re fighting for their lives but they`re also fighting for the Ukrainian way of life and their democracy. And so by having the civilian population come out and try to get to some type of daily normalcy is a good thing not only for the country but also for the soldiers to see because it provides them that continued will to fight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, so good to have you on the program. Thank you, sir. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Shocking revelations in the Oath Keepers trial. What the group`s leader was doing days before January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And Kevin Spacey takes the stand. What the actor had to say in his own defense is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:56]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today jurors heard new details from an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who has been investigating the right-wing Oath Keepers at the seditious conspiracy trial for five members of the militia group. The agent finished her third day on the stand by revealing more about the group`s alleged plans to stage an armed rebellion on Washington.\par \par \uc2 The January 6th plot was foiled before it could take place. When asked by a prosecutor whether the \uc2 FBI\uc2  must let a conspiracy play out before it disrupts it, the agent responded, I hope not.\par \par \uc2 For more let`s bring in NBC`s Ryan Reilly who is at the courthouse, Charles Coleman is a former prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 So, Ryan, a juror this morning was removed after testing positive for COVID but testimony continued. What more did jurors hear about today?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Reporter: So we`re hearing a lot more about what Stewart Rhodes was up to in the days up to the January 6th attack, in addition to sending texts to the legal counsel for the Oath Keepers, who he`s apparently in some sort of sexual relationship with. He also was buying a lot of weapons on his trip and prosecutors actually showed a map of all the stops that he made along the way to load up on all of these weapons as he was making his way to D.C. ahead of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 I think what`s really interesting that we`ve heard from this \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent recently is that there was sort of a dual purpose to a lot of what the Oath Keepers were up to. They were in fact providing security for a lot of these high-ranking Trump affiliated officials or people in sort of the conservative world ahead of January 6th. Of course we know well about the relationship with \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and we know that they were providing some security for, for example, Latinos for Trump, one of the organizations that is closely affiliated with the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 So there also might be some connections that have come under more scrutiny going forward here. But essentially the \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent was saying that you don`t have to exclusively -- this does not have to exclusively be a plot to attack the Capitol. They can have this dual purpose. They can also have been working security for various officials in addition to coming around this plot to oppose the peaceful transfer of power through force.\par \par \uc2 So I think what we`re looking for coming up this week is testimony from some of the cooperators in this case because at the moment the government is about 60 percent of the way through its presentation here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Charles, central to the prosecution`s case is this allegation that the Oath Keepers were assembling a quick reaction force, essentially an armed mob, located at a hotel across the river from D.C. Prosecutors alleged that this QRF was meant to occupy Washington, D.C. and prevent the transfer of power. The DOJ has been successful getting convictions for individuals of course who breached the Capitol, but how much more difficult is it to prove a conspiracy case like this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, Chris, the wider that you go, the harder that it gets. And I think that that`s the whole point of why prosecutors in this case are approaching it the way that they are in terms of laying out the overall plan, number one, so that they can then help people understand on the jury how everything that they are then presenting in terms of evidence and testimony fit into that actual plan.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of those things where you really have to sort of start at the broadest 30,000-foot view and then narrow in and zero in one by one, so as you can see where each and every one of the defendants actually played a role and played a part if you`re trying to convict all of them at the same trial. And so it is much more difficult but I think that the strategy that we`re seeing from the prosecution in this case is exactly on point. It`s what you`re supposed to do. It`s how you actually tie everyone into the central plot by explaining what the plan is and then explaining how each of them fit and played a role into the larger picture.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So let me ask you about one of the specifics. Under cross- examination by the defense and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent acknowledged that the group`s stash of weapons was stored in Virginia, that it was not brought to D.C. on January 6th, and so the defense lawyer asked. So the armed rebellion was unarmed and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent responded the armed rebellion wasn`t over.\par \par \uc2 In a conspiracy case, does the government need to prove that an illegal act was taken or only that it was planned?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, there are different standards and in a case like this given the charges they have to go beyond mere preparation. And so mere preparations are legal standards which basically said, oh, we had an idea of what it was but when you go beyond that and you show the actual intent of what it was to take more stops -- steps, rather, into creating something that fit the plan that you`re alleging was part of the conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Now you are getting to a big part of the actual act which is necessary in order to secure a conviction. The second piece to that of course is going to be intent. You establish the intent in a case like this by laying out the plan so that you can see exactly where they were going, exactly what they were trying to do and how they came together. And so with those two things, that`s how in a case like this, you know, the government will be able to establish a notion of conspiracy without necessarily --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So we obviously have lost Charles. I`m assuming that we still have Ryan.\par \par \uc2 And so, Ryan, I want to ask you about -- no, we don`t have Ryan either? Oh, we have Charles back. Go ahead, Charles. Finish what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I`m here. I was saying that essentially you need both of those pieces. You need to be able to explain the plan so that you can see clearly. And then you have to be able to identify what steps beyond mere preparation the defendants took that fit in accordance with that plan so you can ultimately look for that convictional conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Ryan, let me go back to you for a second. What`s the betting money on? Will Oath Keepers leader Stewart Rhodes take the stand?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: I think he will. That`s been his intent, and the fact he`s done a media interview which his lawyers, I don`t think, were very happy with during the course of the trial, so I do think that we`ll hear from him and will have an interesting defense essentially that he`ll be trying to make which is that -- essentially that he thought that he was -- like he was waiting on Trump to invoke the Insurrection Act and thought he was doing, everything that he was doing would be legal under his interpretation of the Insurrection Act.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Isn`t it generally believed, Charles, that the only way a defendant in a serious prosecution takes the stand is if you`re really desperate, you think you`re losing? But, ultimately, right, it`s their decision. They`re the ones whose future is on the line.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, I think your point is a very well taken one, Chris. Most of the time you take the stand as a defendant when you feel like I`ve got nothing left to lose. So you feel like you`re behind in the score card and you want to try to give yourself a puncher`s chance to try and turn things around by taking the stand and somehow connecting with the jury. But generally speaking, it`s going to be ill-advised.\par \par \uc2 You`re stepping into an arena that you`re not familiar with against trained professionals. These attorneys are really, really willing and ready to actually pick you apart. And so it`s usually not something that attorneys recommend doing. But in most cases, if you see it occur it`s because you have an overconfident defendant or someone who just know that this is their last ditch effort.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We will continue to watch it. Charles Coleman, Ryan Reilly, thank you both, gentlemen. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Sobbing on the witness stand, disgraced actor Kevin Spacey, denied sexual misconduct allegations against him and recounted what he called his terrifying upbringing as the son of a Neo-Nazi. Testifying in his own defense in a New York federal courtroom, Spacey denied actor Anthony Rapp`s allegations that he made a sexual pass at him when Rapp was as minor.\par \par \uc2 Breaking down several times, he told jurors about his hatred of bigotry and intolerance and it comes from complicated family dynamics. (INAUDIBLE) filed a lawsuit against Spacey in 2020. His lawyers are seeking $40 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, how third party candidates in crucial races could potentially shake things up in the midterms. And baggage fees. That infuriating and pricey add-on at the airport, now a major airline is paying the price. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:50]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now polls are showing that critical \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats and control of governor`s mansions could come down to just votes, a few votes, and making things even more complicated third-party candidates.\par \par \uc2 Here is NBC News congressional correspondent Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I), UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m not going to Washington if we prevail to be a boot licker for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Evan McMullin isn`t kidding himself.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I think party does still matter.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: He`s just hoping Utah voters can put it aside.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I`m a registered independent.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: At least for now.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I don`t care what your party affiliation is, I don`t care who you voted for in the last election. You know, if you are committed to our core ideals as a country.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: His long-shot bid against incumbent Trump-backed Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  is as much about Utah as it is about the future of the Republican Party. One McMullin used to call himself a part of.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: There`s no going home to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for true conservatives.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: In 2016 McMullin ran as an independent for that reason, and Lee, his now opponent, actually voted for him then. But times have changed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): Are you ready to stand with me and millions and millions of others who want four more years?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Unlike most third-party candidates, McMullin is on a one-on-one race. Democrats staying out of it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it`s important to look at the reality of the races that we`re running. A Democrat was not going to win the Utah Senate race.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It comes as 39 percent of Americans say they`re disillusioned with the two-party system that nevertheless still reigns supreme.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): In ruby red Utah, McMullin is in uphill climb. That`s not unusual for independent candidates running in a political landscape so often cast in red versus blue. And McMullin is not the only one trying to upend the political status quo in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Betsy Johnson is running as an independent for Oregon governor, a state that selected Democrats to the job for the last 32 years but is now rated a tossup in large part because of Johnson.\par \par \uc2 BETSY JOHNSON (I), OREGON GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I`m not a D, I`m not an R, I`m an O. I am responsible to and responsive to Oregonians.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: With a message that doesn`t stick to the party line.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I believe in a woman`s right to bear arms and I believe in a woman`s right to bear children when she chooses.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Which is, of course, the point.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Independents choose to do it the hard way. The two entrenched parties come with their own base of support, their own base of money.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But that`s also been enough to turn some people away. In Missouri conservative John Wood briefly entertained a bid for Senate to ensure controversial \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  didn`t win the seat. When Greitens lost the primary, Wood dropped out. And ousted GOP Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  also toying with an independent bid. This one for president.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I certainly will do whatever it takes to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t anywhere close to the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But in 2022 independents could cause big election surprises.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: This is a political realignment what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Even if they don`t stay outside the parties forever.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Is it a temporary realignment for you?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: It`s hard for me to look beyond November, to be honest. I don`t know sort of where I`ll be from a party affiliation perspective, you know, 10 years from now. I just have no idea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Our thanks to Ali Vitali for that report.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve ever felt frustrated over those baggage fees at the airport this next story just might bring a smile to your face and potentially add a little cash to your wallet. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  has now reached a settlement in a class action lawsuit over bogus baggage fees, agreeing to pay at least $7.5 million to customers. The settlement applies to passengers who were told their checked bags would be free or who had credit cards entitling them to free bag checks but were charged anyway.\par \par \uc2 So the settlement applies to those two sets of passengers who were incorrectly charged. They have to file claims to get a full refund. But American will have to pay up even if total claims top that $7.5 million.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. And I`m sticking around for "KATY TUR REPORTS" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ellison Barber, Dasha Burns, Cornell Belcher, Ali  Vitali, Kelly O`Donnell, Cal Perry, Ken Dilanian, Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 High-stakes political drama unfolding today in two of the closest Senate races in the country, with two embattled candidates trying to keep their shot at victory from slipping away. In Georgia, Republican nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is tripling down maybe quadrupling down on his denials about an ex- girlfriend`s abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Flat-out denial --\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC NEWS ANCHOR: Possible it happens and you don`t --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No. Flat out they`re all lie, a lie, lie, lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in Pennsylvania, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  giving his first in- person interviews since suffering a stroke to NBC. He claims he`s good to go, but he won`t release his medical records to prove it. We`ll have live reports from both states to show you that interview and ask Pennsylvania Democrat Madeleine Dean whether she thinks voters deserve more. Plus, will there be a smoking gun waiting at the end of the January 6 investigation on Capitol Hill? The answer, just 24 hours away. And finally, it turns out intentionally slamming a $330 million spacecraft into the side of an asteroid is a good thing. How NASA`s grand experiment that could one day save the planet, one from science fiction to science fact.\par \par \uc2 But we start with politics, a new dramatic twist in two critical races, Georgia and Pennsylvania. For Fetterman, national Democrats assumed he benefits from a weaker candidate in Mehmet Oz. But the last two months have seen Oz cut Fetterman`s polling lead in half. And new questions about Fetterman`s health have put the candidate squarely on defense.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was expected to embody the conservative values of the Republican base and, of course, he has Donald Trump`s endorsement but polls show \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  leading, the repeated allegations against Walker undermining his support among women. And now the race is so close. It is very possible neither candidate gets 50 percent in November, and that would force a runoff.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ellison Barber is in Atlanta, Dasha Burns in Pittsburgh, and Cornell Belcher is the Democratic strategist, a pollster, an MSNBC political analyst, and the founder of Brilliant Corners Research. So, Ellison, despite all the allegations, the race in Georgia is still essentially a dead heat. Have you been able to assess on the ground what kind of impact the allegations have had on Walker`s candidacy?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean look, this is dominating headlines here. People are paying attention to it. We`re starting to see all those slight some movement in the polls. You have two new polls out today and in both of them, Warnock is slightly ahead of Walker, and one of them, a Quinnipiac poll, Warnock is actually above that 50 percent threshold.\par \par \uc2 That being said, this is still a really tight race. And while you`ve seen some -- very few, but some Republicans at the state level talk about concerns they have in regards to Walker`s candidacy, we`re seeing national Republicans really rally behind Walker. And in terms of the Republican base here, most seem to still be in Herschel Walker`s corner. He did give his most extensive denial of those allegations that he previously paid for an abortion for the mother of one of his children in an ABC interview that aired last night. Listen to some of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: But at this point, you now know who she is.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Did you ever have a conversation with this woman at any time about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Did you ever, to your knowledge give money to pay for the cost of an abortion?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, she`s lying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: People are going to be paying very close attention to the debate here on Friday. One other thing worth noting today, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is the third outlet to now report that they have seen a copy of the $700 check Walker sent this woman in the days after she reportedly had an abortion procedure. NBC News has not independently verified that check, but the details in the new report from \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , Chris, they match details in reports we`ve seen from The Daily Beast and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And she has -- she also has that receipt from the clinic according to those other sources which we have not confirmed.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you, Ellison. We appreciate that. So, Cornell, we don`t know why because they didn`t ask the question in the poll about the abortion controversy. But Walker is losing ground with women. There is no doubt about that. But the two candidates are still basically tied. So, what do you take away from those two facts?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: What I take away from the -- take away from it that, look, it is a Georgia is a toss-up state. And we -- and by the way, thank you for pointing out in that poll that a 48, 46.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s not really a lead. And sometimes we, in the -- in media, we say well, he leads 48, 46 and for average voters, that means that Warnock is winning, and he`s going to win the race. And that`s true -- that`s not true. It`s a toss-up. That race can go in any direction.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, this was a state that what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got 49.2 percent of the vote in. I suspect that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to get the -- he`s going to get darn near every one of those Trump voters because if it didn`t disqualify \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , why should disqualify \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? So, in the end, this is going to be a tight race. What is interesting about Georgia is if you look at Warnock`s performance the last time around, you know, Warnock won women by roughly eight points over Loeffler in the -- in the last election.\par \par \uc2 The question for Republicans in that state is, is Warnock doesn`t have a chance to get ahead of -- I mean Herschel doesn`t have a chance of getting ahead of Warnock if, in fact, Warnock holds on to that gender gap, if in fact, women voters -- and it looks like in this poll that women voters are still plus eight points for Warnock. So, the question becomes with these -- are these allegations and these -- and by the way, but even before the abortion stuff, allegations about abuse. With all these allegations, how do Republicans in Georgia shrink that gender gap? How do they shrink Warnock`s advantage among women voters, as opposed to -- opposed to a growing? I got a feeling that on the Election Day --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, what about the reverse of that question, Cornell, which is how to -- how do Democrats take advantage of it? Because we know the Republicans are pretty much all in. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  just said I think we`re going to stick with Walker, I think they`re going to hang in there and scrap to the finish. So what do the Democrats need to do?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think that -- I think the Democrats are doing what they need to do and that is actually put his record out there, right? He has a horrible -- horrendous record on women`s issues and all of this are proof points to that so it`s disqualifying to the majority of women voters. And again, I think if Warnock pulls this out, which I think he`s going to pull it out, it`s going to be because quite frankly, that gender gap that we saw developing in 2018, and 2020 is going to continue to grow, not shrink in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I look at -- I look at Warnock and I -- and I -- and I see no way that they`re going to shrink that gender -- that eight-point gap down to three or four points which they needed in order for them to win that state. Democrats as they`re doing in Georgia, they`re doing all across the country, continue to drive these women`s issues, continue to drive the gender gap, and then make the electorate look more like 2018 than it did in 2010.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. Dasha, you got the first in-person interview with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  since his stroke, what were your impressions, and how do you think that all is going to affect his candidacy?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, Chris, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has said time and again, I`m not your typical politician and this was not your typical political interview. I want to set up with what you`re about to see, as I played this portion of the interview for you here, we agreed to use closed captioning during the interview. This is an accommodation that the Fetterman campaign has been open about. He has lingering side effects from the stroke, including auditory processing issues, which essentially means that he has a hard time understanding what he`s hearing sometimes. And so, he uses closed captioning to address that issue. Meaning he`s sort of reading our questions, as we`re asking them. It`s a live transcription there.\par \par \uc2 Now, experts say that this is something that is a common symptom of strokes, it`s -- doesn`t mean that he`s cognitively impaired, meaning his decision-making abilities, his problem-solving abilities, his memory that`s not necessarily affected, and he can fully recover from this. But what every medical expert we`ve talked to has said the caveat they`ve given is that they cannot fully assess a patient without all of the information. And we`ve asked time and again for medical records, we`ve asked to have a conversation with his doctors and I asked in this interview, why he has not revealed that information to reporters. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Don`t voters deserve to know your status now?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE NOMINEE, PENNSYLVANIA: Being on in front of thousands and thousands of people and having interviews and getting around all across Pennsylvania, that gives everybody and the voters decide you know if they think that it`s really the issue.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But the voters aren`t doctors, right? I mean, look, you have styled yourself as the noble candidate, right? It`s why a lot of people are drawn to you. Sometimes that`s easy and sometimes like in moments like this that gets tested, so why not follow through on that and be fully transparent and release all of that information?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Oh, and I said I am putting everything tested. I am being out and being tested day in and day out. And you know if my doctor teams already said that I`m fine and I`m ready to go on that, then I`m not really sure that`s much more beyond transparent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And, Chris, Fetterman is set to debate his Republican rival, Dr. Mehmet Oz on October 25. The voters that I`ve been talking to, I`ve met some folks who are still undecided, they say they are very much waiting to watch that before they make their decision on who they`re going to cast the ballot for, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dasha Burns, great interview. Thank you for that. Cornell Belcher, always good to see you. I want to bring in Pennsylvania Democratic Congresswoman Madeleine Dean. It`s good to have you here. Is it fair for a voter to say yes, maybe I have -- maybe I have a chance to see him on the campaign trail, or I saw the interview that Dasha Burns did, but I want to know what his doctors are saying now before I go to the ballot. Should he just put everything out there right now?\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA): I don`t have an answer for that. But what I will say is I`ve spent time with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and his wife over the course of this campaign and previously, I also was in touch with him after the stroke. He was recently in Montgomery County at our community college in front of nearly 3000 beaming, cheering crowds, a lot of Fetterwomen T- shirts in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 He did phenomenally well. He was funny. He was honest, as he was in that interview, about sometimes smooshing his words together. I have to admit I do that as well. So I have to tell you, just from my personal conversations with him from his work with that entire crowd, and then with a smaller crowd, he was funny, he was capable, and I don`t know what doctor`s reports would reveal today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think -- you know what it feels like to be in the halls of Capitol Hill. Do you believe that currently he can function in that capacity?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Oh, I have every confidence. The other thing that has to come --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think it will hurt him though with some voters?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: It may, but I think what actually helps him is values. What does he stands for and what does the snake oil salesman, Mehmet Oz stand for? He stands for selling you supplements and lying to you. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  believes in the values of, for example, protecting a woman`s right to choose and privacy. Mehmet Oz thinks abortion should be illegal, would criminalize it. Just take a look at their values. Mehmet Oz, election denier, hugs up to Trump. What does \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  care about? Protecting our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Those two are probably the most important issues that really face voters this election cycle. So would you actually let the snake oil salesman who`s mocking \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for having had a stroke, a medical doctor allegedly mocking the man for having had a stroke, be the person to sit? Do you think that`s somebody capable of leading us in the Senate? I certainly know that my suburban Philadelphia voters say no way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Big picture. You`ve talked about the fact that you`re excited about the political talent that is in your party, Fetterman, and others. And you could be somebody who could go into a higher profile role. There are a lot of people who are talking about the fact. They`re concerned about Joe Biden`s age. They`re concerned about \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , people who have been serving for decades and decades and decades. Is it time for fresh blood?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Fresh blood is already coming in. You know that I can --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In leadership.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Yes. And it`s already coming in, in leadership. You know, I came in in `18, sworn in `19, and have had the chance to work with our talented top three, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , and whip Clyburn. But then you know that we also have Katherine Clark. We -- this is generationally different. Katherine Clark, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Pete Aguilar.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is it time for them to replace the Steny Hoyer`s, the Nancy Pelosi`s?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: I actually look forward to number one getting through this election and making sure we hold the House. And to your original point on this, bring in these talented people. It excites me like 2018 did. We brought in such diverse folks who said I want to step up. I wasn`t thinking about politics at all. I want to step up. Women, diversity of all types with great careers in their past, and they are a part of our caucus. This election cycle gives us a second chance at that same kind of wave of talent that will begin to fill in and move up in our leadership.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you quickly about something we know according to the polls. Democrats are very interested in the issues of democracy and their rights being taken away and presumably quite a few independents and rural swing voters as well. Big day tomorrow, you know, all the folks, you`ve been an impeachment manager, you know, the pressure. People are looking for a blockbuster final hearing. Are they going to deliver it?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, I would not be sort of a movie critic or a blockbuster predictor. But I can say that our history tells us they`ve done an extraordinary job. I could not be prouder of this bipartisan 1/6 committee, headed by \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and Benny Thompson. Of course, my good friend, all of the folks on there are my friends.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But should we expect something that people would say, OK, that was worthy of wrapping up this whole thing?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DEAN: I`m confident of it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Really?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: And here`s what will be revealed at a minimum that I know of. The culpability of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the January 6 insurrection will be cemented in our history books forever. If he had only accepted electoral defeat, none of this would have happened. Think about that. But the other thing I know that they will emphasize is that 1/6 was not a single day in history. The precious nature and the precarious nature of our democracy is still in the pangs, in the balance with all of these election deniers on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Madeleine Dean, thank you so much. We appreciate you coming in today.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Appreciate it. This just in, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been ordered to sit for a deposition next week in a high-profile defamation lawsuit, according to the Associated Press. Writer E. Jean Carroll says Trump raped her in the dressing room of an upscale Manhattan department store in the mid-1990s. Trump has repeatedly denied that claim, and Carroll claims his denial of the allegation damaged her reputation. Trump`s legal team has tried to delay the lawsuit and prevent him from being questioned, but U.S. District Judge Lewis Kaplan decided it`s time to move forward writing "the defendant should not be permitted to run the clock out on plaintiffs attempt to gain a remedy for what allegedly was a serious wrong."\par \par \uc2 And President Biden will soon arrive in Colorado, starting a west coast swing that will take him to LA later today, where he has now weighed in on a controversy over city hall mired in scandal. We`ve got more on that next. Plus a new hearing with new evidence, what we can expect to hear from the January 6 committee on Capitol Hill just 24 hours from now. And in historic first from \uc2 NASA\uc2  that could one day, well, save the world. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re getting new details on what we could hear from the January 6 committee tomorrow, and it can be summarized in a single name, Trump. Axios reports today that the committee wants to "minimize ancillary players becoming a sideshow and keep the focus on evidence tying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the attack." Earlier today on this network, a former police officer who was injured at the Capitol reminded us what Trump did or rather did not do that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FANONE\uc2 , FORMER DC METROPOLITAN DC OFFICER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sat for 187 minutes and didn`t do (INAUDIBLE) while hundreds of police officers were fighting for their lives on the Capitol steps. And we now know that he was watching intently throughout that entire period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ali Vitali is with us on Capitol Hill. Nicholas Wu, congressional reporter for Politico joins us as well. Ali, I don`t know if you had a chance to hear Madeleine Dean, but she just indicated and I know she has been talking to committee members, she feels awfully confident about what they`re going to be able to deliver tomorrow. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: And look, Congresswoman Dean is someone who`s been at the back of most of these hearings because she is someone who was in the house gallery on January 6. She and I have talked extensively about her experience. And frankly, for Dean and other members of people in this capital community, the accountability factor that having this aired in the public eye and presenting the public narrative, that`s the key point of this committee.\par \par \uc2 Now the committee members would say that their responsibility here goes a step further and ultimately making a final report that will come at some point before the end of the year. But here we do expect them to tease out new details, new video, things like documentary footage of \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  talking about how even before we were in the month of November of 2020, how he thought that the former President Trump should no matter what say that he won regardless of what the votes actually looked like. And we know that that`s what Trump ultimately endeavored to do.\par \par \uc2 So the focus will inevitably be on Trump, Chris, you`re right. But there were key players in his orbit who can speak to what the committee has tried to do all along, which is establishing his mindset trying to show what the former president knew and what he didn`t do on January 6. There`s officer Fanone points out there too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nicholas, I think one of the most interesting interviews people are waiting to hear about is Ginni Thomas, right, the conservative activist, and wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . That interview was not videotaped, but how likely are we to get some insights into what she had to say to the committee tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS WU, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, POLITICO: Oh, kind of remains to be seen exactly how they`ll deploy Ginni Thomas`s testimony. We`ve seen with past interviews the committee had, where they weren`t necessarily recorded, you`ll have a staff member or a committee attorney reading up part of the interview. But at the same time, committee members that I`ve talked to didn`t really seem to think that they actually gathered that many new leads or new information from Ginni Thomas.\par \par \uc2 The chairman, \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , told us recently that -- you know that what she told them was fairly typical, actually, of what a lot of people who came to the Capitol on January 6 or were involved in election denialism on January 6 thought, right, that being that they thought that there were issues with the election, and that motivated them in some way to you know, join in something else as we saw that she did, and trying to message officials to try to contest the election results.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, all of this, obviously, Nicholas, could be an important motivator in getting Democrats to the polls because we`ve seen polls that show that there is a lot of concern that they see Trump Republicans as a threat to a whole range of their rights. But you wrote earlier this week about showing up in ads and that it`s really not. Is that -- is that because candidates don`t think it`s going to move the needle with undecided, what`s happening in terms of Democrats and them actually putting money into this message?\par \par \uc2 WU: Democrats I talked to saw a lot of the former president`s legal concerns that being things related to Mar-a-Lago, the New York Attorney General investigation, Fulton County in that kind of issue, as really being less of something that played well in swing districts and more of something that was an appeal for the base. It was something that would help them raise money. The difference, though, is that January 6, actually is something that we see show up on the trail in very candidate-specific cases.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen Democrats take out ads, for example, against Republicans that were at the Capitol on January 6. And just now I was watching a debate in Virginia`s second congressional district. They are not too far from Washington DC, where Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is gotten a pretty tight reelection match against Jen Kiggans, a Republican.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Luria, is of course, on the January 6 committee and we saw in her closing argument, she actually tried to make her work part of her message to the constituent. She said that it would be one of the most important things that she`s done in her career as she was trying to prevent a clear and present danger to the country. And so this is where we`re more likely to see January 6 show up on the campaign trail in these individual races rather than in broader messaging.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Nicholas Wu, great to have both of you on the program. Thank you so much. And don`t forget to join MSNBC for special coverage of the next January 6 hearing. That`s tomorrow starting at noon, Eastern on MSNBC, also available on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And now to the escalating political controversy taking over Los Angeles right now, President Biden will be there later today after joining in with calls for the resignations of three city council members. Audio was released of them making racist and offensive remarks about their colleagues, constituents, and different LA communities. Councilman Mike Bonin, whose adopted black son was a target of hateful remarks, did not mince words about how painful this moment is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BONIN, LOS ANGELES CITY COUNCIL MEMBER: I take a lot of hits, and how I know I practically invite a bunch of them. But my son, man that makes my soul bleed, and it makes my temper burn. And I know I`m not alone because Los Angeles has spoken and feels the same way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC News Senior White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell. You saw the emotion there, that president decided he was going to weigh into this full throttle. What do we expect to hear from him today?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I have to kind of characterize this. The president has not spoken about this himself directly. It has been through his press secretary. And she has said that the president`s view is that these members of the council should resign, so we will be watching to see how -- when he is in California, how much he wants to step into this directly. Of course, this is very much in line with the president`s views on matters like this.\par \par \uc2 And often, as you know, so well, Chris, when it comes to subjects that would be at a local city council level, very often that would not rise to the president`s involvement but this is the kind of issue that transcends a lot of American life. And it is the sort of corrosive kind of issue that is part of what brought \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  into the run for the White House that has him in office today because it`s not that far off from the soul of America point of view that has animated a lot of his reason for wanting to be president at the time that he decided to enter the race back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, the president will be in California. We have not been given a lot of details about what his West Coast swing will include in terms of how he will be engaged with some of the Democratic candidates who were on the ballot out there. But Los Angeles has a mayoral race, so he`ll be a part of that. He`ll be doing fundraising and he`ll be doing some official events on the way as well. He`s currently flying with Air Force One, traveling west as we speak, but his spokesperson, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has made it clear that the president does have an opinion on this subject and feels very strongly about it, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kelly O`Donnell, always good to see you from the White House. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, all around the Western world, Ukraine, giving us an inside view into harrowing rescues, like this one in the wake of Russian airstrikes. What President Zelenskyy says he now needs to stop it from happening again and again and again? We`re live in war-torn Kyiv next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL 