this. The people of Kyiv are relying on the air defense systems, which is what they`re talking about. You look at the church behind me, right? The church behind me is usually brightly lit. It is stunningly beautiful. This is a city that is intentionally dark tonight because this is a city that is right now living under air raid sirens. We are under an air raid alert. We`re hearing the distant thud of at least air defense systems going to work. So, it is something that people live with here. Every day it is all the more real because of what happened on Monday. And as you can see this damage on the left side of your screen, we`ve been watching people not just sort of try to repair and get life back to normal but to survey the damage, because this city has not felt this way really since April, since the Russians withdrew from the suburbs of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And even then, we didn`t have air strikes like we do now. We spent the day watching children survey a playground that they played out up, their entire lives that was destroyed by these rockets. So, it has changed lives here. It is changed the way that people are going about their lives and of course the cost in blood has been very real. At least two dozen people were killed. More than 120 were wounded in just 48 hours across the country.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:17]\par \par \uc2 When you look at Zaporizhzhia, that gripping video that you played of that family coming out of that rubble, 70 people have died in just two weeks. So, these airstrikes are effective on infrastructure, and they are killing a great many people. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cal, thank you so much for your ongoing reporting. We appreciate it. So, Evelyn, it`s not just \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who`s warning about Putin and warning Putin, frankly, the head of British intelligence, its spy chief made a rare speech yesterday calling Putin`s judgment flawed and I want to play another key section of that speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FLEMING, U.K. SPY CHIEF: Russia`s forces exhausted, the use of prisoners as reinforcements and now the mobilization of 10s of 1000s of inexperienced conscripts speaks of a really desperate situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s what we are increasingly hearing that it`s increasingly desperate on the part of the Russians. Was he speaking as much to Putin and the Russians as to the west? And is the real message here, though, maybe we have an opening to shut this down. Let`s be united and do it. What did you hear in that?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN FARKAS, THE MCCAIN INSTITUTE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: I think that`s what I heard, Chris, that the Russians are weak, you know, they suffer from low morale, poor equipment, that`s insufficient and in need of repair. They can`t get some of those components for the more advanced weaponry from overseas because of the sanctions. They suffer from poor logistics, poor communications, they don`t have enough trained personnel, the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 So yes, the moment right now is to help the Ukrainians actually push the Russians out of their territory. And frankly, it`s long overdue for protecting civilians. As you and Cal pointed out, there have been 1000s of Ukrainians who have been killed and injured, innocent civilians, the Russians are not playing a clean war, if there`s ever a war that`s clean, which there isn`t. But they`re playing dirty, they`re breaking international law. And so, we need to really double down to force the military out and to protect the civilians.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So obviously, those are the goals, but you`re somebody who worked at defense, you know, this stuff, the asks from the Zelenskyy, can they help shorten the war, or end the war?\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: Well, they certainly can do all both things, Chris. Because the asks, you know, providing the -- he wants tanks, he wants artillery to take out Russian positions, all of those things will help push the Russians back and deaden the impact of their artillery, they will make the artillery neutral. So, the Russian -- so the Ukrainian forces can go forward. Everything he`s asking for will decrease the amount of time the Ukrainians have to fight in theory, if they can get it fast enough.\par \par \uc2 Putin, on the other hand, is counting on us being too slow and decisive not giving enough equipment, and he`s hoping to drag this out. So, it`s really in our interest to back Ukraine up and get as much as we can to them. The four air defense systems the Germans are providing that`s great. It`s not enough. They`re clearly more than four cities in Ukraine that need defending.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yeah, so Evelyn, the President also called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , a rational actor who miscalculated this war. Where do you think nuclear weapons factor into the equation? Because there`s a lot of folks who think the more he`s backed into a corner, the more likely he is to do something irrational, is Biden right to think he won`t go there?\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: I think, Chris, that President Biden`s correct that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a rational actor. Yes, he`s -- you know, he`s motivated by this Neo Imperial vision and to try to cement his place in history. But he`s rational. He`s not a crazy man. He is threatening the use of tactical nuclear weapons to try to intimidate, not so much the Ukrainians because I think he realizes he`s not going to intimidate them. But he`s trying to intimidate the west into halting or slowing our support to the Ukrainian government. It`s in the first instance about that, of course, you can`t rule out the possibility that he might use them because his doctrine does say that if the existence of the state is at stake, or if the military wants to try to defeat an adversary on the battlefield, they can use tactical nuclear weapons in the latter case, but he knows the next day as President Biden put it, the world will be very different. The international community will be up in arms and he likely won`t be able to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Evelyn Farkas, it`s always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The DOJ wants the Supreme Court to stay out of the Mar-a-Lago documents fight, the latest on that, and I`ll be talking to the author who argues it`s inevitable the Attorney General will indict former President Trump, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:44:05]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have breaking news, literally just in on the accused New York City subway shooter you`ll remember that 10 people were shot on the city subway, 13 others injured, while he was supposed to be in court today, Frank James. And he just didn`t want to go. So, when they told the judge that he was having none of it and said among other things in the courtroom. I am not tolerating that. I will tell him this is not a dress rehearsal. It`s real life. And I ordered that he be brought to the courtroom. So, the court is adjourned. They`re waiting for officials to go get him and make him leave where he is in lockup and show up in the courtroom. That could take a couple of hours. We`ll keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, DOJ has made its case to the Supreme Court to stay out of the fight over documents that were seized at Mar-a-Lago arguing, among other things, that Trump has no plausible claim of privilege or for return to the documents. And while that fight escalates, the central question remains will Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  take the unprecedented step of seeking an indictment against the former president?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 A provocative new piece in The Atlantic suggests it`s inevitable and could come sooner than you might think. For more I want to bring in NBC`s Ken Dilanian and the author of that article Franklin Foer, National Correspondent for The Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Ken, let`s start with the DOJ filing, how soon can we expect the Supreme Court to rule on Trump`s appeal? Give us the latest.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE & INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Chris, they could rule at any moment because the Trump appeal was sort of brought on an emergency basis. But the issue in contest here is a very narrow one. It`s the only question before the Supreme Court is whether those more than 100 classified documents seized at Mar-a-Lago should go before the special master. Because you remember, the appeals court decided that the judge was wrong to block the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from using those classified documents in its investigation. So, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has declassified documents, they`re using them in the investigation. And the Trump side is not contesting that part of it. They`re only saying that these documents should be reviewed by the Special Master.\par \par \uc2 But the Justice Department doesn`t want that to happen. And one of the reasons they`re citing is they`re saying that if they do that, that means they have to turn the documents over to Donald Trump`s lawyers. And they`re saying that these are some of the most highly classified documents in the government, very limited distribution, Special Access Programs. Trump`s lawyers, they say have no business with these documents. Neither does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the DOJ says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no plausible claim to ownership of these federal government documents that are highly classified. Now, the question is, of course, whether the Supreme Court even takes up this argument or whether they just decide not to take the case at all.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And of course, the big question, where is all this going? Frank, you spent months researching your story and moving sat down with the Attorney General, you acknowledge he didn`t tip his hand, his colleagues didn`t tip anything. So why do you believe an indictment is inevitable?\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN FOER, THE ATLANTIC NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, two things have happened. I think, one is that, the Attorney General of the United States, you sit in this position where you can see everything that`s happened to American democracy and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who is somebody who came into office as a very cautious consensus minded Institutionalists. I think that a lot of his faith in American institutions has begun to wobble as he`s seen this kind of grant assault on democracy. And I think that`s led to a bit of a shift within his own thinking where steps that he wouldn`t have wanted to take at the beginning of 2021 are steps that he`s now more willing to entertain.\par \par \uc2 But I think the second thing is, is that as the Mar-a-Lago case has filtered forward, you have Trump assaulting the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , accusing them of planting evidence, you have a case that unlike the January 6 investigation, which is a complicated one that`s much more black and white is very much about the rule of law, which is an animating principle for America. It`s the thing that he`s talked about, for all of his life. And so, the question of whether indict Trump over the Mar-a-Lago document of obstruction of justice, or whatever the charge is, then becomes a question about, does the rule of law apply to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? And I think in Merrick Garland`s mind, that won`t be a very, you know, it`d be hard to make that decision. But I think, as a matter of principle, as a matter of morality, it becomes much clearer.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as a reader, I think you made a strong case that given his personality, and given the person he`s been throughout his career, it may be the only choice he can really make. I guess my question is, is there such a person in this day and age who can ignore the political pressures, stay true to his job and stay true to who he is?\par \par \uc2 FOER: Well, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has taken so much heat, we`ve had this split screen where you have the January 6 investigation on Capitol Hill, which has presented a very clear narrative of what happened and Garland has taken so much heat because he hasn`t told the same vivid story you have. The January 6 Commission makes it sound so simple, and Garland who is somebody who is meticulous who is all about procedure, who`s kind of considers himself immune to political rage, has moved in this very slow, step by step meticulous process of investigating the events of January 6, where he started with the people who stepped foot in the Capitol, and then kind of worked his way up the chain.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, according to reports from the Grand Jury is getting closer and closer to Trump`s inner circle, therefore, closer to Trump himself. I think that it`s really important to manage this case in a way that is above politics. That`s not -- that doesn`t reckon any sort of way of political revenge. And even if it`s excruciating for some people, you, know the case if it`s brought needs to be brought in a way that sticks, that gets the desired result and that isn`t half baked, that doesn`t set aside scope of getting evidence. And so, I think that`s the virtue of the way that Garland approached these issues.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I can recommend this article enough whether or not people agree with your conclusion the way you think about this and report this is so fascinating. Franklin Foer, thank you. Ken Dilanian, always good to have you on the program as well.\par \par \uc2 FOER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It may sound like a Hollywood sci-fi movie, but it is Israel as it gets, \uc2 NASA\uc2  now calling its asteroid strike test, a major success. Why that could one day save all of us here on Earth, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If a life-threatening asteroid is ever hurtling toward Earth, it looks like \uc2 NASA\uc2  has this covered. The agency says it`s saved the world test was a massive success. Check it out, the dirt spacecrafts perspective as it barreled toward a harmless asteroid. And yes, knocked it off course. I want to bring in NBC News correspondent Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 Tom, all of us space nerds watch this happen in real time. And we pretty much knew it was the success right away and everybody was cheering. But was even \uc2 NASA\uc2  surprised by just how successful?\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: 100% yes. I mean, this thing was three times more successful than \uc2 NASA\uc2  had anticipated. They wanted to budget off course, and shorten the orbit around the bigger asteroid, right? So, the small asteroid was the target. A big asteroid right here is what it was orbiting, they wanted to budget off that orbit by 10 minutes, they did it by 32 minutes. So, with that they know that this strategy in theory works. They can bump an asteroid off course and then maybe they could use that strategy in the future to deflect an incoming asteroid headed for Earth. But as is always the case, size matters. And it depends on the size, right? This was a 525 foot in diameter asteroid. But by comparison, the one that wiped out life on earth at the time of the dinosaurs was six miles across. So, they`re going to need a bigger dart, if you will, a bigger rocket and a bigger spacecraft if in fact, God forbid we face that kind of an ominous asteroid headed our way.\par \par \uc2 But in theory, this worked. And so now hopefully, they`ll be able to use that technology and keep an eye on the skies, looking for any incoming asteroid as they have for quite a while now. And then that gives them time to prepare in the event one is coming our way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, I was feeling super confident and very relieved and then you just use three words that made me nervous, well, four. In theory, maybe and hopefully.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, what happens next? I mean, did they just wait around 