n the government on the subject of nuclear war.\par \par \uc2 In Biden`s words, "We have not faced the process of -- prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis." He added, Putin isn`t joking when he talks about potential use of tactical nuclear weapons or biological or chemical weapons. It`s not clear whether Biden was referencing any new intelligence in making this prediction, although we`re asking that of the White House right now.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, the assistant defense secretary said just three days ago, America`s nuclear posture hasn`t changed. But the situation on the ground inside Ukraine clearly has. After steadily losing territory over the first six months of the war Ukraine`s military has come roaring back reclaiming 200 square miles from the Russian army in just the past week. In fact, an American source told the London Telegraph this week, it`s now possible Ukraine could recapture Crimea as well.\par \par \uc2 In response, Putin has been making a series of increasingly aggressive moves to try to regain momentum. But so far that hasn`t worked. His decision to illegally annex Ukrainian territory has been internationally condemned, and his effort to call up more reservists has led to thousands of Russians fleeing the country rather than agreeing to fight. But with every piece of bad news for Putin, the chances he could do something desperate, arguably increase.\par \par \uc2 President Biden put it this way, "We are trying to figure out what is Putin`s off ramp. Where does he find a way out? Where does he find himself that he doesn`t only lose face but significant power?" I want to bring in NBC senior international correspondent Keir Simmons who is live in Moscow for us. So, Keir, that really is the million dollar question. How does this all end? What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: It really is. And look, honestly, I think there are no easy answers for the West or most importantly, I guess, for President Putin here in Moscow. It is three- dimensional chess. And it is three-dimensional chess, because in the end President Putin`s ambitions in Ukraine are not being met as you set out, they are apparently in reverse. And that puts him in a very, very difficult position now.\par \par \uc2 He`s doubled down, hasn`t he? He has drafted. They`re hoping for 300,000 Russian men to go to Ukraine. But that, of course, increases the pressure on him because that means Russian families now have sons and husbands and fathers who are being asked to go into this very challenging conflict for Russia. Now, look, experts here tell us that Russia`s nuclear doctrine limits their ability to use nuclear weapons or a nuclear missile very much in terms of the existential threat to Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s important to be borne in mind. And again, there are no easy options. Even a low-grade nuclear missile fired by President Putin would risk a huge nuclear escalation that couldn`t be necessarily controlled. Clearly, the neighboring countries, including Turkey, for example, would be just dismayed. What would India or China have to say about it? So, there is -- we don`t know what happens next, Chris.\par \par \uc2 That is the honest answer. And I think that what you`re hearing from President Biden there was a real concern about that.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ve been out on the streets talking to people here in Moscow and Russians like Americans like anyone around the world of course worried about the idea of a -- of a nuclear escalation. It frightens everyone. It could be that what the Kremlin is doing and continue to do is to try and use that fear to try and gain some advantage to try and get Washington to put pressure on Ukraine to hold back.\par \par \uc2 But many suggests that in that respect, the Russians simply just don`t -- simply don`t understand the West. They don`t understand what the U.S. thinking.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Keir Simmons, we appreciate you being there in Moscow for us. Thank you. And joining me on set, NBC White House correspondent Mike Memoli. And Peter Baker, chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , also former Moscow bureau chief for The Times and an MSNBC political contributor. He`s also co-author of the new book, The Divider: Trump in the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, the question everybody immediately had, I got this information before 11:00 last night, disquieting to say the least. Why was the President saying this and why now? We`re getting some guidance now just in the last few minutes, right?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS. WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, you laid this out, we`re at this unique moment where good news is potentially dangerous news. The better Ukraine is doing on the battlefield the fear is that the more provocative that Russia could get. We just got an important indication from the White House in the last few minutes. \uc2 Karine Jean- Pierre\uc2 , the White House press secretary saying the president`s comments were not based on any new intelligence that he`s received, that there has been no change to our nuclear posture.\par \par \uc2 Other White House officials are saying what the president said last night at this fundraiser here in New York really, certainly raised some eyebrows, but the substance of what he was talking about really mirrored what he`s been saying publicly, including at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The framing, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The framing of it.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: And Chris, I have to say, when I read these comments had the same reaction you did. I immediately went back to 2008. One of my first experiences covering \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at a fundraiser in similar setting. He talked about his running mate at the time, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , if he was elected world leaders are going to test him with an international crisis. We saw in the 2019 camp -- in the early stages of his 2020 campaign, what got him in trouble early on?\par \par \uc2 Talking about a fundraiser about his work with segregationist settings. A senator. So, this setting, being at a fundraiser, camera`s not rolling, but reporters in the room, we`ve found that the president is much more forthcoming and perhaps more than he should be. And so, I think that`s context is important in terms of explaining perhaps why the president was so eyebrow-raising last night.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Peter, what do you see as the potential context here? And from your experience on the ground in Russia, how do you think it`s been received there?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, look, it`s a chilling thing to say obviously as Mike says, the president probably be a little bit more forthcoming than he means to be in public. He didn`t intend probably to scare us the way he did, perhaps, but look, what the American White House officials will tell you, administration officials would tell you is they have not seen intelligence suggesting that Russia is preparing to do this.\par \par \uc2 For all the bluster and rhetoric that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is issuing, there is any kind of concrete move to begin to use weapons or set up to use these kinds of weapons that they have detected. That doesn`t mean you take -- don`t take it seriously. I mean, I talked to a White House official who says something like, you know, on a scale of one to 10, you know, he put around a four. Well, four is pretty damn chilling excuse, my language, for -- when you talk about nuclear weapons use.\par \par \uc2 But they believe mostly that this is Putin threatening in order to get an advantage at time when he`s at his weakest. He is -- he uses the nuclear saber when he is at his weakest and I think that`s what`s happening now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But we know what this administration and every other administration should be doing which is prepare for the worst, hope for the best, prepare for the worst right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What preparations? What conversations are going on right now, as we watch what`s happening on the ground in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes. No, they`re definitely having preparations for all kinds of contingencies. What would they do if, right? And look, I mean, from a military point of view, it makes no sense to use a small tactical nuclear weapon because it`s not going to accomplish anything other than spread terror. And it would, in fact, isolate Russia even further, the few -- cut friends that`s got left like China and India would clearly, you know, react badly to this.\par \par \uc2 So, it makes no sense for him to do it. What he`s trying to do, we think, is to frighten his own people and to justify what he`s doing in Ukraine by making it about the West threatening us. Us being Russia. And I think that`s the theory. But the problem is, he`s not a rational actor, because rationally you wouldn`t have invaded Ukraine in the first place. You have to assume that it`s possible that he does something that we consider to be, you know, erratic and nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike Memoli, it`s so good to have you here. Peter Baker, you`re going to stick around for a little while. We have more we want to talk about with you. But I want to bring in retired Army Lieutenant General Steph Twitty. Former deputy commander of U.S.-European command and an MSNBC military analyst. If I can pick up on what Peter was just talking about, there is a difference between atomic weapons which obviously are larger.\par \par \uc2 And nuclear weapons which -- tactical nuclear weapons which tend to be smaller. It appears U.S. officials are worried more about Putin using tactical weapons. But last night again, Biden said I don`t think there`s any such thing as the ability to easily use a tactical nuclear weapon and not end up with Armageddon. What`s your read on this from a military standpoint?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:03]\par \par \uc2 STEPH TWITTY, MSNBC MILITARY ANALYST: Well, I`ll tell you. I think the president is correct when we talk about a tactical nuclear weapon we`re talking about one kiloton nuclear weapons somewhere in that neighborhood. We call it low yield. That type of weapon to take out easily a city, entire blocks and can take out installation. So it can have a significant impact. And oh, by the way, when it takes something out, it continues to linger and it could go elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 It could go beyond borders in other words and impact others. Keep in mind if he uses the weapon inside Ukraine, particularly in the four oblast that we`re talking about, there are Russian-speaking folks within those oblast and there are Russian soldiers within those oblast. So, they make a decision to use a nuclear weapons, that would mean that he -- he`s interested in killing his own people, not just Ukrainian soldiers or Ukrainian people as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How do you assess the situation on the ground right now, General?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: I assess in a couple of ways. Number one, the reason why you`re hearing all this saber rattling is because Putin is out of options, none of his strategic objectives since he started this war almost eight months ago, he`s been able to conquer, number one. Number two, his soldiers have performed extremely poor on the battlefield. And number three, he`s now receiving a lot of pressure from home based on the performance of this war.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s back into the wall. Ukrainians like they have great momentum. And it looks like they`re knocking on the door of Kherson. They`re in the south now. And they`ve made tremendous gains in the Donbas. And so you heard talk to them headed towards Crimea, I do believe that they have a momentum to potentially take Crimea.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Really?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: And to win this war.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Really? Hold on, General. I mean, you and I were at the Obama White House when all of that was unfolding. When I read that, the first time I read it was in this London Telegraph article today that said that there is an increasing belief that that could happen.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It kind of boggles the mind, given where everybody thought we would be now eight months ago.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It does boggle my mind because the Russians are pretty entrenched there. They`ve had eight years to dig into Crimea to make it their own territory. And they have certain degree of support there among at least some of the people -- the Russian-speaking people living there. So to be able to actually overturn that rule eight years later, it would be an extraordinary achievement. It also may indicate a certain cockiness on the part of the Ukrainians, but they`re doing pretty well, right?\par \par \uc2 And they`re wondering whether their momentum can take them all the way. It may be getting ahead of things to think that they could do that. But that`s a rather extraordinary moment. They`re even thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, I love the use of the word cockiness because you never want to get ahead of yourself, although you can understand, General, why the Ukrainians are feeling that, wow, we really have this in control. But what are the things that might -- that you might say to them to say, hold on for a second. Or do you think that that`s really a reasonable expectation, given what we`re seeing on the ground right now?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Well, there`s a lot more fighting to be had before we even think about Crimea. If you take a look at the train right now about 18 percent the Russian still hold on to. So, there`s a lot more fighting in the Donbas that needs to occur both in Luhansk and Donetsk. And we haven`t -- they haven`t cease Kherson yet. And so, when we start talking Crimea, we`ll probably talk about long term down the road because we want to make sure that the momentum continues in the Donbas and down in the south. Then you set yourself up for further attacks down into Crimea region.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lieutenant General Twitty, it`s always great to have you on the program. Thank you so much. Peter is staying with us.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead this hour, President Biden set to speak on the economy in the wake of that new jobs report that has sent the markets plunging. We`re going to try to figure all that out.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Biden`s pardoned for federal marijuana offenders. How civil rights groups are reacting and where reform might go from here? Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  will join me.\par \par \uc2 And how Republicans are trying to shift topics away from the numerous candidate controversies with the midterms closing in. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:18:37]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: President Trump`s super PAC is buying in putting cash into the midterm ad wars in two hotly contested races in Ohio and Pennsylvania. The first round of what`s expected to be millions flowing into elections across the country by November. It comes at a time when the GOP playbook is laser focused. Inflation, inflation and inflation. Here`s a few Republican candidates from the debate stage just last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Inflation is number one, on the issues that I hear from my constituents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When people across the state consider where we are at today versus when \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  started. There is nothing that`s any better. Our property taxes, our crime, inflation, our schools, things are a disaster.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS\uc2 , REPUBLICAN NOMINEE FOR SENATE IN ARIZONA: The greatest threat to seniors retirement today is the massive crushing inflation that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Mark Kelly caused.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That last candidate there is Republican \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  running against Democratic Senator Mark Kelly in Arizona. He has said he would have objected to certifying the 2020 election. In a vote that happened the day of the attack on the Capitol. I want to bring in NBC Vaughn Hillyard who was covering that race for us in Phoenix. Vaughn, we`ve seen this playbook across the country.\par \par \uc2 Republicans trying to hammer home the economy, move away from their own controversies. What are you hearing about that strategy and whether frankly it`s working for Masters there?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:03]\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right, Chris. We were at that debate between \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  and Mark Kelly last night here and he hammered home for almost the first 20 minutes. The message around the economy and inflation and going toe to toe with Mark Kelly and using actually Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  as a crutch, saying at least one of the senators here in Arizona shot back and fought back against Build Back Better, saying that Mark Kelly was a stamp of approval for anything that the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration wanted to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 And using the policies of the Biden administration and the Democratic senate majority of the last two years placing blame on those policies for the rise of inflation that is hitting here Arizonans. I want to let you hear though from a voter Dustin Ruge who I just talked to a few moments ago. We`re in Scottsdale where some of those more moderate conservative voters will be key to the likes of Mark Kelly and democratic \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in the governor`s race being able to pull off winds here. Take a listen to Dustin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUSTIN RUGE, ARIZONA VOTER: The problem with Arizona is I don`t think there`s a lot of support here for Trump because most people who are very independent minded. In fact, the largest registration block in Arizona is actually independents. So, they`re not Democrats or Republicans. So -- and I think they -- that because of that I think it created some hardness for them to come back to the center on issues.\par \par \uc2 But the good news, at least with Mark Kelly is he`s so liberal anyways, it`s, you know, it`s a very easy choice between the two.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And he said that he`s voting for Kari Lake for governor as well. He referred to himself as a businessman. But it`s those types of voters that Democrats are going to need to flip a significant number of. Folks that are not inclined to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or candidates that are aligned with him. But that is where you heard \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  hammer home the economy as well as immigration there last night in this debate.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the polling here that has come out, not just here in Arizona, but across the country here in recent days, you continually see across the board, the likes of the economy being at the top of voters minds. However, at the same time, I want to underscore when you look a little deeper into those numbers it`s the likes of abortion and as well as the 2020 election and its legitimacy that Democrats have named as their most important two issues here.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot at play between the turnout but then also those potential swing voters like Dustin and where they lie in these races here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Such a fascinating set of races. Thank you so much for that, Vaughn. And just this morning, we learned the nation`s unemployment rate dropped again. But according to some experts that good news could lead to bad news. The U.S. added 263,000 jobs in September, the unemployment rate falling to 3-1/2 percent. But as one analyst put it to CNBC, this puts the nail in the coffin for another three quarter point interest rate hike in November.\par \par \uc2 The Dow initially fell hundreds of points this morning and take a look at where we`re standing right now. Down 564 points. I want to bring in NBC News business reporter Brian Cheung and Peter Baker is back with us. So, that`s the big question here, right, Brian? Are we going to get another interest rate hike? Help us understand how all this plays into each other.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA REPORTER: Well, market pricing after the jobs report came out this morning at 8:30 does imply that the feds going to go with that larger, as you mentioned three quarter percentage point of an increase in their next meeting in the first week of November. What that tells us is that borrowing costs are going to continue to get more expensive for American households and also for businesses.\par \par \uc2 And the business side of that is very interesting with respect to the labor market because if businesses don`t want to take a bank loan out to build a second location and then hire more people, they might take job postings down or even maybe fire some people. Now, the labor market doesn`t look like that right now with the unemployment rate at 3.5 percent. That`s the lowest that we`ve seen in over 50 years. But that picture could change as the Fed continues to hike interest rates.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Peter, we just heard this from Vaughn. And it might become the GOP messaging for the midterm. The GOP faces its own set of risks come November. Obviously, for the Democrats, it`s inflation. But according to a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  analysis 299 candidates or 53 percent of Republicans running are election deniers.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you talk about in your book which I`ve finally finished, but it`s great.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How this party has been reborn in Trump`s image? You wrote this. "Trump is already 76 years old but there is a whole new generation of would-be Trump`s waiting to succeed him whether \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  or \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 . There will be no return to the pre-Trump era of American politics." How does that play out in this election that we`re looking at?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, exactly what you just said, right? And there was -- it has become the required position to take that you disagree with the 2020 election that you think it was somehow messed up fraudulent or whatever, even though there`s no evidence of that whatsoever because if not, you will get on the bad side of the man at Mar-a-Lago and nobody can afford to do that.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, I don`t think they can, the Republican Party right now. So that has shifted things, that has made things in fact a very strong cult of, you know, kind of personality at this point.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:09]\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It`s all about whether you support Trump or not. It`s not about particular positions or issues. It`s all about the man at the top.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Guys, thank you. We`re going to go to Hagerstown, Maryland. The President of the United States \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has just begun to speak.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The reason I`m the most pro- union president in America`s history is because you`re the single best workers in the world, not a joke. You know, a lot of people think that you just show up and you got a job. How about those three, four or five years sometimes in friend`s work, that you`re going to --get in full -- you know, it`s like going back to school.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`ve seen happen now is they`re figuring out, everybody`s figuring out that the supply chain and only on-time purchases is a big problem. Now we`re figuring it out. If it`s made in America, we`re going to invent it in America. It`s made in America. And so, look, I want to thank you, Sam, for that introduction. Thank you, Mayor Keller for welcoming us to your city and for getting your two kids, you got to get dressed on a Friday to come and see me.\par \par \uc2 I don`t -- I hope they`ll forgive me for that. And it`s great to be with Congressman David Trone, who`s always, always, always working for the working people in this district, on mental health, on addiction, in support of our veterans and modernizing infrastructure like expanding I-81 which is going to be expanded because of him. I want to commend terrific leaders who couldn`t be here today.\par \par \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  who leads the Democrats in House and senators Cardin and \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  who are close and dear friends. And look, I want to thank \uc2 John Edwards\uc2  of Maryland`s State Federation of Labor and all the proud members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  here today. This is National Manufacturing Day and just starting to mean something again, National Manufacturing Day, when we celebrate workers were the backbone of the economy of this country, not a joke.\par \par \uc2 Where`s it written? That it says America can`t be the leading manufacturer in the world again? Where is that written? I`m here at this Volvo plant to thank the workers and management for building heavy duty engines and transmissions, axles for trucks and buses, and parts of electric vehicles of the future. And like the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 , that`s a cement plant here in Hagerstown for manufacturing cleaner cement for our nation`s roads and highways.\par \par \uc2 People don`t even realize how much cement the ordinary way it`s made causes environmental problems. The older dirtiest monster accounts for seven percent of global emissions. Well, guess what, clean cement makes a gigantic difference. And like all the workers I met yesterday, the \uc2 IBM\uc2  plant at Poughkeepsie, New York, where they`re investing $20 billion in manufacturing advanced quantum computers here in the United States, again, here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And workers in Syracuse where the company micron is investing $100 billion manufactured computer chips. The biggest investment was kind in America, biggest investment ever in the world. And we all know it`s been four or five years in this country, the last four or five, a lot of things have been tough for people. A lot of things have been tough, and they`re still tough for many. There`s also brightspot where America is reasserting its power for America`s reasserting, Americans are reasserting themselves.\par \par \uc2 Where is it written, as I said, the American can`t lead the world of manufacturing again? We already created -- we already created over 628 years -- 38,000 manufacturing jobs since I`ve been president because we`re making it happen right here in America. Companies are investing in America. And we`re all making sure government delivers. The infrastructure law, the CHIPS and Science Act, I don`t know about you but as my dad used to say, people just are worried about get put in three squares on a table every day and not having to deal with all the politics going on.\par \par \uc2 And all the -- all -- so who should know the names of these pieces of legislation? But We made historic government investment in America, spurring incredible private-sector investment in America. If I could divert for just a second, we had a piece of legislation that led by your congressman, that in fact, says that we`re going to invest $368 billion in dealing with the environment of a major article in a major, major publication yesterday of the -- from industry.\par \par \uc2 That`s generating $1.7 billion in investment. Because guess what, we give a tax credit to somebody and guess what, companies want to build that product, build that product because it`s going to $1,700,000,000.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:05]\par \par \uc2 Meaning literally hundreds of thousands of new job, not a joke, not a joke. All across America, we`re proving made in America isn`t just a slogan. It`s reality. We`re proving that our best days are ahead of us, not behind us. Just look at today`s jobs report. Our economy created 263,000 jobs last month. That`s 10 million jobs since I have come into office.\par \par \uc2 That`s the fastest job growth at any point of any president in all American history. Historic progress. The unemployment rate remains at historic low of 3.5 percent unemployment. That includes the lowest unemployment among Hispanic Americans ever in the history of this country. The second lowest unemployment on the black teenagers ever. And this recovery has been the fastest increase of people reentering the workforce of any modern economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 But there`s something else. Our job market continues to show resilience as we navigate through this economic transition we`re in. For some time, I have been saying that what we need to do in this this transition, we have to move from historically strong economic recovery to a more steady, stable recovery. We need to bring inflation down without giving up all the historic economic progress that working class and middle class people have made. And that`s exactly what we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 Over the past four months, we have created an average of 350,000 jobs a month. That`s down from the 450,000 a month prior -- than the prior four months. And down from 600,000 jobs a month four months before that. The pace of job growth is cooling while still powering our recovery forward. Wage growth for workers remains solid, down from historic high pace months ago but still growing for workers who deserve a raise.\par \par \uc2 And this is the progress we need to see. In the short-term, the transition to a more stable growth that continues to deliver for workers and families will bring inflation down. In the long term the economy will build on a firmer foundation. We still have a lot of work to do. But we`re building a different economy than before. A better one, a stronger one. Not trickle- down economy, that never helped my family very much in Claymont. Trickle down.\par \par \uc2 This is an economy built on building from the middle out and the bottom up, not from the top down. And when that happens, everyone does well. The poor have a ladder up, the middle class do well, and the wealthy do very well. They`re not hurt at all. That`s an economic vision I offer to America when I ran, and I`m pushing it. And that`s what I want to talk about today. And how we have our Republican colleagues that have a very different view.\par \par \uc2 And I know many of you are probably Republicans, but many of my Republican friends are basically arguing that good news for the economy is bad news for America, as if they`re rooting for fewer jobs and lower wages. It`s all part of this trickle-down mentality that says it doesn`t matter what`s happening on our main street, what really matters is what happens on our Wall Street. If Wall Street is doing well, everybody is doing well.\par \par \uc2 Well, I noticed the last -- the previous four years we weren`t doing that well when Wall Street was doing well, and then that took a tumble. It`s not my plan. We continue to grow our economy in a stable and sustainable way. We can build on an economy that works for everyone. Today we`re going to do something that our Republican colleagues and the Congress don`t want us to do.\par \par \uc2 They love to attack the Democrats. They say we -- for what we`ve done. But they really don`t want to see what their plan is. I doubt any of you can tell me what the Republican reelection plan is this time out. What was their platform if they take control of Congress?\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with inflation. Let me tell you how I think about it. I think about the way my dad use used to talk. My dad was a well-read guy. His greatest regret, he never went to college, had to leave Scranton because when coal died everything died with it, and we moved down to Claymont, Delaware, a little steel town at the time. And -- but it`s the way most people at home deal with these things.\par \par \uc2 They talk about it around the kitchen table. Do we have enough money to cover all the bills for the month, and all the necessities that aren`t regular bills? And if we do that, do we have a little bit of breathing room, my dad used to say? Just a little bit of breathing room after that`s done. We don`t have to worry. Well, that`s what we`re trying to do. Give families a little bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`ve done. We passed the Inflation Reduction Act, which the name doesn`t matter a lot to people but it`s going to give Medicare, which a lot of us have been fighting for, the power to negotiate lower prescription drug prices. We pay the highest drug prices of any developed nation in the world.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 It`s going to limit out-of-pocket costs for people on Medicare, no matter what their drug -- as you know, some who have cancer, their drug costs $14,000, $15,000 a year, literally, for the drugs they need. Well, prescription drugs for seniors cannot exceed $2,000 a year, even if it`s $10,000, $20,000, $30,000 that they owe. It can`t exceed $2,000 a year. It`s going to cap the cost of insulin for seniors on Medicare to $35 a month instead of 30 times that.\par \par \uc2 How many of you know somebody who has Type 2 diabetes and needs that insulin? Well, guess what? It costs a whole hell of a lot. A lot of money. And it costs a lot of money for children. I was in Virginia not long ago, and a woman stood up and said, I have two kids with Type 2 diabetes. And we have to split the -- we have to break up what we have. We don`t have enough money, because they didn`t have the insurance, they weren`t covered.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? Well, how do you look at your child knowing that they have Type 2 diabetes and there`s nothing you can do about it? Not a joke. This is the United States of America, for God`s sake. Well, I -- the bill I produced -- I introduced said we`re going to reduce the cost of insulin. And, by the way, it costs -- to make that insulin, it costs $10 to make it and to package it. Ten. T-E-N. Ten dollars. And they`re charging as high as $650, $700 a month for it.\par \par \uc2 Well, the original bill I introduced said we`re going to take care of everybody who`s on insulin. My friends on the other team were able to get enough votes to knock out that for anybody but for the seniors. You know, we`ve locked in savings in healthcare premiums for a million of the people on the Affordable Care Act. You know, one of the things that people forget is, without the Affordable Care Act, anybody who had a preexisting condition could not get insurance.\par \par \uc2 Let me say it again: You could not get insurance if you didn`t have a whole hell of a lot of money to buy a private policy. Well, it guarantees that people with preexisting conditions can have insurance. We`re making it possible for families to save thousands of dollars in energy savings with the legislation we have, which, as I said, it`s going to bring a trillion- seven off the market investing in other jobs.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, for the first time in a long time, we`re going to make sure the biggest corporations begin to pay their fair share of federal taxes with a minimum tax rate of 15 percent. In 2020, of the Fortune 500 companies, 52 made $40 billion and didn`t pay a single penny in taxes. I come from the corporate state of the world, across the border, in Delaware. I know corporations. I got elected six times there. But everybody should be paying something. Everybody should be paying something.\par \par \uc2 And we`re doing all this while reducing the deficit. My friends talk -- on the other team, talk about how we`re big spenders. Well, guess what? They passed a $2 trillion tax cut for the top 1 percent basically and corporate America, and didn`t pay for a penny of it. My first year in office, we reduced the federal debt by $350 billion. 350. And this year, we`re reducing it by more than $1 trillion while we`re doing all the things we`re doing, and allowing Medicare to negotiate drug prices is going to reduce it over the next 10 years, another $300 billion over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 Every single Democrat voted for the Inflation Reduction Act and every single Republican voted against it. Not only that, they`re telling us that the number one priority is to repeal -- if they win, they`re saying they`re going to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act if they gain control of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be crystal clear what that means. Republicans take control of the Congress means the power we just gave Medicare to negotiate drug prices goes away. Gone. Prices will go back up. If Republicans take control of the Congress, that $2,000 cap on prescription drug costs we just passed goes away. Gone. If they take back control of the Congress, the $35 a month cap on insulin for folks on Medicare we just passed goes away. Gone.\par \par \uc2 The savings on healthcare premiums we just got for a million Americans, for the Affordable Care Act, gone. And, of course, it`s not just the Inflation Reduction Act they want to get rid of. They still want to get rid of the Affordable Care Act. That means an end to protection for millions of people with pre-existing conditions who rely on the Affordable Care Act. Gone.\par \par \uc2 Now when it comes to taxes, if Republicans get their way, they`re going to get rid of the corporate minimum tax. They`re not talking about getting rid of your taxes, but the corporate minimum tax.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 The biggest corporations can go back to paying zero in federal income tax. These are facts. Check them out. And, folks, it`s not just the Inflation Reduction Act. They`re coming after your Social Security and Medicare as well. I know that sounds bizarre, but look it up. The senator in charge of electing Republicans in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  this year has proposed a plan to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block every five years.\par \par \uc2 That means every five years, Congress is going to have to vote to either cut, reduce, completely eliminate, or vote for Medicare and Social Security again. What do you think is going to happen? What do you think? But that`s not enough. You`ve been paying into Social Security and Medicare since you started working when you were 16 years old.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s the senator from Wisconsin, Ron Johnson. He thinks waiting five years is too long. And he says Social Security and Medicare should be on the chopping block every single year. If Congress doesn`t vote to keep it, it goes away. Affirmative vote to keep it. You know the games they can play in Congress, everything from dealing with the 60 votes in the Senate and so on.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just Social Security and Medicare. He wants to put everything on the federal budget. Veterans` benefits would have to come up every single year. It`s not just this morning, I saw -- there`s a report. You guys can, as they say, as my grandkids say, \uc2 Google\uc2  it. But the report that came out on CNN, it says, "Republicans called Biden infrastructure program socialism." And then they asked for the money. And it goes through all of the Republicans who -- the most conservative Republicans who call it socialism and how they`re asking for it.\par \par \uc2 A guy named \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 . He`s written three separate letters to the administration asking for projects in his district. He says it enhanced the quality of life, it eased congestion, boosts the economy. He voted against it, says it`s all socialism. Go down the list. Kentucky Representative \uc2 Andy Barr\uc2 . The biggest socialist agenda. Three different projects he wants, citing the importance of the safety and growth of his district. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . I can go down the list. Look it up.\par \par \uc2 Socialism. I didn`t know there were that many socialist Republicans. Think about it. I`m serious. Let`s get serious about taking care of ordinary people, regular people like I grew up.\par \par \uc2 Folks, look, you can`t make this stuff up. You got to say -- and I got to say, I was surprised to see so many socialists in the Republican caucus. And, folks, here`s the bottom line. If Republicans take control of the Congress, these historic victories we just won for the American people are going to be taken away. Every kitchen table cost is going to go up, not down. And I realize costs are going up on food and I was able to bring gasoline down well over $1.60, but it`s inching up because of what the Russians and the Saudis just did. I`m not finished with that yet.\par \par \uc2 The cost of your prescription drugs and healthcare, energy, they`ll all go up. Your protection for pre-existing conditions are taken away. Your Social Security and Medicare are going to be in the chopping block. But they don`t want you to know that. They`re not campaigning on it, but that`s what they are saying. That`s the documents they`re sending out.\par \par \uc2 Folks, when it comes to the next Congress, this isn`t a referendum, it`s a choice. It`s a choice between two very different ways of looking at the economy. You got over 200-some people in the Congress who still think the last election wasn`t fair, that it was stolen, I stole the election, even though every major Republican judge and the Supreme Court said, no, no, there`s no evidence of any of that. None. But I stole the election.\par \par \uc2 Folks, you know, we talk about democracy, whether it`s at risk. Well, democracy is at risk in most places when the only definition of whether you win, you either have to win the election or it`s been stolen. When, in fact, you have -- in fact, you`re in a situation where, you know, a group of people attacked the Capitol like we`ve never seen, smash down the doors, go after people, have three cops end up dying, so on and so forth. And they`re referred to as patriots? Patriots? That`s democracy?\par \par \uc2 Well, look, there`s different ways of