E)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, the music starts and an opportunity for reporters to ask questions. But the president in Hagerstown, where there`s a Volvo factory and really looking ahead to the midterms presenting a contrast. And he put it that way. This is a contrast, he says, between Wall Street and main street. Park Avenue versus Scranton, which of course is his hometown, or in this case Hagerstown.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring back Peter Baker and Brian Cheung.\par \par \uc2 Let me take these one by one because obviously there`s an important economic argument. This president knows that that is on the ballot here. It`s the number one issue in all the latest polls. But gas prices are out there looming. And they`re on their way up. And that`s something that for every single American who is going to go to the polls, this is something that is part of their everyday lives. Where are we with that and how might it play into all of this?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and what was interesting was he said that the economy and the job market is showing resilience, but he was talking a lot about the jobs report from this morning, not talking so much about the inflation, right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: We`re going to get another read on inflation next Thursday morning but it`s at 8.3 percent year over year, and we`ve seen from previous media appearances he has fumbled around a little bit with the messaging on how the administration is handling price increases and oil prices going higher and could be going higher especially after OPEC`s agreement to decrease the production of oil. That`s certainly going to be an issue heading into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But he is correct the president being in terms of describing the labor market as healthy because 3.5 percent unemployment, it`s true that that is at over 50-year lows. But the question is that`s not what voters are bringing with them to the polls. It`s really all about inflation. The prices they`re paying at the stores and at the pump and that does remain a hot issue.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Democrats do want to push this idea of democracy. Also shows up in the polls as something, particularly the Democratic voters, are concerned about. But it`s interesting to me that he`s in Maryland. Back to our original conversation, Peter. When you look at those numbers from the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " of 12 congressional or statewide elections that are key, nine of them in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Republicans are running election deniers. And of course you have a governor now who`s a Republican who says of the Republican gubernatorial nominee, says Dan Cox is a QAnon whack job. So this is also an election that is about, do you belief in what these election deniers do? Do you believe in what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tells you?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Or is this election where we`re going to see a repudiation of that?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, exactly. Part of his referendum is it`s not a referendum but a choice argument is it`s not a choice between two parties, it`s a choice between people who believe in democracy and people who don`t believe in democracy. And you may think I`m not doing well enough on the economy, he`s arguing, but at least we are within the four squares of the Constitution. Those guys aren`t. They don`t believe in the system as we have built it up, and therefore something more existential than the price of gas is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 Now, that maybe a tough argument to make for families that are in fact struggling, but he`s trying to point out there that it`s not just whether you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing a good job, it`s whether you prefer the QAnon crowd, as he`s putting it, the MAGA extremists to come back or not.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. Brian, thank you very much. Peter, again, the book is "The Divider: The Trump White House, 2017 to 2021," along with his wife Susan Glasser. Thank you both for being here and for watching the speech along with us. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And we are back with breaking news out of Uvalde, Texas, the school district there is suspending the entire district police force for an undisclosed period of time. 19 students and two teachers as you will recall were shot and killed there on May 24th. The district said in a press release decisions were pending investigations, but, quote, "recent developments have uncovered additional concerns with department operations which led to the decision."\par \par \uc2 Acting District Police Chief Lieutenant Miguel Hernandez and Ken Mueller have been placed on administrative leave. In its place, the district has requested the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  to provide additional troopers on campus.\par \par \uc2 Despite subpoenas, a raid by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the possibility of facing criminal charges, the Justice Department believes that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still holding on to White House documents. That`s what people familiar with the matter tell NBC News. They also say a top counter-intelligence official recently told Trump`s lawyers that. This is all according to people familiar with the matter and DOJ has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Harry Litman, former deputy assistant attorney general during the Clinton administration.\par \par \uc2 Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If you`re in your old post at the DOJ, what do you even do here? You`ve tried asking nicely. You`ve sent a subpoena. You`ve sent federal agents. What else do you do?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: I mean, you try to develop more evidence to get a search warrant. I think they`re just 