re of the evidence against Stewart Rhodes and his other four co-defendants. Now, Enrique Tario is facing the same dynamic, people who will come forward and really describe the kind of violence they were ready to wreak and were training for.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Harry Litman, it`s always good to see you, as I say. Thank you so much. Appreciate it. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. But stay tuned, I can see right on the other side of the camera, Katy Tur is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 We will start today in Moscow with reporting on the ground in just a moment because if you were like me this morning, you did not need coffee to be jolted awake.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221008eia700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for September 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Tom Winter, Kristen Welker, Carol Leonnig, Charlie  Sykes, Joyce Vance, Ari Melber, Tom Winter, Carol Lam, Vaughn Hillyard,  Barry McCaffrey \par \uc2 9,784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live in New York City. And we start with that unprecedented announcement from New York Attorney General Letitia James just about 90 minutes ago that she`s filing a lawsuit against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , and \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , as well as the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  for what she describes as a deliberate pattern of bank tax and insurance fraud going back years, identifying more than 200 alleged violations that include Trump allegedly falsely doubling, tripling or even more the values of his properties to gain financial advantage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK STATE ATTORNEY GENERAL: The pattern of fraud and deception that was used by Mr. Trump at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  for their own financial benefit is astounding. Inflating the values of assets by whatever means necessary to increase Mr. Trump`s purported net worth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The lawsuit seeks roughly a quarter-billion dollars in penalties and seeks to bar Trump and his three children from serving as executives at any company in New York. And while it`s important to note that this is a civil case, criminal charges could be next. As part of today`s announcement, the Attorney General at her office is making criminal referrals to both the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and the U.S. Attorney in the New York Southern District for possible federal crimes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES: Every day people cannot lie to a bank about how much money they have in order to get a favorable loan to buy a home or to send their kid to college. And if they did, the government would throw the book at them. Why should this be any different? It is a tale of two justice systems, one for everyday working people, and one for the elite, the rich, and the powerful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A statement sent from Trump`s attorney to NBC News says in part. "Today`s filing is neither focused on the facts nor the law. Rather, it is solely focused on advancing the Attorney General`s political agenda. We look forward to defending our client against each and every one of the Attorney General`s meritless claims. I want to bring in a big panel.\par \par \uc2 NBC chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker, Tom Winter, NBC News correspondent for investigations both here on set with me, Carol Leonnig is the Pulitzer Prize-winning national reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and author of the book, I Alone Can Fix It, and an MSNBC contributor, Charlie Sykes is editor-at-large of a Bulwark and an MSNBC contributor, David Fahrenthold is an investigative reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So much to get to here, Tom, I have been -- first of all, you and I ran into each other on the street this morning. You had a sense something was going on. I watched the press conference. I watched Andrea`s coverage. You were both parts of that. But I`m still trying to wrap my head around the breadth and the depth of these allegations that Letitia James is saying a former president of the United States had a years-long pattern or pattern, not just misrepresenting his net worth, misrepresenting the value of a property, misrepresenting the size of properties, but doing it in a way that almost boggles the mind.\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATION CORRESPONDENT: Right. I mean, let`s talk numbers here. We`re talking about according to her, billions of dollars, that he misrepresented himself as far as the wealth that he had over a series of properties and representations to banks, in his tax filings to insurance companies over the years -- 10 years, actually. We`re talking about 200 instances of fraud, according to her in her complaint over the last 10 years. We`re talking about a potential penalty here. It`s called a disgorgement. Essentially everything that he has earned that she says he illegally earned $250 million is the check that Trump could potentially have to write if this lawsuit goes through, depending upon what a judge signs.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about from the small whether it`d be his triplex just a few blocks from us at Trump Tower here in New York City. She says it`s approximately 10,000 square feet, he said it was 30,000 square feet, and that if sold would sell for a number per square foot that had never been for bid and heard in New York City. Or Mar-a-Lago, she says based on the $25 million in revenue that it receives in the based on the value of the property, what can and more importantly, cannot be constructed there. The value according to her office is $75 million, the value according to Trump, $750 million. So these are why the differences in valuations.\par \par \uc2 And the penalties here, not only is that the $250 million, potentially that I talked about, we`re talking about never having to be able to do business in New York -- New York State again, on top of that, maybe not being able to apply for loans for five years for any bank that`s registered to the New York Department of Finance, which -- I`m trying to find banks that aren`t registered to the New York Department of Finance.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 So it could be potentially very difficult for Trump to continue to fund his operations. Going forward, still drilling into the details of that, and how that may all flesh out. Either way, the scope of the fraud here is significant, according to the Attorney General, and according to her continued up and through 2020, after \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  testified in Congress in late February 2019, which is when she says she began this investigation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I`m going to get into some of those specifics that you mentioned. But, David Fahrenthold, since you`ve been covering this for such a very long time, look, it`s no surprise for Letitia James has been doing. She talked about doing it when she ran for attorney general. We know about the depositions that have been had. We`ve known about hundreds of times taking the Fifth, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But when you look at these, what 222 pages I think it is, and when you look at the detail that Letitia James just went into, is this a surprise?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FAHRENTHOLD, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: It is. Even to me who spent years trying to look at the individual properties, both to understand what Trump had done and to understand the scope of her investigation. She said, there`s more than 20 properties, more than 200 examples of this. There was a lot more in there than even I suspected, you know. I had a list of things that I was thinking, you know, someone should give me subpoena power, so I could figure it out. With her subpoena power, she`s found out a lot more than I even knew to look for. So yes, even I was surprised by some of the things she found here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I want to bring in Joyce Vance, former U.S. Attorney, a law professor at the University of Alabama, and an MSNBC legal analyst. Look, Joyce, I`m sure you`ve heard of people doing things like this. They exaggerate the value of something to, for instance, get a better loan term from the bank. But here`s what`s alleged very brazen. Letitia James says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  grossly inflated the values of his properties as we`ve talked about, but here`s just one example.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES: Take Mr. Trump`s triplex, you know, the triplex apartment in Trump Tower on Fifth Avenue. Mr. Trump represented that his apartment spanned more than 30,000 square feet, which was the basis for valuing the apartment. In reality, the apartment had an area of less than 11,000 square feet, something that Mr. Trump was well aware 