d conduct that`s damaging the folks who live in the state of New York, to prohibit them from continuing operations in the state.\par \par \uc2 So you can see how this all plays in with these very specific examples she`s brought forward. In the case of the Trump Tower apartment, she even talked about how the appraiser`s violation or the appraiser`s estimation of value was exceeded by two to three times what their estimate was. And yet in the statements that were turned in, the Trump folks had the valuation to the appraiser`s work. So there are some very clear examples of, I`ll say problems rather than criminality here.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And, of course, they still have to be submitted to a court. And there has to be a fact-finding process before any form of penalty is extracted from the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Nonetheless, this is a strong and a very compelling presentation of evidence in this complaint, which exceeds 200 pages, so I`m not all the way through it yet.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I will tell you that I`m not either. And in fact, I`m going to guess that I`m not even close to go -- haven`t gone through as much of it as you have at this point, Joyce Vance. But, Ari Melber, I don`t know how much you have had an opportunity yet -- we have Ari Melber as well, correct? OK, are still worrying though.\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`m here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There you are. Hello, Ari. So I don`t know how much you`ve had a chance to look at it, but I`m assuming that you`ve had a chance to listen to the press conference. What strikes you when you look again, at the breadth and depth of these allegations?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Well, I think as you`ve been discussing, Chris, the breadth and the depth is it`s not a one-off. It doesn`t look like a mistake. There`s a lot of evidence here over the 220 pages of specific property, specific misrepresentations, specific times that they were put on notice by experts, or the very people that the company went to, to say, what is this worth, what should we do?\par \par \uc2 And then, according to the narrative, at least in this lawsuit, and I`ve emphasized our reporting the Trump folks who have advanced -- a chance to answer this in court, but the narrative and the evidence suggest that they didn`t care. They said, no, we`re just going to put in what we want, we`re going to maximize on every transaction, every interaction, we`re going to lie about things as basic as square footage. So that`s bad for them. That jumps out.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that jumps out is a criminal referrals because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has his hands full of security-related probes, where they seize the illegally obtained security documents in Mar-a-Lago. He`s got two open probes into the January 6 investigation there with DOJ, and in Georgia, where his own aides have been served target letters that they might be indicted. And now he has the prospect, at least according to the Attorney General`s office today, the breaking news, of what they recommend, which is to other federal probes, the SDNY, and \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, those offices will have to determine that as well. So my view is basically, of all the things that Letitia James could do under her powers in the civil role of this attorney general, she`s done everything she can, and she said it goes beyond what she can and that`s why she`s calling in the Feds.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I mean, David Fahrenthold, she said this barely scratches the surface. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: Well, I was really interested in that topic. And like Ari, her referrals to the federal -- the federal prosecutors, you may recall that there is a criminal investigation in this under state law by the Manhattan DA, but that seems to have stalled according to reporting by my colleagues. And one of the problems for that probe is that New York State law is not written really to prosecute bank fraud. That`s the thing. It`s almost always done at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 What she`s done now is explicitly say, OK, DOJ, we think we`ve found bank fraud. Now, it`s your turn to look into that. So I found that a very interesting prospect because we don`t know if the DOJ is going to follow up on that or not.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, what we do know at least according to Letitia James is that this is at least part of what started it and that was Michael Cohen`s testimony. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): Did the president ever provide inflated assets to an insurance company?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Who else knows that the president did this?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Allen Weiselberg, Ron Lieberman, and Matthew Calamari.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ari, Allen Weisselberg was also mentioned by Letitia James here. Something that simple really touched off something this big or what feels this big?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Is that for me?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Ari.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Yes. You know, it`s a great question because the question there was under oath, Mr. Cohen had given multiple types of responses. At one time he had been playing defense for Trump, but then he turned as we all remember, he also did some jail time. And so that question and that pattern is really the predicate for much of this. And so I think -- I think what you see with Letitia James is saying, they went deliberately, they went a time slowly.\par \par \uc2 Folks ask why does it take so long, why are we talking about this now? She afforded \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all of his rights. He invoked the right of the Fifth Amendment not to incriminate himself. He delayed and stalled on that. He was at one time held in contempt civilly and paying fines for that. So she went through and said, hey, if you have a side of the story to tell if you disagree with what we just heard there for Mr. Cohen under oath and the other evidence they`ve marshaled, let`s hear it.\par \par \uc2 For example, Chris, someone might say they were repeatedly misled by their own staff or aides, but they didn`t mean to do this, or they might say, oh, wow, looking back at this, I should have done it differently. That happens in a lot of \uc2 IRS\uc2  cases when people cooperate, and they say I`ll give some money back because I guess it does look like something was off a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 He did not do that. He fought her every step of the way then sat down face to face for hours invoking the Fifth and refusing to provide a response. So, yes, what Mr. Cohen testified to what the congresswoman was asking you about what the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  has not found a good answer on is all at issue in this trial. And then as your other experts and guests mentioned, you have an open DEA case where the chief financial officer is convicted, is a felon, is guilty. And so what swirls around all of this is --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And served time.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: And serving time, five months.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: So the -- what`s rolls around all this is anyone else going to get in trouble because it`s not the Weisselberg.org. That`s not the name of the company.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Carol, I will get back to the legality of all this and there`s a lot more to dig into. But we all know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , so associates who he is with what he has, right? His net worth, his holdings, he loves to talk about the places he owns. He loves to go to the places he owns. And for obviously, financial reasons get publicity for the places he owns. He loves to be on the cover of magazines. What do you think this means for Trump himself?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I think it adds, Chris, another -- I don`t know, another prong of the pitchfork. It`s aimed at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from all sorts of legal fields, you know, state, local, civil, criminal, federal, national security. How many more investigations can \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  be at the center of? And I would just say that I want to emphasize something about Michael Cohen`s testimony. You know, his break with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was important for lots of reasons. And it did spur Letitia James` investigation.\par \par \uc2 But one of the most crucial elements of Cohen`s testimony was essentially right at the heart of intention, possible criminal intent, and this will come up if the Department of Justice decides to pick up this ball from Letitia James, either at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  level or it may be in Justice. Cohen said that the information that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gave about the square footage, the valuation, the amount of sales he`s had-- he had at a particular property, all of those were inflated when it behooves him financially, and they were deflated when it behooved him financially.\par \par \uc2 In other words, for the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , it might be good to say something was worth a lot less money to the, you know, customer who`s coming to buy or the bank that`s considering giving you a loan, it might be a good idea to say it was worth a lot more. It`s that -- it`s that criminal intent, the knowing that you`re telling people one thing if it helps you financially and you`re telling them the opposite if it helps you on another score of the ledger. And I think that`s where Letitia James has some information, but there`s quite a bit more, according to \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s also at the heart of what his children were almost charged with many, many years ago, when the Manhattan DA considered bringing a criminal case against them, for them falsifying information to prospective clients and to clients about the value, the saleability, the market forces around a property they were trying to sell or an end push. And that was a huge problem for them. And it could end up being a much larger problem for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I don`t think he is going to change his stripes in terms of his reaction to this. He`s going to continue to fight it as hard as he can, as Ari mentioned, it`s not going to be sensible to us that this has changed his life. But it`s another war front he has to fight and it`s got a lot of teeth and a lot of bad facts.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you wonder how many fronts on war you can fight at once. And we`ll go back to that, but, Kristen Walker, you covered him from the other side when he ran for president.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You covered his White House. You know a lot of people in his circle. But we`ve seen some statements from his spokesperson. Have we heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself yet?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are just hearing from him on his social media website. He has described himself as a counter puncher. That`s what we are seeing, not surprisingly. Let me read you a little bit of what he posted just moments ago. He describes this as "another witch-hunt by a racist Attorney General Letitia James, he`s called her racist in the past, who failed in her run for governor getting almost zero support from the public and now is doing poorly against law and order AG candidate highly respected Michael Henry."\par \par \uc2 Of course, he`s trying to speak to the politics of this moment. "I never thought this case would be brought until I saw her really bad poll numbers. She`s a fraud who campaigned on a get Trump platform, despite the fact that the city is one of the crime and murder disasters of the world under her watch." In a second tweet, he calls her -- well it`s not a tweet, it`s a post. She is a failed AG whose lack of talent in the fight against crime is causing record numbers of people and companies to flee New York.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He`s so consistent.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You have a tell on that, you`ll say that firm.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The reason why I raised that and why I read all of that to you is because that`s a strategy that we are seeing not just from the former president, but the Trump organization trying to put the focus on crime rates in New York to try to distract from this issue today. And I think you`ll see a lot more of that from the former president from his allies. But just the big picture here and this speaks to something that Ari was talking about.\par \par \uc2 He is currently at the center of an unprecedented legal Firestorm, not just this extraordinary development today, Chris, but there are a number of other investigations swirling around him, including by the Justice Department for those classified documents at Mar-a-Lago, the January 6 Committee, which by the way, has said that it is hoping to hold another hearing next week. And so it`s on and on. There`s also one in Georgia, potential election fraud claims.\par \par \uc2 So this is yet another just unprecedented data point. And it comes against the backdrop. We`re less than two months from the midterms, Chris, and this is going to put a number of Republican candidates in a really tough spot. They`re going to be asked to respond to this. And they`re going to have to figure out how fine of a line will they walk as it relates to the former president. We`re seeing his approval ratings drop, including among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So politically speaking, this could be problematic. And what does it mean for 2024? All of this speculation swirling about a second bid for the White House, a third bid, really. Will he get into the race again? Will this help or hurt? We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: I think to pick up on a point that Kristen raised about all the investigations that the former president now faces, and it feels like we`ve been talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the word investigation together here for nearly six or seven years. And that has been the case obviously, starting back with the special counsel`s probe into Russian interference. But when you look at it, a lot of these investigations are starting to come to a conclusion. Trump`s already been deposed along with his kids. In this case by Letitia James, we`ve got an ongoing investigation involving classified documents at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 But look, a special master is starting their review of that, at the conclusion of that review, that Justice Department may have some decisions to make, that`ll be past the midterms if they`re going to follow any sort of guidelines, it`s not a law, it`s not a rule, per se. But after that, they will be able, inconsistent with their own guidelines to potentially have some charging decisions to make at that point, whether or not the president is actually charged. Well, that, of course, remains to be seen. Of the Georgia probe, it looks like it`s kind of coming towards maybe not quite the finish line, but certainly the sixth or seventh inning. So when you look at all of this, the -- what are seemingly endless investigations and multi-year investigations are really starting to come to a point here in clarity.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And just to make one more point, Chris, while all of this is swirling around the former president, this search at Mar-a-Lago is one that has really struck a nerve with him with a number of his supporters. And the early reports were that it had actually galvanized supporters around the former president, that this ongoing fight about a special master was something that was energizing his core supporters. And in the early days, again, my reporting showed that anecdotally, at least, had started to bring some Republican detractors back into the fold. They felt quite defensive of him.\par \par \uc2 But now, we are several weeks out from that, and I think that the political winds are shifting a little bit. And just big picture, this is the last thing that Republicans want to be talking about this close to the midterms. They want to be talking about pocketbook issues, inflation. They don`t want to be talking about yet another legal challenge for the former president.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And remember what Letitia James said a couple of times, which is no one is above the law, right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Right, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: -- Which is an important message that they`re trying to get at.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: And I have to do a quick fact-check on the president`s statement. You know he talks about the rampant crime in New York City, and certainly, overall crime in New York City has been on the rise in the past couple of years started before the pandemic. There`s a number of reasons for that. But if you murder somebody or steal from somebody in New York City, that does not go to the Attorney General`s office, that is something for the various district attorneys in the five boroughs or for the district attorneys around the state. The Attorney General`s office really is very limited in any sort of criminal matters that they can look into. Certainly, they can look into civil matters. That`s the reason why the three of us are here talking. But just as a fact check, that`s really not Letitia James --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Has nothing to do with what her office does.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Charlie, you wrote on Monday about the way Trump has reacted as this -- as Kristen just points out, this legal pressure gets more and more intense, threats of violence, for example. And you said, "I regret to tell you that the worst is yet to come." What were you saying?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, here you have a president who is -- a former president who`s you know prepared to try to intimidate the Department of Justice. He is playing the victim card. He is rallying his base. And he is prepared to burn it all down if in fact he`s held legally accountable. And, of course, you`re seeing this with his counterpunching right now. So I think -- I think that if you -- if anyone thinks that this will be the moment that will sober up Republicans, it may embarrass them, they may in private say things but they`re not going to break with him right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 He just simply intensifies everything that has been happening. And I -- and I -- and I guess I had a couple of thoughts here about you know, to pull the lens back, David is absolutely right that these specific details are not surprising. I mean -- I`m sorry, the specific details are surprising. They are dazzling details. But the larger picture is that no one is surprised to find out that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fraudster. He`s a serial liar. He`s been a con man. He has built his financial empire and his political career on a throne of lies.\par \par \uc2 And Letitia James talked about the two-tier justice system. And the reality is -- the objective reality is that up until now, there has been a two-tier system that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been above the law. And I`m not criticizing the speed of this investigation. But think back about all the years and all the decades and all the district attorneys, all the Attorney Generals, all of the investigations, and none of them have held \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accountable for something that was widely known, which was that he was engaging in these kinds of fraudulent practices.\par \par \uc2 If I said -- if I had claimed to a bank that my house where I am broadcasting from right now was worth $100 million and I borrowed all kinds of money against it, I could have led a marvelous lifestyle, but I would probably be in prison right now. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been doing this not just for months, not just for years, but for decades. And until now, he has not been held to the standard that other working-class New Yorkers or Americans would have been held to. So it is an extraordinary moment how all of this is coming together right now. And really, no one should be surprised about it at this point.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Worth repeating. As we`ve said several times throughout the coverage, this is a civil case. But, Ari, the Attorney General also talked about criminal referrals. Here`s more from the presser.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES: Show that they violated several state criminal laws, including falsifying business records, issuing false financial statements, insurance fraud, and engaging in a conspiracy to commit each of these state law violations. We believe the conduct alleged in this action also violates federal criminal law, including issuing false statements to financial institutions and bank fraud. And we are referring those criminal violations that we`ve uncovered to the United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York and the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Ari, Carol brought up criminal intent? What will the Southern District look at if they`re going to decide? And so far when we`ve asked them for a comment, they`ve declined to comment, but what will they look at to decide whether or not to follow up on this?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Well, Chris, I think you`re asking right now, the question that scares \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the most. He has been through the court system a lot. He cares a lot more about cases that could land someone in jail, like Weisselberg, his CFO we discussed, or potentially himself than anything else. We`ve even seen with his lawyers, he`s stiffed many lawyers. When he needs a good criminal defense lawyer in Georgia, where there`s a prison on the table, he wises up.\par \par \uc2 And so to your question, Chris, the prosecutors here and the DOJ are now holding this hot potato from the state attorney general. And they`re going to have to decide at SDNY on any fraud or business activities in New York and at \uc2 IRS\uc2 , which we believe is, of course, be aware of this for some time, they`ve had to furnish the tax returns, they`re going to have to decide when they get the referral is there enough here to open an investigation or not? If they open that investigation, they`re going to have to decide whether to act on it if they charge someone we would hear about that. And if they decide not to, then they have to determine whether to announce that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I -- Joyce, the IRS Criminal Division is not somebody you want to necessarily have to go toe to toe with. But is that a kind of more straightforward case, than the Southern District would have, or is that an oversimplification?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I would expect to see the Southern District and the Tax Division working hand in hand on a matter like this. And something that I think is a little bit perplexing here. None of the baseline facts that would have led DOJ to open an investigation years ago are unknown. Southern District, after all, had \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  in its court -- rooms, convicted him, sentenced him to prison. All of this was available.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And what we don`t know is whether DOJ engaged in some form of investigation and zeroed it out because they lacked sufficient evidence. Perhaps they didn`t have enough evidence directly linking the former president. Perhaps they didn`t have evidence of his state of mind. And it may be possible that Tish James has stumbled upon additional evidence that will cause them to reinvigorate this investigation.\par \par \uc2 I think your assessment, Chris, is dead on the money that the tax charges can be the most straightforward ones in situations like this. But Tish James also specifically mentioned bank fraud, 18 USC 1344, and it prohibits anyone who attempts to execute or executes a scheme or an artifice to defraud a bank, which is pretty much the core conduct here. This effort to monkey with valuation on property in an effort to be able to obtain loans at more favorable sorts of situations than you might otherwise be able to obtain them, to pump up your financial net worth to help yourself out in financial transactions.\par \par \uc2 That`s really the core of many of the bank fraud cases that the Justice Department brings every year. And that`s the other half. It`s like two flip sides of the coin. On one side you`ve got the bank fraud. On the other side you have the tax fraud where Trump allegedly manipulated property values to pay a lower amount of taxes. And if there is some sort of reinvigorated federal investigation I would expect those two to go forward hand in hand.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, Ari, I know you have to go. Thank you both very much. Kristen, Tom, Carol, Charlie, David, sticking around with us because we got much more ahead regarding President Trump and the attorney general`s lawsuit announcement right after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:48]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are back continuing to cover the breaking news that the New York attorney general is suing former President Trump, three of his children and their company Trump Org, alleging large-scale business fraud.\par \par \uc2 Back with me NBC chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker, Tom Winter, NBC News correspondent for investigations, Carol Leonnig is the Pulitzer Prize-winning national reporter for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," Charlie Sykes is editor at large of "The Bulwark" and David Fahrenthold is an investigative reporter for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." Also joining us now, NBC legal analyst \uc2 Carol Lam\uc2 , former U.S. attorney for the Southern District of California.\par \par \uc2 David, so the AG, among the many other things she said, she wants to make it so the Trump essentially can`t run businesses in New York anymore. How would that work? And I think she was asked this question, could they just move then their business operations to Florida?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: So this actually is funny. It reminds me of the outcome of the case I wrote about \uc2 the Trump Foundation\uc2 . The end of that case, the AG`s office brought against Trump`s charity. He was barred from serving as an officer of any charity in New York state. This obviously much more important. The sanctions she`s seeking would not only prohibit him from being an officer, his kids from being officers in companies in New York, but also there are provisions in there that keep them from just moving to Florida and continuing to do business in New York.\par \par \uc2 There`s a provision against buying any real estate in New York and more importantly this provision about entering into financial transaction with banks that are licensed in New York. So you can move the business to Florida, you can move a lot of the operations to Florida, but it`s going to be really hard to get money to get loans if you can`t deal with anybody licensed in New York. They`re going to have to choose between the core of their business in New York and doing business with you.\par \par \uc2 And to me, I see that as one of the most important outcomes, maybe even more than barring him from being an officer of any New York company.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And look, we did hear a lot from Letitia James, Tom Winter, but in terms of the specifics again, I don`t know how much of the 222 pages you`ve had an opportunity to go through. But how much evidence is there? What`s in there that struck you?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: So one of the things that we previously talked about is this idea that this is not necessarily he said-he said investigation, in that we have financial documents, but now having a chance to go through just some of the 222-page civil complaint here, you look at it and you see specific quotes from communications.\par \par \uc2 So it`s clear that they have either e-mails or notes so they can paint a picture. I was just going through one particular property where they have communications back and forth with people on how they were appraising a specific property. So you`re not necessarily having to rely on, well, what did Kristen say before the grand jury or what did you say before the grand jury. They have their e-mails real time and they can kind of piece together kind of the machinations of how some of these deals came together, whether it be loans or insurance issues.\par \par \uc2 So I think when they look at it, they have probably here at least on its face, and of course we still need to hear from Trump`s attorneys, they haven`t even had a chance to file their response, but it does appear that they have some really good evidence in the form of hard communication that they can rely on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol, the attorney general said she`s open to negotiations. She says my door is always open. I think that was the phrase she used. But we already know she at least rejected one offer to settle. What would something like that look like, that negotiation, and would you even expect it to seriously happen?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, THE WASHINGTON POST NATIONAL REPORTER: You know, it makes me think of some of the cases where the Department of Justice has had, as Tom pointed out, incredible documentary evidence of a corporation engaged in a long-term scheme and pattern. And I`m thinking of \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  pretending that its sensors for carbon monoxide were capturing all the dirty air flowing from their vehicles when actually they had jiggered with them so that they would not give an accurate reading.\par \par \uc2 When the Department of Justice found that pattern, they reached an agreement, which was boatloads of money in payments by \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and also agreements for what I would call restoration. You know, efforts to improve the climate, donations, and also agreements for certain officers who`d been engaged in this behavior not to be continued as officers.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 So you can see like a possibility that Trump Org might reach that kind of deal, both backward looking and forward looking. But it`s conceptual because I have never seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really reach a settlement until he`s all the way in the back of the corner and has nothing left to fight with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, David, the attorney general did not answer one question. And I think I was sort of raising that a little bit earlier with Joyce, which is how he was able -- if all of this is so, how was he able to get away with this for so long? How was he able to defraud some of the biggest banks? How was -- it`s one thing to have people whether they`re sycophants or whatever they are, who say OK, I`ll go along with this. But it`s another thing, all of us know in our lives of people who either have had an \uc2 IRS\uc2  audit or who have gone to a bank, as I mentioned before, to try to get a mortgage. And you can`t say that a house that`s a thousand square feet is 3,000 square feet. How did he do it, David?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: I think there are probably three factors I would point out. One is the fact that the company is extremely insular. It`s not a public company. It doesn`t give out public information. But the people who run it are either direct blood relatives of Trump or people like Allen Weisselberg that have been there for 30 or 40 years. It`s a very small set of people who don`t leave. New people don`t come in. That`s part of it. It`s easier to keep secrets that way.\par \par \uc2 Another factor is just the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was small. It was not the world`s biggest New York real estate. It was one of the most famous because Trump was such a great self-promoter, but it was just not that big. It was not that important. If you`re going to try to go take down the biggest, you know, organization in New York, you wouldn`t go after them. I think they flew under the radar in a weird sense, even as Trump was such a prominent figure, because the company wasn`t that important.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing is they swam in waters where some kind of puffery, some kind of exaggeration in the world of real estate is normal. People don`t -- you know, they know that there`s some sort of, you know, give in the valuations of properties. And they just went far beyond that normal range. That`s why they`re getting in trouble now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So pull back the curtain a little bit, \uc2 Carol Lam\uc2 , if you will for us because you`ve been in this position. What is going to be going on in the hours and days and weeks to come as Letitia James says, here, I`ve got a case for you, take a look?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it`s important to point out, again a reminder that this is a civil case, not a criminal case. And so the processes are very different when you have a civil case that has been brought. So what happens from this point on is the judge, when the case is assigned to a judge will set a schedule. And then there will be a schedule set for bringing certain motions.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that happens is that discovery takes place. So the government has to turn over all of its evidence to the other side and this is -- this takes a lot of time and both sides will probably agree that this is a very complex matter. And so we are looking at some runway here as you go ahead go ahead. But I do think that the size, the scope, the scale of this complaint is very important because I think that Letitia James has now sort of set into motion the possibility that other authorities are going pick up on what she`s done.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard who is outside Trump Tower, and we`ve been talking about the fact, Vaughn, this isn`t the first time that there`s been a suggestion that there could be charges brought against Trump Org but didn`t happen. What`s the reaction of former prosecutors in the Manhattan DA`s office? There was I think a lot of frustration from them, right, that they were left out of the equation?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right, it was noticeable in Letitia James` press conference there that she referred to this to the U.S. attorney`s office as well as the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to investigate. Who she notably left out of this was Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . This is the district attorney who has come under focus here this year.\par \par \uc2 It was a year ago in the summer of 2021 when the previous district attorney filed those charges, those criminal charges at the district attorney level against Allen Weisselberg, who has been the longtime chief financial officer of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . He started working for the company back in 1973. Filed charges and felony counts not only against Alken Weisselberg, though, but also the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The question was would this ever, would that investigation ever amount to criminal charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and/or his children? It was earlier this year when the two leading prosecutors in the DA`s office working on that case resigned. One of them writing that they believe that they had enough evidence to press charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And yet \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has not done that to date.\par \par \uc2 And that is where you saw Letitia James refer her findings, the attorney general`s findings to the U.S. attorney`s office as well as the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 But it`s notable, in the last 10 minutes, we now have a statement from Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  who says, quote, "Our criminal investigation concerning former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and its leadership is active and ongoing." And in that press conference, she was asked whether she would cooperate with his investigation, she said that she would.\par \par \uc2 Now just a couple of weeks from now or one month from now, October 24th, is when a trial will begin. A jury will begin to be selected relating back to those 2021 charges from Alvin Bragg`s DA office against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . That is going to play out just one month from now. Yasmin?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: So just to add a little bit to that reporting from Vaughn, when you look at this, and it`s important actually at the bottom of the Allen Weisselberg plea agreement, it is signed by a member of the attorney general`s staff. So she doesn`t need to refer anything to them. They are already working together. And they`ve been working together on a lot of this over the course of the past couple of years.\par \par \uc2 So the referral, I think from -- and Vaughn`s right, a report that the referral was to federal authorities, I think the idea there is that there`s some specific federal statutes that exist that don`t exist in New York state law that might be applicable to some of the conduct that James alleges here today. And so particularly when we talk about taxes, of course when we file our taxes, we file them to the state or perhaps a local municipality as well as to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So that`s something that the \uc2 IRS\uc2 ` criminal division and federal prosecutors could look at. That`s why it makes sense to me based on her findings that there`s the potential, it`s not a guarantee, a potential for violations of federal law and that`s why they moved it to the U.S. attorney`s office and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  versus going to Alvin Bragg`s office who they already happen to be working with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And we`ve talked a lot about the fact that there are so many other investigations going on. And I just want to play part of what the former president told \uc2 Newsmax\uc2 , I think it was just last night, about one of those other topics, 