c2 JANSING: And in our final minute, we`ve talked about the fact that, you know, Russian troops have been fleeing, they`ve been leaving behind equipment, but what`s the status as you understand it of Ukrainian troops? I mean, they have, many of them, have been fighting for seven months. Many of them have lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, right, that`s correct. And they have lost more than 9,000 killed in action, who knows, that would be implied 25,000 or more wounded in action.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:10]\par \par \uc2 Along with what could be thousands of civilians dead and the massive infrastructure devastation throughout Ukraine. It`s a very tough situation. Fortunately, fortunately, both Europe and the United States, the Biden administration has been very stalwart on this, stepped forward. We`re now not just supplying immediate used munitions to Ukrainians we`re also trying to build a longer term defensive capability.\par \par \uc2 So I think, at the end of the day, Putin has already lost the war from a strategic perspective. The question now is, how are we going to end it and bring peace?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: General Barry McCaffrey, thank you. I hope we can talk about that question another day. But that`s going to do it for us this hour.\par \par \uc2 Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC, and stay with us because we have much more on that breaking news, New York`s attorney general suing for former President Trump and three of his children alleging largescale business fraud. Yasmin Vossoughian is in the chair for "KATY TUR REPORTS" Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hey, everybody. Good to be with you. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian in for Katy Tur. We are following the bombshell breaking news this hour out of New York state. Today is putting the former president and the future of his real estate empire in legal peril.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220922ei9l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for September 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Shaquille Brewster, Steve Kornacki, David Jolly,  George Solis, Mike Memoli, Tom Winter, Charles Coleman, Richard Engel \par \uc2 9,491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good day, you`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. I`m Peter Alexander, in today for my friend, Chris Jansing.\par \par \uc2 We are expecting President Biden to come out at any time now to push the DISCLOSE Act, a bill that would require Super PACs and so-called dark money groups to disclose donors who give more than $10,000. While we wait for that, we`re also just about an hour or so away from that first hearing by the so-called Special Master, Raymond Dearie, that court-ordered independent third party regarding documents found at former President Trump`s home in Florida. We do not know a lot about what will transpire in that hearing but we do know that Mr. Trump`s team is already taking issue with some of the judge`s directives claiming they do not want to tip their hand over any potential legal defense if the former president might be indicted.\par \par \uc2 More on that and the Trump team`s latest filing and the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals in just a minute, plus, Hurricane Fiona has now strengthened into a Category 4 storm potentially on track to become a Category 5. There is the map you can see Turks & Caicos getting pounded. It has been crushing Caribbean islands for days now, leaving catastrophic destruction in its wake. Coming up, the latest on the devastation and the potential impact for folks up and down the east coast. We`ll have that.\par \par \uc2 Finally, my conversation with the Deputy Secretary of State, \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2 , how the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  that kicks off today and President Biden`s scheduled speech tomorrow. This morning, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . chief started things off by telling the assembly that the world is "gridlocked and colossal global dysfunction." So with war and floods and food scarcity, energy issues all competing for time on the international agenda, what can the \uc2 UN\uc2  do to help?\par \par \uc2 But we do want to begin right now with the politics. And the midterm elections now exactly seven weeks away, the race for control of Congress could not be any closer. Our latest NBC News poll shows it`s effectively a dead heat. So today, both parties are putting their focus on increasing their ranks, marking national registration day with events nationwide aimed at registering and motivating voters ahead of those all-important midterms. I want to bring in NBC`s Shaquille Brewster. He`s in Milwaukee. NBC News, national political correspondent Steve Kornacki, fortunately, for us, is dressed and ready at the big board.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, let`s get to you. In writing about the poll results, our NBC News political team said there are two completely different campaigns that are going on right now.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Is that what you`re finding when you speak and you listen to voters there?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: That is absolutely the case, Peter. And by the way, one of those national voter registration events is happening here in Milwaukee, right behind me. They`ll might be kicking off any minute now going and doing a car parade to encourage voters to come out and encourage voters to register to vote on this day. But in my conversations with folks across the state of Wisconsin, you hear two issues that come up more than others, one, inflation and the economy, and two, abortion. But our NBC poll showed that different parties have the advantage with those issues.\par \par \uc2 You see, Republicans have the advantage when you`re talking about immigration, economy, and crime. Democrats have the advantage with abortion, health care, and education. And that advantage is trickling down to how voters are perceiving the candidates in this battleground state. I want you to listen to some of those conversations and see how they break through that nuance there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE KLUTH, WISCONSIN VOTER: Inflation to me is not -- I mean, it`s just kind of consequential of the world that we live in. If we can find ways to, like fix that great. But things that are way more important are like more of the social issues for me. So, yes, things like women`s rights and health and so forth, improving that landscape.\par \par \uc2 TORI KENPKEN, WISCONSIN VOTER: The cost of living affects everybody. And what`s happening? And I`m not going to say that like abortion rights doesn`t affect everybody, it affects a lot of people but the cost of living, you know, if you improve cost of living that may affect women who feel like they have to go the route of abortion because they can`t take care of their child financially.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: And that is why our politics team has called this the tale of two different campaigns. It`s not as if voters don`t care about other issues out there. They`re not looking into other issues. But in terms of what is most animating, what they bring up the most, what they`re concerned about the most. Those are the two issues. And you see there`s a partisan advantage for Republicans on one and Democrats on the other.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Shaq, thank you. Steve, let me ask you the difference between winning and losing. And these midterms, and we`ve said this pretty much every time, right? It`s going to come down to those voters in the middle that have yet to make up their minds. So help us sort of sort out who these people are. What do we know about these independent-minded voters?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s interesting. I think we got a little bit of data, a little bit of insight into that. Chuck Todd and our political team looked at a recent poll here, our most recent NBC poll, and found out first, a question of where the third persuadable, at least potentially persuadable voters are most likely to be here. You see a sort of different urban core, in a city, in a suburb, in a rural area.\par \par \uc2 You see, actually, it`s the outer suburbs. It`s the excerpts where you`re most likely to find the voters who are sort of up for grabs in this election. You can see 43 percent of them in outer suburbs. How do you define voters who are up for grabs? Well, it`s voters who are sort of sending mixed signals on some of the key questions we asked in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you through again, looking at these outer suburbs in particular, it`s -- a couple of things that would favor the Democrats, at least, on paper here. These are voters who by a slight majority say they want the Trump investigations to continue. They`re with the Democrats on that. They disapprove of the Dobbs decision, the Supreme Court ruling on abortion. They`re with, by and large, the Democrats on that.\par \par \uc2 But they`re not top to bottom with the Democrats because there are some issues here where they`re with the Republicans. They favor the idea of a Republican-controlled Congress. They disapprove by a wide margin of Joe Biden`s handling of the economy. So it`s sort of a clash of, they`re with the Democrats a little bit, they`re with the Republicans a little bit, which of those is going to sort of win out at the end of the day. In the kinds of districts, we`re talking about here.\par \par \uc2 When you say outer suburbs, give you an example here, one that we`ll be looking at closely on election night. This is the seventh district of Michigan. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , she`s a Democratic incumbent. She was elected in that blue wave of 2018. The district has changed a little bit because of redistricting. But under his current lines, you take a look at this here, Biden won under these lines by a single point in 2020. It had been Trump by four points in 2016.\par \par \uc2 So you saw that movement of Trump plus four to Biden plus one. This is the kind of district that incorporates exactly what we`re talking about here, outer suburbs, sort of conflicted voters. This is a classic definition, I think on election night of a bellwether district.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right, Steve Kornacki, we appreciate that. Hard to believe, I think a lot of folks who are engaged in the daily sort of news grind that there`s some whose minds remain unmade up. That`s why we keep a close eye on them. Steve, Shaquille, thanks to both of you. I want to bring in Illinois Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Cheri Bustos\uc2  and former Florida Republican Congressman David Jolly, also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 David, let me ask you first. You saw the way the issues break down and your mind is the overriding factor here we`re going to come down to whether people care more about those economic issues or their cultural issues, or is that sort of a too simplistic view?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. Well, I don`t think it`s too simplistic in terms of the top issues of the economy and a woman`s right to choose. Clearly, at a post-Dobbs world, we have seen the numbers shift more favorably to Democrats. I think what I`m fascinated to see in this cycle is you know you can break down election cycles into turnout races or persuasion races. And truthfully, they`re almost always just turn out races. Can I turn out enough people on my side based on enthusiasm or anger?\par \par \uc2 But what the Dobbs decision did is it handed Democrats a persuadable constituency on the issue of a woman`s right to choose. The nuance, Peter, though is the persuadable voters for Democrats this cycle are not traditional Democratic performing voters, and they probably have never identified as pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: But they`re learning that maybe they were pro-Roe all along. That message doesn`t fit neatly within the Democratic messaging of the past but is there for their taking. And I think that`s why you`re seeing democratic numbers bump up a little bit going into November.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Congresswoman, let me ask you about this. We know that the Fed is kicking off a big meeting today. The economy obviously remains on everybody`s mind. The president said on Sunday that he thinks the worst is over but an article and Kiplinger yesterday said the following. This is a quote, it said. "It`s very likely that the downturn we have been experiencing will get much worse in the bear market is likely not over." If the economy gets much worse, as they warn, how problematic is that for Democrats? Can the Democrats have to demonstrate continued progress to try to keep this issue from becoming a defining one for so many voters?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHERI BUSTOS, (D-IL): Yes, it`s not good if the economy continues to go in the wrong direction. It`s not good for Democrats. However, we have to look at what we have done and we have to keep talking about that. I mean look what just happened for Medicare patients, we are capping their yearly expenses at $2,000. If a senior has to be on insulin, that is capped at $35 a month. Nobody else has gotten that done.\par \par \uc2 We are -- the president just relieved $10,000 in student loan debt or $20,000 if you -- if you went to school on Pell Grants. So we`ve got a story to tell, but we just have to keep telling it. I could not agree with David more though about this is going to get down to doing a good job which I believe the Biden administration is and I believe House Democrats are as well and taking credit for it.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So we have to talk about that and then we have to make sure that we have the best Get Out The Vote plan in the history of our party. We`ve got anger going on. I come from a district that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. In fact, I`m the only woman serving in the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  who won every cycle when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in office. And so I understand those swing voters, I`m a Democrat in a Trump district. And as it pertains to choice, this is a hot-button issue. And it has changed dramatically in these 10 years that I`ve been in office.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: It went from an issue that you just didn`t talk about to an issue now that people are saying, I don`t want Uncle Sam in my -- in my room with the doctor helping me make a decision. This is my decision to make.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: Just like when we`re making Sunday dinner, we want to decide for ourselves whether we`re going to have fried chicken or baked chicken. We don`t want anybody else telling us what to do.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, those personal stories as it relates to women`s experiences with abortions in the past or the ability to have that freedom if they need to, at any point going forward have really I think, affected a lot of people making their decisions now. David, I want to switch 