ALEXANDER: Congresswoman, last question to you. What do the Democrats need to do seven more weeks to the midterms, you talked about Get Out The Vote, that effort there is what you think needs to be in place -- in place right now beyond just talking about their successes to this point?\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: Well, the MAGA Republicans, to the point David made about the Trump loyalists, we have to talk about them. They have begun to take over the Republican Party, which is -- which was a real shame. I -- look, I can look back and say I have many, many friends in the Republican Party but the hardcore MAGA Republicans, that`s a whole different story. And so I think what we have to do is talk about the hardcore nature of the MAGA Republicans that they want to criminalize women`s choices. They want to send Uncle Sam to the doctor`s office with us. They want to send women to jail if they are doing a search for a medical provider.\par \par \uc2 This is extreme and we need to talk about that. Anger is a motivator. And then we`ve got to have a -- the best -- not just to get out the vote plan but also continuing to register voters in every state, in every congressional district where we`ve got a shot.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: And so I -- it -- what I would do if I`m running a campaign is I would take some of that money that is being used. No offense to the airwaves, but it is being in -- the airwaves are being inundated. Let`s use some of that money to make sure we`re registering and then getting out the voter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, that`s a good point to make. Congresswoman \uc2 Cheri Bustos\uc2 , we appreciate your time. Former Congressman David Jolly, always good to have you for your perspective, as well.\par \par \uc2 By the way, on this national voter registration day, our "PLAN YOUR VOTE" tool is ready to give you the key information you need to register to vote. You see that Q.R. code right there on your screen, well, you can scan that code to be taken directly to the plan your vote site.\par \par \uc2 And soon right here, President Biden is set to speak on a new election transparency bill from the White House. When that begins, we`re going to take you there. The president, as we`ve noted, will head to New York next for the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 . He is speaking there tomorrow morning. What`s at stake in this high-level conference with a war on Ukraine set to come front and center? We`ll address it with the Deputy Secretary of State, \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2 . She`s going to join me ahead.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 But first, a tragic scene unfolding in Puerto Rico, we`re going to get the very latest on Hurricane Fiona`s path as millions in the Caribbean remain without power. You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re back. You`re watching MSNBC. Hurricane Fiona right now is a Category 3 storm barreling through the Atlantic pushing north towards Turks & Caicos. In its wake, a path of clear destruction, both the Dominican Republic and Puerto Rico are under a state of emergency adding insult to injury in Puerto Rico. Today marks five years since Hurricane Maria devastated the island territory. Fiona left millions without power after striking Puerto Rico and people are still struggling for access to food and the running water. Search and rescue missions there remain underway, complicated by the destruction. The island and its people are again forced to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC correspondent George Solis where -- he is in Ponce, Puerto Rico right now. George, a threat from this thing you know is far from over. What`s the biggest thing that folks on the ground are telling you about what they need right now?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Peter, right now, they want to know how soon federal aid will get here to help them get back on the power grid. Here we are five years after Hurricane Maria. And instead of talking about how much progress has been made, we`re talking about an island dealing with the effects of a Category 1 hurricane. And this is what a lot of crews are dealing with right now.\par \par \uc2 You see this right here behind me, a snapped power line. And this is just a small sample of the damage and destruction that`s rampant across the island. Not even four -- a couple of feet from me, you can see another downed power line in the street. Luma officials working to assess this now that the weather has cooperated, but we should note that there are still parts of this island that are still getting some rain as the hurricane still moves away from the island. And already, this island has seen 25, 30 inches of rain in some parts. So this, as you said is adding insult to injury here for the people.\par \par \uc2 And with those without power, you also now have the heat to contend with. So what`s happening is that people are stuck in their homes in areas where those search and rescue groups are still trying to get to them but they can`t because of these floodwaters that are still rising in some parts of the island. And resources are starting to come towards the aisle. We know that the FEMA Administrator is expected to touch down and bring with them some resources, some power generators, some much-needed food, some meals.\par \par \uc2 Governor Pierluisi saying this is a catastrophe for the island, and rightly so. But he is saying that it`s not quite fair to call this a disaster to the power grid because they expect their response to getting people back on line with the power to be much better than their response to Hurricane Maria. But people that I have talked to say they are frustrated, they are irritated, and on this day of all days to have to relive this experience with this storm that barreled through this island, it is just a painful reminder of the setback that they now have to face with working to rebuild, working to recover those losses. And they`re wondering how long it will take them to get back on that power grid. The governor is saying this might take days.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: But some wonder if it`s not going to be weeks, maybe even months in those areas that are still being affected by this storm, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, there`s so much for one island territory to have to deal with now again. George Solis there on the ground in Puerto Rico, George, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are some other new developments to tell you about in the Mar-a-Lago probe with that special master conference starting in Brooklyn in just about 30 minutes or so from now between the former president`s legal team and the Department of Justice, what we can expect to go down in that hearing. It`s next here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Breaking news right now. You see the president in the Roosevelt Room speaking about the DISCLOSE Act that would require Super PACs and so- called dark money groups to disclose donors giving over $10,000. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Before I want to speak with you very briefly about a vote in Congress this week that addresses a serious problem facing our democracy in my view. I`ve proposed much earlier on that we should just publicly fund all of our elections, but I`m not -- I don`t have the support for that position. But there`s a second position that is very good. And that is the role of money in politics and how we can begin to solve that problem.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 There`s something I`ve always -- it`s something I`ve always cared about. And at its best, our democracy serves all people equally, no matter wealth or privilege. But here`s the deal. There`s much too much money that flows in the shadows to influence our elections. It`s called dark money. It`s hidden.\par \par \uc2 Right now, advocacy groups can run ads on issues, attacking or supporting a candidate right until Election Day without disclosing who`s paying for that ad. Too often powerful special interests use front groups to run these ads to win at any cost. Even foreign entities that aren`t allowed to contribute to political campaigns use dark money loopholes to try to influence our elections.\par \par \uc2 And here`s this one recent example. A conservative activist who spent as was his right decades working to put enough conservative justice on the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade now has access to $1.6 billion in dark money to do more damage, and from our perspective, and restrict more freedoms. As far as we know, that`s one of the biggest dark money transfers in our history.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the deal. The public only found out about the $1.6 billion transfer because someone tipped off some of your reporters, otherwise, we still wouldn`t know about it. But now we know and there`s something we can do about it. This week, the Senate is going to vote on the Disclosure Act. House Democrats already approved this act, and Senator Sheldon Whitehouse of the -- of Rhode Island helped write this bill as well.\par \par \uc2 Here`s some of the things that would do. advocacy group advocacy groups that run ads to influence elections would have to disclose any contribution above $10,000 that they receive. And foreign entities would be banned from making any contributions for these ads, period. Republicans should join Democrats to pass the DISCLOSURE Act and get it on my desk right away. And dark money has become so common in our politics. I believe sunlight is the best disinfectant. And I acknowledge it`s an issue for both parties.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the key difference. Democrats in the Congress support more openness and accountability, Republicans in Congress, so far, don`t -- so far don`t. I hope they`ll come around. They should follow the lead of Republican governors and state legislatures in Tennessee and Wyoming that have passed disclosure laws. Let`s remember, getting dark money out of our politics has been a bipartisan issue in the past.\par \par \uc2 My deceased friend, \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , spent a lot of time fighting for campaign finance reform. For him, it was a matter of fundamental fairness. And he was 100 percent right about that. Ultimately, this comes down to public trust. Dark money erodes public trust. We need to protect public trust. And I`m determined to do that.\par \par \uc2 So I want to thank you all very much and hope that Congress, the Senate will act on this in a favorable way so I can sign that law -- into law as rapidly as possible. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, why are the borders more overwhelmed under your watch?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it`s an hour later, I`m sorry. (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the border, why is the border more overwhelmed under your watch, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because there are three countries that are never have it. There are fewer -- there are fewer immigrants coming from Central America and from Mexico. This is a totally different circumstance. What`s on my watch now is Venezuela, Cuba, and Nicaragua. And the ability to send them back to those states is not rational. You could send them back and have them while we`re working with Mexico and other countries to see if we can stop the flow. But that`s the difference. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What exactly is happening why is anyone sending these much migrants to Delaware? Do you have any comment or respond to that, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He should come visit. We have a beautiful shoreline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: He should come visit, we have a beautiful shoreline. That appeared to be present Biden`s response to the Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  having recently sent some migrants to Martha`s Vineyard, Texas Governor Abbott sending some of those migrants to Washington, DC where they were dropped off outside the vice president`s residence. There had been reporting today that there was another plane of migrants headed to the president`s home state of Delaware. You heard the president`s rebuttal there.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli, my colleague, is joining us now outside the White House. And, Mike, the intention from this event today before the president heads to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  was to make news on what`s called the DISCLOSE Act ahead of the midterms to try to crack down on that dark money, the money that comes from anonymous sources. Instead, he really waded into this issue about immigration that`s been amplified by those Republican governors in the recent political stunts.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Peter. First of all, I think the value of the President`s del -- over Rehoboth Beach home just went up a little bit with that free ad for the Rehoboth Beach tourism bureau but the president making light of what is a very serious situation at the moment. I`ve been in touch with the governor`s office in Delaware who says that there are officials working with advocacy groups as well, preparing for the potential arrival of these migrant flights.\par \par \uc2 And you also heard the president try to make a distinction between this border issue as a consequence of what Republicans argue has been an open border policy of Democratic administrations, including Biden at this moment, and saying that we`re dealing with a different situation right now. There are many people fleeing the political situations, and you heard him mentioned those countries, Cuba, Venezuela and Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 And one of the challenges facing this administration is that they don`t have relationships with these countries that would allow them to work as they have with countries like Mexico on a "Remain in Mexico" policy or to undo the consequences of the Trump administration`s actions there. So that is part of the challenge now. And the president repeating what we have heard repeatedly from the White House podium from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the press secretary, that these Republican governors are engaged in political stunts using struggling individuals as pawns.\par \par \uc2 Now as to the subject of the president`s remarks there, the Disclose Act, now this is an important issue, the idea of campaign finance, but not one that has been really front and center in our politics today. But I thought, Peter, it was really interesting to hear how the president tried to link this issue of campaign finance to what is one of the main pillars of the Biden team Democratic midterm election strategy, which is to talk about how these dark money groups have been able to, for instance, lead to the overturning of Roe vs. Wade, using the Supreme Court as an issue.\par \par \uc2 And so that is the president trying to use an issue that is important to a number of Democrats on Capitol Hill. We are likely to see this vote go down. It needs 60 votes that it doesn`t have. But to bring it back to what has been a key focus for this White House, two pillars. One, the economy, but second, this idea that voting rights, reproductive rights, other core freedoms are at risk. And in Biden`s case today pointing to the influence of dark money in eroding them.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. So it`s a good question. Why would this go down, why does it lack the votes necessary with Republicans I think across the board opposed to this Disclose Act? Among them some Republicans like Ted Cruz have argued that companies have the right to express themselves through anonymous donations. That is the sort of obstacle that`s existed in this effort as the president indicated there to try to get the Disclose Act pushed forward through Congress.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli, with that breaking news on a topic we didn`t anticipate would come up there, the topic of immigration, obviously. Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Another major headline we`re watching right now is the Mar-a-Lago case and it returns to center stage today as the newly appointed special master, that independent third party, kicks off his first hearing. It should happen about 30 minutes from now. The Trump and DOJ legal teams are scheduled to make that first appearance before Judge Raymond Dearie at his Brooklyn courthouse. This comes after a new filing by Mr. Trump`s lawyers that call the case a, quote, "document storage dispute that spiraled out of control."\par \par \uc2 In an earlier filing, they refused to provide specifics about which of the seized documents Mr. Trump claims were declassified before he left office. Within the last hour, Republican attorneys general for 11 states have filed what`s called an amicus brief accusing the Biden administration of, in their words, ransacking the home of a political rival and attempting to, quote, "trade on the reputation of the Department of Justice and the intelligence community to thwart the appointment of a neutral special master."\par \par \uc2 There`s also new reporting from the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " that suggests that the former president was warned late last year of the legal perils of holding on to classified material. It`s a lot to try to make sense of. And for more to help us do that is NBC`s Tom Winter. Also Charles Coleman, a former Brooklyn prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Tom, tell us more about that new filing by the Trump team right now. What fundamentally is their argument here?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIONS CORRESPONDENT: Well, they`ve got two filings in two cases and fundamentally in the appeals court, Peter, they`re arguing that essentially the Justice Department should just sit back and wait and they should not get what they are asking the appellate court for. They should sit back and wait until the special master has done their job. They made it clear that a judge in Florida, one appointed by the former president at West Palm Beach has made a determination that a special master is the best way to go. That it provides the appropriate oversight, they say.\par \par \uc2 And so as a result, it`s real simple. Let`s just wait until that process is done. That`s their argument. Federal prosecutors say, look, between us and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we need to get moving here. We might have a national defense or national security issue that we need to look into. The Office of the Director of National Intelligence wants to conduct a study and a review on these documents that were found at the property. Some of which were looking at presumably on screen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 And also we still need to conduct a federal investigation. So let`s put the special master issue aside and let`s get to work. We don`t know when the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals is going to make a decision on this whether or not they`re going to have some sort of oral argument or whether they`re going to issue some sort of paper opinion or ruling on this. So that`s just something that we`re going to have to follow.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 The other filing is the one that you talked about. That`s before the special master essentially saying hey, we don`t want to disclose whether or not we declassify or the former president declassified on his way out of White House certain documents. We might want to preserve that if we challenge the search warrant in and of itself. We might want to preserve that for a defense, if there`s any indictment in this investigation. So those are the thrust of the two main arguments they`re making in totally separate courts today but of course all over the same issue.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, no doubt. We`re glad to have you to help explain it to us.\par \par \uc2 Charles, let me ask you about the latter that Tom was talking about here. What`s happening before that special master and this argument that`s being made on behalf of Mr. Trump`s lawyers basically that they don`t want to have to make any argument. They don`t even want to wade into the conversation about what is or is not declassified. To be clear there`s no evidence that any of those documents were declassified to this point. But it does appear clear even though Raymond Dearie, this special master, was one of those individuals suggested as a potential special master by Mr. Trump`s own attorneys, that he has sort of put them on their heels a bit here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHT ATTORNEY: Well, I do expect that we`re going to see a very different tone out of Judge Dearie than we`ve seen thus far particularly out of Florida. And I think one of the reasons for that is when you looked at it objectively from 30,000-foot view, stepping away from it, you realize that a lot of what has happened thus far has been around the length and sort of these obscure tactics in terms of trying to get this distraction off track in terms of dealing with the actual issue.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that Judge Dearie is going to go for that. I think part of what we can expect to see today is a very different tone. I think that he`s going to be very clear about really outlining the issues from both parties, making clear what he believes is not an issue, what he agrees is an issue and then what he will allow both parties to argue even further in brief as to what the issues are.\par \par \uc2 Of course, as Tom sort of intimated, the strategy for team Trump is basically going to be make the issues as wide as possible, make this process go as long as possible. Try to drag it out without being committal so that they preserve as many options moving forward as they might. Whereas the DOJ is going to seek to make it as narrow as possible to eliminate any issues in question or as many as they can to make it so that the judge does not have very much that he has to decide.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, in addition to the concerns about national security, in addition to concerns about delaying the actual investigation, there`s also the issue that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has actually already gone through these documents. And so from the integrity standpoint of what is needed to make this investigation move forward, the DOJ already knows what`s there. So they also have to sort of navigate that with a very thin needle in terms of how they thread with respect to this judge.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Charles, let me ask you. The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that the former president was warned that holding on to classified material when he left office could put him in legal jeopardy. The "Times" writes that former Trump lawyer Eric Herschman sought to impress upon Mr. Trump the seriousness of the issue and the potential for investigations and legal exposure if he did not return the documents, particularly any classified material.\par \par \uc2 So how does that affect a potential criminal case against the former president? Obviously, you know, you can`t say that you didn`t know if there`s now evidence that you were told just that.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I think it speaks directly to the question of intent because you`ve been informed about the criminality of your actions and yet you continued in those actions, even after you had knowledge. I also think that one of the things that it does is that it really casts a strong amount of doubt on this notion of declassification because if in fact you are claiming that you declassified certain documents, at the point that you have been told that these documents need to be returned or at the point that you have been told that you are potentially in violation of the law to hold on to these documents, that for most people would have been the ideal time to step forward and say we have actually declassified documents.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a list of the documents we declassified. And therefore, we are not in violation of the law. That did not happen. And so when you look at the timeline, it certainly does not suggest or support what team Trump is trying to argue now in totality looking at how everything played out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tom, help us, here with some other news that we just learned about over the last couple of hours, that the Republican attorneys general, and think it`s 11 states, West Virginia, Kentucky, I think Mississippi among them.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Texas.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Have filed this amicus brief accusing the Biden administration of ransacking the home of a political rival. They say it was an effort to trade on the reputation of the Department of Justice to thwart the appointment of a neutral special master. Can you walk us through the significance of that? What if any impact that potentially has?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Well, legally, and I think from an impact perspective, it probably has very little. I mean, the court is going to decide this case on the merits. What the Department of Justice attorneys have presented to the court, what Trump`s own attorneys have presented to them, look at the decision that was made in the Southern District of Florida, applying these sort of case law or precedent to that or create new precedent if they think that some should exist here, though I think it`s probably a little bit more straightforward than that.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:12]\par \par \uc2 The amicus curiae or friend of the court filings, Peter, service a way for interested parties to put something on the record and sometimes in hopes of swaying the judiciary`s opinion. But I`m still waiting for the first time in a political sense where I see that come to fruition. Sometimes amicus curiaes that are filed by potential victims, filed by the press, filed by others have sometimes an impact on the judiciary`s decision.\par \par \uc2 I just don`t know here if that`s going to be the case. And so just in, you know, reading that filing and seeing how these cases go in the past, I don`t see the impact of it being particularly high. I would say that if a group of Democratic attorney generals also wrote to the court, I just don`t see this making much of a difference as far as this instant ruling.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tom, at the end of the day, it`s a lot of back and forth. For a lot of people trying to pay attention, when does this thing resolve itself? What sort of timeframe are we playing with here?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: You know, just before coming on with you, Peter, I looked back at the most recent search warrant affidavit that we have that was unsealed and unredacted. So the most unredacted version of this still has approximately 54 percent that is redacted. If you look at the paragraphs that are redacted in full or in part. That`s a long way of saying we don`t still know a lot about this investigation.\par \par \uc2 I don`t anticipate anything happening until the end of the midterms. Certainly until the special master wraps up, if that`s the path that ends up being taken here. So bear minimum, I don`t expect any sort of decisions to be made until at least the end of November.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. Democrats like folks talking about Trump. Mr. Trump likes talking about Mr. Trump, but Republicans haven`t really enjoyed this conversation. It`s obviously taken attention away from other issues they want to focus on.\par \par \uc2 Tom Winter and Charles Coleman, we appreciate both of your expertise.\par \par \uc2 Just a few hours from now, the president as we`ve noted is going to head to New York. He`s going to be addressing the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  there tomorrow. So what do we expect to hear from him as it relates to the war in Ukraine, global inflation and climate change? Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:29]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just a few hours from now, President Biden is going to head back to New York where he is preparing to address the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  on Wednesday. Ukraine of course expected to be front and center there at the meeting of world leaders as the Russian invasion nears its seventh month. But also focus on the global economy as we fight record high inflation here at home and overseas. Climate change as the summer reflected some of the most catastrophic extreme weather events we`ve ever seen is certain to come up as well.\par \par \uc2 And I want to bring in the Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  right now. We`ve got a full plate of topics.\par \par \uc2 Madame Secretary, we appreciate you being here. President Biden, as you know, just vowed that the U.S. is going to stay with Ukraine as long as they need help. He said that commitment is ironclad. How critical is this moment for the U.S. to shore up international support for Ukraine`s counteroffensive?\par \par \uc2 WENDY SHERMAN\uc2 , DEPUTY SECRETARY OF STATE: Great to be with you, Peter. You know, I have been here at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . now for about a day and a half. And the global support for Ukraine is profound. I met with what`s called the B-9 today, which are the eastern flank states. These are states that were part of the old Soviet Union. They understand what Russia is all about. They are talking to colleagues all over the world here at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and I think President Biden will find that the world understands that this is critical.\par \par \uc2 This is about the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . charter, sovereignty, territorial integrity, the right of countries to make their own political choices. These are the principles of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . charter and countries all over the world are committed to that charterer. He`s going to be speaking to that tomorrow. And I think he`s going to have a ready audience.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Secretary Sherman, let me ask you. President Biden warned \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  during the interview he did with "60 Minutes" not to use chemical or tactical nuclear weapons. . He said don`t. He repeated it multiple times. What is your level of concern that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will take drastic actions as Russian troops there are on increasingly it appears on the ropes?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, I agree with you that Russian troops appear on the ropes. We see Putin talking about mobilizing more people in his country. We see him putting forward the sham referenda in some of the parts, regions of Ukraine. A sham referendum that he thinks he`s going to conduct in three days. All of this is desperation by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And he is on the ropes. It of course increases our concern that he will use kinds of weapons of war that he should not.\par \par \uc2 He`s already weaponized food. He`s already weaponized energy. People all over the world are suffering from Putin`s war. And so I think we should be concerned. But hopefully he understands what the president just conveyed. Don`t, don`t, don`t. Russia, the world will be changed forever. And none of it will be good for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or for the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You refer to that partial mobilization. We heard from the National Security adviser. Your colleague Jake Sullivan was speaking at the White House even as we were on the air here. He said that Russia may be resorting to this effort to bring more troops, Russian troops, into the mix. That might require as the U.S. suggests conscripts, basically, a new draft there.\par \par \uc2 Talk about why that is so significant right now, the potential that Russia may try to fuel its fight with more individuals, which, again, you know, the U.S. has spent billions of dollars already? This does have the potential to extend what is already just a dramatic and awful war.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: It does have the potential to extend, but it also is going to send a signal to the people in Russia that their leader is desperate. That he needs to draft people. That he has to bring in conscript. I think Peter we cannot end this interview without talking about the Yale Observatory report today about Izium, the part of Ukraine where we are finding hundreds of bodies that are buried.\par \par \uc2 This follows the horrifying circumstances of Bucha that was destroyed by Russia. We see that the Ukrainians have moved on Kharkiv in a very affirmative offensive. So everyone wants to get to peace. Everyone does not want this war to go on. The people of Ukraine most of all but they also believe that they must defend their country, their democracy and the values that we all hold dear.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And as we speak about Russia, and thank you for raising that, Russia has yet to offer a serious counterproposal according to multiple officials to the U.S.`s proposed prisoner exchange for Paul Whalen and Brittney Griner, the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star. So then how does the U.S. get these Americans back? \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  will be there. Is this likely to be discussed on the sidelines in New York?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, look. The United States has put a really good proposal on the table to bring home Brittney Griner and Paul Whalen. Each one of these situations is quite difficult and you`ve just seen that Mark Frerichs has returned from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: We work on these cases day and night because our first priority is protecting American citizens at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Let me ask you if I can about some of the news the president made on the issue of Taiwan. He said in the interview that China -- he said we know that China is watching the invasion through the prism of Taiwan right now while the U.S. policy on Taiwan has not changed. This is what President Biden said about Taiwan over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, \uc2 CBS\uc2  "60 MINUTES" CORRESPONDENT: Would U.S. forces defend the island?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, if in fact there was an unprecedented attack.\par \par \uc2 PELLEY: So unlike Ukraine, to be clear, sir, U.S. forces, U.S. men and women, would defend Taiwan in the event of a Chinese invasion?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So this isn`t one-off. As you know well, the White House has walked back in the past the president`s comments on a series of occasions on Taiwan and the potential future U.S. involvement. What signal does that send to the rest of the world that`s watching?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, let us be really clear. Our policy has not changed. The president is responding to hypotheticals. Our policy remains exactly what it has always been. Our One China policy remains as it has been.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Let me ask you about one other issue, the policy as it relates to Iran right now. You`ve said that we`re in a stalemate over nuclear talks. Any potential for a direct meeting between the U.S. and Iran on the sidelines of \uc2 UNGA\uc2  this week, the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I don`t expect that at all, Peter. But let me be clear. The Europeans, the E-3, really negotiating as the front of the negotiation on the Joint Conference of Planet Action has put forward to Iran an excellent proposal that ensures compliance for compliance, and a return to the JCPOA, and now things are in Iran`s court. They need to make a decision about whether this is in their interest.\par \par \uc2 I believe that it is. I believe that the president of the United States is committed to ensuring that Iran never gets a nuclear weapon. The JCPOA is a critical step in that regard to ensure that they never do get such a nuclear weapon because they`d be able to project astonishing power into the Middle East and around the rest of the world so I hope Iran understands that this is really important for them, for us, for the world.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  is the U.S. deputy secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Sherman, thank you for your time and being with us.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: New details as we just noted are coming of Izium where rescue workers are pulling out bodies from mass gravesites found at the outskirts of that town just freed from Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 NBC senior international correspondent Richard Engel now with more. And we should warn you that some of the images in Richard`s report here are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: The Russian military has suffered stunning reversals here in eastern Ukraine. They`ve lost thousands of square miles of territory, but what they have left behind as the Russian military receded is evidence of what Ukrainians say is war crimes. And here, they are excavating bodies. We are on the outskirts of Izium, we`re in the forest here. And just today, these rescue workers have pulled out about 90 bodies.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 They`ve already filled up one refrigerated truck and it looks like they are going to fill up a second. There is a combination of Ukrainian soldiers here. One mass grave was filled with Ukrainian soldiers. Others have been filled with civilians and some of these rescue workers say that they are finding ample evidence of abuse that they`re seeing bodies that had broken bones, that had their hands tied behind their backs.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, not far from here, we went to a Russian headquarters that was used as an interrogation and torture center, and when the interrogations and the torture weren`t enough, there was already two executions and the body ended up here. And many Ukrainians are wondering why. Why the Russians were so brutal. Why they were carrying out such atrocities not just here. We saw a similar pattern outside of Kyiv when Russian troops were forced out of that area.\par \par \uc2 There were mass graves discovered there. There was evidence of torture. There were torture rooms. And Ukrainians are wondering why does this continue to happen. And the best answer they can come up with is that the Russians are overwhelmed. They`re not performing properly. They`re getting frustrated and that they`ve dehumanized the Ukrainians with all the propaganda, all the hate, all the anger that you see on Russian television, that you hear from Russian officials.\par \par \uc2 This is the result. The hate policy and you send soldiers off into battle and you reward the very soldiers who do these kinds of things. You create a cycle of violence. A cycle of atrocities and more mass graves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Richard Engel there reporting. It`s hard to watch but it`s critical that we don`t turn away.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. My colleague Hallie Jackson is in the chair for "KATY TUR REPORTS." That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`m Hallie Jackson, in for Katy Tur this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And as we are coming on the air, you`ve got lawyers for the Department of Justice and for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just now sitting down together at a federal courthouse in Brooklyn. We`ve just seen attorneys for the Trump team walk in. I want to show you the live picture of it. You can see who is outside.\par \par \uc2 Both parties asked to be there by Judge Raymond Dearie, that special master or independent third party in the Mar-a-Lago investigation. Here`s the focus, right.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220921ei9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for September 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Joe Fryer, Vaughn Hillyard, Joyce Vance, Shannon Pettypiece, Tim  Miller, Seema Mody, Ben White, Morgan Chesky, Kavita Patel, Jay Gray \par \uc2 8,255 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hello, I'm Joe Fryer in for Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Day weekend kicks off today, we're also accelerating into the final stretch of the midterms with President Biden throwing the first big political punch. He used last night's primetime speech to slam former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and millions of his followers. The president is calling them MAGA Republicans saying they're a threat to America. With that theme, President Biden hopes to set the narrative for the campaign season to come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As I stand here tonight, equality and democracy are under assault. We do ourselves no favor to pretend otherwise. Too much of what's happening in our country today is not normal. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans represented extremism that threatens the very foundations of our republic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The speech featured some hopeful tones, but it made headlines for its dark warnings about what would happen if Americans allowed the MAGA ideology to grow. And with more appearances to come in Pennsylvania and beyond, the President is showing that he's unafraid to draw sharp contrast not just between himself and Trump, but between Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Not every Republican, not even a majority of Republicans are MAGA Republicans. Not every Republican embraces their extreme ideology. But there's no question that the Republican Party today is dominated, driven, and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The \uc2 RNC\uc2  rejected that distinction releasing a statement saying "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the divider-in-chief and epitomizes the current state of the Democrat party, one of divisiveness, disgust, and hostility towards half the country." As for Trump, he'll on his own -- hold his own rally in Pennsylvania tomorrow. The first is the search at Mar-a-Lago. He called last night's speech angry and called the president insane. But if President Biden's goal is to keep election deniers and the January 6 insurrection, front and center, well, then Trump is doing some of that work for him.\par \par \uc2 Just a few hours ago, federal judge Aileen Cannon ordered the release of a new more detailed list of the items seized from Mar-a-Lago. It's part of the court battle between the Justice Department and Trump's legal team over a so-called special master, someone who would comb through the items to determine what's relevant or what's a privilege. The judge has yet to rule on whether a special master is actually needed.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in senior White House reporter for \uc2 NBC News Digital\uc2 , Shannon Pettypiece, NBC's Vaughn Hillyard is in West Palm Beach, Florida, Joyce Vance is a former U.S. attorney and professor at the University of Alabama School of Law, also an MSNBC legal analyst, and Tim Miller is writer-at-large for The Bulwark and an MSNBC political analyst. Good to have all of you with us as we begin this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, let's start with the latest on this Mar-a-Lago search. What can you tell us about this list that was released today? Did we learn anything new here?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, this is a list that was revealed here at the urging of Trump's team yesterday in that hearing. We do not have a ruling at this time on the special master. It could come today over the weekend, next week here. But when you're looking at this more detailed list, it doesn't tell you exactly what the contents of each of these documents were. But it is telling of just the extent to how many documents \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in possession of more than two months after the custodian of his records signed a sworn affidavit on his behalf, contending that Trump had complied with the subpoena requests from the Department of Justice to turn over all such relevant documents.\par \par \uc2 When you look at which documents the DOJ seized on August 8, that is where this is a compelling breakdown. You see 18 documents listed are marked top secret, 54 documents marked secret, 31 documents marked confidential. But then this is where it's interesting here. We don't know exactly what this means to know, but the Department of Justice laid out in its findings that it acquired 48 empty folders with "classified banners," as well as 42 empty folders labeled returned to staff secretary or military aid, and then another important point, more than 11,000 other unclassified government documents and photos.\par \par \uc2 What exactly the contents of each of those are? We do not know. And whether this special master will ultimately be appointed or not, we do not know that at this time. We do know that the DOJ and Trump's team, however, had requested of the judge that they be allowed to put a list of potential special master options forward by September 7, which would be this upcoming Wednesday, Joe.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Joyce, let's talk more about that. It seems like so far yesterday's hearing included something for both sides. On the one hand, the judge's keeping the door open for a special master, on the other, she's not telling the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , hey, stop reviewing the documents. How do you read what's go going on here?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it's important that she has not imposed any sort of preliminary injunction which would have frozen the government in place. They are in fact free to continue doing their work, whether that's the intelligence community side review or DOJ agents and prosecutors continuing to move their case forward. So that's the something for the government. As for Trump, Judge Cannon did hold open this notion and seemed quite frankly inclined to enter an order that would appoint a special master for this case.\par \par \uc2 But that's a very awkward fit. Special masters are used almost exclusively in cases where a lawyer's office has been searched. And you sweep up in that sort of a search as you begin to sort through it, some material that might pertain to clients who aren't involved in the criminal investigation. It can be helpful in those cases to have someone who's not a DOJ employee, someone who's an outsider sorting through items to make sure that nothing improper happens.\par \par \uc2 But that's very decidedly not the situation at Mar-a-Lago where to the extent, there's anything that's attorney-client privilege, it's very few documents, DOJ has already segregated them, and they, of course, relate to the former president, which means we could conceivably see litigation over whether there's a crime-fraud exception that brings them into public view. This isn't a case with a big array of otherwise privileged material that mandates a special master. And that's why it's very unusual and we'll all be watching to see what Judge Cannon does in her ruling, but even entertaining the idea is something out of the ordinary.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Shannon, let's turn to the current president and the speech he gave last night, really setting the tone for the midterms to come. He's always talked about trying to be a unifier. Many would say that goes against what the speech was delivered last night. How is the president trying to square last night's speech with his message of also trying to be a unifier?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Well, the White House has indicated that this is going to be a theme we're going to continue to hear from the president. And well, you heard him in that clip, you played a few moments ago, trying to thread this needle between mainstream "Republicans," people out there who might be viewing who identify themselves as part of the Republican Party, but don't necessarily believe in some of the more extreme ideology that we have been hearing from the former president, from his close allies in Congress when it comes to things like denying the election or a tax on law enforcement. So you see, the president tried to counter that narrative as well.\par \par \uc2 But you're also seeing a broader strategy here of the White House trying to not make this just a referendum election. Oftentimes, midterm elections are a referendum on the party in power. So as much as we're going to hear, the White House and Democrats talking about things they believe they have accomplished around infrastructure, around prescription drug costs, around you know trying to lower gas prices. Also expect to hear more of this talk about painting the picture of what the choice is between the Democratic Party and the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And the picture that the president is paying last night, and we can certainly anticipate to hear more of is a Republican party that has been essentially co-opted by the former president, by extreme "MAGA Republicans" who are, you know, buy into some of these conspiracy theories, election lies. That is the picture that the White House is trying to paint of the Republican Party at this point going into the midterm elections to make this a choice election as much as it is going to be a referendum on the president's past two years.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Tim, I want to read some of Peter Baker's work from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . He said. "The question is whether focusing on the battle for democracy as Mr. Biden framed it, will move his own voters more than inflation, crime, immigration, and other issues will move the other side." In the same article he pointed out, he's tried this appeal for democracy before. It hasn't really changed the national conversation. Are things changing now? Could this be the point where things start to change and democracy does become a bigger issue?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I -- look, I think democracy has been becoming a bigger issue. I think that it's been tied to -- if you look at the polls, it's been going up. It's something that people care about. I think they see these extreme Republican candidates getting nominated. And more and more who are concerned about the anti-Democratic candidates are being nominated. Obviously, abortion has become a greater issue after the Dobbs ruling.\par \par \uc2 So I'm just -- I'm not sure what last night's speech did to advance that objective. It seems like something that was probably cathartic for democratic partisans who wants to hear their president fighting. I understand that impulse. And I think that everything he said was true, by the way, about how extreme the MAGA Republicans are. But as a strategic effort, I think what this president's job is, is not just to uplift -- is not just to satisfy the anger of his own base, but to try to bring more people into the tent. And this is what he did successfully in 2020 that's why he won handily.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And I didn't see anything last night that was -- that was bringing in when he tried to divide between the MAGA Republicans and these other Republicans, what is -- what is he offering to the other group to say, hey, stick with me, you know, we will protect democracy together. That part was missing last night for me.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Vaughn, I mean, I want to bring you in here because you, for years have talked face to face with tons of both Trump supporters and then more moderate Republicans. Clearly, President Biden, he was trying to draw a line between the two, reaching out to Republicans who are closer to the middle and trying to drive a wedge between them and more conservative Republicans, do you think that can be effective as we head into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right. I think -- you know, I've been covering hundreds and hundreds, quite literally, of Republican events over the last seven years from Iowa barns to Alabama churches to MAGA rallies in Phoenix on up through almost every state in the country here. And I can tell you that this Republican Party, you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that the majority are not MAGA Republicans, but frankly, that was the line that turned my head because I think the election results over the last five months in these Republican primaries speak countered to that declaration that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  even made here. We're talking about the ousting of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yes, you know, it's -- actually, some of those early Republican events that I went through back in 2015 were alongside Mr. Tim Miller himself at Jeb Bush events there. And there's not been many of those types of events that I have felt, except outside the likes of you know, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  events in Wyoming or \uc2 Jeff Flake\uc2  in Arizona, that wing of the party is all but gone in terms of infrastructure wise. At the activist level, at the Republican Party apparatus level, the elements of the Republican Party is all but gone here in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 And when you're talking to voters here, by and large, you're talking to election denying-Republicans, you're talking to folks that are frankly bought into an alternate reality of conspiracy theories, at the same time, there are sub-sect of voters here that did at one point call themselves Republicans. They don't identify as such now. That is how the Democrats are able to pull off wins in Georgia and Arizona, in the likes of Alaska congressional seat, you know.\par \par \uc2 I think that Alaska congressional race here this last month, I think was telling. You saw Republican Nick Begich, the -- among the Republicans that voted for him, half of them said we're not going to vote for either \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 , or we're going to leave it blank, which is telling that there is a sub-sect of this American electorate that is looking for something beyond it. But frankly, it has not been presented to them in any form outside of what the Democratic Party has been offering as an alternative.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And no matter which party you do identify with, Tim, everyone seems to think things are bad. There's a new Quinnipiac poll out this week, it shows 69 percent of both parties believe democracy is in danger of collapse, although we can assume it's probably for very different reasons. How do you think this sets the tone for the campaign in 2022 and then the campaign in 2024?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: That's an important poll for this conversation, right? If you look at -- if you're a Democrat, if you're President Biden, obviously the 69 percent of Republicans that are so deluded about the 2020 election that they think democracy is in threat because they think it was stolen from President Trump are not reachable, right, like those are not people that are going to be gettable in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 So it's that other 29 percent of Republicans who President Biden has to reach out to and make in the case needs to be made to them that the rest of the party has gone so extreme, has gone so far off the deep end, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is somebody they can trust to be a steward for them even -- and the Democratic Congress is something they can trust to be a steward for them, even if they disagree, maybe on some of the particulars of the issues. It's a tough sale.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's why as we get further into the fall, you'll see the Democrats, you know, focusing more on animating their own base, which I've done a good job of this summer, and doing their best to try to cleave off the very small portion of folks that Vaughn is talking about, you know, as like happened in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Joyce, you get the last question here from this panel. We know that former White House Counsel in the Trump administration \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , and his deputy Pat Philbin, they're expected to appear before a federal grand jury investigating January 6, that's supposed to happen today, what are you watching for there?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, we won't know anything that goes on because of grand jury secrecy unless the witnesses choose to reveal some of what goes on today. But prosecutors are undoubtedly hoping to hear firsthand about conversations between these two and the former president. The most difficult part of putting together any sort of a white collar case or any sort of a case that's based on fraud or any sort of a case where the state of mind, the intent of the individuals who are subjects or targets is. Proving that state of mind really requires a lot of circumstantial evidence.\par \par \uc2 And here's a rare opportunity to talk with witnesses who spoke with Trump who can explain what they saw of his state of mind, did he intend for events on January 6 to unfold the way that they did or not, this could be some of the most powerful testimony that DOJ will have access to.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right, Shannon, Vaughn, Joyce, and Tim, thank you all. Wishing you all a very happy Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Another investigation to talk about, the January 6 Committee has reached out to former Republican House Speaker \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2 . They're calling on him to testify voluntarily about his role in trying to overturn the 2020 election, A letter sent to Gingrich and released by the committee says members have evidence that Gingrich was talking to top Trump advisors about TV ads intended to amplify false claims of election fraud. It also alleges that Gingrich tried to "arouse anger around the country and specifically in Georgia, despite threats to local election officials there." The committee set September 19 as the date for Gingrich to testify, no word yet if he intends to do so.\par \par \uc2 Hours ago, the new jobs report was released. We're going to break down the latest numbers and what it all means for the economy, for inflation, and for the midterms. Plus the latest from Mississippi where hundreds of thousands still don't have water. We're talking about homes, schools, hospitals, without AC in 90-degree heat. And then about 24 hours, the Artemis One launch will be getting a redo after Monday's delay. So will tomorrow be a success? I'm going to ask a former \uc2 NASA\uc2  official ahead. You're watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The latest jobs report is out and shows a labor market that is still hot but may be starting to cool. The U.S. economy added 315,000 jobs in August. That's a solid number on par with expectations, but far less than the 528,000 jobs added in July. So let's dig into these numbers with Seema Mody, CNBC's global markets reporter, and Ben White, Politico's chief economic correspondent, good to have both of you with us. Seema, first of all, just walk us through the jobs report. What are the biggest takeaways here?\par \par \uc2 SEEMA MODY, MSNBC REPORTER: Well, the big takeaway here is that it shows the jobs market is slowing, Joe, but still remains a bright spot in the overall economy. We did see broad-based jobs growth in retail, healthcare, hospitality, adding 31,000 jobs in the month of August, a slightly slower pace than in previous months. But another encouraging sign is the labor participation rate, which shows us that more people are returning to the workforce.\par \par \uc2 Key question, though, is how this all impacts the Fed's decision on raising interest rates. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  economist writing this morning that this jobs report shows that the rise in unemployment will allow the Fed to slow down the pace of rate hikes, that's partly why stocks are up to date, Joe.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So, Ben, what do you see in this report? Does it mean that the hottest job market we've seen in decades has hit its peak?\par \par \uc2 BEN WHITE, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: It probably has hit its peak. And that's not necessarily a bad thing as Seema is very well laid out there. What the Fed is looking for is a slightly cooling jobs market down from 500,000 to 300,000 or so right in the sweet spot today. And to see the labor force recovering, which is really good news today, 786,000 added to the labor force. We're still not where we were pre-COVID, they're still missing jobs.\par \par \uc2 That is a key element because that means that that does not necessarily need to bump up interest rates very fast, very hard to pull off this economy. It's cooling a little bit on its own, or people coming back to the labor force, taking a little bit of pressure off of wages, or prices should start to come down a little bit without radical Fed action that could drive us into recession.\par \par \uc2 So if you're sitting at the Fed today or you're sitting in the White House today, yes, the headline number is down. But you're pretty pleased with it because you want it to cool off a bit, inflation to come down a bit without stalling out. So I hate to you know bring up the old Goldilocks line. Again, we always use this when we get this not a too hot, not too cold number. That's kind of what this is. Choose your cliche, Goldilocks sweet spot, whatever. We kind of hit it today.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, it seems to be the Fed's goal right now is still -- Ben, we know inflation, and the economy there consistently among the top concerns for voters. President Biden didn't mention either of those in his speech last night that was very much focused on democracy. So what is this jobs report mean for the president and his party? We're less than 10 weeks out from the midterms right now and we know that even when job numbers are good, Americans tend to pay more attention to the prices they pay at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Right. Yes, I mean, for Biden's numbers to really improve on the economy, inflation needs to come down more. We need to be paying less for food. Gas has already come down. Some you've seen that reflected in Biden's poll numbers increasing a bit. I think this number is helpful to him on that front because it probably means inflation is coming down.\par \par \uc2 The bigger overall picture here politically is the economy and inflation remain huge issues and you'll hear Biden talk about it all the time. They've receded a little bit and you know, the Trump stuff and democracy and protecting democracy and abortion and some of the other Supreme Court decisions have leapfrogged in some polls, the economy, which is generally good news for Democrats who do not want to run on high inflation, would rather run on some of these other issues, and see an economic picture that's improving. Right now, they've got both of those. Good for the White House, good for Democrats, and a little bit troubling for Republicans right now.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Ben, the fact that the unemployment rate went from 3.5 percent to 3.7 percent, that was unexpected. What are we supposed to read into that?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes. I mean, that can happen for good reasons and bad reasons. The bad reasons are that people are losing jobs and so the rate goes up. The good reason that can happen is lots more people are looking for jobs. And that's what happened here. Nearly, you know, three-quarters of a million people came back into the labor force. So that ticked unemployment from 3.5 to 3.7 but don't despair over that. That's actually weirdly kind of a good thing.\par \par \uc2 It's still a very low overall unemployment rate. It just means that people who were on the sidelines see that they can get decent jobs now at good wages, and finally, are coming back into the labor force in hospitality and other areas. So that's a good thing. You never want to say, hey, higher unemployment is good, but in this instance, it happened for a good reason which is larger labor.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Seema, markets have been open for a few hours now. What are they telling us about how Wall Street feels about these numbers?\par \par \uc2 MODY: Well, I think the positive reaction in the stock market that we're seeing today with the DOW, S&P500, and NASDAQ all sharply higher suggest that this job support was weak enough to suggest the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will not raise rates at the faster than expected rate rise that many people were expecting going into this report. But we know the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is so fixated on inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not this -- just the jobs report that they will take into account but the next consumer price index data for the month of August, which comes out in mid-September. Once we get that really like crucial read on inflation, that will tell us whether consumer prices are in fact slowing down and could give us much more clarity as to what to expect from chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at their next meeting in September, later this month.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The big one. Yes, that's the big one everyone's waiting for. Seema Mody and Ben White, thanks for helping us break down this August jobs report. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MODY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: New COVID-19 boosters aimed at targeting Omicron are on the way for millions of eligible Americans. So who can get the shots and how much of a difference will they make in fighting this anticipated winter surge? I'm going to ask one of our favorite experts next. Stay with us. You're watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:14]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Right now President Biden is scheduled to meet with a group of civil rights leaders. It's slated to start at 1:30 behind closed doors inside the White House. The meeting comes less than 24 hours after President Biden delivered that big speech on what the White House described as the battle for the soul of our nation.\par \par \uc2 If we get any reporting on this meeting we'll make to pass it along to you.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans are now eligible for new COVID-19 booster shots specifically tailored to fight the Omicron subvariants. The vaccine should roll out next week and come just in time for an expected winter surge. U.S. Surgeon General Vivek Murthy weighed in this morning on these updated shots from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and \uc2 Moderna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY, U.S. SURGEON GENERAL: This is an important moment, a landmark moment in our fight against COVID. These updated boosters are actually targeted against the most dominant circulating variant. We've seen clearly that our vaccines are remarkably effective at keeping us out of the hospital and saving our lives. If we are up-to-date this booster will give us the best chance to be up-to-date.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: I want to bring in former White House policy director Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 . I can think of no one better to help us answer the many questions that many people have right now. Should I get a shot? When should I get a shot? So let's go through some of them.\par \par \uc2 First of all, how big of a deal are these new boosters and exactly who can get them when they come out in the next week?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Joe, they're a pretty big deal and anyone age 12 or above can get them. If you're above 12 you can get \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , 18 and above can get \uc2 Moderna\uc2 . We should get them -- we've already been halting all our current boosters in our own clinics to expect that we will have these new shots available in a matter of days. Hopefully by next Tuesday, day after Labor Day, maybe sooner in some pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 And if it's been two months since your last shot you can get it. So if it's been two months since your last shot or more you can get it. I'll say that over and over again. And people have asked me if you've had COVID, all of these questions I think are on point because so many of us have had infections over the last several months. So if you've had COVID we'd like to have at least 30 days since you've had your infection but also talk to your doctor in case you think that you might need it sooner. But two months since last shot, at least a month since your last infection.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: I don't care how many times you say two months over the next --\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Two months.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Two months or so. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So a lot of folks who are immunocompromised the boosters and the shots have been most important to them. And there's a decent chance if you are immunocompromised you've already had one booster, you maybe even had two boosters.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right. Two.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: What's the timeline for those folks to get this new booster?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes. So in terms of those recommendations that immunocompromised people fall into exactly this category. We're not asking for people to wait any longer. So in terms of whether or not they should get it sooner, we do not want them to get it sooner than the two months. But we also are trying to educate people that in addition to the boosters, Joe, there's also a monoclonal antibody that we're recommending for immunocompromised people. It's been around for months so that we can prevent infections.\par \par \uc2 So vaccines can help prevent hospitalizations and death, and immunocompromised individuals, they don't have enough to mount that antibody response, they qualify for something called Evusheld, which is a monoclonal antibody, to prevent infections especially in immunocompromised people. So there's a lot of options out there that I bet most Americans don't realize. It's a good time to get refreshers on this, and it's going to be flu season soon, so ask about a flu shot when you get your booster shot.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes. We have to get those again, too. I almost forgot about that one.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So, these are the first shots, if I'm not mistaken, the first COVID shots we've had without any sort of human trials completed.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right. Sure.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So talk a little bit about that, and what do we know about the side effects for this? Do we think they're the same as the original booster shots?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, so we saw some preclinical data that was previewed at the CDC meeting yesterday. The preclinical data showed a really strong antibody response. Again, preclinical, it was in animals.\par \par \uc2 But, Joe, I just want to point out that this is something that we do when we update flu shots. We don't necessarily wait for clinical trials in humans. And from a safety perspective and to your point about side effects we really do expect those to fall in line with what we've seen with previous booster shots. Of course we need to monitor this closely but there was no signal or indication that we should see something unusual or more severe from these shots.\par \par \uc2 The doses, the type of technology are all the same, they just use a different virus strain to develop those vaccines. So all the same side effects that you might have had in your previous shot, you could expect, usually not severe but mostly Tylenol, ibuprofen, some pain medication hours after you get the shot can help with some of those side effects.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Do you expect this booster to roll out for kids under 12 anytime soon especially now with school getting started?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, there were hints of that at the recent meetings. There were comments made by \uc2 FDA\uc2  officials that they are expecting action to be taken for the under 12 group.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 I would expect that to kind of roll out similar to what we saw to vaccines, 5 to 12 first and then under 5 after that. Remember we still have a very low rate of under 5 who are vaccinated with that primary series. And this is worth mentioning, in order to get this booster, you've had to have that original series completed. Either the first two shots if you're an adult or the first two or first three shots if you're under the age of 5.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right. Hopefully we've answered most of the questions there.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Hopefully.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Dr. Kavita "two months" Patel, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: That's right.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Take care.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right. We're getting our first real look at the seismic impact of the pandemic on schools, specifically on our kids' ability to learn. A new federal study compared student achievement from right before the pandemic to kids' performance two years later, and their scores plummeted.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. Math scores were down seven points. This is the first decline in the history of the study. And reading scores sunk five points, the biggest drop in three decades. All of this comes, of course, as kids across the country are heading back to school.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands in Mississippi still have no access to drinkable water. How they're coping with a hot holiday weekend approaching.\par \par \uc2 And new fears of catastrophe in Ukraine as \uc2 U.N\uc2 . inspectors assess damages at Ukraine's largest -- at Europe's largest nuclear plant. We're going to dig into what they found next.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:33]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Today is day five of a crippling water shortage in Jackson, Mississippi. And as a hot and humid Labor Day weekend begins more than 100,000 people in the state's largest city still don't have access to drinkable water. Hospitals are really feeling the crisis. One is running its dialysis unit off a tanker brought in from out-of-state to make sure patients get the treatment they need.\par \par \uc2 Today FEMA administrator Diane Criswell is expected to tour the city one day after calling the situation tragic.\par \par \uc2 NBC's Morgan Chesky is in Jackson. So, Morgan, what's the latest on the ground there? How are people coping as we are now several days into this?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Joe, it's a frustrating situation that's impacting every aspect of life here in Jackson. Nearly 180,000 people rely on this water supply. And it's not just people. It's also businesses and restaurants. We are in the kitchen of Bravo Italian Restaurant and Bar here in Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Joe, this is their first day back open this week because it's finally gotten enough water pressure to be able to run some of the water here. But anything they cook with, anything at all, is coming right out of these bottles that they have to have shipped in pretty much every day.\par \par \uc2 And an important note here, Joe, these bottles have been coming in now for the past 38 days because of that's how long this boil water notice has been in effect. They simply lost pressure as a result of this water treatment plant coming down.\par \par \uc2 Now as you can imagine there's no firm timeline on when this plant will come back online, and that is why residents we spoke to are incredibly frustrated. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't drink the water, never have.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: You've never drank the water? And how long have you lived in Jackson?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Since my daughter was 6.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: How long ago was that? How many years?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: About 15.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: 15 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: More than that, about 25.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: So you haven't trusted the water coming out of your faucet for how long now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 25 years.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: 25 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: And that woman has a key point here, Joe, and that is the fact that this is not the first problem this water system has had. It has been decades of issues. They say that this flood that caused this water treatment plant to go offline has simply been the worst that they've seen during this amount of time that they've been experiencing problems.\par \par \uc2 We did have a chance to speak to the mayor yesterday. He says that they hope pressure will come back up to full amounts hopefully within the next couple of days but there's no definitive proof that that's going to happen. In the meantime they have a rented pump trying to help that out. But when I mentioned that to the restaurant owner here she just called it a band-aid for a bigger problem. Joe?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes. And it's because those water bottles there in front of you, Morgan, I'm sure the restaurant burns through them incredibly quickly.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Chesky in Jackson, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The physical integrity of Europe's largest nuclear power plant has been violated. That's according to \uc2 U.N\uc2 . inspectors. Members of that United Nations team inspecting the Zaporizhzhia nuclear plant in Ukraine returned today to continue assessing physical damage there. The head of the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  will establish a continued presence there. Concerns over a nuclear meltdown have escalated as the fighting around the plant increases.\par \par \uc2 NBC's Jay Gray is in Dnipro, Ukraine. So, Jay, what has the team at the plant found so far?\par \par \uc2 JAY GRAY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Joe, they've found some structural damage there. They continue to inspect and investigate the area. It started with more than a dozen inspectors. There are five that remain in the plant and have been there for the last 24 hours or more, and they continue to try and piece together what damages come from the shelling that's been intense over the last several weeks there, and what it could possibly lead to as far as problems with what is Europe's largest nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, depending on who you ask they've either gotten a good look at everything that's going on there or they haven't seen what's really happening. Many of the inspectors have said that they've been through the plant and seen much of it but not all of it, and that's where Ukrainian officials say there is a significant problem.\par \par \uc2 They say that the tours are being led by Russian soldiers that occupy that plant, and so they're not being shown everything including a crisis room that they say is really vital to this inspection that they're not being allowed into, again, according to Ukrainian officials because troops have kind of taken that area over as their spot and are using it and not allowing the inspectors inside.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:140]\par \par \uc2 They also say that interviews with the Ukrainian employees that are inside and being held there to operate the plant are done with Russian soldiers standing there. And so it's not an open discussion, according to Ukrainian officials. One thing that does seem clear, there is, no matter where they've looked or how much they've seen, significant damage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL MARIANO GROSSI\uc2 , \uc2 IAEA\uc2  DIRECTOR GENERAL: I worry -- I worried and I will continue to be worried about the plant until we have a situation which is more stable, which is more predictable. It is obvious that the plant and the physical integrity of the plant has been violated several times.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAY: Yes, and as you said coming into this, Joe, there were five inspectors in there and continue to be five inspectors right now. Now, some are scheduled to leave in the next couple of days. But there's a real push right now from \uc2 IAEA\uc2  to keep a presence in the plant continually, to make sure that it's being run properly, to make sure that there are no problems and to really assess the risk as they move forward.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Jay, let's talk big picture here. The \uc2 European Commission\uc2  says now more than 9.9 million people have fled Ukraine and arrived in the E.U. since Russia invaded. That's just under the population of the entire state of Michigan. And I know you've reported on the border and from refugee camps especially early in this. Real quickly what kind of resources do still exist for folks?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: It's very slim, and it's going to be difficult. We're moving into winter and it's going to get very cold. A lot of people who are riding it out right now won't have power, won't have heat and obviously will struggle to find just the basic necessity.\par \par \uc2 As you know and we've talked about along the Polish border, they've run out of space and so have a lot of other countries. They're going to have to come together and figure out what to do next.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right, Jay Gray in Ukraine. Jay, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's vice president survived a shocking assassination attempt that was caught on video. A gunman tried to shoot \uc2 Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner\uc2 , but the gun jammed. She was outside her home speaking with supporters when the gunman pulled out a pistol just inches from her face. Police arrested a 35-year-old Brazilian man seconds later. The VP is currently on trial for corruption charges and is facing the possibility of 12 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 In just 24 hours Artemis I's moon mission will prepare to launch again. So will round two be a success? Former \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator is here next.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, you know you can hear the latest news and updates from all of your famous MSNBC hosts anytime, anywhere, on any device with tuned in. Just scan the QR code to hear right now. Stay with us. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:51]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: T minus 24 hours before launch or at least that's the hope. Around this time tomorrow \uc2 NASA\uc2  will be gearing up to launch the Artemis Moon rocket. That initial launch was of course scrubbed earlier this week because of engine issues. Now they're looking to take off between 2:17 p.m. and 4:17 p.m. Eastern Time tomorrow in the U.S. space agency's next attempt to kick start their mission to get astronauts back on the moon.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in former \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator Lori Garver. She has worked on the Artemis I mission and is also the author of the book "Escaping Gravity: My Quest to Transform \uc2 NASA\uc2  and Launch a New Space Age."\par \par \uc2 Lori, good to have you with us. First and foremost, what needs to happen for us to actually see this launch tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 LORI GARVER, FORMER NASA DEPUTY ADMINISTRATOR: Well, it's nice to be with you. I have to correct that I was a deputy administrator, not the administrator. What happens tomorrow is going on already. They're counting down to what they hope as you said will be this liftoff in about 24 hours. They are prepared to start everything earlier this time. They learned a lot from Monday's attempt and I think you're seeing even within the language that they're using for the launch tomorrow a little more questioning that it's a certainty.\par \par \uc2 We all know so many things have to go right including the weather and a couple of technical glitches cropped up in the countdown Monday so they are going to be looking at everything very closely and do their very best to get it off.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right. Good to know that, and apologies for giving you that promotion there a moment ago.\par \par \uc2 Now this project we know it has a big price tag. It's over budget and how big of a necessity is this mission right now? And does it really factor into the growing space race?\par \par \uc2 GARVER: Well, \uc2 NASA\uc2  does have so much riding on this because it was supposed to launch in 2016. This is the first test flight but there is another way to launch it again for two years because this isn't a reusable system in any way, and so the next time they are planning to put people on it and obviously can't do that if this one doesn't work. I think this isn't the biggest part of the space race. I think the space program has really evolved to in the launch arena of being the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind \uc2 NASA\uc2  won't have launched now for over 11 years since the last space shuttle launch themselves. They put their spacecraft, their astronauts. their satellite on private vehicles built by \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  and even \uc2 Arianespace\uc2  launched the Webb telescope. So to me the U. S. is a leader in the space race and it's the leader because it's a partner with our industry.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: You know, it seems like we all like a space launch. It's maybe one of the few things Republicans and Democrats can agree on. That being said, I mean what would you like to see from \uc2 NASA\uc2 ? Do you think this is the right focus right now?\par \par \uc2 GARVER: I think \uc2 NASA\uc2  has a number of things they're doing that are the right focus. This vehicle, this launch has cost $43 billion to get here and I do not think this is the way \uc2 NASA\uc2  should have both designed the vehicle to be based on 1970s technology. We are going back to the moon. That is a wonderful thing for the nation and doing it in a way that drives technology is what really increases our capabilities and our economy and our national security.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:13]\par \par \uc2 And we are doing that through partners with industry and we have I think to have. \uc2 NASA\uc2  focus on those things we have not done before. And rockets are really the infrastructure. It's like the truck. And so spending $43 billion and 12 years doing a rocket and a capsule, I don't think was the best use of \uc2 NASA\uc2 's funding. Now that we're here on the launchpad, though, I wish them every success.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right, Lori Garver, thank you so much for joining us. Have a great holiday weekend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And if you're still looking to make some holiday weekend plans, how about a big screen movie at a tiny price? Yes, tomorrow seeing a new flick could cost you less than a gallon of gas. For one day only on Saturday you can catch the summer's biggest blockbusters for only $3 in a number of movie theaters. The promotion is part of the first ever National Cinema Day. National theater chains like AMC and \uc2 Regal\uc2  are taking part and they hope to boost attendance before the release of even more blockbusters later this year.\par \par \uc2 I've got my three bucks ready to go. My treat if anyone wants to come.\par \par \uc2 That does it for this hour. Be sure to tune in to CHRIS JANSING REPORTS weekdays at 1:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. And you can catch me on "MORNING NEWS NOW," that's weekday mornings 7:00 to 11 a.m. on our streaming network, NBC News Now. "KATY TURR REPORTS" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220903ei9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for August 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Dasha Burns, Shaquille Brewster, Sahil Kapur,  Ken Dilanian, Melissa Murray, Josh Lederman, Ben Hodges, Kelly O`Donnell,  John Della Volpe \par \uc2 8,125 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hey, everybody, good afternoon. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian, in for Chris Jansing here at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 So right now, we`re just about 75 days away from the midterm elections, and one special House race may signal a major tide shift for November. In New York, Democrat Pat Ryan defeated Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  in a race experts are saying could be a national bellwether with major questions remaining of what messaging actually moves the needle with voters.\par \par \uc2 Ryan made abortion rights the centerpiece of his campaign in a swing district, Molinaro tried to tie Democrats in the White House to high prices in the economy as well. Ryan`s win appears to show once again that abortion rights are a powerful motivator in helping Democrats get people to the polls. As "MEET THE PRESS FIRST READ" put out this morning, this red wave is looking much more like a ripple.\par \par \uc2 And roughly a thousand miles south of here in Florida, we now know who will take on Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in November. It is a familiar name in that state, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . He`s been the governor there before and he`s running again. He told her colleagues on "MORNING JOE," his plan to defeat DeSantis relies on not playing dirty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2 , (D-FL): I`m going to beat him because I`m running on love. And love always wins. And if he wants to run on hate and culture wars and dividing people and making people hate each other, that`s his turf. It`s not mine. He`s trying to tear my state apart that`s why I`m running against him. I don`t care about the money he`s got.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, a lot to unpack here. Let`s get right to it. NBC`s Shaq Brewster is with us in Fort Lauderdale covering the Florida primary election results. We got Dasha Burns, who`s in Kingston, New York, covering that New York special election. Also with me, former Biden campaign Senior Advisor Cristobal Alex, and NBC senior national political reporter Sahil Kapur. Welcome to you all, guys. Thanks for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Dasha, let me just start with you on this because I know that you had a one-on-one with Pat Ryan after his win yesterday. What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Yasmin, look, this is significant, right? The first competitive congressional race since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, and potentially the last real Bellwether that we`re going to have ahead of the midterms. And the signal that we got out of this race is a hopeful one for Democrats that this abortion issue really does motivate and galvanize voters.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was far from a guaranteed win for Pat Ryan, most polls had him trailing behind his Republican opponent, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 . And this district, it`s a swinging one, and it`s reflected the national mood for the last several years. It went from Obama to Trump to Biden by a narrow margin. So we knew that this was going to be a close one.\par \par \uc2 In my conversation with Ryan this morning, I asked him if his campaign was ready for the flurry of calls from Democratic strategists to sort of find out the secret sauce of this campaign. But the reality is, this is not a one-off. We saw a similar mood, similar shifts in Kansas, you know. When that abortion -- anti-abortion amendment was on the ballot, nobody expected the overwhelming victory that Democrats had in that campaign. And this -- we`re starting to see those sorts of trends in this primary season.\par \par \uc2 Talk -- I want you to take a listen to my conversation with Pat Ryan. I asked him about what message his victory says to the country. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAT RYAN, DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSMAN-ELECT, NEW YORK: I think the message is when fundamental rights and freedoms are under attack, we have to stand up, we have to fight, we have to be strong and clear. And when you do that, people rally. I mean, the issues at stake, reproductive rights, abortion access, are our fundamental rights that transcend partisanship. And we saw that in Kansas, we saw it last night here in New York, I think we`re going to continue to see it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And the framing that Ryan has been using is it`s important to listen to, he`s repeatedly connected the abortion issue with the issue of freedom.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We heard that also in Kansas, they really use the platform of government overreach, trying to reach across the aisle with that kind of rhetoric too, that this is not a partisan issue. This is an issue of constitutional rights and freedoms. And that seems to be resonating with folks. I expect we`re going to hear more similar rhetoric in the months to come here.\par \par \uc2 We did get a statement from his opponent, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , who told us that while this is not the outcome we hoped for, we knew this was an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 When Democrats scheduled the special election on the same day as two Democratic primaries with five candidates pushing their turnout, he said they`ll continue to look for solutions for upstate families. This is -- this is big, yes. And I -- and I think that Democrats are going to be talking about this race up until November 8.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So I think one of the major questions coming out of this all, Cristobal, I`m going to go to you on this one, is how do you kind of harness right, this special sauce as Dasha just put it when we`re seeing what took place in Kansas, you`re seeing what took place in this district in upstate New York? You`re seeing the polling nationally across the country, if you are running a midterm election, how do you harness this and go forward?\par \par \uc2 CRISTOBAL ALEX, FORMER BIDEN CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Yes, that`s a great question. And I think the analysis we just heard was spot on. The message you heard from Pat Ryan is a very powerful, very compelling message. There`s a lot of outrage in this country and for good reason. A right- leaning Supreme Court just stripped away a fundamental constitutional right. Never before has that happened. And what you saw in Kansas were hundreds of people -- thousands and thousands of people turning out in record numbers in a special election that we hadn`t seen before that kind of turnout. It`s actually presidential-level turnout.\par \par \uc2 The New York race we saw, I think we can read a lot of positive into that. As a Democrat, I`m very excited about it. I think that singles where we`re going for this -- going forward in this country, in terms of the midterms. We`re just approaching the fourth quarter. As I like to think about it, politics is a lot like a football game, right?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Or any kind of sport where you have momentum going into the final stretch, and that`s critical. You could be down by a touchdown or two. But if you got that momentum going into the fourth quarter, which to me is post-Labor Day, you`re going into this fourth quarter, and you`ve got all of this -- these wins under your belt. If you look at what the White House and Democrats in Congress have done is one win after another whether it`s the Inflation Reduction Act. Today, President Biden announcing student loan forgiveness that is very targeted, and will lift low-income folks, middle- class folks out of poverty, and help folks like me and others who have student debt.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s a very major win. And I think if you look at the messaging, and what`s coming out of the Supreme Court decision in Dobbs, it`s going to fuel all of this interest and the huge spike in outreach by Democratic campaigns and others to really tap into that, that new interest from voters that may have been sitting on the sideline before.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Sahil, I liked the point that you wrote. And I want to read it for folks to kind of understand where you`re at with this, saying it was a battle between these two competent contenders showcasing their party`s preferred messages, saying Democrats on abortion guns, democracy, Republicans on Biden referendum, inflation, crime. And you said there`s no candidate quality at the problem to pin this defeat on. What did you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL REPORTER: Right. Well, this means that -- what I meant was that Republicans put up their strongest candidate against you know, what Democrats thought was their strongest candidate and Republicans fell short in this district. \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  does not look or sound like an extremist. He is not an election denier. He has tried in the past to downplay and moderate on the issue of abortion. It didn`t work. It seems like voters are mad enough to show up.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: And, you know, take out their anger on anything and anyone with an R by its name because it was Republicans who assembled the Supreme Court that overturned Roe v. Wade. I think Pat Ryan`s attempt, as Dasha was just pointing out, to reclaim freedom is something that Democrats are doing a lot more of. We`ll see how that plays out in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2  also tried to run on the messages that Republicans believe are their strongest messages for the fall. He tried to make this a referendum on President Biden, who is on profit.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It didn`t work. He tried to make it a referendum on inflation, tried to, you know, highlight crime as well, I think in various parts of the race, and that didn`t work. So I think Democrats can look at the fact that this district, which has made or broken majorities in the past for the Republicans before 2018, and since 2018, for the Democrats as a good sign.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So what`s the reality on the ground in Washington, Sahil, as you`re talking to some of your sources there in that how they`re feeling about specifically this race is kind of Bellwether race. What they`re going to do going forward? Are they as worried about control or losing control of the House as they were before? Is this something that they can harness -- is everybody in the back room basically a campaign headquarters saying, all right, guys, this is what we got to do to move forward?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Well, Democrats are undoubtedly much more optimistic today than they were yesterday, around this time about holding the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It is still an uphill climb. It`s not going to take a red wave for Republicans to claim control of the House of Representatives. Even a series of ripples will do given how narrow these margins are. But, you know, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee pointed out yesterday that there were 222 House districts that were more pro-Biden than this one. That is the majority, if they can do -- if they can replicate this performance in the fall then they would be in a pretty good position to hold control.\par \par \uc2 Now, the big caveat. And this isn`t Marc Molinaro`s statement. I was mentioning it yesterday because Republican strategists were always -- were already telling me and others, you know, not to read too much into the result whatever it was.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Because it`s scheduled on the same day as Democrats` primaries in New York.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: This is a blue state. Their argument is voters in primaries will turn out in bigger numbers and that the Republican turnout will be comparably better this fall. Let`s see if that pans out. If so, then the Republicans still have a decent shot at claiming control of the House, but not by a red wave margin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Of course, we know you`re going to be watching it incredibly closely. With that, we talked a lot about New York, I want to head South also to Florida where Shaq is. Let`s talk about former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , Shaq, right? We just heard from him and the sound that I played coming into you guys saying essentially, he`s going to talk about more love, right? Love wins, he says. He`s not going to make this kind of a hateful campaign as he says the current governor is making it. He`s not necessarily worried about that $130 million, I believe, war chest the current governor has, as well. Can this work?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, that will certainly be tested. And let`s be clear, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , even during the primary battle with \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 , he went after DeSantis on both personality and policy. And look, he`s going to have to go after him with everything he`s got. DeSantis is someone who has an approval rating above 50 percent. He has that national profile that he turned into. That war chest that you described, that`s a historic amount of money that we`re talking about before the general election even started. So, already this morning in the hours after the primary ended, we`re getting a sense of where these battle lines are going to be drawn. Listen to what we heard from DeSantis last night and Crist today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): We will fight the woke and the businesses. We will fight the woke in government agencies. We will fight the woke in our schools. We will never ever surrender to the woke agenda. Florida is the state where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: He is for the hard bright red meat vote of Republican primary voters in Iowa and New Hampshire. And his blind ambition for the presidency of the United States of America has taken his eye off the ball of this most beautiful state in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Now, yesterday`s primary also finalized the Senate battle here in the state of Florida. Val Demings winning that primary race easily. She`s going up against Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . That is a candidate that is a popular, as -- I was to say also popular identification on a nationwide level.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: She`s been able to raise a significant amount of money for a House race or for her Senate race. But again, many people are seeing the Democrats in this state as the underdogs coming into the general election. No matter what, this is going to be a fight an expensive fight. They`ll get a lot of national attention, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Cristobal, let`s pick up a little bit on where Shaq left off and specifically talking about the governor`s race there. And I want to read for you what NPR pointed out saying this. Crist is going to have to sway some of the nearly 4 million unaffiliated voters -- Florida voters I should say. Registered Republicans now outnumber registered Democrats in this state, but statewide races have often been close, DeSantis, himself, won by about a single percentage point back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 This idea -- there is this idea as we see in the polls as well, current governor is pretty popular in that state right about now especially amongst obviously Republicans. Does Crist even have a shot here?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: He certainly has a shot. As you pointed out, these races are always extremely tight on the margins, usually just a couple of percentage points. And so Florida can go either way. I`ll tell you, what worries me as a Democrat about Florida though is DeSantis has that gigantic war chest, $140 million.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: I can`t think of another incumbent statewide who has ever had that kind of money. I will also say a challenge for Democrats is going to be getting outside investors into the state whether it`s major national organizations, major donors, and others because Democrats had been burned the last few election cycles there. They`re a little more gun shy about going into Florida and spending that kind of money.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I`ve campaigned with Crist before. In fact, the very first time I traveled with then-Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on a campaign swing was in 2014. We left California, on to Vegas, and then into Miami to campaign with \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and then his lieutenant governor nominee, Annette Taddeo. And that was an uphill battle, but he knows the state extremely well.\par \par \uc2 And I think his messages solid, give -- is a little bit of tightening up there. But there`s definitely a huge juxtaposition between what you heard from DeSantis really almost throwing red meat into the base and what Crist is doing. So he definitely got a shot, going to take a lot of money, but there`s hope there. Val Demings, by the way --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Extremely exciting candidate there and a lot of folks very excited about where she is in her polling right now. We didn`t expect her to be ahead at this point in the game when she is now.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Do you see her having a shot up against \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You do?\par \par \uc2 ALEX: I really do.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Shaq Brewster, Dasha Burns, Sahil Kapur, and Cristobal Alex, thank you, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Also in New York, everybody, these aren`t the only races voters are going to decide this fall. They`re also going to choose a new governor. We are counting down 100 key races across the country now that we`re less than 100 days from the midterms. Here`s where things stand right about now. Incumbent Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  will run against Republican opponent Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . The latest polls give Hochul a double-digit lead less than 11 weeks to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And then a reminder, everybody, our "PLAN YOUR VOTE" tool is here to help you successfully cast your ballot in the midterm primaries, no matter where you live, get key information on the voting rules in your area, head to NBCnews.com/PlanYourVote now.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, new details surrounding the investigation into the former president and the highly sensitive material he was storing at Mar-a- Lago, what does that mean for national security? And then, later on, a long-anticipated announcement on student loan debt forgiveness, that could be a game-changer for millions of Americans, what we expect to hear from the President and how it could reshape the midterms. That`s ahead. You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, some breaking news this hour, everybody. First Lady Jill Biden just tested positive for rebound COVID after testing negative yesterday. She`s not had a reemergence of symptoms and will remain in Delaware where she has restarted isolation procedures there.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning more today about this highly sensitive material \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was storing in his Mar-a-Lago basement. The National Archives has confirmed that more than 700 pages of classified documents were taken in January. Some include the highest levels of classification used to identify only the government`s most sensitive secrets. That is, by the way, in addition to the documents seized in the search earlier this month. But despite this potential threat to national security, four U.S. officials telling NBC News that the country`s top intelligence officials have not yet ordered a former -- formal, I should say, damage assessment.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk more about this, NBC`s justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian, and Melissa Murray, a former law clerk to then- judge \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , and an MSNBC legal analyst, welcome to you both, guys. Thanks for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 Ken, let me just start with you on this one. And just kind of understand if they really know -- if there is a sense of how damaging this may be to national security.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Well, the fundamental question that goes to that issue, Yasmin, is whether there has been an unauthorized disclosure. I mean, we know this stuff shouldn`t have been stored in Mar-a-Lago in an unsecured location. But the further question that intelligence officials don`t know the answer to and neither does the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , it appears is whether this got into the wrong hands somehow.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Whether any of this information leaked to an adversary. So that`s the first question they have to answer. But even if they don`t know the answer to that, generally, the intelligence community wants to go through some process protectively to determine whether any -- for example, any human sources are at risk, any technical intercepts are at risk.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Because let`s remember, this document shows that some of the most highly classified documents in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  were included in this -- in this hall, TS/SCI, Special Access Programs that only a handful of the highest cleared people would be read in on. So this is serious stuff, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So I guess my follow-up to that, Ken, is why are they waiting or what are they waiting for in order to launch this formal damage assessment? And I want to read for you from what the DNI says about formal -- about these formal damage assessments. I`m saying this. Damage assessment shall be conducted when there is an actual or suspected unauthorized disclosure or compromise of classified national intelligence that may cause damage to U.S. national security. Going on to say, damage assessments may also be conducted when there was an actual or suspected loss, misuse, or unauthorized access to or modification of classified national intelligence that could adversely affect national security.\par \par \uc2 What are your sources telling you, Ken, as to why this has not yet been launched?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: So, it sure seems like those rules would call for an assessment, in this case, right? But what our sources are saying is a number of things. One, there was a delay between the time that the government first learned there were highly classified documents in Mar-a-Lago in the January batch. There was a four-month delay before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  actually got to look at the documents. That happened in May. So there hasn`t been all that much time.\par \par \uc2 But secondly, there seems to be some concern in the Biden administration of taking any action that would appear to be political, of any agency other than the independent Justice Department have its hands on this case. We`re sensing that reluctance. And then also there`s a reluctance to put anything in writing that could be later subpoenaed by the president or anyone else who becomes a defendant. It`s called graymail. Sometimes defendants in cases involving classified information will subpoena this stuff and threaten to make it public. And so there`s a great reluctance essentially write any secrets down as part of this process.\par \par \uc2 But look, there -- what we are also being told, Yasmin, is there definitely have been informal damage assessments. They`re looking at these documents. They`re trying to figure out whether there are any possible vulnerabilities here in the chance that there was an unauthorized disclosure.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Before I go to Melissa, Ken, can you just answer as to whether or not a damage assessment -- the results of a damage assessment would become public? Would we see them?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Generally, not. There -- but the congressional committees have asked that it be done. And so the Gang of Eight may see it, and the intelligence committees are likely to see it. And we could see some very heavily censored form of it, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So, Melissa, if in fact, this damage assessment is launched, how could that play into the legal cases that are mounting against the former president?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, regardless of whether or not classified information was unsecured or disclosed, it doesn`t necessarily matter for any of the statutes that were implicated in that search warrant. None of those statutes requires classified information to be an issue. It`s just simply information that could adversely affect the national security. And I think we`ve already established based on the National Archives letter that indeed, this was information that had national security implications. So I don`t know that that necessarily sheds light on that part of the case.\par \par \uc2 I think what the letter does shed light on is the president`s state of mind. And we`ve been speculating about what he could have been doing during this period of months where there is apparently negotiations between the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the National Archives and the former president about the return of these documents.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And some had speculated that maybe this was merely negligence, you know, like -- but this letter and the fact that there`s this back and forth that has been documented, suggests that there`s something that seems more like willfulness or purposefulness in keeping these documents and not returning them to the National Archives as requested. And so I`m actually quite surprised that the former president wanted this information to be public because it seems quite damning.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Melissa, while I have you, I just want to ask you one more question and that`s about this request from the former president`s team for this appointment of a special master. What stood out to you about the judge`s order in that?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I mean, the judge`s order was sort of the jurisprudential version of what even is this?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I mean, the complaint that was filed in the Southern District of Florida with this new judge was so shoddy and devoid of any of the markers of an ordinary pleading that even the judge was forced to ask for a do- over. And so she`s basically asked them to do it again, to do it right to provide a jurisdiction statement -- a jurisdictional 