y earlier on this month. A military official telling NBC News that the U.S. is certain Iranian aligned groups were the source of those attacks.\par \par \uc2 All right. Three months after the shooting at Robb Elementary School and two postponements later, we could finally learn whether the Uvalde school police chief will lose his job. We`re going to look at what parents are demanding ahead of a critical meeting there tonight.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:42]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. Hours from now the Uvalde School Board is expected to discuss whether to fire Police Chief Pete Arredondo three months after that deadly shooting at Robb Elementary School that killed 19 kids and two teachers. The board was originally set to meet back in July but was postponed twice. Arredondo has come under intense public scrutiny over the police response to the May 24th massacre.\par \par \uc2 "Texas Tribune" public education reporter Brian Lopez has covered the story from the (INAUDIBLE) and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Brian, thanks for joining us on this. I mean, two postponements later, right, folks were calling for the firing of Arredondo weeks after the shooting, after I was there on the ground reporting on this. And yet here we are and it still hasn`t happened. How is the community reacting to this meeting that may take place this evening, that is taking place this evening, and the possible firing of the police chief?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN LOPEZ, THE TEXAS TRIBUNE PUBLIC EDUCATION REPORTER: Well, the Uvalde community is hopeful that they`ll get some sense of closure tonight. They`ve been, like you`ve mentioned, pushing for Arredondo to be fired, especially after the Texas House Committee report, he was one of the first to be on the scene with almost 400 officers. And they still took more than an hour to engage. So that`s the hope for tonight for these residents who have been waiting for, like you mentioned, two months now for a resolution and some closure.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What is the likelihood a decision will be made?\par \par \uc2 LOPEZ: That`s one of the, you know, things that we`ll be looking for. We really don`t know if this is going to be the end today or if there is going to be first of many meetings. You know, one of the reasons this was postponed, you know, twice is because his lawyer was calling for it to be done the legal way. At the end of the day, this is a hearing, so he`s going to present his side and he`ll present his arguments, whatever those may be, and we`ll see what the school board, you know, does. You know, I will say if they do not do anything tonight, fire him tonight, there will be much -- a lot more, you know, outrage within the Uvalde community.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: How is the community doing, Brian, now that kids, especially, are back to school?\par \par \uc2 LOPEZ: It really depends on who you ask. You know, I`ve been talking a lot to parents about the decisions they`re making. You know, some are going back to the district, mainly because they have no other choice, right. It`s a working-class community, some parents are working, you know, got to wake up at 7:00 a.m., get home at 6:00, deal with their families. Others have the capability to, you know, go out of the district, go to private schools.\par \par \uc2 The Uvalde CISD is offering an online option as well. So it really depends who you ask but the main key thing is, you know, people aren`t just thinking about, you know, what`s the best education like, how this is going to get people are thinking about, you know, where are they going to be the safest. You know, you have people (INAUDIBLE) expensive, you know, bulletproof backpacks because of this. And things, you know, people in this community will never in a million thought they would be doing.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Just a very difficult and sad state that we are in right now after such an incredibly tragic moment 