\uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: New York City. Right now, you are looking at a live picture of the federal courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida. As we speak, a high-stakes hearing is getting underway that could ultimately reveal crucial details about the search at Mar-a-Lago. A decision on that could come anytime now. The hearing will pit the Justice Department against several news organizations, including this one, that are asking a federal magistrate judge to unseal the affidavit that led to the search.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says that would be a grave mistake warning the judge "if disclosed, the Affidavit would serve as a roadmap to the government`s ongoing investigation, providing specific details about its direction and likely course, in a manner that is highly likely to compromise future investigative steps." Last night on this network, Connecticut Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  who`s a member of the House Intelligence Committee says that`s not the only potential risk here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): Affidavit contains all sorts of information, including testimony by informants, witnesses, and others that now that we know that not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican Party generally will make threats, including threats on the lives of people, including \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents that are involved here. There`s an awful lot of risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So it could be unsealed or it could be kept sealed, but there is a third option for the judge and that would be releasing it with redactions. But the DOJ says if they redact everything they need to, the document will essentially be worthless. Bottom line, that affidavit could answer the why behind the search, about exactly what was taken. Investigators are apparently still figuring that out. Two law enforcement officials tell NBC News they`re still going through the material just as they have been for the past week and a half, trying to figure out what is and what is not relevant to their investigation.\par \par \uc2 And those aren`t the only documents making headlines today. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  says federal prosecutors looking into last year`s insurrection have issued a grand jury subpoena to the National Archives asking for "all materials in whatever form that the archives have sent to the January 6 committee." The Time says a subpoena shows the DOJ is following the committee`s lead to an extent but also that the department believes evidence of a crime may exist in those documents.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to, I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry who`s outside the courthouse in Florida. Jacqueline Alemany is a Congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC contributor. Andrew Weissmann is a former FBI General Counsel, a former member of the Mueller probe, and an MSNBC legal analyst. And Frank Figliuzzi is a former Assistant Director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and an MSNBC national security analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Cal, let`s start where we are right now. Explain what`s going on inside that courtroom. And do we know when we might get word of a decision?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So we may get word from the bench, from the judge today, as you said he could postpone it setting out some kind of schedule, or he could rule at a later date. So we don`t really know on that front. What we do know is that journalists have just been let into the courtroom and they were allowed to keep their phones, which is how I can tell you that five attorneys have arrived, three that are representing that group of media organizations that you talked about, two our representing \uc2 Judicial Watch\uc2 . We expect to hear from one of the lawyers that are representing this group of media organizations, including NBC News, and we expect to hear from a lawyer who`s representing \uc2 Judicial Watch\uc2 . We will then hear from the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you laid out, the Department of Justice is going to say that releasing this affidavit is going to impede their investigation that not only does it put witnesses in jeopardy, but it could show further witnesses and they really don`t want to give away the game plan there. What you will hear from the media is that the public has an interest to this document because "of the historically significant unprecedented execution of a search warrant with regards to a former president." This was from the filing that the media put forward to the judge. It also says "not since the Nixon administration has the federal government wielded his power to seize records from a former president in such a public fashion."\par \par \uc2 And you talk about this happening in public. We have seen Trump supporters in just the last 30 minutes arriving here at the scene at the courthouse. This roped-off area behind me is the area that the law enforcement personnel have set aside for possible protests or demonstrations. And I should add that the judge, in this case, judge Reinhart, who signed that affidavit that allowed for that search warrant to take place, he has received death threats.\par \par \uc2 Our local affiliate here has done some pretty stunning reporting on this in the last few days. But the Palm Beach Gardens Police Department putting out a very brief statement this morning saying that they are "aware of the threats against judge Reinhart and that they are working with their federal law enforcement partners." It is probably why, Chris, you see sort of a high amount of law enforcement presence here on the ground outside of the federal courthouse.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, without a doubt, Cal. And you talked about the report, it`s coming from within the courthouse including one that we just got that the Trump attorney Christina Bobb, and we`ve mentioned her name before, is inside the courtroom. They will not be making arguments important to point out.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to what the judge is doing right now, Andrew. We talked about the options here, unseal, don`t unseal, or maybe redact. What kinds of things is he going to be looking at and what ultimately are you expecting to see here?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, I`m going to go out on a limb and say, there is zero chance that the judge is going to rule that the full affidavit gets unsealed. There are national security interests, there are criminal interests, there are concerns about witnesses, it`s just -- it`s just unheard of. There`s no precedent for that. The only thing that I think the news organizations could hope for is taking up the footnote where, as you noted, Chris, the government said they`ve thought about a redacted version, but they thought it would not be -- it would be so redacted, it wouldn`t be helpful. But that`s all they said.\par \par \uc2 And I could see the court saying, you know, I want the government to give me what they would propose to redact because I need to see myself if that`s really true, that there`s nothing that could be released here. I think that`s the most that could happen. And if that is what the judge rules, it`s going to take some time because then the government will get time to submit that, the court will assess it, and then hear from the government about why it couldn`t go forward and release that. I think that the most that could happen eventually out of this hearing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As we look at what he`s weighing, Andrew, obviously, on one side is the idea of protecting the investigation, the other is serving the public interest, there`s a whole lot of levels to that, but at least in part, it`s trying to keep the country from devolving into violence. How do you balance that against the DOJ`s warning? Is it always going to lean toward the DOJ?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: In this case, it will. I mean, the court is going to look to precedent. And so the precedent here is really lopsided. I hear you about the public interest here, but that`s not really going to be paramount. I mean, eventually, if someone is charged, this document will see the light of day, and even then probably in a redacted form because there`s no way that the classified information that is highly likely in this affidavit is going to be released publicly. So I hear you, but those are -- those kinds of interests that the news organizations have are not going to outweigh the significant national security, criminal interests, and witness protection issues.\par \par \uc2 And then one final thing which you mentioned, Chris, which I found interesting is you know the former president has publicly said that he wants the names of witnesses, but sort of unusually, he actually hasn`t filed any papers in court and his lawyers aren`t going to actually be heard. So that really does appear to be more of a precedent than something that -- seriously something that they want the court to consider.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I suppose, Frank, you could also say, look, if there`s going to be violence and you know, we`re hearing from Cal already there are concerns about the judge, it may not matter one way or another. There are people going to be unhappy if they see what`s in it. There are people who are unhappy just because the raid happened, right? We`ve seen the threats that have been made real and verbal against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , tell me your thoughts on this today.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST AND CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I`m -- I agree with Andrew that we`re unlikely to see any kind of helpful release of the affidavit. And here`s why. There`s a real danger here to human life not only damage to what the DOJ is already called an ongoing criminal investigation and references to witnesses past and future. But look, there appear to be cooperators in this case.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, we`ve tried to subpoena -- we saw DOJ try a subpoena. It didn`t work. We saw that the Trump attorney filed allegedly a document saying we gave you everything and that was wrong. And then something prompted them to go in with a warrant. And that really smells a lot like cooperating witnesses. It`s quite possible. We also have heard that the security footage outside the storage room really alarms DOJ, all of that is in that affidavit.\par \par \uc2 Remember you -- the affidavit establishes probable cause for the three offenses asserted in the warrant. And so for example, we would learn things like well, how many witnesses you know, and when did you talk to them? That would identify human beings who would be at risk. And then with regard to the charge of falsifying or destroying documents that obstruction like the charge that`s in the warrant, we would learn in that affidavit why they think something`s been falsified.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Has somebody blocked out classification markings on documents? Did the former president instruct people to destroy documents or gin up a fake declassification document? That would be revealed. So what would a redacted version of that look like? A black magic marker after black magic marker, I don`t think it`d be very helpful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And one of the things we just talked about, Jackie, is that Christina Bobb is in there. She`s not going to take any participants -- participate in any of the arguments. But there`s -- publicly, Trump`s saying, oh, he`s very happy with his legal team, your reporting is something else. Tell us what you found out.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chris. Well, I think we should reinforce what Andrew pointed out, which is that despite Christina Bobb being in the courthouse today, Trump`s legal team has not submitted any formal motion to the court declaring a stance to release the affidavit in the interest of transparency as the former president has previously said. But in the lead-up to today`s case, we have reported that Trump`s current legal team was seeking prosecutors, defenders, lawyers with more experience.\par \par \uc2 They had reached out to multiple different lawyers, some quite prominent ones in the Republican Party who all declined to join the case. We`d also been told that this was actually a long-standing search that preceded this spilling into public view. And had multiple voices telling us that in the case of such a serious criminal matter, that it`s important that the former president have legal representation with people that have more experience that are suited to this issue of the former president potentially being charged criminally with mishandling classified information.\par \par \uc2 That being said, there are a few members of his team who do have extensive prosecutorial high-level experience. People like John Trusty, Evan Corcoran, but we`re also hearing is that there is some bubbling concern that people like Evan Corcoran, who might have been involved with some of the previous attestations about the presence or existence of certain documents and boxes, might ultimately embroil him in some legal trouble, so much that he potentially have to recuse himself from representing the former president. These are all things that we`re keeping our fingers on the pulse of, and watching very closely today as this unfolds in Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you know, Andrew, that what the president -- the former president has said is these aren`t classified documents, I just declassified everything. Everything that I took out of the oval lie immediately marked who said was -- were declassified. So earlier today, he post this memorandum that appears to show that he did declassified documents related to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s crossfire hurricane, which then led to the Mueller probe, which you know, well, but does that speak at all to what we`re talking about today?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So I actually think that document hurts the president. If I were his counsel, I`m not sure I would have advised to release that. And the reason I say that is one, whether something is classified or declassified is actually irrelevant to the statutes that were listed in the search warrant. And there are other statutes that could be charged as well that where it`s irrelevant that it`s classified or not.\par \par \uc2 Second, it`s not clear that there`s a complete overlap, indeed, it`s highly unlikely that the only documents that were found in Mar-a-Lago with classification marks were the hurricane -- crossfire hurricane documents, to which there`s a clear set of documents that the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had. And the third problem is that document that the former president posted shows that the president knows exactly how to declassify documents in the formal correct process.\par \par \uc2 But that is not what Kash Patel and the former president had been saying about the documents in Mar-a-Lago. They said that there was a standing order where documents miraculously got declassified because you move them from one room in the White House to another room in the White House, which, as you can tell from the way I phrase that, I and many other people are very skeptical of that factual proposition. But I think that memo shows that the president knew exactly how to do this in the correct fashion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as he was talking about that, Frank, I saw you nodding and smiling so, obviously, you agree about what that might show for the president. But let me ask you about what`s happening with the seized materials because attorney Mark Zaid and who`s worked on cases involving seized materials talked about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s process of going through them on "MORNING JOE" today. Let me play a little bit of what he said.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK ZAID, ATTORNEY: I have had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  when they`ve seized clients records of mine or you know, my clients` records take months. I think I still have a client who hasn`t gotten back files in six or eight years. But that`s not going to happen in such a high-profile case, obviously, as the former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But why, Frank, does it take that long? And how long might this take? Obviously, they know that there is a red-hype hot spotlight on what they`re doing right now.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Well, when it comes to classified documents or even just government documents, right, we`ve been talking about the lack of relevance to whether they`re classified or not, they belong to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . So I -- if we`re -- if the question is how long until Trump gets \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  documents back? The answer is, maybe never. If he ever has a library and museum, the National Archives will decide what goes into that library and museum.\par \par \uc2 But these are our documents, yours and mine. They belong to the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , regardless of classification level. If a filter team looks at some things as allegedly they did with his passports and says, yes, these really -- these can go back, they`re not -- they`re not of relevance, they`re not -- they`re really aren`t necessarily of the government`s, yes, then that`ll go back. But what we`re talking about here are things that belong to the government.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a painstaking long process and doing a damage assessment, Chris, which involves tracking back in history, where those documents came from, whether they were originated in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , Allied governments that may have provided the Intel to us, and usually a TS/SCI documents, there are specific markings about who touched them, where they came from. There`s a history and a lot kept on TS documents, I`d have to sign at my desk at FBI Headquarters, time, date, name, I touched it, I gave it back to the career, done. All of that has to be tracked.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, a timeline is going to be established with the security cameras outside that storage room, which Trump has provided apparently. And when people went in? Who are their face -- what are their faces? Who are those people? Did a cleaning person go into the storage room? Go interview that person. Who were all the visitors to the residence at Mar-a-Lago? There was --there were pilgrimages you`ll remember after January where everybody and their brother went down to say hello to the former president. All of that has to be cross-referenced to determine who had access, who saw it, and who shouldn`t have.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We could talk about this for the entire hour, fascinating conversation. Frank, Andrew. Jacqueline, thank you very much. Cal, of course, we`ll come back to you outside the courthouse if you get any updates at all, so let us know.\par \par \uc2 There is one other important hearing we`re following for you, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg facing a judge in New York today with big legal implications for the former president, plus, why Republicans are now targeting the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are looking at a live shot of the Florida courthouse where at any moment a judge could decide to unseal the Mar-a-Lago search warrant affidavit. We have a team of people on site, and we`ll have that decision for you as soon as we learn it.\par \par \uc2 In another courthouse, 1200 miles away in New York City, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO Allen Weisselberg admitted that the company had an off-the-books scheme to avoid paying taxes on lavish benefits and other perks for employees. But this is only about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Rather than turn on his old boss and testify in a broader investigation, the 75-year-old has agreed to do five months in jail. The plea also requires him to testify truthfully about illicit business practices at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  during a trial scheduled for this fall, and he must repay the state and city of New York nearly $2 million.\par \par \uc2 For more, we`re joined by NBC`s Tom Winter at the courthouse, with me in studio, Shan Wu, a former federal prosecutor who now represents clients in white-collar criminal matters. So, Tom, tell us what happened in the courtroom this morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIONS CORRESPONDENTS: Right. So for Alan Weiselberger, he really kind of closes a bit of a chapter for him and yet opens another one of going from staunch defendant to somebody who admitted his guilt and will now have to testify against the very Corporation he used to be the Chief Financial Officer for.\par \par \uc2 That off-the-books payment scheme that you mentioned there, Chris, has the idea that he was able to get payments for \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  -- multiple \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2 , cars that he had, apartment in New York City, a garage, other cash benefits. It`s not necessarily illegal for him to have received those things from the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . It was illegal, according to prosecutors, and now according to Weisselberg himself, for him not to pay the taxes for that compensation and for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  not to present the proper filings for it.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s really what jammed him up here. That`s what he`s in trouble for. As you said, yes, a five-month jail sentence on Rikers Island here in New York City for him. That`s what he has ahead of him. But that`ll be sentenced and he`ll go there after a late October trial involving the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  which was indicted at the same time as Weisselberg, part of the same indictment approximately a year ago. So that`s coming up first. Weisselberg has to provide truthful testimony to that. No indication here at all, Chris, that the former CFO plans to turn against the former president personally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So you, Shan, represent white-collar clients, right? And I`m wondering what you make of this decision by him, OK, I`m going to plead guilty. In this case, that`s about the organization but I am not going to cooperate with this other investigation by the Manhattan DA into Trump.\par \par \uc2 SHAN WU, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: This is a big win for Weisselberg`s defense team and really a loss for the DA. It`s a sweetheart deal for him. It`s very little time. I`m not putting down the fact that a 75-year-old going to jail for any amount of time is not easy. But given what he was facing, 15 years, this is a slap on the wrist for him. It`s another example of really the kind of -- I don`t know what the reason is for it, but the timidity of \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in going after Trump. I mean, he had prosecutors ready to go on Trump, backed it off. Weisselberg is the CFO of the organization. So unless the theory is he was running rogue doing things, it`s inexplicable that the path doesn`t lead to Trump.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So was this a case do you imagine where they took what they could get, they wanted to make sure that he was punished to some degree.\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s the case honestly, as a former prosecutor, now a defense counsel.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s the case where the prosecutors blinked. They had a lot of leverage on him at his age with that kind of time. They could have demanded that they go to trial if it wouldn`t take a harsher plea because of white-collar cases, all about the paper documents. They don`t have a great defense to say, I got this income but I didn`t pay the taxes to it. He`s the CFO, it`s not like he`s a very simplistic minded person unsophisticated so they had a lot of leverage. They blinked, basically, just to check the rocks, and get the case done with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom, I`m also curious. If he`s willing to talk -- to testify about the business, what are the implications for Trump? I mean, it is hard to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  personally, from the business professionally, right? It`s intertwined with his personal wealth, his lifestyle, his image.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: The man is the brand and the brand is the man effectively. And so you`re right, Chris, yes, it is difficult to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from his business or vice versa. But it is a little bit easier to separate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his wealth from his business. He does have his own -- and we know this from his presidential filings, don`t know his current financial condition, but we know from the filings when he was president that he had his own wealth, his own financial securities, and instruments outside of his business. So it`s not as if that`s the only thing that he has if the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was shut down today, every golf course closed, every hotel closed, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would still have some wealth granted, not to the degree that he has.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s also important to remember that over the years, Donald Trump`s shifted away from owning the physical properties himself, from managing the physical properties himself to licensing his name and brand. Now, we haven`t seen as many of those deals, since obviously, he became president. And since then -- and since he`s left the White House, and now former president, but still he was able to accumulate cash wealth that way, is a means of driving his own pockets as well as his -- to the benefit of his corporation. So there is a little bit of daylight there that does exist and as far as the wealthy hold personally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Shan, you mentioned that Weisselberg because of his position likely knew if not everything, most things right that were going on within the organization. In a plea deal like this, where does cooperation end? If there`s some new investigation, for example, would it indemnify -- would it keep him from being charged in something like that? Where it is a cooperation agreement end?\par \par \uc2 WU: In this situation, his lawyers have fashioned a very narrow cooperation agreement. He`s basically agreeing to tell the truth if he gets called to testify, and he`s admitting what he personally did. So if new charges arise, he could easily take the Fifth Amendment to those charges, and basically insulate the organization and insulate Trump from doing that. This is not what we typically think of as a cooperation agreement where they`re coming in and sitting next to the prosecutors, investigators going through the books explaining everything to them. It`s very narrowly construed and that`s why it`s really a big win for him and not a good deal for the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shan Wu, thanks so much for coming in. Appreciate it. Tom Winter, appreciate your reporting as well.\par \par \uc2 One more court appearance of note today, the man accused of stabbing author Salman Rushdie was indicted by a grand jury on one count of second-degree attempted murder and one count of second-degree assault. Hadi Matar appeared in court today for his arraignment after pleading not guilty to charges on Sunday. Matar is accused of stabbing Rushdie 10 times last week at a speaking engagement. Rushdie is still recovering from his injuries in a Pennsylvania Hospital.\par \par \uc2 We are watching the courthouse in West Palm Beach where at any moment a judge could unseal the affidavit over the search of Mar-a-Lago, plus the GOP goes after the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . It`s a pretty shocking letter from a senator about open jobs at the agency that`s causing some controversy. We`ll tell you all about it. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:55]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So while we`re still waiting for word out of the courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida, where the judge is deciding whether to unseal that affidavit over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago, we did just get notice that the judge has said he will release some of the procedural filings that currently are under seal now. These are more uninteresting documents related to the warrant.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ had said they did not oppose them being released but we`ll see what information is in those as we wait to see about that other bigger decision that could come down at any moment.\par \par \uc2 And after the heavy criticism of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Republicans are now escalating their rhetoric against a different three-letter agency, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . Their target, how $80 billion slated for the agency from the Inflation Reduction Act, will be spent. Take a listen to what a few of them are saying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Those \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents are designed to come after you. They`re not designed to come after the billionaires and the big corporations. They`re designed to come after small businesses and working families.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: They`re going to go after the mom and pop, they`re going to go after the small business person, the independent contractor, the \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver. And they are going to focus on basically -- so the country that don`t support what the regime is trying to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And even the House minority leader Kevin McCarthy tweeted that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  will hire an army of new agents to, quote, "spy on Americans." All of these leading to this stunning moment where U.S. Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  urged people seeking jobs not to apply to what he calls the \uc2 IRS\uc2  super police.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC senior Capitol Hill correspondent Sahil Kapur. Also with us Ben Collins who of course covers disinformation, extremism and the internet for NBC News.\par \par \uc2 So, Sahil, let`s do a little fact check here. Are there going to be a group of people who are \uc2 IRS\uc2  super police? And walk us through this letter from \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUT, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: No, there won`t, Chris. That`s the answer to your first question. And secondly, it`s not every day that a United States senator tells Americans not to apply for a particular job by threatening to eliminate it but that`s exactly what Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  did in this rather extraordinary letter.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 He penned an open letter to American job seekers warning them not to apply for new \uc2 IRS\uc2  jobs under the $80 billion cash infusion under this law, arguing that Republicans hope to take control of Congress this fall and one of first things they will do once that happens is defund those positions.\par \par \uc2 Now there are a couple of problems with this. The first is that even if Republicans do capture control of Congress, not a certainty but certainly plausible, they will not -- you know, President Biden will still have the veto pen. And he will be able to block any attempts to undo his signature legislation. And the second is, the premise of this is unsubstantiated at best. There`s a May 2021 Treasury report that suggests the \uc2 IRS\uc2  could hire about 87,000 employees over a decade.\par \par \uc2 That`s a number that Republicans have seized on, but a lot of those employees will be replacing exists employees, and not all of them would be in enforcement so the depiction that 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents would be auditing the taxes of regular Americans is not backed by the facts. The Biden administration has gone to great lengths to point out that they will only use these resources to audit the taxes of wealthy Americans who are not paying their -- you know, who are using sophisticated means to avoid taxes at this point.\par \par \uc2 And the Republican argument at this point is partly based on the fact that this is their best case to make against this Democrats` only reconciliation bill given that other aspects of it such as the prescription drug pricing empowering Medicare to negotiate prices and higher taxes on corporations are popular. This is one aspect that they think they can make political hay of, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and look, Ben, it`s not like the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is the most popular ever organization. Having said that, you know, we`ve seen this playbook before, stir up resentment toward a federal agency. In this case, is any of it sticking?\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It`s sticking everywhere. It`s being conflated on far-right parts of the internet with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago. They`re saying if they`re going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they`re going to go after you for the same reason, even though it`s a completely different agency, obviously. It`s being conflated into one thing. It`s sort of the completion of the Infowars-ification of this party.\par \par \uc2 This is something that Alex Jones specifically has been warning against for 10 or 20 years. The idea that the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is, you know, going to -- poses a front for law enforcement, come and take your guns, come and take all of your assets. That`s what you see on Militia TikTok right now which is a ting. Militias are on TikTok trying to recruit people, but saying, you know, their standard rhetoric about come and take it, they`re pointing it toward the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , not towards any sort of law enforcement agency.\par \par \uc2 They thinks this is part of a much larger plan. To take away both your guns and your money. And by the way, it`s different now than 10 years ago, or even seven years ago. You know, seven years ago there was this conspiracy called Jade Helm, where the idea was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was going to use the facade of a military exercise in Texas to occupy Texas and never leave power. Ted Cruz back then said he called the Pentagon and there`s nothing to worry about it, don`t worry about it.\par \par \uc2 But now Ted Cruz is all up in this \uc2 IRS\uc2  fearmongering thing. The party has leaned closer and closer to conspirational rhetoric like this because they realize it`s a winner among their base.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Collins, Sahil Kapur, both great reporting as usual. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 It`s a huge number. $650.5 million, but that`s what a federal judge told \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  to pay for their role in the deadly opioid crisis in just two counties in Ohio. That combined fine is to be paid out over the next 15 years after the companies were found liable last November for over-supplying pills that ended up on the black market.\par \par \uc2 Thwarting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is Liz Cheney`s goal now, but what can she try that hasn`t already been tried to shift the GOP away from the former president? We`ll talk about it on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:13]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Didn`t take long. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is already moving on. After her nearly 40-point defeat, the congresswoman from Wyoming shifted the rest of her campaign war chest into a new entity, the Great Task, which, as the AP points out, is a phrase from Lincoln`s Gettysburg address. Now Cheney says her one goal is to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out of the Oval. But can she battle the former president in a party that he has singlehandedly reshaped in his own image. How does \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  go up against this big cult of personality?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Eugene Robinson.\par \par \uc2 Always great to see you, Eugene. So, look, we know Wyoming is very red. I was there for a couple of days, I talked to voters at the Hageman victory party. There is no greater treason for them than opposing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So my question is, what is it that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  think she can change? Does she have some secret plan nobody else has tried before against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, WASHINGTON POST COLUMNIST: Well, if she is a secret plan, it`s super-secret, and I can`t imagine what it is. I mean, look, we know where the Republican Party is now. If you look at the primaries that we`ve seen so far, he hasn`t won every single race. Trump hasn`t won every single race with his preferred candidates but he has won most of them. And the members of Congress, the Republicans who dared to vote for impeachment or to vote for conviction, with the exception of Senator Murkowski, who did very well in Alaska, having voted to impeach him, a second impeachment. Nonetheless, the others, I think it`s, what, eight out of 10 are gone.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I mean, and, you know, so, that`s a pretty good record if you`re betting 800. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting control of the Republican Party right now. And I don`t see how \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  cracks that.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which kind of supports the argument that Gary Abernathy made in your paper which is that folks like Cheney, he writes, need to give up on the delusion that Republicans have just somehow temporarily lost their way. So maybe this is bigger than whether \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  can save the Republican Party. But if you`re talking about, say, the party of Reagan and the Bushes, is it dead already?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I don`t think the party of Reagan and the Bushes really exists right now. If it does, it`s kind of in exile. Those people are basically independents now, or they`re Republicans without much of a voice in the Republican Party. It`s a much more populist Republican Party now. This is not your father`s Republican Party. It`s not your Republican Party of just a few years ago before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came in and activated and energized the populist revolution that I suspect was already going on inside the Republican Party. It`s not all Trump. He was a catalyst, but it had to be there before he got there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Eugene Robinson, it`s always great to talk with us. Thank you for being on the program.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, you`ve probably heard the names \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Dr. Oz, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but what about Cheri Beasley? All of them are running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . NBC went on the trail with Beasley in North Carolina to learn why she thinks she can flip a seat from red to blue.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:24]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news, and an update on the hearing to unseal the affidavit that led to the search of Mar-a-Lago. Let`s go to NBC`s Cal Perry outside the courthouse in West Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 What`s new, Cal?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, so this is coming from our producer who is in the courtroom right now, Juliette Arcodia. Now she writes that the DOJ started talking to the judge. It was very conversational to begin with. That`s when we heard that there would be some release of documents, again procedural documents, as you put it sort of not the exciting bit.\par \par \uc2 Then the media took over, that is the group of media organizations represented by their attorney, took over and made their arguments saying that this was in the public interest as we had sort of seen in the previous court filings that the extraordinary nature of what had happened made it necessary for the government to release this affidavit. They said that you can`t see anything in the dark, that they needed to sort of shine a light on this.\par \par \uc2 Now DOJ is making their case. And here`s what they`re saying. They`re calling the document a, quote, "detailed roadmap" of an investigation that is still in its, quote, "early stages." A top counterintelligence and national security official at the DOJ argued that the document needs to be kept under wraps because it contains, as he said, quote, "substantial grand jury information" in a, quote, "unique case" with, quote, "national security overtones."\par \par \uc2 He then said, quote, "This is a volatile situation with respect to the particular search across the political spectrum." That is a mild way, Chris, of saying that this search has set off a lot of opinions, least of all in Trump world, least of all at the DOJ, and least of all with the media. So you have this now back and forth argument. DOJ, we understand, is going to have the last word in these court proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The media lawyers may speak up one more time but again DOJ is going to have the final word. And a reminder, this judge is a former federal prosecutor. So it`s likely he is going to listen to these arguments of national security.\par \par \uc2 And one more time, Chris, we don`t know what we don`t know but the Department of Justice is saying and they`re saying clearly now, and I think this is probably going to be one of the headlines, that this investigation is, quote, "in the early stages," Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Fascinating and important stuff. Cal Perry, thank you. We`ll come back to you as needed.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there`s new poling in the critically important North Carolina Senate race and it shows as dead heat. Millions of dollars continue to pour into a race you may not have heard much about but that could be make or break to determine which party runs the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Antonia Hylton went to North Carolina to show us what former state chief justice Cheri Beasley is doing to try to become the state`s first black senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Former state Supreme Court justice Cheri Beasley has to be a different Democrat to win the Senate seat in North Carolina, one that spends as much time in rural counties as they do in urban centers.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), SENATE CANDIDATE IN NORTH CAROLINA: Certainly industries and corporations are coming to North Carolina. They tend to go to Raleigh and Charlotte but they miss out on the talent in our rural community.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Beasley lost her last statewide race by just 401 votes as thousands of registered rural voters color sat out the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Do politicians come here that often?\par \par \uc2 WHYDON SELLERS, NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: No, no. We usually see them at\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What do you think is at stake right now?\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL COLE JR., NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: Well, again, being a rural county, just getting people out to vote.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Beasley is in a competitive race with Republican Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , giving her a shot at becoming the first black senator from the Tar Heel State.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Stir up that good trouble up there in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Her race also seen as one of the Democrats` rare chances to pick up a Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): With the frustrations that some voters have with the Biden administration over the economy and inflation, has the president hurt your chances here?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: You know, I certainly understand why there are people who are frustrated and who are disappointed in what`s happening in Washington. They expect more and they deserve more.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Beasley`s Republican opponent meantime happy to hammer the president.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC): Every single county is now a border county because of Joe Biden`s policy.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: And critiquing her judicial record when meeting with police groups.\par \par \uc2 BUDD: I`ve got an opponent right now, she may not admit it, she`s going to try to run deceptively so as a moderate.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: At Beasley`s campaign stops in rural counties, it was abortion rights driving the conversation.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: It is a lot of contradiction to on the one hand protect fundamental freedoms and on the other hand support women who want to have their babies.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (on-camera): Has it angered people? Has it changed some voters` minds?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA JOY, NORTH CAROLINA VOTER: I think so because I think people, as far as women, you know, they want to have control and make decisions that`s going to benefit their bodies and not to be told what to do.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): With less than three months until the election, Beasley is betting on rural voters. She needs them and they need someone who won`t forget them once in Washington.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: It felt good here today, right? It felt good here in Anson County. It felt good in Richmond County, and no one should feel discounted. No one should feel a sense of disengagement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Antonia is here with us. I was looking into some of the polls and there`s some analysis that some of this shift towards Beasley is about abortion. Is that what you heard on the ground?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Absolutely. And in fact, at the two stops that we followed her to, the first questions out of voters` mouths were about abortion. And a lot of the voters I just sat with myself, it was top of mind for them. And this was really fascinating for me, Chris, because as part of our "MEET THE PRESS" county-to-county series, I spent a lot of time in rural North Carolina talking to voters who didn`t vote in the 2020 election. And many of them were telling me back in the winter and the spring not sure that anything is going to get me out to vote right now, I don`t trust either party, people have forgotten about us, and now abortion is bringing a lot of these people to events, a lot of them to the table.\par \par \uc2 And Beasley herself told me that she`s seeing that, that even young people, young voters are coming out and saying that now they`re paying more attention to her campaign because of this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And one of the things we know if people show up, they`re invested, they`re more invested and more likely to vote.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating stuff. Thank you, Antonia. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to joins us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 p.m. Eastern. "KATY TUR REPORTS" will have much more on the Mar-a-Lago search affidavit. We are waiting for the judge`s decision to come down. So don`t go anywhere. Katy will be here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur. It could be a pretty big day and it`s all up to one man.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220819ei8i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Sahil Kapur, Ryan Reilly, Jay Gray, Josh Lederman,  Juanita Tolliver, Matthew Dowd, Tom Costello \par \uc2 7,918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: A former aide, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is back in court staring at the possibility of a criminal conviction for defying a congressional subpoena. The prosecutor charging, he thought he was above the law. In Georgia, 16 Republicans who falsely claimed to be presidential electors have now been told by the Fulton County DA that they could face criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 After yesterday's breaking news in this hour that critical Secret Service text messages from January 5th and 6th are almost certainly gone forever, now NBC News has learned that within the past hour, Secret Service employees were sent at least two e-mails reminding them to preserve their texts but they disappeared anyway. And that is all being investigated.\par \par \uc2 From Wisconsin, the mind-boggling revelation that in the midst of all these investigations, former President Trump is still calling election officials on the phone as recently as last week, trying to get them to overturn the 2020 election. All of that setting a dramatic stage for tomorrow's prime- time hearing. The January 6 committee hearing number eight, and if the first seven are any indication, there could be more fodder for these ongoing probes, including the one by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 So lots to talk about, I want to bring in NBC News justice reporter Ryan Reilly who's covering the Bannon trial. Michael Schmidt is a Washington correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . Greg Bluestein is a political reporter for the Atlanta Journal-Constitution. And Daniel Goldman worked on both impeachment inquiries into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and also served as Assistant U.S. Attorney for the Southern District of New York. He is running for Congress in New York's 10th district. Good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Michael, let's start with a talk about the Secret Service text. I mean, this timeline is a key part of this. So I want to run down what we know. According to the Secret Service, this migration to new phones was planned in December of 2020. Committee member \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  says four congressional committees requested data from their phones on January 16.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service told employees how to preserve their phone data on the 25th, although they left it up to them to do it. Then they started migrating to new phones and deleting old data on the 27th. Bottom line here is their what, 10 -- 11 days between the time they knew what they had to do and the time that it actually happened. Is this looking like a series of mistakes or are these investigations looking into the potential -- this was something more?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SCHMIDT, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Well, we know that the National Archives is looking into this issue, they said as much yesterday, and they were deeply concerned about the fact that these messages went away. Obviously, the perception here is not very good for the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 I think from a fact-gathering perspective for the committee, it's significant because, in Cassidy Hutchinson's testimony, she really brought the Secret Service into the story in a new and different way. She talked about what went on inside the president's you know vehicle, what the president's protectees were saying to others, as this was all going on, the showdown for the wheel in the car between Trump and the person, whoever from the Secret Service was driving it after he spoke on the Ellipse.\par \par \uc2 So because Cassidy Hutchinson sort of opened up a new area, a new appendage of this story about the Secret Service, their messages from this period of time are all the more important because they would help answer some of the questions that have been raised about, you know, what was this Secret Service trying to do on January 6 and why did they not take Trump to the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 And you know, a range of other things about, you know, where Pence was and the protection of Pence and such because essentially, you had a president unwilling to call off a mob that was going after another protectee of the Secret Service. So for investigators, it really hurts their ability to get to the bottom of what was going on in this crucial -- increasingly crucial period of time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If one of the best case scenarios is that this was incompetence, that's still not good. But I want to play part of what \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporter Carol Leonnig, who knows the Secret Service as well as anyone, said about this all on "MORNING JOE" today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, REPORTER, WASHINGTON POST: There's an arrogance that runs through the Secret Service and an effort always to cover up. Always to cover up anything that would be embarrassing, humiliating, or just as importantly, to cover up anything that's bad about the president. I can imagine a lot of Secret Service executives, even the director, wanting to put this chapter behind them, not saying delete the records, but oh, isn't it convenient? This stuff's going to go away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Michael, when you combine a culture of wanting to cover things up with a leadership that in Carol's words, is siding with an individual over the rule of law. Has there ever been -- and I'm taking this from something a former Secret Service director told to your paper, a period quite as tumultuous as this for the Secret Service, and what are the potential implications here?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Look, the Secret Service has gone through ups and downs throughout its years. The best days for the Secret Service are when they're not making news. Because of that, they're not very good at engaging with the public when they have to explain something. And I think, you know, there's been a lot of criticism, especially from Democrats on the Hill about their response here. You know, the -- there was -- if you go back to the \uc2 Obama\uc2  years, there were problems with fence jumpers at the White House and the Secret Service's inability to sort of stop that problem, that was a major story at the time.\par \par \uc2 You know, someone wants -- you know flew a drone that landed on the White House grounds that, you know, no one was able to stop from flying over the White House. That was also during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  years. So, look, the Secret Service has had a lot of ups and downs over the years. And this is -- this is obviously -- this is Cassidy's brought them into the January 6 story in a new way where there's a ton of unanswered questions that perhaps those text messages can answer.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And we should say after that guy got into the White House -- got into the private -- I'm not in the private courts, but got into the White House, there was a big investigation, there were changes that were supposedly made, but here we are in a whole new era and another problem. We also have some breaking news. I want to bring in NBC senior national political reporter Sahil Kapur, who has new reporting on an effort on new legislation to help future vice presidents avoid the pressure campaign \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  experienced in 2020. Sahil, what's the latest there?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: That's right, Chris. Just in the last few minutes, we can report that a bipartisan group of senators has released two bills that would attempt to prevent future candidates for president from stealing elections. This is largely in response to the 2020 election when former President Trump and his allies tried to exploit gaps in the election and election law to stay in power, even though he lost that election.\par \par \uc2 What does this bill do or rather these two bills since it's broken up? It would make abundantly clear that the vice president's role is simply ministerial that he or she does not have any unilateral authority to reject electors submitted by states. It would also raise the threshold for objecting to electors from what it is now, which is simply one member of the House, one member of the Senate, very easy to achieve to one-fifth of both chambers.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Trump did not have those kinds of majorities, especially in the Senate that would not -- his attempt would not have been successful if this bill were law. In addition to clarifying the Presidential Transition Act from the 1960s, to make clear that in cases of closely contested elections, both candidates have resources to aid the transition. It would also address the issue of alternate slates of electors by making clear that it is the governor of the state unless explicitly and previously identified by the state who has the authority of submitting the electors, and it creates an expedited process for an aggrieved candidate chooses to challenge those electors.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, there are some provisions in here doubling penalties for intimidating or harassing election workers, poll workers, and that sort of thing. It's a major effort crafted by more than a dozen senators, Republican and Democrat, it has been blessed by party leadership, including \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the Democratic majority leader, and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Republican minority leader who said yesterday he has been in regular touch with Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , the lead Republican on this proposal. It's sympathetic to what she's trying to achieve.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Chris, on the -- one thing these bills do not do is address the issue of Voting Rights. That has become a partisan issue that Republicans don't want to deal with at the federal level. This is all about preventing stolen elections and preventing coup attempts in the future.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Greg, as we're going to talk about this new possible bill being passed, let's talk about these Georgia Republicans because that's about overturning the election. They say they didn't do anything wrong. They didn't know about a plot to "misuse their votes so Trump can stay in office." So where to potential criminal charges fit in?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, POLITICAL REPORTER, THE ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION: Yes. Well, look, there's definitely trouble in the Trump world. We already knew this Fulton County special grand jury investigation was serious, with subpoenas seeking testimony from members of Trump's inner circle. Now, we've learned that criminal charges could be filed against the Georgians who helped the plot overturn his election defeat. And criminal charges could play in if they -- if the members of the 16 phony electors knowingly submitted false documents and knowingly tried to interfere with election results.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the onus will be on prosecutors to prove that they knowingly did this. That you know, because the argument from the fake -- from the phony electors has always been hey, we just wanted to give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  an avenue in case legal challenges would have succeeded. But as we know, by this -- by mid-December, all the legal challenges that were had been filed, pretty much all of them, had already failed. And the election counts in Georgia had already been upheld multiple times along with the -- an audit of absentee ballots. So it's going to be a -- it's going to be a very interesting few weeks in Georgia as we start seeing the testimony from some of these fake electors.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What isn't though, it -- an interesting few weeks in Georgia, but Daniel, what does the Fulton DA -- the Fulton County DA have to prove?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GOLDMAN, FORMER DIRECTOR OF INVESTIGATIONS, HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: You have to prove that you knowingly signed or agreed to participate in a fraudulent scheme which these fake electors would be. I am actually a little bit more interested in whether any of them cooperate. \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is clearly trying to put pressure on these electors. But my suspicion is her ultimate target is going to be higher up the wrong than the actual fake electors who offered themselves to be put on the slate but to somebody else.\par \par \uc2 And the question that she would want to answer, I want to know, is who was involved all the way up the top with this scheme? Was it coming down from the White House, from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , from his lawyers? And did any of these electors have any communication with any of those people? So this is the start, but I don't think it's the end of this aspect of her investigation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Michael, this was constant phone call is fascinating because it does show that Trump still can't stop fighting the 2020 election fight very actively, not just whatever he sends out on social media or what he has to say when he's at a rally, but that he's still doing stuff. I want to let you know what the Wisconsin assembly speaker, a Republican, told the local ABC affiliate in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT SMITH\uc2 , ABC AFFILIATE POLITICAL & INVESTIGATIVE JOURNALIST: When's the last time you talked to the former president, President Trump?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN VOS, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, WISCONSIN: Within the last week.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Within the last week?\par \par \uc2 VOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Before or after he tweeted about it?\par \par \uc2 VOS: Before.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What was that conversation like?\par \par \uc2 VOS: It was one of those that it's very consistent. He makes his case which, I respect. He would like us to do something different in Wisconsin. I explained that it's not allowed under the Constitution. He has a different opinion. Then he put the tweet out. So that's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Michael, I mean, what do we make of this? Frankly, isn't this the kind of thing that his own supporters are telling him he has to stop -- if he's going to run again, he has to stop if he wants Republicans to take the House and Senate?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: There have been different examples of this that have come out over the -- you know, since he left office of calling local officials in trying to do this or even calling members of Congress, members of the House, trying to push this idea. It is -- it is remarkable because most people would have been deterred at this point, based on all the massive amount of scrutiny that has come under his behavior around the election, and his efforts to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 You can make an argument that he's -- there's a continued effort to overturn the 2020 election a year and a half after it occurred. So you know, obviously, undeterred by what happened and still out there trying to do this, trying to overturn this election, that there's been no evidence of fraud, and so, you know, I use the word remarkable before with Trump. We sometimes run out of adjectives, but I guess it is quite remarkable.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Daniel, could that Wisconsin call be part of the larger narrative to bolster any kind of criminal prosecution?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: It's consistent with everything else we know. I think the fact that he is continuing to do this is more a reflection of his efforts to show that he really does believe there was a fraud, and therefore, he didn't have the knowing state of mind that he would need. But it is truly remarkable that we are here after what we've seen in the January 6 hearings that have laid out in such tremendous detail with such compelling evidence, how the scheme to overturn the election started from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and went down the chain. And that ultimately, after December 14, it became just a pure and simple coup attempt.\par \par \uc2 But he is still at it, Chris. He's still trying to not only push the big lie about 2020 but lay the foundation for him to do it again in 2024. This is not over. This continues to this day. It continues both as to 2020, as this call reflects, but it also continues around the country. And you've talked about Sahil about the electoral Count Act revisions, but some of these voting laws that are being put to the side allow for the states to overturn elections based on allegations of fraud -- mere allegations. That is designed to allow for a Trump-installed elected official to overturn the election in a state, overturn the will of the people. So we have to keep a broader look on what is actually going on with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Yes, the January 6 hearings are important.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Yes, any potential criminal charges that arise out of 2020 from the Department of Justice are very important. But this is an ongoing issue that must be addressed.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Ryan, going to where you are, boy, the prosecutor really went after \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  yesterday in court saying among many other things that he thumbed his nose at the law. Where are we in this trial right now?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: Yes, so we're hearing testimony from a senior official on the January 6 committee who was involved in the process of actually serving the subpoena on \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 's lawyer who accepted service at the subpoena on behalf -- his behalf back in September of 2020, or 2021. He was required to testify in October of 2021. And basically, because of the way the judges laid out this case, there's a very limited defense that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 's lawyers can bring forward so we're not expecting an extremely lengthy trial here.\par \par \uc2 After this first witness, there might be a couple of more. There's always the potential, of course, that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  could testify, but I think that that's unlikely as much as he might want that. It won't be televised, I should note, so that might factor into his thinking on that. But I think that you know, overall, there's a -- there's a scenario that we might end up, you know, with this case out to a jury, actually, by tomorrow night when this -- the January 6 Select Committee is expected to go back and have this prime-time hearing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan, Michael, Greg, Daniel, thanks to all of you, much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 A Trump-endorsed election denier is now one step closer to Maryland's governor's mansion and it happened with a little help from Democrats. But first, climate emergency, the deadly extreme heat conditions millions are facing from the Midwest to Europe. So hot in Texas, fires are burning wild and water pipes are bursting. So what's the president's plan? The action he's ready to announce today after big efforts in Congress failed. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Pick a spot on earth as nbcnews.com puts it. Chances are you're likely to find a climate emergency unfolding there right now. Record- setting temps in the UK topping 104 Tuesday. London's mayor says the city's fire service battled its busiest day since World War II. France, Spain, Portugal, Greece, and parts of North Africa, all battling flames of their own right now. It's brutal. And the death toll is only rising. Portugal alone, reporting more than a thousand deaths from this latest heatwave, and Asia as well with parts of China bracing for 107 degrees in parts of the country. It's the kind of triple-digit heat now forecast for 44 million Americans today, 290 million facing 90-plus degree weather.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is the hottest summer that I've seen in Dallas really that I remember since 1980.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This unrelenting and deadly heat providing a powerful argument for action and the president trying to answer the call by announcing new executive actions in little more than an hour to fight climate change. I want to bring in NBC's Jay Gray from Dallas, Texas, NBC's Josh Lederman in Somerset, Massachusetts ahead of the President's speech, and MSNBC political analyst Juanita Tolliver.\par \par \uc2 Jay, there was this young boy that we interviewed in Dallas today. He told NBC that it feels like being on the surface of the sun. How are Texans holding up today? How are you holding up?\par \par \uc2 JAY GRAY, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Chris, he's not going to get any argument from me. It is blistering. It's 98 right now, on the way to a high of 108. That's after two days here in Dallas topping out at 109. A look at the 10-day forecast showing that there are no days in the next 10 days to be forecast below 100 degrees, most at around 103, 104. It's been a rough go for many Slurpees, snow cones, and splash pads, you can see people playing behind me they are the things keeping people going right now. Even summer sports for kids winding up their summer break tough to get to.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIAM FERGUSON, 10-YEAR-OLD DALLAS RESIDENT: So my favorite sport is like football and basketball. So I can't play those anymore. And I get in my pool every single night.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why can't you play?\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: Because it's so hot outside.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So hot and you can't play?\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: Yes. I don't want to practice, at least.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAY: You know, why practice if you can't play? It is just too hot right now. A couple of quick important points here, \uc2 ERCOT\uc2 , the company that runs the grid here in Texas telling us that they had record usage yesterday for the 10th time this year, they could break that record today, water is becoming a problem, the grounds shifting because of all of this heat and water mains are breaking. So that's becoming an issue. And then there are extreme fire hazards across Texas right now. We've seen multiple wildfires here in the North Texas area one just west of Fort Worth that is still burning right now and is chase people from their homes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Josh, I can make the argument that compared to Jay, you and the president got off easy because you've got temps in the low 90s right now. But with this heat wave, providing a powerful argument for the president to do something, what is he going to do here?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: Which include --\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I don't know how easy we got off, Chris. In fact, the phone that I'm using right now to communicate with you just overheated and shut itself off, we had to swap out a new phone. But look, the president is finding on this issue of climate change, just like he has on abortion, on gun rights, and so many other issues that what he would like to do, and what he actually can do without Congress are very different things. And so there's a clear effort by the president today to show that he is taking executive action and doing what he can to address this climate crisis, even if it is far short of the sweeping steps that he promised as a candidate and hoped as president to be able to enact.\par \par \uc2 So today, the president will be visiting this former coal plant that now makes the undersea cables that are used for the offshore wind industry, as he announces new funding directed for FEMA to help communities become more resilient to extreme heat. There are also additional steps the president will be announcing to try to boost the offshore wind energy industry.\par \par \uc2 But all of that is really small potatoes when it compares to the 500 plus billion dollars in funding for the climate the president had sought, the major steps to rein in emissions from power plants that he had hoped to be able to enact, that is now looking unlikely, even as the White House is gingerly dancing around this question of whether they still think there's any possibility of getting some type of reconciliation deal with Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  that would include significant climate provisions before the November elections, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, on a percentage basis, what are the chances? What are you hearing from folks at the White House? Do they think that there's realistically any chance at all?\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Well, they certainly are holding out hope, but they are still leaving a hands-off approach to the Senate negotiations. They're leaving it up to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  to deal with Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and they don't want to do anything that would make that more complicated. But the fact that the White House is going ahead anyway, with executive actions is a clear indication that they're not putting a whole lot of money on the possibility of getting any climate legislation through Congress in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, there's reporting, Juanita, by Jonathan Lemire, our good friend here, that frankly, the folks in the White House are furious about \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  doing this again. But they're kind of backing off publicly not saying what they're saying privately because they do hold out some hope. At what point do you say, OK, this isn't going to happen and you got to figure out some other way? Or do you just say my powers as a president, are limited, I can't move Congress so I'm going to do these executive actions? It's what I can do.\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I feel like this is a yes and sure situation, Chris. Yes. Keep applying pressure to Manchin behind closed doors. Sure. We know he's a physical guy. But I think the reality is that the White House should also be applying pressure to Republicans who are sitting fully on the sidelines watching the world burn and overheat like we all are and still dug their heels in on opposing any type of climate investments or climate change action.\par \par \uc2 And so I hope the White House does that kind of offense and defense playbook simultaneously. But I was shaking my head no on your previous question about whether or not this is realistic to try to get something through reconciliation before the midterms because Manchin has been clear about where he stands this entire time. I think what's frustrating about this latest version or addition of his reneging is the fact that he was so integrally involved in the negotiations with Senator Schumer. He was right there at the table and then decided to step away.\par \par \uc2 So absolutely, the White House has a right to be frustrated. But there are also going to be questions toward the White House about why they're not going so far as to declare this a climate change emergency, Chris. That's the other part of this because on top of the president's executive actions related to climate resiliency, which are important and include special position -- provisions and funding, for example, for low-income families to cover heating and cooling costs in these extreme weather that we're experiencing, but they are still stopping short of declaring an emergency here. And I bet you that is something that young voters who have climate change at the top of their focus for these midterms are going to be paying attention to and asking why the White House is stopping short?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I'm -- how closely are you going to be watching what the president has to say? Because there's some other -- another argument to made -- to be made, right? You're being delivered a set of circumstances where most of the constituents everywhere are feeling the heat. They can see how the future doesn't look great, right, worried about whether or not the grids going to hold up in Texas, maybe in some other places. We're not built for this. The world isn't built for this.\par \par \uc2 So, unless you accept the fact and it's not a fact that most people are climate change deniers, is there a bully pulpit that's not being used properly? And I'm not just saying by the president, but by members of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , by members of Congress, that they are being delivered a set of circumstances that gives them an opportunity to put some pressure on members of Congress to do something.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Absolutely, Chris. And I think we're starting to see signs of that from the letter that Senator Merkley and others sent to the White House. They're like, hey, hold the fossil fuel industry, speak to the fire like you did big tobacco, hey, figure out ways that you can start expanding investments in clean energy programs through the military or on federal- funded lands, make sure that you're exhausting every option because we are watching.\par \par \uc2 What the map you showed earlier, Chris had triple digits across a large loss of the entire country. And so there absolutely are conditions globally and nationally, that, senators and other big speakers who have large audiences can speak to, to get a broader conversation about this going and applying that same pressure I described before to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jay, Josh, Juanita, thank you all very much, the speech to come. And as we inch closer towards the November midterms, Democrats are spending some big bucks on TV ads to elevate far-right candidates in those GOP primaries, but it does come with a risk. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:42]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now we're waiting to find out which Democrat will run for governor in Maryland. Wes Moore is currently leading \uc2 Tom Perez\uc2  by about 10 points, but we only have 61 percent of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 We do know, however, which Republican one of them will face. It's Dan Cox, a Trump endorsed state delegate who attended the January 6th Stop the Steal rally ahead of the attack on the Capitol. Cox got some help, though, from an unexpected source. National Democrats who paid for TV ads that highlighted his right-wing views.\par \par \uc2 It's a controversial strategy that experts say could pay off for Democrats by tying \uc2 Cox\uc2  to Trump in a solidly blue state, although they did elect a Republican the last time around. It also comes, though, with a major risk, putting an election denier one step closer to the governor's mansion.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Don Calloway, a Democratic strategist and founder of the National Voter Protection Action Fund, and Matthew Dowd, political strategist, founder of Country Over Party and an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Don, is this a risk worth taking?\par \par \uc2 DAN CALLOWAY, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Absolutely. We have to understand that there's precedent for this. Remember in 2012, my friend and former home state senator Claire McCaskill invested -- well, not she, but committees related to her. Keep the separation there. Committees related to the Democrats in that race invested substantially in supporting Todd Akin, who was every bit of crazy as this guy Dan Cox is, and it succeeded.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:10]\par \par \uc2 Ultimately Democrats want to run against the weakest candidate, and polling and research has shown in 2012 it was Todd Akin in Missouri, in 2022, it's this guy Dan Cox in Maryland. So there's some risk, but I think we have a much stronger candidate than we did in \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  -- than who ran against \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  eight years ago. Wes Moore and \uc2 Tom Perez\uc2  will both be fantastic standard bearers. And it's a calculated risk that Democrats were willing to make that million-dollar in Dan Cox because, objectively, he is crazy enough to be repulsive to the moderate or undecided Maryland voter.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Don is right, Matthew. This wasn't a few ads here or there. The \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  did spend more than $1 million on ads that were largely Cox centered. National Democrats have used this strategy. Illinois, Pennsylvania. I know you've been vocal about keeping people who deny the 2020 election results as far away from power as possible.\par \par \uc2 So, do you see this as a big risk?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR AND ANALYST: I think it's a huge risk. And for two reasons. One, political. This crass politics is oftentimes, yes, it worked in Missouri but oftentimes what we as a candidate might wish for and get isn't necessarily the best thing possible. I remember all of the folks that said let's make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the Republican nominee because \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  will beat him easily, then a few months later \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the White House, doing all of the things that he did in the course of that.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that you spend millions of dollars on a bank shot to get there, I would -- if I were a strategist again, as I was before, I would rather have those millions of dollars being spent directly in the course of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 That's first. But just more broadly and deeper is we're at a moment, we're not in 2012. We're not Claire McCaskill versus whoever the crazy Republican was then. We now have a series of election deniers on the Republican side that if they take office will cause all kinds of havoc, not only for governor of Maryland or governor of Pennsylvania, but attorney general, secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 There's over 100 election deniers that have emerged in the course of this that may be on the ballot. And I think that if we're really pro-democracy, and that's what we really want, then that's the most important issue, which I believe, then anything that helps a Republican who is an election denier or a big lie pusher is a bad thing. I think we should be supporting the Republicans who are standing alone in their primaries who acknowledge that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won, who believe in voting rights, who believe that elections have consequences and matter.\par \par \uc2 And so, first, crass politics, I think it's a mistake, but just in a deeper pro-democracy sense, I think when you advocate for somebody that's an election denier, even if they are a weaker opponent puts people, again as you said, one step closer. And I think it's fraught with risk and I think we should be supporting Republicans who support democracy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And there's another part of this that we haven't mentioned yet, Don, which some people will raise. That even if the candidate doesn't win this time, by giving them a position in the general election, you raise their name recognition, you raise their profile, all that comes with that, who is to say they wouldn't use that even if they lost to successfully run another time? Maybe not for governor. Maybe for something else.\par \par \uc2 CALLOWAY: Well, all of that is true, but let's be very clear about something. The problem is not with the Democratic strategy to support and ultimately for the victory of a far inferior candidate. The problem is with the Republican electorate who voted for this guy over a classic William F. Buckley, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 -style, Chamber of Commerce, former Cabinet secretary that he defeated.\par \par \uc2 The problem is ultimately with the crazies of the Maryland Republican Party who voted for this guy. Democrats' support of Dan Cox in a primary didn't force people to go to a ballot box and decided to vote for the big lie, decided to vote a candidate who does not support reasonable exceptions for abortion, decided to vote for an anti-democracy Republican. So the problem is in the deep-seated heart of the Maryland Republican Party who elevated this guy with their votes.\par \par \uc2 Now to your question about -- I'm sorry, what was the question? Ultimately --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, the question was, then you raise the profile, you raise the name recognition of somebody, maybe even if they lose this time, does that set them up potentially to run for something else in the future?\par \par \uc2 CALLOWAY: No, it does not. You have to remember that Dan Cox got into the state legislature in the House of Delegates, the lower chamber in 2019. He is effectively a back bencher. This is not a person of substantial profile. Prior to this, this is not a person of substantial achievement and accomplishment in life. And there are frankly Larry Hogans out there in much better, more reasonable candidates that will destroy him once Maryland Republican Party purges itself of the crazy Trumpist element.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 Now does he go somewhere and work at Mar-a-Lago as a cabin guy? Perhaps. I don't know so maybe he elevates his profile among the national MAGA set but I certainly think he's finished as a reasonable alternative for Maryland Republicans against a good and strong Democrat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This is such as interesting conversation and it's an important one, too. Really quick. Really quick, Matthew.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I'll do really quick. I agree completely with Don that the problem is infiltrated in the Republican Party. My only thing is let's not pour gasoline using Democratic dollars on the Republican fire. That's my problem because when you do that it can be a wildfire and start wiping out things you have no idea. Don't pour gasoline on their fire.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Matthew Dowd, Don Calloway, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 A rare moment, Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  praising House Republicans. Why he think a bill to protect same-sex marriage could actually become law soon.\par \par \uc2 You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It what has become a really rare show of bipartisanship on Capitol Hill, 47 Republicans joined all the House Democrats Tuesday voting to guarantee the right to same-sex marriage. It comes after the Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  suggested that right could be in jeopardy after the court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But does that bill stand a chance in the 50-50 Senate? Well, you might be surprised. NBC News reports that Ohio's Republican Senator Rob Portman will co-sponsor the bill. It makes him the second Republican to officially voice support after \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 . And here's what North Carolina's \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  told our reporters on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Senator, the Democrats are talking about bringing up a bill that would codify same-sex marriage over here. Would you vote that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): I'm looking at the bill, and probably will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  saying he'll probably support it. Let's bring in Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, does all the Republican support and signals mean that this could be a game change? Could we be looking at this bill actually going through?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): I think this bill will pass. I think this bill will get more than 60 votes in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And I hope that we bring it up for a vote very quickly. We are scheduled to go back to our states in August, but I think we should be open to staying here as long as it takes in order to get that piece of legislation passed because as you said, the Supreme Court decision is the only thing right now that protects the right of everyone to marry whoever they love in this country.\par \par \uc2 On the books today is actually a federal law that denies the right of gay couples to marry. So if that Supreme Court decision was ever reversed, we would revert to the prior law passed in the 1990s which bans gay marriage. So let's get this done, let's get this done now, let's not leave anything to chance in a Supreme Court that increasingly looks like it is trying to use its slim majority to impose a right-wing ideology on the country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let's also talk about Ukraine. The first lady of Ukraine, \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2 , is in Washington and she asked you, she asked members of Congress to continue supporting her country. Let's take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OLENA ZELENSKA\uc2 , FIRST LADY OF UKRAINE (through translator): We remain completely broken when our world is destroyed by a war. Tens of thousands of such world have been destroyed in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: She wants more, acknowledging that the U.S. has already provided billions in military support. But should we be offering more, not just in terms of weapons we provide, but also particularly humanitarian aid, particularly to children?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, many of us were double booked today while the first lady was speaking. We also have a Foreign Relations Committee hearing on the global food crisis and all around the world more people are starving than at any time during our lifetime, largely because much of the world's grain supply comes from Ukraine and Russia.\par \par \uc2 And so we need to be able to do two things at once. Yes, we should be supplying more aid to Ukraine to defend itself. And I hope that very soon we will reauthorizing another package of military support to Ukraine. We need to stick with the Ukrainian people and the Ukrainian military for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 But we are also going to have to be more generous around the world when it comes to the food crisis that's created by the Russians' decision to essentially deny the world the benefit of Ukrainian wheat. Russia is using food as a weapon, not just in Ukraine, but all around the world. And the United States and other rich countries around the world can step up and do more to keep people from starving because of Russia's decision to hold Ukrainian food goods hostage as a result of this war.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tomorrow, the House Judiciary Committee has scheduled the first markup on the assault weapons ban legislation, first in two decades. It would ban the sale, import, manufacture or transfer of certain semiautomatic weapons.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that there's been a sea change, that you can convince your Republican Senate colleagues to support this legislation? It is something that you are obviously deeply involved in, but they just helped pass one of the biggest pieces of gun legislation in decades without this. Has anything changed since then?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I think a lot has changed outside of Washington. I don't know that enough has changed inside Washington. As you mentioned, we're just three weeks from passing the most significant piece of antigun legislation in 30 years in Congress. And of course it didn't include a full ban on assault weapons but it did include waiting periods for these under 21 buyers that recently have been the ones buying the AR-15s and carrying out these mass atrocities.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 But clearly the only thing that is really going to prevent many of these mass shootings is to get rid of the AR-15s, and you saw in Uvalde that 370 uniform trained officers felt that a teenager with an AR-15 was so scary as to sit outside that classroom for an hour and a half. If 370 trained uniform officers aren't enough to confront an individual with an AR-15, maybe we shouldn't allow teenagers to have them in the first place.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , we appreciate your time. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, could help be on the way? A creative solution maybe to the nation's pilot shortage. Why one airline says it can fix the crisis, while others are saying not so fast.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today the persistent pilot shortage continues to impact air travel all across the country, maybe even your summer vacation plans, but the leaders of a regional airline believe their pilot training can help major airlines meet that demand.\par \par \uc2 NBC's Tom Costello has the latest.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're going to accelerate to 88 before you pull the power out.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Five thousand feet over Indianapolis and the newest student pilots who could one day fly you cross country are learning the basics. Just as the national pilot shortage has led to massive flight cancellations this summer. Now \uc2 Republic Airways\uc2  which flies smaller regional flights for United, Delta and American, says its pilot training is so good with even more its graduates should be treated the same as military pilots, who can qualify for a commercial pilot's license with just 750 flight hours, half the hours required for nonmilitary pilots.\par \par \uc2 Republic CEO \uc2 Bryan Bedford\uc2  is petitioning the \uc2 FAA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): You are arguing also that your training is just as good as the military's.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN BEDFORD\uc2 , \uc2 REPUBLIC AIRWAYS\uc2  CEO: Using more technology, more flight simulation, and we can do exactly the same thing. So working with the military, we can build a better model than what we have today.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Republic's Lift Academy trains 200 students a year in both planes --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're coming into 5-left (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: -- and simulators. Kaylee Price's (PH) dad was a \uc2 UPS\uc2  pilot. Now she's learning to fly.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What does this career offer that kind of entices you?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE PRICE, PILOT TRAINEE: The freedom honestly. It really is the most freeing feeling in the entire world being up in the air.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): After 12 months, graduates typically become instructors or charter pilots to rack up the required flight hours. But becoming a pilot isn't cheap, typically costing $100,000 to $200,000. A big reason for the pilot shortage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom also reports there are opponents of the Republic Airline plan, including Captain Sully Sullenberger who says it's not rigorous enough.\par \par \uc2 That's going to do it for us this hour. Join us again tomorrow from noon to 2:00 Eastern and every weekday for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS at 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220721ei7k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  for July 08, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Jonathan Capehart, Jill Wine-Banks, Ryan Reilly,  Eugene Daniels \par \uc2 7,589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: It`s been targeted, challenged, banned all over the country including ones famously following a viral video of an angry parent attacking the book at a local school board meeting.\par \par \uc2 That`s this weekend at 8 a.m. Eastern. Time now for The Last Word with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  filling in for Lawrence. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ali. It is great to see you, I feel like I haven`t seen you in ages.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, you have a great show. I will be watching it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right, Ali, have a good evening.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thanks, buddy.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So, everyone, how worried is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight? Today, the January 6th committee heard eight and a half hours of testimony from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . Trump`s White House counsel at the time of the January 6th insurrection. And it was all on video. Meaning we might hear from Cipollone during the next hearing on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 As Trump`s former White House counsel, Cipollone was in the room for many of the key events during Trump`s attempts to overturn the results of the 2020 election. Cipollone`s name has come up extensively throughout the six hearings the committee has already held. But it was after we heard from former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson late last month that the pressure really ramped up on Cipollone to testify, under oath, before the committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : I saw Mr. Cipollone right before I walked out onto west exec that morning and Mr. Cipollone said something to the effect of, please make sure we don`t go up to the capitol, Cassidy. Keep in touch with me. We are going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: There are developments today about potential new testimony the committee may hear from two other individuals who were closely aligned with Trump.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the headline for my paper, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Trump considers waving claim to executive privilege for \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . My colleagues Isaac Stanley-Becker, Josh Dawsey and Jacqueline Alemany report, quote, "former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is considering sending a letter to \uc2 Stephen K. Bannon\uc2 , saying that he is waiving executive privilege, potentially clearing the way for his former chief strategist to testify before the House select committee investigating the pro-Trump riot at the capitol."\par \par \uc2 The letter, quote, "would say that the former president is now willing to give up that claim if Bannon can reach an agreement on the terms of an appearance before the panel, according to three people familiar with the matter."\par \par \uc2 Now to be clear, Trump doesn`t have a legitimate claim to executive privilege over Bannon`s testimony. He is not the president anymore and, more importantly, Bannon didn`t even work for him during the insurrection. But wait, that`s not all.\par \par \uc2 The lawyer for the founder of the far-right militia group, known as the Oath Keepers, tells NBC News that his client is willing to testify publicly, under oath to the January 6 committee. But he is setting some conditions. According to his attorney, Elmer Stewart Rhodes will only sit down with the committee for testimony if they agreed to do it during a live public hearing that is carried on TV. Network TV, not just \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rhodes, have you ever heard the phrase, beggars can`t be choosers? Well, considering you are currently locked up and waiting to face trial for seditious conspiracy charges, I think it applies here.\par \par \uc2 The committee`s next hearing, scheduled for Tuesday afternoon next week, is expected to focus on the connections between Trump world and the extremist groups that led the charge on the capitol. Committee member Jamie Raskin tells Politico, quote, "our investigation shows that there was a tremendous convergence of interests between the domestic violent extremist groups and the broader MAGA movement. This hearing will be the moment when one sees both the convergence of efforts at a political coup with the insurrectionary mob violence. We see how these two streams of activity become one."\par \par \uc2 The committee gave us something of a preview of that connection during their last hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): The White House continued to receive updates about planned demonstrations, including information regarding the Proud Boys organizing and planning to attend events on January 6th. Although Ms. Hutchinson has no detailed knowledge of any planning involving the Proud Boys for January 6th, she did note this.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I recall hearing the word Oath Keeper and hearing the word Proud Boys closer to the planning of the January 6th rally, when Mr. Giuliani would be around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now, Jill Wine-Banks, who served as assistant Watergate prosecutor. She is an MSNBC contributor. We are also joined by Ryan Reilly, a justice reporter for NBC News. And Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former deputy assistant attorney general. Thank you all very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 Of course, Jill, let me start with you. So, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  spoke to the committee for eight and a half hours today. Just how important is his testimony to the work that the committee is doing?\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE-BANKS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Based on what we know from Cassidy Hutchinson, and other witnesses it is very important. First of all, he would corroborate some of her testimony and take away any questions about her total credibility. That is important.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly, he was in the room with the former president. He was there. He wasn`t over hearing conversations. He wasn`t hearing \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  say this is what the president wants or this is what the president said. He was in the room. So, he could really be the John Dean of this hearing. He could be the one who takes it to the next level.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Harry, here is what The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has reported about Trump and Cipollone`s relationship. This is interesting. Though Cipollone has been a fairly reliable public ally to Trump, he is not close to the former president, according to multiple people in Trump`s orbit. Trump often castigated Cipollone, saying in private that he was one of the worst lawyers of all-time.\par \par \uc2 He even mocked Cipollone to his face in front of other advisers saying, quote, "why do I have the worst lawyer ever?" Trump yelled that Cipollone always said no to him, according to a former senior administration official. So, Harry, how concerned should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  be about what Cipollone might have told the committee today?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: A lot. He do it a long list by the way, of lawyers of Trump who Trump trashes and throws under the bus. Cipollone is a longtime acolyte of Bill Barr who, of course, has famously parted company with Trump. And that`s how Trump used him. That`s how he became White House counsel.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously no love lost. This hesitation about testifying -- and he had some, it had everything to do with protecting the institution of White House counsel. And nothing to do with loyalty -- or little to do with loyalty to Trump, notwithstanding that he defended him in the impeachment. He has two really important sources of testimony.\par \par \uc2 As Jill says he tells Trump repeatedly, this is illegal, that`s illegal, so it goes to Trump`s knowledge. And then second, he participates in or overhear some of the most incendiary pieces of testimony we have heard so far.\par \par \uc2 For instance, when \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  says, you heard Trump, he doesn`t want to call off the dogs. He thinks they are right and Pence should be hanged. He says that to Cipollone and there is no possible claim of privilege there. There are four or five things like that, including calling the DOJ letter a murder suicide pact, that Cipollone has the goods on Trump and presumably provided them to the committee today.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ryan, let`s talk about these developments from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and Stewart Rhodes, both now apparently willing to testify under oath before the committee. Rhodes already spoke to the committee back in February and pleaded the fifth dozens of time -- times. Give us the latest on his legal situation. Do you see this as a serious offer?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, JUSTICE REPORTER, NBC NEWS: You know, I don`t. I think -- the committee already has six hours of reported testimony, even if he`s taking the fifth for part of that. They already have a lot of this narrative. And I just don`t see the committee sort of handing over the reins to an accused seditionist and basically allowing him to dictate the terms, and essentially give him the platform to recruit potential members of the Oath Keepers going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s pretty unlikely. You know, also setting that aside, the Justice Department isn`t going to be too keen about just allowing him to leave the cell and head over to Capitol Hill. So, you know, he might as well request that he gets a phony ride from jail to the capitol. I just think it`s so (Inaudible) that it`s never happened.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Phony ride. Harry, let me get your read on this. Because the benefit of the committee making a deal with Bannon and Rhodes, is that it would all be admissible, but the downside is that they could turn the sober and serious committee hearings into a circus, potentially at the cost of losing the respect of the public. What would you do?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Forget about it. It`s a stunt on both ends. No chance the committee does it, and that`s what I would do with if you asked. First of all, they had zero witnesses that they haven`t videotaped before. They are not going to start with Stewart Rhodes.\par \par \uc2 And Bannon is, I think, just a ploy to help in his contempt trial and give the impression that Trump does have some privilege claim to waive, which, as you pointed out at the top, Jonathan, he surely doesn`t. It`s a trick having to do with his criminal conviction. No deals with him. The committee won`t touch it.\par \par \uc2 [22:09:56]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: What do you make of this, Jill? We know Trump was upset that no one has been defending him during the committee hearings? Could this be an attempt by Trump world to go on the offensive and at least try to level the playing field?\par \par \uc2 WINE-BANKS: It certainly could be. But I agree with Harry, that it is a stunt. That it is really intended to help Bannon, particularly with his upcoming trial. And there would be no gain in the sense of the overall hearing. They have been very effective in putting forth a narrative that shows all the strands of the conspiracy, to take down our democracy. The conspiracy to stop the counting of the ballots. The fake electors. The pressure on Pence, the pressure on state legislators. It is all part of one big conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 There is also the violence on January 6 that`s part of it. And there is no gain from getting the testimony of either Stewart Rhodes, particularly without it having been a videotaped deposition in advance. And the same is certainly true for Bannon. I cannot believe he would tell the truth. It just isn`t within any part of his heart or his brain.\par \par \uc2 So, why do it? It would only be the same as having had Jim Jordan on the committee to disrupt the committee. We don`t need any disruption. We need the facts. They have to pass legislation based on what they are seeing. The Department of Justice has to see what the case looks like. And that won`t help by having those people testify.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ryan, I got to get you on one more thing before we go. While the committee has been focused on getting new testimony and holding their public hearings, the Justice Department has continued making arrests from the people who were on the ground at the capitol that day. What can you tell us about some of the individuals who have been arrested today?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: That`s right, there were at least five cases unsealed today. There were couple of folks, two brothers actually who were turned in by one of the daughters who turned in their uncle and her father by tweeting about him, live tweeting about it. And then that was a tip they followed up on.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also a John (Inaudible), he is an individual who had an in God we trust tattoo on his head. And then there is Tyler Elbridge (Ph) from Colorado who, you know, brag and said, I hope this doesn`t get me thrown in jail. It may make me lose my reputation but I don`t care. So, a lot of these folks just, you know, brag about it online and then finally, 18 months later, they are getting arrested by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ryan Reilly, Harry Litman, Jill Wine-Banks, thank you all very much for coming to the Last Word tonight.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, Mallory McMorrow will join me to discuss the current state of our politics, which includes -- which includes fighting election stealing, mass murders, police violence, forced birth, even for children and rape victims. That is a lot, I know. But there is a solution. It`s our superpower to change things. Mallory McMorrow and I will talk about it, next.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I don`t think the court or for that matter Republicans who for decades have pushed the extreme agenda, have a clue about the power of American women, but they were about to find out in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Today, President Biden signed an executive order that will safeguard access to medication abortions, protect patient privacy, launched public education efforts and bolster the security of and the legal options to those seeking and providing abortion services.\par \par \uc2 But there`s only so much the president can do, and one thing he can`t do is restore access to abortion. But there is something that can be done. You can vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Based on the reason in the court, there is no constitutional right to choose, the only the way -- the only way to fulfill and restore that right in this country is by voting, by exercising the power at ballot box. Let me explain. We need two additional pro-choice senators and a pro-choice House to codify Roe as federal law. Your vote can make that a reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I believe as I wrote in my \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  column today that voting is your one superpower in our democracy. In order for Biden to do all of the ambitious things that Democrats wants, he needs a House and Senate that will send him legislation that he can sign into law. He needs a bigger Democratic majority in Congress, especially in the Senate, not the way for thin one that currently exists.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are some who will claim they are demoralized because of what they see as Biden`s legislative impotence and then stay home in November. And if they do, Republicans will win bigger majorities and demolish even more rights and then those same people will blame the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On America`s 246th birthday, still raw from losing a constitutional right at the hands of the Republican appointed justices on the Supreme Court, a mass murderer was able to very easily obtain a weapon of war and kill a bunch of people and destroy a bunch of families forever because God forbid, we ever touch that right.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Michigan State Senator Mallory McMorrow who will join us in a moment tweeted this. Yesterday, I texted with a friend in Illinois who said they started their day yesterday walking to neighborhood field games and ended the day sheltering in place from a mass shooter. It`s too much, she said. It`s just too much.\par \par \uc2 We weren`t made to endure this much trauma. That`s why pleas of just vote feel so trite and disgusting. I know there`s a dystopian disconnect. Like elected officials don`t feel that trauma, don`t know the pain, the loss. Don`t understand how hard it is to want to participate in a system that doesn`t care about you.\par \par \uc2 I got into this work knowing this. Gun violence, reproductive rights, discrimination, these were all policy choices. Policy choices made for too long by other people for us. Without us. Politics won`t solve everything, but we deserve people making policy who give a damn.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic State Senator Mallory McMorrow of Michigan. Senator McMorrow, it`s great to see again. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. MALLORY MCMORROW (D-MI): Of course, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So, all right. I want to start with you tweeted. Like elected officials don`t feel that trauma. You are an elected official, you are -- you are one only because people voted for you. Can you just kind of expand on what you see as the disconnect?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Of course, and I think that the president was right on today, because there is a difference between telling people to just vote and then there is laying out a plan. They are saying this is everything I`m able to do with my power and here`s what`s standing in the way of real action.\par \par \uc2 It`s treating voters like we`re on the same team because we are. If we work together and we`re honest about what`s standing in the way of policy changes, then we lay out the roadmap, and then we give people a reason and a belief in voting again. Because it hurts. It hurt that I was walking with my daughter in a 4th of July parade and then got home to the news that another parade where friends of mine live end it in horror.\par \par \uc2 And I think that more of us who express that, the more of us who feel what our constituents are feeling, but then lay out a plan that says that we`re all in this together and it needs every single person on the team.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Isn`t some of this and this anger about a system that favors the minority, the Electoral College, the Senate, but also because the minority has shown it will break democracy in a way that Democrats will not?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Absolutely. And we have to recognize the urgency of that moment. So, it`s not only voting. It`s voting in this election because what we`re seeing around the country and the Supreme Court is already nodded that they`re going to take this up. It is sending everything back to the states which some will argue, you know, that`s federalism at its finest.\par \par \uc2 But it`s sending things back to the states, and then partisan gerrymandering, and taking away the power of the courts to rule against that. So, we are going to get into a place if we don`t act now, where we will no longer be able to fairly elect people who represent us and potentially not even be able to elect the next president. So, it is on all of us and it`s on all of us right now.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I want to bring up something else you tweeted because I think it`s really important. You tweeted, it also has to be said that those who have done the most work have often also endured the most trauma. Black women, LGBTQ people, to start, so it`s critical that all of us -- especially those of us who are generally OK step up. Just because you`re OK doesn`t mean this is OK.\par \par \uc2 Senator McMorrow, we saw Black women push Biden to victory in Georgia in 2020 and give Democrats the majority in the Senate, so effectively Republicans passed a voter suppression law in that state. But I`m just wondering, not voting because you are demoralized is a privilege, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Absolutely. You know, I`ve seen criticism of people who have been devastated because that shooting took place in Highland Park which is, you know, a wealthier community, a largely white community. And we see gun violence every day. We see gun violence every day in communities just here in Detroit. We saw a young police officer, a father of two get gunned down by an assault rifle responding to this -- that happens every single day.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to get off of our butts, pull ourself out of this, it hurts, it really hurts the news every day, and lock arms with people who have been doing this work for a lot longer than people who look like me. Because it`s true, this is not OK, but we are the only ones who have the power to change it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You`ve been posting some photos of meetings that you`ve had with voters. What are they telling you?\par \par \uc2 [22:24:55]\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: That is too much, that it hurts, that this country is in a really dark place that we`ve never seen this divided. But people fundamentally still believe in this country. You know, what`s reassuring to me is the fact that even despite all the demoralization, people are still coming out to meet me and they want to talk to about how we can work together, how we can make our state and our communities and our country better.\par \par \uc2 That`s what this country was founded on. And the fact that people are still showing up and showing a pretty big in Michigan. Michigan can be the playbook that everybody else follows.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I`m going to say this right now. To all the folks who are watching, who have people on the ballot in their states wherever they are, if there is somebody who talks like and has the passion of Mallory McMorrow who you see on your screen, go to the polls in November and vote for them.\par \par \uc2 If you like her voice on the screen right now, find the person who`s on the ballot in your jurisdiction and vote for them and send them to elected office so that they can do what the American people want to. Michigan State Senator Mallory McMorrow, as always, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Thank you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, reproductive rights were first, but what up -- what other rights could face undoing by this radical Supreme Court and what needs to be done to stop it? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:30:51]\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA BEE, TV HOST: It`s not just about voting in November, it`s about doing everything in our power to protect and help vulnerable people access abortion across state lines. And we have to raise hell in our cities, in Washington, in every restaurant Justice Alito eats at for the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: It wasn`t Justice Alito but Justice Kavanaugh who was first to experience the kind of dinner table demonstrations Samantha Bee talked about on her show, "Full Frontal". The deer-loving associate justice who voted to take reproductive rights away from millions of Americans had, at least for one night, his right to a peaceful meal, dare say a right to privacy taken away.\par \par \uc2 Politico was first to report the incident at Morton Steakhouse in downtown D.C., quote, "While the court had no official comment on Kavanaugh`s behalf and a person familiar with the situation said he did not hear or see the protesters and ate a full meal but left before dessert. Morton`s was outraged about the incident."\par \par \uc2 Dessert courses aside, we know that a conservative super majority on the court isn`t ready to stop on abortion. But the decision on Roe came down, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  made it clear in a concurring opinion that the justices, quote, "should reconsider all of this court`s substantive due process precedent".\par \par \uc2 He then listed decisions that legalized the right to obtain contraception, the right to same-sex intimacy and the right to same sex marriage. And smart people are taking notice of that, including my next guest, \uc2 NYU\uc2  constitutional law professor Kenji Yoshino. His latest "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " op- ed is headlined, "Is the right to same sex marriage next?"\par \par \uc2 And yes, even comedians like Samantha Bee are taking notice of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEE: I can`t describe how painful it is to be here now in a place where the Supreme Court has the power to erase 50 years of constitutional law. Make no mistake, this is not where it ends. Conservatives will not rest until they have come for all of our rights. Everything we have fought for could be lost, unless we take it back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining me now is Kenji Yoshino, professor of constitutional law at NYU Law School and the director of NYU`s Center for Diversity, Inclusion and Belonging.\par \par \uc2 It has been a long time, professor. Great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 KENJI YOSHINO, CONSTITUTIONAL LAW PROFESSOR, NYU LAW SCHOOL: Great to see you as well, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: In your piece, you wrote, "Justice Samuel Alito`s majority opinion is at pains to say that nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion. Yet that statement rings false."\par \par \uc2 Kenji, I didn`t believe it when that language appeared in the leaked draft. Why does it especially ring false now?\par \par \uc2 YOSHINO: Well, I mean to start with the draft itself, that`s just not how adjudication works. If you announce a test and that test supports a (INAUDIBLE) to future cases before the court. The test that \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  articulated for the majority in Dobbs, was that unenumerated rights will not be protected unless they are deeply rooted in this nation`s history and tradition.\par \par \uc2 Now, last time I checked, the right to same sex marriage, the right to contraception, the right to same sex sexual intimacy -- none of these were deeply enshrined in the nation`s history and tradition.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s just a QED, it`s just a matter of logic, that underneath that task, these precedents are practically imperiled.\par \par \uc2 Now, whenever I raise this, Jonathan, I get accused of fearmongering in many quarters whenever I say, oh Obergefell is next, Lawrence is next, people say, oh, Justice Thomas is a crank -- he`s an outlier, he doesn`t really know what he is saying.\par \par \uc2 I would say a couple of things to that. One is he knows exactly what he is saying. He came to our country and what ways that he has done in previous cases, like Second Amendment cases, inviting litigation where he wants litigation to occur, right.\par \par \uc2 So to dismiss him as an outlier is simply false. And as you say, in the days since Dobbs, we have actually seen people take up that call to arms.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:54]\par \par \uc2 So whenever I am arguing against somebody who is usually not deeply embedded in the LGBT litigation community say, I am fearmongering. I don`t know whether or not to be sort of hopeful or pessimistic, right because all you need to do is wait and then the other shoe will drop.\par \par \uc2 So in fact, in the midst of having one of these arguments with a colleague \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the Texas attorney general, announced that he would be willing to have Lawrence reconsidered and would defend a (INAUDIBLE) statute if one were to be reenacted.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the 2003 Lawrence case, often seen as the Brown Versus Board of Education, the LGBT rights movement, that is now freshly on the chopping block and people are taking up that invitation that Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  calmly issued in his concurrence.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And a Lawrence case is Lawrence v Texas -- so, the symbolism there is high.\par \par \uc2 I want to pick up on something that you said. Also Professor Yoshino, when I read the draft, the Alito draft, I immediately wrote a column saying, Obergefell is in danger. Just the logic -- you take away Roe and the right to privacy, where does that leave -- where does that leave Obergefell?\par \par \uc2 But I want to pick up on something you just said that Justice Thomas is inviting litigation. But do you also buy the contention that he is also sending a signal to those conservative judges in lower courts, many of whom clerked either for him or justices on the Supreme Court that, hey, I am inviting litigation. And then when that litigation gets to you, you all know what to do.\par \par \uc2 YOSHINO: That`s exactly right. And so again, for those who would say he is a voice crying out in the wilderness, that may have been true about ten years ago. But people need to update their (INAUDIBLE) under the Trump presidency, the lower courts were filled with jurists who, as you say, were if not former clerks of Justice Thomas, at least fellow travelers in the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  who are going to be very, very attentive to the kind of bread crumbs that he drops in these concurrences.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: One more question for you, Professor. With Justices Alito, Barrett, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Thomas all seemingly to the right of him, has Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , in effect, lost control of the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 YOSHINO: Absolutely. I mean we saw that in the Dobbs opinion itself, where he was actually not a member of the court who joined the majority. He was actually waving his hands at the majority saying, you are doing too much, too fast. But you can actually strike down -- actually, rather, you can uphold the Mississippi statute, without actually doing anything to Roe. Because you can say that there is enough time for the woman to make a decision, or the pregnant person to make a decision in the 15 weeks that Mississippi law affords it.\par \par \uc2 So, he was saying, just you know, uphold this particular law. Don`t do anything to Roe or to Casey other than adjusting the viability line.\par \par \uc2 But he lost on that one. And so this kind of maximalist, very aggressive court, sort of run roughshod over him. And that five member majority that you just named is now upholding the reins. So, he is really the chief justice in name only.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Kenji Yoshino, thanks for joining us tonight. Great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 YOSHINO: Always great to see you, even on this sad occasion.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. Coming up -- an even better than expected jobs report was good news for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  today. And a totally expected billionaire tantrum was bad news for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:38:32]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: In tonight`s breaking news that is totally not surprising, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is officially trying to pull out of buying \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . In a new filing to the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 , Musk claimed \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was in a material breach of the $44 billion contract.\par \par \uc2 For the last three months, Elon Musk`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ambitions have given him endless media attention, with vague promises of free speech, lobbing poop emojis at current \uc2 Twitter\uc2  policies and vowing to right the wrong that was stopping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from tweeting.\par \par \uc2 But there were signs early on that he never actually wanted the deal to go through.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: But also isn`t it impossible to put a deal on hold or even back out of a deal.\par \par \uc2 FELIX SALMON, CHIEF FINANCIAL CORRESPONDENT, AXIOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: It is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is totally -- a deal on hold is meaningless, that means nothing. He has a bunch of lawyers and financiers running around trying to make sure that all of the financing gets done. So you can`t just stop that. In fact, I`m sure those people are running around even more right now than they were yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So can he pull out? Not really. There is no obvious way to him to do that although I`m sure he would love to be able to threaten to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: We`ll see what Felix has to say about Musk`s move today in a moment. But first we can`t let Musk`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tantrum make us lose focus on today`s blockbuster jobs report. 372,000 jobs were created last month, far outperforming expectations. The private sector has recovered every job lost in the pandemic and there still room to grow with two job openings for every person looking to be hired.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:56]\par \par \uc2 And even more good news? Increases in full time jobs and in wages. The unemployment rate was unchanged at 3.6 percent, the lowest in modern history. And gas prices are coming down.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Felix amin chief financial correspondent from Axios and host of the "Slate Money" podcast.\par \par \uc2 Felix, let`s start there. There`s been a lot of talk about a possible recession. Prices are still high, but can you have a recession with a jobs market this strong?\par \par \uc2 SALMON: No, absolutely not. There is this famous rule -- well, it`s not really a rule but they call it a rule -- called the (INAUDIBLE) which basically says that you can`t have a recession unless unemployment is half a point below its lows at least. We`re not even close to that.\par \par \uc2 As you said, we`re right at the lows on unemployment, we`re creating new jobs at a really quite torrid pace. Probably, weirdly faster than the government would actually like.\par \par \uc2 So this is not a recession. I see no chance that we`re in a recession right now. There could be one in the future. But as you say, gas prices are coming down and things are looking pretty rosy these days.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ok. So then Felix, that leads to the political question I have. Can people be convinced to feel better about the economy and is the consumer gloom a reflection of inflation or a response to a generalized uncertainty of a pandemic, Russian aggression in Europe, loss of freedom, rights here in the United States, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 SALMON: Yes. But The vibes are bad right? And so when people hear the word recession, they don`t look at the official (INAUDIBLE) definition of what a recession is. They just kind of think, are there bad vibes? And for the vibes question, they`re not really looking at do I have a job. They`re looking at things like how much does it cost to fill up my car? Or, you know, is the stock market down?\par \par \uc2 And they look at these things and they also look at a bunch of talking heads on the television saying we could be in a recession. And they go, especially if they`re a Republican, they say yes, this is terrible, we`re in a recession.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. We got to talk about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  because that`s what we talked about the last time we saw each other. Can he get out of this deal? What could it cost him?\par \par \uc2 SALMON: So he`s certainly trying. It`s going to wind up in Delaware Chancery Court which I know is your favorite place and it`s going to go up against a judge. And maybe there will be a settlement out of court. Maybe he will win, I find that very improbably.\par \par \uc2 Most you know -- it could that he will wind up settling for a few billion dollars. But it`s also possible that the court could just say no, you signed a deal to buy this company, you have to buy the company. It`s called specific performance. And they could force him to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  even if he doesn`t want to.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That would just be genius. All right. So then, what do you think his -- Musk`s motives were here? Because it seems like the effect of Elon Musk`s bid to buy y \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was to damage it.\par \par \uc2 SALMON: I mean he certainly did that. There`s not a lot of productive work going on inside \uc2 Twitter\uc2  right now. All they are doing is glued to the headlines about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . The product isn`t improving. And if the bid goes away then the share price falls to the floor. We know that. I mean it`s already way away from where Elon has promised to pay for.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a huge distraction for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And yes, the minute that he put in the bid, suddenly the stock market started falling and specifically \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stocks started falling. That was where all his wealth was.\par \par \uc2 He changed his mind. He got cold feet. He wants to pull out, but it`s not clear whether he has the legal ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I mean I just -- you`re just forced to buy a company for $44 billion dollars that you don`t even want anymore.\par \par \uc2 SALMON: There are not a lot of people rich enough where a court can force you to spend $44 billion dollars on a company. But Elon really is rich enough. He has that kind of money.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Oh my God, I`d love to see it.\par \par \uc2 Felix Salmon, thank you very much for coming back to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the power of Trumpism is on the ballot in November and some Republicans are clinging to the most extreme Trumpist views in their campaigns. Can Democrats use that to their advantage with voters? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:22]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: We`re just four months from election night in America and the power of Trumpism is on the ballot. We`ve seen some Republicans continue to cling to the lie that the election was stolen from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  while dismissing the investigation of the January 6th attack on the Capitol, supporting election subversion tactics, and working to strip a constitutional rights from Americans.\par \par \uc2 The generic congressional ballot shows a tie between Democrats and Republicans, but some Republican candidates are running on the craziest ideas of the Trump platform. And no surprise, they`re all endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who denied being a father to multiple children and allegedly hid the details from his staff is trailing Democratic candidate Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  by ten points.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 Nevada`s senate candidate \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  who supported Donald Trump`s attempts to overturn the election won the primary with more than 50 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 And State Senator \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who`s running for Pennsylvania governor was plotting with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  to try to figure out how to give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Pennsylvania`s electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 The question now is can Democrats use Republican support for Trumpism to their advantage at the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Eugene Daniels, White House correspondent for Politico and an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 All right, Eugene. So how are Democrats going to take advantage of this. What strategies are they looking to use to defeat Republicans running on Trumpism?\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. It depends on which Democrat you`re talking about. If you are looking at -- if you`re looking at for example the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  that`s working on getting some of these gubernatorial races in the D column, then they are meddling in some of the Republican primaries kind of boosting up some of these Republicans with the ideas that, you know, you talked about quite a bit, kind of anti-democratic ideas. That`s one way.\par \par \uc2 They are also as you see the Supreme Court changing a lot of American life and actually going against what most of Americans in this country wants whether that`s on gun safety, whether that is on abortion where polls are on the other end of the Supreme Court. You have them highlighting that.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s all about trying to, for Democrats trying to figure out how to make it clearer that in their eyes Republicans are more extreme than before. President Biden in fact say you know, this is not your dad`s Republican Party, and tying them \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as much as they can.\par \par \uc2 Now, last year in Virginia, it didn`t really work because you had Glenn Youngkin who was running and ended up winning the gubernatorial race there. Not really wanting to be -- ask Donald Trump`s help -- he didn`t \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to come support him, but these other folks that are running up and down the ballot do want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to come to their side.\par \par \uc2 So it is a little bit different giving Democrats what they feel is an advantage on tying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to these other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Eugene, let`s talk about Roe as a motivator for Democrats in 538 Nate Silver writes that the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v Wade could give Democrats momentum going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts. Is that even possible? Is it enough of the motivator do you think?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Is it enough, that`s hard to say, but it is a motivator, right. If Democrats play their cards right, and you have to admit that sometimes on messaging, they have proven this last year and a half not to really have it together how most of their voters wouldn`t expect and want them to. And even some of the Democratic strategist that I`m often talking to.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that Democrats rested on their laurels on and they know that. Vice President Harris said that in the interview that took place today that Democrats should have codified Roe into law.\par \par \uc2 So now all they can do is tell voters that we are going to continue fighting at the federal level to codify Roe but we need your help, you have to give us enough senators in Congress to do so.\par \par \uc2 And to that is something that we are seeing voters get excited I guess is probably the wrong world, but it`s motivating them for November.\par \par \uc2 Is that enough to kind of counteract all the history that we know that goes in the midterms. All of the wind that`s at the back of Republicans?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Right now, it`s hard to know, but it definitely is giving Democrats a reason to vote and making them take the court much more seriously than they have in the past.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. And part of the message -- part of the president`s message is give me two more seats, just give me two more seats for that majority and we could do all the stuff you want.\par \par \uc2 One more question Gene because we got like 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announces that he is running in 2024, what impact would that have on the midterms? Who stands to benefit? Democrats or Republicans?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I think it makes it really difficult for Republicans who are trying to run and run their own race and not have to answer for everything that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says. It makes their live very difficult, if he runs.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are going to do everything they can to tie folks really tightly to him so if he does announce that he`s going to run for president he`s not only the leader of the parties. He is the presumptive nominee for the Republican nomination in 2024.\par \par \uc2 That makes Democrats` job a little bit easier, and I will say Republicans behind the scenes have been saying for a long time, they did not want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  mucking about and meddling in the primary because of some of the folks he talked about Mastriano possibly and getting the nominations and making their jobs much harder in November.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:50]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I keep saying this, I don`t think he`s going to run, but if he makes an announcement before the midterms, oh baby.\par \par \uc2 Eugene Daniels --\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Unprecedented times.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Unprecedented. Thanks. Eugene Daniels thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 I will see you this weekend on "THE SUNDAY SHOW` starting at 10 a.m. Eastern here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts exactly now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220709ei7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for June 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Tom Costello, Tim Miller, Ali Vitali, Pete  Williams, Carol Lam, Ken Dilanian, Vaughn Hillyard \par \uc2 8,031 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon, you`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 In just one hour, Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is expected to announce new interest rate hikes designed to cool down inflation. But it`s a huge economic gamble. Higher interest rates mean credit cards and mortgages become more expensive and that, risks pushing the U.S. into a recession. But the hope is to bring down prices for everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRENDA HEKIMIAN, FOOD BANK RECEIPT: Everything is just going through the roof and it`s like there`s no middle class, there`s either extreme rich or extreme poor, and the rest of us just kind of fall through the cracks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s exactly those kinds of voters that Democrats and the White House are trying to reach. We heard the president just yesterday insisting that Americans should trust that he has a plan to help working families. But those families are feeling frustrated and overwhelmed. And in fact, we saw a little of President Biden`s own frustration in a letter today where he lashed out at oil companies for making huge profits while gas prices hit record levels. For him, his administration, and the entire Democratic Party, this economic problem has become an escalating political problem. Consider the stakes with more than two dozen states holding primaries over the next three months. Voters are making decisions based on what they`re feeling now, not what might happen down the line.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you like $8 a gallon gas and you like continued rising costs on every single type of goods that we, the everyday people depend on every day, then, vote Democrat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The hardworking people of our country are sidelined. And I`m very upset about this. So I think it`s more important than ever before to have a voice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now, there`s one more important note. The president has consistently argued he`s the only one with a real economic plan at all. But the Wall Street Journal reports that Republicans are starting to fill in details of their own economic proposals, offering Americans something else to consider as the midterms approach.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a fantastic group to talk this over, NBC`s Tom Costello joins me from DC, Ben White is Politico`s Chief Economic Correspondent, Tim Miller an MSNBC Political Analyst and writer at large for The Bulwark, and Farnoosh Torabi, editor-at-large at CNET money and a financial expert with nerdwallet.com, so good to have all of you.\par \par \uc2 Tom, look, the Fed is expected to raise rates by three-quarters of a point, which would be the biggest hike in something like 28 years.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What are they thinking that`s going to accomplish? And how fast could an average American see an impact?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And in fact, we`re already seeing it because mortgage rates actually lead interest rates, lead the Fed. So we`ve already seen mortgage rates go up by almost 3 percent this year alone. And so we`re already paying more. If you took out a $400,000 mortgage in January, compared to taking out one today, you`re going to pay more today than you did on January 1, even before the Fed comes in with this three-quarter point rate hike.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the goal here is as said, to call that to tame the inflation here. You do that by cooling the economy. And by doing that, the hope is that you raise the cost of borrowing, therefore, people are not going to go out and buy a new house or they`re not going to buy a new car. And companies may not borrow money to expand. In fact, if they start contracting and bringing their workforce down, that could lead to layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve got a situation here, that`s very unusual. We got 50-year low unemployment in this country. And right now, you`ve got two jobs for every worker out there. There`s a lot of open jobs so if the -- if companies start to contract and they start laying off employees, in theory, right now, there are more jobs than employees. The question is how long will that last? And if you really raise rates fast, like the Fed is talking about, then we may go towards a recession. And this is a very, very delicate balancing act that the Fed is on right now to raise rates fast because inflation is clearly out of control right now at 40-year highs, but not slam on the brakes and put us into a ditch.\par \par \uc2 They admit they are behind on this. They should have been acting back in the fall to raise rates and tame down inflation. They didn`t get it right. They`ve been raising rates so far this year. And this will not be the only rate hike. We should make the point. They`re going to raise rates again in July, another three-quarter of a point we expect another, half-point in September probably, and then again in December. So they`ve got a lot of work to do to get rates all the way up to what they -- they`re probably going towards a total of 4 percent on where they would like to see rates.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom Costello, always great to have you on the program. Thank you. So, Ben, I was looking at this headline in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that says markets and households lose faith that the Fed can handle inflation. They haven`t always gotten it right. In fact, they`ve been pretty wrong a couple of times, and maybe multiple times. How sure are the economists you`re talking to, that this is the right move this time?\par \par \uc2 BEN WHITE, CHIEF ECONOMIC CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: Well, I think Tom laid out the case pretty well that they have to move and they have to move significantly with inflation staying over 8 percent, it just can`t stay there, Americans can`t keep up with that kind of cost. 75 basis points, which are expected to do is a big increase in mortgage rates, and all the rest of it will go up. But there is not a lot of confidence.\par \par \uc2 And I want to point to an important number of retail sales this morning that came out and actually went down 0.3 percent, which was much worse than expectations. That tells us that the prices are starting to pinch the consumer, and a lot of motor vehicle sales went down. And that`s you know, what underpins most of this economy. 70 percent is consumer spending. So if we start to see the consumer feel a pinch, they`ve drawn down a lot of their COVID-era savings, that`s going to be a problem for the economy at the same time that they`re raising rates.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right, they`ve gotten it wrong more than right, the Fed, generally speaking historically, has preferred to err on the side of maybe triggering a mild recession rather than letting inflation run out of control. I think it`s eight of the last nine times we`ve had at least a mild recession in this kind of rate hiking cycle. So it`s going to be hard to avoid, and it is a very delicate situation for them. And they are, as Tom said, late to the party here in raising rates.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I mean, frankly, just the word recession scares a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Farnoosh, we heard from that woman at the top of the hour, who said, it feels like the middle class is going away, there`s just the rich people who can weather this. That`s what you get when you talk to people like Jaime Martinez. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE MARTINEZ, SINGLE MOTHER: Rent is going to just continue increasing. And who knows when it`s going to be a point where I`m like, OK, I can`t afford this anymore. Where am I supposed to live?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Where am I supposed to live? Rents have gone crazy in a lot of parts of this country. And while people are waiting to see if the Fed can bring down inflation, gas prices are up, credit card rates are going up, mortgage rates going up, what do you tell people in that situation?\par \par \uc2 FARNOOSH TORABI, EDITOR AT LARGE, CNET: It is a devastating time for Americans, no doubt, no argument there. You know, it`s hard to talk about silver linings or the -- you know, any sort of, you know, a light at the end of the tunnel when it just seems like inflation is increasing every single month. What I`m telling everybody is that it`s important to remember history, it`s important to have all of this through a contextual lens, which is that recessions are cyclical. You can`t have growth without contraction. And right now, what we`re experiencing is sort of the aftermath of, you know, many -- a couple of years of stimulus and good wages and good employment. And I think we`re in a period now where we have to sort of pay the price, to pay the price of the stimulus, pay the price of the supply disruptions that the pandemic created.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think we can rate hike our way out of this inflation crisis. I think that the president has an opportunity here, which I don`t think he`s really taken advantage of, to think deeply about communicating -- how we`re going to communicate to the American people about what sort of policies we`re going to create, what sort of tax reform we`re going to create. Because what we don`t just have is a consumer demand issue. We have a serious supply deficit, which was, as we know, caused by primarily COVID, and now with the Russia war, that`s putting pressure on gas prices. So how are we going to address that side of the equation? Rate hikes are important and historically, they can help but I don`t think we`re going to see them help us get out of this period swiftly. But you know I have my fingers crossed.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Tim, she points out really how high the political stakes are, right. And one of the things that President Biden has said just in the last couple of days is, here`s all the things I`ve done, here`s what I`m going to do. The Republicans don`t have a plan. Now we get this report from The Wall Street Journal that the Republicans are indeed developing this plan. And I guess a key question, as we look toward the midterms, and before that, even some of these primaries, is it enough to say I have an alternative to what`s bothering you right now? The details for most people who are not economists, they`re not going to do the deep dive. Is that a fair assessment?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It is. But look, I think that Biden needs to go further than this and creating a choice against the Republicans. I -- look they tried the Build Back better package, which was not very well sold. And then that went under last Christmas. And so they still have, you know, what`s called reconciliation, you know, where they don`t need a filibuster. They only need the 50 votes they have in the Senate, you know, so they can come up with a plan with \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and say, hey, here`s what we want to do to address inflation.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The Republicans are blocking everything. All they care about is re- litigating 2020 and you know, the taking gay books out of kids` schools like that`s what they`re talking about. They`re not trying to solve your problems. We have a plan here. Biden hasn`t really done that. And the Democrats haven`t really demonstrated their proactive plan, which I think, makes the argument that Republicans don`t have a plan weaker. So they need to draw that contrast much starker -- much more starkly between now and November.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Drawing that contrast, of course, Ben, means there has to be coherent and fruitful messaging, right?\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Right, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: One of the things that we`re seeing from this administration is they`re bringing in former Atlanta Mayor \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , who by a lot of accounts did very well with COVID in her city, was a great messenger to her constituents, and got a lot of national attention for that. Is this a message problem? Is it a messenger problem? Can one happen without the other?\par \par \uc2 It`s all of those problems, Tim laid out pretty well. The messaging failures of this White House, they have gone from, you know, it`s oil -- big oil`s problem, it`s Putin his problem on inflation, it`s the Feds job to do it not really ours, so they haven`t settled on the message. But I`ve talked to a lot of people inside the White House economic team over the last couple of weeks and they`re very frustrated by the fact that there isn`t that much they do. They`re trying to pull the levers, they can. Tim`s right, they need to put something in reconciliation, that`s more of a clear message.\par \par \uc2 But Lance Bottoms is a very important role that she`s filling, not only to talk about the economic message and to reach out to community groups, but that`s the office that is the key liaison to the business community. And it`s been vacant since \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  left or there`s an acting, but they need somebody in that role who can get the feel of CEOs and what they`re seeing and bring them into the White House to talk to Biden. They haven`t really had that in the way that they need to have to have that communication. So she`s an important player there. That`s an important job.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is there is no message you can deliver that`s going to make people feel better if inflation stays above 8 percent, gas prices stay above five bucks, rents are where they are, and people are feeling as squeezed as they are. The White House knows the only message that works is one where Biden could say, hey, look, inflation is coming down on my policies, on my watch. Stick with the Democrats in the fall. If that doesn`t happen, they`re braced for some serious losses.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And speaking of businesses, Farnoosh, the letter the president sent to oil companies blaming them for making record profits while prices are high, demanding that they increase supply, here`s what part of what he wrote. He said refinery profit margins well above normal being passed directly on to American families are not acceptable. And then he demanded they increase the supply of gas and oil but his own Energy Department says that refinery capacity will hit 96 percent this summer. So realistically, what`s the answer here? How much relief could any of this provide for these high gas prices?\par \par \uc2 TORABI: That`s a good question. I don`t know. I mean, in the absence of real cracking down, I don`t know what`s going to change. And it`s beyond even just gas prices. Consumers are getting hit with additional fees on top of increased prices at a number of retailers. I just did a story for CNET on how retailers are adding fees to receipts. Sometimes they`re hidden because you`re using your credit card because now they`re saying we want to pay -- we want to pay our employees` health insurance, which is important, but then they`re passing on that cost to us. And so we are now entering a place where we`re not just dealing with inflation, but we`re dealing with some merchants who are price gouging or dealing with additional fees.\par \par \uc2 The thing I would tell consumers is cash is king right now, cash is queen, cash is power, so if you can negotiate even at the gas pump and offer cash instead of credit, using a gas app to find the cheapest gas -- I mean, literally gas stations across the street are having 30 to 50 cent per gallon differences. What`s that about? So really, just doing a little bit of research can go a very long way in saving on gas, which is a huge part of everyone`s budget.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Farnoosh and Ben, thank you both very much. Tim, you`re going to stick around for us. And coming up, just how much sway does former President Trump have with Republican voters? The new clues we`re getting from primary election results and what it could all mean for the next presidential election. Plus, take a look at this surveillance video. It was just released by the January 6 committee. Why they say it raises big questions about a Republican Congressman`s actions the day before the attack on the Capitol? And in just the past few hours, the new charges from the feds against the suspect in the mass shooting in Buffalo, the details about a note they found in a search of his home. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New questions today from the January 6 committee to Georgia Republican Congressman \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2  about a tour he gave on January 5, the day before the insurrection. In a letter to Loudermilk, the committee says members of that tour spend hours in the Capitol despite the fact that it was actually close to the public due to the pandemic. And the letter says they photographed and recorded areas of the complex not typically of interest to tourists, including hallways, staircases, security checkpoints. They also released surveillance video of the tour.\par \par \uc2 Now, take a look. The man you`re looking at was, according to the committee, part of the crowd that protested outside the capitol on January 6. Now, we should make it clear that the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  chief says he`s reviewed the surveillance footage and says none of the activities he saw were suspicious. And Loudermilk provided a statement to NBC News in just the past hour saying in part that the committee is pushing a false narrative that has already been put to bed.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali, NBC Justice Correspondent Pete Williams, and Carol Lam, a former federal prosecutor, and MSNBC Legal Analyst.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ali, if the chief of the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  says the tour was innocent, what leads the committee to think that maybe it wasn`t?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: They have video like the one that you showed that raises questions. I think that what they`re putting together here and the video that they released, again, asking for Loudermilk`s cooperation is they`re trying to draw the thread between some of the people that you`re seeing on your screen there who are taking videos and pictures in places that we don`t typically see tourists take video or pictures just because it`s security checkpoints, it`s stairwells, it`s not very pretty parts of the Capitol that we walk around on the regular.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, juxtaposing that with the fact that some of these people were actually at the march on the Capitol one day later on January 6, certainly, that`s the question that they`re drawing here. And it`s why they want to talk to Loudermilk himself. It`s the second time that they`ve asked him to come in, this, of course, now they`re releasing the imagery behind why they want to speak to him. But it`s one of those tangential threads that we`ve seen the committee sort of pulling on, as it`s asked sitting Republican lawmakers to come before them.\par \par \uc2 I imagine this is not going to be a central focus of their committee hearing, especially not the one tomorrow. That`s going to be all about VP \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the pressure campaign that was placed on him. But at the same time, this is clearly one of those threads, especially something that was talked about in the aftermath of January 6. A lot of confusion and concern around it because of course, the Capitol Complex at the height of the COVID pandemic at that point was closed to tourists. So there were definitely some questions about this and clearly, the committee is not done asking them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Carol, obviously, this is part of the committee`s attempts to show this was a coordinated planned attack. But it also seems to be at least an attempt to suggest that maybe some Republicans somehow facilitated this. Is that how you read it?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, it`s hard to say right now because there hasn`t been any evidence that Congressman Loudermilk actually knew the purpose that for which these folks wanted the tour of the Capitol grounds. But what I do want to point out is how frustrating this is for the committee to have to drag witnesses, especially a congressman, you know, towards the truth, kicking and screaming. He has denied in the past that he gave any tour of the Capitol grounds.\par \par \uc2 He has denied in the past that anybody was wearing red caps during that time, and only when confronted with the video evidence does he concede that such a tour was given. You know, really, if the committee has to do this with all of its witnesses, it takes a long time, and one would think that a congressman could simply own up to the fact that he forgot or was mistaken or such, that would help the committee do its work as it is meant to do.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And someone who`s refused to respond to a subpoena from the committee is former Trump White House adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , and he was back in court today. I want to play just a little of what he had to say before he went inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , INDICTED ON CONTEMPT CHARGES: I want to appreciate the J-six committee canceling today. You know why? They kept their ratings stink and they can`t compete with the trial of Bannon, OK? That`s why they canceled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Talking about the ratings. Bring us up to speed, Pete Williams, on what Bannon was actually doing in court today.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Will his lawyers were asking the judge to dismiss the contempt of Congress indictment against him and the judge has just said, no, I`m not going to do that. So they had several theories. They said that the committee was not properly constituted. Now, this is a claim that many people who are fighting 