ing to need it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sounds like Herschmann and others like him, Carol could be a big part of his hearing.\par \par \uc2 LAM: Yes, they will be a big part of this hearing because what we`ve seen so far is that virtually everybody in the president`s inner circle, including his own hand-picked Attorney General and his own attorneys surrounding him, were telling him it`s over. And the few exceptions that were there, people were telling them. Stop it. It`s over. And that`s very poignant in a way, you know, that this is -- this is -- this is the president`s own group of people who he handpicked saying that what was more important to them was the orderly transition of power with a new president being allowed to come in to play.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that the president and a few people who were on the outer ring -- ranges of society are saying no, hang on, you can still do it, it`s a very dangerous thing for this country and it resulted more in large part in January 6 events.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol Lam, Pete Williams, Ali Vitali, thanks to all of you. And now we have new assessments of the impact of dangerous floods and mudslides on Yellowstone National Park. What experts are saying about how long the park might be shut down? Plus, 165,000 people without running water in the middle of a Texas heatwave, when people there could see some relief as officials warn of an imminent threat to people`s safety? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:40]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some breaking news. The Justice Department filing multiple federal hate crime charges against the suspect in last month`s mass shooting at a grocery store in Buffalo, where 10 people died.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ken Dilanian has been following this for us.\par \par \uc2 Ken, what can you tell us about these new charges? And I understand there was a note the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it found in his home?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Chris. This 26-count federal indictment comes on top of the state level domestic terrorism and murder charges already filed against Payton Gendron. And it carries the possibility, unlike the state charges, of the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 The federal indictment notes that the May 14th mass shooting which Gendron livestreamed on the internet resulted in the deaths of 10 black people, as well as injuries to one black person, and two white people. The indictment says Gendron`s motive was grounded in a racial conspiracy theory, that he wanted to prevent black people from replacing white people and eliminating the white race, and he wanted to inspire others to commit similar attacks.\par \par \uc2 The indictment says his writings say he choose the Tops Supermarket because its zip code has the highest percentage of black people close to where he lived and that he knew that a large percentage of the store`s customers would be black.\par \par \uc2 And during the attack, Chris, the indictment says Gendron appeared to try to avoid shooting white people. In fact when a black security guard tries to fire Gendron, the indictment says the suspect then shot and killed that security guard, then turned and aim his rifle at a wounded white male store employee but rather than shooting him, Gendron said sorry to the employee. The indictment says the AR-15 style rifle he used in the attack had racial slurs written on it as well as the phrase "here`s your reparations and the great replacement."\par \par \uc2 Now Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  who was in Buffalo to meet with some of the families of the victims in this case explained why the federal government was bringing these charges even though the suspect faces life in prison on the state charges. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: No one in this country should have to live in fear that they will go to work or shop at a grocery store and will be attacked by someone who hates them because of the color of their skin. Someone who commits that act because he subscribes to the vile theory that only people like him belong in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Now, Chris, as you mentioned, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said that they seized at his house. A note written to his family in which he apologizes to his family for the attack but says he had to carry it out to protect the white race.\par \par \uc2 And Chris, a word about the federal death penalty. Executions resumed in 2020 under Attorney General Bill Barr after a 17-year hiatus. But they were paused again by Garland for a review of policies and procedures. As I mentioned New York does not have the death penalty so one big question now is whether the federal or the state case will go to trial first. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ken Dilanian, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Out West we`re now being told that Yellowstone National Park has been shut down indefinitely. Roads and bridges are closed after record flooding and more rain is in the forecast. Flood waters have started to recede but crews are taking stock of the damage in Wyoming and Montana, and officials say it is extensive. Area residents anguished by the devastation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This summer was the first summer not as intense with the COVID, no fires. We were getting great rain. This was going to be our season. And our creek turned into a river.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This disaster comes at the start of the peak summer season, forcing countless people to have to cancel vacations, including plans to stay overnight at Yellowstone`s nine lodges, 12 camp grounds, hundreds of camp sites. Last July alone the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  says more than a million people visited the park, the first time that`s happened in a single month.\par \par \uc2 And in Texas, a problem isn`t a lot of water. It`s a lack of it. 165,000 people in Odessa have been without water since Monday. A major water main break forced the city to shut down its entire water system. And the timing couldn`t be worse. Temperatures soaring higher than 100 degrees. People are heading to grocery stores and water distribution sites for bottled water. No word yet on what caused the break or when people will get their water back.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Now to what could be a big step toward getting the youngest children vaccinated against COVID-19. Right now, an \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory committee is discussing whether to recommend allowing the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and \uc2 Moderna\uc2  vaccines for kids under 5. Last weekend \uc2 FDA\uc2  scientists said both vaccines appear to be safe and effective in young children. If this panel gives the thumbs-up, it goes to a CDC advisory panel and the CDC director. Then vaccinations could start as soon as this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Halfway through the primary season, there is one big takeaway, Trump-backed Republicans are winning more often than not. So could that actually help Democrats in the midterms? And what does it mean for Trump`s own decision on 2024?\par \par \uc2 You are watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, the midterm stage is increasingly being set as Democrats against Trump-backed Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , who ran Trump`s campaign in that state, won the primary to take on incumbent Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  for a Senate seat, a top Republican target. And the man who says he would not have certified the 2020 election results, Jim Marchant is the Republican nominee for secretary of state. That of course is the job that oversees elections.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, GOP incumbent Nancy Mace won her primary against a Trump-backed challenger, although later in the campaign Mace did try heal her split from Trump. And Tom Rice, one of 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach Trump, lost.\par \par \uc2 All of this tells us race by race just how impactful Trump`s stamp of approval remains. Take a look at this score card from Axios. For the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , six wins, zero losses for Trump. For the House, 12 wins, three losses, and for statewide offices, eight wins, six losses.\par \par \uc2 And we`re just halfway through a competitive primary season. So there are more endorsed candidates out there.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is live in South Carolina. Tim Miller from the "Bulwark" is back with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Vaughn, what can Republicans or what are they taking away from the playbook in South Carolina?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, look, Tom Rice was the first Republican who voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who willingly stood up and put his name back on a primary ballot and tried to beat back a Trump- backed challenger here. And ultimately he got clobbered. He only pulled in about 25 percent of the vote. And I think what is notable about Tom Rice is the lack of Republican support that he had here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Former speaking \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  came to campaign on his behalf. But it`s not like you saw a battalion, a storm here into South Carolina to defend the -- you know, he calls himself a Christian conservative who has served in Congress for more than a decade now. There was no like Kevin McCarthy or \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  were not here. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was not here.\par \par \uc2 And I asked him specifically on Monday how he felt about essentially being the lone fighter here to fend this seat for himself. And he told me, quote, "They have the make their own choices. I am not going to judge. I know I made the right decision. I know I made the decision to preserve our freedom, our prosperity by defending the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 Now I said that Tom Rice was the first of those impeachment Republicans to put his name on the ballot. There are four others that we are looking at in the next two months. You`ve got Jamie Herrera Butler and \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2  in Washington state. You`ve got Peter Myer in Michigan, and then ultimately in the middle of August, it`s \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  putting her name on the ballot. The one other race here of course was Nancy Mace. And she ultimately did win this primary.\par \par \uc2 She didn`t vote to impeach him, though. She called for his censuring here and she tried to make peace with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  these last few months. I wanted, though, to pose the question to her, what would her message be to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now because he did try to oust her but unsuccessfully? Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP) REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): My message to him as it is to anybody else on either side of the aisle. I am more willing to work with anyone who is willing to work with me. Full stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: So now the question here is, ultimately we`re going to find out a lot more in these next two months. Can Republican members of Congress continue to stand up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and have success in their primary challenges against those Trump-endorsed candidates? Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Tim Miller, do you want to try to answer that question? And are you hearing or seeing anything, say, from the January 6th Committee, that might lead you to believe that the second half of the primary season might be different than the first?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, FORMER COMMS DIRECTOR FOR JEB BUSH 2016: I really don`t think so, Chris. You know, look, I think there is another number that Vaugn didn`t hit on there when he was going over the impeachers, which is the number that have already left of their own accord, which is four of them. Right? So four of them didn`t even put themselves up for re-election. One has now lost. So, you know, the best-case scenario honestly is there will only be four members of Congress who were part of the 10 courageous Republicans who voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if you have any doubt that this is what this is all about, Tom Rice is not like a moderate Republican like I was. Like a \uc2 Jon Huntsman\uc2  moderate, you know, pro-climate change Republican. He`s from the Ted Cruz, \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , Christian conservative hard right wing of the party. You know, his only -- the only thing that separated him from a conservative Republican voter was his vote on impeachment. And he only got 24 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I don`t think that Trump is going to win every single race that he endorses people in. Particularly if the two candidates are both pro- Trump candidates. You know, sometimes local issues are going to overwhelm. But any time somebody steps out and does a courageous thing they are going to go down in these primaries. And Brad Raffensperger`s win in Georgia is really the only example we`ve seen to the contrary of someone who`s actually challenged Trump and ran as a non-Trump candidate and winning.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 Even in the other primaries where Trump`s endorsed candidate doesn`t win, they`re running against someone else who`s running as a Trumper. And I kind of see that as a distinction without a difference.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, the other thing we know, Tim, is that the general electorate, the people we`re going to see going out to vote in November, different than people who come up for the primary. Much smaller generally part of a base. So I guess there`s a broader question as well, could primary wins for Trump-backed candidates actually provide an opening for Democrats? And if so, where do you think?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, maybe. Look, it`s going to depend on the individual candidates. We saw in Virginia in 2021 you can`t run against Trump in a midterm when he`s not on the ballot. But if the candidates themselves are extreme, I absolutely think it does. Look at the Pennsylvania governor`s case. \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  is a far-right insurrectionist, he was there January 6th. I think Pennsylvania should be the type of state in a good year for Republicans where Republicans would win the governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 I think they could lose that race to Josh Shapiro who is running a good race as a center-left Democrat. That`s one example. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia is another example. That should be a state Republican`s win in a year like this, but he is completely unqualified. News broke yesterday that he has a secret child, he can`t answer basic questions, he was also an insurrectionist. So yes, I mean, I think that in certain races Democrats can pick it up.\par \par \uc2 I think the more concerning thing for me, though, is some of these secretary of state races, you mentioned Nevada, in a wave Republican year some Republicans are going to get swept in who are extreme that win these lower races like secretary of state, you know, because voters just don`t split their ballot that often. That`s something that I am looking at and really, really concerned about.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tim and Vaughn, thanks to both of you. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And meantime we`ve got some critical meetings today that will influence what happens next at a pivotal moment for Ukraine. What President Zelenskyy says he needs and what the U.S. just announced it will actually provide.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Mike Quigley\uc2  from the Intelligence Committee joins us to discuss what the U.S. should do now.\par \par \uc2 You are watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In just the last hour, the White House told us that President Biden talked with Ukraine`s president by phone this morning and told him the U.S. would provide another billion dollars in security assistance. Moments ago after a meeting with NATO Defense ministers in Brussels, Defense Secretary Lloyd Asustin announced the contents of this latest package. It includes 18 Howitzers, 36,000 rounds of ammunition, 18 tactical vehicles and additional ammunition for High Mobility Artillery Rocket Systems.\par \par \uc2 But an adviser to Ukraine`s president said before that announcement that his country needs even more than that. A thousand Howitzers, 500 tanks, a thousand drones, other heavy weapons. The mayor fight in Ukraine right now is in Severodonetsk where Ukraine today ignored a Russian ultimatum to surrender.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Illinois Congressman \uc2 Mike Quigley\uc2  who is a member of the House Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Good of you to join us, Congressman. Is what we`re just learning about for this new package for Ukraine enough? It is less than they wanted.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE QUIGLEY\uc2  (D-IL): Look, the total aid package that we`re committed to is, you know, somewhere in the nature of $23 billion and $24 billion. But there`s enough dollars dedicated to this. And it comes in tranches. The president announced another billion today. When I looked at the list that you showed, it was all -- you know, 155 caliber. That`s U.S. and NATO Howitzers. It`s the former Soviet era.\par \par \uc2 So what Ukraine is trying to do is transition from Soviet era weaponry to a modern-era Western weaponry. That`s hard to do in the middle of the war. So added to the fact that they are transitioning the war and firing far more shells, we understand if they want and need more.\par \par \uc2 I was just in Poland, you know, looking at the logistics of this conflict and I do believe that the West is doing what it can as quickly as possible to get there, but you have to add the logistical issues and how tranches move to get the weaponry and the ammunition they need. It`s a tough task. I think we`ll get it done.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Another thing that \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , the Defense secretary, said today was that he believes that the fight that`s going on now in Severodonetsk is pivotal. What is your understanding of the status of the fight right now on the ground, especially having been there in the regions so closely from now. And we`ve been hearing the frontlines in recent days have been some of the bloodiest and deadliest in the past three months since the war began. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 QUIGLEY: Look, I was just on a Zoom call with General Hertling in the Ukrainian Caucus. He described it as a slug fest. And that`s what it is. You know, I`ve heard it called a stalemate but it`s not. There`s horrible fighting taking place right now. And this is a logistics struggle right now and we start to see the Russians attacking the supply line. So our task is to get it to that border. Unfortunately after that it`s up to the Ukrainians to get it in the farther east.\par \par \uc2 The movement east also makes this fight much easier for the Russians from a logistical point of view. The terrain is more to their liking and their kind of tactics. So I do believe these efforts will work. I appreciate that Secretary Austin is going to Brussels. A vital part of all of this is the president did a great job building this coalition. Almost as difficult and perhaps more difficult I guess at times is maintaining it, maintaining the interest and keeping the resolve of our Western allies. We hear \uc2 NATO\uc2  developing a comprehensive package to bring a more modern equipment there and given the ammunition, that needs to happen very quickly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve only got a minute left, but President Zelenskyy also today in an address asked the E.U. to start his country on the road to membership. He warned that Russia`s territorial ambitions will not stop in Ukraine but stretch from, as he put it, Warsaw to Sophia.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on the importance of Ukraine being granted membership in the E.U.?\par \par \uc2 QUIGLEY: Look, they`ve earned it. When I think of why we formed the E.U. to build those coalitions and how important economic relations are, it`s actually vital that we do this. I also think they`ve earned for all the reasons we`ve formed \uc2 NATO\uc2  for \uc2 NATO\uc2  to come to their complete assistance. They`re fighting the fight for the reasons \uc2 NATO\uc2  was formed and the E.U. component will only add to their strength.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressman Quigley, it`s so good to see you. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 QUIGLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. And don`t forget to tune in to MSNBC`s special coverage when the January 6th hearings pick back up tomorrow beginning at noon Eastern on MSNBC and Peacock. And the Federal Reserve`s interest rate decision set to come in just minutes from now. I`ll see you tomorrow morning at 11:00. Katy Tur picks up our coverage after this quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be you. I`m Katy Tur. As we come on the air, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may be about to announce what could be the biggest interest rate hike in almost 30 years. This is the third of seven planned interest rate hikes for 2022. It is a pivotal moment for the U.S. economy. Right now the Fed is trying to create a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220616ei6f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Chris Jansing Reports for June 13, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ali Vitali, Paul Butler, Jonathan Lemire, Barbara  McQuade, Allie Raffa, Gadi Schwartz, Ali Arouzi \par \uc2 7,750 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [14:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: But what happens when these ideas get sent to the full Senate and when the \uc2 NRA\uc2  starts applying pressure?\par \par \uc2 Plus gas prices now climbing over $5 a gallon, up 60 cents in just a month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So I don`t make much money. We definitely don`t go out that much. Yes, it sucks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To change the way that I live my life and I have to change the things that I buy and the things that I spend money on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And while the cost of everything keeps going up, markets are going down dramatically again. We`ve got our expert coming in on what to expect next.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to start with the January 6th Committee hearing. New details coming out today from Republicans about how then President Trump repeatedly endorsed the big lie, raising millions for a so-called election defense fund that never existed, and how he repeatedly ignored his own top officials, even as they investigated one wild fraud allegation after another, and found nothing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD DONOGHUE\uc2 , FORMER ACTING DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: He said dead people are voting. Indians are getting paid to vote. He meant people on Native American Reservations. He said there`s lots of fraud going on here.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN, FORMER ACTING UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: There were instances where the president would say people are telling me this or I heard this or I saw on television, you know, this impropriety in Atlanta or Pennsylvania or something, and we were in a position to say our people have already looked at that, and we know you`re getting bad information, that`s not correct.\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: I told him that the stuff that his people were shoveling out to the public were bull (EXPLETIVE DELETED). I mean, that the claims of fraud were (EXPLETIVE DELETED).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, Kyle Cheney is "Politico`s" legal affairs reporter, Jonathan Lemire is "Politico`s" White House bureau chief, Paul Butler is a former prosecutor and professor at Georgetown School of Law, Barbara McQuade is a former U.S. attorney and law professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Great to have this all-star panel.\par \par \uc2 Ali, I think there`s a pretty clear through line here all about the fact that the president was told over and over that the allegations of fraud didn`t hold up and he went out and spread them anyway.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and that was certainly the focus of this hearing, hammered home not just by the committee members themselves but by members of the former president`s own inner circle, both on the campaign and within the White House and other parts of his government.\par \par \uc2 Of course using that testimony by people like former Attorney General \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  as well as people inside the Trump campaign, including Bill Stepien, who was supposed to be here in person but of course couldn`t come because his wife went into labor and thus he could not appear here today. The committee was able to slice into their report his own testimony. They did it on the fly this morning in preparation thinking he was coming.\par \par \uc2 But of course it underscores the idea that everyone who`s talked to the committee can effectively still be a witness, even if they`re not in the room. But of course the entire premise was to make sure that the audience was aware that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at best ignored the reality that he was being told by everyone in his inner orbit as or as former Attorney General \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  said that he was detached from reality because he believed conspiracies, including things like Dominion voting machines changing votes and suitcases of ballots being wheeled in and out in ways that were of course debunked by his own Department of Justice and within his own campaign.\par \par \uc2 This was them speaking to the mindset of the former president trying to establish that and it`s why I asked Congressman Adam Schiff after the hearing what that means for accountability and if the Department of Justice should investigate the former president. Listen to what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I think the Justice Department should be investigating anyone, including former presidents, if there is credible evidence that they engaged in criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And so look, of course, Chris, as I said to Schiff there, that`s not the job of this committee. They of course also can`t file criminal proceedings on their own but they can do things like refer criminal investigations and of course none of this is happening in a vacuum. Everything that the committee is presenting today as well as going into the next few weeks is all in the public. Everything that DOJ can watch and listen to, Schiff saying that he hopes the Department of Justice is paying attention to what`s going on here and being quite forceful there, laying down the line in ways that I haven`t heard members of this committee do before, making clear that accountability should not stop regardless of the person`s hierarchy or position of power, including for former presidents of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan, Ali just mentioned the president`s -- former president`s mindset. I want to play part of what former Attorney General Barr said about that, about the former president`s mindset after the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARR: I was somewhat demoralized because I thought, boy, if he really believes this stuff, he has, you know, lost contact with -- he`s become detached from reality.\par \par \uc2 [14:05:02]\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, you know, when I went into this and would, you know, tell him how crazy some of these allegations were, there was never -- there was never an indication of interest in what the actual facts were.\par \par \uc2 Before the election it was possible to talk sense to the president and while you sometimes had to engage in, you know, a wrestling match with him that it was possible to keep things on track, but I was -- felt that after the election he didn`t seem to be listening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Jonathan, for anyone who thinks this is a backward-looking hearing, let`s not forget this man is the odds-on favorite to be the next Republican nominee for president.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Certainly the thesis of today`s hearing was to show these election lies, claims of fraud, which of course were not true. We had gripping accounts on election night, accounts from outside the White House of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , the president`s attorney, being described by those close to him as visibly inebriated that night, telling the president he should just go out there and declare victory, and others including Bill Stepien, including \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , two top campaign aides, saying not to do so, saying instead to just say, hey, look, it`s not over, votes are still being counted, but to certainly stop short of declaring victory.\par \par \uc2 Trump of course went with Giuliani and we have reporting that those first couple of days there was a sort of sense that maybe he acknowledged he had lost but as the weeks went on and he wasn`t forced to concede by other Republicans, the lies hardened and took hold. And he started railing daily, multiple times a day on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  about these lies. And certainly the committee in the hearings today did an extraordinarily effective job of talking about this, of painting this picture that Trump knew better.\par \par \uc2 Certainly people around him knew better and repeatedly told him. And whether Trump just willfully ignored them or convinced himself of the truth in that regard, it didn`t matter. He was surrounded by people who said to him, this is not true, you did not win but he continued to push forward, including raising campaign funds based off of that lie.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Paul, since Jonathan brought up Giuliani, let`s talk about the legal implications here. OK. Trump was not listening to his aides, his attorney general, senior officials, lawyers, except \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , who according to two people had been drinking on election night. Let`s listen to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: The mayor was definitely intoxicated, but I did not know his level of intoxication when he spoke with the president, for example.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Paul, Giuliani essentially tells Trump just to declare victory even though all the other aides are telling him not to do that. Trump then goes out and does it and he`s basically repeated it every day since. Does that play in any way to any possible criminal culpability?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Absolutely. So the person who gave Trump this big idea was a drunk, washed-up lawyer on election night when Trump`s campaign manager told him that night it was too early to declare victory but Trump did it anyway. We heard evidence from all of these eyewitnesses that Trump didn`t care about facts or evidence. The committee clearly believes that there was criminal intent, that Trump knew the big lie was a big lie, but he didn`t care. And it has lots of circumstantial evidence to support that claim.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, what we have not heard yet is an admission from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The evidence is incriminating but so far there`s no smoking gun. But we know that`s how Trump has traditionally operated. He doesn`t like to leave his own dirty fingerprints. The question now is whether that strategy will continue to work for him or whether the Justice Department will try to hold justice -- will try to hold the ex-president accountable even if it doesn`t have a slam dunk case.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, it was really interesting, Kyle, former acting deputy attorney general \uc2 Richard Donoghue\uc2  talked about just how hard it was to try to get through to the president by using facts. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONOGHUE: I said something to the effect of, sir, we`ve done dozens of investigations, hundreds of interviews, the major allegations are not supported by the evidence developed. There were so many of these allegations that when you gave him a very direct answer on one of them, he wouldn`t fight us on it but he would move to another allegation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It was, Kyle, I guess and it`s been referred to this, right, kind of like playing Whack-a-Mole with the allegations, right?\par \par \uc2 KYLE CHENEY, POLITICO LEGAL AFFAIRS REPORTER: Yes. That`s what Attorney General Barr described it as. And what`s so interesting is the committee didn`t just have one aide after another saying -- who told the president this was false, they let those aide talk about very specific allegations that the president raised and how they went about debunking them and told them how they debunked them and that it still never broke through.\par \par \uc2 [14:10:08]\par \par \uc2 And what Trump would do is not just ignore them, he ostracized them, he pushed them out of his circle and turned to people like \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , Sydney Powell and others who would validate his world view on this. And so that`s sort of the picture they painted today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Barbara, the committee says that even after the president was told that his allegations repeatedly again of election fraud were false, he kept pushing donors to give money to something called the election defense fund, which they now say never existed and that most of the money sent there went to something called the Save America PAC. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA WICK, SENIOR INVESTIGATIVE COUNSEL, HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: The select committee discovered that the Save America PAC made millions of dollars of contributions to pro-Trump organizations, including $1 million to Trump`s Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ` charitable foundation, $1 million to the America First Policy Institute, a conservative organization which employs several former Trump administration officials, $204,857 to the \uc2 Trump Hotel Collection\uc2  and over $5 million to Event Strategies, Inc., the company that ran President Trump`s January 6th rally on the Ellipse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Barbara, is that legal?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, you know what I think may have happened here, the committee in investigating one crime may have stumbled upon another. This happens quite a bit when prosecutors investigate cases. Turns out we didn`t find Whitewater but we did find \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 . In this case it appears that they are building a very solid case of willful blindness as to the big lie which could contribute to criminal liability for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for election fraud, conspiracy to defraud the United States and conspiracy to obstruction an official proceeding.\par \par \uc2 But in addition if he is using a lie as a false pretense to collect money from people, that could also be a charge of wire fraud. And so in that way I think we may have seen an expansion of the potential liability here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, it also doesn`t go to mindset, right? That`s just following the numbers, follow the money as they said during Watergate.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes. And I think the same mindset that would be a support for any of these other election-based crimes would be the same mindset that would be used as evidence in a fraud claim. And what comes out to me is I thought we heard crystal clear today what I thought is the essence of willful blindness. That is a theory of liability of knowledge of fraud and that was when \uc2 William Barr\uc2  said he showed no indication of any interest in what the actual facts were. That is the essence of willful blindness, turning a blind eye to a fact that is inconvenient but true.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, I`ve seen and heard from a lot of people already, Jonathan, who say today was better than they thought it would be. I mean, we know the first hearing got impressive viewership, more than the World Series, more than the Summer Olympics opening ceremony. And we don`t know how many people watched today but there will be clips, you can bet. Intoxicated, right, play the Powerball, referring to the chances Trump could win after Arizona was called, and how hungry people were to consume- wise, among others. But practically, what do you think, Jonathan? What should we be looking at to suggest the committee`s work is building to something politically?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Well, first of all, they`ve done a terrific job establishing the narrative. And the viewership the other night very impressive. Undoubtedly today will be less, it`s not primetime, it`s during the day. Most Americans are at work. But people will consume it on cable. People will see the clips on social media, the "NIGHTLY NEWS," and this evening on MSNBC. And that there is a breakthrough. And I think that Thursday night the primetime hearing was defined by that gripping video, never-before-seen footage of the riot.\par \par \uc2 Today these testimonies are so damning in terms of painting a picture of the president being fully aware and people around him being fully aware that he did not win. Yet pushing forward with it anyway --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think it matters, Jonathan, to the people in the middle that the people who are giving this testimony were Republicans?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: That`s the goal. That was the plan of the committees because they think this is far more effective. Not just members of the GOP, members of Trump`s inner circle being the ones talking. Not just some Democrats who can be painted as having an agenda, but rather people close to the former president himself. They`re the ones saying this. And they do think this could break through.\par \par \uc2 And maybe down the road, we heard from Congressman Schiff, that he thinks there could be criminal charges in the offing. But even if that doesn`t happen, the goal of this committee, as we`ve said, is not just to investigate January 6th which would prevent something like this from happening again, and that includes preventing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who as you said earlier, right now the favorite to be the GOP nominee in 2024, to prevent him from getting anywhere near power again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So a lot to dissect from days one and two, Kyle. But what more are we likely to learn on Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: So I think we`re going to see even more detail about what played out in the Justice Department than you heard some of that testimony today. \uc2 Bill Barr\uc2  left before all of this really came to a head ahead of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [14:15:04]\par \par \uc2 And so Barr`s successor, Jeff Rosen, his deputy \uc2 Richard Donoghue\uc2 , they`re going to talk about their efforts to run down these false claims and debunk them and the push by Trump to essentially replace them when they didn`t tell him what he wanted to hear and how Trump sort of backed off that plan last minute amid a mass resignation threat. But it just shows how far he was willing to go to use the levers of government to try to further his effort.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Paul, when you look at building a case potentially DOJ, again, this is the political side of it but when you look at what we know so far, what do you hope to learn on Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So today`s testimony was kind of lawyerly and contrasted the opening hearing, which was graphic and dramatic. So on Wednesday I think we`re going to see a synthesis of both those approaches. But we`re going to hear evidence about the Oath Keepers and Proud Boys, and they`ve already been charged, some of them, with seditious conspiracy. What`s the link with Trump? Well, I think some people on the panel think the link goes to people like \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 . So I think we`re going to see those ties established on Wednesday. Again, links between the actual violence of the insurrection and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Paul Butler, Barbara McQuade, Jonathan Lemire, and Kyle Cheney, thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to turn to the other major story on Capitol Hill. Getting meaningful gun legislation to the president`s desk. Now that we have a framework, what will the actual bill look like and when will it get a vote?\par \par \uc2 And we`re keeping a very close eye on the markets. Right now, look at this, the Dow down another 700 plus point while gas prices are up to record highs. The Fed seems ready to take action. So how will it change how much you pay for what you need?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [14:21:03]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s mounting pressure on a group of Democratic and Republican senators to hold together the bipartisan framework deal that they made on gun violence legislation. Can the ideas that they hammered out be translated into actual legislation, maintaining the support of all 10 Republican senators so they can make it across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 Well, this morning the lead Democrat in the talks, Connecticut Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , sounded this note of optimism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): There`s a reason why we haven`t passed any anti- gun legislation in 30 years. This is difficult. And the fact of the matter is we reached a compromise over the weekend that will save lives. This is a compromise but it is a difference maker, it is a substantial compromise that is going to stop a lot of suicides, a lot of homicides, and save a lot of lives in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dialing up the pressure and the urgency even more, Americans taking their demands for change to the streets. In Washington at the second "March for Our Lives" rally on the National Mall, just look at this crowd, tens of thousands of people right in front of the Washington Monument. And it wasn`t just there. More than 400 rallies held in cities across the country in almost all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Allie Raffa is on Capitol Hill for us to dig into next steps with this gun deal.\par \par \uc2 So, Allie, there does seem to be both optimism kind of a nervousness. First, they have to turn this agreement into an actual bill and there`s plenty of time for things to get in the way between now and July when they want to have it done. Give us the sense on the Hill of that level of confidence versus concern that you`re hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Reporter: Well, Chris, I think you laid out the reality check perfect there. There is really nothing signed, sealed or delivered yet. But another thing Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , who as you mentioned, has been leading this effort not just since these shootings in Uvalde and Buffalo but for nearly 10 years now since Sandy Hook, he said that he`s confident that this framework, this initial deal, is solid enough, that it already has the minimum amount of Republicans in support of it to break that filibuster. And there`s really only room to grow from here.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats also have another reason to be more confident than House Democrats right now after House Democrats passed those Senate bills last week on the House side that have no chance of passing in the Senate. So there`s really a sense of optimism on the Democratic side now that at least 10 Republicans have really committed their support to these provisions. And I`m going to lay out a few of them right now.\par \par \uc2 If passed, this bill would provide incentives for states to implement those red flag laws that would allow weapons to be taken away for those deemed to be a danger to themselves or others. It would also boost funding for mental health, suicide prevention and school safety. It would prevent domestic violence offenders from purchasing guns by closing that so-called boyfriend loopholes. It also expands background checks for gun buyers under the age of 21 by setting up a mandatory review of juvenile and mental health records, a step that actually may have blocked the 18-year-old shooter in Uvalde from buying his weapons.\par \par \uc2 So the big question is, what happens now? How long does this take to be voted on? Senators are back today working on drafting this legislation, hoping, they say, to have a vote by July. Democrats say they think they can get more than 10 Republicans to sign on to this, but that would obviously require Senate minority leader Mitch McConnell`s blessing. Yesterday after this bipartisan deal was announced, he issued a statement where he said he appreciates the hard work of these senators but he really stopped short of offering his support.\par \par \uc2 Our team caught up with Texas Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , who`s been leading this effort on the Republican side. As he was walking to his office today, he addressed that actually in a sound bite here.\par \par \uc2 [14:25:01]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I think he certainly supports the process but he hasn`t, to my knowledge, said -- so, yes, I think he`s supportive of the process but that`s where we are right now. People are waiting for text because we don`t vote on press releases, we vote on text. And so that`s why the hard work this week is going to continue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFFA: Cornyn added that he is optimistic he can get a draft of this -- legislation, rather, to Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  by next week - - Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Allie Raffa, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 Kris Brown\uc2 , president of the Brady Campaign which is urging the Senate to pass that bipartisan gun violence framework. She started her career on Capitol Hill and advocated for what would eventually become the Brady Bill, requiring background checks on federally licensed gun sales.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much for being with us. How would you rate this bill as it stands right now?\par \par \uc2 KRIS BROWN\uc2 , BRADY PRESIDENT: I would rate it for our movement as an A-plus. And in terms of the details that actually acknowledge that firearms and gun safety are a part of reducing firearm injury and death in this country also as an A plus. A journey of a long way starts with a single step and this is more than a single step. It`s fairly comprehensive. It covers a number of different issues that contribute to gun violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I think if you look at the assortment of senators on the Republican side and the Democratic side, what you find interesting here is among the 10 Republican senators, eight of them have A or A-plus ratings from the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2 . The assortment of Democrats have F and one D. And that`s \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 . We have not seen this before. And, frankly, we`ve not seen it on any issue out of the Senate over the last year.\par \par \uc2 It is historic. It`s very important because you saw and we all saw Americans across this country are fed up and they know that part of the solution to ending the epidemic of gun violence, a uniquely American problem, is actually reasonable gun safety. This isn`t controversial. And so for that reason, it is very, very important for our movement.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You`re right about the \uc2 NRA\uc2  rating about those 10 senators. They`re also not running for reelection, is what some people have pointed out. And there is also an outstanding question mark of the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , which said in a statement that they do not take positions on frameworks but, the big if, and you`ve lived this before, is whether they can apply pressure, cast doubt as this pressure moves long. As Allie said, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  hasn`t said he`s on board yet.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about what happens between now and the time it presumably gets to the finish line and at least to a vote at the end of July?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, look, I think if you have 10 Republicans in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  agreeing with 10 Democrats in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  on a framework that is not terribly detailed, it needs to be spelled out as noted. I did work on Capitol Hill for eight years and the devil is in the details, but it`s high enough level with enough detail that I know from my conversations with the staff they understand what needs to be put into those bills, and I don`t think these folks, while astute in politics, would be signing on to a framework on a Sunday just to back away from it two weeks later.\par \par \uc2 One other thing I`ll say about the idea that the \uc2 NRA\uc2  does not sign off or comment on frameworks. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  has attempted to scuttle every major piece of gun safety, whether that is in the elemental form or stated form or detailed legislative form for 30 years. They attempted every step of the way to stop the enactment of the Brady Law. That`s our background check system. So it says a lot that they are trying to revise history and say they don`t comment on things like this. Of course they do.\par \par \uc2 What they`re afraid of is the reality that their talking points that gun violence in this country has nothing to do with firearms, that a good guy with a gun is all that it takes to stop a bad guy with a gun, have been proven wrong. But for the American people, I think we see with this framework that it has. The devil is in the details. We all need to have to continue to lift our voices but it`s a big victory for gun violence prevention and the movement that`s about the American people and wanting to be safe in public spaces and dropping our kids at school.\par \par \uc2 This is a parents and kids almost as much as it is about anything else.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about the visible aspect of that movement, which was all those marches over the weekend, those tens of thousands, probably hundreds of thousands across the country.\par \par \uc2 [14:30:04]\par \par \uc2 And you these the commitment from those Americans and the bill, assuming it passes, there`s already a debate. Is this just the first step or is it the final step? So how do you harness that energy and momentum to make this not the last change?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, Brady has been around for a long time. Jim and \uc2 Sarah Brady\uc2  were Republicans, they were gun owners, they achieved the impossible. It took six years and seven votes. What I`m heartened about honestly is that we now have a movement. That rally this weekend was "March for Our Lives." They were formed after 2018. These are kids, these are young people traumatized by gun violence who have said enough.\par \par \uc2 There is no question in the minds of anyone at the helm of this movement or any of all of the folks at Brady and the other groups, this is the beginning, this is the beginning of much more needed change. And, by the way, we`ve accomplished that in state after state after state. The reason we have extreme risk laws, for example, and their support in this package is because 19 states and Republican governors have signed those into law. We are making progress. It`s the beginning, it`s not the end.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Kris Brown\uc2 , thank you so much for taking the time to talk to us today. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago reporters asked Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  about the January 6th Committee hearings. We`re getting those comments for you and we will have them to play for you right after the break.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`re keeping a close eye on the markets right now. Down hundreds of points today. It comes as the average cost of a gallon of gas is now officially above $5 a gallon. Are these prices going to ease up any time soon? Plus, the big warning about what it could mean for unemployment.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [14:35:47]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New comments just moments ago from Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  on the January 6th hearings. Reporters asked him whether he`s had a chance to watch them. Here`s part of his response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: I am watching and I will be watching all the hearings, although I may not be able to watch all of it live, but I`ll be sure that I`ll be watching all of it. And I can assure you that the January 6th prosecutors are watching all the hearings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s bring back Paul Butler, MSNBC legal analyst. He said he can assure us that the prosecutors are watching.\par \par \uc2 Look, we know the thousands and thousands of pieces of evidence. We know the people that they have on tape, hundreds of people that they`ve interviewed, but would it be fair to say, Paul, that any time somebody sits in that chair across from that committee and answers questions, it`s another opportunity, first of all, for new evidence and, number two, to see the ways in which the January 6th Committee is weaving their story?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: That`s exactly right, Chris. So think of two related audiences for the hearings. One is the Department of Justice. Congress does not have the power to bring a criminal prosecution. That`s up to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . The other audience, though, is the American people, and the panel hopes that the court of public opinion will help move the Justice Department. And in that context, think about the evidence presented today that Trump`s campaign raised millions of dollars off of the big lie. Part of that message is to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 It establishes another motive for why Trump perpetrated this fraud to make money, that`s a familiar motive with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But that evidence is also a signal to Trump supporters. It`s a message that the committee hopes will shift their support for Trump. The message is Trump knew he lost, he was just playing you to get your money.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Paul Butler, thank you for reacting to that news very quickly. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 The other news that we`re watching right now is the Dow. Let`s take the latest look at the numbers, down 642 points. Markets still diving after Friday`s brutal inflation report and fears over just how the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might respond. If you add to that gas prices that keep hitting new records, first time ever now, a national average just over $5 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 Of course we know it`s not just gas. At the grocery store the cost of food is up more than 10 percent from this time last year. Housing 5.5 percent. Airfares, 37 percent, and percentage-wise, gas is up a whopping 48.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Investopedia editor-in-chief Caleb Silver.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about those numbers, shall we? I mean, obviously they`re hitting low-income Americans very hard. They want to know what`s the solution and how fast can it come.\par \par \uc2 CALEB SILVER, INVESTOPEDIA EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: Yes, I wish I could tell you. But it looks like we`re going to have these high gas prices through the summer. We`ve seen reports and estimates from some of institutions looking we have some looking at $6, $6.20 gas by the end of the summer if you can believe that on average.\par \par \uc2 Now in California and in Oregon, they`d love to be paying that. They`re paying even more than the national average there. But gas prices are going to remain tight, demand is very high, supply remains low and we`re going into the summer driving season.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And another big week for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . What are you expecting to see?\par \par \uc2 SILVER: Well, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to raise interest rates this week. That`s the only thing they can do to cool down inflation. The worry is that they put the economy into a deep freeze by raising rates too much too fast and they go under a recession. Recessions are painful, especially for low-income people.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`ve heard how if they`re going to make moves, it could affect the unemployment rate. How does that work without getting too wonky for us?\par \par \uc2 SILVER: No reason to get wonky. When they raise interest rates, they raise the borrowing cost. Raises the borrowing cost for us when we`re thinking about buying a car or buying a house. But it raises the borrowing cost for businesses that borrow money to expand, to hire people, to raise wages. We could get that destruction where rates get so high that nobody wants to borrow money, including businesses and they stop expanding and they slow down, starts laying people off. That`s how that could play up.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I think one of the things that have surprised a lot of people, gas prices going up but people are continuing to drive. There certainly is not a correlation between how high gas prices have gone and the very small percentage that gas consumption has gone down.\par \par \uc2 [14:40:04]\par \par \uc2 People`s buying habits don`t seem to be significantly impacted right now. Is there a sense, though, that we`re very near that tipping point?\par \par \uc2 SILVER: Yes. Consumer credit is very high. Credit card debt is rising as well. And for folks who are earning less than $50,000, $1 out of every 10, goes to filling up the tank right now. Then you got rent, then you get all those other expenses you talked about. Very difficult times so people are still spending. At some point consumers are going to back out.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Caleb, thanks so much for coming in. We`ll continue to keep an eye on the Dow for all of you out there.\par \par \uc2 From rising prices to rising temperatures. Millions of Americans are under heat advisories today, which means costs to air condition. You don`t want to hear about that. It was a brutal weekend of extreme temperatures in the southwest with more than two dozen major cities tying or breaking record high temperatures. They included on Saturday Las Vegas, Denver, Phoenix. Phoenix at 114 degrees. They tied the record set more than a century ago.\par \par \uc2 And wildfires are thriving in that high heat, at least three of them popping up across Southern California on Sunday. This heat wave is not expected to ease up. It is heading east over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 But we do have some good news for parents who have lost sleep over protecting their kids through this pandemic. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  now says that kid sized doses of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 `s COVID vaccine appears safe and effect for kids under 5 years old. Independent experts convened by the agency will vote on Wednesday on whether the shots are ready for 18 million babies, toddlers and preschoolers who so far have not been eligible for a COVID vaccine.\par \par \uc2 The panel will also review a request from \uc2 Moderna\uc2  about its vaccine for children under 6. So if even one of those shots are given the all clear, vaccinations could begin as soon as next week.\par \par \uc2 And this hour, we just learned new details about the arrest of 31 men tied to a white nationalist group who conspired to riot at a Pride Parade in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 That`s next on this special edition of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [14:46:11]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just this hour, authorities in Idaho are sharing more details about the arrests of more than 30 alleged members of the white nationalist group Patriot Front. The men were found packed into a U-Haul truck near a Pride Parade on Saturday wearing masks and dressed in riot gear. 31 men were charged with misdemeanor conspiracy to riot.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Gadi Schwartz joins us with more on this. Gadi, what other details did we learn at that press conference?\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Chris. An interesting note. The police chief just said that since those arrests they`ve gotten about 149 calls that have come in so far, and there`s been a 50-50 split, half of them commending the officers for what they did and then the other half, mostly anonymous calls from as far as away as Norway, cursing officers out, sometimes threatening officers, including death threats against the police chief.\par \par \uc2 And they wouldn`t go into many specifics but they say that most of those death threats and most of the threats appear to be hate groups from outside calling. But the police think that most of the calls encouraging officers or commending them are from the local community. And police say they did have some generic information before the event that agitators would be trying to disrupt the Pride event so they did staff up accordingly.\par \par \uc2 The police also say that they were aware tangentially of this group. They had seen videos of them online but they didn`t have any direct contact, and saying it was really the call from a citizen who saw what they described as a small army loading into a U-Haul at a hotel that prevented what they are certain would have been a riot. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Did they have any indication that there was any kind of specific plan that they have, Gadi?\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Yes, in fact there were military -- it looked like what were almost military or law enforcement plans. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " actually detailed how there was smoke grenade that was supposed to be used after a column forming on the outside of the park, proceeding inwards towards these barriers were met. And then, quote, "Once the appropriate amount of confrontational dynamic had been established, the column would then disengage and head to Sherman," which is one of the main streets there in Coeur d`Alene.\par \par \uc2 So it sounded very military like. Almost like something you would see in an action plan from law enforcement. Another thing we know about this group, they do a lot of these almost like live action, role playing, simulation and training events at parks. And then they post those online. Some of those have since gone viral. So a lot of the way that they try to recruit members to this hate group is by having those viral moments, and in this case police say the confrontation that they were trying to set out to do was disruptive. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Gadi Schwartz, thank you so much. We appreciate that update.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, overseas, President Zelenskyy says Ukrainian troops are fighting for literally every meter as Russian forces are very close to seizing a key city. Just how consequential would that be?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [14:53:07]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, Russia is very close to taking control of the key eastern Ukraine city of Severodonetsk. The Ukrainian military reporting they`ve been pushed out of the center of that city. Taking it would give Russia control of the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy says Ukraine is now fighting for every meter, but emphasizes the need for more fire power from the U.S. and its allies. While most of that city has fled, the governor of the region says up to 500 residents, including 40 children, have taken shelter inside a chemical plant in the city.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Arouzi is in Kyiv. He`s been following this fight. Ali, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Chris. That`s right. Well, as you know, the Luhansk region is the industrial heartland of Ukraine, and the Russians are burning it to the ground. The focal point of their attacks for the last several weeks has been that region. And most of their firepower is concentrated on Severodonetsk and its twin city across the river bank Lysychansk. And by all accounts, the battles there are fierce and bloody. Street-to-street, sometimes house by house.\par \par \uc2 And it`s the battle for those two cities that is shaping this war. And the Russians are throwing everything they have at it, and they`re pushing the Ukrainians into smaller and smaller pockets of control. As you mentioned, there`s that Azov chemical plant, where there are about 800 civilians hiding. Many of them women and children. And the Ukrainians are now on the outskirts of the city. And what the Russians are also doing, they are blowing up bridges from Severodonetsk to across the river in Lysychansk.\par \par \uc2 They blew up two bridges the other day and they`ve destroyed the third, and the last one today. So Severodonetsk is essentially cut off now from the rest of Ukraine. The Ukrainian troops can`t get ammunition, men, or humanitarian aid into that city. So the situation is looking very desperate for them.\par \par \uc2 [14:55:01]\par \par \uc2 And they`re having problems fighting the Russians because of the sheer mass of the Russian numbers and firepower and artillery. They just outmatch the Ukrainians, sometimes we`re hearing 15-1 on artillery pieces.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, as we have seen before, the Ukrainians have defied the odds again and again. They`re fighting tooth and nail for that place because it`s just so pivotal in this war to hold on to it. If the Russians were to take Severodonetsk, and then Lysychansk, they control that entire area. And that would be an enormous loss to the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Arouzi, thank you so much for that update.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday at our usual hour, 1:00 Eastern, right here on MSNBC. I`ll be back there tomorrow. And don`t forget to tune in to MSNBC for a special two-hour January 6th hearing recap. That`s tonight at 8:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC. It`s also available on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned. Jose Diaz-Balart picks up our coverage, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, MSNBC HOST: In Washington, the Senate coming into session for the first time since that bipartisan deal on gun reform. We could hear from \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on this in just minutes.\par \par \uc2 This hour, what our team is learning about when the bill could be written and when the votes could actually happen. One member of Senate leadership joining us live here this hour.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of Capitol Hill, the January 6th Committee`s big hearing on the big lie.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220616ei6d00031\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for June 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Hallie Jackson, Katy Tur, Ryan Reilly, Clint  Watts, Garrett Haake, Dasha Burns,  Yamiche Alcindor, Barbara McQuade, Chuck Rosenberg, Frank Figliuzzi, Ali  Vitali \par \uc2 10,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Losing democracy and who was behind it all?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And that`s right, Katy. And while we`ve been sorting through every bombshell from last night`s hearing, there have been new headlines today involving former President Trump, former acting Attorney General Jeffrey Rosen, and even Ginni Thomas, the wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , of course.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: So, in this hour, here`s what we`re going to do. We`re going to take a look at what we have learned about this Trump team`s strategy to try and overturn the legitimate results of the 2020 election before the insurrection. We`ll also talk with former DHS Secretary Jeh Johnson. But, Katy, you know, one of the key things -- one of the key takeaways here has been this connection between the Proud Boys, The Oath Keepers, and how this plays into it all.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Absolutely. We`re going to dig into that -- to that. There were powerful revelations, gripping firsthand accounts, and graphic videos from that day. But while the committee set out last night to make the case that former President Trump was at the center of a plot to overturn a free and fair election, they also argued he had help. We`re going to show you one of the committee`s exhibits from last night that lays out how the Proud Boys and other similar far-right groups organized leading up to the January 6 insurrection and took the president`s words as a direct call to action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARCUS CHILDRESS, INVESTIGATIVE COUNSEL: My name is Marcus Childress. I`m an investigative counsel for the select committee to investigate the January 6 attack on the United States Capitol.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What do you want to call them? Give me a name, give me a name. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, ANCHOR, CNN: White supremacist and --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Who do you want me to condemn?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: (INAUDIBLE) the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: White supremacist and right-wing militia.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Proud Boys stand back and stand by.\par \par \uc2 CHILDRESS: After he 