can see the committee starting to build that case last night by highlighting what he was told by his own attorney general. He was told by his attorney general, and it seems as though his own daughter concurred in The View that there was no evidence that the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 And so that is building block number one, in the case, that what -- that what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying he knew to be false. Very often when you`re prosecuting people, such as people in organized crime, you`re not going to have an e-mail smoking gun, you`re not going to have handwriting, it`s going to be eyewitnesses` testimony about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was told and what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 TUR: All right, so Clint, let`s go back to the Oath Keepers and the Proud Boys. A lot of attention was paid to both of those organizations last night as Secretary Johnson just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And we saw in the video their organization numbers grew as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was calling them out standby stand back, or stand back standby in the -- in that debate with Chris Wallace. They are going to be -- and our central figures to this, what will -- what are you hearing in terms of chatter, right now, online with these two organizations about these hearings and about what might happen in the future?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: It`s really two parts, Katy. I think for some, it`s a let`s celebrate January 6, that`s a smaller group now than it was 18 months ago. I can -- I can tell you that. For the others, I think it`s distancing. You`re -- you`ve seen a pretty consistent trend over the last year where some groups realizing the severity of what happened, and that they could be in trouble just denouncing themselves as part of the Proud Boys in particular.\par \par \uc2 The Oath Keepers were smaller and a little bit more collectively organized militia, same sort of trend. So some have moved further in towards extremism in the small group, many have tried to back away from it because they see the trouble that they`re in and what`s happening there.\par \par \uc2 But separately, I think it`s inspired a new younger generation of people to organize collectively. After January 6, you did see groups that were both militia-oriented and race-based white supremacist groups turn to more organized activity, organized uniforms, collective action showing up in public. That`s kind of a damning sign, I think, for the future if this momentum around white supremacy continues.\par \par \uc2 Above all, what I find remarkable about that presentation that you replayed here at the top of the hour, is how clear this was a conspiracy -- a seditious conspiracy, which is a group making a secret plan to conduct an unlawful act. It is as clear as it can be. I`ve never seen anything with so much evidence to support it.\par \par \uc2 The text messages in both indictments, both the one for the Oath Keepers and for the Proud Boys, it is very clear to me. I don`t -- I don`t know how anyone would argue it. And I`ve never seen any group of individuals that was conducting a conspiracy provide so much evidence in their own indictment. It is quite remarkable. I`ve never seen anything like it before. So I think this is clear. I think the long-run term of this is what is that connection to the Trump orbit? That`s what really needs to be flushed out at this point.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Secretary Johnson, \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , thanks to the both of you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up here on this SPECIAL COVERAGE on MSNBC, the potential political implications of what we`re witnessing with these hearings. That`s next week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: A former Fox News political editor Chris Stirewalt now said to be testifying in front of the January 6 committee. Let me bring in NBC News Senior Capitol Hill Correspondent Garrett Haake into the conversation here. Garrett, this is interesting. Chris Stirewalt, at the center of -- I think it`s fair to say a little bit of drama. Is it related to what was going down where he used to work as the election results were coming in? He is now confirming, I believe, just this morning that he has been asked with -- I think, a subpoena do we know that to appear?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Not clear -- not clear.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: OK. But lawyers are involved, no?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think that`s definitely the case, Hallie. Look, yes, I mean, Stirewalt is an interesting person and he fits the theme of what we know about Monday`s hearing perfectly. That committee has said that they`re going to talk about the big lie. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  talks about that at the end of -- it was \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  at the end of the hearing last night that Monday is big Lie day.\par \par \uc2 And how did it start? It started on election night, or technically, in the early morning hours of the day after election night where you had all the networks as we all are probably slightly scarred by, going late into the night trying to figure out what exactly was going on with this election.\par \par \uc2 And Fox News was the first to call Arizona and at the time, none of the other networks had done it. It was a hugely controversial decision. Stirewalt went on TV to defend it. And we`ve heard in countless other reporting since then, he was getting pressured by Trump campaign officials, getting pressured by potentially Trump family members. He has written subsequently that he got a ton of pressure after the fact from other outside voices, and was forced to really defend that decision within the network.\par \par \uc2 It was later on that same night that then-President Trump went out and said his famous, you know we really did win this election with the data that he was getting both internally and externally from someone like Stirewalt all went the other way. We will hear the genesis of that big lie come Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Garrett, we also hear new reporting that Georgia Secretary of State, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  was, of course, in the middle of an election now, to post their primary, and his Deputy are both going to be testifying and it was \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , who had the phone call from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, find me 11,870 votes. Obviously --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Pressure on a state -- a secretary of state official to overturn an election that had been legitimately won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s right, Andrea. That`s going to be in a committee hearing, not actually this coming week but the following week when the committee gets into the various pressure campaigns on the state level. One of the things that I and my team are trying to figure out is which other state-level officials might be coming to testify because, of course, it wasn`t just Georgia, they were pressure campaigns in Arizona, and in Pennsylvania, and in Wisconsin, Michigan, other places where the Trump campaign apparatus thought they might be able to either exert pressure on specific local officials or challenge ballots through frivolous -- ultimately dismissed as frivolous in some cases, lawsuits, audits and so forth.\par \par \uc2 But Raffensperger has been, by far, the most public, obviously, on all of these and is, you know, backed up by receipts in the form of the recording of that phone call from then-President Trump asking about those 11,000 plus votes. So the state-level part of this, I think, will be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Again, the committee is trying to lay out pretty methodically, a case about you know the big lie, Justice Department, Pence, the states, and really present this again as a multi-prong conspiracy, not just a thing that happened because people were not careful with their language and their rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Garrett Haake, live for us on Capitol Hill. Garrett, glad you`re with us. Thank you so much. So now, the potential political implications of what we have watched unfold, we wanted to find out what it`s meant in a critical battleground state, Pennsylvania. That`s where we find our Dasha Burns who has been covering this state for months now, Dasha, you`re going to stay on it as part of our county-to-county series. You`ve been talking with voters there, I think for much of the morning, tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Hallie, not only is this an all- important battleground state, but where I`m standing right now, you can see Independence Hall behind me here. This is literally where the foundation of our democracy was built. And several of the voters we spoke with this morning expressed real fear that that foundation is now crumbling. Hard to watch was a phrase that we heard a lot but about half of the people we spoke with did watch and here are some of their takeaways.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ELLYN STERN, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: I know that my friends all watched it. But my friends have very similar ideas as mine. I think that if something`s done with the information, we won`t have Trump running for office ever again.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Are you going to watch the following hearings?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TUBBS, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: I think I will. Yes. I mean, I really -- I made it a point to watch it. I think we`re watching history unfold. And I think everybody should watch it. We could do two things at once. I think, obviously, there`s a lot of other problems going on but again, we can`t just say, well, we`ll deal with this later because I just think this is really important.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SOLOT, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: For the country so divided, and people who bought into the lies about the election that -- or about the results of the election, that I don`t think that it`s going to change minds of people who are really solid in that camp.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What do you want to see as the outcome from all of this?\par \par \uc2 SOLOT: I`d like to see a conviction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We also spoke to several people off-camera who said they didn`t watch the hearings. Some of those were young mothers caring for young children, that that was their priority, other people were just out at dinner and said they don`t care about politics. And so I think the key question for this committee is between now and the conclusion of these hearings, can they break through to those kinds of voters too, guys?\par \par \uc2 TUR: Did you talk to anybody who might have felt differently after the hearings, Dasha? Did you talk to anybody who was not going to watch but then heard about it today and thought, hey, listen, maybe it`d be it`s worth it?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The reality, Katy, is no. And those voters themselves admitted that you know, they had been following this story. And they themselves said they hope that some people who haven`t been did tune in. But I`ll tell you last night, I was at Dr. Oz`s event, he was kicking off his campaign for the general election now that he is officially the Republican nominee, about 100 people or so in that room. January 6, was not surprisingly not brought up. Instead, he talked about issues of crime, inflation, gas prices, and that is the uphill battle that this committee has right now as there are a lot of other issues that voters are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 So the people who aren`t paying attention to this and the people who are maybe in the Trump camp who support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or who simply, you know, don`t think this is an important issue can either the hearings themselves or some of the -- you know, the clips that wind up on social media were a lot of people are consuming their information, can some of those things breakthrough? I think that`s going to be what we really have to look out for, Katy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Thank you so much, Dasha. Thank you so much. Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And coming up, what the January 6 committee says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to do at the Department of Justice to further his claims of election fraud? You`re watching special coverage of the January 6 Committee Hearings on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:53]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Among the new facts laid out by Committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was Donald Trump`s attempts to appoint a fellow election denier Jeffrey Clark as attorney general of the United States which would have triggered a mass resignation from the Department of Justice leadership. Clark was planning to send a letter to Georgia and five other states falsely demanding that they investigate the election results for fraud even though there was no evidence of fraud that would have changed the outcome and have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning.\par \par \uc2 The panel heard from acting deputy attorney general Richard Donahue who described the scheme as a grave step.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD DONAHUE, DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: And I recall toward the end saying what you`re proposing is nothing less than the \uc2 United States Justice Department\uc2  meddling in the outcome of a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Back with us now Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Washington correspondent and moderator, of course, of "Washington Week" on \uc2 PBS\uc2 , Frank Figliuzzi, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  assistant director for counterintelligence, former U.S. attorney Chuck Rosenberg and Barbara McQuade, former U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got lots of prosecutors here, and Yamiche, a prosecuting journalist. So how damning do you think all of this is? Perhaps not in a court of law, but let`s talk about politics. The court of public opinion.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, Andrea, the key question after this historic hearing, after these new details, is really how much is it going to matter in a democracy that is fractured, that is divided, that is partisan. I`ve been talking to folks who are hoping that a lot of Americans tuned in, but I definitely have talked to people who did not watch last night.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not talking about just sort of disengaged people. I`m talking about people who are federal workers, people who live in Washington, D.C. who were asking me about whether or not yesterday this hearing was going to be about gas prices. It remains to be seen whether or not these testimonies detailing all this sort of activity that is unprecedented for any U.S. president to do to ask officials in the Department of Justice to meddle in an election, whether or not those clips will make it out into the ethernet, will make it out into the internet, into people`s consciousness and change something.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers sure hope it is, that`s why it was in primetime and that`s why we`re going to see other hearings to really lay out this case. But it`s really a hard case to make because as we saw today, inflation is just getting worse and worse. There are so many challenges between the pandemic, and all the different things happening in people`s lives. But it is stunning to hear that not only was President Trump sort of saying this on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but that he was actually trying to use the mechanics of government, his power as president, to overturn an election.\par \par \uc2 That is of course part of the thesis of this, that this isn`t just sort of a spontaneous riot but the culmination of a multi-pronged effort, a conspiracy to overturn the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Let`s talk about it from a prosecutorial perspective now, a legal perspective. When it comes to Jeffrey Clark, what are the possible charges here, Barbara?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, I think that his role could very well fit into a conspiracy to defraud the United States. That is a crime that is charged when people agree with each other to do something to disrupt the proper functioning of government. Here it would be the proper certification of the duly elected president.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Clark, as you just described, was someone who had drafted a letter to send to Georgia saying to them fraudulently that the Justice Department had found irregularities in the election and suggesting to the state of Georgia that they convene their legislature for the purpose of selecting new electors. With a Republican-controlled legislature there, they could select \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the plan was to send that not just to Georgia, but to six states.\par \par \uc2 And so that is for anyone who has ever spent any time at the Justice Department a really horrifying overreach. One, a misrepresentation that there was an irregularity and two, involving the Justice Department in a state`s decision about how to conduct its elections. So that would place him I believe in a larger conspiracy to defraud the American people over the peaceful transfer of presidential power.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Chuck, how about the piece of information that we heard related to some Republican lawmakers the committee says including Scott Perry asking for pardons, basically. I should note that Congress Perry`s spokesperson calls it a ludicrous and soulless lie. I wonder how you see this.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROSENBERG, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY AND SENIOR \uc2 FBI\uc2  OFFICIAL: Yes, well, I`ll tell you, Hallie, how many times have you asked for a pardon? Or Katy or Andrea? Have you asked for pardons? I haven`t.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Lots of times.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: I`m pretty sure Barbara hasn`t. Yes, right, I mean, so the fact that you would ask for one -- and evidence will demonstrate one way or the other whether it`s a soulless lie as Mr. Perry claims or whether it actually happened. We have to wait for the evidence. But it is evidence of indicia of guilt. Right? If somebody has a guilty mind, they think they`ve done something wrong, they think they need deliverance, they think they need help, one way to get it is from a sitting president. And the form in which you get it is a pardon.\par \par \uc2 So we`ll see. Remember, we have to be careful with what we have heard and seen so far because most of what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said, for instance, was an opening statement. Previewing what the evidence will be. We should wait for the evidence. But if there is evidence that demonstrates that Mr. Perry asked for a pardon that`s problematic.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s be careful about one other thing. It doesn`t mean he broke the law. It doesn`t mean he would have been prosecuted. But it does suggest that he thought he broke the law and that he needed protection. It`s really a remarkable thing.\par \par \uc2 And I want to add one thing to what Barbara said. It`s always so easy to go after her because she`s right and usually I just have to say I agree. And I do agree. But for folks at the Department of Justice, the career men and women, and Barbara and I both came up through the ranks, to see somebody in a position of power misrepresent to one state and perhaps others, you know, the position of the department with respect to this really important pending matter, that`s astonishing.\par \par \uc2 This does not happen. I don`t want anyone out there to think that this is normal because it is not. It`s horrifying. That was Barbara`s word, and I agree with that, too.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I mean, it speaks of the Saturday night massacre of Watergate days when you had a domino effect of one resignation after the other going all the way down in the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Frank Figliuzzi, a lot of this is building towards \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  which we know -- we now know that \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  is going to be testifying on June 21st. So his experience one-on-one, we`ve heard the tape, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it`s also part of that Georgia state investigation. Fulton County, I should say.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: Yes, this is -- you know, I`m not a prosecutor, but I am a career investigator. And whether you`re investigating or prosecuting, you are building a case. And there is a foundation that`s laid and then there`s block upon block put on top of that foundation. So when we combine what we just talked about, Attorney General Barr telling the president there is no fraud there.\par \par \uc2 And that we hear that Jeffrey Rosen is going to be testifying and similarly he`s going to be talking about, hey, not only did we not find any fraud, but this guy Jeffrey Clark shows up trying to replace me because Trump can`t get around me, so he is going through an underling trying to strategize on how to do this unlawfully. Now we`re going to hear from Raffensperger and he`s going to say I was pressured by the White House, I was pressured by Trump.\par \par \uc2 This is block upon block upon block showing that former President Trump knew he had lost, was failing to give up pursuit of overturning the election, and now we`re going to see how he went about subverting the system to make it happen, forced the outcome that he wanted.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So we`re going to hear from Jeffrey Rosen on June 15th. It`s not just him, Barbara, it`s going to be two other DOJ -- former DOJ officials, Rich Donahue and Steve Engle. What are you going to be watching out for from them?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, we know a little bit about some of the things they`re going to testify to because they previously testified, at least Richard Donahue and Jeffrey Rosen did, testified before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 And the story they told there was really quite chilling, that the Trump White House including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself consistently and persistently pressured them to announce an investigation. And this really goes back to a lot of the M.O. that we`ve seen with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before. Remember when he said to \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in Ukraine, just say that you`re investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I just need an announcement.\par \par \uc2 And the same thing here he said to these men, just say that there is fraud and leave the rest to me and the Republican members of Congress. So what he really wanted from them was just enough of a nugget to legitimize this claim of fraud so that he could use that and convince the American people that there was fraud here. So I will be listening for that.\par \par \uc2 And then also as Andrea has suggested, this mass resignation possibility. Before they went into that meeting, that showdown at the Oval Office where Jeffrey Rosen and others said do not replace Jeffrey Rosen with Jeffrey Clark for the purpose of executing this scheme. If you do, we have people lined up and ready to resign. They had an e-mail ready to send to all of the component heads at the Justice Department as well as the chairs of the Attorney General`s Advisory Committee which would go to all the 93 U.S. attorneys in the country.\par \par \uc2 That entire group would have received an e-mail suggesting that they resign en masse. \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  at the White House Counsel was also prepared to resign. We would have had perhaps 100 lawyers at the highest levels of our government resigning on that Sunday night massacre if Trump had persisted with this plan. And even then he didn`t say OK, you`re right, what am I thinking, I`ll stop, he just pivoted to another method. So it`s going to be I think a really important bit of testimony that we hear on Monday.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Barbara, are you still confident in the resiliency of the DOJ going forward if an attempt like this is made again? And then what happens if we come to 2024 election where there are people who are sympathetic to the big lie, installed in positions of power over elections, how is the DOJ going to play into that?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I know we`re all talking about what happened on January 6th in 2020, but we`ve got to look forward in what might happen in 2022, 2024, what we need to look out for and prepare for.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes. Well, within the Department of Justice, I think that this was a very serious, horrifying, astonishing episode that we just described. And every component in the government is only as good as the people that you put there. So choosing people carefully is an important part of this. But also having some mechanisms in place to avoid these kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that happened when \uc2 William Barr\uc2  was the attorney general is he reversed a longtime policy at the Justice Department that permitted investigations of elections even before election results were certified. That was a change that really allowed this ability for people to interfere in election outcomes. That was promptly changed when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became president. So I think looking for things like that.\par \par \uc2 Context between the White House and the Justice Department, that policy was violated by the Trump White House and Justice Department. That needs to be restored. So you need good people and you need good rules in place to prevent these things. And then when it comes to secretaries of state, there are 14 secretaries of state candidates who have joined a coalition called the America First Coalition for Secretary of State who believe the big lie and are continuing to perpetuate it.\par \par \uc2 I think that we need to raise awareness among the American public so that when they cast their votes, they are doing so knowingly.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Hey, Yamiche, so we`ve been for the last 2 1/2 hours talking about what has happened overnight. We`ve been following the new developments as they`ve come up, as it relates to Jeffrey Rosen and \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  as we`ve reported live on the air. You have been covering this since January 6th happened. I know you have been because I have seen you doing it.\par \par \uc2 What is the thing that we haven`t talked about that you think is important to mention? In other words, what set off your reporter`s Spidey sense, if you will, during these hearings and in this discussion that you think is not getting enough attention here?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: I think it`s the future part of this. We`ve always -- there`s been a lot of conversation of course about what happened there. We were on -- we were at the White House, Hallie, I remember being lawn during January 6th getting that real-time reporting that President Trump was in some ways really liking what he was seeing.\par \par \uc2 But what about the future? What about all of the people who aren`t going to tune in or who will tune in but have already made up their minds that the election was rigged and that are going to maybe possibly try do the same thing in 2024? And it might not be the overt violence that we saw on January 6th, but I`m really interested in the idea that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  said that the danger continues. That this is not something that`s stagnant. That this is going to be a conspiracy that is threatening the democracy of our country.\par \par \uc2 I`m really interested in really getting a sense of sort of what this committee thinks it should do to prevent this from happening in the future. Lawmakers have said that they are looking at possibly wanting to have some sort of legislation, wanting to prevent the integrity of our democracy for being hurt even more.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 But I don`t really -- I didn`t really get a sense watching that hearing what that was going to actually look like. So as much as of course I want to hear all about -- all of the different things that have happened and all of the different ties that former President Trump potentially made to the people who carried out this violence, the fact that election deniers are still being elected and winning primaries all across this country, what that means to this democracy and what the committee found on that issue to me is going to be the thing that we`re going to want to talk about and that we`re going to have to continue to talk about.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And let me just follow up on something we were just discussing here, Yamiche, which is the electoral college loophole, this flagrant gaping loophole we talked about it briefly in another segment earlier. But as Barbara just referred to, there are these, you know, secretaries of state now banding together running with Republican legislatures who will control the electoral count in their states if they are elected.\par \par \uc2 And that is going to be the next step in 2024 if this proceeds unless that loophole is fixed before the midterm while Democrats still have some leverage presumably given the headwind they`re facing. At least more leverage. What are the chances that that is going to be fixed on Capitol Hill because there is legislation?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: There is legislation. But I think it`s important to say that Congress is really good at doing nothing and fixing the electoral college along with trying to come up with gun reform and other things and like that will be possibly impacting inflation. All of these different things that Congress is juggling, it doesn`t seem as though that this is going to rise to the level that`s going to make lawmakers say we have to do this. Especially because January 6th and our electoral college is such a partisan issue on top of all the other partisan issues.\par \par \uc2 But what you said is so critical, Andrea. Not only do we have to look at the secretaries of states and local officials, but poll watchers, people who are going to be administering the election, people that are going to be really doing the day-to-day things that at one point were nonpartisan counting the votes even. We remember back in 2020 when we had people -- supporters of President Trump coming and screaming at the people who were counting those ballots. Now those supporters want to be the people counting those ballots.\par \par \uc2 What is that going to mean for our democracy if there are votes thrown out just because they didn`t want -- the people who are counting them don`t want them to go to that candidate? That to me is the scary part of this that we`re going to have to continue to talk about.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yamiche, thanks to you, and to Frank, and to Barbara, and of course Chuck Rosenberg. Thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to -- we expect to hear from the president very shortly. He`s out in Los Angeles of course at that Summit of the Americas. And we may also be getting some more information from our correspondents on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And it`s worth noting -- oh, I`m sorry, Andrea. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No, I was just going to say the president in the midst of all this is facing, you know, terrible inflation numbers today and a lot of pushback from these heads of state and foreign ministers who are present at the summit, not happy about the migration policies, not happy about what Mexico is complaining is that the money that had been promised him from the previous administration, $5 billion for help for Mexico, has not been forthcoming or appropriated. That`s still in dispute. The fact that Cuba was not invited, Venezuela. The president has a lot on his plate.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And of course everyone knows the drama that has gone down with some of the no-shows, everything else, better than you, Andrea, our chief foreign correspondent, what`s interesting here, too, is President Biden is tackling an issue that we know from polls is more important to voters than anything else. It`s more important -- which is the economy. The cost of living. And things that affect their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And speaking of the pocketbooks and what`s really concerning a lot of people on Capitol Hill, inflation, Ali Vitali, you`ve got some more details on what`s coming up at the hearings to come next week and the week after on this issue as well.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Andrea, you`re totally right and I think the conversation you guys are having about the juggling of the different topics here on Capitol Hill, that is definitely the reality here as Republicans try to turn this conversation to inflation, Democrats are still focused on gun legislation and of course then the House January 6th Select Committee still continuing its work.\par \par \uc2 I think taking a step back after last night and hearing the way that these lawmakers laid out what they are going to be doing in these coming hearings, you have to consider each one as its own individual puzzle piece. For example on Monday, we know that there is going to be a focus on the fact that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that he lost the election and pursued a strategy of trying to overturn it anyway.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s coming on Monday. And then of course we`ve got two other hearings throughout the week, they`re going to focus in different pieces ongoing inside the Department of Justice with people who stood up to the former president, that`s according to our sources who are starting to read us in on who the witnesses are going to be that are going to tell these stories.\par \par \uc2 And then of course after that next week we`re also going to hear about Trump`s pressure on Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  using a witness who was a key adviser to Pence and who was with Pence at the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 I think the other important piece here is even as we know who these witnesses are, we saw last night just how compelling it is to have people in the former president`s orbit, they don`t even need to show up to testify. They don`t need to be witnesses. They`ve already done these depositions, whether it`s audio only or in the cases of people like \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  and \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , we have them on video and the select committee is able to use them.\par \par \uc2 So the witnesses are there to bolster the puzzle pieces and the buckets of information that the committee is trying to get at. But at the same time, they have a lot of other information. The witness list of who`s there in person is only one piece of this. They have hundreds of people who are effectively already witnesses simply because they already cooperated with the committee.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Ali Vitali, Ali, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And ladies, so we`re talking all day about the hearings and who is listening and how it will matter to Americans going forward. I`m hesitant to talk about that, though, because they matter in themselves regardless of --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Are you talking about the economy and inflation?\par \par \uc2 TUR: I`m talking about, are they paying attention to the hearings, do they care about the hearings, is the economy more important, is inflation more important? But yes, that matters. But the hearings in themselves matter regardless of the -- you`re looking at me funny.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: No. No. I`m --\par \par \uc2 TUR: The interpretation of the American public.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Because I don`t necessarily disagree with you. I think that it is still, though, a point of fact to look at what the landscape is based on what -- like, this is not -- that`s an objective --\par \par \uc2 TUR: We are talking about --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: But if we are talking about potential criminal charges for people inside this orbit, elections are going to be beside the point going forward. I mean, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is somebody that the DOJ might indict and obviously it`s might, might, might, might, might, an election is going to be beside the point. And I have to leave it here, although I know you want to say something, because President Biden is almost on time today. He is in California. Let`s go to him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I think that Sal must be related closely to my good friend from Holy Rosary High School in Claymont, Mike DiCostanzo (PH). You think I`m kidding but I`m not. I`d tell you what, anyway, thank you, Sal.\par \par \uc2 Look, everybody, before I begin, I want to say a few words very briefly about the January 6th hearings that were highlighted last night. The insurrection on January 6th is one of the darkest chapters in our nation`s history, a brutal assault on our democracy, a brutal attack on law enforcement, some losing their lives. And we heard about it last night again.\par \par \uc2 It`s important to the American people to understand what truly happened and to understand that the same forces that led January 6th remain at work today. It`s about our democracy itself. We have to protect our democracy. I know it sounds corny to say it. We`re all raised in school of democracy, every generation has to protect it.\par \par \uc2 I have to admit to you, I never thought that it was going to be this straightforward a challenge before. The rule of law matters in democracies and we`re seeing how the battle for the soul of America has been far from one. But I know together and I mean this, we can unite and defend this nation, Democrat and Republican, allow no one to place a dagger at the throat of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 That`s what those hearings are all about. You`re going to hear a lot more. I didn`t get to watch them last night because I was doing other business but I tell what, there`s a lot, lot going on.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, to the reason I`m here. You know, thank you, Sal, and thanks to Gene and the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  as well, Mario, thank you for Port Long Beach, and thank Mayor Garcetti of Los Angeles and the mayor of Long Beach, who I expected soon to be the congressman of Long Beach from the way it looks like.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 You guys did a heck of a job when I came out here a couple years ago to try to get things moving. Everybody did, management, labor, elected leaders, as well as at the time we also had Governor Newsom pushing hard as well.\par \par \uc2 Look, we have two excellent senators, Dianne Feinstein and \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 . I`d tell you what, Alex, you don`t look any worse for the wear, man. You`re looking awful good, you`re looking awful good. And Nanette, I think you`ve always looked good, particularly compared to these guys, you know what I mean? And me. But seriously, thank you, Nanette, for the fire you bring. You bring some fire to this. I really mean it. It`s in your heart.\par \par \uc2 And Alan Lowenthal has been working on this for a long time. Alan and I just had a little meeting together. We got to change it, Alan. We`ve got -- we can`t give up. We`ve got to turn this corner.\par \par \uc2 And, John, I can`t thank you. You`re the real deal. Anybody -- well, I won`t get into, you`ll get you in trouble, but thanks for sticking up for me.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 And, look, and we have a Labor secretary that -- I`m his translator. He`s \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  from Boston. And -- but if you need any -- the only thing you got to know, he always has your back. He always has your back. I had an uncle who used to say, "Joey, you`re labor from belt buckle to shoe sole." Well, he`s labor from forehead to the tip of his toes. He used to run a labor out -- union.\par \par \uc2 Look, folks, today I`d like to speak about my top economic priority, fighting inflation. I understand Americans are anxious, and they`re anxious with good reason. I was raised in a household when the price of gasoline rose precipitously, it was the discussion at the table. It made a difference when food prices went up.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve never seen anything like Putin`s tax on both food and gas. Americans should also understand our economy has unique strengths that we can build on. The job market is the strongest it`s been since World War II, notwithstanding the inflation. We added another 390,000 jobs last month. 8,700,000 new jobs since I took office. An all-time record. Never that many jobs in that period of time.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate is near historic lows. Millions of Americans are moving up to better jobs and better pay. And since I took office, families are carrying less debt on average in America. They have more savings than they`ve had. And we`re doing it all while cutting the federal deficit by $1.7 trillion this year and $320 billion last.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 If I hear one of my MAGA friends once again talk about debt and deficits, I`m going to -- anyway, I`m going to be good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 They increased the deficit by $2 trillion -- anyway, that`s another story. But, look, this is the largest decline in American history. Because of the progress, America can tackle inflation from a position of strength unlike any other country in the world, because every country in the world is getting a big bite and piece of this inflation, worse than we are in the vast majority of countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake about it. I understand inflation is a real challenge to American families. Today`s inflation report confirmed what Americans already know. Putin`s price hike is hitting America hard. Gas prices at the pump. Energy and food prices account for half of the monthly price increases since May.\par \par \uc2 Inflation, outside of energy and food -- what the economists call "core inflation" -- moderated the last two months. Not enough, but it moderated, it`s come down. And we need it to come down much more quickly. My administration is going to continue to do everything we can to lower the prices for the American people and the Congress has to act, and they have been of late.\par \par \uc2 One of the key ways to fight inflation is by lowering the cost of moving goods through the supply chain. When I first started talking about the supply chain, when I came here well over a year ago, the American people understandably wondered, supply chain, well, I mean, that`s not a usual part of their jargon every day. But they understand it fully now. They understand it.\par \par \uc2 If you can`t get the material needed to build a product you`re building, whether it`s an automobile or whatever it is, it makes it difficult to be able to move, and that`s called the supply chain. That`s why I`ve been focused on ports. Last fall, ports around the world were congested due to disruption caused by the pandemic, so we brought together port operators, shipping companies, and labor and -- to ease the bottlenecks.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, over the holidays last, 97 percent of all the packages were delivered on time and on shelves when you went Christmas shopping. Remember, we weren`t going to have anything on those shelves. You all did it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 No, no, not a joke. You did it. On time with minimal delay. Delivery times were actually quicker than they were before the pandemic. And today, there are about 40 percent fewer containers clogging the docks for long periods of time than there were last November.\par \par \uc2 This May was the strongest month in the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 `s history, the strongest month in its history.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And we`re helping fund improvements. Because of what my friends in the Congress have done, we passed the -- we passed the Infrastructure Bill and we`re funding major new initiatives on the docks, on-dock rail systems, the \uc2 Port of Long Beach\uc2 , move goods, more quickly. And port electrification so communications near the ports can be -- and communities can breathe cleaner air because they`re using electric machines that are not generating using gas, coal, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And we`re continuing to expand capacity of our ports, thanks to the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a time -- we took a different approach also to trucking. Remember that last December, we brought together industry and labor to tackle problems facing truck drivers.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220611ei6a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for June 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Mike Memoli, Blayne Alexander, Priscilla Thompson,  Dermot Shea, Cornell Belcher, Antonia Hylton, Keir Simmons, Wilfred Frost \par \uc2 8,202 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I`m Chris Jansing at MSNBC headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 And we come on the air this hour with some brand new information about yet another mass shooting as well as the president`s response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING (voiceover): We learned in just the last hour, President Biden is going to give a rare primetime address, the topic, gun violence at 7:30 Eastern tonight. It follows the 233rd mass shooting this year in the U.S. In Tulsa, Oklahoma, a gunman killing four people, two doctors, a receptionist, and a patient.\par \par \uc2 Just in the past, our officials told reporters, the shooter bought an AR- 15-style weapon just a few hours before going into the hospital. They also found a letter from the suspect making clear he came in to kill a specific doctor and blaming that doctor for the pain he felt after back surgery. The mayor detailing the horror of those minutes as police swarmed the building.\par \par \uc2 G. T. BYNUM, MAYOR OF TULSA, OKLAHOMA: I spoke with an officer yesterday, who`s a seasoned veteran of the department. And he was one of the first people to get here. And he said, mayor, it was like the beaches of Normandy out there. Everywhere I look to my left and my right, there were officers running towards that building jumping over bushes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And when they got there, police say it was a catastrophic scene. And yet again, it is umping up the pressure on the White House and President Biden. But will it move Congress to action? Democrat \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  who is driving gun legislation talks in the Senate right now was asked about the chances of getting something done on MORNING JOE today, specifically on getting 10 Republican votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2 , (D-CT): I cannot say I have confidence. And I`m confident in our negotiators. And the question then is, are there enough votes in the Senate to get this -- to get this passed? I don`t know yet. We`re on recess. We`ve -- already the negotiators have been working around the clock this week. But we`ve got to get back to Washington next week and see if there are enough votes on the Republican side to get this done.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ahead, we`ll get an update with one of his senate colleagues, Democrat Chris Coons of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All of this right now leading to one big unanswered political question, whether those polls that show a major majority of Americans back gun reform will actually move the needle for one side or the other in the November midterms, or will it be instead, the baby formula shortage, especially after the president, his staff and manufacturers appear to contradict each other over when the administration knew this crisis was coming? Or maybe the November elections will be about rising prices that every American family is seeing. We did just break the record for high gas prices again today, for the fourth day in a row.\par \par \uc2 But let`s start with that breaking news. President Biden`s planned primetime address tonight we just learned about it. Mike Memoli was the first reporter to break this story. So, Mike, what do we know about how this all came together, and do we have any idea what the President plans to say at this point?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, really there had been two questions around the White House in the wake of that horrible shooting in Uvalde, Texas. One, will the president issue more executive actions? Is there more in the toolkit that he can take in terms of trying to deal with this gun crisis? Or secondly, how much will the president lend his voice? Will he get involved with some of those negotiations happening on Capitol Hill about a legislative solution to this? And now we get the answer in the form of this announcement from the White House, which we got ahead of earlier.\par \par \uc2 The President delivering remarks tonight as the White House puts it to the American people on the recent incidents of gun violence in this country, but also to urge what they`re calling common-sense actions -- legislation on Capitol Hill to try to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s really clear, as we`ve seen the president over the course of his time in office so far have to deal with a number of gun incidents in which he`s made calls for this kind of legislation that the unfortunate shooting in Uvalde, Texas, 19 innocent children, two teachers, has galvanized the country in a way that we haven`t really seen since the Sandy Hook massacre in 2012.\par \par \uc2 And 10 years ago, it was Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who was asked by President \uc2 Obama\uc2  to take the lead in trying to find executive actions and other solutions that might be able to try to remedy this while there was an effort on Capitol Hill to do the same.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re seeing a President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who has White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  -- excuse me \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  yesterday, but it has done everything more than any previous president in the realm of executive actions, now urging Congress to do so the same.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s interesting to see -- you know, one of the questions, Chris, is whether at a speech like this will help or hurt those very sensitive negotiations happening on Capitol Hill. We know that they`ve been happening behind closed doors, and the White House has been involved at a staff level in those discussions.\par \par \uc2 But clearly, as the president put it the night he returned to Washington and addressed the country about the Uvalde shooting, he asked, where is the backbone? And this is a moment that the president really -- the White House feels is fit for the president to really raise the stakes to speak directly to the American people about really what he has called an intolerable situation for this country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike, thank you so much for that. We`re going to see you back here a little later this hour. But we mentioned 233 mass shootings just this year, just in the United States. So let`s go to Tulsa and that update from investigators that just wrapped up in the past hour. NBC`s Blaine Alexander is live for us. So, Blaine, this was an extraordinarily detailed as well as harrowing account, we learned a lot. Tell us about the timeline of what happened here. What else you can tell us?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, Chris, what`s happening right now we really are getting a stunning picture of just what unfolded here in Tulsa yesterday afternoon. You know, we heard from news officials or from police officials, rather, speaking very candidly talking about the fact that this was a targeted shooting. This was an individual who had had back surgery from a doctor, Dr. Preston Phillips here on the campus of St. Francis.\par \par \uc2 And he essentially blamed him for his subsequent back pain. So that he had called the office a number of times. I asked hospital administrators, was the doctor responsive? And he said, absolutely. They scheduled a follow-up with this patient. He came in and saw the doctor the day before, but he still said that he was in pain and still blamed the doctor.\par \par \uc2 A couple of stunning details that come out of this, Chris. One, we understand that the two weapons that were used in this crime were purchased just within the week. In fact, the rifle, the AR-15 style rifle as police told us, was purchased just a couple of hours before that shooting unfolded. Both of them were purchased legally one of them at a gun store the other at a pawn shop.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning that this individual had a letter on him essentially spelled out the reason that he was coming in, armed with two guns, and said that he planned to not only shoot and target and kill Dr. Phillips but "anybody else who stood in his way." And to that point, the police officer -- the police chief, rather said explicitly, that`s exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 There were a total of four people were killed. One of them was the doctor, Dr. Phillips, who performed that surgery. The other was another physician there in the orthopedic clinic, along with a receptionist and another patient. And the chief said that all of them were in some way in that gunman`s way.\par \par \uc2 I`m told that at one point, one of the victims was actually holding the door so that somebody else could escape. That person who was holding the door was shot and killed. So this really is just a stunning picture of what all unfolded here pm Tulsa and police say, Chris, that they`re not done with their investigation just yet.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I always hear stories of heroism in these situations. It`s tragic that in being a hero, they`ve lost their own lives. Blayne, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`re also getting some new details about what the mayor of Uvalde, Texas was doing during last week`s school shooting. He told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  that as soon as he heard about the shooting, he rushed to the area and then he says he stayed with an official, he didn`t name them, but he called them the negotiator who is trying to reach the gunman by cell phone from a funeral home across the street. Here`s what the mayor told Telemundo.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON MCLAUGHLIN, MAYOR OF UVALDE, TEXAS: Let me be clear, I am not law enforcement. The only person I had communicated with the negotiator was trying to get the shooter on the phone and so forth. I was in the room. No, he wouldn`t answer. They were trying to be childish. Every number they can find --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Priscilla Thompson is on the ground in Uvalde. I also want to bring in \uc2 Dermot Shea\uc2 , former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Commissioner now an MSNBC Law Enforcement Analyst, and if I can start. With the sound, we just heard from the mayor. And I`m wondering in a situation like that is negotiation a prime priority or given the fact that we know that there was firing going on, given the fact that calls were made to 911 where there was clearly an active shooter, what would be a role a negotiator play?\par \par \uc2 DERMOT SHEA, MSNBC LAW ENFORCEMENT ANALYST: Well, I think it speaks to the chaotic nature of the event. In any situation like this, you want to have command and it starts at the top with an incident commander controlling multiple agencies, not all law enforcement. This is why training and exercises are so important.\par \par \uc2 And when one of the tools in the toolbox for that day would be to have a negotiator immediately respond to the scene, have them available if the circumstances dictate that the use would be prudent. As you said, in a case like this with the violence that`s imminent and ongoing, that`s not one where negotiated typically would be used. You have to separate yourself and take a step back here.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We have a pre-planned horrible premeditated incident where they`re coming with tactical gear, bulletproof vests, watching videos, and streaming it. So the likelihood of a person willing to negotiate with a negotiator, I would put is very small in a situation like this. But from the law enforcement perspective, you have to be ready in case the incident does slow down. And that becomes something that you can do.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: When we heard the Tulsa police officials talking today, just an hour or so ago, it was such a stark contrast to what we saw unfold in Uvalde. The Tulsa police chief was asked if his officers didn`t go in as quickly as they did, would the gunman have kept firing, here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WENDELL FRANKLIN, TULSA POLICE CHIEF: In our mindset, we believe that we have to stop that threat and we have to do it immediately. And time is of the essence. And so we are trained to go in there and stop the threat regardless of what may happen to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Regardless of what may happen to us, we are trained to go in and respond to the threat. When you look back on those two shootings back to back different ways police responded isn`t in many ways, a textbook for other police around the country, a kind of affirmation for why the training is a certain way, you go in you neutralize the shooter?\par \par \uc2 SHEA: And the tragic -- tragedy of this is this is something that we`re seeing more and more, and it`s becoming common across the country. And this has become part and parcel with law enforcement textbooks and training across the country. Unfortunately, not something that you saw with the prevalence that you see it today where people are armed to the teeth going into locations with one thing in mind.\par \par \uc2 So, because of that, law enforcement has changed their mindset of th surround, put up parameters, and slowly incident down. They know full well these days that when active shooting is going on that the number one thing you have to do is address that threat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Priscilla, in Uvalde today, more visitations more memorials. What is the latest on that and what else can you tell us about any progress you`re hearing about investigators piecing together the response to the shooter that day?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Chris, today the lives of Eliahana Torres, Miranda Mathis, and Nevaeh Bravo are being remembered. There are also visitations being held for several of their classmates today. And those services are happening not far from here as people continue to gather at this memorial to grieve.\par \par \uc2 And just a few moments ago, state senator Roland Gutierrez actually began speaking just across the courtyard from where I am, and he is expected to provide an update if he has any new information to share about this investigation. And that, of course, as there are still many questions about what exactly happened here.\par \par \uc2 Just this week alone, we have received new information, again, sort of contradicting some of what was originally said by officials specifically about whether or not a door had been propped open by a teacher. Officials are now saying that that teacher closed that door but that it did not lock.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, questions too, about the level of cooperation that the police chief -- the school police chief has been providing with the department of public safety officials saying that he has not responded to a request for a follow-up interview while the police chief yesterday saying that he has been in touch with DPS officials every day. And so still a lot of unanswered questions.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that the state senator is talking about and actually started off speaking about today is about this special legislative committee that the governor and the lieutenant governor have now convened. That committee has 11 senators -- state senators on it, eight Republicans, three Democrats, and state senator Gutierrez not on that list. He is calling this committee meaningless and a "slap in the face." Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Priscilla, Dermot, thanks to both of you. Mass shootings, of course, often get all the attention but children are shot in the United States every day. Sandy Hook Promise, an organization created in the wake of the Newtown school shooting notes that each day, 12 children die from gun violence in America, and another 32 are shot and injured.\par \par \uc2 One of those victims we want you to know about is 12-year-old, Adrian Hawkins-McDougles, an honor roll student with an infectious smile. He was shot and killed in Jackson, Mississippi last Thursday, just hours after his sixth-grade graduation.\par \par \uc2 He had been walking with his older brother to his grandmother`s house to get some batteries for a video game controller. Adrian died on the sidewalk. His brother was also shot but he survived. Their mother had just moved the family out of Jackson for their own safety a few weeks ago because she said she would hear gunshots daily.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 They were only back in town for Adrian`s graduation. His grandmother says she would -- that he would end every conversation with a signature farewell. "I love you be safe." So what do we do to solve our nation`s gun violence problems? How do we fix it, or at least try? We`re going to head to Capitol Hill for new NBC reporting on the state of negotiations among that key group of senators.\par \par \uc2 And what kind of framework could win over skeptical Republicans? I`ll ask Democrat from Delaware, Senator Chris Coons, next. And later, the bed Platinum Jubilee from carriages to Corby`s to Buckingham Palace is fly by fit for Queen. We`re live in London with the latest on celebrations marking Elizabeth`s 70 years on the throne. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We are getting new details about what`s being discussed in those bipartisan negotiations on gun reform in the Senate. Here`s what Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  who`s leading the charge told MORNING JOE about the conversations involving assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We are having a conversation about what to do about the fact that a lot of 18 to 21-year-olds are getting their hands on these complicated weapons. So I can`t tell you, yet you know whether we`ll have any agreement there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And a source tells NBC News Senate negotiators to have a framework for gun legislation. Joining me now, Senator Chris Coons, Democrat from Delaware, thanks for being with us. In that same interview, the senator also said flat out he cannot say he`s confident the votes are there to pass a plan.\par \par \uc2 And I know you`ve been a skeptic in the past. This is what you told NPR after the Uvalde shooting. "This is a bad day for anything even vaguely looking like hope or optimism around legislative process or progress. I usually want to be more optimistic, but I don`t think it`ll change. A week later, assuming some conversations watching what`s happening with \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 . Are you any more optimistic?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS, (D-DE): Chris, I have had a series of conversations with colleagues, both Democrats, and Republicans, and I`m more optimistic today. Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  of Connecticut has been a great leader in this effort in my caucus and Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas is engaging in a proactive and thoughtful way. There`s a lot of different ideas that are being exchanged between small groups of senators.\par \par \uc2 I think any one of these ideas would make some progress in terms of making our country safer, and helping us make it harder for folks who would do harm to others to get their hands on guns, in particular, prevent things such as what tragically happened in Uvalde, or in Sandy Hook or in Parkland.\par \par \uc2 Senator Dick Blumenthal of Connecticut and Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina have been championing a so-called Red Flag Law or an emergency risk protection order, that with due process protections would enable local police to remove someone`s gun -- someone`s guns for a period of time after they`ve made threats or engaged in a publicly disturbing threatening behavior.\par \par \uc2 That`s something that`s been seriously discussed. And that in part because of the fact pattern around several of these mass shootings, I think he`s getting serious consideration. Raising the age at which an 18-year-old can walk into a gun store and buy an AR-15 is also something that deserves serious discussion.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin and Senator Toomey after Sandy Hook worked hard to bring forward a strengthening of the background check system. And I also think the ideas that they have, and their engagement is positive. So, Chris, my short answer would be yes, a week later, I am more optimistic. But it will still be very difficult to get 10 Republican senators to come to the table and to work with a bipartisan group that is going to try and get legislation to President Biden`s desk.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me ask you a couple of questions about strategically how we get there. And let`s start with tonight, the President has decided to do this primetime address. Most Americans we already know, support most of the things that you`ve talked about, you know, from background checks down to limiting these AR-style rifles -- AR-15-style rifles. Is there something you think the president can say that can sway members, not just of the public who already are with him on this large but members of Congress making that connection somehow? What can he say tonight that might move the needle? Or is that unrealistic?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, if we can`t expect that the President alone will change the political reality on the ground, that in far too many states, you`ve got folks who are resistant to the idea of improving gun safety, of making significant investments in mental health and community based mental health and tying that to making it harder to get access to a weapon for those who`ve got a conviction or a background that makes them a person prohibited.\par \par \uc2 I do think President Biden`s focus and attention on this is positive. And he gets a long history of engagement as a senator and vice president and now his actions as president are important. And the way in which he went to Uvalde and witnessed to or comforted the parents who had lost children and was present with that community gives him just another stage of credibility that comes with having been a compassionate public leader in our community, in our country for decades.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, members of the Senate, a body in which he served for 36 years, are going to have to make some hard choices to put the majority of their constituents ahead of a voting record or some partisan interests. I am, as I said, encouraged that Senator Cornyn is at the table with Senator Murphy and the preliminary conversations have been positive.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s great, President Biden is going to remind the American people of the human cost. Just a few moments ago, Chris, you put up a graphic that reminded us that well, what gets our attention is these mass casualty incidents, where a dozen or two dozen or more people are killed in one incident, that every single day, handguns, in particular, take the lives of children, as many as a dozen a day all over the United States.\par \par \uc2 That is a tragic number that we should not be comfortable with. There have to be more ways that we can improve gun safety, that we can address community mental health challenges, and that we can make our country one that truly values the lives of our children.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you very quickly the, again, the political reality of this. Yesterday I had the mayor of Buffalo on and he talked about a congressman who used to be on the Buffalo city council, who in fact, has done a complete turnaround on his position on guns after what happened in Buffalo after that racist shooting.\par \par \uc2 But he also said the reality is he`ll probably get primary now. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  will support somebody to run against him. How do you fight back against that reality? And again, I don`t have much time. But is there a way to address it?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Chris, the whole underlying premise of democracy is that reality changes when more people in his district in Buffalo come forward and say we`re proud of you following this horrific shooting at a buffalo grocery store. We to your constituents want to come out and support someone who is willing to be brave and change their position and vote for responsible gun safety reforms. That`s how change happens.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Senator Chris Coons, appreciate your time today. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And coming up, President Biden announcing a new shipment of baby formula now on the way to the U.S. Why though there`s new controversy about who knew what and when about that shortage? How that, and high prices at the pump could hurt Democrats at the polls this November if those issues don`t get fixed? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:54]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some more breaking news this hour. In just the last few minutes former Trump attorney general Bill Barr was spotted leaving a conference room used by the January 6th Committee. There you see the video. He did not respond to requests to come to talk to a pool camera there.\par \par \uc2 Just to remind you, the committee`s public hearings start a week from today. That is on June 9th.\par \par \uc2 Also today, word from the White House that more help is on the way to ease that baby formula shortage. President Biden says new shipments of the imported formula should be available in Target stores in the coming weeks. But new questions have been raised about why the president didn`t know about the crisis until months after his staff says they were already working on it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I became aware of this problem sometime in -- after April -- in early April about how intense it was. And so we did everything in our power from that point on and that`s all I can tell you right now, and we`re going to continue to do it until we get the job done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So April for the president but baby formula executives who were meeting with Biden yesterday said they knew right away after the factory shut down in February just how bad that shortage could get.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now NBC News White House correspondent Mike Memoli, and then, the press secretary, as you know, Mike, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , said the White House had been working on this for months. Where is the disconnect?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, Chris, I think what yesterday`s event at the White House really brought to life was that great reporting from my colleagues in the White House unit about the frustration the president has felt about the messaging at the White House but also the fact that he felt he should have been informed sooner about this infant formula crisis because we saw over the course of more than an hour the president asking these CEOs who have been invited to participate if they knew immediately that this shutdown of an Abbott factory in Michigan was going to create the kind of supply crisis that we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 And to a person, each of those CEOs said we knew immediately this was going to be a serious moment. So the president really I think revealing his frustration that he was not informed of this sooner. And this crisis really illustrates the question that any White House has to deal with, Chris, which is -- especially for this one with so many different challenges, crises competing for the president`s time and attention how are decisions made about what gets elevated to a presidential level for action.\par \par \uc2 And clearly, in this case, as much as the White House is now trying to make it clear that they`re on top of this and doing everything they can to deal with it, something as Biden likes to say was missed between the cup and the lip in the months preceding when the president himself was informed about this in terms of dealing with this crisis to try to head it off and take more actions sooner. And ironically, Chris, this was an event at the White House yesterday that was designed to show the White House is on top of the crisis but it`s only really deepened the questions about when they knew what they knew and whether they should have done more sooner.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Cornell Belcher, pollster and Democratic strategist and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of Democrats that I`ve talked to, they`re concerned about what they saw yesterday, Cornell, and I`m wondering if it`s yours is that this raises questions of competence.\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, look, I mean, to a certain extent the president is being asked to deal with a lot of things that quite frankly from -- that the president really doesn`t have a great control over. I mean, even if you look at inflation which he does get the blame for.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But let`s be fair. His -- the whole point, as Mike just said, of having that yesterday was to say I do have some control, I can help make this better. If not completely fix it, I can help make this better. Was something missed if his staff knew about it but he didn`t know about it, if they were working on it but he wasn`t told about it for a couple months?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, I`ll stick to my original point. And that is, look, yes. I mean, as the president rightfully -- this president rightfully takes is all the buck stops with him, right. If something goes wrong he takes responsibility for it. But at the same time, when have we ever had a president responsible for baby formula? And if you look at inflation, which quite frankly, you know, the public blaming him inflation for, look, Chris, we have had this conversation before.\par \par \uc2 You`ve had this conversation plenty of times on your broadcast before. You know, there`s not a lot the president can actually do with these big market and international events that are actually driving sort of inflation.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 So, yes, the president has to take, you know, take responsibility for it and try to fix it, but going into the midterm, he also has to pivot and put out a vision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s talk about that vision because is there any doubt in your mind, Cornell, that these are these things whether it`s the shortage of formula, it`s today`s record gas prices again, it`s when people go to the grocery store and they see how much milk and eggs cost, ultimately that is what Americans are going to vote on no matter how much of it is under the president`s control.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes and no, Chris. I mean, here`s -- that`s kind of myopic from a political campaign standpoint because for one, Chris, the economy is always a top issue. Going back from when pollsters first started asking what`s your top issue, the economy is always the top issue. But you got to show a vision. Look. If you look at the -- first, set it up. You know, the historic dynamics here that the Democrats lose seats. Right. The president`s first term, their party tends to lose seats.\par \par \uc2 If you look at going into 2002 with George Bush the economy was also to begin to tank and what did George Bush talked about? He didn`t talk about the economy. He dominated the conversation about national security. To a certain extent, and I saw your other -- your previous story where you talk about gas prices, and hopefully that`ll be fixed, Chris, gas prices aren`t going to be fixed by the time the election is coming up.\par \par \uc2 So the Democrats have to in fact make the election about something larger. It can`t be a referendum simply on the economy. I worked for a guy, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , in 2008 where the economy was absolutely cratering and we made the election about hope and change. The interesting thing about, from your conservation with the senator, is Senate Democrats think they have some framework on gun reform.\par \par \uc2 Gun reform is now becoming a dominant issue. Two and three independents right now want stricter gun reform laws and you take that along with abortion. I think where women overwhelmingly don`t want Roe v. Wade overturned and is growing in NBC polling as a voting issue there are opportunities here to have a deeper conversation about some of these other issues and not simply about the price of gas.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I can promise in the next couple of months, we will dive deeper into the importance yet again of suburban women in the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 Cornell Belcher, always good to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And right now a racially charged controversy in Mississippi has the school district`s first black superintendent on leave. Up next, some employees claims of hostile work environment and how she says she just wanted to try to make sure kids of every race and every background got the same opportunities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. KENITRA EZI, SUPERINTENDENT PLACED ON LEAVE LESS THAN A YEAR AFTER BEING HIRED: Students at Bay Springs would take drug education whereas students at Stringer would take theater.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:56]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vice President Harris now addressing the Tulsa shooting. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- of course is that four innocent people lost their lives and many more were injured. We of course all of us hold the people of Tulsa in our hearts but we also reaffirm our commitment to passing commonsense gun safety laws.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And I don`t have to tell anybody in this room but President Biden has taken more executive action to combat gun violence than any other president at this point in their administration. But we cannot as an administration or those of us who are here address this alone. No more excuses. Thoughts and prayers are important but not enough. We need Congress to act. And so with that I will turn to the topic at hand but I know that we all feel similarly about the importance of leaders leading on issues like this. So today, today, we are here to make a milestone --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vice President Harris, this is a preplanned set of remarks at the Education Department about the cancelation of almost $6 billion in debt at Corinthian Colleges, but giving us perhaps a very brief preview of what we expect to hear from the president tonight who if you missed it earlier at 7:30 Eastern Time, and you can watch it here on MSNBC, is going to give a primetime address on gun violence. So make sure you want to be tuned for that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime we have some exclusive reporting for you on that racially charged controversy in a rural Mississippi school district after the district`s first black superintendent was suspended. Dr. Kenitra Ezi was on the job for less than a year when she was put on leave and replaced by a white acting superintendent.\par \par \uc2 Devastated students believed Dr. Ezi was targeted for trying to fix long existing disparities in their district when it comes to race.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Antonia Hylton has our report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Nadasha Jones is a straight A student at Mississippi`s Bay Springs High. Lately she and her friends have become activists, questioning the school board`s decision to place their district`s first black superintendent, Dr. Kenitra Ezi, on leave less than a year after she was hired.\par \par \uc2 NADASHA JONES, BAY SPRINGS HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT: The only thing that was ever surprising to me was the fact that we ever got a black superintendent that brung out all the disparities, all the discrimination that has been going on.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (on-camera): Was it important to black students to see the first- ever black superintendent?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, it was very.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): According to Dr. Ezi trouble began soon after she took the reins of the rural Mississippi school district she describes as separate but unequal. The district`s gifted and talented programming is at the predominantly white Stringer K-12 campus. The remedial programming at predominantly black Bay Springs schools.\par \par \uc2 EZI: Students at Bay Springs would take drug education whereas students at Stringer would take theater.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: And Dr. Ezi was concerned about the condition of Bay Springs facilities. There was no AC in the school`s gym for almost seven years.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ezi says she wanted to make Bay Springs equal to Stringer in building and program quality. She proposed placing a new district wide pre-K program in Bay Springs. Stringer parents objected, calling the 10 to 20-minute drive too far. One parent sent Dr. Ezi an anonymous letter.\par \par \uc2 EZI: The parent told me that I was being watched very, very closely. That I had no right to try to make changes.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (on-camera): When you look back at that letter now, was it prophetic?\par \par \uc2 EZI: It was absolutely. When I was hired I was told don`t poke the bear in Stringer. Leave Stringer alone.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Then on March 3rd the school board called a special meeting. Multiple white employees spoke out against Dr. Ezi. Some saying she created a hostile work environment. Black employees spoke in her support.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Is this about race?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 EZI: I didn`t want this to be about race but the facts are about 20 white employees came forth with concerns about the first black superintendent in the district.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): The board voted to place Dr. Ezi on paid leave pending termination. She says within minutes her e-mail was deactivated and the next morning her office had new locks.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Hi, my name is Antonia Hylton. I`m with NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): NBC News placed numerous calls and e-mails to district and state officials. All refused to provide any comment for our story. One Stringer parent spoke to us outside the local post office.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Are you all surprised that it looks like she`s losing her job less than a year into it?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY WOOD, RESIDENT, STRINGER, MISSISSIPPI: Yes. And I think she was -- seems like from what I hear is causing problems as soon as she come into it.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Do you think this would be happening if she were a white man?\par \par \uc2 WOOD: No. I don`t know. I think you need to get off the racism thing and just get along and do your job.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): Dr. Ezi is still waiting for a hearing. She`s been replaced by a white acting superintendent. Student activist Nadasha still hopes Dr. Ezi will be reinstated but with every passing week doubts grow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We have to fight so the people that come after us don`t have to go through the same thing we`re going to through.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Antonia Hylton is here with me now. So she`s waiting for a hearing. What else can you tell us about what`s happened to her since she was put on leave?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: That`s right. She was placed on leave in March and it`s now June and she says not only has not received information about a hearing but she hasn`t actually received a form or a letter letting her know why the district wants to remove her. She doesn`t even know the reasons why she is potentially being fired. She says over the last several weeks she`s felt intense hostility at times.\par \par \uc2 She went to a baseball game in her school`s district and was asked to leave by the Stringer principal. Asked to leave part of the premises there at the game in front of students that she had served, colleagues, and she`s described feeling really hurt and humiliated and she just wants answers now about what`s going to happen with her employment and if she`ll ever get to serve her students again, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Antonia, I know you`re going to keep us posted on this. It`s extraordinary and concerning story. Thank you so much as always for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 And then we have the Queen`s platinum jubilee. For the first time in history a British monarch celebrating an unprecedented 70-year reign. We`re live in London with a look at how Britain is now honoring Queen Elizabeth II and looking ahead to what the future of the monarchy looks like, next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:39]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now we have begun a four-day stretch of pageantry, parties and pomp and circumstance in England. And it`s all brought a huge smile to the face of Queen Elizabeth. It is her platinum jubilee and she`s surrounded by her family on that famous balcony today, watching planes of the \uc2 Royal Air Force\uc2  fly past while the crowds cheered below, packing streets all around Buckingham Palace and beyond.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Keir Simmons is on the ground there in London. Wilfred Frost, Sky News presenter and MSNBC contributor, is here with me.\par \par \uc2 Keir, I`ll tell you, you guys across the pond know how to throw a party.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I thought the Queen`s face said it all. She was smiling. I though she looked great. Talk a little bit about what it was like there and how significant this really is to the Queen and her family.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Yes, Chris, I mean, the Queen looked as if she was loving it. Prince Louis not so much necessarily. If anyone saw the little one who is fifth in line to the throne, clearly has to do some work before he really enjoys a flyover. So yes, that was a magical moment really. And one of the things about the royal family and the way that they get it so right is we have all the pomp and ceremony, everything is on time, you have that ocean of red and gold marching bands.\par \par \uc2 The whole thing is just so well done. And then you have Prince Louis kind of playing up a little bit. But watch closely because you don`t see Kate or his great grandmother, the Queen, scold him. They know that people enjoy seeing that, that`s part of the story. And they know also that just in terms of, you know, as they say history rhymes, they know that we`ve seen that again and again on that balcony in generations past.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a very sophisticated message that they`re sending. And I say sophisticated because of course today was all about celebrating the Queen and huge numbers of people came here on to the area behind me to celebrate Her Majesty. But it was also about I think sending a message about a new era. We`ve seen Prince Charles front and center in a way that he hasn`t been in the past, standing in for the Queen for Trooping the Colour. And then standing by the Queen on the balcony there.\par \par \uc2 So they are very, very good at managing their message, the royal family. And that`s what you saw today, just gently showing us that the Queen is still there, matriarch, monarch. But that they are showing us the future. I think Kate looked incredibly glamorous and fantastic and is a really good kind of beacon for the royal family, if you like. And again, how you manage to pull that off whilst your children are there and one of them is being a bit naughty, that`s impressive.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: She was cool as a cucumber. And by the way, when doesn`t she look actually spectacular. But that`s a story for another day.\par \par \uc2 Wilfred, look, a lot of folk that I know who live in the U.K., they love to hate the royals. But then you have something like this, the history of it, what it has meant, not just for that country but folks around the world. We know almost every American president in their lifetime has met with her. So talk about what you saw in terms of the crowds there and the excitement there.\par \par \uc2 WILFRED FROST, SKY NEWS PRESENTER: Well, it`s interesting you mentioned that because even the most ardent Republican in the U.K. has to love the fact that you get a free four-day weekend.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 FROST: The pub hours are extended. And I think that`s a good starting point to bring people along with you. But this isn`t just about the Queen giving the nation an excuse to celebrate, an excuse to party. Maybe there was more of that 10 years ago and 20 years ago, the last two jubilees, but I think why are we seeing such big crowds turn out and expected to do so all through the weekend is this time around it`s almost turned on its head.\par \par \uc2 It`s almost like the people want to show to the Queen how grateful we are to her. And there`s that individual aspect that she carries that`s so powerful. She`s carried out this job for 70 years. The nation is grateful to her for it even if you`re not necessarily a believer in the royal families institution forever. And I think people just want to come out and show that respect they have for her, you know, while we still have a chance to do so.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Among those not in the family photo, Harry and Megan, but they are there, right, the American and the prince?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 FROST: Absolutely. And they`ll be there at the service tomorrow. You know, I think the Queen threaded a very tricky needle today to have this official moment where they`re not current working royals so they weren`t there on that first balcony photo, but I think we`ll see a lot more of them throughout the weekend, which is only right and proper.\par \par \uc2 And it was great to see some of those pictures come out already of them watching the Trooping of the Colour, and seemingly getting on incredibly well with some of their extended members of the family. And who knows, maybe this is a weekend where the Queen can help try and repair some of those relationships they have with closer members of their immediate family. That remains to be seen. But I guess it`s a chance for that to possibly happen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wilfred Frost, great to have you here in New York.\par \par \uc2 Keir, always great to see you in London. Take care of that voice. I know you`ve got three more days of coverage out there.\par \par \uc2 And be sure to tune in to Keir`s new podcast because it`s out today and it`s called "Born to Rule: When Charles is King." Seach "Born to Rule" on \uc2 Apple\uc2  podcast or just open the camera on your phone and point it at the QR code that you see on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC. But stay tuned, "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur. Another mass shooting, another, quote, "crazy person with an AR-15." Four more innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220616ei620002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for May 31, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Carol Lee, Ashley Parker, Shaquille Brewster, Sam  Brock, Pete Williams, Richard Engel \par \uc2 8,383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I`m Chris Jansing at MSNBC World Headquarters in New York today.\par \par \uc2 Two families in Uvalde, Texas are holding visiting hours, each preparing to bury their 10-year-old daughter, with 19 more funerals to come over the next two devastating weeks. As flowers pour into Uvalde and the community`s grief spills out, the pressure could not be any more intense right now on Congress and the president to get something done on guns. And let`s remind you of the personal promise President Biden made to folks chanting do something while he was in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will. We will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We will. But the reality is we don`t know whether that will happen. And in fact, after landing back in Washington, President Biden himself acknowledged his own power here is limited.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There`s a Constitution. I can`t dictate this stuff. I can do the things I`ve done and any executive action I can take, I`ll continue to take. But I can`t outlaw a weapon. I can`t you know, change the background checks. I can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The best hope right now a bipartisan Senate working group is diving in with a source telling NBC News that the group feels a new urgency. They have met twice in person continue to text and e-mail and we`ll meet again virtually tomorrow. But there may be one key voice standing in the way, the top Republican in the Senate, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . As one of our guest`s latest headlines puts it, he`s behind a decades-long effort to block gun control.\par \par \uc2 So what is really on the table? Let`s dive right in with NBC News. White House Correspondent Carol Lee, Washington Post`s White House bureau chief Ashley Parker is also MSNBC senior political analyst. Thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Carol, you are one of the reporters who have cheaply reported a story with the headline inside of Biden White House adrift and it points specifically to the Texas shooting. As a horrific reminder, the President has not successfully pushed Congress to pass legislation on gun violence. So what`s the plan inside the White House right now? How involved will President Biden himself be?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, we`re hearing from the president today, he was asked in the Oval Office was meeting with the leader of New Zealand, whether he would meet with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on gun control measures, and he said that he will be meeting with members of Congress about this.\par \par \uc2 Now that is significant because the president just yesterday said that he didn`t -- that that wasn`t happening that he has not been part of these negotiations. There was been a bipartisan group in the Senate, as you noted. And the president also said yesterday that he feels as though everybody is becoming on her -- his words more rational so expressing some hope that something might actually get done, that remains to be seen. But the President today is saying that he`s ready to step in and do some negotiating, presumably if he`s going to be meeting with lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Now, while the president was in Texas, he talked about that, too. And he said that he -- you know, the pain that he was seeing there was palpable. He said that some 250 people were waiting there to meet with him. And he met with each of them over the course of four -- of four hours. And again, remarking on this. Take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There`s an expression by an Irish post is, too long of suffering makes a stone of the heart. Oh, there`s an awful lot of suffering. We`ve been -- I`ve gotten to more mass shooting after mass than I think any president in American history, unfortunately. And it`s just so much of it is -- much of it is preventable. And the devastation is amazing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: So there are a number of things according to our reporting, Chris, that are frustrating the president right now, but this is really a top among them is gun violence. As you heard him there say he`s been to so many of these places that have been, the place of mass of a mass shooting, and met with so many families of victims. And this is something that it seems now he`ll be ready to lean into, at least when it comes to Congress because as he has said, and everybody knows, there`s a very limited thing that he can do executively.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ashley, we know that there are these meetings on the Hill between Democratic and Republican senators or at least conversations from folks who normally are on the Hill. Your latest reporting, as we mentioned, is the detailed look at Minority Leader McConnell`s long history of blocking gun legislation. It goes all the way back to the 80s. Is there any reason to expect anything different now?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the thing that`s important to understand about Leader McConnell is that he almost always is reflecting the will of the Republican senators in his conference. He is actually -- he`s a believer in the Second Amendment, but in talking to people in his orbit, he is trying to protect his members from taking hard votes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And his pattern is often, if members are under some pressure, often, as we`re seeing here in the wake of a mass shooting, he may suggest some sort of moderate incremental measures to release the pressure on his members. Chris, there`s an openness to sometimes those things will get through more often than not, the momentum will somehow be lost and nothing will happen.\par \par \uc2 But again, I do think this fits this pattern where if you even listened to what they are talking about on Capitol Hill, these are not the broad legislative measures that gun control advocates say would really make a tremendous difference. Although on the other hand, everyone says this would be a start. They have done so little in 10 years that even something like red flag laws would be a start and what -- you know, Democrats and gun control advocates hope to be the beginnings of a movement.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, it was interesting, Ashley, because yesterday, the president said he considers McConnell a rational Republican who could compromise on the issue of guns. Let me play that for you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think there`s a realization on the part of rational Republicans, and I consider McConnell a rational Republican. I think Cornyn as well. I think there`s recognition on their party that they may -- they can`t continue like this. They can`t do this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I think there are a lot of Democrats, certainly progressive activists in the party who would disagree with that issue well, no, Ashley? But let me tell you something we just got in here, which is that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is home. In his home state, he`s been talking to constituents, and that he did not bring up anything, no commentary on the early stages of bipartisan gun safety reform conversations, instead talked about the divide on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Even mentioning the divide, does that tell you anything? The fact that he didn`t bring up guns, given how much conversation there is a car -- across the country telling you anything? Should we read anything into it at all?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: No. Again, this fits a pattern going back to 1989 when there was a mass shooting back when mass shootings were not actually part of the fabric of our society. In Kentucky, where Leader McConnell will express, you know, his deep concern over the tragedy, but then really not talked about guns or the issue. And going back to that clip you played of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 He`s right that McConnell is a rational Republican in the sense that as a leader and someone who wants to continue to be leader, wants to be majority leader should Republicans likely win in November, he`s rational and that he`s doing exactly what Republicans want. If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  means that he`s rational, and he will be swayed by a Democratic president whose approval ratings are incredibly low, no, he will not because that would not be rational from Mitch McConnell`s point of view.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ashley Parker, as always, thank you, great reporting as usual. Carol, stick around.\par \par \uc2 But for now, I want to go to Uvalde, Texas, where those first funerals for the victims of the shooting at Robb Elementary School are starting to happen. NBC`s Shaquille Brewster, as well as, Sam Brock are both there for us. Guys, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Look, Shaq, I don`t want to lose sight amid all this conversation about politics and even the investigation. I don`t want to lose sight of what was lost here. And two little girls are the first of 21 funerals that are going to be held in that community. Can you tell us more about that? And I also know you`ve had conversations with many members of the community.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and something that`s abundantly clear when you talk to the members of the community is how much this is shattering the community, how much grief it`s causing, not just people here in Uvalde, but people all across the state. I just met a couple that came from Mission, Texas said of about a four and a half-hour drive to come in. They just did that to lay flowers and share their grief with the family.\par \par \uc2 But you mentioned we are now in the period where you`re going through funerals. There are visitations underway, the first two visitations yesterday of two 10-year-olds, Amerie Jo Garza. She was a girl scout. She was someone who her family said had a heart of gold. One thing that we know is that her family said she died a hero. She was trying to call 911 from inside that classroom when her grandmother says she was shot.\par \par \uc2 We also know Maite Rodriguez. She was someone who wanted to be a marine biologist. She wanted to study at \uc2 Texas A&M University\uc2 . A piece of her obituary read those who know and loved her were blessed with her kind, ambitious and sweet, friendly soul. That`s what`s being lost here. And that`s what`s being honored as people come out to not only this memorial site but the memorial site at the town square to pay their respects. And it`s just going to be a tough period of days and weeks for the families here.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: And you talk to the family, Shaq, and Eliahana Torres was another one of those victims, 10 years old. And I interviewed her grandfather and he told me that it felt like a piece of his body had been pulled out of him.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Just ripped away. And his -- her aunt also said like this is going to leave scars on our hearts not for months, not for years but for decades. And you look at some of the other cities that have been impacted, Chris, by gun violence, and we know this doesn`t heal in a year or two years. So Uvalde right now, all the people that are out here, Shaq, was really describing from San Antonio to Del Rio, which is an hour in the other direction to outside of Texas. There`s so much support across the state and across the country because we are looking at one of the ugliest chapters of gun violence that we have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I saw on one of the local papers, there are so many flowers pouring in that they had to bring in extra help. They just -- the local florist couldn`t handle it. Sam, let me ask you about the other thing that we know. And having spoken to members of that community they want and they want this investigation done. They want to know what happened. Tell us the latest on police Chief Pete Arredondo.\par \par \uc2 Of course, he`s the guy who was in charge of the response. He was supposed to be sworn into the City Council today. So first of all, what can you tell us about reports of a postponement? And do we know if he`s been spoken to? I know there have been hundreds of interviews. How has he been spoken to, and is he cooperating with investigators?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: So, to the first part of your question, reports of a postponement, it`s not even reports. It`s been canceled. The city council meeting that was supposed to be held today, this evening, Chris, is no longer going to happen. And not only that, no one has seen Pete Arredondo. The last sighting we have Arredondo was a campaign flyer that I saw and sticks on the ground down the street that`s been pulled up since. But he was only a few weeks ago elected into that office. And radio silence. No one has seen him.\par \par \uc2 And I will also add, Chris, at this exact time, one week ago, there were 90 law enforcement officers inside of that building and they weren`t pursuing the gunman, then Customs and Border Protection agents, they showed up. They were held back allegedly, according to senior law enforcement officials. And the man at the center of that was Peter Arredondo.\par \par \uc2 So, we know that DPS, the Department of Public Safety here in Texas, is conducting, Chris, its own investigation. They said that they`ve done hundreds of interviews. You would have to imagine that Arredondo would be one of them. We have reached out directly to DPS but so far, they cannot clarify whether or not he`s been interviewed. But to think that he would not be given the sequence of events and what we saw certainly would defy imagination.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: And I think it`s just worth noting, we haven`t heard from DPS since Friday. That was the last update that we got when we heard from them things changed drastically from everything that we heard in the days after the shooting. So there are a lot of unanswered questions. And that`s something that you hear people who are coming to this site, that`s something that they want answers to what exactly happened, who`s responsible, why was that decision made? And that`s just something we haven`t heard.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: And to your point, the reason perhaps, that we haven`t seen anyone since Friday, is because every time they`ve taken a public stage and tried to answer questions about what happened, facts have got jumbled, and they`ve been pilloried.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: So the question then comes up, why will someone not stand up? The mayor for the first time in a week, the mayor of Uvalde, had not said a word since last week. Finally, he granted an interview, Chris, to a local \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate yesterday. It was a pool interview. So it was disseminated ultimately to other networks and news stations. There was no one else there to ask any questions except for this one reporter. You might argue that`s not exactly stepping up to the plate and answering the difficult questions when you`re controlling the environment under which you`re interviewed.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And, Shaq, I also just want to point out that you arrived in Uvalde from Houston at the \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention, and I wonder, in your conversations with the community since you`ve got there. In addition to wanting those answers to what happened, are they talking about gun reform?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: It does come up, Chris. I mean, it depends on who you talk to and what you ask, and what they want to bring up at that moment. I mean, some people come here and they are too emotional to even think about anything outside of the victims here. But it does come up. And you know one thing that I have seen is some overlap. I mean, it was jarring to be in Houston at the \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention where you had on one side gun activists and on the other side, protesters yelling at each other a much different scene than what you`re seeing here.\par \par \uc2 But I met as an attendee who had tears in her eyes, who said she couldn`t imagine what it would be like to lose her daughter, lose her granddaughter in a school shooting. But she said that doesn`t change how I fundamentally believe or think about this issue. She said she may be willing to change a law that would allow you know, 21-year-olds -- that would raise the age to 21-year-olds have access to the long gun, but she said even if that were the case, she doesn`t believe that would have changed or stopped what happened here.\par \par \uc2 I think the big thing is that`s been untested. We don`t really know if that`s true or not, but I think you get the sense that it`s something that everyone says they want something to get done. But there`s differences in what they want to see happen.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: But you can buy a gun, you can buy a rifle in Texas before you can buy a beer.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Think about that for a second. I mean, most of the families that we`ve spoken with, Chris say, regardless of what you think about gun reform and legislative efforts that are going on right now, no 18-year-old should be able to access an AR-15 style rifle. And they cannot comprehend or bring their minds to process why that would be the case.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shaq and Sam, thank you so much for your fantastic reporting. We do appreciate you being there. I want to bring back NBC`s Carol Lee with more of the reporting from inside the Biden White House from our team there. So, Carol, gun control is just one example of something that has frustrated this White House, right?\par \par \uc2 Inflation, high gas prices, a continuing rise in COVID cases, and of course, now these mass shootings. You report Biden is rattled by seeking approval ratings, and even "White House officials saying I`ve heard him say recently that he used to say about President \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s tenure, that everything landed on his desk but locusts, and now he understands how that feels."\par \par \uc2 So as he faces this crisis and many others, what are folks watching for? What moves might be made in the White House to turn things around?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, Chris, part of what you are outlining there, the feeling inside the White House and what the president feels, according to our reporting is that he just can`t catch a break, meaning that it`s crisis after crisis. He came in, obviously, with a really tall number of crises. We had the COVID pandemic and you know other issues that he was facing, the economy.\par \par \uc2 And so now it -- the feeling is after a year in some -- in to being an office that things just keep piling up and that his White House sometimes appears to be caught flat-footed. If you just take the baby formula shortage, for instance, we`re told the president was frustrated that it didn`t look like his administration was on top of that. That he felt like he maybe should have been brought in a little bit sooner in terms of the extent of the crisis that was happening.\par \par \uc2 And his poll numbers are something that he`s frustrated with. One person told us and others echoed this, that President Biden has lamented and talked about how he`s lower than former President Trump and that that`s something that he just can`t really get his head around. So there`s generally this moment where a lot of things are piling up. The midterms are approaching, and the president is frustrated.\par \par \uc2 Now his aides say that he`s not frustrated, but that is not what we found in our reporting. And the question about what is going to be done about that, there`s speculation about a potential staff shakeup that, Chris. We`re told that`s not something that would likely happen until after those midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol, thank you so much, and folks can find that report on NBC news.com. Meantime, right now, many teachers across the country are absolutely pleading for gun reform. A Maryland teacher just wrote an op-ed for EdWeek. "At every school, I took stock of each classroom. Bookcases, whiteboards, and chairs could all serve as barricades. Water bottles, computers, books, and coffee mugs could all be flying projectiles that just might slow down the shooter." I`ll ask her now about the change she wants to see next.\par \par \uc2 And then, high gas prices, high housing costs, and expensive groceries. President Biden just detailed his plan to tackle inflation, but will it help? I`ll talk to one of his top economic advisors.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A new study just out today shows that seven in 10 public schools see a rise in the number of children seeking mental health services. 76 percent said faculty and staff members have expressed concerns about depression, anxiety, and trauma in students. And that`s from the pandemic. Doesn`t even take into account the impact of what we`ve seen over the last week from Uvalde. We do know that after Sandy Hook nearly 10 years ago, no new gun laws were passed, after Parkland more than four years ago, no action from Congress.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Mary McConnaha, a middle school English teacher in Montgomery County, Maryland, that`s just outside of DC. She is also a new mother and wrote a devastating teacher`s plea for gun reform for Education Week. First of all, congratulations on the baby --\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCONNAHA, MIDDLE SCHOOL ENGLISH TEACHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But thank you for writing this because I think you expressed what so many teachers are thinking and feeling. Let me ask you first about that study. Does it surprise you at all just how stressed out students are these days? And do you think that school shootings will exacerbate an already difficult situation?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNAHA: I`m not at all surprised by that study. In every school that I`ve worked in as a teacher, the students even before the pandemic struggled with mental health and with stress, and with all of the burdens that are placed on young people these days, that pandemic only exacerbated those.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, we thought -- showed the headline of your article and it`s a plea. Tell us what you believe teachers what you need and want to see happen?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNAHA: I would hope that our elected officials would do something, anything to address these ongoing tragedies. If that isn`t going to happen, then I would hope that individual school districts or states could better address education and mental health services funding, especially with anti- bullying programs and social-emotional education in schools.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Can you be more specific for us? Like, what do you hear in your conversations with other teachers that you need that you don`t have right now?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNAHA: I am not the person to talk to about specific policy. It would - - it would just help emotionally feeling like I guess someone an elected official had our backs when things are proposed like arming teachers. That to me feels like we just assumed that another tragedy is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I want to read something you wrote that made me think about that because you write about both your hopes. But your fears for your new son and where he`ll be five years from now. I`m going to read from your article.\par \par \uc2 Will teacher still be planning escape routes and strategically placing large bookcases near the door for an easy barricade? Will politicians still be unable or unwilling to adopt any laws that will keep my tiny baby safe? I hope to God that something changes.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Do you worry realistically and sadly that America might be resigned to a reality that school shootings are now just part of our culture?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNAHA: I think if you look at what`s happened historically, that holds up. So yes, I`m afraid that these will continue happening. But I know that there are steps that individual schools can take that can make those schools safer, and hopefully, someday, something will happen nationally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s hope folks out there listening because we all know how much teachers take on particularly now. Mary McConnaha, thank you so much. We take -- we appreciate you taking the time to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 And any minute now, we expect to see President Biden meet with Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at the White House. The president just released a new op-ed on his plan to fight inflation as gas prices surged to new records. What can the president do to fix this and how high can prices really go? We`re going to talk to one of his top economic advisors next on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Any minute now, President Biden is set to meet with Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , and you can summarize why it`s so important with one word, Inflation.\par \par \uc2 We`re all feeling it. The price of everything from gas to groceries to housing all climbing. Now in a new op-ed out in the "Wall Street Journal," President Biden says tackling inflation is his number one economic priority.\par \par \uc2 He writes, "The economic policy choices we make today will determine whether a sustained recovery that benefits all Americans is possible. I will work with anyone, Democrat, Republican, or independent willing to have an open and honest discussion that delivers real solutions for the American people."\par \par \uc2 Let`s dig into those policy choices with \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you. I was listening to Brian Deese earlier today and he was saying, you know, that Fed chairs and presidents sit down from time to time. Is that what this is or is something going more substantiative going to get done or be discussed this hour?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA ROUSE, CHAIR, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Well, the president, as you noted when you opened, the president wants to emphasize that he shares Jerome Powell`s emphasis on inflation, but what`s most important about this meeting is for the president to meet with his --you know, the newly confirmed, reconfirmed chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2 , but also to emphasize how much he respects the independent of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 That has not always been the case with presidents. But he understands that the Fed is going to be taking actions in order to address inflation. And the president wants to emphasize he wants to respect that independence because ultimately that kind of action that the Fed will be taking underscores and underlines his own economic strategy and policy and focus on getting us -- transitioned us from a period of recovery with record growth, record job growth, the record drop in unemployment to a period where we have more stable, steady growth, where we`re not going to see the kind of job gains that we`ve seen in the past because we`re at a place where the economy is working more normally.\par \par \uc2 But importantly the president`s policies are about really making the economy work better for the average American where the growth is sustainable, where we`ve lowered costs for the American people and increased economic capacity.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But let`s talk about some of the specifics that obviously the American people are concerned about. This year marked the first time where the average price of a gallon of gas was $4 or higher in all 50 states according to AAA. Today`s price for a gallon of regular unleaded is the highest it`s ever been. Breaking the record. It hit just yesterday on Memorial Day. And now you have the E.U. making some new moves against Russia, obviously, because of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What is the expectation of the White House in terms of what Americans might see in terms of gas prices and what else can the president do to mitigate that impact?\par \par \uc2 ROUSE: So there`s no question that the increase in gas prices is painful for Americans. The president understands that which is why he`s been taking actions that he can take such as increasing reserves from the Strategic Petroleum Reserves. It allowing for more ethanol to be used throughout the summer, which allows for that mix of gasoline that doesn`t rely on the fossil fuels quite as much.\par \par \uc2 The president will be looking at other options on the table as well. So he completely understands that. But let`s be very clear that the increase in gas prices is largely, especially of late, due to Putin`s war on Ukraine and it is really important that Russia stop this war. And that is what the E.U. is trying to do, by putting more pressure on Russia by cutting off part of his == the reserves and the resources that he`s using to fund the war because they understand just how important this war is for preserving democracy and for protecting the E.U. going forward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Understanding the impact that the war has had on gas prices, you said there are other things that are on the table? What are they?\par \par \uc2 ROUSE: So the president is looking at a range of options. You know, many of them require working with Congress. Whether that is considering what should we do about the federal gas tax, many people have suggested that we look at tariffs. We`re looking at these various kinds of options and trying to understand what kind of effect could they have in the long-term, trying to understand what impact it would have on the U.S. economy writ large because fundamentally, this president is interested and focused on having stable, steady growth that involves getting inflation under control, but ensuring that we have an economy that is growing sustainably in a way that really benefits the average American, the middle class American, and not the very top.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So to the point of the meeting that`s going to be taking place this hour let`s talk about interest rate hikes for a moment. Specifically for people buying homes. As an example, a $425,000 house, that was the median listing price last month. I want to do a little unofficial napkin math here. With a 10 percent down payment, that house in January when the average mortgage rate was about 3.75 percent would mean a monthly mortgage of about $1771.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 With an average interest rate of 5.3 percent, according to "Forbes," that payment was now going up to $2,124. It hurts for people. So what do you say to someone who`s, for example, been saving to put a down payment on a house and are interest rate hikes really the only prescription for cooling inflation?\par \par \uc2 ROUSE: So starting with the housing supply, the housing supply challenge we`ve got in this country goes back a decade, it goes back to the Great Recession and the financial downturn. And the president has been focused on trying to increase housing supply. Again some of this will need to be in concert with Congress. Some of it he`s taking administrative actions in order to get more units on the market.\par \par \uc2 His proposals in fact in the budget would put a million more units over the next five years and close the gap in housing over the next five years. So we are focused on that because largely the increase in housing prices is due to constraints in supply. So we understand that housing prices have increased a lot. The pandemic certainly exacerbated that. There`s new research that suggests that a lot of it is because people are working from home and so they want to have more space in order to do so.\par \par \uc2 So as we start to get our economy back together, as people -- you know, housing arrangements start to normalize, that will take some of the pressure off and that doesn`t just involve increasing interest rates. I will note that, you know, the most recent read has actually the 30-year mortgage rate coming down a bit. So we have many miles to go in this process, but the president is focused on increasing housing supply and doing what he can because he recognizes the importance of housing for Americans.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cecelia Rouse, coming to us from the White House, thank you so much for taking the time. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And we have some breaking news to tell you about. A former \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  campaign attorney was just found not guilty of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Important to point out here this is the first case to go to trial from Special Counsel John Durham`s probe into the origins of the Trump-Russia investigation.\par \par \uc2 NBC News justice correspondent Pete Williams has the latest for us.\par \par \uc2 Pete, tell us about this ruling and is this significant? How significant a defeat is this for Durham?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Well, it is certainly a defeat for him. It`s his first persecution and it really isn`t about the thing his homework assignment, it`s about something different. His claim here is that in September of 2016 a law officer for the Hillary Clinton campaign named \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  went to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters and made a claim that experts on data analysis had found a link, some sort of communication back channel between the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and the bank in Russia with close ties to the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 And according to the prosecution in this case, according to Durham, when Sussmann said this he said he was acting as just an interested citizen, that he was not representing any specific client when in fact Durham charged he was doing this on behalf of the Clinton campaign to try to stir up trouble, get negative news stories about the Trump campaign at the height of the election. That`s what Durham said the lie was. But the jury deliberated just seven hours and found Sussmann not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Sussmann`s lawyer had said that the man that Sussmann talked to at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the former general counsel, James Baker, had shifting recollection about exactly what Sussmann said and his lawyers also said that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  ended up looking into this any way and found there wasn`t anything there so this was a prosecutorial overreach they said.\par \par \uc2 So I think frankly many people didn`t think this case was going to go very far. So for some people who have been following this case, it`s not a big surprise. The big surprise was when the charges were filed in the first place. But nonetheless, this case was closely followed because it is peripheral to Durham`s main assignment, which is looking at whether there was any \uc2 FBI\uc2  misconduct in the opening and the prosecution of the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  case. And when I say prosecution, I mean the way they handled the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Pete Williams, as always, thank you for that breaking news.\par \par \uc2 And still to come, President Biden meets with the prime minister of New Zealand, a nation that banned military-style semiautomatic rifles after 50 people were killed in a mass shooting there in 2019. How other countries including our neighbor to the north are now tackling gun control. And what the U.S. can learn from them. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:38]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Senator McConnell, got any comments on the gun control issue going on right now? Because you had said possibly that you were open to something.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes, what we`re doing, we had -- led by Senator Cornyn and Senator Murphy on the Democratic side discussing how we might be able to come together to target the problem, which is mental illness and school safety. We`ll get back at it next week and hope to have some results.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, you`ll note he said mental illness and school safety. Nothing about gun control. But that is Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  last hour in Kentucky. He is someone President Biden just called a rational Republican. And while Biden hopes for a rational response from Congress, his counterpart to the north is proposing new gun laws. Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  introducing legislation to impose a national freeze on handgun sales in response to a spike in homicides in his country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 , CANADIAN PRIME MINISTER: We republic news that the vast majority of gun owners use them safely and in accordance with the law. But other than using firearms for sport shooting and hunting, there is no reason anyone in Canada should need guns in their everyday lives. And Canadians certainly don`t need assault-style weapons that were designed to kill the largest number of people in the shortest amount of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That announcement by Trudeau comes two years after the deadliest mass shooting in Canadian history when police say a gunman in Nova Scotia killed 22 people with a gun purchased here in the U.S., in Maine. Canada almost immediately banned assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Biden just wrapped up a meeting today with the prime minister of New Zealand. New Zealand passed stricter gun laws three years ago in the wake of a mass shooting at a mosque there less than a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 And while we continue to see other countries quickly pass stricter gun laws, we`re also seeing individual states here in the U.S. address the country`s gun violence epidemic. As the "L.A. Times" reports gun deaths dropped in California as they rose in Texas. Gun control seems to work. It says, quote, "California`s rate of gun deaths has declined by 10 percent since 2005, even as the national rate has climbed in recent years. And Texas and Florida, their rates of gun deaths have climbed 28 percent and 37 percent respectively."\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by the reporter behind that story, "L.A. Times" senior editor David Lauter.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, David. Look, a couple simple questions. What laws have California passed that have helped contribute to the drop? And when they passed, did they do it with Republican support?\par \par \uc2 DAVID LAUTER\uc2 , LOS ANGELES TIMES D.C. BUREAU CHIEF: Well, mostly not with Republican support. California of course is a very Democratic state. So the Democrats don`t need many Republican votes, unlike the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 . But what California did was pass a whole series of laws. And it`s important to note that it was a lot of laws, no one law is going to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 And so, for example, California does not allow the purchase of semiautomatic rifles by people under the age of 21. The shooter in Texas would not have been able to purchase the gun that he used had he lived in California. California also has a red flag law that allows people who have signs of mental illness, emotional stability, other problems to have those guns taken away. California doesn`t allow gun purchases by people who`ve been convicted of a violent misdemeanor. In some states it has to be a felony in order to disqualify them.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a whole range of laws that California has passed over a series of 15 years or so, and as we reported, that has shown a decline, not a complete solving of the problem, California obviously still has shootings. But many fewer under per capita basis than states like Texas where the rate of gun violence and deaths has gone steadily upward.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As you probably know, Texas Governor Greg Abbott has a different take on the impact of tough gun laws. Let me play for you what he said last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS: Let`s talk about some real facts. And that is there are, quote, "real gun laws in Chicago." There are, quote, "real gun laws in New York." There are real gun laws in California. I hate to say this, but there are more people shot every weekend in Chicago than there are in schools in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Given the data you report out when you look at the big picture, what do you think of his comparisons?\par \par \uc2 LAUTER: Well, it`s kind of weird to compare the number of deaths in a city like Chicago with the number of deaths in a school. But leaving that aside, the fact is New York, for example, is one of the safest states in terms of gun deaths in the country. Texas is one of the most dangerous states in terms of gun deaths.\par \par \uc2 Now does that mean that, you know, Texas ought to adopt all of New York`s laws, not necessarily. But it does mean that Texas is making a choice that they are putting a priority of gun ownership over safety. New York, California have made the opposite choice and it has had an effect. If you look at the number of gun deaths in states, the states have the toughest gun laws have much, much lower gun deaths than the states that have the most lax laws.\par \par \uc2 It`s about an eight-fold difference between the states at the safest level and the states at the most violent level in the United States. The safest states in the U.S. are not exactly at the level of Canada, but they`re close. Whereas the most violent, dangerous states in terms of gun deaths states like Mississippi, Louisiana, Alabama, and unfortunately, Texas, are close to the level of some of the most dangerous places in the world.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Lauter, from the "L.A. Times," thank you so much. We appreciate you taking the time to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 We want to go to some video we just got. This is the president of the United States, the Fed chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . One of the big topics inflation. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Congratulations, Mr. Chairman, on your recent confirmation for the second term. Along with Doctors Brainard and Cook and Jefferson`s recent confirmations.\par \par \uc2 And I`m meeting with the chairman today and Secretary Yellen to discuss my top priority, and that is addressing inflation and -- in order to transition from a historic recovery to a steady growth that works for American families. Excuse me. And my plan is to address inflation. It starts with a simple proposition, respect the Fed, respect the Fed`s independence, which I have done and will continue to do.\par \par \uc2 My job as president is not to nominate highly -- not only nominate highly qualified individuals for that institution, but to give them the space they need to do their job. I`m not going to interfere with their critically important work. The Fed has dual responsibilities. One, full employment. Two, stable prices.\par \par \uc2 Chair Powell and other leaders of the Fed have noted, at this moment, they have a laser-focus on addressing inflation, just like I am. And with a larger complement of board members now confirmed, I know we`ll use those tools of monetary policy to address the rising prices for the American people.\par \par \uc2 So I look forward to Chairman Powell`s continued leadership at the Fed. And I look forward to the Senate considering my final nominee to the board, Michael Barr, in the near future. And that`s why we`re meeting, folks, and thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So the president not taking any questions. You can see the cameras moving, that means that the press is being moved out of the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, we talked to \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  a little earlier in this hour. They`re talking about inflation. One of the things that they`re talking about is the impact of Russian oil imports and new E.U. tariffs on top of ones -- sanctions that were already placed on Russia and the impact that has had on gas prices here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get you up to date on what`s happening on the ground in Ukraine. Right now in the east, Russia is pushing its frontlines forward with heavy bombing in the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Richard Engel shows us just how severe the damage is, reporting from a building that was shelled overnight. Richard?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (on-camera): Russia is making advances here in eastern Ukraine. And this is what it looks like, it is a highly destructive campaign as Russia is moving its frontline forward. And the frontlines are in towns and cities. And this city, Sloviansk, was hit last night. And this fight is all about range. It`s all about the capabilities of the weapons systems that both sides are using.\par \par \uc2 It`s primarily an artillery and rocket war. And what Russia has been doing is raining down rockets, raining down artillery on to towns, cities, villages, to try and beat Ukrainian forces back, depopulate them, cause an enormous amount of destruction. And then once they`ve rained fire on to these Ukrainian areas, then the Russian troops will advance, sometimes just a mile or two, and every time they advance, they extend the range of their weapons so that they can advance a little bit further.\par \par \uc2 And now even here, Sloviansk, is clearly within range. Several people were killed in this building overnight and Ukrainian defenders say they need weapons to stop this, they need advance weapons now particularly from the United States, otherwise this brutal and somewhat relentless assault is going to continue, and help is on the way to a degree.\par \par \uc2 The United States is sending in weapons, flooding in weapons, including advanced artillery systems that can out range the Russians, giving the Ukrainians an advantage. The weapon systems that are coming in, the American artillery, can outshoot the Russian guns by several miles, which is a significant advantage.\par \par \uc2 But the United States, the Biden administration is not sending in really long-range fires with rockets and artillery that can go hundreds of kilometers. The Biden administration worried that those weapons could be used to fire deep inside Russian territory and further drag the United States into this war and potentially into a war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Also economic help is coming. It`s going to be slower. But it is coming with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  overnight facing opposition from Hungary, agreeing to dramatically cut oil imports, banning oil imports by sea from Russia, and European officials say that by the end of the year, it will have the effect of reducing European oil imports from Russia by two-thirds.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:12]\par \par \uc2 It`s going to take time for those sanctions to bite. Already sanctions are in place. These are significant new sanctions. The weapons from the United States are coming in. But there`s a lot of time pressure because the Russians aren`t waiting. They are using their artillery. They are using their rockets to do this slow, destructive step-by-step advance every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Richard Engel with that reporting. Thank you, Richard.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure you join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Stay right here. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 The first two funerals in Uvalde are being held today, two of 21 funerals. So many it will take multiple weeks for the small town to bury them all.\par \par \uc2 The morning of course will take much longer. Today the families of Amerie Jo Garza and Yuleana Rodriguez are saying their final good-bye, two 10- year-old girls who will never turn 11, never graduate, never get a job, never have families of their own. Today their loved ones aren`t just burying their memories, they are burying two lost futures.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220616ei5v0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Yamiche Alcindor, Matt Bradley, Clint Watts, Garrett  Haake \par \uc2 9,466 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Plus, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  kicks off his first trip to Asia as president to rally and reassure American allies, as U.S. officials warn of a possible nuclear test by North Korea. Of course, North Korea is in the middle of a COVID battle. We're live in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 And Russia makes some games in eastern Ukraine amid a grinding battle that President Zelenskyy says has turned into a region -- turn that region into hell. What it means for the future of this war and the U.S.'s role in it?\par \par \uc2 Happy Friday. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I'm Chuck Todd. Right now, both political parties are trying to reshape the political battlefield to their advantage as we approach the midterms. So what does that fight look like? And major issues from abortion to inflation and beyond. Well, we're going to dive into some brand new numbers out today from our NBC poll. For the last two editions of our poll, we've been asking respondents a battery of questions about what sort of qualities and attributes they would like to see that would make them more likely or less likely to support a candidate for Congress.\par \par \uc2 And looking at the numbers it becomes pretty clear, which is huge. Republicans and Democrats would like to lead the way. For Republicans, no surprise. But their most favorable terrain is on inflation and the economy. Among all voters the belief that government should cut spending to combat inflation. And increase domestic oil production to cut gas prices. That's pretty popular across the board.\par \par \uc2 And we know Republicans know this. Because guess what, it's all over their campaign advertising.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE LOMBARDO, SHERIFF OF CLARK COUNTY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  are spending like drunken sailors and inflation is at a 40-year high. As Governor, I won't raise taxes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Biden's bad decisions are costing Alabama families $5,000 a year. Well, I'm not going to take it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KLEEFISCH, FORMER LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF WISCONSIN: No amount of buy one get one free deal can overcome what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  have done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks a lot, Biden.\par \par \uc2 KLEEFISCH: I'll cut taxes and waste to lower your costs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For Democrats, their most favorable terrain is actually in positions held by Republicans. Looking at our MDC polls. Voters are not crazy about candidates who believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's big lie about 2020. They also don't look favorably on candidates who would like to overturn Roe v. Wade. We only have a draft opinion on Roe right now, but Republican state governments are giving Democrats plenty of examples on abortion rights under Jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, Oklahoma lawmakers passed the most restrictive abortion bill in the country. It's a Texas style measure that bans abortion after fertilization. Democratic ads are already leaning hard into abortion rights being on the ballot this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If Senate Republicans win in November, they will light women's rights on fire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jessica Cisneros is the pro-choice candidate who will fight for our health care and always protect our rights to make our own decisions.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GODLEWSKI, STATE TREASURER OF WISCONSIN: I'm at the Supreme Court where it looks like Ron Johnson is going to get exactly what he wants, overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As your Senator, I will never sit by and allow our rights to be stripped away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So bottom line here and it may be obvious, but we've got the data to prove it. It sure looks like Republicans are going to be fighting to make these midterms all about the economy and inflation. Well, Democrats are going to be fighting to make it a bit more cultural about abortion, and Trump. So let's dive in. Joining me on set to dig deeper into these numbers is NBC Washington Correspondent Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 Democratic strategist \uc2 Xochitl Hinojosa\uc2 . And Republican strategist Brendan Buck, who was an advisor under former House speakers, Ryan and Boehner. Yamiche, I want to put out a couple other things up here. It's that it sort of reinforces the point we're making here just to understand on Trump and abortion rights for Democrats and on government spending and oil production.\par \par \uc2 First, let's put up full screen to best terrain for Republicans while willing to spell the candidates of 55-point advantage on inflation, using government -- downsizing government spending 52-point advantage on gas prices. Those are some big advantages. Here are some of the better sort of advantages for Democrats sort of reinforcing our point about Trump and Roe v. Wade here, minus 35 on Trump, minus 26 on Roe. That's still somewhat lopsided.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: That's still lopsided. And when you when you opened up by saying Democrats want to make this about cultural issues, and Trump and Republicans want to make it about the economy, I almost had to say, wait, is -- Chuck, did we get that flipped? Because obviously Republicans have been the ones wanting to talk about culture war, wanting to talk about race theory and abortion.\par \par \uc2 But now that they've sort of had these big wins, they don't really want to talk about those wins. And as a result, you have Democrats now reacting to them. When I've been out on the campaign trail, this is the thing that vexes Democratic voters, they say that Democrats are being so reactive, they're not being aggressive like Republicans, and as a result, it's only after things have gone bad. Only after you have election deniers being elected only after you have Roe v. Wade likely to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 Are they really getting sort of the fire in their bellies? Meanwhile, you have Republicans looking at the numbers saying, well, let's just argue at the idea that President Biden hasn't done enough on inflation. And you have of course, President Biden in Asia today making an international trip very domestic talking about \uc2 Samsung\uc2  Opening plant in Texas. He's going to be at Hyundai talking about their investments in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:04]\par \par \uc2 So, you see President Biden trying his best to will this but it really the numbers tell the story here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What's interesting here -- and Brendan, I'll start with -- to be it means Republicans, if they're disciplined, they got a winning message. But they get distracted by these culture wars.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: Ye. But yes, the things that show up in their ads, as you're saying is they put money behind are the issues that we're talking about. So, the abortion issue gives Democrats something when I think a few weeks ago, we would say they didn't really have anything. But the thing that they have to think about is, what are the issues that people are voting based on?\par \par \uc2 And you also asked in this poll, what are your top issues? The top issue was inflation. The second issue is jobs in the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: You know, abortion is higher than it was but people only vote on one or two things. And so the challenge for Democrats is how do you make this issue where maybe you have an advantage, the issue of the year. And that's a big, big lift, when inflation in the economy touches everybody.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Xochitl, we won't even -- if John Roberts pulls a rabbit out of his hat and preserves Roe, that would sort of complicate it seems the path that Democrats think might be the best one they have.\par \par \uc2 XOCHITL HINOJOSA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, Roe escalates the state's -- the stakes in this election. And I think that -- and everybody understands that and you're seeing Democrats running as already and trying to make this an issue. But Democrats also understand that if they don't talk about rising costs in, you know, our day to day lives, then we aren't going to win the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 But I would also look at the map. If you're 