ts Dem versus Dem and one of the bluest states that redrawn congressional -- in New York. That set to be approved today is going to spark some serious drama on the Hill that will reach the leadership of the House Democrats. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Biden kicked off his five-day Asia tour with his first stop is Seoul, South Korea. His message so far in day one, manufacturing supply chains and the global economy. Obviously thinking about the midterms, a little bit and domestic politics. The president toured a \uc2 Samsung\uc2  semiconductor plant today, a model for a plant being built here at home in Texas, and he touted the company's commitment to invest and create new jobs in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our two nations work together to make the best most advanced technology in the world. And this factory is proof of that. And that gives both Republican Korea and the United States a competitive edge in the global economy. If we can keep our supply chains resilient, reliable and secure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president also renewed his call to Congress to pass the Bipartisan Innovation Act, a bill that the administration believes will help the U.S. better compete with China. It has a ton of bipartisan support. This has just been bogged down by typical ridiculous Senate politics. NBC White House Correspondent Mike Memoli is traveling with the president. He joins us now from Seoul.\par \par \uc2 We also have Bonnie Glaser. Director of the Asia program at the German Marshall Fund. But Mike, let me get started with you. Obviously, you're in the middle of the night there. So we know. Tell us what's on the agenda tomorrow and where we go from here.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, happy Saturday, Chuck here from Seoul. Yes, indeed, the president will kick off later today sort of the more traditional official portion of the trip and North Korea is expected to be the focus of his meeting with South Korea's new President, President Yoon. He'll be laying a wreath also at a memorial for U.S. service members, of course, who died in the Korean conflict meeting with U.S. service members.\par \par \uc2 But you talked about this White House trying to focus a little bit on domestic politics while the president is here. I would say a lot about domestic politics. While the President is here, you've done these foreign trips with the president before, Chuck. You know, that often on the course of a trip like this, there might be an announcement about an economic package back home involving companies based in the country of presidents visiting.\par \par \uc2 But the president started his trip on Friday here in South Korea. And he -- before he leaves here on Sunday, he will end it with companies announcing not just investments in the U.S. economy, but plants in the U.S. Jobs that will be in the U.S. And we've also seen this as a White House, dealing first and foremost with inflation as a challenge now and they've tried to put that as much as they can on Putin's price hike, as they call it.\par \par \uc2 But remember so much of the supply chain crisis that it took up the last year and a half as well. It's something that they route in China, the disruptions because of COVID, the lockdowns that really snarled the global shipping and system for bringing goods to market here in the United States. So, that's really what the president is trying to put a spotlight on here. Yes, it's good to have alliances with democracies in this region, as well.\par \par \uc2 But it's also important to make things in America. That's something Biden talked about a lot on the campaign trail. And he's trying to bring that message home by saying these companies making investments in the U.S. will make us stronger at home, but also help the global economy as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Mike Memoli in the middle of the night on Saturday morning there. Mike, thank you. Let me bring in Bonnie Glaser here. And Bonnnie, I'm going to start with first North Korea and I actually want to start with COVID in North Korea. I know that we're -- there's a concern about a missile test. And it's sort of typical with the typical Kim Jong-un looking for a little attention right now.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, this looks like we're about to have a human catastrophe in North Korea. What can the world do about this in this closed society that feels as if this thing is spreading, I think -- I saw a stat today, two million people may have it right now?\par \par \uc2 BONNIE GLASER, GERMAN MARSHALL FUND DIRECTOR, ASIA PROGRAM: North Korea has only recently acknowledged that it has any cases at all. And we don't know if the numbers that they are providing to the outside world are reliable. But it's hard to believe the COVID isn't rampant in North Korea. They don't have the health infrastructure or the vaccines to cope with it. So the world can provide humanitarian assistance and the new president in South Korea, I believe has already offered to provide assistance, especially in the form of vaccines and protective medical equipment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to what I think the president's goal here. I mean, number one is as somebody who - I remember when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  went to Asia to unveil the Trans-Pacific Partnership and it seemed like it was going to be the beginning of what would become a check on China economically with the United States and some Southeast Asian countries and of course the domestic politics between \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  essentially eviscerated that treaty.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:10]\par \par \uc2 And now there's a baby step version of this, Bonnie. Can this be the beginning of an Economic Alliance? That is a check on China? Or is it too small of a -- of an idea?\par \par \uc2 GLASER: Well, the Indo Pacific economic framework that President Biden is going to launch in Japan is an important step toward writing rules in ways that favor open economies, and strengthen the rules based multilateral trading system. But it doesn't provide or won't provide access to U.S. markets which is what countries in the region want. They welcome the initiative, the interest in the United States and stepping up economically in the region, but they prefer the United States to return to the Trans- Pacific Partnership.\par \par \uc2 And indeed, many countries, I think, hope that the United States will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think this is really the -- our intent and that we're just sort of taking a step here, seeing if we can get there domestically and then another step down the road?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: I think that the Biden administration has held his cards very close to its chest on this issue. There are obviously political risks in even talking about returning to the CPTPP. And so, I would not be surprised if even after the midterms, that it is not a policy that the Biden administration will pursue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as someone told me, it is not going to be something, they're not going to pursue something that they want a vote in Congress to have to approve, on something like that. Let me move to the quad. And it's sort of the beginning of what it feels like a security alliance that could continue to grow. What is the best way to grow the quad and to make it more than just sort of an informal meeting of four countries with interest in worrying about China?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: Well, the quad, which includes the United States, Japan, Australia, and India, is now sort of in its second incarnation, it's really focused on providing public goods like vaccines to countries in Southeast Asia, climate is on the agenda, as well as issues like maritime cooperation. But there's more and more going on in the realm of security. And I think that there is potential to do more.\par \par \uc2 These countries are concerned about protecting their interests and supply chains, of course, but also, they are concerned about Chinese assertiveness and potential aggression in the region. And so, I think that there is a lot of potential to grow cooperation and also to include other countries not as members of the quad, but just as working with the quad countries in these working groups that they have set up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And before I let you go, I want to ask, and this is not something we told you we were going to ask about. So my apologies beforehand, but in the past, whenever a U.S. president was going to have a meeting with India, we always went out of our way to make sure we had some sort of event conversation whatever with Pakistan, that's not happening this time. Is this just another sign of just how far away we are?\par \par \uc2 The United States and Pakistan, how far apart they are these days compared to where they were even just five years ago?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: Well, yes, I think that the relationship with the United States and Pakistan has evolved, but more importantly, the relationship with India has evolved. President Biden has had many conversations with India's President Modi, as part of this quad, they are working together very closely. Obviously, Pakistan has been a thorn in the U.S.-India relationship.\par \par \uc2 But I think going forward, this is going to be an area where, you know, there are other -- this is one issue on which the U.S. and India agree to disagree. There will be others. But I think that the Biden administration felt that that phone call or heads up pre notification just was not necessary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it shows you how much as your -- as you put it. The U.S. and India relationship is growing closer all the time. Bonnie Glaser with the German Marshall Plan. Always appreciate getting your expertise. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GLASER: Thank you\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next to Russia gets ready to turn its threats against Finland into action. They plan to cut off the nation's gas supply just one of many possible consequences. You could see unfold amid \uc2 NATO\uc2 's expansion. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Russia has threatened to retaliate against Finland for pledging to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and now we may be seeing Russia attempt to make good on that threat. Finland's state-owned energy provider says Russia plans to cut off its gas supply to the country tomorrow. Russian gas exports account for just about 3 percent of Finland's total energy mix, according to European officials.\par \par \uc2 The company gave no reason for the move but the announcement came just two days after Finland and Sweden formally filed applications to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Finland would be the third European country cut off by Russia's state-owned gas company. Cut off Poland and Bulgaria last month. All three countries have refused to pay for gas in rubles.\par \par \uc2 Russian forces meanwhile appear to be making progress in their offensive in eastern Ukraine. One intelligence analyst says its troops have captured several villages in the Luhansk province. And President Zelenskyy says Russia is turning the Donbas region into hell. But while Russia may now control much of Mariupol, the protracted battle for the key port city may have come at a steep cost. According to the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," the fierce fighting badly depleted its ranks and now we're seeing talk of Russians looking for older Russians to join the military.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley joins me now from Kyiv. \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  is also here, our national security analyst and former \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  infantry officer to talk about Russia's military strategy these days.\par \par \uc2 But, Matt, let me start with you and what we're hearing today, and give us some more context of what President Zelenskyy meant when he says the region is just a hell scape.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, we've heard this before. This is a region that has actually been at war since 2014. Remember it was only recently that the Russians made their push out since the actual invasion on February 24th to try to take the entire Donbas region and since 2014 they really had control over 30 percent of what we would consider the Donbas. And that's the Donetsk and Luhansk Oblast in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now those 30 percent of that had been under essentially the dominion of Russia under the guise of Russian backed separatists in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 Now it looks like the Russians have really in earnest made a push to take that whole region. And this shouldn't be too much of a surprise because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself had declared that this was his motive. Ever since he withdrew from close to where I am now here in the capital of Kyiv and in other places, in the northern part of this country, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his military said that they would be concentrating on taking the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 But now that they've tried, and this was weeks ago that they made this announcement that really their efforts have been so aborted, so faltering, it's gone to show that they haven't learned a lot of the lessons that they should have been taught from their attempts to take Kyiv and other places in the northern and central part of this country. Instead they had a really faltering effort to try to reconstitute their units and to try to make those efforts to retake the Donbas region, and instead it looks as though they went just headlong in with a lot of really damaged forces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And didn't try to re-constitute them in any way that would allow them to really make strong advances. So, so far just like around here in the capital, all those weeks and months ago their efforts there have been faltering but now we're starting to see some shifts. We could see -- we just what looks like the real victory over Mariupol, and the Russians have said that 1700 Ukraine troops from underneath that Azovstal steel plant, and even though \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  had declared victory over that southeastern city of Mariupol, it looks as though now they really can declare victory.\par \par \uc2 Now it looks as though they're going to actually be able to move forces from Mariupol to elsewhere in the Donbas region and really start to focus on retaking that. But we got to remember at the same time, Chuck, we haven't actually seen the full thrust, the full appearance of all of the material, all of that really big military equipment that had been promised from Washington, from London, from elsewhere in western Europe. None of that -- only some of it has actually made its way to the eastern part of the country so far and has been used in battle.\par \par \uc2 So we haven't seen the full effort by the Russians in the Donbas and we haven't seen the full effort of the Ukrainians, so we can probably see a lot of more violence and a lot of more civilian death in that region in the weeks and months to come. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, now we understand why President Zelenskyy is describing it as hell now and it looks like it could get worse.\par \par \uc2 But let me move over to \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  because, Clint, it's one thing for the Russians to win battles right now in the east. The question is whether they're going to be able to hold this region. And we got reports today that they're looking for soldiers, you know, that they will take volunteers up to age 40 now which tells you that they're running out of soldiers, does it not?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: That's right, Chuck. They're definitely running out of soldiers. That's because they don't want to do mass conscription. Inside Russia part of that rule is around going from a special operation, that's a limited incursion the way that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  briefed as a defensive action to all-out war. He doesn't really want to do that.\par \par \uc2 I think big picture, though, it speaks to what Matt was talking about. Here in the east they just not underachieving their objectives, rewind 10 days, we see the Ukrainian military doing counteroffensives, taking out some of these swaths of what was Russian held territory, pushing a lot of the Russian artillery away from the center of the city so they can't reach it. Almost threatening this corridor right here.\par \par \uc2 This is a line of logistics and communication from this major Russian military base down to the Izyum access and the original plan, Chuck, was to go from Donetsk to Izyum, a much more bold strategy by where the Russia would try and flank many of these Ukrainian forces here in this pocket of Donbas. That has already failed again. Now that's really a second failure for all of these force there. And when you zoom in, the battle in the coming days and weeks is going to be much more limited.\par \par \uc2 When you watch some of those villages in this Luhansk region right here that are being taken, it seems that Ukraine is trading ground for time as we would say in the defense. If it's not critical you retreat back, you let the Russians take small gains. When they do that they burn more logistics and it opens them up oftentimes for attack. So you will see that they've taken small areas. But these are small movements. This is not the big bold maneuvers that are out there.\par \par \uc2 They'll try and secure pockets. The key town is a town called Sloviansk. It's about right here. Sloviansk is the corridor that if they can get there, they can start to bring out a much more limited encirclement of some of the Ukrainian defensive positions. But I am not sure they can do it. The troops at least on the Russian side, they don't believe in what they're doing, they don't believe in the plan, they don't have good teammates and good leaders. This is just a cobbled together unit. And the Ukrainians have all those things plus U.S. weapons coming in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, so this is a case where if we have -- if the West has some patience it's going to be brutal, it's going to be bloody but it sounds like you'd rather be the Ukrainians here than the Russians in that over time you expect Ukraine to continue to make advances getting their country back?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Not only that, Chuck. I think this is -- the big debate right now is how far does Ukraine go and how far does the West will support Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 Because when you look big picture, the Russians are trying to set up two areas or breakaway republics which will become part of Russia. One is in around Kherson, they're talking about an independent Kherson Republic. Same in Zaporizhzhia Oblast, a region which they don't actually control Zaporizhzhia but this region to create this land bridge linking all of these places together.\par \par \uc2 However, for the Russians, the big problem, the Ukrainians are counterattacking in several locations. And in and around Melitopol which is about right in here, you also see Ukrainians fighting in the rear areas of the Russians, attacking some of these so-called Russian planted officials to take over these republics. So I'm not sure, big picture. When you watch what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants that's essentially what he wants is to bring this area here, he wants to unite all of this and try and bring it together over time. He may not be able to do it. And for the Ukrainians they've heard this story before.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Whenever \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says OK, let's stop, let's negotiate, that's just pausing until he can rebuilt combat power and he's never stayed true on any of those. So for the Ukrainians I think they believe they need to advance all the way back to the territory that was once theirs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. If you're on the Russian side of things, where's the point -- and if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  came to his commanders and said I need to declare victory and go home, what's the best way to do it for him in the east?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yes, it's really in these provinces. And Chuck, just to put this in perspective, rather than doing the very stupid "Hail Mary" plan to try and go for Kyiv and surround half of the country, if Russia had just taken all its pores and poured into this area here, this is the Donbas region, he would already had it. He probably would had it in two to three days. Just all of that combat power focus he would have had it.\par \par \uc2 Now I'm not even sure he can take all of Donbas. This is the Luhansk region. You can kind of see that border right there. That is not that much more ground for them to take. But that's only half of the Donbas. Here, this is the border all the way out to the western part of Donbas, all of eastern Ukraine. The Ukrainians are fighting here, they're reinforcing, the weapons are pouring in, as I was talking about.\par \par \uc2 I don't know that he can get here which makes it very hard for him to trade off and say I'm done. And if he tries to take these areas down here and turned them into part of Russia, he would have essentially a Ukrainian insurgency in his rear area that he would have to defend. I'm not sure that they can last on that either. So it brings the question of what happens come July, August when sanctions are really kicking in? What can Putin really do here over time with 40-year-old soldiers that he's trying to reinforce in these broken units?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. How this all goes in the summer both with Western patience and whether the Russian people start to feel the pain is going to be fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , at the map for us. Clint, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, the former mayor of New York City has decided he wants to become a member of Congress. He's throwing his hat into what is becoming an extremely messy Democratic primary map for Congress all over New York state, mostly in the New York City region, as party leaders brace for a series of potentially brutal inter-party fights that will drag leadership into taking sides.\par \par \uc2 Those details are after this. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:29]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO, FORMER NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: People are hurting. They need help, they need help fast, and they need leaders who can actually get them help now, and know how to do it. I do know how to do it from years of serving the people of this city and so today I'm declaring my candidacy for Congress in the 10th Congressional District of New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Former New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  announced he's running for a New York City congressional seat. He did that on MSNBC earlier this morning, joining an extremely messy set of Democratic primaries that are going to put some prominent New York City lawmakers against each other.\par \par \uc2 De Blasio, he's running to represent the 10th District. Right now not the current 10th District. The new one. Because changes in the boundaries of the 10th District are part of the new New York congressional map that's slated to be approved by a judge later today and unlike other newly outlined New York City district where two incumbent Democrats are vying for one nomination, this district would be an open seat.\par \par \uc2 It will cover part of Lower Manhattan as well as parts of West Brooklynn. The 10th District was previously represented by longtime congressman and Judiciary Committee chairman \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  but under the new map his Upper West Side will now be part of the 12th District. That would pit him against another longtime representative who's mostly got the Upper East, \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So suddenly it's a primary of the Upper West versus the Upper East. Meanwhile the head of the DCCC \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  finds himself roiled in a potentially messy inter-party fight with his Democratic colleague \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 . And that's just a taste of the Democratic infighting in and around Capitol Hill that could set -- be set off by this new map.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have more on all of this messiness after the break. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned before the break, the likely New York congressional map has exposed some deep cracks in the Democratic Party overall as some established New York incumbents are being forced to face off against each other, we have to bring in our senior Capitol Hill correspondent for this.\par \par \uc2 Normally, look, you love covering House races.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But this one is going to potentially break up the party. \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  is in leadership, New York City Democrat, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , a Hudson Valley Democrat, he's DCCC chair. This is a mess. Can it get resolved without Maloney losing his job as DCCC chair?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2  may get some help from the special master today when they released the final version of this map.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we think it could change?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think there's a possibility. I mean, the special master has not made any public comment except to say they are listening to all the incoming that they've been getting and they have been getting a ton. So it's possible you could see a little bit of --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the real incoming has to do with how many congressional districts represented by African-Americans were broken up.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. This is a huge part of it and this is what \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  has been most upset about. I mean, if you've been to New York City you know, it's a collection of tiny neighborhoods. Now whatever you think about the boroughs, it's the neighborhoods. And you've seen historically black neighborhoods get essentially cracked by this map, both in Brooklyn and in the Bronx and other areas.\par \par \uc2 And so that has been Jeffries' big focus. And so I think actually the civil rights angle to this is probably more like to push the special master back than \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  which district he's going to jump into. But he's got a powerful ally in this. Speaker Pelosi was asked three or four different ways about this yesterday by reporters and she has defended him and said that he can still do his job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And run in the district where he lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, what other response could she have, though? I mean, I was sort of thinking about it. No matter -- you're in her seat.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you throw your DCCC chair under the bus five -- you know, we're not that long before the general election, right?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You can argue that's probably already one of the worst jobs in Washington to try to be holding together the Democratic caucus. And forget about expanding the map, but just trying to hold on to that majority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I don't know who else could be qualified, who else would want that job if not Maloney who's got his own operation.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  to oversee or something like that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: My gosh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because you just -- as you said, who would take it now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I mean, it's going to be a very difficult assignment and made all the more difficult if, you know, you still got to have these member-on- member primaries that he's going to have to manage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it sounds like you think or perhaps based on reporting that if any change made that makes Maloney save face here he can grab it and say, no, no, no, this district looks good now and I'll do it. I can't wait to support \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  over here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You know, they could do a little carve-out line of just his house and put his house back in his -- you know, what was left of his original district and that might be enough. But he's faced so much blowback from --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ritchie Torres, a member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. Who's not somebody who goes out and starts trouble either. I mean, he basically tweeted all but accusing \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  of racism by suggesting that \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  might somehow be a better fit for a different district which he had already won, right. So, I mean, this is an absolute debacle, and we haven't even talked about the two committee chairs who are going to have to run against each other in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And here's another part of this that people don't realize. If they're having to spend all this money, this is fewer dollars that these committee chairs would be giving to the DCCC, which is essentially free hard money getting a little political wonky here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: But that's important dollars that suddenly are going to be poured into beating up each other.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And these are prime --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nadler-Maloney?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: These are prime fundraising positions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My god.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: These are people who have prime committee spots to raise money and to give to their colleagues, and if you're running a big expensive primary all through the summer, you're not doing that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The irony here, Nadler and Maloney had to fend off progressive challengers in the last couple of cycles. They're not going to be alone in this race. I don't think. This is just going to be one of these real Upper West, Upper East, and I think progressives is going to come in there and watch out.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, and arguably the most liberal city of the country, there have been generations of Democrats waiting for either one of those two lawmakers to potentially retire and clear up --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Any inkling of that, by the way?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Of them retiring? I think there was --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anybody who's thinking about retiring?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The thought was perhaps that Nadler might have been looking towards the exit a little bit earlier on. He's had family health issues and things of that nature that made people think perhaps that he was getting near the end of his tenure. But he went quick to say that he was going to run in this district, even before Maloney had done so. There's been some, you know, back and forth this week that he didn't even give a head's up. That doesn't sound like somebody who's eyeing the exits.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And correct me if I'm wrong, and \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  obviously wouldn't say this, but if he wins that congressional district, does he become the member of Congress for Wall Street?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That's correct. \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , the man of Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Garrett Haake, happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We'll be busy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We'll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC's coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220521ei5k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Emilie Ikeda, Shannon Pettypiece, Garrett Haake, Dasha  Burns, Vaughn Hillyard, Antonia Hylton, Cornell Belcher, Richard Engel \par \uc2 8,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Good afternoon. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd on what is the biggest Election Day yet in 2022, five states are holding primaries today, including the MAGA battleground state of Pennsylvania, the most pivotal battleground of this year`s midterm elections in open Senate seat, an open Governor seat.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have a lot more on the big night ahead in a moment, but we`re going to begin in Buffalo were in the wake of Saturday`s supermarket terrorist attack. President Biden has just finished delivering remarks about white supremacy, and the rise of radical ideologies in America. The president also renewed calls for stricter gun laws and urged Americans to simply reject racism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: White supremacy is a poison. It`s a poison running through -- it really is. Running through our body politic, and it`s been allowed to fester and grow right in front of our eyes. No more. I mean, no more. We need to say as clearly and forcefully as we can, that the ideology of white supremacy has no place in America.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voiceover): In those remarks, the president didn`t just condemn Americans who are actively spreading hate. He called those that are silent, complicit. And he also issued this warning about the threat white supremacy poses to our country and the democracy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The American experiment in democracy is in danger like it hasn`t been in my lifetime. It is in danger at this hour. Hate and fear are being given too much oxygen by those who pretend to love America but who don`t understand America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president`s remarks come after he and the First Lady paid their respects at a memorial to the 10 people killed in that supermarket terrorist attack Saturday. His trip also comes as we learn more about the 18-year-old suspect in Saturday`s attack. Investigators looking at his online chat logs say he considered attacking elementary schools, churches, and other locations that he thought would have high percentages of black people. It`s one of three shootings nationwide in the last two weeks that are being investigated as hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  announced today that it`s opened a hate crime investigation into a shooting at a hair salon in Dallas that injured three women of Korean descent. A suspect is now in custody. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has also opened a hate crimes investigation into a shooting at a Taiwanese church in California on Sunday. Police say the suspect is a Chinese immigrant who appears to have been motivated by political hatred of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill, meanwhile, House Democrats plan to move toward a vote on legislation that they say will combat the threat of white supremacy and extremism. President Biden meanwhile, just spoke to reporters before leaving Buffalo saying there`s not much he can do on the issue as far as executive action is concerned.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by three of my NBC News colleagues, Shannon Pettypiece has the latest from the White House ahead of President Biden`s -- excuse me in the wake of President Biden`s trip to Buffalo, Emilie Ikeda is following the aftermath of the shooting on the ground in Buffalo, and Garrett Haake is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to Emilie where we saw the president say these remarks. And when we hear from the community, did the president meet the expectations of what the community wanted to hear, Emilie?\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So, I will say there`s still a sense of restlessness on the ground here. You might even be able to make out in the background. There`s some yelling over megaphones, and people walking through the streets. I think people were very eager to see the president. He spoke at a nearby community center. There was a massive crowd outside, some people thought more people, more victims, families members should be allowed inside.\par \par \uc2 I think there also is a little bit of concern around that it`s more symbolic. They`re worried that this won`t bring about change. But others are hopeful by the president being here that this will really strike up a new conversation. You talked about how President Biden really called white supremacy a poison. He called for a crackdown on assault rifles and the need to address the dangers of online rhetoric on many platforms.\par \par \uc2 He also offered in a particularly poignant moment really empathy for the families, saying that he has a profound understanding of the depth of the tragedy that they`re facing, naming all 10 of the victims and really describing a little bit about them from the father who died inside the supermarket behind me trying to buy a birthday cake for his son, to the security guard who`s been hailed a hero for standing up to the suspect and actually firing shots at the suspect. Then there`s the community leaders and the person who tried helping someone load groceries into the car. Really, President Biden stressing that these 10 lives were lost simply too soon, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Emilie, what more do we know about the investigation? We`ve learned over the last 24 hours that clearly all of these -- all of these sorts of, what I would call sort of little changes into the laws that we made that were little baby steps, red flag laws, none of that seemed to work with this because he started checked every box that should have been somebody that was detained, it should have been somebody that was red flagged from getting a gun and yet none of that system worked.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Yes. Unfortunately, it feels like we`re learning kind of new disturbing details with each day, you know, or I`ve been reporting throughout the week that authorities say the suspect had been canvassing this area the day before the attack. Well, then police confirmed that he actually had been in the Buffalo area back in March.\par \par \uc2 There`s some new discussion we`re learning something that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is looking into these messages on a chat forum called discord that the user shares the same handle as the suspect, and it kind of breaks down plans back in March of him looking at what areas he would scope out to potentially carry out this attack. Also, like drawing out a map of the supermarket inside.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with several employees who worked at Tops Supermarket, who managed to survive this atrocious shooting spree, who say the moment they saw his pictures surface that they immediately recognized him. So it appears more and more that this has been something in the works for some time, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Emilie Ikeda on the ground for us in Buffalo. Emily, thank you. Let me go to Shannon Pettypiece. And, Shannon, you know, candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  told us that the reason he ran more than any other reason was Charlottesville. And here we are, essentially, the Charlottesville protest creates this monster, maybe. I mean, I`m connecting a dot here, not quite but that`s sort of the mindset. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 And I`m hearing Emilie`s report about here`s this guy online. If this were an Islamic extremist, there was plenty of \uc2 FBI\uc2  and law enforcement resources focused on Islamic extremism, and trying to track it online and this or that. We`ve heard a lot of rhetoric about trying to take our focus off of that and focus it more on domestic extremism. But it`s pretty clear we`re not -- we`re not rooting it out the same way we aggressively attempt to root out Islamic extremism online.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Well, you know, and I will say, Chuck, the president brought up this Charlottesville anecdote again in his remarks just now a few moments ago saying that he wasn`t intending to run, people who know him or around him can attest to that.\par \par \uc2 And then he saw this resurgence in white supremacy and those Charlottesville protests. The president certainly didn`t hold back in calling out what he says is terrorism with this action, domestic terrorism, using that word to describe it. Of course, as you noted, the Justice Department under the Biden administration has been pushing and moving in this direction towards domestic terrorism, but it was simply not a priority at all under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 It has extensive reporting around that at the time about how, you know, white supremacy wasn`t seen as a focus within the Justice Department when it comes to this area of domestic terrorism. You know, I will note a few other things we heard from the president. He, without using it called out those who have sort of propagated this, "great replacement theory."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: And he didn`t name names here but he said, he accused people of profiting off of it of spreading lies, political -- for power, political gain, and for profit. And he also talks about those who follow this. He called them angry, alienated, isolated, lost individuals who have been radicalized online by believing in this theory.\par \par \uc2 So, calling out and going after not only those who believe in this and subscribe to it but also those who have been promoting it, as well as talking about once again, hoping to see some sort of gun reform legislation through Congress, which does not have the votes to move through the Senate at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Shannon, it was interesting to hear the president say in the gaggle that, you know, there isn`t a lot of executive action left for him to do. You know, what is going to be their follow-through on this, though? Is it going to be focusing on you know, \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Homeland Security and Justice to get their focus on domestic terrorism? Is it going to be on trying to find a gun bill? Is it going to be on trying to force the internet companies to actually moderate and monitor their content?\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Well, I mean, I think the heavy emphasis you saw today from the president when he was talking about hate and radical ideology and mentioning that online, that is an area we haven`t really seen emphasize as much in this administration or past administrations when it comes to going after a violent crime.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: A lot of the focus is on the guns. I will say another element the president and the White House had mentioned is mental health and mental illness and addressing serious mental illness, another area that really hasn`t been tread on as much as gun control regulations have been. Things that the Justice Department has done around gun control legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, I think those are a couple of areas where there`s still some room for the White House to make moves you know because we have seen attempts to the Justice Department, they take a while. And gun reform advocates say they`re just not really moving the needle that much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shannon Pettypiece at the White House for us. Shannon, thank you. Let me move over to Garrett Haake. And, Garrett, to set you up, I want to put up on our screen here this letter that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has sent to Fox, we see where the Senate Democrats want to put the focus here.\par \par \uc2 And he notes this. An AP poll found that your viewers are nearly three times more likely to believe in Replacement Theory than other networks. This should come as no surprise given the central role these themes have played in your network`s programming in recent years.\par \par \uc2 A recent \uc2 New York Times\uc2  investigation found that \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  alone amplified this dangerous and unfounded theory in more than 400 episodes of this show. I urge you to take into consideration the very real impacts of the dangerous rhetoric being broadcast in your network on a nightly basis. Have you heard from a single Republican signing on to that letter?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: No, Chuck, I haven`t. I mean, look, that letter there is totally in line with what the president was saying. And President Biden didn`t want to name names. It`s pretty clear that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is willing to do so by sending this letter and trying to focus on the hateful rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 But what I saw in that letter, there`s also an implicit acknowledgment that there will be no legislating on this issue in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . I mean, we`ve talked about gun bills, Shannon teed it up nicely. There aren`t 60 votes. There is no Republican support for even modest changes to the background check laws, to raising the age to buy an assault weapon to the same age you would need to be to buy a light beer. I mean, there`s nothing happening on the legislative front in that regard.\par \par \uc2 There`s a sliver of hope for this house bill that will be considered later this week, that would expand the Justice Department`s authority to investigate domestic terror cases, add \uc2 FBI\uc2  task forces, and so forth. But that may be the only legislative action that we`re likely to see on this. And so when I read that letter and when I hear Democratic members of Congress focusing on that portion of it, it`s a bit of a tell to me that legislation is at a brick wall when it comes to guns and legislating against this kind of violence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Garrett, look at the way the right tried to weaponize the idea that the department -- the DHS was going to essentially try and attempt to monitor hateful rhetoric, right?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They want to make it seem as if it`s some sort of Big Brother, and it`s like, this is always what the right does to appease the white supremacist movement by saying, hey, free speech. Don`t touch me.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, right. The Ministry of disinformation, they called it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I had kind of a classically frustrating conversation with \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2 , the Republican senator from Indiana this morning, where I asked him, OK, what should Congress do? And he said, well, we should do something about mental health, we should try to find the people who, you know, want these weapons who might do something dangerous, and we should -- we should be monitoring them. I said, well, you know, there`s a bill in the House that would monitor people, but it -- you know he calls it domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Oh, well, no, well, we can`t do that. You know, the attaching of labels or to make it you know, right-wing domestic terrorism, because that`s what we`re talking about, at least in this specific case, all of a sudden changes the conversation that Republicans are willing to have about this, and we`re right back at square zero.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, this is sort of the chickens coming home to roost. They`ve been appeasing this wing of the party for years and years and years and now they`re stuck with this group. Garrett Haake, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to a big update, by the way, on the COVID-19 pandemic. For some parents of younger children out there, this morning, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  authorized a booster dose for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  COVID vaccine for kids ages five to 11. We`re still dealing with five to 11. Still have no vaccine under five but kids will be able to receive the booster shot at least five months after completing their first two doses of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Currently, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is still the only drug-maker with an approved vaccine for any kids under the age of 18. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  decision will now go to the CDC, who will make an official recommendation about how the booster should be used for the age group. The agency is expected to discuss the booster at a scheduled meeting on Thursday. Again, though, for those of you with kids under five, we still do not yet have a timeline for a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. If it`s Tuesday, and we`re in an even number dear, you know what that means. Voters are voting on the biggest primary day of the election cycle so far. We`re tracking the most contentious and consequential races with our reporters all over the country right after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s the wonderful music least to my ears. It means only one thing. It is an Election Day. Right now, polls are open on the biggest primary day so far in these midterms. We`re going to learn a lot how where both parties are headed tonight. What sort of candidates, voters want to see on the ballot in November? How angry are voters?\par \par \uc2 75 percent of them say we`re headed in the wrong direction. That`s not