s taken out of him as he`s been supporting the candidate he endorsed, Dr. Oz and he told us he`s used to rough and tumble. He was a wrestler in high school. So we`ll see if anything could happen before the night ends tonight, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`ll be fascinating is it a race to 35 percent, who can get their first? Dasha Burns on the Senate race, Thank you. Let`s move over to Bucks County where we find Vaughn Hillyard. Bucks County will be a fascinating county to watch today and in November. You`re focusing a bit more on the governor`s race and this is where the panic first began about 10 days ago when a January 6 election denialist, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  appears to be pulling away. What do we see today? What do you hear on the ground there, Mr. Hillyard?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, we`ve been talking to numerous Mastriano voters. \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . He`s a current state senator here. And polling would indicate that he will pull this off. And if you look at what it was a crowded GOP fields here, you know, Lou Barletta, he was a longtime congressman, former mayor of Hazleton, Pennsylvania. He`s number two there.\par \par \uc2 I actually caught up yesterday with him at a diner and he seemed frustrated, a little disheartened here by the reality that the rest of the field hasn`t consolidated enough. The fifth and sixth place candidates, they dropped out in the last five days and supported him. But those third and fourth place candidates, they did not. They`re sticking in this here. And I`ve even talked to some voters here today who said they`re voting for \uc2 Bill McSwain\uc2  and Dave White, those third and fourth place candidates here.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s important, Chuck, that we talked about tonight and all the way to November about who \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  is. He is on the cusp of being the Republican nominee and he would ultimately be responsible come 2024 for certifying Pennsylvania`s election results. And when we look at the rest of the lineup around the country, you`re talking about potentially Michigan, Wisconsin, Arizona, having Republican governors and if \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  were to win and beat Democrat Attorney General \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , they`d be responsible for certifying the election results.\par \par \uc2 This individual was outside the U.S. Capitol on January 6. He even suggested essentially tossing out the results of Pennsylvania`s Biden win here in 2020, and sending Trump electors to be counted in the U.S. Capitol on January 6. He`s even suggested essentially throwing up the voter rolls here in Pennsylvania, starting all over and requiring Pennsylvanians to re- register to vote.\par \par \uc2 That is who we`re talking about, walking away with his Republican nomination. And finally, Chuck, it`s also important to note here in the state of Pennsylvania, the governor is tasked with selecting the Secretary of State, the individual who had run a futures elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Vaughn, there`s actually Mastriano has probably been the single- most-important supporter of Kathy Barnette in the Republican Senate primary when she was not being paid attention to the front runner there on the Republican gubernatorial race was they were sharing resources, correct, pairing each other`s paid advertising?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right. They were hand in hand in this. And I think that that`s where the big question mark comes down to, ultimately, how many voters are willing to split their ticket in terms of who Trump endorsed here. And you know, and I talked to one woman who is telling me that she just came in and cast her vote for Mastriano and Trump here and that she ultimately took Trump`s word on, who the cast should be that represents this state. So the question is how much is Kathy Barnette able to make inroads despite that Trump endorsement?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. This is again, people need to realize, if -- of all 