lly moved toward November and what looks like a likely matchup between \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  and Democrat Cheri Beasley, who if elected, would be the first-ever black Senator here from North Carolina. And so people are already turning their attention toward that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: They`re assuming \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  secured the win. It`s really Congressman Madison Cawthorn, who has to worry about which way this could go. One Republican we talked to said that about 50 percent of his friends were Republicans have been on the fence about Cawthorn because of the string of controversies and tough news that`s been out there about him. And so that`s really the race to watch here in North Carolina, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Antonia, it`s a 30 percent threshold to avoid a primary. So if the winner gets 30 percent plus one, they`re the nominee. How many candidates are going to get double digits in this Madison Cawthorn primary?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Man, that`s the question. It`s a very crowded field. He has seven challengers. The number one challenge or the person who`s on the minds of the folks who are on the fence right now is State Senator Chuck Edwards. And you know, when you have a crowded field like that, it makes it hard for any one of those people to knock the incumbent out of the way.\par \par \uc2 And Madison Cawthorn, despite all the challenges he`s faced, he`s still got that Trump endorsement, he`s got the name recognition, and he`s still seen by many as a star on the right, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And again, if this were a normal 50 percent plus one, Cawthorn on would be deader than a doornail. But because of this 30 percent threshold, he has a chance to survive this. Antonia Hylton, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: For more of our Pennsylvania special primary coverage on MSNBC, my colleague, Katy Tur, will be hosting her show live from Philadelphia next hour. You know, it`s a little bit wind