`ve got 75 percent wrong track.\par \par \uc2 If this were 55 percent wrong track, you might get \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and David McCormick for instance. That`s what 75 percent wrong track means. And by the way, could see an incumbent lose tonight in a primary. These are terrible numbers for incumbents whether you`re in a primary or a general.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s go to the president`s job rating here and this is also sitting in shellacking territory, 39 percent. Anything really he`s got to get to 45 percent as I said to you before. 45 or above and Democrats in battleground states have a boxer`s chance. When you`re sitting below 40, it`s not good in many places and you might even see light blue states, say Colorado, suddenly come into play.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s look at the ballot -- let`s look at the congressional ballot here. This is where Democrats can feel slightly better. You`re starting to see, look, a tied race is still. There are more Democrats packed in congressional districts than there are Republicans. So the tide national numbers still means advantage Republicans but it`s not in shellacking. We put it in a, quote-unquote, "bad territory" here.\par \par \uc2 Democrats need a three, four, five-point lead usually in the generic if they are going to have a chance at holding the House. Tied or slight Republican lean and that puts you in some dangerous territory there. So as you could see overall, just to sum up, our three major markers here, when it comes to job rating, direction of the country and congressional preference, shellacking, shellacking, bad.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:10]\par \par \uc2 But you know what, none of this accounts for a bunch of unelectable nominees that Republicans might end up with key battleground states which is something of course we`re looking out for tonight.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, what the results tonight could tell us about what Democratic and Republican voters want to see on the ballot in November and beyond. We`re joined by a couple of top notch strategists right here in studio.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Two parties with two very different sets of challenges this midterm year competing for the soul of America. It`s what an election day is all about. And polls open right now in battleground Pennsylvania and a whole bunch of other key primary contests. It`s shaping up to be an interesting night. We`re going to learn a lot about the direction particularly of where the GOP could be headed.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to look at tonight`s action in Pennsylvania and beyond, Democratic pollster, MSNBC political analyst Cornell Belcher, Republican strategist Dough Heye with a bunch of North Carolina ties, which is also on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Hello to both of you.\par \par \uc2 DOUGH HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Up on my tie. It`s obvious.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re making a tar heel. You`re no dookie.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: No, absolutely. We retired Coach K. Twice. Correction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not to say that people shouldn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 Cornell, look, neither party is in great shape. But the Democratic Party is suddenly more underwater than the Republican Party. And what is -- when you look at that, there`s something to organize against, there`s no doubt. What does that tell you? What extra challenges does that mean?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, there`s a couple of things. One is, look, it`s been (INAUDIBLE) for the House for quite some time. And you actually said both are underwater, but you don`t see a great deal of like --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Democrats used to be the tallest little person, though.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes, right. Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now not so much.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: But still overall Americans are not happy with either party. And - - but I think when you look at -- and most of this sort of disappointment, we talked about this before, Chuck, with the disappointment especially around Biden and his job approval numbers, it`s with parts of his coalition.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:10]\par \par \uc2 Right? It`s with parts of his majority coalition. We talked about it before. Younger voters. If you tell me, Chuck, what percentage of the electorate is going to be younger voters on election day in the midterms, I can tell you whether or not Democrats are going to have a good night or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s been -- I looked at all the midterms. It`s always 12 percent, 13 percent, 12 percent, 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Not in `18.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. In `18 it was 14 percent, I think, though. It was just slightly higher but it didn`t hit the 18 percent, 19 percent, which what you need in a presidential year.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: It`s not a presidential year. But again, if -- and again, African- Americans, look, if you look at his job approval among African-Americans right now, it`s like it`s in the high 60s. You know, insider stuff, I never had Obama anywhere close to a below 70 job approval among African- Americans. Right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: But below 80.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right. Right. So if you look at sort of -- and you know this -- base energy is the key issue for Republicans going -- for both parties going to midterms. Republicans have always done a better job of energizing their base and especially for this midterms. And that`s going to be a real problem for us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meanwhile, the Republican challenge seems to be is making sure they don`t step on themselves.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it does seem as if tonight there`s a whole lot of primaries that are the equivalent of stepping on your you-know-what.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Deja vu all over again. This is 2010 potentially redone. As we saw in 2010 we picked up 63 seat in the House but we left seats on the table in the Senate. Christine O`Donnell, for instance, in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I`m not a witch.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, exactly. Well. And Sharron Angle in Nevada. We also saw that in other elections with Todd Aiken and Richard Murdoch in Missouri which will be a key state later. And in Indiana. So it`s why \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is very nervous about who emerges from these primaries, obviously governors races as well, as to whether or not they can be electable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s going to happen -- look, you`re in North Carolina, Madison Cawthorn. This 30 percent, people don`t realize it. It`s a quirk, it`s a quick on the law, look, all runoff laws were done to keep black people from being in office. That`s why the south invented the runoff law. North Carolina at least has attempted to moderate it a bit here. But ironically, this 30 percent maybe what saves Madison Cawthorn.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, absolutely. The only thing that saves Madison Cawthorn from himself would be a 30 percent threshold. You know, and the reality is as we view things in all things being about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because we all have Trump on the brain, if 70 percent or 69 percent of Republican primary voters vote against the person that Trump not only endorsed but then again said, hey, let`s give this guy a second chance, or as we saw in Ohio with JD Vance or potentially in Pennsylvania, it means that Trumpism is here to stay, but Trump doesn`t control things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that`s what --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I agree with that so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. And this is what I`m curious about because I`ve talked with some Democratic strategies who believe that, look, no, Democrats can`t just run against Trump. But the Republicans are nominating enough candidates where there is a choice being created here where they can run against Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And not feel like they are just somehow out of touch.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, I think there is -- I think if Democrats have an opportunity to break through, I think there`s a couple of things. And it is, these Republicans are giving it to us. One is, we talked about this before, I`ve got polling back now that says, you know, women are being more energized about turning out voting because --\par \par \uc2 TODD: We saw in our NBC News poll.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is a -- you see that.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I called that, it`s sort of like watching a hurricane develop. It`s not a hurricane yet, but it could be.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: But it could be. We talked about it before, it`s like, but also, and look, if the gender gap looks like it did in 2018 versus how it looked in 2010 or 2014, it`s a different kind of dynamic. The other piece is in a state like Pennsylvania, it`s really interesting. Because you do have -- you have a candidate on the primary for governor there who as "the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " said this morning is a Christian nationalist. Right?\par \par \uc2 So how many other candidates are sort of outside the mainstream and give Democrats an opportunity to run against them, right. This can`t be about Biden, right. I know they want to make this a referendum on Biden, but I think we have an opportunity to make this a referendum on some far-right ring candidates that they`re putting out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, I mean, look, I don`t even want to get Idaho. I mean, you have this lieutenant governor is way on the fringe.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now I think she`s going to come up short, but does she come up short with 45 percent or she`s going to come up short with 30 percent? I mean I think it tells you where this party is headed.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, and it`s why Republicans are very, at least privately very nervous in talking amongst themselves. We don`t say the quiet part out loud too much. But we also know what the headwinds are politically for the president. You know, when I was at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2010, our magic number internally for Obama was 46. We thought if he was at or below that, we take back the House.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- and he was ultimately 44, 45. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , your poll had him at 39 with 75 percent of the country saying that we`re moving in the wrong direction. If those numbers hold, you know, it`s going to be a big night for Republicans and they don`t need it to be a big night for it to be a very good night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But let`s talk about one of the elephants in the room and that is the appeasement of this white supremacy stuff.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 And look, it does appear there are a lot of Republicans who don`t want to denounce it because they are afraid of turning off the base.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is a huge virus in the GOP.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes, you know, my first job in politics was Jesse Helms. Then I worked for Michael Steele, first African-American RNC chair. Then for \uc2 Eric Cantor\uc2 , highest elected Jewish Republican. So I`ve seen race in the GOP in every kind of guys. And this is -- these are things I thought were dead and buried that have emerged over the last five years or six years, who would help stir that, if we could think of just one person, right, former president, and Republicans -- politicians respond to their voters, good or bad.\par \par \uc2 And this is a growing voice and movement within the party means that unfortunately it`s not going away any time soon. And leadership can and should condemn it, but that`s not necessarily going to stop anything and that`s what`s troubling for me. There aren`t clear answers here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I think the president wants to make this part of the national message. Do all Democratic candidates?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, listen, I tell you this. If a Democrat doesn`t want to make bringing all Americans together and stopping the violence part of the national conversation, well, shame on them. Because it should be. And look, I agree with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  on this. You know, there`s too much enabling of this sort of language and sort of rhetoric. And the end result --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Which explaining it away.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, I can`t you how many times, no, no, this immigration policy isn`t racist. It`s this, and it`s like.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Go look, go to Ellis Island.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`ll show you the posters that look like they could be today on FOX News.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, that`s right. But also sort of long-term -- I know we`ve talked about this over, but long term, that`s not where moderate voters are. I think Republicans narrow their tent when they don`t denounce this sort of behavior when clearly listen, moderate middle swath of America is not where they are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  is trying to be where those voters are and he`s getting crushed.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Well, \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  unfortunately hasn`t had a message this entire campaign. You know, he`s talked about electability but he lost. Meanwhile, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  has had a message. He`s run well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you put this more on McCrory than Budd.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Well, I put it on Budd running a good campaign and getting $11 million from Club for Growth. It`s not Trump. You know, when Trump endorses somebody, they get a small bounce. But he never bounces anybody to 50 plus.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is going to do very well because of \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  and McCrory just has floundered in this.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, the other thing I`m watching here is it`s almost Club for Growth versus Trump in some of these races.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Which I think is interesting.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: And when they`re allied they win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, well, fair point. All right, guys. I think I`m going to see a lot of you tonight. So I`m looking forward. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And again be sure to tune in to our MEET THE PRESS election night special where all of the power of NBC News will be covering this election. We track and race results as they start to come in. We begin at 8:00 Eastern wherever you can get NBC News Now. It is everywhere. You can go anywhere and find it. You don`t have to just download one app.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the war abroad as Ukraine ends its fight for Mariupol and as Sweden and Finland are set to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We`re live on the ground in Ukraine right after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:45]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. More than 200 Ukrainian fighters in that Mariupol steel plant have now been evacuated and taken to Russian held territory following an announcement by Ukraine`s military that they have, quote, "fulfilled their combat mission." Of those evacuated 52 of the severely wounded will be part of a prisoner exchange with Russia according to Ukraine`s deputy prime minster. In a video message, President Zelenskyy said Ukraine needs Ukrainian heroes alive.\par \par \uc2 Fighters in the steel plant have been under heavy bombardment for weeks despite Russian claims of success in the city last month. The steel plant was the last remaining holdout of Ukrainian resistance in Mariupol. With Ukraine`s decision to cede the plant, Russia is now effectively in control of that port city.\par \par \uc2 But Russia may soon have another \uc2 NATO\uc2  country on its border as Finland and Sweden announced their intent to join the alliance. Turkey`s President Erdogan is threatening to complicate the process as he doubles down on his opposition to the Nordic states joining the defense pact but it`s likely a negotiating ploy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Biden will host the leaders of Finland and Sweden at the White House later this week.\par \par \uc2 This is perhaps the heaviest price Putin has paid so far. Our chief foreign correspondent Richard Engle is going to join us from Kharkiv, Ukraine, and Richard, how about the fact that Putin is suddenly saying, well, if that`s what Finland and Sweden want to do, go ahead. There will be repercussions later? That sounds like somebody who doesn`t really know how to follow through on a threat.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think he`s embarrassed by what is happening, and he can`t show weakness. If you remember, Vladimir Putin`s main argument for launching this war, aside from saying that they were a bunch of Nazis was that he didn`t want Ukraine to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  so that \uc2 NATO\uc2  wouldn`t come closer to Russia`s borders, and this is the media -- the message that state media and Russia have been hammering home day after day to the Russian public.\par \par \uc2 And now as a result of this action to push \uc2 NATO\uc2  away from Russia, and if you remember, the initial arguing point that Russia said was that they were going to drive \uc2 NATO\uc2  back to its position in the 1980s. They were going to shrink \uc2 NATO\uc2  to a degree that it would no longer have any Eastern European states. Instead, because of his action which most of the world considers to be a great failure, \uc2 NATO\uc2  is expanding including Finland -- both Sweden and Finland are likely to be accepted, and Finland has an 800-mile border with Russia.\par \par \uc2 So not only is \uc2 NATO\uc2  expanding closer to Russia, it`s gaining a long, long contact border with Russia. So it`s embarrassing. It`s against the narrative, and he seems to be downplaying it for now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and that gets us to another point here, Richard, which is he controls the information flow in Russia. He still can declare victory any time he wants, which is why we probably shouldn`t take his threats that seriously, right?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Well, he can declare victory, but Russians will recognize that if \uc2 NATO\uc2  suddenly grows from 30 to 32 nations and Finland is part of it with an 800-mile border, he can tell the people as much as they want, but history has shown that information does flow across iron curtains and bamboo curtains, no matter how hard you try. So people will eventually realize that this backfired against him.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 But going back to the point you were raising initially about the fighters in Mariupol. Their fate is unclear at this moment. This is going to become a real sticking point between Ukraine and Russia, and I think in the information war it is something that we`re already seeing Russia trying to use. Russia has been widely accused of carrying out atrocities, war crimes particularly in the areas around Kyiv, the suburbs like Bucha and Irpin.\par \par \uc2 And we only know about those war crimes because Russian troops were driven back. By the way, Russian troops have just driven back from the area where I am right now, in Kharkiv, so there is an anticipation that you could find more evidence of war crimes in the villages that the Russians were just forced to abandon.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: But to counter those claims that Russia was carrying out atrocities, Russia is now saying that these men who are now in Russian custody are war criminals and Nazis, and Russian officials say that they want to declare those fighters members of a terrorist group and that they will all be interrogated to see about their involvement in crimes against civilians, presumably Ukrainian civilians in cities like Mariupol --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I guess --\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: -- which they were trying to defend against Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess we shouldn`t be surprised that that propaganda attempt, considering what happened last week in the first war trial, war crime trial being held by the Ukrainian government against a Russian soldier.\par \par \uc2 Richard Engel, on the ground for us in Kharkiv, always good to have you there. Thank you, sir. And please continue to be safe.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being with us this hour. Don`t forget, our election night special, myself, Kristen Welker, NBC News tonight at 8:00 p.m. streams on NBC News Now, wherever you can get it. You can download an app, you can go on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . Anywhere. It is available everywhere.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage will continue with Katy Tur. She`s live from the city of brotherly love after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220518ei5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Emilie Ikeda, Dasha Burns, Antonia Hylton, Maria  Teresa Kumar, Eugene Scott, Brendan Buck \par \uc2 9,080 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday. The city of Buffalo mourning, 10 people killed in a grocery store rampage. Officials say the gunman was motivated by racist hate. What we know about the suspect the victims and the investigation ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the rise in right-wing extremism in this country as some in politics stoke the rising hate for political gain, and the country returns to the seemingly endless political debate over what to do about guns.\par \par \uc2 And one day to PA, tomorrow is primary day in five states but it`s battleground Pennsylvania, where some last-minute twists are shaking up what was already one of the closest watched Senate primaries this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd. As we learn more about the suspect and the victims in this weekend`s mass shootings in Buffalo, we find ourselves in depressingly familiar territory, asking what if anything could have been done to prevent this massacre and what can be done to prevent even more tragedies from happening in the future.\par \par \uc2 Police have now released the names of all 10 people killed, Saturday at the Tops Friendly Market. The youngest victim was 32 years old, the oldest was 86. Three other people were injured in the shooting and nearly all of the victims were black. Police say the 18-year-old suspect was dressed in tactical gear as he opened fire inside the supermarket and then he planned on continuing his rampage before he was stopped in what they`re calling a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FLYNN, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, ERIE COUNTY, NEW YORK: Well, obviously, from what -- all the evidence that we`ve gathered so far, the motivation at this stage appears to be pure hatred or pure racial hatred. Now, the -- there happens to be a penal law in the state of New York that is entitled a domestic act of terrorism motivated by hate. That particular crime right now is a crime that I`m focusing on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Investigators say the suspect appears to have posted a 180-page manifesto, citing a racist conspiracy just days before the attack. New York State Police also say they investigated the suspect last June after he made a threatening statement about wanting to carry out a shooting. He was a minor at that time. He was taken to a hospital for a mental health evaluation and never charged for the crime. Meanwhile, the White House says President Biden will travel to Buffalo tomorrow to grieve with the community there.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by NBC`s Emilie Ikeda with the latest from Buffalo. I`m also joined by Erie County Executive \uc2 Mark Poloncarz\uc2 , and director of American University`s Polarization and Extremism Programming, Cynthia Miller-Idriss. She`s the author of the book Hate In The Homeland: The New Global Far Right. That paperback is out now.\par \par \uc2 So, Emilie, I`ll start with you. Just give me the latest here. What`s happening on the ground in Buffalo today in the wake of the shooting, and what`s the latest on this investigation?\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Garrett, unfortunately, new disturbing details coming out by the day. We know that the suspect had been in this area the day before the atrocious attack, scoping out the area, canvassing the area looking for where he would act out on this rampage. Remember, the police commissioner there told us that yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re also learning from the Police Commissioner this morning that had this suspect not been stopped he would have planned on continuing his act of rampage down Jefferson Avenue continuing to target black people in this shooting, according to a commissioner speaking out to another news outlet earlier today. So believe it or not, there could have been even more bloodshed in this just an absolutely horrific tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Investigators have recovered three weapons on the scene. And one of the things I want to point out is police arrived on the scene within a minute of the call. Also, there was an armed security guard within the supermarket who did confront the suspect, slow the suspect down.\par \par \uc2 He did -- the police Commissioner told me actually shoot the suspect but because he was wearing tactical gear and just the assault rifle that he was wielding, the security guard was no match for the suspect as he was continuing throughout the story. He went on to kill even more people, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Emilie, what can you tell us about the victims of this shooting?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Well, these were beloved members of the community between the ages of 30 and 90 years old, friends, family, cousins, uncles, aunts. We know one -- the governor telling me that one of the people killed, he was simply trying to buy cupcakes for his son`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Another person, a grandmother who devoted her life to service, she ran a food pantry for a quarter of a century. We`ve talked about that security guard Aaron Salter, who confronted briefly the suspect he`s now being hailed as a hero. Another person, a driver who was shot and killed in the parking lot as he was trying to help someone put groceries in the car. So, just an absolutely atrocious act of hate, and one that you had mentioned is being investigated as a hate crime, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Mark, watching the rainfall there, it seems somehow appropriate. Talk to me about the mood in the community there today.\par \par \uc2 MARK POLONCARZ\uc2 , ERIE COUNTY EXECUTIVE, NEW YORK: Well, Garrett, it`s very somber here, Buffalo and Erie County. What we`re seeing is a community that`s reeling. We`re trying to come together to start the healing process. But we`re shocked that an individual from outside our community drove three-plus hours to our city of Buffalo to inflict as much pain as possible primarily on African-Americans, there`s no doubt that this was a targeted hate crime, domestic terrorism in our own backyard.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Buffalo is now joining a long list of communities that have unfortunately dealt with mass shootings but there`s a much smaller list of those with regards to hate crimes and targeting African Americans. And buffalo is now part of that list. So our goal is to show the world that not only will we not be defined by the incident, but we will be defined by how we overcome it by supporting the families of lost loved ones, assisting those in need.\par \par \uc2 We have mental health counselors, as well as we`re going to be doing food distributions for people in the neighborhoods so that we can all overcome this and show that we`re stronger. If he tried to divide us he failed. We are united in our efforts to show that this community is stronger than one person coming in with a rifle.\par \par \uc2 And we will overcome it though we`ve lost, unfortunately, 10 individuals, many of them who are making a difference, not only in this community but other ones in New York State.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How can President Biden help in that effort? What do you want to hear from him when he comes to Buffalo tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: Well, I think he`s going to come in and basically say that the nation mourns with you. I received a call from Secretary Mayorkas, the Homeland Security Secretary two days ago right after the incident and pledging the full support of the federal government for our efforts to not only prosecute but to heal.\par \par \uc2 And that`s exactly what we have is a two-track method of government now prosecuting through the Erie County District Attorney`s Office and the other departments in Erie County assisting with mental health counseling, food distribution when whatever`s needed in the community.\par \par \uc2 So I think the president is going to come here, I know he`s going to talk to some of the relatives of those who are deceased to help them grieve but also pledged the full support of the federal government to not only ensure that this individual is held responsible for his crimes but to help his community overcome what is a tragic, horrific event that will leave a scar on the city of Buffalo and Erie County for years to come.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I wanted to get on that government response a little bit. Play for you some of what \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , your governor told Chuck yesterday. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D-NY): Well, in many states legally, yes. In the state of New York, what he was able to buy was an AR-15. But he was able to enhance the magazine capacity. I mean, you can`t have that many rounds purchased legally here in the state of New York. But you just go over to Pennsylvania, go to a gun show.\par \par \uc2 Since my time in Congress, we`ve been trying to get a national response. And so individual states will do the best they can. And I`m going to be posing more gun laws on Tuesday, that pre-scheduled press conference even before this event occurred but we need other states to step up. We need the federal government on our side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you agree with that sentiment here? What more can New York state do? What does the federal government need to do right now or better yet you know, last week?\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: Well, the federal government could have banned this weapon. It was previously banned. Remember, there was an assault weapon ban that expired, and unfortunately, since that, we`ve seen these horrible incidences across the United States. You often hear the argument well a good person with a gun can defeat an individual who has these types of weapons. Well, Mr. Salter -- a former retired Buffalo Police Officer Aaron Salter, a trained professional was unable to stop this assailant because the assailant was wearing body armor and wearing --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: And using a weapon which is designed for war. These weapons are not designed for hunting. I know a lot of hunters nobody uses an AR-15 to go hunting and if that`s how you`re going hunting, you`re doing the hunting wrong. They`re only used for one purpose, and that`s to kill as many people in a shorter period of time as possible. And Mr. Salter is a hero. He slowed down the assailant. Unfortunately, he took -- his own life was taken in the process. And this is a former trained professional, someone who was a Buffalo Police Officer for more than two decades, and he couldn`t stop him.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: It just goes to show we need to get these guns off the streets.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. It doesn`t get more good guy with a gun than that. Cynthia, this shooting is just the latest now in a long line of racist shootings, motivated by white supremacist conspiracy theories and spread by online communities. What do you believe needs to happen to stop this pattern of racist violence like we saw in El Paso and in so many other cities?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MILLER-IDRISS, DIRECTOR, PERIL CENTER, AMERICAN UNIVERSITY: Absolutely. I mean, we have been seeing this obviously, for quite a long time. But this specific conspiracy theory has mobilized terrorist shooters in Oslo and Christchurch, here in this country, in El Paso, and Pittsburgh, and now, here in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 And you know, what we`re seeing again, and again, is in this case is, unfortunately, textbook. Young men are radicalized in deep spaces online often through memes, through videos, through propaganda, that sharing false claims about scientific racism, and creating a sense of threat and then they calling on them to behave heroically, so -- what they believe to be heroic engagement.\par \par \uc2 We need obviously, gun -- you know, better gun laws, we need social media content moderation, but none of that`s going to work if we don`t reduce the susceptibility of people to racist propaganda, to begin with. And so, you know, people are going to believe this. If they`re not being taught otherwise in schools or at home, about structural racism and hierarchical systems of inequality, they get online and peep -- and propaganda offers explanations for them and tells them they`re a threat.\par \par \uc2 And so we -- you know, we`re in a moment in this country where we can`t even have conversations about race and we see the consequences of that in real-time right now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So we should sit back for people who maybe haven`t followed this closely. This shooter`s manifesto talks a lot about what`s called the great replacement theory. It`s racist, xenophobic, anti-semitic idea that non- white people are somehow attempting to outnumber and replace white people in the United States. Talk to me a little bit about how you`ve seen this ideology grow over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 MILLER-IDRISS: It has grown exponentially online. And I think you can track that to the rise of social media and to the way that online spaces, particularly for young men, are exploited in online gaming and meme sharing sites and other places that use video and other technology to embed humor, irony, satire, and share what are really hateful and dehumanizing ideas.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have seen that spread, it has been used in terrorist acts in many countries now at this point, and we`re seeing it here again and again. So that Great Replacement conspiracy theory is a conspiracy theory that has roots obviously, in long generations of racist belief systems here -- white supremacist belief systems.\par \par \uc2 But something about a decade ago has changed in it becoming a global rallying cry. The terrorist incident in Christchurch New Zealand, his manifesto was called the great replacement. I mean, we have been seeing this again and again and again over the last several years with a very delayed preventative response in this country.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So the -- last week, before this shooting happened, you wrote a piece for msnbc.com, in which you called out \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , the top-rated Cable News host in the business for perpetuating replacement theory. Then we had just this morning, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  tweeting the House GOP leadership has enabled white nationalism, white supremacy, and anti-Semitism. History has taught us that what begins with words, ends in far worse. GOP leaders must renounce and reject those views and those who hold them.\par \par \uc2 How problematic is it to have these ideas getting mainstreamed on the far right? I mean, you want to talk about sort of cultural solutions to this. These are folks with very large megaphones kind of flirting at the -- at the one end of the spectrum, maybe the least you could say, flirting with really dangerous ideas.\par \par \uc2 MILLER-IDRISS: Absolutely. I think there are two really dangerous things about it. The first, obviously, is what you just mentioned, that it`s mainstreaming and legitimizing the idea that there`s some kind of plot out there, some kind of nefarious plot by elites, by Democrats, in this case, in the case of the -- of the \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  type of illusion to it, to replace Americans with voters that would be more compliant by importing them, you know in -- you know, through immigration.\par \par \uc2 But what`s really dangerous -- I mean, in this case -- the buffalo case, it`s very clear this teen was radicalized not through mainstream media, but in his own words in online spaces but it makes it less likely that the adults around him. I mean, in our -- we have a guide for parents and caregivers that`s online, I can share, produce with the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  to warn them about online radicalization.\par \par \uc2 And the number one, our first bullet point under recognizing warning signs is about the great replacement theory. How less likely is it that parents or teachers, coaches, or other adults will recognize that as a warning sign if they`re hearing a version of it on cable news?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Mark and Cynthia, we got to leave it there. Folks, you should follow Cynthia on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . There`s a lot of good resources there if you`re interested in reading more up on this, especially for parents.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, there were deadly mass shootings in multiple states this weekend. Democrat of Illinois, Majority Whip, Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  joins me next on what is now decades of inaction from Congress on guns plus, the latest warning signs for Democrats in our new NBC poll, and later, we`re live on the ground ahead of some game-changing Senate primaries tomorrow. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. It`s not just Buffalo. Communities nationwide are grieving today after a weekend of gun violence took the lives of at least 14 people. As we said, 10 of those lives were lost in Buffalo where the black community was targeted in the city`s worst-ever mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Then there was Orange County, California, Houston, and Chicago, 12 were killed in shootings that wounded eight others. Now, some elected Democrats are once again talking about doing more to address gun reform and racism. But folks, we have been here before, and it`s all been talk.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Senate`s second-highest-ranking Democrat, Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  of Illinois. Senator, I think my first question is, do you think the country has just accepted this idea that mass shootings and gun violence are something we`re going to have to deal with every couple of weeks forever?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL): Well, I think it`s a reality. It pains me to say it, but in the great city of Chicago, which I`m honored to represent, hardly a day goes by that someone isn`t shot and on the weekends, there was a shooting spree with 20, 30 people shot, seven or eight dead when it`s over. This has become commonplace, not just in Chicago, but certainly in that great city as well.\par \par \uc2 And you say to yourself, well, Durbin, you`re the chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , you`re from Illinois, so what are you going to do about it? Well, you know the math. There`s a 50-50 Senate. 50 Democrats, 50 Republicans, it takes 60 votes to do anything significant or controversial.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 We don`t have 60 votes for any kind of gun safety legislation, even legislation accepted by the vast majority of people, whether it`s background checks to keep guns out of the hands of convicted felons and people who are mentally unstable, whether it`s getting rid of ghost guns, these guns that are being sold without any identification or serial number, the list goes on of common sense things we can do to make America safer, we cannot get virtually any Republican support for it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You don`t have 60 votes, but both you and majority leader Schumer, 14 months ago, one day after a mass shooting in Colorado took 10 lives, and a week after a shooting in Atlanta took eight said you were going to bring background checks to the floor regardless. And this is what you and the Majority Leader said at the time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): This Senate will be different. The Senate is going to debate and address the epidemic of gun violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Congress should do its job. Keep guns out of the hands of people who shouldn`t own them. We need to do more than reflect. We need to act. We need to show that we care and prevent the next mass shooting if we can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know you don`t have the votes, but you brought voting rights to the floor knowing you didn`t have the votes, you brought protections for abortion rights to the floor twice knowing you don`t have the votes. Where`s the fight? Where`s the showing people you care on this issue, Senator?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I can tell you we tried behind the scenes to work with members to member conversations and try to find some compromise, any compromise that moves us in the right direction. We`ve had no luck whatsoever. Buffalo was a horrible, miserable, hate film incident, which all of us across America really grieve for the people and families who were touched by it. But can we still bring anyone from the other side of the aisle across to join us in a bipartisan effort? I`ll see, I hope for it, I work for it but I`m very skeptical.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Why is it a good tactical idea then to have the big fight on voting rights or abortion rights to put those bills on the floor to put folks on the record, but to not do something similar with gun legislation? I mean, the Democrats broadly have not spent political capital on this issue.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think our position on the issue is pretty well known. With only a couple exceptions in our caucus, the things that I`ve described about background checks, about ghost guns, people really understand where the Democrats stand and the Republican stand on. If there`s a decision that there`s going to be a vote to demonstrate that on the floor, I`ll support it. But I really want to continue to work to see if there`s any hope, any hope whatsoever, of getting Republican support for sensible gun safety.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I hear you. And you know, I do the reporting, I`m up there, I`m talking to Republicans too, and they`re not moving on this issue so I get it. On the Republican side, I did think it was interesting that you had Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  calling out her own party in a tweet this morning accusing Republican leadership of "enabling white nationalism, white supremacy, and anti-Semitism." What more would be helpful to hear from your Republican colleagues today on that part of this issue?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: The first hearing of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  that I chaired was with the superintendent, the Secretary in charge of the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 . He came forward and told us quite clearly the biggest threat to American terms of terrorism is domestic terrorism, its white nationalists, and white supremacist who are setting out to an agenda of violence.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen it play out over and over again. We saw it last year, January 6, 2021, on those who descended on the Capitol, and the message that they brought. We`ve seen it over and over again, sadly, repeated maybe by a lone wolf situation in Buffalo. This is a very real problem and I would hope that the Republicans could join the Democrats in saying whatever the political motivation far-left, far-right, whatever it happens to be, we`re going to condemn this activity.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: On a different topic, the Minority Leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , was in Ukraine over the weekend. He met with President Zelenskyy. On his way out, he got a call with reporters. He says the Senate is going to vote on this $40 billion package that`s -- this week. He`s confident it`ll get a bunch of Republican votes. You are the guy who counts votes on the Democratic side. Do you expect this to pass and do you expect it to send the kind of message that McConnell appears to hope that there is this broad bipartisan support for helping Ukraine in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I was disappointed last week. I support that money for Ukraine. I want to send it as soon as the president suggested. We had a chance to do it last week. It was stopped by Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  of Kentucky. He demanded an amendment on the floor. It stopped our progress in place. We had to set put it off until this week. I`m sure we`re going to get back to it soon, very quickly, and I hope we can pass it quickly. I want this to always be bipartisan. We need to stand behind these brave people.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And it sounds like you`re going to get at least Republican leadership voting for that bill. That was McConnell`s message yesterday. I want to look ahead a little bit to the midterms here. The -- our new poll that came out over the weekend has the Democratic Party with a net negative rating of 19 points in the poll.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s the worst it`s been in 30 years. Our viewers can see it on the screen the Democratic Party doing worse than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , worse than the Republican Party, and worse than the Supreme Court. To what do you attribute the branding part -- problem that Democrats have right now or is it -- is it more than a branding problem?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I think it reflects the reality -- history`s reality that off-year elections are not coming to the president`s party. That`s the starting point. Secondly, we`re dealing with a transitional economy coming from the shutdown economy under COVID-19, reemerging, creation of jobs at record levels but with it comes the fires of inflation, every family`s feeling that. They want to know that Democrats are going to do something to change it.\par \par \uc2 The president used -- has used several tools in his kit. For example, releasing oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve was one, dealing with agricultural prices, and other there`ll be more jawboning if you will, those who are price gouging, and that`s happening at gas stations, it`s happening with baby formula. We`re finding a lot of that insidious activity taking place. So we need to be identified with that effort and helping families find an affordable place where they can deal with this economy.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there more in the Senate`s toolkit quickly, Senator?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: In terms of what we can achieve with the 50-50 Senate, it`s limited when it comes to policy but we continue to look for ways. The competitive bill that passed a bipartisan bill, that passed out of the Senate now in conference committee is going to give us a fighting chance to create good- paying jobs in the future. We`re sensitive to the economy, but it is difficult to fight the fires of inflation legislatively.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  of Illinois, the majority whip, the Chairman of the Judiciary Committee, perfect guests for this topic today. Senator Durbin, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And up next, last-minute developments on both sides of the aisle in Pennsylvania. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:37]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. If it`s Monday, we`re less than 24 hours to polls opening in another round of midterm primaries. Now if Chuck were here, he`d either be quoting "Les Mis`" "One Day More," or maybe the Ramones. His eclectic taste notwithstanding, voters are voting tomorrow in Kentucky and in Oregon, North Carolina, Pennsylvania and in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 There was a big weekend in the closely watched Pennsylvania primary. Democratic frontrunner John Fetterman suffered a stroke. He says he`s on the way to a full recovery and according to him doctors say it appears he has no cognitive damage. His campaign now says Fetterman will not be at his team`s election night rally.\par \par \uc2 And on the Republican side, there`s new focus on Kathy Barnette`s past homophobic and anti-Muslim comments, and her appearance at President Trump`s January 6th rally in Washington, D.C., all this as she surges in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with the latest on the ground from all over Pennsylvania is NBC`s Dasha Burns.\par \par \uc2 So, Dasha, let`s start on the Democratic side. What do we know about Fetterman`s condition and how is that likely to impact the closing hours of this race?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Garrett, the twists just keep coming in this race. Even in the last 24 hours multiple pieces of breaking news here. Yesterday we just found out that John Fetterman, you mentioned it there, he had a stroke.\par \par \uc2 We were actually at an event of his on Friday morning, a hundred or so people in the room, and well after start time his staffer came in and announced that he wouldn`t be there, that he is not feeling well. And we haven`t seen him on the campaign trail since. Well, it turns out that that morning he wasn`t feeling well and his wife Gisele made him go get checked out.\par \par \uc2 And Garrett, I hope you hear this, I hope my husband hears this, your wife is always right. Taking that advice might just save your life. OK?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So thankfully he did get checked out and they caught a blood clot that had made his way to his heart causing that stroke. He is now recovering. He says he`s feeling much better. But he will not be at that Tuesday night rally. He`s going to still be in the hospital and his wife Gisele, the lifesaver, is going to be the headliner on Tuesday night, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Let`s talk about the Republican side. You`ve got new reporting verifying images of the ascendant Republican primary candidate Kathy Barnette at the January 6th "Stop the Steal" rally. What do we know about her participation there?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes, so right around the time we were getting the news of Fetterman`s stroke, I was looking on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and started to see images circulating of Barnette marching toward the Capitol on January 6th. Now we did know before this that she was there on January 6th. In fact she organized buses to the rally. But this is the first time that we`re actually seeing images of this. NBC News did verify.\par \par \uc2 Now in those videos, she`s marching towards the Capitol alongside known members of the Proud Boys that were later arrested and indicted on their alleged role in that riot. Now we talked to Barnette about this. She says that she views January 6th in the same way that the left views the summer of 2020 and the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I asked her campaign to respond to these images. Here`s what they told me. They said Kathy was in D.C. to support President Trump and demand election accountability. Any assertion that she participated and/or supported the destruction of property is intentionally false. She has no connection whatsoever to the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 Now Barnette is fresh to frontrunner status. And just now the campaigns are doing their opposition research. They`ve been putting out statements and ads. I just had a chance to speak with McCormick, David McCormick, her rival in this primary. Here`s what he had to say about Barnette.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Do you think she is fit for office?\par \par \uc2 DAVID. MCCORMICK (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, listen, I`ve got -- I know Kathy on the campaign trail. I respect her personal story. I mean, she`s been tested. She was tested 18 months ago when she ran for Congress and lost by 20 percentage points. And now what`s happening is she`s getting the spotlight and a lot of questions that should have been answered a long time ago that all the scrutiny that I got and others have gotten are now coming to the fore. And she has to answer all those questions, whether it`s about military service, career, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Do you think she`s answered them properly?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, she needs to answer them, and I don`t think she`s answered them yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Garrett, right now we`re packing up right after this. We`re headed to a rally that Barnette is hosting in Scranton. And we`ll see if at that event she does in fact answer any of these questions, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Dasha Burns, is there a candidate you haven`t talked to this in race? You`re killing it out there. And yes, you`re right, my wife is always right. Thank you, Dasha.\par \par \uc2 And Dasha will be one of the faces you`ll see tomorrow night when Chuck and Kristin Welker host a MEET THE PRESS election special streaming on NBC News Now. That starts at 8:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And up next, with North Carolinians headed to the polls tomorrow, too, how abortion and other hot button issues are playing on the campaign trail there.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. And as we`ve been saying, tomorrow is primary day in North Carolina, too. So what`s on voters` minds there as they head to the polls?\par \par \uc2 As part of our county-to-county reporting project, we are talking to people about exactly that. In Anson, North Carolina, black voters struggling with the impacts of inflation and low wages say they`re frustrated with lawmakers in D.C. They say elected leaders are not doing enough to address their urgent needs.\par \par \uc2 NBC News correspondent Antonia Hylton is in Anson County, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, Antonia, talk to me about what you`re hearing from these voters about whether they feel like they`re being listened to, if their issues are being addressed ahead of this primary?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, people here feel like they`ve been completely ignored and they are feeling financially squeezed right now. Anson is a rural community close to the border with South Carolina, the kind of place where the plants that used to provide jobs have gone overseas in recent years. So people here tell me they`re mostly working low-wage jobs and they`re seeing the cost of everything around them go up.\par \par \uc2 But their income isn`t increasing to meet that. And they don`t feel like the Biden administration or even frankly leaders here at the statewide level are doing enough to bring them relief. And so many of the voters particularly black voters under the age of 40 here tell me that they`re not engaged with the midterms right now. Many of them haven`t voted yet early in the primaries and aren`t even sure they`re going to go out and vote tomorrow because they feel fed up with the entire system.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 In fact, I spent the day yesterday at a black-owned convenience shop and the owner there was telling me that his prices have gone up and he`s had to leave some of his shelves empty because he can`t raise prices on the people that he serves because they just can`t take it. Take a listen to a conversation that I had with one of his patrons there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC STURDIVANT, ANSON COUNTY RESIDENT: People have this agenda, well, they say, I`m going to do this, I`m going to do that. But then when they actually get in the seat.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: You just have lost trust?\par \par \uc2 STURDIVANT: Yes. Basically. Nobody never followed through and do what they say, you know what I`m saying? They have all the promises, if you vote for me, I`m going to do this, I`m going to make this change, I`m going to do this. It never gets done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Garrett, interestingly, some of the non-voters who I met there yesterday told me that the one issue that might get them reengaged is abortion. They were aware of the leaked Supreme Court draft. Many of them while they don`t like abortion say that they do support choice and were concerned about the rollback in women`s reproductive rights. And so they were telling me that they may go out and actually reregister to vote and get reengaged ahead of November if they feel like they can get behind a candidate who they think will take serious action to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But right now, because of financial issues being their number one concern, they frankly just feel a bit disconnected and disrespected by people who they hope to see act on their behalf sooner, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Antonia Hylton in Anson County, North Carolina. Thank you for that reporting.\par \par \uc2 And up next, as we`ve been saying, our new NBC News poll shows that the political environment for Democrats is looking pretty bleak this November. What, if anything, can change that trajectory? The panel is here next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:21]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As we get ready for another round of primary elections tomorrow, the overall political environment for Democrats looking ahead to November is still very stormy. In our latest NBC News poll out just yesterday, just 16 percent of respondents say the country is headed in the right direction, with a whopping 75 percent saying the country is on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 Those negative feelings extend to the party in power with President Biden and the Democratic Party`s numbers well under water less than six months out from the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is Eugene Scott, national political reporter for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," Maria Teresa Kumar, president and CEO of Voto Latino, she is also an MSNBC contributor, and Brendan Buck, former adviser to Republican speakers \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Eugene, you saw these numbers here. When the wrong track numbers are this bad, what, if anything, can Democrats do to turn it around? They are going to be the guys who get blamed it seems like as we approach November.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE SCOTT, THE WASHINGTON POST NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: They certainly are. And one of the big criticisms of the Democratic Party from people on both sides of the aisle is not having a very clear message in terms of being able to articulate what they have done so far, which is not the same thing as not having done something, but when you talk to a lot of voters they really aren`t sure what it is that Democrats have accomplished and why it is that they should back them, you know, for two more years at least in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s a real challenge that the Democratic Party are going to have to figure out what they need to do in terms of convincing people differently.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maria, do you agree with that assessment, that it`s a messaging problem, not an accomplishment problem? Has enough been done that the Democrats can present to the American people come November?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I was like to half joke and say for any mom who has a kid in school, they`re very excited that they`re no longer Zoom schooling. So it`s this progress that we have seen since a competent government has been able to get people back on track with the pandemic. Has it been perfect? Absolutely not. But we have shots in arms, we have businesses open, and we do have kids back in school.\par \par \uc2 The biggest challenge I think we`re finding is part of it is communication. Part of it is the far left of the party hasn`t received everything that they would like to see, but what we have seen is there was a recent poll about two weeks ago with ABC and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and they found that among independent voters and among young voters, they were leaning Democratic by 12 points, even though the Democratic Party itself and Biden was under water.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s because we`re going to start seeing more issues driving this campaign whether it`s abortion, whether it`s access to voting rights, whether it`s gun violence. That is where the grassroots organization came together for 2018 and 2020, and again I think it`s going to be a grassroots movement that`s going to get the Democrats at least within striking distance despite the party itself.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But when you look at the issue numbers, it is interesting because the coronavirus numbers -- Biden`s numbers are great but it seems like people don`t give him credit for it. And the abortion numbers do seem to be the kind of green shoot for Democrats. The issue moved from 3 percent to 10 percent on the list of Democrats -- voters rather most important issues. That`s before this opinion has come down.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: And I can tell you, like, our -- the work that we do when it comes to voter registration, trying to get people jazzed up about the primaries, no one is interested. We started serving connecting abortion to the voting booth, we`ve seen close to 40 percent increase in people actually registering to vote under that in Georgia, in Pennsylvania, even in Florida and Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I wonder how it`s going to affect the (INAUDIBLE). Brandon --\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: Yes, so that`s the big challenge obviously Democrats have. Turn this election from one about inflation to something about other issues. Elections are usually only about one or two things.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Right now the election is about inflation, about the economy, about people not feeling well. If Democrats can make this about abortion or about gun control, then maybe they have a chance. But that`s a big bet. Maybe it`s the only bet they have. But you can`t make an election about five or six different issues. Right now it`s about cost of living and until that changes, they`re in bad shape.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there a risk for Republicans here. I mean, you look at all these states that put in place these trigger laws. I mean, Americans` abortion politics is actually somewhere in this mushy middle. You know, most Americans say they don`t want, you know, sort of complete access necessarily and they definitely don`t want these full bans.\par \par \uc2 Could there be a blowback for Republican politicians in some of these states that have put trigger bans and other really restrictive bills in place?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Certainly. I mean, what Republicans have long said is that if you strike down Roe vs. Wade you`re going to have states that put in reasonable limitations. And what you`re seeing in some places less than reasonable limitations on abortion. So that`s the risk. But again, as you said, I don`t think that this is a black and white clear win for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: There are a lot of Republican voters who are very animated by this as well. I think it`s largely awash if you even get to the point that it is about that this this election.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, it maybe, Eugene, the key here and maybe the special sauce for Democratic enthusiasm, because we did see that in our poll here that Democrats are starting to feel more enthusiastic about the election.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 They`re closing that gap a little bit for Republicans. If Brendan`s theory in the case is right here and there`s only so many issues you can care about, maybe abortion isn`t the issue that, you know, closes the deal for Democrats but if it`s the one that sort of wakes people up that this is an election they need to pay attention to, that`s not nothing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, a lot of Democrats are certainly hoping so, and it`s important to remember that this is May and we don`t know what will be happening by the time, you know, most voters go to the poll. And we have seen in the past that things that people thought would motivate voters in the spring were like dead when it came to time to actually vote.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so what happens in terms of whether there`s, you know, a leak of the draft actually moves I think will determine how severely people focus on this issue moving forward.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to talk about Pennsylvania a little bit. We could end up with a Republican ticket that was like all in on January 6th. I mean, these are all people who, you know, participating in some of the activities, not necessarily inside the building.\par \par \uc2 But, Brendan, I think about all the time is \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has talked about trying to get the most electable people to the finish line here. It does not seem like that`s what he`s going to get in Pennsylvania. How big of a problem is that for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes, it`s funny you say that. I ran into somebody in McConnell world last week. He was despondent.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Really?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: About the fact that -- yes. That Kathy Barnette was potentially going be their nominee. This is a race that I think both sides are trying really hard to lose. On the Democratic side, you have Fetterman probably going to beat \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . When I was in the speaker`s office, we used to joke that \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  was the most electable candidate in the country. We used to joke we`re never going to beat him because everybody likes \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And yet Democrats are not going to nominate him probably. And then on the Republican side, you have Kathy Barnette. You know, there are often sort of kooky characters, with, you know, questionable backgrounds who won but they usually get ignored. And here we are in 2022 potentially the nominee for Senate in a swing state. So I hope that Republicans are still the favorite in this state but both sides are trying really hard to nominate probably the least electable of the possible candidates.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What do we make of the Democratic field on this? I mean, I agree \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  on paper was supposed to be like the golden child Democratic candidate. He`s getting clobbered right now according to the polls. But all of a sudden Fetterman is off the trail. Pennsylvania is a state that everybody votes on election day basically. How do you see that playing out?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, I think it`s very similar. I`m going to take you guys across the Atlantic to the race that we saw against Macron and a white nationalist. And you have a lot of folks going neck to neck on that because they weren`t really enthusiastic. But the moment that the runoff came and you saw that it was either democracy or nationalism, that`s when a lot of the folks basically switched sides and they came out in droves for Macron.\par \par \uc2 I actually think that there is a group of Americans that are moderate Republicans and independents that are waiting in the wings. They`re not interested in the primary but if they have someone that is so anti- physicist, they`re fascist, running against any Democrat or independent, I think that there`s going to be a break. And it`s not because they like the independent but it`s because democracy, little D democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s always been the Biden theory of the case which is compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Gosh, I would never know.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Not to the Almighty, right?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right. Right. Well, that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Like you want an alternative.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Eugene, when we wake up Wednesday morning, what`s the story going to be about the Pennsylvania primary? Like what -- open seat primaries can be very eye-opening to the dynamics that are going on in both parties. What do you think we might see?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think one of the things people will be focused on specifically related to the Republican primary is figuring out how influential President Trump still is in Republican politics in this state. We talk about Kathy Barnette, you know, rising in the polls. Trump has come out and said that he doesn`t think she could win but he will supporter her if she does. And so ultimately I think people really want to see where the GOP is still going and how influential the former president remains in this party.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: His decision to endorse Mastriano in the governor`s race, feel like trying to hitch his wagon to somebody who is already winning.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, let`s talk about the Republican Party here. This Cheney-\uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  new back and forth about great replacement theory, how we got into a place where the Republican Party are trying to like tap dance around this question of whether or not there is a white replacement effort going on in this country? How big of a problem is that for the GOP?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, it`s certainly not something you want to be talking about but maybe it`s something --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When it`s some member of leadership --\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Maybe it`s something we need to be talking about. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: In what context?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, I think for a long time a lot of people wanted to say that there was no undertow of racism anywhere within our party, and I think at this point I have to admit of course there is somewhere within the party there`s racism. But then you have some members of Congress who are actually flirting with that.\par \par \uc2 And what we have is a situation where if you`re a member of Congress, you`ve decided that you`re going to go on an electoral path where it`s Republicans only, you don`t ever try to worry about the middle. When you get to a situation like that, you can`t cast aside any votes. Even if they`re racist, you can`t cast them aside.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is the dynamic that is flowing through a lot of the Republican Party. It`s not that anybody is embracing racism. But they don`t have the electoral margin to get rid of it. And I think that`s the problem we found ourselves stuck in when you take this Republican-only, play to the base approach and ignore the middle. I think you could cast that aside very easily if you would just embrace a little bit of the middle.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maria, I want to give you probably the last word here. I was struck in my interview with \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  when I asked him about guns and the idea that they were going to do something. I thought he was very fatalistic on it, that this is not an issue that`s going to get addressed in a 50-50 Senate, which I think anybody who can do the math can kind of see this, but I don`t sense there`s the fight there for Democratic voters or for Democratic politicians.\par \par \uc2 Can that be an issue that breaks through? Again, if we`re operating on a theory that there`s only so many issues that are going to matter to voters, can Democrats capture an anti-gun voter base to make a difference on this issue?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think that we have to look at folks like the, you know, (INAUDIBLE) Montana. He is a Democrat who has very much a constituency that believes in safe right guns. And so that`s something that they`re realistically in the Democratic Party they`re not going to want to touch. Is it good for America? No. I do think that we need to have sensible gun laws.\par \par \uc2 The fact that there are -- when we`ve talked about minority communities, we fail to recognize that we`re talking about 145 million Americans. We`re over 40 percent of this population. There`s nothing minority about us and when we walk outside and we are concerned that our children are going to get targeted because of the color of their skin, we need to have frank conversations on this changing demographic that we`re seeing, what it means.\par \par \uc2 And what I see in Trump and the extreme right and the Republican Party, it`s political expediency. They`re talking about a future that`s already in our classrooms. My children represent a majority-minority America. Your children represent a majority-minority America. And we are not having the difficult conversations of what that means and we need to, both parties need to embrace that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So maybe the action is on hate, not on guns. It`s something. We`ll cover that in our second hour. No, I`m just kidding. Because that`s all the time we`ve got.\par \par \uc2 Maria, Brendan, and Eugene, thank you all for coming in.\par \par \uc2 And thank you for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220517ei5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 13, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Dasha Burns, Carol Lam, Erin  McLaughlin, Clint Watts \par \uc2 9,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:23]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it's Friday it's Republican panic time apparently in Pennsylvania as the party grapples with a pair of surging far-right candidates just four days to go until primary election night and one of the country's most important battleground states dare, I call it keystone.\par \par \uc2 Plus, to what lengths can or will the January 6 Committee go in its effort to compel five Republican lawmakers to comply with subpoenas for testimony? We're about to find out.\par \par \uc2 And later, I'll speak with former President Trump's Pentagon chief who's now calling Trump a threat to democracy. \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  joins me here on set to tell us what he saw and why he's speaking out now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I'm Chuck Todd. Just four days before primary day there is panic in Pennsylvania among some Republicans, as the party faces you could call it a monster of its own creation. Some of the party fear could cost them in a battleground state that might decide control of the senate. Leaders in the party are now grappling with the sudden surge by Kathy Barnette, candidate drawn from the world of far-right political commentators.\par \par \uc2 A world that has been curated and sort of seated by former President Trump and his allies. While in the Republican Senate primary, she's within the margin of error in the latest Fox News poll. And judging by every how everybody's acting in the last 48 hours, she must be actually ahead in some tracking polls, because all of a sudden, a world of hurt is coming down on her paid for by Mehmet Oz and businessman David McCormick.\par \par \uc2 And with Barnett's growing support comes growing concern within the GOP that her past including Islamophobic tweets and organizing of buses to the January 6 rally could cost them the seat being vacated by \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 . Somebody who actually voted to impeach the former president. Not too long ago, Barnette style and substance would have been considered way too far out of the mainstream, right to win a nomination and battleground state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But now, in an exclusive interview, Barnette told my colleague Dasha Burns of NBC News that her notoriety may be new, but her campaign has been around for more than a year. And she believes it represents the true will of Pennsylvania Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: We've been running this race for 13 months now, going thousands of miles all across the Commonwealth every single week. And yes, I was trying to tell whoever would listen, that when I'm walking into these rooms that were filled and yes, I asked people who in this room are new to politics as 2020, almost half the room would raise their hand.\par \par \uc2 Almost every single time about half the room. And I kept saying there's something new here. There's something going on here. We need to pay attention to this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Barnette's pass has become a target suddenly for Oz and McCormick with pro-McCormick super PAC and a Oz himself raising questions about what we don't know about her. Here's what she told Dasha about her message to the -- what is today's Republican establishment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You're stoking some fear within the GOP right now. What do you say to those in Washington who are concerned about your candidacy?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I will see you on January -- on May 18 and I look forward to working with them, to win a very important seat. Not just in Pennsylvania but for our nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republican angst is also very real in the race for primary for governor where \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , a state senator and key figure in Trump's efforts to overturn the 2020 election is becoming the clear front runner and a sign of that desperation among what remains of the Republican establishment, a candidate named Jake Corman, the top Republican in the state senate dropped out of the race for governor and endorsed former congressman Lou Barletta, who has been running second in most polls.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line here, Republicans have allowed a lot of fringe views to fester inside their party. And now they're wringing their hands about the rise of candidates that are embracing them in one of the nation's most important battleground states. Frankenstein has been created, and then suddenly Frankenstein has turned against the Republican leadership. Dasha Burns joins me now from Lititz, Pennsylvania and Lancaster County.\par \par \uc2 Also joining me on set, Leigh Ann Caldwell. Co-author of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Early 202 and an anchor of \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  live. Some of you may remember her from her days at NBC News. Republican strategist Jim Dornan and \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2 , an advisor to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and founder of the More Perfect Union. But Dasha, welcome to front runner status for Kathy Barnett. This is a fast. It just shows you I think how our information ecosystem has changed particularly on the right side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Her rise in many ways is a creation of this misinformation world of sort of Republican Trumpism and now they're trying to stop it. Do they have enough time?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:11]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: That is the million-dollar question, Chuck, because this is a major, major twist in -- perhaps the most high stakes race of the midterms. And listen, a lot of people did not see this coming. But here's from the ground up. We've been spending a lot of time for -- in Pennsylvania. Here's what sort of has been happening here on the ground. You have Oz and McCormick who have been battling it out on the airwaves, have been hitting each other hard with attack ads.\par \par \uc2 As we've been talking to voters, they've been watching that happen. And they haven't been thrilled with that. They -- a lot of the voters we've been talking to see those two front runners as carpetbaggers. They see those attacks and they buy them but they buy them from both sides. So, I have felt for months now voters sort of looking for that third option. Meanwhile, Barnette has been quietly traveling thousands and thousands of miles across Pennsylvania in rooms with voters.\par \par \uc2 And you heard her say there, something new was happening, people were paying attention, but something different was happening. And I think a lot of people were listening to her on the ground. We're on the internet instead of watching those T.V. ads and buying what some of the other campaigns were selling. And now you see this rush, Chuck, to do that oppo research that a lot of campaigns hadn't done because no one had seen Barnette as a threat before.\par \par \uc2 I'll tell you though, we sat down with her earlier this year back in February at the Republican State Committee meeting here in Pennsylvania. And she over predicted this, she said people are looking at these two guys with big money, but they're from out of state and we're here we're talking to voters and you're going to see what's going to happen. And you know what, she was not wrong.\par \par \uc2 But her campaign is going to have to be prepared. Now what happens when you're a front runner, the attacks start coming and now we're seeing some old tweets resurfacing, Islamophobic, homophobic tweets that we asked her about and take a listen to what she told us about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I believe is very unfair to take a snippet. I don't know how many characters that is, and say, OK, we kind of take this clip. And this is what she meant, right? We have no beginning, we have no end, we don't know what the context was.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I mean, this it says pedophilia is a cornerstone of Islam.\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes, no, I don't think that's me. I would never have said that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So Chuck, the heat is going to keep turning up for the next few days on Barnett. We'll see how their campaign responds. But that's the million- dollar question that you asked, is it too late? Are voters going to be watching this or have folks already made up their minds and you're seeing some of those numbers, she's going up, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha Burns has a couple more pieces of the interview. I'm going to play here, but I'm going to let you go to get back to some reporting. But I got a terrific panel here. So thank you, Dasha. And in fact, I want to play another response she has here guys, because in this case, I think this is a pretty good analysis of the Republican primary. And take a listen to what she said about the frontrunners, the other frontrunners in this race besides her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: There's been very little vetting that has taken place with these two "frontrunners." And so -- but the only thing I believe most people were interested in is that they were very rich, and they were spending a lot of money. I think it's going to say a lot about who is the Republican Party. Is it truly all you have to have is a high name I.D. and a lot of money? And that's all that matters to get someone through?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, whatever you think of her views, I think she actually broke down the primary. I can tell you the voters I talked to, I talked to some very, you know, very supportive of Trump, Republican primary voters in Pennsylvania who said, and I'd like to know more about Oz's position on abortion. What is in his head? They don't know. There are stuff they don't know about the -- she's absolutely right. And I think that's what's going to hurt them. Is there, hey, she's not vetted. Yes, are you?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Right. And also, when the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  decided to endorse in this race he went with Mehmet Oz. The one who is probably if you look at his "record" or what he has said in the past, the least conservative, the least -- most unknown and -- but he's on television, he had the highest name I.D. because people daytime television knew who Dr. Oz was.\par \par \uc2 And maybe that was the qualification for the former president to endorse him. But -- and that helps Dr. Oz for a while. But now you have this person who's coming from behind, because like you said, people don't know who these candidates are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And here's the irony. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I want to put up this full screen of what he said Faiz and Jim. Kathy Barnette will never be able to win the general election against the radical left Democrats. She has many things in her past, which have not been properly explained or vetted. But if she is able to do so she will have a wonderful future in the Republican Party and I will be behind her all the way.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trump sounds like a whole bunch of Republicans talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2015, Faiz. But listen to this. I want to play for you what she says about January 6, because this is what I mean when Frankenstein.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: You organize buses for January 6, seeing what happened in the aftermath of that, do you -- do you regret being a part of that at all?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I feel about January 6, the way the left feels about the summer of 2020 when you have \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . And so we need to be very mindful of what it is. Cancel Culture is real. I'm experiencing it and is not coming from the left, right now is coming from those -- from the -- some of those from the right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So Jim, I, you know, it's -- they're trying to stop her. She sounds more supportive of Trump than either Oz or McCormick.\par \par \uc2 JIM DORNAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Right. At shades of Mike Bryan, right coming up the middle now with Rokita and (INAUDIBLE) Indiana.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We see there's a lot and through and when you have two people that beat each other up.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The three gets benefit.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: I will tell you that I know folks inside Kathy Barnett's campaign. And they took Trump's statement as an endorsement. And you know what? It sounded like that to me. And I've always thought that Pennsylvania was going to be the toughest for us to hold here in the Senate. And it's proven me right, I'm afraid.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Faiz, I actually think it's fascinating. We could have John Fetterman and Kathy Barnette, not \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and David McCormick discuss.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think it'd be great.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whatever those parties here in Washington stay.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR, ADVISER TO \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2 : Yes, there's a remaking of the party going on, which is -- I think you sense as a -- an anti-establishment mood.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I do.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: And an anger towards who the leadership deems to be front runners. Well, if you're a big name in politics, and you said this should be the candidate, well, screw you. I still get to choose here. And I see quite frankly on the left too (INAUDIBLE) Senator Sanders yesterday with Summer Lee and Summer Lee is getting attacked by Super PACs, like \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  and a bunch of others.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's going to redound to our benefit because people are seeing it. They're getting inundated on T.V. and just like, what, you're telling me how to pick?\par \par \uc2 TODD: I'm telling you I saw this -- the Democrats are talking -- they're just so angry at Washington leadership. So, they're all sitting there and I think this is \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 's problem.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: His he's got the terrible position of having the first name of congressman at a time the Democratic Party is going. What the hell are they doing up there? So they're like, give me the guy in the -- in the -- in the cargo shorts.\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: Yes, absolutely. There is a Washington leadership, Schumer, McConnell, et cetera. They think about electability, but they try to determine electability months before the primaries actually happened, before the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Both the voters get to decide electability?\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: Right. Before they understand the mood of the country. But one something that's going to be really fascinating depending on what happens, especially on Tuesday, in Pennsylvania and after what happened in Ohio last week with J.D. Vance winning. The makeup of the Senate, especially the republican conference could be so different next year. You have someone J.D. Vance, perhaps replacing Rob Portman who's former OMB Director.\par \par \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , who is someone who is extremely wonky and policy, well-versed on economic policy. We'll see who wins the senate race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jim, the Republicans may have two nominees in Pennsylvania, the governor nominating \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who's basically an election denialists. And Kathy Barnette who's an election denialist in -- perhaps the single most important battleground state in the country.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: Absolutely. And they're in -- they're in serious trouble there. I mean, again, I'll go back to what I said before, I said, I knew this was going to be a problem. We're not offering candidates that can win. And to go to Leigh Ann's point, I've worked for the Senatorial Committee. Yes. They sit and meet a year ahead of time and say, this is who we think is going to be our best candidate. And you know what? Voters don't care about that anymore. They used to.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: It reminds me at the time when Republicans put up \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2  in Alabama and you get \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  in the Senate. You've had \uc2 Joe Donnelly\uc2  in Indiana, when you had Claire McCaskill win because the Republicans put up nominees that quite frankly, weren't (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: McConnell has been worried about this the whole time, but there's nothing he can do about it. He's powerless.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: But I think the power here for the Biden team too is to think about this as not just Pennsylvania, there's going to be an argument about do these people reflect the modern Republican Party, a national argument if you will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Jim, tactically you speak -- you can speak to this -- well, the two of you know this is former campaign managers, Pennsylvania, no early vote. So, all -- what Oz and McCormick, if this were Florida, or Georgia, or Texas with an early vote, they could have banked a bunch of sort of traditional Republican primary voters. Not Pennsylvania, that's the same day vote state. This to me is even more problematic for Oz and McCormick.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: Well, and this is where Absentee Chase Programs come into play very heavily. Because no early voting. It -- it's early voting without being early voting, so to speak.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: And it -- I haven't -- I have no knowledge of any program that these guys are running at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Pennsylvania's laws just make it very difficult.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They've never had a huge mailing thing. What does this mean for Missouri Senate? Right? I mean, you look at this and it goes through the -- I mean, are they really going to basically Donald Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here's Trump trying to find them a better candidate. And he can't control his own electorate anymore.\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: No. But the seeds have been sowed. This is years and years of like you said at the beginning, you know, the leaders accepting and playing into the far-right --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: They cultivate it.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: They did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn't -- they didn't tell it to go away.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn't do -- they didn't tell John Birch to leave the party. They cultivated it and now it's biting them.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: You have -- you have Leader McCarthy who embraced it. You have Leader McConnell in the Senate, who hasn't embraced it, but hasn't done anything to tamp it down. And this is what you get.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Jim, you're --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: It's amazing to me how cautious McConnell has been. Because he -- he's -- he -- history doesn't show that at all. Usually he does get involved. He does try to be the kingmaker and I think with Trump, he's just trying to keep his head above water.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Usually got this super PACs set aside, but he's usually dispensing a lot of money and endorsements and putting power behind what he picks as an establishment. And he's -- seemingly to my mind, afraid of Trump and not wanting to go out against his endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: I don't think he's afraid of Trump. In fact, I would -- I would think that if he thought it was to his advantage to take on Trump, he would.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don't forget how unpopular McConnell is inside the Republican -- this version of the Republican electorate. McConnell is aware that he could actually hurt somebody if he suddenly came along. I mean, put it this way. I don't think \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  would want \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DORNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: It's more subtle, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It's not -- it's, you know, it's -- he -- McConnell also has a lot of money though in his super --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: -- Trump has dragged down McConnell's name, brand and identity within the Republican ranks?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trump himself has poisoned Republicans against \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Again, they have does this to themselves. They are literally shooting themselves in the electoral foot. Jim, Faiz, Leigh Ann, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. NBC News (INAUDIBLE) Still to come. A one on one with former President Trump's Pentagon chief who's now calling the former president a threat to democracy. The former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  will join me up ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, Ukraine holds its first war crimes trial against a Russian soldier as fighting continues in the east. We get the latest from Kyiv coming up. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we discuss former President Trump's hold over the Republican Party, one of his former cabinet members are speaking out about what it is like serving under Trump, including that time he thought the Republic was "getting wobbly." The former president often publicly praised his military leader, sometimes calling them out by calling them of central casting.\par \par \uc2 But we are learning more about how he frequently turned to the U.S. armed forces to tackle foreign and domestic issues. Former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  chronicle some of the more outrageous requests, he says Trump made. Some previously unknown to the public until the release of his book, along with attempts to protect the country from some of the other former president's worst impulses.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Esper joins me now. His new book is called A Sacred Oath: Memoirs of a Secretary of Defense During Extraordinary Times. All right. Secretary Esper, I get a lot that I'd love to discuss about the book. But there is a thing that nags at all of us in journalism, all of us that believe in public service, and that is the fact that you waited until your book to tell us these things. Would you -- have you ever told us these things had President Trump won reelection?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Yes, absolutely. I would have told you these things. I would not have been in office. The reason why I waited was because I knew that if I told the stories at the time, I would be fired, which is a president's prerogative. Right, which is so. And here's my rationale. If you believe in putting your country first and living up to your oath, I thought that my duty was first and foremost to staying in the job and doing two things, advancing a really good agenda we had going on at the Pentagon, right?\par \par \uc2 Building cyber command, proposing a new Navy, modernizing the armed forces, taking care of our people, but at the same time beating back or reshaping some of these outlandish ideas that were coming out from others. And look on top of that, I didn't know who was coming in behind me. I was very concerned it would be some Uber Trump loyalists. Look, Chuck, I could resigned.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There was a bunch of Uber Trump loyalist.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I could resign, it would save me a lot of heartache. But I went as far as to talk to my predecessors from both parties to include the late General Colin Powell, to a person who said you need to stay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But here's where it -- and I say this, I understand your rationale. But here's you on May 15th of 2020 praising the president and I -- and I have the statement down for a reason. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, thank you, Mr. President, for your leadership of this bold and historic initiative. And, Mr. President, thank you again, for all that you've done.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: We will get the job done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: According to your book Six Days Earlier, the president met with you and top military leaders and members of the Joint Chiefs of Staff and you write the following. I couldn't imagine a worse performance or poor example by any commander in chief. Six days later, you praise him. You intentionally misled the public.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No. Not at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: About the commander in chief.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No. Not at all. No, not at all. Look where the president, President Trump had a lot of accomplishments during his administration. I made the some that the Democrats don't like the lower taxes, rebuilding the military, building on wall on the southwest border, so forth, and so on. And there were moments where I thought he exercises good leadership. Operation Warp Speed was clearly a success.\par \par \uc2 You and I wouldn't be sitting here today, if it wasn't for Operation Warp Speed. I thought that was a great decision that he moved forward on that. And by the way, others have complimented him as well for him. So, I think those are two distinct things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But all this praise, and then you go -- in here, it seems like you were privately, constantly worried about the president.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Certainly, after June 1st. And look, I came out on June 3rd, and publicly rebuke him and said that I did not support an invocation of the insurrection act before that. I had to come out in January 2020, and said that I did not support, that the United States military would never attack Iranian cultural historic sites. You can go back further when I came out and spoke out publicly. Look, it wasn't all bad. It wasn't all good.\par \par \uc2 So, at times, I would come out and say what I thought was appropriate to the moment with regard to the president and other times, not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If he had won reelection and you for five months knew of his behavior. In June 1st, you talk about one June and I've heard this from General Milley. June 1st was a -- was a marker for a lot of you. And I -- and I accept that line of demarcation. You still didn't tell us for the election. Do you all have any regrets about that?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, I feel better today about it. Because I wrestled with this decision to time. And look, like you, I was also watching the polls. President Trump was anywhere from four to 10 percentage points behind President Biden at the time, right? I could see where things were born. But again, I thought given the position I occupied as the head of the department defense, given ideas being proposed from shooting missiles into Mexico or quarter million troops to the border to the point where there was talk about should we strike Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I thought it was better that I be in the position to prevent those things from happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did you not trust your Joint Chiefs? So mean. General Milley had said he was not going to execute --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Yes. But general -- but this is what you have to understand about the Pentagon. General Milley has no authority. He's an advisor. The authority runs from the president to the Secretary of Defense to the combatant commanders. That's the chain of command. And look, Milley did a great job trying to explain this to people, but he's not in the chain of command, Chuck. And that's why that position matters so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had the temperament to be president?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When did you know this? When did it become clear to you that this man is not --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, I think crystal clear to me on June 1st, right? That's the most I've ever seen him upset the way it was. Yelling, walking around, ranting about the issue what was happening in Washington, D.C. That became the pivotal moment for me. That's what resulted in, you know, General Milley not coming up with the so called foreknows.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell us about the foreknows. I mean, you went through it but I -- and I -- and I think it's important that you had these, but the fact that you had them, again, I'm trying to square. Why didn't you warn the country soon?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: So, as I write about in the book, there's this time after impeachment in January 2020 when it seems we move to a new level, if you will, the White House brings in fresh troops, I say more loyalists. And we start seeing more of these outlandish ideas about troops to the border, striking Mexico, so forth and so on. And what firms up into the foreknows becomes this notion of we can't allow no strategic retreats, no unnecessary wars, no misuse of the military and no politicization of DOD.\par \par \uc2 And that became for me the marker by which I would got myself. Look, I struggled with this, like I said, I consulted with folks, many times I talked to my wife, she said famously, if you will, look, as your wife, please quit, as an American, please stay. This was not an easy decision for me, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you saw him do what he did with Ukraine, hold aid hostage, which of course now looks even worse in hindsight. Was that not a moment before June 1st that you thought, boy, he does not understand the job.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, let's expand the lens. He also deserves credit for approving the sale of lethal weapons to defend -- to Ukraine. Nobody else had done that, right? To include the javelins. He also supported to continue training of the Ukrainians by the United States in western Ukraine. I was there, I visited the site. So yes, I think that was a mistake. It should not have been held up I, John Bolton, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  and others pressed him on that.\par \par \uc2 But look, in end of the day I said it, then I'll say it now. I don't think it made a material difference with regard to what's happening today in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you saw what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I'm guessing number one you were glad you weren't in your job. Is that fair to say? Or did you -- did you suddenly get nervous that you weren't there?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, you always think that if you were there, you might have handled things differently. But who knows? It doesn't matter now. I was out of office for two months. I got fired a week afterward. And, you know, I did my duty as I saw it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Post election. Did you see January 6 coming? Did you have a feeling that he was going to start now to play -- to play these games?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, I write about this in the book. And keep in mind, some people say you waited two years. I finished this book four months after I left office. And I wrote at the time that the -- I thought that he would contest it in certain states. And he wouldn't be the first president to contest an election in certain states. And then it would all fade away. He'd reconcile with himself. And I didn't see. I don't think many people did see January 6 coming.\par \par \uc2 And it was a tragic, horrible event. I write about that as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- right. I guess -- I keep coming back to the fact that it does seem as if you were -- you saw the potential of his irrational behavior. And suddenly it came to fruition on what he did in and around January 6. Looking back now, you still have no regret about speaking out sooner, you still have no regret about speaking up --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, because I don't think it would have made a material difference. What's -- I mean, he lost the election. What's the complaint? He lost the election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There's a lot of people in this country that thinks his -- that thinks his complaints are legitimate. And they're beyond illegitimate. It seems like you seem to know that. It doesn't it -- wouldn't more people know the truth about him if you'd spoken up soon?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, that didn't change the election. What was -- to me, what was -- he was getting to the election without enough political interference involving DOD. That was just one piece I talked about this about the final days, I'm concerned about with troops somehow being employed the day after the election. So, look, me coming out would not have changed the election. It would have left DOD leaderless at a critical time in our nation.\par \par \uc2 We're fighting COVID, we got troops possibly in the streets. We'd already seen seven months without DOD leadership once before which panic Congress in some ways. I thought stepping away would have been the wrong thing to do for our country. Would have been great for me, Chuck, but the wrong thing to do for the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know how comfortable he seems to be. He seems to almost look up to the authoritarian leaders. Your Putins, your Erdogans, your (INAUDIBLE) he seems to thumb his nose at the Merkels.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Yes, I mentioned that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You talk about that. Do you think his instinct is of an authoritarian leader that he's -- does he look to them because he wishes he had that kind of authority? What does he -- why do you think he seems to hold them in higher regard?\par \par \uc2 [13:30:06]\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, he tends to put people into two categories, right, strong and weak. And if you're somebody he liked, you're strong. If you're somebody don't like you're weak. And I don't understand it, it's something you had to factor in and try and, you know, put into your calculous as you handled issues, but I can't explain it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, there's a lot of people who would look at you and say, huh, you waited to tell the best stories when you could make a dollar out of it. What do you say to those?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Look, I want to tell a story about what was a very consequential time in American history. I think it's critical for the American people to know this critical moment, what happened, the mistakes I made, successes I achieved, et cetera. And particularly before we go into the midterms, like I said, I wrote this in four months right after I left office and suffered through the DOD review process for about 10 months. And so look, it's important that the American people know these stories. And I'm not the first secretary of Defense to write a book. I think nearly all my predecessors have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I know that. But there's plenty of reporters where you guys denied stories that turned out to be true.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Name one that I denied.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think --\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Name one that I denied. Name one that I denied.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I'm not going to put -- I'm not going to violate some agreements that other reporters make. It's not my agreement that I made. I'm not going to violate that. But there's plenty of things that seem to be denied by the Pentagon that later you've confirmed.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: You'd have to cite it because I was always honest with the press in terms of how I engage, if I didn't want to talk about it, I wouldn't talk about it. Look, it's important the American people know this history. There's always going to be these questions about when should you say this. People criticized one of my predecessors \uc2 Bob Gates\uc2  because he released his book during the Obama administration, and Democrats complained then you shouldn't release a book while you have a sitting president. OK, this is not new news in this regard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That's a fair point. Former secretary of Defense, \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 . You're certainly making a lot of noise during your book tour. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Good. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the House January 6th Committee takes the extraordinary step of subpoenaing five Republican members of Congress for testimony. But how far is the committee and the Democratic Congress willing to go to actually compel the testimony? That's next.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:45]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It was during this hour that we brought you the breaking news that the House committee investigating the January 6th insurrection has decided to take an unprecedented step in subpoenaing sitting members of Congress, five of them in total. Committee members Jamie Raskin and \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  both said yesterday they fully expect the five Republican members including the House Republican leader Kevin McCarthy to cooperate with the subpoena, but based on the reaction so far, compliance sounds far from certain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): Well, I think this is an illegitimate committee and they don't really have the authority to issue subpoenas in my opinion so we don't want to dignify what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Does that mean that you will not comply with the subpoena?\par \par \uc2 BIGGS: Well, I haven't even seen the subpoena so how can I -- I really can't tell you whether I'm going to comply or not.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Look, my view of the committee has not changed. They're not conducting a legitimate investigation. It seems as though they just want to go after their political opponents.\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT PERRY (R-PA): This is all for headlines and sensationalization. The fact that they sent it to the press before they sent it to the members just proves it's all about headlines. This whole thing is a charade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And in a lengthy written statement Congressman Mo Brooks who's in the middle a Senate primary right now in Alabama said he would wait and consult with the other subpoenaed members before announcing if he'll comply. He's been back and forth about whether he will tell more stories especially after former President Trump pulled his endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Still, defying a valid subpoena is against the law. That's clear. What's unclear is what comes next, and how the legislative branch will enforce the subpoena because all five members were asked to testify at the end of the month ahead of public hearings in June, but a lengthy court fight of the committee subpoena powers could complicate that. Then comes the question of enforcement and what legal tools the committee and Congress is willing to use to hold its own members accountable.\par \par \uc2 So here to answer a couple of these legal questions today, former federal prosecutor and MSNBC analyst, Carol Lam.\par \par \uc2 So, Carol, this is one of those cases where it's all taking place in one branch of government. You have separation of power issues that could come into play here, which is why there is -- we're all figuring out what is the precedent, what's the precedent. Is Congress the judge and jury here?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, that's exactly the right question to ask, Chuck, because Congress may be the right branch to ask, but it depends how they decide to go about this. So they can take one of several options. They could do what they did with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and they could -- and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , and they could refer this as a criminal content upon an entire House vote over to the Department of Justice for enforcement of the criminal subpoena -- I'm sorry, enforcement of a subpoena in the criminal process if in fact these representatives decide not to cooperate and testify pursuant to the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 That then puts the decision in the hands of the Justice Department and as you can see that's a very slow deliberative process that the Justice Department has to go through. And to date they have only proceeded with the subpoena enforcement against \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . They could also try a civil enforcement proceeding in the courts but that also takes a long time, and time is not in the committee's favor on this.\par \par \uc2 They've set a very strict self-imposed deadline to get these hearings done during the month of June. The last possibility is the possibility that you raised, that they keep this within the legislative branch and they try to find some kind of -- something that they could take away from the members or some sanction they could impose on the members, that would in fact persuade them to cooperate and testify in the form of financial penalty or taking away some committee assignments. But again will that really persuade these members to testify? I think there's a big question mark there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have one idea that could actually persuade them to testify, Carol, and it would be this. Do they want their subpoenas complied with if they're in the majority?\par \par \uc2 LAM: Right. And that comes to the unprecedented nature of this. And you know, Chuck, I think that when you're looking at this picture as a -- you know, is the committee going to, quote, "win," by getting the subpoenas complied with, or are the representatives going to win by playing out the clock? I think that's assuming that the members of the committee really think that they're going to comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 And I know that statements have been made along to that effect, but I have to think that realistically the committee doesn't really believe that all of these members are going to really comply with these subpoenas. And so what is the end game here given the tight timeline. I think the end game is, you know, it comes to a point before you have these open hearings, it comes to a point where you have to say, all right, they haven't cooperated voluntarily so far.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAM: Which they haven't. And so we're just going to do it. We're going to subpoena them with an actual official subpoena, which is an unprecedented situation and we're going to put them in the position of having to either comply with it or be the first sitting representatives in Congress to defy a subpoena from the House.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And then, Carol, I guess I would throw in one other, you know, as a news organization when we are reporting a tough story and the person we're reporting it about, we want to give them a chance to respond, hey, look, we've got -- there's these allegations, here's what we have, and we want to make it clear. We gave you plenty of time to respond. This could be the other reason they did this, right, which is they know a lot of what these guys did.\par \par \uc2 They have a lot of text messages. They may tell a story and they say, hey, we'd love a clarification, but they've refused to tell us their side of the story that I assume is now the committee can say we gave you ever chance, in fact, we tried to compel you to give us your side of the story.\par \par \uc2 LAM: There's no question, Chuck. There is something that you do in criminal investigations as well, you often say all right, we have the evidence from third parties that we're -- we think we can gather. Now if you want to come and testify in front of the grand jury we're going to give you that opportunity, and so if you decide not to do that, don't come back to us later and say, hey, you didn't give me a chance to explain my side of the story. I think that plays into this move a great deal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carol Lam, former U.S. attorney, always good to get your expertise and clarification on all this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Russian forces ramped up their assault in eastern Ukraine as one of their soldiers faces the first war crimes trial in Kyiv. We're going to go live in the Ukrainian capital just ahead.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back. The first war crimes trial of the war in Ukraine started this morning in Kyiv as a Russian soldier appeared in court this morning on a charge by Ukraine's head prosecutor that he shot an unarmed 62-year-old man riding a bicycle in northeast Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 He's the first member of Russian armed forces to be prosecuted for a war crime in this conflict, as allegations of unprovoked civilians killing and other horrors just pileup against members of the Russian military. The soldier's lawyer told NBC News that his client was detained after he surrendered. And he's asked for a trial by a judge, not a jury. He has not entered a plea.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Biden spoke with leaders in Sweden and Finland this morning after Russia threatened military action against both countries if they decide to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Finland's foreign minister has explicitly stated the country's intention to become part of the alliance. Sweden is expected to decide in the coming days whether they will do it as well.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Kyiv with more after the break. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. For the latest on the war in Ukraine, my colleague, Erin McLaughlin joins me from Kyiv, and NBC News national security analyst Clinton Watts is here to break down where Russian troops are right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:01]\par \par \uc2 But, Erin, I want to start with you and I want to talk about this war crimes trial. And this is one. How many more in the queue? How many more soldiers are they investigating? And why this one, and, you know, is this just one of many or one they want to use to make a point?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, this is just the tip of the iceberg. There are potentially thousands, maybe even tens of thousands of war crimes allegations, potential trials, especially if you look at some of the horrors that have unfolded in cities such as Mariupol. But this is significant because this is a Russian soldier that the Ukrainians currently have in custody.\par \par \uc2 He himself has admitted through his court-appointed attorney that he turned himself in to Ukrainian forces. The prosecutor has put together her case. She's made it very public how she thinks this killing unfolded. She alleges that this 21-year-old sergeant shot and killed a 62-year-old Ukrainian civilian. And it happened at the beginning. He was traveling in a column of tanks, Russian tanks that were moving toward the Ukrainian city of Sumy.\par \par \uc2 That column came under fire by Ukrainian forces. They took out the lead tank in the column. From there he and three other Russian soldiers tried to make an escape. They hijacked a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and drove it toward a Ukrainian village. As they approached the village, they saw the civilian on a bike. He was on the phone. They sort of assumed, according to the prosecutor, that he was calling for Ukrainian backup, and they shot this particular soldier following orders from another soldier in the car, shot and killed him.\par \par \uc2 That is the case as stated by the Ukrainian prosecutor. We have yet to hear his version of events. His attorney today declined to comment on the details of the case. Again, it is a court-appointed attorney. This particular attorney is a private attorney, a Ukrainian, that has a contract with the government to offer sort of defense. And that's the facts as we know them. But again it's potentially a very significant moment for the Ukrainian judicial system because again this is one of potentially tens of thousands of cases. And in speaking to the prosecutor general myself, she says she is adamant to pursuing justice for every single case. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it shows you the strength of this democracy in the middle of a war that everything is still working as it's supposed to. That's the hallmarks of a small D, democratic, nation there.\par \par \uc2 Erin McLaughlin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  here. And Clint, we had a little breaking news, which is that Turkish President Erdogan is starting to -- may object to both Sweden and Finland coming into \uc2 NATO\uc2  due to -- where they are on the issue of the Kurds. So I wanted to slip that in there saying that this -- there could be a little wrench here and of course we know that Erdogan and Putin have had their on again-off again ties before.\par \par \uc2 But give me the update. It's been -- for this week have the Russians made progress or lost progress?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Chuck, I would say on aggregate lost progress. And here's why. Last week we saw the Ukrainians in the northeast very close to Kharkiv advance east tens of kilometers. And when they did that, they put pressure on essentially the command lines that goes down to Izyum. Izyum is a key access, basically due south of Kharkiv, and the Russians need to take that location so they can create a larger envelopment around Ukrainian forces in the eastern Donbas.\par \par \uc2 They have not been able to advance. This is also where there's reports of General Gerasimov meeting with a lot of senior officers, potentially being wounded there, but also many other senior officers been killed. If that is true, and that would make sense based on what we see on the ground, we're seeing very slow progress on that access and it's causing the Russians to have to move back north and essentially trying to defend their rear lines.\par \par \uc2 That is a setback for them. That's that light blue area you see there around Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Separately in and around Luhansk, very small incremental advances by the Russians. And in many ways it's just the Ukrainians trading ground for time because amidst this, what you see is Ukrainian reinforcements, use of artillery picking up, the U.S. is now trained along with \uc2 NATO\uc2  partners who many individuals in Ukraine and military use those new artillery pieces and you're starting to see those switch blade drones being deployed for the first time. That is going to be devastating for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 In the south, the other aspect is those troops that were in Mariupol for the Russians, they're being deployed north to Donetsk. While they're going up there, they need a rest. They've been in intense combat for more than two months, and then the question is, how motivated are they really to advance north whenever they've been in such intense fighting and it hasn't gone well. I think they're probably not too motivated.\par \par \uc2 They've also seen a lot of their leaders die. And so when you look and aggregate at the manpower, particularly in the south and the east, what is success?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 You've got to believe in the cause, you've got to believe in the plan, you have to believe in your leaders, and you have to believe in each other. And I think Russia is 0 for 4 and Ukraine is 4 for 4 in those categories.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I'm curious, does making a big public show of the war crimes trial have a morale impact on Russian soldiers? I mean, it's an unanswerable question, but you can't help but wonder that it might.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I don't know at this point but it is an interesting sort of contrast to what the Russians have been hearing. So imagine you're a Russian soldier, you thought this was going to be a cake walk when you go into Ukraine. You meet resistance, you commit war crimes, now you're seeing a lot of your comrades essentially being detained and now being put on trial for war crimes.\par \par \uc2 The question is, do they hear it? Do they even know about it? Or are they aware? I think it will trickle through over time. It will take some time. But it would definitely be a current that they probably didn't anticipate and one that they might be concerned about. Either way I think it's the right thing to do because what you're seeing is the Russian way of warfare that is often ignored on other battlefields. Syria is a great examples of this. Chechnya, great example of this. Ignored by the West, Ukraine now we're starting to see what really happens in this conflict.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You're right, it is important for the world to get an idea of how they're trained in combat.\par \par \uc2 Erin McLaughlin before Clint, and \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We'll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And of course if it's Sunday, it's MEET THE PRESS on your local NBC News station. We've got a good for you including a brand-new NBC News poll. It's a big midterm shift. You don't want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC's coverage will continue with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220514ei5d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ellison Barber, Ali Vitali, Courtney Kube, Carol Lee,  James Stavridis \par \uc2 9,612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday. Paying at the pump, rising food costs, inflation above 8 percent, market turmoil, rising interest rates, and recession fears. We are entering some uncharted economic waters on the most important issue to voters.\par \par \uc2 Plus, day 78 of the war in Ukraine still with no end in sight, as Russia threatens retaliatory action if Finland joins \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and Zelenskyy claims to make battleground games in the north.\par \par \uc2 And what Democrats plan to do next to tackle the abortion issue as a bill to codify Roe v. Wade fails in the Senate. I`ll speak with one of the co- authors of that legislation still ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. Back from our midterm trip to Pennsylvania. And we`ll be digging more into the midterms a little bit later. But we`re going to begin today`s program with the single most important issue for voters, obviously, the economy, which is rapidly entering some uncharted waters, with costs continuing to rise, gas prices at an all-time high, a hot war in Eastern Europe, and a whole lot of market uncertainty as policymakers try to cool down demand without tipping the U.S. into a painful recession.\par \par \uc2 The issue obviously brings with it enormous political fallout here at home, that`s obvious, could also rattle the West`s solidarity to counter Russia abroad. That`s of concern. And it`s why we start the show today with this question, though, what the heck is going on with this economy? And what should our leaders be doing about it? And how fearful should we be that we don`t know what the bottom looks like yet?\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with the numbers. It`s no surprise to anyone who`s been at a gas station or grocery store recently, that you`re paying more for the essentials, meat, eggs, and fuel, those rising costs are inescapable to anyone right now in America. AAA says that the average price of a gallon of gas right now in the United States is $4.42 for regular and $5.56 for diesel, both are record highs.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you in northeastern Pennsylvania, diesel was $6.50. It`s all up -- that`s up 40 and 50 percent -- 50 cents, respectively from just last month. When the price of gas and diesel goes up, it puts a pinch on everything, manufacturers, consumers, you, and the people you work for.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pain out there, there`s a severe baby formula shortage hitting the country right now with shelves empty in stores. That is more about how those companies manage their supply, more so than it is anything else. But it only adds to the consternation that people feel right now about the economy. The president is expected to announce some action on the baby formula supply emergency later today.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said earlier this week that inflation is his top domestic priority. But what does that mean? The administration points to actions it is taking too like tapping the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and expanding the use of ethanol as a -- and fuel as a way to try to ease some pricing. But the reality is that so many of the forces fueling this issue are beyond the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s control. And Biden`s top economic advisors suggested that a lot of this price pay could be around for a while.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA EISEN, CNBC ANCHOR: Where do you think CPI is in a year from now?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA ROUSE, CHAIR, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Well, that is -- you know, it`s a tough call. There`s a lot of uncertainty, but forecasters really do expect that prices will moderate over the coming year. So I expect it to be lower but you know, it`s not necessarily going to be linear. We can`t just focus month to month and we understand there are headwinds. But I do think it will be -- it will be lower than it is today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And folks, what makes our current situation so unique right now, is that all of this concern and uncertainty is coming amid strong job growth and low unemployment. That`s what makes us so weird. Wall Street likes to look for green shoots, but right now, even they aren`t finding much or they don`t like the green shoots that they see as economists, employers, workers, consumers, policymakers and elected leaders try to figure out what happens next and as President Biden told donors last night, inflation is going to scare the living hell out of everybody. I don`t know if it`s going to scare I would argue it is scary.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber is in Gainesville, Georgia. She`s taking a look at the human toll of these rising prices. We`re also got a pair of an economist with us. Joining me on set is Douglas Holtz-Eakin, the president of the American Action Forum, and a former CBO director under -- during the Bush years, CBO, of course, \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 , and \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , a former labor department chief economist under President Obama, now, a professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Ellison, I want to start with you. And again, you know, this is not about covering markets. This is about the day-to-day lives, people are living. You`re in Georgia -- this you know, this is not a lot of public transportation to mitigate fuel costs. What are you seeing and hearing?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: People are frustrated. People are worried. We have been to three different counties, all of them politically important and very different politically. When we ask people who or what they think is to blame for the prices that they`re seeing at the gas pump and also in the grocery store, they gave various answers to that question.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BARBER: But everyone we spoke to, they said the prices that they are seeing right now, especially at gas stations like this one, they say it is ridiculous. And for some of them, it is up-ending their budgets. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Do you have an option other than driving?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN WHALEY, GWINNETT COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: I don`t. I do not. It`s my only transportation and I`ve got a little one, so it`s like I feel like all my gas is going -- or all my money is going towards gas at this point.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: How much of your salary does it end up cutting into?\par \par \uc2 JOSH, DEKALB COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: A lot of -- I think my groceries, got cheese, got everything that goes go in with this, so it is kind of hit me like crazy.\par \par \uc2 ALEX SPEIR, HALL COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: I work at the clinic down the road, and a lot of patients have come in and complained about it. How -- I`ve had so many people have to cancel their appointments because they can`t afford gas to come, get to their appointments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: I am a Georgia girl, I grew up in Atlanta. My parents live a little over an hour away from here in a more rural part of the state, and they don`t have a lot of doctors where they live. When they need to go to the doctors, they have to come here to Gainesville. It`s an hour and 15-minute drive each way. That is not an uncommon story. And rural communities around here, they`ve seen a lot of jobs go over the years, people are having to look further for work, they have to drive more to get to work.\par \par \uc2 And you touched on it, there`s not a lot of public transportation options here. There`s some in Atlanta, but it`s not that great. They`re in rural areas, it really doesn`t exist. And what people are telling us is they`re looking at what they have to pay to fill up their car to get to and from work, run basic errands, take their kids to school.\par \par \uc2 Then, they`re looking at their paycheck and they say that they really feel like they are having trouble affording the basic necessities. It is costing them so much money just to get to work. This is a real pain for a lot of people here in the big cities, in the suburbs, and especially in rural parts of Georgia, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ellison, I`m so glad you shared exactly how a lot of people outside the acylic corridor (PH). I really am concerned that people that mostly live in New York or the DC area just don`t appreciate how regressive rising fuel of a -- regressive tax rising fuel prices are. Ellison, terrific on the ground, people-focus reporting there.\par \par \uc2 So let me bring in my two economists here. Douglas Holtz-Eakin, good to see you here on set.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CBO DIRECTOR UNDER PRESIDENT BUSH: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , you`re on satellites, I`ll let you go first with the first comment here. And it`s -- this is the conundrum is that a rising fuel price hits basically those with the fewest -- the lowest savings the most and hits them the hardest, it`s working people, may have to drive to two jobs. Working people have to drive farther. This is what I think makes this feel like such a rough period of time that we`re entering.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY, STEVENSON, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN: People are definitely frustrated from rising gas prices. And I mean, that`s because they see it every single day as they go to the pump and they fill up. So, you know, I know this is hitting everybody in their wallets in a way that`s making them unhappy, scared, and forcing them to cut back in other areas.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it`s worth pointing out this. The inflation we`re facing more broadly is not being driven by rising fuel prices, not being driven by this rising gas price, and unfortunately, this -- what`s happening at the pump has everything to do with Putin and it has everything to do with the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think the broader concerns about the economy is the inflation we`re seeing in other places and whether we`re seeing any kind of receipt -- you know, any kind of pullback in that. You know, we see obviously rent is going up. Rent is going up because the value of people`s homes are going up, they`re able to charge more and rent, and that`s going to continue to spill through these numbers as people sign new leases.\par \par \uc2 But one of the reasons why inflation is so awful is because it is very uneven. It hits some people harder than others. If you own your own house, inflation is not getting to you as much because you`re not paying higher rent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: If you just signed a new lease, inflation really put a damper on what you can spend. Just like if you`re someone who drives two hours to work every day back and forth, inflation is hitting you a lot harder than somebody who can walk down the road to work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this gets at the class dividedness, right? Basically, those who can afford to live close, those -- and this is why I go to this Washington, New York Nexus here. I think, truly, this is a uniquely out-of-touch situation here for the acylic corridor.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And it`s way worse than the numbers would suggest. If you look at the food, energy, and housing, that -- what you think of as the essentials, that`s 50 percent of the typical family budget. The average inflation rate and an annual rate since the president took office is 19.8 percent. That`s huge. And that`s what people are going through every day, all across this country. And so, obviously, they`re scared about the future. They want to know what`s going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s what -- my question to both of you and Doug, I`ll start with you.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do we stall low unemployment? I mean at some point, does business pull back -- you know I think the next thing we expect is that OK, inflation means the business will pull back some investment that usually also translates into hiring fewer people.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So, as it turns out, we have an incredibly strong labor market, low unemployment you know, 1.7 job openings for everybody out there who`s looking for a job. And that`s the flip side of the same coin is having the inflationary pressures. This economy is running so hot, and the labor market is so hot, that it`s generating inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 So, we got that, in part, because we did big stimulus in an already hot economy, did that 1951, we`ve got a six percentage point increases inflation, did it again last year. So can`t do that. And the key figure is \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the chairman of the Fed whose job it is to tamp down on that red hot economy. That means slowing down the labor market. That means slowing down the housing market. But it also means we`re leaving the inflationary pressures. He`s got no good choices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: You got inflation, or you got to slow things down a bit. And he`s trying to do with a latter in a way that doesn`t tip us into recession.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Betsey, how do we avoid a recession?\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: I think you avoid a recession by making sure that the Fed doesn`t act too quickly, doesn`t -- you know, as we think about slam on the brakes, they need to ease on the brakes, and we`re seeing them do that by right -- raising rates slowly. You know, there`s -- when you think about what we need to happen, we actually need more people to apply for jobs.\par \par \uc2 The prop -- the problem that`s causing high inflation is we can`t provide all the goods and services that people want. As a result, prices are getting pushed up. So what the Feds going to do is try to reduce demand. What the -- you know, there`s pressure on the government to do is reduce demand, by a sort of, you know, we`ve already seen them pull away from the support they were giving to families, so pulling away from that income, reducing demand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: And that feels really counterintuitive to people because what they actually want is they want to see supply increase. They want to be able to buy -- you know, they want to be able to buy as much gas as they`re buying. They don`t want for everybody to pull back on buying gas, and that, to me, is what drives the price down. But at the end of the day, that`s what the Fed is going to try to do. It raises rates. Tried to convince people to buy fewer cars. They buy fewer cars. That tried to slow the rate of increase in the price of cars, and it lowers the demand for gas.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the process. And the question will be how quickly it happens versus how slowly whether or not they can realize, hey, we`ve slowed demand enough that we don`t need so far that would put us into a recession. I think the real uncertainty is that there`s -- problems all around the globe and they`re having to steer through, you know, really turbulent times with what`s happening in Europe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, and look, the two of you are not diplomats. I mean, you know, the fuel -- meaning the fuel -- the fuel issue could be solved by Saudi Arabia But that`s a -- that`s a whole another ball of -- a ball of wax that I want to get you. And Doug, do you want to jump in?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes, I think a theme underneath both our comments is that in the absence of a mistake, we`re not going to have a recession. I think the recession talk is way too big right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what is this going to look like?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me -- tell me what the bottom looks like here? And is -- what are we seeing in the market -- as the markets so drop a little bit of a crypto thing and a little bit of a bubble issue on top of inflation?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Lesson number one, do not evaluate the economy based on the stock market.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Never do that. Mark has been terrible recently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: No question about that. Why? It was probably too high, to begin with. A lot of -- a lot of cash flowed into the financial markets, both because the Fed printed $5 trillion in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Put it in financial markets. And because we send out checks and people got \uc2 Robinhood\uc2  apps and (INAUDIBLE) crypto is cool, and they do look game stop on the side. And now that`s going away, rates are going up and they`re coming back to reality. I`m not worried about the stock market. It always moves first and anticipates what`s going to happen down the line. It`s readjusting to the new reality. That`ll be fun.\par \par \uc2 I think the economy itself is going to grow solidly this year, solidly next year in the absence of something we don`t know about. And then there are only three names to worry about, Powell, and we pray that he does a good job, and land softly, Putin, and G. And I think the underappreciated part of this is China, which is a realist --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I agree that`s the least. Betsey, talk to the 60-something-year-old that was contemplating retirement and they`re looking at their 401k going oh, Jesus, I guess not this year.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Well, you know, I think I would say take a longer-term look at it because boy, you`re probably in a better position today than you thought you were going to be five years ago. So --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You could say?\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Let`s try to take a glass half full instead of a glass half empty look at this. So I think that`s part of what I would have said to them. What were your retirement plans a couple of years ago, because you haven`t lost everything? It`s just not where he thought it was going to be a couple of months ago, or six months ago. And I think the other thing to think about is to look at the other assets you own. Do you own a house? Because the house went -- price went up a lot.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: So you`ve probably seen your portfolio is not doing as bad as you might think it`s doing. And I think if you`re 60 years old, you`re probably on track to retire when you want to, which is hopefully not too soon because we actually want to see labor force participation of people in their early 60s, you got a lot of live-in love to do, and I hope you`re really successful in your job for at least another five years, if not longer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Doug, you know, I want to bring up the baby formula issue because that appears to be more of a -- of an issue of the just-in-time supply chain approach to economies.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I think this is a question that I wonder. Does the pandemic make a bunch of industries rethink how they do this? And, and maybe with baby formula, is it an argument that the government says hey, you got to have a -- this is not something we can -- we can have sort of go up and down based on, you know, one disruption screwing up everything here?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So point number one is, I think they`ve already rethought a lot of this just in time, particularly across the global supply chain.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And that`s a business risk, it costs them a lot of money, they have every incentive to fix it themselves and certainly, with the baby formula, that`s a combination of an outage at an important supplier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And then the -- and the no inventories. We don`t have inventory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s the point, it`s the no inventory approach to business.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: People are doing whole more inventories because they can find out the business requires it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Douglas Holtz-Eakin and \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , thank you both, really appreciate it. I got -- sorry about that. I got some breaking news we got to get to from Capitol Hill because the January 6 committee is out. They`ve been obviously investigating the insurrection. They just issued subpoenas to five sitting members of Congress, including the House Republican leader, Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 This sets up quite the legal showdown here. So let`s bring in our Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali. So, Ali, there has been a debate for months in this January 6 committee whether or not to issue formal subpoenas to sitting members of Congress because it`s not clear they`ll ever be able to make them stick. But here we are.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chairman Thompson has said in the past too that he doesn`t know if they`re actually able to do this, but nevertheless, the committee ultimately decided after several months of deliberation here to subpoena five House Republican lawmakers, including the top Republican Kevin McCarthy. You see these other members on the screen, Scott Perry, Jim Jordan, Andy Biggs, and Mo Brooks, all allies of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in the press release that we just got from Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 . He says that these colleagues have brought information relevant to our investigation into the attack on January 6. He goes on to say before we hold our hearings next month, we wish to provide members the opportunity to discuss these matters voluntarily, they have issued letters asking all of these lawmakers to come before them and cooperate.\par \par \uc2 All of them have said no, some of them in the case of Mo Brooks have even preemptively said that they would say and fight a subpoena so we`re about to get into that legal period. But Thompson goes on to say that they urge their colleagues to comply with the law, do their patriotic duty, and cooperate with our investigations as hundreds of other witnesses have done.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was striking these letters are in some cases pretty short. But the one to McCarthy just says we are left with no choice but to issue you a subpoena. So this tees up a huge legal battle, Chuck, the first time that this committee has actually issued subpoenas to their colleagues, and a rare, rare move here. We can`t think of anything in recent memory of another time that a House committee has subpoenaed House lawmakers, but here we are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, this almost feels like a bit of a, surrendering in this respect. There`s no other way they think they`re going to get cooperation so they figure all right, let`s try the subpoena route because it -- I feel like everybody had explained to me that they thought the subpoena route would actually delay their ability to do the investigation, and perhaps run out the clock if control of the House of Representatives changes.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So is this an acknowledgment that there`s really -- they`re no, they`re not going to get cooperation so they`re just going to make this hurt?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I think it`s an acknowledgment that they need to do this because they`ve got nowhere left to go if they want the cooperation here. Perhaps the headline of the subpoena the fact that this is so unprecedented or with very few precedents in this body, I think that is the point here because you and I both know, they are up against the clock on the midterms.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans were to retake the House, this committee goes away. But more than that, as soon as things reach the courts, they tend to slow down or just grind to a halt. Think of all of those criminal contempt referrals that are just sitting waiting to be taken up by the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Once things get to the courts, that`s where the process gets really gummy, really slow, and I think that the committee was well aware of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say do they have any sort of plan to expedite these court -- these court hearings?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. Look, we just got this release. So if it`s in there, and I missed it on my first very, very quick scan of this letter, then I haven`t seen it.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But at the same time, you got to imagine that is where their heads are out here on the committee. They`re four weeks away from the first rounds of public hearings. This is something Chairman Thompson previewed to us that they were going to be reissuing letters in some cases to these lawmakers. Certainly, the question of the subpoena has been up in the air. But there are also other letters that were waiting for them to issue too. Chairman Thompson who told me a few weeks ago that they wanted to talk to Republican senators, we haven`t seen those letters yet, either.\par \par \uc2 And so while they`re still trying to make this the most compelling case to get these people to testify, now, actually issuing subpoenas, there`s also still other things that we`re waiting for them to put out there because they`ve told us that those are avenues that they want to explore. And again, for weeks until that first public hearing, they wanted to have their fact-finding done before that, and obviously now that we`re entering the legal arena, that`s going to be really hard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, there`s three Republican senators today, Tommy Tuberville, we now got a call --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Either that was by accident. Mike Lee, and obviously, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  had a lot of --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I have to be specific.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We now know of conversation. So there`s at least three.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill, thank you. Well, obviously keep an eye on the story as it develops. And as we figure out the legal fight, and what that looks like, it`s going to be up ahead.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, internationally, Russia is threatening retaliation as Finland look gets ever closer to joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  without delay and Zelenskyy claimed some battleground gains in northeast Ukraine, while Russia claims gains of its own in the east and the south. We`re going to get the latest on the fight in Eastern Europe next.\par \par \uc2 And later, President Biden hosted Leadership Summit without Southeast Asian nations at the White House as tensions intensify in the Asia Pacific region with China and the question about Taiwan. We`re actually going to take you inside something that`s never been seen on camera. It`s a full-day war game that imagines how the United States would react to war with China. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the war in Ukraine now enters its 12th week, we`re seeing signs that Russia`s military may be weakening in some places just as \uc2 NATO\uc2  appears to be strengthening.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finland`s leaders announced today that their country which shares an 800-mile border with Russia, plans on joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  without delay and Sweden is signaling that it plans on joining the alliance as well. Moscow says Finland now poses a threat to Russia and is vowing to retaliate. Although it`s still not clear how that would happen.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been threatened retaliation ever since Finland opened the door to \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Finland hasn`t blinked for what it`s worth. All of this comes as the UK`s Defense Minister Ministry tells us that Ukrainian forces have reclaimed some of the areas north of Kharkiv with Russian forces retreating east.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials say Russian forces aren`t even making significant progress in their offensive in eastern Ukraine. But despite those setbacks, officials and Mariupol say Russia continues attacking the steel plant where the last remaining Ukrainian troops in that city remain holed up.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Courtney Kube. She covers Pentagon for us and she joins me now. Courtney, I wanted a sort of -- I`m a bit confused on the status of Russia`s forces. I understand, on one hand, there`s some success in reclaiming parts of the north but Russia has been in retreat to the south and the east. And it appears that they actually are making it slow, but they are making gains here. And perhaps it`s -- we`re trying to keep hope alive by talking about the North. Is that what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So there are some areas where they`re making some progress. There`s a little city called Popasna, it`s in the east, they have taken that in the last 48, 72 hours. They are also pushing, as you said very, very slowly. Kharkiv is getting a lot of attention because that`s a city that was under dramatic assault for weeks and weeks and weeks.\par \par \uc2 And what`s critical here is that Ukraine has been able to push the Russians far enough back that for most parts of the city are now outside of artillery range. Artillery has been sort of the big weapon that we`ve seen in the East recently, largely because the Russians can`t fly but -- it`s a contested airspace. But being outside of artillery range really makes that city much safer and it`s significant for them.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the map that we`re showing right now, there`s a town called Izyum that you see there. That is an area where the Russians were trying to sort of have three lines of axes or lines of assault out of there. That`s where they have made a little bit of progress.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s very slow, it`s definitely much, much slower than the Russians are believed to have thought they would be able to push forward towards the Donbass area, but it`s not insignificant. You know, if you consider that the Russians really are in this for months and months and months now, you know, they could make some actual progress towards the Donbass on its offensive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, a constant concern that`s underneath this war has always been poking Putin, you know. There`s always been a concern, be careful poking him too much, whether it`s President Biden saying he had to go, things like this. There`s no bigger poke at Putin than Finland joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , right? I mean, to me, this is, forget anything else. How seriously, is the Pentagon taking some of these threats now coming from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, as of now, the -- of course, the two big things that they`re watching that they`re the most concerned about are the use of some sort of a tactical nuclear weapon. As of now, they don`t have any indications that Russia is planning that or that`s really could work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Medvedev`s throw it out there again, today.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: I know. I -- but I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They keep saying it.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Exactly. They talk about it, but they aren`t seeing any movement of any kind of capabilities into the region. So that -- but that`s something that they are watching very closely. Chem bio is another one very concerned about it, still no indications that they`re planning on it.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest concern, though, is any chance that Putin may decide to branch outside of Ukraine`s borders. And according to the officials I`m speaking with, there still are no indications that they want to do that. They`re still in -- and actually even Secretary Austin said this yesterday he doesn`t think they want to go to war with that with \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: They don`t have the military capability to take on Finland`s.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Military a little of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , correct?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Right. Finland and Sweden you know, there`s a relatively small militaries when you look at, especially considering some of the other \uc2 NATO\uc2  members, but they are very small -- they are -- they are very adept, and they`re strong, and they`re well trained. And they`ve been training with \uc2 NATO\uc2  partners for a long time. So will --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, this is going to be an easy transition to that, you know.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Absolutely. They`ll fold right in. They have strong air capabilities and very strong naval capabilities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are the concerns that they -- should Finland -- Finns be worried about cyber-attacks? I mean is that the type of realistic way that Russia is probably going to try to retaliate?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Cyber, I think is a big, one more information operations. So will that have a real impact on them? It`s not the -- it`s the kind of thing has been --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They have trending West for a while, though haven`t they?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Exactly. And it`s -- and they -- and look, they`re on the border, you said it yourself. They know -- they know what they`re going to be facing, they`ll be ready. It`s the kind of threats that they`ve been facing from Russia for years now, anyway, so I don`t have any sense that, according to the officials I`m speaking with, that they`re worried about this sort of tipping some sort of a tripwire. That it`s going to make Russia lash out with these decisions. But the reality is Putin is going to be pretty upset by this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is the biggest expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  that you can add, you know, since the initial expansion (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KUBE: In the symbolism of Finland joining? Enormous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symbolism -- of all -- of all countries. It`s unbelievable. Courtney, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you. Coming up, war games, serious ones. How the U.S. is gaming out its role in a potential conflict with China over Taiwan, and why the strategy is a lot more complex with potentially higher stakes than what we`re seeing in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the White House continues its focus on providing Ukraine would the support it needs in its war against Russia, President Biden will turn its attention to another region today with his own security concerns. It`s Southeast Asia. The president will host Southeast Asian leaders at the White House at the U.S. seeks to assure our allies in that region that it is committed to their security amid a looming threat from China.\par \par \uc2 The timing of this meeting happens to come as our latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" takes you inside something that`s never been seen on camera, a full-day war game exercise that imagines how America would react to a Chinese attempt at essentially invading and taking Taiwan. Here is a sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As you can see here on the map is a very large concentration of Chinese People`s Liberation Army forces at potential ports of demarcation for an invasion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to focus on a last-ditch effort to deter. This is a time to be sending the strongest possible message to Beijing both privately and publicly that there will be very severe costs if they actually go through with this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: China has sort of seen our reaction to Ukraine, and we want to make sure that we are surprising them with how we react here.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hit the Americans as hard as we possibly can in the Western Pacific. Keep them out of the fight while we move on Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would support early knockout punches that`s strong. I think we start very forcefully with the missile bombardment on Taiwan. I think we ought to bring the military to the mix.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Stacie, high-level here. What just happened with move one?\par \par \uc2 STACIE PETTYJOHN, CNAS DIRECTOR OF DEFENSE PROGRAM: China has invaded Taiwan and began by attacking Taiwan`s outlying islands near the mainland, then it followed it with a large air and missile strike on Taiwan and on U.S. bases in Japan and on U.S. bases in Guam and the Northern Marianas. In response to that the United States followed up with bomber attacks on U.S. -- on Chinese ships and ports, and there was an air battle over Taiwan where American aircraft flying from the Philippines came in and engaged in combat with Chinese aircraft that were trying to bomb Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So after move one, can you assess which team is winning?\par \par \uc2 PETTYJOHN: I think it`s a stalemate right now. China has strategically blundered by pulling Japan in, and the United States is still well- positioned to defend, but China also has a lot of its assets left and has a lot of power that it can apply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now on set is my colleague, NBC News correspondent Carol Lee. Also with me is Admiral \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2 , former Supreme Allied commander in \uc2 NATO\uc2  now an NBC News chief international security and diplomacy analyst and the man who helped us put together this partnership with CNAS.\par \par \uc2 Carol, take us into the years 2027. There`s a bit of a pretext that got us to the point where China and the United States ended up engaging.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, exactly, and Chuck, the thing that was so striking about the war game was how significantly it escalated so quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So fast. This is over about a seven-day period. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Over seven-day period. And the way in which that each team decided to approach, as you heard there, Michele Flournoy talking about deterrence and sending the strongest message possible. None of that worked with Ukraine and Russia, and it also didn`t work here, and the parallels that are there between the battle in Ukraine and the war game on Taiwan are really striking.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it was a really interesting exercise in the sense that it showed that it was -- even a weekend it would be a stalemate. How deeply the U.S. could get drawn into something like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEE: With direct conflict with China, and just in the region, it would be - - I mean, talk about World War III so that was fascinating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was. You know, Admiral Stavridis, the big lesson I took out of it and the big lesson -- we have two members of Congress, Democrat Mikie Sherrill of New Jersey, Republican \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  of Wisconsin, both in the Armed Services, both really -- both veterans, so they really understood the concept, but they were struck, and I was struck by, boy, this would be a lot harder than rearming Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This is a lot harder when you don`t have a \uc2 NATO\uc2  in Asia. That was the big takeaway I had from this. What have you learned from these exercises over the years?\par \par \uc2 ADM\uc2 . \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2 , NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Yes, and I`ve done many of them, Chuck, as you can imagine. This is something the U.S. Indo Pacific Command does constantly. Always updating, and Carol Lee is exactly right, which is currently drawing the lessons out of the Ukrainian conflict.\par \par \uc2 Two points really jumped out at me. One is the technical level of this, that you`re going to see as opposed to Ukraine. In other words, this is going to be maritime, air on air. It`s going to be cyber going in the background, which is certainly part of the conflict, so it`s going to be a very technological play.\par \par \uc2 And then secondly, Chuck, it`s leadership that`s going to matter here, and that`s the hard one to assess. Just as we blew it on Afghanistan, taking the Afghans would stand fight and we blew it on Ukraine, thinking the Ukrainians were going to fold, we`ve got to get this one right, and so I think a subtext for me that I am looking at is what is the fighting spirit and the will of the Taiwanese? That`s going to be quite determinative here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s the thing, if I were to say the biggest question I have in my head through this whole exercise is, I think, and the hardest part about this exercise is putting ourselves into the Chinese ring, OK? The strategic mindset of the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was a highly experienced Chinese experts that we had running the red team. But ultimately, you know, there is still a western bias that is in any western educated person`s head here, and I think that`s the hard part. We assume that China would be, OK, this is how we`re going to keep Japan out, and of course, we`re like all that did was draw Japan in.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 But I imagine that`s the hardest part here is what is in Xi`s head and what do we know about the Chinese military`s?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Let`s start with President Xi himself. He is a patient figure, and that is also of a piece with China in general. As opposed to that kind of impulsive activity that you see out of a \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the Chinese are patient, and Sun Tzu is their greatest military strategist, and pulling out his very slim but brilliant volume, "The Art of War," is well worth doing for anybody who wants to understand the mind of the Chinese military.\par \par \uc2 What I will say, Chuck, is if you dive into Sun Tzu, it`s all about deception. The best battle is the one you never actually fight because you`d surprised your enemy, but Sun Tzu also says -- and it`s worth knowing this -- went on dead ground fight. And that I think is something that has shot through China`s thinking about military.\par \par \uc2 I thought the first move, as you gave us a snapshot there, made a lot of sense with the one exception. My eyebrows went up significantly with China immediately striking those bases and instantly drawing the United States into war. I think they might take a more patient approach , but if you want to kick off a war game and move it fast, there`s nothing like a strike.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`ll tell you this. The one other thing, Carol, I came away with here is, number one, water, it`s just harder.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That 90 miles may only be 90 miles.\par \par \uc2 LEE: That`s a big difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a big difference. But there is something else here, and I`m curious if you`ve gotten this in any of your reporting. Both Gallagher and Sherrill seemed to indicate to me, going, hey, we`d like to be more involved in things like this. This seemed to be an exercise that a lot of times the Pentagon will do these exercises and then will report to Congress what they need, and it`s, you know, like no, no, no. Us participating it`s a lot easier to understand what you need here.\par \par \uc2 It was a sense of like maybe the Pentagon has been going about this all wrong, they need to loop members in on how this works.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes, open it up and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, because a lot of what they do is going to depend on whether or not they have support for Congress. One of the things -- I mean, you heard the director of National Intelligence Avril Haines this week saying that China wants to take Taiwan or trying to do it by coercion, but they are determined and will do it by force if they needed to, and so, part of what the inclusions were from the war game was, should we be arming Taiwan in a different way?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE\uc2 : Should we be more involved? And those are questions that you have to deal with Congress for, and so bringing them in could be a little bit of a game changer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Admiral, let`s bring it to the real world right now. President Biden is with some Southeast Asian leaders. Here`s one of the big just sort of practical takeaways. Yes, we have Guam, yes we have Japan, yes, we have a base now in Australia. I was there for the opening up of it, when President Obama went there, and he realized how important that was.\par \par \uc2 Strategically, what allies would you like to see the United States have in Southeast Asia that could help protect the Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I`m going to add one who is a strong ally, and I think we can depend on, and that`s of course Singapore, and as was indicated, Japan is very strong, and South Korea is very strong. So if you really look around the circuit, we`re missing one slot, and it`s the Philippine Islands, and don`t forget we had wonderful bases there. We fought hard there in World War II. We had Subic Bay, Clark Air Force Base. That, in many ways, was the centerpiece of our Western Pacific strategy, so if we could possibly get them back on board, we`ve had a very rough run, but they`ve just had an election. That would be the one I would like to add.\par \par \uc2 Final thoughts, Chuck, Vietnam is no friend of China.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: That`s another distinct possibility. Cam Ranh Bay is one of the best natural harbors on the South China Sea, so there`s two to work on, I would say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess, look. People in the 1940s probably did not expect us to be so close of allies with Japan. Perhaps people would be shocked if we started basing in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Admiral Stavridis, Carol Lee, thank you both. Really proud of this episode, folks, and Jim, you were terrific in making this happen as well.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to check our entire episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." It streams tonight for the first time at 10:30 Eastern. That`s on NBC News Now. NBC News is home for news, and will be available tomorrow on demand in Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, a symbolic abortion bill fails in the Senate. What do Democrats do next? Democrat of Wisconsin, Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:58]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It has been a busy day on Capitol Hill, as we broke just a few moments ago the January 6th Committee has subpoenaed five members of Congress, including the top House Republican, Kevin McCarthy, in its investigation. It comes as the committee says all of those members declined voluntary cooperation with the committee.\par \par \uc2 In addition, amid calls from their base to protect abortion rights, Democrats failed to secure the votes necessary yesterday to advance a bill that would ensure abortion access nationwide. The Women`s Health Protection Act would`ve insured abortion beyond what is protected by Roe v. Wade right now, invalidating some state regulations on abortion before 24 weeks. The vote failed 49-51 as Democratic Senator Joe Manchin joined Republicans against the bill.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also failed to convince two self-described pro-choice Republican senators, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, to vote in its favor as well. Murkowski said the bill allowed the abortion too late for her comfort, saying the legislation went, quote, "well beyond the precedent established in Roe and Casey." She urged lawmakers to vote for another bill she put forward that would simply codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 Democrats knew this bill was not going to ever get 60 votes no matter which one they put on the floor. Instead, they`re going to try to use it as a marker for where lawmakers stand on abortion ahead of the midterms, but voters across the country are expecting action as the threat of the Supreme Court decision looms.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you do now? I`m joined by Democratic senator from Wisconsin, \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , and Senator, given the state you come from and the immediate impact the -- what Roe would have come I can`t think of a better person to talk to right now. But first, I want to talk about the decision. You know, it seems as if we knew yesterday was a messaging bill, and there are two strategies one can take.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the final result, was it better to show that Democrats divided, or would have it been better to show the Republicans divided on this issue?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY BALDWIN (D-WI): Well, I would contend that this is a very substantive bill. It was around for a long time prior to this leaked decision. In fact, I teamed up with Senator Blumenthal to introduce it for several previous sessions because we saw all the states acting -- introducing bills that would limit access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:12]\par \par \uc2 And in some states this passed, in other states they didn`t. But we`ve had this bill for a long time. In terms of bringing it up at this particular moment, number one, it shows that we are engaged in this fight and this is only the first step in the fight given the anticipated opinion of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And secondly as you noted, every senator is now on record as to whether they would strip away the constitutional right, the first time in history we`ve seen the court poised to peel back a constitutional right. We`ve always been expanding rights in our nation. Never looking backwards. So everyone is on the record. And now we organize. So we fight, we educate on the impact the Supreme Court decision will have on women and men, and then we organize for the elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept the premise, though, that you politically might have been able to show the Republicans as more extreme on this issue if you put a bill that allowed for some of the limitations that Roe and Casey have allowed for and yet they still wouldn`t support it. Right? Do you accept the premise that it would have painted them into a deeper corner?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: You know, I really dispute their characterization in going beyond Roe vs. Wade. What has happened is the states have limited and limited and restricted and imposed obstacles. These proposals that have come to pass have severely limited a woman`s right to choose. And they won`t stand up against that nor will they stand up against this anticipated Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 And when we want to talk about what`s extreme, you know, denying a teen who has been raped the access to abortion care, denying a victim of incest abortion care, denying a woman whose life or health has been placed in jeopardy because of, say, an ectopic pregnancy or something like that, the chance to live, that`s extreme.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about Wisconsin specifically, and I can`t help but get you to react to your Senate colleague that also represents the state. This is what he said according to "The Wall Street Journal."\par \par \uc2 This is Ron Johnson, it might be a little messy for some people but abortion is not going away, he said, saying that driving across state lines to Illinois would likely be an option. I just don`t think this is going to be the big political issue everybody thinks it is because it`s not going to be that big of a change.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re in Wisconsin, just drive into Illinois. Is that a real solution?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, first of all, let me tell you what the law would be in Wisconsin if Roe vs. Wade is overturned. In Wisconsin we have a law on the books, it was passed in 1894, sorry, 1849, one year after Wisconsin`s statehood. That`s where we`d be, that would be the status of our law in Wisconsin. And I think we can all agree that lots have changed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, at a time when women weren`t even allowed to vote in the state of Wisconsin. It might be worth bringing that up. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Women were property. They didn`t have independent rights of all sorts, let alone voting. You know, we`ve advanced quite a bit since 1849. And I have to say that the idea that women don`t have the same rights from state to state is ridiculous. But I would also say to voters that if our current Senator Johnson wants to send women to Illinois, we ought to send him packing this November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about what can be done in Wisconsin. I mean, it`s the most polarized state in the nation, and I look at a -- I look at this -- it feels as if that there`ll be nothing and there will be no protection, nothing at all. I mean, is there any sort of plan E, plan F here in the near term? Because you guys are sort of stalemated in the federal level. I mean, it just feels like this is going to be draconian in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I fear that it will. I really do. I will say that while our political situation in Wisconsin is overwhelmingly Republican legislature but a Democratic governor who can and has vetoed anti-choice measures that have gotten through the state legislature, what I will say is our general public, the people of Wisconsin are quite in tandem with the rest of the nation in terms of views about whether or not Roe vs. Wade should be overturned.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:23]\par \par \uc2 A significant majority believe that Roe vs. Wade should remain intact and a small minority believes that it should be overturned outright.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there any constitutional amendment, you know, the state of Michigan at least can put that on the ballot. I think they have a shot at getting something enshrined in their state constitution. Any path like that? Because, look, your legislature is gerrymandered in one direction and you have the governor, the Democratic governor that can sort of stalemate things. But is there a third path at all in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: The legislature has to pass in two separate sessions a bill to amend our state constitution and it goes to the voters of the state at the first election thereafter or an election thereafter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a long process. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Right. So what I will say is go to the ballot box, everybody who is concerned about this and a whole series of other issues where we have seen Republicans siding, you know, with -- well, siding with the idea of repealing a constitutional right, the first time in our nation`s history and setting in the United States tens of millions of women back.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: We need to vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , Democrat from Wisconsin, I really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m closing in on my hour. I appreciate you all for being with us. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Dasha Burns, Yamiche Alcindor, Vaughn Hillyard, Lauren  Mayk \par \uc2 9,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I can hear you.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday. Here we are, another special Meet the Midterms edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from Independence Hall here in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, at the pivotal moment in these midterm primary elections.\par \par \uc2 Good day, I am Chuck Todd in Philadelphia. It`s a city rich in American history and a fitting place to talk about the future of our country less than a week before some of the most important primaries on the 2022 midterm calendar. Pennsylvania will be a key to Senate control in November and, of course, it is a major battleground in 2024. It may have replaced Florida and Ohio. It`s the state living up to the phrase Keystone if you could borrow a word.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, we were in the northeast part of the state focusing on the future of the Republican Party. We spoke to voters, officials, and one of the top Republican Senate candidates at the center of a very muddled and messy primary. Today, our focus is on the future of the Democratic Party. And the message the Pennsylvania Democrats appear to be sending is clear as a somewhat unorthodox politician appears to be pulling away in the Senate primary. We`ll see what his opponents think of that characterization.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t expected to be a very a blowout if you will but according to at least one poll, Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman is up nearly 30 points over the more moderate Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and Philadelphia State Representative Malcolm Kenyatta. We`re going to talk to both Lamb and Kenyatta live coming up.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got an exclusive look at the Fettermans, both he and his wife in a moment as well, because the question that is going to be on the minds of Democrats across the country, is whether John Fetterman is the future of the Democratic Party in some ways. His style, his focus whether his style of politics and potentially the way he talks to voters that are -- that respond by saying, oh, you`re not a typical Democrat, is that what the party needs to find at this moment when the party is clearly struggling politically?\par \par \uc2 Fetterman has campaigned with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  in the past, but today, he avoids the label progressive instead, preferring populists or just plain old Democrats. And he`s a film layer you face to voters, people need to realize this isn`t just a flash in the pan, he`s running for the third time statewide. He failed in a Senate run in 2016, he succeeded in a run for lieutenant governor in 2018 so in some ways, he`s paying his dues if you`re a rank and file Democrat.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve discovered from talking to voters suggests that some of Fetterman`s appeal is because Democrats here like many places that we`ve gone and traveled to around the country, they`re simply tired of what they believe is the party`s current status quo. I talked to a group of Pennsylvania Democrats just a few hours ago that included one that`s backing Lamb, one backing Kenyatta and one backing Fetterman but they all had the same gripe. They feel like their party`s messaging is stale and doesn`t speak to them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SCHMIDT, KENYATTA SUPPORTER: I think there are so many times where the Democrats, in politics in general, many times have this like messaging problem, where it doesn`t meet you where you are.\par \par \uc2 JAIME BASSMAN, LAMB SUPPORTER: Definitely a thousand times. The messaging of the Democratic Party needs to be completely overhauled from the top down.\par \par \uc2 TABITHA SSONKO, FETTERMAN SUPPORTER: The people who have showed up for years to support the Democrats, especially in the states, like Pennsylvania, are tired of showing up and not being responded to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The discontent among these democrats was palpable, and it appears they`re getting ready to air some of those frustrations at the ballot box if they come to the ballot box. We`ll discuss that later. Join on set with two of my colleagues who`ve been traversing the state, Dasha Burns, and Yamiche Alcindor. They`ve been on the ground doing what I`ve been doing here talking to voters, talking to candidates. Yamiche, let`s start out West. Is Fetterman the strong front-runner? Is that what you`re sensing too when you are out in the western part of the state?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: That`s when I`ve been sensing. I sat in on a union town hall meeting with the \uc2 United Steel Workers\uc2  and they told me that John Fetterman is the person that has been connecting to rank and file union voters, people, of course, who were pulling away from the Democratic Party and where Republicans were making gains. They really do feel strongly that he`s someone who`s speaking their language, who also liked their style. You think about the shorts, you think about the sort of dress down style, and they are really excited about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But I also should tell you that I talked to the mayor of Aliquippa, who told me that Democrats, one, are running too many candidates that they need to really coalesce around a message, and coalesce around who they want to pick different races. So listen to a little bit about what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DWAN WALKER, MAYOR OF ALIQUIPPA, PENNSYLVANIA: Having good candidates, not running for all the same seats. And we spread that wealth out over different avenues like Congress, House of Representatives, Senator, Lieutenant, spread that out because you got -- in the Senate race, you got like three top Democrats and that are running for that one seat. The frustration part of the Democratic Party. That was so splintered. We need to get back to our roots, invest in good candidates who can do the job, and then stay with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And part of those roots, he says are talking to union workers and reminding people that Democrats were the ones who pushed for those unions to be established.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Dasha, I`ve had some Lamb people push back on our characterization that he`s the strong front runner and they`re pointing out. He`s got a lot of institutional support here in Philadelphia, we saw it. He`s sort of the, hey, this is the guy that can win as far as the sort of the democratic power brokers are around the state. Could we be underestimating Lamb right now?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Look, he has institutional support now. Fetterman, in his first run in 2016, he told me their campaign barely had gas money.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And I think this says a lot about the direction of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The fact that now he does have not only a lot of voters, according to the polls, but also the institution behind him went over --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, but the institutional support is still with Lamb, even though there are some people peeling off for Fetterman. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes. I think that`s -- I think that`s fair to say. You know, I quipped sometimes about voters in Pennsylvania, kind of feeling the vibe, right? It`s about a vibe -- it`s about a brand if you want to speak, you know, political terms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But there`s something about the way Fetterman approaches people that just hits home. I`ve heard people use the same language about him that I`ve heard voters use about Trump, frankly, not your typical politician. You know, he`s a real guy. He talks to people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you spent a lot of time with both John Fetterman and his wife, because the two of them run together at times. She`s a very important part of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: She was a part of his initial reaction to the leaked Supreme Court documents, so let`s take a look at what you learned in that sit-down that you had with the Fetterman`s.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): Senate candidate John Fetterman`s road to Washington, swerves between left and middle.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN, LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF PENNSYLVANIA: I don`t want to be crass, but we need to keep making (AUDIO GAP) this country\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trying to win back blue-collar Pennsylvania voters that swung to Trump but with a brand all his own.\par \par \uc2 If you walk into Washington day one to do one big thing --\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What do you do?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: What do I do? Well, again, that would be like picking my favorite child. I just -- I just couldn`t --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m asking you a fair question.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: I just couldn`t do that. So again, I rattle off three priorities. Minimum wage, it`s a disgrace that it has to change. We have to codify Roe v. Wade. And third is voting rights.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Are you a progressive?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: No, I`m just a Democrat that has always run on what I believe and know to be true.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: In his signature card hard hoodie, Fetterman wears in, not your typical Democrat type of brand. His approach is more at home with a commonwealth`s common man than with the party elite. As lieutenant governor for going to Harrisburg mansion for his car dealership turned home across the street from a steel mill in Braddock, where he had some choice words for his fellow Democrats in Washington.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: We are squandering, in my opinion, an enormous opportunity to do some transformative good through legislation that is being stopped by a senator like Joe Manchin. And I`m not criticizing him. I`m simply saying I would vote differently.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: It`s not just the candidate, but the couple that makes this campaign unconventional.\par \par \uc2 GISELE BARRETO FETTERMAN, SECOND LADY OF PENNSYLVANIA: Did you pick one already? OK, so you can grab the cake. I can take you two. Are they nice?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If John is bringing the casual, his wife, Gisele, is bringing the polish and the personality.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Are you ready to go inside?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Did you want to pick the cake? There`s two different kinds.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: She`s been front and center at rallies on social media and in campaign e-mails. How did that come about? How did you guys decide to really come as a package deal in this way?\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I think we are. I mean, our family is the package deal, right?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: She`s a force in the community running several philanthropic efforts. She says she hates politics but hopes her story will transcend all that.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Hi, how are you?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Born in Brazil, Gisele came to the United States as an undocumented immigrant.\par \par \uc2 How much does that influence his position on the issue?\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I think it does very much so. We`ve seen cases where I`m out with the children and people think I`m the babysitter and not their mother.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Gisele says she gets more hate mail than her husband.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said that you have three strikes against you. You`re an immigrant, a woman, and a cannabis patient. Do you worry that those three strikes might count against John in November if he wins the primary?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I don`t think it should count. And if it does, it just means there`s more work to do, and we`ll continue to do it.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Those strikes may be home runs for the progressives in the party, but Fetterman is also trying to score with a waning working-class base.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: It`s like this idea that if you feel left behind or you feel that you`ve been ignored or you -- the party has moved on to something different or better in their minds like well, what`s wrong with us?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The Fettermans together, hoping their brand can bridge the gap between old school and new school Democrats in a state with razor-thin margins.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: Our first campaign, we barely had gas money and now we`re one of the most resourced -- well-resourced campaigns, you know, of a challenger nationally. So the only thing that`s changed is that we`ve been getting our message out and she`s a big part of the package.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, very interesting there, by the way, it is -- Game Six is coming up here with the Sixers and the Heat.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: It`s silly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously, the Sixers know their season is likely about to end. Oh, did I say that as a \uc2 Miami Heat\uc2  advocate here? But that is the noise you`re hearing in the -- in the background. Dasha, if there is some doubt about Fetterman that I`m sensing here in Philadelphia, it is about whether African-Americans will be enthusiastic enough to show up to the polls for him.\par \par \uc2 The incident -- the incident that happened when he was mayor comes up time and again with activist voters that I talked to here in Philadelphia. There`s a concern, Reverend Mark Kelly Tyler put it said the -- what super predator did to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , it suppress turnout in `16. That is the concern about Fetterman. How are they going to address this?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Look, they`ve been addressing it sort of in the same way from the beginning. I asked him the question. He didn`t really drip from the very same answers he`s been given at every debate. He talks about how he`s brought down crime when he -- when he was mayor, that gun violence went down. That`s what he`s been coming back with. I asked if he -- you know, should change that answer, that he kind of gave the same answer again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`ll tell you though, when I have been talking to folks in western Pennsylvania, especially people that kind of know the Pittsburgh area, the Braddock community, and including voters of color at some of Gisele`s events, for example, the free store where folks come and pick up goods for free, it doesn`t really come up with the voters I`ve been speaking with, with everyday voters. Activist, absolutely, but with everyday voters, it`s not a lot of people when I asked him about it don`t really know what I`m referencing. So it`s hard to say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Here, it does feel like here folks know it, Yamiche.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know the incident.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Right. The issue is, of course, that Philadelphia is still the heart of the black voter -- voting in an -- in this state. And I think it`s very -- it`s a hard swing to ask African-American voters to forget about that. And it`s also hard -- when I talked to a lot of African American voters who say, we want candidates who also represent our ideals and we want candidates of color who don`t remind us of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that`s the sort of risk there where you have African American voters who have told me that they`re -- they frankly, think that the transformational change that they were promised, a lot of them with the Biden administration, while they`re still supporting Biden because they won`t go to the Republicans, they`re still wondering, why aren`t Democrats more aggressive? I heard that time and time again, as I was talking to people saying why not policing and voting (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can Fetterman overcome it -- I mean, look what he said his top three issues were to Dasha, if voting rights was part of it, can that help overcome some of the skepticism?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: It can possibly help overcome some of this activist -- skepticism, but democratic messaging in this state and with Fetterman, in particular, has to be the how are you going to get that done? Because I talked to you -- a voter who told me, you`ve already said that, like, if you give us this power, here`s what we`re going to do. If you -- if you give us this power, our lives are going to be infinitely better.\par \par \uc2 And they say, OK, we got \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , we got the first black VP, the White House press secretary is going to be African American, but how does that change my lived experience in America? I heard that question over and over again. So, Fetterman promising if you give me the power, I will do this isn`t going to be enough because they say we`ve already seen what it looks like in the hands that were tied at the White House when you had the Senate and the House and the President -- the presidency?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Real quick.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s sort of the question at hand for the Democratic Party too, right? Is it going with what Fetterman is trying to do in trying to win back some of the voters in areas that Democrats lost those strongholds like Luzerne County that was blue until Trump? Is that the strategy or is it what Yamiche is talking about and really appealing to these voters and not just promising but taking action and getting stuff done?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, then there`s the other door three, which is, is there an and rather than an or, right?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s I think what Gisele might be trying to bring to be honest to the campaign.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you -- and you bring an end to that equation, which is a good transition to my next guest, who I think is campaigning as hey, I`m in and if he`s listening right now. Yamiche Alcindor and Dasha Burns, thank you both. Joining me now is a Senate candidate we`ve been talking about quite a bit here. It`s Democratic Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . And, Congressman, I`m sure you just heard that.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is this -- and I think the reason why I hear from a lot of the institutional support for you, Congressman, is there`s this idea that you can do both, that you`ll be able to appeal to voters that are necessary for the Democratic coalition in Philadelphia, and also appeal to some of the same voters that Fetterman is doing. But you tell me, that coalition doesn`t look like it`s coming together yet. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 REP. CONOR LAMB, (D-PA): Well, Chuck, it is coming together. I wish you would share higher-quality polling information with your viewers than the one you lead this segment with. I mean, you`ve been doing this long enough to know how low quality that is. So what the numbers currently are, who knows, but the fact is, we`ve put in the work with voters in Philadelphia and all over the state.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that they know, Reverend Tyler knows this, other know it -- other people know it is, on the day after the primary, you have to step out into a larger world in Pennsylvania and you do have to win swing voters, you have to win independents, even moderate Republicans. That`s not a luxury item. It`s something you actually have to have to be successful.\par \par \uc2 I`m the only candidate in this race that has ever done it and I`ve done it in red areas. President Trump came to a town last Friday for his rally that I won by 500 votes the year after he won it by 500 votes. I`ve done that in a lot of parts of western Pennsylvania and that`s what will make me successful as our nominee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think this is more of a myth that Fetterman connects more with these voters than you do?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I just think it`s totally unproven is my point. You know, I have been in three elections in a row where I had to win swing voters in order to win. John has only ever been in Democratic primaries, you know. Here in our state, you don`t get to vote separately for the lieutenant governor in the general election, and so he`s never faced a Republican head to head in a general election. This is the most important general election our state is going to have in a really long time. The entire balance of the Senate is there.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m just asking people to consider what it means to be proven. And having spent time in these areas, I`ve come by my beliefs, honestly, a lot of the swing voters we need are not big liberals. They liked the fact that I served Marines or that I was in law enforcement wherever the case, but they agree with us on things like Social Security, and Medicare. And that is what I would make the focus of my campaign. That`s how you win in a state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I had someone say to me that they think your biggest problem is something you can`t control and that is that your first name became congressman, and that there`s this palpable disappointment with Democrats right now in Washington and that that is -- that has been a bit of an anchor around your ankles simply because you`re a member of that group. Do you accept that theory?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Well, I like to tell people that I am the biggest optimist in the entire Pennsylvania Democratic Party so I`m not going to say that you`re wrong about the feelings people have about Washington. But the fact is, when I first ran for office, it was in a district that was supposed to be impossible to win. The way I`m being outraised and outspent now is nothing compared to what happened to me in that first race. We were outraised and outspent 10 to one. It -- but we just did the hard work every single day of showing up and answering people`s questions and I`ve done that as a member of Congress as well. So I`ve really learned a lot about our community, and the way people care about issues.\par \par \uc2 And all I`m trying to talk to the Democrats right now is, let`s not throw away what made us successful. You know, when I was first elected, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  was the Speaker, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was the majority leader, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the president. None of those three things are true anymore. And it`s because all around the country, we got behind candidates like me who could appeal to this larger world, and we won, so let`s remember that and get ourselves more numbers to do all the things we want to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there something that the party could do now that would help not just your chances, but the party`s chances by November? What do you need to see done? Let`s assume you`re the nominee here. Let`s assume that our friends of Franklin and Marshall really whiffed on this one. What do you need -- what kind of action do you need from the Biden administration to sort of back you up when you`re on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Well, I think -- you know, your previous question had a grain of truth in it, which is that people are a little mistrustful of Washington and disappointed in it right now. So I don`t think you need a message coming from someone who is not me or coming from Washington. What people need is to get to know me and see, first of all, I`ve spent almost my whole life in this state. I`m devoted to it. I`m not an out-of-state multimillionaire like the Republicans that we`ll be running against.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: And as a result of that, I know what people are going through. I can`t tell you how many people I`ve talked to already, who their entire life is about the fact that they cannot afford sufficient childcare for their children. Or I go to a lot of senior centers and I play cards and bingo with them. They are enraged that the Social Security cost of living increases do not cover the true cost of inflation. Those are two things right there where a majority of Pennsylvanians, not just the majority of Democrats are with us, and that will be the contrast I`ll make in this race with a bunch of out of state multimillionaires that don`t know these issues and they don`t understand what people are going through.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the issue of abortion rights, where do you think most Pennsylvania voters are?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I think most want Roe v Wade to be upheld and stay the same. I think that is totally uncontroversial. I mean as I told you, I ran in a very, very heavily red district my first time, and my position, then as now is Roe v. Wade needs to remain the same, people need to be able to choose for themselves, whether to get an abortion, you know, and that includes even in the difficult later stages of pregnancy, where it is almost all having to do with medically -- medical uncertainties. And I`ve been elected three times with that position in a pretty red area, so I`m confident that`s where a majority of people are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem as if like both parties are trying to define the other is extreme on this issue. Is there -- you know, Roe v. Wade, do you think - - do you think you need to be saying what limits that you would put on abortion in order to meet voters where you think they might be on this issue?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I think you know, my policy is anyone that wants to ask me a question can ask, and if that`s what someone asked me, I`ll tell them, you know, what`s in the Women`s Health Protection Act, which is, you know, after viability it has to do with the health and safety of the mother. So there is a reasonable medical standard to apply. But in general, I think a lot of people just want the same rule that we`ve had for 50 years to stay in place and they may not want to talk about it a whole lot more than that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: But what`s radical about what the Republicans are doing, is they`re overturning something people have counted on for 50 years, just like they`re overturning their support for voting rights and just like they want to hold a vote on whether to eliminate Social Security and Medicare within the next five years. I mean, they make me feel like a conservative sometimes because I want to conserve these things that I think have been so vital to our national life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you win this primary, it`ll be because why? What would be the explanation for you winning?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Because I`m not afraid to go anywhere and answer people`s questions and that`s something John Fetterman has been afraid to do. And I think whether it`s the gun issue that you talked about, or others, part of the way that you overcome some of this cynicism and mistrust is to show up and answer people`s questions, take their feedback and fight for every single vote. And that`s what I`ve been doing every day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , a Democrat from Western Pennsylvania, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective. Be safe on the trail, sir.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Up next, we got much more on the future of the Democratic Party. We`ll have another candidate vying for the Democratic nomination here. The third strong candidate that people have been buzzing about for a while, Malcolm Kenyatta will join me later in the show. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live from historic Independence Hall in Philadelphia. It`s a great backdrop. Is there a better one? I don`t think so. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`re in the heart of Philadelphia this afternoon. The home, actually of one of the candidates running in the Democratic Senate nomination fight, it`s state representative Malcolm Kenyatta. In fact, his district is just a little north of where I am right now. Kenyatta is trailing Fetterman and Lamb in most of the polling we`ve seen, but his supporters say he`s in touch with what voters want and speaks to more real- life experiences, particularly of younger and progressive voters on the ground. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: And it feels like his experiences guide his values and his policy. And I think he also represents -- he is someone that I feel represents me the best being from the Philadelphia area, being young, being a part of the LGBTQI community. All of those things, I just feel like he represents me in the most and the best, and we`ll fight for the things that I want him to fight for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the Democratic Senate candidate, current Pennsylvania State Representative Malcolm Kenyatta joins me now. And I`m going to talk to the motorcycle only goes over my voice --\par \par \uc2 MALCOLM KENYATTA, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, PENNSYLVANIA: So that`s a \uc2 Miami Heat\uc2  fan with their --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They got their show that -- well, I hope so. I`m not going to stand in that way. Let me ask you this and why -- and I`m going to sound like I`m advocating for your campaign but here`s what I`ve heard from various voters, hey, it`s progressives are picking between maybe you are Fetterman, then there`s those that are worried about who can win in the general and they`re picking between you and Lamb. There`s a common denominator here. Why so far do you feel like you`ve not been able to view the progressive electability argument?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Well, you know, I think so much of this race has been about trying to handicap who`s going to win and not enough about who should and why. In this moment, it`s not going to be about what the pundits say this flawed idea that the race has been about two white guys from Pittsburgh and that only they are electable in this race. If we do not have record turnout in places like Philadelphia, in southeastern Pennsylvania, we will lose.\par \par \uc2 We have somebody running from Philadelphia in myself, who has spent every single day talking about what I`m going to do to deliver for working families, and why it matters to have somebody who`s not just asking for folk`s votes, but who understands their life. I grew up in a working-class family here in North Philly.\par \par \uc2 My parents separated when I was young. I got my first job at 12 washing dishes to help my mom make ends meet. Move six different places by the time I graduated high school. Burry both of my parents by the time I was 27. And have been talking about things that I know and things that voters know here in southeastern Pennsylvania, but also all across the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said something to me off-camera that others have said to me on this trip to0. It is -- there like so many Democrats don`t understand Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You were saying who basically really at the Democratic consultant class, probably more so than most. Explain.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: You know what, if you are -- if you are a cynic, there`s always a job for you in politics. But this idea that Pennsylvania is just there are parts of our state that are just so racist, that they would never give somebody named Malcolm Kenyatta from North Philly, I kept --\par \par \uc2 TODD: The James Carroll (PH) descriptor of Pennsylvania, we won`t get into it but you`re basically you feel like that`s a virus that`s going around amongst some Democratic strategists.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: And it`s from people who actually haven`t spent time going to places like Curwensville, where I just was last week, where a bunch of people are voting for me are excited about my candidacy, watch the debates, and saw me go up against two career politicians, and really layout in a clear, fresh way, what is at stake and what we need to do for their families. I`m talking about America`s basic bargain.\par \par \uc2 This idea that you can have one good job backed up by a union, that your kids can go to a good school, that if you get sick, you can go to the damn doctor and fill the prescription, and then you can retire with the level of dignity. It`s not going to be good enough for Democrats to say we`re not the Republicans. I think people get that. The question is why should people trust us to go to Washington and deal with the Senate that has not worked?\par \par \uc2 And if we want to change the Senate and the way that I think we need to, we have to change the senators and I represent that fresh vision. And unlike the Lieutenant Governor, I am a progressive. And so for progressives watching, there`s one progressive in the race. They can vote for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Like I said, I`ve run across a lot of people who seemed to be rooting for you, wanted to support you, and they`ve decided you can`t win. Let me play for you Reverend Tyler. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. MARK KELLY TYLER, MOTHER BETHEL AME CHURCH PASTOR: He is a Philadelphian, he`s African-American, he`s young, a lot of people outside of Philadelphia are not, you know, necessarily OK with who he is. I mean, even though in the city of Philadelphia we love him for who he is and we`d support him no matter what. But that is an issue in the general election.\par \par \uc2 JAIME BASSMAN, LAMB SUPPORTER: So I really like Malcolm a lot and I also like \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  a lot. For Malcolm, I was just a little concerned that he wasn`t gaining enough momentum in terms of like the polling and the fundraising.\par \par \uc2 TABITHA SSONKO, FETTERMAN SUPPORTER: He just has not made enough of an effort and a presence in the centrist part of the state. I am looking for a candidate who really is exciting all of Pennsylvania, not just the typical parts that we talk about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If I were in your shoes I`d react to that. Go ahead. Boy, that`s a lot of punditry, right. It feels like more of an analytical argument. It`s not about issues.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Yes, I think that, you know, there is a self-fulfilling prophecy in some of that. The candidate that you vote for is the one that can win. And in this moment there there`s so much at stake the real question is who -- when this election is over, when you`re not coming here to talk about Pennsylvania politics for a little, and the election is over, people need to ask the question of who do they trust to actually go to the Senate and be a productive disrupter to the status quo and to actually fight for them.\par \par \uc2 People know that if they want somebody who`s going to be a champion for working-class people there`s one working-class person in this race. His name is Malcolm Kenyatta. You can go to my Web site and support our campaign and vote for me in six days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Reverend Tyler said something else about Fetterman and the reason he`s supporting Lamb, right. He chose Lamb over you. But was this electability argument but his concern about Fetterman is that if he`s the nominee what super predator did to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  among black voters in this city, meaning they didn`t go vote for Trump they just didn`t vote.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That that`s his concern. That it will be a wet blanket. And he`ll have a hard time convincing his congregation. You may, if you`re not your nominee, have a hard time convincing your fellow Democrats to do this. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: You know, if people go look at the debates I was very clear to the lieutenant governor that what he did was wrong. And John doesn`t have to be a racist to be wrong. But he was dead wrong. And the fact that we`re still nine years later and he cannot say two simple words, I`m sorry, is an incredible problem. But if folks look at the support that we`ve earned, the woman who replaced him as mayor of Braddock endorsed me. OK. And I was just in Braddock two weekends ago.\par \par \uc2 If you go around of Braddock outside of just that one little block that it feels like a lot of people go to, you`ll see a lot of Malcolm Kenyatta signs. And so I think folks have really -- folks who`ve been covering this race have done a real disservice by frankly not covering my campaign that much and deciding a long time ago that this race was only about two people from western Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be about what happens here in places like Philadelphia. And there is a reason that the president trusted me to be the voice and face of his campaign all across the country and he doesn`t just ask me to go to black churches, OK. He was sending me to rural Iowa again and again and again, where they`ve never met somebody named Malcolm Kenyatta from north Philadelphia but people understood that I was the person who can speak with a level of urgency and authenticity about what is broken.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans on the other side are out of state, they`re out of touch, they`re out of their damns minds. And what people want is somebody who they know is going to fight to pass the (INAUDIBLE), raise the minimum wage, who`s going to fight for a woman`s right to choose. Who is going to be bold on issues like expanding the Supreme Court and who is going to be somebody who`s actually been a legislature, which the lieutenant governor has not been, and also who has the lived experience which none of these folks have in this race.\par \par \uc2 Time and time again we talk about how black voters are the heart of the Democratic Party. How this whole debate is about how do we win working- class voters. And then when a working-class black candidate runs, everybody says we don`t know if they can win. But on the Republican side they might be on track to nominate an African-American woman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Woman. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: And so folks think the Republicans won`t vote for a black candidate. They might be voting for one in just a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Malcolm Kenyatta, it`s great to have you on.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Be safe on the trail.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, by the way, we were supposed to have an interview with Kathy Barnette. She pulled out. We hope to have one before the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the latest test of the power of the Trump endorsement. We`ve got results from those two Republican races we told you about that we are watching pretty closely.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live right here. You see what`s behind me. Philadelphia. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it is Wednesday it means, at least in an even numbered year, it means we got some election results to digest. As we told you yesterday we were watching a couple of primaries in Nebraska and West Virginia as the latest in former President Trump`s power and influence over the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump went 1 for 2 in last night`s primaries in Nebraska. The Trump- backed candidate \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2  lost the Republican governor`s primary to Jim Pillen. Herbster who faces credible allegations of groping at least eight women which he denies got 30 percent of the vote while Pillen got close to 34 percent. So Trump`s endorsement certainly gave Herbster probably a little more of a chance than he should have had given those credible allegations.\par \par \uc2 So not a landslide loss for the former president who was in Nebraska last week and stood by Herbster despite those allegations, perhaps always thinking about allegations that are made against him over the years.\par \par \uc2 But Trump did get a win in West Virginia where his pick, Congressman Alex Mooney, beat fellow Congressman David McKinley in a member-versus-member fight in a redrawn district. Trump chose Mooney simply because McKinley did two things that Trump didn`t like. One is he voted to certify the 2020 election and created an independent commission to investigate January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 But the real sin apparently was Trump told him he would endorse his he opponent if he voted for the bipartisan infrastructure deal. Well, he voted for it, Trump kept his word, Joe Manchin did endorse McKinley but it didn`t work. More evidence of how Trump is trying to reshape the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard is in West Virginia. And Vaughn, you say people told you that Trump`s endorsement was the deciding factor for them at the end of the day especially in a district that actually was more of a home game for McKinley than Mooney.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It was. And in fact when we were at the polls we tried to gather a little bit more from folks who were voting for Mooney. Why they were voting for Mooney. But frankly, Chuck, I wish we had a livestream going for everybody to watch our interactions because ultimately the answer came down to Trump and what Trump says, and that is exactly what Trump wants here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think about a month ago, Chuck, we were talking about some of these GOP incumbents around the country, kind of the big 12. Individuals who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  perceived as opposition forces within this Republican Party. One of those individuals was David McKinley because as you said he voted to certify the 2020 election and he voted for what would have been an independent commission to investigate the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Well, so far, one month later, two of them, they`re already down. \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2 , shortly after you had him on your show, he announced his retirement, bowing out of a race that would have been a competitive primary against a Trump-backed candidate and now David McKinley is gone. Of course we`re going to look a month ahead to Tom Rice and Nancy Mace. They`re the next ones up on the list in South Carolina. They`re also threatened to be ousted from this Republican Party as Trump looks ahead to his potential 2024 presidential run.\par \par \uc2 Of course Herbster, the other race there, three-point difference. That`s how close. A man who just in the last month was accused by eight women of groping them including a state senator on the record who said that he had reached up in 2019 up her skirt.\par \par \uc2 I want to also, though, this is sort of breaking to us right now, Chuck. Well, you`re in Pennsylvania, we`re here in West Virginia. There is a new video that we just obtained actually out of Arizona, another Senate race that we are going to be looking at that is still crowded. Trump has yet to endorse in this race but one of the candidates who`s already made a trip to Mar-a-Lago is Blake Masters, the other \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  candidate along with JD Vance, who`s been heavily financed by the billionaire.\par \par \uc2 I want to let you listen here. This is the first time we are airing this, anybody is hearing this. This was Blake Masters back in February when he was asked the question about the future of the Equal Rights Amendment. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: Believe it or not, women are not paid less in America than men. It`s a left-wing narrative, this gender pay gap. When you control for the occupations, you control for people taking time out to, you know, birth children, things are actually pretty equal. And men do the most dangerous jobs. Men are the ones who are -- the police officers. Not all of it but, you know, 80 percent of it in this country.\par \par \uc2 Men are the ones who are doing risky, you know, fishing, crab fishing in Alaska. Sometimes those jobs pay more. Sometimes those jobs pay more. And so I think we`ve got to push back on the left-wing narrative that women don`t have equal rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 I want women to have equal rights but we don`t need an Equal Rights Amendment that would actually just be a Trojan horse to give the government more power to implement \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` national gender strategy, right. This is what the left does and you have to resist it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Now, Chuck, in this video here, this is what we`re talking about when we`re talking about the new Republican Party of today. And there is much we could tackle here around what you just heard from Blake Masters. But one of them, I think it`s important and fair to note, there is a gender pay gap here in America.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, there is a disparity between maternal leave and parental leave in this country. There`s a systemic and cultural divide between what companies have long offered their employees. And we should note, there is no maternal leave required by the federal government here, meaning, oftentimes women feel forced ultimately to choose child care over their careers.\par \par \uc2 And of course there has long been a pay disparity because of the way in which women have been not as eager to be promoted in the workforce over time which hits at the root of that pay disparity. These are the issues here that this Republican Party is coming to terms with here in these months ahead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it is yet another example of perhaps explains why the gender gap seems to only grow as we see this divide between the two parties more so particularly among gender and it may only grow in this midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard, on the trail for us. Vaughn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the abortion fight could scramble next week`s primary races. We`re going to hear from a group of conservative keystone state voters about where they stand after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live here in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:12]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The leaked Supreme Court draft on Roe vs. Wade last week has made it clear that abortion rights will be a key issue in the Pennsylvania primary next week, more so than even I expected, I`ll be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what Republican voters I spoke with yesterday told me organically, unprompted about the potential repeal of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HERRING, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: I am pro -- I`m anti-abortion. And I`d like them to speak out a little bit about where he actually stands on it. If you have a life inside of you and someone stabs you in the stomach and kills it, at what point is it murder and at what point it is nothing. And I don`t think any woman would tell you the life inside -- it wasn`t murder.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA FINLON, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: No, but I think it should be the woman`s choice.\par \par \uc2 HERRING: Well --\par \par \uc2 FINLON: And I think they should --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINLON: I respect everyone`s views. But I definitely am strongly, I am definitely pro-life with exception.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you going to go into the ballot box thinking about that issue?\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Absolutely. Depending on who it is. And I`ve already thought about that for our governor race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting. Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Because there were two governors that came out that said I am pro- life with exception, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the exception mattered to you.\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Yes, the exception mattered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s just a reminder of what voters are listening for. It`s not about pro-life, pro-choice. They`re listening for a lot of the details. And this is why the threats of full bans I think make this politically such a potentially problematic issue for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have much more on all aspects of that race, what could changes its trajectory in the final six days with somebody who`s been following this race a lot longer than us. When we come back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. As deep as we like to say we take things in politics, we`re going to go deeper and more local here. I`m joined by Lauren Mayk. She covers politics for our NBC station here in Philadelphia, WCAU.\par \par \uc2 And Lauren, you`ve been covering this primary when there were more candidates running, when there were more better candidates running, before we even knew a TV doctor was going to be running. So here we are less than a week out. Are you prepared for Oz-Fetterman? Is this that what we`re staring at here or is this that fluid?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MAYK, NBC 10 PHILADELPHIA REPORTER: Could be, although, you know, when you look at the latest polls coming out, it looks like you`ve got a three-way race on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At least on the Republican side. Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: On the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me about Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes, Kathy Barnette, I have interviewed her and she has been running for quite a while. She got in this race before Oz, before McCormick. She`s been running for about a year or so. So she has been sort of slowly building a base. She also is closely tied to \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who is running for governor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: So --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re almost an official ticket of sorts.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Of sorts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: An alliance type of thing.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: An alliance of sorts. She`s appeared in an ad for him. He has endorsed her. They appear at events together. So you can --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re pretty out there on the Trump stuff. Not Mastriano and Barnette, right. They don`t believe in the election results, is that fair?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes, I`ve had a conversation with her about, you know, about the election, and sort of went back and forth with her trying to get information about whether she believes that the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: As I`ve had with multiple candidates to be fair. That is a conversation that we had.\par \par \uc2 TODD: She leans into it a little bit more, though? Is that fair to say?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. Yes. And she previously ran for Congress in Montgomery County. So she also has experience as a candidate, which gives her a little bit more name recognition. But really, just, she has been sort of building this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s interesting, in talking to the group of voters we talked to in northeastern Pennsylvania yesterday, I got this sense that both Oz and McCormick, there`s still skepticism.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 Are they for real? Are they really Trump guys? Are they really American firsters? Are they really -- I mean, the one voter they wanted to know what Dr. Oz`s real abortion position was. He`s just not sure. That`s how what sparked that conversation for him. And it seems like Kathy Barnette is benefiting from those who are like, well, I know where she stands.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: She is benefiting from people looking for an alternative. And I`ll tell you the airwaves here in the Philadelphia area had been full of Oz and McCormick. So if you are looking for someone who is not them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: I think Jeff Bartos may have thought that he might have benefited from that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: But it looks like she is benefiting from that right now, although what we don`t know is whether it will ultimately benefit Oz.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Because you now have two strong candidates splitting an anti-Oz vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me about the Philadelphia Democratic machine. Is it not going to deliver for Cobor Lamb?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  has gotten quite a few endorsements in the Philadelphia area. I know that you have talked to Malcolm Kenyatta. He is from Philadelphia. And John Fetterman, the lieutenant governor, has a lot of support here but \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  has gotten a lot of the official endorsements.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: The Democratic Party here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Quote-unquote, "machine," if you want to call it that anymore, you know.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. Yes. So the question is, will they be able to turn out for him, will they have people on the street for him. Malcolm Kenyatta will have a strong union, SCIU is backing him. They will be on the street for him. The black clergy of Philadelphia is backing \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . They are telling me that they are going to have people getting his message out. So we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to be fascinated to follow the results just from Philadelphia. It`s going to be fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Mayk, it`s been great to have you as a colleague on this race so thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`re going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. That I promise you. We`ll be back in studio. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur, who will be carrying President Biden`s remarks right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Dasha Burns, Andy Mehalshick, Lisa Sylvester, Yamiche  Alcindor \par \uc2 9,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday and it`s an election year. It`s a special MEET THE MIDTERMS edition of MEET TH PRESS DAILY. We are live from battleground Pennsylvania, on the banks of the mighty Susquehanna River.\par \par \uc2 It`s fun to get the music, right? Good day. I`m Chuck Todd. I`m here in Luzerne County, behind me, Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania. We`re on the ground here in Pennsylvania with one week to go until some of the most important primaries on the 2022 Midterm calendar. We`re talking to voters, candidates, and officials in a state that could very well decide which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in November. And of course, Pennsylvania might be the most pivotal battleground in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The Senate race features primaries in both parties that are crowded, contentious, and ideologically diverse. And they`re very reflective of the national issues facing each party right now. For Republicans, the testing of the power of Trump. For Democrats, is the latest round of the moderate versus progressive fight.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be in Philly tomorrow for more on the Democratic side of these primaries, but today, here in northeastern Pennsylvania, we want to talk more about the Republican Party and Trump`s power in it. It`s our focus here in battleground Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And we might get some additional clues about the current trajectory of the GOP because Republican primary voters are voting right now in Nebraska and West Virginia, a pair of GOP races that will put Trump`s power and populist stances and influence to the test.\par \par \uc2 Both states feature races, where the former president is backing what some believed, is a more flawed candidate while others are sticking with a more establishment candidate. It could shed some insight on the big Republican race right here where television personality Mehmet Oz has the former president`s backing in the Pennsylvania senator race, but he has struggled to break free from the primary pack.\par \par \uc2 Oz is a newcomer to politics, has had to dive deep into the Maga world after having a completely different political identity while appearing on television. Oz and his Republican rival David McCormick are on the airwaves big time. They`ve been spending millions attacking each other`s Maga bonafides.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, it`s political commentator and newcomer, Kathy Barnette, who may be the spoiler here. She has seen a late rise in polls despite spending a fraction of the money, perhaps benefiting from Oz and McCormick fighting each other. We`re going to talk to Barnette live on MEET THE PRESS DAILY tomorrow. So, all of the Pennsylvania Republicans are trying to out-Trump each other despite only Oz having Trump`s formal backing.\par \par \uc2 This morning, what was interesting, I was at a campaign event where McCormick stopped to talk to me for a few minutes. The event itself was about the need for better-trained workers and didn`t hear much red meat at all. You didn`t hear about any of the primary, all the silly Trump stuff. But then when I asked him how he played to win the nomination without Trump`s endorsement, well, this is all he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you win this without Trump?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK, PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: All right, listen. I`m not doing any more interviews but I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, no. How do you win without Trump? You got to -- you met --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All right, thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: See you, Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For what it`s worth, we had a robust conversation about whether immigration or they need to do a labor workforce here in northeastern Pennsylvania. That was some substance there. After that, though, when Trump`s name came up, they had to go. So look, we got a lot to talk about here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now my NBC News colleague, Dasha Burns, who`s basically been spending most of her time in this part of Pennsylvania as part of our MEET THE PRESS county to county midterm project, also with us is Andy Mehalshick. He`s the local political Guru here at our NBC affiliate WBRE in Wilkes-Barre. And in a moment, we`re going to be joined by Lisa Sylvester from our NBC affiliate WPXI in Pittsburgh, on the other side of the state. She`s had the benefit of moderating both a Republican and Democratic Senate debate in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, you`ve been on the ground here. Let me start with you. The fascinating part to me about McCormick`s event today is what wasn`t said.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think I was the first one to bring Trump`s name up. And fair -- and fairness, it was not -- in fact, he didn`t say the word Republican until the very end. It was a very -- if you didn`t watch any of the TV ads, you`d have -- it was just -- it was almost discombobulated.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes. I mean, that`s a very different picture to what we`ve seen on the airwaves, to what we`ve seen at the rallies, and to what we`ve been seeing from all of these candidates. Like you said, Oz has a Trump endorsement but all of the other candidates are trying to represent the Trump brand. And when you talk to voters, which we`ve been doing nonstop -- listen, I called -- as soon as the Trump endorsement happened, I called voters and every single one of them was scratching their head. They didn`t understand why Oz was running. They didn`t understand why he was running in Pennsylvania. He was from --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you`ve got that same reaction that had -- he endorsed McCormick?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No. I don`t think so. They know him as a celebrity doctor who talks about products on television.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: They do not know him as a politician, as somebody who understands the issues. And they don`t see the Trump alignment there other than the celebrity. And when we watched the first debate, every single time he brought up the Trump endorsement, people groaned. The groans grew louder each time he brought it up.\par \par \uc2 And the voters that we watched that debate with came out saying, these two front runners we`re seeing their names everywhere, but there are these mega-rich guys, I don`t really understand how they can connect with me and represent me. So they came out of this a little bit disillusioned.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, I think, I -- if I -- one thing I did hear McCormick say a lot was how close -- how many ties he has to the state of Pennsylvania. Seventh Generation Pennsylvanian, he said at one point. Obviously, he thinks he A, has to continue to establish that. But is that the way to sort of keep knocking us?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MEHALSHICK, WBRE-TV REPORTER: I think it is because I`ve covered several McCormick events over the last several weeks and that is a common theme.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: My farm -- I own a farm in Bloomsburg, Pennsylvania, right in central Pennsylvania. He`s the native homegrown guy. I`m not the guy who parachuted in from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He named checked all the places he played high school sports, right?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, he`s been very careful with that title.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Entitle -- when you`re talking about in -- anywhere in Pennsylvania, especially in northeastern Pennsylvania, Shikellamy, Shickshinny, Bloomsburg, Catawissa, small towns, they are the folks who are the Trump supporters, will be driving through the highways and you`ll see the Trump flags. You`ll bring America back, that kind of thing. So yes, he`s getting keynotes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think Oz and McCormick have had either one of them actually tapped into the Maga base? I mean is it -- is it a turnout that those folks are sniffing them both out here?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I don`t think they have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Again, anecdotally, I don`t think they have because it`s so close. And I see Kathy Barnette and we were talking -- you know, people I`ve spoken to on the street said that she is resonating her message with folks, is she the real conservative Republican in this race in Pennsylvania. But you mentioned before you`re talking about the celebrity of Dr. Oz. I think people do get a good vibe -- maybe they get a vibe from Oz because he`s like a Trump-type personality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: No, Trump wasn`t a lawmaker. He was involved -- he was a celebrity. So they`re trying to think is this the same kind of a blueprint to success?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, you`ve been talking to a bunch of voters.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What have you learned?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I don`t think that`s necessarily sticking though. Look, when you look at the polls, Oz 18 percent, McCormick 16 percent, that`s not a whole lot of the vote. 40 percent --\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s not -- this is a big undecided. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: 40 percent are undecided, Chuck. That is a massive number of people. One week out, what do we got, 40 are undecided? We went door-knocking with two ladies. They call themselves Trump gals. They are huge Trump fans. And they`ve been door-knocking for three weeks for Carla Sands and another underdog in this race. They love Trump. They don`t get the Oz thing.\par \par \uc2 They think he made a mistake. They`ve knocked on doors in Westmoreland County, deep, deep red. They said they have not yet met a single Oz supporter. And they were themselves surprised by that. And when we went door-knocking with them for you know, a couple of hours, every single door that opened was an undecided voter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very interesting. You know, one thing that I think some of us overlooked in `16 and none of us overlooked at -- in `20 is, Andy, and I`m sure you -- Trump`s political organizations quite strong in Pennsylvania. They did a bunch of voter re-registration between 16 and 20. Are you seeing evidence that he`s able to flip that switch for us yet?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think he can. I know. Well, you`re -- well, you`re a week away from the May 17 primary. And it seems you know in the political world, that`s like a blink of an eye. However, because the race is so close, if you have all these undecided which is what I`m hearing on the street, undecided, even there -- those supporting OZ are not a -- or of McCormick are not attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They`re saying we don`t -- we respect him. We don`t respect his decision. Do I think he can flip the switch? Absolutely. We were talking before air back in 2016 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took the ride down the escalator, OK?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: And I could tell you why here in northeastern Pennsylvania, there was a long line of people saying they need to laugh at him right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: But he resonates - -his message. He`s speaking about what we`re talking about at the dinner table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to -- about to bring in Lisa here and I want to get some of her impression from the other side of the state. But, Andy, I got to ask you this just in case I don`t get another chance. Oz, Fetterman, in northeastern Pennsylvania, who would -- if -- you know who would play better here?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: That`s like Rocky Balboa.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: The sequel, first, second, the third fight who would fare better? Again, anecdotally, I think right now, at this point in time, again a week before --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fetterman could over-perform an average Democrat right now.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s Oz. Is it easier against Oz or McCormick?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Easier against Oz I believe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Because he`s from Braddock, Pennsylvania. He`s a local guy. He speaks the language of the Democrats -- but also a little bit of conservatism in there also. So he`s not your traditional Democratic candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. This is the story from the east part of the state. Let`s go to the other side of the state where I have my colleague, Lisa Sylvester. Lisa, you`ve moderated both debates so you get an idea, obviously of what`s resonating -- who`s resonating the best in this Republican primary out there.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 I think of Beaver County. I know that`s Trump country. Who`s playing best in Beaver County?\par \par \uc2 LISA SYLVESTER, WPXI-TV ANCHOR: Well, it really comes down to celebrity versus sincerity. So, Oz obviously has the celebrity factor. He`s got the wow. People know him. They know his name from the years of being on television. But I got to tell you, coming out of the debate, the one person that a lot of people were saying, hey, let me take another look at was actually Kathy Barnette. And you have actually seen her numbers rise since the debate. And the reason why I think is because she comes across as more sincere.\par \par \uc2 So if you are looking for the America First candidate, a lot of people are taking another look at her because she doesn`t just sort of speak the talking points, you really get the sense that she lives them, that she has a history, that she has believed and has held these beliefs for a while. With McCormick and Oz, it`s a little bit of a head-scratcher because you`re like, well, maybe this is what they really believe but maybe it`s not, especially when you see the attack ads.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing too is Kathy Barnette, if you look at just the amount of money spent in this race, she has spent nowhere near the amount of money that McCormick and Oz have spent, yet she is resonating. And I think it`s because she`s doing very much a grassroots, 1500 miles traveling across the state, and people see her as sincere and they see her as authentic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Lisa, I know we have a big satellite delay. I really appreciate you giving us a few minutes there. Dasha, let me bring it back to you. What she just described with Kathy Barnette is what I hear people describe John Fetterman to, right? It is sort of the hey, they`re not traditional, but they`re speak -- it`s this -- you know, there`s a new paradigm.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And it`s the language that was used about Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m hearing voters say the exact same things, not the typical politician about Fetterman and about Kathy. She has an unconventional story and she really has been doing that grassroots work. She has been all over Pennsylvania. And in many ways, her talking points when you actually listen to her match up very closely with what a lot of the Trump base wants to hear. And like I said, some people are impressed that someone with a lot less money, with a lot less name recognition, people liked an underdog.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trump was outspent left and right in `16. People forget this. On every level, he was outspent. So if you do catch enough traction on primary, you don`t need to be the biggest spender right, Andy?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right. And I could tell you back -- going back to 2016, who would have thought in northeastern Pennsylvania, a rich guy from New York with a lot of money who`d never been involved in politics, far as running for office can win in Luzerne County, and flipped his county, which as we all know, is a bellwether state in the whole country? And we`re doing shows here. Why? Because we saw it in 2016. Can it happen again? Can he flip it to Oz this time around? I think he can.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not out of the realm of possibility. But as Dasha was talking before, there`s different dynamics -- and I think people also anecdotally, they don`t want your typical politician. We`ve seen that, been there, and done that, what`s coming down that somebody who really knows what we`re feeling.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s why I think McCormick isn`t necessarily running away with it because if the Republican Party couldn`t chisel a perfect candidate, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, he looks like a perfect 2012 candidate.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I say this with no disrespect. He looks exactly like Mitt Romney`s Republican Party and that would have been a winner.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: At the same time, the question I have is for those 40 percent of undecided voters. At the end of the day, will they say, listen, there`s not enough time to do all this research?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trump says this guy`s it. You know, I talked to one gentleman who owns a bar, Dave Magill. They call him Mogie. He owns Mogie`s Irish pub in the western part of the state. He told me he flipped from Democrat to Republican to vote for Trump. He still considers himself a Democrat, but he voted for Trump. He wants a Republican victory because of the economy, he blames Biden for inflation for his business suffering.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But he told me he`s undecided but Trump might be the final factor because he said Trump picks winners.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, before I let you go, I got to ask you about \uc2 Bob Casey Jr\uc2 . voting the way he`s voting on abortion rights. Now, I -- you`ve been covering politics slightly longer than I have but the Casey name in pro- life Democrat this was -- you know, when I thought of the picture of pro- life Democrat in the political dictionary that you and I grew up with, Bob Casey Sr. was on it.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s why Jr. was recruited, why he won by so many points. How will that play here? How does his shift on abortion rights play here? And is -- what is the line? Are you going to get punished more for being thrown out a right ban or for being for fewer exemptions?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think it will be a factor with the abortion issue for sure. I remember -- I can`t remember what presidential convention it was. And Bob Casey would not allow --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, we`ll leave it.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: To come to -- into the rooms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think it was `96, right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I believe, 1996.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I`ve been around a little bit longer as well, but I`m not sure who`s been around longer, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re close, I know.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: But will it play big time? I think so, too. I mean, as you saw Casey defeated Barletta several years ago. Casey is a juggernaut in Pennsylvania. He`s a respected individual, well-liked. But I think on the abortion situation, and real conservative folks are going to say, we respect it -- we respect him. We don`t respect his decision on this abortion issue. I think it could may be a big factor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What was interesting to me today, and granted, it was a -- this was an event that McCormick was having at a technical school wherein the -- in the -- essentially the energy capital of the state this area right now so it`s understandable, but not a bit of any of that social conservative red meat, not a lick of it. Are you seeing the rhetoric on the trail heat up at all on abortion rights or no?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I got you.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No, that -- and that`s been the big surprise to me. On the Democratic side, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, but not on the Republican.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But not on the Republican side. Even the voters I`ve been talking to have been repeating what you`re hearing from Republican politicians talking about the leak, the issues with the Supreme Court, not about the actual contents of that leak. I`m not hearing that from voters. I`m certainly not hearing that from the candidates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I get the sense. Dr. Oz does not want to have a detailed conversation about abortion.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If I do hear about abortion, I hear about it from voters saying wait a minute, that Oz guy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is he really is --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is he really pro-life?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: And your -- when you watch political ads, we know what they are. It seems it didn`t -- we know what the beast is. But there`s not denying what you`ve said. Whether you`ve changed your mind, there`s not denying what you said. And I think a true conservative is what I know in northeastern Pennsylvania and hearing about the Keystone State that matters to them. Your word, what -- how -- it`s such a big issue how do you change that quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: If you said any words --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s hard for some people to understand how you switch on an issue like that.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s one thing on trade policy, right? It seems a little bit different for people.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I say one of these deals was --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: That -- not with abortion, though.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Same here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Get --\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: My pleasure and honor to you\par \par \uc2 TODD: Get the Intel -- get the Intel from you here.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Thank you for having m.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, you`ve been doing terrific work. So thank you both.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And to Lisa Sylvester out there at WPXI, I really appreciate your help as well. Up next. One of the reasons we picked Luzerne County as a place to watch in 2022 is because it is representative of a sea change in American politics where long-standing loyalties to the Democratic Party have shifted to the right.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to speak with two Pennsylvania officials who have had an up-close and personal experience with this changing electorate. They both join me here on set after this short break. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from the Keystone State.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: As we mentioned at the beginning of the show, we`re in beautiful Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, one of the areas of the state that has grown increasingly Republican over the last few election cycles. Luzerne went for Trump in 2016 and again in 2020 after going for Democrats in the previous four presidential elections. The County also voted for Senator Bob Casey`s Republican challenger in the 2018 Midterms. So we`ve been watching this shift go on for most of the decades.\par \par \uc2 So, what does that shift continue to look like here in Lucerne and what can it tell us about the larger sea change in American politics ahead of next week`s primaries? I got a couple of guests here who`ve been dealing with this changing electorate. Two politicians have had a front-row seat to these changes, the current independent state senator and a former Democrat, John Yudichak, and the mayor of Wilkes-Barre, George Brown. Mayor Brown, Senator, good to see you both. Mayor, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE C. BROWN, MAYOR OF WILKES-BARRE, PENNSYLVANIA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, this used to be a pretty strong democratic area, a lot of Union Democrats, culturally conservative but economic populist. We`ve watched this shift. Tell me -- tell me about it on your own -- through your own lens.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Sure. Well, Wilkes-Barre still is a strong democratic city. The majority of voters are democratic in Wilkes-Barre. But when you talk about the county, it`s more of a 50-50 split. And in the last elections, we saw when President Trump ran against Hillary, he had more of a commanding lead with the Democrats when he ran against Mr. Biden, about 5000 less Democrats than he had when he ran against Hillary. So, it really depends, I think, on the candidates that are running.\par \par \uc2 And whether you`re a Democrat or Republican, that`s how they decide which way to vote as far as the candidate. Do they feel a relationship with their candidate, do they feel that the values that candidates putting out is what they`re looking for. So -- but Wilkes-Barre still is a predominantly Democratic city.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, you`ve got elected as a Democrat, you decided to leave the party. You didn`t go to the Republicans, you want to be independent. And these days, it`s tough to be in the middle. You -- trust me I`m aware of left and right don`t like that these days. Tell me in your own words why you did it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YUDICHAK, INDEPENDENT STATE SENATOR, PENNSYLVANIA: Well, I think the party that captures the middle, whether that`s the middle class or the middle of the road voter is the one that can govern. We have the two bases of the respective parties, the Republicans and the Democrats. Their base, they`re bankrupt. They`re driven more by outrage and hate against one another than they are about rallying around behind the city, the Commonwealth, or the country. That`s why I moved from the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 I grew up -- my father was a united mine worker, my mom worked for Ask Me, a public-sector union. Blue-collar roots are very, very deep in my family for generations. When the Democratic Party -- particularly at the national level, not only started to dismiss those blue-collar voters but to demonize them, that`s when I decided I need to get to the --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Where did they demonize them? I mean, could they --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Policy-wise, you get -- I`ve had progressives will argue, hey, who`s actually advocating for a better policy for these folks, and they take --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I`ll give you a great example.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I`ll give you a great example. Chuck, I was a big advocate for an energy project here in Luzerne County, a $6 billion project that`s going to be transformative for the region, $25 billion economic impacts, and also right on the environment, but because it was tied to natural gas, 50 percent of the Senate Democratic Caucus voted against -- voted against legislation that would provide an incentive to this company.\par \par \uc2 And not only did they vote against it, when we had a rally with Union building trade workers, union building trade workers fighting for their jobs. The rallying cry of the progressives on the far right was your jobs don`t matter. And when you tell a man or a woman that their job don`t matter -- that their job doesn`t matter, you`re telling them they don`t matter. That`s when I decided I need to get to the center to get away from the extremes on both the right and the left to try to rally the middle, middle class, and the middle-of-the-road voters to get back to common-sense policies in this commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Brown, how would you -- tell me how do you -- this energy sector has been pretty important to economic development around here. Obviously, there`s a -- there`s concern about climate change and there`s some concern about -- what would you say to national Democratic leaders about both the importance of that, and how do you pitch climate change proposals that don`t scare people around here?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, let`s be honest. There is a change in the climate. People can debate that. They can say yes and no. I firmly believe there is.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ve held rallies in Wilkes-Barre on Public Square addressing the climate change. We have to take a realistic approach to this. There is a problem. We have to make sure that we address it. But at the national level, the leaders at the national level have to do this. Naturally, as a city, I will get to follow whatever they put out if I believe in my heart it`s the right way to go. But it is serious, it`s real. And I -- also, I do implore our congressmen, our senators, our president, to take this seriously and make sure that we take the proper stance here and the proper methods.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How would you sell it? How would you -- how would you appease, Senator -- the senator, Yudichak here.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: How would I?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appease. You know, the concern about the issue of, hey, these regulations are there, you know, that the half of the Democratic Party essentially doesn`t want to use government investment for the energy sector. What would you say to the national level?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, I think that it`s going to be necessary down the road. It`s going to have to happen. Senator Yudichak and I, we`ve agreed on several key issues over the last three years that I`ve been in office, and the Senator has been a wonderful help as far as securing funds for us for different projects we have going on during the city.\par \par \uc2 So, we are like in several ways as far as the major policies that we want to push through. You`re exactly right. I mean, we have to get together in unison and work on this. We have to do something about the energy problem. Right now, $4.59 a gallon, that`s not going to cut it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I keep seeing $6.50 for diesel. That is just scary.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you see that number there. I`m curious, Senator, when you move to -- when you -- when you got rid of the D and you put the I there, is it makes it harder to legislate? Does it make -- you know that`s the one you know that there`s a huge desire. Voters say they want more independent candidates.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it`s hard to let -- it`s hard to function when you don`t have somebody --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: It`s a great question. As a student of politics as you are --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The conventional wisdom would be yes, it makes it much harder from the middle and from the independent party. That wasn`t the case. Some of my biggest legislative achievements have been in the last three years because I was one independent, two in the Republican majority, and three have many Democrat friends like Mayor Brown here in the city of Wilkes- Barre. We`re able to cross the aisle. That is really what voters want to see. Can you accomplish tangible things?\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Pennsylvania is closed primaries, and independents like myself can`t vote, so only the extremes dominate the primary election. And I think that`s what you see playing out in states that have closed primaries. If you had a more robust primary and open primary, you would be able to have that debate, and legislators would not have to be looking to primary opponents. Rather, they`d be looking to be appealing to general election voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Give me the general election in a Senate race that would be the most difficult for you to decide to pick.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The -- we try --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meaning, you do -- tell me the two candidates that if they face each other, that`ll make your harder.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: In this Pennsylvania --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Pennsylvania primary? I think that you mentioned it earlier. I think Oz, Fetterman --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Beats up to see if you breathe them.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: It will be a very tough decision for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why?\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK:: I think now someone on the Democratic side like \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , who would represent this area very well, who just got the endorsement from the Philadelphia Inquirer, a rather progressive newspaper. They said he`s a candidate that could add value versus a candidate like Fetterman, who just adds a vote. I think both candidates, Oz and Fetterman, are more about celebrity than they are substance. So that would be a very difficult challenge for voters in the Northeast between Oz and Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Brown, who would -- who would do best here? Come you`d think could do the work better?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I feel it would come down to \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  -- Congressman Lamb, and also, Dr. Oz. Dr. Oz has the name the publicity, the money. Congressman Lamb has using Congress and Marine Corps experience. I think I liked some of his values that he`s put forth. So I would say that as a battle between Congressman Lamb and Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I appreciate it. I have a feeling I got much better political handicapping from the two of you than from anybody we`re dealing with today. Good to be talking to both of you. Good to meet you.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Welcome to Wilkes-Barre.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What Chamber of Commerce weather you brought here? We got to give the mayor a credit for that.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The mayor gets all the credit out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, what we did looks out --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We knew you`re accompanying --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is -- I mean, not a --\par \par \uc2 BROWN: When Lester was here --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Literally, not figuratively, literally not a cloud in the sky if you can find it.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: When Lester was here two years ago, we couldn`t do anything for him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Same Picture?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: But rain it was there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Rain, all right. I`ll let him know. Lester now knows you know -- gets more love here. All right, Mayor Brown, John Yudichak, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to head to the other side of the state, back to Beaver County, another place that we put on our county-to-county spotlight and another place where Republicans are making inroads into what used to be traditional Democratic-leaning union workers. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live here in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Since taking office President Biden has vowed to be the most pro-union president in American history. Just last week he met with the leader of Amazon`s labor union and in Ohio he called unions the single best workers in the world. Union members, that is.\par \par \uc2 Union workers have long been a key voting bloc for Biden and Democrats. But Republicans have made some major inroads with these voters as well. In some places really scrambling the political terrain and battlegrounds across the country. We saw it in eastern Iowa a few months ago. We see it right here in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, and we see it where we find my colleague NBC News Washington correspondent Yamiche Alcindor who is in Beaver County, Pennsylvania. A place that was heavily, heavily a Democratic pro-union county.\par \par \uc2 And Yamiche, it`s still pro-union, but it`s not so pro-Democratic.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: That`s certainly right, Chuck. And last night, I was about an hour north of Pittsburgh, right on the border of Ohio, and listening to a town hall with the \uc2 United Steel Workers\uc2  talking about sort of the things that are important to them which is worker rights and retirement, but also talking about the divisions among them, the differences.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what Bernie Hall, one of the leaders in that union, had to say about those differences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE HALL, UNITED STEEL WORKERS DISTRICT 10 DIRECTOR: I think union members are no different than anybody else, right. They don`t live in a vacuum. So it`s not just about, you know, labor issues. They also are concerned about women`s rights. They are also concerned about education. In some parts of the state, gun rights are a big issue. But at the end of the day, typically what we find is working people vote their paycheck.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 You know, what`s going to pay my bills, what`s going to keep a roof over my head, what`s going to put my kids through school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: So Bernie Hall was really trying to make the case in that meeting that workers should really be voting their pocketbooks. But he also made the case that they cannot be thinking about the political atmosphere where they`re voting. And he was talking about the Supreme Court case and the poised decision to possibly overturn Roe v. Wade. He said if the Supreme Court can overturn a law from 1973, what about the 1935 Wagner Act that said that companies had to organize and had to sort of negotiate rather with the unions that were helping employees get better wages? So it was a real interesting connection to hear him make that case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Yamiche, I`m curious. And look, if the answer is no, it`s no, did you see any evidence -- any of the Senate candidates sort of making a play for any of those workers last night?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: John Fetterman was very, very popular among that crowd. They were saying that he came, he talked to rank-and-file workers. It might be the shorts. It might be the fact that he feels more like them. But he apparently made a very, very good pitch to them. And they feel good about that.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, though, that one union leader said something to me that had stuck in my head ever since he said it. He said it`s really going to come down to the three G`s among the rank-and-file and that is God, guns and gynecology -- Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s an interesting third G that we`ve not heard before. The reason I asked about, and I`m curious on the Republican side, we have -- they`re closed primaries and I know there aren`t a lot of registered Republicans among labor union members, but it is something that is growing. No evidence of any Republican candidates trying to win these votes?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, Republicans are doing -- a lot of the work that`s being done for Republicans I`m being told is on the media. It`s sort of what information that people are taking in. So I was listening to union leaders talk about how do we balance what our workers and our fellow union workers are listening to on the radio. Basically they`re saying that the message from conservatives are that Democrats want to take your gun.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are going to be against abortion. So he shouldn`t be voting for them for that. So they are really in some ways going with -- leaning into the cultural issues while on the other side, you heard union leaders really try to explain to a lot of their members and really try to strategize about how to make union workers understand that workers` rights, retirement, the infrastructure bill, some of the things that President Biden has passed that that is what`s in their best interest.\par \par \uc2 But I have to tell you, you could feel the tension in the room and the culture it seems to be working. Except where I said John Fetterman, who was in some ways meeting them with the culture, the way he dresses, the way he talks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Really trying to give a message to working-class Americans.\par \par \uc2 I will also say that they were handing out these forms, which really had X`s by all the Republicans and checkmarks by all the Democrats trying in a subtle way to say that the Democrats have your back and the Republican do not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, I`ll tell you, boy, the evidence just keeps piling up as to explain why so many national Democrats have turned away from \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and have started focusing on John Fetterman here. I think they do see him as their best candidate to win this Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche Alcindor, I`m going to see you tomorrow on the other side of the state. We`ll meet in Philadelphia. Thanks for your work out there in the western of the state.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, President Biden tries to ease fears of surging inflation nationwide. Vulnerable Democrats face some anxious voters in their district.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2  will join us next. I told you I have seen $6.50 diesel. $6.50. She`s in a competitive Pennsylvania district that Democrats are trying to hang on to in November.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. While much of the focus here in Pennsylvania this November will be on that vacant Senate seat, thanks to retiring Republican \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , Democrats here and nationwide are also facing the increasing likelihood of losing control of the House. Last hour President Biden laid out his plan to address the issue that`s become perhaps the Democrats` biggest liability and the top concern for voters this year. Inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want every American to know that I`m taking inflation very seriously, and it`s my top domestic priority. My plan is to lower everyday costs for hard working Americans and lower the deficit by asking large corporations and the wealthiest Americans to not engage in price gouging and to pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 The Republican plan is to increase taxes on the middle-class families, let billionaires and large companies off the hook as they raise profits and raise prices and reach record profit amounts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m joined now by Congresswoman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2 , Democrat from Pennsylvania, right here in Pennsylvania, whom the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee says holds one of the most competitive seats this November. I`m sure that is not music to the congresswoman`s ears.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, it`s good to see you. Thanks for spending a few minutes here with me.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRISSY HOULAHAN (D-PA): It`s nice to be with you. Thanks for joining us in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, look. $6.50 diesel. Right. You just -- you see it. It really stings. Right? $4.50 is bad enough for regular gas. You see that. So you see this everyday reminder of this inflation hit. Look, you heard the president`s remarks. What more do you want from him? What more do you want from the administration right now?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: Sure, and thanks for having me. And you`re not wrong. Those are difficult prices to see and something that we all collectively need to address in whatever capacity we can from each of our roles. And so what I would say for my role is the message I would like to project to you is that Democrats in this Congress have for the last several years been able to stop what could have been a catastrophe, what could have been a depression of the likes of which we haven`t seen in a long time.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t experience that. And as a result of the American Rescue Plan and the Infrastructure Act and the CARES Act and a number of other things, we`ve been able to layer on the help that has allowed our economy to be able to survive. And now we are absolutely experiencing some of the other side effects of that help that we`ve provided.\par \par \uc2 But we do very much need to recognize, we are in a place where we now in my community in Chester County where I`m sitting and talking to you right now have 3.2 percent unemployment, a really good economy. And we certainly need to address the fact that gas prices are a problem and grocery store prices are a problem. But we also need to recognize that we are still here standing two years after a literal pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there`s two things that the administration could do arguably. Look, the Fed, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has what it can do with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 But there are two things that could be done with policy. One is, you mentioned the unemployment rate. It means we actually have a labor shortage. We need to open the borders a little bit. Obviously, politically, that`s a different conversation. The second is to repeal some of those tariffs on Chinese products.\par \par \uc2 Are you for a spaghetti at the wall approach right now by this Biden White House? Hey, pull some tariffs, add some immigrant labor, whatever it takes to lower cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: Well, what I certainly am for some of those things. As an example, I did encourage the administration to make sure that we open the Strategic Oil Reserves, which we did. I have encouraged the administration to encourage the fact that all of our pipelines and oil that had been pre- pandemic be activated for one reason or another, we`ve reactivated the rigs that were put into a dormant position were reactivated. So I have done that.\par \par \uc2 In terms of immigration, yes, I absolutely believe that we have a real problem with not enough people to fill our jobs. Whether they are on our mushroom farms or in our \uc2 QVC\uc2  headquarters which is here in my district. We do need to have comprehensive and bipartisan immigration reform. And I really do think that`s something, frankly, that the Congress can do that I`ve been encouraging my colleagues across the aisle to do alongside with me.\par \par \uc2 So you`re right, each one of us has a different role to play. The administration has their role. The Fed has theirs and the Congress has ours. And I believe that we, as Democrats, are working very, very hard to make sure that we`re working on behalf of all of the people, the working people of our communities and our economy to make sure that we are instead of taking things away from them, that we are instead providing opportunities for us to survive and to thrive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me your midterm message. Look, between the issue of reproductive rights and the fact that women may actually have rights taken away from them that were once granted. You have the inflation issue. They`re not two issues that easily go together when you`re making your elevator pitch, when you`re making your campaign messaging. How do you plan to do it?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: So my community is that purple place in Pennsylvania. And that`s why you`re probably sitting here talking to me. It is that place that has 40 percent Dems, 40 percent Republicans, and 20 percent independents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: And we want to see our government work for us on behalf of us. We are a pragmatic people and we want to work on what I call endearingly, affectionately the no duh agenda, which is making sure we provide terrific health care opportunities for all of us, making sure that we have terrific educational opportunities for us all. Great jobs that treat one another with decency and civility.\par \par \uc2 All of those things, but the other thing which you kind of alluded to in terms of choice, we want to make sure that we`re protecting the rights that we already have and the fact that we are all equally able to be successful in this economy and in this society. And so the no duh agenda is the kitchen table kind of conversation. But overarching that is the civility and decency that I think we all demand from one another. And choice is part of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think you`re going to have a hard -- you know, Democrats had a hard time selling the economy last year when there was -- seemed like it was a lot more positive news. So you`ve got to sell this hey, look, we`re the party that can, you know, we got handed another crisis, third straight Democratic president that gets handed a crumbling economy, is able to sort of turn it around.\par \par \uc2 But at same time, you`re going to have a voter that says, boy, I don`t like the way Biden is managing this economy, but I don`t like the direction of the Supreme Court. Are you worried about losing that voter, that the voter may care more about the pocketbook than what the Supreme Court is doing?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: So I think that our community is a very -- as I mentioned a very pragmatic community. And it`s weighing its options very, very thoughtful voters here. And so my job is to make sure that I`m communicating what we brought home to our community. And as we began this conversation, what we brought home for our community is schools that are open, one of the highest vaccination rates in the entire country here in this area, in this district.\par \par \uc2 We brought home PPP loans, emergency disaster loans, shuttered venue loans, restaurant loans, we brought home the opportunity for teachers to be able to be vaccinated. These are all of the things that we have as messages that have allowed our economy to be still open at this point in time. And so that alongside with some of the larger overarching issues of choice and freedoms I think are what I hope is the right message and the strong message to say that as I mentioned Democrats are very much about unifying people whereas I really believe that Republicans, at least the ones in this race, in my particular race, are about dividing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Do you have a preference in the Democratic primary for Senate?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: I do not. I very much look forward to finding out who that person is. That gentleman is. And I very much look forward to throwing myself a thousand percent behind support of that candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough. Congresswoman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2  from a pretty important swing district in the suburbs of Philadelphia, thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:08]\par \par \uc2 This special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY, we`re live from Luzerne County, it continues after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Tomorrow, MEET THE PRESS DAILY will be heading south past Allentown and King of Prussia to Philadelphia for much more on next week`s primary elections in Pennsylvania where folks are a little bit more on the D side of things. But before we bid Wilkes-Barre farewell, here`s some conversations that our own Dasha Burns had with voters in the area about next week`s big primaries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELANIE PATTERSON, CAMPAIGN VOLUNTEER: It always says this is the race, the Senate race that Dr. Oz is in and you get a reaction from people right away. You know, no, we don`t trust him. That he`s gone too woke.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And what about you? I mean --\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A man that you admire, Trump did endorse Oz, did that have any impact on your view of him?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I thought he made a mistake. That was a major error.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I agree 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: And thought he`s getting the wrong advice, you know, from people. And that`s what everyone is saying. He`s getting the wrong advice. Who advised that?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trump, the guy that you changed parties for endorsed Oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Does that influence your influence your opinion?\par \par \uc2 DAVID "MOGIE" MAGILL, OWNER, MOGIE`S IRISH PUB: It does in the sense that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in my opinion picks winners. I mean I don`t think -- he knows he`s electable and who is not. And that has a lot to do with how you can move your party forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Dasha, I guess if I were to say what did I come away with so far on this trip, it`s there`s a lot of clarity on the Democratic side all of a sudden. I mean, almost everybody seems to expect Fetterman to be the nominee. Nothing but a muddy picture on the Republican side. Trump`s endorsement of Oz muddied things up. It did not clarify.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Pennsylvania is not Ohio. As you say, Pennsylvania is not Ohio. And here, you kind of have like the ink blot test, right. You can see MAGA in whatever candidate you look at. So if you are a die-hard Trump fan of Pennsylvania, you kind of have your pick right now. But the reason I think Pennsylvania is going to be biggest Trump test yet is because of this resistance to Oz. If Oz does win, that`s going to say a lot about the Trump influence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if Oz wince here, it really is -- that means that 40 percent undecided was waiting for the Trump patsy.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Ultimately said, OK, I`ll do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s very interesting and no matter what, we`re going to have quite the general. So you don`t get to leave. I think northeastern Pennsylvania becomes even more important as a swing area.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m renting a condo in Luzerne County.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, they`ll be happy, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, nice work. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you all in Philadelphia tomorrow. We`ll have exclusive profile of John Fetterman and his wife. A fascinating interview that Dasha has. I`ll also have a Senate hopeful from each party, live on set with me, Kathy Barnette and Malcolm Kenyatta, will join us in Philadelphia. You won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur after this break. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220511ei5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali, Charlie Sykes \par \uc2 8,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Unease within the GOP as it faces the potential midterm fallout over Roe v Wade being overturned. And right-wing activists are pushing for a nationwide ban on abortion. The electees are getting nervous. This, as abortion politics takes center stage and it contentious democratic runoff in the Lone Star State.\par \par \uc2 And nine-term incumbent, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , is trying to hang up despite his self-described pro-life stance. His Democratic rival, Jessica Cisneros, will be here ahead.\par \par \uc2 And where we stand on day 71 of the war in Ukraine as the White House denies giving Ukraine intelligence designed to target and kill Russian generals as bloody battles reportedly rage on in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 Good Thursday to you and Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. For decades, Republicans loudly pushed a rollback the right to an abortion but now that the Supreme Court seems poised to actually deliver on that promise that Republicans have been making for decades to overturn Roe v Wade, suddenly, Republican lawmakers are trying to change the conversation, signaling a growing level of anxiety inside the party particularly among those that actually face the voters that the issue could complicate their plans to take back Congress this November.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at what we`ve already seen from some Republicans since the court`s draft Alito opinion leaked. Former Nevada Republican Attorney General \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  now running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  went out of his way to specifically note that the people of Nevada voted to make abortion rights legal and the matter is settled law, no matter what the court does. Translation, he doesn`t want to have a debate about abortion rights in the state of Nevada, where a large majority are in favor of it.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who`s lone called Roe to be overturned, and his back pro-life causes in the Senate told CNN that he doesn`t know if abortion is a party issue. Many other Republicans have directed their comments on the leak of the draft rather than its content.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the NRSC, which is the GOP Senate campaign on. They`re trying to urge Republican candidates to put out statements that carefully tread around the issue. In a polling memo, the group argued that pushing a 15-week ban could be politically popular when coupled with hitting Democrats for their position on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that these kinds of political messages are going to run into a conflicting reality, that all made sense in a world where Roe v Wade was law of the land. But if this draft opinion is handed down, 13 conservative states will automatically ban all abortion, with little or no exceptions. This is not about 15 weeks it`s about legal or not legal. Several other states have six-week abortion laws in place should Roe be overturned and those are basically bands. People don`t know it -- six weeks, whether they`re pregnant or not. Oklahoma just passed one of those, by the way, this week.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s places like Michigan and Wisconsin which have laws that are so old from the 19th century, that are on the books that actually banned abortion completely and those would become automatic law should Roe v Wade go away.\par \par \uc2 And just last night, Louisiana Republicans voted to advance a bill that would classify abortion as homicide, which then would allow prosecutors to criminally charge abortion patients. Folks, in a post-Roe world, the debate won`t be about limits on abortion. What week is it? Things like that. Late- term, the things like this, as Republicans have long argued. It`s simply going to be about whether abortion is illegal or illegal for millions of Americans. And it`s a debate that Republicans are signaling pretty loudly right now that they want to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is our Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali, \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2  is the founder and editor-at-large of The Bulwark, he`s also an MSNBC Contributor, and The Cook Political Report, House Editor Dave Wasserman, also with us the Washington Post Caroline Kitchener. She`s a politics reporter who focuses on reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Ali, let me start with you. You`ve been on the front lines of chasing Republicans who -- let`s admit it, it`s hard to find many elected Republicans that want to talk about Alito`s opinion. And surprisingly, not many of them want to seem to celebrate it. And you would think -- after years of pushing for judges that would overturn Roe v. Wade, you would think there would be a celebratory atmosphere with some of these elected Republicans, but I haven`t seen it.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I haven`t seen it either here. It`s almost as if the theory of overturning Roe was better for politics in campaigning for Republicans than actually seeing it settled by the Supreme Court, which is what we`re likely heading towards in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans here on the Hill, the conversation that we`ve been having as we`ve been asking them and we have, whether or not they would push forward their own kinds of codifications at the federal level once this decision comes down from the Supreme Court. The ways in which they`ve engaged with that question have really been fascinating because in many cases, they`ve sidestepped it all together instead, just focusing on the leak.\par \par \uc2 In other cases, people like Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  said at one point, he imagined that pro-life activists would push for federal restrictions on this. That`s not him saying that Republicans are going to move forward on this quickly, there doesn`t really seem like there`s an appetite for it.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But I would also add just as the reality check we`re doing on Democrats here who are trying to codify this at the federal level in the other way to protect access to abortion, Republicans would run into the same numbers of issues likely because they`re not going to have 60 votes, even if they retake the majority in the Senate come 2022.\par \par \uc2 So, the number`s reality here would stop that from making it a reality, but I think the more important trend that you touch on in your lead, there does really seem to be a divide happening right now between national Republicans who are here in Washington speaking to the more national audience, and then state Republicans and conservatives who have been working, frankly, for years to make these laws possible so that they could be challenged at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, seeing now what the court is likely doing, you`re watching other conservative states like Louisiana, Texas, South Dakota, and others who are trying to push forward on the abortion issue, and then some.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious, Ali, have you seen it, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  introduced a bill to create a new floor on the viability of 15 weeks in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Not that I have seen yet. But I also think when you talk about the NRSC, and of course, that`s where \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is focusing all of his energies ahead of 2022, their polling tells Republicans that they need to be compassionate on this issue. And at the same time, it`s hard to look at this as anything other than when Republicans start talking about restricting abortion, that`s a conversation that means taking things away from people, the option away from people.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, too, I think the thing I`ve been keeping in mind with this NRSC polling is it`s all stale. It`s all done in a world where a lot of people may not have been focusing on Roe as an important issue because it wasn`t. In theory, it was settled. And for the past 49 years, that has been the case.\par \par \uc2 And so sources that I`ve been talking to on both sides of this, Republican and Democrat, you`re sort of moving into uncharted territory because for both Republicans and Democrats, this has been theoretical, the threat to Roe, from both sides. Now, it`s actually happening, and we don`t know yet what that`s going to look like.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in Caroline Kitchener here. You`ve been reporting on what`s been going on in the States. And it does feel as if elected Republicans on a federal level are trying to compartmentalize what`s happening in the States. I mean, to hear \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  arguing, hey, the Democrats are the ones that are radical, they don`t support any limits at all. And you`re going well, wait a minute, look what we`re seeing in Louisiana? I don`t -- I don`t think this is going to be the debate they think it`s going to be?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE KITCHENER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, we are seeing states push more and more extreme legislation. I mean, I think what happens if -- yesterday in Louisiana, it really shows, you know, the -- how far they might be willing to go. I mean, it -- this was a bill that, you know, would make it a murder charge for a woman.\par \par \uc2 And that`s really something that we haven`t seen before, Chuck, you know, up until this point. It`s really been about punishing the people surrounding the abortion procedure. It`s been about the providers. It`s been about the various people who facilitate the procedure. But, you know, here, we`re seeing them actually moving forward on a bill that would -- you know, could potentially, you know, make this a crime for the woman who receives the abortion. So I think that`s a really significant change that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you see already -- Caroline, I`ve seen a few legislators, all of a sudden, the reality of Roe`s being eliminated and you`re already seeing, and it`s been in a couple of places, but I`m curious if you`re picking up on it? Well, maybe there should be exceptions for X, Y, and Z, that all of a sudden, the reality that, oh, this is actually going to become law in our state maybe we rush to judgment here.\par \par \uc2 KITCHENER: I mean I really have not seen that yet. I mean, I spend a lot of time in my reporting talking to state lawmakers, and anti-abortion lawmakers all over the country, and they are just so energized by what`s happening right now. They -- you know, this is something that they have been working on for decades, and you`re actually seeing a trend of getting rid of exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 You know, in Texas, there`s only an exception for medical emergencies. And even that, the way that it`s played out, I mean, even ectopic pregnancies, doctors are concerned that that might not even -- they`re so worried to do the procedure because they`re worried you know, does this actually qualify as a medical emergency, when of course, we know that it does. But there`s a lot of fear, I think is the point among providers about what they can do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , look, you were -- you were a well-known, you were conservative talk radio host in Wisconsin, extraordinarily influential. The issue of overturning Roe v. Wade, I`m going to be a bit -- I come at this a bit cynically, I don`t think many of these elected Republicans when they were saying it believed it and they felt comfortable saying it because they didn`t think it was ever actually going to happen. Am I being too cynical?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES\uc2 , FOUNDER & EDITOR-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: No. I mean I think Ali made a really good point that it`s been theoretical up until now. And so, this was -- this was a -- this was a free shot for Republicans. It was also a free shot for Democrats. I mean, you know, in Wisconsin, we have a law on the books criminalizing abortion, and Democrats never repealed that because I don`t think they ever thought this day would come.\par \par \uc2 But you know, in -- on Capitol Hill, Republicans may be able to dodge answering questions about this, but at the state level, it becomes a very, very urgent question. So, here in Wisconsin, we have a governor`s election and Attorney General`s election this year and it`s a binary choice.\par \par \uc2 48 hours ago, if you would have asked me, I would have said that the odds overwhelmingly favor Republicans winning those races but now it becomes really an upper down on the criminalization of abortion without any legislative action. If Republicans win the governorship and the attorney general position here in the state of Wisconsin, that law goes into effect if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 And the other big question in terms of the energizing of the base and I think it`s too early to know is what will the litmus test among Republicans be we`ve seen how these things can move very, very quickly? And the elimination of all of the exceptions is an example of that. This is a base lead party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : This is a party that is responsive to what the grassroots says and is reluctant to get crosswise with them. And if the Republican base demands radical restrictions on abortion, you are going to see more legislation like this and you will see pressure at the national level, particularly if in 2025, you have a Republican president and a Republican Congress to do something about that. And I think that`s very unpredictable right now how intense that will be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dave Wasserman, break this down for me on some election terms. Look, the national polling is overwhelmingly in favor of Roe but I -- obviously, it`s different depending on the state. And if you take out New York and California, you probably see a more narrow divide among the other 48 states on this issue. Where do you expect this to become more problematic for the Republicans, and does it surprise you at how nervous they`re acting right now?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WASSERMAN, HOUSE EDITOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Well, Chuck, just go back to the 2020 exit poll, 28 percent of Trump voters supported legal abortion in all or most cases, and that`s a significant chunk of the Trump base. I think this has the potential to drive a wedge into that base, particularly in the Midwestern states where that percentage of Trump voters was even higher.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, in 2016, he downplayed social issues like abortion and gay marriage and played up trade and immigration issues where he had a lot in common with those white-working class voters, including women. And then on top of that, consider that the energy level on college campuses before this decision leaked was pretty much dead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMANN: And now it has the potential vote to very much come alive, particularly for state races, and we`ve got 36 governorships on the ballot this fall, that has the potential to mitigate democratic losses at the federal level. I think the reelection stock for people like \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who`s got \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  in her district.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: Or \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  in Toledo has gone up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you see this as the -- one of the things I`ve wondered here is that is this a, essentially an equalizer when it comes to base energizing, that the Democrats were lacking that sort of fear issue to get the -- you know, at the end of the day, we know this fear motivates a base more than hope. And the Democrats now have their fear issue, right, Dave?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: You know, in March, the NBC News poll said at all right, 67 percent of Republicans rated their interest in the midterms as a nine or 10 out of 10 versus just 50 percent of Democrats. We almost never see an enthusiasm gap that wide. Now, I don`t think that gap will close entirely, but it will close significantly as a result of a ruling. And if that is the case, you know, that has the potential not only to lift Democratic governors as a check on Republican legislatures in a bunch of key states but also could cut Democrats` losses in the House, probably not enough to save their majority but still important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , Wisconsin is ground, to me, ground zero. And my goodness, considering how polarized that state is, and in many ways, Wisconsin, as you could argue, is exported their polarization quite expertly to the rest of the country. I feel like it`s going to be the last state that settles its abortion divide.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, I don`t think we`re going to settle that abortion divide, and I mean it. It is going to be intensified.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : This -- again this has been a very theoretical debate, free shots on both sides but it`s going to die. I don`t see any other way -- I don`t see how it does not dominate this November`s contest. And then if Republicans take control, they will have to figure out, do they pass some sort of a restriction or did they let the old law go into effect? Yes, again, we`re a very, very closely divided state.\par \par \uc2 And to Dave`s point, there were a lot of Trump voters who voted on non- cultural issues. This seems like it`s going to be front and center, and obviously, there`s a lot of headwinds involving inflation, and other things that are going on here that might not be dispositive. But Democrats had a real enthusiasm problem here in Wisconsin. I think they were hoping that the opposition to Ron Johnson would be enough to turn them out.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no question that in order for Democrats to win in Wisconsin, you have to have a massive turnout from the college towns, massive turnouts from Milwaukee, the Madison area, and they need to continue to make inroads into the suburban areas. And this is exactly the issue that I think will cause a real problem for Republicans in suburban -- in the suburban WOW counties in Wisconsin, Waukesha, Ozaukee, and Washington counties.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s got to be real anxiety that once again, they use the grossly overused clichAc. They`ve been chasing this bus for a very long, long time, and the dog finally caught it, and they`re not sure what to do with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Caroline, there`s an unintended consequence that a doctor friend of mine pointed out to me, which is the states that create these strict laws that govern reproductive health care, what will follow are a whole bunch of people who are OB-GYN who say, maybe I`m not going to practice medicine in Louisiana, maybe that`s not, and all of a sudden, you`re getting low-quality health care in these places that put these restrictive laws in place that may criminalize medical care.\par \par \uc2 KITCHENER: That`s absolutely true, Chuck. The American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology, actually, just last week, released a report on this. You know, it`s a -- it`s a really high number of these residency programs are just not going to be able to train people and abortion procedures.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, that is going to have huge ripple effects for the number of doctors available across the country who can do this procedure. And that -- you know that affects not just Republican-led states that are banning the procedure, that could also affect you know, how many providers are available in states like California and New York and Connecticut?\par \par \uc2 You know, one thing that you`re really seeing a lot of our, you know bills from these Democrat-led states that try to open this up and say, you know, we`re going to let you know, more people become abortion providers. But you know that`s just not going to be possible if people can`t get the training.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Ali, let`s go to the Democratic side of the aisle here. They have control of Congress but in theory. But not to do anything on this. Is there any path that they have of actually passing anything that might protect some access to abortion?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: No, because right now, what they`re trying to do is effectively a messaging bill. And there are some Democratic senators that will cop to that because the realities are what they are, in terms of the numbers. They don`t have the ability to do this by a simple majority, by getting rid of the filibuster on this issue. There`s not the political will for that.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the other side of this, to do it through the regular process would require 60 votes, and they certainly don`t have that. So what we`ve just heard in the last few minutes is that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is going to hold a vote on this next week, it`s going to be Wednesday, most likely, and what they`re going to do is put senators on the record here.\par \par \uc2 There has been some tinkering around the edges with the Women`s Health Protection Act. The version that failed in February is pretty similar to the version that they`re going to put on the floor next week. It has some less non-binding language at the top, but the crux of it is very much still the same.\par \par \uc2 One of the hang-ups though, that we`re going to be watching to see if maybe they end up getting potentially Murkowski or Collins on board, the only two really curious Republican senators on doing something like this, they would have to remove one of the religious exemptions from the bill. The reason they have it in there right now, as it`s been explained to me, is that they want to make it basically unassailable in court.\par \par \uc2 Again, there`s no court to make it unassailable from because it`s not actually going to become law, but that one piece could be a sticking point that makes this just most Democrats voting for it and all Republicans voted against it. And by the way, I would say \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  doesn`t like he`s going to vote for that either so it`s not even all Democrats who are likely to vote for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, in a Roe -- in a world where Roe existed, this creates all sorts of wedges and divides on the Democratic side. In a world where Roe doesn`t exist, you see where suddenly, all of those attempts go away, and all the wedges and problems show up on the right. Anyway, Ali, Charlie, Dave, and Caroline thank you all for providing your expertise. Coming up.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: How the abortion fight has taken center stage in one democratic runoff, it is an exception for what it`s worth. House candidate Jessica Cisneros who`s running against a nine-term incumbent, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 And as we go to break, a look at the markets right now. The Dow is tanking, shall we say, it comes a day after the Fed raised interest rates yesterday by half a percentage point. A lot of this has to do with the perception now that Wall Street believes this shaky economy is now locked in retail sales, not so great, tech getting hit hard, this sort of post-pandemic economy is a reality, and this is Wall Street`s reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 This is the biggest for whatever it`s worth, this is a pretty large drop for one day. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned, the debate over abortion rights is now center stage in this year`s midterm contests and it`s mostly going to be an issue with Republicans. But there is one place where it`s a democratic debate if you will, and it`s down in Texas where longtime incumbent \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is under fire from his opponent, Jessica Cisneros, and some progressives for his self-described pro-life stance on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders in the House for what it`s worth are standing by the embattled moderate. House Majority Whip \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , the third-highest ranking Democrat in the House, rallied support for Congress Cuellar in San Antonio yesterday. Shortly before taking the stage, he even defended his support in an interview with my colleague Garrett Haake. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Is there still room in the Democratic Party for a self-described pro-life member?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): I hope so. I hope so. We ought not to have fitness tests on our body. We`d never have had and we should not stop now. If this district has anything like it was, pretty odd to me, it gives us a much better chance of winning a seat than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All of this comes three weeks out from Election Day with Cisneros and Cuellar set for a heated runoff rematch in the 28th congressional district down there.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Jessica Cisneros, an immigration lawyer and Democratic candidate for Texas joins me now. Ms. Cisneros, I really appreciate you coming up.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS, DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE FOR TX-28: Of course, happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with, you heard Congressman Clyburn`s remarks there. And I understand you believe that his position on abortion should be enough for them to pull their support. But you`ve run on abortion rights before and the voters know that he is a self-described pro-life candidate. Why do you think voters have re-elected him with that abortion rights stance?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I mean it`s a challenge going up against someone who has been entrenched for a very long time. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  has been in office at one level or the other, longer than I`ve been alive. And for me to at that time, be a 26-year-old first-time challenger with running a completely 100 percent -- 100 percent grassroots campaign, we`re not taking a single dime of corporate PAC money and come within three percentage points of defeating \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  last cycle, we just had to come back and finish the job this time around.\par \par \uc2 We had our election -- our primary election on March 1, we were just 800 votes difference -- there was an 800-vote difference between our campaigns so we`re getting closer and closer on March 1. The voters elect -- they -- the majority of voters in the district voted for a pro-choice candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: And for us, it`s about trying to turn out those voters on the May 24 runoff election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Talk about -- let me ask you this, there seems to be the district is a bit more divided on abortion rights than maybe your typical Democratic- leaning district. How much of that is more conservative religious beliefs among Latinos in general? What do you ascribe that to?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I feel like people have just taken Henry Cuellar`s word that this district is as conservative as it is when it comes to this issue. I mean, we`ve been running on it since the last cycle as you said, back in 2019, when I first decided to run. We`re bringing it up again the cycle because we have noticed and I think this week really points out the urgency of protecting not just Roe but so many of our fundamental freedoms and civil liberties that stem from this kind of case law.\par \par \uc2 And I know that it`s important to voters because I`ve been out there talking to them myself. I remember when \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  was the only Democrat to vote with Republicans against codifying Roe last year, we had a phone bank right after that, we didn`t plan it that way, it just so happened, and the first few voters that I got on the line were talking about, you know, they were telling me about how upset they were that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  had sided with Republicans on this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You heard \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  there. He said he doesn`t think there should be a litmus test. On this, you believe there should be a litmus test. Why? What would you tell \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: In this moment, we are watching the fall of Roe and the erosion of our fundamental rights. And this moment, we truly -- I mean, it necessitates a champion that`s going to fight for our people`s rights in this district. And I`ve always said that this race truly goes beyond South Texas.\par \par \uc2 The last thing we want is to hold on to a slim Democratic majority and then have someone like \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  who`s going to keep signing with Republicans, not just on this issue, but he`s done it on things like the PRO Act. He`s voted to you know fund the border wall. I mean, has an A rating from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . There are so many key issues where he`s always siding with Republicans. And he could become the \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of the House.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  to be the deciding vote on the future of our fundamental freedoms and rights in this country. We just can`t risk that. And if you`re watching this, and you want to help us out, we`re 19 days away from our election, and we need all the support we can get.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- it is not -- I`m sure it`s not lost. I knew that you`ve seen in some of these primary matchups that have put the progressive wing and the establishments against each other. The establishment has come out on top. You saw that in Ohio. How do you plan to buck that trend?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I mean, again, we`re getting closer and closer. We were -- there was just a one percentage point difference in our race during the March 1 Democratic primary. We know this is a winnable race. And we`ve seen it happen before. I mean, Murray Newman and \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  did it last cycle, and we`re planning on doing it this cycle as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you say to \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  who says that he -- it`d be he clearly is endorsing Cuellar because he thinks Cuellar can hold the seat. And there`s concern that you cannot hold the seat. That you`re going to be considered too progressive for this district. Make your case.\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: Sure. People in this district aren`t voting for me because I am progressive. They`re voting for me because I`m putting forth policies that are actually going to enact change in this district. This district, for example, my hometown has a 30 percent poverty rate. We`ve historically had very low rates of people that are insured. I mean, people are coming out and voting for us and are you know, giving this entrenched incumbent the rest of his life because we need change.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And I plan on delivering on that. And I really hope that the Democratic leadership doesn`t stand in the way of the change that South Texans want to see. There are so many people rallying behind this campaign. We`ve been able to nickel and dime our way to -- with support, with votes, with donations, and go toe-to-toe with this very entrenched corporate incumbent. I guess it just shows the power that we have here in South Texas. And this is the kind of race where we`re centering people, we are centering the campaign around people as well with our voter outreach efforts to keep this district safely blue in this midterm election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, if you get elected, President Biden may be calling you and saying, hey, you`re representing folks on the border. What do they want in a border policy? What do they want when it comes to Title 42? What would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: To repeal this Trump relic. It`s not surprising that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is supporting keeping Title 42 because he`s known as Trump`s favorite Democrat. He voted with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  70 percent of the time during Trump`s administration. So it`s not a surprise that he`s supporting, you know, keeping Title 42 instead of repealing it.\par \par \uc2 I am an immigration and human rights attorney. I was born and raised in this border district in my hometown of Loredo. I can put forth, I`m excited once elected to shape our border policy in a way that takes into consideration the joys and the challenges of living here on the border. So whenever we do win, I am more than happy to take that call from President Biden and help.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jessica Cisneros, one of the two Democratic candidates in the 28th Congressional District, it`s a run-off that we will have later this month. Appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as the U.S. passes that grim milestone of one million COVID deaths, one lawmaker in Capitol Hill is pushing for more research into treat long COVID. The Democrat of Virginia, Senator Tim Kaine, who himself suffers from long COVID, joins me to discuss that and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:35]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is promising to hold a vote on the Women`s Health Protection Act. It`s a bill that would codify Roe v. Wade despite the incredibly slim odd it passes a 50-50 Senate. This is on top of all the other legislative priorities the Senate is passing, passing the president`s Ukraine aide, request securing most voted fund and then passing a budget.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is increasing interest rates as Americans and markets continue to worry about rising inflation, another pocketbook issue.\par \par \uc2 So for more on this, I`m joined by Democratic senator from Virginia, Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I want to start with, look, when you first got into politics, you were a self-described pro-life Democrat, but there was sort of this -- I would call, it was personally pro-life and maybe legally pro-choice, and I guess that probably would describe your position. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): It is, Chuck. I think about 30 percent of Americans have the position I do. We`re personally pro-life and we live that way, but we think people ought to be able to make their own reproductive decisions. And so, it`s a huge swath of the American public even if it`s not necessarily that much are represented position in Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, is this -- you know, it feels like this is something that I see already \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  isn`t going to vote for this version of the bill because there`s some fine -- piece of fine print that she thinks is not protected there. Is this something that politically is even possible to get 51 people to agree on what the -- I mean, it feels like -- it seems impossible that you guys are going to agree on a floor?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, I think to get 60 votes right now in the Senate is virtually impossible, Chuck, because the Republicans want to overrule Roe vs. Wade. There are a couple of exceptions, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, but most of them want to return to pre-Roe, where states get to decide if they want to criminalize women`s reproductive health care decisions. So we can`t get to 60. You might be able to see us get over 50 for some version of a clear statement that, you know, Griswold, Roe and Casey are good law and Americans have a constitutional right to make their own reproductive health care decisions. So we`re going to -- we`re going to make the effort to do that beginning next week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get -- I want you to share your story in a few minutes about your fight with long COVID, but I want to ask quickly about the economy. I had a pretty high-level Democrat over the weekend say to me, I wish the White House had a inflation czar the way they have a COVID czar, and get out there and sort of acknowledge that, yes, we are dealing with inflation. This is not politically caused. This was -- you know, we got a lot of reasons for it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But here`s what we`re doing every day to work on it, and apparently this idea was shot down by the White House. What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, Chuck, it reminds me, I`m older than you but I think it was Nixon or \uc2 Ford\uc2  that had the whip inflation now buttons, the wind buttons that people would wear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And I can`t remember if they had a czar, but they made it a front and center priority. I think that explains what the Fed is doing with interest rates. We have an economy that job growth is strong, wage growth is strong, GDP growth has been pretty robust, and I think it`s going to continue to be, but we do have an inflation worry. There are things we can do in Congress.\par \par \uc2 For example, if we were able to pass a reconciliation bill that would bring down childcare and health care cost, we could really help people with two big pocketbook items. But I also think that what the Fed is doing under Jay Powell`s leadership is trying to, without choking off the positive growth that we`re seeing in jobs especially.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Can we narrow down and reduce the inflation challenge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me what it`s like to live with long COVID? What is it like today?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes, so it`s like today, I mean, even as I am talking to you, I got COVID when we were passing the CARES Act more than two years ago, and it was early, and I got hit with non-standard symptoms but one of them was this -- like a light switch went off and all my nerve started to tingle. It`s like I`ve got, you know, my skin dipped in an Alka-Seltzer that`s fizzing. And it never has gone away. So my other symptoms went away quick, this fizzing sensation, others are experiencing it. It`s not painful. It doesn`t stop me from working or exercising or sleeping. But it`s just weird and it`s 24/7 and it`s been going for two years, and at this point I guess I kind of have to assume it`s probably never going away.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: What kind of -- you know, and I`m sure you`re probably more aware of how many people have similar stories as yours, how many have you come across and what kind of medical research has gone into this?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: So, Chuck, tons of people, as soon as I shared my story, you know, my office has become a bit of a magnet for people who want to share theirs. And I decided to share my story after about six or eight months. I`ve been to a neurologist. I kind of felt like I understood what was going on, but I was running into others with much more serious symptoms who are not being believed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And when they were not being believed, I thought, you know, if I am on the committee and I say hey, look, I believe you because I -- it`s real, then that would give them some comfort but then I can also on the committee try to drive toward solutions. So here`s what`s going on, in the American Rescue Plan, we put more than $1 billion into research for causes and cures. And interestingly, Chuck, this one, we may know cures before we know causes.\par \par \uc2 So for example, my nerve tingling thing, there are other cures for nerve tingling not related to COVID, well, maybe those cures could work for people who have it connected to COVID. People with long COVID often have racing heart, irregular heart rates, well, there`s other cures for that. Would that work for somebody who got that because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 So we`re doing research but the Long COVID Act that I put in recently is sort of four big pillars, gather patient data, more research into causes and cures, spread the information to providers, employers and families, but then also let`s provide support for those, for example, who might be so debilitated that they actually are disabled under Social Security or they need an accommodation to be able to work.\par \par \uc2 And so I am very focused on this because I am living with it, and dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: But there are many who are really suffering under it and we owe them answers and we owe them treatments.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you talked about getting medical professionals to believe their patients. You`re almost describing what many times people who get Lyme disease go through. Right? Their doctors are like, we can`t find it. We can`t find it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you make sure insurance companies don`t deny benefits based on these symptoms that again are not as well-known yet?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, Chuck, it`s a really good question. And you`re right. The research that we`re going to do is going to answer some questions not just about long COVID but about other conditions that may have followed viral infections because Lyme disease and others often have a tail of neurological and other consequences. We want to research that, too.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, the coverage issues are fascinating. What if you got COVID at the start of COVID before there was a lot of testing and so you never really got a test, but you did have COVID? Then you`ve been vaccinated, so now you have antibodies in your system, but somebody could say well, those antibodies, they are from your vaccination, they don`t prove you had COVID.\par \par \uc2 So making a claim for insurance, establishing a disability, asking an employer for an accommodation to help you work consistent with what you can do. These are all thorny questions, and that`s what the fourth pillar of my bill would do, patient support to help folks who have been through these tough two years. We just passed a million deaths. I mean, my god, who would have ever believed that two years ago.\par \par \uc2 But it`s millions more that are going to be not dying of COVID but having to live the rest of their lives with long COVID. And I think this bill which does, I think have some significant bipartisan support, particularly on the funding side, you know, can help us provide some of that support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, I have seen your interviews on this, and I felt as if the more people pay attention to this, I think you`re right, people may have been shamed by doctors saying they are imagining things and things like that, you`re not somebody that does this. This is happening to you. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your story, Senator.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Absolutely. Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat from Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the bloody battles are raging in Mariupol, as the U.S. denies giving Ukraine intel with the singular intent of killing Russian generals. Where we now stand on day 71 of the war in Ukraine. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Any moment now we expect to hear from the Pentagon spokesperson, John Kirby. That briefing comes as "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that the U.S. is providing specific intelligence that`s helping Ukraine target and kill Russian generals. According to "The Times," the targeting is part of a classified effort by the Biden administration to provide real-time battlefield intelligence to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NBC News cannot confirm that reporting and the National Security Council has decided to put out a statement that calls the headline of the story misleading and it`s framing irresponsible. The spokesperson for the NSC Adrienne Watson goes on to write, "The United States provides battlefield intelligence to help the Ukrainians defend their country. We do not provide intelligence with the intent to kill Russian generals."\par \par \uc2 All this comes as intense fighting had continued at the steel plant in Mariupol with the last remaining Ukrainian forces in that city have refused to surrender. I`m going to speak with the former deputy commander of the United States European Command after this break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Joining me now is retired Lieutenant General Steph Twitty. He`s a former deputy commander of the United States European Command. He`s now an NBC News military analyst.\par \par \uc2 I got to start with the news about whether we`re providing intelligence to target Russia generals. It is -- it feels like the statement from the government is a bit of a distinction without a difference basically saying, look, that isn`t the motivation for this intelligence sharing, it`s about helping Ukraine in the battlefield but targeting generals, if the Ukrainians are doing this, there`s a motive to this, is there not?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY (RET.), U.S. EUROPEAN COMMAND Well, Chuck, first of all, I would tell you I would have to agree with the government. I`ve been a recipient of this intelligence for 40 years having served in the military and what the U.S. is doing I must say is unprecedented because normally we share our intelligence with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies and, as you know, our Five Eyes partners. So opening up this intelligence and serving -- helping the Ukrainians, that is different and something that I have not seen in my 40 years in the military.\par \par \uc2 But what I will tell you is it is making a difference on the battlefield obviously for us to share that intelligence, whether it`s going after the command-and-control systems is what I would think that the Ukrainians are doing and as you know most of the command and control and operation centers, most of those have generals in them. And so in a round-about way, yes, you`re probably killing some generals by going after command-and- control systems, but they`re also going after other things, whether it be logistics or the artillery or the missile systems.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: So I would put this in a greater context of they`re going after things to really cripple and weaken the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s look at this from the flip side, General. Being former head of the European Command, I`m sure you thought you knew everything there was to know about the Russian military. Are you surprised at certainly how poorly their leadership structure has performed?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Not really, Chuck. I`ve spent a lot of time in Ukraine. My first tour in Ukraine training and assisting the Ukrainian forces was in the 1996 and I saw then the Ukrainians themselves supports the wall had come down a couple of years prior but the Ukrainians were still under the old Soviet system of training and fighting, and throughout that time, those three months I spent in Ukraine, I had U.S. forces there and they are amazed at the autonomy, the accountability, the morale that the U.S. service members bring to training and to the fight.\par \par \uc2 When you took a look at the Ukrainians back then, it was the senior leaders leading operations where we had noncommission officers leading operations.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 And so I saw a vast difference in the way U.S. soldiers fought and the way Russian-type, Ukrainian soldiers fought during that time frame. And I will tell you --\par \par \uc2 TODD: So --\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Go ahead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it sounds like, General, it`s inevitable that Russia was going to have a morale problem the second they essentially hit a hurdle?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Well, it`s more than just a training. First of all, the Russian soldiers need to know why they`re fighting. And as you know, you`ve heard the stories, they don`t quite know why they`re fighting. And when you call it a special operation, that is not telling your soldiers in honesty what they`re going into battle for. They need to know why they`re fighting, and they need to have the will to fight.\par \par \uc2 That carries a long way when you`re dealing with combat operations. Don`t underestimate the will of the fight. And part of it is their leadership being honest with their soldiers so they`ll no why they`re fighting.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe, knowing what you know about the Ukrainian military, that they can win this war with everything we`re providing them, or do you think we`re going to need to do a little bit more to get them over the top?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, I think we`re going to have to do a little bit more. You probably heard, this is a battle of wills, this is a battle of attrition. And what you`re seeing now in the east you`re seeing that attrition start to fall out here, where the Russians will win a battle, a small battle, that you come back, and you wake up the next day and Ukrainians have taken back over that particular city or that particular ground support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: It will continue to slog through this unless we get some overwhelming combat power to the Ukrainians for this fight to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, General Twitty, I`ve got to leave it there. It`s a pleasure to get your expertise on the air, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with my friend Chris Jansing after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 27, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali, Alex Rossi, Carlos Curbelo, Julia Ainsley \par \uc2 8,188 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday. It`s another audio leak and another moment of truth for Kevin McCarthy and the House Republicans, as the caucus meets today still unwilling to reckon with what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Russia cuts off gas supplies to \uc2 NATO\uc2  members Poland and Bulgaria in a major economic escalation of the war as the Biden administration negotiates a prisoner swap to free American Trevor Reed.\par \par \uc2 And Homeland Security Secretary Mayorkas testifies before Congress, as the administration releases a long-awaited plan to address the expected migrant surge when it ends Title 42 restrictions at the southern border at some point.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. As you saw from that intro, it`s a pretty busy day, a lot of topics that could have vied for the lead today, but we`re going, to begin with, the new fallout inside the Republican Party as House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy had to meet behind closed doors with his members this morning after getting another leak of audio from the immediate aftermath of the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Last time, it was leaked audio of McCarthy saying he was planning to urge President Trump to resign after January 6. This time, it`s audio showing how concerned McCarthy and his entire leadership team, mind you is were about what was going on inside their party, as they tried to tamp down on some of the unhinged rhetoric and attacks that were coming from their own members. People like Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . Here`s some of that new audio released by reporters at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA): I am calling Gaetz I`m explaining to him, I don`t what I`m going to say, but I`m going to have some other people call him too but the nature of what -- if I`m getting a briefing and I`m going to get another one from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tomorrow, this is serious (AUDIO GAP) to cut this out. Yes, that`s -- I mean, it`s potentially illegal what he`s doing. Well, he`s putting people in jeopardy. And he doesn`t need to be doing this. We saw what people would do in the Capitol, you know, and these people came prepared with rope, with everything else.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was, of course, this is from the upcoming book that will be released next week by \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporters, Alex Burns and Jonathan Martin. So that was that. And what is happening right now inside the Republican Party illustrates just how dramatically and suddenly the party has turned its back on small D democratic principles in the wake of that attack on the seed of our democracy because while McCarthy`s comments from them seem rational in the moment, even morally upstanding, they are verboten now in today`s GOP. They do not conform with former President Trump`s lies about January 6, and the 2020 election as a whole.\par \par \uc2 And according to reports from inside McCarthy`s meeting with the Republican caucus today, he received a standing ovation after distancing himself from what he said after the insurrection. Here`s some Republican reaction after the meeting this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BARRY LOUDERMILK\uc2 , (R-GA): Basically, he said we need to keep on track and keep pushing forward. This is a distraction, folks come on. This is simply a distraction by the left trying to drive a wedge in a very unified Republican Party and a very unified conference.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R-SC): He got a standing ovation. He`ll be a speaker.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So did you listen to the McCarthy audio that was released?\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIA ELVIRA SALAZAR, (R-FL): Sure I did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And what was your response as an American?\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: My response to that was that it was edited. You see, I know the art of editing so I don`t --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think he didn`t say that?\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: I am not sure. I`m not sure in what context.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Folks, McCarthy`s conduct over the last 15 months tells the story of the current Republican Party in the immediate aftermath of the attack on the Capitol. He said the former president had responsibility for the attack and that some of his members were acting irresponsibly. But once the political winds blowing, the other direction were clear to him.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy followed those winds, seemingly comfortable to follow Trump`s marching orders as a way to further his own standing in the party, his own obsession with getting the speaker`s gavel no matter what it takes. So what happens next inside the GOP, and what happens if a party built on lies and the ultimate pursuit of power, potentially gets that power next year? For one thing, it means this version of the Republican Party may not have any hopes of actually governing.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill. She has some new reporting from inside that caucus meeting this morning. And with me on set is Audrey Fahlberg, a reporter for The Dispatch. Eugene Scott, National Political Reporter for The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , and also joining me is former Florida Republican congressman and an MSNBC Political Analyst \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 . But let me start with what we heard today. Ali Vitali, it seems as if Kevin McCarthy is trying to get a pass by saying hey, Trump forgave me after no mean all this anyway and none of this is new, so it worked?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. It does seem like it worked. I think the interesting thing here, Chuck, is I ran into Kevin McCarthy last night as he was leaving votes, and we asked him, does he think that this could derail his bid for a speaker? It was the only question of several that he responded to in real-time and he just simply told me, no.\par \par \uc2 So, that gives a sense of where he thinks that his speakership bid is, especially because we all thought that this would only matter if it mattered to Trump, and it doesn`t seem like it does. But what the new audio does is present a wrinkle in that, which is that it shows that McCarthy was talking not just about the former president, but about members of his own conference in the days after January 6. And that came to a head today inside that GOP huddle.\par \par \uc2 While lawmakers did present a united front outside with reporters, what my sources are telling me and other members of our Capitol Hill team is that Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  actually took Congressman Steve Scalise to task over the comments that he made in that leaked audio, asking him effectively to provide a basis or evidence for what Gaetz was saying that could have been thought to be illegal. What our sources say is that Scalise then pushed back, and they described this exchange as very direct and heated. They weren`t yelling, but certainly, both sides were standing their ground.\par \par \uc2 We`re also told that Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  told Scalise he should apologize to Gaetz. There was no such apology, but it does sort of show that even though McCarthy may be skating above this, at least for right now with the majority of his conference, you know, he did get that standing ovation, you saw there on camera, people leaving the meeting, saying that he was going to be speaker, that`s certainly the chorus here. At the same time, though this meeting does present that there are still some feelings even in the fringe, but very Trump-aligned parts of this conference, that could potentially be a problem going forward here.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX ROSSI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Along forest roads and across farmland, Ukrainian forces race to fortify the Donbas. Every day brings new fighting, with Russian artillery hitting the small villages on the front line. Black smolders on the horizon. Eventually, we reach Oleksandrivs`k, Russian forces are about two kilometers away, and it`s now almost deserted. As we follow, the soldier tells us to use the trees as cover.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 there are drones above, he warns. And standing in the open here can get you killed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 The smoke over there, you can see places that have been shelled at the moment, and then one just landed over here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 He shows us a farm. It`s too dangerous to go further, he says. It`s just been struck. The family escaped but it soon comes under attack again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hide.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 Let`s go, guys, come on. Let`s go, mate. Let`s go. Let`s go. Is everyone good?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, everyone is good.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 Yes. (INAUDIBLE). Let`s go. Well, again, this place has come under attack. They`ve managed to get everybody out of the village but we need to leave now too.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Ukrainians have been displaced or trapped by this conflict. The front lines are shifting here all the time. And as Russian forces advanced, many villages like this one are now in the firing line.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 Nearby, this group of medics takes a break behind the front line, a chance to grab rest and share food under the cover of the woodlands. Every day is difficult treating comrades, but they are convinced Putin`s forces can be defeated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 ROSSI: \uc2 This war is now entering its third month. And as Ukraine reinforces its army in the east with fresh soldiers and new weapons, its army knows the next few weeks will be decisive. Alex Rossi, Sky News, Eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We have resolved our big tech problem. It is back. Ali Vitali, I want to round up one quick question to you as we were wrapping up our conversation there for that very long interlude. And that is, is there anybody that`s going to use this moment of weakness with McCarthy to try to launch their own campaign for the speaker`s gavel?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, look, that`s kind of the open question here, Chuck, right, because it`s one thing if McCarthy had a challenger. But right now, we`re not really hearing any names. And notably, people in the past who have been countered by McCarthy are now saying that they think he`s going to be Speaker. People, like, for example, Congressman Jim Jordan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: The Freedom Caucus widely has not been critical of him in the aftermath of this audio. I do think though, the piece where this goes forward is with the January 6 Committee, and I ran into \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  here just a few minutes ago and asked him, are they talking about not just asking McCarthy to come before them again, because they are going to ask him that, but are they thinking of subpoena in him? He told me that they want to talk to him. They don`t have him agreeing to that yet. But they`re going to be talking about if they move forward on subpoenas by the end of the week, whether that`s a sliding timeline, I guess we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But they didn`t -- they haven`t subpoenaed Mo Brooks, correct? So they haven`t subpoenaed Mo Brooks.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Exactly. They haven`t subpoenaed other lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Yes, I think they -- there are some legal questions on that. I think, very much.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anyway, Ali Vitali, I know you got to run do some more reporting for us. I`m going to let you go.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me continue with my other three guests. And Carlos, let me start with you. I think I know the reason why McCarthy seems safe and that is because nobody wants to deal with the alternative McCarthy and everybody thinks they can mold McCarthy into what they want him to be. If you`re a moderate like Nancy Mace, you think McCarthy will sort of have your back, or if you`re Jim Jordan, you think, yes, I`ve got him by the short hairs, and he can`t move that much either. Is that what keeps Kevin McCarthy in charge?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chuck, I think that`s right. Kevin McCarthy is a people pleaser. He gets along with everyone. He has a very gentle, happy disposition about him. So he really doesn`t make anyone angry. But I will say this, Chuck. It is always easier to portray unity when you are the minority party.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, Republicans are looking ahead. They think they can win in November of this year. The polling certainly indicates that they can, and well, if the election were held today. Once they come to power, depending on how large that majority is or isn`t, these divisions may reappear, and members like \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , are always going to be up for stirring the pot.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: And then you also have other members who are now listening to what McCarthy said in private, which is what they feel, and they may have some resentment toward him on the other side, more -- you know the less Trumpian members or the more centrist members. So for now, things look good for McCarthy, but once Republicans come into power, if they do win the majority, that could all change.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Eugene, I want to play this one, I want to play out the second -- the audio side, guys. It`s the one where McCarthy talks about tensions being too high. Let`s play that and then I want to ask you something on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The other thing that we have to do is, these members on either whatever position, you are calling out other members that stuff got to stop. Tension is too high. The country is too crazy. I don`t want to look back and think we caused something or we missed something and someone got hurt. I don`t want to play politics with any of that.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Eugene, I go back to sort of the sin that he committed here. You know, this is -- that he reacted the way I think any rational human being would have reacted in a moment like that. That`s verbal.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE SCOTT, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Absolutely. And we have that reporting in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . I think there are two main reasons why he`s going to get away with this. One is because he seems to have the support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still. But Jody Arlington from Texas said he pretty much was recorded saying what many Republican lawmakers were thinking at the moment, the reality --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They were telling many of us.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. Well, many of us are behind these things.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, without question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so they cannot be that upset with him for processing this insurrection the way any rational lawmaker would have. He ultimately made the right decision in the end, according to President Trump, but in the moment, I think other GOP lawmakers could just really relate to how he handled everything.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Audrey, what are -- what are you seeing, what are you hearing from Republican members? Because this seems to have empowered \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  in that crowd again, which is the last thing a majority of these members want.\par \par \uc2 AUDREY FAHLBERG, REPORTER, THE DISPATCH: I think that \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  here really is an exception to the rule. Most Republicans really do not want to speak on the record about this or even in private, just trashing McCarthy in private to reporters. I think right now, members are going to do what`s convenient for them right now, right? And that`s retaking the majority, keeping the focus on inflation, the border crisis. I was at the House Freedom Caucus meeting last night. That`s what they told us, wouldn`t answer us. Any of our questions really just said border, border, border. That`s what we`re talking about. A lot of House Republicans -- moderates told me that after the conference today, you know, some people might be frustrated in the short term. But at the end of the day, this is a snapshot in time when tensions were high. People are going to rally behind him. But I think that Carlos is absolutely right in that. Right now, they`re focused on retaking the majority. But these frustrations really might come to a head after November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- You know, I think about that. Carlos, do you think about \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  couldn`t govern? Right after a while as Speaker, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  struggled to do it, which is why he decided to walk away. And I look at a guy like Jim Banks, you want to talk about ambitious, the only person that might be more ambitious than Kevin McCarthy in the House leadership is Jim Banks. I see actually, you`re nodding your head pretty strongly on that. You tend to agree with that, right?\par \par \uc2 FAHLBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He definitely is on the move.\par \par \uc2 FAHLBERG: Absolutely. As I mentioned, I mean, people are going to do what`s convenient for them, and for somebody like him or somebody like Steve Scalise, who wants to rise in the ranks, this is an opportunity to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. So, Carlos, you know, to me, Jim Banks looks like the guy if I were Kevin McCarthy, I`d be the most concerned about.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, there`s Jim Smith -- Jim Banks, and there are a couple of others who are just watching very carefully for a while. With Steve Scalise, it seems like he`s receded a little bit. But I remind people. When Republicans were waiting to take the majority during the Obama presidency, everyone was excited about \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 , everyone was behind \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 . \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  came to power and that all fizzled away quickly.\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of the Ryan`s speakership, I was there for that. People were very excited about Ryan. He had a lot of support and that eroded over time. There are just a lot of threats surrounding Kevin McCarthy and this delicate balance of having to keep not just his own members, but for the first time ever, someone external to the House, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , happy and pleased. That is very difficult. It`s too many balls to juggle. And at some point, one of them is going to fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I mean, Eugene, we can picture one scenario. There`s a movement that wants to start impeachment, wants to try to get some impeachment -- Articles of Impeachment out there, whether it`s for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Mayorkas. I`ve heard all sorts of -- you know, all sorts of things. And McCarthy says, you know, this is not the time, you know, then, they threatened this. And it feels as if he is going to be a leader in the -- you know, we talked about rhinos, he is going to be a Lyno (PH), a leader in a moment.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, it`s a more diverse caucus, and I think a lot of people outside of it realize they`ve had hardest -- had -- they`ve had a difficult time getting on the same page on quite a few issues moving forward.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so if they are successful with getting the House back and McCarthy is the leader, we`re going to see that in ways that we haven`t presently. And that`s going to make it much more difficult for them to govern moving forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Audrey, this is a numbers game, right? If the margin is small, McCarthy`s never going to be a speaker, right? But if the margin is a good one, 20, if they win 20 plus seats, he probably has the padding.\par \par \uc2 FAHLBERG: Yes, we`ll see. I mean, as I mentioned, I spoke to a lot of moderate Republicans who he fundraises for. His leadership pack takes back the House, fundraises even for House Republicans who voted to impeach, I believe quite a few of them, and so there`s really no incentive for them to trash him right now. But I think the important thing to remember right now is that when he lied to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , he didn`t just lie to the American people, he lied to his own members.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAHLBERG: And a lot of them have -- had to defend him on TV, right? I know of a pretty high-ranking House Republican who defended him on TV the other day, and afterward, he expressed privately that it was really embarrassing, and frankly, frustrating that he had to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Carlos, this gets to the larger picture here and you`ve expressed your own version of disgust on this, which is the party is allowing itself -- it`s built on a lie. So -- I mean, it -- how do you -- you know they`re going to get power based on a lie, and then what? How do you govern?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, that`s right, Chuck. The party right now is a fully owned subsidiary of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , and that is not an entity that can do its job, that can lead without fear. I mean, House Republicans, especially House Republicans, I think it`s different in the Senate are constantly looking over their shoulders to see what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing, where he`s going, what he`s going to say. And when you`re motivated by fear, in this case of the former president, it`s very difficult to lead under those circumstances. So you`re going to see a lot of unity up until November if they do take over the House. I think all these cracks are going to emerge again, and we`ll see what comes after that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The irony, Eugene, here is I don`t -- I don`t know if this matters on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Oh, absolutely, at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes. Glenn Grothman from Wisconsin told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , nobody cares about January 6 except for the press. He literally said then. So as long as voters are not going to hold these lawmakers accountable for this, they don`t have anything to worry about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think I`ve seen -- I have probably watched a thousand television and so far this cycle, I think I`ve seen two on January 6, Audrey. I mean, that is something -- and even Kevin McCarthy. He`s not yet -- he hasn`t -- what the right is done with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the left has not done with Kevin McCarthy. How about you?\par \par \uc2 FAHLBERG: I think that right now, vote -- Republicans are really trying to express that their voters care about inflation, the border, it seems like that. I mean, focusing on January 6 is simply not something that they care about. They always change the topic of conversation when I ask them about this, either in public or in private. So I think that it`s going to continue to be like that leading up to the midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And sadly, Carlos, even though what would be right for the history books, what would be right for this investigation, there is no upside for Kevin McCarthy to voluntarily participate in the January 6 investigation, is there?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: No, I think the most we`re going to get from him, you know, are these leaks and secondhand accounts of things that he told other people. And again, the sad part is that it`s obvious where he stood as -- I think a couple of people have said already.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: McCarthy is a normal guy. I mean, his reaction to January 6 was visceral. He was agitated. He was anxious. He was nervous. He was worried. But he can`t say any of these things. He can`t admit them. That`s just very frustrating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Audrey, Eugene, and Carlos thank you all. Thank you for bearing with the technical issues here. But hey, it`s live television. We have even more on the future of the Republican Party and how all signs are pointing to their inability to govern on the -- on the next episode of "THE CHUCK TODDCAST." It`s available right now. Get it free wherever you get your podcast. It`s a fun conversation with Matthew Continetti.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, Washington and Moscow engage in a surprise prisoner exchange, even as Russia halts all gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria. Isn`t that an act of war? We`ve got the latest on the war in Ukraine ahead. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. With the war on Ukraine about to enter its ninth week, we`re following a number of diplomatic developments today. This morning the White House announced that American Trevor Reed had been released from Russian custody as part of a prisoner exchange between Washington and Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Reed is a former Marine who`s arrested nearly three years ago after Russian officials accused him of assaulting an officer, although his family maintained his innocence and the U.S. considered him an unjustly detainee. In exchange for Reed`s release, the U.S. agreed to free a Russian pilot that was convicted of drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as the U.S. embassy in Ukraine tweeted that team members have travelled to Lviv to meet with key partners as the embassy prepares to resume operations in Kyiv, not just all of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But all of this comes as Russia decided to cut off gas supplies to Poland and Bulgaria. Both are E.U. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  members. They`re cutting them off for refusing to pay for their shipments in rubles. European leaders called the move gas blackmail.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on the ground in Ukraine, officials say Russian have continued attacking a steel plant in Mariupol where the last Ukrainian troops in the city as well as civilians remained hold up. And officials in the Odessa region say a railway bridge that had come under attack has now collapsed.\par \par \uc2 So with me now is retired General Philip Breedlove. He`s a former NATO Supreme Allied commander in Europe and a distinguished chair at the Middle East Institute.\par \par \uc2 And General, let me start with the Russian decision to -- let`s use the phrase gas blackmail. What they`re doing in Poland and Bulgaria. It seems as if they keep trying to find ways to have the West stop helping Ukraine. I don`t know what they think they`re going to accomplish here. However, it does rattle the alliance economically.\par \par \uc2 GEN. PHILIP BREEDLOVE (RET.), FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER EUROPE: So, first of all, thank you for having me on the show. This is not new. If you remember back to 2013 and `14 when they first attacked Ukraine, they did the same thing. And one of the coldest winters in history they shut off fuel and heating oil, et cetera, to Ukraine and people died as a result of freezing to death in their apartments.\par \par \uc2 So using energy as a tool in warfare is nothing new to Russia. And I would offer that Russia is specifically in a war with Ukraine, but they are also, in general, at war with the West. And we should expect them to use these kinds of tools.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At what point in your mind, General, does this become an act of war?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: I believe it already is. You know, basically Mr. Putin is seeking to further deter us. We are already deterred, as you know, from doing things like introducing forces and other things. We`re deterred by his nuclear threats. But he`s seeking now to further deter us with these energy and economic tools.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What should be a response here? I mean, I know where you stand. I think -- in that, I think that -- and you`re not alone in thinking this, is that quite a few people believe that we`ve allowed him to declare lines of deterrence that perhaps we shouldn`t be abiding by. This one feels like an easy one not to abide by. Nuclear is a whole another story. What`s the best way to push back on this?\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Well, what we have done in 2008, 2014, and in the lead up to 2022, we`ve done sanctions. And what is very clear is that sanctions have never changed Mr. Putin`s actions. They hurt Russia. They`ve hurt the Russian people, they`ve hurt Russian economy, but they have not changed Mr. Putin`s actions. So we need to reach for different tools in our toolbox to affect Mr. Putin`s actions. And I think that we started down that path at the Ramstein conference in Germany just recently when all of the defense ministers were in and met with Secretary Austin.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem -- so there`s another way to look at this reaction, though, by Putin. It does seem as if he`s getting -- you know, is he getting desperate to stop the help of Ukraine? Ukraine is making his military`s life miserable. He`s trying to do whatever he could to at least, you know, halt or disrupt the flow of arms going into Ukraine. This a bit of a sign of desperation, is it not?\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Well, clearly he suffered a strategic defeat in the north. Defeated in detail by the Ukrainian forces. And now he`s trying to reset and do better in the east and in the south. And frankly, that hasn`t got off to a great start either. We have yet to see the big thrust of armor that we expect. So it`s too early to start celebrating. There`s lots of dangers still in the east. But it hasn`t started as quickly as he wanted. So you`re right. He`s beginning to reach for other tools to try to affect this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, two days ago, it was Lavrov again trying to talk about nukes here. And I did get the sense that the West kind of brushed that off.\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Well, we need to respect the idea of nukes. Mr. Gerasimov, the lead general, and Mr. Putin have written and talked many times about how nukes are a logical extension of the conventional battlefield, something we don`t believe in the West at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: So we can`t just -- can`t just blow it off. But we have to remember, also, and I don`t mean to be disrespectful to a diplomat from another nation, but Mr. Lavrov is the chief disinformer of the disinforming campaign. So we need to just be very careful about Mr. Lavrov and what he says.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You spent a long time studying the Russian military. Are you surprised at how poorly its performing?\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: So this is going to be something that we in the field and many look at for years to come. What is very clear is that Russia arrived especially in the north expecting a very different war. And when you decide and you equip and you pulled the logistics for a three or four-day quick campaign where you`re then open with -- breached forward with open arms by the Ukrainians, when you plan for that and then you get engaged in a fight you didn`t expect, it`s really hard to recover.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: And, frankly, this two-week pause where he went back and refit and refurb to now attacking the east, I`m not sure that`s long enough to even recover. They started a war they were not ready for. And they`ve taken a whipping. Now there is a lot of force left in Russia. So we don`t want -- I don`t mean to be flippant, but we don`t want to do a happy dance yet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: We`ve got a lot of tough slugging to go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, and there`s no doubt this could a certain -- it could still end upturning in a bad direction. But a phrase that I`ve learned a lot more about recently, the porcupine strategy here. Isn`t that making conventional warfare that many folks have learned for decades obsolete given how well counter insurgencies seem to work now?\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Well, I don`t mean to argue but I wouldn`t call what they`ve been doing counter insurgency. Rather they have built a magnificent defensive depth with skirmishing and ambushes, et cetera, et cetera. And so -- and frankly Russia made some huge mistakes. Any person who is involved with armored warfare will tell you, you don`t send tanks into an area without a dismounted infantry support.\par \par \uc2 Because when you do, you get exactly what they got in the north. Infantry, sometimes as close as 10 meters shooting into your tanks. And they took a whipping because they did a basically un-fundamental thing and sent tanks in without dismounted support. So if they had done it more appropriately, we might be looking at a different position on the battle field.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What you`re describing then is a hubris issue.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, General Breedlove, it`s always good to get your perspective, sir. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as the White House urges action from Congress on immigration and Title 42, we`ll speak with Congressman \uc2 Adriano Espaillat\uc2 , a member of the Congressional Hispanic Caucuses leadership who met with the president this week on this issue and much more. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas is on the Hill for two different hearings today as he defends the administration`s new plan to secure the southern border once Title 42 is lifted. Whenever it`s lifted. This six-point plan comes after weeks of criticism from both sides of the aisle painting the department as unprepared for the surge of migrants expected to come to the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Now Mayorkas has laid out responsibility for the crisis to the feet of lawmakers because there are some things he`d like to do but can`t because of the law, and says Congress has to do its part.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DHS SECRETARY: We inherited a broken and dismantled system that is already under strain. It is not built to manage the current levels and types of migratory flows. Only Congress can fix this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When exactly this DHS plan will be set into motion could be up in the air after a judge said early this week that they plan to issue a temporary restraining order to halt the wind down of the Tile 42 policies, so this could be in perpetuity for a bit. DHS says it will comply with the order.\par \par \uc2 Julia Ainsley who of course covers the Justice Department and immigration issues for NBC News, joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Julia, this plan is somewhat limited like I`ve heard lots of ideas that say hey, let`s surge asylum judges to the countries where they`re coming from but that all needs -- those have laws changed here. There`s only so much they can do with this.\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right. Yes. There`s only so much they can do and I think it also might come down to a funding issue. I mean, we reported last week that they`re looking at hundreds of millions of dollars in a budget shortfall if these numbers turn out to be as high as they`re predicting. Anywhere between 10,000 and 18,000 border crossings a day. For context, Jeh Johnson used to say his red line was a thousand a day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Crossing the border. And so now it looks like --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve heard estimates it could be up 12,000 a day.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Yes, 12,000 actually seems pretty realistic. In fact today Mayorkas said 18,000 something they prepare for, they prepare for all scenarios. 12,000 seems realistic. We`re already looking at about 8,000 a day even now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: So basically all this comes down, in fact they might need more money. And he said today that they may have to ask Congress for a supplemental but that they haven`t done that yet. But he`s giving them a heads up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there some sort of emergency that the president can declare that can open up some money? Because I`m sorry, good luck trying to get money out of this polarized Congress.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Yes. You`re right. I mean, certainly Trump did that. Right? I mean, he shut down the government over the border wall and used that -- kind of that pulpit to try to get National Guard down there. That seems to be the exact opposite direction that this administration wants to go. They want less attention on the issue. They don`t want to the claim that it`s a national security issue that requires the Defense Department.\par \par \uc2 They want to handle this with their available resources. They do have a new asylum program that they want to try out at the end of May but when I`m hearing is that because that wasn`t congressionally funded, they wanted $15 million to be able to do more asylum adjudications right there at the border. But without that funding, it`s a drop in the bucket.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this about changing the status of asylum -- giving them the ability to be -- basically they`re immediately creating new asylum judges essentially?\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Yes, essentially. I mean, if they decide that you don`t pass that initial interview, which they`ve been doing those interviews all along, then you can be deported. And what --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that person could adjudicate them?\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Now if they`re negative, they do have, you know, in other words if they`re found not to qualify, they do have the right to then go to appeal to a court, which can sometimes be a two to four-year wait. But the idea is to be able to get this done more quickly. The whole issue with this is that you have backlogs. That`s the last thing this administration wants.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Backlogs is how we get those pictures.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Exactly. That`s what they don`t want again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And they`re worried about pictures.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: They don`t want the people under the bridge. They don`t want people who can`t sleep in shelters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Julia Ainsley, this is a never-ending beat for you.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: It is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, anyway, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m joined now by Democratic congressman from New York, \uc2 Adriano Espaillat\uc2 . He`s the number two vice chair of the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it is good to see you. So let me just start with what you heard from the plan that Secretary Mayorkas rolled out today. Look, there`s a lot of things they`d like to do. Congress would have to change some laws. But based on what you saw is this a workable solution?\par \par \uc2 REP. ADRIANO ESPAILLAT (D-NY): This is a good start. I think it`s an excellent start. It increases Border Patrol at the border. It also helps some of the NGOs that are working with the migrants and it targets and disrupts drug smugglers. It also increases transportation. Remember there seemed to be an issue reuniting unaccompanied minors in the part across the country with their relatives.\par \par \uc2 So this is a good start. But the president also has to present their budget that calls for $621 million increase for immigration court. It calls for a $6.3 billion allotment to the Office of Refugee Settlement. And really, I think, also asks for the kind of funding that we need to address what is going to happen at the border. I think that we need funding to do that and Congress must provide that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a way to, look, a larger immigration bill is going nowhere.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 I know, you know, the White House likes to say, hey, they introduced a bill on day one. OK. But they`re not putting a shoulder into it. They`re not trying to get it passed. And we know what the politics of this.\par \par \uc2 Is there a way to break it out and just get a deal to reform the asylum system solely? Is that possible?\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: Well, first, we met with the president this week and part of our efforts is exactly to do that, to see how through executive action we could get some of the pieces, the important pieces of comprehensive immigration reform passed. One of them of course is reuniting families. There is a backlog for petitions, families that petition for their children, their family members have to wait decades. Sometimes their petition when they`re children. By time they`re here they get up to the front of the line, they`re adults.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s four million people waiting to reunite with their families. In addition to that, TPS, which is an important program, temporary protective status, we want to make sure that countries that have been in the program since 1999, they`re redesignated. And I think we heard something an open window from the president on these two.\par \par \uc2 Now the asylum procedures are one that we should address through funding. And of course Mayorkas has come out with what I think is the beginning of an effort to deal in a transparent and justifiable way with anybody that seeks asylum. This is a nation that has a long-standing tradition.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: To accept asylum seekers, whether they come from Ukraine or they come from El Salvador, you know, if a mom is taking their 12 and 13-year- old because they`re being recruited by a violent gang and walks several thousand miles to the border, that mom should be heard. And if she cuts mustard, if the story and her reasoning is legitimate, then she should be allowed in. If not, then she should go back home.\par \par \uc2 So this is something that`s very important for our country. That`s what we stand for as a nation and we should provide the mechanisms to allow for this to continue to happen when it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have an issue with the asylum program being done in country? You`d have to change sort of the definition of things. You know, right now I think technically that becomes a refugee program if you`re doing it in country sometimes. But would you have a problem and you talk about, you know, somebody from Honduras who wants to seek asylum? Is that a problem for you if that asylum claim is adjudicated in Honduras?\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: There`s no reason why the greatest country in the world cannot have the capacity to process people as they come in. I think that`s the humane thing to do. To keep them on the other side of the wall is kind of inhumane and I think it doesn`t speak well of America. We are a country that is a nation of immigrants. I`m an immigrant. And now I`m a member of Congress. You know, I had to be petitioned to come here.\par \par \uc2 You know, so this is something that`s fundamental for our country. I think that we can do this if providing that the right funding levels, if the public policy is in place, and if we have the political will to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think that nations along this basically that are part of the trail that comes here, not just Mexico but Honduras and Nicaragua and Panama, what could we be doing with them to incentivize them to help us in this situation rather than essentially, and I know in the case of Panama, they actually tried to accelerate the migration process up to the north?\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: Well, you know, I`m glad you used Mexico because most of the migrants are not coming from Mexico right now. In fact, there is a surge in migration back to Mexico from Mexicans in the United States because they have better economic opportunities right now in that country. And because they have taken a deep dive and seen what the root causes are and they`re trying to address it.\par \par \uc2 It is countries in the triangle countries like Honduras, Guatemala and El Salvador where we`re getting the vast number of migrants and we should be able to help those countries. I`m a supporter of reassuring, making sure that we provide Latin American countries in our hemisphere the opportunities to have a trade with the United States, rather than rely on us as we did during the pandemic on countries that are on the other side of the planet, in Asia.\par \par \uc2 We could rely on countries that are our neighbors, our allies, to produce the kind of goods and services that we need here in the United States. That will create jobs. I think that`s the best migration program for these countries is the creation of jobs. Another one, of course, is dealing with violence. Hopefully we`ll pass the Caribbean Basin Security Initiative today in Congress, which would allot money to combat crime, to interdict drugs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: And these are things that we must do to lead the hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ESPAILLAT: If we`re really the leaders of the hemisphere, we should act like it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman, the lost opportunities this country has had by failing to be leader of this hemisphere is too much to finish this conversation today.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Espaillat, Democrat from New York, really appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, moments ago the funeral services remembering Madeleine Albright concluded. Current and former government officials, members of Congress, members of the diplomatic corps all gathered here in Washington to honor her extraordinary life and legacy, the first woman to be a U.S. secretary of state. President Biden, former President Clinton and former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  all delivered very moving tributes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: To Madeleine, from my perspective, there is no higher mission, no greater honor than to serve this great experience of freedom known as the United States of America. May her memory continue to be a blessing to our nation.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON, FORMER PRESIDENT: She made us laugh. She made us cry. Some of us she made mad. But she had a full, hopeful life because she knew what she believed in, she knew what she was for, she knew what she was against.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: She didn`t just help other women. She spent her entire life counseling and cajoling, inspiring and lifting up so many of us who are here today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Madeleine Albright passed away last month at the age of 84.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. Again apologies for those technical difficulties at the top. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220428ei4r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Matt Bradley, Carol Lee, Clint Watts, Steve Kornacki,  Marc Caputo \par \uc2 9,138 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday. President Biden announces more military aid for Ukraine and plans to ask Congress for another round of emergency war funding as Zelenskyy looks to the West, desperate for even more arms. This, as Russian forces intensify their attacks in the east. Putin claims success in the critical port city of Mariupol, but did the Kremlin declare victory prematurely? That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 And check this out a memo from the \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  camp says he is not ruling out a run for president in 2024 depending on what the current president decides to do. What does that memo really mean? Why include the 2024 line? I`ll be joined by the advisor who wrote it. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 Happy Thursday, welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. With the war in Ukraine now in its ninth week, believe it or not, President Biden is once again sending hundreds of millions of dollars in military aid to Ukraine. The additional aid comes amid new questions about the war and efforts to end it.\par \par \uc2 Is the U.S. doing enough to support Ukraine? Are we actually giving Ukraine enough to win the war rather than just simply keeping Russia from winning the war? And is Russia`s declaration of victory in the key city of Mariupol premature?\par \par \uc2 This morning, President Biden announced yet another $800 million package to Ukraine for heavy artillery, 144,000 rounds of ammunition, dozens of howitzers, and some tactical drones, among other things. And in addition to that aid, the president also announced a ban on Russian affiliated ships from all U.S. ports, along with plans to ask Congress for another round of emergency funding for the war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Every American taxpayer, every member of our armed forces can be proud of the fact that our country`s generosity and the skill and service of our military helped arm and repel Russia`s aggression in Ukraine to beat back Putin`s savagery that tried to seize Ukraine`s capital and wipe out Ukraine`s government.\par \par \uc2 Now we have to accelerate that assistance package to help prepare Ukraine for Russia`s offensive that`s going to be more limited in terms of geography, but not in terms of brutality. Not in terms of brutality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president`s announcement came hours after Ukrainian President Zelenskyy once again made an urgent plea for assistance from the U.S. and other Western allies wanting more than what we`re delivering. With the mayor of Kharkiv, Ukraine`s second-largest city reporting intense shelling, Russian officials are now claiming to control 80 percent of the Luhansk region in the east.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is declaring success in the besieged southern city of Mariupol where the last remaining Ukrainian troops as well as hundreds of civilians do remain holed up in a steel factory. They`ve not surrendered. So despite Putin`s claim of success, he told us -- defense minister today not to storm the factory. And President Biden says he`s seen no evidence that Mariupol has totally fallen into Russian control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We don`t know how long this war will last but as we approach the two-month mark, here`s what we do know. Putin has failed to achieve his grand ambitions on the battlefield. After weeks of shelling, Kyiv still stands. President Zelenskyy and his democratically elected government still remain in power. And Ukrainian Armed Forces joined by many brave Ukrainian civilians have thwarted Russia`s conquest of their country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ukraine`s prevented Russia from winning. In the next phase, are we going to do what it takes to make sure Ukraine wins? For more on this, I`m joined by Carol Lee at the White House, Matt Bradley`s in Ukraine, and I`ve also got NBC News National Security Analyst \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  to try to help us dissect what remains of the Russian strategy. But let`s start in Ukraine with Matt Bradley.\par \par \uc2 Matt, here, we`re delivering more aid and already Zelenkyy feels as if it still needs more. He`s still not getting enough. And I`ve heard a phrase yesterday, send us -- if you give us more, we can win this war. But essentially what Zelenskyy seems to be saying is the aid he`s getting is not enough for them to win. It`s just enough for them to fight.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, Chuck, one of the big questions here is, where`s this aid going to go and how fast can it make it to the front lines? I`m all the way in the East in the city of Dnipro. It was an epic journey for me getting out here and it will be an epic journey for all of that material that the U.S. is putting to bear in Ukraine, getting it across the entire country to the front line.\par \par \uc2 Before, as you mentioned, this massive assault on the eastern part of the country, this is where the aid is needed most. Now, so far, we haven`t really seen this huge assault happening and it`s sort of started with a whimper rather than a bang.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But it has -- I mean we`ve been seeing -- we`ve been seeing airstrikes all over the eastern part of this country. What we haven`t seen yet is a ground incursion and that is probably going to happen very, very soon. It looks as though Putin and his generals are giving some time to try to reconstitute their military. How much time? We don`t really know. And that`s the question because the clock is ticking. Can all of these weapons make it from the U.S. to here, -- it or from elsewhere in Europe to here in Ukraine, and then all the way across the country to the frontline?\par \par \uc2 That`s going to be the big question because there are troops, there are people who are ready to use this. So when -- you know, we hear from President Zelenskyy that it`s not enough. We also have to remember it`s not enough in -- you know, not enough time. This is the real question. It`s a logistical nightmare getting this stuff across the country and that`s one of the things that President Zelenskyy is going to be worried about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt, what came -- what do you know, what are your sources tell you about the situation in Mariupol? I know you`re not there, I know that`s difficult reporting but what have you learned from Ukrainian officials?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, we actually spoke with -- well, I got some videos from a major general who is in -- he`s a parachute -- or he`s a marine, he`s in Mariupol. He sent these videos to a couple of news organizations. And he was saying that you know, time is ticking. This was before \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  - - or the clock is ticking excuse me. This is before \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announced that he wasn`t going to be moving into those underground bunkers underneath the Azovstal plant.\par \par \uc2 You know this is like an underground city. It`s a huge compound. It`s really well fortified. It wasn`t meant for war. It was meant to withstand the heat of making steel. But it served very well for the soldiers and the estimated thousand civilians who are underneath there. They say that they are, you know, subsisting on very little food. The Russians have hit some hospitals. There -- as I mentioned, there are a thousand civilians who are down there. They are in desperate need of help, according to this military officer we spoke with.\par \par \uc2 And then I spoke today with the family who left a month ago and they were describing it, it was just heart-wrenching hearing their story about leaving that city and making it up to Zaporizhzhia, which is about an hour from where I am here in Dnipro. You know, just crossing through checkpoints with Russian soldiers, trying to make it out alive.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these humanitarian corridors -- they didn`t leave through one of the established humanitarian corridors but, you know, a lot of these humanitarian corridors have had to be canceled in the past month because while -- is President Zelenskyy said yesterday, while they`ve been offering the Russians time and time again, options to try to get civilians out of the city, the Russians have complicated things by firing in the area of these humanitarian corridors. So it really is a desperate situation for those who are trying to get out, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt Bradley, painting that grim picture for civilians in Ukraine, Matt, thanks very much. Let me bring in Carol Lee. Carol, I feel like we`re on repeat here. I think it was two weeks ago, we announced an $800 million military aid package to Ukraine. Now, we just announced another $800 million package to Ukraine. We know Zelenskyy believes this is not enough and it`s not getting there fast enough. Our -- do our own -- do our own folks believe the same thing that we`re doing what we can, but maybe we can do more, and maybe it can go faster?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, you heard President Biden say he wants to see a steady flow of weapons going into Ukraine, and that that needs to be accelerated, so there`s definitely a recognition of that. And of that -- and of what President Zelenskyy has been saying that he needs much more -- much more quickly.\par \par \uc2 We are told a senior -- from a senior defense official telling our colleague Courtney Kube that within the next 24 to 48 hours, those first flights from the U.S. with some of this equipment that`s in this $800 million package will be leaving the U.S. and this is going to include those howitzers and those artillery rounds. And this official says that artillery will be in the hands of Ukrainians by the end of this weekend. So that gives you a sense of the timeframe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Now, if you`re President Zelenskyy, you wanted them yesterday. So that`s not exactly, you know, going to satisfy him. But I think from the administration`s perspective, and that`s why you heard the president say he`s asking Congress for more money to send more weapons, you know, they recognize that they need to do this.\par \par \uc2 They need to send more and they need to get it there faster. The question, though, is, you know, the president talked about this being a critical window or Russia is laying the groundwork for this next phase of the war, is what they`re sending enough for Ukraine to be successful in this critical window, though, Chuck. That`s -- it has to be the question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carol, I want to dissect something. The president said he talked about it at the end of our opener there and we quoted him as saying he talked about the issue of hey, we`ve denied Putin Kyiv. They`ve denied him -- you know, we`ve protected the sovereignty. The democratic government is still there. I note this and still wonder what is our policy. Is our policy for Ukraine to win this war or simply to prevent Russia from winning the war?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 LEE: It`s a really great question. In fact, it`s a -- it`s a critical question. When I`ve talked to administration officials, in fact, one said to me last week we want Russia to lose. And that, you know it`s kind of a little bit of a passive way of saying we want Ukraine to win and defeat the Russians. But that`s not really been the visual policy that the U.S.`s goal here is to put in completely move Russia out of Ukraine. It`s a little more murky than that. I think, though, the question that is paired with that is, are the Ukrainians getting enough to be able to be successful too as that official said, for Russia to lose?\par \par \uc2 TODD: And one final thing, Carol, this new -- I guess it`s a temporary immigration status with Ukrainians. Explain how it would work.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, basically, it`s designed to help the administration reach its goal. It said it would allow 100,000 Ukrainians into the U.S., but you have to have a sponsor. So a family member, for instance, would have to be sponsoring someone. And so it`s designed, the administration says to make this happen a lot faster. The question is will that actually be the case?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carol Lee at the White House for us -- outside the White House, Carol, thank you. Let me bring in \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  to try to discern what the -- what the Russian strategy is. And I want to start with what we heard from Putin about Mariupol and his essentially saying, well, we control it enough. And then President Biden seemed to question whether they did have full control of that city. Do you have a sense just by troop movements and what you`ve seen what is happening in Mariupol?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Hi, Chuck. The Ukrainian military is basically just at that steel factory at this point. And from the Russian perspective, they absolutely do not want to assault that steel factory any more than they`ve already done. They`ve taken significant casualties in Mariupol. That was an objective that they thought they would take in the first week. It is now starting month three, and they`re still trying to secure all of it.\par \par \uc2 Separately, I think Putin needs to claim victories, even though there`s no real way for his audience to know that it isn`t a full victory. And then separately, Mariupol is just about dead center in the middle of Russian- held territory, not far from the Ukrainian-Russian border, also on the Sea of Azov, which is completely encircled by the Russians. So they know that over time, they can literally just wait it out in terms of said warfare.\par \par \uc2 I would not be surprised and I would hope that some sort of negotiations can happen between the Ukrainians and the Russians to actually remove those remaining people from Mariupol both the civilians and the military. I think it would make sense for both parties. The Russians don`t want to go in there and fight, you know, room to room and in underground spaces. They will take massive casualties. At the same point, the Ukrainian military can`t last too much longer just due to food and water.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s go big picture here, Clint. Are we giving Ukraine the type of weapons it needs to win this war in the east?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I think slowly it`s happening, Chuck but it`s really just a race of logistics at this point. Can the Russians move their combat power to the east and put it into attack corridors? There is some artillery, there is some preparation on the battlefield, you`re seeing some Russian reconnaissance, slow movements forward however, those are armor formations that are going to be facing off against what is essentially dug in Ukrainian defenses. And so far, they`ve done fairly well.\par \par \uc2 Now, over time, when you add all of that Russian combat power focus on one front instead of three, Russians have a sizable advantage. There`s another aspect of this too, which is it is the wet season, it is muddy out in the east of Ukraine right now, it`s very difficult to get off roads, so the Russians are essentially buying time with indirect fires, artillery, and missile attacks.\par \par \uc2 But just as we heard, you know, at the start of this, to get all of that military support out to the east to employ it effectively and to train the soldiers, many of the howitzers that we`re sending there, they`re not howitzers the Ukrainians already have, they have to know how to use this equipment. Lastly, there`s just not enough tanks for the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: They`ve recovered some from the Russians. They do have some on the way but it`s not going to be near enough probably over the longer term to hold off these Russian assaults in Donbass.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, so what you`re -- in short, you`re saying they do not -- is it a weapon issue, or is it a manpower issue for the Ukrainians when it comes to fighting Russia`s needs?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: It`s a weapon issue, Chuck. You -- the fighting is going to be very different. In the east, we`re not talking about Ukrainians who are in their hometown defending from dismounted positions with anti-tank weapons, anti- air weapons. They did very well in that situation. This is wide open, more rural areas the town spaced out over long distances.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 I do think the Ukrainian military will again impose significant losses on the Russians. But when you just look at the numbers, the Russian manpower and equipment is staggeringly more than the Ukrainians have at present.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Clint watts, who is one of our national security analysts here really has been the guy dissecting the Russian military strategy for us. Clint, I always learned something from you, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead. With the next phase of the war in Ukraine now underway, where exactly does the conflict go from here, and what is Putin`s next move? We`re going to be joined by another top military expert, that`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 And then a fierce political battle in the Magic Kingdom. Is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` moves to put an end to some special privileges for \uc2 Disney\uc2  after the company denounced the governor`s controversial so-called don`t call it Don`t Say Gay bill that DeSantis gets all fired up about? Where that feud now stands and what it says about the Florida governor`s broader ambitions inside the Republican Party and for the country. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How long can the U.S. maintain the level and pace of its military support for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I -- well, we had a capacity to do this for a long time. The question is, how are we going to continue to maintain the support of the international community and keep the pressure on Putin to prevent him from overrunning the country, number one, and number two, make sure we continue to maintain the economic sanctions, which over time, and we`re beginning to see it are devastating their economy and their ability to move forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Biden may say the U.S. can sustain this level of military support for Ukraine and economic pressure on Russia for a while, but the question now is whether that`s the best strategy for ending this war and assuring the Ukrainian victory and the president seem to hit he knows the bigger challenge may be keeping the entire coalition together. Joining me now is retired Army Colonel Peter Mansoor.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 He previously served as an executive officer to General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , and is now the Mason chair in military history at the \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2 . Colonel Mansoor, I got to get that right, just so I don`t get all those Buckeyes on my back. Let me start -- let me start with what the president said there that I led him to this segment with. That was very reassuring about what he thought that there feels like there`s a clock here on the Alliance, that there`s a clock here on the economic pressure and it felt like the president was acknowledging that. How much should that dictate how much we accelerate our pace of help of Ukraine in this war?\par \par \uc2 COL. PETER MANSOOR (RET), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Well, we should accelerate our pace of help to Ukraine, regardless of the clock or the lack thereof. It`s just the right thing to do. Putin cannot be allowed to seize portions of the neighboring state because if we were to do so it would tell any nation that has nuclear weapons, say Taiwan -- say China visa vie Taiwan, that you can invade a neighbor and not pay any consequence for it. So I think the president should ignore the clock and he should continue to arm Ukraine and seek victory -- and define victory as a free and sovereign Ukraine with its territorial integrity intact.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s that second part. Let me ask you, from a military standpoint, are we giving Ukraine enough weapons to win this war?\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: We are beginning to. I mean, we`ve given them now five battalions of artillery, 90 tubes, 144,000 rounds, that`s an -- that`s a nice down payment. But we need to figure out a way to get them tanks that they can operate, which means the Russian-made tanks, and we need to get them more drones, and eventually, we need to get them better aircraft so that they can compete for the skies over the battlefield. And that will prevent the Russians from winning but it will also give the Ukrainians the capacity to launch a counter-offensive and take some territory back.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is the issue in your mind with the fighter jets? Is it logistical or is it really this sort of well, maybe that`s escalatory, which, at some point, I`m wondering why that continues to be a concern when the only person escalating is Putin?\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: You`re exactly right. I think at first, we didn`t want to inflame the situation any more than it already was and now we realize, you know, we`re -- we`ve gone down that road already. The fact that we are parsing what types of weapons to give to Ukraine is ridiculous. Now, in terms of the -- like, say, the Polish MiG-29, you see those are a 1980s technology, we need to figure out a way to get Ukraine the kind of weapons that can actually compete with top-end Russian fighters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Russians, obviously tested this ICBM, it was a -- it was about sending the West a message. What should -- what should our response be to something like that? You know, if he`s doing that, you know, should we be sending our own fighter jets there or something, you know, sort of calling that bluff? And I guess the question is, is there a downside of not responding with some form of rhetorical strength here?\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: Yes. I think we should just ignore it and not rot (AUDIO GAP) take his bait. You know, this is what he wants to get into a nuclear saber- rattling contest, and that`ll inflame the passions of the Russian people and perhaps put fear into the Western public. This is exactly what Putin wants. So I think in this case, just ignore it and move on, continue to arm Ukraine to the best of our capability, and help them win this war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your diagnosis of what`s been a surprise to many military experts, and that is Russia`s utility so far with their military?\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: You know, this is a -- this is just -- it`s astonishing. They have had since the fall of the Soviet Union 30 years to resurrect their military. And although they have pretty good equipment, in some cases, they just have not shown the leadership and the capacity at the staff level to combine arms and services to gain and maintain air superiority to handle logistics.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is the blocking and tackling of modern armored warfare, and they just can`t do it. And I`m not sure that the war in the Donbass is going to turn out any differently than the battle for Kyiv did. It`s going to take a while to sort out this mess and put in leaders that actually know what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think we over -- we all overrated the Russian army here? Is it just a case of where we believe the hype?\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: Yes. I think the Russians did a pretty good job of trumping up their military. We do know the capabilities of the equipment, so that was unknown but it`s very difficult to understand the culture of the military and to understand how good the leadership is. It`s not like we have observers out there who maneuver is looking at what they`re doing so you know it takes a battle -- it takes a war to figure out who actually can talk -- walk the walk and not just talk the talk.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Colonel Peter Mansoor, a retired colonel, a one-time member of General Petraeus`s team, now, a military history instructor at \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2 . Thanks for coming on and sharing your expertise with us.\par \par \uc2 MANSOOR: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s turn now, to get an update on the fight over the federal transportation mask mandate. The Department of Justice has now officially moved to appeal the Florida federal judge`s ruling to overturn the federal mask mandate on planes trains and transit after a request by the CDC. They didn`t ask for a stay on the order, though for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 This comes after some uncertainty, mixed messages about how the Biden administration would respond to Monday`s ruling, if at all. But the DOJ has not asked the appeals court to block the judge`s order. So passengers will still be able to travel maskless while the decision is being litigated. That could take months. So this appears to be about future CDC rulings really than about this one.\par \par \uc2 Still to come. 15 months later, now, some big-name progressives and Democrats on the ballot this November are signaling their concern about President Biden`s standing. How does Biden stack up against the competition of his recent Oval Office successors? Our very own Steve Kornacki is here to break it down at the big board. That`s next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Biden took his oath of office just 15 months ago. And his standing in the polls has gone down significantly since his first days in office to such an extent that we`ve seen Democratic candidates on the ballot this November begin to distance themselves from the White House. And we`ve also seen some progressives like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren begin to cautiously raise their public profile perhaps jacking for a scenario where Biden chooses not to run for re-election. \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  folks put it in writing.\par \par \uc2 But before we dive into that, let`s put President Biden`s standings in context. Steve Kornacki is here at the board. He`s taking a look into how President Biden`s numbers stack up with his immediate predecessors and what it could mean for him going forward.\par \par \uc2 We already know where the midterms are headed at this point. It`s either going to be bad or catastrophic. But this is about Biden in `24. What do you got, Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Take a look here, Chuck. We`ll basically go back to Bill Clinton in his first midterm in `94. That sort of ushered in this modern era. We`ve had three presidents starting with Clinton who lost control of the House in their first midterm. Biden if that happens this mid-November would become the fourth.\par \par \uc2 So where does Biden stand right now? If you average all the polls together, Biden`s approval rating is 41.3 percent. If you average the polls together for all those predecessors at this exact same point here in mid, late April of their midterm year, well, here, you can see how he stacks up. The number most similar to his predecessor, to his immediate predecessor, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Obama was still actually slightly more favorable than unfavorable at this point, though the trajectory was going in the wrong direction, of course.\par \par \uc2 W., this was post 9/11, just a completely different political universe. Bill Clinton even still had a 49 percent approval rating at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That was considered bad back then, Steve, if you recall.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Under 50, oh, you know now 49, everybody would be, like, whoo-hoo.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Back then that was not great.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And it was bad and nobody knew how bad it could get that November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Which (INAUDIBLE) in the Republican revolution. The other big change, though, I think, starting with Trump is it`s this right here. And again this is where Biden`s numbers are most similar. The disapproval number. Biden is over 50. We talked so much about how Trump`s was over 50 for the bulk of his presidency. Again, Obama at this point was getting into trouble in 2010 but the disapproval was mid-40s.\par \par \uc2 Clinton was getting into trouble but the disapproval was low 40s. So you`ve got that disapprove number for the past two presidencies now moving over 50 percent, staying over 50 percent, a lot earlier a lot more consistently. Again these two presidencies very similar when you look at the numbers. Then when you look at where they ended on election day, midterm election day, that`s where you`ve got converging.\par \par \uc2 You take Bush out of it, you know, a year after 9/11, but Clinton was at 44 percent. His party lost 54 seats. Obama was at about 45.5 percent, Democrats lost 63 seats. There`s Trump, 43.5 percent, Republicans lost 40 seats. I mean, that is the history in the midterm year they`re all up against. And again, just to underscore the point, you mentioned it, here`s the generic ballot right now.\par \par \uc2 If you average our NBC polling, we`ve taken this a few times this year, on average Republicans right now leading the generic ballot by a point. You know, in 2018 at this same point it was Democrats leading.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You could see that wave coming in 2018, that one-point Democratic lead was very shaky in 2010. You could see Bush`s big year for Republicans coming in `02. And you go back to `94, and again, `94 was a little different. It was the start of all of this, sort of.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: The president comes in, loses control in the midterm.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You couldn`t quite see it coming in `94. But since then you have been able to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy, Steve, the idea that Biden and Trump are aligning here, that`s calcifying the polarization potentially. You know, what you`re outlining, and it`s just one instance, is that that`s a recipe for a lot of one-term presidents.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Well, it is. It also raises the question, too, this idea of Trump hovering over our politics to the extent Biden was elected as the anti- Trump, does that just still lock in and maintain the basic polarization of the Trump era.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Now just kind of transfers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I saw a story in the French newspapers talking about how both presidential candidates are under water and they`re -- wow, we`ve exported American presidential politics to France.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Steven Kornacki, appreciate you at the big board, as always, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still to come, feeling the burn? 2024? Maybe. We`ve got details on a new memo from the \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  camp telling supporters the independent Vermont senator is not ruling out another presidential run if Biden doesn`t run. The person who wrote that memo, senior adviser \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2  to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , he joins me next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:37:46]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`re not yet at the midterms but it appears there`s an invisible 2024 presidential primary on the Democratic side that may not be so invisible anymore. A leaked memo from \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  top adviser to his allies said that the two-time presidential candidate has not ruled out another bid for high office in 2024 if there is an open seat essentially, if no incumbent is running, meaning \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The memo went on to spell out that Sanders and his popularity and his appeal to progressive voters. of course President Biden has given no indication that he will not seek a second term in office and apparently tells people behind the scenes all the time that he is going to run. But his political standing has raised concerns among plenty of Democrats across the ideological spectrum about the future of the party in this year`s midterms and beyond.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is the author of that memo, senior adviser to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , his former campaign manager \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Faiz, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR, FORMER SANDERS CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Good to see you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. So I`ve got to start with a leaked memo. Is this a memo that was intended for public consumption?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: No. It was not intended for public consumption. But --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you`re here. So you don`t seem to be upset about it.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: I`m not running away from the memo, a better way to put that. So let`s discuss.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough. I want to start with why you chose to write this memo.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems as if, you know, what motivated it and what I thought I took away from it is what is happening in the Pennsylvania Senate primary where you have \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  attacking Fetterman for having the support of Democratic socialists like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: There is a divide still within the Democratic Party, and Senator Sanders has been winning that. And I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been supporting that effort and trying to move in a progressive direction. There are still some stalwarts. We`re fighting for a corporate version of the Democratic Party and this kind of ideological debate, do we stand with working people, is exactly what we`re trying to drive at. Why is \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  popular? That`s the honest question to reckon with. Look, if any Democrats running out there reckon with the question, why is Bernie popular? What does he do differently?\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is your theory in this? Is it personal or is it his position?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: There`s a credibility of fighting for working class issues, one, right, an authenticity and a belief over lifetime that`s what he does. Secondly, somebody with progressive muscularity, wields power and authority with a sense of a moral vision of justice for our economy. I`m going to take the fight to \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . I`m going to take the fight to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . And when workers are standing up, I`m going to get behind them like a freight train.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:03]\par \par \uc2 And I think that has resonance. If we`re losing -- just ask the question, the Democratic Party is certainly losing some support. Who are you losing some support with? Young people, working-class people, disaffected people who`ve become cynical about politics. Who speaks to them? How do you get people who aren`t already with you? And I think Senator Sanders` vision does that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the fact that a super PAC was spending money on Fetterman on this was a motivator --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Not only Fetterman. I mean, if you look at the congressional races, you`ve got like these crypto billionaires spending money. You got all kinds of people spending money in these races and they`re going to make you feel afraid of, oh, the progressive vision, oh, you know, inflation. We can`t go down this rabbit hole with people who want to cut stimulus checks that are wildly popular, by the way. We can`t stand with people on a fight for the working-class America.\par \par \uc2 And what I`m trying to do and what we`re trying to do with the memo is say embrace those attacks. There is a fight. There is a divide. Don`t get soft on it. Don`t get weak. Kind of lean into the fight. Elections are about choices, Chuck. And I think one critique, constructive critique I would have for Biden and the Biden team is you`ve got to elevate fights. Pick fights and elevate them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I would argue that you say -- you said here at the end, well, this is the critique I`d have. I think everything you just laid out sounded like a critique of the administration right now. You feel like they`re not fighting --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Not just that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re not picking fights.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: I look at Senator Schumer on the Senate floor schedule. I`m like, why haven`t we had a vote on Build Back Better, Chuck? Like these are -- I urge them to put up things and I see them saying, you know, we don`t want to lose. I get it. But sometimes people just need to know the fight. What are you -- how much do you believe in this that you`re willing to go to the mat and call out people even within your own ranks who aren`t with you? Wield power, wield authority with muscularity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The -- by the way, why haven`t you endorsed Fetterman? Why hasn`t Sanders endorsed Fetterman yet for the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: We expect -- hopefully Senator Fetterman but he hasn`t solicited the endorsement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I got you. You`re not going to endorse anybody that --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hasn`t asked yet.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: That`s a premise of Senator Sanders` operation is that we don`t endorse unless somebody asks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you get a sense he`s waiting until after the primary to get the endorsement? I mean, doesn`t he want it during the primary?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: You can speak to the Fetterman team. They`ll have their judgments.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you have no reason to believe that the senator has less enthusiasm for John -- for Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Oh, I mean, if Fetterman were to emerge in this primary, we`d be supportive, certainly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously the thing that caught the attention of most reporters was the decision on a frequently asked -- you had FAQs. So I`m curious, are there been frequently asked questions to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  that he`s going to run again in `24?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: I mean, maybe not from Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, no, I understand.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: But certainly from our allies, and there are a number of them, both people who have endorsed us, groups who have endorsed us, supporters around the country who have been saying, you know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So he`s not going to primary Biden, correct?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s out of the question.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Well, right. I don`t think -- he`s supporting President Biden aggressively, trying to get the agenda passed. Yes, I -- the primary is not (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: But he does not have that same deference with the vice president?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: No. He respects Senator Harris. I think at that point --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Vice President Harris. Of course. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t want -- that would get you in trouble.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes, of course. As I should. I think, you know, that the question on Vice President Harris if she`s to run or anybody is to just say, what are you going to run for? What`s your agenda going to be? And then Senator Sanders would make his judgment and determination at that point. It`s just to say, hey, what are you going to be fighting for? It`s not to say I`m running in 2024. I`m leaving my options open to see what does the field look like and what kind of agenda are we as a Democratic Party putting for?\par \par \uc2 TODD: What would you like to see done now that`s realistic the way the Senate works to have for Democrats to campaign on in the fall?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: God, this hurts me in my soul, honestly. The question is just like, not your question, but it hurts my soul that the answer is so easy, which is like right now you have \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers organizing in work places in Staten Island. Senator Sanders is visiting there this weekend. You have \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers all across the country, it`s like a national field campaign for those of us who talk in politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: You have a national field campaign. These workers are the courageous heroes of the moment. They are doing the hard slogging. And you would think of a Democratic Party, does this align with your values? Are they elevating the fight that you need to be in? Yes. And, you know, again, constructively for my friends in the White House, they should be invited to the White House. You should elevate them, put them on the platform, on behalf of a grateful nation we thank you for taking on tough fights against corporate power because you care about basic dignity for the working class.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re not the only progressive that`s been speaking out. Elizabeth Warren had an op-ed in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " where she said, in part, Democrats cannot bow to the wisdom of out of touch consultants who recommend we simply tout our accomplishments. Instead, Democrats need to deliver more of the president`s agenda or else we will not be in the majority much longer.\par \par \uc2 I know that Senator Sanders and Senator Warren share the same ideology, shared the same views on this. It does look like you`re both jockeying to see who`s the leader of the progressive movement. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: I don`t know about the leader of the progressive moment. They`re certainly -- they`re putting forth ideologies and they`re advocating for them. There`s a lot of Venn degree, Venn diagram overlap here. So I wouldn`t see it as a competition, Chuck. I think we both putting forward visions that we believe in strongly and advocating for it, trying to get people engaged in politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept the idea, though, that you publicly in this memo talking about 2024 that it sort of rips the band-aid off of an invisible primary on the Democratic side that has people wonder what the president is going to do?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:08]\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: It`s -- you and I knows politics is politics. Arguably, the campaigns are always going on, people are always thinking politically. I think that that`s the case. And for Senator Sanders, in particular, let me speak about him. Yes, he`s built of a movement of millions and millions and millions of people, again, talking about how popular he is across the country. He would be doing a disservice if he weren`t speaking to the fact that there`s a lot of people out there who want to know, is somebody representing? Is somebody making the fight? Is someone making the argument for working people? He`s going to continue to do it as long as he can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious, where is -- does the senator accept, though, the idea that the checks that you said were enormously popular actually contributed to our current inflation?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Again, that hurts my soul around this fight because if you`re the Biden administration, the Democrats, this is an opportunity to lean in. What happened with the stimulus checks? We gave money into people`s pockets. Child tax credit, you know, unemployment insurance benefits and stimulus checks. What happened when we gave that money out? Corporations came around and stole it.\par \par \uc2 That`s what happened. They took it back out of your paycheck. Why? Because they wanted to report, not profits, Chuck, record profits. S&P 500, you look at their profits, off the charts. That was a choice by corporations. So yes, I could raise that bag of potato chips by 25 cents. I could increase the cost of this car by a thousand dollars. But everyone think it`s supply chain scenarios out there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what you`re saying --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: And in that environment --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- inflation issue should be on the corporate.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: A hundred percent. Lead that fight to say, hey, yes, maybe there should be some price increases but what happened was price gouging, price collusion, corporate power in America trying to get -- take -- reach into your pocket, oh, I see you`ve got some money there coming back to me. And if you just look at the record profits of these corporations, that is the fight. That is the fight. And people are asking who the hell is going to take them on? It is the government`s responsibility to that fight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think you have made clear you`d like to see the Democrats have some fight in it. I mean, you`ve used that word a lot. I`d say that --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Muscularity. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem like that that is among the biggest problems.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Right. Executive order -- I mean, we appreciate -- everyone is going to appreciate it. Sometimes these fights are hard, you`re going to lose.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: But you`ve got to lean in, whether it`s executive orders, putting legislative votes on the floor again, you know, go around the country, wield it. You know, wield that authority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2 , senior adviser to Senator Sanders, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, DeSantis versus \uc2 Disney\uc2 . Why the Republican Florida governor is trying to take down some special privileges in place where dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 We`re live in Tallahassee at the state capitol after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. \uc2 Disney\uc2  will no longer be able to govern the magic kingdom by its old rules anymore as Florida statehouse voted this afternoon to pass a bill that will strip Disneyworld of its special self-governing status. The bill that will make \uc2 Disney\uc2  part of the real world or at least part of Florida again will go into effect next summer after its signed into law by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move is likely an attempt by DeSantis to punish \uc2 Disney\uc2  for pushing back on the so-called -- simply for saying this phrase, he got mad that \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s executive said call it a "Don`t Say Gay" bill earlier this month. So DeSantis decided to fight back. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , formerly a DeSantis campaign contributor, condemned the law last month after outrage from its own employees and they went so far as to call out DeSantis by name and announced the company would work to repeal the legislation. \uc2 Disney\uc2  has also paused all political donations in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s national political reporter who`s based in Florida is Marc Caputo. He joins me now from lovely Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 Marc, first, I want to start with, this is such, to me, like when you watch Governor DeSantis as sort of a political analyst here, this special session was about his congressional map. Are we even having a conversation about the congressional map and he quickly flipped it to be about \uc2 Disney\uc2 ? Is this an accident or a coincidence?\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, it`s not an accident or a coincidence, however, Democrats just seized the spotlight a few minutes ago by shutting down the Florida House for about an hour in a protest over the redistricting maps. They took to the center of the floor. They weren`t recognized. The speaker had to throw the Republican-led House into recess --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: -- while the members, the Democratic members chanted, and then they came back and then the Republicans just steamrolled the Democrats, just finished the redistricting bill, and then without any discussion they brought up the \uc2 Disney\uc2  bill as three black lawmakers were chanting, and they passed the \uc2 Disney\uc2  bill without much of a discussion. So it was done pretty quickly after quite a kerfuffle, let`s say, on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: After the conversation I just had, this is an example of people in power wielding power. When you have it, you wield it, sometimes --\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- without emotion or anything else. Let me talk about this \uc2 Disney\uc2  fight. A month ago the Florida legislature didn`t care about this. You know, I`m a former Floridan, and you`re one, we`ve always joked about \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s special status. But they`re not the only entity that has it. The Daytona Speedway, for instance, airports have it. Is this newfound interest in this going to expanded beyond \uc2 Disney\uc2  or is this just about punishing \uc2 Disney\uc2  politically?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: It`s about punishing \uc2 Disney\uc2  politically, that`s pretty clear. Understand, though, that Reedy Creek Improvement District which is what we`re talking about in relation to \uc2 Disney\uc2 , that`s different from all the other independent special districts. It`s massive, like 25,000 acres, spans two counties. And they even have the ability to build with less regulation than even a county a nuclear power plant if they want. I`m not making that up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. No, I remember that punchline.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Now that having been said, it`s not like the legislature is -- yes, well, it`s not like the legislature is like, oh, we need to stop nuclear power. No, we want to punish \uc2 Disney\uc2  for saying don`t say gay, and that`s what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting picking a fight with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . I have seen some polling on this. Those that are politically following the story, I think DeSantis is building his base, but there`s a vast middle of America that`s sitting, they`re going, what`s with the fight against \uc2 Disney\uc2 ? This seems to be a politically, a little riskier of a move than maybe he thinks.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Maybe. I mean, let`s wait and see the polling. I mean, currently when you look at the data polling, fundraising, the amount of media attention he gets, which is kind of the coin of the realm nowadays, DeSantis is doing quite well. I mean, you know, for what it`s worth, he`s able to launch attacks that his base loves, that his opponents hate, that the media covers, that produces more campaign goal. And so right now all the incentives for him are to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 One thing in that kind of campaign goal territory or discussion, this bill doesn`t instantly go into effect by dissolving Reedy Creek Improvement District when the governor signs it. It waits about 13 months. It`s going to happen in June of 2023. What does that mean? The legislature is going to have a year to decide, do we really want to do this?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: And \uc2 Disney\uc2  is going to have a year to kind of come to heel and say, OK, we`re sorry, we`re going to do this, we`re going to do that. So hey, you know, \uc2 Disney\uc2  pauses donations, this, you know, to a degree, especially when you talk to critics, has a bit of Mafia feel to it, which is nice theme park you have there, shame if something happened to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: So that discussion is going to on for about 13 months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and that`s sort of a larger question here. Is Florida a good place to do business if you`re a private corporation, or is it only a good place to do business if you`re a private corporation that cheers the ideology of the Republican governor?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Or if you are a corporation that keeps its mouth shut when the Republican governor wants you to. Now that`s going to be up to these various corporations. But so far DeSantis has drawn his line in the sand on, quote-unquote, "woke corporatism." You know, some of the -- he calls it CRT, but it`s more of the anti-racism training. If you engage in that you engage in sort of progressive social justice values publicly. The legislature right now and the governor right now is showing a willingness to come after you if you happen to get some sort of public benefits that others don`t really have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as many of us analysts have been saying for a couple of years now, small government Republicanism is dead. It is strong government. That is what the ideology on the right is these days.\par \par \uc2 Marc Caputo in Tallahassee. Mark, thank you. And thank you all for being with us this hour.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A quick programming note. Be sure to check out the latest episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," "THE HARDEST PROBLEM: CLIMATE SOLUTIONS." That`s tonight at 10:30 p.m. Eastern on NBC News Now. You`ll be able to get the program on demand tomorrow on Peacock and \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220422ei4l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Shannon Pettypiece, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Cornell  Belcher, Matthew Continetti, John Sparks, Ali Arouzi, Jeremy Bash, Pete  Williams \par \uc2 8,595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , SUPREME COURT NOMINEE: Is no meetings with 97 sitting senators. And we had substantive and engaging conversations about my approach to judging and about the role of judges in the constitutional system we all love. As a brief aside, I will note that these are subjects about which I care deeply. I`ve dedicated my career to public service because I love this country and our Constitution and the rights that make us free.\par \par \uc2 I also understand from my many years of practice as a legal advocate as a trial judge, and as a judge on a Court of Appeals that part of the genius of the constitutional framework of the United States is its design and that the framers entrusted the judicial branch with the crucial but limited role. I`ve also spent the better part of the past decade hearing thousands of cases and writing hundreds of opinions.\par \par \uc2 And in every instance, I have done my level best to stay in my lane, and to reach a result that is consistent with my understanding of the law and with the obligation to rule independently without fear or favor. I am humbled and honored to continue in this fashion as an Associate Justice of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , working with brilliant colleagues, supporting and defending the Constitution, and steadfastly upholding the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 But today, at this podium, my mission is far more modest. I`m simply here to give my heartfelt thanks to the categories of folks who are largely responsible for me being here at this moment. First, of course, there is my family, Mom and Dad, thank you not only for traveling back here on what seems like a moment`s notice but for everything you`ve done and continue to do for me. My brother, Ketajh, is 1here as well. You`ve always been an inspiration to me as a model of public service and bravery and I thank you for that. I love you all very much.\par \par \uc2 To my in-laws, Pamela and Gardner Jackson who are here today, and my sisters-in-law and brothers-in-law, William and Dana, Gardie and Natalie, thank you for your love and support. To my daughters, Talia and Leila, I bet you never thought you`d get to skip school by spending a day at the White House.\par \par \uc2 This is all pretty exciting for me as well. But nothing has brought me greater joy than being your mother. I love you very much. Patrick, thank you for everything you`ve done for me over these past 25 years of our marriage, you`ve done everything to support and encourage me and it is you who`ve made this moment possible. Your steadfast love gave me the courage to move in this direction. I don`t know that I believed you when you said that I could do this, but now I do. Then for that I`m forever grateful.\par \par \uc2 In the family category, let me also briefly mention the huge extended family both Patrick`s and my own, who are watching this from all over the country, in the world. Thank you for supporting me, I hope to be able to connect with you personally in the coming weeks and months. Moving on briefly to the second category of people that want special recognition, those who provided invaluable support to me professionally in the decades prior to my nomination, and the many, many friends I have been privileged to make throughout my life and career.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that everyone who finds professional success thinks they had the best mentors, but I truly do. I had three inspiring jurists for whom I had the privilege of clerking, Judge Patti Saris, Judge Bruce Selya, and, of course, Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . Each of them is an exceptional public servant, and I could not have had better role models for thoughtfulness, integrity, honor, and principle, both by word and deed. My clerkship with Justice Breyer in particular was an extraordinary gift and one for which I`ve only become more grateful with each passing year.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Justice Breyer`s commitment to an independent impartial judiciary is unflagging. And for him, the rule of law is not merely a duty, it is his passion. I am daunted by the prospect of having to follow in his footsteps. And I would count myself lucky, indeed, to be able to do so with even the smallest amount of his wisdom, grace, and joy. The exceptional mentorship of the judges for whom I clerked has proven especially significant for me during this past decade of my service as a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, that service itself has been a unique opportunity. For that, I must also thank President Obama, who put his faith in me by nominating me to my first judicial role in the federal district court. This brings me to my colleagues and staff of the federal district court in Washington DC and the DC Circuit. Thank you for everything. I am deeply grateful for your wisdom and your battle-tested friendship through the years. I also want to extend a special thanks to all of my law clerks, many of whom are here today, who have carved out time and space to accompany me on this professional journey.\par \par \uc2 I`m especially grateful to Jennifer Gruda who has been by my side since nearly the outset of my time on the bench and as promised, as promised not to leave me as we take this last big step. To the many other friends that I have had the great good fortune to have made throughout the years, from my neighborhood growing up, from Miami Palmetto Senior High School, and especially the debate team, from my days at Harvard College, where I met my indefatigable and beloved roommates, Lisa Fairfax, Nina Coleman Simmons, and Antoinette Sequeira Coakley, they are truly my sisters.\par \par \uc2 To my time at Harvard Law School and the many professional experiences that I`ve been blessed to have since graduation, thank you. I have too many friends to name, but please know how much you`ve meant to me and how much I have appreciated the smiles, the hugs, and the many other girls that have propelled me forward to this day.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I`d like to give special thanks to the White House staff and the special assistants, who provided invaluable assistance in helping me to navigate the confirmation process. My trusted Sherpa, Senator \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2  was an absolute godsend. He was an absolute godsend. He`s not only the best storyteller you`d ever want to meet but also unbelievably popular on the Hill which helped a lot.\par \par \uc2 I`m also standing here today in no small part due to the hard work of the brilliant folks who interact with the legislature and other stakeholders on behalf of the White House, including Louisa Terrell, Reema Dodin, Tona Boyd, Minyon Moore, Ben LaBolt, and Andrew Bates. I am also particularly grateful for the awe-inspiring leadership of White House Counsel Dana Remus, of Paige Herwig -- where`s Paige? And \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 . They lead an extraordinarily talented team of White House staffers in the Herculean effort that was required to ensure that I was well prepared for the rigors of this process and in record time. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Thank you as well, to the many, many kind-hearted people from all over this country and around the world who have reached out to me directly in recent weeks with messages of support. I have spent years toiling away in the relative solitude of my chambers with just my law clerks and isolation. So it`s been somewhat overwhelming in a good way to read simply be flooded with thousands of notes and cards and photos expressing just how much this moment means to so many people.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 The notes that I`ve received from children are particularly cute and especially meaningful because more than anything, they speak directly to the hope and promise of America. It has taken 232 years and 115 prior appointments for a black woman to be selected to serve on the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 . But we`ve made it. We`ve made it, all of us -- all of us. And our children are telling me that they see now more than ever, that here in America, anything is possible.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: They also tell me that I`m a role model, which I take both as an opportunity and as a huge responsibility. I am feeling up to the task primarily because I know that I am not alone. I am standing on the shoulders of my own role models, generations of Americans who never had anything close to this kind of opportunity, but who got up every day, and went to work, believing in the promise of America, showing others through their determination and yes, their perseverance that good things can be done in this great country.\par \par \uc2 From my grandparents on both sides, who had only a grade school education, but instilled in my parents the importance of learning, to my parents, who went to racially segregated schools growing up, and were the first in their families to have the chance to go to college. I am also ever buoyed by the leadership of generations past who helped to like the way, Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 ., Justice Thurgood Marshall, and my personal heroine, Judge Constance Baker Motley. They and so many others did the heavy lifting that made this day possible. And for all of the talk of this historic nomination and now confirmation, I think of them as the true path breakers. I`m just the very lucky first inheritor of the dream of liberty and justice for all.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, I have worked hard to get to this point in my career and I have now achieved something far beyond anything my grandparents could have possibly ever imagined, but no one does this on their own. The path was cleared for me so that I might rise to this occasion. And in the poetic words of Dr. Maya Angelou, I do so now while bringing the gifts my ancestors gave me. I am the dream and the hope of slave. So, as I take on this new role, I strongly believe that this is a moment in which all Americans can take great pride. We have come a long way toward perfecting our union. In my family, it took just one generation to go from segregation to the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And it is an honor, the honor of a lifetime for me to have this chance to join the court to promote the rule of law at the highest level, and to do my part, to carry our shared project of democracy and equal justice under law forward into the future. Thank you again, Mr. President, and members of the Senate for this incredible honor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: And Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd. Here, the president and vice president and the newly confirmed soon- to-be Justice, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , just wrapped up an event at the White House celebrating Jackson`s historic confirmation on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And I have to tell you, that was quite the speech by the next justice. It`s a major victory for the Democratic Party, but what if it`s their last for this calendar year? We`re going to get to that in a moment. But Judge Jackson will be the first black woman on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And when the new nine meet next session, will be the first time in this nation`s history, the highest court will not have a majority of its members, the white men. President Biden called this a day that history will remember. Judge Jackson said her confirmation means her family, went from segregation to the Supreme Court in one generation.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from the White House is NBC`s Shannon Pettypiece. So, Shannon, I want to talk about this. What struck us all a little bit here watching this event is a reminder this White House hasn`t had a celebratory moment like this in some time, and some of its COVID, some of it is tough stories. They haven`t had some, you know, that be -- even the bipartisan infrastructure bill signing ceremony didn`t have this. And this obviously, is a bigger moment for the country. Well, so that adds, I think, to this celebratory atmosphere. But Shannon, have you seen anything like this since Biden took office?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: This is certainly the happiest, the most enthusiastic, we seem to have seen the President in a while. And I mean, to your point, Chuck, I was talking to a Democratic strategist yesterday, they work a lot with African American voters, particularly black women and, you know, they said, as we were reflecting on the confirmation yesterday, how they remembered how back in February when this nomination was made, how the White House was really hoping that this was going to be a moment they could leverage with Democrats and the Democratic base.\par \par \uc2 So much, though, has happened in that past month and a half, though, that usually a Supreme Court justice confirmation hearing is one of the biggest stories in Washington get in his head to compete with one of the biggest conflicts in Europe. In a generation, it`s hard to complete -- compete with rising gas prices, inflation, COVID, now appearing to make a bit of a resurgence in DC, certainly among a lot of high profile officials. So the White House didn`t necessarily get the moments they had been hoping for out of this at the start of it, but they are getting one today with an event like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shannon, what -- look we got the war is the war and that`s going to consume more time from that west wing, then perhaps politically the Democratic Party wants it to happen but that`s the job that they signed up for. But what does the White House hope is next on the domestic front? Do they have a next before November?\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Well, they`re still trying to get their COVID funding. I mean, they`re still -- I mean, remember when we were talking about trying to get, you know, a trillion-plus for, you know, they`re law -- for childcare tax credits and drug price negotiations and all that. Now, they`re just fighting to try and get 10 to $20 billion in COVID funding for all the things that we have been accustomed to for the past year, like free vaccines, free testing for people who aren`t insured, they don`t provide vaccines overseas.\par \par \uc2 So I mean, to give you an idea of the sort of where we`re at, they can`t even get that ball across the court or maybe they have a deal, but it`s been a bit of a struggle. So there were never any really great expectations about the domestic agenda, there were hopes that maybe bits and pieces of the president`s Build Back Better package could be picked off. But really, the focus is so narrow on what they need to get done just to get through the next month or two. I`ve just not hearing much talk about anything else beyond the immediate issues that are right on the agenda at the moment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, one could argue after over-promising and under-delivering at the start, maybe they`re better off not trying to promise anything and see if they can come up with something while we`re not watching. Anyway, Shannon Pettypiece at the White House, Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Well, as we said, the 53-47 confirmation vote for Judge Jackson is the Democrats` biggest victory in Congress since passing that Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill last November. As Congress heads on its spring break, democratic priorities are at best on pause, as you heard there, or at worst, dead. Funding for additional COVID release is stalled over an immigration fight a fight that`s divided members of the president`s own party. Senators Manchin and Sinema have not shown much indication that they are open to reviving pieces of President Biden`s Build Back Better agenda.\par \par \uc2 And if they do revive it, what parts of it are they going to revive? And while the House passed a bill capping the price of insulin, the Senate wants to make changes which could draw the process out with legislative days rapidly dwindling away before November. And yesterday on the show, the Congressional Progressive Caucus Chair, \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , who typically projects optimism when it comes to the president`s agenda in Congress, all but said the legislative window has closed and pivoted to pushing the idea of executive action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL, (D-WA): Two-track strategy. We`re trying to push as hard as we can for anything we can get through and we`re going to keep doing that legislatively. But that`s also why we rolled out an executive action agenda. We`re moving both tracks and I`m very optimistic on the executive agenda front. I am still hopeful on the legislative front and we`re not giving up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s not every day you hear a member of Congress refer to executive actions in the royal we. But look, you make some big promises when they took over a unified government, with a narrow majority pledging action on everything from climate change, universal pre-K, long term child care tax credit, by every measure they`ve under-delivered. At the moment, Democrats are heading into a midterm election with those campaign promises unfulfilled and asking voters to give them another two years running Congress.\par \par \uc2 And a big one that`s unfulfilled that I wonder how they`re going to handle is the prescription drug issue. With me on set, NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent Leigh Ann Caldwell, Democratic Pollster, MSNBC Political Analyst Cornell Belcher, and \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  Senior Fellow Matthew Continetti.\par \par \uc2 So, Cornell, I`m going to start with you because I want to start with the Supreme Court. And when this nomination got started, I think you and I even had a conversation, hey, boy, the president really could use some improvement among African Americans. Just in particular, will this be that moment? The process so far -- look, his poll numbers have not improved over the six-week process. If anything, they`ve you know either stayed stagnant or depending on the pollster declined a bit. Does this help? Will this be - - or is there just too much doom and gloom on everything else?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: A couple of things, Chuck. One is, you know, internally, his numbers have improved with minority voters. Actually, his numbers started taking a slight uptick after he came out and say, you know, we should do away with the filibuster to pass voting rights. But that aside, Chuck, this is a big deal, right? It`s politically it`s a big deal. You have -- you have a woman standing for the cameras saying to America, I am the hope and dream of slaves.\par \par \uc2 The exceptionalism of America is on display, right? This is the -- it shouldn`t even be partisan, right? You know, the arc bends towards justice. And this is an example of, we fight and there`s a lot that I can be cynical about, about America, but that is an example of the hope and the promise of America. So it`s a big American thing. The politics of this is this. When you look at how Republicans displayed a particular kind of disdain and subjugated her to a special kind of disrespect over this process, and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It felt very racially motivated.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, look, you know, what is it about the ascension of this woman of color to a position of power that terrifies them and makes them behave this way? The image of them walking out while \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  stood there and clapped, the image of them walking out after that vote yesterday, Chuck, not only African American women but there`s -- but women all across the country, middle of the road, women across the country will look at that image of Republicans walking out on that woman. And they have probably done more to help us mobilize women of color than we could be done in a billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe this moment could linger in a way that --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: This is an energizing moment if Democrats capitalize on it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Leigh Ann, you said something about Lisa Murkowski to us while we were chatting during the event --\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That the behavior of Republicans in the judiciary committee was a big motivator for her to support this nomination.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It was. She alluded to it in her statement and announcing a lengthy statement, announcing that she was going to support \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . But my Republican sources also tell me that she has talked to Republicans who said that she was pretty disgusted with how Republicans on the Judiciary Committee handled that hearing, completely felt like -- she felt like they were attacking this woman and she was not pleased with it.\par \par \uc2 And so she thinks that the process is essentially broken. She doesn`t think that it needs to be that way. And while she also supports Ketanji Brown Jackson`s thinking that she is qualified for this position, the way Republicans dealt with it really left a bad taste in her mouth.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matthew, the \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  -- I can`t -- I mean the vein moves so fast sometimes I can`t keep up. He really seemed to go out of his way today to send -- yesterday to send a statement about this nomination that seemed just unbecoming.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, SENIOR FELLOW, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: I don`t pretend to know the mind of Senator Graham. I will say --\par \par \uc2 TODD: With the whole tie thing and ticking it off to make sure he wasn`t on the for --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I know. He had a favorite candidate in this judicial nominations process and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Indeed, he really can`t --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: that candidate was not nominated. And that seemed to really annoy him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I tried to understand like, what --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I don`t --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What part of his relationship with President Biden made him think that President Biden should take his advice on that?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think there`s no question that this is a victory for the president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: And it`s a victory for America in a lot of ways. And it`s a short-term loss for the Republicans because they went in here with the opportunity to really make it about judicial philosophy and judicial philosophy alone, and some of their free agents with an eye toward 2024 decided to make it about other things. And that I think, not only may have cost some Republican votes but also just ended up making soon to be Justice Jackson more popular than when she did --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: So it`s clearly their strategy not -- was not effective. Now, it may be effective for them in their Republican primaries in 2020.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I got it. I remember getting a text right in the middle of one of these -- one of the nasty exchanges at one point and someone said, wow, are -- move over, R.B.G. -- K.B.J., meaning that she`d become bigger than just a Supreme Court justice, Cornell?\par \par \uc2 BLECHER: Yes. She an icon and she`s a symbol, and there`ll be teach --\par \par \uc2 TODD: People are going to wear T-shirts of her.\par \par \uc2 BLECHER: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s my -- I guess is the way to look at right?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: She will be in political advertising in the fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Look, just like how Amy Coney Barrett was for Republicans in the last election, ACB, you know?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: There`s two --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Then quite stare to say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean --\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: He`s just a sign like I don`t have a little help to pump it out.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I don`t think, though this is -- this victory is happening with enough time before the midterms that the Democrats will have to work very hard, I think to remind the public --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that`s a --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: In the final --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that`s fair --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Final weeks of this campaign that yes, I think Justice Jackson is there and we fought about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: That`s a big if.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And here`s the uncomfortable question for Democrats, and Leigh Ann, you cover Congress. Is this the last big thing they pass? Is this the last big thing this Congress accomplishes before November?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: We`ll see. They are talking about a scaled-back reconciliation bill but these are not formal negotiations that they`re talking about in some quarters of the Democratic Party on the Hill are talking about just passing one thing like prescription drugs, doing one more thing for the American public before the midterms. And there are -- the Democrats are divided on how to move forward. They don`t know if they should do it one, go -- you know, one last attempt, and risk failing before the midterms, or kind of, you know, support what they`ve already passed and move forward and promote what they`ve done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I`m going to pick a scab on prescription drugs. I`m going to go through the Wayback Machine and show you how often and how long Democrats have been advertising on this issue. Take a look. We put the mush together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATES: The drug companies have been overcharging long enough. It`s time to fight back.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BALDWIN\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES SENATOR: I`m \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , and I`m fighting against drug companies that jack up their prices. That`s why I approved this message.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZZIE FLETCHER, (D-TX): I`m Lizzie Fletcher, and I approve this message because I`ll work with both parties to bring down drug prices.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the Republicans in Congress have taken millions from drug companies, blocking bills that would lower drug costs and insurance premiums.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: They don`t argue they gone up, put a bill on the floor, right? How do you go or are you really going to put this in the Democratic advertising in the fall again?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because people want it but look --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Look, watching that was fun because it reminds me it goes back and forth. I remember Carville`s was -- is the economy, stupid change versus, you know, so much the same.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: And don`t forget health care, right? It`s always health care. But healthcare is still a top three or four issues for Americans, especially the sort of the affordability --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s usually cost.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s a cost issue.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right. But if you look at what the White House was doing this week -- and by the way, I`m not one of those people who say, oh, the Democrats need to pass like five more things. Like there`s a magical silver bullet number of things that Democrats pass and then there`ll be OK.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s got to be one, right?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, but I think if you look at what they`ve done around -- this House Democrats, if you look at what they`ve done around prescription drugs, you look what they`ve done around infrastructure, I think they have a pretty good case to sell things. I don`t think they need to pass eight or nine more things and say, OK, this is the magic number. Now, we`re fine. I think they have plenty of stuff under their belt right now. But the American people just out -- just -- they just don`t know about it. I think they have to do a pretty good job of selling what they`ve actually already been able to pass.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Yes. Healthcare has traditionally been a very strong issue for Democrats but I think it`s been subsumed into larger economic questions, particularly about inflation and the cost of living in general, which is driving a lot of the erosion in the president`s number. The other strong democratic issues, education, and we`ve seen beginning with the Youngkin election --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: That issue seems to be slipping out of the Democrats` fingers as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me go -- let`s end with where we started which is also in our conversation. Cornell, you positive -- maybe the cameras are a mistake in this Supreme Court nomination?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think they are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell us. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think they are because this process is so broken right now that it is -- it`s a terrible process. It`s just a terrible process. If we take cameras back -- and look, I`m always, look, I`m a media guy. Like --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hesitate talking about it but I completely agree.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: But I tell you what, Chuck, this is an example where I think if we take cameras out of the process, I think a lot of the politics are taken out of the process. I think senators will be behaving differently. I think the questions are asked differently. And by the way, I think that the justices actually answer some questions off camera in a way that they don`t do on camera. I think the cameras have spoiled this.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: But then the public won`t ever know about it if there`s no cameras in the process.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are they going to know, though?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are they learning through -- I mean --\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: Push to get the cameras into the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: You think it`s bad now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Just wait.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But by the way, I don`t know if that`s a good idea. I think the audio -- look, I think audio, I`ve been really pleasantly surprised to how, A, satisfied I am with audio but it does still feel like it`s about the case.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It`s about fundraising. Supreme Court confirmation processes are now about party fundraising.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The rest of us, go, Leigh Ann. It was about fundraising. You`re right.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It is. Except --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Take out the cameras then. Make it not about fundraising. I know, I`m in the minority on this.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Transparency, I mean, we need to know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The unintended consequence is that the black robes are no longer black to too many people, they`re red and blue. And that`s the unintended consequence. Whatever it does to fix that, maybe it`s this. Who knows.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That`s true. But we have lots of colors for you for your birthday.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s very kind of you. Yes, this is the last one I`m supposed to have. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: They just go backwards. It`s not the last one. They just go backwards.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Somebody said this is the youngest birthday I`ll ever have again. Anyway, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a horrific attack on a train station in eastern Ukraine. It was filled with women and children trying to get out of a warzone. And Russia appears to be almost escalating on purpose against people trying to leave and maybe gearing up for a military offensive.\par \par \uc2 YOU`RE WATCHING MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:22]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The death toll is continuing to climb from that attack on a train station in Ukraine. Officials now say at least 50 people were killed, including five children. It was a strike on a train station in the Donetsk region of Ukraine. This is in the eastern part of the country. NBC News has not been able to independently verify the numbers of the death poll. But a senior Pentagon official tells NBC News the attack appears to have been carried out by a Russian short-range ballistic missile fired from inside of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 John Sparks from our sister network Sky News in Europe was on the scene of the attack shortly after it happened. Here is some of what he saw.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPARKS, SKY NEWS REPORTER: What we think has happened is that it has exploded in the air over this area because it has distributed explosives throughout this area in multiple locations, seemingly randomly. And I`m going to ask my colleague Garwen McLuckie just to focus on the word on the side of that missile. It means "for children" or "for the children."\par \par \uc2 Just remember that there will have been hundreds of people queueing up to get inside the train station. We`ve seen it. And they will -- they would have been killed. A lot of them. Terrible damage here. You can see people have brought their lunch, their packed lunches, a few bags here. And then they would have tried -- they would have been queueing up to get into the booking hall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The attack comes as U.S. officials warn that the war could be entering a new and protracted phase with Russia focusing its attacks on cities in eastern and southern Ukraine. As you could see they`re going to be brutal with it.\par \par \uc2 With me now is Ali Arouzi. He`s in Lviv in western Ukraine for us where many Ukrainians pass through in their efforts to flee the country. Also Jeremy Bash with us, former Pentagon and CIA chief of staff, now an NBC News national security analyst.\par \par \uc2 Ali, we saw what our Sky News partners there got for us. It is just -- it`s stomach turning. You`re dealing with -- you`re talking to a lot of people that are in the midst of essentially Lviv is the way station when people can escape from the east. What kind of reactions are you hearing and seeing?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, it`s shock and horror again that civilians are being targeted in this very deliberate way. You know, they`re still reeling over what happened in Bucha, about their brothers and sister in Mariupol. And this just adds an extra layer of horror to what`s going on in this country.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy said this morning that this was a deliberate attack on civilians. He said that there were no military personnel at that train station and this was designed to terrify the civilians that are trying to escape the eastern part of Ukraine, just as they`ve tried to terrorize civilians in many other parts of this country.\par \par \uc2 And just following on what our Sky correspondent said, the regional governor of that region said that this was a Tochka-U missile. Now we can`t independently confirm this but this is what the regional governor is saying that that had cluster munitions in it that explode in midair and then they spray all over a very large area with bomblets to cause that kind of damage that we`ve seen there.\par \par \uc2 And of course the Ukrainian authorities have been telling people in these areas, look, get out of the eastern part of Ukraine as fast as you can before the window closes, and before there is an all-out Russian attack in that area, which the Russians have been focusing on since their failed attempt at taking Kyiv. And obviously there were around 4,000 people at that train station, as you mentioned earlier. Many of them women and children trying to get to safety to places like Lviv where I am.\par \par \uc2 And you hear account after account from before that, you know, everybody who is trying to escape Kyiv before they attacked that place, people were just crammed to the rafters on trains trying to get out of these areas. So it was probably a very similar situation at that train station. I mean, you can see the scene of carnage there, dead bodies, blood, suitcases are strewn all over the place.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is designed to terrorize the local population and to stop them from leaving these areas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: That`s what the Russians have done time and time again in this war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: They`ve stopped civilians getting out of these areas that they`ve hand them in because they want them to capitulate in one way or another. But incredibly, Chuck, as well as all the other atrocities that have been committed here, the Russians are denying they had anything to do with this one either.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Arouzi, on the ground for us in Lviv. Ali, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 Jeremy, I keep coming back to a quote that Amy Acton, she was the health official of Ohio, right, at the beginning of the pandemic, and she said, when you look back on pandemics, never regret what you did, regret what you don`t. Are we going to look at how we`ve handled the situation -- are we ever going to regret what we did or are we going to regret what we didn`t do?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY BASH, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Well, I think we`ve got to do more, Chuck. I mean, I think we`ve got to do more to ensure that Russia cannot exact these kind of attacks on civilian targets. It requires arming the Ukrainians with additional advanced weaponry so they can take the right to Russia and deprive \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  of any ability to be on the ground in Ukraine. There`s some discussion now about whether an off-ramp for Putin would entail him staying in the Donbas because of course his plan --\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you offer him -- that was an off ramp that I think a lot of Europeans thought was -- it`s totally closed, right?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Totally closed. I don`t think Zelenskyy is going to permit it. I don`t think any country in the West is going to strong-arm Zelenskyy into allowing it. And so I think with attacks like these --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Three weeks ago, there were some.\par \par \uc2 BASH: There were some. And that was part of the discussion. In fact one of the points of the discussion in Istanbul was that the Russians said we don`t want to have any prosecutions on war crimes. You take a look at an attack on a train station like this, targeting civilians, potentially, using cluster munitions. A missile where it was written "for the children." This missile is for the children. That`s obviously a war crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s interesting, you -- I want to go -- I`m glad that we`re showing the missile. The way it was described, you immediately thought it was a cluster bomb.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes. When you talk about a munition exploding in the air and actually several munitions coming out from it, fanning out and essentially being like 5,000 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: These are supposed to be against the Geneva convention, right?\par \par \uc2 BASH: They are against the Geneva convention. 5,000 bullets coming and shredding civilians. The only purpose of a cluster munition is an anti- personnel weapon. Not to debilitate a train station but to kill people who are standing in line to get their train.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So this is a terror attack in the middle of a war.\par \par \uc2 BASH: This is a terror attack designed to intimidate. Designed to keep hostages there so that when the Russians come in, the civilians can`t leave.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what more do the -- I mean, we keep hearing, give them more, give them more.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is more and what is it that, you know, is there some offensive capabilities we need to be giving them now at this point?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes. And in terms of more, first, the U.S. today announced that Slovakia was going to be moving an S-300 anti-aircraft sophisticated --\par \par \uc2 TODD: In four weeks that we thought Slovakia was going to do this.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes, and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What took that so long?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Well, we finally got our Patriot battery into Slovakia. So that was what Secretary Austin and the White House announced today. We have to do more of those kinds of sophisticated anti-aircraft systems in there to effectively create a no-fly zone. But I also think we need more sophisticated anti-rocket, artillery and mortar systems to protect against attacks like these.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there anything that we can do, when I say we, it`s \uc2 NATO\uc2 , to help create humanitarian corridors? Is there a line you can do without being the full no-fly? I know that that`s -- essentially if people want to leave a war zone, let`s at least help them evacuate safely.\par \par \uc2 BASH: I think we can do it in the west but I`m not sure we can do it in the east. I mean, that`s the problem. So the countries along the borders in the west like Poland and Romania, they might be able to provide some capability to make sure that people can leave Lviv, where Ali Arouzi and others are. But in the east, in Donbas, I don`t see how \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries can do that. This is going to require offensive to get back to the point.\par \par \uc2 Offensive weapons that the Ukrainians have to go against static Russian military targets to take out their infrastructure and let civilians leave.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, one of the rationales for not doing certain things always is, well, we can`t give Putin the rationale. Putin lies about everything.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it about time, oh, we haven`t sent any MiGs but MiGs show up? Like, is there a point where you -- it`s time for \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the West to essentially, I don`t know what you`re talking about but, you know, some weaponry that maybe we weren`t comfortable giving publicly shows up?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Absolutely. And I think the reason why we haven`t been louder about pronouncing some of these weapons transfers is that some of the countries in \uc2 NATO\uc2  were uncomfortable with us publicly describing it. But to your point, we have to do a little bit covertly and a little bit more with some deniability, provide those capabilities to the Ukrainians so they can hit back on Russia. There is no negotiating with Putin. That`s out the window.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, is that out the door?\par \par \uc2 BASH: I think that`s out the door.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And so now, I mean, so now it`s sort of he`s got -- he`s got -- Ukraine has now got to win this war.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Ukraine has to win, period.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jeremy Bash, always good to get your perspective. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the misinformation crisis by the numbers and the impact on one group in particular in this country these days, Latinos.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. On this week`s episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," we took a deep dive into the infodemic. No one is immune to misinformation but evidence shows the problem is most acute these days in this country for one group in particular, Latinos. The most recent \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  study found that Hispanic adults report using social media platforms in much higher rates than non-Hispanics.\par \par \uc2 So for example, Hispanics are twice as likely to use \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and WhatsApp than non-Hispanics. And not only are Hispanics more like to have social media accounts, they also spend more time on the platforms every day. So that brings us to the real problem, it lies in the content.\par \par \uc2 According to a Morning Consult poll, more than a third of Hispanic Americans say they see misinforming often online and one analysis by activist group \uc2 Avaaz\uc2  found that nearly 70 percent of Spanish language misinformation went unlabeled by \uc2 Facebook\uc2  compared to less than 30 percent of misinformation in English.\par \par \uc2 When we reached out for comment from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and WhatsApp gave us this statement, "We`re running our entire strategy on misinformation in Spanish. We remove Spanish-language voter interference content and false claims about COVID-19 and vaccines, and connect people with authoritative information in Spanish through our voting and COVID-19 information centers. We have four fact-checking partners in the U.S. who review and rate Spanish content and WhatsApp partners with Telemundo and Univision on fact-checking services to help connect users with trustworthy information."\par \par \uc2 Trust me, there`s a lot more to learn. A great discussion --\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Oh, there`s a lot to learn.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, my word.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, as we say it in MEET THE PRESS, if it`s April 8th, it`s Chuck Todd`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you putting this on TV, Louise?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Happy birthday to you, Chuck Todd. For you, from as we say all of us at NBC News, I remember my 50th birthday during the Harding administration.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So congratulations and happy birthday to Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I am ready for my reverse mortgage. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Many happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess we`re going to break. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:37]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. There was a new legal filing today that revealed some new information about the two men arrested earlier this week for posing as \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  agents. Prosecutors say that the men posed as federal agents for years. These pictures show one of them posing in tactical gear and used the lie to gain access to Secret Service members including an agent responsible for protecting First Lady Jill Biden.\par \par \uc2 After the men were arrested agents found tactical gear, weapons in their apartments, along with a list of the building of residents. So this story is just one of those that just keeps getting, shall we say, curious-er.\par \par \uc2 Our justice correspondent Pete Williams is here. We`ve already seen him once today. But we`re here for a more serious reason. This is one of those stories that, I`ll be honest, I was like, wait a minute, I thought they -- what`s going on here? It is one of those, you`re like, wow, there seems to be more to the story.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, maybe, and we don`t know what that more is, and the what`s going on there part is the part that hasn`t been answered at all. I mean, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knows a great deal about these men now, having searched their apartments. They controlled five apartments in this building near the Navy yard, three of them had paraphernalia that could outfit a small police department, six weapons, ballistic vests, guns --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think they acquired it from the government?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think this is stuff that they may have acquired from --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By posing as federal agents? No?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, there`s no chance of that. And then two of the apartments, they led out to the gate, basically loaned to uniform officers from the Secret Services, and they offered gifts to two agents, one of whom was on President Biden`s detail, the other was on the first lady`s detail.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 They`re all now on administrative leave. One of these men, the one you`re looking at in the picture here has now -- the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says has started to talk to investigators about what he was doing. They say that he has actually claimed all sorts of things, including that he was a U.S. Army Ranger. He says he doesn`t know where the money came from, that it came from the other man, but he doesn`t know the source of that cash. Now that other man, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has found passports and visas. He is apparently from Pakistan. There`s a Pakistani --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Didn`t you say he pose as a Pakistani intelligence agent or something?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He claimed to one person that he was a Pakistani intelligence agent, but the government says the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has not been able to substantiate that. And I think you can assume that somebody who`s a real agent is probably not running around telling people that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How long did they know who these guys were and realized that they need to close in? Do you have that sense of when they sort of found these guys?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, it`s just very recently.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The \uc2 Postal Inspection Service\uc2  came to the building because there was a complaint that somebody had assaulted a letter carrier, and these two turned out to be potentially witnesses to the assault. They started making all these claims about who they were and the postal inspector said but that doesn`t sound right and called in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was the postal inspector that ended up --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So this is really almost like the taillight was out and all of a sudden look at what they just discovered.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You got it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow. Pete Williams, with the story that I guess, like I said, curious-er and curious-er. Thank you, sir. Thank you for playing the little surprise game as well.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us today. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, MEET THE PRESS on your local NBC NEWS station. I`ll look forward to seeing you.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220409ei4800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Alexander, Richard Engel, Courtney Kube,  Shaquille Brewster, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 9,047 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Descriptions of war crimes he says Russia committed in Bucha and saying there are many more cities where Ukrainian suffered similar fates from the hands of the Russians. God only knows what they`ve done in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 And a new warning from the Pentagon today that the violence will get worse as pressure grows for the U.S. and the international community to do more to punish Russia and help Ukraine. We`ve got new reporting on new sanctions that are rolling out in 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And later, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  returns to the White House for the first time since he left office. We`re going to hear from him and President Biden this hour at an event touting \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s signature achievement, the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd. As the atrocities unfolding in Ukraine come into clearer view, Ukraine`s president is urging a stronger response to Russia as the U.S. warns that the war could be entering a new and protracted phase. A warning, the images we`re about to show you are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD (voiceover): In his first address to the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2 , President Zelenskyy offered new details of torture and killings in the Kyiv suburb of Bucha, where Ukrainian officials say Russian forces killed at least 300 civilians. Russia is denying the allegations blaming Ukraine for staging the tragic scenes. Our own Richard Engel, who will join us in a moment, he saw firsthand what happened in Bucha earlier today.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: This was the Abramov`s (PH) house where Irena (PH) live with her husband, Oleg, and Irena`s father. And after Russian troops shelled the house, the family came rushing out and they found the Russian soldiers here. The Russians grabbed Oleg, started to talk to him and they brought him down this street and they put him on his knees. Irena was just coming out of the door and she was able to see the Russians executing her husband while he was on his knees. And there`s still some of the blood here on the pavement. The body was taken away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Zelenskyy accused Russia of committing the worst war crime since World War II. He compared Russians to Nazis and demanded an immediate response from the United Nations including a war tribunal.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The massacre in our city of Bucha is only one, unfortunately, only one of many examples of what the occupiers have been doing on our land for the past 41 days. And there are many more cities similar places where the world will -- has yet to learn the full truth.\par \par \uc2 I know and you know perfectly well what the representatives of Russia will say in response to the accusations of these crimes, they will blame everyone just to justify their own actions. They will say that there are various versions -- different versions, and it is impossible to establish which one of those versions is true. But it is 2022 now, we have conclusive evidence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: After Zelenskyy spoke, the United States Ambassador to the United Nations called the killings in Bucha, brutal, and then added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR LINDA THOMAS-GREENFIELD, U.S. AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: Some of them, according to credible reports, including by the Mariupol`s, city council, have been taken to so-called filtration camps, where Russian forces are reportedly making tens of thousands of Ukrainian citizens relocate to Russia. I do not need to spell out what these so-called filtration camps are reminiscent of. It`s chilling, and we cannot look away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just digest that phrase a minute, filtration camps. Digest that. Meanwhile, moments ago, NBC News was the first to report that the Biden administration will announce new sanctions on Russia in coordination with the \uc2 G7\uc2  and \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That comes as the Biden administration is warning of a new strategy by Russian forces. One that could indicate this war is weeks or months from getting close to an end. Here`s Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin moments ago, testifying on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: The Russians thought that they could very quickly move into Ukraine, capture the capital city and install their leader of choice and they weren`t able to do that. As they enter this phase, it will probably be a lot more deliberate, there`ll be able to mass fires a lot more and so the violence will probably go up a notch there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you`re wondering why Secretary Austin is on Capitol Hill, it`s a budget hearing that he`s technically testifying at but we expected that he and General Milley, we`re going to get asked questions obviously about the war itself.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Let me go to Peter Alexander, he`s our Chief White House Correspondent because the news about additional sanctions literally broke about 10 minutes before we came to air. So before we get to Kyiv where Richard Engel is, we wanted to get this news out there.\par \par \uc2 So, Peter, the fact that it`s in coordination with the EU and \uc2 G7\uc2 , this is, obviously, they`ve been working on this over the last 48 hours, is this going to be considered in response to these awful pictures we`ve been seeing over the last 72 hours?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I spoke to a source familiar with the matter about these new sanctions to be announced, Chuck, we are told tomorrow and they say, in fact, that they have intensified these new sanctions that were in some form already in the works as a function of what they have seen take place in Bucha. So this didn`t happen independent of what happened there but they have intensified it. And on top of that, they say they`re going to use all of the accountability tools at their disposal to respond. So this is only part of that.\par \par \uc2 But let me read more information that NBC News has now confirmed, the first two reports coming from multiple senior or we should say sources familiar with the matter. And they say that this announcement will, in their words, impose significant costs on Russia and send it further down the road of economic, financial, and technological isolation. It`s going to include, according to one of the sources, a ban on all new investment in Russia, increased sanctions on financial institutions and state-owned enterprises in Russia, and sanctions on Russian government officials and their family members.\par \par \uc2 You`ll remember at this time yesterday, we heard from the National Security Adviser, Jake Sullivan, who said that the U.S. had already -- had already confirmed that already come to the conclusion that Russia had committed war crimes in Ukraine and that the information from Bucha appears to show further evidence of war crimes taking place there.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is the most immediate response, it is not an isolated response and most notably, administration officials would say this is happening in conjunction with America`s allies and partners as they try to ramp up and further tighten the screws on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as we witness just these awful atrocities happening overseas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, I`m curious, I don`t think these -- there`s no new energy sanction directly, though, in here right? And I know there`s some talk in Europe about cutting off Russian coal and things like that. We shouldn`t expect that in this round?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You know, it`s a good question. We wouldn`t -- we did not get any further details on specifically what this will address. I will tell you what Jake Sullivan said when we spoke to him yesterday, he said this is going to sort of hit the industrial sector in Russia, part of the war machine or what`s helping, perhaps fuel the war machine but not clear that it`s what`s helping fund the war machine, which obviously, is energy. So, Chuck, to be honest, I think at this point, it still remains to be seen exactly what these sanctions look like. They haven`t further detailed exactly what it will target.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter Alexander, with that breaking news for us from the White House. Peter, thank you very much. Please stay dry. It sounds like a little bit of a rainy day there. But let me go to Kyiv where Richard Engel is, also with us as Courtney Kube at the Pentagon, and former Defense Secretary \uc2 Bill Cohen\uc2 . We showed a little bit of your -- of your reporting, Richard, in Bucha earlier today, I know you spent the morning there. Just do your best, tell us what you saw and compare it to other -- you have seen sadly, other war zones where atrocities were committed. Compare it if you could.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: So, I`ve been to many war zones, and you go into a place where a battle is taken has just occurred and it`s not uncommon to see bodies. That is a natural result, unfortunately, of war. That`s what these weapons are designed to do. But this felt more like a crime scene. The bodies were -- they`ve been many people had been executed. People have been executed at close range. Soldiers were going house to house conducting searches, stealing everything that could get their hands on.\par \par \uc2 So, I spoke to one woman and her apartment building sort of encapsulates the entire mood in Bucha. First of all, on the doors, they had written in handwriting we`re just peaceful civilians, please don`t bother us. It didn`t help. So the Russians would burst in, most of the doors internally in the building had been pushed in off their hinges.\par \par \uc2 The Russians, if you didn`t come to your door immediately, and a lot of people didn`t, they were trying to pretend that they weren`t home, the Russians would kick in the door, they would shoot the locks in, then they would start searching through the house, mostly they were looking for military-age males. Sometimes they would take the military mage -- males away.\par \par \uc2 But if you talked back to them, if you annoyed them in any way, we were told they might just shoot you on the spot. And that happened in several cases in just this one building. One of the people on the third floor was so afraid, he jumped out of the third-floor window and ran away. He managed not to -- not to -- not to get badly injured and was able to escape the area.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 A woman who lived on the -- in this building told us that four people, just from that apartment compound, just from her building, were buried. And that she and her husband buried them themselves behind the building in a little plot of land. And she took me to go and see it, and there was a small wooden cross over a freshly dug grave. That was just in one building.\par \par \uc2 And each building had a similar story of Russian troops who were firing at well, at anyone and anything they chose to, terrorizing the people, stealing from the people, and if they chose, they would take you away or they would execute you right there.\par \par \uc2 And that is not a common battle scene. Like I said, if you go to a battle scene, there`s a lot of damage, sometimes you see dead people, you see dead animals, a lot of destruction, that`s normal, but going house to house terrorizing people, stealing from them, and shooting them for any perceived transgression is unique (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Richard, do you have a sense of this was as the Russians work, so-called retreating, repositioning? Is this as they were leaving Bucha they decided to essentially ransack the city as they left? Is that -- is that what happened here?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: No. I -- that was my theory as well. And I went in, and I was thinking these were angry Russian soldiers who were leaving in retreat and decided to just flatten the place, but it sounds like this started almost from the beginning. Butcha was one of the first towns that were taken by Russian troops, it was taken almost on the first day of combat when that airport nearby Hostomel was taken. Bucha and Hostomel are just a few miles apart.\par \par \uc2 And residents told me that for the first week or so, the Russians were not acting in this way but then they quickly started to round people up, shoot people at will, sending people into hiding, just rolling down the streets in their armored vehicles, or they were described as tanks, but they could have also been fighting vehicles, and just shooting at buildings to their left and right. And that happened not just in their last few days as they were leaving, but happened right away.\par \par \uc2 One woman we spoke to, her husband was rounded up because he was a military-age male, and they just caught him on the street, put him on his knees, and shot him in the back of the head. And his body was left on the ground for weeks because they were not able to recover his body. So now this was not done by an army frustrated in a desperate way on its way out the door.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On its way out. And, Richard, I got to ask you about these filtration camps. How many stories are you hearing? Did you hear any of that in Bucha?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Not in Bucha. That is something we`re hearing more in Mariupol, something we`re hearing in and around Donbass, right along the border area, because so many people want to leave Mariupol and want to leave the areas where Russia has a high concentration of forces.\par \par \uc2 Russia has been playing with the idea of a humanitarian corridor saying you want to have a humanitarian corridor, we`ll give you a humanitarian corridor, and it leads to Russia. So get on this bus and we`ll take you to a camp in Russia, where you`ll be safe.\par \par \uc2 And many Ukrainians say that these people are openly being used as bargaining chips, that they`re being held, hostage. That Russia is accumulating these people to use for some propaganda purpose, or for some other purpose down the road.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard Engel, who`s now in Kyiv for us with that just chilling footage from Bucha, and I imagine we`re going to see a lot more of your reporting with Lester Holt on NBC News later today. Thank you, Richard.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: You will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move over to Courtney Kube. Courtney, first of all, do we have satellite eyes on any of these filtration camps -- so-called filtration camps? What do we know about them if we do?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: If we do, the U.S. isn`t acknowledging it yet, but we do know that they have a lot more satellite imagery than we hear about publicly, including seeing things like the -- places before they are attacked, places like Mariupol early on before the Russians really started to pound that city.\par \par \uc2 And given the fact that the U.S. has such so much coverage over some of these places, the assumption is they have been able to see some of these as, Richard was calling them, you know, sort of fake humanitarian corridors where the Russians were taking Ukrainians out of there. But again, it`s important to point out the U.S. is not acknowledging specific, you know, evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Right. Let me move to the budget hearings today that both General Milley and Secretary Austin are participating in. Given that they have outlined what they -- what they expect to be, this is -- this is going to be perhaps in the trench warfare of sorts for some time. Do we have the resources to continue essentially nonstop a conveyor belt of weapons assistance to the Ukrainians?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, yes and no. The reality is, on the Hill, there seems to be this endless interest in providing support to the Ukrainian people -- Ukrainian military. So every single time there`s been a need for more equipment, there`s been no question that the U.S. is going to provide it.\par \par \uc2 And last week, on Friday, the U.S. -- the Pentagon even announced that they were spending hundreds of millions of additional assistance that they were actually purchasing directly from the defense industry and sending it to Ukraine, as opposed to taking it from the U.S. stock. So they`re taking new and different and much more aggressive ways to get Ukraine the kind of equipment that they`re -- that they need. I also -- you know, you played the soundbite from Secretary Austin leading into the segment where he talked about the way forward and how it was going to potentially be more violent.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s important to unpack why that is. So what Richard was talking about, the Russian troops that are leaving some of these areas, frankly, they`re retreating from some of these areas, right? Around Kyiv, specifically the Northeast and the northwest axes that were going into the city, where the Russian troops have been moving away from those, moving up into Belarus, up into Russia, and they are going up there.\par \par \uc2 The belief according to the U.S. assessment is that they`re sort of refitting. They`re restructuring, they`re getting their act back together for what is believed to be another large offensive in the coming days or more likely, in the coming weeks, that they`re more likely to move in from the east down towards the Donbass area.\par \par \uc2 And why that is so significant, Chuck is that the problems that the Russians have been plagued with, two of the big ones are logistics, right? They simply haven`t been able, in many cases, specifically around Kyiv, and in the eastern area, to resupply their troops. They can`t get them food, they can`t get them logistics, food, fuel, the belief is that they are going to try to figure out what they`ve been doing wrong and fix that for the next part of this offensive.\par \par \uc2 But another thing that they are -- they are doing, according to this U.S. assessment, is that they are figuring out their commanding control issues on the ground and their coordination issues. So the easiest way to think about that is if you have Russian air forces and Russian ground forces, they weren`t doing a good job of communicating with one another and working together. So ground forces were moving to an area and they didn`t have air cover.\par \par \uc2 And so, that made them -- it made them much more vulnerable to the Ukrainian military. That`s a big part of the reason that they haven`t been able to advance in some of these areas, that the U.S. believed that they would be able to advance soon -- that frankly, the Russian military probably believe they`d be able to advance. So again, why this is so significant what Secretary Austin was laying out on the Hill today is because --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: We could see a very different offensive in the east than what we`ve been seeing in the past several weeks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I think the question is, should we be -- what are we -- can we do right now for the Ukrainians, essentially, to help them deal with what could be a much tougher Russian force. It sounds like they focus their offensive just on the Donbass in that part of eastern Ukraine. Courtney Kube, with that report, Courtney, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So let me bring in former Defense Secretary \uc2 Bill Cohen\uc2 . Mr. Secretary, let me talk about that. You listen to what Courtney just described. So we have this idea. Our commanders have decided they know what the Russian -- they think they know what the Russians are going to do here. They`re going to regroup, try to sort of get their act together and focus on the east and make these gains.\par \par \uc2 What should we be doing right now for the Ukrainian military? What should \uc2 NATO\uc2  be doing? Number one, to secure the gains that they`ve made, and make sure we don`t -- you know, they don`t feel like they are vulnerable, again, in Kyiv, and in some of the places that would they`ve pushed him out. And what can we be doing for the Ukrainians right now while the Russians regroup?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM COHEN\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Well, first, let me commend Richard Engel and the other war correspondents you have. They`ve been doing outstanding reporting. But in terms of what we can do, we can accentuate the positives so to speak. We can accelerate the weaponry that the Ukrainians need to kill more of the Russian soldiers. There has been an example of Russian incompetence, and that starts at all levels. And I`m kind of tired of hearing that President Putin may not know what`s going on, he may be misinformed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I`m, with you on that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: You know the problem (AUDIO GAP) Commander in Chief`s job is to know what`s going on. He can turn on MSNBC --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This KGB, if he wants to -- and we know this. If he`s in the dark, he`s choosing to be in the dark.\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : Exactly. Well, no commander in chief should want to be in the dark. He obviously knows what`s going on and he has miscalculated. And it`s obvious that he has poorly trained troops. They are being beaten on the ground. And so we say they`re retreating. They`ve lost. They lost in their goal to take over Kyiv. They lost in their goal to throw out the government. They`re now losing on the battlefield, even though they outnumber the Ukrainians five sometimes 10 to one in terms of manpower and capability. And so they`re on the retreat because they`re poorly trained.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 They may be adequately equipped but they`re poorly trained in how to use it. They haven`t had training and combined arms so to speak as pointed out Air Force. So as Courtney pointed out, Air Force and on the ground as well. So they`ve made a big mistake. The problem is, it`s still a big country, they have lots of weapons, they still have lots of people and they`re determined to take as much of the Ukrainian territory as possible.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve always believed that the real issue for us is whether or not they are right, Putin is right, that we will lose faith before the Russians do in terms of the imposition of the sanctions. Will they hurt us more than they`re actually hurting the Russians? I don`t think that`s the case in the long term. But in the long term, a lot of people are going to die, and a lot of innocent people are going to be killed, murdered, and executed in a way that we haven`t seen since the end of World War II.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, I`m stuck in these filtration camps.\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It feels like a -- we know what it echoes.\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This chain -- look, when there were starving Kosovoans basically being detained, it changed our calculus in the mid-90s in this country and \uc2 NATO\uc2 . If -- what if -- what Russia -- if they`re doing what it appears they`re doing with Ukrainians and taking them to camps, should this change our calculus?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Well, it may change the charges that were brought against them. I don`t know that will change the decision by President Biden to say, we`re not going to initiate or in any way contribute to initiating World War III. But I think what it does point out to the rest of the world is that what we`re seeing is not the de-Nazification of Ukraine, but the Nazification of the Russians. What Putin is doing is very consistent with what the Nazis were doing.\par \par \uc2 And if you look just outside of Kharkiv, there is a -- there was a ravine there, Babi Yar, and a very famous Russian poet wrote a poem about Babi Yar, there was a trench that they dug out there where there was an existing ravine that the Nazis killed, they firstly, they had the people stripped naked, then they shot them, dumped them in a mass grave. 100,000 people were dumped in that grave. 33,000 of them are Jewish -- European Jewish that were in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is similar to what the Nazis were doing and yet he`s using the language of that de-Nazification in order to deflect from what he`s doing. So the real Nazis here are President Putin and his soldiers. And that has to be taken into account when the trials finally come and they will come. They will be charged for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, so how do you negotiate? How do we end a war with Putin? And we need to end these atrocities and I`ve admired how President Zelenskyy has said, my personal feelings I have to -- I have to suspend because I`m the president of the people and this wars got to stop. But we - - you know, he`s committing war crimes right in our face.\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This man cannot be allowed back into the world order under any circumstance, can he?\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : I don`t think he`ll ever be accepted back in the world order. I don`t think he`d be accepted in any form in which that has the legitimacy to say that he should be there. I think he`s going to be regarded as having engaged in butchery and it`s evident every day.\par \par \uc2 And if the Russians were really serious -- I mean, they have -- they have increased the level of lying to the pathological level. The fact that they can have an ambassador to the United Nations say this is all a myth, this is all it`s fake, these are the Ukrainians shooting themselves in the back of the head, this is all kind of fake news, I mean, that`s pathological in terms of the capacity to lie to the Russian people and to the world community. So I don`t see he`ll ever be able to be accepted as anything other than pariah status.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as you said that we`re watching the Nazification of the Russians here, at least the Russian government. And I hope that -- I hope the Russian public is finding some way to get some of these facts. Former ambassador \uc2 William Cohen\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 COHEN\uc2 : Eventually, they`ll know it first.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, and there`ll be ashamed. \uc2 Bill Cohen\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Good to be here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still to come, President Zelenskyy`s latest call for international help, we`re going to talk to his former press secretary. Also, if it`s Tuesday, voters are voting somewhere, we`re going to dig into the new law in one key swing state that is making voting more complicated today as unfounded claims of fraud are more likely heart-changing laws for no reason. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. You heard Courtney Kube mention satellite images that have been informing the White House and the Pentagon. Some of them like what we`re about to show you are very graphic. According to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis, these images from U.S. intelligence contractor, \uc2 Maxar\uc2 , show bodies lying in the open streets of Bucha, a suburb of Kyiv before Russian troops left town.\par \par \uc2 They appear to corroborate recent social media posts and videos revealing bodies in the streets left in the open for weeks. You`ve heard Richard`s reporting firsthand accounts that he`s got. And they directly contradict Russia`s claims that troops were withdrawn from the area before war crimes were reported there.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the former press secretary to President Zelenskyy, Iuliia Mendel, who`s been essentially a journalist these days, reporting on what she`s hearing and seeing in her country. Iuliia Mendel, thank you for joining me. Let me start with what -- look we`ve seen -- you`ve probably -- I know you`ve seen some of these photos the government is released. What more can you add to the atrocities that the Russians committed in Bucha and elsewhere?\par \par \uc2 IULIIA MENDEL, FORMER PRESS SECRETARY TO PRESIDENT ZELENSKYY: Oh, thank you, Chuck, for having me. First of all, let me say that in the first day of this revelation, horrible revelation, the Ukrainian government picked up 410 bodies of civilians and took them for forensic study to have proof of war crimes for later, but this is already the number that is being counted in hundreds.\par \par \uc2 And Ukrainian government has recommended 1200 war crimes committed in the Kyiv region only. We are really scared here, everyone is scared to understand what`s going to be revealed after such large cities as Mariupol or Kharkiv will show us the consequences of a war because for you to understand, Bucha is a home for around 30,000 people, as well as Borodyanka, Hostomel, and all ours the other cities that you`ve seen -- towns that you`ve seen in Kyiv region. And Mariupol was a home to 400,000 people as well as Kharkiv had around 1 million inhabitants. So it`s really very painful to imagine what the future is going to bring to us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just -- you know, we don`t know some of the things we haven`t seen, right. We don`t know what we don`t know yet in places like Mariupol or in the Donbas region. Let me ask you this, we heard at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . these so-called filtration camps. We hear story after story of Ukrainians essentially being taken away by Russians, those that aren`t killed. Tell me what have you heard on this? What can you add to these stories?\par \par \uc2 MENDEL: Yes. First of all, we have the capacities to see here in Russia, what Russia spreads to us and to see the Ukrainian side. But of course in Ukraine we have the connections. We talk to people in every region. And I`m myself from southern region and I know a lot of people from the Donbas, which is in the east. And of course it is horrible because people want aid. They can`t -- for weeks being bombed without heat, without food, without water.\par \par \uc2 And then they got the chance to go out and everything they saw were Russian soldiers, and Russian soldiers that -- nobody needs you, everybody e forgot about you, Ukraine doesn`t need you anymore, so this is your last chance to get out of the city. And they provided some kinds of buses or transport and then take these people out of Ukraine to Russian territory. And then they check the passports, documents.\par \par \uc2 They check if these people have someone from Ukrainian army, you know, some relatives from officials. Someone who would be for Ukraine, who have pro- Ukrainian position. And the worst that I`ve heard, they even check the body. If Ukrainians have some kind of tattoo that would represent some national symbol, you know, or something like that. It`s very scary. Right now in occupied territories, where, for instance, there is no that level of bombing like in Mariupol, they also go from home to home.\par \par \uc2 They have the list of activists and they try to rob their homes. And they catch the activists and they also go and check the passports so that these people belong to this home, they try to get out if there is someone who is not from this region. So it`s pretty scary everywhere where Russians commit the war affairs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. President Zelenskyy said he has this -- he may have personal conviction or feelings that are different than the president of Ukraine has to have in a moment like this, dealing with, having to, whether he likes it or not, perhaps having to negotiate with Putin just to end the hot war, if you will.\par \par \uc2 But when you see these atrocities, I`ve got to think it makes it harder for anybody to accept. And yet I assume the average Ukrainian wants this war to stop. Do you expect to be able to live next door to Putin for the foreseeable future?\par \par \uc2 MENDEL: We`ve been living next to him for years, and we understand that we are having a large border with Russia. And I understand that President Zelenskyy has a lot of personal attitude to this, as every Ukrainian does, and he is very emotional. It is clear from his speeches. He is very open and he wants the world to see all these atrocities and war crimes that are happening.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, he understands that he has the responsibility to stop this war, and he understands that Putin is the only person who can withdraw Russian troops from Ukrainian territory. And that`s why he continues to fight for this ability to have a dialect with Putin, and there`s why he encouraging these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 This is a very civilized approach, though it`s very difficult on a personal level to move with negotiations when you see the death around.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. When one side is being civilized and the other isn`t, it`s very hard sometimes to find any common ground.\par \par \uc2 Julia Mendel, who I said former press secretary to President Zelenskyy, now in the role of journalist here for us. Julia Mendel, thank you for sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 MENDEL: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still to come, as you can see in the box in the corner of your screen, we`re keeping a close eye on the White House. We`re going to hear from former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . He`ll be appearing with the current president, his former running mate, at any moment at a health care event.\par \par \uc2 The first time we`ll be seeing the two together here since Biden was inaugurated and the last time they got together on this date talking about health care the first time when it was President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Vice President Biden, you`ll recall the vice president had some colorful words for how big of a deal it was.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:28]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`ve got a piece of "Meet the Midterm" news for you today. Michigan Republican Congressman \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2  announced that he will not be seeking re-election and he`ll instead retire after 18 terms in office. Redistricting in Michigan meant that Upton would have been squaring off in a primary against fellow Republican Bill Huizenga.\par \par \uc2 He actually been on the air with some TV ads recently, but Upton of course is one of the 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach former President Trump in the wake of the January 6th insurrection at the Capitol. And with his announcement, that means four of the 10 will not be seeking re- election. The former president put out a gloating statement saying, in part, four down and six to go.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, just a few days ago, my colleague Vaughn Hillyard sat down with Upton to talk about his future in Congress, how the target former President Trump put on his back weighed on his decision to seek reelection or not. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Is Trump a factor in your decision whether to potentially not run for Congress again?\par \par \uc2 REP. FRED UPTON (R-MI): It is a little bit in that some of the folks here are so beholden to Trump that they don`t accept those of us that are willing to stand up. Number of people that have come to me, colleagues, saying, Fred, you can`t leave because then Trump might think that he pushed you out, you know, it`s a win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2 , he is a legislator`s legislator. This was a guy who went to Congress, whatever you think of most members of Congress, he was one of those that actually wanted to get stuff done.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Coming up, if it`s Tuesday, voters are voting somewhere, and that somewhere is Milwaukee, where a new law is complicating voting today. We`re going to explain after the break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it`s Tuesday, somebody is voting somewhere and today one of those somewhere, because there`s a few places voting today. But the place we`re focused on is Wisconsin where ballots are being cast in municipal elections including a special mayoral contest in Milwaukee after longtime Mayor Tom Barrett resigned to become the U.S. ambassador to Luxemburg.\par \par \uc2 Today`s elections have us taking a look at how voters are voting in Wisconsin after a state judge recently declared unmonitored ballot drop boxes illegal in today`s election. Whether that ruling will stand for federal elections this year is pending a ruling from the state`s Supreme Court, but at least today, Wisconsin voters will not be able to place their ballots in unmonitored drop boxes as they have long been accustomed to doing.\par \par \uc2 When you put them in unmonitored, regular old mailboxes. The ruling also specifies that a voter must personally deliver or mail his or her own absentee ballot, which is making it nearly impossible for some people to vote.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille Brewster is on the ground for us in Milwaukee with the story.\par \par \uc2 And Shaq, I have a few questions here and I know probably more than we have time to answer, but, you know, is it being enforced? I guess that`s the number one question I have. And the second is, are people not able to vote?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It depends where you are. That`s the answer to the first question. Here in Milwaukee, they`ve gotten a workaround. They`re saying that the law doesn`t require election clerks to confirm that a person is returning their own ballots. So they`re just not doing that. But I talked to some voters in other areas of the state that said they went to their clerk and said, hey, I have my wife`s ballot, can I submit this? My wife is -- she has foot reconstructive surgery, not able to leave. Can I mail her ballot or can I hand that to the clerk? And they said they were denied from doing so.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 And that is the consequence of this ruling that a circuit judge made back in January. You mentioned the Supreme Court here in the state is going to review that, but you have disabled voters who want to participate in this election here now saying that they`re forced to make a moral dilemma, whether or not -- or they`re facing a moral dilemma whether or not they vote and participate in this election or whether they ignore or just go ahead not participate.\par \par \uc2 I want you to listen to my conversation with Martha Chambers. She was paralyzed from her neck down about 27 years ago. And listen to how she put it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA CHAMBERS, MILWAUKEE VOTER: I should be able to vote. It`s my right to vote just like anyone else. And this particular ban goes against my ability to vote by saying that someone has to physically put their ballot in a mailbox or hand it to someone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: She told me that she just doesn`t think the judge was considering people like her when he made his ruling.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, I also talked to the person in the group who led that lawsuit that ultimately led to these changes, the elimination of drop boxes statewide. And the intent behind the law he says is that they wanted to avoid partisan actors, partisan groups from being able to collect absentee ballots and then submit them whenever they wanted to. Ballot harvesting as it`s called in some areas, but he said he admitted for cases like Martha there may be future litigation or future legislation needed to help correct some of the errors of this circuit court ruling.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I mean, Shaq, the American Disabilities Act, it`s very possible this is in violation of it, pure and simple.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: That`s exactly what the director of the Wisconsin, or excuse me, Milwaukee Elections Commission told me. And that is why she has this workaround where she says that her clerks are just not going to check because she`d rather be in violation of, you know, one judge`s interpretation of election law, a new interpretation, than being in violation of the American Disabilities Act and also the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 So it`s putting clerks in a pretty precarious situation. But you know the national conversation, Chuck. People are going to see folks like Martha and say, oh, she`s voting illegally, she`s not doing what she`s supposed to do when she physically doesn`t have a way to hand her ballot to a clerk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the whole goal is to -- it appears is to shrink turnout, particularly in a place like Milwaukee and that is something I`ve heard today about that look, they`re electing a new mayor, the new voting rules at the same time. We`re going to have, what, like 8 percent of the city actually deciding who the new mayor is? I mean, it feels like -- plus the timing of it. Not everybody is familiar with this timing. I mean, it appears to have had some impact on turnout, no?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes. And you look back, the primary for this election was back in February. If February drop boxes were still there, the state Supreme Court led -- they essentially said that circuit court ruling was on hold for that election. But for the actual general election, that is when the drop boxes are no longer there. So ways that voters were able to participate just two months ago, that`s not the case right now. You`ll see what happens with the August primary and the November election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: For the same election?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: So a lot of confusion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So they`ve changed the rules --\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: The same election, the primary for this election, had different rules.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. That`s supposed to be illegal, too. That could -- I mean, this could complicate this election, if it`s a close election, somebody could essentially say you can`t change the rules in the middle of an election like this. It could very well be a grounds for challenging the election results, Shaq.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes. And I mean, that goes to the confusion. The director of the Milwaukee Election Commission just said each clerk is just put in a tough position. They have guidance. She mentioned that she`s getting letters from the Republican Party advising her on how her workarounds might not comply with law or at least suggesting that, but she`s saying she is doing what she believes is right.\par \par \uc2 But you have different clerks in different areas doing different things. And I mean, voters in other parts of the state don`t have that same benefit as you`re seeing here in Milwaukee, especially when, you know -- I spoke to a disabilities rights activist, and she made the point that this is a protected class that any one of us can join at any time. I could have gotten in a car accident on the way here and didn`t have the ability to vote. And that is why it`s such a problem and that`s why it`s going to be looked into in just about a week or so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, congratulations, Wisconsin. You`ve invited a bunch of litigation and have only made lawyers money.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille Brewster, on the ground for us in Milwaukee. Shaq, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Ali Vitali. We are -- she`s covering the White House event. The vice president is getting ready to introduce the former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And Ali, let`s get to what this is about. Are we really to believe this executive order that President Biden is going to sign actually is a fix or is this a dressed-up press release?\par \par \uc2 [13:50:09]\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, they`re touting it as one, to fix the family glitch is what they`re calling it. So effectively we`re talking about family members of folks who may have individual coverage that`s affordable through their employer, but their families then cost them all of that extra money when they`re put on to that plan. So this is for folks whose health care cost them more than 10 percent of their income. The White House estimates that this impacts millions of people and that by fixing this glitch they can put roughly 200,000 uninsured Americans onto insurance.\par \par \uc2 But of course, Chuck, this is a moment where they`re trying to tout the \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden administration, the successes of health care at a point when in our NBC News poll we know Americans are looking at their cost of living. They are looking at the economy. It`s a time of inflation. This is one of the things where this White House and the Democratic Party writ large are trying to remind people that they want to be seen as the folks who are bringing down household costs.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen this White House try to take steps to alleviate prices at the gas pump overall with inflation and, of course, this is just another reminder of that as they try to set themselves up for a midterm that looks like it`s going to be bruising but they try to make it as best they can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, they`re only doing this because they can`t get the fix through Congress, correct?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. That`s exactly right. This is one of the ways that they can do it without having to go through Congress. But that being said there`s more that they are trying to put pressure on Congress to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: You heard \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  there just a few minutes ago saying that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Vitali, on the beat for us at the White House. Ali, thanks very much. Let`s dip in here as the vice president.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Please welcome President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you very much. Thank you, everybody. Have a seat. Have a seat. Thank you. Vice President Biden -- Vice President --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: That was a joke.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: That was all set up.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: My president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Vice President Harris --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Our dear friend, Madam Speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: All the members of Congress who are in attendance today, members of the Cabinet, it is good to be back in the White House. It`s been a while. I confess I heard some changes had been made by the current president since I was last here. Apparently Secret Service agents have to wear aviator glasses now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: The Navy mess has been replaced by a Baskin Robbins.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: And there`s a cat running around.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Which I guarantee you Bo and Sonny would have been very unhappy about. But coming back, even if I have to wear a tie, which I very rarely do these days, gives me a chance to visit with some of the incredible people who serve this White House and who serve this country every single day. A lot of times out of the limelight they make this government function and they help people in ways big and small.\par \par \uc2 And from the outside sometimes people don`t understand just how grueling this is and how many sacrifices people make because those of us who are in front of the cameras oftentimes get the credit. But it`s a lot of people who are devoted day in and day out to making this country better that matter. And a lot of them are represented here. And that`s not just in the West Wing, by the way. It`s also in the residence.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people who looked after our families that I will always be grateful to. So it`s wonderful to be back to say thank you to all of you. But, most of all, coming back here gives me a chance to say thank you and spend some time with an extraordinary friend and partner who was by my side for eight years. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I did a lot together.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:16]\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: We helped save the global economy, made record investments in clean energy. We put guardrails on our financial system. We helped turn the auto industry around, repealed Don`t Ask, Don`t Tell. But nothing made me prouder than providing better health care and more protection to people across this country. So --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: So when President Biden said he was not going to just celebrate the ACA but also announce actions that would make it even better, I had to show up. I think it`s been well documented just how difficult it was to pass the ACA. There --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: You can get a lot of testimony here in case folks haven`t heard. As a country we have been talking about reforming health care for 100 years. Unlike almost every other advanced economy on earth we didn`t have a system that guaranteed access to health care for all of its citizens. Millions of people didn`t have health insurance often because their employers didn`t provide it or because it was too expensive.\par \par \uc2 But despite the fact that our health care system didn`t work well, it was hard to change. Health care represents about one-fifth of our economy. That`s trillions of dollars that are involved. So there were a lot of different economic interests that were vying to maintain the status quo. And because the majority of Americans did have health care, some people naturally worried that they`d lose what they had.\par \par \uc2 The media was skeptical of past failures. There was a lot of misinformation, to say the least, flying around. And it`s fair to say that most Republicans showed little interest in working with us to get anything done. That`s fair to say. But despite great odds, Joe and I were determined because we had met too many people on the campaign trail who shared their stories. And our own families had been touched by illness.\par \par \uc2 And as I said to our dear friend, Harry Reid, who is missed -- I wish he was here today because he took great pride in what we did -- I intended to get health care passed even if it cost me re-election, which for a while looked like it might.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: But for all of us, for Joe, for Harry, for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , for others, the ACA was an example of why you run for office in the first place, why all of you sign up for doing jobs that pay less than you could make some place else. Why you`re away from home sometimes and you miss some soccer practices or some dance recitals because we don`t -- we`re not supposed to do this just to occupy a seat or to hang on to power.\par \par \uc2 We`re supposed to do this because it`s making a difference in the lives of the people who sent us here. And because of so many people, including a lot of people who are here today, made enormous sacrifices because members of Congress took courageous votes, including some who knew that their vote would likely cost them their seat. Because of the incredible leadership of Nancy and Harry, we got the ACA across the finish line together.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: And the night we passed the ACA, I`ve said it before, it was a high point of my time here because it reminded me and it reminded us of what was possible. But, of course, our work was not finished.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220406ei4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donell for April 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Jonathan Capehart, Ali Arouzi, Michael McFaul, Glenn  Kirschner \par \uc2 7,236 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: All those cats has relocated to the top of the refrigerator, whether this will be a Bavaria, the dogs forever home, or whether he`s reunited with his original people, for now, this is a happy ending for everyone except possibly the cat.\par \par \uc2 That does for us tonight. I will see you again in the morning from here from Lviv on my show Velshi. Celebrity turned activist Jose Andres will join me from Kyiv. That`s tomorrow morning at 8 a.m.\par \par \uc2 And now it`s time for THE LAST WORD. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  is in for Lawrence tonight. Good evening, my friend.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ali. Thanks very much. And I`m just going to say, I like dogs, but I`m team cat. Just putting it out there. Stay safe, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, noted, my friend. Have a great show tonight.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces are ramping up pressure on Russia`s struggling military, as the humanitarian crisis gets worse in Ukraine by the day. Today, a Russian blockade prevented the Red Cross from delivering aid and facilitating a mass evacuation of civilians trapped in Mariupol. They were forced to turn back and to try again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Red Cross called the situation quote, "horrendous and deteriorating." Peace talks between Ukraine and Russia resume virtually today. Russia`s foreign minister said some progress has been made, but added, talks will quote, "inevitably have to continue."\par \par \uc2 There are more air and missile strike near Ukraine`s capital today, despite Russia`s claims of deescalating its attacks there. Ukrainian forces continue to fight back, reclaiming several villages in the area, in areas surrounding Kyiv, and Chernihiv. Kyiv`s mayor is warning the situation is still very dangerous, but says he is surprised by citizens who are willing to fight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI KLITSCHKO\uc2 , MAYOR, KYIV, UKRAINE: I tried every day, I go to every district of our city. I stop by (Inaudible) post and civil defense. I am very surprised the people with very peaceful profession. For example, musicians, actors, doctors, right now they stay with the uniform. They never expect to take the weapons in their hands. But right now, they want to defend his city.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Meanwhile, inside Russia, a regional governor claims two Ukrainian helicopters attacked this oil depot over 20 miles from the Ukrainian border. Ukraine`s defense ministry is refusing to confirm or deny involvement. If confirmed, this would be the first time Ukraine executed an attack on Russian soil.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered the drafting of 134,500 new conscripts into the army. Russia claims that they won`t be sent to Ukraine. In a new video tonight, President Zelenskyy warned those drafted in Russia against joining the war. Speaking in Russian, he said, quote, "warn every such conscript, their parents, we do not need new dead people here. Protect your children so that they would not become the villains. Do not let them join the army. Do whatever you can to keep them alive at home."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, NBC News correspondent, Ali Arouzi in Lviv. Ali, what`s the latest on the ground tonight?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Jonathan. Well, starting with that helicopter attack, the Russians are saying that the two Ukrainian helicopters attacked an oil depot just across the border. The Ukrainians haven`t confirmed or denied it. But if they did, it`s fairly significant. It will be the first time in this war that the Ukrainians penetrated Russian airspace and launched an attack over there. And it would be in a brazen attack.\par \par \uc2 It was done in early hours of the morning, under the cover of darkness. The Ukrainians would have found it very low altitude, just above tree level, hitting that oil depot and then flying back into Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also significant because it would hit an oil depot that the Russians are using to send fuel to the troops on the other side of the border. And it`s a big morale boost for the Ukrainians. It shows that their air force is functioning and capable of carrying out this sort of attacks. But the Russians said that that attack might complicate further peace talks.\par \par \uc2 But further down south, the news isn`t so good. There is a cascading disaster still in Mariupol. There was meant to be a big humanitarian rescue operation there guided by the Red Cross. They were going to take a convoy of buses and humanitarian aid into the besieged city of Mariupol, where around 170,000 people are hemmed in by the Russians. That didn`t happen. No aid got in. No civilians got out.\par \par \uc2 [22:04:57]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ali Arouzi, thank you very much. And please, stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Officials estimate more than 10 million Ukrainians have been displaced from their homes since Vladimir Putin`s war began. Of those who have been displaced, four million people, half of them children, now have left Ukraine. Many did so by traveling through the western city of Lviv.\par \par \uc2 Keith Gessen was in Lviv`s train station, as many of these Ukrainians arrived. He writes in the New Yorker, quote, "some of the people who got off the train knew where they wanted to go and how to get there. Most did not. There were volunteers inside the station and outside of it, but there were too many people coming off the trains. The scene was chaotic. Many people wanted to get to Poland, others to Germany, Spain, Greece. Some wanted to stay in Lviv, at least for a little while. Others hadn`t thought that far ahead. These people had not lost their rights, but they had lost their homes. And they were adrift now in a sea of deprivation, cold, fatigue, confusion, despair."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Keith Gessen, contributor for The New Yorker. And then assistant journalism professor at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Keith, what was it like being in that train station as people were literally fleeing for their lives?\par \par \uc2 KEITH GESSEN, CONTRIBUTOR, THE NEW YORKER: It was, it was -- it was harrowing. I mean, it was, you know, every -- about every hour, a train would pull up, and just people would still out of it. And it was -- it was women and children. The men stay home. The men are not allowed to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 So just seeing, you know, whenever a train comes in, people going out, again most of them as you read at the top, you know, they don`t have time to make a plan about where to go about -- you know, where to stay. And where they would end up.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, a lot of them were in real distress, kind of from a journalistic perspective, I try to talk to the people who, you know, looked like they are -- they were in a place where they can talk. And just, you know, these people, this was about, it`s about a week ago, eight or nine days ago, so a lot of these people who would actually try to stay.\par \par \uc2 These were not people who have left right away. They hid in basements, in subway stations. They have really tried to kind of wait it out. And you know, for most of them, there came a point where they just, you know, either the Ukrainian army came and said, look, you must leave now. Or they just reached a conclusion that they just couldn`t take it anymore and they were going to, you know, leave their homes behind.\par \par \uc2 There were people there who really did not want to leave because they have already been evacuated. There was a woman who said, you know, I really hang on, as long as I could, because I was evacuated from Chernobyl in 1986. And I didn`t want to go through that again.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, you kind of learn a lot of history of the country just by -- just by being there and talking to people. But it was, yes, it was really hard.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You mentioned that you learned a lot about the history of the country by being there and talking to the people. But one has the reporting revealed about the Ukrainian spirit? What do people say about how they feel about Russia?\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: People were -- people don`t feel good about Russia right now. I mean, you know, it`s it`s a catastrophe. It`s a catastrophe for Ukraine. It`s a catastrophe for Russia. You know, the kind of irony or paradox of it is that these people are coming to Lviv, which is the most Ukrainian speaking city in the world, it`s a city where Ukrainian culture has deep roots. It`s a city where Ukrainian culture took refuge, you know, when it was being oppressed in as far as Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, but the people fleeing, a lot of them were free fleeing from the east. They were -- a lot of them are Russian speakers. So, these are the people who ostensibly, certainly in Russian propaganda, are the people who are sympathetic to Russia, who want close relations with Russia. And these were the people who were fleeing Russian bombardment and you know, Russian devastation. So, they were not doing very positive about Russia for sure.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Keith Gessen, thank you very much for being here tonight. And for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 GESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [22:09:57]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now is Michael McFaul, who served as the U.S. Ambassador to Russia from 2012 to 2014. He is an MSNBC international affairs analyst. Ambassador McFaul, Ukraine didn`t comment on that attack in Russia. But how significant of a development would that be, if Ukraine shows it can reach Russian soil?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Well, whenever I hear a senior government official say, I can neither confirm nor deny, that sounds like they`re actually confirming. Because otherwise, they would have denied it. So, it sounds like they did complete this operation. It sounds like they wanted to demonstrate that they have that capability.\par \par \uc2 And I think that they wanted to demonstrate that a war is war, and if you`re going to fuel your planes, and you`re going to fuel your weapons, your trucks across the border from Kharkiv, we`re going to strike you where you are fueling your weapons and your trucks and your automobiles that are striking us.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Any concern, Ambassador, that the Russians will view this attack on Russian soil, by Ukraine and say, OK, war is war. But where those weapons come from. And of those weapons came from the west, use that as a reason to lob bombs into Poland, for instance?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, first, those weapons didn`t come from us from the reporting. So that`s good news. The second, I think that`s highly, highly unlikely. We keep hearing about, if you do that, if you do that, there`s going to be escalation.\par \par \uc2 Well, let`s define escalation. Russia, the Russian armed forces are having an incredibly difficult time fighting against an army that is not as well equipped as it should be. And has nearly, not nearly the capacity of the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, let alone the most powerful military in the world, the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 So, in this moment right now, I think it`s highly unlikely that Putin would say, I want to expand this war. I want to get \uc2 NATO\uc2  involved. And I want to get the United States of America involved. I don`t think that`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that what they`re doing is they keep using those words, they keep saying those things, precisely, to make the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, including the United States worried about arming the Ukrainians. And I think we should be strong here, and just do what we said we`re going to do, continue to arm them.\par \par \uc2 I applaud what the Pentagon just announced a few minutes ago, $300 million more in military assistance. The only way there will be peace in Ukraine is if there is a military stalemate. And until there is not a military stalemate, Putin will continue to attack. And therefore, if you want peace, you got to get help bring about that stalemate.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: One more question for you, Ambassador McFaul, speaking of the Pentagon, the Pentagon canceled a missile test launch over concerns Putin would view it as escalatory. Was this the right call? What should the U.S. be doing right now? You talk partially about that, to defeat Russia without escalating the situation. But talk about the cancellation of that missile, that missile test.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: I think that`s fine. You know, I used to work at the White House for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , we used to do that from time to time, in terms of when there were tense moments. And it`s about misperceptions that we are worried about. So, I think that`s a prudent move.\par \par \uc2 We do not want to escalate. We do not want to get into a war with Russia. I completely 100 percent, support the president when he says we`re not going to enforce a no-fly zone, because that`s tantamount to declaring war. But anything short of that, I think we need to be doing as much as we can.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: One more question for you, Ambassador McFaul. Given what we learned this week about Putin being misinformed by his advisors, and now reporting that Putin is conscripting 100 plus thousand new conscripts, what does this tell you about how things, how things might get worse for Putin`s war?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, I`m a little skeptical about that reporting that he was misinformed. You know, we have no way to check our intelligence community. But I wonder about that. And you know, maybe that some disinformation game we`re playing about him, and maybe he wants to signal that that he`s upset with his generals.\par \par \uc2 And most certainly, the reporting I read from Russian sources that he`s very upset with the way the war is going. But I think you raise a very good point. The draft just happened. You know. This is normal standard operating procedure. Russia, April 1st, it`s one of those days. And I think it has been a tremendous success by the Ukrainian military so far.\par \par \uc2 And at this same time, Russia has tremendous military capacity still left. And that makes me worried, and frankly, you know, concerned that this was going to go on for a long time.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Former Ambassador, Michael McFaul, thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 [22:14:57]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, today brought another blockbuster job support and a milestone for the Biden/Harris administration. In just two years, nearly all jobs lost in the pandemic are back.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the good news about wage increases. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Not one Republican voted for the American rescue plan, which brought $1,400 checks to people when they needed it most. When we had millions of people out of work through no fault of their own.\par \par \uc2 Not one voted when we were extending the child tax credit, and working parents, now what that meant and what it meant in terms of helping them get through the days and the month and satisfy their basic responsibilities to parent their children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:19:54]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in an interview today with my colleague, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 . The vice president had more to say about the accomplishments of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: When we look at what we achieved in terms of putting in place a system around getting vaccines for people, so now over 200 -- I think it`s in 15 million people have been vaccinated in our country. And as result, we`ve been able to reopen our schools, 99 percent of them are reopened. Businesses are reopening.\par \par \uc2 These are the achievements that were made possible in spite of the fact that one Republican in so many of these policies voted. So, I`m not going to get caught up in kind of an internal firing squad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Today, the Biden ministration is also celebrating what President Biden is calling an historic discovery. The United States added 431,000 new jobs in March, with the unemployment rate falling to 3.6 percent. A pandemic-era low. And 93 percent of the 22 million jobs lost at the start of the pandemic, they`ve been recovered.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Heather Long, editorial writer and columnist focus on the economy for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Heather, great to see you again at this hour. What jumped out to you from today`s jobs report?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER LONG, EDITORIAL WRITER AND COLUMNIST, THE WASHINGTON POST: This was undeniably good news. I don`t care if you`re Republican, Democratic, independent, we have a really strong job market. The gains were across the board, in the retail, in the restaurant sector, in manufacturing. Even in things like childcare, that I was really worried about at the end of last year. We saw some employment coming back there. Even in dry cleaning, and in healthcare, in K through 12 education, in performing arts.\par \par \uc2 So, the other thing that really jumped out at me, it was another -- another decline for the Black unemployment rate. So, we are seeing widespread gains going, not just to the top, there was also big gains for women in March. A lot of us were very worried about the she session were women going to be able to return to the workforce after this pandemic, and we`re starting to see a lot of encouraging signs.\par \par \uc2 The last time that looks really strong is wages continue to tick up. The average hourly earnings with pay is up 5.6 percent in the last year. Now that obviously comes with a big asterisk, because inflation is up 7.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, we are, people are still falling behind overall, although the biggest wage gains are coming from leisure and hospitality workers. They have seen nearly a 15 percent wage gain. So, they are outpacing inflation. These are workers who were paid less than $15 an hour before the pandemic. Now, their average pay is 1,750. That`s still not great, but it`s really moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, speaking of inflation, you know, as you said, wages are up 5.6 percent, but inflation is at 7.9 percent. So, can we expect to see inflation cool off anytime soon?\par \par \uc2 LONG: I wish I had good news there. I don`t think we`re going to see inflation cool off anytime soon. Obviously, we continue to see gas prices and energy prices remaining high. We continue to see a lot of problems getting various food products to grocery stores and continue to see a lot of supply chain glitches that just aren`t going away.\par \par \uc2 Economist and business leaders kept saying, it will be better by Christmas, it will be better by Easter, you know. Now we`re saying it will be better maybe by this fall. And it just hasn`t happened yet. I am very worried that we could see potentially a 10 percent inflation rate, at some point this spring.\par \par \uc2 So, Americans are definitely worried about this, and this is why it reflected in the poll numbers. I think the good news, that we`re seeing today, is that the job market remains strong. People are able to get a job. This is one of the best times ever to go ask for a raise. You are likely to get one right now. And that`s encouraging, and it certainly does not look like a recession is immediately on the horizon, which some people were worried about earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: We`ve got less in a couple minutes left, but I`m just wondering, Heather, do you know, does the Biden administration have a strategy to deal with the fears about inflation?\par \par \uc2 LONG: Well, they`re certainly trying a lot. And we saw this week, they`re doing the biggest ever released from that Strategic Petroleum Reserve to try and help bring down gas prices, or at least prevent them from going even higher than where they are now. They`re sitting in about $4.20 a gallon per average.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re starting -- they`re doing pretty much everything they can. Unfortunately, what really needs to happen next is for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise those interest rates to make it more expensive to get the mortgage, to get a car loan, to get a business loan.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 And you know, that`s starting to hurt people. We`re starting to see a cooling off in the housing market, as those interest rates rise.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right.\par \par \uc2 LONG: And unfortunately, in the past, when those in interest rates rise a lot, sometimes it tips us into a recession. So, it`s a very tricky path in the next two months.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Heather Long of the Washington Post, thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 LONG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has more power on the court than you probably realize. And it could do lasting and outsized damage to our democracy if he does not recuse on cases involving the attempts to illegally overturn the 2020 election results that his wife participated in. We`ll talk about that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:30:22]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: On Monday, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  will vote to advance Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson`s Supreme Court nomination. A full Senate vote is expected shortly thereafter with Democrats looking to wrap up her nomination, before a two-week Senate recess begins on April 11th.\par \par \uc2 The soon-to-be Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , will be joining the court, not only facing its biggest scandal ever courtesy of Virginia Thomas but also shifting power dynamics in the conservative wing of the court.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is not just any Supreme Court Justice. He`s the senior associate justice and ranks second behind only Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As my colleague at the "Washington Post", David Von Drehle points out, we could soon be looking at a scenario where it`s Thomas who`s calling the shots among the conservatives. Von Drehle writes, "Given changes to court personnel in recent years, a solid majority of five conservative votes can now be assembled without Roberts. Should that happen, Thomas decides who will write the courts opinion.\par \par \uc2 Suppose the breakfast and the conversation at the Thomas household is half as overheated as Ginni Thomas`s exchanges with Meadows, that`s a lot of loopy nonsense in which to marinate a supposedly impartial justice. The seasoned jurist goes to work with influence over authorship of controversial Supreme Court decisions. Uh-oh."\par \par \uc2 Uh-oh is right.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Michele Bratcher Goodwin. She is a chancellor`s professor of law at the \uc2 University of California Irvine\uc2 . And Glenn Kirschner is a former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Professor Goodwin, I`m starting with you. Last night on this show, Lawrence played a clip from a 2018 interview with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Talking about how it would be impossible to be a Supreme Court justice without Ginni Thomas.\par \par \uc2 I want to play another clip from that same interview, to start off our conversation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: To be able to know that you have done what you`re required to do, that you have been truthful, that you`ve not deceived people, that you can put your head on your pillow and sleep soundly. My grandfather always talked about the sort of peace that you had when you did what was right.\par \par \uc2 And so, there is nothing that the critics can either give me or take away that`s a value to me. They can`t take away sort of what I think about what I do, because they have no input into that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Professor Goodwin, does that sound to you like a man who cares much about the controversy his wife has pressed (ph) the Supreme Court into?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE BRATCHER GOODWIN, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA IRVINE\uc2 : Well to be clear, that`s a statement from a few years ago. But he certainly should be concerned, and so should every member of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  and as well as members of our Congress. And members of the Congress are now deliberating about imposing, setting law that would impose ethics rules to be apply to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is our one branch within law where there are no ethics rules.\par \par \uc2 And lawyers are held to a very high standard of ethics. In fact, before students are able to sit for the bar they have to take an ethics bar exam. The fact that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  justices do not have to abide by a set of ethics rules is really antithetical to justice itself.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court right now has the lowest approval rating by the American public in almost 50 years. And so all of this is disconcerting, especially the content of the texts by Ginni Thomas. And the fact that Justice Thomas could be sitting while there are cases that come about, due to the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 It`s caused a lot of problems, including the fact that most recently, he was the only no vote, when it came to questions of releasing information relevant to the investigation of the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Glenn, here`s how Frank Bruni put the situation in an op-ed for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 He writes, "What a terrifying moment in which the wife of a serving Supreme Court justice unabashedly exploits her insider access, ignores the idea of checks and balances, promotes conspiracy theories and essentially endorses insurrection.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:57]\par \par \uc2 "Her conduct isn`t some passing curiosity. It`s a sign of the times, and it`s a warning to us all."\par \par \uc2 Glenn, what do you think it says that these crazy theories have now essentially made it to the highest court in the land?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, Jonathan, it says that we have a crisis at the Supreme Court. A crisis of legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 And one thing that I think we have learned by Justice Thomas`s failure to recuse, remove himself from a case where it sure looks like he has a conflict. You know, you could be called upon to decide, for example, whether his wife`s own texts, which really are inappropriate at best and incriminating at worst, he would be in a position to decide whether they should be disclosed to the J-6th committee and by extension to the American people, or hidden from the J-6th committee.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is a crisis at the Supreme Court. And it seems like there are a number of fixes that need to be seriously considered. As the professor says, we need a code of conduct. Because the Supreme Court has shown, it can`t be trusted to police itself.\par \par \uc2 So Chief Justice Roberts better take this seriously, because I think confidence in the court is at an all-time low. It continues to erode. So there needs to be a code of conduct. There needs to be an enforcement mechanism to make sure that this situation doesn`t recur and to deter future misconduct by the justices.\par \par \uc2 And then, there needs to be a serious inquiry, a public inquiry about what Justice Thomas did, what he knew, why he failed to recuse. And I`m not saying, let`s rush to impeachment, but I am saying, let`s rush to transparency so the American people can see what went on, and can see that the institution is taking seriously the need to fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well Professor Goodwin, let`s talk more about this transparency that you have both -- you have both mentioned. I mean Chief Justice Roberts just talked about Congress adopting a code of ethics for the Supreme Court. That`s not likely to go over well with the chief justice because here`s what he wrote in his year-end report.\par \par \uc2 "The judiciary`s power to manage its internal affairs insulates courts from inappropriate political influence, and is crucial to preserving public trust in its work as a separate and coequal branch of government.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Roberts is going to do here, particularly with him not being able to force Thomas to do anything he doesn`t want to do?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Well, let`s be clear that the court is not above the law, nor is the court beyond being fallible. It has been, we can see overtime with rulings from the Supreme Court, that biases influence what the court does, whether we`re talking about Dred Scott, Plessy v Ferguson, cases that still stand as some of the worst jurisprudence in the courts history.\par \par \uc2 And Justice Roberts may have been resistant to this, but now with these texts, with the actions of the justice himself, with information that has been revealed about Ginni Thomas, it would be a huge mistake for justice Roberts to ignore this.\par \par \uc2 And I will say one thing really quickly here. Which is that, there have been times in which the justice`s hand has been moved, when he was pressured during the Trump administration saying that these are \uc2 Obama\uc2  judges who are ruling against me.\par \par \uc2 The chief justice came out and was pressured by other federal judges, to say there is no such thing as just \uc2 Obama\uc2  judges, Bush judges, et cetera. We are one unified court.\par \par \uc2 And so, his hand is being pushed, and I believe he probably will respond. We may not see it. It may be behind closed doors. But I imagine this is something that is very bothersome to them.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Michele Bratcher Goodwin, Glenn Kirschner, thank you both very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what Vice President Harris said when asked how she felt watching conservative senators disrespect Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  in her confirmation hearing. And what that has to do with the slap? That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Earlier tonight, we witness a remarkable and hopeful moment watching the first black woman primetime cable news host interview the first black woman vice president about how it felt to watch the confirmation hearing for the first black woman Supreme Court nominee. But - - and you know there has to be a but -- it couldn`t only be about Judge Jackson`s qualifications and performance and ignore the headline making disrespect shown to her by some conservative white senators.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: We all sat and watched the \uc2 Ketanji Brown-Jackson\uc2  hearings, in which she very calmly sat through what`s, I think a lot of -- particularly black women, let`s just be honest, that was brazen disrespect from senators like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , Senators like Tom Cotton, Senators like \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 . What did you think when you watched that hearing?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will tell you, Joy. I experienced great joy when I watched this brilliant, phenomenal black woman jurist be so smart and just cut through the political gamesmanship that they were attempting to incite. And she just was composed, and as far as I`m concerned, was taking a whole lot of people to school.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:56]\par \par \uc2 And I watched that with incredible joy because it was just brilliance being displayed for the entire country to see. And I cannot wait to see -- that will only be matched by the joy that I experience when I see her take the oath to be the next justice on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Vice President Harris is right. There was great joy in watching Judge Jackson, because she did keep her composure and she didn`t allow the Republican attempts to incite her to succeed. She didn`t succumb to the political gamesmanship, as Vice President Harris diplomatically put it.\par \par \uc2 But her response is additional evidence of a phenomenon white people have been watching play out on national television in the last two weeks. Starting with Judge Jackson`s Confirmation hearing and ending on the slap.\par \par \uc2 No, we`re not done talking about it. I wrote about Will Smith walking on stage and slapping Chris Rock at the Oscars for the "Washington Post". And the column opened by reflecting on this hilarious vignette from comedian Wanda Sykes`s 2009 HBO special, "I`m a be me".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WANDA SYKES, COMEDIAN: When I was growing up, my mother, she wouldn`t even let us dance in the car. You know, just sitting in the car, a good song come on the radio -- my mom`s like she would stop the car. Do you want to dance or do you want to ride? Because you aren`t dancing in my car. White people are looking at you.\par \par \uc2 Wait, ha? White people are looking at you. I`m like, oh damn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: White people are looking at you. Sykes goes on to say black folks, we always got to be dignified. Yes, because we know if we screw up, we just set everybody else back a couple of years, right?\par \par \uc2 Among the many reasons to be upset with Will Smith, what I keep hearing is that his violence did damage to us. Us, being African Americans who are mindful that the foibles of one person often and unfairly affect us all.\par \par \uc2 Some African Americans will slam this as the worst of respectability politics. That notion that there is acceptance and safety in speaking and acting in ways that make white people comfortable.\par \par \uc2 We know that`s not true, but respectability is the tax we African-Americans are forced to pay, as we try to live out our versions of the American dream.\par \par \uc2 It is an exorbitant and unfair tax, but it exists, nonetheless. We saw it levied every single time we watched Donald Trump`s and crudeness and said imagine if \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  -- dot, dot, dot. Remember President \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s anger translator was a running gag during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  presidency.\par \par \uc2 He didn`t get to be the first black president elected twice by letting himself be provoked by giving the bigots what they want.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s Judge Jackson, who had to publicly endure a torrent of racist nonsense because she wants to be, and she will be, Justice Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Now, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , Vice President Harris and Judge Jackson are bound by the expectations of American politics, which may be higher than Hollywood, but maybe not.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Will Smith issued this statement. "I have directly responded to the Academy`s disciplinary hearing notice, and I will fully accept any and all consequences for my conduct.\par \par \uc2 My actions at the 94th Academy Awards presentation were shocking, painful and inexcusable. I am resigning from membership in the Academy of Motion Picture, Arts and Sciences, and will accept any further consequences the board deems appropriate.\par \par \uc2 Change takes time and I am committed to doing the work to ensure that I never again allow violence to overtake reason."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a federal judge in Florida has struck down most of the Republican voter suppression law in Florida in a blistering decision. Citing Florida`s quote, "grotesque history of racial discrimination".\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , a voting rights attorney, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:26]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: There is bad news for Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his fellow Republicans trying to suppress the vote in Florida. A federal judge blocked the state from enforcing most of the measures in its voter suppression law. What is even more remarkable is the judge`s blistering critique of the law.\par \par \uc2 Senate bill 90 in what can only be described as a "scorched earth" ruling. Across 288 pages, federal Judge \uc2 Mark Walker\uc2  notes Florida`s grotesque history of racial discrimination. He says the plaintiffs in the case, alleged the law runs roughshod over the right to vote, unnecessarily making voting harder for all eligible Floridians, unduly burdening disabled voters, and intentionally targeting minority voters. All to improve the electoral prospects of the party in power.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 The party in power in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` Florida is, of course, also known as the Republican Party. Judge Walker points out that the court reviewed thousands of pages of evidence, thousands more pages of briefing, and has heard two weeks worth of testimony from 42 witnesses.\par \par \uc2 He then concludes, "This court finds that for the most part, plaintiffs are right", mincing no words. Judge Walker says, "This court recognizes that the right to vote and the Voting Rights Act particularly are under siege."\par \par \uc2 In the final pages of his lengthy ruling, Judge Walker wrote, in Florida, white Floridians outpace black Floridians and almost every socioeconomic metric. In Florida, since the end of the civil war, politicians have attacked the political rights of black citizens. In Florida, though we have come far, the realistic fact is that we still have a long, long way to go.\par \par \uc2 And if that last line sounds familiar, he`s quoting \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. who said in a 1967 interview, "Some of the old optimism was a little superficial. And now, it must be tempered with solid realism. And I think that the realistic fact is that we still have a long, long way to go."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , Democrat of Texas, who`s also a civil rights attorney. Congressman Allred, always great to see. I love your reaction to Judge Walker`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 REP. COLIN ALLRED (D-TX): Yes, well, I think that is the young people say Judge Walker understood the assignment. He understood what was at risk here, and he called out other judges, he called out the Supreme Court. He called out the state of Florida and he said and spoke the truth.\par \par \uc2 And not only did he strike down these provisions, but he also bailed Florida back into preclearance, which is really, really important. Because if that ruling stands, then any changes Florida tries to make will have to be preclear, because Jonathan, as you know, they can try to restore that provision with the overall voting rights act. But there is another way to get into it, and that`s why being bailed in, because of your activities, and judge found activities rose to that level.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Yes, I mean that was a huge part of the ruling. You know, Republican leaders in the Florida legislature blasted the decision. And a Tallahassee Democrat reports, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said he expected an unfavorable ruling from Walker, an appointee of President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , calling it quote, performative partisanship.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, that sounds like the governor is projecting, don`t you think?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: It does. And you know, when I got involved with voting rights, I really didn`t see it as a partisan issue. I never asked somebody, who they`re going to vote for when I was registering them registering them to vote.\par \par \uc2 When the Voting Rights Act was re-authorized in 2006, and I know you`ve discussed in the past. It passed in the Senate, 98 to nothing.\par \par \uc2 You know, my constituent President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  or Republicans. The one who signed it into law -- this is a relatively reasoned thing where it has become just used purely for partisan gain and so openly.\par \par \uc2 So to project this and to say that trying to protect Floridians voting rights, it was a partisan act. I mean obviously we know that`s not true. But you know, I have to say, our democracy is at risk and it`s under attack.\par \par \uc2 We see in Ukraine these brave Ukrainians fighting for their democracy. I`m hopeful that at some point we can understand that we have to reinvest our democracy here at home. And that starts with preventing laws like this from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Do you think this decision, could have some impact on other voter suppression laws?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: You know, I hope so. I hope that other judges will see Judge Walker`s, you know ruling, and follow it. I would love to see a similar ruling in Texas. Obviously, you know, we had an election just now, a primary election, in which 13 percent of our mail-in ballots were rejected -- 13 percent. When the previous election, it was just 1 percent. And that`s because of a change to voting by mail that Texas Republicans, passed specifically just to try and satisfy \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . and so this is happening across the country. And you know, what we`re seeing is that this is happening at a primary election in Texas. And I`m really worried about what we`re going to see in a general election, when we have more lower information voters being involved.\par \par \uc2 So we got to stop this. We have to, you know, protect our democracy, understand that it`s not about helping Democrats. It`s about keeping our systems in place.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right, we`ve got less than a minute. But I got to ask you about this, because the House passed a bill today that would legalize marijuana nationwide, establishing procedures for expunging previous convictions from people`s records and impose a tax on the sale of cannabis products. You voted for the bill Congressman. Real quickly, in 20 seconds, tell us why.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, 37 states, the District of Columbia, medical marijuana. Eight states plus D.C., they don`t use recreational use as legally. We got to bring the federal government into line with that.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:47]\par \par \uc2 Also, we recognize that this is something who`s time has come. It`s a civil rights issue. Drug use is equal across racial groups. So we see only certain groups being prosecuted under it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. I had to ask you about that. But we got to go. Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I am \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 . I`ll see you on Sunday on "THE SUNDAY SHOW".\par \par \uc2 But right now, stay tuned because "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220402ei4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Richard Engel, Raf Sanchez, Maria Teresa Kumar, David  Frum, Betsy Woodruff Swan, Morgan Radford \par \uc2 8,718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:08]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: It's Friday another U.S. intelligence assessment points to growing dismay over the war from the highest levels of the Kremlin. What it means for the response in Washington, State Department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  joins us head.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the day after President Biden announces his wartime plan to combat higher gas prices. Democrats face another brewing political challenge, a potential surge of migrants and asylum seekers at the border.\par \par \uc2 And the president delivers remarks on the state of the economy as the U.S. employers add more than 400,000 jobs for the 11th straight month, a record that goes back to the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I'm Chuck Todd. The U.S. and our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies now say Russia appears to be redeploying its forces continuing this brutal assault on Ukraine. No pulling back as what was promised earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 And while peace talks do continue, either side is expecting a resolution anytime soon. So, right now, there is a renewed focus on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . What he knows, what he's being told and how both of those things could influence his next move. The White House was released additional intelligence again continuing to troll Putin about his military in his relationship to it. According to an \uc2 Intel\uc2  assessment gathered last month, some senior officials within the Russian government disagreed with Putin's decision to invade Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And his military's poor performance has likely amplified the frustrations of those around him. Russia, of course denies U.S. and British reports that the Russian generals are keeping Putin in the dark. The assessment was released the day after the White House put out another assessment suggesting Putin was being misinformed by his advisors about the true, dire situation on the ground.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday, President Biden seemed to downplay the significance of that intelligence, even as his own administration went public with those findings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How badly \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  being misinformed by advisers?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That's an open question. There's a lot of speculation. But he seems to be. I'm not saying this with a certainty. He seems to be self-isolated and there's some indication that he has fired or put under house arrest some of his advisors. But I don't want to put too much stock in at this time because we don't have that much hard evidence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For what it's worth, we know Putin can be informed correctly anytime he wants. And in a sign of how tenuous the White House perceives the situation Ukraine. NBC News was first report today that the Pentagon announced it was canceling and intercontinental ballistic missile test over concerns that Putin would view it as escalatory. That test had already been postponed once, but obviously there's concern about poking him.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Moscow was blaming Ukraine for an airstrike on an oil depot in Russia about 20 miles from the border. Ukrainian officials are not confirming or denying the allegations, NBC News could not independently verify who was responsible. But the idea that Russia is suddenly offended that Ukraine might have targeted something in Russia's soil versus of course, what Russia is doing to that sovereign nation.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes amid renewed efforts to evacuate Ukrainians still stuck in the war zone. The Red Cross says Russia is preventing it from be -- from bringing aid to the besieged city of Mariupol, will try again tomorrow to evacuate civilians, but the Russians aren't allowing it. Spokesperson for the organization says, we're running out of adjectives to describe the horrors that residents of Mariupol people have suffered.\par \par \uc2 The situation is horrendous and deteriorating, I think we're all fearing what the actual carnage is going to look like when we're able to actually see what the Russians have done to the city. More than 4.1 million Ukrainians, by the way, so far have already fled to neighboring countries. That's according to the United Nations. It's nearly 10 percent of the entire population of the country. Millions more had been displaced inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Many from the east, temporary living in the west part of the country. Just a moment I'll speak with the State Department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 . But joining me now is Richard Engel. He's in Kharkiv. And Raf Sanchez is in Hungary, where voters are set to go to the polls this weekend. Among the issues on the ballot in Hungary, the war in Ukraine and Hungary's relationship with Russia. But let me start with Richard Engel in Ukraine for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Richard, what's going on on the ground today and what if anything should we expect from today's round of peace talks?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So, the -- I think the -- what's happening on the battlefield and the peace talks are -- as they always are very much related. Let's start not with the main headline of the day which we've been talking about a lot. The attack inside Russian territory against the fuel depot. But back in Kiev, the mayor of a suburb just on the outskirts of the city announced a short while ago that Russian forces had been driven out of a little area called Bucha.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Now, that might sound very specific, very local to this conflict. But it is a major milestone for the Ukrainian government, for the Ukrainian resistance. You have Kiev, the main capital with its sort of under government control, and it's a government bubble. Then there was an area called Irpin which was right outside the capital. That had been used or the Russian forces were trying to use that suburb to enter the city.\par \par \uc2 First, Russian forces were driven out of Irpin. And now we're told what the mayor is claiming that Russian forces have been driven even further back from the following suburb, which is called Bucha. So, this -- when you -- when you let it all up, it sounds like the Russian forces are taking significant losses around Kiev and the government in Kiev is really establishing a much, much wider bubble.\par \par \uc2 Making the people in the capital feel much -- more comfortable that they are now in very little risk of being surrounded and very little risk that the capital itself could be invaded or taken over, at least at this stage of the operation. Then the new development of the day, which is that strike inside Russia, on an oil depot, which is actually not very far from here, just across the border about 45 miles from where I'm standing right now.\par \par \uc2 And according to the Russian officials and it's not just one Russian official, but multiple Russian officials. There were two Ukrainian helicopters under the -- under the cover of darkness that crossed the border, traveled about 20 miles, attacked this fuel depot from the helicopters and then returned to Ukrainian territory. And they were apparently not shut down. They were not harassed of Dmitry Peskov, Putin spokesman was specifically asked, how is it possible that these helicopters were able to enter Russian airspace and not be harassed, not be shot down return to their home base.\par \par \uc2 And Ukrainian officials are using this classic formulation when you want to confirm something but don't want to acknowledge it that we can neither confirm nor deny. But had they not been involved? And this was something that the Russians were launching themselves as a false flag, they certainly would have been in a position to deny.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard, I mean, I just -- it's just hard to sort of -- are the Russians are just outraged by this? I mean, it's sort of like that's a bit rich.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: I don't think they are outraged. I think if anything, they're a little bit embarrassed. They have not been banging on about it all day. It is not a cover of wall-to-wall coverage on Russian media. You asked -- your initial question was how are the negotiations going? And I said, well, I think what's happening on the battlefield, influences the negotiation. So, there was -- there has been a prisoner exchange that involved dozens of Ukrainian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 So, that's a sign of progress on the battlefield. Dmitry Peskov, he said that he informed \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  about this attack on the oil facility, he said it would harm the negotiations. But there was not a tremendous outcry of opposition from Russian officials, maybe because the message had already been delivered by the Ukrainian military that although they're not confirming it, that they can reach out and touch the Russians inside their own territory. So, the Russians are kind of leaving it be for now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting. Richard Engel in Ukraine for us. As always, Richard, thank you. We move over to Hungary where we have Ralph Sanchez because in the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, this is the first election that we're going to have since the start of this war and the war is having an impact. This was an election. \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , the Prime Minister, I would say two months ago, nobody thought this was going to be that competitive.\par \par \uc2 Lo and behold, there's a lot of -- a lot of thought that this is going to be a long weekend for Mr. Orban. Rob, what have you learned?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck. \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  has historically had a very close relationship with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Probably the closest relationship of any leader in a \uc2 NATO\uc2  country. And that relationship is now a major issue in the election here. Orban was in Moscow meeting Putin just a couple of weeks before the invasion started.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: He has been refusing to allow the direct transfer of \uc2 NATO\uc2  weapons over his border from Hungary into Ukraine. Compare that to the situation in Poland where they are sending anti-tank weapons and all sorts of missiles every day. Orban had a rally here just a couple hours ago, and he's presenting himself as the man who will keep Hungary out of the war next door. He says he doesn't want to see a single Hungarian caught between the Russian hammer and the Ukrainian anvil.\par \par \uc2 But his opponents are hammering him over that relationship with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . We met yesterday with the leader of the opposition, the man who wants to replace Orban as the leader of Hungary come that election on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 [16:10:07]\par \par \uc2 And I asked him, how the war next door is featuring in the election here. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: You've said in this election, it's a choice for Hungary between the west or Putin. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 PETER MARKI ZAY\uc2 , HUNGARY OPPOSITION CANDIDATE: Well, Orban has been serving Putin for 12 years. Now, all of a sudden, everybody realized that Putin is a war criminal who is killing innocent children and pregnant mothers. Shelling hospitals and shooting get nuclear power plants. So, of course, now, it's an alarming stretch to Hungary's safety and security that Putin might actually get a -- might actually become a direct neighbor of Hungary.\par \par \uc2 Orban is still trying to stop Brussels and that's Putin. So, we asked him to stop putting instead of Brussels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: Now, Chuck, Orban is of course, an authoritarian leader. He's been in power here for more than a decade. He has a strong grip on the state institutions here in Hungary. He has control over much of the media. I asked the opposition leader, do you stand a real chance in this election? He said, this is not a fair fight. But we can still win. We can still take back this country. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Raf Sanchez in Hungary for us. Raf, thanks very much. I'm now joined by State Department spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 . Ned, it is good to have you on. Let me start with a simple question. Officially, does the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  believe that a free and fair election is being held this weekend in Hungary?\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, SPOKESPERSON FOR THE UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: Well, Chuck, we'll be watching very closely as we always do. It is the right of the people in Hungary, the people everywhere to be able to express their will at the ballot box. We expect to see a free and fair election. But we'll be in a position to judge that after the election is completed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we have observers on the ground? U.S. observers?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Chuck, I couldn't speak to whether we have American observers on the ground. But in many of our partners, many international organizations often do monitor these elections. So, I do expect we will have credible information to convey after the election has completed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What -- can you add any additional information to the Russian allegation that Ukraine attacked an oilfield on Russian soil? As you heard in Richard Engel's reporting, it seems to be a non-denial. Not going to confirm or deny situation. Anything more you can add?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Well, we are going to let our Ukrainian partners speak to their operations in the first instance, there's nothing I can add from here. But what's undeniable, of course, is that this war is something that President Putin started. It is President Putin, it's the Kremlin that has been prosecuting this brutal war against the Ukrainian people, against the Ukrainian state, against Ukrainian government in the territorial integrity of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, we are going to continue to support the right of our Ukrainian partners to defend themselves. They've been proven -- proving their effectiveness every single day on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Will the U.S. denounce Ukraine for attacking, if it was an intentional attack on Russian soil or not?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: I'm just not going to entertain a hypothetical like that, Chuck. We're going to let our Ukrainian partner speak for themselves on this. But look, they have a right to defend themselves. Not only do they have a right to do that, they have been doing that quite effectively as I said before. President Putin clearly made a miscalculation when, as you heard from our intelligence community, he expected to roll into Ukraine to take over Ukraine's major population centers, including Kiev, a population of 2.9 million people within 48 or 72 hours.\par \par \uc2 We're now more than five weeks into this premeditated, unjustified conflict war on the part of the Kremlin against Ukraine and Russian forces have been stalled. President Putin has miscalculated but also, he has faced the iron will of the Ukrainian people. And we're going to continue to support them providing massive amounts of security assistance precisely what they need to continue to hold the progress of the Russian servicemembers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before the war began, the public posture of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to the Kremlin was, look, we're willing to discuss where certain things are geographically, where we put troops in \uc2 NATO\uc2  sometimes or missile sites that, you know, while they're not going to tell us where, that certainly it was -- we were open to a conversation. I'm about to speak with a Latvian official here in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Is that now off the table? Is there -- at this point, is the U.S. posture - - we don't care what the Russians think it's all about securing \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies this point, particularly those on the front lines and that border, Russia.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Well, you're right. The nature of the dialogue in our focus has of course shifted. Our focus right now is supporting our Ukrainian partners, reassuring \uc2 NATO\uc2 , including our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies in the eastern flank, in putting tremendous pressure on the Kremlin, on President Putin, on the cronies and oligarchs that are around him. But your point is a good one. Before this started, we made very clear to the Kremlin that we were prepared to engage in a genuine good faith conversation about this stated concerns that the Kremlin had put forward.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:11]\par \par \uc2 But the Kremlin's posture, the fact that they did not respond positively or affirmatively to that, I think proves what we've been saying all along. In many ways. It was just a farce. The Russians were engaging in the pretense of diplomacy. They were making noises, like these were issues that could be resolved at the negotiating table, that there was -- there were issues we could discuss together to improve our security situation, to address their concerns.\par \par \uc2 But their stated security concerns were nothing more than a pretext. A pretext that Putin then use to go into Ukraine to take on this brutal war against the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  have any issue of Zelenskyy's posture in negotiations or, you know, while Crimea is something we can -- we could table for a few years and discuss that later. You know, what the -- what Ukraine's borders were before the war, or what Ukraine's borders are going to be, period. Is the U.S. willing to back Zelenskyy on that? we are willing to back and we will back the Ukrainian government in\par \par \uc2 PRICE: We are willing to back and we will the Ukrainian government in the decisions they make. You raised our posture before the invasion started. Our mantra back then was nothing about Ukraine. Without Ukraine, that remains very true today. There are a number of countries who have been supporting the dialogue and diplomacy. We're grateful for that. The efforts of our Turkish allies or Israeli partners, our French allies, our German allies.\par \par \uc2 We, along with our allies have made very clear that we are supporting and supportive of diplomacy. But it needs to be the Ukrainians who were in the lead here. We're not going to make decisions for Ukraine, it is going to be up to President Zelenskyy and his government to determine what's in the best interests of the Ukrainian people, of the Ukrainian government going forward.\par \par \uc2 What we're doing is putting Ukraine in the strongest negotiating position possible. And we're doing that by providing massive unprecedented amount of security assistance, $2 billion over the course of this administration, providing them with precisely what they need to halt and to take on this invading Russian force while as I said before, putting that pressure on the Kremlin. Hitting the Kremlin where it hurts.\par \par \uc2 So that, as we strengthen Ukraine's hand to the negotiating table, we do everything we can to weaken the Russian hand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there any way this -- why should the Ukrainian settle for anything that if Putin is still in power next door, how can the Ukrainians ever feel secure?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Well, Chuck, that's precisely the point we've made before. We want to see to it in the end, that this is a strategic defeat for Moscow. Already, we have seen that Moscow is weaker, it is more isolated, it is enfeebled on the world stage. That is true today, it will be truer when this end. Not only will Moscow strategic positioning be a far cry from what it was before this conflict began but we also want to see to it that Moscow won't be in a position to do something like this again.\par \par \uc2 Whether it's within months or years down the road. That's something we're going to consult on very closely with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies with our Ukrainian partners as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 , spokesperson for the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2 . Ned, really appreciate you coming on and sharing the administration's perspective. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 PRICE: Thanks, Chuck. Good to see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, in the shadow of Russian aggression, Baltic States plead for more action from the West. I'm going to talk to the Minister of Defense and the deputy prime minister of \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally Latvia. We're doing it there with Mr. Price. We'll talk to him and see what he thought of Mr. Price's answer. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:21:47]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the war in Ukraine enters its sixth week of violence with no immediate end in sight, \uc2 NATO\uc2 's top commanders are assessing the alliance's posture for the long term. \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  said yesterday that Russia's invasion of Ukraine marks a "New normal for global security." And on Tuesday, the Pentagon said it sent 200 U.S. troops and additional fighter jets to \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, Lithuania, a week after \uc2 NATO\uc2  already agreed to double its true presence all over Eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 Some countries say it's still not enough. As our latest NBC reporting highlights the Baltic states of Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania, all \uc2 NATO\uc2  members have been raising the alarm of a Russia aggression for years. And now, unless they get further support from their ally, some worry, they could be the next target of the Kremlin. On set with me here is Latvia's defense minister who's also a deputy prime minister, \uc2 Artis Pabriks\uc2 . Mr. Pabriks, it's good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ARTIS PABRIKS, DEFENSE MINISTER OF LATVIA: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Than you for coming in. Let me start with -- look, we can talk about prewar here in a minute. What do you need right now? What is -- what is the -- what is the concern of the average Latvian citizen right now when it comes to Russia?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: The average concern of Latvians are not the strengths of Russia, but the weakness of the West. And what we need as a nation, which already spending 2-1/2 percent from our GDP for our defense, and being a \uc2 NATO\uc2  and \uc2 European Union\uc2  border country, we need more firepower. And we need also a larger presence. Because seeing what kind of bullying neighbor, we do have, we cannot rely that if they invade our territory in a certain moment, that we can stop them immediately.\par \par \uc2 And this is exactly what's our strategy must be. Not to recover and liberate after but to stops them immediately.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don't want to be put in the Ukrainian situation where suddenly you need all these -- yes, and after the fact that there's no doubt it has helped Ukraine do this, but it was after the fact.\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Yes, because afterwards, it'll be always some doubts and talks about ceasefire and et cetera. We don't want to risk anymore. We were risking enough in our past.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right now, there's 2700 American troops across the three Baltic states. How much more would you like to say?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: We do not want to more. The thing is, these troops that you're counting are actually not on a permanent basis. They're coming and leaving. So, they come maybe for one or two months, they train with us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You want permanent bases.\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: We want a permanent stationing of certain American number of troops or certain capabilities like for instance, air defense. Or for instance, rocket artillery, which is this moment American stationed in Germany but obviously is no dangers in Germany where you could use rocket artillery, but you could easily put them in Latvia. So, we could train together and eventually purchase ourselves.\par \par \uc2 And additionally, we are, of course, asking Americans additional financial and military support to build our armies further on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As you're watching, what is your biggest concern as these peace talks are attempted and an attempt to try to end the -- end the hot war here? What's your concern of the various potential endings to this conflict that we can say?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: The biggest concern is that there are some western allies or politicians or experts who are wishing well and good to reach ceasefire but the ceasefire alone is simply a Russian and a Kremlin trap because if he would agree on the ceasefire, result withdrawal of Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:09]\par \par \uc2 At least as a line of 24th of February, they will begin, they will destroy Ukrainian nation and country and they would have a long-term possibility for the next war again. So, if you want to ceasefire, if we want to ceasefire, withdraw immediately out of Mariupol, out of Ukraine, that's the point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bottom line is if it's anything that essentially gives him what he -- some form of what he wanted in the Donbas region, you think that's going to send the wrong message?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Well, I think serious talks can start with Russia only when they are ready, and they are not ready now. And secondly, once they withdraw from the positions, which I took after 24th of February, all the rest is just fake things and lying from their side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I'm curious, what kind of Russian flight have you seen come into Latvia? We hear about the brain drain that's been taking place that Russian professionals, you know, your dentists, your lawyers, your accountants, the ones with means who are like looking around going, I got to get out of here. Are you seeing an influx?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Yes, we do. Yes, we do. But of course, we have to be also a little bit careful now. Because, first of all, of course, we see different types of news agencies and journalists flee, people who simply cannot do their work in Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Then we see also people who were earlier purchasing some apartments or houses, they simply use the opportunity to go to the west to come to us. But I believe that also some other people who simply wants convenience or don't want to be -- I don't know, even drafted. So -- and I believe there could be also some moles coming. So, we have to be a bit careful.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How are you dealing with that? I mean, how do you vet?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Well, we are interviewing them very carefully.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you really?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the -- I'm curious, when you see the rest of Europe, right now, the Western alliance has a -- has a bunch of democracies nervous because of the rising cost of energy. And as you know, that can have, you know, the concern about, oh, we got to keep the energy flowing, they want to keep a relationship with Russia. Is that something that can be done? Or is it -- or do you want to see Europe cut itself off completely from Russian energy?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: No. Look, I mean, we want relationship with Russia. We have to be very careful when we pronounce these words. Because yes, there have been a lot of mistakes actually, by our Western allies made it previously, which partly also facilitated this war. For instance, the engagement, these authoritarian and totalitarian regimes by thinking once we engage economically, we will change them. No, say did not change, with the change.\par \par \uc2 We got more corrupted, we got to installed a different -- let's say economic power since the west so in brief, I believe it's time to deputinize the west. And that means if we want to stop this violent war, then it is very important that we are actually putting maximum sanctions on Russia now. And the maximum military support to Ukraine. This is the only way how we can finish it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you see the ruble recovering in Russia. Does that mean our sanctions aren't working as well as they could?\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Well, sanctions will never stop tanks. Sanctions are long term and Russians and Putin is quite able to endure. So, we have to mount it up, as I mentioned, along with a military support to Ukraine and I think we should be capable also to spill more money, including stopping gas, oil, closing ports to Russian ships because if you want to -- if you do not want to spill our blood, we must fill money.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Artis Pabriks\uc2 , the defense minister and deputy prime minister in Latvia. Appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And be safe out there. Thanks for being on the front line.\par \par \uc2 PABRIKS: Thank you for support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Coming up. Part of my conversation with Senator Elizabeth Warren about a topic that is attracting a ton of interest, not just in political circles, but economic and cultural. Plus, in the midst of a whole lot of domestic problems for the president, they could soon have another problem on their hands. A potential surge of migrants at the southern border. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:40]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it's Friday, it means we have our latest episode of our streaming show of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." And it's now available on demand on Peacock. This week we took a deep dive into wild west of cryptocurrency. According to our new NBC News poll, one in five Americans surveyed have invested, traded or own some form of cryptocurrency. A little bit larger than I expected.\par \par \uc2 But Americans' overall feelings towards cryptocurrency is a bit mixed. 19 percent had a positive or somewhat positive view of it, versus 25 percent view it negatively. Now those are the folks that weren't involved in it. A majority, 56 percent, say they feel neutral, they really weren't sure about what the crypto industry is.\par \par \uc2 So for this new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," I spoke with a couple of senators, one is more of an evangelist and one is more of a critic, including Democrat Elizabeth Warren who doesn't really think a lot of bitcoin. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If I buy bitcoin, am I buying a share of stock or am I buying a pork belly or am I buying euros? What am I buying?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): You're buying air.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, wow. OK. You're going in. What is it?\par \par \uc2 WARREN: I'm going in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. What is it?\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Because that's the point is that it's -- you're buying something that other people believe either is going to go up in value, presumably that why you're buying it, or think it's going to go down in value, which is presumably why someone else is selling it. But that's it. It's a bet on whether this thing, this ephemeral token will become more valuable or less valuable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So of course the question is, what is Washington going to do about cryptocurrencies? A lot more of this interview, plus an interview with Republican Senator \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2 . I think when you see it both, you'll have an idea of where Washington may be going when it comes to digital currencies.\par \par \uc2 Catch the full episode, "CRYPTOCURRENCY: THE WILD WEST." It's on demand on Peacock right now. You can also catch it on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:37:48]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It was another solid jobs report today with President Biden crediting -- credit to the administration's pandemic rescue plan in remarks this morning, but it comes at a moment when Biden is struggling to translate good jobs numbers into good economic will with the voters. Despite at being the 11th straight month of job gained above 400,000, Biden's approval rating is at its lowest point of his presidency, according to our new NBC poll -- News poll.\par \par \uc2 It's driven largely by his handling of the economy. Big majority of Americans, 63 percent, disapprove of the handling of the economy as soaring inflation wipes out wage gains. Meanwhile, the administration is facing the resurgence of another issue, the Democrats constantly worry could drive them even lower in the midterms the situation at the southern border. As the "Washington Post" reports some top Democrats are lashing out at the administration and each other over the plans to lift an emergency order at the border, which could lead to a surge in migrant crossings.\par \par \uc2 I'm joined today in the panel, Maria Teresa Kumar, president and CEO of Voto Latino, MSNBC contributor, Betsy Woodruff Swan, national correspondent at "Politico," and David Frum, writer at the "Atlantic" and former speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and also an MSNBC contributor.\par \par \uc2 Maria Teresa, let me start with you. Title 42. First of all, on May 23rd, do you expect Title 42 to be lifted?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, VOTO LATINO PRESIDENT AND CEO: I -- we would hope that to be the case. But I'm hearing that it's not going to be such a rush.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If they find a reason to extend it they're going to take it?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: They're going to. And I think the challenge with that and them heading into the new midterms is that they really haven't been able to define anything that they've really given to the Latino community very squarely, whether it's been on extending student loan debt, whether it's immigration specifically, immigration reform or actually feeling that the Latino community is actually feeling more at ease.\par \par \uc2 And the reason I bring up the Latino community is that we just launched a poll and found that close to 30 percent of the people who had voted for Biden out of the Latino community aren't sure if they're going to come out in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you know why? Did they get a good explanation as why is it all in one direction or is it just -- are there many reasons?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think fundamentally it's the economy. And it's not the economy at large is we are talking bread and butter issues. We're talking about that, yes, you can be back to work and yes, we have seen a modest raise. But if the price of milk all of a sudden is, you know, pushing on $8 a gallon and you're still living on $15 of minimum wage, that's not enough and you don't have anything else to demonstrate that the government is working for you. It's really challenging.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Betsy, the administration is panicked about immigration. How can you tell? Their own spokesman said, hey, this wasn't our decision, this is what the CDC's decision. And you're like, wow, you don't want to own this at all. It seems like they have -- look, our economy is struggling on the labor front because we don't have enough immigration and this administration has been afraid even to touch that.\par \par \uc2 BETSY WOODRUFF SWAN, POLITICO NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: It's always something that Democrats have struggled to talk about and where Republicans are often very comfortable being in sort of the peanut gallery. It's easy for Republicans to say the Democrats are doing a terrible job and blame the federal government for whatever is going on on the migration front, on the southern border.\par \par \uc2 This is very much for the GOP, this is their total comfort zone, the situation that the administration is in right now. And of course it's not a situation that anybody would envy. The politics are really complicated and, you know, to the point about the NBC poll, I think didn't -- the president's support among Latino voters drop by something like 9 percent. That's a scary number for an administration that has rhetorically really committed itself to this community. So it's hard to see politically if they have an easy way out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, David, part of the problem right now is, as somebody was explaining it to me yesterday that if you took the Gang of Eight bill from 2013, not only would you not have as many Republicans supporting it, as did then, you couldn't get all 50 Democrats to support it. The middle ground -- we've gone further away from each other in the two parties on immigration.\par \par \uc2 DAVID FRUM, THE ATLANTIC WRITER: The Gang of Eight bill is entirely unresponsive to the situation today. And this is the thing I think people don't understand about what the nature of the immigration crisis is. I wrote in the very first month of the administration this is going to be the issue that sinks them in 2022 if they're not careful. This is no longer the illegal immigration of your unattached young men, in groups singly or in groups, crossing the border, defying the law, looking to elude detection on their way to the American job market.\par \par \uc2 This the overwhelming of the asylum system. The border crossers are not breaking the law. They are abusing the law that was written with in mind the victims of Nazism and fascism, people who are fleeing political prosecution. Now you have hundreds of thousands, millions of people often in family groups --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Economic dispositions.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: Yes. And crossing the border and saying I want to be found. I want to enter the system because the system will not give me a speedy trial, it will release me into the American economy pending my speedy trial. So long as I think I'm winning, I will comply with the law and have a driver's license, and many rights of residency, and at such time that it looks like I might lose, then I drop out of legality into illegality. And that might take years. And this a problem that American law is completely unprepared to deal with.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: And so the Gang of Eight bill, that was all about the illegal immigrant of yore who came across as a single young man trying to elude detection.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I mean, the Gang of Eight bill, the biggest challenge is -- you sort of alluded to. There is two issues right now. One that was happening at the border and folks seeking asylum. The (INAUDIBLE) bill addressed the people that were already in this country, the 11 million or so who had been here for about 20 years on average. I mean, you think about when the --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Remember it was 11 years then. Because we're eight years later, we're now saying two decades.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Later, right. Two decades. If you can imagine, when DACA passed, we're talking about 18-year-olds. 18-year-olds are now pushing 30, 32, and they're still in limbo, even though they have temporary asylum for -- you know, for all intents and purposes. And so what the administration needs to look at, and I know that Congressman Raul Ruiz has been working very closely, behind closed doors, what are the executive actions that could address those individuals that are stuck in limbo in the United States?\par \par \uc2 The issue of migration and asylum seeking, we have to absolutely address but they are two distinct issues and what the Republicans have done very well at is conflating the two and we have to talk to the American people because they recognize that that neighborhood restaurant that you love to go to, the person -- the owner might be in (INAUDIBLE), he's part of your family and part of your community. How do you address his status?\par \par \uc2 TODD: There seems to be no -- I don't want to say bandwidth, forget bandwidth, no appetite in the administration to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: They don't want to talk about it. It's not something where you see them going out front, being aggressive, proposing anything sweeping or inspiring that they can use to appeal to immigrant communities, rather it's an issue where the Biden administration is very much playing defense and very much talking about it in a way that's responsive and reactionary to what Republicans are doing.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are essentially laying out the terms of the debate, defining the issue. They've done it in the way that's very effective and the Biden administration now is just trying to essentially push back and, you know, staunch the bleeding to the extent that they view it that way. One thing that I would note about the asylum system that makes the asylum legal challenges so complex is that while some people who come to the border seeking asylum, maybe coming for economic reasons, those northern triangle countries are so, so troubled and many of the people who come to the United States and seek asylum are doing it because they need asylum because the countries that they're in have become in some ways almost borderline failed states.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: That's exactly right. And part of the things --\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 FRUM: But the asylum system was not designed to deal with the problem of I live in a bad country. It was designed to address the problem -- they're coming, for me personally, because of something I can't control. If asylum is for anyone who lives in an unstable, violent, and poor country, that's many billions of people.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think the challenge with the triangle countries that you're recognizing is that the United States exported the gang problem to Central America. These were individuals that were basically picked up on immigration, went into Los Angeles County jails for the most part, got indoctrinated into the gang system and then were sent --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, it's so bad in El Salvador they just declared a state of emergency.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: State of emergency.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They basically suspended rights for a lot of its citizens. They're a little concerned.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: If you're gay, if you're a woman, if you're a child and you don't want to be part of the gang, you are targeted and we have to recognize the hand that America had in actually creating the disruption.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: We have to recognize that asylum cannot be the problem for global slow development. It cannot be the problem for instability everywhere on earth. And what you would do --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think you should have that conversation and talk to the Ukrainians, to the Afghanistanis. You should have a conversation with the Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: You will destroy the whole government of the United States. And I keep -- I've been saying this for a long time. But if people who have responsible politics will not accept that the border is an important issue. It has to be stabilized and the state has to be in control of the movement of people. That is something that voters everywhere want. And if they can't find responsible people who will do it, they will find irresponsible people who will promise it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to shift at why isn't an economic argument an effective one here? I mean, we're desperate for workers. I mean, I met with the mayor of San Antonio earlier this week. And, you know, he's like we got 3.8 percent unemployment, we need workers. I think he's working on a pilot program that's going to -- that's interested in working with the government of Mexico. I'm sure you're a little bit familiar with it, it sounds like the way you reacted.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: But no -- but Chuck, this is the thing, is that it's --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn't there an economic argument to be made?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: There's an economic argument to be made if you actually recognize the aging in of Americans in general. You know, for the very first time the white population according to the Census dropped significantly. And if you don't have people coming in and to renew our economic engine, we're going to look much more like Japan, like Europe than we would be in the thriving country that we are. And I think that -- I'm not disagreeing with you that we need to make sure we have -- we need to have responsible laws. But we have to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FRUM: We can't live without water. But it's not a good argument when I say there's a flood in the basement and my house is going to be destroyed. We say, without water you won't be able to live.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that's the -- and Betsy, that's why the administration doesn't know what to do. And they'd rather throw up their hands, not talk about it and hope people pay attention to something else.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: But it's a strategic decision not to actually be ahead for the 21st century. And I think that is the conversation we need to have next, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: And they did volunteer --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: We're not going to stop, are we, David?\par \par \uc2 FRUM: And they did volunteer for the job.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair point. Fair point. This is why it's good to have panels back. So thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Up ahead, a new focus on fighting a growing threat to the safety of women worldwide. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:36]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This week the White House proclaimed April as National Sexual Assault Awareness and Prevention Month. One of the fastest growing threats to women is online harassment. The latest government statistics show 1 in 3 women under the age of 35 have experienced a type of essentially sexual harassment online. It's often underreported and many times not taken seriously despite its serious risk to mental health and physical safety.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is now committed to a new global partnership for action on gender-based online harassment and abuse. The goal, deliver an action plan at combatting technology facilitated gender-based violence by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Radford joins me now. She's been doing some reporting on the impact of this type of harassment especially the impact it has on women in journalism. And it's not even a close call, Morgan, as nasty as the attacks can be on some of us in this business, if you're a woman it is at another level.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that's right, Chuck, and you know, before this was all sort of anecdotal, but now we have hard data. I mean, this is one of the biggest issues facing female journalists right now, and we have a closer look at how online attacks against female journalists right now and unprecedented study is giving us a closer look at just how some of those online attacks against female journalists are actually getting started and what it looks like for those who are on the receiving end. But a warning to our viewers, some of the language you hear in this report, it may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RADFORD (voice-over): Female reporters are often at the center of the bull's eye. 73 percent of women journalist saying they've experienced online attacks while 30 percent say it has impacted their work.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR LORENZ, THE WASHINGTON POST TECH COLUMNIST: I don't know if I can say this on TV, "I'm going to rape you."\par \par \uc2 RADFORD (on-camera): Somebody said -- wrote the words, I'm going to rape you?\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD (voice-over): Journalist Taylor Lorenz is a columnist for the "Washington Post" and was targeted nearly one year ago in a segment on FOX News.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX HOST: She's at the very top of journalisms' repulsive little food chain. Host Tucker Carlson mocking her after she called for an end to online harassment. Now she's at the center of a new study by \uc2 NYU\uc2  researchers, among the first to actually quantify online hate against female journalists.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): This is the moment that Carlson aired that segment?\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: Yes. We see this really dramatic rise.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD (voice-over): Using large-scale data to measure online language, they tracked violent and threatening tweets directed at two female journalists after being targeted by two male media figures. Researchers found that attacks against Lorenz went up as much as 144 percent after just one \uc2 Twitter\uc2  thread. For another journalist, they went up 65 percent. Lorenz and digital reporter Kate Sosin say these types of attacks have changed their lives and their work.\par \par \uc2 KATE SOSIN, THE 19TH LGBTQ PLUS REPORTER: There's reporting that I know that I would like to do, that other journalists would like to do that we're not able to do because it's not safe enough for us to do them.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And they're not alone.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): This is after I did a report on an increases in the number of white supremacists running for office. Condescending journalist, C word, deserves a rope. Obviously I'm a person of color. Obviously there's a reference to a noose.\par \par \uc2 Are you getting messages like this?\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: Yes. Hey, nice job on that story. You soulless, effing (EXPLETIVE DELETED). Then also you'll see there's many people that are tweeting, you know, here's -- these are Taylor Lorenz's loved ones, of everyone.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: They have photos -- wow, these are all photos of your families members.\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Children?\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: Yes, they'll threaten children. They'll threaten my parents. I have had to remove every single social tie.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 I had severe PTSD from this. I contemplated suicide, it got really bad. You feel like any little piece of information that gets out on you will be used by the worst people on the internet to destroy your life. And it's so isolating.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And terrifying.\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: It's horrifying. I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: You're fine. You're fine.\par \par \uc2 LORENZ: It's overwhelming. It's really hard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Now, Chuck, we reached out to Glenn Greenwald and FOX News about this study. Greenwald responded saying in part, "As a member of various marginalized groups, I don't want or accept some special immunity shield against being criticized and no journalist with any dignity or worth should want that either." Neither \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  nor his employer, FOX News, have responded to us, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Morgan, folks who live online know exactly what they're doing when they do target a journalist that way, and unfortunately the online community -- well, as was said in the piece, the worst of the worst are the ones that come out and respond.\par \par \uc2 Morgan, I'm glad you did that story. It's important for it to be out there. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We'll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And it's Sunday, it's "MEET THE PRESS" on your local NBC News station. Among my guests, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MSNBC's coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I'm Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220402ei4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 31, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Alexander, Hadley Gamble, Ali Arouzi \par \uc2 10,501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday. President Biden prepares to deliver remarks this hour on the administration`s new wartime plan to tame soaring gas prices and rising inflation including a record-setting release of oil from the country`s emergency supply.\par \par \uc2 All of this as the White House and members of Ukraine`s parliament brief senators on Capitol Hill about the situation on the ground in Ukraine. I`ll speak with a couple of top Senators from both sides of the aisle about what they`re learning and what it means for the U.S. response.\par \par \uc2 And even more, evidence that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is in the dark as British intelligence has Putin massively misjudged Ukraine. It`s the day after a U.S. official said U.S. intelligence indicated Putin was being misled by his own advisers.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. President Biden is scheduled to speak sometime this hour on some new efforts to combat soaring energy prices here at home, as Americans continue to feel the pinch of inflation amid the latest impact on our problems, Vladimir Putin`s war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 According to the White House, Biden`s plan will invoke the Defense Production Act to force companies to boost domestic energy production. He`ll also call on Congress to impose penalties on oil companies if they don`t make full use of their current capacity. And he`ll announce a plan to release about a million barrels of oil a day from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve over the next six months. That will be up to 180 million barrels, which would be the single-largest release of oil from the U.S. emergency reserves in our history. All of this comes as the administration is facing some massive political headwinds from rising gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Just our own most recent NBC News poll showed just how frustrated voters are and how much they blame Biden for their frustrations right now. According to a poll, the cost of living in the economy is easily the top issue facing the country right now, much more so than the war in Ukraine or any other matter in Washington. In a plurality, 38 percent blamed rising costs on the president, rather than the pandemic, corporate greed, or the war, and nearly seven in ten say they want the president to make reducing inflation, his single top priority rather than working towards the end of the war in Ukraine if they had to pick between the two. This is where voters want the president`s focus to be.\par \par \uc2 As for the war in Ukraine, which has significantly worsened inflation concerns, the conflict is now entering six weeks and there are no signs it`s going to end anytime soon. Ukrainian President Zelenskyy said today that thousands of civilians have died in the city of Mariupol. That`s a figure that NBC News can`t confirm. No one`s going to be able to confirm it as yet. It`s going to take a while. Ukraine has also said that Russia has blocked humanitarian corridors in Kharkiv after shelling overnight.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Italy`s Prime Minister says that Putin told him that conditions are simply not right for a ceasefire and that Putin has no immediate plans to meet with Zelenskyy. Just this morning, \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary-General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  said NATO`s intelligence confirms the West`s suspicions that Russia was simply repositioning its forces, not withdrawing, and obviously, Putin`s comments to the Italian Prime Minister seemed to reinforce that intelligence.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy, meanwhile, has renewed his calls for the international community to cut off its purchases of Russian oil, as it fuels Putin`s war machine, and we are seeing the ruble start to come back. Why? Because they`re making money off of energy and oil contracts still.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is our chief White House Correspondent Peter Alexander, also with us from Dubai, CNBC`s International Correspondent Hadley Gamble. And in a moment, we`re going to talk to Foreign -- our Foreign Correspondent Ali Arouzi on the ground in Ukraine. Peter, let me start with you. The defense Production Act, I -- wasn`t that long ago that I had folks at the White House going I`m not sure we`re going to invoke that for oil exploration, but that -- is that a 180?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, this is obviously a pivot that`s for darn sure. The White House really, as you noted in your introduction here, Chuck, acknowledges the political headwinds they are facing right now, the fact that Americans say their number one issue is the rising cost of living, those high inflation figures.\par \par \uc2 And that`s all just seven months ahead of the midterms this fall. So as it relates to the Defense Production Act, White House officials say that the President will detail this as part of an effort to try to speed up America`s transfer to a clean energy economy effectively here. And specifically what he`s going to do is he`s going to support the processing of materials and minerals that are used in large capacity batteries like those you would find in electric vehicles, minerals like lithium, and like, nickel to try to take it away from the reliance on China and put that back in the hands of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Separately, what the president will do today is to call on Congress, Chuck, for what they describe as the use it or lose it policy where the White House, they`ve been making this case for a while now is basically going to try to tell Congress that they should impose penalties on companies that least public lands right now but do not use those leases -- those lands to produce energy at this time.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Obviously, he`s going to face some stiff resistance from Congress on that. And those in the oil production community say that, frankly, a lot of those wells that aren`t being used simply are dry and they don`t have access to oil. Nonetheless, the backdrop to all of this is the real urgency the White House senses, given the fact that gas prices right now, Chuck, are $4.22 a gallon. That`s up 60 cents in just the last month.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, I guess I`m a bit -- are they invoking the Defense Production Act to push oil companies to speed up exploration, or is the Defense Production Act only being for the other energy, the Clean Energy stuff that they`re pushing?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: As I understand it, and I want to let the president speak for himself, but from the conversations we`ve had with officials here, it sounds like the Defense Production Act is being used to support the processing of those additional minerals and materials that would be used to help the clean energy type economy. So to find more of those minerals, specifically, that we noted there that go into the production of electric vehicles in the like.\par \par \uc2 As I understand it right now, and again, we`ll hear from the president within half an hour and he may be pressed on these more broadly, as it relates to companies there, they`re not doing anything beyond these companies but trying to pressure them publicly calling on Congress to do more. But sort of publicly pressure them and in some ways, shame them, saying, hey, you guys should be doing more right now. We have 9000 outstanding, you know, unused leases, in effect right now. You guys should be doing more about this, instead of making money on the backs of Americans who are struggling right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Peter, so the separate part of this is getting Congress to see if they can pass a law that would essentially speed up an oil cut? Because look, there`s a report out of the \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  that indicates that one of the reasons for oil companies least in this country not pushing for more exploration is they don`t want to make the capital expense. Translation, they don`t want to spend the money it takes to tap the -- to tap these lease -- to use these leases, maybe it`s dry, maybe it doesn`t get the return that they want, because they`re having to deal with shareholders.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I think you`re right. So you`re seeing what you`re seeing politically, at least here, right, is, you`re seeing the Republicans who are blaming \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  -- excuse me, were blaming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for this right now. And frankly, Americans blame Joe Biden`s policies more than they do for a war that`s happening overseas. Democrats and the president himself are trying to blame \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . That`s why you keep hearing him say this is Putin`s war, Putin`s price like that. I bet you`ll hear that five more times from the president later today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But in terms of what they can do to pressure companies, they can only go so far right now. What they`ve tried to do again, just to make this point is to try to have Americans sort of put that -- put that anger, that blame, that direction and say, hey, there are plenty of companies that can do this. But obviously, they`ve been running up against the brick wall trying to make that argument so far.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I would say this. I think there`s a number of ways to interpret our poll. Look, 38 percent blame Biden, another 62 percent blame either COVID or oil companies or Putin --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That, you know, I think there are some people overreacting to that poll. It`s not a -- it is not as binary I think, as some have read it. Peter Alexander, on top of this at the White House, for us, Peter, thank you. Let`s go to our guru when it comes to the oil markets. At least to I turned to, to understand this. Hadley Gamble, our friend at CNBC. So, Hadley, 180 million -- I know \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said it`s a drop in a bucket but this is the most we`ve ever done, which means we`re going to be spending money as a government to replenish it obviously, as fast as we`re emptying our reserves here. What is this going to do to the markets?\par \par \uc2 HADLEY GAMBLE, MSNBC INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: OK, essentially, what we know is that 20 million barrels per day are what Americans generally consume, right? That`s the number. This is going to add eventually 1 million barrels per day to that number that`s going to basically breathe out free oil that eventually the United States is going to purchase back at around $80 a barrel to replenish the SPR.\par \par \uc2 But as you say, this is the biggest release that we`ve ever seen in U.S. history. 180 million barrels per day, that dwarfs the last two releases, they were around 40 to 60 million barrels per day. Those releases, you`ll remember, they move to the market just slightly, what we would call a blip. So you`ve got to think about this a bit more broadly. This is a president that, as you were just saying is under severe political pressure. He`s also attempting to put major pressure on U.S. oil producers.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is a bit of a double-edged sword. This is a president who hasn`t been in favor, really of backing the oil production lobby for a multitude of political and climate reasons. They have not been happy about this. I`ve been talking to them for the last two years and I have never heard a positive message about the fact that in spite of the fact we kept hearing from the Biden team that they were, you know, reaching across the aisle if you will, trying to talk to them, I`ve had multiple leaders of these companies say to me, Chuck, we have never gotten a call from the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, already you`ve got a problematic relationship there. Take a step back from that and think about what`s happening in the global market at this point. We`re talking about Russia.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 They produce the most petroleum of any country in the world, right? They, of course, have been having a hard time selling those barrels in the last several weeks as a direct result of the invasion of Ukraine. There`s a political risk to that. Buyers just don`t want to take it, they`re afraid that they`re going to be more sanctions beyond what the United States has already done.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve got this problem, they`re not able to get out as much oil as they have in the past. Plus, they are a member of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 . They put the plus in OPEC Plus. That`s a whole other kettle of fish because the \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , of course, has been under severe pressure from the West to do more for the last several months and has been warning I might add way before the invasion of Ukraine that we were entering a period of energy crisis. A couple of reasons for that are the disconnect between policies and production, and at the same point, major under-investment for years.\par \par \uc2 Remember, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  back in 2020, decided to put caps on production in the market. They did that because we saw those prices falling through the floor as a result of the pandemic. That production has yet to come fully back online. They just made another decision this week, essentially saying we`re going to stick to our original plan of only releasing 430,000 barrels per day in May. So you got to take a step back and think about the market fundamentals here.\par \par \uc2 I was speaking to oil producers this week. I was also speaking to some of the big names in oil companies. I`m talking about \uc2 Patrick Pouyanne\uc2  of \uc2 TotalEnergies\uc2 , the head of any of the Italian producers. Essentially what they were saying to me is we`re not just worried about the prices that are happening right now, over $100 per barrel. We`re worried about this getting much work as a result of underinvestment. Why is there under-investment, even though we know that we`re going to have to use fossil fuels for a long time to come? It`s because the investors and the shareholders just don`t want it.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen that over the last several years as they become much more attuned to climate change risk and policies. They just don`t want to put the money in there. And as you know, and as your guest said earlier, when we talk about oil production and shale production, it is a cost-intensive industry. You got to pay to play.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And then, by the way, we`re about to hit a season in this country where we consume more oil in the summer as more people hit the road. So is it possible that all we`re -- that what the strategic oil reserve release is simply going to keep us at status quo? Maybe is that the best we can hope for?\par \par \uc2 GAMBLE: Potentially. You know, when I was speaking to these oil ministers, I was asking them, you know, give me the -- give me the real juice on this, because these are guys that I have been speaking to, Chuck, in Vienna, in Saudi Arabia for 10 years now. I said, you know, cut to the chase, what is this really about?\par \par \uc2 And for them, they said, listen in, we can`t get rid of Russia as a member of OPEC Plus. And the reason for that is over the last several decades, we have always included those, even when they are under sanctions, even when they`re starting wars, those Houthi attacks on infrastructure in Saudi Arabia, and right here in the UAE, they still have Iran as a member of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that`s because they keep telling me that in order to stabilize the market, they have to keep a dialogue going and they have to know just how much oil is on the market. Listen in very quickly to what His Excellency, \uc2 Suhail Al Mazrouei\uc2  had to say to me this week in Dubai.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SUHAIL AL MAZROUEI, MINISTER OF ENERGY IN THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES: And we`re not taking decide today or saying this is right or this is wrong when we are inside that organization. We have one mission and only mission, which is stabilizing the market.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAMBLE: The stabilizing market is what they`re sticking to. And one of the things that kept coming up again and again in an hour and a half that I had with these guys, they`re very worried about inflation, just like President Biden is, and they`re very worried about the humanitarian aspect of this, not just, you know, the millions of refugees that are flooding into Europe, but they`re also worried about energy supplies into Europe, what that does to the global economy and right here in the Middle East because we`re talking about food inflation.\par \par \uc2 And just as an FYI, we`ve seen in the last several days, not only Saudi Arabia, but Qatar as well making major pledges to Egypt because they`re starting to worry about the idea that people are going to be hungry and they`re not going to be able to afford bread. And where is the bread coming from?\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re bringing at the other issue. Right -- that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAMBLE: Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ukraine and Russia are huge wheat exporters. It`s also causing a rise in the price of wheat. I`m going deep with you on the rising price of oil here. Wheat is another kettle of fish. That`s for sure. Hadley, I always appreciate the education we get on the markets. Thank you very much. Let me check in with Ali Arouzi. He is on the ground in Lviv for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, let`s go to the war footing itself. We are along -- it feels like we`re a long way away from 72 hours ago when the Russians claimed that they may be, might kind of, sort of, will start pulling back from their military escalation near Kyiv, near the suburbs of Kyiv. That`s clearly not the case. What have you observed and is -- are we still seeing occasional shelling of Lviv still?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Chuck. Well, that`s right. I mean, Jen Stoltenberg, the U.S. officials, Vladimir -- Zelenskyy himself, and all of his generals have confirmed that there hasn`t been any major military withdrawal from the areas that they said they were going to come out of.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 There are still a lot of Russian troops around Kyiv, around Chernihiv. And even though they said that they were going to scale back the number of troops, they had there to build mutual trust, they continue to hit Kyiv, fight in the suburbs of Kyiv since they said that they were going to drawback. Right after they announced that they were going to pull troops away from Chernihiv, they bombed Chernihiv all night, and they bombed it all through the day.\par \par \uc2 So, as Secretary Blinken said what Russia says and what Russia does are two completely different things. And part of the reason that they are still continuing this offensive is that they don`t want to leave the areas of Kyiv. That`s still an objective for them, even though they`re saying that the emphasis has now probably shifted to the Donbass region where the Pentagon says they`ve recruited a thousand foreign mercenaries to fight along with the Russian troops, there may be a concerted effort to take the Donbass region.\par \par \uc2 And that`s just because the assault on Kyiv has been such a disaster. So they`re probably regrouping, rotating some troops, giving them a rest, to then concentrate at a later point on Kyiv. And then so the Donbass region is probably going to get hammered pretty badly in the coming days, in the coming weeks, until they can then refocus all of their strategies back on the Kyiv area, and the eastern parts of Ukraine. So whatever Russia has said, they certainly haven`t done. And you see that very clearly on the ground here because they haven`t left any of the areas alone that they claimed that they were going to.\par \par \uc2 And it also underscores that the Russian military might is much weaker than Moscow had anticipated before they invaded this country. They are taking heavy losses, not just the troops, but of machines, and that`s having to make them re-strategize. So even though there`s been a change of emphasis now looking on the -- to the Donbass region, the overall plan probably hasn`t changed. They will probably refocus on these areas.\par \par \uc2 And as U.S. officials say, there probably will be in the coming days and weeks, a huge offensive again, on those key locations that are so strategic for them. And as we`ve seen, you know, they haven`t left places like Mariupol alone, they`ve pounded that city, 90 percent of it is gone. Because it`s so strategic for them, it will connect Russia to Crimea. And it will also choke off Ukraine`s economy because they export so much of that wheat and corn and coal that you mentioned, from the port of Mariupol to the Middle East, and further away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Arouzi, with a lot of the fallout from this war and how this is going to only become worse in a lot of regions of the world. Ali Arouzi, thank you. And as we mentioned, we do expect to hear from the president this hour he announces what we`ve been reporting on this new wartime plan to try to combat the rise of gas prices here at home.\par \par \uc2 When those remarks begin, we will take you to the White House live. But first, how the former president`s messaging to Putin is complicating the GOP`s positioning in Congress? Republican senator from North Dakota, Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  joins me next. He`s also gotten a briefing on the latest out of Ukraine. We`re going to ask him about that as well. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we wait to hear from the president in a few minutes on his new energy plan in an attempt to bring down oil prices that have spiked in part due to the war in Ukraine, but mostly due to COVID. Let`s turn to Capitol Hill where both the House and the Senate received classified briefings yesterday about the latest intelligence on the war in Ukraine. Some lawmakers both Democrats and Republicans left that briefing expressing frustration that they felt the administration could be doing more to deliver aid to Ukraine and to deliver it faster.\par \par \uc2 Senator Chris Murphy warned that Congress will need to pass supplemental aid to Ukraine as this conflict continues. And reporters for what it`s worth, also press many Republican senators about some bizarre comments made by the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again, for some reason, calling on the dictator \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to release information he may or may not have on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Let me bring in Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2 , a Republican from North Dakota, and let`s start with your briefing from administration officials on Ukraine. Everybody wants to do more. What`s the more that you think is reasonable that we should be doing that we`re not?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND): Yes. So great question because the first more that we often talked about is the request by President Zelenskyy for MiGs or some sort of fighter jets, and that`s always at the top of the list. Setting that aside for a moment, I think most Americans believe that what they really need are better surface-to-air missile systems. They agree with that. They`ve been -- Ukrainians, they want more advanced systems. You know, we talked about the S-300s of the Soviet-era Defense System, which is pretty effective, but it`s quite old.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, more long-range systems as well, because as you know that the Stingers that we`ve provided to this point, are really short- range. Therefore, the missiles when they get really close to you -- they want to be able to hit these missiles further away and I don`t blame them and higher in the air. We need to do that for them. But I think a lot of the urgency besides the, what is the, how fast?\par \par \uc2 You heard, you quoted Chris Murphy, that -- I think that`s what`s being expressed both by the Ukrainians and a number of us prior to the briefing yesterday, those of us in the Ukraine caucus met with five members of -- rather the parliament in Ukraine, and they said the same thing. It`s like we need these things yesterday. If you can`t get them to us yesterday, please, today or tomorrow at the latest.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the real frustration, the lack of certainty of how -- you know how soon they can get these things and how effective they can be. But that`s met with all kinds of other complications, Chuck because you know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, right. I was just going to say this S-300 issue. I -- there`s only one \uc2 NATO\uc2  nation that`s ready to do it, I believe it`s Slovakia. And it seems like there`s a couple of other nations that haven`t and they don`t want to do it, I guess, out of fear of irritating the Russians. So how much of this is a Biden issue and how much of this is a unique \uc2 NATO\uc2  issue?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, you`re right. But there -- I think the number of nations willing to do it is growing, I get that sense yesterday. They have to get their stock files. They have to find them. They have to see if they even work, these are very old systems. The United States does have some as well. So without revealing too much information, there are a lot of them out there.\par \par \uc2 But let`s also remember, these aren`t the latest and greatest, although they would be welcome. But then you have the additional challenge of restocking those stocks wherever they might be. And we`re looking at a Biden budget that`s -- you know, the defense budget, which it grows at a rate of about half of what inflation is growing at.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a lot of, again, complications. We also have technology transfer challenges with newer systems, obviously, that we have to deal with. So I think one of the takeaways for me at least as we`re not very well prepared for war. We`re prepared to maybe slow down defeat for the Ukrainians, but I don`t see that we have the policy to help them win right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what`s the -- what kind of -- what kind of impression do you have of what the negotiations -- what the negotiations are about these days, what`s in play? And what`s realistic? I mean, did -- is there any sense of how -- did you get any sense of how this could end?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: I think you`ve been reporting this, I`ve been listening. And by the way, I could spend an hour listening to Hadley talk about the oil markets. But I think you`ve been reporting pretty accurately what the state of play is. And that is that there`s not -- it`s not very realistic, at least at this point. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says one thing, he does something different, you can`t really rely on it, and understandably so. I mean the guy -- the guy hasn`t really been shooting straight with us for a very long time, obviously.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s a frustration. Evidently, he`s not feeling enough incentive yet to negotiate in good faith. Obviously, he needs to come out of this looking like he won something. And I think right now, if he got out of it, it would look like he did not. I think we have to be sure he doesn`t ever leave this war looking like he won something. So I think we`re a long way from a truly negotiated favorable outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to hit you on that last statement because how does this end with him in power and him as a loser, right?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Like, that`s the -- that`s the part of this. It`s hard to -- look and I`m maybe asking you an impossible question. But it`s understandable what the President said last week, everybody wants a man out of power. Should that be a policy? Should we pursue this to their -- our foot`s on his throat?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Yes. So it`s interesting you say that, Chuck because when he said what he said -- when the President said what he said last week, that implied a regime change, I`m going -- I think he just said when a whole bunch of people are thinking.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: But of course, he`s the president, when he says that it has ramifications. I personally don`t believe -- first of all, I don`t believe that a lot of our allies and other adversaries took it as seriously as we seem to take it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: And second of all, I think you don`t need to do anything to torque off \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  enough to be dumb. He is there. And so I think it was -- you know I kind of get your point. At the same time, is there an outcome where he loses and this ends well without him being taken out?\par \par \uc2 All I would say is that I think that a revolution in Russia would be pretty welcome right about now but I don`t want to oversimplify what a revolution in Russia would look like. That -- which means we need to continue to apply enough pressure and provide enough tools, both on the sanction side, as well as on the military side to make sure that Ukraine wins this war, and that we don`t just have a policy of delaying their defeat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk about what the president is likely to unveil here in a few minutes on an energy strategy here to try to speed up, get some more oil into the market. And it`s the issue of trying to incentivize, and I think, issuing penalties if you don`t use a lease on government lands is one way I guess, of incentivizing. But do you think that is -- at this point, could you support something like that to essentially incentivize oil companies? Hey, make these capital investments. We know your shareholders don`t want it but your country does, democracy does. This is not everything should be for shareholder profit.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, first of all, Chuck, there are 2200 leases that have been granted that are in litigation right now, litigation. There -- the biggest problem or not least is 75 percent of the leases are being used. The problem is they also have to speed up the permitting process, the applications for permits to drill, the days that that takes have doubled since just a couple of years ago, maybe even tripled.\par \par \uc2 So, you can`t say we`re going to punish you if you don`t use it but then at the same time, not issue the permits that you need to be able to drill. That`s just one of many regulations that provide a lack of certainty for this investment. I thought Hadley Gamble made a great point when she said these are capital-intensive industries, you don`t just turn a valve in the middle of the -- you know federal grasslands and oil comes out. It takes years.\par \par \uc2 And you have to have enough certainty. You don`t need to, you know, 20, 30 years, certainly, but you need probably five to 10 years of certainty to attract that investment. To -- you know, to do the draw if it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What part of -- all right, but in all, honesty, forget what -- you know, it feels like some people are looking for a political excuse not to do this on some of these oil companies.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: To that -- I mean, it`s pretty obvious the next five to 10 years, there`s going to be plenty of people consuming fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why isn`t this seen as a good investment?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, again, because the regulatory side of it is so unpredictable. When you -- when you have pipelines, Chuck --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think that`s just -- that I`m looking for an excuse to not have to spend the money? I mean, that`s what it feels like to me.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: I would never meet--\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or are they using it as -- are they using it to get rid of it?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: No, I`d never even met an oilman that doesn`t want to produce more. In many cases, the oil man that I know, oil people that I know, they overproduce as soon as the price gets high if they`re able to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Which of course brings the price back down. They`re built to do that. The problem is under this administration, the market signals have been so negative that there`s not a relationship as Hadley talked about. There are these signals like blaming them, I mean, blaming the oil companies for the rising prices of gas. They don`t benefit on the rising prices of gas. They do benefit on the rising price of oil but only if they can produce it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: The natural incentive is to get out of the way and let them produce more, and you can do that responsibly. In fact, it`s irresponsible to put this on other countries that are bigger polluters than we are, whether it`s Venezuela or Russia or African countries, or enemies like Iran. The last thing we want to do is empower them. So we can do it here in the United States, but the president has to change his tone as much as his policies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I feel like us and Canada are the only, quote, "good actors" around the world that have an oil supply.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cramer, I wanted to ask you about this response you gave to a reporter about former President Trump`s decision yesterday to call on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to release information, whatever information he might have on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . Why would you want that information? Why would you trust propaganda from somebody who is, you know, right now based on the conversation I had earlier, President Biden called for him to be removed from power?\par \par \uc2 Why would you want to trust anything? Why would we want to have Putin at all, and why would you want to trust anything Putin had to say about Hunter Biden?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, sort of interesting because Russian collusion, if we were going to believe everything that was coming out of Russia a few years ago when it was about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But that said, my answer to the question was more sarcastic than it was anything because I said, this is a war criminal that he`s suggesting provides evidence that there`s bene a crime committed by \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and by the Bidens.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you think the former president -- so what you`re saying is that was sarcasm, you believe the former president shouldn`t be -- I mean, I`m sorry, it comes across as almost a bit unpatriotic to ask literally the enemy of the free world right now for propaganda for a political opponent. I mean, it just seemed like a horrendous thing to do by the former president. No?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Chuck, here`s the reality. If Hunter Biden`s name was \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , you guys would be treating the war in Ukraine like there was voice of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: No what about -- enough of this. That is -- Senator, that is the laziest attack. It`s not about the media.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: It`s also honest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It doesn`t matter about the media.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: It`s also honest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it the right thing to do for the former president of the United States to ask an enemy of the free world to do this?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, it`s -- what the former president is doing is not being compared to what the current president is doing in this circumstance. That`s really the bigger issue. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He hasn`t changed. He`s not going to change. It`s more interesting to the Washington press corps than it is to the general public. Was it the wisest thing in the world to say? Perhaps not, but he`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he says these things.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a war criminal. I wouldn`t trust anything that came out of it. If he had actual evidence that a crime was committed, if any of us had evidence that a crime was committed, we ought to present it, that`s obvious. The problem is, to your point, the source would not be a trustworthy source, obviously, but once again \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  baited you all and you all took the bait. And now we`re having to, you know, now having a discussion about this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I`m not going to debate you about whether Trump gets too much coverage or not enough. But he is the leader of your party, sir. At the end of the day, whether you like it or not, he`s the leader of your party. Are you comfortable with the leader of your party playing footsie with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, here`s what I know. Playing footsie with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the evidence that has come out of the recent investigation shows that there`s evidence the Democrats fed information into the White House to plant false --\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. Again --\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: He colluded with the -- you`re never interested in those things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does that make what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did -- how does that make what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing somehow better?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, first of all, it doesn`t necessarily make it better, but here`s a former president. We have a current president who`s called for regime changes in Russia. That`s dangerous. We have a current president who says that sanctions are never intended or would never deter \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . We`re all going, why did we issue sanctions then? And so, no, I`m way more concerned and I think the American people are with the current president and his behavior and really the lack of scrutiny compared to a former president who has a long history of saying sort of outrageous things, whether seriously or not seriously, and being taken way, way, way too seriously, frankly, by a press corps that`s all too anxious to jump on an interesting personality to say the least.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well --\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: But I`ll also add, Chuck, that when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, Russia didn`t invade Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Russia`s invaded Ukraine only when the Bidens have either been vice president or president. So, you know, I kind of like that style of leadership. And by the way, he brought the price of oil and gas down by unleashing American innovation in energy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again, look, rationalize on that stuff, that`s a political argument. That`s fair. Again, working with the enemy seems to be a bridge too far.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2 , Republican from North Dakota, appreciate you coming on. You always take my questions.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Always my pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: I appreciate you. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, you got it.\par \par \uc2 As we await remarks from President Biden, the latest U.S. intel that suggests Putin has no real idea how Russia is really performing in Ukraine right now, the chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee, Senator Mark Warner, is here after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We are now just a few minutes away from President Biden`s expected announcement of new efforts to combat rising energy costs and inflation.\par \par \uc2 With me now, Senator Mark Warner, Democrat from Virginia, chair of the Senate Intel Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator Warner, let me start with what we`re going to hear from the president and this issue of how to encourage American oil companies to increase production as fast as possible and this sort of, you know, they don`t want to invest, you`re a former -- you know, you were in the business sector, capital investments, you get it, you know, your costs go up, your share prices go down. We all understand they`re worried about their own -- their shareholders, and many a CEO gets fired faster than they should because of shareholder revolt.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 But what is the best way to incentivize these oil companies? Is this idea of penalizing them for not using leases a good idea?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Well, Chuck, I want to address that, but I just can`t bite my tongue about what happened in the segment before the break. I think it is both despicable and pathetic that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out appealing to a war criminal, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , when he is a war criminal against not only the people of Ukraine but against democracies around the world. His behavior is despicable and again pathetic.\par \par \uc2 This is the same \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that I remember when we brought together countries who had been attacked by Russia in misinformation, and we all gathered in Washington on the very day that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  met with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in 2018 in Helsinki and was a total lapdog to Putin and basically sided with Putin over all of our American intelligence agencies. And it`s the same \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that basically would not provide Ukraine with defensive weapons for years because he wanted to extract a political promise from Zelenskyy to help him on his -- getting dirt on Biden.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, anyone that is defending Trump`s recent activities of appealing to Putin, I don`t know where you get off on that. And I just -- my blood boils when I think about what Trump has done and a pattern that has continued literally for years. Now let`s move to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Senator, I want to stay on this topic a minute. And I`ll get you -- I want to stay in this topic a minute. Do you think it`s been a mistake to overly censor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the stuff he`s been saying? You know, earlier this week, he also came out and admitted that, yes, I don`t like \uc2 NATO\uc2  much. Even now, where you`re sitting here and it was just, you know, hit me like a ton of bricks. \uc2 NATO\uc2  has been -- this is the most effective \uc2 NATO\uc2  has looked in a decade or more.\par \par \uc2 And to just also trash \uc2 NATO\uc2 , a former U.S. president doing it over the last 70 -- literally in the last 96 hours, you know, de-platforming him has sort of protected the public, right, allows people to say hey, no one cares about it, because I understand people have compartmentalized him. You know, there`s this idea that he can be incendiary. But sometimes, this is what happens when you try to censor.\par \par \uc2 If you try to censor even the good stuff and you think it`s a good idea, are we inadvertently de-escalating the threat that he really presents?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And Chuck, it`s a very fair question. And I`ve got to tell you, \uc2 NATO\uc2  a year ago when Biden came in was broken. And the ability to put this coalition back together again in a united way when you`ve got the Germans actually paying their fair share and taking on \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , that took work. And I don`t know the answer to the question. I mean, I don`t want Trump out there being able to violate the very standards of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and I think they need some standards.\par \par \uc2 And clearly we`re not silencing -- he has an outlet on some of these far- right wing media where he`s speaking to people that believe this garbage, but when the Ukrainian people are literally, as I believe, not just fighting for their freedom, but they`re fighting on behalf of democracies across the world, and you have a former president of the United States once again kowtowing to Putin and asking for, you know, political dirt and undermining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , I never thought -- I never thought I`d see this behavior.\par \par \uc2 How you regulate or deal with that I wish I had a better answer for you. I don`t. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a lot of people in this country that are rightly outraged about a former German chancellor playing footsie with Putin. It`s outrageous when a former German chancellor does it. And it`s outrageous when a former U.S. president does it as well.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, though, the first question that I asked you, which is, is this the proper way to incentivize the oil industry to increase production, which is what the president`s going to ask you guys to do, to essentially put a time stamp on these leases, if you don`t, you know, use them, you get fined?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, let me give you two or three things he`s going to do. One, the million barrels a day releasing from the Strategic Reserve, that`s a good effort. It is a bit of a band-aid but, it`s, you know, it`s something we can do right now. On the lease --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that better than a gas tax holiday?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Yes, I absolutely believe --\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I`m very reluctant to go to gas tax holiday. Releasing the strategic -- this is a strategic time to release those barrels of oil. On the leases, they are basically acquired at a very cheap rate. These guys sit on these assets, waiting for a time to use them. So the idea that if you don`t use them there`s going to be a fee assessed to you I think makes sense. But I do agree with Senator Cramer that if we`re going to put that fee, we`ve also got to have the Biden administration to allow them to be permitted in a rapid enough fashion that they can actually move into production.\par \par \uc2 You can`t have it both ways. You can`t put the fee on and then hold them up on the permits. The other thing, one of the things I`ve been disappointed with the Biden administration not making -- bringing more attention to, in the last two weeks, the Biden administration has issued two export liquefied natural gas permits for us to take some of our gas and get it to Europe, to get them off Russian oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Germans are paying seven times more for natural gas than it is in our country. Getting them off Russian gas and exporting some of that, I`m not sure why Biden hasn`t made that. It shows we`re all in with our Europeans, it also addresses some of the Republican arguments that have been made out there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you ready to make the investments in our ports in order to -- I mean, the process to essentially ship liquefied natural gas, it`s my understanding that this is part of the holdup here, that not everybody is ecstatic about the amount of investment it would take to essentially make this viable to ship across the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: We have one of these export facilities already, but when you`ve got a 7 to 1 arbitrage between the same amount of cubic feet in gas price in Germany versus America and we have a lot of gas, I think the markets, as long as you give the permits -- and they`ve issued these permits. I mean, they`ve done it. Why not go ahead and at least let everybody know? Because long term, we want to get Germany and all of the Europeans off the Russian oil and gas on a permanent basis.\par \par \uc2 And as you said again before the break, there are for few democracies, as you said, America and Canada, that have got these resources, and frankly, you know, I`d rather have them --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WARNER: All right. Deal. I`d rather them use democratic energies. And that does not mean that we should in any way slow our movement to deal with climate change and greener energy. But there`s going to be this transition.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. We`re about to hear from the president. The issue of more anti-aircraft weaponry. Look, let`s set the MiG issue aside, you were in this brief briefing, what is the delay here?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, first of all, we are literally at tens of thousands of anti- tank tools that have gotten to the Ukrainians, not just from us but from others. Thousands of Stingers and other shoulder -- human vein based short range, we do need to get more of these S-300s, but before European countries like Slovakia gives up that weapon --\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: They`ve got to get it backfilled.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve got to cut you off. We`ve got to go to the president. Thank you, sir, as always.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Here at home to brutally and savagely invade a sovereign nation. The fact is, he`s causing thousands of deaths and untold destruction.\par \par \uc2 Working with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies and our European partners and beyond that, we -- we`re responding. We`re aiding the Ukrainian people both economically and militarily while levying the most punishing economic sanctions against Russia ever used against another nation in place and increasing them. Thus far, these actions are crippling Russia`s economy, isolating Putin from the world, and helping Ukrainians fight for their country and ease their suffering.\par \par \uc2 But, as I`ve said from the start, Putin`s war is imposing a cost on America and our allies and democracies around the world. Today, I want to talk about one aspect of Putin`s war that affects and has real effects on the American people. Putin`s price hike that Americans and our allies are feeling at the pump.\par \par \uc2 I know how much it hurts. As you`ve heard me say before, I grew up in a family, like many of you, where the price of a gallon of gasoline went up, it was discussed at the kitchen table. Our family budgets, your family budgets to fill a tank, none of it should hinge on whether a dictator declares war.\par \par \uc2 So, today, I`m laying out a two-part plan not only to ease the pain that families are feeling right now, but to end this era of dependence and uncertainty, and to lay a new foundation for true and lasting American energy independence.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 Parenthetically, just imagine if, in fact, Europe didn`t have to count on Russian oil, if they were energy independent. It would change the nature of so much. The problem we`re facing with gas prices has two roots. First, the pandemic. When COVID struck, demand for oil plummeted, so production slowed down worldwide. Because of the strength and the speed of our recovery, demand for oil shot back up much faster than the supply. That`s why the cost of gas began to rise last year.\par \par \uc2 The second root is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . At the start of this year, gas was about $3.30 a gallon. Today, it`s about averaging $4.20, $4.22. It`s higher in many states. Nearly a dollar more in less than three months. And the reason for that is because of Putin`s war. And now, many people are no longer buying Russian oil around the world. I banned the Russian import of oil here in America. Republicans and Democrats in Congress called for it and supported it.\par \par \uc2 It was the right thing to do. But as I said at the time, it`s going to come with a cost. As Russian oil comes off the global market, supply of oil drops and prices are rising. Now Putin`s price strike -- price hike is hitting Americans at the pump, which brings me to the first part of my plan, to immediately increase the supply of oil. Our prices are rising because of Putin`s actions. There isn`t enough supply. And the bottom line is, if we want lower gas prices, we need to have more oil supply right now.\par \par \uc2 For U.S. oil companies that are recording their largest profits in years, they have a choice. One, they can put those profits to productive use by producing more oil, restarting idle wells, or producing on the sites they already are leasing, giving the American people a break by passing some of the savings on to their customers and lowering the price at the pump. Or they can, as some of them are doing, exploit the situation, sit back, ship those profits to their investors, and -- while American families struggle to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a moment of consequence and peril for the world and pain at the pump for American families. It`s also a moment of patriotism. I want to acknowledge those companies that have already announced they`re increasing immediate production. They`re investing money to produce more oil and also clean technologies we need to reduce our dependence on oil in the future.\par \par \uc2 They have everything they need. Nothing is standing in their way. And they`ve indicated they will be producing an extra one million barrels of oil per day, probably starting as early as this fall. That`s progress. But some companies have been pretty blunt. They don`t want to increase supply because Putin`s price hike means higher profits. One CEO even acknowledged that they don`t care if the price of a barrel of oil goes to $200 a barrel. They`re not going to step up the production.\par \par \uc2 I say enough. Enough of lavishing excessive profits on investors and payouts and buybacks when the American people are watching, the world is watching. U.S. oil companies made nearly $80 billion in profit last year. And this year, those profits are expected to continue to soar. This is a time -- not the time to sit on record profits. It`s time to step up for the good of your country, the good of the world, to invest in immediate production that we need to respond to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ; to provide some relief for your customers, not investors and executives.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m a capitalist. I have no problem with corporations turning a good profit. But companies have an obligation that goes beyond just their shareholders. To their customers, their communities, and their country. No American company should take advantage of a pandemic or Vladimir Putin`s actions to enrich themselves at the expense of American families.\par \par \uc2 Investing those profits in production and innovation, that`s what they should do. Invest in your customers. And it isn`t just like -- it`s not the patriotic thing to do, it`s good for your business as well. Right now, the oil and gas industry is sitting on nearly 9,000 unused but approved permits for production on federal lands. There are more than a million unused acres they have a right to pump on.\par \par \uc2 Families can`t afford that companies sit on these -- their hands. So to help execute this first part of my plan, I`m calling for a "use it or lose it" policy. Congress should make companies pay fees on wells on federal leases they haven`t used in years and acres of public land they`re hoarding without production.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 Companies that are already producing from these wells won`t be affected. But those sitting on unused leases and idle wells will either have to start producing or pay the price for their inaction.\par \par \uc2 Look, the action I`m calling for will make a real difference over time. But the truth is it takes months, not days, for companies to increase production. That`s why the next part of my plan is so important. Today, I`m authorizing the release of one million barrels per day for the next six months -- over 180 million barrels for the Strategic -- from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.\par \par \uc2 This is a wartime bridge to increase oil supply until production ramps up later this year. And it is by far the largest release from our national reserve in our history. It will provide a historic amount of supply for a historic amount of time, a six-month bridge to the fall. And we`ll use the revenue from selling the oil now to restock the Strategic Petroleum Reserve when prices are lower so we`ll be ready, we`ll be ready for future emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I`ve coordinated this release with allies and partners around the world. Already, I have -- we have commitments from other countries to release tens of millions of additional barrels into the market. Together, our combined efforts will supply well over a million barrels a day. Nations coming together to deny Putin the ability to weaponize his energy resources against American families and families and democracies around the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, for the first part of my plan is about meeting an immediate crisis. The second part is about declaring real American energy independence in the long term so that we never have to deal with this problem again. Ultimately, we and the whole world need to reduce our dependence on fossil fuels altogether. We need to choose long-term security over energy and climate vulnerability. We need to double down on our commitment to clean energy and tackling the climate crisis with our partners and allies around the world.\par \par \uc2 And we can do that by passing my plan that is literally before the Senate right now, the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , right now. It`s been there for well over a month. To speed the transition to a clean energy future that is made in America with American products and American values. We need to embrace all the tools and technologies that can help us, free us from our dependence on fossil fuels and move us toward a more homegrown clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Technologies made by American companies and American workers so we can bolster democratic supply, excuse me, domestic supply chains here at home and export those technologies around the world to reduce greenhouse gases. That`s why, today, I am issuing a directive to strengthen our clean energy economy. I`m going to use the Defense Production Act to secure American supply chains for the critical materials that go into batteries for electric vehicles and the storage of renewable energy, lithium, graphite, nickel, and so much more.\par \par \uc2 We need to end our long-term reliance on China and other countries for inputs that will power the future. And I`ll use every tool I have to make that happen. Yes, building a made-in-America clean energy future will help safeguard our national security. Yes, it will help us tackle climate change. Yes, it`s going to help us ensure that America creates millions of good-paying jobs for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 But most important, the most important thing my plan will do right away is save your family money. And here`s what I mean. Under my plan, which is before Congress now, we can take advantage of the next generation of electric vehicles that a typical driver will save about $80 a month from not having to pay gas at the pump. If your home is powered by safer, cheaper, cleaner electricity, like solar or heat pumps, you can save about $500 a month on average.\par \par \uc2 Don`t take my word for it. The CEOs of 11 of America`s largest utility companies came to see me at the White House several weeks ago. They told me if we pass my plans before the Congress now, typical families will see savings show up in their utility bills immediately. And costs will come down even more as we innovate and develop cutting-edge energy storage technologies, clean hydrogen technology, advanced nuclear technology, carbon capture and sequestration technologies.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, this week, the benefit I included in the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law to help families weatherize their homes are being delivered. My administration is making $3.2 billion available from this legislation to provide up to $6,500 direct payment for working-class families to be able to weatherize their homes, to save them money, to keep them warm in the winter and cool in the summer. It`s a direct grant.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220401ei3v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press Daily For March 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Engel, Shannon Pettypiece, Garrett Haake,Chris Jansing,  Garrett Haake \par \uc2 8,418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:30]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Return from meetings in Europe, with U.S. military leaders and Ukrainian refugees.\par \par \uc2 And later, a historic week in Washington, as the confirmation hearings for Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson, the first Black female Supreme Court pick, have officially begun on Capitol Hill. How hard are Republicans willing to go in an attempt to disrupt her nomination? We`re about to find out.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Garrett Haake, in for Chuck Todd at the start of a consequential week for both NATO and the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 At any moment, we expect the White House to give us more details about President Biden`s conversation this morning with fellow NATO leaders from France, Germany, Italy and the United Kingdom. It comes just two days before the president heads to Brussels for an emergency NATO summit to address the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 He will then head to Poland to meet with Polish President Duda, whose country sits at the center of a massive refugee crisis. In recent days, Russia has intensified its assault on cities all across Ukraine, including in the West, near the Polish border, with many of those attacks targeting civilians, according to Ukrainian officials.\par \par \uc2 There was a shopping center in Kyiv, a mental health center in Mykolaiv, and several homes in Odessa. A senior Pentagon official tells NBC News that Russia`s military advance has not made significant advances over the last 24 hours. In fact, the Pentagon`s current assessment is that Russia hasn`t achieved any of its military objectives so far.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has rejected Russian demands to surrender the southern city of Mariupol. Officials believe that as many as 300,000 people remain trapped there, with 90 percent of the city destroyed.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine`s capital city, Mayor Klitschko has imposed a new curfew from now until Wednesday, as shelling in and around Kyiv intensifies. Meanwhile, this morning, at the \uc2 United States Holocaust Museum\uc2 , Secretary of State Tony Blinken called out Russia for its completely false claim that the war is about ending genocide by the Ukrainian government.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: I can recall few times when that history felt so urgent or the responsibility it imparts on all of us so pressing.\par \par \uc2 As we meet, the Russian government continues to wage its unprovoked, brutal war in Ukraine. The Kremlin has tried to justify this war by falsely claiming that it`s intervening to stop genocide, abusing the term that we reserved for the greatest atrocities, disrespecting every victim of this heinous crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And now, despite Ukrainian President Zelenskyy`s openness to meeting directly with Vladimir Putin, a Kremlin spokesman says both sides have a long way to go before that meeting can take place, and they say peace talks have yielded no significant progress.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Richard Engel in Kyiv and Shannon Pettypiece at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And, Richard, I will start with you.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine rejected this Russian offer to surrender Mariupol? Do the Ukrainians have confidence they can keep Mariupol from falling under Russian control? Or is this just about fighting for every inch of their country?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: It`s about fighting for every inch of their country.\par \par \uc2 Mariupol is a significant city. It has 400,000 people in it -- or had 400,000 people in it. And it is one of Vladimir Putin`s main objectives, because it lies right in between Russian territory and Crimea. So, if Russia can take Mariupol, then it could effectively establish a land bridge between the Russian border and Crimea, which it annexed.\par \par \uc2 But taking areas -- and Russia has been able to take very, very few areas - - and holding them are completely different. So, even if Russia were to be able -- were able to take Mariupol -- and it has made the city unlivable -- it is unlikely that it would be able to hold it or fully pacify the population, at least based on what we`re seeing in other places.\par \par \uc2 Kherson, which is a smaller city, not very far from Mariupol, was captured by Russian forces, but almost every day, the people have been coming out onto the streets, protesting against Russian troops. And it is not just in Kherson. There was an extraordinary scene today where hundreds of people came out to protest. They were walking up against -- walking up to Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And the Russian troops started firing loudly into the air, throwing what looked like stun grenades and smoke bombs into the crowds. At least one person was injured, shot in the leg. The Russians, at least in this case, didn`t level their guns and fire directly into the crowds.\par \par \uc2 But it shows that they`re having serious population controls in the small and -- small and few places that they are controlling. So we are now over three weeks into this offensive. And Russia has achieved very few of its military gains. And it is continuing now instead to pound population centers, to hit the center of Kyiv, driving this city once again into a curfew, which is going to start very soon and last for the next 35 hours or so.\par \par \uc2 But they have not managed to break this country. They haven`t managed to take key population centers like Kyiv. And the places that they have taken, they don`t control.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Richard, can you expand on how you`re seeing the tactics change, with more and more of these civilian areas like where you are in Kyiv being targeted?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: So, the Russian military strategy, which has been complicated, confusing, disorganized, not just according to Ukrainian and American officials -- it has been quite clear that it has been disorganized here from the beginning -- has changed repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 In the opening days of the operation, when everyone thought that it was going to be quick, that the Russian army, numerically so superior to the Ukrainian army ,would quickly roll over this country, quickly capture Kyiv coming down from Belarus, not only did that not happen; the Russian advance quickly stalled. So they changed tactics.\par \par \uc2 They went after cities in large -- with large numbers of troops, attacking Kharkiv, attacking Mariupol. That hasn`t been successful. So now the Russians are more or less stalled. They`re dug in, and they are just lobbing weapons into cities, Kyiv included, with the hope, it seems, that they can punish the government, inflict enough pain that they can extract higher prices at peace negotiations.\par \par \uc2 No indications at this stage that either side is very close to peace negotiations. But the Russians are trying to at least use some of their muscle to improve their hand. And it would be a very significant move for them if they could take and keep Mariupol. But, just today, as we started out, the Ukrainian government rejected that out of hand.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Shannon Pettypiece, it seems like President Biden has been in more or less constant contact with his European counterparts over the last couple of weeks. He had another call this morning with European leaders.\par \par \uc2 Do we know anything about what came out of that?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, NBC DIGITAL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Yes, we haven`t gotten a readout yet from the White House as to what was discussed.\par \par \uc2 And I certainly anticipate that will come up in the press briefing in about an hour from now. Certainly, this G7 group of world leaders is a very significant group for the president to be talking to once again, and he will also be meeting them in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just a little reminder of how things have changed, a few years ago, during the Trump administration, then-President Trump was talking about having Putin join the G7, to join this group of leaders that is now talking about how they can economically, humanitarian -- from a humanitarian standpoint, militarily come together to support Ukraine and fend off this Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the president is going to be, as you noted, in Europe next week. He will not only meet with these G7 leaders, but the NATO leaders, European allies. And White House officials, administration officials have signaled that those meetings are, of course, going to be about this coordinated response and what more these world leaders can do as we approach one month of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 But it is also very much, they say, about showing a united front, about reassuring the NATO allies that the U.S. will indeed come to defend any NATO country if Russia and Putin are to expand this invasion beyond Ukraine into NATO territory.\par \par \uc2 And there`s definitely an indication they hope that could be a deterrence to Russia and Putin from expanding this any further. Of course, they are focused on trying to resolve this conflict, trying to assist Ukraine in any way they can for the current conflict.\par \par \uc2 But there is also a big element of this that is about trying to deter any future aggression by Putin into those NATO countries.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Shannon Pettypiece at the White House, Richard Engel in Kyiv, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And I`m joined now by West Virginia`s Republican senator, Shelley Moore Capito. She was a member of the congressional delegation that traveled to Germany and to Poland over this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 And, Senator Capito, thanks for being with us, now that you`re back.\par \par \uc2 I`m just curious at the start here, I mean, what struck you most about what you saw and what you heard while you were in the region?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:02]\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELLEY MOORE CAPITO (R-WV): Well, a couple of things, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 And thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 First of all, this is an unjust and immoral war, where Putin is bombing and killing children and families. Second, the Ukrainian people are united and they`re fighters, and they`re going to fight to the end.\par \par \uc2 Third, and not necessarily in this order, but NATO is strong, and NATO is together, and probably in a much more powerful position to have a united position than probably since World War II.\par \par \uc2 And then the -- also that the United States is doing so much, but Poland is incredible as a partner, as a military, and also as a humanitarian partner. We went to refugee centers, saw those families. It`s heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Talk to me more about this refugee crisis.\par \par \uc2 I -- the reporting has been so excellent, but I feel like we`re still only seeing the tip of the iceberg about how bad the situation might get there. What did you see at these refugee centers? And are we in the West, in the U.S. prepared for what might be a very long-term effort to support these people?\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: Well, what we`re seeing is, on the Polish border, which is where we were, at that refugee center, we saw probably 1,500 families, mostly women and children, elderly as well.\par \par \uc2 We saw NGOs and others feeding as much as 6,000 meals per day. And then they are sort of processing into this and then moving into either Poland, Germany, or the -- the woman that I spoke with was going to the U.K. with her 6- and 8-year-old. She left her husband, her home, her parents behind two miles from the Russian border.\par \par \uc2 It`s so compelling. She kept saying: "Please help us. Please do more."\par \par \uc2 And I think the human crisis of millions of people leaving that country has only begun to sink in.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you think there will be more for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to do, for the administration to do?\par \par \uc2 You guys passed $14 billion, nearly, in aid just a week or two ago now. The president`s started to spend some of that money, $800 million, I think, for Ukraine`s military. How much more can Congress, should the administration do to help on both those fronts, the humanitarian and the military front?\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: I think there`s no question we can and will do more.\par \par \uc2 They gave us a list, one of the groups we met with, a list of more Javelins, Stingers, helicopters, air support, anti-aircraft. These are the things that our $14 billion is going to go towards on the military and lethal side. But we need to keep doing this. And I think that we don`t want to give them just enough to stay in the fight.\par \par \uc2 We have to win this. They have to win this. And they`re going to fight to the end. And so I think that, on the humanitarian side, our NGOs are moving fast. Time is another issue. When we think of, oh, in two weeks, we`re going to do this, well, we`re 23 days into this, so every day is crucial.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Can enough be done for Ukraine to win this only with the U.S. sending money and materiel?\par \par \uc2 The discussion about a no-fly zone continues to come up. It continues to be knocked down. Do you think we might ultimately be headed that direction? And would you support such a thing maybe as a red line for some future act in that country?\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: You know, at this -- you know, at this point, it is not just the United States. So we need to remember that.\par \par \uc2 These are the NATO nations. This is their, not backyard, their front yard.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: And they know that, if Putin is successful, who`s next?\par \par \uc2 Is it the Baltic states? Is it Moldova? This is a question that was recurring. So what stops him? And then would he use nuclear weapons, which is a very critical question here.\par \par \uc2 I don`t see us going to a no-fly zone. I think that, if we equip the Ukrainians with what they need, as NATO and as a coalition, ramp up even more sanctions on the oligarchs, I think that we can put more pressure on Putin than he`s felt right now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What was your conversation like in Poland and in Germany about the possibility that this might spill over and become a NATO-involved conflict, either by intentional Russian aggression or by miscalculation down the line?\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: Well, I would say that, obviously, everybody`s on heightened alert about that.\par \par \uc2 And, as I said, this is -- this is their front yard. Certainly, Poland is - - would be, I can think, considered at the tip of the spear with the involvement they`re doing with moving humanitarian goods, with moving people in and out, with staging NATO troops along -- that include American troops in and around.\par \par \uc2 We have always had a training presence there. And we maintain that. So I think it`s more of a readiness question, quite frankly. We have got to be ready. Hopefully, we won`t have to use that as a readiness measure, that we -- we`re prepared, but not have to move in that direction.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think we can sit back at this point and think there`s nothing he won`t do. Oh, he won`t come into NATO. I think he`s shown us, if he can kill women and children and innocents in a country like Ukraine, he will do anything.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Senator, while I have you, I got to ask you about the other big thing going on, on Capitol Hill today, the Supreme Court confirmation hearing for Judge Jackson.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:01]\par \par \uc2 You`re not on the Judiciary Committee, but you did meet with her last week. I`m curious if you consider her qualified for the High Court, and if you are still open to potentially supporting her after these hearings are done.\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: I did have the pleasure of meeting her.\par \par \uc2 She is qualified. She has a very distinguished, not only academic record, but also her judicial career has been filled with decisions. So it`s not like we can -- we`re going to have to guess what direction or what -- what leanings she might have.\par \par \uc2 I think what we will see here is the Judiciary Committee asking questions. I`m going to reserve what I`m going to do at the end after I hear that, but I do think judicial philosophy is a part of it. I`m remaining open-minded.\par \par \uc2 But she is a very impressive woman, and I found her quite engaging.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I have to ask you about one of your colleagues who`s been very public with his criticisms of Judge Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Josh Hawley has been out there talking about her record on sentencing sex offenders. Her sentencing has been similar to what other federal judges have done. I wonder if you think that`s an unfair line of attack from Senator Hawley, whom our viewers are seeing with his opening statement now.\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: I don`t know the details of what Senator Hawley is basing his conjectures on, quite honestly.\par \par \uc2 I know that she told me more than a few times when I would bring questions about certain issues, she said: "Please look at my record. Please" -- because I would ask her about getting cases overturned, and what does this mean and how to frame it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we have got to stick to the facts here. I certainly think that the best thing that we can do is treat her with the dignity that she deserves. If we disagree with her and her -- what -- how she might serve as a Supreme Court justice, that`s what we should be basing our decision on.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Senator Capito, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 CAPITO: Thanks, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And we will have much more on the start of today`s historic Supreme Court hearings later in this hour.\par \par \uc2 And to livestream the full hearing on any device, you can scan the Q.R. code you see on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 Up next: As President Biden prepares to meet with U.S. allies in Europe this week, we will speak with a top government official from one NATO nation that used to be a Soviet republic.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:21]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s upcoming trip to Europe will be an important display of unity among the U.S. and our allies against Russian aggression. It comes as some NATO countries, particularly the Baltics, push the West to do more in security assistance for Ukraine and for its neighbors.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the parliaments in Estonia, Lithuania and Latvia, all NATO countries that were once under the thumb of Soviet rule, all passed resolutions urging the U.N. to declare a no-fly zone over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Here on set with me is one of the lawmakers behind those resolutions. Rihards Kols is a member of Latvia`s Parliament and the chair of its Foreign Affairs Committee. He`s been in D.C. testifying on the Hill about Russian aggression and Baltic security.\par \par \uc2 So, Mr. Kols, thank you for being with us here.\par \par \uc2 I want to start on this issue of a no-fly zone. You have got the Parliaments in all three of these countries who`ve come out in favor of it. But the presidents of these countries don`t support it. What`s the politics behind that? And what`s your belief in whether or not this should or could be put in place?\par \par \uc2 RIHARDS KOLS, LATVIA PARLIAMENT MEMBER: Well, first of all, we are parliamentary democracies. And this is actually a call from our people, not just politicians.\par \par \uc2 So, therefore, of course, we are delivering of what our society is demanding of politicians. I know the rationale on a certain no-fly zone, particularly in the U.N., Russia having veto and et cetera.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KOLS: But I guess, if Baltic countries had the capabilities that the rest of NATO member states do have, like, let`s say, the major players, we would not hesitate to actually provide these no-fly zones over the corridors for humanitarian dimensions and also when it comes to nuclear power plants that are 15 of them in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And if something happens with those, it`s not just a disaster in Ukraine. It will affect the rest of the world as well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Aggressive is not the right word, but I feel like the Baltic countries have a much more robust posture towards Putin.\par \par \uc2 What do you know about Russian aggression that the West is -- the rest of the West is learning now?\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Well, first thing is that you cannot trust and you cannot even dive into those lies that are coming from Russia.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you have to be very clear on your defense posture. And when it comes to, right now, discussions on what might be the next plans for Putin, I think this is precisely what Putin wants us to do, to be preoccupied of thinking what might be his next steps.\par \par \uc2 I think what the West has to do right now is actually put on the table all the worst scenarios, and, accordingly, prepare -- preparation works, and et cetera, because I think the worst thing right now we have done so far -- it`s not the worst, but it`s not a good thing -- is that we have said already what we`re not going to do.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KOLS: And this is something for Putin is always going after -- if you hesitate, he will strike.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So you have been here meeting with lawmakers talking about security needs what. What does Latvia, what do the Baltics need from NATO and the United States?\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Well, from the United States particularly, that is -- last week, we were -- three Baltic chairs of the foreign affairs visited capital. And the main message was that we need to have a more larger U.S. troops presence in the Baltics, not...\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Permanent troops presence?\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Permanent, exactly, permanent, because one thing that Putin really fears is U.S. army, absolutely, no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are very appreciative already of the commitments \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has made. We have U.S. troops on the ground. But, I mean, when it comes to numbers, it`s still not enough deterrence for Russia and for the -- Putin as well.\par \par \uc2 Another dimension is, well, as we all observe what`s happening in Ukraine, how Russia is conducting the war on Ukraine, we look at its strengths and its weaknesses. And we see the Russia strength is the supremacy over the air.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Baltic countries, I mean, we have the same weakness. That is the air defense systems. So this is what we are calling right now urgently to address that, because on top of all these things that we are doing, we are supporting militarily Ukraine as well, sending Javelins, sending different air -- the air defense manpower -- MANPAD systems, whereas which means our stocks are empty.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. That`s a hard calculation when you`re...\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOLS: And now, in alliance, we have to understand we are right now the frontier defending democracy, the West.\par \par \uc2 So, therefore, right now, either we stand or we fall. So, therefore, all we ask is really, really rational. It`s not just making panic. This is really assessment of the security dimensions that we are seeing in our region, because we don`t -- we can`t forget that, on top of Russia`s invasion in Ukraine, Belarus is playing a crucial part as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:16]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 KOLS: In our view and assessment, Belarus has become already 100 percent dependent on Russia, Russian Federation. I think it`s de facto already Russian Federation, particularly when it comes to military dimension.\par \par \uc2 I would call it -- it`s the sixth military district of Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 So we have got President Biden traveling there next week. What does he need to say? What does the world need? What does Latvia need? What do you want to hear from President Biden when he`s on the ground in Eastern Europe?\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Well, you see, this is exactly what I stated on the \uc2 Helsinki Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is -- I will use the Latin pronunciation. It`s the facta non verba. What is needed right now are deeds, not words. I mean, we have received reassurances when it comes to defending NATO member states, not an inch.\par \par \uc2 But I think what we have so look is go from the words to deeds, and really go among the allies, so, how more we can assist Ukraine, and also how more we can booster the defense capabilities on -- of entire eastern flank.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Rihards Kols, thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s all the time we have got. But thank you for your work here, and safe travels on your way back.\par \par \uc2 KOLS: Thank you very much, Garrett. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And up next, the highlights so far from this historic day on Capitol Hill, as confirmation hearings are officially under way for Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:17]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Welcome back to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chris Jansing, following the latest on the historic confirmation hearing for Supreme Court nominee Ketanji Brown Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Now we are live on Capitol Hill. Multiple Republicans already giving some pretty consistent clues about how they will question Judge Jackson over the coming days including on sentences for sex offenders. Senator Ted Cruz asking whether Jackson would defend the rights of conservative the same way she would defend the rights of liberals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Will you follow the law? What does your record indicate? Will you protect the rights of every American citizen regardless of race, regardless of party, regardless of views? That`s what the focus of this hearing should be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Democrats like Senator Chris Coons pushing back on that potential line of questioning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): In every case you methodically consider the party`s arguments, the relevant facts and the law, including of course the foundation of the Constitution. The text of applicable statutes and the precedence that apply. And ultimately from what I`ve seen, you come to a decision based on that methodology, not on any particular policy preferences or activism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now one line we`ve heard from both sides of the aisle is that this is not going to be a circus. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN SASSE (R-NE): A lot of people are willing to take a sledge hammer to the structure if it offers them a quick political win. We have an opportunity today to do something different and we can demonstrate that the Congress can carry out its constitutional duty responsibly without a lot of that past ugliness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That was one of the multiple, not-so-vague references of what Republicans believe was the tough questioning of now Justice Kavanaugh. Again something we heard from Republicans on several times already today.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to keep an eye on this hearing as it develops and bring you updates throughout the day. Garrett Haake will be here right after this quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:24]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Welcome back. You just heard the highlights from opening statements so far in the historic confirmation hearings of Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson. Now let`s dive a little deeper into what we can expect from the hearings this week and what Judge Jackson is facing as the first black woman to undergo the confirmation process for a seat on our country`s highest court.\par \par \uc2 For more on what we could see in these hearings, I`m joined by Melanie Campbell, president and CEO of the National Coalition on Black Civic Participation, and by Paul Butler, law professor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 And Melanie, I`ll start with you. Just give me your reaction to watching the start of these hearings and how do you think we`ll see that interplay between who Judge Jackson is being a woman and an African-American going through this and her lengthy legal resume?\par \par \uc2 MELANIE CAMPBELL, PRESIDENT AND CEO, NATIONAL COALITION ON BLACK CIVIC PARTICIPATION: Well, first of all, I think the moment is finally here so many of us exhaled, to be honest, and said, OK, this moment is really happening. It`s really happening. So that was my initial reaction. And then I started tuning in and listening to see the tone and the tenor. And I think, first of all, Judge Jackson.\par \par \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson, I love to say that name because it means so much, really showed very well a balance, even when things were being said that may not have been really pleasing to hear. She`s just focused on the task at hand to get through this confirmation hearing and a lot of us are watching to encourage Republicans, Democrats, everyone, to give her a fair hearing and not judge her on anything but her record. And that`s what we`re looking to see.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The Senate itself is a good reminder of the moment we`re in. Judge Jackson won`t be questioned by any black women because there aren`t any on the Judiciary Committee. And she won`t be voted on by any black women because there aren`t any in the entire \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . How does that play into what we`re going to see over the next few days and few weeks?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Well, we`re started out with a rally with the Black Women`s Roundtable and National Women`s Law Center. And she will rise in three other groups. That really is what we`re headed towards. It was so diverse. There was men there, women there, young, old, faith leaders, labor leaders, (INAUDIBLE), a very eclectic mix of what the melting pot of America really is. Very United States of America looking.\par \par \uc2 We don`t see that in the Senate yet. But, you know, women are moving. Black women are moving. We have a VP, right, president. And so I think what she`s going to break that glass ceiling will, as a black woman myself and as a woman, it`s something that I think our daughters and nieces will be able to see. And our nephews, right?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: To be able to see.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Very good point.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: That is how we should be if we say the will of justice to be for everyone. You have to see that in the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s a very good point.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: And so -- and we need to see more of that in the Senate, too.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, yes, that`s definitely true.\par \par \uc2 Paul, we have seen Judge Jackson go through the confirmation for other judicial positions before. But obviously this is the NFL, this is a whole other level of scrutiny. Republicans have started to preview a little bit of what they`re likely to ask her about it, and a lot of these cases she took on as a public defender, her role in reducing sentencing guidelines, and the rest of her judicial history. As our legal expert here, how do you expect her resume which is unique in some respects to hold up through this process?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: So, if this is the Super Bowl, then Judge Jackson is the most valuable player. She`s a seasoned pro at confirmation hearings. This is her fourth time at this rodeo and she`s a master. So, yes, the Republicans will try to come for her. They`ve pored through over 600 written opinions and 12,000 pages of hearings from the Sentencing Commission.\par \par \uc2 They`ve looked at everything she`s written or done or said in public and they got nothing, Garrett. And so that`s why they will try to change the subject to Judge Kavanaugh`s sentencing hearings or confirmation hearings way back when or President Biden`s record on crime because when they look at Judge Jackson`s record, it`s unimpeachable. When you`re the first, you have to be the best.\par \par \uc2 President Biden had an embarrassment of riches when selecting an African- American woman to be the first on the Supreme Court. So many qualified. He found one of the absolute best.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: One of the things that is interesting in many ways unique about her resume is her background as a federal public defender.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 And Paul, I`m curious as a former prosecutor, what do you make of the claims that as a public defender, you know, she was essentially working for the bad guys or that she would potentially be soft on crime as a justice?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: What judge Jackson did as a public defender and a lawyer and a judge is to stand up for the Constitution, which guarantees every accused person effective assistance of counsel and the right to a fair trial. That was her work as a public defender.\par \par \uc2 As a judge, Judge Jackson has ruled in favor of prosecutors many times. And she has three family members who are police officers. One was the chief of police in Miami, and of course the Fraternal Order of Police has endorsed her. So soft on crime, no way. Again, they`re grasping at straws, the Republicans are, because this is a person who has an unimpeachable record.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Melanie, you get the last word here. Judge Jackson`s appointment has been downplayed a little bit because it won`t change the balance of the court but it could still change the way this country operates and looks at itself in some ways, no?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Well, it`s a lifetime appointment. And I`ve lived long enough to know the pendulum does swing. And so I think that the fact that she also will be in there, whether she`s a dissenting opinion, right, or an affirming opinion, that lived experience that she will bring is valuable beyond measure. So I think people can get into -- well, it won`t change the bottom line. Yes, it does. It really does.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s going to be a very interesting couple of days to watch. Melanie Campbell, Paul Butler, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And up next, sizing up the political fallout from these confirmation hearings. My panel is next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:30]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The confirmation hearings for Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson are under way, casting a spotlight not just on the judge herself and the historic nature of her nomination, but there`s also several Republican members of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  who may be looking at these hearings as a chance to audition for a new job of their own.\par \par \uc2 Tom Cotton, Ted Cruz and Josh Hawley are all considered potential presidential contenders for 2024 on a mission to prove their GOP street cred and build a fundraising base, which probably means the question isn`t if we`ll see some fireworks at this hearings but how many will we see.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is "Politico" national correspondent Betsy Woodruff Swan, she`s also an MSNBC contributor, Republican strategist and MSNBC political analyst Rick Tyler is with us, and president of the Center for American Progress Action Fund, Navin Nayak, is here as well.\par \par \uc2 And Betsy, I`ll start with you. The Judiciary Committee has all of these Republicans on it who may or may not be running for president. It was similar with Democrats a cycle ago. Are we functionally in Iowa for the next couple of days? How do you see those ambitions affecting this process?\par \par \uc2 BETSY WOODRUFF SWAN, POLITICO NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Several days of Iowa is a really embracing fire.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`re not ready to think about that yet, I understand.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: Way too early, although I guess technically not. There`s no question that these are the most high-profile moments these senators are going to have likely this entire year in terms of the work that they`re doing. It`s already campaign and election season on Capitol Hill. There`s very little getting that`s getting done that doesn`t involve Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 These confirmation hearings, this one of course is uniquely historic but confirmation hearings are always these pivotal moments because it brings together all three branches of the government. It`s a presidential nominee, it`s the future of the Supreme Court, and it`s this uniquely powerful panel in the Senate that has this chance for these members to distinguish themselves.\par \par \uc2 What`s really interesting about Senate Judiciary, though, is that in addition to these three likely presidential contenders, you`ve also got some Republicans who have been a little more critical of Trump, Ben Sasse from Nebraska, perhaps most notably for being willing to go after the president. Mike Lee, who might face some political headwinds because of criticism that he made of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So ideologically, it`s kind of a cross section of the Republican Party and that`s just another piece of this that`s going to make the next few hearings really interesting.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Navin, in 2016, having an open Supreme Court seat was pivotal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becoming president, and how Republicans focused their voters on that seat. I wonder if you think during the intervening years Democrats have learned, perhaps are learning how to focus their voters on the courts in the same way?\par \par \uc2 NAVIN NAYAK, PRESIDENT, \uc2 CENTER FOR AMERICAN PROGRESS\uc2  ACTION FUND: Yes. I think there`s a real sense of enthusiasm actually on the Democratic side about her nomination, about the confirmation process actually showcasing a talented judge like this and the nomination process that I think is going to really highlight an accomplishment for Biden. I think there`s only really an upside to this for Democrats and a real risk for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Because I think that most likely the scenario is that this will be like some of the confirmations that actually got a lot less attention because her credentials are pretty unimpeachable and the people tuning in are going to be, you know, African-Americans and people who are really excited about seeing change in this country that, you know, it`s been long overdue. And I think the risk for Republicans is pretty high because there`s not really a record.\par \par \uc2 You know, Ted Cruz himself basically said that there`s nothing in her record that he can find to object to. And so I think there`s only real threats that they`re going to find, you know, fall into some traps that I think do them more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, to that end, Josh Hawley has found something in her record that he has tried to attack, and Rick Tyler, he got pushback from Andrew McCarthy at the "National Review" who wrote, and I`ll read from this, "There are strong philosophical arguments for opposing Judge Jackson`s nomination to the Supreme Court. And she may in fact be too solicitous of criminals. But the implication that she has a soft spot for sex offenders who prey on children because she argued against severe mandatory minimum prison sentence for the receipt and distribution of pornographic images is a smear."\par \par \uc2 So did Hawley go too far here or can you not go too far in the service of owning the libs and going after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, before I answer directly, let me put it in context because Josh Hawley, as you indicated in the opening, is auditioning to be president and is using these hearings to audition for president. So he wants to oppose her on these grounds and he wants to oppose the judge -- a nominee against President Biden. But I`ll take you back to 1987 where another member of the Judiciary Committee, in fact chaired it, used the Judiciary Committee as chairman to launch a presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 That was Judge Bork and that was President Biden who was chairman of the Judiciary Committee. So nothing`s really changed but at the same time I read McCarthy`s article and I think he`s right. I think that Hawley did actually try prosecute and sentenced her in tweets because there`s a lot of context missing, none of which we probably have time to explain why.\par \par \uc2 But if you are going to smear someone with the idea they are soft on child pornography, you damn well better be right and he wasn`t right, he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Betsy, soft on child porn out, but soft on crime probably in for Republicans as a messaging issue in 2024. We`ve heard Mitch McConnell kind of seize on that. How effective is that going to be as a message for them, do we think, looking ahead both for these midterms and for these presidential aspirants? And how much do you expect to see that issue get a test drive over the next few days?\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: Just going into the midterms, there`s no question that it`s going to be a central focus for Republicans and that people at the RNCC and the NRSC are very confident, at least based on the assessments they`re making that it`s something they expect to really capitalize on. In terms of this confirmation hearing, it`s a little tough because it`s hard to say wow, someone worked as a federal public defender, that makes them tough on crime.\par \par \uc2 Let`s remember there are numerous Republican, you know, politicians and officials who have availed themselves of criminal defense lawyers, pretty famously, in the last couple of years. You don`t really get to go after someone for just having worked in the criminal defense space. So it`s hard to see how that particular line of criticism could function in a meaningful way for Republicans going into these confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 That said of course we do know that congressional Democrats particularly moderates like Abigail Spanberger from central Virginia have raised concerns about some rhetoric within the Democratic Party in relation to the criminal justice issue. So it`s something that Democrats are going to have to be thinking clearly in terms of what do they really think is the right message to take, the right policy positions to take on this issue as they know Republicans are gearing up to make it a key focus of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Navin, for President Biden, this is another huge week, though it seems like a string of huge weeks where he has to do all of the jobs of being president, all at once at a very high level. He`s going to Eastern Europe, a lot of the focus has been on Ukraine, but how does the White House, how do Democrats make sure that if this is a win for them, they get credit for a win and it`s not just, oh, well, yes, sure, she was always going to get confirmed and we`ll just -- you know, it`s just sort of a flat, you know, check the box?\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Well, I think -- there`s a few things you mentioned, one, Garrett, is I think this is the kind of moment that I think the president in some ways has been waiting for to both showcase his preparedness for an international challenge, and I think, getting, you know, handling it incredibly well both in terms of foresight, rallying the world, really strong response, and still being able to, you know, do things, multiple things at once in terms of advancing a Supreme Court justice, tackling, you know, a lot of the challenges at home in terms of the economy.\par \par \uc2 So I think that`s one thing that actually I think is an opportunity for them to displace sort of his strength across the board, and I think there`s another challenge here which is they just have to go and talk about it, to your core question, right. I think that`s --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s always been the challenge for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do the thing and then go sell that you did the thing.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I think that`s it. I think, I mean, I think we`re learning this unfortunately a little bit in arrears when it comes to the infrastructure bill, which was, you know, the largest investment in infrastructure in ever, and the American people don`t realize it, right? The fact that we still see data showing that a majority of Americans don`t recognize that we`ve created six million jobs in the last year, a record number of jobs ever in the president`s first year, highlights that I think weakness and opportunity on our side to talk about actually some of the successes and then hopefully this confirmation is an opportunity to fix some of that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Rick, you`re going to jump out of your chair if I don`t let you respond to that.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: I would give Biden every credit for recovering six million jobs. I don`t think that he created six million jobs. The infrastructure, as you know, the reason people are not giving him a lot of credit, which he does actually deserve, is because both Republicans and Democrats have failed on infrastructure for decades and have not made the investments. This is why the bridge collapsed in the city of Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 On Ukraine, I think Biden has been a good follower on Ukraine. He did not lead the charge for closing the SWIFT messaging system. He`s in every case followed Europe, which by the way followed Zelenskyy, and what`s amazing about Zelenskyy is he`s changed the dynamics of the Republican Party. Putin apologists are not going to survive this round. He has changed the dynamics of going into a primary. He`s changed the dynamics of European security.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Has he forced Donald Trump`s hand to change the way he talks about Putin?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: I don`t think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will have it both ways. Remember he also said if this happened on my watch, it wouldn`t have happened, right? But at the same time he said Putin was -- well, Putin has gone from being, you know, popular to being as popular as, you know, Saddam Hussein or Osama bin Laden. And Zelenskyy continues to be a huge force.\par \par \uc2 But, I don`t -- look, I am looking forward to Biden`s message in Europe. But for instance, I can`t explain why are we giving -- why aren`t we doing more to close down the airspace, and they say we don`t get involved.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:08]\par \par \uc2 But you have -- we`re giving them Javelins, we`re giving them lots of -- we`re already involved. What is distinction between those two things?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I hear you on that, I want to put a pin on that because I`ve got -- I`m luckily to have Betsy Woodruff Swan here, and I want to ask you a question about your other beat before we do run out of time. I don`t have time to even play the clip, but Liz Cheney on this program yesterday talked about new information, big presentation coming from the January 6th panel. You`ve got to beat wired, that story wired. What should we be looking for?\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF SWAN: It`s a good question. One of the key things that we`ve been asking about the January 6th probe is, does the probe result in just a lot more color, details, fill-in-the blanks as regards to that attack, or do we actually learn big major new facts that are going to be sort of bullet points in history text books about what happened?\par \par \uc2 And right now I don`t think we`ve gotten to that big major new narrative altering fact space. Although all the things we would learn even thus far in the fill in the blanks, you know, color in the numbers category have been enormous and deeply consequential, so Cheney is very much setting a bar high for the investigation. Obviously she`s steeped in it. She`s been an extremely hands-on member, unlike some members in some congressional investigations.\par \par \uc2 But she is really, you know, teasing something big here, and we know Democrats are putting in a ton of leg work, Democrat and Republican members, to try to put together a report that breaks through. They`re going to have a lot to try to break through, but she set the bar high.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: She set the bar high. She has been the most aggressive member of that panel.\par \par \uc2 Guys, we did 10 minutes of TV. It felt fast. Thank you all for coming on, Navin, Rick and Betsy.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. Please be sure to tune in to MSNBC`s special coverage of that historic Supreme Court hearing tomorrow beginning at 9:00 a.m. Meantime MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220322ei3l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR for March 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Cal Perry, Ali Velshi, Raf Sanchez \par \uc2 7,901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [00:02:14]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Good evening. I`m Stephanie Ruhle. It is now just after midnight on the East Coast, and we are still following breaking news on Europe`s largest nuclear plant in Ukraine. Officials there say a fire broke out in a training compound behind the plant after it reportedly came under attack from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Tonight the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  says no essential equipment have been damaged. The White House said its latest information showed no signs of elevated levels of radiation. The fire at the nuclear plant comes as Russia steps up its attacks on several areas across Ukraine. Earlier tonight, one security analyst explained why the strike on this power plant is so concerning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH CIRINCIONE, NUCLEAR SECURITY ANALYST: This is unprecedented. A nuclear power plant has never come under direct attack. There are international conventions to prohibit such attacks. This is yet another violation of international law and international norms by Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s go right to Cal Perry in Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Cal, we know you`re not in the same area as the plant, but you`re following the story. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so at this hour we understand that the plant is secure. As the IAEA has said that essential equipment was secured and we understand that it took 40 people and 10 vehicles now from the Ukrainian Special Services to put out the fire. The fire now, we understand, was in a training building behind the plant. But, look, not any less alarming when you look at that video and you see that consistent, sustained fire on the plant.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, there`s nothing to guarantee there won`t be some kind of Russian counterattack. We have this time and time again, in fact at this plant 24 hours ago, there were two Russian tanks, two armored personnel carriers, and it took civilians standing in the road to stop them from progressing. They came back 12 hours later, and we saw the sustained firefight.\par \par \uc2 Now just in the last few minutes, we`re hearing from the United Kingdom`s Prime Minister Boris Johnson saying that last night`s events or the events of just a few hours ago, I should say, putting the entire continent, putting all of Europe at risk. And similar words from the Ukrainian president, releasing a video this morning on Telegram saying it was time for Europe to, quote, "wake up."\par \par \uc2 Look, we`ve seen sustained attacks on civilian areas in the past 24 hours, especially in the northern part of the country, the Sumy Region, where the Sumy Reion head is saying it is, quote, "hell on earth," that there is no power, there is no water, and there is no heat, as civilians continue to bear the brunt of this war, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Cal Perry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our experts. Peter Baker, chief White House correspondent for the "New York Times," Tom Nichols, professor at the Naval War College at Harvard Extension School, and Michael McFaul, former U.S. ambassador to Russia and an MSNBC international affairs analyst.\par \par \uc2 [00:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Tom, what sticks out to you tonight in what we`re learning about this nuclear plant?\par \par \uc2 TOM NICHOLS, PROFESSOR, NAVAL WAR COLLEGE AT HARVARD: One of the things that`s very striking to me is the certitude that a lot of folks have that the intention here was to destroy it somehow. My guess is they`re trying to capture that part of the grid, shut off the electricity. You know, people have to remember that if there were a disaster at this plant, most of that would actually blow into Russia because of the way the winds go.\par \par \uc2 So, once again, the thing I`m struck by is the recklessness and incompetence of the Russians in this, you know, iron fisted attempt to seize Ukraine and to intimidate its people. I don`t think, at this point, I don`t think the goal was to destroy this plant. But, you know, this is classic Russian military playbook. The classic Russian military playbook of being, you know, completely reckless around very dangerous things like nuclear plants.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And we just got word moments ago that the fire has now been completely extinguished. That is an enormous relief.\par \par \uc2 Peter, whether or not the fire is extinguished now, the broader picture, what does this attack tell you about the Kremlin`s strategy for war now, where they`re going?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, NEW YORK TIMES: Well, I think this reminds us is that the Kremlin doesn`t play by the same rulebooks that other countries do. Right? That, you know, when the United States goes to war, there are rules and sometimes they`re violated and sometimes, you know, we have consequences that could go wrong. But they`re at least supposed to be rules about the kind of targets that you can kind of hit.\par \par \uc2 Precision weapons for a reason. A nuclear plant obviously would be on the no hit list for any normal country, for Russia, either by intent or by accident, or by incompetence, that wasn`t the case. And it just shows how dangerous this war really is. How quickly it can get out of hand. How something unintended or at least unimaginable could occur at any moment. And I think that that`s a reminder of the Russian way.\par \par \uc2 And I think in the previous hour, we talked about Chechnya, there was a reference to Chechnya and Grozny. I was in Grozny as a report after the Second War, at the end of the Second War, and they have destroyed the count. That was the capital of Chechnya that was -- you know, revolt against Moscow. They carpet-bombed it, and worse than almost anything that anybody has seen in Europe since World War II. And we see that kind of brutality there. We can see it here. And we`re already seeing it in Ukraine day after day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, Ambassador, to Peter`s very point, we shouldn`t be surprised to see Vladimir Putin breaking international norms. But when we sit here and say, you know, this could constitute war crimes. This is unprecedented, or yesterday when the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 , you know, condemned this, urged Russia to change course. Does any of this matter? Are we speaking a language that need means nothing to Vladimir Putin? He was never in it for the norms.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: Great point. You know, after World War II, we wrote down a lot of norms. We created the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2 . One of the norms was, thou shall not annexed the territory of thy neighbor, because that`s the way World War II started, if you`ll recall, through annexation. And sure enough, he did that in 2014. He did it again just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, an unprovoked war with no threat whatsoever to Russia from Ukraine. That reminds me of September 1st, 1939. So you`re absolutely right, he doesn`t care about the norms, but I do think both Tom and Peter talking about the other part of the way Russia fights which is reckless. You know, we keep using the words, precision guided missiles. Maybe they are not so precise in the Russian arsenal as we think they are.\par \par \uc2 I`m not convinced that they were trying to attack this plant. This could be something else. We need to be careful. We`re all guessing that we know what the intentions of what happened here. But the reckless part, we have a lot of evidence of, and the terrorist part, we have a lot of evidence of.\par \par \uc2 Peter was, and witnessed, you know, go everybody and \uc2 Google\uc2  the photos of Chechnya in 1999, 2000, and then add to your search Aleppo in 2016, and you`ll see what the Russians did there deliberately, which is terrorizing civilians to try to bring them into submission. And tragically, I think that`s the chapter we have now entered with Putin`s war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, Ambassador, this recklessness you speak of, who pays the price for it? Is it Putin? Because it`s Ukrainians who are running for their lives and losing them.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, of course, Ukrainians first and foremost. Second, you know, everybody, go look at your map, too. And look at where this power plant is and how close it is, as Tom said, to Russia.\par \par \uc2 [00:10:05]\par \par \uc2 It`s a lot closer to Russia than it is to Poland. So, obviously, disregard for his own people. And remember, a lot of these cities that he`s attacking now in the east, they are predominantly Russian speakers and ethnic Russians. As there are a lot of ethnic Russians in Kyiv as well. So remember, he has no disregard even for his own people.\par \par \uc2 The big question I think you`re hinting at, will there be some moments in the course of this war where, because of sanctions, because of disgust with this war back home in Russia, that they`ll actually try to do something about it. And of course, we don`t know the answer to that, but this will add to the debate. There`s no doubt in my mind, it already is on my \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed. Just so you know, Stephanie, I tweet in Russian quite a bit these days.\par \par \uc2 There is already people saying, what are we doing here? This is crazy. And those are Russian saying that, not just Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tom, do we dismiss, do many people dismiss the impact of sanctions too quickly? Right? You can`t see sanctions, you don`t see it in the street. But what it`s actually doing to Russia, to the Russian economy, it`s strangling them. We are engaging in warfare, economic warfare.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: The problem with sanctions is they can`t bring in immediate end to military activity. I think people expect too much from sanctions in that sense. You know, war breaks out, we impose sanctions, and we assume that the enemy in this case will just say, well, we`ve been sanctioned, I guess we have to stop doing what we`re doing. Sanctions take a long time to work. I think Americans have an almost undue faith in sanctions.\par \par \uc2 With that said, sanctions can impose a huge amount of pain on the people that Putin has thought of as, you know, the kind of under bosses and capos he relies on. Although it should also be pointed out that some of the oligarchs that are being sanctioned are not people Putin particularly cares about. And the Russian people who are going to bear some of the brunt of these sanctions, Putin doesn`t care about them at all.\par \par \uc2 But the sanctions are really important in that for those people out there that are worried, rightly, that Putin could seek a wider war, this is really undermining his ability to continue. This mires down, this blocks down as economy, which undermines his ability to make war. It undermines his ability to have a stable home front, so sanctions do have a lot of impact. They just don`t have the immediate impact that people wish they would have, and that they wish they could immediately see in some kind of immediate cessation of hostilities.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But there is immediate suffering. We`re looking at it, we`re looking at images on the left side of your screen right now. Because of that, Peter, how much pressure is this White House under to do more?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I think it`s under enormous pressure, obviously. These pictures are devastating to look at. This kind of devastation in any country much less in the heart of Europe is heartbreaking and tragic, and it has an impact of course here at home, too. Now there`s a lot of, you know, mood in America right now. It`s not very interested in overseas issues. They said, it`s not our problem, it`s somebody else`s problem, and that`s a challenge of course for President Biden to explain to them why it isn`t just other people`s problem.\par \par \uc2 Why it does impact and mean something to America, even though the bombs are falling so many thousands of miles away. And he has, in fact, made a point in the last nine days of ratcheting up, day by day, the pressure. Each day, squeezing even further on Russia. Tom`s right, it hasn`t had impact on the immediate actions being taken by Russia, but it is having an impact, absolutely in Russia.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Ukrainians are suffering more than anybody. But what`s also going to happen as a result of it is that Russia itself is going to be cut off from the rest of the world. And tens of millions of Russians who had nothing to do with war, who didn`t want this war, who oppose this war, are now facing a country where what little freedom they had left is being squashed even further. Where their ability to leave the country is being cut off. Their ability to live and part of the world is being cut off. They are retreating again behind an iron curtain.\par \par \uc2 And that has consequences for the rest of the world, as well, not just for Russia itself, because if suddenly they are more isolated, more of a pariah state, imagine North Korea on the scale. You know, 11 time zones with the nuclear arsenal and the capacity that Russia has. That`s not a good thing for world security. So there`s a lot of consequences coming out of this and a lot of uncertainty ahead as what it would mean in the future even beyond the immediate crisis on the ground in Ukraine, which is devastating.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Isolated from information as well. Ambassador, earlier today when we heard from Russia, from Putin saying things are going exactly as planned, we obviously know that`s not the case.\par \par \uc2 [00:15:07]\par \par \uc2 But is he saying that because he`s speaking to the Russian people who have such limited information in a country where the government controls the media, that they really have no idea what`s happening in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, yes and no. Actually there was two giant events that happened just in the last 24 hours that has gotten enough attention and precisely about this. The Dozhd TV, the number one independent television station, was taken off the air. And the iconic Echo of Moscow, a radio station but also a multimedia organization, also was shut down. One of the most important sources of independent news in Russia.\par \par \uc2 So both of those events happened in a day. That`s not a coincidence. Right? Putin is doing that because he`s worried about news. And by the way, I was on both of those programs just in the last five days. They don`t want people like me talking about this war in Russian. And he cut them off. At the same time, you know, it`s a demographic thing. Right? The older you are, the more rural place you live, the less educated you are, the more likely you are to listen to Putin`s national television stations.\par \par \uc2 And when you listen to those people, I just watched the clip of a dozen of those people being interviewed, they all support Putin`s war. They all say this is the right thing to do. We have to eliminated Nazis just as we did it before. So there`s a much bigger divide I think in Russian society about the information they receive and therefore the way they perceive this war.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ambassador, you`ve been all over U.S. TV, but to your point, you`ve been on Russia TV over the last --\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: And Ukrainian TV.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are you worried about your own safety?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, I was also on Ukrainian TV, and I just want to say, and I really appreciate, by the way, that you have Ukrainians coming on your show tonight and earlier tonight. These are people I know. I think the Ukrainian voice in this debate needs to be louder here in our country so that people can understand and listen to what they`re doing. I really applaud that. I hope we do more of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I was on TV today with Ukrainian journalists broadcasting from the big television station. That big tower in the middle of Kyiv. That was literally just bombed, you know, I think 24 hours ago. Right next to Babi Yar, I can`t remember. The time is all blending to me. So it`s hard for me to worry about my security when those people are being brave to try to bring information in their country.\par \par \uc2 The least I can do is to participate in the discussions in Ukraine but especially in Russia because that`s -- my Ukrainian friends, they say please, please, please help us get the message of what`s happening on the ground here in Ukraine into the people of Russia.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you`re certainly getting the message across here.\par \par \uc2 Peter, I see you nodding your head. You lived in Moscow. Do you want to elaborate?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: No, I think it`s exactly right. To see that the cloud falling in basically on Russians is tragic because they are going to be shut off from information. The Russians who are paying attention, many of them do not want this war. They are out there risking their freedom and their lives, in some ways in protesting against it. There`s talk about legislation in the Russian parliament that would punish people who protest by conscripting them into the army and sending them to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 There is talk of -- people, you know, are literally telling stories about the police knocking on their door saying we hear you`re against the government. This is returning to a different age, you know, it`s not been a free country under Putin to begin with. But increasingly, what small bits of freedom that are still there, what shreds of freedom were still there, he is trying to snuff out at the same time because he knows I think that if the Russian people as a whole had access to free information, this isn`t something that they would want to see.\par \par \uc2 These are their Slavic brothers. They think of Ukraine as a part of the family, if you will. And to see their own children, their own sons, shooting at their, you know, Slavic brothers I think is something that would devastate a lot of Russians if they saw these pictures that you`re showing right now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tom, before we move on, we`re talking about different support that we`re offering Ukraine. When NATO offers weapons and support, get technical with us. How does it get there? Who organizes it? Especially since so much of what`s happening in Ukraine is run by civilians. We keep hearing it`s a race against time to get Ukraine supplies. But who is running this race?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Well, before I talk about that and, you know, even if I knew the exact way the weapons were getting there, it`s probably not something I`d want to talk about. But there is something really important to point out and something that Mike and Peter just said, which is this generational divide. The first time I was in the former Soviet Union was almost 40 years ago and you could seal off a country like that.\par \par \uc2 [00:20:01]\par \par \uc2 Putin doesn`t understand you cannot seal off the Russian federation from the rest of the world from pictures and from information, and from telephones and communication. He can try. He wishes it was the Soviet Union 40 years ago. But I just don`t think he`s going to be successful in that.\par \par \uc2 As for how things get there, you know, the country is not under some kind of Russian quarantine or embargo. This is still a country that`s capable of moving goods and services and people, and weapons between its neighbors and other places in Europe. So there is a lot of different ways that weapons can get there. But this isn`t -- we`re not at the point yet of some kind of, you know, Russian, kind of Cuba like quarantine where they`ve somehow sealed it off.\par \par \uc2 We keep talking about Russian control of areas in Ukraine. But I really want to emphasize that right now the Russians are occupiers. They only control the areas they happen to be standing in at any given moment. This is not -- I think we, you know, we have to be careful about assuming too much about what the Russians are capable of. It`s not like they`re marching into town after town, and the locals are saying OK, you win, you know, run the city, set up a tent.\par \par \uc2 That`s not happening. The Russians are basically marching in and trying to control the streets and the highways that they are on. Mostly for their own safety.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tom Nichols, Peter Baker, Ambassador Michael McFaul, thank you all so much for coming back and staying up so late with us.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to now turn to the other big story we`re following right now. The flow of refugees out of Ukraine. More than a million forced to flee their homes. My partner Ali Velshi, let`s go back to him at a train station in Budapest.\par \par \uc2 Ali, the people you`re talking to, it appears that they have got the clothes on their back, a suitcase or two. Did they think they`ll be going back to Ukraine anytime soon or do they plan to be gone indefinitely?\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST, VELSHI: So that`s s a really good question. I spoke to the people who seemed very hopeful that they will go back, some said in a month, some said three months. Some are relocating. Some are going home to countries that they came from. People who are not from the Ukraine, but they`ve there studying or they`re on visas. Some have family, you know, in Eastern Europe, there are people with connections in different places in Hungary or in Poland or Romania or Germany.\par \par \uc2 So some people are going to resettle with some of their relatives. There are still some people here, most people have gone, but since we`ve last talked there are still some people here looking for some final plans as to how to get to places. You can see here there are people with the Red Cross. There are church groups helping people out.\par \par \uc2 I will be clear, Stephanie. There is no official government presence here at all. There are police, they`ve been very helpful, but they`re just Budapest police. There is no government presence here at all. These are Hungarian civilians who have come out here. There`s more food than they can even handle.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How did they organize?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: They`ve got too much food. They`ve got too many donations. I`ve asked several times tonight. It is self-organizing. There is nobody running this operation. These are various groups who are dealing with accommodation, with transportation, with food, with medical needs. Somebody here didn`t have a euro to buy a train ticket and somebody walked up out of nowhere with a little wallet that had train tickets and gave them the train ticket and said where are you going? They showed them on their phone. And they walked them to where they needed to go.\par \par \uc2 There is a sign over here, it says warming bus. If you don`t have a place to go, it`s cold, right, the sun has just started to come up. But there are buses just to wait on while you get warm. There`s hot soup that they`re giving out to people. There`s tea, there`s coffee, there`s hot chocolate, there`s food. And none of this makes up for it, Stephanie. None of this makes up for the fact that somebody`s country is being invaded.\par \par \uc2 But in all of this despair and horribleness, there is some strength of the human spirit and warmness about people who are coming out here to help strangers whom they don`t know. The languages are not interchangeable. Ukrainians do not understand Hungarians and vice versa. They`re entire different root languages. But there are people out here who see that this is wrong, and they`re just trying to help their fellow people.\par \par \uc2 It`s not going to stop the war in Ukraine. It`s not going to help those people who have to be displaced from their homes. And it doesn`t matter how nice the Hungarians are to them. They`re still -- they`ve had to leave their homes and you`ve seen images of them. They`re crying, they`re desperate, they`re said, they`re stunned. But it is worth it to see that there are people who have come out and said, come into my home, come into my country. Let`s do what we can to try and least make this a little bit right in the horribleness that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then I`m sorry to tell you, Mr. Velshi, you`re mistaken. There is one organizing force behind all of this. They`re being led by love.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ali Velshi, stay safe.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It really -- it`s something that`s very evident right here, Stephanie. Thank you, friend.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Love, compassion, and humanity leading the way. Ali Velshi, stay where you are. We`re going to check in with you in just a few.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ambassador McFaul just said it. He said the Ukrainian voices are critical. So next, we`ll have one when we talk to a member of Ukraine`s parliament about what`s happening in her country right now.\par \par \uc2 [00:25:05]\par \par \uc2 She`s not fleeing, she is there defending her country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ukraine`s State Emergency Service said tonight that the fire has been put out near Europe`s largest nuclear power plant. Officials said the fire broke out in a training compound behind the plant.\par \par \uc2 We are joined now to discuss by Oleksandra Ustinova, a Ukrainian member of parliament. She joins us from Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining us very late this evening. What can you tell us about the situation at the plant?\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDRA USTINOVA, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER: Well, all I know about that as was reported is that the fire was stopped so far but unfortunately and to be honest, we have been telling to the world might going to happen one day. We have four nuclear plants in our country.\par \par \uc2 [00:30:03]\par \par \uc2 One of them is totally occupied by the Russians already. It`s Chernobyl that everybody probably knows about. One is in Zaporizhzhia. Zaporizhzhia is one of the biggest nuclear factory plants in Europe. Unfortunately, unfortunately, we cannot control the Russian missiles, and that`s why we keep begging and telling the world is we need a no-fly zone and to shut down the sky, because otherwise, one of the rockets, one of the missiles one day is going to hit one of the nuclear stations, and this is going to be the world`s next disaster.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You didn`t evacuate, you happen to be in the United States traveling when the war broke out. What does this like for you, despite how dangerous it is, does some part of you actually want to be there right now?\par \par \uc2 USTINOVA: Yes, actually, the first day I saw when Putin invaded, I told my husband I had to go back home. And his first question was, how? We have all the airports but they are already bombed to ashes. And there is no wait even come back home. And I`m supposed to have a baby in a month. And you know, there is just one dream that I have, that this baby sees their homeland and sees what it looks like one day when we rebuilt it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So how do you think you`re going to get home?\par \par \uc2 USTINOVA: I don`t know at the moment. But I know for sure we`re going to win because, you know, I remember the first days when the war started everybody was saying the phrase, if we win. Today everybody says, when we win. So this is a matter of time. And unfortunately, it`s a matter of lives. How many lives have to be put down there?\par \par \uc2 First of all, for the United States, for NATO, and for other countries that they promised, signed the treaty with us, that when we give up our nuclear weapons, that they would step in and support us in our fight for freedom if something happens. Unfortunately, we gave up our weapons 20 years ago, a little bit more than 20 years ago. And today, when we are in danger, when our civilian population is being -- let`s be honest, executed in front of the whole world because the whole world is watching, 24/7, how Ukrainians are being killed. How their cities are being erased.\par \par \uc2 Now, when we need help, the international community does not want to step in and support us with basic equipment to shut down the sky which is called a no-fly zone. We need the dome, the iron dome so-called, like Israeli has or the no-fly zone so that the Russian airplanes cannot go through the Ukrainian air space and hit the civilian objects.\par \par \uc2 Putin became crazy. He`s a psycho. If the first two days, he was shooting the airports, the army bases, now he`s shooting exactly the civilian objects. And you can see it right now on the screens, because he wants us to lose as many lives as we can so that the government and the president sit down at the table for negotiations. He doesn`t know Ukrainians. It`s not going to happen. We`re going to fight until the last blood.\par \par \uc2 But we are not giving up our freedom. And unfortunately, so far, the world`s response was very weak. Everybody keeps talking about the sanctions. Well, these sanctions were partial. We were asking for sanctions for their banks. And unfortunately, if you look at the SWIFT that was switched off, it was switched off only for seven out of more than 300 banks in Russia which means the accounts would just be relocated to another bank.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the sanctions on their economy sectors, sanctions, it would be the energy sector, which is the main pocket for Putin. We saw sanctions on oligarchs today, but for some reason, Roman Abramovich, who is a very close ally to Putin, who owns \uc2 Chelsea Football Club\uc2 , and has a lot of companies here in the United States, he has three steel companies in the United States. He`s not on the sanction list.\par \par \uc2 So we keep begging the world to be stronger in their reaction. But unfortunately, so far, we see that everybody`s trying to protect their economies that are worth more than Ukrainian lives.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Oleksandra, perhaps Joe Biden, the president of the United States, is watching. What is your message to him?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Please support Ukrainian children, women, who are dying right now, thousands of them are dying right now. We need a no-fly zone just to protect our civilian population. We don`t need people to fight for us on the ground. We don`t need American or NATO soldiers. We can do it on our own, and we had already proved it, against one of the biggest armies in the world.\par \par \uc2 [00:35:05]\par \par \uc2 But if you don`t shut down the sky and don`t help us with this equipment, Ukrainians are literally going to be executed in front of the whole world.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And what is your message to Vladimir Putin?\par \par \uc2 USTINOVA: You know, I don`t want to rephrase it. I can just say exactly the same things that our soldiers did. And one day, this criminal cycle would be responsible and would die for everything he has done to the world.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow. Oleksandra, thank you so, so much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it, and good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 USTINOVA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, more on the attack on the nuclear plant in southern Ukraine when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:40:23]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`ve following the situation in southwestern Ukraine where the fire at a nuclear power plant has been put out. I want to get to NBC`s Raf Sanchez for some reaction from Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Raf, you know, we know information vacuum people in Russia are in. Are they aware of what`s happened tonight at this power plant?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So, Stephanie, no official response from the Kremlin yet. It`s a little after 8:30 in the morning. We normally get the daily briefing from Dmitry Peskov, Vladimir Putin`s longtime spokesman, at about midday, but people here know because of what they`re seeing in Russian state media, which in some ways throughout this entire war has been our best window into trying to figure out what it is the Kremlin wants the Russian people to hear.\par \par \uc2 And state media has been consistently playing down the facts that there isn`t a fire burning outside the largest nuclear power plant in Europe. They have not been making much of a connection between the fighting and the fire. They are certainly not acknowledging that it looks like it was Russian fire that caused this. At one point, they actually quoted Jennifer Granholm, the U.S. Energy secretary saying, we`re not seeing any alarming levels of radiation.\par \par \uc2 That is not something Russian state media normally does, quoting American officials as sources of authority. But in this one instance, what a member of Joe Biden`s Cabinet was saying lined up with what the Kremlin wanted the Russian people to hear. So they went ahead with it.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy did speak with Boris Johnson, the British prime minister, as well as President Biden tonight. The British are calling for an emergency meeting of the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  in light of this fighting outside of the power plant. The British prime minister said, the reckless actions of President Putin could now endanger all of Europe -- Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why wouldn`t they want to own up to it? They were actively shelling the plant. Is it because we just had the map of their -- that power plant is very, very close to Russia. If there was radiation, and the winds were blowing in one direction rather than the other, it could be dangerous to the Russian people. Is that why they don`t want to admit it?\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: I think that definitely could be part of it. Memories of Chernobyl are strong here. I think another part of this, Stephanie, is the Russian people are being told day after day this is a limited military operation in the east of Ukraine to protect the Russian speakers there. They are not being told the reality. This is a sprawling invasion of Ukraine across multiple fronts. This power plant, it is close to Russia, but it`s also far-ish from these two breakaway republics that the Russian media are saying are the source, you know, the reason that Russian forces are inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I think there is a general unwillingness to admit to the Russian people quite how big this war has become. Quite how, you know, difficult it is to see how Vladimir Putin is going to wrap this up and get his forces out -- Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Raf, thank you so much. Raf Sanchez.\par \par \uc2 This Russian attack on the nuclear plant, the fire may be extinguished, but it`s raised a lot of concerns. So let`s bring in our experts. Joining us now, Frank Figliuzzi, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  assistant director for counterintelligence, the author of the book, "The FBI Way," and the host of the podcast, "The Bureau," and Joel Rubin, from deputy assistant secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Frank, the danger from the attack has passed, at least in the short term. But what`s your take away from tonight when you think about Putin`s recklessness?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: No question, Stephanie, very reckless. Very anxiety producing. You need only look across social media tonight to see Americans` reactions. In fact, global reaction to this. But I also see this, Stephanie, as a teachable moment. So when I look at what was happening tonight in the video that we were able to get our hands on, I saw this through the lens of my experience and training, domestically here in the homeland with regard to defending and protecting against attacks to existing nuclear power facilities in the United States.\par \par \uc2 What I saw on my screen was not what it would have looked like if you were trying to penetrate, to try to do catastrophic damage, to try to release radio activity or cause a meltdown. What I saw tonight was an attempt to either take over that facility because of its obvious strategic importance for power or to simply have them powered down, and no more heat tomorrow for Ukraine. And send a message.\par \par \uc2 It`s still unclear quite frankly where the Russian troops have gone. What this was all about but I didn`t see what a lot of people were panicking about.\par \par \uc2 [00:45:05]\par \par \uc2 And that`s because this is a teachable moment here in the United States without going into a great deal of very sensitive detail. I can assure you that, number one, an external attack against a nuclear power plant here, and this is really true around the world, would be resisted fiercely by every federal resource and 24/7 robust security, guarantee of that.\par \par \uc2 Number two, it`s extremely hard to do catastrophic damage via external attack to a very hardened nuclear power facility. Internal, sabotage, mishap, a Chernobyl accident, you bet. External attack, extremely difficult. So that`s not what I saw tonight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joel, you have been involved in several White House briefings on this crisis in Ukraine. Take us to tomorrow. Will tonight`s attack on this power plant, will it change the calculation, the position this White House is taking?\par \par \uc2 JOEL RUBIN, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, Stephanie, the White House is very measured in the way it`s dealing with this situation as well as with Vladimir Putin`s threats about advancing a nuclear wars, part of the military tactic. But, you know, this is a crossing of the Rubicon. What Vladimir Putin did by having his hands showing a nuclear facility is violating international law, violating his commitment to ensure to reunite nations, to ensure that there would not be attacks and that one would not attack a nuclear facility that`s of civilian use.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s kind of cross the Rubicon and it really put us into a whole other zone of strategy. Now, he`s probably, yes, trying to scare and terrorize, clearly, the Ukrainian civilians cut off their power. Create civilian pains will never some victory because he`s failing in the battlefield. But this is a different level of fear. There are nuclear power plants all over the United States. There are also 47 in China, 38 in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Once this pandora`s box is open, and it has been tonight, it`s hard to put it back in. And that is a very deep danger to global security and something that we should not take lightly.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joel Rubin, Frank Figliuzzi, thank you both for joining us this evening. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, millions of children are waking up, day nine in the middle of a war. Some in a war zone, others, thousands of miles from home. We`ll be speaking to one of the people who`s trying to help with this refugee crisis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:51:46]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re closely following the refugee crisis happening out of Ukraine tonight. 7.5 million children all at risk. I want to bring in James Elder. He`s a spokesman for \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , the U.N. agency that helps children worldwide. He`s in Lviv tonight or in the morning where you are right now, James.\par \par \uc2 You have dealt with crisis situations around the world. Put in perspective what it is like in Ukraine right now for all these kids who`ve never known war.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ELDER, UNICEF SPOKESPERSON: Certainly we`ve never seen the speed and swiftness of half a million boys and girls having to flee their country. Many, many millions more fleeing homes across the country. They`re in bunkers, there are rats in bunkers as that little girl told me. They`re terrified. Their parents are fleeing. They`re hearing bombs. It`s a conflict zone. It`s simply gut-wrenching.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For families who can`t get out, what do we know about them? Can you help them inside Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Yes. Yes, we can. We have supplies come in across the border almost as you and I speak. A convoy of medical supplies. Oxygen cylinders going into hospitals in Kyiv, water supplies, even child protection experts who to deal with trauma we are seeing. But let`s not forget, there are some children who simply can`t. I was at a children`s hospital, (INAUDIBLE), in the west of Ukraine yesterday. We`ve got little babies, boys and girls on Drips. Very difficult to get them out of this conflict. Their only answer, their own prayer is that the missile stopped.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How do these hospitals, when I look at these images and we hear about hospitals now treating their patients in basements. How do they keep them clean and sterile?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: It`s very difficult. I have to say that the selflessness, the relentless work of pediatricians and doctors that I`ve seen here is extraordinary. But those setups are very difficult. They do everything they can, they even have a system whereby now when children come in and put belts on them to see the criticality of black dot, heaven forbid, means very, very bad. You know, yellow means priority Q. So this is what conflicts does to look like. So they`re pretty good when it comes to sanitation because they`re experts and they`re working 24/7. But it is not possible to get it all, as it should look for a child who needs emergency medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What are these children telling you that you speak to, their mothers?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: You know, it`s heartbreaking. I`ve had hundreds of conversations. People are very willing to speak. They do not want to leave their country. They love their home and their family. It`s hard to have a conversation where after a few sentences they`re not in tears. They`re stressed. They`ve left a loved one somewhere. Maybe fighting, maybe in a bunker. So they`re just telling us they want this to stop.\par \par \uc2 Children sometimes sleep a lot more. Sometimes they cry a lot more. Sometimes it`s the child who grabs their mom`s leg and says mom, it`s going to be OK. So it is all matters of things. But it is trauma that will stay with children, along with this conflict as it continues. And that`s not what \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  wants. It`s not what the world wants. It`s not what Ukraine wants. Another nation of children who come to learn conflict, who are out of school, but know what an air raids mean? I must flee my bed. I must run downstairs to a bunker. That`s not childhood.\par \par \uc2 [00:55:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When did you arrive in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Almost a week ago. I walked across the border into -- from Romania into Ukraine and then caught transport up here into Lviv, which is a real central point about 50 miles now from Poland.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You walked across the border, and over the span of that week it has gotten extraordinarily worse. What has this been like for you? Did you think it would be this bad?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: No. No, I honestly didn`t. As I said, we`ve never seen this in history. The scale and speed of half a million kids. A million people. No, no, I didn`t. It`s traumatic, you shake, very hard to hear so many people having their hearts broken and watching their lives turned upside down. And losing that power of control. I mean, our job is to be here. Sometimes that job is just to hug a mom.\par \par \uc2 I get inspiration about what I see Ukrainians do. Grandmothers cooking, young kids doing (INAUDIBLE), welcoming people, people taking people up at train stations to give them a bed for a night. That warms and keeps us going. But our jobs is to be here and hope. Hope that this, you know, that the fighting stops. The conflicts stops. And these children and their families get a moment and are able to stay in their country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: James, you are doing a lot more than giving a hug. James Elder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And for you at home who would like to support \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  and do what you can from here, please, go to \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 , hyphen, USA, dot org. Always remember, if you need help ask for it. But if you can give help, please give it. And right now there are millions of people on the other side of the world that need ours.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for staying up late with me. I will see you at the end of the day tomorrow. My friend and colleague Alicia Menendez picks up breaking news coverage on the other side of this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220307ei3400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Richard Engel, Carol Lee, Al Sharpton \par \uc2 8,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:22]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: It`s Friday, just how far will Putin go? Zelensky tries to rally the people of Europe, as Russian forces attack and seize a major nuclear power plant in Ukraine. They`re dropping cluster bombs on the country. So, the U.N. is alleging. We`ll get more after this.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest in the U.S. response as Secretary of State Antony Blinken will speak this hour for Brussels amid a flurry of emergency meetings at NATO and the U.N. And we`ll take you to his press conference when it begins.\par \par \uc2 And one million and counting. That`s how many refugees have already fled Ukraine since the beginning of this war just eight days ago. We`ll speak with the leader of an emergency rescue organization about this massive humanitarian crisis ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd on a day in which we`re following a number of developments in the war in Ukraine. Russia appears to be ramping up the size and scope and yes brutality of its attacks. Right now, we are also keeping a close eye on the White House as President Biden is about to speak about his Made in America commitments. We will be listening closely to see if he has anything to add to the situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Also, right now, the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  is holding an emergency meeting to discuss Russia`s siege of Europe`s largest nuclear power plant late yesterday, which set off global alarms a shelling hit the area of fire broke out. Ukrainian officials begged for help to avoid a disaster. Here`s the U.S. Ambassador to the U.N. just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINDA THOMAS-GREENFIELD, U.S. AMBASSADOR TO U.N.: By the grace of God, the world narrowly averted a nuclear catastrophe last night. Russia`s attack last night put Europe`s largest nuclear power at grave risk. It was incredibly reckless and dangerous, and it threatened the safety of civilians across Russia, Ukraine and Europe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The attack killed three Ukrainian soldiers according to officials there and while U.S. officials have assured us that there`s been no radioactive leakage from the plant, and that the damage has been fairly limited. The Ukrainian President Zelensky said the attack could have created a disaster six times larger than Chernobyl in 1986 as he pleaded with European leaders for more assistance in his fight against the Russian.\par \par \uc2 Ten minutes ago, we saw Zelensky speak again, urging the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to admit his country immediately. And he also called on Europeans to take to the streets and a show of support for Ukraine. The White House says President Biden also spoke with Zelensky last night. That was basically over the nuclear plant situation. He also spoke a short time ago with Poland`s president. Poland right now is dealing with basically half of the refugees that have fled Ukraine so far.\par \par \uc2 And this morning, the head of NATO condemned the attack on Ukraine`s nuclear power plant, calling it as the U.N. ambassador said reckless. And he also condemned Russia`s increasingly brutal tactics. Listen to what he`s alleging.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENS STOLTENBERG, NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: The Russian invasion of Ukraine is a blatant violation of international law. And we have seen the use of cluster bombs, we have seen reports of use of other types of which -- weapons will be -- which will be in the in violation of international law. This is brutality. This is inhumane.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Later this hour, we expect to hear from the Secretary of State Tony Blinken who`s been meeting with NATO and G7 counterparts in Brussels. And a short time ago, he issued this somber assessment of the situation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Unfortunately, tragically, horrifically this may not be over soon. So many other countries around the world who are united in their abhorrence of what President Putin has unleashed. We have to sustain this until it stops. Until the war`s over. The Russian forces leave, the Ukrainian people regain their independence, their sovereignty, their territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now, we got Carol Lee at the White House, Richard Engel in Kiev. Also in Kiev, Ukrainian Member of Parliament Kira Rudik. But I want to start in Kiev with Richard Engel. Richard, I know you`ve been doing a ton of reporting today. What is the status Kiev does seem to be -- that the Russians seem to be only literally inching closer seeming that they`re only moving inch by inch?\par \par \uc2 I am curious what is the status of that convoy and when do we expect Russia to truly engage in the city of Kiev?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:07]\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So, there have been more airstrikes around Kiev throughout the day there. They not, however, the full-bourn assault. This city remains more or less at the -- in the eye of the storm. But we are all watching that Russian convoy. The Russian convoy, according to a U.S. military official has been where it has been, which is about 15 miles outside of this city for the last several days.\par \par \uc2 And does not appear to be moving any closer. But U.S. military officials do believe that Russia is in a -- in a phase of regrouping and that it will soon start to assault the city in the same way that it has been assaulting other cities. But in preparation of that and in preparation for an urban campaign which they have already -- which Russia has already begun to unleash on other cities, including Kharkiv and Kherson in the South, people here are starting to leave in large numbers.\par \par \uc2 So earlier today, we were at the train station and witnessed a large number of people. Crowds, entire families rushing there in an effort to get out of the city before that convoy comes. Before the full Russian invasion hits the city.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: As Russian forces are getting closer to the city, people are streaming out of it. Parents are tethered to their children. They`re taking their pets, they`re taking their grandparents, and everyone is heading west.\par \par \uc2 Hearing the train announcements and the train whistles in these long lines of people climbing onto the track. It feels to me like another era.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Then most crazy to feel when you hear how kids screaming, mother screaming is so crazy, that panic, you see how people are so afraid.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: It`s been like this all day. There`s a lot of confusion about where these trains are departing from. People had been waiting here on platform nine, there was just an announcement a few moments ago that the train to the west is actually leaving from platform 12. Now everyone is rushing there and nobody wants to miss it.\par \par \uc2 This train is heading toward Lviv in the West. And now people are trying to crowd on that everybody is able to get on.\par \par \uc2 This is the push right up at the door. There`s been some people dropping bags. We`ve seen people holding their babies up in the air as they`re trying to cram as many people as possible onto this training. A lot of families we spoke to said they decided this morning to pack up their house, come here to the station and just head west.\par \par \uc2 It just feels like another time here. It feels like we`ve stepped back into World War II when people are loading onto trains and you hear the whistles and the families have come with all their belongings and people are hugging and saying goodbye. And not sure if they will ever come back to this place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: And that man, you see waving goodbye to his family. He like many young men in this country have decided to stay. He said he`s staying to fight against the Russians.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I imagine. I know we`ve seen a lot of family split up so that the younger men can stay back. Richard Engel in Lviv for us tonight. Richard, thank you. Carol Lee at the White House. And Carol, there is a growing group of bipartisan members of Congress that want to impose this full embargo on Russian oil. The White House continues to be a bit reticent on this. My guess is the European partners are really the most reticent on this.\par \par \uc2 Where does this stand and do they acknowledge that maybe the politics is getting ahead of them?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, look there -- they`ve left the energy sector to -- after they`ve done a number of other sanctions for a reason, Chuck. This is where the really hard stuff comes in. And what you`ve seen the White House tried to do is really do, you know, all hands on deck in terms of keeping the allies united, the NATO allies and U.S. partners. And that`s something that President Biden talked about every time he`s talked about Ukraine this week.\par \par \uc2 He`s really emphasized. They see that as a critical asset going forward, not just with the allies and overseas but domestically. If they`re completely on all united there, then it gives the President some leverage here it -- with Congress. But the pressure is increasing. And we know that the administration is having discussions about what sanctions on the energy sector would look like.\par \par \uc2 Are there ways they can do it so it`s not super harmful to the U.S. economy, that can Europe do it in ways that there are carve outs and things? And they`re going to have to figure that out because as you heard from Secretary Blinken this is something that could go on for some time.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:02]\par \par \uc2 And so, what you see is a real effort by the administration to keep those allies united. The President spoke for almost an hour with the President of Poland. President Zelensky as you said, he`s going to meet with the President of Finland. And it all comes, as you said, on the wild, Congress is putting a lot of pressure in the White House kind of wants to take a more cautious approach, at least to make sure everybody`s aligned because again, they see that as their big asset here and confronting Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But last night, the phone call between President Zelensky and President Biden, it certainly sounded like Presidents Zelensky was, I think, hoping that the attack on the nuclear plant would change the minds of the West on their -- on this idea that there would be no -- a no-fly zone. Obviously, that is still considered a non-starter on the U.S. side. What about the rest of NATO? Does the White House feel like they`re feeling more pressure to do more?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, I think everyone that feels in the White House, and it feels more pressure to do more because that`s what you keep hearing from Congress, from President Zelensky is they want -- that they want more, whether it`s sanctions or assistance to Ukraine. They`ve been very clear, the President has been very clear that they are not sending in U.S. troops to fight Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And they`ve really tried to squash this idea of a no-fly zone saying essentially, that is a gateway to getting drawn into a military conflict with Russia, which is something the president said the U.S. is absolutely not going to do. But this isn`t going away, their questions are going to be continued to be asked and the pressure is still there. And so, you know, right now, that`s their position.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to see that changing given the redline that the President has drawn, but they also don`t know exactly where Putin is going to go with this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Events on the ground could very much dictate a lot of things there. Carol Lee at the White House for us. Carol, thanks. I want to bring in Kira Rudik. Member of the Ukrainian Parliament. Really appreciate you spending a few minutes here. So, I want to start first with President Zelensky`s ask for immediate admission to the E.U. Explain why that`s so important, right in this moment.\par \par \uc2 KIRA RUDIK, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER: Hello. Thank you for having me. This is important because it was one of the Putin`s demands that we give up our aim to join NATO, and we give up our aim to join the E.U. and we give up -- and we become a neutral country. And it is written in Ukrainian constitution that we want to join E.U. and we want to join NATO. This is the will of Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why right now we see that -- because we are fighting together and because the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is definitely one of our strongest allies that right now could be a great time for us to not only be part of the Union, virtually, but become a part of the Union physically. When we are talking about the war, it`s not the war between Russia and Ukraine anymore. And I think everybody realizes that, especially after the events of tonight, when the nuclear plant was attacked, this will take everybody in the -- in the world.\par \par \uc2 Definitely will take every single country because radiation doesn`t really care which passport you`re holding. It doesn`t really care if you`re a NATO member or not. And that`s why it`s so important right now that if we are fighting in this war against tyrants, against past time, and the -- your -- our fellow journalist was just saying that it seems in Ukraine like Second World War, and it does because Putin is trying to rebuild the Russian Empire or Soviet Union and drag all of us to the past.\par \par \uc2 And we want to go to the future. And we see our future with the E.U. and not with some Russian Empire or whatever the hell is in his head. So, that`s why it`s so important for us to become a real part of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you feel how -- tell me the sort of -- I`m guessing on one hand, you feel somewhat supported by the West. Money`s coming in, arms are coming in. But that`s all that`s coming in. So, do you feel supported or do you feel alone in this fight against Russia?\par \par \uc2 RUDIK: We do feel supported. And we are grateful for all the help that we are getting. We all know that also, the next step that we see is a no-fly zone over Ukraine. And I know that everybody is saying no, no, no, the Third World War will start. I will be very blunt with you. It has already started because the attack on the nuclear plant again affects everybody. It`s affect -- it affects \uc2 European Union\uc2 . It affects United States.\par \par \uc2 It affects United Kingdom, it affects India, it affects Arab Emirates. Everybody, because again, there will be no answer to what in Putin`s head.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:01]\par \par \uc2 And the condemnation that President Biden and Boris Johnson made a statement about the condemnation of Putin, well, great, but Putin doesn`t care about condemnation. He doesn`t care about e-mails from the United Nations where he`s called to stop doing what he`s doing. The only thing Putin cares about is force and power. And this is what we need to give him. I am as Ukrainian who are born armed and ready to fight for her country.\par \par \uc2 I know that I will be very good on the land fight. And Ukrainian army was very good and is very good at the land fight. But we cannot protect ourselves from the air. No matter how hard I`m trying, I can only shoot Russian soldiers but I cannot protect myself and my family and my country from the Russian missiles coming from the air. And this is why we will have to get the support.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, we will fail, then there will be Russia-controlled country in the center of Europe. Is there something that NATO wants? Is there something that United States needs? I really doubt it. And that`s why the no-fly zone question will never be off the table until we are getting it this or other way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I would -- just to put it -- put another exclamation point on here. You have -- you believe you have no chance to push Russia back without a no-fly zone?\par \par \uc2 RUDIK: Absolutely. I believe we will stand up to Russia. We will die if we have to. But the only way that we will be able to definitely win is the no- fly zone over Ukraine right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kira Rudik, a member of the Ukrainian Parliament. We`re all -- we`re all pulling for you. Hang in there. Stay strong. Stay strong in this fight. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 For a lot more on these topics, be sure to tune in to the latest episode of the Chuck Toddcast. That`s out today. It features former Energy Secretary Ernest Moniz as we dig into nuclear safety, deterrence, and actually a little bit of climate and nukes at the end as well. Get it wherever you get your podcast. Still to come. This massive humanitarian crisis is emerging in Europe as more than a million people have fled Ukraine, and it`s just been eight days of war.\par \par \uc2 Such a massive humanitarian crisis is going to need a massive amount of humanitarian aid. That`s next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Turning now to the escalating humanitarian crisis in Eastern Europe. It`s exploding in size at a pace so rapid that even the U.N. High Commissioner refugee says he`s rarely seen an exodus of this magnitude unfold so quickly in his 40-year career. More than 1.2 million Ukrainians have fled the country since Russia invaded. It`s more than 100 people every minute in the last eight days.\par \par \uc2 And it`s more than two percent of the population. It`s only a fraction of what`s to come. The U.N. estimates as many as four million could flee the country in the coming weeks. Millions more in Ukraine forced from their homes, due to the violence of destruction. They`re going to need aid. In total, the U.N. expects 10 million people, nearly a quarter, 25 percent of Ukraine`s total population will be displaced by this growing conflicts.\par \par \uc2 There`s a glimmer of good news. Yesterday, Russian and Ukrainian negotiators did agree to set up humanitarian corridors to safely evacuate civilians away from the battle. And the U.S., E.U., U.K. and Canada all announced programs this week to relax immigration requirements for some Ukrainians. But to understand the scale of this challenge, I`m joined now by David Miliband. He`s the president and CEO of the \uc2 International Rescue Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A former -- by the way, foreign secretary of the United Kingdom. David, it`s good to see you. Let me just start. Look, we tried to put the introduction here. Explain what your organization is doing. What needs are not being met? And I assume it is Poland is ground zero here for this challenge. What help do they need?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MILIBAND, PRESIDENT AND CEO OF THE INTERNATIONAL RESCUE COMMITTEE: Thanks, Chuck, for your interest. I`ve been speaking to a team on the ground in Poland today. And I think the way to understand this is to see that there are three parts to the humanitarian crisis in Ukraine and around it at the moment. Part one, and the most extreme is the people who are stuck in the cities where there is fighting.\par \par \uc2 They`re hiding of shelters. They`re hiding in undergrounds, in metro stations, and they`re under bombardment. We`ve got the prospect of siege of cities. And we remember that from the 1990s in Sarajevo, but also from (INAUDIBLE) second part of this is people on the move inside Ukraine. Richard Engel is doing an amazing job of showing some of the needs at the train stations. And then you`ve got the third group are the people who make it to safety in Europe.\par \par \uc2 And you`re absolutely right to say there`s been a strong European reaction, a united European reaction to welcome these refugees, a million of them so far. But the needs inside Ukraine are only going to grow. And that`s where our greatest fears are at the moment. Healthcare, water, food, those are all going to be extremely important in what I think is going to be days, weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, there have been some reports as much as the neighboring countries have liberalized their border laws and some of their documentation needs. There has been some documented proof. I want to read you a statement from the \uc2 International Organization for Migration\uc2 . That some non-Ukrainian nationals that have been trying to flee have been held up. We have credible -- this is what they wrote.\par \par \uc2 We have credible and verified information from partners and humanitarians present on borders with neighboring countries that have documented discrimination against several third country nationals arriving in neighboring countries. They also have documented act of xenophobia based on people`s race, ethnicity and nationality. This, if you recall, during the North African migration crisis in Eastern Europe, we saw a lot of prejudice essentially get used in keeping people from going across the border there. Are you seeing this and what are we doing about it?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. We had a statement out on Wednesday about the same thing. This is an appalling abuse. Refugees are teachers, they`re accountants, they`re journalists, they`re charity workers, they`re farmers, they`re people. And it doesn`t matter what their race or their religion is, or it shouldn`t matter. And there`s been an appropriate outcry in the last few days. My information this morning, is that there have been significant changes on the European side of the border.\par \par \uc2 There need to be changes on the Ukraine side of the border as well. You`re absolutely right to draw attention to this because every one of these people is a story of heartbreaking tragedy. And it`s absolutely essential given the values that are at stake in this crisis, the values of frankly, decency and the rule of law that they`re not sullied by appalling discrimination. The overwhelming number of people are obviously Ukrainian.\par \par \uc2 But there are people from Africa, from South-Asia, from India who were students or workers -- country and they need to be treated properly as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do we protect a situation in Ukraine that clearly has happened in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:03]\par \par \uc2 So, if Ukraine falls into Russian hands, we got to get humanitarian aid in there and not have those folks be held hostage. The Afghan people are essentially being held hostage by perhaps its distrust of the west of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , however, we want to look at it. But there are a whole bunch of people suffering as a result of this. And we`ve not been able to break that. How do we prevent that from happening in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, it doesn`t need to happen in Afghanistan, it doesn`t need to happen in Ukraine. And I don`t want to be glib. But we`ve got to just do the right thing. If we were doing the right thing in Afghanistan, it wouldn`t have a starvation crisis. And doing the right thing in the Afghan context is about international economics. In respect of the Ukraine crisis, it`s going to be about establishing really effective mechanisms for getting aid into Ukraine, across borders, across conflict lines.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also going to involve the political and economic pressure to stop the fighting. We as humanitarians can staunch the bleeding if you like, but we need the politicians to stop the killing. And that is going to be a long process if one takes seriously, which I think we should read out of the Putin-Macron call yesterday. Things are going to get worse. But they`re -- the essential requirement on us is to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 And from my point of view, there is a danger here that the people in Afghanistan or in Ethiopia or in Yemen who were also serving, they end up paying the price of the focus of the world`s attention on -- that will be quite wrong. We need to do the right thing in both theaters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know that you are focused on the humanitarian crisis, you are a former politician, you`re a former foreign secretary. I am curious watching Europe unite right now, it took a while. Europe seems more united than ever. Without Zelensky`s bravery would Europe be this United right now?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I think so. That President Zelensky has done an amazing job. But frankly, the penny really dropped when the Russians invaded Ukraine eight days ago. And I think that the European reaction over that Friday, Saturday, Sunday, where they moved from doubt about intelligence, at least on part of some of the Western Europeans, to a full embrace of a new posture, a new military posture, a new economic posture, a new geopolitical posture.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s very significant indeed for Germany to be so out and so loud about its defense commitments. For Sweden and Finland to be thinking about joining NATO. For Ireland to be party to European decisions on the military front. This is a major change. And the critical thing is that -- is that a momentum is now built. I do recognize, and I`m happy to answer questions about the divisions that you`ve spoken to in the past.\par \par \uc2 The time for divisions is over, the time for real action and real unity is now and that needs to go beyond the Transatlantic Alliance. This is now a global effort to counter unity with accountability in the name of a rules- based order. And that applies not just on military questions, it applies across the peace, climate, health care, et cetera, as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: David Miliband, as well said, I got a bunch to follow up on that. Really appreciate you coming on. And I`m glad that you helped bring some attention to your efforts here. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 We are still expecting the Secretary of State Tony Blinken to speak this hour from Brussels where he`s been meeting with NATO and E.U. allies all day. We`re going to bring you those remarks as soon as they begin.\par \par \uc2 Still to come. A wartime presidency brings wartime politics. How the crisis in Ukraine could reshape the landscape here in Washington. And on the campaign trail. A couple of important issues. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:28]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We are starting to see a growing level of political fallout at home to the war abroad, both in Washington and on the campaign trail. The White House is facing pressure from lawmakers on both parties to sanction the Russian energy industry or ban the import of all Russian oil, with many also pushing legislation to increase American drilling in hopes of keeping gas prices from spiking further.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the reelection campaigns are ramping up. Lawmakers` records on Russia are getting some renewed scrutiny. In fact take a look at this ad from former presidential candidate Evan McMullen, who`s the first one who`s using Putin and Ukraine in an ad. He`s challenging Republican Mike Lee for his Senate seat as an independent in Utah. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As the world rallies around Ukraine, Mike Lee was one of only two senators to oppose sanctions on Putin. Then he flew to the Kremlin and discussed dropping sanctions. Lee even opposed arming Ukrainians fighting for their lives.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lee abandoned our values, making us weak and unsafe.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So to help us break down what`s at stake amid this new wartime political environment, joining me on set Democratic strategists, Xochitl Hinojosa, Bill Kristol, founding director of Defending Democracy Together, and Betsy Woodruff Swan, national correspondent at "Politico."\par \par \uc2 Betsy, I want to start with you. This is the first TV ad I`ve seen that`s invoked Putin. I want to get into the energy debate that is about to be sparked with that here and in fact it feels like that`s one where you have a growing number of Democrats who say, yes, let`s ban all Russian oil, and I do think you`ll have a growing number of Democrats ready to join Republicans saying yes, let`s drill for more, too.\par \par \uc2 BETSY WOODRUFF SWAN, POLITICO NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: The idea of the U.S. ceasing imports of oil and gas from Russia is something that just based on my conversations with folks in the administration is a total nonstarter. And the argument that Biden`s team makes, which is totally reasonable, is that oil is fungible. It`s a global commodity. If the U.S. stops importing it, the Chinese will just buy it. It won`t do anything magical to damage Russia`s economy. That`s the position they`re at.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Ukrainian members of parliament have been really, really banging the drum here because what they`re looking for are moves that not just have an actual impact on the state of play for Russia`s economy, but also that are symbolically important and their hope is that momentum will build. But I just don`t see this happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Xochitl, symbolism, look, that`s like hearing a pushback on the idea of suspending the gas tax. Yes. Doesn`t make any sense. Sometimes symbolism matters more than what makes sense.\par \par \uc2 XOCHITL HINOJOSA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, listen, we have been hammering Joe Biden for the last few months about rising costs.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that they have said is that it could impact supply. If it impacts supply cost could go up, which is totally understandable. I think what the White House is doing right now is smart, is they are trying to rally Republicans and Democrats around ensuring that they are tough on Russia and rallying around Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What Republicans are doing is, yes, they are going to play partisan politics regardless. You have Joe Biden who stood up at the State of the Union, showed leadership, spent half of the State of the Union talking about what he was going to do, and he has the American people on his side. About 76 percent of the American people support the economic sanctions. And so as long as Biden continues to show leadership and continues to show that he`s putting America first, then I think he will be in a solid standing.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, you know, during tough times leaders are tested. Joe Biden is being tested and right now I think he is holding on strong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bill, you worked in -- Congress is frustrated because they have nothing to do. They have no way of having an impact on this. What`s a way to have an impact? Hey, we can do this. Right? They`ve already -- in some ways, and so I think there are some of that is a motivation. They want to show their constituents, hey, we`re doing something, too. And this would be that, I think.\par \par \uc2 BILL KRISTOL, DEFENDING DEMOCRACY TOGETHER FOUNDING DIRECTOR: Yes, but actually Congress can play a useful role in these circumstances. I mean, I was there in the `90s. John McCain, Bob Dole, many others, including many Democrats, pushed the Clinton administration to be more quickly in Bosnia, which I think was the right position and eventually Bill Clinton did.\par \par \uc2 Now some of what I think is happening from both Republicans and Democrats, didn`t Speaker Pelosi also call for not letting it go on? I think reasonable policy, as Betsy says, it`s a complicated policy choices, it doesn`t hurt to have people on the outside saying well, wait a second, if we thought this through, what about one of the downsides, maybe we could be a little more aggressive, and encouraging, getting some arms, more arms to Ukraine, what about other things.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there`s some good faith instances where the system is working. On the other hand you also have Republicans, I saw these two tweets back-to-back this morning. Gas prices are up a dollar since, you know, Kevin McCarthy had his outrage, terrible Biden doesn`t care.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: And the second one was literally and incidentally we should be tougher on the oil imports. Well, you can`t have both. That`s just politics. So I think so far I would say given how tough our politics has been, don`t you think it`s been better than I would have expected. I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s been better so far.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think let`s see what happens with these gas prices.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Most of the criticism has been reasonable policy criticism or nudging so to speak. Some demagoguery, but let`s see, right?\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF-SWAN: I think part of the reason the politics have been the way they are is the situation is changing so far.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF-SWAN: For the first 48 hours or so, there was an expectation that Kyiv was going to fall right way. The fact that the Ukrainians have hung on for as long as they have, the fact that there`s been the level of popular outcry in Europe and the United States and around the world in support of Ukraine. That`s the kind of thing these elected officials are really sensitive to. I think the politics will be different in a month depending on what`s happening in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This Putin sanction stuff, I think about on that -- so Evan McMullen was hitting on Mike Lee and I think Ron Johnson was on that trip, and there was a couple of head scratchers if I remember, the Fourth of July, John Thune was like, what are you doing there on that trip? It was a weird decision. Is there going to be a political price to pay, though, or do you think we have gold (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Let`s hope not. I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the leader of the Republican Party. They were all pledging fealty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he was pro-Putin until basically three days ago. Right? I mean, Saturday when it was pretty what was happening with the devastation that Putin had unleashed that Putin is smart and clever, and then yesterday didn`t he sort of encourage basically China to take Taiwan or at least made an excuse ahead of time anything that President Xi --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He seemed to say this would be a good time.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Yes. Good timing. Biden is weak.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good timing to do this if you`re China.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: That is so, so deplorable, if can use the term. I mean, genuinely, the former president, he is not just a random senator on the backbench, this is the former president of the United States. And the fact that other Republicans don`t feel any need to say, wait, that does not speak for our party. The fact that Mitch McConnell --\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. How long have we heard that statement?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m sorry, I mean --\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Since 2017? Is this 2018? Is this 2019?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: We`re at war now so supposedly it might be different.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s what I`m wondering. Does war make all of this different, Betsy?\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF-SWAN: What`s important for Republican politics here is that there`s a very easy way to gauge how exercised Republicans are about what`s going on in Ukraine. The closer our Republican politicians is to the GOP base the less they care about Ukraine. The farther away you get from the base, the more you care. So on this end of the spectrum, you`ve got Inhofe who was about to resign, you`ve got Rob Portman who`s not running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Enthusiastic champions of Ukrainian democracy. And then meanwhile, all the way over on the other end, you`ve folks like J.D. Vance, Blake Masters, people who are running in competitive Republican primaries who are basically saying, why do we care, nobody in the United States even knows where Ukraine is, really downplaying any sense of responsibility the West may have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And yet, Xochitl, there was this focus group and it`s a focus group and it`s qualitative, not quantitative, where swing voters, Trump-Biden voters, they seem to buy the idea that Putin wouldn`t have done this when Trump was president. How does the Democratic Party answer for that? I don`t buy that. I don`t think that that matches logic. But voters do. That`s a perception issue.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:07]\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Well, one of the eerie things about this is just picture an America where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is our president right now. I mean that is scary. And what Democrats have to do is they have to ensure and go out there and run ads against Republicans that are not standing up against Trump and at the same time say we`re protecting American interest. Look at the leadership that Joe Biden brought.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden was brought into office and elected into office because people wanted some normalcy, people wanted a leader. People wanted someone who wasn`t going to alienate our allies and that`s what they`re seeing from Joe Biden. And it`s in stark contrast from where Trump was and even where Trump has been in the last few days, right? But you do have a Republican Party that is loyal to Trump, especially those who are in primaries right now and Democrats should use that against the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bill, I think a lot of people kept wondering when are Republicans going to take their offramp? They`ve had a few Trump offramps. Right? I got to think Mitch McConnell is -- I`ve always thought Mitch McConnell will never admit it but he will second guess that vote decision he made in the second impeachment probably in perpetuity. But nobody wants to take this offramp. The most obvious offramp right now from Trump is what he`s been doing with Putin and they`re not taking it. Why?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Well, you know why because they think they`re going to win the House. They have a good chance to it. In the Senate they have paid no price for not taking all those offramps that you and I and most -- a lot of my friends think they should have taken for the sake of the country. And until they`re made to pay a price, and maybe they will, Democrat, if I could say, I love the Democrats these days but they can do a little better job of kind of not having dissident Democratic members of Congress offer their own State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: It really isn`t. I mean, you know, you mentioned the focus group. But there`s a new NPR poll that has Biden up a few points.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: That cannot be due to the State of the Union. I think there is still a rally to the flag effect, and Biden has shown good leadership. I have tiny criticisms of a few things. But good leadership throughout this crisis, and that`s why we elect presidents because of crises like these.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Piece of raw politics here. Rick Scott announced his campaign for Senate Republican leader apparently, Betsy. I say this because he decided to write an op-ed defending his decision to put out his own 11- point plan and said, yes, everybody should know what it`s like to pay taxes, despite McConnell`s rebuke. I mean, this seems to be -- he`s put it down on the floor, here I am.\par \par \uc2 WOODRUFF-SWAN: It`s really an amazing level of tension within the Senate Republican conference, which is one of the few remaining usually collegial groups of people on Capitol Hill, the fact that Scott is really taking a shot at McConnell in this op-ed is, I imagine, just a symbol of much more to come. And it points to the fact that McConnell has a real weakness with the Republican base that Scott is positioned to exploit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, I hate to put it in these stark terms. If Republicans win the Senate is Rick Scott majority leader? And if Republicans don`t win the Senate, Mitch McConnell`s the minority leader, Bill? Is that what we`re looking at?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: I think Scott -- I think there`s more of a chance of Scott opposing McConnell -- we`re also used to --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think it is, too.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: We`re also used to McConnell being leader, always got real loyalty. Well, does he have that much loyalty? An awful lot of these senators were elected in the last two, three, four cycles. Scott may look more like an up-and-coming guy, younger. Scott also has in the back of his mind obviously running for president in 2024. So one or both.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think Xochitl is suddenly a big fan of the NRSC chair.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Very.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They like Democrats. It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 Xochitl, Bill and Betsy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, if it`s the first Friday of the month, we had a jobs report, it`s quite something. What it means for workers and the White House. "Data Download" is next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. "Data Download" time. First Friday of the month and that means we get an update on the jobs picture here in America. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  says that the U.S. added a whopping 678,000 jobs in February, beat the forecasts that were sitting around 440,000. The unemployment rate fell to 3.8 percent in the process. Pretty much where it was before the pandemic. We are this close to recovering every single job lost during the pandemic. I mean, that`s like we`re within a nose hair of it, it`s 3.5 percent on the unemployment rate by the way in 2020.\par \par \uc2 All around it`s a solid jobs report for the president but there was -- is likely going to be overshadowed by the news from Ukraine. It`s all part of an ongoing issues facing this White House as it struggled to make positive economic numbers break through, other headwinds and crises, particularly when it comes to people`s perception of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Looking at the full picture of this Biden presidency hundreds of thousands of jobs have been added each month, particularly after revision. The economy has added an average of half a million jobs over the last three months, even amid the Omicron peak. But thanks to inflation and other issues, Americans say they have a dim view of the Biden economy.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today"-Suffolk poll from last week had a combined 51 percent of the country saying that we are in an economic recession or depression. Even as job numbers like today show that the economy is rapidly recovering from the pandemic shock, we do not have a jobs problem, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 We are still waiting to hear from Secretary Blinken. Those remarks are possible when we are back from the break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Vice President Kamala Harris heads to Selma, Alabama, this weekend. It is to mark the 57th anniversary of Bloody Sunday. The anniversary commemorates a turning point in the fight for civil rights when activists John Lewis and Hosea Williams led hundreds of marchers across the Edmund Pettus Bridge only to be met with horrific violence by the state troops barring their way.\par \par \uc2 The officers attacked the activists with their night sticks and tear gas. John Lewis, then only 25, suffered a fractured skull. Two weeks later, Martin Luther King Jr. led another symbolic march across that bridge. Five months later, the Voting Rights Act of 1965 was signed into law.\par \par \uc2 This will be the second anniversary of Bloody Sunday since the passing of Congressman John Lewis, as voting rights activists across the country continue to carry on his legacy.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by civil rights icon and host of "POLITICS NATION" here on MSNBC, Reverend Al Sharpton. He`s going to be delivering the key note sermon at this Sunday`s anniversary.\par \par \uc2 Reverend Sharpton, it`s good to see you. And I`m curious, you know, to me, this moment of Bloody Sunday and what we`re seeing in Europe, there is a connection. It`s a fight for freedom, it`s a fight for democracy.\par \par \uc2 And Rev, it`s a statement I thought we would hear from the president on Tuesday that connected the two. He didn`t quite do that. I image you will be.\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST, POLITICS NATION: I certainly will be. And I think that you said it perfectly, Chuck. And I wish the president said it. You cannot fight and you should for the right for the people of Ukraine to have a democratic state and make decisions based on voting and then not deal with that same right to be upheld here in America, when we`re looking at laws being changed in Alabama where we`ll be this weekend, in Texas, in North Carolina, and on, where you have going up to the Supreme Court, this whole question of an independent state doctrine, where they`re trying to say that state courts should not be even able to stop state legislatures from changing voting regulations in voting districts.\par \par \uc2 So as we look for and pray for the people in Ukraine, we need to also deal domestically. And I think that connection is very clear and we need to make it clear. And I will be doing that in the sermon at Brown Chapel on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know the 2020 turnout in Selma was just 57 percent. In Selma in 2020. It was among the worst in the state of Alabama. What does that tell you about the current state of voting access in this country?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It tells us a lot. It tells us that, one, access is stopping things and it tells us that we have to inspire people to go past the roadblocks, as they did in the `60s, as you talked about John Lewis and Hosea Williams. You must remember that according to reports, over 30 percent of the early voting ballots in Texas in this past Tuesday`s primary was rejected based on new Texas laws.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to see low amounts of voting everywhere if we do not challenge and turn around a lot of these restrictive laws that have been put in place since 2020 in 19 states or more. We are dealing with a voting emergency in this country, as we deal with Ukraine, and we need to deal with it, and that`s why this weekend in Selma is extremely important because we need to underscore that we cannot be global liberators and at home ignore the threat to democracy right here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Vice president is going to be marching with you. Who else is going to be with you? And this to me is always such an important -- it`s symbolic, but it is an important, it`s always been an important moment of unity. Are we going to have that on Sunday as well?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I believe so. Senator Sanders and others have worked there in Alabama, Congresswoman Sewell and Martin Luther King III and many of the civil rights community, all of us will be marching. And I think it is important that as the vice president comes and I salute her for breaking into her schedule to be there, because had it not been for the march in Selma in `65, a black woman wouldn`t be the vice president.\par \par \uc2 A black woman wouldn`t be nominated to the Supreme Court. But we cannot take them as symbols without substance. And that`s what Sunday is about and that`s what every day ought to be about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Reverend Sharpton, I look forward to hearing your keynote sermon on Sunday and be sure to tune into "POLITICS NATION" this weekend at 5:00 p.m. right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Rev, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS" on your local NBC station. Got a busy show. Very Ukraine heavy.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage will continue as we await the secretary of state`s press conference from Brussels right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220305ei3400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 3, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Carol Lam, Mike Memoli, Richard Engel, Kelly Cobiella,  Clint Watts \par \uc2 8,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Intensifies, Zelenskyy speaks out. A massive humanitarian crisis though is unfolding, and officials warn we have yet to see the worst of this. Plus, the White House prepares to announce new sanctions against members of Putin's inner circle, as it seeks 10 billion in emergency aid from Congress to help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee joins me up ahead. And later, in a new legal filing, the January 6 Committee suggests it has the evidence to recommend criminal charges for former President Trump as it seeks access to key documents. How will this legal fight play out? We'll discuss that ahead as well.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I'm Chuck Todd. One week into this war in Ukraine, Russia is gaining territory. Lives are being lost and more than a million people are seeking safety outside of Ukraine's borders. U.S. and European officials warn though we haven't seen the worst of this. Kherson, in southern Ukraine, has become the first major city to fall. Its Mayor confirmed NBC News that Russian troops have captured the city.\par \par \uc2 Russia now controls much of eastern Ukraine as its military regroups in an effort to try and seize the capital of Kyiv. Up north, however, a senior Pentagon official tells NBC News that a Russian convoy has not made any dramatic advances and remains about 25 kilometers outside Kyiv. Residents in the city though continue to prepare for an attack, building tank blockers like this one, as Russian forces attack Ukrainian cities by, air firing more than 480 missiles in the last week, that's according to a senior defense department official.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russia and Ukraine are holding a second round of peace talks today. But honestly, neither side expected much progress to be made in it. And this was the scene in Kyiv earlier today with President Zelenskyy holding a news conference, including answering our question from our own Richard Engel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: You just mentioned that you want to talk to Vladimir Putin. Vladimir Putin has so far not been willing to meet with you. Do you have a message for him now that Ukrainian cities are under attack, this city is under attack, a convoy is on its way here, is there a way to prevent this war from escalating even further now?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: It's not about I want to talk with Putin. I think I have to talk with Putin. The world has to talk with Putin because there are no other ways to stop this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Despite stronger than expected resistance from Ukrainian forces and volunteer Ukrainian militias, U.S. and European officials fear the darkest days of this invasion still lie ahead. One Western intelligence official tells NBC News the violence level is going to go up, the numbers of refugees will go up, the numbers of civilian casualties and civilian dead will go up. The UN says more than a million Ukrainians have already fled the country in just the last seven days and that number could go up significantly as this conflict escalates.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Mike Memoli from the White House, Richard Engel's in Kyiv, Clint Watts, a research fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute, and MSNBC Contributor. He's at our big board today in New York. He will explain what's going on with these Russian troop movements. And Kelly Cobiella is in Poland.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with Mike at the White House. And, Mike, I want to start simply with what's next. A lot of senators are asking for a full embargo on Russian oil, that seems to be the next most significant thing we can do, Whitehouse has been hesitant on this, where are they today?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, yesterday you were asking me why we didn't see President Biden make a more fulsome rewrite of his State of the Union address to focus on the situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And we're getting more insight I think, to the answer as to why he did not in the form of that reporting you were referencing from our colleagues Carol Lee, Josh Lederman, Courtney Kube about really the dire assessments coming not just from the U.S. but from Western intelligence officials about what we see coming potentially in the days ahead in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The fact that despite the valiant, despite the real heroic efforts on the part of Ukraine's military, the civilian population to stymie Russians invasion, that ultimately there is the assessment that the Ukraine Government will fall and that it will pave the way for a civilian-led resistance, something of an insurgency in the days ahead. So that does pose, as you say, even more, difficult questions to the Biden administration as to what more they can do. We are reporting that there will likely be more sanctions levied today on some of Putin's inner circle and oligarchs around him.\par \par \uc2 But the real question is about oil imports, and this is where the domestic agenda and domestic politics sort of, impedes potentially the administration's response throughout this crisis and throughout the imposition of various forms of sanctions. They've always been clear about wanting to mitigate the harm to Americans at home and their own pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 And what we're hearing from the administration is that they are now in frittering forms of sanctions and forms of a blockade on the Russian energy sector but not necessarily on daily oil sales because the concern there is that would drive the price of oil even higher, it would allow Russia to profit even more from the sales of wherever they can find the market for it.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And so instead, they're looking at things like further export controls on materials that would allow Russia to further expand their drilling, their oil exploration capacity. But as we saw from Speaker Pelosi today here in Washington endorsing the idea of a full blockade of oil and Russian energy imports, and when you hear that from Pelosi, who's very mindful of domestic politics, as well as the international politics, that's a very interesting signal and perhaps puts more pressure on the Biden administration to move forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, Congress does seem to be accelerating this. And the President met virtually today with the QUAD, which is a sort of a new unofficial group of Asian Pacific allies, Japan, Australia, India, and the United States. Notably, India has its own relationship with Russia, has not voted with the rest -- most of the world to condemn what Russia did, they stand at the United Nations, any insight on that meeting and how it went?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, we just got a readout from the White House on that meeting, it was the typical language about the Indo Pacific region and the -- you know, countering China's influence. What was interesting and notable was that there was a reference to a meeting of these QUAD leaders in Tokyo in the coming months, we expect the President to be traveling to Tokyo later this year.\par \par \uc2 But you're right, the pressure there, I think the reason this was convened, we've seen President Biden focused on doing everything in unanimity with our Western allies, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  with NATO, but India clear and conspicuous hold out and so that was likely why this meeting was held today. But we'll be reporting out to see more of whether we'll see India moving in to join the rest of this opposition to what Russia is doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard Engel in Kyiv, Richard, we obviously saw your exchange with President Zelenskyy. He is obviously trying to be as out there as possible. It -- his mere presence clearly has moved the Europeans to do more in the last week than they were prepared to do just a week ago. I assume he is still hopeful that somehow a no-fly zone could be in -- could be invoked and secured by NATO. That seems to be a bridge too far for the United States or NATO. What are some of his other asks? And are any of the things that the U.S. in the West can actually do?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So he had several asks, one -- yes, he is being much more visible than he used to be before this war began. He's giving two or three video statements every single day. And today, he gathered some reporters here in Kyiv and spoke with us at length, over an hour in a -- in a government compound. And he seemed calm, cool, relaxed, a little tired.\par \par \uc2 But his main ask was for the no-fly zone. I don't know if he's hopeful that it'll happen but he repeated that they need it. And he needed it -- and he said if the world recognizes the threat and recognizes that there is a threat that Putin won't stop at Ukraine, then countries need to take action. That they can't just talk about the threat, they need to help Ukraine stop the threat.\par \par \uc2 He also needs more weapons. They -- there are supply issues. And he said that they need as many weapons as they can get real weapons. And so his main asks were to help them fight, help them fight with a no-fly zone, and help them fight with weapons. And of course, as was mentioned in the exchange, he wants a dialogue with Vladimir Putin, which is something that Vladimir Putin has so far not been interested in doing.\par \par \uc2 And based on the conversation that Putin had with the French President Macron today and the statements that Putin gave on Russian television, he is not backing down from this -- from this fight by any means. He still is determined to continue to object -- to carry out his objective of overthrowing the government and specifically overthrowing Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard, is Zelenskyy at all indicated that he is trying to prepare for continuity of government, a government in exile even if he physically doesn't have to Lviv for instance, or maybe even outside the country? Are there folks in his administration that are preparing to in case Kyiv gets overtaken by the Russians?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: If they are planning that, they are certainly not making it public, and he didn't discuss that at all today. In fact, when asked if he intends to leave Kyiv or if there's any circumstance in which he would leave Kyiv, he said no, he intends to stay here whether they are working out alternative plans for fallback government is hard to know.\par \par \uc2 But I think the Ukrainian government does realize that it needs to keep him visible and needs to show that he is still in Kyiv because there is a concern that if you were to leave if the government were to set up in a foreign country or in the far west, that it would be a pretext to have a coup here.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 That some Putin sycophant is already in Ukraine could then claim that the - - that Zelenskyy has abandoned his post, abandon the government, and announced the creation of a new government. So he definitely wants to show that he's here and to show his people that he's still in charge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard Engel, on the ground for us in Kyiv, Richard, thank you. Let me move to Kelly Cobiella and the growing humanitarian crisis. We're up to over a million Ukrainians have left the country and obviously, those numbers could escalate, assuming the Russian violence escalates. Kelly, how overwhelmed are things right now?\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Poland said before the fighting broke out that they were prepared to take in at least a million Ukrainian refugees, they're at more than half a million at this point. And some of the border towns are starting to get a bit squeezed.\par \par \uc2 And it takes a lot to process this vast number of people in this short time. There are a number of reception centers set up. But really these shelters that are along these border towns are more set up for people to just take a breath, get warm, rest. These are people who've been traveling, as we've said in the past couple of days for days at a time and they're exhausted.\par \par \uc2 But then to move on to other cities, we've said before Poland has a Ukrainian population of about 2 million and a lot of the people we're seeing is sort of being absorbed into the community. They're meeting up with family, relatives, friends, and staying with them. But that is starting to shift a bit and it's definitely shifting in the other border countries like Moldova, which has a tiny population of 4 million, like Romania, like Hungary and some of these countries are now starting to turn to the EU for support.\par \par \uc2 Another development today, Chuck, all seven member nations of the EU unanimously agreed today, and this is unprecedented, to enact that directive which allows Ukrainian refugees to stay in the EU and work, importantly, work and access services in any EU country for up to three years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is a huge development there. We shall see how this grows there. Kelly Cobiella in Poland for us, Kelly, thanks. Let me move over to Clint Watts. And Clint, you're doing something a little bit different for us today, and that is trying to help explain what the Russians are up to, what is this military strategy, and how has it changed over the last week?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: That's right, Chuck. We've heard a lot about Russian failures and difficulties but overnight, we're really seeing what happens when Russia has successes. What we see here is a map of the South. Remember, in 2014, the Russians took Crimea, and the one axis of advance that they've had into Ukraine has been from the south where they've been successful.\par \par \uc2 Right now, you see forces that have broken through are on this axis here, running along the coast of the Sea of Azov. They are now laying siege to the Mariupol' in such a way that it will be absolutely devastating in the coming days. We're talking large indirect fires, artillery, missiles, munitions they are devastating this town, total warfare.\par \par \uc2 Separately, you started off, Chuck, talking about Kherson. Kherson is important because it is the land bridge. It's an essential bridge that connects Crimea to the rest of Ukraine over the Dnieper River. Kherson, if once taken, allows them to advance forces all the way out to the east.\par \par \uc2 And if they are successful, if they can make this landbridge, they can then pursue all the way to the Moldovan border, essentially cutting off that small sliver that you see down there in the southwest and eliminating the possibility for Ukraine to really be supplied by sea. This would just cut off the entire southern area and we create a landbridge to their forces out here in the east.\par \par \uc2 Now, separately, Chuck with that, we still need to be talking about what's going on in Kyiv. You brought up logistics and President Zelenskyy mentioned it as well. The battle from here on out is really about three things, food, fuel, and ammunition. Those three things are what keep armies alive.\par \par \uc2 And a big topic of conversation has been this enormous convoy that we've seen here in Ukraine. This convoy, hopefully, I can get his pull up here, the board's moving about the same speed as the convoy at the moment. But this convoy is not what we would typically see. I was an infantry officer when I was trying to talk to about Soviet or Russian forces, they are stacked up. This is a traffic jam right here. That is an indication that I'm worried about airpower. There are some reports that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say they look like a sitting duck.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: They are, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: And that really tells you about why you're hearing President Zelenskyy say, hey, what about a no-fly zone? What about essentially bringing in some airpower? There are some reports that the Ukrainians have hidden part of this convoy. This is essential for the log jam to be broken for the Russian military going into Kyiv. This is also not a disciplined military force.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 This isn't the vaunted Russian armored divisions that we -- we're really told about leading up to the invasion. They're sloppy, they're bogged down, and they're going to struggle in terms that are advanced. Now, with that, one thing to think about is when you look at the broader map in terms of Kyiv, what they have been able to do is start to advance in other areas.\par \par \uc2 You see massive shelling here in Chernihiv. Chernihiv is not a major strategic -- important point except for one thing. This is the rear area, essentially, of that advancing force. They were sloppy, they were bold, and they got caught trying to go in the first week. In this new period, what you're seeing them do is build that lodge head, build that combat power so that they can sustain the siege of Kyiv, and what we're seeing in Mariupol starting right now, that should foreshadow what we might see in Kyiv as they build up a combat force. It can be devastating, and it -- just a little bit of airpower, make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So we're going to slowly -- assuming that nobody else intervenes. Is the Russian strategy is a very slow choking off of Kyiv, and is that what we're going to be watching? It's like a slow-motion car wreck. We're going to just watch this slow advance from the South, the East, and the North with really only the West being perhaps a way to get supply lines to Ukrainian soldiers in Kyiv. Is that what we're looking at here?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: You're absolutely right, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yes, they're going to lay siege to Kyiv in hopes that they will topple the government. The initial plan was crazy from one perspective with the Russians, but it depended on toppling the regime in Kyiv. Remember, they were trying to do a political coup essentially knockouts Zelenskyy and his administration.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Take over the country. Now, how are they going to do that, they're going to lay siege. Critical to watch here is once they start to envelop here to the West -- hopefully I can get this going.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: When they envelop here to the west, they will be cutting those supply lines that we're been hearing about coming in through Poland. So we're hearing humanitarian aid, lethal aid, that reports there are 2000 Stinger missiles. How are they going to get there? If they're cut off here in the south, and they're cut off in the West, Zelenskyy will have to really think about what his options are in terms of keeping this force going over time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Clint, can you imagine -- A, being flown in by, say, Polish forces that we -- that NATO sort of protected as a way in there, or do you think we wouldn't want to risk, NATO would be uncomfortable risking that?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I think NATO is totally uncomfortable due to the nuclear option. That's really, you know -- Putin's got three cards he plays against the West, nuclear, cyber, and disinformation. That's essentially his asymmetric tools. He will threaten immediately if he sees any NATO forces, and you've heard President Biden and many other leaders say that. But I'm worried about another hypothetical. Let's say, it's 30 days, the resupply is still going, they still don't have Kyiv. We should be worrying about it out here.\par \par \uc2 Right now, when you look at the border area, we've got refugees going out, but soon we're going to see resupply trains coming in. And there's this other issue that's there. President Zelenskyy calls for foreign fighters. If foreign fighters start showing up, if they start flowing into the country, Russia will want to go after that combat power as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I would think that the Russian forces, while they're focused here if they get bogged down, they're going to try and move their forces and fires out towards the west.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Clint, the bottom line is this no longer looks like a fast operation that the Russians are planning on. This is now a long siege. Is that -- is that how you would assess this?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Not only a long siege, but Putin's got trouble at home, Chuck. You're starting to see a lot of panic at home. I'm worried about what if Putin is losing in 30 days. What does he do at home? What is he doing in Ukraine? I think all his options are on the table. We've heard of executions, assassinations, and nuclear threats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I'm worried this really turns into a very dangerous situation in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Clint Watts, really appreciate you taking some time and sort of walking folks through this, very well done. And before that, Richard, Kelly, and Mike, thank you for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as this war intensifies, the White House is asking Congress for billions on emergency aid to support Ukraine. Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , he's chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, he's going to be here after the break.\par \par \uc2 And later, we're going to dive into the January 6 Committee's latest legal filing. And the fallout, you saw it, the word criminal in Trump, in the same headline, got a lot of people wondering what is this about. We're going to break it down for you later in the show. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As U.S. officials warn that the worst is yet to come in Ukraine, the Biden administration is preparing to open its wallet. The OMB Director, Shalanda Young, officially sent a letter to Congress requesting an additional $10 billion in humanitarian and defense assistance to be added to this year's budget. And at the same time, a source tells NBC News that new sanctions against an expanded list of Russian oligarchs and their families are expected today. But there is a demand for more to be done.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Senate Foreign Relations Committee Chairman \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . Senator Menendez, it's good to see you. Let me start with the next perhaps most realistic punishment we can inflict and that is a full oil embargo on Russia. Where are you on this, and is it something that's gaining some traction?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): Well, I support the fall embargo, even though it would equate to about if it's just the U.S. alone, about 4 percent of Russian sales globally but I think it is an important step to make, and I think it is gaining traction. And I think that you know, the President will have to look to the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and other measures to offset any domestic challenges. But look, an evil horrible war needs some dramatic actions. And so I think this is one of many that can be taken.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You -- I don't know how much you were able to watch our previous segment, sort of we now see what the -- what the Russian military strategy is, and it's -- the initial strategy didn't work. This one is going to be costlier for him but perhaps more violent, more messy, more bloody. Senator, it's going to be hard to sit here and watch. Is it not? And to watch a slaughter, to watch that convoy and do nothing about it, I get the fears. Is there anything you think we should be doing more than you think folks are too hesitant to try?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, listen, $10 billion of both defensive lethal equipment added on to the billion dollars of just defensive lethal equipment that we've supplied to Ukraine is a huge boost to the Ukrainians' ability to fight at the end of the day. I think we strategically have to think about the West as well. We need Zelenskyy to be safe because as we face the challenges of Kyiv, we need to have a government that we can work within the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And so, looking at the West and how we protect that line of assistance is important. I see a lot of the bombing, the indiscriminate bombing, including us civilian targets, notwithstanding what the Russians say they are targeting civilian targets, residences, hospitals, and other key areas are coming from ballistic missiles.\par \par \uc2 So in terms of the question of airspace, that won't necessarily stop cruise and other missiles that the -- that the Russians have and have been using in this regard. So I think we need to get this $10 billion passed, we need to get the aid to them. And I have been approving, as the Chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee that has jurisdiction over arms sales and arms transfers from other countries that we have sold to, I have been approving it as quickly as they send it to me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, are you -- how are we getting? I mean, are you convinced we're going to be able to continue to actually get these weapons into the hands of Ukrainians? I mean, it looks like we really only have one way to do it right through Poland, through the West?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: That's basically our transit point now and that's why, you know, speed is of the essence because the more that we can put in the Ukrainians' hands, then the more that they'll have both to take the fight to the Russians, as well as to impede the process of any decision of the Russians to move towards the west to cut that transit line. So time is of the essence and that's why I strongly support the administration's call for the 10 billion, it's further than what was originally thought. But from every estimate, I've heard from both the humanitarian side and the defense side, it's an appropriate number.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When it comes to if we do this oil embargo, and it does seem like there's a growing number in Congress that'd like to see it happen, a lot of Republican members say it should be in conjunction with a sort of opening up of more exploration here. Where are you on that, and is there -- is there a line where you think, yes, maybe we should -- maybe we should expand some production for a short period of time of fossil fuels? Where are you on this?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, look, I don't think one hinges on the other. I hope my Republican colleagues aren't saying we should stop Russian oil only if we have more exploration. That would be pretty outrageous. So I think we should stop Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Now, how the president chooses to meet the challenge of that percentage of Russian oil that equals the entire oil of the United States that it imports is a question for him to decide whether he opens up more Strategic Petroleum Reserves, whether he gets allies that are oil-producing nations to step up to the plate, or whether he permits some increase domestic production on lands that are already in production.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, big picture here. For now, it looks like none of this has had an impact on the Russian government, right? Putin is more defiant, right? He seems to be more intent on trying to strike back and he may be reacting emotionally. It isn't easy to see how this ends. Do you have a sense of where this goes next, and do you have a sense of where Putin's -- what is it -- what are -- what has been shared with you about Putin's sort of his persona these days?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, Chuck, I disagree with you a little bit, that hasn't hurt the Russians. They're hurting mightily. You know, a friend of mine passed to the airport and you know, in the exchange rates where you can exchange currency, and the ruble is being bought at zero. The reality is, is that their reserves abroad are frozen. He needs those reserves to continue his war. You have long lines of Russian trying to get their money out. Believe me, you know, their sovereign debt has been reduced by the major rating agencies to junk bond. This is having a real consequence here.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether he is continuing willing to accept all of those consequences on behalf of the Russian people in order to pursue his goal of taking Ukraine and extending to what was the Soviet Union as much as possible, that's another question. But you know -- but it isn't that he isn't being hurt. He may be willing to take the pain but more importantly, the Russian people are taking the pain.\par \par \uc2 As to his mental status, look, you know, he has been clustered largely for the last two years. COVID was supposedly the reason. But I don't think there's anybody telling truth to power there about what's really happening in the field, what's happening among the Russian people, and eventually, that's going to blow through.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , the Chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, as always, sir thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Earlier today on Capitol Hill there was a surprise participant in a Commerce Committee hearing on FCC nominees. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARIA CATWELL (D-WA): All of us today know that these nominees are familiar with these subjects --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was New Mexico Senator Ben Ray Lujan returning to Capitol Hill, showed up at his Commerce Meeting there. It's a month after suffering a stroke. It's great to see him back in action after surgery. The fact he's walking around looks like he is in terrific shape after this stroke.\par \par \uc2 His return gives Democrats their working majority back, paves the way for party line votes again on the Senate floor. Our camera caught up with Senator Lujan when he got out of the hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN RAY LUJAN (D-NM): I got -- did a little trim and I'm ready to work and like I said, feeling strong. The inspiring videos that you see from colleagues that would send them every day or send notes or prayers, people have just been so generous. And so on behalf of me and my family, we're just so grateful to so many people, some we know, some we don't.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still to come, after the Russian siege continues, we're asking this, how much longer can Ukraine and its people hold up? Former U.S. ambassador to Ukraine is here next after the break.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. In just a few hours Secretary Blinken is going to arrive in Brussels for five days of meetings to discuss ongoing support for Ukraine and in just about 30 minutes the president will be discussing the crisis in a meeting with his Cabinet. All of this comes seven days after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine. And while Putin's forces aren't progressing as quickly as many expected the damage done in one week is enormous.\par \par \uc2 One million refugees have already fled the country. Thousands of Ukrainian and Russian lives have already been lost. Countless civilians continue to be impacted by war every day and beautiful cities are essentially getting destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the senior of the Atlantic Council's Eurasia Center, former U.S. ambassador to Ukraine, John Herbst.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, it's good to see you. Let's start with the big picture here. It's only been a week and yet we are decoupling Russia from the civilized world, right. Certainly from Europe, here we are perhaps again. How much has Europe in some ways changed in a week or at least where Europe's going to be in a decade where this week changed the direction of Europe?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HERBST, SENIOR DIRECTOR, ATLANTIC COUNCIL EURASIA CENTER: This has been an historic week not just because of Moscow's unprecedented aggression against Ukraine, but because finally in Europe, generally, and in Germany in particular, they recognize the very dangerous foreign policy Putin has been pursuing for at least 15 or 16 years. This is just huge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is -- it is hard to see how this ends diplomatically because Putin seems to be a changed person for whatever reason. He seems to be reacting emotionally. Ukraine seems to touch him emotionally. Maybe this is just a Ukraine thing, but maybe this grows into more. Do you see a realistic way this ends less violently than it appears we're headed?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: I'm afraid it's going to become more violent before it becomes less violent. Putin is determined to impose his will on Ukraine, and the Ukrainian people have determined not to accept that, and they're going to fight. That's why his invasion so far has not gone very well for him. But one thing to understand is this is Putin's war. It's really not Russia's war. I'm not even sure it's the Kremlin's war.\par \par \uc2 We know Russian polls have shown for a while that the Russian people don't want this. We know the Russian elite, the oligarchs, technocrats don't want it. And what we learned last week in those very strange video conferences, one Putin with his national security team, the other with the defense minister and the chief soldier, Gerasimov, they don't want this either. Their body language in saying yes to Putin's policy changes was extremely uncomfortable. This is Putin's war, and he's not winning it at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would an oil embargo on Russia be effective? And how economically harmful is that to Europe?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: We've already put substantial pain on Russia with the enormous slew of sanctions, but there's no question that an oil and a gas embargo would dry up Putin's immediate cash flow. I'm not certain that the West, especially Europe, could sustain a full hydrocarbon embargo on purchases from Russia, but they might be able to sustain dropping that by 30 percent, 40 percent, 50 percent if the United States joins that.\par \par \uc2 That would be -- you know, that might cut Putin's cash coming in 40 percent, 50 percent, which, again, would constrain his ability to wage war. He wouldn't stop it, however, and he could still continue this abominable campaign.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You're basically saying, though, Europe can't afford -- they can't be at zero when it comes to Russian gas and oil?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: Not right now. Not as they depend on Russia for 40 plus percent of their natural gas. Oil might be a little bit easier, but natural gas, not so easy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, this is why I think the larger picture, it seems like there's going to be two big changes with Europe overall. Do you expect more of defense spending from European nations? Do you expect more countries that weren't in NATO applying to be in NATO in the next decade?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: There's no question that Putin's big invasion of Ukraine has been a strategic disaster for him because Germany was actually -- had a quite accommodation as policy towards Putin's aggression before last week. Now it's changed. Over the last several years, two quintessential neutral countries, Sweden and Finland, have become increasingly interested in joining NATO. And last week a poll showed that over 60 percent of the Finnish population now wants to join NATO because of Putin's aggressive foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 They understand that Putin's objectives do not end with Ukraine. They extend further into Europe, starting with the Baltic states, which of course are NATO members. That's why it's so important for us to help Ukraine beat Putin in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the decoupling in energy from Russia, how long will that take Europe if they want to do this long term?\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 HERBST: Well, if both Europe and the United States were willing to develop their own hydrocarbon resources extensively, it might be possible within a couple of years. But I understand the great requirements to move towards green energy, but that requirement has to be exercised in the real world where there are other costs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finally, are you -- there's been a lot of inspiration that the Ukrainian people have given the world. You've watched them fight, fight back at the Russians. It's going to be a rough couple of weeks going forward. How long do you expect the Ukrainian military to be able to stand up to the Russians? And do you think that maybe, just maybe, they can push them back or not?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: Well, I don't expect the Ukrainian military to push the Russians back, but they have done a remarkable job holding them thus far, and I think that ability will diminish with time. That depends a lot upon us. I'm delighted to see this additional aid going to Ukraine that the Congress is going to vote on, but frankly, we should be sending Ukraine a billion dollars or $1.5 billion of immediate military aid.\par \par \uc2 They need to get from us a lot more drones, a lot more stingers, a lot more Javelins, and we also need to make sure that our NATO allies who have former Soviet aircraft like Sukhoi and MIG fighters, send them to Ukraine. We've been too timid in arming Ukraine. We need to --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think -- do you think NATO shot that down? The United States shot that down? Because that seemed to be the idea that any NATO nations that had Soviet aircraft were going to help, and then all of a sudden that went down. What do you think shut that down?\par \par \uc2 HERBST: My understanding based upon good but not absolutely court-perfect information is that the timidity in the White House shut it down just as the timidity in the White House prevented us from sending, as some people were arguing for a year or more, to send for example anti-ship missiles to Ukraine. If we'd sent anti-ship missiles to Ukraine eight months ago, the invasion of Kherson, the invasion of Mariupol would be very different. The Russian ships would have been taken out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy. Ambassador Herbst, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 HERBST: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 HERBST: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we're going to "Meet the Midterms," as one Texas Republican calls it quits in his re-election campaign. That is a not-safe-for-work story.\par \par \uc2 You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back. In today's "Meet the Midterms," time to meet the end of a runoff before it even started. Texas Republican Congressman Van Taylor announced he is ending his bid for reelection after admitting to an extramarital affair. A day before the primary, right-leaning tabloids reported that had paid a woman to keep his affair quiet.\par \par \uc2 Taylor was pushed into a runoff after multiple challengers attacked him for his vote for a bipartisan independent commission to investigate the January 6th attack on the Capitol. Now that Taylor has pulled out of the runoff Republican nomination goes to former Collin County Judge Keith Self who will likely become the next member of Congress from this Republican-tilting district.\par \par \uc2 And we are not going to tell you the details of this gory stuff but let's just say the word ISIS pride is also a part of this scandal.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the January 6th Committee seeks key documents in its investigation as the committee goes public with its conclusion that former President Trump engaged in criminal acts. So what happens now?\par \par \uc2 You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back. The January 6th Committee issued their strongest indicator yet that when the committee has completed its work it could refer criminal charges against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the Justice Department. The committee argued in a court filing released last night that they believe and have enough of a legal basis for a court to take them seriously that Trump and his associates engaged in a criminal conspiracy to try to overturn the 2020 election/ Now key part of the filing lays it out pretty clearly.\par \par \uc2 The Select Committee has a good faith basis for concluding that the president and members of his campaign engaged in a criminal conspiracy to fraud the United States. Now the filing itself is a part of an attempt by the committee to force John Eastman, a lawyer for Trump, the infamous lawyer who tried to come up with this scheme, to turn over documents he wrote arguing that Mike Pence had the legal authority to somehow overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Eastman claims the memos are protected by the attorney-client privilege between himself and Trump. But the committee argues that since their evidence points to criminal acts by Trump and his associates, Eastman cannot invoke privilege to keep the documents out of their hands. Trump responded in a statement this morning falsely claiming that the real conspiracy was Democrats rigging the election.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Carol Lam, a former U.S. attorney and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Carol, first in this court filing, if the court finds that the committee is correct and that a crime may have been committed, then what?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, so, Chuck, there are actually a couple of arguments that the January 6th Committee makes to this judge, and again it's important to understand the context here. This is not a final report by the January 6th Committee, but it is a legal brief that they filed in the California federal court arguing that these many, many documents that Eastman has declined or refused to produce should be produced.\par \par \uc2 And there are a couple of interesting arguments. One, as you've described is the argument that these fall under what we call the crime-fraud exception to attorney-client privilege, that is you can't communicate with your client -- even if the president was his client, you can't communicate with your client in a way that furthers a crime or a fraud. So that's one basis on which the January 6th Committee says you should produce these documents.\par \par \uc2 Another one is a very easy argument that these e-mails were all done on the \uc2 Chapman University\uc2  e-mail system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAM: And it's clear that those are not private communications and they should be produced. So there are a couple of arguments that the January 6th Committee makes that could be persuasive to this judge. If the judge finds that these are good arguments, particularly the crime fraud exception, that's just one step in the proceeding because what the January 6th Committee is asking is that the judge take a look at these, you know, 11,000 documents and determine which ones do fall under the crime fraud exception.\par \par \uc2 So it is just one step along the way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAM: And it's sort of the midst of the committee's investigation, but it does show what they believe the evidence is pointing to at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carol, I'm curious, I assume that many of federal government has attempted to do this with mob attorneys. What is the success rate of being able to sort of overcome an attorney-client privilege claim by making this criminal that hey, this attorney may have helped facility a crime?\par \par \uc2 LAM: The crime fraud exception is a real exception to the attorney-client privilege and it has been used many times. I've argued it and succeeded several times, because, you know, an attorney cannot hide behind the attorney-client privilege if they are part and parcel of a fraud or a crime. I think the lift for the January 6th Committee here is to persuade the judge that there is enough evidence that this actually is a crime, and not just a lawyer sort of advising his clients.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, certainly the optics of John Eastman having invoked the Fifth Amendment in his --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAM: -- subpoena to testify and refusing to testify for that reason it's not good optics for him in this context.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now the bar is pretty low -- you don't have to prove that a crime was committed, you only have to prove that a crime may have been committed, right? Like it isn't -- there is -- it's a lower level than perhaps what the Justice Department may have to deal with in deciding whether there were crimes committed by these folks?\par \par \uc2 LAM: That is a really good point. It's certainly not the criminal standard of beyond a reasonable doubt, the judge is not sitting as an ultimate juror in a criminal case here.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 The judge has to believe that there is simply enough evidence to clear the bar of believing that there's a crime. And it is a little more difficult here. I think we should be -- you know, we should all acknowledge that. That it is -- it is not -- you know, it is not like a drug crime or a murder where it's absolutely clear. You know, there are still arguments about that. But again what the January 6th Committee is just trying to do right now is get those documents, and again they have a couple of arguments that are pretty good for getting those documents, even aside from the crime fraud exception.\par \par \uc2 But yes, you're right, if a judge actually says yes, I do believe that the January 6th Committee has produced enough evidence to clear the bar that, you know, it looks like a crime may have been committed here, that is not dispositive in any way of whether the Justice Department would decide in its discretion whether it should bring a case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You are a former U.S. attorney. Would you be ready to take this case to Merrick Garland and ask him to go?\par \par \uc2 LAM: You know, I was a fraud prosecutor and we tend to be a pretty cautious bunch, and I would want to see all of those documents or as many as I could before I made that decision, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I imagine.\par \par \uc2 Carol Lam, really appreciate you coming on and explaining this to the audience. It was very helpful. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 LAM: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We'll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220304ei3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 2, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Mike Memoli, Erin McLaughlin, David Ignatius, Cornell  Belcher, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 8,700 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday. Is Joe Biden prepared for a wartime presidency? That a fresh wave of Russian attacks the U.S. is now warning that Putin is preparing to ratchet up the brutality of his campaign against the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the president hits the road after a State of the Union address that sent a pretty clear warning to members of his own party not to tack too far left on issues like crime, immigration, and yes COVID restrictions.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of COVID, the White House rolls out a new plan for the endemic but doesn`t say the word. We`ll dive into the administration`s COVID reset with the person in charge of rolling it out, the U.S. Secretary of Health and Human Services of Xavier Becerra, coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd on this date after the State of the Union. And right now Russia is intensifying its attacks on Ukraine. The death toll from the war continues to climb including civilians. The Biden administration is warning that Russia could be on the brink of committing war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Kharkiv, Ukraine`s second-largest city faced renewed shelling overnight. The senior defense official tells NBC News that Russian missiles and artillery are targeting Kyiv with increasing aggressiveness. One official tells NBC News, around 21 people have been killed and more than a hundred others injured today alone.\par \par \uc2 This comes after yesterday, the Russian advance on Kyiv appeared to stall, prompting warnings that Putin would double down on violent tactics perhaps from the air. During an emergency session at the United Nations this morning, the U.S. indicated it has the intelligence to back this up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINDA THOMAS-GREENFIELD, U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: And now it appears Russia is preparing to increase the brutality of its campaign against Ukraine. We`ve seen videos of Russian forces moving exceptionally lethal weaponry into Ukraine, which has no place on the battlefield. That includes cluster munitions and vacuum bombs, which are banned under the Geneva Convention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The bottom line, actual war crimes we may have the evidence to prove. Shortly before those remarks, reporters asked President Biden about the situation in Ukraine and whether he intended on taking additional actions against Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How worried are you about President Zelenskyy, and do you think he could stay in Ukraine, or do you think he can try to leave?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it`s his judgment to make and we`re doing everything we can to help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you considering banning Russian oil imports?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nothing is off the table.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think Russia is committing war crimes against Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are following very closely. It`s early to say that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Justice Department today also formally announced a new task force to enforce sanctions already on the books against Russia and Russian oligarchs. But while the world grapples with a worsening war and humanitarian crisis in Europe, the Biden administration isn`t losing focus on issues at home. The president is on his way to the Midwest, to the border of Wisconsin and Minnesota right now a part of his effort to sell the Building A Better America Agenda, which he laid out a bit in last night`s State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 And while the address began with new measures against Russia, including a ban on all Russian aircraft from entering U.S. airspace, the war comprised just a small part of the speech. Folks, the war in Ukraine is poised to redefine this presidency. But from what we`ve seen and heard of the last 24 hours, the administration is not yet ready to believe -- to accept the premise that the entire presidency is going to be transformed by this. That doesn`t mean they don`t believe that it could. But for now, which is why I think you had that bifurcated State of the Union last night.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Mike Memoli, who is in Superior, Wisconsin where the President is set to deliver remarks later this afternoon. I also got Erin McLaughlin with us in Lviv, Ukraine in a moment, and Washington Post foreign affairs columnist and MSNBC contributor, David Ignatius.\par \par \uc2 Mike, let me start with you. I`ll just be honest with you, in the middle of the war in Europe, it`s odd to see the president out there today after selling the State of the Union agenda in a battleground state like Wisconsin. Yes, local politics, national politics, it makes a lot of sense. If you just have domestic glasses on, less so when you`re looking globally.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, obviously the President typically would hit the road after a State of the Union address, and that`s something the White House thought was still important to do. I think the extent of his post State of the Union road trip is curtailed because of what`s happening in Ukraine. We only know of this trip other Cabinet officials traveling in the next few days, but no more travel yet for the president. But I think, Chuck, it is important to sort of look at the State of the Union as a snapshot of how the president views both his domestic agenda and where Ukraine fits into his presidency long term.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 This is a White House that is very proud of the work that it has done and feels it deserves more credit for the work the president has done to strengthen our alliances, to enable the president to take the kind of unified actions along with our allies in a much more comprehensive and fulsome way than a lot of people thought to punish Russia for its invasion.\par \par \uc2 This is a White House that also recognizes that President Zelenskyy, the sort of heroic status that he`s attained, and helping to galvanize both Ukrainian but also global public opinion behind him was a moment that invited him last night to bring the parties together, singling out the Ukrainian Ambassador talking about the heroism of the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 But this is a White House that really prides itself on taking the long view understanding that he will have more opportunities to command the country`s attention, speaking about Ukraine, but he will not have the kind of opportunity that he had last night to send Democrats into the midterm elections with a really Super Bowl viewership level opportunity to explain what Joe Biden`s Democratic Party is, not the right-wing or republican caricature of the Democratic parties.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s why the heart of his speech was Joe Biden, economics, manufacturing, build it in America, made in America, elements of Build Back Better, if not the Build Back Better agenda as a package. But also as a candidate, I covered who was to the right of where all the rest of the Democrats were on so many issues, making it clear for those frontline Democrats who have to run in races this year, that he is yes for comprehensive immigration reform, but also for border security. That, yes, he wants, racial justice is at the forefront of what this president is doing but also safety and support for law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Those are opportunities with the kind of viewership last night that doesn`t come along often for politics. And that was really what this speech was viewed at, as by the White House all along, an opportunity to help set the table for Democrats moving forward. And that`s why it was, as Peter Alexander has put it, a tale of two speeches, Ukraine at the top, but then the rest of what they plan to do all along.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Mike, do you get a sense that there`s a little bit of a -- I don`t want to say a dispute inside the West Wing, but maybe a debate about whether what`s going on in Europe is going to change this presidency and transform this presidency in a way that 9/11 change the Bush presidency?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, I think they see that there`s an opportunity for this to be an opportunity for the President to showcase that his view, his worldview, his approach to international politics is right. But I think one reason you didn`t want to see the kind of a wholesale rewrite of his State of the Union speech about the situation in Ukraine is because, frankly, they don`t know where this movie ends, right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Vladimir Putin might have more to say about how this situation resolves than anything the West can do. And there`s a risk involved in sort of making this a Ukraine speech when the movie might not have a good ending. And then that ends up being a sort of a bigger anchor on this presidency than anything else.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike Memoli, getting started in Superior, Wisconsin, Mike, thanks very much. Let me go out to Erin McLaughlin who`s in Lviv, Ukraine, where obviously that`s in the western part where things are -- where the Russians are not as aggressive. But Erin, tell us the situation as best you know, right now in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chuck, Ukrainians essentially say they want to see two things from the west at this point as outlined by Andriy Yermak in an op-ed in the New York Times. He is the president or chief of the president`s office. He says he wants to see NATO enforce a no- fly zone over Ukraine. Ukraine has a massive military disadvantage in the skies. He wants to see that rectified. He said that he realizes what that means that NATO for obvious reasons, given that Russia is a nuclear power, is reluctant to get involved in this conflict.\par \par \uc2 But he says that the alternative is if Ukraine falls and Russia succeeds, the next stop for Russia will be a NATO ally, testing Article 5 of saying a country such as Poland or another ally within the Alliance. His other big ask of the West is more sanctions. They want to see more sanctions against more Russian banks. They would also like to see an oil embargo -- a full oil embargo against Russia. He also said that you know, he realizes the economic costs of such extreme measures, but these are extreme times.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you that I was at the train station here in Lviv, the western city where many refugees are fleeing, and it was shocking and heartbreaking to see so many families fleeing this violence. I`ve been speaking to Ukrainians all over the country in Mariupol, in Kyiv, as well as Donetsk, and it`s really just -- it`s a heartbreaking situation right now, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure it is.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Erin McLaughlin in Lviv, Ukraine, again as she noted in the West so in some ways they`re separated from the war for now. Let me bring in David Ignatius of The Washington Post. And David is probably as well source as any national security reporter in this town these days. So let me start with what the next week is going to look like and let`s go with some of the asks that Zelenskyy made. A no-fly zone seems like an impossible asks because how do you enforce a no-fly zone that does not engage NATO and Russia directly, which of course, could trigger a hotter and broader war? Is there a scenario where a no-fly zone is an actionable idea by the West?\par \par \uc2 DAVID IGNATIUS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Chuck, I don`t think there is for now. President Biden and others in his team have made clear from the beginning that they are going to avoid care -- as carefully as they can any direct military confrontation with Russia. They keep saying that. I`m told that Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin began every planning, meeting with his top staff outlining U.S. national interests and closing those meetings saying we do not want a war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 So the no-fly zone, I think, for the moment is out. You can see the dilemma that`s ahead for President Biden last night, the intensity of support for Ukraine, the passion that was in that audience as he gave the State of the Union shows how the United States like the world is standing behind this plucky country. The plucky Ukraine that we`re growing the love in is going to be crushed in the coming days. You can see it coming. With the attack on the television station, last night in Kyiv says they want -- they don`t want the world to see.\par \par \uc2 They don`t -- they don`t want communications to show what they`re going to do in Ukraine. And as that goes forward, I think the pressure on Biden but every leader to take stronger steps to help this country that`s going to be beginning absolutely shattered by Russian pressure, it`s going to grow and grow and it`ll be very hard for the president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, David, when you sit there, and we`re going to be watching this slaughter. And in some ways, Vladimir Putin is going to make it even easier to unite the world against him. He is going to make himself more of a pariah. I mean, he`s got to double down here because he can`t accept that he didn`t do this right but it`s going to make the situation worse. It`s hard to find an exit ramp here or an end game that`s realistic anywhere here, is it?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: So the administration officials that I talked to continue to underline that they need to provide some exit ram, if not for Putin, for Russia. This is going to go on for a long time. I mean, the idea of this going to be a short war with a decisive, happy ending, we just have to put it out of our minds. The leverage of the United States since allies have is that they are slowly going to strangle the Russian economy. And what Russia does in that situation, it`s hard to predict. But if they want to shatter resistance, at least in the short term in Ukraine, they have the forces to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And oil embargo, that is the next potential vise grip we could put Russia`s economy on. Is that something we would do? Is that on the table?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: So I think you could tell from the president`s response that he`s more willing to think about it now. I think the refusal of the Saudis and our other Gulf allies to support the United States and the -- all the West by making more oil available. It should really bother people. They are siding with the Russians for now. I think the pressure on them is going to -- is going to increase. But I do think that if you know we see continued Russian pressure on every tool the president has including refusing to buy Russian oil, the overwhelming pressure on him to take those actions, sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it`s a small thing here, David, but I`m noticing I just got the voting list here on the -- on the UN vote. And among those that -- the folks that were not on the list of abstainers, no, UAE, did not abstain this time and neither did Saudi Arabia. Perhaps that is a sign that they`re inching back towards the West.\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: Well, that`s encouraging. That would be -- that would -- this would be an important move. I know the unhappiness with the Saudis and Emiratis in Washington has been -- has been growing. So if they`re hearing that message and reacting, that`s positive. It`s -- the degree of global unity on this is something I don`t think I can remember.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean you know from every country in Europe all the way to Asia --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Iraq, Kuwait, maybe?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: -- Japan, North Korea --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: -- Well, it`s just -- you know it`s a global movement and there - - as you said earlier, Chuck, there really is no way back for Vladimir Putin. And he will live in this pariah status now whatever the outcome on the ground is. There`s no recovery for him. And I wonder what the people around him in Russia will say as this -- as these sanctions take down Russian business, take down Russian personal fortunes, you know, the pressure will grow.\par \par \uc2 TODD: David Ignatius of the Washington Post, as always, sir, thanks very much. Appreciate your reporting. Up next, it has been a week of war but after last night`s address, Biden is signaling he`s not yet ready to take up the mantle of a wartime president. There`s some domestic politics to take care of. The panel is next. Plus, Steve Kornacki will join us from the big board with the results from Texas, the first primary of the 2022 midterm cycle, which will go into overtime. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Putin may circle Kyiv with tanks, but will never gain the hearts and souls of the Iranian people. He`ll never -- he`ll never extinguish their love of freedom and he will never, never weaken the resolve of the free world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden at last night`s State of the Union address. And while his remarks began on Ukraine, the president then focused entirely on his domestic agenda and some other issues. An indication the White House isn`t quite ready to embrace the idea that the Biden presidency has been totally transformed by war. Let`s dig into the President`s State of the Union politically if you will. Joining me now, Democratic Pollster Cornell Belcher, he`s also an MSNBC Political Analyst, and Republican Strategist, Brad Todd.\par \par \uc2 So, Cornell, I would say the way folks critiqued the speech last night, you had sort of, I`ll accept the media Washington elite who was thinking about Europe going hey, there wasn`t enough on that.\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Democratic members of Congress going -- for those that are in swing districts just going thank God he said to fund the police and secure the border. And I talked to a lot of Democratic strategists were like, hey there was a lot of sort of weed pulling, if you will, tending to the garden that he had to do it.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s what they felt. So they were relieved by the speech. Where do you fall?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I fall here. And you -- and the three of us know very well, a month from now, Chuck, no one will remember those details of those policies that were in his speech. I think a month from now, and I think this is important for the president is America`s at a place right now, where Americans are very anxious. They`ve gone through a couple of years of the pandemic, they`re being hit by inflation, and all of our polling data, voters are anxious, they`re confused, they`re scared.\par \par \uc2 Yet, last night, the most important thing for him to be was the soother and chief. He needed to make a Reagan-like pivot to the morning and tell Americans that we`re going to get through this, and it`s going to be better on the outside because we`re working together and bringing -- and bringing each other together, and we`re going to get through this, and we`re going to be better and stronger. That`s what middle America will remember. They won`t remember all the policy and the wheat pulling that you and I sort of will talk about endlessly. But as that sits that our leader got bigger in a moment where he needed to get bigger.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brad, what were you expecting him to do and what do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: I thought the Ukraine part of the speech, which was widely regarded on both sides of the aisle. I thought that covered up what would have been the takeaway otherwise, which was this was a president that didn`t pivot, you know. After you`ve had a really bad year, you have to tell the people I get it, I messed up, and we have to change direction.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Did he say I get it?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: He didn`t pivot though, he didn`t say I made a mistake, he didn`t say this administration hasn`t focused on your problems, inflation is out of control, we botched Afghanistan. You know, Bill Clinton in 1996 in his State of the Union said the Arab big government`s over. He thinks -- he said, I heard you, to the voters.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: Joe Biden didn`t do that. And I think the voters -- if you don`t do that the voters will make sure they scream louder at the polls in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Cornell, some progressives would disagree with Brad and say, oh, no, he didn`t pivot and he pivoted at the expense of progressive issues. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, I mean, look, what is he pivoting from? Like, if you look at build back -- if you look at the elements of Build Back Better, and if you look at the Infrastructure plan, all the things that he`s done -- and by the way, the infrastructure plan is a big deal, all things that he`s done is -- are wildly popular. He`s being stopped in by the process of Washington -- the process of a broken Senate. So this idea that he needs to sort of dramatically pivot from his policy --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: You don`t accept the premise that he should pivot.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Of course, I don`t accept that. But so I`m afraid that he`s got -- that he`s got to pivot. I do think -- I do think he`s got to tell his story and tell the story better, which is why he and the vice president are going to be traveling all over the country so.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: The importance -- look, you know how -- what messaging seems to be effective in the right? There was a part of his State of the Union last night that felt like he was answering some of almost the most common critiques he gets. He said I don`t want to say -- he said the words I want to secure the border. He said the words I want kids in schools. He said the words fund the police. It seemed to me he was answering somewhat he thought were top-line critiques.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: I think he was trying to power the pig. You know, he didn`t --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: What are you, Southerners?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: -- He didn`t admit the problem here. You know, he didn`t say we`ve messed this up. What we`ve done at the border hasn`t worked. Sentinels coming over the border right before the bigger than it was before I became president. He needed accountability. And I think that`s the only way you`re going to get it. The other thing I thought was missing was he didn`t see him lash out of Republicans much. And we know that --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: He didn`t mention January 6.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: Joe Biden is a brawler, right? He always has been a brawler.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: It`s interesting you say that.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: And on the Judiciary Committee that it produces brawlers. And he didn`t feel like he had the leverage to do it last night and I think that`s a testament to his unpopularity.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: That`s it should -- look, I agree that he didn`t try to poke in fact, didn`t mention January 6 at all, which is there`s a part of you that wonders why he could have made a larger democracy argument and had looped that in but he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think -- well, I think Mitch McConnell is lashing out at Republicans and we appreciate that. But --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: I`ll get to Brad in a minute.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: -- But -- I know I think -- I do think that`s right. I think some of us were surprised that there wasn`t a more direct connection between fighting and securing democracy, both at home and abroad. I think that was an easy -- an easy connection.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Look, last night for us was something else that appeared in politics because it also coincided with a primary night. Let`s bring in Kornacki at the board. And, Steve, we got a little bit of overtime in Texas. The larger picture in Texas to me was its sort of the appetizer for what primary season is going to deliver both parties, which is we`re going to have progressives versus the centrist or the establishment primaries like Cisneros, Cuellar. And then you`re going to have MAGA versus establishment or MAGA versus light MAGA like we have in the AG race and a few other places. So Texas was a little bit of the appetizer, I guess.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. You got a couple of those setups. Here`s the progressive versus Henry Cuellar, you could call him conservative Democrat, least Liberal Democrat in the House, something like that, moderate Democrat pick your label. Cisneros, backed by AOC, backed by national progressive groups, looks like neither is going to hit 50 percent here so the race is going to head to the runoff.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And the backdrop though for this runoff to is this is brand new for Democrats. This district here in south Texas, the 28th, is not one they`ve ever had to worry about recently winning a general election but the political terrain in South Texas also moved dramatically toward the Republicans in the 2020 election. Just give you a taste of how dramatic it was in parts of this district. Just look at Starr County, Henry Cuellar last night did great in Starr County.\par \par \uc2 Let me just show you the shift that took place in Starr County between 2016, Hillary Clinton won Starr County on the border. This is 90 percent plus Hispanic. She won it by 60 points in 2020. It was Joe Biden by five. We saw a dramatic 20, 30, 40, 50-point movement in the Rio Grande Valley on the border in those counties, heavily Hispanic counties. Republicans think Cisneros is too liberal.\par \par \uc2 They think they could beat him in a general election. Cuellar, his office was raided by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  a few months ago. There`s an investigation going on. Republicans think they could have a chance in this district no matter who emerges from that primary. So that`s the one on the Democratic side to look at.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, where to look is here in North Texas in the third district. The Republican primary, here you go, Van Taylor, he`s going to finish under 50 percent. It`s going to a runoff. Keith Self is the name to keep an eye on, that`s going to be his opponent here. Van Taylor, why would he be under 50 percent in a Republican primary? He voted for the independent January 6 Commission, ran afoul of the Trump folks on that. That was mentioned over and over by his opponents here.\par \par \uc2 He is close to 50. He is 20 points above his nearest competitor here. But the question right now really is does Trump himself didn`t -- Trump stayed away from this. He didn`t endorse Taylor, but he didn`t do anything else. Does he get interested? Does the national pro-Trump media get interested? Does this become an event for them? This runoff will be May 24 could end up being interesting.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: I do think the pro-Trump media has already weighed in on that race, and I`ll just leave it at that. I think -- I think I see that smirk on your face, Mr. Kornacki. Steve, thanks very much. We got a two-month reprieve right? Next primary, not until May?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: May 23. Hey, you`re going to have Georgia in this runoff, this Van Taylor on the same night, so a lot of that Trump factor in one night.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: That`ll be good stuff. Thank you, Kornacki. Let me bring back our strategist here. Cornell, there`s a part of me that says if Cisneros didn`t get Cuellar now, a little bit harder as you go. I mean everything was going her way. I --This was one that surprised me that he forced the runoff.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, look, I say this time and time again. I think the organized left -- the organized left is not what the organized right is. It didn`t start, all right? I think it`s harder for the -- for the left to take out incumbents in primaries than we`ve seen happen on -- happen on the right. I think if she -- if you can`t take him out now --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: With a scandal.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: -- With a scandal, good luck.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Yes. Now the runoff may help. There`s a bigger takeaway here though, Brad, and that is Texas don`t -- doesn`t -- you know, for about the last six years ago, Texas might get competitive. Texas might -- Texas does not look competitive.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: You know, Starr County it feels -- I`m glad Steve brought Starr County up. You know, four years ago in Starr County, there were 12 humans that voted in the Republican primary. Last night, there are 1700, all right? Republican primary turnout was up 50 percent or more in every Valley County. Statewide, it was up 23 percent from 2018. Democrat turnout was flat. And if you look at metro areas, San Antonio`s where all the growth was, guess what that is, Hispanic.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Seems like there is irregularity there to me if you only hit 17 and how sad to have it.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: I know you do a lot of work with there, Brad. I got to ask you about Rick Scott versus Mitch McConnell. Mitch McConnell seemed to dress him down using democratic talking points about his plan. How divisive has this been inside the Senate?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: You know, I think it looks like Republican conferences anticipating governing. And I think that they -- if they thought they were about to lose the majority -- and not have a majority next time, everyone will be yawning. But Republicans are trying to have an honest debate about where do we go when we take over? What is the agenda when we take over? And you know, Senator McConnell today came out and listed a few areas where he thinks the GOP would have an agenda. That`s something he didn`t do before Rick Scott`s plane came out. I think Senator Scott`s done a lot to further things.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Does he want to be the majority leader? Is that why he did this?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: He wants to have a GOP majority that`s worth having. That`s his goal. He`s not like --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: He doesn`t like that somebody who isn`t interested in meeting the GOP majority.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: His goal -- his goal is for the Republican Party to govern on conservative principles next year. Next year. Not wait -- not wait to see if we get 60 with to govern (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Explain why the Democrats were ecstatic when Rick Scott put that out. That -- I never had seen him in Mitch -- again, I go -- Mitch McConnell uses democratic talking points to his tactics.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes. Well, it`s because it was an unforced error, right? Because now gives Democrats something to put on the board say, look, this is what they`re for. And what they`re for is not popular, right? And Mitch McConnell is playing the smart hand is don`t give them anything to run against.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Brad, what do you -- respond to that.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: I think everything in Rick Scott`s plan, there`s 128 -- I encourage you to go to rescueamerica.org and look at it if you want. I think everyone who will vote for Republicans this year is going to agree with almost everything in the plan. Maybe a few things they don`t. I think it`s going to further Republicans` ability to take control.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Real quickly, they keep bringing back kill the Department of Education. I`m sorry, that`s -- that old dog won`t hunt, talk about southerners again. That old dog just won`t hunt. We`ve seen that -- them try trying to hunt with that before. Women and voters, particularly suburban women voters, don`t want you to kill the Department of Education. That`s something that we`re going to attack.\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD: I think the argument of whether we should send more dollars in education to local schools instead of Washington is one Republicans will welcome.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD: Brad Todd, Cornell Belcher, gentlemen, thank you. Good stuff. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, big day on Capitol Hill for the president`s nominee to the Supreme Court. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Here`s our "Data Download" for the day. Today`s number is 4. It`s the number of meetings on the schedule for Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson on Capitol Hill today as she begins the confirmation process for a seat on the Supreme Court. In just a few minutes ago, \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  chairman Dick Durbin announced that the hearings for her will begin on March 21st. That is less than three weeks away right in the middle of March Madness, in case you`re keeping track.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jackson`s first four meetings today include the big four, if you will. Majority Leader Chuck Schumer, Minority Leader Mitch McConnell, Dick Durbin, the Judiciary Committee chairman, and the Ranking Member of Judiciary, Chuck Grassley.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 More meetings will obviously follow, especially given that we`ve now got a date for those confirmation hearings. But this is a normalcy, by the way. Speaking of normalcy, this is a normal way Supreme Court nominations go. Judge Jackson already wrapped up meetings with Schumer and McConnell earlier today. The Democratic leader called her brilliant and beloved, and said she belongs on the court.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden says Americans should no longer let COVID control their lives. Health and Human Services Secretary Xavier Becerra joins me to talk about the White House`s new plan to tackle future variants.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The White House officially announced their new long- term roadmap for living with COVID. This morning President Biden unveiled it in the State of the Union address last night. The president`s speech delivered in front of a largely maskless chamber, (INAUDIBLE) emphasized the return to normal. That includes reopening schools and businesses for good. And he previewed a return to a maskless America.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for more on this new COVID plan, Health and Human Services Secretary Xavier Becerra.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA, HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY: Good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what we should view this plan as being, and is this a way of essentially saying the national emergency is over?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: It`s a way of saying, America, thank you. Shout out to everyone who vaxxed, who got boosted, who wore mask, who got tested. Thank you for letting us get to this point where we know we`re in a better place. We can let people now move forward, go back to work, get to school, continue to be safe, but, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, if you look at the data today and you compare it to a year ago, not a lot has changed. We have about 60,000 daily cases today. It was 68,000 a year ago. Little under 2,000 deaths today a day. It was a little over 2,000 a year ago. We had 50,000 in the hospital a year ago, 50,000 today, 56,000 a year ago.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 You see where I`m going here. Obviously the biggest difference is fully vaccinated. 215 million versus 30 million. And I guess, is -- outside of the vaccinations, if we still had those stats would we still be in an emergency?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well, I would hope that any time that you`re losing more than 2,000 Americans a day, when you`ve got tens of thousands of Americans who are still getting infected and several thousand who are in the hospital because of a particular disease, that we would say, this is serious and that`s where we are. It is, even though it`s a tale of numbers that almost look the same, it`s a far different place and it is mostly because of the vaccination.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, can we really move beyond masks until everybody can get vaccinated? Is it fair to say that the mask mandate on planes, the mask mandate in sort of the -- on federal public transportation, trains, we`re going to keep it until everybody from infant forward can get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Chuck, it`s more -- this is not a light switch. You just can`t go on and off. It`s a matter of doing this, knowing the science, being thoughtful about how we do this. You know, there will be people who will continue to wear masks. Some have to. Those who are immunocompromised. Under 5 years of age, children, still aren`t vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: And so we still have reason to be cautious. But, look, we`re in a different place today and we can move forward, and we know what it takes and we`re putting the tools together. That medicine cabinet is a lot more full.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Who will make that call? The mask mandate expires on federal public transportation I think in about three weeks. Will that likely get renewed and extended for a period of time for sure, or is that up in the air?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: CDC will continue to take a look at what all the information is. Again, we`ll take all the elements, put them together, be thoughtful, we`ll consult with the experts. At the end of the day the president made it very clear. We`re going to follow the science. We`re going to urge everyone to be not just thoughtful but responsible and then we`ll move forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do the scientists tell you that we should expect this to be seasonal, that we will, you know, we will have peaks? If you look at the last two years it felt like the south was where we`d get hit hard in the summer, the north is where we`d get hit hard in the winter. Is that a pattern scientists are telling you to prepare for this year?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: There are some real clear patterns, Chuck. You`re not vaccinated, you`re more likely -- way more likely to end up in a hospital, way more likely to die. Winter, just like with the flu, you`re going to see more cases because it`s a virus and it proliferates more during cold seasons when we tend to huddle together in spots that aren`t as ventilated. So the patterns are there. That`s why we can know how to treat this, how to move forward because the patterns are there and now as I said we`ve got a toolbox and a medicine cabinet that are far more equipped.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it fair to say we`re now in an endemic? Because we`ve got to -- we`re living with COVID is our version of defeating it?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: What`s fair to say is that Americans, we owe you a great deal of gratitude for vaccinating, for following the guidance, and we`ll continue to do that because we want to get back to a place of normality. Thank you for what you`re doing to help us get back to that spot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I get what you`re saying, you do not want to be caught saying we`re going to have to learn to live with COVID. You`re not there yet?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: I`ve got staff who got kids who are under 5 years of age.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: What do I say to them? You know, let`s all be cautious. Let`s be responsible. Chuck, it`s not rocket science anymore. The rocket scientists or the disease scientists are doing all what they have to do. We know what we have to do. Let`s do it, let`s be responsible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The biggest reason we may have gotten that last variant is how under vaccinated the world is. We have talked about vaccinating more of the world. But it feels like that progress has been kind of slow. What`s your explanation of why this has been difficult to get -- to speed up vaccinating the world?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: The president said it last night and the president walked his talk on vaccinating not just Americans but helping get the world vaccinated. We`ve done more than any other country, four times more in fact than any other country, and we will continue to do it because we understand that for Americans to be safe everyone in the world has to be safe because those variants travel and they travel very fast.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to do what we have to do and we`re going to urge our brethren around the world to join us this week on holding a ministerial with my counterparts, our health ministers, to talk about this more in preparation for the president`s global summit to follow up last year`s summit, so we can all be prepared to handle not just COVID, but any pandemic and know how to respond.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And finally, just to wrap this up, you brought up the immunocompromised. I brought up the folks under 5.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 I -- is it folks should prepare that until we get -- until everybody can get vaccinated we`re likely to have some national areas where we all have to wear masks, airplanes, trains, or is that still not a decision that`s going to be made yet?\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Again, that`s the part of being thoughtful about how we do this. But, Chuck, I`ll pose it back to you and your audience. If you know -- if someone in your family who has got a 4-year-old.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Is it up to you to help that 4-year-old and your relative, the parents of that child, to be safe? I think all of us have a responsibility. It`s not fair for those of us who think we`re through to say it`s all good. Spike the ball in the end zone. No, we`ve got families. We`ve got an American family to take care of.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Xavier Becerra, Health and Human Services secretary, appreciate you coming on and sharing your views on this new plan. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So if you`re preparing, it sounds like that mask mandate is on airplanes is probably going to get extended.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as Russia`s attacks intensify some of Ukraine`s neighbors fear they could be next. The Lithuanian ambassador to the United States joins me after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As I`ve made crystal clear, the United States and our allies will defend every inch of territory that is NATO territory with the full force of our collective power. Every single inch.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: That was President Biden doubling down on his commitment to defend NATO countries during last night`s State of the Union. It comes as the war in Ukraine intensifies, and new U.S. sanctions go into effect. But many of Ukraine`s neighbors have been calling for the toughest possible sanctions for weeks now. In the Baltics, all NATO allies where Russian aggression is part of their recent history, have been some of the loudest advocates of that.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Lithuania`s ambassador to the United States, Audra Plepyte.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 AUDRA PLEPYTE, LITHUANIAN AMBASSADOR TO THE U.S.: Good afternoon. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me just start with, I know you have been a very strong ally of Ukraine. You joined a number of other Central European countries looking to fast track Ukraine`s membership into the E.U. Is that plausible? Is that something that could happen, you know, in weeks or even sooner?\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: Maybe, I think at this point it`s not worthwhile to talk about the real timeline of something. I think it`s the most important thing that such process has started. And I think Ukraine as well by standing up to Russia`s aggression and fighting for all our values and principles show that it really is a European country and deserves to be that. Usually that`s a long process and there`s lots of homework to do, but I think the most important thing is the message, that they will be in E.U. and they will become one day a member of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: I am curious, what you heard from the president last night about the crisis in Ukraine and in Europe, what you heard from him, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: I think it was a very strong message from President Biden on the current situation, on the aggression of Russia against Ukraine. So he really showed the leadership and very strong emotional points on that, how we have to support Ukraine in the struggle against Russia, how we have to put sanctions on Russia and how to assist in the support the NATO eastern planks` strength. I think that was an example of a leadership of the U.S. on that, and this leadership which we show during the last month as well that helped to unite all the allies, and not only allies, Trans-Atlantic allies but also countries from Asia and regions like Australia, Japan, South Korea, so that`s very, very important. For the first time so many countries being united in that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I would argue that the Baltic nations have been raising the alarm bells. Baltic nations plus Poland arguably have been raising the alarm bells about Russian aggression, the threat of Russian aggression for a while now, to the rest of Europe. Obviously now Europe sees this threat. Unfortunately it took what happened to see it.\par \par \uc2 Do you lament that it took Europe so long to see the threat of Russia and Putin?\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: I think it`s better to speak about the future, not about the past. The most important thing last week was I would say really historic week for the whole world. We saw so many important decisions by a number of countries, like Germany raising the defense budget and deciding to help Ukraine, or neutral countries Sweden and Finland also assisting Ukraine with weapons. Switzerland which decided to join others in sanctioning the Russian banks. So I think that`s the most important thing. We started on the right track now and we have to continue that as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Zelensky, many Ukrainians are begging for a no-fly zone over Ukraine. The practicality of that would mean NATO would have to enforce that. It`s something the United States is not ready to endorse. Is this something Lithuania wishes NATO would do?\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: I think it`s one of the many questions and many areas how we can assist and help Ukraine, as well, so we at the very beginning of this conflict, of this war, we want everybody united, all the countries, so I think the most important is now to keep that unity and to decide unitedly, jointly, how we can support Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 There`s lots of needs Ukraine needs, a lot yet, so I think that should be our future. So we cannot -- we don`t need to focus only on one issue and to be stuck and (INAUDIBLE) rest so many issues. Again, humanitarian corridor and supporting refugee civilians, as well as assisting Ukraine with other needs. So we do keep close direct contacts with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:08]\par \par \uc2 We constantly talking with all our allies and I think we`re really unprecedently united and on the right track now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The history of Russia and the Baltic states is not great. How concerned are Lithuanians that after Ukraine the Baltics are next for Putin?\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: I wouldn`t speak about Baltics or something when we listen to Putin`s declaration of war or his arguments, why he`s invading Ukraine or decided something. It was against Ukraine, so he doesn`t see Ukraine as a separate country or Ukraine, as a nation, but what he also was clear on that, that he was declaring war against the whole our values and principles, rules-based order which we`ve been having already for decades now, or questioning and really trying to change European security architecture. So I think that`s his main target and we all have to be prepared for that and have to fight that jointly as well by all means.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Lithuanian ambassador to the United States, Audra Plepyte, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PLEPYTE: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I thank you all for being with us this hour. Be sure to check out the latest episode of "Chuck ToddCast" out today for more State of the Union analysis featuring my new colleague here, NBC News, Yamiche Alcindor, and "USA Today`s" Susan Page. So get it wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 Tonight my colleague Lester Holt interviews the Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin at 6:30 Eastern on "NIGHTLY NEWS" wherever you get your NBC stations.\par \par \uc2 Back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220303ei3200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Carol Lee, Richard Engel, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 8,912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday. President Biden prepares to deliver his first State of the Union address. This perhaps is a wartime president amid an intensifying crisis abroad and a stalled domestic agenda and falling poll numbers here at home. Can a single speech meet this complicated moment?\par \par \uc2 Plus, we are live on the ground in Ukraine where the death toll continues to rise, the fighting continues to escalate, and a massive 40-mile long Russian military convoy heads towards Kyiv, as Putin decides how brutal he`s going to get.\par \par \uc2 And if it`s Tuesday, and it`s a midterm year, we`re going to Meet the Midterms. It`s the opening day of the 2022 primary season. We`ll tell you what we`re watching tonight as Texas voters become the first to cast 2022 ballots.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd here in Washington where tonight President Biden will deliver his first State of the Union address. At home, the president faces an array of challenges including a stalled domestic agenda and falling poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 Abroad, the Russian siege on Kyiv continues where five more deaths were reported late this morning and an attack on the city`s communications tower. The Ukrainian government released this video showing smoke near that tower. And these satellite images distributed yesterday appear to show a 40-mile long convoy of Russian military vehicles heading towards Ukraine`s capital, that`s nearly twice as long as initially thought.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. defense official though tells NBC News the convoy has stalled right now, with troops facing gas and food shortages. U.S. intelligence agencies have determined that Putin is growing increasingly frustrated by his military struggles in Ukraine and he may see his only option as doubling down on violence and brutality. That`s according to what current and former U.S. officials briefed on the matter have told NBC News.\par \par \uc2 As Russian troops try to approach Kyiv, Russia`s shelling of Ukraine`s second-largest city, Kharkiv continued. Ukrainian officials say this explosion at a government building killed at least 10 people. Russia has now attacked nearly every region in Ukraine. Senior Pentagon official tells NBC News it has fired more than 400 missiles in the last week.\par \par \uc2 And as those attacks continue, more than 660,000 Ukrainians have now fled that country, according to the \uc2 UN refugee agency\uc2 . And in an address to the \uc2 UN Human Rights Council\uc2  delivered today, our Secretary of State Tony Blinken question whether Russia should be removed from that council altogether.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Reports of Russia`s human rights abuses and violations of international humanitarian law are mounting by the hour. One can reasonably ask whether a UN member state that tries to take over another UN member state while committing horrific human rights abuses and causing massive humanitarian suffering should be allowed to remain on this council.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re committing war crimes folks. Russia appears to be committing war crimes. During that meeting, about 100 diplomats walked out in protest during a speech by Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov. And the Ukrainian President Zelenskyy received a standing ovation before his address to the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our people are very much motivated, very much. So we are fighting for our rights, for our freedoms, for life -- for our life, and now we`re boring -- we`re fighting for survival. And this is the highest of our motivation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Moments ago, the White House confirmed that President Biden and Zelenskyy have spoken again today about the crisis. Carol Lee is outside the White House for us. Richard Engel is in Kyiv. Carol, let me start with you. That phone call, what do we know? Is it initiated by President Biden or by President Zelenskyy? And who was making the most asks?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, we know, Chuck that they spoke for 30 minutes. The White House has yet to issue a readout of that call just saying that they spoke for 30 minutes. President Zelenskyy though tweeted and said that they discussed both the need for aid to Ukraine and sent more sanctions on Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, you can bet that President Zelenskyy pressed President Biden for more supplies, more aid, more arms, frankly, so they can continue to fight Russia as Russia advances across the country and also put more pressure on Russia. So the -- President Zelenskyy tweeted that he -- they spoke about anti-Russia sanctions in his words. So both of those topics, obviously it came up again, we don`t have a readout yet from President Biden but this is something that certainly is going to be a topic in his State of the Union address later tonight. And whatever the contents of that call were like -- it`s likely something that he`s going to convey as part of his discussion on Ukraine in that speech.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look it wouldn`t surprise me if that was one of the moments President Biden wanted to have to bring everybody together, you know, quoting Zelenskyy, you know, who knows, dare I say even having a recording of Zelenskyy saying something to that chamber, something like that. Who knows, right? We -- it wouldn`t shock us there. Do we have an idea of what new announcements are? We assume there`ll be some new announcements for the president on Ukraine, do we have an idea what those are yet?\par \par \uc2 LEE: We don`t. But look, we know where the red areas are that announcements could potentially come from. We know what President Zelenskyy is asking for. We know what the U.S. has said it`s willing to do. There could be more sanctions announcements, for instance, that`s something that President Zelenskyy would like, obviously, and announcements of more support. The U.S. announced some 30 -- $350 million in support the other day.\par \par \uc2 But again, that`s not necessarily the limit of that. And so we could see that also, Congress Chuck, as you know, they`re going to potentially move on putting more support into the hands of Ukrainians. But how to get that there is really the question now and sort of the challenge that we`re hearing from administration officials is that you can put all the money you want towards sending arms and supplies, but getting them into Ukraine and into the hands of people who need them as a tapered story.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we have to remind people. Think of what we were talking about just six days ago, and even the first 12 hours after the invasion, the fact that not a single Ukrainian city is under Russian control yet, is not something anybody I think would have thought where we are right now. So I think everything is a bit in flux.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move over to Ukraine, in Kyiv where Richard Engel is now. And Richard, it looks as if whatever strategy Putin and the Russian army had, they realize it failed. And now something more brutal may follow. What are you saying?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So this city is bracing for a frontal assault, it is literally staring back down the barrel of a Russian gun, actually 40 miles of Russian guns, that convoy which is heading toward this city. But then a short while ago, the U.S. military official briefing at -- the Pentagon briefing, Courtney Kube and others, that the convoy has slowed, that it is facing serious logistics issues, fuel, food.\par \par \uc2 That is also consistent with what we`re hearing across this country. There are numerous videos that have emerged. There are numerous anecdotal reports of Russian tanks, not just running out of fuel. And we`ve -- there have been numerous verified accounts of Russian vehicles just stopping because they don`t have enough diesel and sometimes Ukrainians going up to them and mocking and cheering the soldiers, but of vehicles that have fuel in them that are in good condition that have just been abandoned.\par \par \uc2 And something does seem to be very wrong with the offensive. Perhaps it`s the moral of the troops, perhaps there is a breakdown. It is unclear. But the massive convoy heading toward the city facing those logistical issues hasn`t moved much since yesterday and then these pockets of abandoned Russian vehicles, which is highly suspicious, all of this is very encouraging to Ukrainians. They don`t just think they want to defend -- they don`t plan to just defend this country. Ukrainians tell me they want to beat Russia.\par \par \uc2 They think that if Vladimir Putin loses here, it could be the end of his rule in Russia. They hope that they can reverse the tide here and take back Donbas and take back Crimea. Now, these are still early days but the spirits are certainly high in this city, even as some bombs are falling and the city of Kharkiv is taking a very heavy assault with a major blast right in the center in Freedom Square, which is described as the largest square in all of Europe, with a -- with a huge explosion at the administrative headquarters that also damaged an Opera House and at least 10 people were killed there.\par \par \uc2 So, they`re encouraged, they`re optimistic, they`re curious about these vehicles that keep turning up abandoned. And now they`re taking some relief in the fact that this huge convoy is -- may still be heading this way, but it slowed down.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t know if this is what you`re trying to apply but there`s sort of been some reports. Is there a morale problem in the Russian army right with the rank and file is that -- is that what`s leading to these abandoned vehicles, is that the speculation, Richard?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: That is the speculation. It is hard to know if that`s the case, but there have been many videos, including from CCTV cameras of Russian troops going into grocery stores, foraging for food, other accounts that the Russians have meals that have been found in their -- in their abandoned vehicles that were five and six years old expired.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: So it`s hard to know if they`re serious -- if they`re experiencing morale problems, but the evidence in the vehicles that have either broken down or been left behind suggests that there -- that it is not a well-oiled military operation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Zelenskyy, his ability to speak to various groups, he`s been on his own, I think, pushed the EU, push European allies much further than they would have gone a week ago, whether it`s on sanctions or whether it`s on aid to Ukraine. Is he going to be able to get Ukraine into the EU by his sheer force of will?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Who knows? Normally, it`s a process that takes years, but he received a standing ovation today. And we`re seeing unprecedented moves with Monaco sanctioning Russian oligarchs and Russia, and Switzerland doing the same and Sweden sending weapons, and Germany, which refused to send anything but helmets, suddenly switching on major sanctions.\par \par \uc2 So, it is possible. These are -- these are I don`t want to say unprecedented times because we have been in these times, but we haven`t been in these times for 80 years. So could it -- could the \uc2 European Union\uc2  swing open its doors? It`s possible. Everything still seems to be in flux these days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Absolutely. And again, as I mentioned, Carol, and you heard that a lot has changed in six days, and a lot more could change in another six days. Carol Lee and Richard Engel, thank you both for getting us started. Joining me now is Doug Lute. He`s a former U.S. ambassador to NATO. Ambassador Lute, let`s start with the status of the Russian military right now.\par \par \uc2 And it seems, as if they didn`t go well, obviously, he`s got to alter his plans. It could get more brutal, it could get more violent, it`s going to be a lot harder to sit back and watch, isn`t it? As a NATO member, nation, no matter who you are, it gets as brutal, as some officials seem to expect, is that what you expect?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS LUTE, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO NATO: Well, Chuck, I do expect to be more brutal. Look, we should appreciate that we`re in the very early days, just a few days into this. And we should expect this to be a long, protracted, brutal campaign. I mean, if we want an example of what the Russian regime is capable of, we should think back 20 years to the campaign, they took to their own Russian citizens in Chechnya, and look at the pictures of the city of Grozny, the capital of Chechnya from 20 years ago, they leveled it.\par \par \uc2 You can imagine that kind of city fighting, mass bombardment, disregard for civilian casualties, that`s what`s bearing down on Kharkiv and in Kyiv now. If given a choice, Putin will double down before he backs down.\par \par \uc2 TODD: His decision to amp up his nuclear posture. I read one analysis that said the U.S. should be careful not to over-respond because that`s exactly what Putin wants, that he`s trying to take the focus off of the fact that he`s failing in Ukraine, and he wants to make this a war with the United States, a war with the West. How do we balance that and still make sure we`re keeping an eye on the idea that the thing we`ve always feared is a madman with a nuke, what if we suddenly have a madman with a nuke?\par \par \uc2 LUTE: Look, I think the administration -- the Biden administration`s response to this has been spot on. And that is very prudent, not escalatory, in any way and just reassuring that the American deterrence posture, our nuclear force is safe, it`s secure, and it`s effective. That`s the right way to go here. Somebody`s got to be the adult in this room. I think what we`ll watch very carefully now going forward is to see if President Putin has any actions that match this increased readiness level of his nuclear forces.\par \par \uc2 You know, there are four levels of nuclear readiness in the Russian system. They just move from the normal status quo level one level up, we would now expect to watch for the movement of warheads. Does he put nuclear-armed submarines to see? Does he disperse nuclear-capable bombers? Does he change -- does he change protocols in the command and control network? So these are things that are knowable, they`re seeable, and we`ll watch this very carefully.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got you. Do you think -- do you think he is a rational actor right now, or do you think we have to assume that he might not be?\par \par \uc2 LUTE: Well, I think they`re just some disturbing signs. But one thing that is clear without debate is that he`s an isolated actor, he has placed himself at the very pinnacle of this autocratic regime. I don`t know that he has any trusted advisors who are willing to give him send views. And that alone, that isolation at the top is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So this is what -- you can`t help but wonder you know three or four days ago there was a concern, hey, the sanctions might not be tough enough, it might not strangle the Russian economy enough, it might not move Putin. Now there seems to be a reverse fear. Wait a minute, he`s really feeling cornered by the Ukrainians have been a much tougher resistance, and the economy is getting choked off. Oh, and by the way, this turned out to be unpopular at home. It`s that feeling of feeling cornered and humiliated. What`s the exit ramp for him now?\par \par \uc2 LUTE: Well, it`s not clear. Now, he`s burned most of the exit ramps that might have to do -- might rely on diplomacy. It`s quite clear that given the events of the last weeks leading up to and then through the invasion itself, that no one`s going to trust Vladimir Putin. He`s also led himself into a pariah state status, both politically and economically. So there are no clear exit ramps. Now, this will now become the art of diplomacy and the art of trying to give him a way out while keeping him under the pressure of the sanctions and the political isolation. That`s it. That`s a tough call.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious to walk that line. Let`s say Helsinki, said today, we want Finland and NATO. And the Helsinki government said we`re applying formally. I had Admiral Stavridis on my podcast and he said, you know, if they applied on a Wednesday, NATO should approve them on a Friday. First of all, how fast do you think the process would work with Finland, number one? And number two, how walking that line of, would Putin view that as escalatory by the West, even if we`re doing it in response to a nation in distress?\par \par \uc2 LUTE: Well, the question does start rightly with the Finnish people, right? And I understand the Finnish Parliament considering this topic now. And that`s appropriate. That`s where all NATO membership questions begin. It might start on Wednesday and end on Friday, but I don`t know that that Wednesday and Friday --\par \par \uc2 TODD: In the same week? All right.\par \par \uc2 LUTE: -- So it may take a bit of -- a bit of time to deliberate. But what`s quite clear if you go into the Washington Treaty, the NATO Treaty from 1949, Article 10 outlines what qualities a new member, an aspiring member might -- must-have. It`s quite clear that Finland meets those qualifications. So really, the first step begins with the Swedish people or sorry with the Finnish people, and then interestingly, may will spill over and have a similar effect in Sweden next step.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, one would assume if one comes, the other is likely to come as well. Ambassador Doug Lute, appreciate you coming on sharing your experience and perspective with us, sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the turmoil abroad is shaping the president`s address tonight and the difficult task of delivering this speech amid all that and his domestic political struggles. House Majority Leader Steny Hoyer will be here to discuss what Democrats want to hear from their president tonight. That`s up ahead. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, as we mentioned at the top of the show, as Biden prepares for tonight`s State of the Union, his presidency perhaps has been totally transformed by the war near and by Putin`s threat of nuclear engagement. The turmoil overseas comes at a moment of major political weakness for Biden here at home. Every single major poll released in the lead up to the State of the Union has Biden`s approval rating below 40 percent, most of them show support from his own party on approval in the 70s, that`s a sign of growing unrest inside the Democratic base.\par \par \uc2 And the public`s views of the Biden economy do not reflect the job gains or the GDP growth that have been touted by the administration. Huge chunks of the country describe the current economic climate as a recession or depression and even more describe it as not good and poor. Despite the fact, it`s never been easier to get a job, not been easier to get a high- paying job.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is Jen Palmieri, former White House Communications Director under President Obama. Ayesha Rascoe is a White House reporter for NPR. Republican strategist, Matt Gorman is also joining us. Ayesha, let me start with you in the balance what`s your understanding is? You know, obviously, he`s going to lead with Ukraine and in some form or another.\par \par \uc2 AYESHA RASCOE, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, NPR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I think the question is what -- how does he do the domestic messaging? We know he`s going to do some of it. How does he do it? How is it framed? What are you reporting?\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Well, interestingly enough, they are going to try to strike bipartisanship. You know, surprise, surprise. It hasn`t worked out much too far. I mean, they got infrastructure done, but supposedly they`re going to try to talk about issues where they think that there`s overlap between Republicans and Democrats and things that they can get done. Kind of acknowledging probably not tacitly, but probably -- but acknowledging the fact that look, they`re not going to get all the stuff that they want it to get done. It`s not happening.\par \par \uc2 So what are those issues where they can say, well, maybe we can get some Republicans on board, like mental health stuff, or you know, other things, environmental stuff that they might be able to get some people to come together on? It`s going to be a tough sell either way, though.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Jen, I heard that if you`re progressive, you might not be thrilled with the laundry list tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEN PALMIERI, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But because it`s a -- as it was portrayed to music, look, it`s going to be things that lower the cost for people`s lives, right?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They want to emphasize that it -- whether it`s prescription drugs, right, you could see where they`re going but I don`t think we`ll hear the phrase Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: It is the way -- if that`s the way that they`ll say they`re going after inflation is to try to -- is to take these steps that will lower costs and people`s lives is that. And I think, particularly with the war in Ukraine, whenever everything to have -- you know, to have a bipartisan ethos run throughout the speech is, you know, is a smart thing to do.\par \par \uc2 Because even with the domestic agenda, you can`t just look at it in the silo of domestic politics, you have to realize all of the speech. I mean, if you`re writing the speech in the White House, you have to consider all of it is going to be looked at like through that lens. This is the leader of the free world, this is the leader of the Western world, it`s not just the Democratic president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s an interesting opportunity tonight because as I discussed with somebody that he`s probably going to have a more bipartisan audience for State of the Union than any president had in a while.\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because we`re in a moment, yes.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes, right. And look, the first five or so minutes is going to be all about Ukraine. You`re going to get nice bipartisan applause for that. But the risk is there`s so much to cram into this speech, are the headlines all about Ukraine, right? You`re going to get those first five minutes to hear that story --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But we know he will be.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean that`s inevitable.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Exactly. And I think the danger for him is, are you playing small ball without that unifying theme that you can take out of it? I think that is the risk.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Well, there`s so many risks for him because even when you`re talking about bringing prices down, the president can only do so much about inflation.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Right.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: -- The problem for you know, Biden right now as Americans want him to fix that, but it`s not clear that he can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to go back to this, Jen. I mean, I -- 51 percent believe the economy is in a recession or depression.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, look it`s just not true.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: I know\par \par \uc2 TODD: This economy`s frustrating. This economy with COVID has been, I`ve got money and I can`t find something. But my God, there`s jobs -- there`s good jobs. Is this a -- the right-wing echo chambers better than the democratic echo chamber or --\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- Or is -- or is there something else missing?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: I mean, for sure the right -- the right-wing echo chamber is always better than the democratic echo chamber. It`s not that Democrats aren`t smart and strategic it`s because we just don`t repeat what we`re told to say. And we never well.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes, you`re not going to echo.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: You`re not -- no --\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: No echo.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hear you out.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PALMIERE: So yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, how`s (INAUDIBLE) to leave right now? She`s going to be quite the echo chamber today, whatever that is.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Right. There`s a number of democratic speeches being given tonight. But I think you know -- but moreover, people don`t feel like the economy is secure, and that they`re doing well.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s really good. They don`t feel secure about it.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: The thing is they don`t feel it but the good thing for Biden is there, you know the economy -- there`s a lot that is going right in the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: So people`s feelings could turn around quickly. You know, it could -- by the time you get to the fall, they -- people could be feeling a lot better. And then you know, you`re going to see his (INAUDIBLE) much do better.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt Gorman, I mean you guys are every hit works right now.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: It does.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: I mean where it was in 2018 when I was (INAUDIBLE) comes. I mean, it`s been great, but I think it`s broader than just the economic, right? People go to a restaurant and there`s not enough waitstaff, they go to the grocery store, they can`t always get what they want at the time they want it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We don`t feel the scroll on that.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Exactly. Well, yes --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean that it weirdly enough.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: We don`t feel comfortable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it`s like I want to tell people to go live in another country realize we have a pretty good.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: We don`t feel comfortable enough. And I think the mask movements are finally getting an all across the country. I think it`s just broader. I think the recession is the idea that`s a manifestation, but it`s so much brought about our way of life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Ayesha, every President always ends up with a crisis they never campaign.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They were going to touch, OK. Every one of them you think your presidency is going to be about one thing and said, there is an opportunity here for Joe Biden if you were to say, oh, my God, we`ve got to have a cold war president. There probably isn`t anybody in the Democratic Party that has better credentials to manage this moment. This could be an opportunity for him.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: It could be. Interestingly enough, like he`s not polling well on this issue, though --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s not.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: And probably, he`s not polling well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, this is the Afghanistan effect to that.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes. He`s not polling well on it. And unfortunately, if you have gas prices and energy prices go up, which they almost certainly will go up, even more, that`s going to be counted against Biden. But this is a moment, right. Like, the fact is that Biden wanted to turn towards China, he didn`t want to be dealing with Russia, he wanted to kind of like put them to the side and focus on Asia that did not happen but he is someone who has been able to unite NATO, unite European -- like, you know, he has been able to do what other presidents have not been --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jimmy Carter wanted to make peace with Israel and Egypt, and he end up with an Iranian crisis.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I mean, it just, you know, Barack Obama ran on getting us out of Iraq, ended up with the great recession.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes, but it is -- this is a kind of moment that defines the presidency, right? And this is -- this is going to be a defining moment in American history. So I think that even though it`s not what the White House expected or wanted the State of the Union to be, you have -- when you are doing the State of the Union, and when we`re crisis like this, you have to look and say, what does the world need to hear from the American president tonight.\par \par \uc2 And then, I think, in terms of politically, I would think, what is going to put him in the best position to lead the American people to like, restore his credibility, restore his leadership. So even though we`d love to layout a great agenda for midterms, having him lead at this moment is probably the best thing that he can do to also boost his (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t put it -- don`t try to hit bass notes, meaning like, oh, I`m going to give that constituents or group something and that could -- is that a waste tonight?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes, I -- it just won`t break through. I would -- I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This meet is what you called the Xylophone. You know hit that note, that note, that note, you know.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: The State of the Union does allow you to lay out a blueprint for the entire year and you can maintain that. But I would not -- the real opportunity in front of you is to capture the leadership mantle and that is probably the most important thing to get out of this speech. And I wouldn`t sacrifice that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Here`s the dirty little secret, in 36 hours, this will be forgotten. This changes nothing in the broader thing. This I think we`re Ukraine, this is a defining moment in a long term --\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Long term.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: -- But in the short term, you`re right, it`s going to be the inflation, and it`s going to be the economy. So look, if I`m a Republican right now, and if I`m advising them, I`m saying keep exactly what you focused on with Virginia, and what you`ve been focused on the whole time with what works inflation, the economy, crime, those kitchen-table issues.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you would ignore Putin, isn`t it? Where would you -- or would you sort of Amen whatever Biden`s doing for now on Putin and then shift towards economic?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Look, I don`t think it`s either or right? It`s -- elections are about the contrast of -- most of -- almost everyone is on the same page regarding Putin goes what the contrast is for the fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, one of the odd things here that sort of, I think gives credence to What Jennifer`s saying to Ayesha is the President`s polling below every other elected Democrat in the country.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:11]\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is just the fact. I can`t think of another one who`s polling less than 40 percent. Right? And so their ballot number -- so in a weird way, they`re already separated from the president.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Well, you know, part of the problem that I think that Biden has, and I`ve been thinking about this a bit, is the fact that, like, he does not have this locked-in base that was in love with him, right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s got a George H.W. Bush problem.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Same thing that happened to him back then.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes. He`s a very -- you know, he can function. He can do well. He knows government. He knows all of that. But people are not in love with Biden. And so he has that problem. So there are a lot of people who are ready to be, like, OK, you didn`t get voting rights, you didn`t do anything on police -- they`re ready to be like I got a problem with Biden.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hate to draw a broad brush here, but the two one-term presidents we`ve had, the three one-term presidents we`ve had, before Trump, the two that we had before came in without real bases. Right? Carter and Bush didn`t have a real base. Biden, that`s something that he has in common with them. It also is why Bush got 91 percent approval at one point, too.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re sort of --\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JEN PALMIERI, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR, UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: What that 91 percent approval rating really is about --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it is an opportunity because he went after a strongman. In a weird way, Biden might want to emulate that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: You just want that 91 percent to last.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: It didn`t work out with for George H.W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 MATT GOMAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Never does. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It never does. I didn`t think it helped in his midterms actually.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appreciate you guys coming in. Happy first day here in D.C. that we can do this.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RASCOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: That`s nice. That is nice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 A little bit of breaking news here, surrounding the stagecraft at tonight`s State of the Union, the Ukrainian ambassador to the United States Oksana Markarova will join First Lady Jill Biden as her guest tonight, sitting in the first lady`s box during the address. Obviously it will be a very public show of support for the Ukrainian people on a very large stage.\par \par \uc2 That won`t be the only visual show of support in the chamber tonight. Several lawmakers on Capitol Hill have already been spotted wearing yellow and blue, colors of the Ukrainian flag, on the Hill today ahead of the address.\par \par \uc2 I will say this, I have two ties with yellow and blue. I wore one on Sunday and another one here so we`re all doing our best, I think, to get those colors out.\par \par \uc2 And another quick programming note. Be sure to tune in to our special "MEET THE PRESS" coverage of the State of the Union tonight. It`ll starts right after the speech ends and the reaction begins. I`ll be hosting alongside my colleague Kristen Welker streaming on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 Up next, House Majority Leader Steny Hoyer joins me to discuss his party`s message at this critical moment.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. "Data Download" time. The number you should know today, 31. That is now the official number of House Democrats who have chosen not to seek re-election this year. The latest Florida Congressman Ted Deutch. He announced his retirement, seven-term congressman, whose district includes parts of Palm Beach and Broward Counties, including, if you`ll recall, Parkland, will become the CEO of the \uc2 American Jewish Committee\uc2  when he leaves office.\par \par \uc2 Deutch joins 30 other Democrats who opted out of running for another term. Nearly 14 percent of the Democratic House caucus. That number is larger than the margin Democrats currently hold over Republicans in the House. The majority of Democrats are fighting an uphill battle to keep.\par \par \uc2 In comparison, 14 House Republicans have now announced their retirement. Republican Congressman Fred Keller said yesterday he won`t seek re-election after redistricting put him up against another sitting Republican member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the Ted Deutch seat will likely stay in Democratic hands. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hundred days since I took the oath of office and lifted my hand off our family bible and inherited a nation, we all did, that was in crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That was President Biden addressing a Joint Session of Congress last April where the message above all was a hopeful one. But the last year has been a roller coaster for the president and his party. Delta, then Omicron, rising inflation, falling poll numbers, the Afghanistan withdrawal, and now war in Europe.\par \par \uc2 So as the president prepares to once again address the nation amid a crisis in his first official State of the Union, let`s bring in the House majority leader, second highest ranking Democrat in the House, Congressman Steny Hoyer from Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to see you. And I was thinking today, you know, you`ve been serving in Congress since the end of Cold War.\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOUSE (D-MD): A long time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. No, when I say this, and we`re coming full circle. And tonight is going to be the first speech in at least 20 years, State of the Union in 20 years, that is a foreign policy focus. What do you want to hear from the president tonight? And what don`t you want to hear from the president tonight?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, what I do want to hear is a call for unity and focus, and a unity of purpose. We are now confronting the worst real risk of war that we have since 1945. General Milley said that yesterday, and I agree with him. The fact of the matter is we have one president at a time. He has I think handled himself extraordinarily well leading up to what apparently was this inevitable intention of Putin to violate international law and make a criminal act of aggression against a nonthreatening country, a free country, an independent member of the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 And I think that our country needs to come together in unity. We`re obviously have a lot of differences and we have a lot of polarization, but now is not a time for polarization. Now is the time for unity not only to confront the challenge in Ukraine and to assist the brave and courageous people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: But also to come together on domestic threats that we have, the pandemic being one, obviously inflation. We need to deal with that as a unified country because that is a great stress on the American people, and we need to come together and do what the president is going to talk about, and that is of course building a better America.\par \par \uc2 I think we`re in the process of doing that. We`ve done some with the bipartisan pieces of legislation like the infrastructure bill and some with partisan legislation like the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What should be Congress` top priority now starting tomorrow morning? And I say this because look, the president has got his own priorities I`d argue confronting Putin right now. I think everybody would agree should be the commander in chief`s first focus. Congress has got a lot of things it can focus on. What should be the congressional priorities starting tomorrow morning?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, the most immediate challenge we have is making sure that we pass an omnibus by the 11th of March, because at that point in time the authorization for funding ends. But we also need to have a priority of getting the competes bill done. That was not the Senate name, but that was our name for a bill that in effect makes America stronger and better by making sure that we can manufacture and be independent of supply chains from abroad as we found during the pandemic, where we needed to rely on China and other entities to provide us with medical equipment that we needed. So I think that passing that bill is critical.\par \par \uc2 I also believe that we need to continue to focus on voting rights, absolutely essential to make sure that Americans have the right, not only have the right to vote, but are facilitated in casting that vote, making it easier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Rather than so many countries are doing -- excuse me, states are doing around the country, making it more difficult.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You didn`t mention the three words Build Back Better. And I say that not -- it`s my understanding that we are going to hear a pared-back version of maybe two or three highlights from that original big piece of legislation. What do you think should be -- what`s realistic from that agenda that can get passed this Congress?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: The president is going to be talking about building a better America. I think as I`ve just said that we`ve done two legs of that. The competes is the third leg, and the Build Back Better is the fourth leg of that very, very critical agenda. And those are really the major items. Voting rights I mentioned is also a critical agenda item, but I think we`re moving in that direction.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 I would hope we could do so in a bipartisan way and to the extent that Republicans differ from specifics, we ought to bridge those gaps. But building a better and stronger America is I think the president`s agenda and vision.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How is unity inside the Democratic caucus? I say this because I think there are three different House Democrats that are giving their own reaction to the State of the Union, Rashida Tlaib`s decision to do that, has not sat well with moderate members, but I think there`s a centrist member who`s with the Republicans that`s going to be doing one. This seems like --\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Chuck, I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- like self-inflicted political wounds, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Chuck, the reality is there are going to be 535 responses to the president`s speech tonight as there always do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think with \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and TikTok, I think we`re going to be headed there soon. I do sort of -- I am amused by that. But I am curious, have you had that conversation with the progressive wing? Because this is going to be a very frustrating time for the progressive wing of the party right now. Focus is going to be on this national security. Certain things are not going to get the same level of attention. Are they as focused on finding bipartisanship as you are?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Look, I think we`ve been a very solid party in terms of our unity. And I think you see that in the bills. For instance, Build Back Better, which we just mentioned briefly, not only had one negative vote. It was not one of the progressives, it`s one of the moderates who didn`t like one of the tax provisions but liked the bill. We unified voting rights, unified in the compete sect, unified in almost every piece in the infrastructure bill, so we`ve been a very unified party.\par \par \uc2 Are there voices who want to talk about the nuance or difference that they have or addition or subtraction they`d like to have? We`re going to have that. But when you look at the votes, Chuck, and you know this, you see a unified party that we can only give up four votes and we have passed everything in the House that we wanted to pass.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: We`re a unified party. But, yes, do we have different voices and different perspectives?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Of course we do. And they want to be heard and they speak out. But when it comes to passing legislation --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your -- yes. What`s your diagnosis for why the American public thinks the economy is in such poor shape? It is not near recession or a depression, right. It is certainly a frustrating economy. There are certainly a lot of things we wish were better, but there`s not high unemployment, Target is having to raise wages up to 24 bucks an hour. You know, this is certainly -- and yet if you just look at the perception the public has, you would think we are on a third great depression here. Why do you think the public thinks this economy is so bad?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Because I think they`re fed up with this pandemic. They`re fed up with masks. They`re fed up with having to be worried about getting sick. They`re fed up with, you know, relatives getting sick and they can`t go in and see them. That`s not Biden`s fault. It`s not anybody`s fault per se. I`m not going to go into what Trump`s response to the crisis was, saying it would go away in 30 days. But having said that, and they`re fed up with inflation.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is at a very high rate. We need to recognize that and we need to recognize -- and I think the president will -- the pain that that is causing people. They go in the grocery store and they want to buy meat or bacon or whatever they want to bring home, and they find out how much it`s gone up in the last year or the last six months or last two months. And that is causing them a great deal of angst.\par \par \uc2 And you combine the pandemic with inflation, you know, who takes the blame for that? The president of the United States historically takes the blame, notwithstanding the fact it may not be in the president`s power or fault for that occurring. But I think that explains we`re not in the right direction. I think when this pandemic turns around, as it is turning around, why, because of the American Rescue Plan, we put shots in arms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: And the president has been disciplined in giving a truthful message of the challenge of the pandemic. And I think you combine those two, and then the supply chain, which is probably as a result of one of the shortage in -- when you go to the grocery store and you don`t see the shelves filled, you think, gee whiz, things are in bad shape. They`re not in bad shape, as you point out, but I get why there is so much angst among the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Steny Hoyer, the number two Democrat in the House, always good to have your perspective with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Thank you very much. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Up next, if it`s Tuesday, it`s opening day of the 2022 midterm primary season.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk Texas with Kornacki at the big board after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As you like to know around here, we like to say if it`s Tuesday somebody is voting somewhere. Well, the somewhere today is in America`s second largest state. They happen to be the first state to hold a primary this midterm season.\par \par \uc2 Steve Kornacki is at the big board to break down today`s races. It may be a big state, Steve, but there`s not a lot of action going on. In fact, it`s somewhat -- I don`t want to say it`s uncompetitive but it`s less competitive.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Certainly less competitive when we`re talking about the House races. Texas the only state in the country to gain multiple House seats through redistricting. They`re going to have 38 House seats as opposed to 36.\par \par \uc2 But check this out when you say noncompetitive. Here is the old map. This is the one that`s no longer in effect. This is the 36-district map. And when you see yellow on here, that`s the key here. Yellow means it`s a district with a margin between Trump and Biden in the 2020 election with single digits. Was inside 10 points. You could call it potentially competitive.\par \par \uc2 And look, you see him all over the state here, 14 out of the 36 districts - -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nearly half. Nearly half. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Where at least potentially competitive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Now one of the problems for the Republicans who redrew the lines in Texas is the demographic changes in Texas were making a lot of these Republican-held seats vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 So not only do they get two new seats but they redrew the lines pretty dramatically and now you see a noticeable lack of yellow. There are now 38 districts in Texas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And there are a grand total, you see them, there are three where the Trump-Biden margin was within 10 points in 2020. So a lot of Republican seats were fortified by this. Some Democratic seats were also fortified as kind of a tradeoff but not a lot of competition. And what it does is, with just a couple of these primaries worth keeping an eye on tonight, I would circle three Republicans here, Van Taylor, Chip Roy, and Dan Crenshaw.\par \par \uc2 The same thing happened in each one of these three districts is what we`re talking about on those last couple of charts. These were all politically competitive districts under the old map. They are all dramatically Republican districts now like 15, 20-point Republican districts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Very, very core Republican primary electorates now and each one of these Republicans for different reasons has gotten maybe a little bit, you know, cross wise of the MAGA forces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not MAGA enough. Not MAGA enough. Right. They had to say something back to when a swing district or a swingiest district and suddenly it`s coming back to bite them.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And so it`s a question, I think it`s an open question. Trump hasn`t directly thrown himself into these races.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: There`s been some talk radio resistance to these -- I`m curious when the results come in tonight if there`s a penalty in any of these three.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And very quickly, Steve, one of the spins we`re going to see out of Republicans tomorrow is going to be look at the number of Republicans who voted versus the number of Democrats. Is there a competitive statewide Democratic primary at all? I mean, Beto O`Rourke doesn`t really have one and there are some competitive statewide Republican primaries, right?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, I think you could say, look, the marquee statewide race just in terms of competitiveness will be the Republican primary for attorney general.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Ken Paxton, the incumbent, he`s got a lot of baggage. George P. Bush is running. Looks like there`s a question there of what top two emerge to get into a run-off.\par \par \uc2 There is a competitive Democratic race on the attorney general`s side, and some Democrats will tell you they think especially Paxton with his baggage could be vulnerable in the fall. But I think that Republican AG race is going to be the closest thing you see to a marquee statewide race tonight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And probably will help tip primary turnout towards the Republicans, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Steve Kornacki, thank you, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You got it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`ll be back tonight after NBC News` coverage with NBC News Now`s State of the Union coverage and then MSNBC`s coverage, though, continues right now or after the break with Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220302ei3100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press Daily for February 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Mike Memoli, Courtney Kube, Richard Engel, Keir  Simmons, Andrea Mitchell, Symone Sanders, Scott Wong \par \uc2 8,978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday. Putin prepares to address the Russian people at any moment as U.S. intelligence suggests Russian forces have been ordered to proceed with an invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So what`s next for Eastern Europe as they brace for war? We`ll speak with a former military commander who led forces in Europe the last time Russia invaded Ukraine, along with a top U.S. lawmaker who just returned from meeting world leaders in Munich.\par \par \uc2 And later, the impacts of war abroad here at home as the President and his party prepare for a prolonged political battle while also attempting to reset their midterm messaging.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd. And we`re getting even more indications of just how tenuous the situation in Ukraine really is right now, as the U.S. warns that Russia could invade Ukraine at any moment, amid reports of increased shelling and tensions in the region.\par \par \uc2 And we expect Vladimir Putin to make an address to his country at any moment. It comes as he says he`s deciding whether to recognize the independence of Ukrainian breakaway regions, a move that would likely incite tensions even further.\par \par \uc2 Putin already met today with his security council with a spokesman for the Kremlin acknowledging that tensions are rising in Ukraine. At the White House, President Biden is meeting with his national security team that includes Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin and Joint Chiefs Chairman \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , as well as Secretary of State Tony Blinken and CIA Director William Burns.\par \par \uc2 And today`s developments even further diminish the likelihood of that potential meeting between Biden and Putin which the White House had agreed to, in principle, provided Russia does not invade.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a U.S. official tells NBC News that intelligence indicates Russian military commanders have already been given an order to proceed with an invasion. And just this morning, National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan offered a grim depiction of what that invasion would look like, including the prospect of Russia arresting and killing Ukrainian dissidents. Russia denies this allegation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, U.S. NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, we believe that any military operation of the size, scope, and magnitude of what we believe the Russians are planning will be extremely violent. It will cost the lives of Ukrainians and Russians, civilians and military personnel alike. But we also have the intelligence to suggest that there will be an even greater form of brutality because this will not simply be some conventional war between two armies. It will be a war waged by Russia on the Ukrainian people to repress them, to crush them, to harm them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Russia now has as many as 190,000 troops along its border with Ukraine, and Moscow has extended its military drills with Belarus, drills that were supposed to wrap up yesterday. And while the world awaits Russia`s next move, Secretary of State Tony Blinken says everything the U.S. warned would happen ahead of an invasion has already taken place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We`re seeing false flag operations taking place in Eastern Ukraine, the manufacturing of provocations, and justifications for Russia to go in. So all of this seems to be following the script that I laid out at the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2  which President Biden talked about to the nation just the other day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Joining me now is Mike Memoli at the White House, Richard Engel is in Ukraine, NBC Pentagon Correspondent Courtney Kube is here with me, and Keir Simmons is in Moscow, and we`ve also got Andrew Mitchell sticking with us from Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, I`ll start with you. Just how likely is it that we will get to this stage where we may yet see a Biden meeting with Putin?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Garrett, this has been sort of a slowly building crisis over the course of several months, which, in recent weeks, the warnings have been much more dire, much more grim, as far as what Russia is capable of. But we do appear now from going from simmer to boil with all signs pointing to an imminent invasion.\par \par \uc2 And you see that both in the public and the private choreography from both sides at this point, the Russian president holding that security meeting today publicly. The President, for the second time in nearly as many days, meeting with his top national security team behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 And at this point, you also see much more clearly these two paths that have been laid out by the U.S., by our allies, for the Russians, diplomacy versus deterrence much closer and much more clear in terms of the difference than ever. You have the President making it clear -- the U.S. making it clear that Biden is willing to sit down with Vladimir Putin if they were not to invade, of course, the big if in all of this.\par \par \uc2 But also the flurry of diplomacy that we saw over the weekend from the Vice President, from the Secretary of State in Munich with our allies, making sure all the deterrent steps are also in line. And now I think both sides have been clear. They`re open to this meeting but both sides basically signaling today that that is very unlikely.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Andrea, of course, will have the first signal as we see a potential meeting first with the foreign minister-level, Secretary of State Tony Blinken, and then Sergei Lavrov. But all indications are that this is a White House that has been very public with their warnings being ever more public about the potential consequences as well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Mike, as you will know, the President was supposed to be going to Delaware last night, he decided to stick around, we had this national security meeting today. Do we expect to hear any more from the White House today or are they in kind of the same wait-and-see posture that the rest of us are?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: I got my \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  points and then some yesterday, Garrett, yes. At this point, the White House has not given any indication that the President will speak from the White House today but that`s obviously an option that they reserved. Terms of the choreography at the White House, we have not gotten a lid for the day.\par \par \uc2 This is a holiday, of course, Washington largely closed, the call time for the pool in which the earliest possibility of seeing the President`s not until this afternoon. But clearly, this is a team that`s monitoring events very closely and the President would be prepared to speak if and when is necessary. But obviously, they`re waiting to see what the Russian president wants to say first.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Courtney, we`ve got this reporting that U.S. Intel believes the order has been given to start this invasion. Do we have any idea what that looks like when the word go is given?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So the reality is it`s already begun the military campaigns. That`s not the ground invasion, yet.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: An important decision.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: The military would mean we`ve been watching, right? So, that includes cyber-attacks, it includes these false flags or pretense attacks that we`ve been seeing only increase over the past several days. It also includes, we`ve not seen this yet, but we`re expecting to see more electronic warfare, stats jamming.\par \par \uc2 So, things like we`ll hear about cases of the lights going out in Kyiv, or in Kharkiv, or in some of these other cities, we`ll hear about the internet no longer working in some of these places. Those are the kinds of things we need to watch over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 So this is phase one. We are firmly in it right now. Phase two would be airstrikes, cruise missile strikes, artillery fire, and then eventually the ground invasion tanks, mechanized units coming across the border. Phase three is the one that we aren`t talking a whole lot about yet but that would be a potential occupation. Would Russia actually have some large- scale occupation force?\par \par \uc2 That`s one thing that we just don`t really know yet. They`ve been calling up some of their National Guard and Reservists potentially for that but nowhere in the area of the potentially 100 to 200,000 people that it could take for a large-scale occupation of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I feel like for weeks, we`ve been talking about the options that Putin has in front of him here. You just laid out what sounds like a pretty specific path. Is the military establishment in this country, confident now that not just a decision is made, but the specific decision has been made about how Russia wants to go about this?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: There is A. According to U.S. officials, a high degree of confidence that Putin has decided on a large-scale invasion. So what exactly is that? If you look at a map of Ukraine and you draw a line, basically, from Kyiv, down to Odesa and everything east of there, there is a high degree of confidence that that is really what Vladimir Putin is going to focus on right now.\par \par \uc2 So, that is about, about half the eastern half of the country for now. That is a large-scale invasion, in and of itself. It is more than 50 percent of Ukraine`s military forces are in there, there`s a large amount of the population and of course, Kyiv is the capital city.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So yes, there is, again, the intelligence, the military analysis, and assessment from the United States, and many allies, is that he has decided on a large-scale invasion.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Richard, how has the situation changed on the ground there? I think also for weeks, we`ve been talking about this sort of preternatural calm in Ukraine. You had Zelenskyy saying that he didn`t think this was going to happen, it now seems as Courtney was just laying out, not only do we have a sense that this will happen, but we know how it will happen, including that reporting that I mentioned at the top about the possibility of lists of folks who would be killed or essentially disappeared. What`s been the reaction in Ukraine to that and from Zelenskyy`s government in particular?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So the Ukrainian government for the last 24 or 48 hours has not been reacting so much to statements from U.S. officials. They have been fighting a full information war against comments coming from Russia, coming from Russian intelligence, coming from Russian state media.\par \par \uc2 There has been a constant barrage of accusations that not only are the Ukrainians attacking separatists in a -- in an enclave that they control in eastern Ukraine, but that starting today that the Ukrainian military has begun assaults directly on Russian territory. And without providing evidence, the Russians are saying that they are now facing a threat -- a direct military threat from the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 And the Ukrainian government has using WhatsApp and you a variety of other social media channels have been reaching out to reporters trying their best to swap these rumors and reports and false information down as soon as they come up but there -- it is almost a full-time job because they seem to be new allegations every few minutes coming from Russian media or from Russian officials.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So there is a definite concern here that, as Courtney were saying -- as Courtney was saying, we are in an -- in an information war phase of this where Russia is actively trying to build a case for a larger invasion. What I`m not sure Ukrainian officials are completely convinced about is that it will necessarily only be an all or nothing kind of operation, that the only option Putin has is to come in and take half the country or all of the country.\par \par \uc2 What they are focusing on right now is that in the next hour or so, Vladimir Putin is expected to come out, make an address to the nation, he`s expected to either acknowledge the independence of the separatist regions or not. And by acknowledging the independence of the separatist regions, he could be creating a cause for Russian intervention.\par \par \uc2 It`s important to note. Russia already has troops in the separatist areas, they just don`t acknowledge them. So if Russia were to come out and publicly acknowledged these -- the independence of these areas -- the separatist areas, which Russia has been saying, for a long time, are under threat by the -- from the Ukrainian government, are under siege that the Ukrainians are carrying out genocide against the pro-Russian population in these in these areas. If he comes out and recognizes their independence, the next logical step would be for him to offer some sort of protection for the separatists, which could come -- could come fairly soon. We`ll see what Putin has to say.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When we talk about the Ukrainians facing this on both every metaphorical and literal multiple fronts here, talk about those exercises, Richard, that were supposed to end in Belarus and the challenge that presents for Zelenskyy and Ukrainian armed forces to have that, you know, Sword of Damocles of all these other Russian troops hanging out in Belarus, just across the border to the north.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: So the Belarusian border is just about 50 miles from Kyiv and Belarus. When, years ago -- a decade ago, we used to play a game where it would try and balance Russian interests and European interests and it put itself somewhere in the middle. But roughly a year ago, Belarus faced a mass uprising, a street protest to try and overthrow the government, I was in Belarus at the time, and I watched some of the crackdowns, and we had to play cat and mouse hiding from authorities.\par \par \uc2 But Putin moved in and he backed up the Belarusian government, he backed up the dictatorship. And since then, Belarus has been beholden to Putin. So he has now a satellite country, Vladimir Putin has a satellite country in Belarus, which he got without toppling the government by supporting the government.\par \par \uc2 And now, that government is allowing Russian troops there, probably for as long as Vladimir Putin wants to keep them there. Ukraine, they`re in a very different situation. It was exactly eight years ago tomorrow on the 22nd that their pro-Russian dictator, their pro-Russian president was overthrown by a popular uprising and put a government in place that has been a constant source of trouble and annoyance for Vladimir Putin ever since.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Richard Engel, I know I`ve got to let you go so thank you, for your reporting there, stay safe as whatever happens next starts to unfold.\par \par \uc2 Keir Simmons, I`ve got you in Moscow now. We`ve been watching this -- we watched some of us this extraordinary meeting of Vladimir Putin and some of his advisors today, appearing to set the stage for whatever decision he might make about these breakaway regions of Ukraine. What can you tell us about that meeting about what we expect to hear the Russian President say tonight?\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, MSNBC SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think it`s really important to differentiate, unpick if you like, what we know and what we don`t know. So we do know that President Putin at that Security Council meeting, had advisor after advisor stand up and tell him whether they thought that that region of Donbas should be -- should be recognized by Russia as independent of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now, at the end of that meeting, President Putin was highly choreographed. At the end of it, it took him an hour and a half, and it was broadcast on all the Russian major channels. At the end of that, President Putin said I`m going to decide today. And now we are seeing on Russian television that he is preparing to make an announcement. So we assume that that announcement is whether or not he recognizes this region of Donbas.\par \par \uc2 And it looks highly likely given the opening advice that he was given in this early stage-manage -- meeting, the open advice he was given by his advisors it seems highly likely that he will say that he will recognize that region now. Will he recognize just the area controlled by the separatists or will he recognize a wider area of Luhansk and Donetsk which goes further than that area of well, controlled by the separatists close to where -- which it is right now?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s a really important question because that will impact what kind of military action Russia believes it ought to take. Well if President Putin tonight does announce that Russia has recognized that area, will Russian troops then openly move into just that space controlled by the separatists, or will there, as Courtney has been describing, be something like the beginning of an all-out war? And we don`t know. Look, President Putin is notorious for keeping people guessing.\par \par \uc2 One thing we do know and Western officials have been making that clear again today is that, as Courtney was saying, Russian troops -- military are poised along the border -- that border with Belarus near Kyiv, and also nearer to that region of Donbas. So we know that too, they can`t stay poised for so long. And that, I think, is one of the things that Western officials are seeing and reading and taking in some implications from.\par \par \uc2 And then another thing, just today, Garrett, that we heard -- that we heard from Western officials in a briefing that I was listening to, they said that they are aware of Russian military and security officials who have had serious doubts about whether this conflict would be a good idea, that suggests that Western intelligence do have insight into what`s the thinking within the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 However, and I`ll just finish with this, do always remember that President Putin has a very small clique around him fewer than even when sitting at that council meeting and it is very, very difficult to know what`s being said within the confines of that very small group. They don`t use computers and phones. Don`t say things on open electronic media.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So we`re left to wait. And Andrea Mitchell, if President Putin makes the decision that, as Keir pointed out, this highly choreographed meeting seems to suggest he`s going to make and either recognize or lay some kind of claim to these breakaway regions in Ukraine. How much further does that box Zelenskyy and the West in? Here, you`ve got Putin such is saying these places are no longer part of your country, what are you going to do about it?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: That`s a trip where -- that is just the last step or the penultimate step if you will, before an all-out invasion, according to American nationalists. And certainly, Zelensky has already responded. He has said that you know, he`s convening his own National Security Council. It`s difficult to imagine that that announcement would not be followed by military strikes.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Courtney has laid out the stages that we are anticipating. One of those stages certainly would be cyber, it would be knocking out command and control from the Ukrainian forces. So there`s a lot that would take place once that announcement is made. And it`s an announcement to the Russian people.\par \par \uc2 So, he does not seem to be deterred by the calls from Macron, the calls from Olaf Scholz, by the threats of sanctions, certainly, by the unity of the NATO alliance in Munich this weekend, by the congressional delegations who have without the participation, I should point out, of some Republicans like Ted Cruz and Marco Rubio, who are saying, you know, this is going to be forced by to pay a political price, we`re going to blame, you know, Joe Biden for losing Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But -- at this point, they are outliers, but that`s certainly going to be part of the political calculus. But it seems to me that President Biden has done everything to unite the allies, to declassify to try to set Putin off of his game, to send every signal that one could possibly send, you know, from Macron, from the Germans now, from everyone around him and whether or not you can penetrate this circle -- this closed circle, which is Vladimir Putin, his defense minister, and his former KGB buddies. It doesn`t seem to be working.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Andrea, I think we have Keir Simmons back from Moscow with some news. Keir, what`s happening there?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Well, Garrett, Reporter Matt Bodner has just come over and handed over a note here and this is obviously unconfirmed by NBC News but the Russian state media, RIA is now reporting that President Putin told both French President Macron and the German Chancellor Scholz that he will sign that recognition of that Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 In that report -- that Russian state media report, it does cite the Kremlin. So again, unconfirmed by NBC News, not verified by NBC News but Russia -- one Russian state media outlet now reporting that President Putin will recognize that the Donbas region is independent of Ukraine and that he was signing that recognition, so an indication of the announcement that we think we may get from President Putin at some point this evening here in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. So Andrea, then with all the relevant caveats here as we work to confirm that information, I suspect if indeed it`s true, we`ll probably hear from the French and the Germans about those conversations. What does that mean if Putin has not only apparently made this decision, but he`s communicating it to Western leaders?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes. And we have heard that from the -- from those other sources as well. That confirmation does seem to be the trip where -- let me just share other reporting from NBC News exclusively. This comes from -- Courtney was working on this, I know and she`s sitting next to you as well, and Carol Lee, Kristen Welker, Andalusia (PH), Erin McLaughlin. So this is that our sources are indicating that President Zelenskyy has been given, you know, options.\par \par \uc2 They`ve discussed plans with him to leave Kyiv in the event of an invasion, to go towards Lviv, where the American Consulate is now, where other U.S. - - other, you know allied embassies are. So that would indicate that they also have a game plan for the safety of President Zelenskyy. And he has his own options as to what he does.\par \par \uc2 But clearly, he is now convening his National Security Council and they could move -- in effect move his government out of Kyiv, which is directly in the -- in the targeting line of the advanced planning that we see that Richard Engel has been reporting on from Belarus, where they could really be in Kyiv. They could hit Kyiv with artillery, but they can have ground forces in Kyiv in two -- in two days fairly direct down that highway that comes from Belarus. So not ending that exercise in Belarus was a real signal -- a Sunday signal this thing is --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That doesn`t -- that doesn`t --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That the game is on. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It does appear to be that way. And, Courtney, I want to bring you back into this. So if the -- I can`t help but think about the parallels to what we saw in Afghanistan, and the idea that you know, I think the American people were told the Afghan military was going to fight, there was going to be this big Dugan thing, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  couldn`t approach it quickly.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we`re dealing with a whole different set of geopolitical actors, a whole different part of the world. But I feel like discussions about abandoning Kyiv, pulling back, that there might be some kind of government in some other city, how does that affect strategic planning, command, and control whether or not the Ukrainians will fight? I mean, this is part -- as you laid out, we`re not even at really part 1.1 yet of what`s going to be a multi-step process here.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Yes. So I mean, no matter what happens here, and no matter how different these situations are between Afghanistan and Ukraine, there`s going to be parallels because these are two sets of situations where you`re going to have Americans caught behind enemy lines, for all intents and purposes, just as we had in Afghanistan. The big question is what does the Biden administration do about it?\par \par \uc2 Afghanistan was actually very different than what we would -- we would tend to see in other situations. So for instance, right now, there is still a free flow of commercial air traffic, there -- the highways are open, Americans can get out right now versus what we had in Afghanistan, where the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  very quickly encircled Kabul and many people got stuck there and couldn`t get out.\par \par \uc2 So a big question for the Biden administration is if airstrikes begin, and people -- Americans are stuck there, potentially thousands of them, will they go in and get them? All indications I`m getting is they`re not really planning to do that, at this point. They`re urging them to get out on their own.\par \par \uc2 But I have to also say about this vote that we`re hearing some about -- a little bit about. If Vladimir Putin recognizes these as independent states, what people don`t realize is we hear so much about these pretenses for an attack and these false flags. This is an enormous pretense for attack because now he can say, well, these are independent states and they are just -- they are -- they`re in chaos.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: They`re begging me to come in and protect them.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: I`m not sending troops, I`m sending peacekeepers. I`m sending people in there to stabilize the situation. We should be watching for that because he can now -- it`s not about sending forces in there to attack, it`s about sending them in there to stabilize the situation and to help all these Russian-speaking citizens who are there.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s hugely important context, thank you, and so, Mike, Keir, Courtney, Andrea, thank you all. And if we hear from President Putin in the next few minutes, we will come back to you all.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a former military commander who led troops in Europe the last time Russia invaded Ukraine joins me next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As we reported at the top, U.S. officials believe that Russian military officers have already been ordered to invade Ukraine. On Saturday, with world leaders gathered at the Munich Security Conference, Ukraine`s President vowed to defend his country and warned that a Russian attack was not just a threat against Ukraine, but a threat against Europe and the entire globe`s security.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: (Speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are going to protect our country with or without the support of our partners. But just putting ourselves in coffins and waiting for foreign soldiers to come in is not something we are prepared to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Join me now on what comes next is retired Lieutenant General Ben Hodges. He was the commander of NATO Allied Land Command in 2014 when Russia annexed Crimea and was later the top General of the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  in Europe. So general, you said on 60 MINUTES yesterday that you don`t think Putin is going to launch a full-scale invasion, like what Courtney just outlined for us. Can you explain why and assess how the Intel released in recent days getting conflicts with that or how you come to that conclusion?\par \par \uc2 BEN HODGES (RET.), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2 : Garrett, thanks. And I have to say, I thought Courtney did a terrific job of laying out some very important aspects of what an attack is going to look like. But you know, there are so many people, so many of us have been using this phrase if Russia attacks or an all-out invasion. I mean, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 There`s no doubt that there`s going to be violence, that there -- people are going to get killed, there`s going to be the destruction of property, and can be a continuous violation of International Law and disrespect for International Law by the Russian Federation. That`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But is it going to be at a level that our allies are going to have to invoke sanctions for example? I think that the Kremlin has been doing everything they can to avoid that, while still conducting operations inside the Donbas. I do not believe that they have the capacity.\par \par \uc2 And this is where I would -- I would disagree with some analysts that they have the capacity to launch an attack, drive all the way down to Kyiv and encircle it and somehow capture it. I mean, 150,000 soldiers, that`s the big number that we`ve been hearing. 150,000 is not a lot of troops.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Wembley Stadium hosts 100,000. Michigan Stadium holds 100,000. So inside a country the size of Ukraine, I think that they will be absorbed. And that`s why I don`t think a full-scale attack to overrun even half of the country is feasible.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So if you`re right, and maybe there`s still time for diplomacy to have some role here, do you think that the possible meetings we`ve been talking about happening this week, a Blinken-Lavrov meeting, a Biden-Putin meeting, are a good idea? Could those still be meaningful? Or if Putin is going to bite off a chunk of eastern Ukraine and just sort of try to keep it, then what`s the point?\par \par \uc2 HODGES: Yes, well, that`s a great question. And obviously I hope I`m wrong and that there`s no attack but I think we`re past that point. Of course you`ve got Belarus is happening also. Those troops that were kept there after the exercise, I think they are there more to participate in the unsolicited absorption of Belarus into the so-called union state. I think that`s been the long-term objective. While we`ve all focused on Ukraine, those troops in Russia are probably there not only to threaten Ukraine but also to help put Lukashenko under early retirement.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Can you expand on that a little bit?\par \par \uc2 HODGES: If there is in fact --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Can you expand on that a little bit? Because the role of Belarus in all this I think has been confusing perhaps for some people, I mean, including they are allied with Russia in this, they are now hosting all these Russian troops. But are you suggesting that that`s not the end game for Russia as involves Belarus?\par \par \uc2 HODGES: Yes. The Kremlin has been working on the creation of something called the union state for years actually. And it seemed to pick up speed here in the last several months. I think as one of your other teammates there talked about Belarus and what`s been going on, I see Belarus becoming absorbed into Russia, into this union state. President Putin then becomes head of the union state, which means he doesn`t have to worry about elections for the rest of his life, and he`s got his own guy in Minsk who will not be Lukashenko, and then whoever takes over in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 So this is bad news for a variety of reasons. One, that Putin is going to be around for that much longer but also you`re going to end up with thousands of Russian troops and all that comes with it sitting in Belarus right there threatening what we call the Suwa?ki Corridor, that little strip of land between Kaliningrad and Belarus that connects our Baltic allies to the rest of Europe. This is not a good outcome.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the president and others, Secretary Blinken, who have done a terrific job so far I think on the diplomatic front, they would have to really drop the hammer on sanctions, all these things we`ve been talking about, if there is one more Russian soldier that enters Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: General, if all of this diplomatic effort has been largely to buy time for Ukraine, just quickly as someone who commanded land forces in Europe, how would you assess how Ukraine has used the time that has been bought for them ahead of what may or may not be an invasion?\par \par \uc2 HODGES: Well, I think that, again, another good question. I think that in Ukraine there is a strong belief and I support it, that the Russian plan for Ukraine was always to be like a boa constrictor, to strangle the economy until the economy collapses leading to the collapse of the government so that then Russia could find a new person to take over there that will be more sympathetic, might even be willing to make concessions.\par \par \uc2 And so the deployment of the Navy and all these troops that have basically surrounded Ukraine is like this boa constrictor. They block access to all the ports. And so when I was in Kyiv three weeks ago, you could already feel that the economy was under massive pressure. And I think that Ukraine has recognized that that was the main threat. And again, I`m not sure how it`s going to turn out.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Lieutenant General Ben Hodges, thank you. I`m sure we`ll have you on again as we all learn exactly how it`s turning out.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we`ll talk to a lawmaker who just returned from that high- stakes security conference in Munich.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:13]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. We`ve been getting a lot of new information this hour so let`s do a quick reset on what we know. According to a readout from the Kremlin, Russian President Vladimir Putin has decided he will recognize two key regions of Ukraine as independent states. This will be a major escalation intentions. Putin is expected to address the Russian people coming up shortly. And all this comes as U.S. officials are warning that a Russian invasion of Ukraine is imminent.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Democratic congressman from Rhode Island, Jim Langevin. He was part of a bipartisan group of lawmakers that just came back from Munich and he serves on both the House Homeland Security and House Armed Services Committees.\par \par \uc2 So, Congressman, let`s just start off the air with your reaction to these latest developments this morning. If President Putin is recognizing these breakaway regions in Ukraine, how should the U.S. react to this and what does that intersect with what you learned in your time in Munich?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM LANGEVIN (D-RI): Well, good afternoon, Garrett. It`s great to be with you. Obviously what you just described if Putin does make that recognition, it`s a serious escalation in the situation. Remember those regions were taken by force and what we`re seeing here now is a further potential of Vladimir Putin using his military to again take more of Ukraine by force. So it`s a very troubling situation.\par \par \uc2 All of our NATO allies along with the United States are very focused on this issue, and I give high marks to President Biden and his team for everything they are doing to pursue a diplomatic outcome that would be favorable, but in giving Putin every opportunity for an off ramp possible.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 But clearly the mood among the members that went to the Munich Security Conference are that if Vladimir Putin does take military action, that there needs to be harsh and severe consequences to punish him for what he`s done.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Let`s go a little bit deeper there. At the conference what more did you hear from our allies about what they want from us now and what was your sense of the willingness among those allies, I`m thinking about Germany, I`m thinking about Italy, that if economic sanctions go in and squeeze Russia and it squeezes back on them that they`re going to hold the line and continue this united front?\par \par \uc2 LANGEVIN: So the sense is that all of our NATO partners and countries within Europe are becoming more and more resolved that what Vladimir Putin is doing cannot stand. You remember Putin has broadcast what he really wants in the long term. He said that the dissolution of the former Soviet Union was one of the greatest political catastrophes of the 21st century. He`s made it clear that he wants to reconstitute those borders once again.\par \par \uc2 So think about what this means. It`s not just Ukraine. It`s the question of also who`s going to be next. How far out is he going to push this? It might not be tomorrow, might not be next year but the year after. Clearly this is the direction that Putin is heading. So members of NATO and our European allies are becoming more and more resolved. Some are taking longer to get there than others but that`s where I give President Biden such great credit.\par \par \uc2 I met with Vice President Harris while we were at that Munich Security Conference along with Secretary Blinken. Vice President Harris was briefing us, giving an update on her discussions with world leaders and our NATO allies. During that meeting President Biden called in and he gave us an update on the extensive work that he is doing, working around the clock, consulting with our NATO allies, our European partners, to try to get everyone on the same page.\par \par \uc2 We are getting stronger and stronger together. It`s the irony of what Vladimir Putin is doing because he`s not dividing our European partners now. If anything, it`s making us grow stronger and he`s going to make NATO stronger as a result at the end of the day. So I hope that we`re giving Putin every diplomatic avenue as an off ramp not to go into Ukraine, but we are ready to respond with higher sanctions if Putin goes in.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to let our viewers know that it appears Vladimir Putin has started speaking in Moscow. We`re going to try to get that turned around about as quickly as we can get translation on it. But while I still have you, Congressman, I do want to ask, you talk about NATO not being divided but there did seem to be some division coming from President Zelensky who was asking, you know, why wait to put these sanctions in place when the bombs start falling on Ukraine? Why not do it now? Does his argument carry any water with you?\par \par \uc2 LANGEVIN: Sure. I understand where President Zelensky is coming from. I was in the room there at the conference when he addressed the Munich Security Conference. And in his voice it`s both a sense of desperation but also a resolve that they do not want Russia to overtake Ukraine. And they want to leave nothing undone to prevent that from happening.\par \par \uc2 So yes, President Zelensky would like the sanctions to be imposed before Russia even does something. That`s certainly one course of action that could be taken but the Biden administration is trying to give Putin every possible off ramp not to go into Ukraine but has warned, President Biden has warned that there will be harsh, immediate and severe consequences.\par \par \uc2 So I think perhaps -- I think it would be a good idea to let Putin know a significant number of what those sanctions actually would be. That`s one of the things that some have argued that we should clearly articulate and define what the sanctions will be. I know that President Biden doesn`t want to tip our hand completely, which I defer to the president on that. I just in my mind think that -- we don`t do a whole hand but we could let Putin know exactly some of the harshest hitting sanctions, what they would be and how it`s going to hurt Russia and the Russia economy and Russia`s future if they go into this, into Ukraine by force.\par \par \uc2 So I feel for what President Zelensky is going through right now. He`s under tremendous pressure but he conveyed to all of us that he needs our friends and allies now to be united. He needs them to come to the aid of Ukraine to prevent Vladimir Putin from invading.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Rhode Island`s Jim Langevin, freshly back from Munich, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 And still to come, as if the political challenges weren`t enough right now for the president, his hopes for a political reset at home have run into a potential war overseas with his first official State of the Union address now a week away.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:12]\par \par \uc2 We`ll break it all down after this break. You`re watching MEET THE PRESIDENT DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:24]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The potential for war abroad has thrown a big wrench in the political plans for Democrats here at home, as President Biden and his party are attempting to reset their messaging heading into the midterms. Now the White House had hoped to use this time ahead of next Tuesday`s State of the Union address to test out a new message and a new tone. But needless to say, things haven`t gone exactly to plan.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is Symone Sanders, an MSNBC host and former senior adviser to Vice President Harris, David Frum, former speechwriter at the George W. Bush White House and a senior editor at "The Atlantic," and NBC News Capitol Hill team colleague Scott Wong.\par \par \uc2 So, Symone, I`ll start with you. How difficult is it to do this reset when you`re trying to steer the country around a potential war in Europe, and you may not use the phrase walk and chew gum at the same time?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO VICE PRESIDENT HARRIS: Well, Garrett, one could argue that the White House is utilizing this crisis as a real, for lack of a better term, opportunity to demonstrate that they are the adults in the room. Right? I remember not a couple of weeks ago where folks were talking about the pitfalls of some of the COVID messaging, the pitfalls of some of the pull-out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And now you have the White House, a very coordinated effort across the administration from diplomacy particularly with Russia and Ukraine to asserting and reasserting and reassuring our allies across the world, Vice President Harris in Munich this past weekend, to the president being very clear with not only our allies but the American people on what is happening. This is, you know, a working government really putting its best foot forward, I would argue.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: David, you have some experience with this, trying to message around a war. On the one hand it is an opportunity to show presidential leadership as only the president can in a moment of crisis.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 On the other hand Ukraine feels very far away in some ways from inflation or pocketbook issues here at home.\par \par \uc2 DAVID FRUM, FORMER SPEECHWRITER FOR PRESIDENT GEORGE W. BUSH: It might be about to get very close. We may be 48 hours, 72 hours away from real mayhem in international energy markets. If sanctions are imposed, the Russian could retaliate with interruptions to the gas supply to Europe. Gas is not a globally traded commodity in quite the way that oil is. But Russia is also an important oil supplier.\par \par \uc2 So what is very different now, whoever is writing the speech have better have a plan B and a plan C, and maybe a plan D and E, because everything from inflation to the price of gasoline, financial markets, could look very different very soon.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Scott, we were on the Hill together last week when Ron Klain came up and met with Senate Democrats. After he left there was this notable tone shift. The Senate Democrats came out talking about, they were going to talk about bipartisanship, and they were going to talk about this other set of accomplishments they want to go chase.\par \par \uc2 I remember the last eight months on Capitol Hill. There wasn`t a ton of talk about Senate Democrats pursuing bipartisanship. Talk about what you`ve seen in the change in tone, the change in direction that perhaps will be part of what we do hear from the president next week?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT WONG, NBC NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think there`s a real recognition on the part of the White House, the president of the United States, Democratic leaders in the Congress, that people are hurting, that over the last two years, there has a fatigue from the pandemic. They`re suffering from high gas prices, you know, when they go to the grocery store, the cost of milk and eggs and other things are more expensive, and so there`s a real push by the Democrats to show empathy.\par \par \uc2 And so you`ve heard that message not only from President Obama who`s been advising congressional Democrats on how to talk on the campaign trail, but we heard that from Ron Klain just last week, talking about, you know, we have to show empathy with the voters, show that we understand. And as David pointed out, there is a connection here with Ukraine. We could start to see, you know, continued high gas prices, supply chains interrupted again, because of a potential Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so there is a connection here between, you know, the bread-and-butter issues that people are facing right now and this crisis in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Symone, when I hear empathy and bipartisanship, you are singing from the Joe Biden song book, right?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I mean, these are kind of the staples of how he has tried to have his political career. But when you`re looking at the midterms, which is, you know, traditionally, historically would be a referendum on this president, does it make it harder to try to disqualify the other guy if you`re also saying we want to work with you, we want to be bipartisan? Like, how do you balance those competing tenets of a midterm strategy?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Voters want their government to work for them, and I think President Biden and the vice president also uniquely understand that they have talked about this issue of democracy, if you will, over the last couple of -- over the last year of their administration, and democracy, the tenet of a democracy, free and fair elections, but also people, you want them to believe in democracies, and they want to believe that their government can and does work for them, that it will deliver for them.\par \par \uc2 And that is what Democrats are going to take in the midterms talking about empathy and that I hear you and that we`re working for you. Democrats are going to demonstrate to voters on the campaign trail. They`re doing it right now, I would argue, actually that your government can and does work for you, and we have been doing our part, some of our friends, OK. Democrats might say some of our Republican friends haven`t done theirs, so reelect us, send us back to Congress so we can continue to make good on our promise.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And David, do Democrats get a big favor here if the COVID wins continue to break their way? I mean, the COVID politics have been changing really rapidly the last two weeks or so as have the case counts with Democratic governors changing their policies across the board. How do you see those policies coming into play in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 FRUM: Well, one of the systematic unfairness in politics is when a problem recedes you don`t get a thank you for it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No credit.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: So if COVID recedes by November, as we hope it will, it will just -- that means it will just cease to be a voting issue. As to the point about the bipartisanship, whatever else you say about President Biden, he was not born yesterday.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That is true.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: I think he cannot seriously imagine he`s going to get anything. What I think he`s doing if he is shrewd is setting the table for the very raucous partisan politics of 2023. I`m putting up my hand now not because I think you`re going to take it but to make you look worse when you don`t.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When you don`t. Scott, we`re 54 minutes into this broadcast and nobody has mentioned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But he`s going to be a huge player in this midterms. We`re seeing it now with someone like Nancy Mace who`s trying to prove that she can win a Republican primary without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Is there a congressional Republican or a wannabe congressional Republican who can win a primary without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or some of these competitive general elections with them?\par \par \uc2 WONG: I have yet to see one emerge. I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still is the dominant figure in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 You know, for the most part, certainly in the House, which, you know, are much closer to the Republican base, you know, those candidates up and down the ballot want to be associated with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, weighed in in the race against Liz Cheney, endorsed her primary challenger. We saw some of the top leaders on Capitol Hill, Kevin McCarthy, Elise Stefanik follow suit and go ahead and endorse Liz Cheney`s primary challenger. Something we very rarely see Republican leaders get involved in these, you know, Republican-on-Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still is the dominant figure on Capitol Hill and his -- you know, those reverberations are being felt, you know, throughout the country in every single race.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: My question is, who has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gotten elected other than himself?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, he takes credit for basically every Republican whose endorsement he hands out.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think that`s one of the big takeaways from me is that regardless of what he does, we know what he will claim.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Exactly. Which I think is extremely important. So you talk about why essentially Democrats themselves are not trying to center Trump on the campaign trail -- leading into this midterm election is because he`s not going to help you get any Democratic votes and I don`t think he`s going to help get any independent vote.\par \par \uc2 I think for Democrats, they have to run their race because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to do what he does regardless, but if folks really want to speak to the issues they have to speak to the issue and speak to the people, and the people don`t care about Trump. They care about their gas prices. They care about eggs at the grocery store. They care about the things that are affecting their daily lives.\par \par \uc2 FRUM: Well, he may have an impact on the Senate.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, hang on, David, we`ve got to leave it there because as usual international events are interceding as is the next hour of this broadcast. So I`ve got to wrap it up here. We`ll do more if we could but we`ve got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Symone, David, and Scott, thank you. And thank you all for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC coverage continues right now with Aaron Gilchrist right now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220222ei2l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Richard Engel, Shaquille Brewster \par \uc2 8,549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: That is it for this edition of "ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS." Chuck Todd with MTP DAILY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: It`s Friday, President Biden convened U.S. allies, prepares to speak to the nation this afternoon amid reports of a potential Russian false flag operation in -- with shelling in Eastern Ukraine. What is going on, as Putin put himself in a corner and what is he going to do to get out of it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the effort to purge Liz Cheney from the House Republican Conference continues as the House Republican leadership endorses her primary opponent officially leaving both parties on Capitol Hill battling some major messaging issues.\par \par \uc2 And an emotional day and a Minneapolis courtroom as a judge sentenced his ex-Police Officer Kim Potter to 24 months for killing 20-year-old Duante Wright at a traffic stop last year.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd here in Washington. A White House has announced a major change in the President`s schedule when you look at the timing, should really be of concern. Later this afternoon, President Biden is going to deliver remarks on the crisis in Eastern Europe. Amid a flare up intentions and efforts to prevent a Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is going to be -- announced that it`s sending more troops to Hungary as part of its effort to bolster NATO`s defenses. Remember, Hungary is a NATO nation. President is set to speak by phone in the next hour with key world leaders about the situation as fears of a war in Europe worsen seemingly by the hour. The timing of his remarks about 4:00 our times going to be getting close to midnight. Kiev time for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 Fighting in eastern Ukraine announced an impact -- some fighters in Eastern Ukraine, that`s an evacuation of civilians in the region. Another sign that Moscow was planning to use the recent shelling in the region potentially as a pretext for war. Ukraine`s military commander says that claims of a Ukrainian-backed defensive are simply not true. Now the escalating concerns of a Russian false flag operation come as a top U.S. diplomat in Europe says that Russia now has up to 190,000 military personnel in and around Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Nearly doubling its total in just the last two weeks. The Russian military also announced today that Vladimir Putin will monitor a vast exercise of the country`s nuclear forces tomorrow. That exercise will involve several practice missile launches.\par \par \uc2 President Zelensky meanwhile is scheduled, and we say this, emphasizing scheduled to leave the country to meet with world leaders in Munich for a security conference. But sources are telling NBC News that the Biden administration is concerned that Putin could exploit Zelensky`s absence from Ukraine. Zelensky is supposed to meet in person with Vice President Kamala Harris tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today. She issued this warning to Russia, if it were to move forward with an attack on its neighbor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We remain the course (INAUDIBLE) and desirous of diplomacy as it relates to the dialogue and the discussions we`ve had with Russia. But we are also committed (INAUDIBLE) action to ensuring there will be severe consequence in terms of the economic sanctions we have discussed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meanwhile, the White House says Moscow has accepted Secretary of State Tony Blinken requests to speak with his Russian counterpart Sergey Lavrov next week if Russia has not invaded Ukraine. Blinken who`s also in Munich said today that unity is the greatest tool to keep Putin at bay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: The single greatest source of strength that we have in dealing with this issue in dealing with this challenge is the solidarity that I`m going to talk about. I think President Putin has been a little bit surprised at that solidarity, at the way that NATO has come together. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  has come together as long as we maintain that solidarity, we will -- either way, whichever path President Putin chooses, we`ll be ready to respond.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now from the White House was Kristen Welker. Richard Engel is in Ukraine. Kristen, I think the timing of the remarks, particularly 4:00 this afternoon, it`s almost as if they`re trying to get ahead of something. And we all have that something in the back of our head. Is this another case where we`re going to hear the words imminent? Quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: I would not be surprised. And of course, President Biden just yesterday, Chuck delivered his most urgent warning yet as it relates to a potential Russian invasion of Ukraine saying that he was bracing for it to happen in just days. And so, this weekend is a critical period. And that is why the President I am told, wants to speak yet again from the White House.\par \par \uc2 This will be his second time this week, addressing the nation to describe what he is going to argue are these efforts at diplomacy and deterrence. And that is going to be the focus. Now I want to stress, Chuck, as you laid out at the top. This is a fast-moving situation. And so, there could be a number of developments between now and when the President speaks later on this afternoon. And so, this is an evolving speech.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But as of right now I`m told he`s going to focus on that diplomacy and deterrence I`m also told you will hear him talk about the fact that Lavrov has agreed to meet with Secretary of State Blinken. And he is going to reiterate in very firm terms that that meeting will be called off if they believe that Russia has moved one troop in to invade. So, I think you are going to hear yet again, a very urgent warning from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But also, he`s going to detail what the administration is doing is really all hands on deck approach at these last-minute diplomacy that is happening both here at the White House and overseas at the Munich Security Conference, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kristen, we have a -- it does sound like this is not about sending a message to the American people. This is about him sending a message to European allies into Putin at 4:00 today, correct?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Absolutely. And so much of what we have heard from the White House has been about sending a message to allies and to Putin directly, quite frankly, we have heard the administration for the past two weeks talk about the false flag operations. And you just detailed that increased shelling that we`re seeing in Ukraine and I just spoke with a senior administration official who said yes, there is concerned that this is in fact what they have been warning about the possibility of a false flag operation.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we watch and wait to see what happens next. But part of this is to say Putin, we know what you`re up to, we`re going to tell the world and that is yet another tool the administration believes in its toolbox at deterrence. It does come as we are told from a number of administration officials, there is concern about Ukraine`s President Zelensky traveling to Munich for that security conference, security concerns and also the message that it would send to Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 And in this extraordinary statement that we are getting from a spokesperson for President Zelensky, it makes it very clear that this decision is in flux. It says that now that we are observing the situation, which is getting more and more dramatic, the final decision will be made in the next hours or so. We still have time before departure. The President is scheduled to depart tomorrow morning, if there is a dramatic escalation or some worrying messages that he might change his mind. It underscores Chuck just how tense and how volatile this situation is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I think it does tell you perhaps what Zelensky, his concerns about his own -- shall we say his own status in the country and for some reason he leaves.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move to Richard Engel in Ukraine. So, Richard, there have been exchanges of fire before between these Russian separatists and Ukrainian soldiers. They`re these skirmishes that -- you tell me. You could use it as an excuse if you wanted to. And it`s been that way for years, has it not?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: No, what we`re seeing now is quite different. I`ve been coming to Ukraine for years. I`ve been going to the frontline for years, and particularly as this crisis has unfolded, I`ve been up and down for the last several months along the frontline -- the frontline, that`s separate Ukraine from the separatist held areas. Normally, it is a very quiet frontline.\par \par \uc2 Occasionally you have exchanges of fire, but we`re talking once or twice a week in some areas. It is once a month. It is a very low-level conflict. That frontline was very active in 2014. But since then, it is -- it has died down considerably. What we`ve been seeing over the last 24 hours is a dramatic increase and a dramatic increase coming primarily from one side. A dramatic increase with Russian-backed separatists firing into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And that is what Ukrainian officials say, it`s what the Ukrainian military says. It is also what journalists have independently been able to verify. It is what a -- what the video supports. It is what witnesses say that this has been 90 percent of fire coming out of the separatists` areas into Ukraine. Now, the area because it has been a low-level war zone for a long time, it`s not densely populated.\par \par \uc2 So, the artillery and mortars that have been coming out of the separatist areas, thus far have not resulted in any fatalities. But having been there and where I am, right here, I`m in Mariupol which has a lot of Ukrainian soldiers, we are not seeing the Ukrainian army mobilizing for any offensive, nor have I seen that in this city or have I seen any evidence of it in any other part of this country.\par \par \uc2 But that is what the separatists are now claiming. They say that they are the ones under attack that the Ukrainian army is in the process of assaulting them and is about to launch a much greater assault. And to back up what is relatively transparent show, a relatively thin theater, they are now evacuating or ordered the evacuation of the separatist areas.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:07]\par \par \uc2 Testing the air raid sirens. According to Russian media anyone who leaves that area and is forced to flee will get 10,000 Rubles in a law signed by Vladimir Putin. And according to Russian media to TASS in Rostov-on-Don, they plan to soon have ready refugee centers for 10,000 people from the separatist areas who cross into Russia. So, the Russian media and the separatists are making a case that not only are they under attack, but that they are in a position that they have to flee for their lives right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard, the fact that Zelensky, you know, a week ago, Zelensky was lecturing the West about amping things up. The fact that they are now publicly acknowledging maybe they shouldn`t leave the country. That`s quite the change in public tone.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Just in the last couple of days, the Ukrainian government has shifted tone. It has -- it was initially following very much to Zelensky`s line and that there`s sort of nothing to see here. We`ve seen this all before to now taking a much more active stance, putting out statements, putting out tweets in English, trying to counter Russian disinformation, trying to tell the world their case that they think that Russia`s could imminently use this pretext to launch some sort of invasion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kristen Welker and Richard Engel, thank you both for getting us started from two sides of the story, the White House and Ukraine. Joining me now is the chairman of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , Senator Jack Reed. Senator Reed, I want to start with what you can share with us about what we should expect to hear from the President later today. And I say this because this certainly the timing, you look at -- I mean, this feels as if there is concern that every hour matters here. What can you share?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACK REED (D-RI): Well, I think the President will direct this message as your correspondents indicated to Putin, and also to our allies. And I will be one that we will not tolerate any type of intrusion into to Ukraine, and also, we stand together. NATO has never been I think, as close together in many, many years. And this solidarity as Secretary Blinken said is our strongest strength.\par \par \uc2 You`ll also will once again indicate that any action by Russia will trigger significant sanctions. Just a -- I believe within a few hours ago, that German Minister announced that the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  project would be definitely cancelled if Russia entered Ukraine. So, those are some of the messages. It`s basically repetition, but you can`t say it too often in the case of Putin.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, I guess the biggest fear is this -- how do we get out of the status quo, if we successfully have convinced him there`s too much of a penalty to do the invasion. But we also know Putin doesn`t want to admit defeat.\par \par \uc2 REED: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do we get out of this uncomfortable status quo? How do you get him to take an exit ramp?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, what would you do? I think, and perhaps, the meeting with Lavrov which I hope takes place is a way to do that, to sit down, began a process of negotiation. There are steps that both sides can take that would be useful, much more transparency and military operations, notification of certain events. Those steps would give him I think, at least, the ability to go back to the Russians and say, you know, I`ve made progress and protecting his nation. There has to be a graceful exit for everyone.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The President is expected to announce that some more U.S. troops via NATO request will be going to Hungary. And, you know, when you see Hungary and we know Viktor Orban was with Putin a couple of days ago, it -- how confident are you that Hungary is going to be -- is going to stick with the coalition here in this confrontation with Putin?\par \par \uc2 REED: Well, I think the acceptance of American forces in Hungary is a strong indication to the Russians, that Hungary too is with us and will be with us. They could easily come up with a justification to not allow us to go into the country. So, I think that`s another signal that we`re united and I think what Orban is probably thinking of the worst possibility would be an engagement in Ukraine which could deliberately or inadvertently involve a NATO member.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:07]\par \par \uc2 And now you we`re really into the thick of things, and he`s very close to the action and Hungary would suffer grievously. So, I think, everyone, I hope, the NATO side believes in sticking together to avoid a conflict.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, if we avoid a conflict and we don`t do the worst of all sanctions, does that mean Putin got away with us?\par \par \uc2 REED: No, because what Putin was demanding was basically, we go back to 1997. We ignore the sovereignty of nations in the Baltics and Poland, et cetera. Not at all. I think what we`ve done is taken a step back, hopefully from a confrontation that could lead to even more serious confrontation. And again, there are steps that would be mutually satisfactory to both sides that would lessen tensions and provide a diplomatic exit ramp, as you indicated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You expect the Minsk accords to ever be a part of this resolution or do you think those are dead? And because maybe Zelensky doesn`t want them to have to go through with them?\par \par \uc2 REED: I think there`s very complicated consequences of pursuing the Minsk accords. But frankly, I don`t think we`re even there yet, in terms of, you know, what`s the diplomatic formula will apply. The immediate concern is to basically cause Putin to reconsider any type of military action, then sit down and start seriously talking about regional issues, about military issues. But I don`t think we`re that far along to say, you know, the formula would be some variation on Minsk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Jack Reed, Democrat from Rhode Island, the Chairman of the Armed Services Committee, appreciate your coming on sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 REED: A pleasure. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Still to come, as the U.S. engages in efforts to shore up the NATO defenses if Putin invades Ukraine, I`ll speak with a former Supreme Allied Commander of NATO forces next.\par \par \uc2 And later, cue the Democratic hammering after a very liberal city, in a very liberal state set a very strong signal that some Democratic policies have moved too far to the left, you`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:33]\par \par \uc2 EDWARD PRICE, SPOKESPERSON FOR THE UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: At almost every step of this, we have not seen de-escalation, in fact, we have seen escalation. All of this really calls into question whether the Russians are in fact committed to a diplomatic path.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was the State Department Spokesperson Ned price speaking last hour with my colleague Andrea Mitchell from the Munich Security Conference. Focus, of course has been on containing Russia`s aggression towards Ukraine. No one knows what Putin will do next or what exactly his goal is. But one thing we keep -- do keep hearing from officials is that NATO is now united, and perhaps more united than they`ve been in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 As the economist put it in this week`s cover story, Putin has painted himself into a corner. The question now is what comes next. Joining me now is retired four-star U.S. naval officer, former Supreme Allied Commander of NATO forces, it`s Admiral James Stavridis. Admiral, it`s good to see you. Let me just start with your reaction to the -- to the fact that both the United States and Ukrainian officials are nervous about Zelensky leaving the country.\par \par \uc2 What does that tell you about the current situation both politically inside of Ukraine and obviously, militarily?\par \par \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS, FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: Yes. I don`t think you have to be a former Supreme Allied Commander of NATO to understand why him getting out of dodge starts to look bad under any circumstance. And by the way, think of President Karzai suddenly leaving Afghanistan, a lot of echoes there. I think he needs to park himself in his nation, rally his people and stand with them in the face of this. I suspect that`s where he`ll land.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, it does seem as if in the moment we`re in and it, you know, whether you talk to national security type left or right center, however you want to look at it. There`s sort of unanimity that this strategy is working so far. But I say so far in -- I think we`re all trying to wonder what happens if Putin doesn`t listen? What`s your sense?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: If Putin doesn`t listen and invades, I think that NATO Alliance is going to hang together reasonably well. I think sanctions package will be decisive and massive. I think that some of the NATO partners who we all identified, you know, we worry about Germany, we worry about Hungary. But I think in the end, Chuck, they`re going to stay on the bicycle and keep pedaling with us.\par \par \uc2 Some of them will be pedaling faster than others. But in my view, the alliance will hang together. And of course, that`s the irony of this is that Putin has managed to do something very difficult. He`s got Republicans and Democrats working together, more or less. He`s got the NATO alliance speaking with one voice, more or less. He`s going to raise defense spending. And oh, by the way, Sweden and Finland may decide to join NATO after all, this is done.\par \par \uc2 So, very counterproductive. And that`s why the economist cover I think, is actually quite apt.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I did too. It`s why I wanted to use it today because -- and it also seems that it`s rally Ukrainians who have been seemed to be a bit more patriotic about this in a way that I think Zelensky is feeling pressure from -- are you tough enough on the Russians? Where I don`t think we thought that pressure would exist. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: That is fair. And a part of the reason that this is occurring now, the answer of the question, why now is that Putin looked at Zelensky, you know, a former television comedian, and thought I can push this guy around. Well, it hasn`t worked out that way. And I think Putin has decided, well, I need to try some new things. But your point, Chuck, it has rallied the Ukrainian people, I think, behind their president, and behind the idea of being Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s assume Putin`s a rational actor here, which I think everybody is assuming for now. What`s the most realistic exit ramp in your -- in your view?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I think it`s what you were discussing with my good friend, Senator Reed a few moments ago. It is the idea of putting on the table some things that allow Putin to turn around and say, there were exercises all along. And some of my issues are being addressed and I would put in this category, conventional forces Europe, where are they going to be stationed? Missile defense systems, open skies treaty. Perhaps we`ll return to that.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:02]\par \par \uc2 There`s a package of things we can talk about as Churchill said jaw, jaw, jaw. Meaning talk is better than war, war, war. I think everyone agrees with that. Except at the moment, Vladimir Putin. Let`s hope he makes the right choice here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are sending some troops to Hungary, you were talking about some NATO Allies pedaling faster than others. Could Orban have said no to that, by the way? I mean, explain how that works. These are NATO asks, right? Explain how that works. I mean, I how significant should we look at it that hey, as much as Orban likes to flirt with the illiberal world, if you will. He`s not stopping this.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: He is not stopping this. And, you know, all of those Eastern European countries had a choice at the end of the Cold War. No one was rolling NATO tanks into Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, those nations begged to join NATO. And in my view, it`s a good thing we allowed them in. Believe me, the president of Hungary remembers Soviet tanks rolling into Hungary. In the end, they`ll stand with us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hindsight, we know what it is, it`s obviously easy to backseat draw to things this way, but going, would this strategy have been effective in 2014, during the Crimea Crisis?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: That`s a good question to which there is no known answer. But I`ll give you -- I`ll give you my version of it. I don`t think it would have. I think that what enables the strategy now is the fact that Putin pulled the playbook out in 2014. And oh, by the way, in 2008, against Georgia, but what the administration is doing now is, it`s like you`re in the house, the house of Ukraine, you`ve turned on all the lights, so everybody in the house can see what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 And more importantly, you`ve turned the lights on outside. So, the neighborhood sees what`s going on. And if the neighborhood knows that that burglar was active back in 2014, it`s pretty effective. If it was the very first time might be a little harder. That`s another challenge for Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a very, I think, reasonable assumption that you`re making there that you sometimes need to see a bad action first, before unfortunately, this is why -- this is why we sometimes we are better as a reactive society. Admiral Stavridis, as always, sir, I appreciate you coming on sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we have an escalating feud inside the GOP as the top House Republican endorses Liz Cheney`s primary challenger. Not a shock these days, but still interesting. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:30]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Time now for some midterm politics. Some of the biggest names in the Republican Party version of the establishment these days are doing everything they can to defeat Wyoming congresswoman and January 6th Committee vice chair Liz Cheney in her upcoming Republican primary. The House majority leader Kevin McCarthy and the Republican Conference chair Elise Stefanik who replaced Liz Cheney in leadership have now both decided to endorse Cheney`s primary opponent Harriet Hageman.\par \par \uc2 The move is hardly surprising as Republican leadership has already ostracized Cheney attacking her since booting her from her leadership position last May. And she`s called on Republicans to reckon with former President Trump`s role in the insurrection. They of course chose not. Here`s Leader McCarthy on FOX last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Wyoming deserves to have a representative who will deliver the accountability against this Biden administration. Not a representative that they have today that works closer with Nancy Pelosi going after Republicans instead of stopping these radical Democrats for what they`re doing to this country.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans aren`t just focused on who is running against Cheney in the primary, they`re also looking at how the votes are cast. Essentially the former president is trying to rig the primary in favor of the Cheney opponent. The former president`s backing an effort in Wyoming to try to prevent cross-over voting in the state`s primary election, meaning only Republicans could vote in the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 They`re trying to close the primary from independents or Democrats. According to Politico Trump has talked to the state`s Republican governor directly about supporting this bill. Just a few months ahead of the primary, this is a pretty blatant effort to limit the voting pool in Cheney`s elections in hopes of making Trump loyalists a bigger chunk of the GOP primary electorate. This is essentially rigging democracy, folks, changing the rules in the middle of the game.\par \par \uc2 I know, we`re shocked.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the White House sets its sights on empathy ahead of President Biden`s State of the Union address. The panel will be here but first we have some sad news to report. Minnesota Republican Congressman Jim Hagedorn died last night after a two-year battle with kidney cancer. Hagedorn represented Southern Minnesota since 2019 winning close elections over Democratic opponents in both 2018 and 2020. Congressman Hagedorn was 59 years old.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:44]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The situation in Ukraine is dominating President Biden`s time, as you might imagine, and agenda at a moment when the White House is hoping for a domestic messaging reset, if you will. Meanwhile some Democrats continue to wring their hands about backlash to some of the progressive messaging.\par \par \uc2 After what happened in San Francisco this week, senior administration officials tell NBC News that they are looking to recast the president`s economic message ahead of the March 1 State of the Union to one of more empathy especially when it comes to the issue of inflation. The fear of course in the White House is that rising prices are drowning out anything else the president says. One senior official told us that people are not going to hear the litany of accomplishments if they don`t believe that he understands the current squeeze that people feel on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And outside the White House, Democrats are soul-searching about the right tactics to take in the face of backlash to some progressive lawmakers on the local level.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now on the set is Anna Nawaz, chief correspondent at PBS Newshour and an MSNBC contributor, Faiz Shakir, a former campaign manager for \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and former senior adviser to Harry Reid, and Sara Fagan, former White House political director under President George W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 And Sara, I`m going to start with you in this issue we were just sort of hinting at, which is obviously the president`s got a lot of political things they`d like to fix in the moment, they`ve got the State of the Union coming up and they`ve got the security crisis that they have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 You were in a White House at a time when you wanted to do anything else but talk about what was overseas and yet it didn`t matter, overseas dominated. Just walk us through that tension between the national security and the political folks that may be taking place in the West Wing right now.\par \par \uc2 SARA FAGAN, FORMER WHITE HOUSE POLITICAL DIRECTOR UNDER PRESIDENT GEORGE W. BUSH: Well, I can tell you the political folks are going to lose no matter what they intend and no matter how powerful they are. Because in a -- welcome to the NFL. This is the job. You know, this is what happens when you`re president, and it is critical for the country and the world that he be focused on -- he obviously is focused on it. But it also matters in the aftermath, assuming there`s an invasion, we`ll see what happens here in the coming days, is, you know, he -- the president said there`s going to be swift and severe consequences if Russia invades Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: And so there`s a situation where this is a couple weeks and he handles it well or it`s a situation where it`s months and months and months and it`s a disaster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Faiz, it feels like there`s no political upside to this because you avoid calamity, and everybody says that`s nice, what about inflation? And then the situation where if it drags on and it`s suddenly a drag on your presidency.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR, FORMER BERNIE SANDERS CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Well, and the best politics is to -- put the politics aside and manage it competently. To execute it well and have a plan for if Russia does this, we`re ready to go with the, you know, an explanation -- the sanctions regime that we want and here`s what we`re going to move on, and \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , here`s some penalties. If you`ve got to execute the strategy, the politics will work out. Right? But if you don`t, the politics will not.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, as you`re writing a State of the Union address, which is obviously on the front of their minds, I think it`s going to be hard to think about what the drafts look like right now. But you have to have inflation be a core part of it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: And I think you`ve got to get on your front foot on this. Empathy isn`t enough. I think people need a sense of a friction and a fight here. If you`re fighting for them, if people are angry and upset, you`re coming and living in the pandemic, right, I think there`s not only I feel your pain, it`s that you`re angry about how an economic system is screwing you quite frankly. You`re witnessing on a day-to-day basis. Does the president get my anger?\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s interesting. So you`d like to see a little bit of --\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: A friction. There`s got to be a fight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Friction is an interesting, Anna.\par \par \uc2 ANNA NAWAZ, PBS NEWSHOUR CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think the other thing we`re hearing a lot from Democrats, right, is that they don`t take enough credit. And you hear this from inside Democratic circles that that State of the Union address also needs to talk about what has been done. That`s a difficult message when people are paying more for things every day, they feel that pinch of inflation, they see the uncertainty of COVID, they`re living it every day.\par \par \uc2 And now, on top of that, you have this crisis unfolding overseas that a lot of Americans can`t quite get their heads around and also probably aren`t sure why the president keeps coming out to talk about. A lot of people are wondering how does this matter to me? And of course the president hit that home on that speech on Tuesday when he came out and said this could have repercussions if Russia does invade.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s having repercussions right now.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Gas prices could also continue to go up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, the gas tax holiday. Right? What`s the best way to lower gas prices is probably we solving this crisis, right?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Absolutely. Yes, but again, talk about things within your control and outside of your control, this is a crisis that very much landed on the lap of this administration. How they handle it amid all of these other political things and their actual agenda, that`s going to be what the message the Americans will probably get.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Well, and I think, you know, look, this feels really bad and it can get so much worse, and that is what is so scary for Americans and that`s where the president has to be calm and steady, and really work toward a solution because how he handles the Ukraine is going to have a huge impact on what happens in the Far East with China and Taiwan and, you know, he hasn`t had a very successful administration. He needs victories, whether it`s Afghanistan, the border. I mean, they have continued --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Taking Putin back down would be a big -- that`s a big win.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: It would be a huge victory for him. It`s going to be very difficult for them to pull that off.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But let`s switch to the hand wringing inside the left of the party, Faiz. I`m curious where you are on this and I`m curious what do you take away from what happened in San Francisco?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Well, in San Francisco, you don`t want to too overread it but you do have a problem with, you know, if you`re elected to be a school board member, your main job is get kids in school and make sure they learn, and they couldn`t handle that part of it. You know, they literally struggle with kids --\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you feel that`s a competency issue.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes, I mean, and if you go back to Biden, you pull back, that`s also what`s afflicting him. You need to have some muscularity. You`re hired for the job. If you`ve got a majority people expect you to have a vision and get stuff done. And I would say right now, if you aren`t able to, if Joe Manchin is standing in your way or if Sinema is standing in your way, you`ve got to highlight what the fight is about. We`ve largely had backroom conversations. You have not seen a White House on their front foot try to say I`m firing for lowering prescription drug prices and I want to tell you who`s in my way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re having a very robust debate inside the Republican Party right now, whether, you know, it`s not a very substantive debate, I would argue, but it`s about with Trump, without Trump essentially. Do you want to see a louder argument inside the Democratic Party right now?\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: I`d like to see us own what the philosophical differences are. And I, you know, it`s no surprise to you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You were not on the Biden side of things during the primary.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Well, no, actually I give him credit. I actually think that Joe Biden came forward with an agenda that said I`m going to work for working people here, and that`s what the American Rescue Plan was about. That`s what Build Back Better was about. To say, hey, I`m going to change this economy to work for working people. A few corporate Democrats got in his way. And my point is that you`ve got to have that ideological, philosophical debate. You got to own what it is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you think that needs to be settled.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes. Yes. Take on the corporate forces who say hey, we want to do well for money in this.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: That`s the problem, though.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: We want to make the rich -- we don`t want to make the rich pay more in taxes. That`s what they believe. So have it out. Let`s have it out.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: You`re right, in San Francisco it was a competence issue but it was also a focus issue. You know, you had a very active school board. They were focused on the wrong things. They were focused on renaming schools, including schools named after Abraham Lincoln and George Washington. And even progressive liberal parents said that`s enough. And, you know, we`re seeing a sea change in this country I think around face masks in schools and this is going to be a huge challenge for Democrats. You start to see them signaling.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: That they`re going to back down on this.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: The thing about the school board fight that I think we should also point out is it wasn`t about any one thing. Right? I mean, I think this renaming schools issue has gotten a lot of attention. There were a lot of other things that got controversy from this particular school board. This has been a recall effort that`s been going on for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: There was another controversial policy about them trying to balance some better representation racially in a very elite school in the district.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, it`s the elite school --\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: And that`s gotten a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Probably with much bigger vote.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: That`s a big battle in a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Every parent gets fired. What do you mean I can`t get my kid in that school? Right?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: That`s a battle right here in the D.C. area. There`s a school nearby, Thomas Jefferson High School for Science and Technology. I am an alum, full disclosure. But there`s the same conversation around that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: Like a lot of other issues, schools and school boards have become the battlegrounds for quite frankly populist parent outrage, whether it`s about masking or what people are taught or who gets into elite schools.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Democrats are on their back foot on this. And my two cents for anyone who`s thinking about as a Democrat running for office, you got to get on your front foot. What do you want to be talking about? You got to stand with teachers. This is a time to boldly increase teacher pay in this country. Go up to $60,000. Every teacher should have $60,000. You see teachers getting burned out. They`re being told by Republican legislature across the country, hey, we want to surveil you, want to put cameras in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 We want to tell what you can`t say, don`t say this word, don`t say that word. And you can stand boldly with the teachers. Get on your front foot and put forward proposals, say I`m with teachers. They got a hard job right now. We`re putting the screws to them in an unfair way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hate to say, I am surprised I have not seen more Democrats come out on teacher pay. I thought that might be but they haven`t.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Blown it.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Well, I mean, you`ve seen, you know, initiatives around the country where this has been popular across the board with voters, not just Democrats. But this gets back to the -- you`re right, the Democrats need to have this out. The problem is all the indications are that the progressive messaging, whether it`s on Build Back Better, whether it`s on schools, whether it`s on any number of issues, immigration, is not popular with the American people. And so that`s why you see these centrist Democratic operatives coming out and saying, you know, we`re going to see a blood bath in November because of --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: What`s your view on what`s unpopular? Because I see lowering prescription drug prices, I see taxing the rich as very popular. I mean, just --\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Well, sure, they sound very popular in a poll but when it gets down to actually what it takes to implement that and what it costs, that is where it is incredibly unpopular with the -- you know, the amount of money in the Build Back Better plan was too much for the American population. Had Joe Biden come out and done universal preschool or some element of it or prescription drugs, he could --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Or paid leave. Those are all --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Yes, but it was you had to do every single thing at one time. And that is the problem with the progressive movement right now is they`re not being incremental.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s a debate about tactics.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right? And then I think that`s a debate that (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: And going to the public with all of those popular measures I think is a winning message.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Clearly not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me put --\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Well, not (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me put a little bit on the spot.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s about what`s going on in Wyoming Republican politics. Is there a sense of where you can overdo it and actually it starts benefiting Cheney? Like Trump trying to change the rules, all of a sudden the national leaders are piling in. You know, that`s not always the best message.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: It`s not always the best message when particularly in any state. I mean, one thing about politics that I`ve learned is calling from Washington, the first thing people say to you is we do that a little differently here. And, you know, that`s true in Wyoming as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: It is a complicated issue and there`s no question that the Trump element of the Republican Party is loud and active and successful in a lot of these states but I do think they can overreach.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anna?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: I don`t think we know how successful just yet. Right? I mean, I think that`s the big test, that`s the big question right now. The money that the former president has poured in, over a hundred candidates I think he`s endorsed up and down the ballot, in big races and little, and that, you mentioned before the battle within the battle, that`s what we`re going to see play out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, we`re going to find out what Trump`s real influence is in this party in this primacy season. He`s put himself on the line in Georgia. He`s put himself on the line in a couple of these places and if he doesn`t succeed, there`s a bunch of people that we`re going to want to see use it as an excuse to shove him out.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: And there`s going to be a lot more people who run for president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Which would be --\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Which is maybe still ahead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just what we need. 20 candidates for president. That works out really well.\par \par \uc2 Sara Fagan -- you guys were terrific. Thank you, and I hope you enjoyed being in here.\par \par \uc2 FAGAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Drinking out of mugs that are clean. Anyway, up next, an emotional day in court as the former police officer Kim Potter gets sentenced for the killing of Daunte Wright.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we are back. Former Minneapolis area police officer Kim Potter was handed a 24-month sentence for shooting and killing Daunte Wright during a traffic stop last year. It`s a sentence well below what was expected. The judge told Potter she would serve 16 months in prison and could be on supervised leave for the remainder of her sentence. Potter spoke again in court this morning and gave a tearful apology to the Wright family and to the community she used to serve.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIM POTTER, CONVICTED OF KILLING DAUNTE WRIGHT: To the family of Daunte Wright, I am so sorry that I brought the death of your son, father, brother, uncle, grandson, nephew and the rest of your family.\par \par \uc2 To the community at the Brooklyn Center, I do owe you an apology, too. I loved working for you. And I am sorry for what has happened to our community since the death of Daunte. And the men and women who work for you still are good, honorable people.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Members of Wright`s family delivered emotional victim impact statements. His mother Katie Bryant broke down describing the painful loss of her son, promising to continue fighting in his memory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE BRYANT, MOTHER OF DAUNTE WRIGHT: You took his future, what he could have done and there was so many things. April 11th was the worst day of my life. A police officer who was supposed to serve and protect took so much away from us. She took our baby boy with a single gunshot through his heart and she shattered mine.\par \par \uc2 My shattered heart has been on display for almost a year now and I have to live in this nightmare watching my son shot and killed over and over and again.\par \par \uc2 Daunte Demetrius Wright, I will continue to fight in your name until driving while black is no longer a death sentence.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Judge Regina Chu justified the departure from sentencing guidelines telling the court that Potter`s case was a unique circumstance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE REGINA CHU, HENNEPIN COUNTY, MINNESOTA: I find the facts and circumstances here justify a downward departure from the guidelines. This is a cop who made a tragic mistake. She drew her firearm thinking it was a taser and ended up killing a young man.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: NBC News correspondent Shaq Brewster is outside the court in Minneapolis. And, you know, Shaq, the judge was convinced it was an accident. That`s what she said there, which is why she justified her sentence. Does the family believe that?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, according to the comments that we heard after that, and as they were leaving the courtroom, no, they did not believe that this was an accident. I mean, this was a family that was distraught as they were leaving the courtroom. You heard the father say that he felt cheated by the sentence. The mother saying that she believed that it was the justice system today that murdered her son.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 And you know, it really got complicated in terms of the sentencing process and the suggestions, recommendations that we walked into today with. The prosecution was arguing for a sentence that fit well within the sentencing guidelines in this state, about seven years. But even the prosecution acknowledged that this is a unique set of circumstances and that Kim Potter, they believe, was not some malicious person, was not acting maliciously.\par \par \uc2 They seem to also suggest that this was a mistake. The defense was arguing for probation. And you heard Judge Chu kind of side more with the defense in her ultimate decision. That sentence by the way, Chuck, we know it`s 24 months, that`s two years that she will be in prison, but we also know that 16 months of -- there`ll be 16 months that are supervised release, and she will get credit for the nearly two months that she`s already spent behind bars. So much less time than that two-year headline that you hear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shaquille Brewster on the ground for us in Minneapolis. Shaq, thank you, and thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday it`s MEET THE PRESS on your local NBC News station. I will be speaking with the Secretary of State during this very, very tense time.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage will continue after the break with my friend Aaron Gilchrist. We`ll see you then.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220219ei2i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Josh Lederman, Courtney Kube, Richard Engel, John  Allen, Mike Memoli, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 8,690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`ll be reporting from Munich in the next couple of days. But first, Chuck Todd with MTP DAILY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd here in Washington at what may be one of the more consequential days of this Biden administration so far, we think, we`ll find out. The President just spoke in Ohio as the White House looks to road test a new message and a new tone on the economy ahead of the State of the Union address, by the way, that`s less than two weeks away.\par \par \uc2 But right now, there are bigger problems. He`s also facing the prospects of a war in Europe, particularly in Ukraine, potentially at any moment. Ukraine is accusing Russian-backed separatists of shelling a kindergarten in the country`s east. Russia is claiming that Ukraine is responsible for sounding alarms that the Kremlin might look to use the incident as a pretext for heavy military action. U.S. officials now say Russia has moved an additional 7000 troops to the border, not away from it, as Russia had vowed to do earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 And making a diplomatic solution to the crisis even less tenable, the State Department says Russia expelled the number two U.S. diplomat from Moscow today. President Biden told reporters this morning that all evidence suggests a Russian invasion could be imminent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How high is the threat of the Russian invasion right now?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s very high.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s very high because they have not -- they have not moved any of their troops out, they have moved more troops in. We have reason to believe that they are engaged in a false flag operation to have an excuse to go in. Every indication we have is they`re prepared to go into Ukraine, attack Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is there a sense that this is going to happen now?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes. In my sense, this will happen in the next several days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just a few hours ago, the Secretary of State Tony Blinken echo the -- echoed the President`s warning telling the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  in very stark terms what U.S. intelligence suggests Russia is planning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATES: First, Russia plans to manufacture a pretext for its attack in response to this manufacture provocation. The highest levels of the Russian government may theatrically convene emergency meetings to address the so-called crisis.\par \par \uc2 Next, the attack is planned to begin, Russian missiles and bombs will drop across Ukraine, communications will be jammed, cyber-attacks will shut down key Ukrainian institutions.\par \par \uc2 After that, Russian tanks and soldiers will advance on key targets that have already been identified and mapped out in detailed plans. We believe these targets include Russia`s capital -- Ukraine`s capital, Kyiv, a city of 2.8 million people. But let me be clear. I am here today not to start a war but to prevent one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Some Stark language. Blinken is now headed to Munich for the Munich Security Conference, Vice President Harris left for that earlier this morning. Let`s bring in our team of NBC reporters for the very latest. Josh Lederman is at the White House, Courtney Kube is at the Pentagon, Richard Engel is in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Josh, let me start with you. If you look today, in some ways, the President`s rhetoric hasn`t changed. We expect this to be imminent all of this, except it does look like it`s the Russian rhetoric that has changed, they -- now, here at the end of the week, as to where it was at the beginning of the week. How -- obviously the White House is on high alert, is the change in tone have them thinking this is now almost a fait accompli?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: I think the main event of the last couple of days that is heightened concern for the Americans, Chuck, is the fact that the Russians put out this information suggesting they were de- escalating and U.S. intelligence then coming to the clear conclusion that that was basically head fake.\par \par \uc2 And so the Biden administration is trying to be very narrow in their messaging in response to Russia`s moves right now because they`re trying to avoid falling into one of several different misinformation narratives that Vladimir Putin has in play right now.\par \par \uc2 So if Russia does something that looks de-escalatory, moving some troops away, or responding positively to a diplomatic engagement, then the U.S. dismisses it out of hand, the Russians are going to use that to say it`s the jingoistic Americans who are beating the war drum and are not open to diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, they don`t want to be made for fools by letting the pressure off of Putin in response to supposedly de-escalatory moves that then turn out to be just a lot of nothing, which is what U.S. officials are now telling us, more than 7000 additional troops moved into the area even as Russia said it was deescalating.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was so interesting that when Tony Blinken spoke to the UN today, Chuck, the same venue where the U.S. -- Colin Powell presented that intelligence from the Iraq War, he did not shy away from that issue. In fact, he went straight at it making clear that this administration has no ethical qualms about using declassified intelligence in this situation to try to shape public views about this the way that they are.\par \par \uc2 They feel like it`s different because they`re trying to stop a war rather than start one. But they also feel confident that some of what they`re saying can be openly seen by folks like you and I, who are looking at satellite photos, who are watching what`s happening on the border, and that that corroborates the public case that the Biden ministration is trying to make.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney Kube, I`ve seen some reports that if there is some concern with our intelligence, is that all of our intelligence about Russia`s military movements seem to be coming from observational information, meaning satellite feeds and things like this, that there is a concern that we`re not as wired in his -- to his inner circle, and that we are blind to some thinking. What are you hearing at the Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So I understand that. I think that if there`s going to be a time where the U.S. is really going to be blind to what`s actually happening there, it`s going to be after an invasion more so than right now.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, it`s not just the U.S. has what -- you know what you`re talking about satellites, right? They have overhead imagery that they`re able to see what Russia is doing along the border. But there`s also a lot of information that comes in from partners, allies who are in the area, who spend a lot of time gathering information about Russia, and they`ve gotten really good at it in recent years, especially. So the sharing of that information does give a much more fulsome picture.\par \par \uc2 But, Chuck, when you`re really getting to the heart of the issue right now, the question that I keep getting is, when is this going to happen? And I -- and the reason that it`s so difficult for U.S. officials to know exactly whether Vladimir Putin has made the decision, and when it could happen is because everything that they`re looking at at this point is somewhat circumstantial, right?\par \par \uc2 So, the reason that there is so much confidence that he is -- that he is likely to invade is because of the -- what they`re seeing happen around the borders, the buildup of this personnel of equipment moving closer to Ukraine, bringing in some very sophisticated equipment and weapon systems that Vladimir Putin wouldn`t necessarily bring in just for an exercise or just because putting himself at risk and other parts like in the Far East because he`s really taken so much of his military from that area and moved it in here.\par \par \uc2 So, from that perspective, it is somewhat circumstantial, what he`s deciding to do here, as opposed to having some kind of a real smoking gun. Vladimir Putin himself or someone saying, we, you know, we`ve decided to invade on this day at this time.\par \par \uc2 One thing that Anthony Blinken said that I also found really interesting at the UN earlier today, was when he talked about preparing for Russian missiles and bombs. That`s something that we have been hearing about for several weeks now as the possible way, the oath -- sort of an opening salvo of the military might into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 If this does happen, some sort of an invasion is that it would most likely start from the air. There`s a lot of emphasis on Russian military mechanized units, ground units, tanks that are surrounding the border right now. But what we`ve been hearing is that in fact, in addition to the potential false flags, cyber operations, potentially some electronic warfare, jamming, the original -- the opening salvo would most likely be from the air.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard Engel, take us on the ground today in Ukraine. We`ve got -- first of all, there`s been a couple of developments, the Russians officially accused Ukraine of some violations with the United Nations, obviously, there appeared -- the U.S. believes what`s happening on the ground appears to be an attempt at a false flag. What are you hearing? What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So the stage is set for a potential conflict. You have 150,000 plus Russian troops, and as Courtney was saying, aircraft, missiles all positioned around Ukraine. The question is, what will -- what will start this potential war that the President thinks could start in the next several days?\par \par \uc2 And it goes down to a pretext. Russia has been building a case that the -- this is all hysteria, that it is Russia that faces a real threat, and that is Russian -- the Russian speakers in this country who are facing a potential genocide, to "Vladimir Putin," from the Ukrainian government.\par \par \uc2 This is the narrative that Putin has been telling his people, he has been trying to tell the world that the Russian-speaking population here and particularly a group of Russian backed-separatists are persecuted by the Ukrainian government. And this morning, what we saw in the early morning hours here was according to the Ukrainian military, a large attack on Ukrainian towns and villages that started from the separatist areas.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So the largest attack from the separate areas since 2018 with dozens of mortars and rockets fired into Ukrainian territory. A lot of that border area is not heavily populated, so it did not cause significant damage or casualties. One kindergarten, Ukrainian police say, was damaged but no children were hurt.\par \par \uc2 And what the Ukrainians are saying is that this is an attempt to provoke them into a fight, to go them into a fight, to give Vladimir Putin an excuse to move in potentially as peacekeepers, to protect that besiege Russian-speaking area, those -- that besieged pro-Russian enclave.\par \par \uc2 Now, the pro-Russians are saying that just the opposite happened. They say that it wasn`t an attack with dozens of mortars and rockets -- dozens of mortars and artillery that flew from their territory into Ukraine, they say it was Ukraine that started it, that they are the ones who are at risk, and they are the ones who potentially need Russian protection.\par \par \uc2 So, it is a, he said she said, but the Ukrainian government is going out of its way and producing evidence and photographs to back up its claim, while the Ukrainian separatists have not produced any kind of physical evidence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- Richard, I`m curious, from the Zelensky government, how are they -- I mean, if he didn`t like the use of the word imminent two weeks ago, what do they feel about it today?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Well, the Zelensky government for a long time now, and we`ve been talking about this has been accused of having its head in the sand of trying to have it both ways, to prepare for war, but also to tell the people that to remain calm because no war is coming. They seem to have rounded a corner a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Just in the last 24 hours since this attack, which they say came from separatists held areas took place, we`re seeing the government much more active. They have been putting out photographs, they have been actively contacting journalists, they are -- Zelensky himself is touring the country meeting with troops. So if they were accused of being passive before, that passivity seems to have gone away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Courtney Kube, when you talk to folks in the Pentagon, is there -- you know, sometimes Putin is just buying time because he`s got another strategic reason here. You know, is he just -- is it possible he`s just waiting another couple of weeks because there`s a specific time? He wants to wait till after the Olympics or something like this. Is there any evidence that the Pentagon has seen aiming -- pointing to that?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: No. I mean, in fact, so it`s, there`s no -- the officials I`m talking to, say they -- look, they don`t have any intelligence that says he`s made the decision to go at this time on this date. But at the same time, they don`t have intelligence that says he`s decided to wait either. So again, they`re looking at sort of the evidence surrounding it whether has been a big one.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard about light illumination at night, you know, how big is the moon and when? We`re in a period of high illumination, right now. That`s something that military planners look at. But you have to -- keep in mind, they`re looking at this with a U.S. military lens, a U.S. lens, as opposed to how Putin looks at it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: I mean one thing that I have been hearing is that Putin has a history of making a decision at the very last minute. So theoretically, he could do all of this, he could move all of this might -- military might in and look like he`s going to invade and at the very last minute, turn it off. But I have to say what we were hearing about that as a potential several weeks ago, in the past couple of days, people aren`t saying that as much as they were, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finally, Josh Lederman, does the Biden folks accept the fact that they may have to go public with more of this intel, show the work a little bit if you will, instead of just saying take our word for it?\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Absolutely. We saw Antony Blinken, the Secretary of State do that just this morning when he talked specifically about how the U.S. has information that Russia could go after specific communities in Ukraine in addition to the type of military confrontation that a lot of us have been anticipating.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. -- we`ve really never seen work like this in real-time to put out declassified information, not necessarily to try to yield one outcome but to try to prevent the other side from being able to act without people knowing exactly what they`re doing. U.S. officials say they continue to plan to call this out in real-time to the extent they can while protecting their sources and methods, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s a fascinating new strategy to watch, particularly here, as we`ve been conditioned to cover intelligence in the past. That`s for sure. Josh Lederman, Courtney Kube, and Richard Engel thank you for getting us started. We`ll have much more ahead on the situation in Ukraine. We`re going to break it down with a panel of experts and a top Senator on the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 But later, we`ll also get into a little domestic politics with Joe Biden`s plan that shifted domestic messaging means for the President and his party, State of the Union less than two weeks away, midterms fast approaching, a lot of hand wringing, making hands even drier these days. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLYOD AUSTIN, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: I have served and fought alongside NATO allies for the better part of my adult life. But I can honestly say that I have never seen the Alliance more relevant and more united and more resolute than I see it today. Mr. Putin says that he doesn`t want a strong NATO on his Western flight. He`s getting exactly that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is the most fascinating, unintended consequence of this showdown that Putin sports. It is actually restored NATO`s relevancy and I think for a lot of people. That was Secretary of State -- Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin this morning at a press conference as he wraps up his meetings at NATO. Turning on for more on the Ukraine crisis is Ben Rhodes, a former Deputy National Security Adviser in the Obama White House, and MSNBC Political Analyst and retired Four-Star General, John Allen, who has led NATO forces and he`s now the President of the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 General Allen, I`m going to start with you and just react to this. I mean, obviously, it`s not a good sign if we catch Russia lying about the escalation. What does this mean to you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALLEN, PRESIDENT, BROOKINGS INSTITUTION: Chuck, thanks for having me on. And, Ben, it`s great to see you again. Look, this is a warfare of the 21st century, Chuck. We fight in multiple domains, conflict occurs, not just in the physical domain and your Pentagon correspondent, which was absolutely correct a few minutes ago, we`re focusing on tanks and airplanes, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But much of conflict in the 21st century occurs in the information domain and in the cyber domain. And in that regard, we have seen the Russians are manipulating the information domain dramatically throughout the last several weeks and attacking the cyber domain infrastructure within Ukraine. In some respects, it`s to unbalance Ukrainian leadership and the Ukrainian population but it`s also to prepare the battlespace potentially for physical operations.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 So the fact that the Russian are lying, shouldn`t come as a surprise to anyone. In fact, anyone who knows anything about Information Operations knows that, the terms dezinformatsiya, and maskirovka, which we use all the time to characterize deception and information. Those were coined by the Russians and the Soviets. And so this is completely the conduct that we should be expecting from the Russians at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ben, given your experience dealing with National Classified Intelligence, are you surprised by how quickly we`ve made public some of these things? I mean, obviously, there`s been a strategic case for it. Have you been surprised -- and put that in context, compare it to how you guys would have done this just say 10 years ago?\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, FORMER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, I`m very supportive of it. You know, Chuck, back in 2014, and 15, for instance, you know, I felt frustrated at times that it was harder to get information declassified.\par \par \uc2 To give you a big example, Russia was providing lots of military equipment into eastern Ukraine, where Russia was claiming that separatists were fighting in some kind of natural uprising against Ukrainian impression, where, in fact -- in fact, what was happening is, Russia was pouring in military equipment and some advisors across the border.\par \par \uc2 And just to declassify some overhead imagery of that would take us a period of days. And I think it`s very important that you don`t allow Vladimir Putin to set the narrative as he wants it around an incident here because what he`ll want to do to his own public and to the global public opinion, is make it look like Ukraine was somehow the aggressor, and he had to respond.\par \par \uc2 Now, we may not believe that as Americans, but that`s the message that he really needs to try to bring the Russian public along with a word that this time, be much more costly than Crimea, or what`s been going on in eastern Ukraine. And also, to hold the alliance together, it`s important that the United States establish that Russia is the aggressor to hold the Europeans together behind sanctions.\par \par \uc2 And so I think the reason they`ve been so upfront and putting this information out is it allows us to control the information environment around what is the perception of a potential Russian invasion. And that will help us both blunt Putin`s effort to gin up domestic support in Russia, but also help bring along allies behind sanctions and the fortification of NATO, all the things that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: General Allen, it does feel as if we were -- it`s clear to me that there are exit ramps here for Putin if he wants them. And in fact, he`s in some ways, whether we want to admit it or not, this pressure campaign, it has worked to a point where there are a couple of if he wants to take an exit ramp, declare some form of victory, he can. Why do you think he hasn`t taken it yet?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN\uc2 : Well, I think he`s hoping to position the United States and our allies to make potential security concessions to him ultimately, in the -- in the context of the security architecture in Europe. I think he had hoped, ultimately to unsettle NATO, and as Secretary Austin just said it strengthened NATO. I think he had hoped to unsettle the sense Europeans had about its relationship to NATO and the United States.\par \par \uc2 In fact, what you`ve seen is pretty dramatic leadership from the United States, along with our partners in Europe in creating a really solid front with respect to Russian provocations, and this completely Russian contrived Crisis and Emergency. Remember, Ukraine is the innocent bystander here. And this is a Russian crisis that has been created for his purposes. And we have to be resolute in this process and then set it well. There are a whole series of sanctions that are out there that can be brought to bear.\par \par \uc2 But I think Putin never imagined that this could backfire in the way that it has in creating solidarity, and creating American leadership in the aftermath of the last administration and American support for NATO. We may well see with American troops streaming to Europe by the thousands, we may well see, and according to Jens Stoltenberg just yesterday, the establishment of battle groups on the eastern frontier of NATO that never needed to be there before but Russia`s threat to the eastern frontier of NATO may, in fact, create not just a greater resoluteness if you will, in NATO, but also, action now to defend the NATO area.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ben Rhodes, your take on the Zelensky government here is he`s strong enough to sort of, withstand this squeeze. I mean, I -- there`s been a part of me that fears that what Putin really wants to do here is just sort of, antagonize, antagonize, antagonize, antagonize, and create sort of domestic exhaustion if you will with the -- with the current Ukrainian government.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: It`s a very tough spot that he`s in because there is a degree of polarization in Ukrainian politics. There are people to his right, who are much more fervent nationalists in Ukraine and then there are some people, there are some progressive factions.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Although -- the one thing I would point out, you know we`ve been talking about how Putin`s actions have had the reverse consequence in terms of strengthening NATO unity and strengthening that U.S. presence in Eastern Europe. The other thing that`s happened is that Putin`s aggression against Ukraine has consolidated support inside of Ukraine didn`t move in the direction of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and to move in the direction of NATO.\par \par \uc2 If you looked at Ukrainian public support for NATO membership before 2014, before the annexation of Crimea, it was very, very low. And it has skyrocketed since Russia annexed Crimea and moved into those eastern regions. So Zelensky`s -- yes, he`s in a tough political spot, he`s going to face very difficult days to come if there`s an invasion, frankly, could just really try to top (INAUDIBLE) government.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing that is a factor that has to be reckoned with by Putin is his own actions that consolidated Ukrainian support, to not be dominated by Russia. And that means that if there is a conflict, he`s going to face much more resistance. Now, just because he`s moving into parts of Ukraine is not as easy for him to move into as Crimea, but also because of the way in which public opinion shifts in the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Putin`s is in a box of his own making. The question is, how did you get out of it? General Allen, and Ben Rhodes, I appreciate both of you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN\uc2 : Thank you, Chuck, good to be here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still to come, President Biden`s growing list of challenges here at home as the White House looks to shake up its messaging around the economy. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. While President Biden navigates the escalating situation on the Ukrainian border, he`s also got a host of domestic challenges to tackle. The president spoke last hour in Ohio about the economy as administration officials are telling NBC News the White House is trying to craft a plan to refocus his economic message ahead of the State of the Union, which is just 12 days away.\par \par \uc2 Those officials tell our own Mike Memoli and Carol Lee that Biden will be leaning on empathy when it comes to inflation in an effort to show Americans that he feels their economic pain.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli is the one traveling with the president in Ohio. Also with us is Patrick Gaspard. He was the White House political director for President Obama, he`s now the president of the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Mike, this is the, you know, to borrow a phrase from the `90s, feel your pain type of pitch here. I`ve heard this similar pitch that nobody will listen to the accomplishments until people feel as if things are getting better.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s exactly right, Chuck. There are really two tracks to what the White House is trying to do as he`s doing these public events even if we`re not necessarily paying as much attention to them, with the other big crisis and the state of -- Supreme Court pick getting most of the attention, but what we saw very clearly today is that the White House thinks they have a very strong economic argument to make, especially as it relates to the work he`s done on manufacturing, on infrastructure, of course, supply chain issues.\par \par \uc2 And you heard the president, without Build Back Better to talk about anymore, still making a number of sort of thematic tests. You heard him say we`re building America better than before, we`re rebuilding America, build America better than we found it. We rebuild, we`re buying America and we`re betting on America`s workers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I noticed the awkward Build Back Better is gone, which has always been awkward to say. Right? It`s not -- and they went with building. They put a sign. They changed the signage, right? I mean, if you have signage, it means you --\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: That`s right. So that`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That seemed to be significant for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: So that`s track one, which is what they say tests really well not just with union workers but with suburban mothers, which is a key demographic this White House pays a lot of attention to. It`s also really important in this region, which you know the map, Pennsylvania, Ohio, Michigan, Wisconsin, the Senate and governors races here are huge for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 But then there`s track two, Chuck, which is no one wants to listen to him talking about the things they`ve done until they really feel like he understands that people are sick and tired of COVID and they are still feeling the pain at the pump and the issues with higher food prices. And so last week is a good example of what the White House says was trying to show that he feels their pain.\par \par \uc2 He says I grew up in a family where for the price of gas went up a dollar a gallon, it really had an a big. The signage behind him last week was lowering prices for American families. So that`s step one. Step two is to make the economic argument and then step three is to go out on the road and sell it, which the White House says he`ll be doing much more of after the State of the Union.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike Memoli in Ohio, I see they`re breaking it down. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, you were smirking there a little bit. And I want to get at this because I know -- I think I have an idea what you`re smirking at because I get this same frustration, which is, look, it`s obvious, right. We are in a difficult situation as a globe, right. It`s been a rough couple of years. Nobody feels totally stable. And, yes, the political environment stinks for the party in power.\par \par \uc2 How do you deal with it? Does it feel like -- does this feel like rearranging deck chairs? You have to do these things to show that you`re doing these things? Or is there just some things that can`t be fixed right now?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK GASPARD, WHITE HOUSE DIRECTOR OF POLITICAL AFFAIRS UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: I was smirking at the notion that it all hangs on signage, Chuck, but I think that you`ve kind of -- you`ve nailed the moment. It`s a scratchy moment. Everywhere in every community across America right now, we have the pandemic drag, we have the inflation drag, we have the partisanship drag. All of that. It`s hanging on for the president, there`s no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, can I just caution? You know, I think there`s a lot that Mike just said that makes a lot of good sense. But I really want to caution us here because the politics, as I understand it, as I have experienced it, is a business of incessant repetition. President Biden, this administration can`t push away from the successes. Yes, they have to make it clear that they feel Americans where they`re at right now.\par \par \uc2 They feel the pain. There`s empathy. Joe Biden is good at that because that`s an authentic thing for him. But at the same time, they have to remind folks month after month from now through November about the 5.7 percent growth in the economy, largest impact since 1984, about the 6.6 million new jobs, about all the shots in arms, that matters. And most importantly, Joe Biden needs to say during the campaign, Chuck, don`t compare me to the Almighty, compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: They`ve got to make the comparison every day and remind Americans that the things that have made it possible to open the economy again, Republicans voted against every single one of those measures. That`s critical.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s funny, you just sort of responded to what I was going to ask you to respond to, which is the David Alexrod op-ed in "The Times" that sort of frames at this debate. Let me put up an excerpt of it because I think you just retorted it. "The State of the Union is stressed. To claim otherwise, to highlight the progress we have made without fully acknowledging the hard road we have traveled and the distance we need to go would seem off key and out of touch. You simply cannot jawbone Americans into believing that things are better off -- better than they feel."\par \par \uc2 You know, I read that last sentence, I heard what you just said, and you know, what went in my head, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wouldn`t take that advice.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: He would not at all. Thank you, Chuck. And you know, I`ve made a career out of agreeing with David Axelrod and he`s right more often than I am, but on this one I think if Republicans were in power they would not be apologizing for the economy we have right now. They`d be crowing on every cable broadcast, on every hill everywhere in this country.\par \par \uc2 I remember when unemployment hit 4 percent when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in office and Kevin McCarthy was out there calling historic. Now the same Kevin McCarthy is calling 4 percent unemployment in this country a sabotage of American opportunity. Republicans would be crowing. Democrats in a measured way have to absolutely brag about the things that they have done and be clear about the way that Republicans have stood in the way progress for American working families.\par \par \uc2 Let`s stop apologizing for our success as Democrats. Let`s be clear that there is a road to go, yes, there are challenges here with inflation, but let`s also understand that in 2019 the economy was in far worse condition than the economy that we have today in 2022.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: Let`s be honest about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious what your thoughts are on this issue that I`ve noticed. There`s a lot of -- and this happens during presidential campaign seasons in like Iowa, New Hampshire, specifically, where one party is doing all this negative advertising against the party in power. But you`re seeing this in a lot of key states right now, in your Pennsylvanias, in your Wisconsins, where some of these -- Texas, where these primaries are very sort of advanced.\par \par \uc2 And there isn`t a counter message. Right? They may be attacking each other but they`re all sort of attacking Biden, too. Do you think Democrats should be responding more forcefully to this negative paid messaging that is all over the battleground states right now?\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: You have to respond in a fulsome way. I think President Biden is showing us that by uniting NATO and our allies against Russia and the Ukraine right now. But there`s a need to unite Democrats behind an oppositional message to the tens of millions of dollars of misinformation that`s being spread by Republicans in state after state.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Chuck, it`s interesting. If you look at all these contests, look at a place like Missouri, Republicans are really challenged with the kinds of candidates that are likely to emerge from their primaries, the fact that they`re still yoked to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s going to be a challenge for them and they have this kind of incoherence as they struggle still with January 6th and a national party that the Republicans have that calls that legitimate political discourse at a moment when Americans are repulsed by what happened then and continuing polarizing violence.\par \par \uc2 So there`s an opportunity to push back on the economy, on the state of our democracy, what it takes to hold this nation together to make progress. Republicans actually don`t have a coherent message and there`s a huge opening from the Senate playing field, the gubernatorial playing field and in Congress as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. It`s an important point. Democrats may be debating what the message should be. Republicans, I mean, even Mitch McConnell said we`re just running against them.\par \par \uc2 Patrick Gaspard, head of the \uc2 Center of American Progress\uc2 , good to see you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Democrats grapple with what to do about Russian sanctions abroad, election integrity concerns at home and the president`s political standing and the changing landscape of pandemic politics. Tim Kaine, at the center of all of these issues these days, as a U.S. senator from Virginia, he joins me after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:22]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we said before the break, it`s an understatement to say the White House has a lot on its plate right now. The same is true for Congress. Lawmakers are up against a midnight deadline to fund the government and prevent a shutdown before the long weekend. Senators could vote as soon as today on a nonbinding bipartisan resolution in support of Ukraine. It comes after weeks of failed negotiations on a Russian sanctions package and does nothing but express the shared feelings of discontent in the Senate that Russia should be punished further if aggression occurs.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, a member of the Senate Committee of Foreign Relations and Armed Services, it`s Virginia Democratic Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, I want to start there. I know a bunch of you are headed to Munich for the security conference tonight. Is that going to be enough to keep the lights on for the federal government?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Chuck, I think we`re going to get there. We will pass this continuing resolution for a few weeks. The good news about it is I think we`re now moving toward a breakthrough deal for the full appropriations bill, which will likely pass in March. That`s a very good bill on priorities like national defense, you know, to have a credible deterrent threat against authoritarian nations like Russia and China, the budget that we`re queuing up I think is going to do -- is going to be a strong one and we`re going to get this CR done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This sense of the Senate on Ukraine, does it look a little bit empty if there isn`t an agreement on the sanctions bill itself?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Chuck, let me tell you what there is agreement on and the one area of dispute. We agree on what the sanctions package should be, and we agree that Russia will suffer these sanctions very drastically if they were to invade Ukraine. And the third thing is we forged a consensus with our European allies.\par \par \uc2 The reason we couldn`t get a deal this week on the sanctions bill was because there was one area of disagreement. Democrats said we should do the bill and impose sanctions only if there`s an invasion. Republicans wanted to impose sanctions right now regardless of whether or not there was an invasion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: But the package itself is bipartisan, and we share it with our allies. If Russia invades Ukraine, they will suffer very, very dramatic diplomatic and especially financial and economic consequences.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to shift a little bit domestically, if you will, and I`d like to get you to react to what you hear out of the White House. They sort of - - they say essentially hey, we`ve got to have a more-I-feel-your-pain economic message. But look, some of this is -- there`s not much you can do, but you have to say.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it going to be enough?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, you know, Chuck, you and I have talked about this before. If I were Joe Biden for State of the Union, I`d be real candid and I`d say, look, this has been the toughest two years in my lifetime for this country. If you just look at the number of people who have died, who have gotten sick, economic, job losses, people who couldn`t see a new grandchild that was born in another state, people who couldn`t go to a funeral of a friend because there wasn`t a funeral.\par \par \uc2 This has been absolutely brutal, unlike any time during my lifetime. I think he should say that. But he should say, but look, there`s an American comeback coming on. We`ve got record job growth, we got record GDP growth, we have wage and salary growth that`s dramatic. We do have an inflation challenge, yes, we do. We`ve got to keep the other three trends going because they`re great and manage in the fourth trend and let me tell you how we`re going to do it.\par \par \uc2 So yes, he should acknowledge that the last two years have been brutal, but that Americans are comeback people. They like a comeback story. Joe Biden can give a comeback speech.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to make you do something here that probably you`re not going to be crazy about, but the San Francisco story this week, the recall of the school board folks.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: To me it reframes a little bit the Virginia governor`s race and what we saw at parental outrage and I think it`s a clear picture for people that this was not about critical race theory. But this was about frustrations at government competence, if you will. You know, can you get this done, can you do what you`re saying, can you take care of our kids? How should Democrats look at what happened in Virginia now given what we saw in San Francisco and those school board recalls?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, look, under Democratic leadership in Virginia, we have some of the best public schools in the nation, right? I mean, what do you want to look at? SAT scores, (INAUDIBLE) scores, the quality of our student performance, the quality of our higher ed institutions? Under Democratic leadership it was high. But look, the pandemic has taken a toll. It`s taken a toll on parents, taken a toll on kids, taken a toll on teachers and others.\par \par \uc2 And sure, there was huge frustration, and I`m not -- I don`t live in San Francisco but, you know, what I hear is overwhelmingly the voters like -- you guys are more focused on renaming schools than you were on creating a learning environment that was safe. And if that happens, yes, you`re going to find that local people will switch out their local leadership and they`ll put somebody else in.\par \par \uc2 What I worry about, and we saw this in Virginia, it`s one thing to say I don`t like what the Louden school board does, it`s another to send a letter to their house saying I`m going to kill you and your children. The violent threats against these poor school board members, most of whom are parents with kids in the school who want to run for next -- no money, no pay to try to do a good job.\par \par \uc2 The kind of violence and threats and insults against them really go beyond the pale. I hope we can rein that back in, but you have to acknowledge it`s been a real difficult time for parents and kids.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. And that gets -- let me close with sort of this debate that the Democrats, I just had a version of it. You seem to be where David Axelrod is. You`ve got to just sort of hit the moment here. Patrick Gaspard says, hey, you`ve got to cheerlead what you`ve done, too. Where do you come down?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I completely agree with Gaspard. I think if I were Biden on March 1, I would start off with let`s just acknowledge -- you know, he`s speaking almost on the two-year anniversary. I think March 4th might be the anniversary of the first American death to COVID, the two-year anniversary, but it`s essentially two years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And I think he should start just acknowledging the brutal reality of the last two years. It`s touched everybody, not everybody equally, but it`s touched everybody. But again, Americans like a comeback story, and I think there`s some -- there`s trends in Omicron cases coming down, hospitalizations coming down.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Vaccination rates going up. Those are positive trends. There are some positive economic trends. There is the tough one on inflation. We`ve got to grapple with it, have strategies to deal with it, but it shouldn`t be just a I-feel-your-pain speech, it`s yes, it`s been tough and we`re addressing it and we see some of those strategies starting to work and we`ve got more to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat from Virginia. Appreciate you coming on, and sharing your perspective. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: All right, thanks, Chuck. See you, man. Bye.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, an alarming new climate report warns that sea level rise is accelerating rapidly and that the American coastline could be hit with centuries worth of rising water levels in just 30 years` time.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:51]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. In less than 30 years Americans could see a foot more of water at our shores as sea levels rise at an unprecedented rate. It`s a sobering new report from NOAA, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Association. They released it Tuesday and it predicted that the sea level will rise as much by 2050 as in it did in the entire last century.\par \par \uc2 With the East Coast and the Gulf Coast predicted to see the most dramatic changes. At least a foot of sea level rise means this, that even without extreme weather coastal flood is going to become a major issue for the nearly half of the American population who live in coastal communities and for infrastructure. High tide is going to create a flood.\par \par \uc2 The report says we`re looking at another two feet of water by 2100. On the lowest end of the spectrum, by the way, and if we don`t lower emissions we could be looking at seven more feet of water flooding our shores.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to fully visualize this, but essentially rising sea levels, you think we talk about daytime flooding in Miami, we`re talking daytime flooding in your Charlestons, maybe you got sewer systems that will flood in Camden, New Jersey. That`s what we`re talking about here.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, in today`s "Meet the Midterms," we`re going to meet the impact of Texas`s new voting laws and the confusion it has caused with mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching "MEET THE PRESS DAILY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:44]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Early voting for the Texas March 1st primary is under way and voters are already feeling the impact of the state`s new controversial voting reform bill that was enacted last year.\par \par \uc2 Antonia Hylton is down in Houston following how the changes are impacting voters.\par \par \uc2 And Antonio, I`m just waiting for very close Republican primaries where Republican candidates are going to be upset at perhaps how many ballots got thrown away. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, it`s a whole new election landscape here thanks to SB-1. First it ended drive-thru voting and 24-hour voting. Those are two measures that were really popular here in communities like Houston where a lot of people who work a typical or really long work hours, it`s added new freedoms and movement for partisan poll watchers which is something that`s worrying some voters and some campaign and election workers who say they`re worried about intimidation and confrontation at polling sites over the next couple of days as we get closer to March 1st.\par \par \uc2 And there is this mail-in ballot change that has really confused folks here. And this is, if you are applying for your mail ballot or filling out your ballot and sending it back in, you have to fill out your exact identification number of Social Security number that you used years ago when you first registered to vote, and for elderly people who in some cases registered, say, 30, 40, or 50 years ago, many of them are finding they don`t remember which one they used when they first did that.\par \par \uc2 And I want you to take a look at the actual envelope for the ballot here. We have a sample. The information where you need to put in your identification number or your Social Security is hidden under a flap in very small writing. So what we`re hearing from the election administrator here is that a lot of folks here who are rightfully eligible to vote are struggling to fill this thing out, sending it in, completely forgetting or not noticing what`s here, and then finding that their ballots have been rejected. And there is grave concern. Take a listen to some of what she shared with me when I spoke to her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ISABEL LONGORIA, HARRIS COUNTY ELECTION ADMINISTRATOR: What we`re seeing for voters is changes to mail ballot law saying you have to add your ID numbers on there. They have to match a database that you probably haven`t updated for years. We`re seeing mail ballot applications rejected at a higher rate. Mail ballots being rejected and that`s just mail ballots. We are seeing an increase to our call centers, on voter confusion. If we reject the application, if we reject their mail ballot, their opportunities to vote are dwindling.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [14:00:06]\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: There are serious concern now that many voters are simply not going to be able to get their votes counted in time, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Antonia Hylton, with sort of the consequences of more government intervention if you will in a process that perhaps didn`t need anymore government intervention.\par \par \uc2 Antonia Hylton, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220218ei2h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 3, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Alexander, James Stavridis, Courtney Kube, Mike  Memoli \par \uc2 10,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TONY CAPACCIO, DEFENSE REPORTER, BLOOMBERG: What happened to his body? Was it carried away like what the U.S. did with Bin Laden`s body, or was it too badly damaged to do anything with? And two, an Administration official told reporters today that this had been planned for a long, long time, the President had been presented in December with a tabletop model of the compound or the building. Why was the raid now versus maybe two or three weeks ago or even a month ago? Can you give any light shed -- any light on that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Yes. His body was left at the site. As for the timing -- it is correct. This was a mission long-planned, months in the planning, as I mentioned to Jen, but look, you have to build enough contexts around the individual`s pattern of life and you have to factor in external factors that you don`t get to control, like the weather.\par \par \uc2 So lots of things go into making a decision about when you -- when you execute a rate of this complexity and this danger. And there were a lot of factors that had to line up to be just right, you have to, you know, make sure that your intelligence is solid, and that the individual that you`re going after, is in fact, at the location you believe him to be.\par \par \uc2 And that all the external factors, the -- not just the weather, but the ability to operate at that particular time of day and have enough visibility, all those things factor in so it was multiple factors. It all came together with such that this was the best window to execute this mission.\par \par \uc2 And I would remind you that it`s not like we were just sitting on our hands for those months. I mean, these, as you probably saw in the Secretary`s statement, I mean, these special operations forces conducted dozens of rehearsals to make sure that they could get this exactly right. OK. Mike Breast (PH).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, Mr. Kirby, thanks for taking my question. I`m hoping you can provide a little insight into where ISIS stands right now. The threat they pose between the rate at the prison two weeks ago, and Abdullah`s assassination, where do things stand both in Syria as well as global?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I`ll tell you what, they`re leaderless today and that`s a significant blow. This is not something that we believe ISIS is going to be able to just get over really quickly and really easily. That said, they are not the force that they were in 2014 as we all remember that their growth and rapid acceleration across Iraq and Syria, this is a -- ISIS is not the significant -- the threat of the same significant nature that they were back then.\par \par \uc2 But they still remain a viable threat and we`ve talked about that many times that this is a group that wants to reconstitute its strength, wants to continue to attack and kill and maim and terrorize. And Haji Abdullah was very much involved in trying to resuscitate the group and to grow their capabilities. They remain -- as we`ve said many times they remain dangerous, they remain a threat to our national security, to the lives of other innocents. And we`re going to -- as the Secretary said in the statement today, we`re going to stay at it. Travis Tritten.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS TRITTEN, DEPUTY MANAGING EDITOR, MILITARY.COM: Hi, John, thanks. A couple of quick questions. First, on the efforts to get civilians out of the building, you said that they had called out to them with a bullhorn. Can you say specifically how long they gave civilians to leave the building? And secondly, when we look at photos of the compound and we see the damage to the building, was that primarily from the suicide bomb, or were U.S. ammunitions used on that building? Thanks.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The damage you see in the picture with the third floor collapsed onto the second and even more than that, and that was caused by Abdullah igniting an explosive device, which as I said, killed himself and his family. And as for time, look, Travis, I don`t have a minute by minute. There were numerous calls made to encourage everyone in the building to leave. Numerous calls were made. And at some point, not too far into the operation, Abdullah exploded this device in that precipitated more activity by at least Just one shooter from the building that led to U.S. forces going in.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And to your point about you know how much time do we give people that -- I would remind that the U.S. forces actually extricated four children from the second floor after they went into the building, so the efforts to save the lives of innocence wasn`t just in the opening gambit here with the bullhorn. I mean, it was -- it was well into the operation, it was something that was ever-present on their minds. Mike Glenn.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GLENN, PENTAGON REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON TIMES: Thanks, Tony (AUDIO GAP) as well. But do you believe that this raid has prevented a future specific terrorist mission that they were going to carry out?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You were kind of breaking up, Mike. I think your question was, do we have information that his death is disrupted by a specific attack? Is that what you asked?\par \par \uc2 GLENN: Yes, that`s it -- yes, that`s it.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don`t have information that suggests that Mike, but I would remind you that this was the leader of ISIS and he was a very hands-on leader. He was involved in helping direct a lot of operations. So we have no doubt that his death will have a blow on ISIS and their potential to conduct future operations. I do want to remind you that they are still a threat and we`re -- and we`re not -- nobody`s taken, you know, a victory lap here. We`re going to stay at this. They still remain a threat, they still espouse this extreme ideology and this intent to kill the main to terrorize, and so we`re going to stay focused on it. Dan Lamothe.\par \par \uc2 DAN LAMOTHE, REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Thanks for your time. I`m hoping maybe we can draw you out and flesh out some details on some of these tabletop exercises. It sounds like they went back at least as far as December, involving you know senior commanders and that sort of thing. Can you layout a bit of what this looks like in terms of how far back it goes, who would involve, that kind of thing? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, Dan, I`m not going to be able to go into more detail than I already have. Again, this was months in the planning -- several months in the planning. It included actual physical rehearsals, as I said dozens by the Special Operations Forces as well as you can imagine, some tabletop planning as well. But I don`t have a tick-tock of exactly how all that process worked out. It was extensively planned, extensively resourced, and quite frankly, extensively informed over the course of several months. Jen.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: Sir, if I could take you to Russia, Ukraine, there`s a report that the U.S. has evidence that Russia has developed a plan approved at the highest levels in Moscow to create a pretext for invading Ukraine by falsely pinning the attack on Ukrainian forces that could involve alleged casualties in eastern Ukraine, but also inside Russia. It`s being reported by The Washington Post. Is that report accurate? Is -- has that intelligence been declassified? What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: What I can tell you is, first of all, you know, we`ve discussed this idea of false flags by the Russians before. We`ve made no secret of that. And we do have information that it is -- that the Russians are likely, do want to fabricate a pretext for an invasion, which again, is right out of their playbook. One option is the Russian government, which we think is planning to stage a fake attack by Ukrainian military or intelligence forces against Russian sovereign territory, or against Russian-speaking people, to therefore justify their action.\par \par \uc2 As part of this fake attack, we believe that Russia would produce a very graphic propaganda video, which would include corpses and actors that would be depicting mourners and images of destroyed locations as well as military equipment at the hands of Ukraine of the West. Even to the point where some of this equipment would be made to look like it was Western supplied Ukrainian into Ukraine equipment.\par \par \uc2 So this is -- and this is just one example that we can talk about today. We`re watching this across the board. We`ve seen these kinds of activities by the Russians in the past and we believe it`s important when we see it like this and we can, to call it out.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: This is being briefed on the Hill right now -- part of them. Austin and Milley on the Hill --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: They`re on the Hill in a classified briefing to talk about the whole spate of things we`re concerned about with respect to Russia and Ukraine. I won`t -- it`s a classified briefing, so I`m obviously not going to get into an agenda item with you here in terms of everything they`re talking about but they`re both up on the Hill communicating with members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 What we`re seeing writ large, and what we`re doing about it to help our NATO allies.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How do you know that it has been approved at the highest level of the Kremlin, this particular case?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I would just say that our experience is that very little of this nature is not approved at the highest levels of the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And by that, you mean Putin?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The highest levels of the Russian government. Kelly Meyer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY MEYER, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, NEXSTAR MEDIA GROUP, INC.: Hi, John thanks for taking my question. I have two if I can. You mentioned the mechanical issue with the helicopter. Are those involved OK? And any other issues we aren`t aware of or did everything run like clockwork. And the second, on the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  dismissing unvaccinated soldiers, are any of them, the ones deploying are on high alert to deploy to Eastern European allies around Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We`re free to the army in terms of who they`re discharging with respect to the vaccine mandate, but I suspect that there are privacy concerns there, Kelly, so I don`t want to speak for the army but I don`t know that they`re going to be able to give you that level of specificity.\par \par \uc2 It remains a lawful order. It remains a readiness issue. The vaccines work. And we want everybody to take them and to keep safe -- to keep their units safe. As for the helicopter crew, my understanding is that they`re all fine. As we said at the top, there were no U.S. casualties and that would include the helicopter crew. And it wasn`t a crash landing. They landed safely.\par \par \uc2 Again, it was a drive train issue that rendered that helicopter unusable for the rest of the operation. So obviously, it was destroyed, but the crews are safe and sad. Zowie, from AFP.\par \par \uc2 MISHA ZOWIE, AFP: Hello, sorry, sorry. Can you tell us how many helicopters and how many U.S. troops were involved?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, Zowie, I`m not going to answer that question, actually. Back when I opened up the press conference, I told you there`s going to be some detail we`re not going to be able to provide because we want to preserve our ability to continue to conduct these sorts of operations. So what I would tell you is that we had exactly the force levels and exactly the resources we needed to conduct this operation and I`ll leave it at that. Tara Copp.\par \par \uc2 TARA COPP, SENIOR PENTAGON REPORTER, DEFENSE ONE: Thanks, John. Following up on Bob`s question, can you confirm whether any sort of intelligence was taken from the compound, any computer drives, or cell phones? And then secondly, to follow up on Louie`s question, the decisions that were made in this raid, did they incorporate any lessons learned from the civilian casualty report done after the August 29 strike? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I`ve kind of addressed that second question before as we move yesterday, I mean, we always try to avoid civilian harm. This operation was a completely different character than the airstrike that happened on the 29th of August. We -- but in general, we always try to learn from previous missions, from previous operations. These are two completely different operations and the desire to avoid civilian harm was baked in months ago into this particular plan.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the decision itself to conduct a raid using special operations forces, speaks volumes of the degree to which the President was trying to avoid civilian harm, and, quite frankly, putting our forces at greater risk because of the -- because of the decision to make a raid like this. And I`m sorry, but I should have written your other question down and I didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 COPP: How about the -- can you confirm whether any sort of computer drives or cameras or anything else are taken from the compound that could provide actionable intelligence moving forward?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. Look, as I told Bob, I`m not going to get into talking about intelligence matters one way or the other. It is common practice when we conduct raids like this that we try to collect material that can help us and inform us in terms of our ability to disrupt future attacks and to conduct future operations. And I think I`m just going to leave it at that. Nancy Youssef.\par \par \uc2 NANCY YOUSSEF, NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT, WALL STREET JOURNAL: Thank you. I wonder if you could clarify a couple of points. On the explosion that happened on the third floor, does the U.S. believe that the ISIS leader was wearing a suicide vest or that was explosive separate from that, and was it one explosive? Can you tell us if there was any deconfliction that happened with any other nation? And can you tell us who will lead the review of any possible civilian casualties by the U.S.? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: There`s been no decision to do a review or investigation right now, Nancy. This -- again, this operation is not even 24 hours old. As the Secretary said, we certainly are willing to take a look to see if there`s any possibility that anything that we did might have caused harm to innocent life. But I have no investigation or review to speak to today.\par \par \uc2 As for the explosion, all I can tell you is what we`ve already said there was a large explosion early on in the operation on the third floor, which killed Haji Abdullah and his family. We believe he detonated that explosion. We cannot say at this time exactly what this device was. So I leave it at that. And then you have -- your first question. I`m not good at -- I got to write these down. What was your first question?\par \par \uc2 YOUSSEF: Just to be clear, I was just asking when you say that, that review, though, that you said the Secretary to be doing, I`m just trying to understand who would do that. And then the question was, was there any deconfliction on the other nations in the run-up to this?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: So look, we`re not calling -- we`re not calling for a review right now, Nancy. Again, this operation literally just happened last night. What the Secretary said in the statement was we were going to take a look and see if there`s any possibility that any action that we conducted might have led to the loss of innocent life. And if there is, then we`ll make decisions follow on that.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t want to leave you with the impression that there`s an investigation in the offing here. That`s not where we are at this point. As for deconfliction, I would tell you that appropriate -- deconfliction at the appropriate time was conducted in order to make sure that this operation could proceed safely. And I think I`ll leave that there.\par \par \uc2 YOUSSEF: Do you mean to rag them?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Appropriate deconfliction as happens in that part of Syria, was conducted in this case. See, Helene Cooper.\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Hi, thanks, John. Um, does this commando raid in Syria send a message of any kind of Vladimir Putin?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: This raid was not meant to send a message to any other nation, it was meant to remove Haji Abdullah from his leadership of ISIS. And to that degree, it was successful. And we are immensely proud of the forces that conducted this, planned it, resource it, executed, rehearsed for it. It says a lot about our capability in counterterrorism, our focus -- our continued focus on the ISIS threat and our ability to plan and conduct an execution of this magnitude and with this complexity with the -- with the utmost professionalism and skill, and quite frankly, secrecy. I`ll take a couple more. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did the U.S. forces, collect any information or documents, whatever materials from the building?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I`ve already -- I`ve dealt with this question before a couple of times. It is routine that we try to collect useful material when raids of this kind are conducted. I`m not going to speak with any specificity about this one. OK, thanks, everybody. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: You`ve just been listening there to Pentagon Spokesperson John Kirby giving as many details as he`s -- can to the raid that took place earlier today. He`s been briefing reporters, answering questions following the President`s remarks this morning on the raid that took out ISIS top leader and his deputy.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as President Biden`s in New York City at this moment. He`s addressing a major domestic issue that could pose a big political risk for Democrats in this year`s midterm, rising crime and gun violence across the country, particularly in our urban centers. We`ll have much more on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, we`re learning a lot more about this early morning raid by U.S. Special Operation Forces in northwest Syria. It resulted in the death of the current top ISIS leader, Abu Ibrahim al-Hashimi al-Qurashi. Al-Qurashi, who the White House and John Kirby have been referring to as Haji Abdullah, which is believed to be his given name, blew himself up during the raid, according to officials, killing himself and his family.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to a senior administration official, U.S. forces were on the ground for nearly two hours. Fairly early into the operation, al-Qurashi exploded a bomb, killing himself, his wife and children, and others in the building on the third floor as you`ve been hearing.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Pentagon confirmed there were no U.S. casualties. Some are drawing comparisons for the 2019 U.S. raid that targeted then-ISIS leader al- Baghdadi in which he also detonated an explosive, killing himself. Now, before he left for New York, President Biden delivered remarks on this raid and the casualties.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our team is still compiling the report, but we do know that as our troops approach to capture the terrorist, in a final act of desperate cowardness, he with no regard to the lives of his own family or others in the building, he chose to blow himself up not just to the vest, but the blow-up that third floor. This operation is a testament to America`s reach and capability to take out terrorist threats no matter where they try to hide anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now is Peter Alexander. He`s at the White House. Courtney Kube was in the briefing, and she`ll be joining us in a moment as she gets out of the Pentagon Briefing Room. And also in a moment, we`ll hear from former Supreme Allied Commander of NATO forces, Admiral James Stavridis.\par \par \uc2 Peter, let me start with you. Let me just ask it straight up. Did President Biden watch this happen in real-time?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The White House says that he did that he was in the situation room last night. They said he was monitoring it in real-time. So it`s unclear exactly how much of it he could see in real camera terms and how much did he and the Vice President and others were able to hear about. They were there throughout the entirety of it until the troops were safely off the ground. And we were told by U.S. officials who were in the Situation Room that before he left, he said God bless our troops as it relates to those U.S. Special Forces Operations -- operators on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning new details about the way this all played out. As we heard from John Kirby moments ago that they had drilled on this, they had been focused on this effort with rehearsals and with planning that dated back months. President Biden was shown a tabletop model of that safe house back in December and ultimately, it was on Tuesday morning of this week in the Oval Office that he gave the green light, the final go-ahead order to Secretary Austin, the Department of Defense Secretary at the Pentagon, as well as General Milley.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to this specific individual there, we`re learning more details about what happened. He was on the third floor as we have now learned when he blew up his suicide vest killing himself, his wife, and two of his children. The U.S. officials, including those at the Pentagon right now we`re going to great lengths to demonstrate what they did to try to lessen the potential for casualties among civilians.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, Chuck earlier today saying, that instead of an airstrike, they chose to do this as an in-person raid, we heard from John Kirby moments ago that there was a U.S. military translator or someone who was communicating with a loudspeaker to those in the building to come out, to give themselves up and ultimately that there were at least 10 civilians who were safely evacuated from that building, among them eight children.\par \par \uc2 On the second floor, that`s where the lieutenant to this ISIS leader had been living alongside his wife. We heard from Kirby that both of them engaged in a firefight with American Special Operations forces on the ground there, and at least one of their children is believed to have been killed in that firefight.\par \par \uc2 We heard from Secretary Austin a short time ago, Chuck, acknowledging that it is possible that some innocent civilians were harmed or even killed in this effort. But the details are pretty remarkable about this. At the safe house, we learned from U.S. officials that this ISIS leader, al-Qurashi, that he never left the home that he was basically commanding.\par \par \uc2 ISIS has global operations effectively through couriers. So a lot of new and remarkable details about it, not the least of which is that the U.S. forces there had to blow up their helicopter on the ground, not after a hard landing, but after landing, and it was determined unsafe to take off. They detonate it so that hardware wouldn`t get into the hands of anyone on the ground.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Peter, was he the operational leader of ISIS? Do we think he, for instance, made the call for the attack in Afghanistan, for instance, on our soldiers?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, it`s a good question, one that was posted earlier to senior administration officials and posed again right now to John Kirby. And while they`re not drawing a direct line between al-Qurashi and that awful attack that took place outside what`s called Abbey gate at the Kabul Airport last summer that killed 13 American service members, they do say that that was conducted by \uc2 ISIS Khorasan\uc2 , ISIS-K, as it`s commonly known, which is an affiliate of ISIS and ultimately he was the head of ISIS.\par \par \uc2 As we`ve heard from some of the top counterterrorism officials in this country right now, ISIS has affiliates or other offshoots at about three dozen countries around the world right now. There have been real concerns, Chuck, as you know, well, about a resurgence of ISIS in Syria and this individual al-Qurashi was effectively overseeing what was going on in both ISIS and Syria. It had tentacles that stretched around the world.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in Admiral Stavridis in here. So, Admiral, how would you -- this is -- it feels as if, if you look at what we`ve done with ISIS over the last 10 years, OK, or since we`ve dealt with them, we degrade them a little bit more, we degrade them a little bit more, we degrade them a little bit more, is this how to look at today? And we`re probably going to have another leader and there`s probably going to be more of this or is this going to be whack-a-mole for some time?\par \par \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS, MSNBC CHIEF DIPLOMACY ANALYST: It will continue to be whack-a-mole, Chuck. And that`s, you know, the nature of these terrorist organizations, as we`ve all come to learn all too well over the last 20 years since we embarked in these forever wars, these are shadowy organizations, new leaders will emerge, it`s quite inevitable. I can`t tell you the number of times we`ve killed the number two, or the number three of Al-Qaeda, which we used to joke in the Pentagon that the most dangerous job in the world was a number three guy at Al-Qaeda. We killed that position so many times but occasionally, you get a big one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: This is a big one. This is certainly not quite getting Bin Laden, but I`d put it in the top five kills we`ve accomplished, it will rock the organization. But to your point, another leader will emerge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney Kube, and that`s what I want to focus on here. Now, what in northwest Syria? On one hand, here`s somebody who`s been operating out of there for quite some time, does this at all create space in northwest Syria in a positive way?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So -- yes. I mean, so one thing we need to keep in mind is ever since he came into this leadership position, ISIS has not had its own territory. They have not had their caliphate. They`ve been trying to rebuild in that time, and they haven`t really been that successful.\par \par \uc2 Well, there have been pockets of times where they`ve been able to show that they`re rebuilding and that they`re regrowing, they have not been a successful terror organization under his leadership. So the question is, is there someone who comes up behind him who`s able to actually rebuild this terror network there in Syria, potentially across the border in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 No matter what the answer is, though, this is a significant blow to that organization. It`s not only a symbolic -- a big symbolic strike to them, but it also does have an operational piece. We heard from John Kirby that he was involved still in the operations of ISIS.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: He was specifically asked about the Abbe gate attack in Kabul airport, whether he may have been one of the people who was planning or was behind it. And he said, look, we don`t know about a direct connection. But the reality is, he was in charge of ISIS, he was controlling them and telling them what to do, so there is that correlation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney, I guess, the other question, though, is about what does this mean going forward? What did the -- what does the Pentagon think comes next with ISIS? And the other intriguing factor about this guy, apparently he was -- was he a full-on asset to the United States or just somebody that provided a lot of information after we captured him at one time?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Yes. So I mean, there`s not a whole lot that`s known about him, even when you look at there`s a Rewards for Justice page with a $10 million bounty for him that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has. But even without, we don`t know a whole lot. We do know that he was -- he was born in Iraq, he was actually captured, he was a member of the Baathist army under Saddam Hussein then he joined Al-Qaeda. He was captured, and he was actually imprisoned at Camp Bucca in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 And somewhere in there on the 2004 timeframe, that was where I actually met Baghdadi, after he somehow got out of that prison, he left Al-Qaeda and he joined ISIS. So yes, in that time, there are some reports that he provided information to the U.S. -- the U.S. military or to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  in some capacity in a way to make his life a little bit more pleasant there. We don`t know a whole lot about that but there have been reports about that in the past.\par \par \uc2 But that being said, you would think that we would know a little bit more about him, given the fact that he was in U.S. custody for some time in Iraq. But I think Admiral Stavridis makes an excellent point here when he talks about how you know, one of the worst jobs you`re going to have in a terror network is the number two or the number three person because those jobs don`t tend to last very long. It is -- the fact that he was taken out, that he`s the number one leader of ISIS, again, this is a huge counter- terror win for the United States, it was symbolic, it was a big win. But someone else will most likely come up after him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Admiral, that`s what I want to get at, you know, sometimes we outline, you know, we say ISIS and its affiliates. And some people here, is this some corporate structure, is this some, you know -- or is it really just affinity groups, and people that share a set of beliefs and maybe an ambition, and they don`t necessarily are operational together? I mean, help me out in understanding these disparate groups of ISIS affiliates.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Chuck, it`s a little bit of both as in there is a kind of hierarchical aspect to it and yet it`s pretty disaggregated. So, a way to think about it would be the mafia gangs, which we all know from "The Godfather," the five families of New York, all of that kind of thing, each of these elements. And here I speak of \uc2 Al Shabab\uc2 , for example, in East Africa or \uc2 Boko Hara\uc2  in Nigeria, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 There are similar groups in the Philippines, similar groups in Mali. They kind of pledge allegiance to the greater cause. They were very excited to be doing so during the absolute peak of the Islamic State, you know, kind of five years ago-ish when the Islamic State was rolling in tanks toward Baghdad. We all remember that moment. In that period of time, a lot of these affiliate groups pledged allegiance, they took formal names.\par \par \uc2 Theoretically they`re part of this idea of a caliphate with individual areas. So they`re not direct chain of command kind of entities, but they share information, they work together in the world of cyber and online recruiting, recruiting, proselytizing, conducting operations. They try to take glory from each other.\par \par \uc2 I`ll close, by the way, by saying this is a wonderful moment to kill this leader of ISIS, but look for a reaction, look for this organization, either it or one of its affiliates to try and do something dramatic in the days ahead. And I`m sure all of our intelligence and security agencies are focused on exactly that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I want to switch national security topics to the issue in Ukraine and the latest news, this intelligence report, and I guess, Peter, I want to start with you, that once again, and this appears to be second time now I think in the last few week where the U.S. is declassifying intelligence saying that they have evidence the Russian government has a plan to essentially create a false flag operation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What more can you tell us about this?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, we heard from John Kirby on this. He wouldn`t detail what`s happening on Capitol Hill right now, but we know even as we speak the top administration officials are briefing lawmakers about the circumstances related to the situation on the ground between the United States and Ukraine. We know in recent weeks, we`ve witnessed this, as you look at that satellite image there, this swelling operation or population of Russian forces now surrounding Ukraine, numbers that reach up to 130,000.\par \par \uc2 As administration officials have been saying in recent days, though not details that specific new reporting today about a false flag operation, it has long been their belief that Vladimir Putin and those at the top of the Russian government would try to create some sort of a false flag operation here, which is, in effect, a moment where they make it appear as though Ukraine is doing something aggressive, which requires Russia to respond so they can claim that any offensive, any assault, any attack, any invasion they take into Ukraine is a reactive effort, not an offensive one right now.\par \par \uc2 The president, the White House has said that the potential for an attack remains imminent, Chuck, as you heard, but they`ve now changed their language saying that imminent makes it sound like they think Vladimir Putin has made a decision. They don`t know that he has a made a decision what to do right now. They just know that he could. He has the capability to do so at any anytime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Admiral Stavridis, I`m curious what you make, and "The New York Times" got at this today, they sort of wrote it, though, from one angle. It looks like we`re really amping up the information warfare against Putin, making it clear. We`re declassifying things that maybe five years ago when you were there they wouldn`t have declassified. But they want to make it clear, they`re shouting it to the world what Putin is up to. Is this squeeze effective, do you think?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: We`ll know in time whether it`s effective, but in my view it`s the right thing do. This idea of shining a light on Vladimir Putin both personally and the oligarchs around him and the entire Russian system of control it tries to exercise in the, if you will, near abroad, these nations around his periphery like Ukraine, Armenia, Belarus, Moldova and so forth.\par \par \uc2 Shining a light on that, Chuck, helps rally the democracies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: It helps put pressure on Putin inside Russia to some degree when we`re illuminating his wealth, his ability to move funds around. And then thirdly, it is a warning shot to Vladimir Putin about the level of knowledge we have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter Alexander, Courtney Kube, and James Stavridis, really appreciate you helping us unpack this.\par \par \uc2 I want to move over to the president right now. He`s actually in New York City speaking about crime. He`s just been mourning those police officers in New York that were killed. Let`s dip in and take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to -- I spent 12 years going to school there, actually not 12, I started in third grade.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 At any rate, I went to a school, little Catholic school called Holy Rosary across some small police department and a fire hall. And everybody that I grew up with either became a cop, a firefighter or a priest. I wouldn`t qualify for any of them, so here I am. But I admire the hell out of all of you. I really do. I`m not being solicitous. I mean it sincerely.\par \par \uc2 And to the many parents, spouses, brothers and sisters, who had to bury a piece of their soul in a deep earth that`s really tough stuff. It`s hard as hell. You all know it, to lose a colleague, to lose a son, a daughter, a husband, wife. And I want to thank -- there are not many here, but I want to thank all the spouses of every one of you or your significant other because every time you pin that shield on and walk out the door, they`re worried whether they`re going to get that phone call, get that phone call.\par \par \uc2 Too many have gotten a phone call lately. And every day in this country, 316 people are shot, 106 are killed, and six \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers have been victims of gun violence so far just this year. The same in a town north of Philadelphia and my much smaller town of Wilmington, Delaware, Washington, D.C., 64 children injured by gun violence so far this year, 26 killed.\par \par \uc2 It`s enough. Enough is enough because we know we can do things about this but for the resistance we`re getting from some sectors of the government and the Congress saying the state legislatures and the organizational structures out there.\par \par \uc2 You know, Mayor Adams, you and I agree, the answer is not to abandon our streets. That`s not the answer. The answer is to come together, police in communities, building trust and making us all safer. The answer is not to defund the police. It`s to give you the tools, the training, the funding to be partners, to be protectors, and the community needs you, and know the community. You know police --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Police need to treat everyone with respect and dignity. That`s why I called on Congress to pass a budget later this year to provide cities like New York and others with an additional $300 million for community policing. You know, where the police interact with the community, get to know the community, build trust in the community.\par \par \uc2 And I have noticed in my experience when I wrote the first crime bill, I noticed that, you know, I don`t hear many communities, no matter what their colors or backgrounds, saying, I don`t want more protection in my community. I don`t know. I haven`t found one of those yet. And so I`ve asked the Congress to provide $200 million to invest in community violence intervention programs as well.\par \par \uc2 They work. They work. Where community members with credibility work directly with people that most likely to commit crimes or be victims of gun crimes. And they work. For example, in 2017, the program I`m going to see this afternoon, which sends people in the community to interrupt violence, to mediate conflicts, to de-escalate. Succeed in preventing a single shooting from occurring in this largest public housing development last year.\par \par \uc2 No shooting for a full year because they engaged directly with the community. You know, I know this is a priority for Senator Schumer, what you all are doing here. If I hear one more call from him that we need more money for housing and more money for cops, I don`t know, I`m going to send him back to y`all. But all kidding aside, this is a half a billion dollars of proven strategies, and we know we`ll reduce crime.\par \par \uc2 Congress needs to do its job to pass the budget. Every one of these folks here, a couple from Congress, are all supportive. But you know, it`s time to fund communities, community police, and the people who are going to protect them.\par \par \uc2 Look, as I said, we`re not about defunding, we`re about funding and providing the additional services you need beyond someone with a gun strapped to their shoulder -- to their hip. We need more social workers, we need mental health workers, we need more people who when you`re called on these scenes as someone was about to jump off a roof, and not just someone standing there with a weapon.\par \par \uc2 It`s someone who also knows how to talk to people, talk them down. We can`t expect you to do every single solitary thing that needs to be done to keep the community safe. It`s time to fund community policing to protect and serve the community. And so I`m also calling for increased funding for the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms\uc2  and the U.S. Marshal`s Office\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 I`m confident that if we fund these programs, we`ll see a reduction in violence. In the next year`s budget I`m also going to try to double down on this investment. I think I got a lot of partners here in New York going to help.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Adams, you say that gun violence is a sea fed by many rivers. Well, you know, I put forward a plan to dam up some of those streams. You know, you can count on me to be a partner in that effort. And I have the United States attorney general here with me today, and we`ve put together a comprehensive strategy to combat gun crime in cities like New York, Philadelphia, Atlanta, and many other cities, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 First, we want to crack down on the flow of firearms used to commit violence. That includes taking on and shutting down rogue gun dealers. And it`s about doing background checks as well as outright selling of making sure that people who are not allowed to have a gun don`t get the gun in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And again, this doesn`t violate anybody`s Second Amendment Right. There`s no violation of the Second Amendment Right. We talk like there`s no amendment that`s absolute. When the amendment was passed, it didn`t say anybody can own a gun and any kind of gun and any kind of weapon. You couldn`t buy a canon when this amendment was passed. And so no reason why you should be able to buy certain assault weapons. But that`s another issue.\par \par \uc2 And, look, one of the things that we focused on, the attorney general and I, and we`re getting to the point where I think we`re going to be able to have a real impact on it, includes going after ghost guns. Ghost guns are the guns everyone in this room knows that can be purchased in parts, assembled at home, no serial number, and can`t be traced. And they`re as deadly as any other weapon out there.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is they are out there. And, you know, this spring, the Justice Department -- this spring the Justice Department will issue a final rule to regulate these so-called ghost guns. But there`s more we can do. Across the country, police departments report sharp increases in the number of ghost guns found at crime scenes. That`s why today the department is launching an Intensified National Ghost Gun Enforcement Initiative to determine and deter criminals from using those weapons to cover their tracks.\par \par \uc2 If you commit a crime with a ghost gun, not only are state and local prosecutors going to come after you, but expect federal charges and federal prosecution as well. We`ve also created a strike force to crack down on illegal gun trafficking across state lines. As the mayor said, as he pointed out, guns have been used to kill people in New York City, they aren`t made in New York City, they aren`t sold in New York City. They are sold in other places.\par \par \uc2 Today the attorney general directed all U.S. attorneys in the United States to prioritize combatting gun trafficking across state lines and city boundaries. The Justice Department is sending additional prosecutorial resources to help shut down what`s referred to as you all know the iron pipeline that funnels guns from shops in states like Georgia to crime scenes in Baltimore, Philadelphia and New York and so many other places.\par \par \uc2 Governor, you worked with the mayor and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and nine other states to create an interstate task force on illegal guns. That`s the kind of leadership that`s going to solve the problem. And I think you`re going to hear more about that progress.\par \par \uc2 And folks, the second thing I want to point out is we want to help every major city follow New York`s lead, put together partnerships like this one you put together and meet on a daily basis. Every day here in New York City, like this meeting today, federal, state, and local enforcement meet to share intelligence about arrests, shootings from the day before, and work to take those shooters off the street as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 Just look around. This is what partnership looks like, and this is what you put together. And it`s an important partnership. We need more cities adopting the same model. That`s why today the attorney general is also directing U.S. attorneys to work with state and local law enforcement to strengthen partnerships like this one and to get repeat gun violence offenders off the street and behind bars.\par \par \uc2 You know, I want more cities and states to use some of the $350 billion we sent to them on the American Rescue Plan to fight crime to keep our communities safe, by hiring more police officers for community policing and paying police overtime and purchasing gun-fighting technologies like the technologies that hears, locates gunshots so there can be immediate response, because you know exactly where it came from.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:08]\par \par \uc2 The third thing our plan calls for investing in critical services that reduce crime and violence. Community violence intervention programs like the one I`m going to see after this meeting, summer school, after-school programs for teens. As the saying goes, teacher taught me, an idle mind is the devil`s workshop. We`ve got to have things for these kids to do, jobs for young adults, more school counselors and nurses. More mental health required in school, and mental health substance abuse treatment as well.\par \par \uc2 Fourthly, when someone finishes their time in prison, all our experience tells us you just can`t continue to give them 25 bucks and a bus ticket. They`ll end up under the same bridge they arrested him in the first place from. And so I don`t want them ending up back in prison or being there because they`ve committed another crime. We need to be able to train for and get a job, find stable housing, reenter society, and have a second chance at a better life.\par \par \uc2 My Department of Labor is funding programs to help formerly incarcerated individuals, including young adults receive the education and training they need and then connect them to quality jobs. I`ll keep doing everything in my power to make sure the communities are safer, but Congress needs to do its part, too. Pass the universal background checks, ban assault weapons and high-capacity magazines, close loopholes and keep them out of the hands of domestic abusers, weapons.\par \par \uc2 Repeal the liability shield for gun manufacturers. Imagine we had a liability -- they`re the only industry in America that is exempted from being able to be sued by the public. Only one. Imagine had that been the way with cigarette manufacturers. Where the heck would we be? We`d be in tough shape. Why gun manufacturers? Because of the power of their lobbying ability. It`s got to end, end. They`ve got to be held responsible for the things that they do that are irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, you know, it`s the only industry in America as I said that`s exempt from being sued. And I find it to be outrageous. And, folks, these laws, if we`re able to pass them, are going to save lives, and more equally importantly, help you protect one another and protect yourself, put law enforcement in a safer circumstance. We have an opportunity to come together and fulfill the first responsibility of government and our democracy to keep each other safe.\par \par \uc2 So I want to thank you all. There`s much more to say but I probably already said too much because a lot of people are going to speak. But let`s get this done. Let`s get this done. And God bless the men and women who put their lives on the line every single day to keep our communities safe.\par \par \uc2 Now I`m going to turn it over with your permission to the attorney general, Attorney General Garland. General?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thank you, President Biden. As the president said, the Justice Department is doubling down on the fight to protect our communities from violent crime and from the gun violence that often drives it. But we are not doing this work alone. As the president also said, what you see in this room represents the core of our efforts, our partnerships, which are essential to success in disrupting violent crime.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday when the president and I spoke with the mayor on the telephone, the mayor talked about the importance of the excellent cooperation he has seen between the city, state, and federal law enforcement agencies who are represented here. At the Justice Department, we know that the best anti- violent crime strategies are tailored to the needs of and are developed by and in partnership with individual communities.\par \par \uc2 That is why all of our Justice Department law enforcement agencies and all 94 of our United States attorneys` offices including the two represented by the United States attorneys who are here today are working with their partners in the state and local law enforcement to develop and implement district-specific anti-violent crime policies.\par \par \uc2 We also know that while many of the public safety challenges our cities face require this tailored approach, gun violence is a universal challenge and one that demands comprehensive action. That is why as the president said the Justice Department is taking action to crack down on ghost guns and to hold those who illegally sell firearms to criminals accountable. That is why we are strengthening our firearms trafficking strike forces to disrupt the pipeline that floods our community with illegal guns.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 And that is why we are sparing no resource in identifying and holding accountable the repeat offenders who are the major drivers of violent crime. As we work in partnership with state and local law enforcement, we are also working in partnership with the communities most affected by this violence. Shortly President Biden and I will be meeting with leaders of the community violence intervention program here in New York.\par \par \uc2 In 2021 the department provided over $37 million in grant funding directly to community violence intervention efforts, and we will expand that work in the year ahead. The Justice Department will use every tool at our disposal to protect our communities. We will hold perpetrators of violent crime and gun violence accountable. We will work alongside the communities most affected by that violence, and we will work together to build the public trust that is essential to public safety.\par \par \uc2 Before I close I want to note that in times of crisis, both large and small, the American people look to law enforcement to help. And every day officers like those who are with us here answer the call without hesitation. Despite the difficulty and the danger, they show up and they put their lives on the line.\par \par \uc2 That is what Detective Jason Rivera and Detective Wilbert Mora did. There are no words I can offer that could capture their bravery or the pain that has been inflicted on their loved ones. What I can say is that the Department of Justice we will seek to honor Detectives Rivera and Mora in the work we do to help keep our law enforcement officers safe as we work to keep our community safe.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll now turn the program over to Mayor Adams. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve been listening to the president and the attorney general. They`re in New York City today with the mayor of New York City, Eric Adams. You`ve heard the remarks on crime. In some ways the president trying to pivot almost the entire Democratic Party back to a place that they think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been more comfortable in politically when it comes to the issue of crime.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring Mike Memoli in, who of course our NBC News White House correspondent who has been following \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for years.\par \par \uc2 You know, Mike, we`ve been talking about the president trying to get his political identity back over the last couple of months. In his polling we`ve seen this is always one of those things, the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that I have covered over 30 years usually has an arm around a cop. And here`s \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  today taking on the defund phrasing, saying we`re not talking about that, we`re talking about -- but he`s talking about reforming police. How important is this pivot to the White House?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Oh, it`s critical, Chuck. I mean, the president there was speaking to a room that included obviously New York City and state leaders as well as the leadership of the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , some of the rank and file of the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , but the real audience for this speech is exactly what you say, the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 For President Biden, you`ve covered him a long time, too, you remember when he was vice president, he was the Obama administration`s ambassador to law enforcement. Whenever there was a meeting that involved law enforcement Biden was at the table. And a lot of the rhetoric we heard from the president there, we heard from him over the years, and in fact some of the policies that he was discussing, the White House in fact already announced frankly last summer, but they`re doing this in New York because Mayor Adams, the White House feels that they have an ally who has a similar sort of example for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Remember that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as chairman of the Senate Judiciary Committee, he even referred to the crime bill today that you heard him just a few moments ago. That was used against him by the nearly two dozen Democrats who were running against him in the Democratic primary, and as the Biden campaign pointed out, guess who won the nomination?\par \par \uc2 Well, Mayor Adams now -- now Mayor Adams ran in a crowded mayoral primary in one of the most liberal cities in the country, and he won that election in part because in New York there was an appetite to ramp up law enforcement as well. So the president is trying to send the message to his party to model the behavior he thinks that they should be running on.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, Chuck, as the political unit pointed out this morning, a lot of governors in tough races this fall are doing exactly the same thing in their state of the state addresses, getting on the funding, not defunding the police side of things and talking about stricter enforcement, not less.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike, I`m curious. It almost feels as if the election of Eric Adams gave the White House a permission slip to lean more in again.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 It`s almost they were waiting for, yes, they won the argument but they were a little nervous where progressives were, and then when Eric Adams won, and you see, you know, this is a guy who`s got approval ratings already pretty high and obviously he`s in a honeymoon period, and yet progressives are still ankle biting on him.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: And think about, Chuck, what Joe Biden`s experience politically is as well. His secret to success in winning seven statewide elections in Delaware was winning among black voters in Wilmington, but also among suburban voters in New Castle County. This is the message that he wants to make sure Democrats understand as the formula for victory not just in primaries as Adams showed here, but also in the general election.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Adams is a perfect illustration of what Biden talked about as he recalled his own experience in Wilmington, that African-American leaders are among those who are often vocal in wanting additional resources for law enforcement. Now some of the messaging has gotten complicated especially as progressives have maybe an outsized voice on social media and the like, but you heard the president twice there saying, distancing himself from the defund movement.\par \par \uc2 And Adams over the course of his mayoralty so far he`s called himself the Biden of Brooklyn. He said he`s my guy. So there`s a real kinship here that the White House couldn`t wait really to put on display publicly, and that`s what we see today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I am very curious. We`ll see if Eric Adams is that kind of icon for the Democratic Party, and in the fall, does he become a surrogate in some purple states and in some certain areas. It`ll be fascinating to watch if this issue takes on that kind of traction nationally.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli on the ground for us, as always, sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour, obviously a very busy day here in Washington, ended up in New York City. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220204ei2300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for January 25, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Cornell Belcher, Henry Gomez, Matt  Bodner \par \uc2 8,526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday, it`s time to meet the midterms as a flurry of intra-party fights feels discord and disconnect content in both parties. What does it mean for the President and our politics, with control of Congress on the line?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Ukrainian government, now urging call as more than 8000 U.S. troops have been put on high alert, ready to deploy to our NATO allies if Russia invades Ukraine. We got the latest from Moscow.\par \par \uc2 And later, clinical trials are getting underway for a new COVID vaccine specifically designed to fight Omicron as the FDA halts the use of two antibody drugs because they don`t work against this variant.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd. There is a lot of unrest inside both parties these days. Today`s headlines make it pretty clear from the left and the right, the current political climate with an unpopular President, unpopular parties, plural, donor classes that don`t match the makeup of the parties these days, and frustrated political basis. All of it is a recipe for some Bullough politics for the President, the parties, this year`s primaries, and perhaps November`s midterms.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Democratic groups are not happy with Senator Kyrsten Sinema for opposing an effort to change Senate rules to make it easier to pass partisan Voting Rights legislation. After Arizona Democrats officially censured Senator Sinema for her stance, the powerful Democratic PAC Emily`s list officially pulled support.\par \par \uc2 And last night a prominent Democratic activist group, Voto Latino launched a paid campaign down cedar called Adios Sinema for what they say is disloyalty to the party`s left flank.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats are fuming behind the scenes with White House Chief of Staff Ron Klain. They`re accusing him of steering President Biden into troubled political waters by being too beholden to the party`s left flank.\par \par \uc2 Senate Leader Chuck Schumer has tried in various ways to appease both the moderate and progressive wings of his party. A lot of Democrats aren`t very happy with him either and how he`s attempted to do that.\par \par \uc2 If the situation inside the Democratic Party is bad, it`s arguably worse inside the Republican Party, where the few remaining column moderates are sort of rational conservatives are trying to contain the spread of Trump`s influence in this year`s midterms as he pushes lies about our elections, and endorses candidates with an eye on loyalty, not electability.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, so much of the tumult in Washington right now is being driven by the instability of our two major political parties, which are both pretty unpopular, according to a recent NBC News poll. In fact, you have to go back to 2015 to find the last time either major party -- either party was viewed more favorably than unfavorably by registered voters.\par \par \uc2 And what happened in 2015? It`s right around the same time we saw the meteoric rise of two politicians who defied their party`s orthodoxies at the time, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  on the left, Donald Trump on the right. Coincidence and timing, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann Caldwell is on the ground for us in Phoenix with more of the situation with Kyrsten Sinema. Also with us is Politico`s Michael Kruse, who was just in Ohio, covering one of these inner party fights on the right side of the aisle, this one for governor of Ohio. And Maria Teresa Kumar, President of Voto Latino, that`s the group that plans to launch a new campaign to primary Senator Sinema in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Anne let me start with you on the ground there on the discord over Sinema and what it means for 2022, she`s not up till 24. There is a Democratic senator who is up and 22 and Mark Kelly, I got to think what the state party did only put him in a tougher spot. Leigh Ann, what do are learning?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, they definitely took different approaches to this filibuster vote and regarding voting rights. Senator Mark Kelly said that he voted to change the Senate rules last week, something that Senator Sinema did not do.\par \par \uc2 And it was interesting, people know that Senator Kelly is the one who came out with a new position, not that he really had a position before but he was undecided and Senator Sinema has been mostly consistent on this idea of the filibuster, at least for the past three years but it was Senator Sinema`s unwillingness to change is what caused a lot of problems on the ground here in Arizona, especially among the Democratic base who are furious with her.\par \par \uc2 Now, if it`s going to impact senator Kelly and his reelection, it`s hard to tell. It definitely draws a contrast between the two. And there`s some new polling that I got my hands on that -- by Oh Predictive that hasn`t yet been released that shows this divide between the two candidates.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 As far as the Democratic Party is concerned, Senator Sinema just has a 42 percent favorability rating among Democrats compared to 74 percent for Senator Mark Kelly. Now, I talked to the head of the Arizona Democratic Party who oversaw the censure of Senator Sinema over the weekend and she just talked about how she was representing the base of the party in the frustration that people felt. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAQUEL TERAN, CHAIR, ARIZONA DEMOCRATIC PARTY: Well, people knocked on doors, people put their confidence in her and we are mindful, you know that she did work towards the American Rescue Plan and the Bipartisan Infrastructure bill. But when it comes to protecting our voting rights, it`s something that people feel is very important. And so, the fact that she got in the way of seeing a Voting Rights bill move forward, was something that people have been very upset about and disappointed, and some outraged.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: So Senator Sinema, though, does have some support in the state, Chuck. There are Republican voters that we ran into on the street who say they love Senator Sinema. The business groups are like Senator Sinema, I talked to some components of the Chamber of Commerce, who says that she`s been so great for the business community.\par \par \uc2 And sure, that`s like, you know, she`s kind of maverick apparently like Senator John McCain, and that could help her in a general election but she has to get through a primary first in a couple of years. And Democrats right now are determined to defeat her in that primary, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if you don`t have a united party, I don`t care more popular than dependents. You know, it`s one of those -- it`s a tricky balancing act. You know, to win over independence, you got to look independent without alienating your political base. It`s a -- right now it doesn`t appear she has found that balance just yet.\par \par \uc2 Let`s look at the other side of the aisle, and somebody who was caught in a similar trap, but for different reasons. It`s the incumbent governor of Ohio, Mike DeWine. Michael Kruse, you spent some time you know, we talked about the Ohio Republican Party mess, and it`s mostly J.D. Vance, Josh Mandel, and that insane Republican primary that`s taking place on the Senate side.\par \par \uc2 But in many ways it -- the dynamics of DeWine and what`s -- in his primary race, actually seem like they`re emblematic of what we`re seeing nationwide as Trump gets involved in these primaries. Lay it out for us.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KRUSE, SENIOR STAFF WRITER, POLITICO: As you say, in Ohio, that Senate race is a sprawling circus-like primary in which most of the candidates are vying for Trump`s endorsement gets most of the attention. Right below, it`s the governor`s race between the incumbent Mike DeWine establishment brand new Iowa Republican politics, and Renacci is his top primary challenge, not his only primary challenger, but his top governor challenger.\par \par \uc2 And what we see happening there more and more is Renacci, who has been advised by Brad Parscale, the former Trump campaign manager (INAUDIBLE) himself as the Trump candidate. And I`ve been in Ohio, a pretty fair amount over the last year and you do get a visceral sense that not only is there real support for Trump still within the Republican base or a considerable portion by the Republican base.\par \par \uc2 Even more so, an anti-DeWine sentiment primarily because of these stiffer COVID restrictions and we`ve seen from many other Republican governors. I hear all the time when I`m in Ohio. Why can`t Mike DeWine be like lawn to save this? So that`s what you have at play here and Jim Renacci in the governor`s race is the one trying to take advantage of this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, is the -- is there venom against Mike DeWine or frustration on the right? Because it doesn`t seem like it`s angry the way you see it maybe level that Lisa Murkowski or Liz Cheney. Is it -- is it angry or is it sort of disappointing? How would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 KRUSE: Well, there isn`t sort of, in a way disappointment and anger just in general because of the last year because of the last couple of years at this point specifically with respect to the pandemic. But I wouldn`t underestimate the amount of felt anger that is on the ground in Ohio. When I met events of rightward activists, I do sense -- I do hear very real anger from them directed very specifically at Mike DeWine. The time for a lighter comes COVID Restrictions there in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He seems to be deciding though to stay underground. He`s not trying to poke the bear, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 You could say - I`ve heard complaints on the left for instance about Kyrsten Sinema that while they respect that disagree -- they would respect the disagreement for but they feel like she`s not -- she`s almost -- they would ask she`s trying to have a public conflict. He seems to be trying to almost avoid public conflict these days with his face, no?\par \par \uc2 KRUSE: Successfully it`s his point. He is -- he`s been doing the job and Trump, the former president has not gotten involved in a specific (INAUDIBLE) yet, nor has he gotten involved in a specific form of an endorsement just yet to the Senate race. I think what we`re seeing from Mar-a-Lago slash Bedminster is hands-off and he`s really enjoying watching Ohio do what it`s doing on an electorate. Hash these battles out all of which, of course, revolves around the enduring influence and in some respects, fear of the former president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Michael, it`s fascinating and we didn`t even get to the fact that in Ohio Senate candidate today, J.D. Vance actually was proud to announce that he was supported by Marjorie Taylor Greene. I think that just tells you everything you need to know about J.D. Vance`s about-face and about what he thinks, who he thinks Ohio primary voters are. It`s astonishing. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRUSE: It really --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Go Ahead, Michael.\par \par \uc2 KRUSE: It`s surprising. I went to J.D. Vance, not Josh Mandel, but we knew Marjorie Taylor Greene would get involved in the Ohio primary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s quite astonishing. Let me move over to Maria Teresa Kumar. And Maria Teresa, let me start with the decision you guys made? Why now? Why not wait until 2023 to start Adios Sinema?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, PRESIDENT, VOTO LATINO: Right. And I think, I mean, Chuck, you and I, we`ve known each other for a very long time. This is not a decision that the organization took lightly. It has everything to do.\par \par \uc2 And it -- you know, we could have political differences, policy differences, but right now, the fact that Senator Sinema, after deep conversations with her, decided and chose to go against her very own constituencies by not allowing a vote on Voting Rights, speaks volumes to the impasse that we face right now in this country. If we cannot secure that every single American, regardless of zip code is being -- had access to a voting booth, we have a fundamental breakdown in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Just now, we have the Arizona state legislator presenting the most draconian voter suppression laws that include in this legislation, Chuck, the ability for the state legislator to overturn a fair free election. That`s unheard of. And this is happening at her doorsteps. And if we can`t all agree on the same rules of the game to ensure that we all have equal enfranchisement, then we have to pick up our marbles and go home.\par \par \uc2 And again, this was not -- this was not done lightly. Voto Latino does not believe that this is what we should -- you know that we should do it unless we are trying to sound the alarm. And we are sounding the alarm that our democracy the state legislators at the local level are disenfranchising Latino voters, young voters, African American voters, Native American voters, and it`s a multicultural America. That is -- we felt the brunt of these laws, and she decided to go on to the wrong side of history.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you say though, that it -- and I`m not saying this as her -- she`s upset about the -- about the filibuster itself and doesn`t like the different how to go about changing this. But there are others that make an argument that everything you`re -- you`ve just talked about that needs to be done, these two bills didn`t directly deal with this issue and so that there was a little bit of a disconnect about what these bills do, and what needs to be done to sort of to protect from essentially -- we`ve got a referee problem in this country, right?\par \par \uc2 We know that essentially one party is trying to basically be the referee -- be the referee of their -- of the elections as well as a participant in the elections. These bills really addressed this -- I mean, these bills didn`t address this directly.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: -- I appreciate this conversation because when Voto Latino started 16 years ago, my job was to get people to register and to participate. It - - since the gutting of the Voting Rights Act, Voto Latino now has got into courts to talk to individuals and to actually challenge these court cases because they`re disenfranchising people left and right.\par \par \uc2 If we look at what`s happening in Texas, in Texas, the affair-certified election of 2020 at the hardest vote state has now a slew of disenfranchisement laws that we find ourselves having to basically file lawsuits against. If you`re turning 18 right now in Texas, and you are going to go on your college campus, you have to show a residency requirement, something that was not a factor just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 I registered a vote on my college campus. I`m sure you did as well, Chuck. That is just a way of trying to get young people involved. But it`s one after the other and it`s not like both parties are trying to do disenfranchised voters, it`s one party.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And what I would welcome right now is for a Mitt Romney or a Lisa Murkowski to be a patriot and say we need the Voting Rights Act again so that we could have an equal playing field. I`m not saying that the Latino vote should be out weighted more than the white voter, I`m just saying that we should all have equal access.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Answer this criticism, money spent now against Kyrsten Sinema does not help Mark Kelly in 2022. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I -- you know, someone asked me, are you doing -- are you still working in Arizona? I said I`m a mom. We juggle things all the time, we can do both things. And what this signals is that it tells the Latino community that we have someone that is not necessarily on their side, but it also opens the field so that other people can start building their war chest and start really considering throwing their hat in the ring.\par \par \uc2 Voto Latino is not just the one in the game. You mentioned, Emily`s list, we have local groups on the ground and we have other folks, even in the business community that recognizes that this is not tenable. And again, we could have policy differences. But what we have to commonly agree on is that everybody should believe in democracy and enfranchisement at the booth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If she somehow leads a bipartisan deal that gets some pieces of the Voting Rights Act attached to an electoral Count Act, which there`s some Democrats talking about, Jim Clyburn has been open to this, supposedly Susan Collins is open to this. If she`s a part of that deal, do you pull back this campaign? Do you -- do you sort of give her some credit for that or she lost --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: We have to be -- it has --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- She lost it? Yes, good ahead.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right. It has to be meaningful. I will share with you I mean, a lot of times what folks don`t realize is that a lot of the disenfranchisement right now is going through redistricting, the fact that we are packing young voters and people of color into these districts. Even Arizona which is considered of a commission of the -- of the redistricting is having a problem because a Republican governor is basically outweighing the individual so that it can be a fair exercise in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And so, people -- there are different ways of going about it. But my big thing is, and I would encourage everybody to really look at is making sure that we are taking getting away from the free of the gerrymandering, and that we`re accessing so that we could have universal voting. Let`s compete for America`s policy positions. Let`s compete and have to make sure that everybody`s participating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann Caldwell, Michael Kruse getting started, Maria Teresa Kumar of Voto Latino, I always appreciate getting your point of view and perspective on this. So thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, we`re going to continue this discussion in our next break. How will these inter-party divisions impact races this November and governing before November? The first ads and the first big Democratic primary, by the way, are already on the air.\par \par \uc2 We got a lot more Meet The Midterms next. And later, Senator Mark Warner will be here. He`s part of a bipartisan effort to try and get some electoral reform done. Plus, he is chairman of the Intel Committee. And we`ve got a lot to talk about the escalating crisis in Ukraine. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hope that, at least, once every Tuesday that I`m on the air between now and November, you hear that wonderful music. Welcome back. As we mentioned earlier, both parties find themselves in the same place ahead of the 2022 midterms. They`re underwater with the voters.\par \par \uc2 Going back to 2015, neither major political party has had a net positive standing with voters in our poll since. That`s where our politics is right now, two unpopular parties fighting it out with each other and amongst themselves.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my NBC News colleague, Henry Gomez. He`s following the early stages of the midterm calendar very closely, particularly in Ohio. Also with us is Cornell Belcher, Democratic pollster and an MSNBC Political Analyst, and former Virginia Republican Congresswoman Barbara Comstock.\par \par \uc2 Cornell, I want to start with you since you`re the pollster of our bunch. For seven straight years, both parties have been underwater. And one of the things that I like to show people also, and I think we have this as a graphic is, to look at the tumble actually with the American Electric over the last 20 years, all but once since 2004.\par \par \uc2 Have we gone through an election cycle without voting for some change, without changing at least the White House, House, or Senate? Only in 2012, did we have a no-change election with Obama-Romney and I think we all would go, huh, Obama, Romney, that suddenly the good old days of politics, right? Do you think it`s just a coincidence that the party`s both been underwater since the rise of Bernie and Trump?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: No, I don`t. But I also don`t -- I also don`t put it directly at the feet of Bernie and Trump either. I think there are some -- look, you will remember a guy by the name of Barack Obama, his speaking of 2012 was running on his idea that our politics was broken. And Washington itself was broken, right?\par \par \uc2 He was making an argument not about -- against Republicans but he was making a much broader argument. I think I could make the argument that most of Washington -- most voters think that Washington is broken, and it has been broken for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And you know, and I`m not even being partisan, but you know, they put the blame, as you see in your in NBC polling on both -- on both parties is a pox on both Houses. I think most Americans think that the problems in Washington are not necessarily partisan in a sense, structurally, there`s something that happens when people get here.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- and it`s bigger than just -- than just one party. I will note that also, look, both parties have been underwater for a long -- for a number of -- a number of years here but you know, Democrats were underwater, put seven points or six points in that NBC polling, but they still had one of the best midterm off-year elections in history there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, and this is what -- you know, Barbara Comstock, you`re the ones face voters of our trio. And you`ve talked to voters, and I`m -- you know, you`ve probably benefited from people not liking the parties. And then it punished you once you were an incumbent, feels like this is the world that an elected official, in the very rare thing that you represented a swing district has to deal with.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. BARBARA COMSTOCK, (R-VA): Sure, exactly. I ran in 2009 in Virginia`s General Assembly, and that was a referendum on Obama and we did very well in electing the governor. In 2018 when I left, it was a referendum on Trump. So the elections are usually a referendum on the incumbent.\par \par \uc2 And right now, Biden is very much underwater. The top issues which haven`t been discussed yet are inflation in the economy, first. Number two is a crime, and the increasing crime and the failure there in the cities and then -- and also in the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 And then education, were Democrats who have been you know, carrying water for the Teachers Unions for years, and I come from a family of educators, but they have failed these kids during the pandemic which is why you saw here in Virginia, Governor Youngkin advance and is now in place dealing with those issues.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So if you`re not listening to the voters which I think the previous discussion you had about Arizona and Ohio, those discussions, they weren`t really talking about what the voters want. And those are the top three issues that may favor Republicans now. I think in Ohio, Governor DeWine is going to -- he`ll do fine in that primary because he does listen to his voters and he`s very much in touch.\par \par \uc2 I think in Arizona if we`re fortunate enough for Governor Ducey who has done smart things like he`s having summer camps for kids to get back into school, and to all the time they`ve missed and gotten behind. He`s going to have summer camps and summer school for them. I think he would be the most popular candidate for Senate. And he has an independent streak just like Senator Sinema.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this, Barbara --\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: So I think that people stop looking at Trump --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: -- Republican side, they`ll do much better because Biden is giving them plenty of ammunition to run against him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you just sort of answered my question, which is, I think -- I was -- I actually think Republicans should have looked better in our poll. And I think the one reason they`re not right now is because of Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Henry Gomez, you`re on the ground there. How unsafe is it to be an incumbent versus being, you know, sort of part of the establishment? I mean, is that what this is, this unhappiness with the political parties? What do you see it as?\par \par \uc2 HENRY GOMEZ, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, that`s a very big part of it, Chuck. You know, there`s this mentality right now I mean, I don`t want to call it a, throw the bums out mentality. But people on both sides are really frustrated with the leaders they have. The one -- if you look at Ohio with Mike DeWine, which you talked about in the last segment, he should be OK.\par \par \uc2 But the reason that probably will be the case in the primary is that there`s more than one candidate running against him trying to run to that Donald Trump right-wing wing. If he succeeds not only because of that vote being split but also because he`s a prolific fundraiser and on quantity, he should be OK. He`s a little bit of an outlier.\par \par \uc2 But the one number that struck me in the NBC poll is the 50 percent -- the 56 percent of people who identify as Republicans who say that they are more supportive of the party than they are of Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I think once we get out of these primaries and cruise toward the general elections, that`s what we`re going to be looking at. And we`re going to be looking at races that are structurally a lot more like Virginia`s gubernatorial race last year between McAuliffe and Youngkin where Youngkin was able to sort of, keep Trump at a distance, except his endorsement, but not rally with him.\par \par \uc2 These people aren`t necessarily animated by the things that Donald Trump is saying and doing anymore and I think that`s what showed through in the poll. One other point, the voting issues, whether you frame it as Voting Rights or election integrity, and it kind of depends on which party you`re in, it`s not as (INAUDIBLE) concept just said it`s not one of the top two or three issues necessarily for Democrats or Republicans.\par \par \uc2 They want to talk about the economy, they want to talk about COVID, maybe we`ll be past COVID by the time the general election rolls around but these are issues that are particularly near and dear to Trump when we talk about the voting issues that may not, you know, we`re going to be hard to reach when we talk to voters on the campaign trail this fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Cornell, penny for your thoughts on the Kyrsten Sinema back and forth. You know, I understand the frustration, should this be this public? And is this harmful to the party?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, is this democracy, right? This is -- this is how democracy works. And it`s not always pretty but when people don`t like what`s happening, they let you know. And look, I`ve been trying to tell Democrats I needed them to listen for the last two years that Voting Rights is not a secondary issue consideration to the base of the Democratic Party, it`s in fact -- it`s, in fact, a first and foremost issue consideration.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re not paying attention to their issues, and following along with the issues -- and by the way, a lot of Democrats ran on the idea that they would do something about voting rights. You should not be surprised if you block Voting Rights from happening in their eyes, right? I`m not being one-sided about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: In their eyes. You`re the reason why Voting Rights isn`t happening. You shouldn`t be surprised that then they`re going to challenge you in a primary because quite frankly that`s how it`s supposed to work. I have to go back to one thing here, however, Chuck that I actually wasn`t trying to be partisan in this conversation. But Republicans do have to in fact run on something, right? And we outline so all the bad things about sort of Biden and the economy and all these other things. What exactly these Republicans are for?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I ask what happens tomorrow or something.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I -- it`s funny you say that. Cornell, though, I wonder do you -- that`s sort of the question I have here in our politics right now. If you`re the out party, voters are not punishing you for not running on something.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: And we -- if we allow it, right?\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Well, they run-- they`re running on.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: And that`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: -- that quite frankly, we have allowed it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, on Barbara.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: But if you do look at -- and NBC was reporting on this earlier. There`s a lot of Republicans right now, who were out there chiming on and running along with the fact that they were for -- that you know, they`re getting all these infrastructure dollars were not one of them voted for these -- for this infrastructure -- this infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Barbara, look, I know you`re going to make a case that Republicans are for something. That`s not how Kevin McCarthy is doing it. They`re both basically saying we`re not running on anything except against Biden, which is good politics but they`re not really running on an agenda here. It isn`t clear what they`re running on.\par \par \uc2 COMSTOCK: Listen, I think they`re running on the economy and inflation and kind of the worst inflation we`ve had since Jimmy Carter`s years. They`re running on keeping kids in school and education and coming up with innovative ways to make sure your kids are going to get that make-up of what they have lost over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 And then crime. Defund the police has been a disaster for Democrats. And that has given Republicans an opportunity to stand with their local police while still being for reform. Tim Scott, a Republican senator, is one of the leaders on having criminal justice reform while standing with the police. So I think you have the ability to be strong on those key issues. And then you have foreign policy problems that Biden has, too.\par \par \uc2 But I think the Republicans who focus on those issues instead of Donald Trump, put him in the rearview mirror, don`t let him muck out things, they`re going to do fine. And I point out Brad Parscale, if he`s running any one`s campaign, he knows how to raise a lot of money and he`s a loser. So don`t hire him.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a lot of grifters out there you should stay away from but I think you`re going to see a lot of Republicans who focus on the issues and not on Trump advance in these primaries and do well. Like Liz Cheney, like Lisa Murkowski, and that`s how we`re going to get good Republicans in office.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Henry Gomez, last word to you. There is a bit of a Democratic primary in the governor`s race. Not really one in the Senate race. Have you noticed the difference between not having a primary for a guy like Tim Ryan, and what kind of -- you know, how has he positioned himself, versus perhaps the two candidates that do have to worry about a primary in the governor`s race? Have you noticed any difference?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I have. I mean, Tim Ryan is going to a lot of places in rural and southern Ohio that Democrats haven`t been paying a whole lot of attention to over the last, you know, eight to 12 years here in Ohio. But he does have a primary problem. And I`ll leave with you this interesting tidbit. Morgan Harper, who`s the more progressive position toward the left candidate in the Democratic primarily. Because she can`t get Tim Ryan to debate her is debating Republican Senate candidate Josh Mandel later this week in a one-on-one debate that I think is -- I mean, you think you`ve seen it all in the Ohio Senate race then you see this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: So I mean, it`s kind of appointment viewing if you`re an Ohio politics nerd like me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, Henry, I think you should thank your lucky stars you have the Ohio beat this year and we`ll leave it at that.\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: Come visit. It`s going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think we definitely will. Cornell Belcher, Barbara Comstock, I appreciate both of you as well, as we bring a little political panel back to 2022.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as the Omicron surges begin to slow in some places, there`s now an Omicron specific vaccine that might soon be on the way.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Now for the latest on the pandemic, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and BioNTech just launched a study into a vaccine designed to target the highly contagious Omicron variant. More than 1400 adults are enrolled in the clinical trial. Participants will be broken up into three groups depending on their vaccine status. And they`ll receive one, two or three doses of the modified shot.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Pfizer CEO acknowledged that the current vaccine and booster regimen may not need to be tweaked in the end but a variant-based vaccine could prove more effective and more durable, which is what they really wanted discussed.\par \par \uc2 Also new in the world of drug makers, two popular antibody treatments from Regeneron and \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  are no longer authorized to treat COVID-19 in the United States. The FDA revoked authorization of both drugs yesterday because they say they simply do not work against the Omicron variant which now accounts for 99 percent of the country`s new cases. As a result, all of Florida`s state-run antibody treatment sites were forced to close today abruptly canceling appointments for thousands of patients.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Florida`s Republican Governor Ron DeSantis called the FDA`s decision, quote, "reckless." But the FDA says other more effective treatments are available and authorization of the drugs could return depending on future variants. What Governor DeSantis did not show up with was any scientific data to somehow push back on the FDA`s decision.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Ukrainian government is now urging calm as those on the front lines and the Western allies brace for a possible Russian invasion. What is Putin`s next move? And are we about to do something before Putin invades? We`re live in Moscow, next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:18]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. 8500 U.S. soldiers are now on heightened alert for possible deployment to eastern Europe. And the Pentagon is sending military aid to the region. This is video of that aid, which includes weapons and equipment. It`s arrived in Ukraine just a few hours ago. In a meeting with European leaders yesterday, the White House says President Biden and leaders reiterated their support for Ukraine`s sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is NBC`s Matt Bodner in Moscow. And Matt, it does appear that the U.S. and the allies continue to say this is in Putin`s court. There had been some hesitance by the U.S. and NATO allies to begin some military preparations for what Russia might do for fear of provoking Putin. But now they appear comfortable doing that. Does this have any impact on Putin`s timing?\par \par \uc2 MATT BODNER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I don`t think so, Chuck. So basically, for the record, Vladimir Putin has not actually spoken publicly on this issue about Ukraine at all since his end of year press conference in late December. He`s just kind of sent his messengers out. One of those, the Kremlin spokesman, Dmitry Peskov, we heard from him at his daily press briefing today saying that none of this has any impact on the negotiations because from Russia`s perspective, those negotiations, or at least the very first phase of it, is already completed.\par \par \uc2 What they`re waiting for now is a formal written response from the United States to that very heavy list of demands that they put forward. Those demands of course amounting essentially to the demobilization of NATO in eastern Europe. So they`re sitting there waiting for it and they`re very much giving the impression that they`re not really going to make their move until they see it.\par \par \uc2 But I`m not sure what they`re expecting because, you know, the United States is very clearly telegraphing, they`re not getting what they`re asking for, quite the opposite, if Russia came into this hoping they could somehow break NATO or push NATO out of eastern Europe, more NATO forces are moving into eastern Europe now. So we`re waiting now, not clear what they`re going to do when they get that letter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, Matt, I mean, how are they responding to the fact that Finland has shown some openness to wanting to join NATO. I mean, I sort of half joke that that ought to be the response of the European allies. Like OK, we`re going to begin the process with Finland and see how he reacts. I mean, is he at all aware that he may have actually helped NATO the way he`s gone about this?\par \par \uc2 BODNER: It`s definitely one of the most ironic kind of parts of all of this. But they haven`t really been on it. Basically Russia has fallen into this kind of standard play they do. The talking point is set at the top. Everyone down below just has to go out and keep repeating the message. Stay on message, they keep hitting the message. And so, you know, this is all in their minds, or at least what they`re telling the Russian public.\par \par \uc2 This is all Western provocation. Everything going on right now is all part of a grand U.S. conspiracy against Russia. Probably Finland and Sweden, if they joined NATO, it would be explained to the Russian people exactly in the same way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BODNER: Russia has kind of a bias as it approaches the situation where it simply assumes that everyone west of Russia is somehow completely under U.S. control. That`s just how they see it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BODNER: So that`s why they want to talk directly to the U.S. They don`t really want to talk necessarily to anyone else. It`s very much a bilateral game in their minds.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m sure the U.S. is trying to figure out how they control as much of Europe as Russia thinks we do. Anyway, Matt Bodner on the ground in what is always a snowy Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So what does the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  need to know next to keep the Russians at bay? Is there something they should do before the invasion? Senator Mark Warner, chairman of the Senate Intel Committee joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:37]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I read that the president was huddled at Camp David Sunday with his team. And what I`ve been hearing since them is encouraging, that they are prepared to take steps forward and encouraging not afterwards. I`ve not been near the news this morning so I`m not sure what additionally may have occurred but it appears to me the administration is moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was Senator Mitch McConnell earlier today at an event in Kentucky praising where he thinks the Russian policy is headed from the Biden administration. Some congressional staffers are apparently going to be briefed today on the situation in Ukraine. Aides to congressional leadership and committee staff will be briefed and all House members and senators will receive briefings of their own next week when they return to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone who`s likely already had many of these briefings, Senator Mark Warner, Democrat from Virginia, chair of the Senate Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 So I want to start with Senator McConnell`s comments. You know, there is this gang of eight that many of us inside Washington know where when there`s major national security decisions being made by a White House, the top two in the Intel, the top two leaders of the Senate, the top two of the House, top two of the House Intel, you guys get briefings a little bit before everybody else.\par \par \uc2 So I`m curious. What is Senator McConnell referring to specifically where he likes the idea that we`re going to act before potentially Russia acts? What is your sense there?\par \par \uc2 [13:50:08]\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Well, Chuck, let`s step back and look where we`re at. You know, a year ago this president came into a NATO that was not united, that Donald Trump had ignored for four years, and particularly over the last six months has built, the president and his team, has built up a unified NATO. That is not easy to do. These 30 separate nations with very divergent views. As you said earlier it would be great if we had as much control over Europe as our European allies as Putin assumed, and we have seen a ratcheting up of pressure.\par \par \uc2 And clearly, there was a challenge for a number of months even convincing the Ukrainian leadership itself that Putin was serious this time. Putin stays very isolated in terms of where he gets inputs and what we`ve seen in the last few days is we`ve seen, for example, the French troops indicating in eastern Europe, they`d be placed under NATO command. A Danish ship going into the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen the American government then the \uc2 British government\uc2  calling out what the Russians might be trying to do in terms of a false flag effort in Ukraine and the British even identifying who Putin might want to put in charge. The question about positioning of additional troops. This elevation is happening real time and yesterday the president had a previously unscheduled call with the presidents and prime ministers of all the European nations, all I think ratcheting up the pressure to say to Putin, you take up this effort, there`s going to be huge, dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By all these troop movements, I was just going to say, what do those consequences look like? What should Putin expect? And is this -- you know, does the nature of the invasion matter when it comes to the response the West has?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, I believe from our government we`ve been very clear that any physical incursion, for that matter destructive cyber incursion, will be met with the strongest of sanctions. Keeping that European alliance together on, for example, cyber. We`ve made great progress in the last couple of weeks. I think the next few weeks are absolutely critical. We know that Putin is going at least planning on going to Beijing for the opening of the Olympic Games. These next two to three weeks are the absolute most critical, and we need to keep ratcheting up.\par \par \uc2 We also need candidly that as the government as -- our government has said, you could see cyber incidents from Russia not only striking out against the Ukrainians but against Europeans and potentially against American companies and private interests as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You didn`t mention Germany. And, you know, what is your sense of Germany`s commitment to being as tough on Russia here as other key members of NATO? I mean, it looks like they are more concerned about their energy deficit with their decision to phase out nuclear than they are about Putin encroachment into western Europe.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Chuck, I wish Angela Merkel was still the chancellor in Germany and interestingly enough the Green Party is actually more adamant in who has the Foreign Ministry right now than the Social Democrats. But we have seen I think the evolution of Germany`s position, their willingness to be very clear that \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  would be shut down should Russia invade. There is enormous economic ties between Russia and Germany as we think about some of the sanctions on Russian banks.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the areas that I think we`ve -- that the administration has made huge progress and I think this is an area where Leader McConnell may be saying that he`s seeing movement in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me shift towards another thing that you`re involved with and that is reforming the Electoral Count Act. This could be the last realistic place to get some sort of election reform path. Let me ask you this, I`ve heard Jim Clyburn say, hey, is there some preclearance parts of the Voting Rights Act that could be including here? It doesn`t seem as if Mitt Romney is dismissive of it or Susan Collins. We know Lisa Murkowski is in favor of the Voting Rights Act so we know she`s there. Could this become something more than just the Electoral Count Act?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, let`s remember the electoral count was what, in many ways, led to the activities on January 6th because there was this false narrative that somehow Mike Pence could override and stop the certification of the election. So the fact that there`s bipartisan support for clarifying that makes enormous sense.\par \par \uc2 The fact that there is at least some of my Republican colleagues have said harassment of independent election officials.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Nobody is going to want to be a poll worker if they get harassed on a regular basis is a good step. But these good steps are not a substitute for the need I believe we have to take in terms of major electoral reform to make sure that people have a fair and equal access to the polls. On that third point, we still got a long way to go to convince our Republican colleagues. But when we`ve got 16 of us talking in a place that, as you know, Chuck, has been pretty much, you know, one team or another on these issues, I think that`s a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious of your thoughts on the state Democratic Party in Arizona censuring Kyrsten Sinema, and if that at all, what you make of that decision, appropriate? Whether you think that this is sending -- what message this sends to independents? So I`m just curious of your thoughts on this.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, Chuck, as you know, I pride myself on being somebody that tries to be bipartisan. I was a Democratic governor when there was a 2-1 Republican legislature. The only way I got things done was by working together. I`m a big believer in a big-tent Democratic Party. I disagreed with Senator Sinema on her position particularly on rules changes. I think that we should have changed the rules when it comes to protecting, you know, the idea that we`re going to protect the minority rights in the Senate but not protect the rights of minority Americans to vote doesn`t make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 But I am really hesitant to say to our big-tent Democratic Party we ought to be following the Republican lead in sanctioning members who, you know, eight times out of 10, nine times out of 10 clearly vote with other Democrats. That seems to be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: She votes with the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, she votes with Biden 99.7 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: As someone who --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is astonishing. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: It is. I have to tell you, Chuck, as somebody who occasionally broke with President Obama and then got accused of voting with him 98 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I`m proud of my support with President Obama. But, you know, some of these stats can be used in a variety of ways. So I think your point is well made.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair point there. Senator Mark Warner, chair of the Intel Committee. I know a lot of nervous days these days in eastern Europe. So I appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`re going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220126ei1p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for January 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): And it is an anniversary of a violent terrorist attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sen. Ted Cruz canceled for observing reality.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: The way I phrase things yesterday, it was sloppy and it was frankly dumb.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON, HOST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: I don`t buy that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the real world danger of disqualifying Republicans who oppose political violence.\par \par \uc2 Plus, can we just talk about the surprise appearance of Dick Cheney on the right side of history? Then --\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Courts are not politically accountable. Courts have not been elected. Courts have no epidemiological expertise. Why in the world would courts decide this question?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today`s Supreme Court arguments to overturn vaccine requirements during a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And Paul Krugman on the underappreciated evidence of a booming Biden economy.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I reject the idea that we should somehow punish people because they finally have a little more breathing room.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. There`s breaking news tonight about the January 6 Committee and Mike Pence. The chair of that committee investigating January 6, Bennie Thompson, told NPR the committee could send former Vice President Mike Pence a request to appear as early as this month.\par \par \uc2 "I think you could expect that before the months out." The Vice President was put in tough spot. The President was putting a lot of pressure on him to break the law and he stood fast. And because of his respect for the law, there were people who came to the Capitol a year ago, wanting to hang him. And so, for no other reason our committee really needs to hear what are his opinions about what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has now confirmed that reporting with a committee aid saying, "the chairman`s comments indicate the Select Committee is contemplating issuing an invitation to the former vice president sometime this month.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the Chairman said he was hoping to speak to Pence directly. The vice chairwoman of the committee, Liz Cheney, said yesterday she is looking forward to Pence`s "cooperation." Of course, it`s unclear if Mike Pence would agree to talk to the committee voluntarily, or if the committee would ever consider issuing a subpoena.\par \par \uc2 But there are a number of lingering questions the former vice president can answer. He was one of the few people with security detail on the sixth and those agents can provide more information about what the White House knew about the threat of violence.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps most importantly, Pence can also answer questions about conversations he had with Donald Trump in the days leading up to the insurrection. We know from extensive media reporting that Trump himself, along with his allies, repeatedly pressured Pence to enable his coup plot by throwing out tens of millions of votes on the sixth and handing the election to Trump instead.\par \par \uc2 Now, Pence who we`ve talked about here all week was the one man who may have most prevented in the final moment Donald Trump from destroying American democracy on a day an angry mob was looking to hang him and chanting so. But a year later, Mike Pence seems very much back in the fold or trying to be back in the fold downplaying January 6 as just another day, just another day where they were chanting to hang me. And sucking up the fringe elements now fully in charge of the Republican Party. And he is of course, is by no means alone.\par \par \uc2 And perhaps the most pathetic example of this we have seen is the sad Republican senator from Texas, Ted Cruz. He released an official statement that began "The attack of the Capitol was a despicable act of terrorism and a shocking assault on our democratic system." That was one year ago. He used that language again in a statement on February 13. "As I said repeatedly, what we saw on January 6 was a despicable terrorist attack on the United States Capitol. And again, on May 28. He said, "The January 6 terrorist attack on the Capitol was a dark moment in our nation`s history."\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s all over the past year, month after month after month. The day after, month after month. And then, on Wednesday this week, commemorating, memorializing this anniversary, Cruz said it yet again this time during a Senate Rules Committee hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: We are approaching a solemn anniversary this week. And it is an anniversary of a violent terrorist attack on the Capitol where we saw the men and women of law enforcement demonstrate incredible courage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, we should know that over the past year, Cruz has managed to make those occasional statements while simultaneously downplaying Donald Trump`s role in the insurrection and, of course, attacking liberals for being too obsessed with it. Of course, Cruz was also one of the key enablers and plotters of the coup on January 6. The only reason that it had a chance of working was senators joining the House in objecting to electoral certification. And he led a group of senators planning to object the electoral vote, voted to overturn the election results on that fateful day after the violent insurrection.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s be clear that Cruz was -- is on the side of the mob in its goals if not its means. All of that said, I should say for myself, terrorism is not the word that I used, tend to use to describe what happened at the Capitol. On this show, we tend to call it political violence or violent insurrection, which it was. But putting definitional questions aside, the important thing here about rhetoric from Cruz and other Republicans when they engage in it is simply to draw a line between what is and is not acceptable in political conflict, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:05:09]\par \par \uc2 What happened on January 6 was a mass mobilized violent attack. Five police officers who defended the Capitol died in the following days and weeks, 150 officers were injured with some sustaining brain injuries, one even being stabbed with a metal fence of steak. The insurrection was one of the worst days for injuries for law enforcement since September 11.\par \par \uc2 There was also, let`s remember, an attempted pipe bombing at the headquarters of the Democratic National -- Democratic and Republican National Committee`s. In fact, a year later, no one has been apprehended in relation to those bombs. In fact, we just found out that Vice President Kamala Harris was inside the DNC headquarters on January 6 when the pipe bomb was discovered outside the building.\par \par \uc2 What exactly would you call someone planting pipe bombs outside of two major party headquarters? Whatever term you want to use, the attempted insurrection was a violent act meant to overthrow a free and fair election to install a ruler over the will of the people by force. But of course, either tacitly passing over that fact or strenuously endorsing it are the only open positions allowed the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Vehemently condemning it, as you would think all sensible and decent people across the spectrum would is not allowed anymore. It`s not allowed. The litmus test for Republicans is that the attempted coup was all fine and good that whatever Donald Trump says goes and political violence is not to be condemned certainly when it is done by your side.\par \par \uc2 One of the people who enforces that line along with Donald Trump and his increasingly authoritarian political movement is the 8:00 p.m. host on the insurrection channel. Tucker Carlson seeing here posing with Roger Stone and members of the far right group the Proud Boys, attacks Ted Cruz for disparaging those finepeople who tried to violently overthrow our government that day.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know that Ted Cruz has a long, well-established and well documented thing for self-humiliation. And I mean, I don`t want to kink shame here, OK. He genuinely seems to enjoy it. He seems to seek it out. That`s his -- that`s his business. You may remember, when Cruz endorsed Donald Trump in the 2016 campaign after Trump insulted his wife and accused his father being involved in the Kennedy assassination.\par \par \uc2 So, in that regard, it is not at all surprising that Ted Cruz went on Tucker Carlson show last night the night after Tucker attacked him to humiliate himself, to grovel, to apologize in a mealy mouthed fashion and get walloped.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You call this a terror attack when by no definition was it a terror attack. That`s a lie. You told that lie on purpose, and I`m wondering why you did.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Well, Tucker, thank you for having me on. When you aired your episode last night, I sent you a text shortly thereafter and said, listen, I`d like to go on because the way I phrase things yesterday, it was sloppy, and it was frankly dumb.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I don`t buy that. I don`t buy that. Look, I`ve known you a long time since before you went to the Senate. You`re a Supreme Court contender. You take words as seriously as any man who`s ever served in the Senate. And every word you repeated that phrase, I do not believe that you use that accidentally. I just don`t.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And so, Tucker, as a result of my sloppy phrasing, it`s caused a lot of people to misunderstand what I meant. Let me tell you what I meant to say. What I was referring to are the limited number of people who engaged in violent attacks against police officers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, we cut it off before he says, thank you, sir, may I have another. But Ted Cruz still had not had enough. So, he then proudly tweeted out the video of that interview along with some extra sniffling. He said in part, "I was not calling the thousands of peaceful protesters on January 6 terrorists. I would never do so. I have repeatedly explicitly said the opposite denouncing the Democrats shameful efforts to do so and to try to paint every Trump voter in America as terrorists and insurrectionists." OK.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind, the violence on January 6 was not an accident. It was the point. I really feel like people haven`t quite like gotten their heads around this. Like, oh, how did we let that happen? How did Donald Trump let it happen? No, no, no, no, it was the plan, OK.\par \par \uc2 According to a former aide, Donald Trump initially refused to include the phrase stay peaceful in the tweet he sent while the mob was descending on the Capitol. You refuse to say stay peaceful. Why would he refuse to say stay peaceful? Well, because he did not want them to be peaceful. He didn`t want to tell them to be peaceful because he didn`t want them to be peaceful, because he wanted the insurrection to work.\par \par \uc2 He wanted the mob to occupy the Capitol so as to overthrow the fairly and democratically elected government and install him. The violence was the point. And supporting that violence is the litmus test for those remaining in the party.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Chris Murphy of Connecticut put it plainly in a tweet replying to Ted Cruz. "The scariest thing about this is it`s now 100 percent clear you can`t win the Republican nomination for president if you oppose political violence."\par \par \uc2 [20:10:07]\par \par \uc2 And he is right. We are seeing that play out all the time. This is not just high-profile Ted Cruz with his humiliation fetish, OK. Even at the lowest levels of the Republican Party, a Republican state senator in Wisconsin who had the audacity to say that the election was not stolen and to oppose a Republican phony investigation into the results, has now foreclosed the possibility of her future in the Republican Party. Today, she announced she is not running for reelection. She says she was not forced out, but one of the leaders in the election investigation recently called on her to resign. So, you know, you do the math, no career for you.\par \par \uc2 Opposing the big lie, opposing the insurrection, condemning political violence whipped up by Donald Trump, they are not allowed in the Republican Party. That`s where they draw the line, opposing political violence where your side is behind it and Donald Trump is in favor of it is not allowed. So, Ted Cruz is a lot of things, but his decision to do what he did yesterday shows that he understands where the future of the Republican Party lies.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Ruben Gallego is a Democrat representing Arizona seventh congressional district. He`s spoken extensively about his experience on the sixth and he joins me now. You know, I spent a lot of time around politicians, and there`s a lot of things politicians do. I think there`s a lot of stereotypes in them that are unfair, that they`re shifty, that they speak out of both sides of their mouth. They do one thing in public and one thing in private. They`re ambitious and conniving. I`ve never seen anything quite like Ted Cruz in terms of embodying these. But what do you make of what he did?\par \par \uc2 REP. RUBEN GALLEGO (D-AZ): I mean, he is the ultimate craven politician who will do anything, sell anything, you know, not defend anything that matters to him, provided that it, you know, ultimately leads them to his whatever aspirational goal is, which I think is still president.\par \par \uc2 But look, you know, this is a problem that is across the Republican Party, they`ve lost all sense of direction. They`re no longer a party of policy and ideas. They`re a party that is just a cult of personality. And if you are a good Republican elected official, you have to stand by Donald Trump, and you have to basically align with him.\par \par \uc2 And part of that is the sheer denial of the fact that there was political violence, that there was terrorism, you know, on January 6. It`s a sad statement for Cruz. And you know, I`ve been in politics now for 10 years. It`s just not worth it. It`s not worth selling out your family like he did when he sold out his dad and his wife. It`s not worth selling out your own soul, especially for someone like Donald Trump. And to grovel at the, you know, at the knees of somebody like Tucker Carlson, it`s definitely not worth it. I would rather be out of politics than have to do something like that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I remember -- you know, I once -- I had a friend once who ran for office, and I remember having a conversation with him before he ran where we were going to, like, make a list of things he wouldn`t do or say and put it in a safe when he went to run so that we -- and we would have the key for it that we could go and like, take it out and be like, you can`t do that, buddy. And I think someone should do that there.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: It`s your family that does that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: It`s usually your family that does that for you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, or someone, some sort of intervention. But your point here -- I mean, Cruz is sort of ridiculous example, but like this is now -- this is the litmus test. I mean, I think what you saw a year ago was people felt like they could say we can -- this was horrible. And the Senate -- I remember the Senate Republicans tweeted out like, this is not who we are. There`s a lot of like -- now, granted, the majority of the caucus in the house that you serve with voted for the coup, OK, but they could at least condemn the violence.\par \par \uc2 Even that now condemning violence, it seems to me important thing if you`re going to run a liberal democracy in which conflict is resolved through nonviolent means, even condemning the violence now is not allowed.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: Again, like because the violence points directly at Donald Trump, it is a direct attack on Donald Trump. So, if you are attacking -- if you`re saying this was a violent coup, or if it was a violent act, you`re basically saying that that was pushed by dear leader. And when you can`t attack dear leader, dear leader is perfect, right. So, that`s what`s happening right here.\par \par \uc2 This all points back to the cult of personality that is surrounding Donald Trump. And it is being reinforced by the unified media messaging that they have there through Fox News, through, you know, their particular, you know, blogs, you know, telegram lists, WhatsApp lists. And what`s happening more and more is that because these decisions are being made in primaries, the Republican Party is becoming even more extremely because, you know, moderates are just leaving the Republican Party and not participating in primary.\par \par \uc2 So, this thing is going to keep evolving. The way you stop this -- we`ve seen it pushed back here in Arizona is you have to stop -- you have to start giving them ultra-losses and prove to the point where extremists can`t win. And then, hopefully, there is some type of fixing.\par \par \uc2 Now, the problem you have here is that Donald Trump controls the message and the money of the Republican Party, so it`s going to be very difficult for them to do that until Donald Trump is defeated one more time.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:33]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, Cruz is one figure, Kevin McCarthy strikes me as somewhat similar figures. I mean, you know, he has gone from, I think, you know, saying the President bears responsibility to, you know, going kissing his ring. And now essentially -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: I mean, Kevin McCarthy is that -- is Ted Cruz but just not as smart. So, he is -- he`s the -- you know, he`s the dumber version of Ted Cruz, still empty shell of a human being, just as crazy as a politician, but just not as smart. But he will do anything to become Speaker of the House. He`ll align himself with QAnon crazies, you know, Matt Gaetz who is, you know, pretty close at some point to hopefully be indicted for being a pedophile.\par \par \uc2 You know, this type of person that he is, and he`ll do it because he wants that title of Speaker of the House. And you know, he`s just again, the dumber version of Ted Cruz in the House.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is your judgment of Mike Pence and all this who it looks like the committee is going to ask to speak to voluntarily this month.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: Look, I think Mike -- the former president -- former Vice President Pence needs to understand he`s not going to be president. He`s not going to win a primary because Donald Trump is never going to support him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: And so, he should, you know, go and follow up on the integrity he showed on January 6. I was on the floor on January 6. I waited until the final vote was done and I went up and shook his hand. I`ve disagreed with him a lot. And -- but, you know, that day he showed some integrity, and I was going to commend him for that.\par \par \uc2 He should solidify his reputation and, you know, if anything, cleanse his reputation from being so in line with Donald Trump. And, you know, that`s exactly what I would be doing. Sorry. I have a little guy that just popped it in.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, good. Oh, we love -- we`re big fans of little guys. Hey, buddy. Well, good --\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: I got to buy him a dessert after this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, you just offer good advice and for any children within earshot about -- earshot about how to conduct yourself. Congressman Reuben Gallego, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: Thank you for your time too.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You don`t have to be that old to remember the pretty dark legacy that Dick Cheney earned during his tenure in the Bush administration and the less than flattering nickname that went along with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON, FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Vice President Cheney came up to see the Republicans yesterday. You can always tell when the Republicans are restless because the Vice President`s motorcade pulls into the Capitol. And Darth Vader emerges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s kind of an amazing turn to see people like Nancy Pelosi embracing Darth Vader on the floor of the house last night. I have the perfect guest to talk about Dick Cheney sudden appearance on the right side of history next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So here`s a plot twist -- plot twist I did not see coming. In the season five finale of this national nightmare ahead of the one year anniversary the January 6 insurrection, Karl Rove, the Republican architect of George W. Bush`s political success, wrote an opinion piece in The Wall Street Journal on the Republicans January 6 responsibility.\par \par \uc2 In it he writes, "To move beyond January 6, 2021, we must put country ahead of party. There can be no soft-pedaling what happened, no absolution for those who planned, encouraged, and aided the attempt to overthrow our democracy. Love of country demands nothing less. That`s true patriotism."\par \par \uc2 OK. The twists do not end there, though. Yesterday, as the House commemorated the one year anniversary of the insurrection with a moment of silence, the only Republicans in the floor observing the moment were Congresswoman Liz Cheney of Wyoming and her father, former Vice President Dick Cheney.\par \par \uc2 As they made their way around the Capitol, Vice President Cheney was asked his thoughts about the Republican response of the insurrection. It`s a bit hard to hear but you can follow along with subtitles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What is your reaction to the Republican leadership`s handling of this, of the reaction to January 6?\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s not a leadership that resembles any of the folks I knew when I was here for ten years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right, everybody, thank you very much. I appreciate it. Come on, guys, we need to stay. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you disappointed with the way that they`ve treated your daughter?\par \par \uc2 D. CHENEY: My daughter can take care of herself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Dick Cheney, Karl Rove back in surprise appearances as our democracy hangs in the balance. So, to make these former Republican leaders with I got to say truly vile pass, trying to step in as a moral conscience for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Bart Gellman is a staff writer for The Atlantic who literally wrote the book on Dick Cheney in 2008, which is titled Angler: The Cheney Vice Presidency, now covers democracy and the threat to it for the Atlantic. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 My first thought was about you, Bart, because I read your book, right when it came out. It`s a fantastic biography. And I had been thinking watching Liz Cheney like, where`s the old man on all this? And we got our view. What do you make of his appearance yesterday?\par \par \uc2 BART GELLMAN, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: Well, the first thing you got to say is he`s a really good dad. I mean, he`s there as the father supporting his daughter. Now, it happens to be that they both have been minority whip of the House of Representatives. And he`s been standing back watching her career and letting her do her thing.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s clearly loathe Donald Trump. And he, you know, he did a lot of things that were terrible for the country. He made a lot of big mistakes, but they were always sincere. He`s a -- he`s an ideologue. He`s a zealot. But he truly believes what he`s saying. And he truly believes in the virtue of what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things he believes in apparently is the constitution. In fact, one of his final acts as Vice President was to go do the USS Constitution up in Boston, the old wooden fighting ship and swear in some Navy Seaman for realism and he had them do the oath right there with him.\par \par \uc2 He`s there for his daughter. I don`t know whether he or she think that she has a way back politically having made the stand she`s made but -- and they`re standing up with it.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:42]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, my sort of take on this is A, the Bush people and people around Bush genuinely loathed Trump personally, viscerally, hate him. Hate him because of the way that he talked about George Bush and Jeb and we`ve seen this over the years. I got lots of you know, Bush people, you know, hate Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a sort of personal animus. There`s a principal objection to what he is and what he did. I think Liz Cheney truly believes the January 6 was horrifying. But I also wonder like, you know, so many people in the Republican Party had basically gone broke shorting Trump`s stock. But at some point, somewhat like, it can`t last forever, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, I think you got to be thinking if you`re Liz Cheney that if the thing blows up, if it does come to some sort of end, someone is going to be there to pick up whatever`s left. And I got to think that`s part of at least the strategic political thinking as she negotiates the situation.\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: True. And she`s young enough to ride this out. I mean, her dad is in the Trump and Biden generation. He`s walking pretty slowly. He`s only one year older than Biden. But she`s got time. She`s got time if the Republican Party falls apart, and has to remake itself, and someone standing there and saying, you know, I told you not to go down that road. But we`ve seen quite a ways off from that at the moment.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that`s the thing. I mean, I do think the primary that she`s going to get in Wyoming is going to be a real showdown over this. And obviously, there`s someone who`s incredibly ambitious. I mean, she primary incumbent Republican in Wyoming for Senate and last for basically no reason other than her last name was changed. She wanted to be a senator.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean there`s going to be a lot of -- that fight will end up being a kind of fascinating war of the roses proxy fight, you know, between I think that -- the sort of Cheney-Bush world and MAGA world.\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: Yes , I think that`s right. I mean, look, she got real risk in the primary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: Because of the primary electorate but -- and because of who turns up at primaries. But in a general election, she would easily be the favorite with all Republicans and Democrats voting. She`d be easily the favorite to win again, whether she gets past the hard core and the kinds of loyalists who turn up at the primary is a different question. And I don`t know why I`m in politics well enough to tell you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. She may have to pull Murkowski there. We`ll see what happens. Bart Gellman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Statewide elections are inundated with Republican candidates who`ve embraced Trump`s bogus election frauds, including races for governor, attorney general, secretary of state. So, what happens if they win? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:32:39]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The now concerted effort underway right now to co-op elections of local level across the country.\par \par \uc2 As the Washington Post reports, at least 163 Republicans who have embraced Donald Trump`s false 2020 election plans are running for statewide positions that would give them authority over the administration of elections, that includes 69 candidates for governor in 30 states, as well as 55 candidates for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , 13 candidates for state attorney general and 18 candidates for Secretary of State in places where that person is the state`s top election official.\par \par \uc2 Trump tried to overturn the will of the voters in at least six battleground states in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, it was only through the integrity of the officials in charge of those elections, some of them Republicans, that he did not succeed. So, those are the stakes at the local and state level right now.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by officials from frontlines of two of these vital battleground states. Jocelyn Benson, the Secretary of State of Michigan, and Josh Kaul the Attorney General of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And Josh, let me start with you with what`s going on in Wisconsin, which has not quite gotten the amount of national attention as the, you know, ridiculous fraudulent audit that happened in Arizona. But you have Republicans in your state undergoing their own kind of investigation of the 2020 election as we speak right now.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAUL, ATTORNEY GENERAL, WISCONSIN: That`s right, what`s happening in Wisconsin is different than what unfolded in Arizona, but equally harmful. And it`s doing a similar thing to perpetuate the big lie. It`s being led by a former state Supreme Court justice, who has made a number of wild accusations about what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 But so far, it hasn`t proven anything. And we`ve had a number of reviews in Wisconsin and time after time, they`ve shown what we all know and have known since the election happened, which is that we have a safe and secure elections in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Secretary Benson, talk to me about how the sort of authority of elections flows in a state like Michigan, and who the people are at these sort of local levels, and how much who they are matters to preserving the integrity of the system itself.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN BENSON, SECRETARY OF STATE, MICHIGAN: Well, thanks, Chris, for having us and really again, highlighting this very critical matter for all of us tonight and this whole year.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, we have a very decentralized system of elections, which is good because it ensures that we have over 1,600 clerks administering the process elected or appointed at the local level.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:04]\par \par \uc2 And then, as the state chief election officer, I help and oversee and support those individuals. But then, after the election is done, we have a similarly decentralized certification process, where partisans appointed in the canvassing boards for every county (AUDIO GAP) at the state, review the details of the election and certify it. And that is how an election results become finalized.\par \par \uc2 And the interesting thing that`s happening in Michigan that`s also happening in different ways around the country, is that the Republican appointees, particularly in Wayne County, where Detroit is and at the state level, who essentially did the right thing with integrity, follow the law and certified our election results in 2020 have since been replaced by individuals who believe falsely that the 2020 election results were inaccurate, the election was stolen and everything else about the big lie.\par \par \uc2 So, we are facing a potential challenge in future elections where you have people in charge of certifying elections who don`t seem to believe in democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I want to come back to Attorney General Kaul in a second. Let me just follow up with you on one more question about that.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, not to get too into the weeds of Michigan State of election law. But, you know, if you`ve got these bipartisan boards, right, they essentially have these kind of ministerial roles, like, from their administrator, right? We counted it, these are the votes, we looked at the spreadsheet, we made sure there`s no errors, boom, stamp it.\par \par \uc2 If you have essentially people on those boards who are like, no, we don`t like these results. We`re not certifying this, what happens?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: We go to court to force them to do their jobs and follow the law. And that`s exactly what will happen if that occurs in future elections, it`s what happened (PH) in 2020.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. OK. That`s right. That`s the recourse and Attorney General Kaul, you talked about the sort of this investigation, and Secretary Benson just talked about, you know, Republicans that sort of, you know, upheld integrity last time being weeded out.\par \par \uc2 Today, where it was news, we mentioned her in the top, Kathy Bernier, who`s a GOP state senator, who`s been quite critical of that investigation in your state, said she`s not running for reelection. And it seems pretty clear that`s because that view has placed her outside the acceptable boundaries for the Republican Party in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 KAUL: You know, Senator Bernier is the one Republican in our state legislature who has had the courage to stand up and call out these attacks in our democracy. She`s also the chair of our Senate Elections Commission.\par \par \uc2 And so, when she leaves office, she`s going to be replaced by somebody who hasn`t been willing to stand up to these attacks.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s really important to follow up on the point that Secretary Benson made, which is that since the attacks in the 2020 presidential election, and since the insurrection, rather than seeing the Republican Party step back and realize they had gone way too far, we`ve seen efforts to purge Republican officials who did the right thing in 2020. And of course, there are going to be efforts to unseat the Democratic officials who stood in the way of those attacks on our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And our system worked in part because people who were in those key positions did the right thing. But 2022 is going to have an impact on what happens with those positions in the future.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is the path forward here? I mean, the thing that seems most key here is the position of Secretary of State, Secretary Benson, where, you know, you would be the one, right, so in that -- in the hypothetical effort (PH), right, it would be your office bringing suit.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the absence of that someone else would have standing probably to bring that same suit. But those positions, I mean, really seem crucial and important heading into these midterms.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Oh, yes, the ballgame. I mean, in Michigan, I`m fortunate enough to have a great partner in the Attorney General`s Office, where we can work in concert to defend and protect democracy.\par \par \uc2 And unsurprisingly, we`re both being challenged up for reelection this year, being challenged by advocates of the big lie.\par \par \uc2 So, certainly, these statewide roles are pivotal. And democracy is ability to survive in the future will be determined by who wins and loses this November.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think it`s important for our citizens to know they have the power to either reject a proponents of the big lie or support, in some cases reelect those who`ve stood guard on both sides of the aisle to protect their vote, their voices and their democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we should -- we should be clear here that I think there are -- there are -- there exists Republicans who are perfectly trustworthy to run an election. It`s just a question of whether they can make it through a primary in this situation.\par \par \uc2 Michigan Secretary of State Jocelyn Benson, Wisconsin Attorney General Josh Kaul, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Thanks for having us, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, you don`t hear this really anywhere. But I`m going to tell you, there really is a victory lap to be taken over the booming American economy. And today, Joe Biden did just that.\par \par \uc2 Paul Krugman on why the American Recovery is going so much better than you may think, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:44:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To the most morally indefensible aspects of the political right during the COVID pandemic have been its increasing celebration and embrace of anti-democratic methods and means, and its nihilistic vaccine skepticism and outright denial. Both of those traits on full display today, when Donald Trump`s six-three Supreme Court heard oral arguments on Biden`s vaccine mandate for large employers.\par \par \uc2 We witnessed firsthand why Mitch McConnell did everything in his power to get conservatives on board with Trump in exchange for the ability to remake the judiciary as a tool of movement conservatism.\par \par \uc2 Because you see back in 1970, both Houses of Congress passed the Occupational Safety and Health Act with overwhelming bipartisan support. It was then signed into law by Republican President Richard Nixon, which established the Occupational Safe and Health -- Safety and Health Administration, better known as OSHA to oversee worker safety.\par \par \uc2 And according to the law, OSHA, "Shall declare an emergency temporary standard to take immediate effect if employees are exposed to grave danger from exposure to substances, or agents determined to be toxic, or physically harmful, or from new hazards, and that such emergency standard is necessary to protect employees from such danger.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:15]\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m not a lawyer, but that seems pretty cut and dry. OSHA is obligated to take emergency measures to protect employees from substances, agents or hazards that put workers in grave danger.\par \par \uc2 Like say, oh, I don`t know, a highly infectious virus that`s already killed 830,000 Americans.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 Federal action to fight COVID. He won that election by seven million votes, he won the Electoral College and then he used his victory to take that action within the scope of the OSHA law, again, passed by Congress and signed by President Nixon.\par \par \uc2 But the thing is, conservatives don`t like it. So, guess what? They`ve got their own little supervisory legislature we now call the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 It`s a court the solid Trump majority, which now looks at to overturn the vaccine requirement for large employers.\par \par \uc2 In an emergency appeal session today, the audio of which was broadcast live, the conservative justices made it clear what they thought of Biden`s plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: The 20-year-olds who are unvaccinated are actually safer than the older workers who are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, there are obviously some differences. Would you just talk about how efficacious the vaccine is in the workplace?\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: So, now, full vaccination might not just be the two jobs, it might include a booster as well.\par \par \uc2 So, when does the emergency end? When must OSHA actually resorts to its regular authority and go through notice and comment and not simply be kind of doing it in this quick way, which doesn`t afford people the voice in the process that they`re otherwise entitled to?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: You`re saying that Congress acted, don`t complain the Congress hasn`t done anything. And that, you know, that was 50 years ago that you`re saying Congress acted. I don`t think it had COVID in mind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There hasn`t been any official decision by the Supreme Court yet. But it sure sounds like this handful of right wing judges are just going to legislate from the bench and be very activist and quite open to swooping in to tell the duly elected president that despite the authority explicitly granted to the executive branch under the OSHA law, his mandate is nonetheless 