ersary of the January 6 insurrection that American democracy is in danger?\par \par \uc2 What are they going to do about it? Tim Kaine of Virginia will be here ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 One day after the President`s fiery address about defending the U.S. democracy, the administration`s focus has yet again had to lurch back towards issues surrounding the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Cases, of course, are continuing to skyrocket, the seven-day average of new daily infections is now above 600,000 and that has pushed hospitalizations to levels that we haven`t seen since the worst of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 It`s maybe mild for most people, but it`s still triggering hospitalizations. Why House being -- is being urged by outside advisors to retool the message to focus more on ways for the U.S. to live with this virus given its spread and frankly, our unwillingness to do more about it as a society.\par \par \uc2 CDC Chief Rochelle Walensky said this morning that our agency is preparing for the COVID endemic, which would suggest a looming pivot in terms of public health policy.\par \par \uc2 But then, the President responded to questions from reporters about whether his messaging to Americans should be COVID is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 His response, no, but then seem to clarify that, well, yes, COVID we`re going to deal with it now does not mean it`s here to stay, and yet, it`ll be around the world.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the question surrounding the administration`s legal powers around vaccine mandates are before the Supreme Court today.\par \par \uc2 The court has been hearing emergency appeals this morning challenging the administration`s vaccine or testing remedies, a soft mandate, not a hard one.\par \par \uc2 Vaccine or testing requirements for large employers, in addition to the vaccine requirements for certain health care workers.\par \par \uc2 So the COVID confusion, questions, testing shortages, and all the disruptions that is caused are now layering on top of one another, from hospitals to schools and workplaces, airports, you name it.\par \par \uc2 So what should the administration`s current strategy look like and what powers do they actually have legally and politically? Well, Supreme Court`s going to let us know soon, we hope.\par \par \uc2 With me now is our own Carol Lee outside the White House, Ken Dilanian is outside the Supreme Court, the hearings are still going on right now, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Sharona Hoffman is a Professor of Bioethics at Case Western Reserve University and a Co-Director of Case Western Law Medicine Center.\par \par \uc2 Carol, I want to start with you because, you know, another day and its sort of I don`t want to call it mixed messaging, but sort of it does seem the White House and the President got -- is trying to straddle a fence of they`re going to pivot to learn to live with COVID strategy but he doesn`t want to sound like he`s totally given it up on an ending this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, and Chuck, from the President`s perspective, or the White House`s perspective politically, you know, that pivot is a little hard to do given that he had declared our independence from the virus earlier last summer.\par \par \uc2 And so this is a bit of a crisis for the White House, for the administration just in terms of public confidence in their messaging and their ability to handle the virus.\par \par \uc2 And look, this was a President who said that he would come in and he and his team would fix all of this.\par \par \uc2 And what we`ve seen is repeatedly these mixed messages and a lot of confusion largely coming out of the CDC and there is frustration, obviously, with the White House, towards the CDC.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also confusion coming from the President himself at times.\par \par \uc2 And so the question I think now is whether or not just a CD -- headedness, CDC turn this around and try to fix this, can they do it with this particular CDC director, and how does -- how does that happen?\par \par \uc2 And there are some tests that are -- that are faced -- the White House is facing in this respect that could either compound this issue or maybe set them on better footing.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned that the Supreme Court issued -- but also they`re supposed to send out these tests to homes and that involves obviously a heavy lift just in terms of getting 500 million tests out to individuals who want to get that them.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 They have to set up a website, that hasn`t always been the government`s you know, the best thing that they learn how to -- know how to do.\par \par \uc2 So whether they can clear those sorts of hurdles and try to get this on a more stable footing where people can have confidence and what they`re saying is a really real open question now and there are some things ahead that will determine whether or not that can be the case.\par \par \uc2 But this is a President who promised that he could do that and so he set the expectation, and I think people are expecting him to meet it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 Look, I want to play something that Rochelle Walensky said today on the Today`s Show on you know responded to questions, why the guidance seems to be murky.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: You know, we have the CDC, our 12,000 people who are working 24/7 following the science with ever-evolving nature in the midst of a really fast-moving pandemic, and we are doing so putting our head down to keep America safe.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to update, we will continue to improve how we communicate to the American public. This is fast-moving science.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carol, I think the question I have is, is the CDC have a messaging problem or does the CDC and the COVID Task Force and the White House, is it their own communications problem, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I can`t figure out what this is, is the CDC not able to put the guidance out that they want because, say we don`t have enough tests -- at- home tests, or are they just unable to communicate it very well because the White House isn`t giving them any guidance on that?\par \par \uc2 LEE: I think, Chuck, it`s a -- it`s a little bit of both. I mean, there is some folks have sympathy for the CDC because of what this -- the Director just said, which is, this is something that`s fast-moving.\par \par \uc2 And look, anytime that you have a virus like this, that where things change over time, you have to come back in and make adjustments.\par \par \uc2 And so the problem that they have is that that -- while that may be true, they seem to be making adjustments very quickly.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if -- they announced something and then you know, the following week have to clarify like their isolation guidelines, for instance, and you hear different things from different phases of the administration where Dr. Fauci came out and said that they were going to fix it, they were going to clarify this, whereas the CDC Director was had dug in on saying that they didn`t need to test after those guidelines.\par \par \uc2 So, some of this is self-inflicted and some of this is also just the nature of a problem like this, means that they`re going to have to revise some of the things that they`re putting out as they learn more.\par \par \uc2 The problem is they don`t have the confidence from the public to make those revisions and have people believe that that`s -- they`re necessary and that it`s not just the CDC making a mistake again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I don`t know how this happened but they were supposed to help raise up the CDC`s profile when they took over.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s not good that Late Night television is making CDC the butt of jokes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: It`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That is not a good place to be, the White House should be a lot more concerned, CDC should be concerned about that as well.\par \par \uc2 Let me move to the Supreme Court arguments there before Ken gets blown away. I know you are out there, my apologies of these gusts of wind, I`m sorry. There is a better situation there. So these arguments have gone on a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Based on what you`ve been able to hear, is this a court that is giving the benefit of the doubt to the federal government as history says it will, or are the challengers being -- have a chance here to stop this?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: It`s a little bit hard to read, Chuck. But there has been some skepticism expressed by some of the conservative justices, particularly Justice Roberts. So you would expect to be a compromiser, a swing vote, you know, very sympathetic to executive authority.\par \par \uc2 He was pointed in his questioning of the government`s lawyers about why this approach -- why this piecemeal approach, a rule from the Occupational Health and Safety Administration, a separate rule for healthcare workers, a separate rule for the military, he asked why can`t Congress just impose a broad vaccine mandate?\par \par \uc2 And, of course, that`s not practical, it`s not going to happen and the government responded, look, we have the authority to do that.\par \par \uc2 And Roberts came back, well, yes, it`s a 50-year-old law I don`t think they envisioned Coronavirus back then.\par \par \uc2 And that there`s never been this kind of broad vaccine mandate imposed on all American workplaces because recall, this is a rule that would apply to every employer of more than 100 people.\par \par \uc2 And what the opponents came in today and said was, look, this is overly broad and it`s going to cause us to have to fire workers, workers are going to quit at a moment where they`re already is labor shortages and supply chain shortages is going to cause significant economic disruption.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And they cited the example of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  which is under a separate federal vaccine mandate, which had to suspend their own vaccine mandate because they said they would have had to cut back on train service if they implement it on time.\par \par \uc2 So this is a perilous issue and it looks like it may be in a little bit of trouble at the moment, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ken, do you did --are you able to denote any difference between the two challenges? Is there any, you know, the health care worker mandate got more open -- openness on with the conservative justices or not?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Well, you know those arguments have only just gotten underway, but --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got it.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: -- They -- that -- they do have a stronger hand to play there because that`s involving the spending of federal money and strings attached to that, so it`s a slightly different legal principle.\par \par \uc2 The government is funding this healthcare and saying, you know, you`re taking our money, therefore, we`re imposing this vaccine mandate and they do seem to have a little more leeway to do that there.\par \par \uc2 But, they -- also, in that case, the Conservatives are expressing skepticism about -- essentially what they`re saying is COVID it`s not just a workplace issue, it`s a thing that affects everybody.\par \par \uc2 So is the workplace mandate, that the right solution here?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ken Dilanian, get out of that wind, get out of these weird conditions, thank you, sir. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in Sharona Hoffman from \uc2 Case Western University\uc2 . So you just -- I know you`ve been following the arguments as well, our reporter there, Ken, is that the sense you got here that there`s a bit more skepticism from the conservatives, including John Roberts, who`s sort of our I guess, the person that`s right in the center of the court these days, is that the -- is that the read you had there and do you see this going in that direction?\par \par \uc2 SHARONA HOFFMAN, PROFESSOR OF BIOETHICS, CASE WESTERN RESERVE UNIVERSITY: It`s hard to tell. This is certainly an unprecedented mandate. Usually, public health mandates come from the States and there is a long-standing precedent that the states have the right to impose vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 So this is a new issue, there certainly is skepticism. I have not heard the argument about the health care worker case so I can`t --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, it`s just started.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: -- can`t touch that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I get here. Yes, it`s just starting. So, I did hear some skepticism but it`s not clear because this is a matter of life and death. And OSHA is a powerful administrative agency that has done lots of things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me play for you what the Ohio Solicitor General argued about saying these mandates are too broad and I want to get your response to it. Here`s what Ben Flowers said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEN FLOWERS, OHIO SOLICITOR GENERAL: OSHA could regulate COVID-19 in the workplace when the employer does something, like attacking individuals very closely together in a poorly ventilated area that enhances or changes the nature of the risk, I should say.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not the risk they say they`re regulating. Again, 61,411 of the Federal Register, they say the risk is you`ll come into contact with individuals.\par \par \uc2 And the risk of encountering an individual is an ever-present risk we face at home, at work, and everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Seems to me he seems to be arguing that it is too broad and that there could be a federal government mandate, say it sounds like for meatpacking plants or for a certain amount of people that work in the same room. What do you make of that argument?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, this disease is airborne, there`s a risk anytime you`re in a room rather than outside and so it is a workplace risk. So it`s not clear that there`s a better fix.\par \par \uc2 If you really want to eliminate the work -- the risk in the workplace, you do have to take broader measures and the vaccine is the most effective measure that we have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In your mind, is there a difference in the mandate between mandating a vaccine and mandating weekly testing?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, a vaccine is a lot cheaper and it is an effective measure in the face of the grave danger which we have. With testing, you get into much bigger expenses, you also get into problems of well, did the person get sick between tests?\par \par \uc2 So, testing is certainly better than nothing but it`s not nearly as effective as a vaccine. And if you are trying to eliminate the risk effectively, then you want to go for a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would there be any difference in this legal argument if the mandate or for mandating masks in the workplace?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I think we would see the same kind of resistance. We have -- we have cases ongoing about mask mandates and school here -- in schools here in Ohio, and I think if it was just a mask mandate, you would see the same kind of challenges, though vaccines do seem to be an even more emotional issue.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know you haven`t heard the arguments of the health care one, but given that it is tied to federal money, right, that Supreme Court can tell the federal government not to mandate a vaccine for the military, things like that.\par \par \uc2 In general, do you think there`s a stronger case for the federal government on their mandate for health -- some health care workers?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I think there probably is a stronger case because it is federal spending. But you can also argue in the same way that this is a very broad mandate, that it requires an invasive procedure of vaccine.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t seen that to the same extent with a similar federal mandate in the past, and so there are arguments to be made still about whether the federal government has this power, though I think it is a stronger argument in favor of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Any unintended consequence to OSHA`s power long term if the Supreme Court says, no, you can`t use OSHA for this?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I don`t think so. This is an unusual mandate. It`s under the emergency powers. Other OSHA mandates have been long accepted having to do with other workplace hazards. So I don`t think OSHA will be gutted by a negative decision.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got you.\par \par \uc2 Professor Sharona Hoffman, from \uc2 Case Western University\uc2 , appreciates you coming on, before that, my colleagues, Carol Lee, and Ken Dilanian thank you both.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to keep an eye on the State Department briefing this hour, here`s why. Secretary of State Tony Blinken is now speaking with reporters.\par \par \uc2 The briefing comes in the U.S. and Russia are poised to start security talks next week amid mounting tensions over Ukraine, where Russia has amassed more than 100,000 troops along the border.\par \par \uc2 State department says Blinken spoke with Ukraine`s Foreign Minister today where he reaffirmed the U.S.`s support for Ukraine`s independence.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Russia`s got a little complicated factor going on as they -- their ally, Kazakhstan, has called them in to help with those protests.\par \par \uc2 And that`s actually complicating Russia`s focus on what it`s trying to do with the West regarding Ukraine. Anyway, a lot going on there, a lot of moving parts, if news is made, we will share it with you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, from messaging to testing to questions about whether this is a new normal, the Biden administration continues to struggle to respond to this latest COVID surge. We`re going to talk to a former White House senior advisor for COVID next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the unemployment rate is now at 3.9 percent, below four, a record- breaking number of Americans are quitting their jobs. Omicron-related staffing shortages though are wreaking havoc on businesses and schools nationwide.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, I don`t think COVID is here to stay that having COVID in the environment here and in the world is probably here to stay.\par \par \uc2 But COVID, as we`re dealing with it now is not here to stay.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be able to control this, the new normal is not going to be what it is now, it`s going to be better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden last hour on whether COVID is here to stay. Many experts including six of Biden`s former campaign health advisors and transition health advisors are now saying that COVID is on its way to becoming an endemic and we need to plan for it.\par \par \uc2 But right now COVID is very much still a pandemic. 10 straight days, the Nation`s daily case average has been at a record high, the number of patients hospitalized with COVID-19 is fast approaching last year`s peak, and the plan to distribute 500 million free rapid tests to Americans this month is still unclear. And oh, by the way, we still have a testing shortage for those who need it quickly.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former Senior Adviser to Biden`s White House COVID Response Team, it`s Andy Slavitt. Andy, I was talking with somebody, a public health -- a former public health official for a fairly large state earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Who is of the -- my -- it`s a -- basically wondering what more can be done? What`s realistic given our environment, realistic given our political divide, realistic given the public`s exhaustion?\par \par \uc2 And I`m curious before I get into some specifics about whether it`s time to deal with this as an endemic, is there something we haven`t tried that you think we should?\par \par \uc2 ANDY SLAVITT, FORMER SENIOR ADVISOR, WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE TEAM: Well, a lot does rest on today`s court case because I think this is the most forceful and by the way, lawful steps to basically protect the American public.\par \par \uc2 Mark McClellan, who was the administrator of CMS, Medicare, and Medicaid during the Bush administration, and I co-authored a piece in The Washington Post today on that -- on that very topic.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re in the middle of a tsunami and in that tsunami, there`s a lot of things that are going to get wet that wouldn`t in a normal wave.\par \par \uc2 And so you run out of things, you have shortages, you manage through the best you can, you take action, you put stuff on a wartime footing, and for states and the country, people around the country, that`s where we`re going to be for at least the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s a bit frustrating and I`m sure you feel this, and I`m sure a lot of people watching feel this is, it`s pretty clear we`re in like you just said we`re in the middle of a storm and it basically we all -- we all need to get off the road, right? Not for long.\par \par \uc2 This is, I think, the one piece of good news about Omicron, it`s coming and going. But it feels like the -- you know COVID staffing shortages are forcing shutdowns in places that say they don`t want them. But if we had two weeks to slow the spread, how much of a better place would we be in two weeks?\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: Well, look, I think -- I think you`re exactly right. We -- you know, we don`t get -- we were to the point now in the country where we don`t get serious at a local level about taking precautions until the hospitals are already full -- until the hospital beds are already full.\par \par \uc2 And even though this is a virus that doesn`t invade the lungs as much, doesn`t cause as much pneumonia, and that`s that is good news, there are so many people getting infected that there are enough people going into the hospital, that as we can see, they are beginning to fill.\par \par \uc2 And as you said, Chuck whether we choose to decide not to go to restaurants, or go to college, or go to schools, or hospitals or not, one way or the other COVID`s deciding for us. It`s essentially saying we can`t staff these things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: And if we can stop these things, you can`t go to those things. So, we -- either will have it done to us or we will take those precautions, ourselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. The Phoenix airport today, they`ve two security lines totally shut down because of TSA staff shortages, be a COVID. But the airport is open, people are trying you know to keep things open but they just physically can`t.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to the issue of preparing for an endemic. What does that look like in the next six months?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: Well, look, I mean, we have to take a little bit of peace with the fact that we won`t -- we can`t really perfectly predict the future here, you know.\par \par \uc2 I think Omicron was as much of a surprise as Delta, and we can all look back in hindsight and say, well, we should have done, we should have prepared.\par \par \uc2 But you know, we`re at a point where, on some days that`s debated, one to 2 percent of the public is coming down with COVID in a single day.\par \par \uc2 So there`s very little to do with that about that.\par \par \uc2 But you`re absolutely right, Chuck that the thing we should be thinking about is, how do we get back to a Steady State?\par \par \uc2 In a Steady State where we really have the tools, so that COVID is manageable, where we have enough rapid tests, where we have really good surveillance, where we have school protocols, where we have the antivirals that are easily accessible as they -- as they come online so that you have a simple process, take a test, get a positive result, get access to a particular prescription, get an antiviral all in one sitting.\par \par \uc2 So that -- a new normal will look like -- not like hey, there`s still COVID but it will look like is, hey, we have enough science, and enough tools, enough good sense that COVID is no longer a major threat to us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, is it as simple as creating some sort of metric like look, all right, this is uh -- you know, that -- I remember in the 80s, you`d find out today`s a bad Air day in LA, it`ll be OK and put on the mask.\par \par \uc2 Today`s a bad -- it`s a bad COVID month or do you think we`ll get to the point where COVID is semi seasonal like we know, geez, it goes south in the -- south in the summer, north in the winter, so maybe winter state, you got to wear masks, things like that.\par \par \uc2 Is that what a new normal for the next 18 months to two years looks like?\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: I think that`s a perfect analogy and a perfect description. Because when things are more predictable, they`re less scary. You know, I live in Los Angeles, and you know, you know, if there`s a bad weather or bad Air Day here because they publish it or if you`ve got allergies.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`ll be able to pretty much do the same thing here. And that`s what this surveillance is all about.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s you know, wastewater surveillance, whether it`s surveillance of variants that we`re doing, those are the kind of tools that allow you to wake up in the morning and say, you know what, I`m not going to fly to visit my family in Tulsa because tells us more dangerous than where I am here in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 So that kind of information will inform us and help us. And I think with a -- with a regular booster shot in the fall, probably going into winter, particularly for older people, much like the flu, it`ll keep more people safe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The issue of testing.\par \par \uc2 I understand, you know, I have to say the excuse that oh, there wasn`t demand for testing three months ago, so that`s why we didn`t manufacture enough feels like being shocked that we`re in the middle of a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 You know, some things are going to be loss leaders, too many tests all the time ought to be loss leaders. Why don`t we have that mindset?\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: Well, look, back in September, President Biden put $3 billion into scaling more test production. We didn`t have any at-home tests when the President came into office. We have 12.\par \par \uc2 Today, we just approved two more at the end of the year. And these things, I think, scale-up -- we probably would have had a problem over the Christmas Holidays anyway, given the demand, but there`s a couple of things going on. One is you just have way too many cases. There`s just no possible scenario.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: We can keep up with personnel in cases. And then secondly, we got to make sure these things work because there are some questions about when they work and how well they work until the FDA is smart to make sure they really fully check these things out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy Slavitt, it`s always good to have you on with your directness, and your expertise. Thank you, sir, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SLAVITT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, schools, parents, and teachers all across the country are struggling two years of learning loss is now being compounded by extreme staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 One big City School Superintendent has to spend her day being a substitute teacher this week. She`s joining me next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:52]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Staffing shortages are plaguing seemingly almost every industry in every part of the country. Some workers are leaving their jobs because of COVID restrictions while others are searching for better and safer opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week the Labor Department`s job openings and labor turnover survey noted that 4.5 million Americans quit their jobs in November. That was a record. That means labor shortages are having huge impacts on daily life. We saw earlier this week how significantly the lack of airline staff and TSA workers disrupted holiday travel. Frankly it`s still disrupting some travel.\par \par \uc2 Today the northeast is facing a snow day with fewer snowplows available to clear the roads as driver shortages persist. And one of the hardest hit areas and one of the most pressing for families across the country is education as teachers leave their in-person classroom jobs and surging COVID cases further exasperate the problem leaving school staff in an even tougher spot as schools fight to stay open as teachers and staff test positive.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by the superintendent of Boston Public Schools, Brenda Cassellius. She stepped in to teach fourth grade this week as staffing shortages hit Boston schools.\par \par \uc2 I really appreciate you coming here, Madame Superintendent. What`s interesting here is whether it`s teacher aides who were just starting to sort of apprentice as teachers, who have had to have classes all yearlong in some schools, I know of other high schools in my area where they`ve let kids out early because they don`t have enough teachers to supervise. Tell me, paint the picture in Boston.\par \par \uc2 BRENDA CASSELLIUS, BOSTON PUBLIC SCHOOL SUPERINTENDENT: Well, we have been pulling together as a team here in Boston. We`ve had about 1,100 teachers out, staff out. That includes about 459 teachers that we had out with COVID and over about 600 teachers out overall. So that has put a strain on the system. But our team has really rallied to put together a staff that is licensed into our classrooms as well as other staff from central office who have been filling in for recess and lunch. Lunch duty to really help out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So there`s one thing in sort of getting through a moment.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this. You`re the superintendent. You`ve got to start thinking about the next school year, and you`ve got to start thinking about the next couple of years. What is your biggest concern in the next two years? Is this going to become contagious where people continue to leave?\par \par \uc2 CASSELLIUS: Well, you know, that is a huge concern for all of the superintendents across the nation as we were already having labor shortages in education over the past several years. And now the pandemic has exacerbated that for us and teachers are really, really tired. So we are looking at some early incentives for teachers. Also trying to make sure that we have working with our preservice teachers and our higher education institutions, looking at ways to make licensing easier for teachers.\par \par \uc2 It would be nice to have a national reciprocity across our nation`s licensing boards which would be great. So that would really help. We`re also, like the snowplows today that you just showed, having a huge problem with bus drivers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CASSELLIUS: And what we really need is like a marshal plan around our labor shortage here. And we need to marshal all of the best thinking that we have to ensure that we have teachers in our classroom today and teachers in our classroom this fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, I believe it was the state of Massachusetts where your governor there had to call up the National Guard for the start of the school year. Is this just simply -- is this a licensing issue or is it the same issue, just, you know, the pay is not that great, and so if people think they can have an opportunity somewhere else, they`re going to quit and go look somewhere else?\par \par \uc2 CASSELLIUS: Teachers go into this profession because they love children. And it is a worthy profession. So I think that those folks are still out there. I think we need to make it easier and create pathways for teachers, especially diverse teacher work force, into our classrooms for today. And so there`s a lot we can do around licensing. A lot that we can do around pre-service programming. Tuition reimbursement for our teachers, taking care of these loans.\par \par \uc2 I`m hoping that the Biden administration will help our teachers out that way. It would be a great way to reward them for these past two years of serving in our schools. You know, we always say that the police and fire and emergency personnel are frontline workers as well as our amazing health care workers. And now our teachers are also on the frontlines, and I think we need to compensate them accordingly and treat them like the professionals that they are. And I think that is going to help us get teachers into our classrooms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, not to try to make policy on the fly here, but a good friend of mine worked for \uc2 Teach for America\uc2  for years. And it sounds like you`re thinking, hey, there`s this debate about do you cancel all student debt, maybe start with teachers first? Or maybe if you go into teaching, you get to cancel your debt, something like that. Do you think that would improve the number of applicants you would have?\par \par \uc2 CASSELLIUS: I think our democracy depends on our children being educated, our citizenry, and that we are at a critical tipping point for our nation in our public schools. And I think to get to your long-term question, we have got to have teachers in our classroom who are competent and caring. As a principal, I always said my number one job is to make sure I have a caring, competent teacher in every single classroom.\par \par \uc2 The only way we`re going to be able to do that is to be able cancel this debt and have tuition incentives for our teachers to go into the field particularly for a diverse work force which we know research shows that if you have a teacher who is -- looks like you, understands your culture, they are -- they speak your language, they are absolutely going to be helping you achieve more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, if we`re going to demand that teachers get the same type of education that lawyers get, we have to think about paying them way as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Boston Public School superintendent Brendan Cassellius, thanks for coming and sharing what you are going through up in Boston.\par \par \uc2 CASSELLIUS: Thank you, Chuck. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, I`m going to talk to Senator Tim Kaine about his efforts to move his seemingly immovable colleague Joe Manchin on voting rights and Build Back Better, and of course we`ll ask him a question or two about those 27 hours that he spent this week stuck on I-95.\par \par \uc2 But first, we`re waiting for the sentencing hearing to resume for the three men convicted of murdering Ahmaud Arbery. It`s an emotional morning in court as you might expect as the family spoke about their loss asking the judge to hand down the maximum sentence for each man, which would be life in prison without parole.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASMINE ARBERY, SISTER OF AHMAUD ARBERY: Amar had a future that was taken from him in an instance of violence. He was robbed of his life pleasures, big and small. He will never be able to fulfill his professional dreams, nor he`ll be able to start a family or even be a part of my daughter`s life.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:11]\par \par \uc2 The loss of Ahmaud has devastated me and my family. So I`m asking that the man that killed him be given the maximum sentence.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Turning to Capitol Hill on the anniversary of the January 6th attack on the Capitol, the legislative branch got the hard sell from the executive branch on the need to pass voting and elections reform.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: From the brutality of Bloody Sunday in the Edmund Pettus Bridge came historic voting rights legislation. So now let`s step up. Write the next chapter in American history. Where January 6th marks not the end of democracy but the beginning of a renaissance of liberty and fair play.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let`s be clear. We must pass the voting rights bills that are now before the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But passing such legislation is easier said than done. As we`ve been staying for days, weeks and months, to get voting rights legislation through the Senate, Democrats are going to have to either sway their entire caucus to change the chamber`s rules to allow a bill to pass with 50 votes or get at least 10 of their Republican colleagues on board. Both are complicated and uncertain paths and one might argue there hasn`t been an urgency until frankly this week to see the leader of the Democratic Party push for this.\par \par \uc2 So a Democrat that is in the center of these discussions is Virginia Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it is good to see you warm, rested. I assume somewhat rested. Before I get to the I-95 mishap and our governor`s interesting take on what happened there, I want to speak about the voting issue. You know, these bills were introduced right when this Congress started. That was a year ago.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 A lot of people assumed that after COVID relief, it sort of -- the two tracks here were COVID relief, which you guys got done, and the issue of the democracy because of what happened on January 6th. And then that urgency stalled. And everything got focused on the infrastructure and Build Back Better. And we can have a long debate about what should have come first.\par \par \uc2 But how do you get the urgency back? Because I would argue you had it in the first three or four months of the year, and it got lost.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Well, Chuck, you`re right. I mean, at least the base bills were filed early in the year. But we didn`t have all Democrats on board with the Freedom to Vote Act. We had 49 on board but not 50. And in May Senator Schumer asked if I would work together with others to try to get Senator Manchin on board. And we worked from May until September looking at the For the People Act which had been filed, starting some provisions of it, dramatically changing others, adding some.\par \par \uc2 So the Freedom to Vote Act was actually introduced in September. We`ve got the John Lewis Bill to restore preclearance under the Voting Rights Act. We have this Freedom to Vote Act guaranteeing minimal access to the ballot and federal elections and other safeguards of our democracy. And we`ve been working very hard since getting that bill filed to do a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 First, in the Senate, as you know, because of the rules here, normally you can`t pass legislation without some bipartisanship. So we undertook massive efforts in September, October, November, to put the bills on the floor, to talk to Republicans about, do you have amendments. How about counter offers? We`ll give you unlimited amendments. What do you think we should do?\par \par \uc2 And it took us two months to demonstrate what many of us believed, but we had to at least demonstrate it, that there was going to be virtually no Republican help on it with the exception of Lisa Murkowski, who`s a co- sponsor of the Lewis bill. Once that was done and we had really convinced everyone Republicans weren`t going to help, now we`ve been working on looking at the Senate rules to facilitate getting this bill on the floor, having a debate and hopefully passing it. I think you`ll see us doing it no later than Martin Luther King Day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But are you going to get Joe Manchin on board to change the rules by Martin Luther King Day, and what`s your best case to him?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: My best case to him is that these voting restrictions all around the country are abhorrent that Joe Manchin, before he was a senator and before he was a governor, was a secretary of state who cares deeply about voting, that he is a co-sponsor of both of these bills and we can`t simply allow partisan efforts, whether it`s an attack on the Capitol to disenfranchise 80 million people on January 6th or state legislature.\par \par \uc2 We can`t allow the democracy destroyers to win. We`ve got to be democracy protectors. Am I going to get Joe Manchin? That`s for Joe Manchin. But I will say this. We`ve had really good discussions about how to look at Senate rules not in a way to destroy the filibuster. Some of our options are actually to really restore the filibuster to what it was once during most of the Senate`s history.\par \par \uc2 So we`re looking at different options. We`re going to have to fight real hard and in 1965 the bill was on the floor for about five weeks. I don`t expect -- I mean, we shouldn`t expect this is just going to be an easy one. If we want to do something momentous, we`re going to have to do the work. And so I`m getting ready for a big, long floor fight about this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re talking a lot about Joe Manchin. Kyrsten Sinema on board with the rules change? Do you think you can get her on board with this?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Senator Sinema is also I`ve talked a lot to her and others are, too. I`d says she`s -- again I want to let Senator Sinema speak for herself, but she`s in a slightly different position maybe than Joe. I think she`s always been more forward-leaning on the voting bills themselves. She was a co- sponsor of the For the People Act, but she`s tougher on the willingness to make rules adjustments.\par \par \uc2 She wrote a piece in "The Washington Post" last June about why she wouldn`t abolish the filibuster. So again in my discussions with her, my point is we don`t have to abolish the filibuster. Senate rules are often changed. You don`t have to abolish the filibuster. Would you be open to restoring it to what it used to be or other paths that could facilitate passage? And again, there`s no deal yet.\par \par \uc2 There won`t be one until we announce that there`s a deal, but the discussions are very cordial and candid and respectful. And she is very, very worried. Arizona was the first of the states that got challenged. The electoral vote challenged on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I`m (INAUDIBLE) damaging these democracy attacks are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you`re a guy that always seems to -- that if somebody is going to offer you a half a loaf, you`ll say OK, let`s think about it.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 The Electoral Count Act. You have more 10 Republicans that want to fix it. I think a lot of people agree it needs to be fixed. I know it`s not a replacement for doing these voting rules. It does seem as if, you know, I know Senator Schumer said no, he doesn`t want to touch that because he thinks it`s somehow a replacement. Why can`t you say yes to both?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, I think we could. I think we could. It is the case that it`s not a replacement, because the voting rights issues have been going on around the country, are very (INAUDIBLE), for example, the Raphael Warnock and Jon Ossoff elections in Georgia. Let`s not let people register during a runoff, they`re trying to do that. The electoral count fix would be a good thing for one every four years but the challenge is to people`s rights to vote are counted go far beyond that.\par \par \uc2 But it`s a good thing, and if we can do it as part of a comprehensive voting legislation or separately, I think it`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve got to ask you about the I-95 thing because Governor Northam is not accepting that anything went wrong. And in fact he said this on a radio show, I`m not going to play it because we`re running short on time. He says we knew that this storm was coming, we put warnings out, why don`t you start asking some of these individuals who were out in the highways for hours? Did you know about the storm? Why did you feel it was so important to drive through such a snowstorm? In hindsight, do you think maybe you should have stayed at home wherever you were instead of going on I-95?\par \par \uc2 Senator, care to respond?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, Chuck, let me just say this. The reason I got on I-95 in Richmond at 1:00 on Monday afternoon is we had votes in the Senate. That was shortly after I (INAUDIBLE) the vote was scrapped. But I also had a voting rights meeting that night at 8:30 and there was no signage, electronic signage or anything along the highway that said you can`t get there in seven hours. It`s normally a two-hour trip.\par \par \uc2 I have, you know, a navigational system on my car, that was suggesting there is a drive time to D.C. but yes, it`s slower than it would normally be, but the navigational system was not coming up and saying turn around and go home or get off and get a hotel. And so, you know, I -- of course if weather is bad you want to really think about, is it important that I`d be on the road? I thought trying to make it for votes and then after those votes were scrapped, I thought trying to make it to D.C. so that I can talk about voting rights, I thought it was important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bottom line, was there enough -- do you think the state did enough? It doesn`t sound like you think they did.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, I, look, they`re analyzing that and they`re going to determine it. And they`re going to have a test about it, you know, soon because we`re in the snow season. Got some snow last night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here we are.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: They`re going to get a chance to test it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I didn`t say anything critical. I tried to be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I know you didn`t. Happy warrior is definitely a great definition of that, that`s for sure. Obviously the governor a little crankier about the questions.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Senator Tim Kaine, appreciate you coming on, and glad you`re warm and healthy.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: All right, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ll be right back. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:17]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. And turning to the unrest abroad that we mentioned at the top of the show, the ongoing protest in Kazakhstan. Dozens of protesters and law enforcement have died during these violent clashes. And in an address to the nation, the president of Kazakhstan authorized law enforcement to shoot to kill without warning, going as far as calling the protesters bandits and terrorists, they have to be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 The president is seeking to quell the largest protest the country has seen since gaining independence three decades ago. The protest that began on Sunday were initially over rising gas prices. They soon escalated with government buildings including the president`s residence set ablaze.\par \par \uc2 The Secretary of State Tony Blinken just wrapped up a briefing with reporters ahead of those meetings. (INAUDIBLE) in his remarks on Russia`s disinformation campaign against Ukraine and NATO and then he also condemned the violence in Kazakhstan and reiterated the U.S. support for Kazakhstan`s constitutional institutions.\par \par \uc2 Again, Russia playing a bit of a role in that Kazakhstan mess. And it is impacting what they`re trying to do and manipulate with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And of course if it`s Sunday, it`s MEET THE PRESS in your local NBC station. Do not miss it.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur. We begin this hour with a rare emergency session at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220108ei1700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for January 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 7,553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And that same fearless fight for justice is being fought today by folks like Latasha Brown, Stacey Abrams, and all the election workers who face death threats for counting our votes, these people are bending the arc of America`s moral universe towards justice. They are waiting for it to just happen.\par \par \uc2 So, remember, with 10 months to the next election, we are not powerless. The greatest defenders of democracy are not those in power. It`s you, the millions of Americans living your lives quietly trying to do what`s right. If democracy has any hope of surviving, it will take all of us to keep bending that moral arc towards justice.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: One year ago today, in this sacred place, democracy was attacked.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One year later, the president attempts to rally a nation.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I will stand in this breach. I will defend this nation. I will not allow no one to place a dagger in the throat of democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, what history will record a full year since Donald Trump`s failed coup, the fight to stop the slow-motion insurrection in the states.\par \par \uc2 And what happens next, in a country where one of the two major political parties is abandoning democracy?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON, RADIO HOST: Cease the day.\par \par \uc2 REP. MATT GAETZ (R-FL): We`re ashamed of nothing. We`re proud of the work that we did on January 6.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Special edition of ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening, I`m Chris Hayes. Today is January 6, 2022, the one year anniversary of a violent mob storming the U.S. Capitol in a deadly insurrection aimed at disrupting the peaceful transfer of power for really the first time in this country`s history.\par \par \uc2 And this date, January6, has taken on the sense of power and resonance in the cultural imagination in a way that few dates do. It`s probably the most infamous date in this country`s history since September 11.\par \par \uc2 And today, we saw in full display the battle over the meaning of this date. Because right now, we do not know what January 6 will mean generations down the line. You know, as you travel across the world, you encounter lots of places named after dates, usually marking the occasion of a revolution or a military victory or hard-won battle for independence.\par \par \uc2 It`s the case of the city and street Nueve de Julio in Argentina or the neighborhood Diez de Octubre in Cuba, both of which celebrate their nation`s Independence. Or 26 July Street in downtown Cairo celebrating the revolution that overthrew the monarchy in that country.\par \par \uc2 Years from now, we could see something similar celebrating January 6. There could be plazas dedicated to the insurrectionists all over the country. It sounds preposterous now, but in the aftermath of the Civil War, it probably sounded preposterous there would be an avenue named after Confederate General Robert E. Lee in Brooklyn, New York. But General Lee Avenue exists as such to this day.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, in this very moment, there`s a fight over how to signify what this date today will mean in our country`s collective memory. Earlier today, President Joe Biden offered his vision of January 6, as something of a historical inflection point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So, now let`s step up. Write the next chapter in American history where January six marks not the end of democracy, but the beginning of a renaissance of liberty and fair play. I did not seek this fight brought to this Capital one year ago today, but I will not shrink from it either.\par \par \uc2 I will stand in this breach. I will defend this nation, not allow no one to place a dagger at the throat of democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, it probably goes without saying I believe that Biden`s position is the correct one here. And one year ago, it appeared as though Republicans agreed. I mean, Republican leadership in both the House and the Senate condemned the violence, even as a majority of House Republicans then after the violence went into the chamber to vote to abet the coup, voting to refuse to certify the results of the election.\par \par \uc2 But today, one year later, the response from congressional Republicans has been overwhelmingly silence, exemplified starkly by this image of the only two Republicans in the House Chamber today. On the lower right side of your screen, you will see vice-chair of the committee investigating the insurrection Congresswoman Liz Cheney of Wyoming in red and to her right her father of all people, former Vice President Dick Cheney.\par \par \uc2 That is it, just the two of them. The rest of the Republican Party, it seems, has yet to reach a consensus on how to frame the events of January 6, except to agree that it really was not all that bad.\par \par \uc2 Florida`s Republican Governor Ron DeSantis decried what he says is the unnecessary politicization of the 6th which of course was well, an inherently political event perpetrated by one party in response to an electoral loss.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:14]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I think it`s going to end up being just a politicized Charlie Foxtrot today. I don`t expect anything good to come out of anything that Pelosi and the gang are doing. I don`t expect anything from the corporate press to be enlightening. I think it`s going to be nauseating, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Nauseating. Fox News played whataboutism with the events of the day with one anchor publicly speculating why Speaker of the House Nancy Pelosi only paid tribute to the police officers who lost their lives and not Ashli Babbitt, the insurrectionist who was killed by law enforcement as he tried to force her way through a door leading to the lobby of the House chamber.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: One went, the other went, and now it is done. One year later, January 6, she recognized the police officers only not the fifth person who was also killed that day. We`ll move on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, let`s move on. Republican Congressman Tom Reed of New York who as recently as last May called the insurrection and assault on our democracy and backed creating a bipartisan committee investigate it put out a statement today saying that January 6, was, "The darkest day of my congressional career." But then turned around and said he is still supporting Donald Trump, the man who incited the assaults in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM REED (R-NY): President Trump is the candidate that`s representing the Republican Party. I will support him because the Republican Party that I believe in that would put him in that position is the party that`s going to see us into the future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And speaking of Donald Trump, the ex-president who fomented the insurrection, who watched it unfold, refusing to intervene, indeed, by some accounts, watching with excitement, released a predictably unhinged statement today, in his own bid, to cache the memory and the significance of the date lying about the results of the 2020 election again, predictably, and implicitly endorsing the events of the sixth as a necessary political uprising, which has been his position for a while now.\par \par \uc2 "To watch Biden speaking is very hurtful to many people. They`re the ones who tried to stop the peaceful transfer with a rigged election. America is a laughingstock stock of the world. And it`s all because of the real insurrection which took place on November 3, the real insurrection. Never forget the crime of the 2020 presidential election. Never give up."\par \par \uc2 What do you think that means? What do you think people hear when they hear that? What do you think he wants to do again? That position that the real insurrection was the democratic election in which a majority voted him out, that`s a position that is shared by -- well, articulated at least by Trump`s most aberrant most anti-social followers, who are the vanguard of the MAGA movement, and the vanguard of a movement to turn this into a day of celebratory occasion.\par \par \uc2 Like Florida Republican Congressman Matt Gaetz who openly expresses admiration for the insurrectionists. Some of them beat and tased police officers and said he would celebrate their actions today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON, RADIO HOST: Cease the day.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: We`re ashamed of nothing. We`re proud of the work that we did on January 6 --\par \par \uc2 REP. MARJORIE TAYLOR-GREENE (R-GA): That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: -- to make legitimate arguments about election integrity. So, we`re going to make those arguments today and a press conference at 2:15. And we`re actually going to go walk the grounds that patriotic Americans walked from the White House to the Capitol who had no intent of breaking the law or doing violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is where the Republican Party will try to take this, mark my words. And the meaning of January 6 depends on what comes next. It`s just not set. History doesn`t decide. It gets made. Gaetz will not be the only one pushing ashamed or nothing line. There`s a contingent, a faction in American life, a minority faction, but one that has essentially seized one of the two parties that want to turn today into a holiday, an occasion to celebrate. And they could win.\par \par \uc2 If everyone who believes in American democracy, who believes as I do, the core vision of a multiracial multi-sectarian, self-governing people across all sorts of lines of difference, commanding our own fate together collectively for ourselves, if all of those people across those lines of difference do not band together in the face the people who refuse to be ashamed of their attack on that principle one year ago, then the recollection of this day could look very different in the future.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jamie Raskin is Democrat from Maryland. He is a member of the House Select Committee to investigate January 6. He was the lead impeachment manager for the second trial of Donald Trump for his role in the insurrection. He has a new memoir about the insurrection and its aftermath titled Unthinkable. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, what do you see as the importance of shaping the understanding of this day one year after it happened, one year after you were there with your daughter and your son in law?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:10]\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Well, it`s everything. I mean, either people are going to understand that this is the expression of a basically fascistic movement that connects the highest reaches in the Republican Party, and Donald Trump with violent fascistic streak criminals where they don`t understand that.\par \par \uc2 But we are up against a serious right-wing authoritarian attack on American political democracy. So, if we allow it to be swept under the rug, or trivialized, or minimized by people like Matt Gaetz, then we are in very certain danger of opening the road to right wing authoritarianism to take over American government.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I thought this image today of Dick Cheney and Liz Cheney and that chamber where they are the only two Republicans who show up for the sort of commemoration and the moment of silence. I found that stark and worth 1000 words and unnerving. What do you see when you see that?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, first of all, there were no Republicans in the chamber at all. It was just completely empty, devoid, not as usual just of ideas but devoid of physical presence over on their side of the aisle. And that was chilling was a little bit like a sci-fi movie.\par \par \uc2 And I was happy that Liz came and I`m happy that she brought her dead because I`ve been trying to check out the history of coups around the world in, you know, Greece, Argentina, Chile, whatever. You can`t find an example of an attempted coup and serious fascist pushing the society where progressives and liberal parties alone defeat fascism. You need a coalition of liberals and the center-right.\par \par \uc2 And Liz Cheney, has a high burden of Hope placed on her as well as Adam Kinzinger and a handful of others who stood strong, because they represent the possibility that we will really have a broad coalition of constitutional patriots in America who are resisting what Donald Trump is trying to do to us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I should note that Kinzinger I think issued a statement saying he wanted to be there but could not today. And he would have been there if he -- if he could have. This question of accountability which sort of haunts the spectrum, I mean, I think you have Republicans basically en mass not wanting to say anything. A few, like I said, in the vanguard, who will -- who will celebrate it and go on Steve Bannon podcast.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also the question of the -- a year later, I mean, who has really paid a price for this, other than a hundreds of sort of foot soldiers who are have been arrested?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, but that`s an important point to make where, you know, there are more than 700 prosecutions that are underway, which should give some comfort to people who feel like the Department of Justice isn`t doing anything.\par \par \uc2 But it also should be a very important message to Donald Trump`s own followers, that he`s perfectly happy to send in these foot soldiers to go and essentially ruin their lives and put their families at risk and kept people injured and killed. He won`t even come to our trial to defend his own conduct. He wouldn`t even come and testify or turn anything over.\par \par \uc2 He`s a complete snowflake. And he`s willing to get other people killed and other people hurt, including the people who`ve fallen into his cult, but he`s not willing to stand up and to defend his own conduct. It`s really disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and in fact, you will note that while money has been taken from donors to pay for the president`s legal defense and a few people in his inner circle, that large jest has not extended to the hundreds and hundreds of people who essentially answered his call to storm the Capitol on that day, which goes to your point.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, and remember, he authorized his lawyers at the Senate trial when we pressed them on it to denounce the violence. And they said they thought it was disgusting and sickening, and nobody would ever utter a positive word for the insurrectionists on their side of the aisle, and, you know, on their defense team. And of course, Trump now is saying that they greeted the police officers with hugs and kisses and it was basically like the second coming of MLK`s march on Washington. It`s utterly fraudulent. And Donald Trump refuses to take a stand.\par \par \uc2 You know, either you should denounce his lawyers for denouncing the insurrection, or he should, you know, say that he`s opposed to criminal violence. But he won`t say that because fascist political tendencies like his are always surrounded by violence, and he has egged him on from the beginning, the Oath Keepers, the Three Percenters, the Proud Boys that Aryan Nations, you know, the militia groups, everybody who showed up that day, he has egged on their violence.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Your book is excellent and I recommend it highly. I`ve actually read it. We had a conversation about it. You are -- you have suffered a tremendous personal loss in the death of your son a year ago, a little more than a year ago, and obviously living through this day. And you remain a fairly hopeful person.\par \par \uc2 What gives you hope as you stare down the barrel of this year, as you do the work on the January 6, Select Committee because I think there is a temptation to succumb to a kind of despair and doom, watching just how malformed, how aberrant, how insidious the highest levels of one of our two parties has become?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, first of all, thank you for reading my book. That gives me some hope that people are reading my book in a really serious way and engaging that. But look, my dad used to say when everything looks hopeless, you`re the hope. And we don`t have time for despair. And we don`t have time for despondency.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, we`ve got to help people who are going through that who have no other choice, but we need everybody out here engaging in democracy this year. We need everybody. That`s why mental health is such a critical issue. But I look also to the past. I look to Frederick Douglass who was a great Marylander, who said power concedes nothing without a demand, it never has, and never will.\par \par \uc2 I looked at Abraham Lincoln who I quoted on January 6 a year ago on the floor who said that if Doom and downfall ever come to America, it will not come from monsters abroad, it will come from the monsters within, who are here. And by that he meant violent white supremacy and vigilante violence.\par \par \uc2 And he talked about a mob attack on a newspaper editor, Mr. Lovejoy, who was killed who was -- an abolitionist who was killed by a racist mob. But we also finally have to look -- and most importantly, we`ve got to look, Chris, to the future, and to the young people, because this generation of young Americans, they are beyond everything that Donald Trump wants to pump into the blood of America. They`re beyond the poisons of racism and anti- Semitism, and misogyny. They`re beyond all of that.\par \par \uc2 And we just have to galvanize the young people to get engaged, which is why my whole campaign has been turned over to Democracy Summer, a project sponsored by the (INAUDIBLE) to try to get young people involved integrally and passionately in this election year. We need all hands on deck because the Democratic Party today is the Democracy Party. FDR used to call us the democracy and that`s really who we are at this point.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Congressman Jamie Raskin, the memoir Unthinkable is out now. We talked about it at length on my podcast Why Is This Happening? That will be out next week. You`re also, Congressman Raskin, the subject of a new MSNBC film`s documentary Love and the Constitution, which is premiering here Sunday, February 6th.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thank you so much for having me, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Our coverage of what happened a year ago has never truly ended. We covered every single night, the ongoing assault on democracy. But it can be easy to forget the absolute horror and violence that unfolded before our very eyes.\par \par \uc2 When we return, January 6 as it happened, the first-hand accounts of those who survived it as they honor those who did not.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. COLIN ALLRED (D-TX): Had those officers not held that line. I would not have met my son Cameron. So, for me, January 6, I don`t see it as a member of Congress so much. I see it as a father, as somebody who, because I didn`t know mine, I`ve always been committed to making sure that my boys knew me. And so, to the Sicknick`s family, I`ll say to you, your son`s sacrifice allowed me to meet mine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Earlier tonight, on the steps of the Capitol building, a group of lawmakers held a candlelight prayer vigil marking the anniversary of the insurrection and honoring those who defended the Capitol and those who lost their lives. It`s been a day full of remembrances and commemoration of the worst attack on American democracy frontally at least since the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 But as President Joe Biden noted in a speech delivered in Statuary Hall at the Capitol, in order to really comprehend what happened one year ago, you have to bring yourself back to the terror of that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you hold former President Trump personally responsible?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are praying for former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Bible tells us that we shall know the truth and the truth shall make us free. We shall know the truth. Well, here is the God`s truth about January 6, 2021. Close your eyes. Go back to that day. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 Rioters rampaging, waving for the first time inside this Capitol Confederate flag that symbolizes the cause to destroy America.\par \par \uc2 What else do you see? A mob breaking windows, kicking in doors, breaching the Capitol, American flag on poles being used as weapons, as spears, fire stickers being thrown at the heads of police officers.\par \par \uc2 We saw it with our own eyes rioters menace these halls, threatening life of the Speaker of the House, literally erecting gallows to hang the Vice President of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can I speak to Pelosi? Yes, we`re coming (BLEEP). Oh, Mike Pence? We`re coming for you too (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hang Mike Pence! Hang Mike Pence! Hang Mike Pence!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`ve got the gallows set up outside the Capitol building. It`s time to start (BLEEP) using them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Start making a list. Put all those names down and we start hunting them one by one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE) of in the rotunda. Please be advised, there are masks under your seats. Please grab my mask placed in your left and be prepared put on your mask (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): When the order came to evacuate, I stayed behind for a while until two Republicans came up to me. One of them said, you can`t let them see you. I know these people. I can talk to these people. I can talk my way through these people.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA4): I remember every moment vividly. I viscerally feel the pounding on the gallery doors.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We want Trump! We want Trump! We want Trump!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right, no violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s too late for that.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: I hear the shot ringing out. I replay how I made plans to use my gas mask and my cane newly at my side from a five-week-old knees replacement surgery to fight back if attacked. And I remember not knowing if I would make it out of our seat of democracy alive or if our democracy itself would survive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re afraid of Antifa? Well, guess what? America showed up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Knock, knock. We`re here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think Cruz would want us to do this.\par \par \uc2 REP. RUBEN GALLEGO (D-TX): Until like many you -- with many of you, luckily a former linebacker standing next to me. We had to contemplate actually defending the floor of the House of Representatives. And at that point, I thought about what it meant. Was I willing to die for my country? Was I willing to die to make sure democracy continues? And at that time, around this time last year, I said yes, yes I would.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re outnumbered. There`s a (BLEEP) million of us out there. And we are listening to Trump, your boss.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What the extremists who roamed these halls targeted was not only the lives of elected leaders, what they sought to degrade and destroy was not only a building, hallowed as it is, what they were assaulting are the institutions, the values, the ideals that generations of Americans have marched, picketed, and shed blood to establish and defend.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One year later, the fight for democracy is far from over. I`ll speak with a member of Congress who lived through the horror of the 6th and is warning about what is yet to come next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BRIAN SCHATZ (D-HI): One year later should be a simple, solemn commemoration of what happened in a collective unified determination to never let anything like that ever happen again. But I`m even more worried now.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump is now defining fealty to him by one thing and one thing only. Are you willing to install him into power regardless of the vote count? And so now, every Republican politician and elected officials, secretaries of state, county elections commissioners, \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  candidates have to promise to put Trump above democracy itself. And many are doing it. They are now organizing the next coup in plain sight.\par \par \uc2 So, this week, we commemorate the fallen. We thank everyone who came to democracy`s defense across the country and in our great capital city. But we know that this was round one. We know that authoritarians rarely give up and we know that they aren`t doing their preparation for the next coup in hiding. They are doing it all in plain sight and they must be stopped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Brian Schatz of Hawaii standing exactly where he was a year ago today inside the Senate as rioters stormed the Capitol warning that American democracy itself is at stake. And Senator Schatz joins me live tonight.\par \par \uc2 I talked to Congressman Jamie Raskin over on the House side today. And I`m curious your reaction to the day and whether there are a lot of Republican senators around.\par \par \uc2 SCHATZ: Republican senators are not around now. My good old friend and colleague Johnny Isakson, his funeral is today, so 30 of the -- of the Republican senators are in Atlanta, Georgia for that purpose, but 20 are not. And I just think it`s noteworthy that there was not a single Republican United States senator in the building.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I thought as I was watching that speech, that one -- there was a chance to do something right afterwards, which is the president was impeached for a second time the first time in American history. It was the largest bipartisan vote of impeachment and the largest bipartisan vote for conviction, but not the threshold.\par \par \uc2 And one of the punishments that you could have dealt out had he been, you know, convicted in the Senate was just barred from future office. And I wonder if you think about that at all, and how different things might look in America had Republicans understood that that was their opportunity to sort of like cauterize this wound.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:32]\par \par \uc2 SCHATZ: Yes, that could have been it. They could have just used that opportunity to get rid of Trump. They used him as an instrument to get their Supreme Court justices installed, to get their tax cuts done. And then they could have thrown him on the ash heap of history having achieved a bunch of right wing goals, but basically saying, we`re going to draw the line at democracy itself. They did not draw the line at democracy itself. They have tethered themselves to him.\par \par \uc2 They had that opportunity. That offer was proffered. But remember, at that time, Mitch McConnell was still in charge of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And so those articles of impeachment were not going to be dealt with until we took the gavels several weeks later.\par \par \uc2 So, that would have been their opportunity. And I think looking back on it, they probably -- many of them probably wish they had gone through with it because they wouldn`t have had to deal with him ever again.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That night, when everyone came back and reconvene for the vote, I remember thinking that the mood was bizarre, there was a kind of strange jocularity to it. And I think it was just how people were processing what had happened. There was, you know, solemnity when everyone came back, but then, you know, a lot of jokes and Lindsey Graham said, I`m off the train, and you know, Ben Sasse did a monologue about how awesome it is to shovel your neighbor`s driveway when it snows.\par \par \uc2 I remember thinking that it hadn`t sunk in then. And I wonder a year later, do you feel it has sunk into the body?\par \par \uc2 SCHATZ: No. I think it is sunk in for many of us. I think it`s sunk in for Ben Sasse. I think it`s sunk in for Mitt Romney and others. But there are others who are, you know, whistling past the graveyard. I think they really are dancing on the edge of overturning the American experiment.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t understand their justification on a human level, on a moral level. To stay in office in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , it is the greatest honor of my life professionally, but nothing is worth that. And why be a United States Senator if you`re going to be a party to overturning democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these people ran for office, they ran for mayor or Secretary of State or lieutenant governor or governor, you know, because they actually wanted to help their community. And now they`re sitting there all privately telling us, this guy`s insane, this guy`s an autocrat. But if it`s not me in office, it`s going to be someone even crazier than me.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I`ve come to the very sad conclusion that the Republican Party is no longer committed to American-style democracy. There are individual Republicans who still remain committed to American-style democracy. But Donald Trump is the indisputable leader of the Democratic -- excuse me -- of the Republican Party. There is no way to win a primary without Donald Trump`s endorsement. And there`s really no way to get his endorsement unless you promise to install him into power regardless of how the votes fall.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You called out by name two people -- two of your colleagues in that speech, Josh Hawley and Ted Cruz. Both of whom led the effort on the Senate side to volunteer to object to electors based on nothing, a very tortured, preposterous constitutional theory that states didn`t have the -- they weren`t allowed to determine their own election provisions.\par \par \uc2 Those two basically escaped accountability. Like, they`re -- they aided and fomented the coup. Josh Hawley famously gave them the fist to the marchers that morning. And there was some pressure on them for a while, but that seems to have gone away.\par \par \uc2 SCHATZ: I think you`ve described the situation accurately. They use their fancy educations, their big brains and their unlimited ambition to at least dance on the edge of, if not go over the line and try to overturn the American experiment. These are people who want to know better, but they have been overcome with their ambition.\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the failures of official Washington, of pundit Washington, of lobbyist Washington is that these guys are back in the fold. They`re, you know -- Ted Cruz talking about \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , and Josh Hawley is treated as like a thoughtful person on tech issues. They shouldn`t be allowed in the door in terms of public policy, if they`re not willing to stipulate to the basic premise that whoever gets the most votes gets to take the office.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. That basic premise is really under assault and it`s kind of the whole ballgame at a certain level. Senator Brian Schatz, thank you for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 SCHATZ: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One year since the attack, just 10 months to the Midterms, what needs to be done before November, ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN KILDEE (D-MI): As many of you know, for me personally, the path forward after January 6 has not been an easy one. It`s been made more painful, however, by the fact that most of our colleagues on the other side of the aisle continue to accommodate that big lie that was the predicate for the attack on our country.\par \par \uc2 To truly protect our democracy, we need truth. Truth is clear as this shard of broken glass that I have carried with me for the last 365 days. The glass that I picked up from a broken window in the capitol in the aftermath of January 6 as a reminder, a constant reminder in my pocket of the brutality of that day. We must have truth, we must have accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s often the case and people live through violent events or tragedies. They come out on the other side of it with some sort of trauma. In fact, the nation right now is living through the enduring trauma of COVID and the lives we`ve lost to that. When we look at the images of January, it`s hard not to feel some pain just as an observer. Hearing the screams of police under attack and the war crime from the mob creepily hunting down elected officials, watching a rioter get shot killed trying to break into the House chamber.\par \par \uc2 Today members of Congress were inside the Capitol when it was happening share their very personal stories. They`re moving. And I just want to play a few of them for you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SARA JACOBS (D-CA): January 6 was my fourth day in office. January 6 was my first time ever to the House gallery. My team had to walk me there because I didn`t know how to get through the Capitol. I`ll never forget the buzzing of the escape hoods, the fear when I couldn`t open the packaging, the sound of the doors closing and being locked, introducing myself to my colleagues as we were hiding under the chairs, Annie Kuster grabbing my hand, fashioning weapons out of stanchions and pens, and my high heels ready to take on the rioters who were banging on the doors behind us. Climbing over chairs and under rails, not sure where the writers were and if we were going fast enough to escape them.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): I remember, I was laying on the floor and had just a very little bit of juice left in my cell phone. And I wanted to call my husband. I wanted to call Stan. I was afraid to say I love you, because it hearkened back to September 11 and those last calls.\par \par \uc2 So I just said, I`m all right. Tell the kids, I am all right. They are going to get us out of here. And I hung up the phone.\par \par \uc2 REP. LISA BLUNT ROCHESTER (D-DE): I reflect on that day, being trapped in the gallery, ultimately praying for all of our safety and peace in our nation. I also reflect on just how close, how close we were to losing it, to losing our democracy. Those of us trapped in the gallery, we lived it, ducking, crawling under over railings, hands, knees, the sounds, the smells.\par \par \uc2 We had a front-row seat to what lies hate or plain old misinformation conjures. We went from victims, to witnesses. And today we are messengers. We reflect on the fact that January 6 was about so much more than an effort to break into a building. It was an effort to break down our institutions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A year later, on the sober anniversary, the question of what American democracy will be is very much an open one. Democrats have 10 months until the next election to save it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You can`t love your country only when you win. You can`t obey the law only when is convenient. You can`t be patriotic when you embrace and enable lies. Those who storm this capitol and those who instigate it and incite it and those who called on them to do so held a dagger at the throat of America and American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A dagger at the throat of American democracy that President Biden invoked at the capitol today is still right there. Time is ticking. As election lawyer Marc Elias wrote earlier this week, "10 months from the 2022 Midterms, it is up to Senate Democrats to decide whether to advance these pro-democracy laws alone or allow Republicans to use the filibuster as the weapon that murdered democracy. If these two voting rights bills fail, our democracy will suffer and may never recover.\par \par \uc2 Ian Bassin served as associate White House Counsel under President Barack Obama. He`s a co-founder and executive director of Protect Democracy, a nonpartisan organization dedicated to fighting efforts to undermine democracy. Heather McGhee is the board chair of Color of Change, the nation`s largest online racial justice organization, and the author of The Some Of Us: What Racism Costs Everyone And How We Can Prosper Together.\par \par \uc2 Let me start with you, Heather. How are you thinking about these next 10 months towards these midterms which take on this kind of existential weight that I don`t think any midterm election I`ve ever encountered before felt like it took off?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER MCGHEE, BOARD CHAIR, COLOR OF CHANGE: It does, because it feels like because of the baked-in counter-majoritarian weight of instruction of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  combined with the rules that Republicans at the state level have put in to gerrymander the House and to suppress the vote, we have a situation where we are looking at a question of do we have a pro-democracy, not a Democratic Party, a pro-democracy party, because we only have one of those, sadly, in this two party system right now, that is willing to do everything to preserve the American experiment.\par \par \uc2 And so, I agree with the idea that there`s 10 -- there are 10 months here that we have. And obviously, the thing that`s standing in the way is an arcane Senate rule that allows people who represent 41 million fewer people than the Democratic Party to hold up a set of democracy reforms that have nearly supermajority support across the country.\par \par \uc2 And so, that is the silver bullet. It is filibuster reform. I was glad to see President Biden finally come out and very -- you know, full-throated Lee endorse it. And here`s what we -- here`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:43]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I will say -- I mean, we`ve been rattling around in circles obviously on this for a very long time. You know, they`re pushing again. There`s questions about the conversations and Sinema and Manchin. You know, people who are closest effort will point out that there has been tremendous progress already made in the number of senators who have come over to decide of rules change for this purpose, which is significant, and you shouldn`t lose sight of.\par \par \uc2 Ian, how`re you thinking from your perch about these sort of next 10 months in the context of preserving these bulwarks of democratic representation?\par \par \uc2 IAN BASSIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PROTECT DEMOCRACY: Well, clearly, step one next week is the filibuster. And I think we should understand a couple of core facts about it. First, we are the only advanced democracy in the world that now effectively has a supermajority requirement for basic legislation.\par \par \uc2 Second, contrary to arguments that the filibuster fosters bipartisanship, it`s the exact opposite. We did some data analysis. And over the past 30 years, nearly 80 percent of bills blocked by the filibuster were bipartisan, with the average supported by five senators from the other party. Congress would have been much more bipartisan without the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Third, contrary to the argument, that without the filibuster, we`d see wild swings in legislation that would destabilize the country. We went the first 200 years as a nation without regular use of the filibuster and became the strongest country on Earth. It`s only in the past 12 years that the filibuster has been abused, that America has lost its shine in the world.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, we have been here before. After the passage of the 15th Amendment guaranteeing African Americans the right to vote, Southern Democrats impose all manner of restrictions from poll taxes to literacy test to Florida. So, in 1890, Republicans in the House passed the Lodge Act, which would have created a federal backstop to prevent Southern attacks on democracy. It was filibustered by segregationist in the Senate and died. And with it, democracy died in the American South for the next 75 years. We cannot let that happen again.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is a great precedent to cite there. And I had sort of encountered that before but had kind of forgotten about that specific 1890 fight over this. And I think also it`s important, Heather, I mean, my perspective on this is, in politics in general, and American politics and democratic struggle, small D democratic struggle, there`s no permanent victories, no permanent defeats.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s important for people to understand that in this context because the works got to continue. Like, I mean, this idea that people have to be engaged at the ground level, you know, in a state of kind of quasi- permanent emergency is a hard thing to hear but I think the truth.\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE: No, it`s absolutely true. I mean, this -- if you come -- I feel like the Black community understands this in a very visceral way, right? Because the idea that we have this democracy that has always been so free and fair and it`s been smashed away just in the past number of years obviously not our truth, right? It`s not the historical truth. It`s not the truth that we, we viscerally have lived in our parents and grandparents have lived. And so, that sort of constant vigilance is so necessary.\par \par \uc2 And if you zoom back, I think it`s really important I wrote about this today, in a Medium post, I think it`s so important to remember that the core logic of January 6 and of the big lie is a racial one, right? It is the lie of the racial zero-sum. President Biden alluded to this in his speech today, this idea that there`s only so much democracy to go around, that we are in this battle for supremacy.\par \par \uc2 And so, if people of color vote, that must be sort of stealing something that is rightfully the province of white people. Like, that`s the core logic. There was a great \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  study about the insurrectionist and what is their core ideology, what drove them. It wasn`t economic insecurity. One and four were business owners, right? It was this idea of the great replacement, the idea that as people of color gain more rights, those will come at the expense of white people.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, that idea is being sold, right, packaged and marketed. The same people who funded the Stop the Steal Movement and or funding ongoing attacks on our democracy are the ones who win financially when Republicans stay in power despite losing the majority of votes, because that is the economic page --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE: For where the moneyed elite that has been funding this racism as a way to drive their greed. And ultimately we are at a real inflection point. Do we understand that the racial zero-sum is -- has always been the Achilles of our democracy or not.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ian Bassin and Heather McGhee, great to have you both on this night. That is ALL IN for this evening. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220107ei1600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press Daily for December 22, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Josh Lederman, Gary Grumbach, Raf Sanchez \par \uc2 9,170 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday. Why weren`t we more prepared from Omicron? The Biden administration is only now ramping up testing, sending help for healthcare workers.\par \par \uc2 But at this point, with the speed Omicron is moving, is it already too late?\par \par \uc2 Plus, Americans, once again confused about what they can and should be doing this Holiday season to keep themselves and their family safe.\par \par \uc2 This as the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  researchers say they`ve developed a one-shot vaccine that can protect against all the variants, and SARS.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to talk to a public health expert about that development.\par \par \uc2 And later, we`ll hear from a Democratic senator about what happened in last night`s meeting between Senator Manchin and the rest of his Democratic colleagues, and from a Progressive House member on what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Hello, there. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is trying to ramp up the nation`s testing capacity and also tamp down the fears for vaccinated people as Omicron sweeps across the country.\par \par \uc2 But faced with a fast-growing number of COVID cases, long testing lines, and in many places, simply just not enough testing, it appears like once again, the country was caught a bit unprepared for this variant.\par \par \uc2 The new strategy the White House rolled out yesterday includes making half a billion at-home tests available for free to any American who wants one, something people have called for, since the start of this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But those tests won`t actually be available until next month after Holiday travel and potentially, after the worst of this current surge and it comes just a couple of weeks after the White House downplayed the need for at- home testing.\par \par \uc2 Here was Press Secretary Jen Psaki less than two weeks ago and then yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not just make them free and give them out and have them available everywhere?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, PRESS SECRETARY, WHITE HOUSE: Should we just send one to every American?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Then what happens -- then what happens if you -- if every American has one test? How much does that cost? And then what happens after that?\par \par \uc2 I would say there`s not a day that goes by that I don`t leave this podium and wish I would have said something with a greater context or more precision or additional information.\par \par \uc2 And that day, there was a lot of good questioning on testing. And during that briefing, I conveyed a lot of information about our expansion of testing about the 50 million tests that we were making available, about the 20,000 free testing sites.\par \par \uc2 And should I have included that additional context again, in that answer? Yes.\par \par \uc2 Going back, I wish I would have done that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Keep in mind, more than half a dozen cities and states have already begun handing out free at-home tests to their residents.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the President the Vice President, the CDC director, Dr. Fauci have all faced questions in recent days about why the U.S. seemed unprepared.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it a failure that you don`t have an adequate amount of tests for everyone to be able to get one if they need one right now?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, it`s not because COVID is spreading so rapidly if you notice.\par \par \uc2 It just -- it just happened almost overnight, just in the last month.\par \par \uc2 If I had told you four weeks ago that this was spread by -- a day-to-day basis, it will spread by 50, 100 percent, 200 percent, 500 percent, I think you will look to me and said Biden, what are you drinking? But that`s what it did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And while the White House continues encouraging Americans to get vaccinated and boosted, Israel is preparing to give the fourth dose of vaccines to residents 60 and older.\par \par \uc2 And in hopefully some encouraging news, cases in South Africa, which had been at the leading edge of the Omicron surge are now down 20 percent just one week, suggesting that maybe our own Omicron surge may not last all that long.\par \par \uc2 Josh Lederman is at the White House for us. Gary Grumbach is in DC, one of the places already distributing free tests to residents. And Raf Sanchez joins me from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Josh, let me start with you. You know, yesterday, the President framed the battle this way, essentially saying we have the tools to deal with this, and in -- implying that we have the tools to live with this.\par \par \uc2 But this issue of testing, they seemed overly defensive on this front because it`s frankly been a question that`s hung over this administration`s head ever since they took control of the -- of the response.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, there`s no question, Chuck that the White House is trying to thread the needle here.\par \par \uc2 They are trying to prepare Americans for a reality of living alongside this virus. And we heard that from President Biden yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Of course, they don`t want that to sound like failure or acquiescence to something that the U.S. was simply unprepared for, but both President Biden and Vice President Kamala Harris have acknowledged at this point that they did not anticipate the virility and the fact that this new -- this new strain would be quite as infectious as it has been.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Now, White House officials will argue that`s different than saying we were unprepared.\par \par \uc2 They say we have been preparing by stockpiling all kinds of equipment and PPE and gowns and other things in anticipation of a winter surge.\par \par \uc2 But of course, you know, what they didn`t prepare for was this half a million -- these 500 million tests that they`re now trying to speed up the process of getting out to Americans.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I think we want to mention is we`ve all been waiting for the results of the President`s latest COVID test after he had a close contact about five days ago, the White House telling us that today, he took another PCR test, the result just came back negative.\par \par \uc2 But it`s an indication, Chuck of how -- you know, the White House is struggling with their own issues with trying to stay safe from COVID within their own ranks, even as they`re trying to scramble to figure out new ways to protect Americans around the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I was just going to say, Josh, it does -- it did seem as if six months ago, they believe the vaccines were the best tool, not testing. Now, they seem to at least admit they needed more testing.\par \par \uc2 After this initial 500 million that they`re going to provide, is that it? Or is this going to become a recurring thing until the pandemic ends?\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: That`s the big question. Because obviously, everybody having one test is not going to be functional when we are basically being encouraged to test as regularly as possible.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House would say -- they can`t exactly predict what we`re going to need because this has been quite an evolving situation.\par \par \uc2 But I also think there`s kind of a Goldilocks element to this as well, Chuck, because you know, every time the White House takes additional drastic measures, new restrictions, new steps, they get a lot of criticism, obviously from the right, even from some on the left.\par \par \uc2 And of course, when they don`t do enough, there`s also that barrage of criticism that they`re getting right now.\par \par \uc2 So they`ve been trying to find that happy medium, and they`re discovering that so far, they`re not really pleasing anybody.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly. And I know this is a one-off here but there was another announcement today, they`re postponing the student loan.\par \par \uc2 They`re essentially keeping the moratorium in place for another three months.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean this is likely this moratorium is going to last through the election year?\par \par \uc2 I say that because I can`t imagine now that they postpone this until May, I think is the month -- there`s no way they reinstate it before November 22. Is there?\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: The White House is certainly leaving open the possibility that they`re going to extend this even further.\par \par \uc2 This had been really signposted just a week ago when the Education Department said we`re going to extend it for a week while we figure out the situation.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden himself announcing they`re going to extend it for months and saying in that statement he just released, that it`s possible that they will look to do that again depending on what the economic conditions are like early next year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I appreciate that, Josh.\par \par \uc2 Let me go over to Gary, who`s dealing with testing in DC is as good as any place to go to figure out these days of the testing capacity. What are you finding?\par \par \uc2 GARY GRUMBACH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Chuck. Yes, you know, we`ve seen over the past to the Holidays -- over the past 2 years, there`s been - - there`s been lines of testing sites every Holiday.\par \par \uc2 And this is no different before Christmas here. We`re seeing lines here in DC, and around the country.\par \par \uc2 And DC is seeing as you know 3000 cases just this weekend alone of Coronavirus. It`s a 350 percent change just from two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 So DC Health is really scrambling into action here. One of the things they`re doing is increasing the testing capabilities.\par \par \uc2 They`re starting a new program called Test yourself Express where they`re handing out one thousand rapid COVID tests at eight different libraries every single day across the city.\par \par \uc2 We were just at a center there just down the road and there were hundreds of people in line, probably more people in line than were actually they had the capacity.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a lot of frustration too among people. One, because Mayor Muriel Bowser lifted the mask mandate last month and then just this week, put it back in.\par \par \uc2 But, two, because they believe -- people believe in these lines, that the officials should have seen this coming. Here`s what some folks I talked to had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXA ALARCON, DC RESIDENT: Yes, I feel like there should be more available and this should already be anticipated. I mean, if I can expect it, I`m sure they can anticipate the quantities needed for different regions.\par \par \uc2 GLEN GERBINO, D.C. RESIDENT: Why is this -- why are we at this point? Why was it this pre-thought-through that this inevitably was going to happen, that they should have had tests in surplus a long time ago?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRUMBACH: And just announced in the past hour by Mayor Muriel Bowser, there`s now going to be a vaccination mandate in DC if -- in starting in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 If you want to enter a restaurant, a club, a shopping center, if you want to go to any of the indoor entertainment venues, you`re required to be vaccinated to do so starting in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to make life very uncomfortable in DC for people who are not vaccinated, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Gary, does the city have a definition of vaccinated with or without the booster?\par \par \uc2 GRUMBACH: So actually -- yes. So we would -- it seems like they`re doing right now and this is actually gone going as we speak.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like January 15 is going to be the date where they want people to have at least one dose and to answer these places. By February, mid- February, you need to be fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gary Grumbach in Washington, DC for us, Gary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me move over to Raf Sanchez in Tel Aviv. And it does seem as if what Israel ends up doing is something we eventually get there.\par \par \uc2 It was, after a meeting with the Israeli Prime Minister, that President Biden in August thought, oh, maybe we need to do boosters at five months.\par \par \uc2 Turns out that would have been the right decision.\par \par \uc2 It was "getting ahead of the science" or using Israel science to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 Instead, that didn`t happen, and we have this booster confusion.\par \par \uc2 So, Raf, explained to me how Israeli officials are communicating the need for a fourth dose of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, that`s right. Israel has been ahead of the curve at so many points during this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The recommendation right now from this \uc2 Israeli health ministry\uc2  panel is that the most vulnerable get this fourth dose.\par \par \uc2 So that is people over 60s, that is people with compromised immune systems, and that is people who work in the medical field.\par \par \uc2 The question, of course, will it eventually be rolled out that the entire population here in Israel is getting that fourth dose?\par \par \uc2 Now, I spoke earlier to an advisor to the Israeli Government, he said, there wasn`t a single smoking gun piece of research that led them to this decision on the fourth dose.\par \par \uc2 But there are two major factors.\par \par \uc2 One, Omicron is growing here as it is in the U.S. and they are trying to do what they can to get out in front of it.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is there is some evidence here in Israel that those third booster shots do wane in their effectiveness over time.\par \par \uc2 I want you to take a listen to what he had to say about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERAN SEGAL, PROFESSOR, WEIZMANN INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE: Yes, we are seeing some increase in the number of people being infected, as more and more time passes since they received the booster. So I would carefully say that I think there is a waning effect of the booster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: Now, those fourth doses still need to be given final approval by Israeli health officials. But that`s expected to happen very soon, possibly as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli Prime Minister welcoming that recommendation and he`s telling the public here, get ready to take that fourth shot as soon as you can, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Raf, what is the hospitalization situation there in Israel right now?\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: So far, it`s manageable. Israel is not seeing yet the kind of spike that we`ve seen in the UK and other countries, but the rate is grow - - it`s doubling every couple of days.\par \par \uc2 They are expecting to see growing numbers.\par \par \uc2 There is some feeling here in Israel, that the Omicron may be slightly milder than Delta.\par \par \uc2 But of course, Chuck, if you have lots and lots and lots of people with you know, positive with virus it doesn`t matter. You will still end up with a lot of people in the hospital, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: True. But it`s a pretty well-vaccinated country. Josh, Gary, and Raf thank you all for getting started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up.\par \par \uc2 What is the next month going to look like as Omicron sweeps through?\par \par \uc2 Plus, what we`re learning about a vaccine that the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  is developing that would protect against every COVID variant there`s been?\par \par \uc2 I`ll ask a medical expert about that ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, we`ll dig into what we did and did not learn after the National Archives released over a thousand confidential documents related to the assassination of John F. Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Wait, wait, wait, wait a minute, I -- he asked the first question, did I get my test today?\par \par \uc2 Yes, I did before I walked in here. OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was it negative?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I haven`t gotten the result yet. I -- just I walked over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we actually do know is resolved. Now, it was negative. That was President Biden earlier today taking questions after a Supply Chain Task Force meeting.\par \par \uc2 He was obviously talking about yet another PCR test and as we told you, it was negative.\par \par \uc2 It`s at least his third negative result this week. But the incident reveals just how prevalent COVID is even around the President.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands if not millions of Americans have been exposed to Omicron and many more will be exposed in the coming days and weeks.\par \par \uc2 The difference, very few Americans have easy access to testing like the President does.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Dr. Ashish Jha, he`s dean of Brown University School of Public Health.\par \par \uc2 So Dr. Jha, this -- I`m going to basically treat you like a -- you know, ask you anything type of thing about what we`ve learned just in the last day because I think there`s a lot of confusion out there.\par \par \uc2 Number one, there`s yet more studies that seem to indicate Omicron is less severe. How should the public take in that information?\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA, DEAN, BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH: Yes. So, Chuck, there is I would say a moderate amount of data that it -- may be less severe. That`s good. We want it to be a sort of mild as possible.\par \par \uc2 There is also some data out of the UK and Denmark that maybe it`s not that much less severe.\par \par \uc2 The big picture point here in my mind is if you are fully vaccinated and boosted, you are going to have a mild course with this virus.\par \par \uc2 If you are fully vaccinated but not boosted and you`re in a high-risk group, I would still assume that you could end up having a pretty bad breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re unvaccinated you really are on your own here like you`re really taking risks.\par \par \uc2 It`s not going to be mild enough to get you through this without potentially getting you into trouble.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, what -- how much comfort should a vaccinated boosted person have traveling in the next month?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Pretty good. Like I think domestic travel`s pretty reasonable, wear a high-quality mask because you don`t want to have a breakthrough. Even if it`s mild, it`ll ruin your travel plans.\par \par \uc2 So I would wear a high-quality mask. I think that`s important.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, if you`re visiting high-risk people, you know, take extra precautions, use testing if you can get the tests.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think a vaccinated boosted person needs to be holed up at home right now.\par \par \uc2 This is not March of 2020. We are in a very different place. TODD: All right. At -- the other news -- the news out of South Africa it`s just how fast cases now are dropping.\par \par \uc2 As fast as they went up is especially going down it was described I believe by one of their leading epidemiologists sort of claiming the north face of Mount Everest and now we`re going down the south face. Go -- up fast down fast.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we about to see a burning through this country that fast too? And is there an upside to this if this is how we -- how Omicron goes here in the United States?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes. So first of all, we don`t know if it`ll play out the same way, obviously. I`m hoping that it will.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JHA: That I`m hoping that we`re not going to see a big spike up and then a high plateau but instead a climb down pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 Look, the dynamics of the virus are going to be different in South Africa than they are in the U.S., right?\par \par \uc2 They`re in a different season, they`ve got a different population immunity profile so I wouldn`t bet on it but I`m hoping it is.\par \par \uc2 If it gets through us pretty quickly, yes, like it`ll cause a lot of disruption.\par \par \uc2 But then the spike will be over and we`ll be able to kind of recoup from that.\par \par \uc2 That would be much, much better than an ongoing sustained high level of infection.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The issue of a fourth booster shot.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hotez brought it up, actually, on this show yesterday, the Israelis have rolled out a fourth booster, both of them citing the same evidence that they`ve been seeing scientifically is that this booster wanes quickly after about the third month.\par \par \uc2 First of all, why is -- why would that be the case in our -- and should we start treating this as the flu vaccine?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, those are two good questions.\par \par \uc2 So first of all, very little data so far on what Israel is sort of put out. Israel has been terrific, so their data has been really the guiding post for most people in the world.\par \par \uc2 And so if they`re doing this and we have to take this very, very seriously, my take is we`re going to have to make some decisions about our goals here.\par \par \uc2 Antibodies weighing after every vaccine, that`s just a normal part of -- an after every infection.\par \par \uc2 The question will remain. Are we going to be able to protect people from severe illness and death after two shots or after three shots?\par \par \uc2 That is not a question that I`ve seen at all begun to be answered.\par \par \uc2 So is there an annual booster? It might be.\par \par \uc2 Do I think we`re going to be taking shots every six months forever? No.\par \par \uc2 But somewhere in between, you know, we`re going to find out more in the next couple of months. We just got to see what the data says.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s interesting you say that. I guess we have to make a decision as a society. What are we tolerating is if the vaccines prevent death and hospitalization for a five-year period, but they don`t prevent you from getting -- look, I have a -- I have a COVID positive on my house.\par \par \uc2 Her strep throat was worse than what she`s gone through this week.\par \par \uc2 It`s a pretty mild case, vaccinated not boosted.\par \par \uc2 If that`s what this has got to be for the vaccinated you know, do you -- do you -- know how much do you worry about boosters?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes. That is the fundamental question.\par \par \uc2 There are two things really. We want to prevent severe illness and death, and we want to prevent long COVID, right?\par \par \uc2 Because there is also the -- some proportion of people who end up -- my best read of the data right now on vaccinated people who have breakthroughs is they`re much less likely to have long-term complications of any kind.\par \par \uc2 At some point, we`re going to say our society is pretty immunized, pretty protected.\par \par \uc2 Maybe the highest risk nursing home residents continue to get a booster every six months.\par \par \uc2 But maybe for the rest of us, knowing that if you have an infection, this can be mild, you might be at home for two days, but you`ll be back in the office five days later.\par \par \uc2 Maybe that`s a level of infection that we tolerate with lots and lots of other viruses.\par \par \uc2 That maybe we`re thinking about whether we were willing to do that for COVID.\par \par \uc2 I think we should be willing to do that for COVID.\par \par \uc2 If we can get to COVID -- get COVID to be like a bad cold, or a mild version of the flu, that`s a victory in my mind.\par \par \uc2 I think that we stop obsessing about it that we are right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what is Paxlovid and the approval of that mean?\par \par \uc2 JHA: It`s going to be helping a lot --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that kind of flu? Yes.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes, it`ll help a lot. Especially for high-risk people, you can imagine, you get an infection, you`re having a bad one, you`ve got high fevers, it`s day two, you call your doctor, you get on Paxlovid, and boom, you`re going to see actually reduce the likelihood that it`ll get worse.\par \par \uc2 It should be a really important part of our toolset.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Our friends at defense, one is reporting that the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  is developing a vaccine for all of it, a one-shot.\par \par \uc2 This feels like it`s out of nowhere because we keep having Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  tell us well, we`re working on this, we`re working on that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  has a single vaccine against all of this.\par \par \uc2 First of all, what do you make of this news? And if that`s the case, how soon are the rest of us going to see this available?\par \par \uc2 JHA: Yes. So this is a phase one trial.\par \par \uc2 Phase one`s very early, we`re just sort of testing out dosing, we don`t know how well it`s going to work against all the existing variants, and whether it`s going to work against every conceivable variant in the future, I don`t think we know that either.\par \par \uc2 That said, this is phenomenal research, we should absolutely invest in it, we should figure out how sustainable it is.\par \par \uc2 And if it ends up being as good as it sounds, then we want to ramp up production and make it available to everybody.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very, very good research group working on this. Bless to see where this goes. It`s really, really early stuff right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And final last question is Delta.\par \par \uc2 Delta has been horrendous. I don`t think we fully appreciate how destructive Delta has been in this country. It`s still got people -- killing people today.\par \par \uc2 That I think, I don`t know, we`ll know this but over time, we`ll probably learn that the current deaths now are more Delta-related than Omicron- related.\par \par \uc2 Is Omicron going to be able to make Delta go away? Is it going to put the Delta fire out? Or if Omicron surges and receives so fast, do we still have to live with Delta?\par \par \uc2 JHA: It`s a really good question. That -- one of the big questions we`ve all been thinking about is, right, as you said, does Omicron snuff out Delta, or does Delta stick around, doing that -- of having to deal with two?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JHA: So far, the early day from -- data from the UK is that Omicron has taken off in the UK but Delta is still there.\par \par \uc2 One is not out -- not eliminating the other.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have to see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 I do think Delta will also recede once we get into the New Year because it`ll have infected so many people.\par \par \uc2 But between Delta and Omicron, I`m really worried that we`re probably didn`t have as many a third of all Americans, or quarter a third of all Americans getting infected in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that all lead to herd immunity?\par \par \uc2 JHA: It`s kind of --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 JHA: It`s kind of -- it`s certainly going to make a big difference in terms of getting high, high levels of population immunity.\par \par \uc2 And that means future waves of a variant will have much less impact on us because of all the immunity we`re building up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Dr. Jha, appreciate you playing the ask-you-anything game with me. That a -- there`s a lot of these questions in the last 24 hours. You were very helpful. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 JHA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up. President Biden and the Democratic leader say they`re not giving up on passing the Build Back Better bill, and there was a small sign it`s possible at last night`s special caucus meeting that they might find a way forward.\par \par \uc2 We`ll hear from a Senator who was in that meeting next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Turning to Capitol Hill, now, Senate Democrats held a special virtual caucus meeting last night to plot their way forward on what is the stalled Build Back agenda.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin did join the call just two days after he said he could not support the bill.\par \par \uc2 From what sources have been telling us here at NBC, the call wasn`t quite a Festivus-style airing of grievances if you will.\par \par \uc2 But Manchin did reiterate his concerns with the bill.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer reiterated that the Senate will vote on Build Back Better soon, even though it appears doomed to the fail at least first version for it now. But the very fact that he was on the call suggests he`s still on board to pass something which for close Manchin watchers is not surprising.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now one of the Senators who`s on that call, Minnesota Democrat Tina Smith.\par \par \uc2 Senator Smith, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TINA SMITH (D-MN): Great to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with, so, Manchin`s on the call, everybody feels as if at least there`s a -- you guys are having a conversation. What does this mean going forward? Do you still think the caucus wants to have a vote just to say they tried or is it time to actually put together a bill that you know Manchin can support?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, well, so, I think we heard Senator Manchin say last night what he said publicly, which is I just can`t get there. So, what we need to know from Senator Manchin is where can he get to? Like where does he want to end up with this? I heard yesterday on the call from my colleagues a real sense of determination to do the best that we can and a sense of urgency to get through with this.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I also kind of think that in this moment, people are pretty mad on all sides and sometimes the more you talk, the worse it gets. And so I think right now it`s not bad that we take a little bit of time (INAUDIBLE). And then you come back together again and there are some signs of optimism that we are going to be able to find a path forward. Not everything that I would want, not everything that the progressives would want but some things that would be really good for the country and that`s what we have to stay focused on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, tell me your priorities then. This whole thing is not going to survive, we know that. What in your mind has to be in there and, you know, where you sit there and you`re like, boy, this was a waste of time if we didn`t do this?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. Well, there`s a lot in Build Back Better that I think are really great. But here are two places where I think you could find some important common ground. I worked really, really hard on expanding access to child care that is affordable. By all accounts, this is something that Senator Manchin thinks is a good idea. If we have 50 votes in the Senate for that, it`s a priority of the president`s.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just get that done so that we can help families go back to work and make sure that children have a great place to be. We have a terrible shortage of child care in this country and it is hurting families and hurting our economy. And, of course, the other issue that I had some hope for is taking strong action on climate and carbon emissions. The reports are that Senator Manchin left that on the table in the last discussions he had with the White House.\par \par \uc2 That gives me hope because this is the number one thing that we have to do to reduce carbon emissions, create jobs and put the United States on the forefront of the clean energy transition in terms of technology and innovation and building domestic supply chains.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did you think that that was the reason why Senator Manchin can`t really support this bill, that there`s too much climate in here and he thinks it`s too impactful to West Virginia`s economy?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I mean, to tell you the truth, I worked really hard on the clean electricity plan which would have made great headway in reducing emissions and Senator Manchin said to me, I just can`t get there. He seemed to be saying, you have the same message on Sunday, I guess it was, when he went on FOX News. So, now the question is where can he get? That`s what we`re all trying to figure out.\par \par \uc2 And I take Senator Manchin at his word when he says that he wants to protect domestic energy resources and energy sources and that`s what clean power is all about. So I`m hopeful that we can find a path forward there. But there can`t just hope. It`s got to be action and we`ve got to finally get this done, for God`s sake.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Is there any part of Build Back Better you`d say, you know what, let`s take that out of the reconciliation process? Because Manchin clearly would like to at least try to get it out of reconciliation. I think he`s probably on an island. I`m not going to -- I get that. Is there any part of Build Back Better, though, that you think it`s worth putting Republicans on the record on?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: In terms of the votes that we would ask people to take, I mean, there is so much on this bill, in this bill that the vast majority of Americans support it. I mean, this is what President Biden ran and won on. Let`s talk about paid family leave, which is so desperately needed for American families and is something that is very difficult for small businesses to offer. Let`s talk about lowering prescription drug prices which has been something we`ve been working on for a long, long time. That`s in Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 Let`s see whether Republicans are just going to vote no on that even though that`s what their constituents want. Let`s talk about the child tax credit which is helping to lift so many kids out of poverty and is helping families pay their bills right now. Now, those are all the things that I love about this bill. The question is what`s going to get 50 votes. And your point is a really good one, Chuck, because none of those provisions as far as I know has a single Republican vote. And that is a big problem.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president just announced that they`re extending the moratorium on student loan debt. Student loan debt, you heard it on the campaign trail, look, you talked to a lot of younger voters. If this isn`t dealt with, this might be a demoralizing effect on younger voters turning out. It`s put off to May. The cynic in me assumes it will be put off to `22. But how does this get resolved? How do we get to a point where there is some relief but there isn`t a free ride at the same time?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, yes. Well, two things. I think the president is absolutely right to do what he did today. But, again, I have to point out in Build Back Better there are provisions that would dramatically expand Pell Grants which haven`t been -- you know, they`ll be come close to meeting the current needs of today`s students.\par \par \uc2 So, if we pass Build Back Better, we would be able to nearly double Pell Grants and that`s going to make a huge difference for college affordability, for people going forward. Now the folks who have occurred this massive debt over the last, you know, 10, 15, 20 years still need assistance and still need some help, and the point is that`s good for them and their families but it`s good for our economy, too. Because they`re going to be using that money to, you know, grow their families and buy houses and do the things that we want people to be able to do in this country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, is this Build Back Better, sort of this is a get it done by the State of the Union or it`s not going to get done? Do you see any magical end date here or we just go until we go?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, one thing I know about negotiating is sometimes, as I said, the more you talk, the worse it gets. I think that we need to figure out what`s doable and then do it and not extend this much longer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think the best advice you gave is that everybody needs a cooling off period. That`s why we have the holiday break. Anyway, Senator Smith, appreciate you coming on. I hope you can have a little bit of rest during the holidays.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I`m looking forward to and I`m just glad the dogs didn`t bark during the middle of this interview. So they`re all around me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it means they`re paying attention. That`s good to hear. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to hear from the other side of the Capitol about what`s next. A leading member of the Progressive caucus on the House side joins me.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:26]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The most liberal members of Congress did not hold back their criticism of Joe Manchin earlier this week. But now the leader of the Congressional Progressive Caucus is opening the door to reengaging with the Senate`s chief moderate. Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal told the "Washington Post" that she called Manchin yesterday morning and asked him to go back to the October framework released from the White House and specify what he cannot support.\par \par \uc2 And the "Washington Post`s" Greg Sergeant said that Jayapal acknowledged there are some things Manchin would never support like the child tax credit. So where do we go from here? Where do progressives go from here?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is another one of the House`s leading progressives, California Democrat Ro Khanna who represents much of Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to see you. I want to start with something that the president said about Manchin and the House Progressives yesterday. Let me play the sound bite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Joe went on TV today, I know on TV or not, I`m told he was speaking to a liberal caucus in the House and said Joe Biden didn`t mislead you, I misled you.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Were you a part of that meeting where Manchin talked to you and what can you tell us about it, if you were?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): There was no such meeting. And Chuck, I don`t think it`s productive to speculate people`s motives or assign blame. What the American people want is for us to make progress, and what we ought to do, as Representative Jayapal said, is sit down with the president, with Senator Manchin, and see what can we agree on. We can agree on universal preschool as I understand it, on child care. We can agree on expanding healthcare coverage and then let`s get to something that can pass. And I think that`s what Democrats need to do going forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you about what I also noted in the lead in which is this issue of the child care tax credit, do you think it`s going to end up off the table and end up a separate issue? What is the future of this? Because it sounds like Congresswoman Jayapal came away with her talks with Senator Manchin believing that`s going to be the highest hurdle to clear.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I certainly hope not, Chuck. Let me just put it in very simple terms. Here`s what it means. It means 300 bucks a month for families that are working class, middle class who have kids. That means can you buy your kids Christmas presents. That means can you get them winter clothes for the holidays? Now Senator Manchin has a problem because there are a few couples who make up to $400,000 who may get some portion of that credit, that we`re willing to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 And we can look at what the appropriate thresholds are. But the idea that someone making 50 grand, 40 grand who has kids shouldn`t get some help, I just think that`s something we all should agree on, especially in the Christmas season. I mean, I just saw "Christmas Carol" at the (INAUDIBLE). We need a little more Christmas spirit here and child tax bill is one of the biggest things we can do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So then the definition it means testing you is if you want to lower the threshold from, say, 400 to what? 250, 300? That is something that you think is negotiable that maybe that answers Senator Manchin`s concerns?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Chuck, As I understand it right now the child tax credit to get the full credit it ends at $150,000.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: But there are a few cases over making over $200,000 as a couple where you still get some benefits. I`m willing to negotiate that. Look, I have a high cost of living area in Silicon Valley. You know the money doesn`t go as far as in a place like Silicon Valley as West Virginia, but I`m willing to negotiate that. And that`s where we are open to compromise, we always have been. But let`s not just get rid of the child tax credit which every economist said has cut child poverty and actually one of the things I`m proudest I`ve ever done and voted for it then.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I think what`s frustrating to watch in this debate is there seems to be bipartisan agreement on the idea, just not bipartisan agreement on how to do it. And I think I`m always, I`ve been curious, do you think more of an attempt should have been made to see if there could have been a growing, just taking the child care tax credit.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got a Mitt Romney and a Marco Rubio who are supportive of the idea. Do you think more effort should have been made to try to peel that off the reconciliation or no?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I just don`t think we could get the 10 votes. You know, if we could I mean 10 more Republican votes, if you can -- if Senator Manchin or Senator Rubio could come up with a package that has 60 Senate votes, I would be willing to look at it and consider it but they always say let`s have bipartisanship and then they have one or two Republican senators that gets us to 53, 54, and that`s not really real.\par \par \uc2 So the only way we`re actually going to get it done right now is through reconciliation. But, of course, if you can get 60 senators on something.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: That`s a compromise, I`m willing to look at it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nobody likes to sit here and pick and choose what`s left. A lot of people argue what`s left in Build Back Better are essential.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s no more picking and choosing. But I want to read you something that Robinson Meyer wrote in "The Atlantic" today. And he said this, "Something has to pass. Some version of a climate bill, any version of it. If you believe what Democrats say about climate change and virtually any social policy including the child tax credit, it`s worth sacrificing so that decarbonization can become law."\par \par \uc2 Is there any part of you that says, yes, we got -- if you got Manchin on something here, take it and run?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Absolutely. I think that climate is the highest priority. I do think the president has executive authority under executive order to be able to increase Treasury federal financing along and significantly fund climate policy, but if we can get significant climate provisions in my view, that`s the highest priority and that`s where I think Senator Manchin shouldn`t dilute it because it`s not going to affect in my view West Virginia negatively.\par \par \uc2 Most of the bill is an investment in alternative energy, renewable energy, innovation, competing with China that Senator Manchin has always said he`s for that. So I agree that`s the highest priority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Any regrets about untethering the infrastructure bill from Build Back Better?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: No. It was -- it`s a good bill that passed. It`s a bill that`s going to protect the rural. It`s going to help get lead pipes out of people`s homes and infrastructure. I`m proud to have supported it. I think the idea of leverage was always overdone. You don`t -- people in the House aren`t going to have leverage over a sitting senator. I think what we achieved is we got the president to a compromised framework and I still think that ought to inform the debate of what the Build Back Better bill that passes should be like.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Your optimistic Christmas spirit, perhaps it will get contagious.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Ro Khanna, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you. Happy holidays to you and your viewers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the JFK files. What we learned and maybe more importantly what we did not learn in the latest small release of documents about John F. Kennedy`s assassination.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:39]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We are waiting on three juries to return verdicts in some high-profile trials this week. In New York a jury is deliberating on the fate of Ghislaine Maxwell, the disgraced socialite accused of trafficking young girls for Jeffrey Epstein. Four of Maxwell`s accusers testified in the trial, painting a graphic picture of her alleged actions, grooming them for Epstein`s sexual abuse or assaulting them herself.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell pled not guilty to the charges and did not take the stand in her own defense. She faces up to 80 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 In Minneapolis, a jury is deliberating over two manslaughter charges in the trial of former police officer Kim Potter. She shot and killed Daunte Wright, a 20-year-old black man, during a traffic stop in April. Potter testified that she meant to pull her taser but instead drew and shot her gun. The jury may already be at an impasse. They asked the judge yesterday what happens if they cannot reach consensus.\par \par \uc2 And after hearing three months of testimony -- yes, three months of testimony -- in California, the jury is taking a break from deliberations today in the trial of Theranos founder Elizabeth Holmes. Holmes is charged with multiple counts of fraud and conspiracy for allegedly lying to investors about her blood testing start-up. She pled not guilty to those charges. Jury deliberations do resume tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk a little bit of JFK files right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. One week ago, the Biden administration released a trove of previously classified documents, over 1,000, on the 1963 assassination of President John F. Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Papers did include filings and memos from various agencies, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , State Department, and yes, the CIA. Among the papers are tips and leads the agencies chased in their investigations of the tragedy and insights into the movements of Lee Harvey Oswald in the months before the assassination.\par \par \uc2 This JFK document dump, while the largest since the files were first mandated to be released, it`s still a little bit sort of -- just a small percentage of the files that have yet to be declassified.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Jeff Morley. He`s author of the book "The Ghost: The Secret Life of a CIA Spymaster James Jesus Angleton" and editor of the JFK Facts blog.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, I`ve known you for quite some time. I know how well you pour yourself into this. So let me ask the bigger question. Why isn`t everything released? We were told these things were going to be released at a 50-year anniversary. We`re not there yet. Why?\par \par \uc2 JEFFERSON MORLEY, JFK FACTS BLOG EDITOR: The agency, the CIA, has something to hide. That`s the only plausible conclusion you can draw, I think. You know, Congress put a 25-year deadline on this material, enacting a law in 1992 saying everything should be released by 2017. In 2017, the CIA director, Mike Pompeo, went to President Trump and said we can`t possibly release this. And Trump acquiesced to their demands and there was four more years of secrecy.\par \par \uc2 The same question came to President Biden in October 2021, same result. He acquiesced to the CIA`s demands that most of this material remain secret. What we saw last week was about 10 percent of the 15,000 documents that continue to have redactions in them. So why is it taking so long? The CIA doesn`t want to obey the law is my conclusion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, the easy hypothesis to me, no matter where you are on what the -- whether there was a conspiracy or not, it certainly looks as if the CIA doesn`t want to admit what it knew about Oswald before the assassination.\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: That is true.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There seems to be no other hypothesis to come up with. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: Yes, and two documents came out last week that confirmed that. These are two cables that were written about Lee Harvey Oswald in October 1963, six weeks before the assassination. And for the first time in 58 years we have a complete unexpurgated version of the CIA`s pre- assassination file on Oswald. It took them 58 years to come totally clean on who in the CIA knew about Oswald before the assassination.\par \par \uc2 So I think you`re absolutely right, the question that they don`t -- the question that they want to avoid, it seems to me, to delay, is what did we know about Oswald before the assassination. The CIA really doesn`t want to talk about that. That`s what we found out last week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, they don`t -- is it possible they were trying to make him an asset or he was an asset, and they don`t want to admit he turned out to be an asset, oh, my god, we didn`t know he was going to do this?\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: Well, that`s what`s so interesting about the two cables that were released in their entirety for the first time last week. One of those cables was a cable to Mexico City written by six top CIA officers, and I mean top, the deputy assistant, director, the foreign intelligence liaison officer, the chief of the Mexico desk, the chief of operations in the Western hemisphere. So that`s the sensitive part of all of this is, how did top CIA officials know so much about Oswald before the assassination and do nothing? OK?\par \par \uc2 That`s the question. Some people say, well, they were just incompetent, they were asleep at the wheel. Other people say, to me it looks more like they were running an operation. But given that we don`t have all the records.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: We really can`t answer the question. The CIA needs to come clean on that. I mean, I think that`s the story here. It`s common sense.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: Why wouldn`t the CIA want to release all of this material?\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. Let me ask you this. Why is William Manchester`s interviews with Jackie and Bobby -- why is that in control of the CIA or the federal government, excuse me? Why are they in control of those interviews?\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: Because when Congress wrote the JFK Records Act in 1992, they included a provision that nothing in the law would override the deed of gifts. And so when William Manchester gave those papers, those transcripts of his interviews with Jackie and \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  in 1964, he put on them a 75-year time limit. And so the law does not overrule the deed of gift provision.\par \par \uc2 So that material, according to the Deed of Gift, will stay secret until 2039. And the reason why that`s significant is, it`s known that Jackie Kennedy and \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  did not believe the Warren Commission. They did not believe President Kennedy was killed by one man alone. They privately thought that he had been killed by his enemies.\par \par \uc2 [14:00:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Jeff Morley who basically is about the best person you can go to, to separate fact from a fiction when it comes to JFK assassination. Appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 MORLEY: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211223ehcm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Role of GOP Lawmakers in Trump Coup Attempt; The January 6th Investigation and Trump`s Pardons; Climate Change and the New Normal; Interview with Senator Bernie Sanders (I-VT) about Striking Kellogg`s Workers, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,663 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:05]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): I don`t if I spoke with him in the morning or not - - I just don`t know. I`d have to go back and -- I mean, I don`t, I don`t know that -- when those conversations happened, but --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The calls were coming from inside the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I think that Congressman Jordan may well be a material witness.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What Jim Jordan did on January 6th and Mow mark Meadows was in the thick of it.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEADOWS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: I`m not aware of anybody in the West Wing that had any advance knowledge that the security was going to be breached at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight the latest on the investigation into the insurrection and how Roger Stone plans to plead the Fifth before the committee.\par \par \uc2 Then, the Build Back Better bill in serious jeopardy in the Senate, and Bernie Sanders is here on that. And historic storms and historic warmth in the middle of December. The new normal under climate change when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump is the only U.S. president to be impeached twice, and while he has always had loyalists and sycophants in Congress, his first impeachment over a threatening phone call he made to the Ukrainian president asking him to dig up dirt on the Bidens, is when his Republican attack dogs really began to close ranks around him, at least in the way that we`ve come to think of them.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a particularly telling anecdote that would be funny if it were not so desperate and sad. Back in 2019, in the middle of that first impeachment, Donald Trump was unhappy with the way his press coverage was going. He thought Republicans were not doing a good enough job counterprogramming his impeachment hearings.\par \par \uc2 In a meeting with the right-wing House Freedom Caucus on October 22nd, then president Trump reportedly said something to the effect of, quote, "We need to be tough because Democrats are fighting dirty and have been fighting dirty. We try to take the high road in general, but maybe it`s time to take the gloves off," which itself is a hilarious thing to say.\par \par \uc2 Now Republican Congressman Matt Gaetz of Florida, who is one of Trump`s most self-debasing simps in Congress, heard the boss` call to arms and the next day Congressman Gaetz and dozens of his Republican colleagues stormed a secure room in the basement of the House, where an impeachment hearing was being held. Most did not have clearance to be in the area, and they brought cell phones and other smart devices, which are strictly prohibited.\par \par \uc2 And they were demanding, quote, "transparency," but it is clear the real purpose of the stunt was to put on a show for an audience of one, to let Donald Trump know that they were willing to fight for him on TV. Did I do it good enough? Was this OK? To take the gloves off.\par \par \uc2 The group ordered pizza, and they ate fast food and generally made a circus of things, unless you believe for a moment this was a genuine spur of the moment protest. Congressman Gaetz actually issued a press release the day before, still available on his Web site. And as press releases often do, it can take guidance for where the media should station its TV cameras. That`s what, of course, this is really all about. Performing outrage on TV so that Donald Trump would like them.\par \par \uc2 Gaetz`s press release also included a list of RSVPs including two familiar names, Congressman Jim Jordan of Ohio and then-Congressman Mark Meadows of North Carolina. Both men are die hard Trump supporters, so their presence does not come as much of a surprise except here`s the thing. They both sat on the House Oversight Committee, OK? That`s the committee that was conducting that -- the committee that was allowed to be in the room. They already had access to the hearing they were crashing to complain about a lack of access.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: It`s finally reached a boiling point where members just said they are so frustrated at the idea that they can`t be a part of this and see what`s going on, and so we`re at a standstill here. We`ll see what happens with today`s -- today`s witness.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again at the time, Congressman Jordan was actually the ranking member of the House oversight committee. He was already part of the process he was crashing. But, again, the most important thing was to please Donald Trump, to let him know that, I`m fighting for you. And in fact, we later learned that Trump was made aware of the entire sham protest ahead of time. I`m sure he thought, nice job.\par \par \uc2 A few months later as a reward for his blind support, Trump appointed Mark Meadows to be the White House chief of staff, and Jordan and his freedom caucus buddies now had a direct line to the White House. And together over the next year, Meadows and Jordan worked behind the scenes to engineer a coup and keep Donald Trump in power.\par \par \uc2 As "The New York Times" reports in a great new piece today, Meadows, Jordan and a handful of others, quote, "moved well beyond words and into action. They bombarded the Justice Department with dubious claims of voting irregularities. They pressured members of state legislatures to conduct audits that would cast doubt on the election results. They plotted to disrupt the certification of January 6th of Biden`s victory."\par \par \uc2 Leading up to the election, Congressman Jordan worked to sow doubt in its integrity. He accused Democrats of causing, quote, "chaos" with mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:01]\par \par \uc2 After the election, he worked tirelessly hard and hand with Donald Trump in his administration to try to steal a free and fair election, to overrule the will of the voters and to end American democracy as we know it. As the "Times" reports, two days after the AP called the race for Joe Biden, Jordan huddled with top Trump staff at campaign headquarters, and quote, "The group settled on a strategy that would become a blueprint for Mr. Trump`s supporters in Congress. Hammer home the idea the election was tainted, announce legal actions being taken by the campaign and bolster the case with allegations of fraud."\par \par \uc2 All those allegations being false. In December of last year, a month after Joe Biden was declared the winner, Jordan was still arguing that Trump should not give up. When asked if Trump should concede, Jordan told a reporter for CNN, quote, "No. No way, no way, no way. We should still try to figure out exactly what took place here. And as I said, that includes, I think, debates on the House floor potentially on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Ah, yes, January 6th. Go get them. Because on January 6th, Jordan of course was one of 147 members of Congress who voted not to seat the electors, to throw out the votes of the American people, and replace them with the votes of the people of Congress. To fire the voters and install themselves as the deciders of an election. And since then, news about Jim Jordan`s active involvement in fomenting the coup has been trickling out.\par \par \uc2 He admits to having spoken to Donald Trump once after the January 6th insurrection, but there are reports they had multiple calls that day. What did they talk about? Just watch what happened when one local reporter just tried to press him on the exact timeline.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First off, yes or no, did you speak with President Trump on January 6th?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes. I mean, I speak -- I spoke with the president last week. I speak with the president all the time. I spoke with him on January 6th. I mean, I talk with President Trump all the time. And that`s, that`s, I don`t think that`s unusual. I would expect members of Congress to talk with the president of the United States when they`re trying to get done the things they told the voters in their district to do. I -- I`m actually kind of amazed sometimes that people keep asking this -- of course, I talk to the president all the time. I talk to him, like I said, I talked to him last week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On January 6th did you speak with him before, during or after the Capitol was attacked?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I would have to go -- I -- I spoke with him that day after? I think after? I don`t know if I spoke with him in the morning or not. I just don`t know. I would have to go back -- I mean, I don`t -- I don`t know that when -- when those conversations happened. But what I know is I spoke with him all the time.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that seems entirely on the up and up, doesn`t it? And knowing all that, Republican leader Kevin McCarthy still put that guy, Jordan, or tried to put that guy on the committee investigating the insurrection, the guy who worked directly with Trump to sow doubt in the election, the guy who spoke to him on the day of the insurrection itself.\par \par \uc2 Now House Speaker Nancy Pelosi vetoed Jordan`s placement on the committee. There was some tut-tutting among the civility crew in D.C. And Congressman Liz Cheney, who sits on the committee, elaborated why his appointment would be problematic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I think that Congressman Jordan may well be a material witness. He`s somebody who was involved in a number of meetings in the lead-up to what happened on January 6th, involved in planning for January 6th, certainly for the objections that day as he`s said publicly. So he may well be a material witness.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yesterday we learned even more. Congressman Jordan admitted that on the day before the insurrection, on the 5th, he forwarded a text message from a right-wing lawyer to Mark Meadows with guidance to have Mike Pence simply throw out the results of the election on the 6th. Up until the very end, he was actively plotting a coup with the White House. And he`s not alone, most definitely not.\par \par \uc2 There are about 2,000 texts that Mark Meadows turned over to the committee, many of which are from lawmakers about how to overturn the election. But this is what years of blind loyalty to Donald Trump gets you. Congressman Jordan may very well be a material witness in a congressional investigation. Mark Meadows has just been held in contempt of Congress. There may be a referral. There will be a referral to the Justice Department because he`s now refusing to cooperate with the same congressional investigation, something Jim Jordan wants you to know he is very upset about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Mark Meadows is our former colleague. He is a good man, and he is my friend. And this is -- this is as wrong as it gets. And I think deep down, everyone knows it. But your -- your lust for power, your lust to get your opponents is so intense, you don`t care.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lust for power? Lust for power? That dude, that guy you saw, tried to install a ruler over the will of the American people. That`s lust for power. Meadows is now reportedly on the outs with Trump. More on that in a moment. The committee is continuing its investigation, getting a lot of cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Just today it subpoenaed a man named Phil Waldron who briefed Mark Meadows and members of Congress on truly bonkers bogus election fraud theories leading up to January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:03]\par \par \uc2 He`s the guy who put together that completely bananas PowerPoint presentation that Meadows had titled "Election Fraud, Foreign Interference, and Options for 6 Jan." So as long as the subpoenas keep coming, Jim Jordan may want to start refreshing his memory of when exactly he spoke to Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Luke Broadwater covers Congress, "New York Times," and he was on the byline for that new piece I cited titled "Meadows and the Band of Loyalists: How They Fought to keep Trump in power. Ashley Parker is the White House bureau chief for the "Washington Post." Her latest article is titled, "Role as Trump`s gatekeeper puts Meadows in legal jeopardy and at odds with Trump."\par \par \uc2 Luke, let me start with you. Just some of the reporting from that piece which I thought was fascinating. You know, we saw what they were saying, you know, in public, but there was a lot of effort being undertaken by this group to sort of further any kind of theory they could that would result in the election not actually being called for Joe Biden and counted and made official.\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Yes, that`s right, Chris. So I think what we hopefully demonstrated with this piece was that for at least a half dozen congressmen -- and the number may be more -- the claims of election fraud went beyond just sort of grandstanding in the halls of Congress or at press conferences. There were concrete actions they were taking, planning, sending e-mails, putting pressure on different people to try to do something about that, to try to effect Donald Trump remaining president even though he had not been selected by the voters.\par \par \uc2 And so we laid out a timeline basically starting shortly after the election where these plans get going, and they lead all the way up to January 6th. As you`ve highlighted in your intro, the e-mail -- I`m sorry, the text message that is forwarded by Jordan just the day before to Meadows advising that Mike Pence should throw out the votes of legitimate electors, and part of this plan was to have this sort of fake slate of electors be the ones to replace them and seat President Trump for another term.\par \par \uc2 So we tried to lay that out in as much detail as possible, and hopefully the readers` understanding of these events were illuminated some.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think, you know, the point you make in the other reporting about sort of bombarding the Department of Justice, we know the Department of Justice is getting a lot of incoming at this point. They`re getting it from Meadows. They`re getting it from Trump and others. Like we`ve got reporting that shows that the, you know, that the U.S. attorney in Atlanta, right, is being told that he`s got to go find some cases.\par \par \uc2 So it`s interesting members of Congress are doing it as well because that`s not isolated. You say this in a piece you wrote about the committee`s scope, that according to one prominent witness, who`s interviewed by the committee, investigators are interested in the relationship between Freedom Caucus members and political activists who organized Stop the Steal rallies before and after the election. Nobody, Committee Chair Bennie Thompson said, is off limits.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, in terms of Meadows, who you wrote about today, I mean, there`s two things that are striking here. One is that he seems to be an active participate in this. You know, there would always be these sort of John Kelly stories that would come out, that John Kelly was sort of like yes- bossing Trump and trying to like, you know, steer him away from things. And I never know the degree to which those were true.\par \par \uc2 But in this case it seems like Meadows is a fully passionate, active participant in these actions at every step of the way as far as what we know from what you and others have reported.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF, THE WASHINGTON POST: That`s exactly right, and it`s important to note that the traditional role of a chief of staff is that of a gatekeeper. There`s a well-known book on chiefs of staff called "The Gatekeepers." But Meadows in many ways was the exact opposite. He was a gate opener, as it were, to the president and to the West Wing, and it was very clear that he was sort of this conduit and hub for conspiracy theories, for misinformation, and for the people who are peddling them.\par \par \uc2 And at a certain point, we reported that the people in the president`s orbit who sort of felt a little uncomfortable with this -- and that wasn`t all of them, but there were some who`s sort of after the election was called started distancing themselves from the administration. The common refrain became go to talk to Meadows because they knew Meadows was the type of person who would be at the very least be a sounding board and listen to this. And in some cases, as Luke documents, as we have documented, he would pass it on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And you know, there were senior administration officials who would get what they believed were sort of very problematic voicemails from these conspiracy theorists, and the voicemails would begin, hey, Meadows gave me your number and told me to call. And that was the role that Meadows was playing. It was less chief of staff and more chief enabler.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. You laid this out really well in the piece. I want to sort of read from part of it because I think this is key.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Michael Pillsbury who`s a Trump adviser said Meadows was eager to enter the Oval Office to present Trump with a new theory or new information of election fraud, knowing Trump was hungry for any tidbits that would help him claim victory. He was trying to please the president, said Pillsbury. He was living and breathing to serve Trump. And we know, for instance, Ashley, when you talk about this gatekeeper, it`s really important, like, take a step back.\par \par \uc2 Everyone in the world wants to get to the president of the United States with their pitch, with the idea they`re selling, with the bill they want, with the tax cut they want, right? Everyone. The chief of staff is the bouncer who says, like, you can`t come in here because otherwise it`s madness. And otherwise, you get people talking to the president who are like, I have this theory that an Italian satellite beamed into the voting machines and hoovered out a bunch of votes, and in fact that`s the kind of totally nuts thing that we have Mark Meadows e-mailing the Justice Department about to look into.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Yes, that`s correct. I mean it was twofold. In some ways, Mark Meadows is a bit of a conspiracy theorist himself.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: You know, we`re told from other people that he actually believed that there might be some merit to the idea that there was actually foreign interference in the elections, and not in terms of sort of, you know, Russian misinformation but in terms of an Italian defense contractor working with the CIA to switch votes from Trump to Biden.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Which I should be clear did not happen.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: So part of it is he was personally open to bits of this. And the other part was that, again, he was a supplicant trying to please then- President Trump. And what Trump wanted, Trump wanted to hear these things. You know, Trump, and you mentioned John Kelly, his previous chief of staff, they clashed because John Kelly said if you`re going to call the president, go through the White House switchboard. And the president wanted to dial people late at night from his cell phone.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And Meadows again wanted to do what the president wanted. So that meant making the West Wing sort of this, you know, "Star Wars" style bar of whoever wanted to come in and get to the president.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Yes. You got Sidney Powell and Overstock.com dude and Michael Flynn like come -- I mean, we saw it all. The cameras caught them like going in with their, you know, presentation, whatever the nuttery of the day was.\par \par \uc2 Luke Broadwater and Ashley Parker, both great, great pieces of reporting in today`s respective papers. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 If you watched the show last night, you saw my interview with two of the organizers of the January 6th rally at the Ellipse, who are now cooperating with the House Committee`s investigation. Now there was a lot in that interview, but there was one specific moment I want to return to about pardons.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Goldman is here to talk about that and the news that Roger Stone plans to plead the Fifth. All that and more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:21:15]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Back in October, journalist Hunter Walker first reported on an alleged blanket pardon offer made by Republican Congressman Paul Gosar of Arizona to a pair of conservative activists who helped Steve Bannon start their organization We Build the Wall. You may recall that`s an organization which raised tens of millions of dollars from Trump supporters in order to privately fund a portion of privately constructed border wall.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman Gosar`s chief of staff reportedly implied that if this pair of organizers continued staging rallies for Donald Trump around the country, including the huge Ellipse rally on January 6th, that would help their chances of getting a pardon. Didn`t say that explicitly but the two were sort of talked about in the same conversation.\par \par \uc2 Now earlier this week, Hunter Walker revealed those sources to be Dustin Stockton and Jennifer Lynn Lawrence. The two were subpoenaed by the committee investigating January 6th and are now cooperating. In fact, we had them on this program yesterday where Dustin basically confirmed the core facts reported in "Rolling Stone."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You had a phone conversation with Paul Gosar`s chief of staff, right?\par \par \uc2 DUSTIN STOCKTON, JANUARY 6 TRUMP ELLIPSE RALLY ORGANIZER: We did talk to Tom Van Flein, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And he said that Paul Gosar had been talking to the president specifically about pardons.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON: Correct. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. So in a conversation with you in which he is praising your work organizing the rallies, he also tells you that the congressman had a conversation with the president of the United States about pardons, correct?\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON: Yes. That did happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. Tic-tac-toe. There we go. For his part, Congressman Gosar has called the claim false and defamatory, adding that the allegations came from, quote, "People I have never met," which is clearly not true. Here is Congressman Gosar in a photo with the pair. Maybe he doesn`t remember it.\par \par \uc2 Now Stockton and Lawrence have not been charged with any crimes, but we should note that in a general sense, that pushing pardons is not something that`s outside the norm for Gosar. In fact, Gosar himself admitted that he wrote a letter in support of a pardon for Steve Bannon. In August, 2020, Steve Bannon was arrested and charged with embezzling some of the money he duped Trump`s base into donating for that We Build the Wall project.\par \par \uc2 And Donald Trump did go on to pardon him in the final 24 hours as president. And that pardon did not sit well with one Dustin Stockton.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON: Of course, Trump pardons the one guy, like the guys who were indicted, who has all the money and political connections to fight it. The triple amputee war hero who wasn`t a lawyer, wasn`t a political operative.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON: Right, Brian Kolfage who started it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON: We brought people like Steve Bannon and Kris Kobach, and we feel really bad about that at this point. But we brought them into protect Brian as he did this project. And yet, the one person who still got the charge -- it`s not one, there`s two other guys, too, but Brian in particular got left out to dry.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Donald Trump left someone out to dry? Oh, my god. That guy, by the way, Brian Kolfage is under indictment, faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted. Didn`t get a pardon.\par \par \uc2 Another person pardoned by Donald Trump who was also subpoenaed by the committee investigating the insurrection is one Roger Stone. He is set to appear before the committee tomorrow where he reportedly plan to invoke his Fifth Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 Dan Goldman served as lead majority counsel in the first impeachment of Donald Trump, as an assistant U.S. attorney in the Southern District of New York which is where Steve Bannon got charged and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Dan, first of all the context to your, and we`re talking about pardons and we`re talking about Stone, is pretty remarkable. I mean, here`s a guy who basically gets his sentence commuted in the fall in the run-up to the election, essentially like sprung from prison by Donald Trump effectively to go out and get back out there doing whatever Roger Stone stuff he was up to.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:03]\par \par \uc2 He`s around January 6th. We know that. You know, he`s in D.C. There`s pictures of him meeting with people. We don`t know the level of his involvement. And then he gets a pardon, a full pardon subsequent to that. And that itself is just like a fairly remarkably corrupt fact pattern before you even get to anything else.\par \par \uc2 DAN GOLDMAN, FORMER HOUSE MAJORITY COUNSEL: Yes, we were saying that at the time, Chris. I think I was talking to you about what a sort of naked corrupt effort it was by Donald Trump. And Roger Stone admitted it. Roger Stone, when he got the commutation of his sentence, said that Donald Trump knows what I know, and I didn`t talk. It was implicit if not explicit admission that he was receiving this pardon in return for not cooperating.\par \par \uc2 Now what are we looking at with Steve Bannon in this case? As things are unfolding and Steve Bannon was integrally involved in the planning for January 6th, it puts a slightly different gloss on the pardon that Bannon received a couple of weeks after January 6th, before Donald Trump left office. So we know that he`s willing and able to do this for his cronies because he did it with Roger Stone, and then he did it again with Steve Bannon.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder to what degree that influenced Steve Bannon to defy the subpoena from the January 6th committee. I have no information about it, but, you know, once you start to see patterns and you know that someone as corrupt as Donald Trump is in the mix with all this power, it`s not a far leap to think that may have had something to do with it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So he`s saying that he`s going to plead the Fifth, which is interesting. I want to read this piece that NBC Think published by appellate order Teri Kanefield, in a non-criminal proceeding, which of course this is, the indication of privilege is limited to those circumstances in which the person invoking the privilege reasonably believes his disclosures could be used in a criminal prosecution or could lead to other evidence that could be so used.\par \par \uc2 But of course in practice even if that`s the sort of law guiding it, in practice you have an absolute right not to answer questions under the Fifth Amendment.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, you don`t have an absolute right just not to answer questions. You have to believe that the answer to those questions may tend to incriminate you. The problem is and what a lot of witnesses will say is, I don`t know what your investigation --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: -- is unfolding. I don`t know what other people are saying, so I don`t know how you will use what I say, and so therefore it could incriminate me in a way that I can`t anticipate. So they broaden out the scope of the Fifth Amendment. But the thing that jumps out at me about taking the Fifth Amendment and John Eastman, the lawyer, is also purportedly going to do that, is it`s somewhat of an admission that there could be something of a criminal nature wrapped up in this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: And that just then leads to the question of what`s going on with the Department of Justice? If these people are going to take the Fifth in front of the January 6th committee and therefore they`re not going to get their testimony, they`re taking the Fifth because they think that -- or they`re getting advised by their lawyer that there`s something criminal afoot here. There may be, not that there necessarily is, but that it could incriminate you with the Department of Justice, not with the January 6th committee.\par \par \uc2 So it jumps out that, wait a minute. Maybe this is something that is a little bit bigger than, you know, a congressional committee and needs to be looked at by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, I mean I think that`s true prior to this. And the other thing I would say here, I mean I think it`s really important, right, jury instructions, it`s a sort of bedrock principle of American criminal procedure, right, that juries do not draw an adverse conclusion by the failure of a defendant testifying on their own behalf. That`s incredibly important and sacrosanct. We want to make sure that that right is there for a reason in the Bill of Rights.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn`t extend to like how we publicly perceive these people. Like, you know, your reputation is not governed by constitutional law. If you, John Eastman, want to say, I`m a humble law professor, but I think that I have some criminal exposure, like everyone in the -- you know, just onlookers gets to draw their own conclusions about like the measure of the man that is John Eastman, I think.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yes. Well, you can extend that even further. I mean, first of all, if you want to know what it means to take the Fifth Amendment, just ask Donald Trump who once famously said, only crooks take the Fifth. But for someone -- while there is -- for someone like John Eastman, let`s take as an example, while there is no adverse inference in a criminal proceeding, there can be an adverse inference in some sort of civil proceeding.\par \par \uc2 That happens a lot because if you take the Fifth, then in a civil proceeding, they can assume that what you would have to say would not be helpful to you.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:03]\par \par \uc2 But John Eastman has another problem, which is his bar license. If you take the Fifth, you are indicating that something you say could incriminate you, and that`s going to get him in trouble with his state bar association.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Daniel Goldman, as always, great pleasure. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, have you noticed the weather? December heat waves, massive windstorms, an outbreak of deadly tornadoes? The unbelievable scenes unfolding all across the country, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Five people are confirmed dead after an unprecedented storm system swept across much of the country yesterday, hitting especially hard in the Midwest and Great Lakes. Record-high temperatures came along with extreme winds. In Kansas, gusts reached up to 100 miles per hour. Tornadoes reported in Minnesota, Nebraska, and Iowa, leaving a trail of destruction. That`s just days after 90 people were killed by tornadoes in Arkansas, Illinois, and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:04]\par \par \uc2 Wildfires kicked up by wind spread smoke across Kansas. And while this area of the country is no stranger to severe weather, this storm was absolutely unheard of for this time of year and broke all kinds of records.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Official warning stretching to at least five states. Thousands bracing for cover as a massive storm wave pushes through the country. The unprecedented storm first landed in southern California on Tuesday. Record-breaking rain quickly turning the L.A. River into raging rapids.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE MITCHELL, METEOROLOGIST: With a record low pressure storm, a lot of heat, a lot of moisture, that sets the stage for severe weather that we have never seen this late in the season.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re talking record warm temperatures, but it`s also going to be coming along with a lot of wind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This was a historic storm system. Des Moines, Iowa, in particular, their highest non-thunderstorm wind gust and their warmest December day on record.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the same day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the same day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we`re in the middle of December.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Quite a December day, one we haven`t seen in history here. Pretty intense storm system moving through.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Record high temperatures. Our high today is 63 degrees. Now keep in mind the previous record was 52 set back in 1923. 1923 was 98 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They broke it by 11 degrees. 11 degrees. 63 degrees in December. Now, you can really see the intensity and diversity of this weather in this just one map showing the advisories, watches, and warnings for Colorado on Wednesday. You got snow, wind, fire, avalanche, and blowing dust across the state. That almost feels like an "Onion" graphic, but that`s the actual CBS Denver warning about what`s going on in the state.\par \par \uc2 And that`s our increasingly extreme reality. We, of course, should not be seeing tornadoes whipping through the Midwest in December or temperatures in the 60s in Minnesota and New York. That`s not normal. None of it is. But that is the reality of our present and future as we continue to live with the impacts of a rapidly changing climate.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Cleetus, the policy director of Climate and Energy Program at the \uc2 Union of Concerned Scientists\uc2 , and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, first I guess I just want to start with a "what on earth?" I mean, you know, for a long time I think there was a little bit of like trying to get to people about things are changing, and because of the way that we perceive climate and because it`s slow, it took a while. But I do think that when you see this, we`ve reached a kind of tipping point just in awareness where everyone understands if it`s 55 degrees in St. Paul, in Minnesota, in December, like something is deeply wrong.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL CLEETUS, POLICY DIRECTOR, CLIMATE AND ENERGY UNION OF CONCERNED SCIENTISTS: Yes. Scientists have been warning us about this for decades. That as our carbon emissions from burning fossil fuels increase in the atmosphere, they`re leading to these temperature increases. And as temperatures rise, we`re going to start to see really extreme kinds of weather. And the unfortunate reality is those warnings are now coming true all around us.\par \par \uc2 Wherever you live in the country, this year you have seen some kind of extreme weather, whether it`s the wildfires in California, the extreme drought that gripped much of the southwest and the Midwest, Hurricane Ida that swept through from Louisiana all the way up to New York and New Jersey, casting a deadly path of destruction. The heat waves that we`ve seen, record heat waves in many parts of the country, including in the southwest. This is just unprecedented. It`s abnormal, and we should never get numbed to this because it is taking a deadly toll.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen thousands of people die this year because of weather-related disasters, where there was the extreme heat waves that we saw in the Pacific northwest or people who died because of Hurricane Ida. The heat, the storm, and the deadly rain that came with Hurricane Ida. And what we need to understand about this is none of this would happen without our human-caused emissions that are fueling and driving climate change.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: Climate change is here. It`s now, and we`re all experiencing it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one thing about this that I think is -- and I agree, you know, we`ve watched, you know, covered extreme weather on the show. One of the things that I think the last few days that feels distinctly uncanny and kind of science fiction-y to me is that in other cases, you have, you know, a particularly severe wildfire season, but we know wildfire seasons happen a certain amount, and then the wildfire season might extend, or severe drought.\par \par \uc2 This feels so fundamentally wrong, like as opposed to an intensification of the phenomenon we`re used to, which is like hurricanes come through the Gulf in the fall, tornadoes in December just feels like wildly off the map wrong. The kind of constellation of weather we`re seeing at an almost visceral level feels it even more severe than some of the other weather disasters we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:09]\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: Yes. The reality is climate change is coming at us in so many different kinds of ways, and they`re colliding. We don`t get to experience this as one single thing happening in one place at one time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: We`re seeing them collide in time. And this is what we can expect even more of if we fail to sharply curtail our heat-trapping emissions. We do still have choices, and it`s critical that we embrace them now because we can see that the alternative, we`re just at a little over a degree Celsius temperature increase, global average temperature increase since pre-industrial times, and this is already our reality. So we just simply cannot take this risk much more further.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and one of the lessons of the pandemic, right, is that you want to -- you know, moving early is better than moving late. Now the window for moving early, early has been essentially closed, but we have a $550 billion of climate investment which is insufficient to the task but would be the largest ever in the country`s history.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s sitting in the BBB bill, which has now been kicked to next year. I mean I don`t know any other, like, creative way of saying, like, we absolutely need to pass this, but we just absolutely do.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: We have to embrace this. We have to grasp this opportunity. So that Build Back Better Act you mentioned includes many provisions that would help kick-start a clean energy transition, one that we desperately need. We need to get off fossil fuels. We need to embrace wind, solar, electric vehicles, more electric-charging infrastructure. We need to do this in ways that every community benefits.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of communities have been left behind in the past. Black and brown communities, indigenous communities particularly. We need to make sure we`re doing this in a fair way that benefits communities everywhere in the country, creating good-paying union jobs as we do it. But that huge opportunity is there in the Build Back Better bill. Let`s jump and get it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: And right now what we have is a Congress that seems so dysfunctional. We`ve got an entire party that`s sitting on the sidelines as this really important opportunity -- let`s remember, climate change is affecting red states and blue states.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: It is not a partisan issue.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No. It`s coming for everyone, and you`re right. Basically 50 U.S. senators have just basically said we`re doing nothing about this. It`s up to the other 50 to get their acts together to act.\par \par \uc2 Rachael Cleetus, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CLEETUS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, the fight to protect America`s workers. Bernie Sanders packing his bags, heading to the Kellogg`s plant in Battle Creek, Michigan. There`s some big news developing on that very, very important strike. He will join me here first coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:46:55]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: On any given day there is so much to talk about. There`s a finite amount of time on this TV show. But one of the great things I get to do is host a podcast called "Why is This Happening?" we actually have some of the longer conversations we don`t have time for in the confines of cable news.\par \par \uc2 Next week, though, we`re going to air the very first "With Pod ALL IN crossover episode. It`s a special televised edition of "Why is This Happening?" podcast on ALL IN. And I sat down for extended interviews with the great \uc2 Sherrilyn Ifill\uc2  of the \uc2 NAACP Legal Defense and Educational Fund\uc2 , and the "Atlantic`s" stellar reporter Barton Gellman about the Republican Party`s embrace of authoritarianism, a threat to civil and voting rights in this country and what we can all do about it.\par \par \uc2 You can check out our special edition of "With Pod on ALL IN" next Friday right here at 8:00 p.m. on MSNBC, and the full unedited interviews will also be released as with pod episodes. You`ll be able to find them wherever you get your podcasts along with many other great conversations I had this year on everything from the minimum wage to UFOs.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:52:05]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So last week the president of the United States, Joe Biden, fired off a warning to a private company, Kellogg Cereal, which has 1400 employees who are out on strike and he told them do not try to replace those workers. Quote, "Permanently replacing striking workers is an existential attack on the union and its members` jobs and livelihoods. I strongly support legislation that would ban that practice."\par \par \uc2 Now for over two months now the workers at four Kellogg Cereal plants, Michigan, Nebraska, Pennsylvania and Tennessee, had been demanding enhanced benefits and pay after working longer hours during the coronavirus pandemic. And a main issue for striking workers has been the companies created a kind of two-tiered wage system which offers lower pay and fewer benefits to newer workers.\par \par \uc2 This is fairly common in a lot of places. It`s a way of kind of breaking up the solidarity between the two groups. Last week members of the union rejected a tentative contract negotiated by their own union. Today Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont wrote this opinion piece for FOX News titled "Kellogg`s strike, workers must get our support." In the first line of the piece he announced his plans to head to one of those striking plants in Battle Creek, Michigan, tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Well, late this afternoon we got news the union has now reached another tentative deal. This means members will meet tomorrow to discuss the deal and vote on the new proposal on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 And Senator Bernie Sanders, the chair of the Senate Budget Committee, joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you on, Senator. Why do you feel this is important enough to go out to Battle Creek tomorrow for?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Because it is a struggle against the kind of corporate greed, Chris, that we are seeing all over this country. It`s the same old story. This is a company that last year made $1.4 billion in profit. A company that can afford to provide $12 million in compensation to its CEO. And meanwhile, the reason it made this money is because during the pandemic, these workers, unbelievably, they were working 50, 60, 70 days in a row, sometimes 12 hours a day, in order to feed America.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a guy there who worked 120 days in a row. Can you imagine that? That`s totally insane. So these workers literally put their well-being, their families, their lives on the line in order to feed America, in order to keep the company going, and then the company`s response was, to the strike is, well, we`re going to cut all of your health care benefits. So you`ve got workers out there with no health insurance now.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to bring in replacement workers. Oh, yes, I know you have worked for me for your whole life. Maybe your dad, your mom worked for me. That`s the case there. People have devoted their entire adult lives to Kellogg`s and now they`re prepared to replace them.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 And then on top of that, they`re planning to ship 275 more jobs to their plant in Mexico where the starting wage is 97 cents an hour. So what Kellogg`s is doing, the rich get richer, the CEO does fine, dividends are high, and yet workers are giving up their lives in many respects. I don`t know what it`s like to work 120 days in a row.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Yes. I want to just emphasize something you said there because I think it`s an important part of the story. You said it`s the same old story. It is in many ways the same old story, but there is something new here, which is, you know, a year and a half ago when the whole world, I mean literally the entire world, millions of people were on essentially stay-at-home orders, millions of people in this country, people go to the grocery store once a week to feed their family when you couldn`t go out and do anything, and there was food in the aisles.\par \par \uc2 And the reason there was food in the aisles was because there were workers at the grocery stores who are showing up every day in the midst of the once century pandemic and at the other end of that supply chain in Battle Creek, Michigan, or wherever there were people in the factory day in and day out in the midst of a pandemic making sure that food got made. I mean, these are those -- literally those people who fed the country during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: They were called appropriately so heroes. These are heroes and heroines feeding us, working insane hours and now because they want better wages, they want to end this disastrous two-tier wage system by which younger workers earn substantially lower wages and benefits than older workers. These workers are standing up for the younger generation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Real solidarity, doing exactly the right thing. The company responds in an incredibly abrasive and outrageous way. So I am proud. I mean, these people are really heroes and heroines. They are incredibly courageous. And what they are fighting against, that type of corporate greed, is what we are seeing all over this country during the pandemic. When thousands of workers died because they had to go to work in order to feed their families.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The billionaire class made huge amounts of money so I am proud to stand with these workers and their union tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You are standing inside the Capitol where I hear your voice reverberating off the marble, and so I have to ask you as well about what looks like what will be the end of the year without the Build Back Better agenda passing out of the Senate. There is multiple interpretations of the ongoing conversations with Joe Biden and Joe Manchin. President Joe Biden and co-president Joe Manchin? About whether this is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 And I guess there are two ways to interpret it. One is Joe Manchin is essentially acting in bad faith and kicking the can until it can die. And the other is the fact he`s still talking and the fact he`s still engaging on details means that there`s an actual negotiation ongoing. And which of those two are your interpretation?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, let me just say this, and I hope everybody in America understands it. You`ve got 50 people in the Republican caucus. We have gotten no support from them to lower the cost of prescription drugs, to expand Medicare, to include dental, hearing, eyeglasses. No support from them for child care, for housing, which is all in this Build Back Better plan. No support to deal with the existential threat of climate change.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve got 50 Republicans who are not prepared to do anything for the environment or working families. You`ve got 48 people in the Democratic caucus who are prepared and a president of the United States prepared to think big. And you have two Democrats who in my view are kind of acting like Republicans. And to me, I respect other people`s points of view, but I do not respect the arrogance of any member of the Senate who says, you know what, I`m going to torpedo this entire bill, supported overwhelmingly by the American people, who are sick and tired of paying outrageously high prices for prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 Sick and tired of seeing billionaires not paying their fair share of taxes. Tired of seeing people sleeping out on the streets. Kids and families not being able to enjoy decent quality child care at affordable cost. You`ve got two people saying, you know what, hey, if you don`t do it my way, I don`t care what the president wants, I don`t care what 48 of my colleagues want, it`s my way or the highway.\par \par \uc2 And that I regard as arrogance. You can disagree. Look, I have disagreements, as you well know.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: All right? You fight for your ideas but you don`t say my way or the highway, and that I feel very strongly about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, we`ll see where we end up, whether we`re on the highway or not.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders headed to Battle Creek, Michigan, tomorrow. Thank you for making some time for us before you depart. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020211217ehcg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for December 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Monica Alba, Nahid Bhadelia, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 10,055 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Tom is on his way.\par \par \uc2 TOM MOSES: We`re on the highway.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Jacob Soboroff, NBC News Joliet, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Great story. That`s going to do it for this edition of ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS, I`m Garrett Haake. Andrea will be back tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Todd with MTP DAILY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday.\par \par \uc2 This hour, President Biden is scheduled to unveil his winter plan to fight COVID as health officials confirmed a second case of the Omicron variant, this time in Minnesota and it was from someone who just attended a large conference not overseas but in New York City.\par \par \uc2 This as Congress scrambles to avert a government shutdown tomorrow night, as a group of Senate Republicans attempt to defund the administration`s vaccine mandates. I`ll speak with a Republican senator at the center of these talks coming up.\par \par \uc2 And later, an update from Michigan as multiple school districts close today over threats of more violence in the wake of that deadly shooting at a high school in suburban Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Hello there, welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 President Biden right now is just landed at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 , where he will deliver remarks scheduled for later this hour to formally roll out what they say is an updated plan to fight COVID this winter.\par \par \uc2 And all of this comes as health officials out of Minnesota earlier today confirmed the second case of the Omicron variant in the United States from an individual who had not traveled overseas but had just attended a conference in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the announcement out of Minnesota, New York State Governor Kathy Hochul delivered remarks. She said there are no confirmed cases yet detected in New York, but she does expect that to change.\par \par \uc2 New York declared a state of emergency last week in anticipation of this variant.\par \par \uc2 Both confirmed cases, the one in California, and the other in Minnesota had been breakthrough infections, but officials say both experienced only mild symptoms. But these were vaccinated folks.\par \par \uc2 All week, the administration has been saying that getting vaccinated and boosted is still the single best way to protect yourself against COVID and all of its potential variants whether Delta or Omicron.\par \par \uc2 Not surprisingly, getting more shots in arms is going to be the centerpiece of the President`s winter plan to fight COVID when he officially unveiled that later this hour.\par \par \uc2 President`s remarks later today come as the administration has taken this opportunity to significantly ramp up its public messaging campaign on COVID all this week.\par \par \uc2 Monday, the President delivered his first extended remarks on Omicron and the CDC strengthened its booster recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, the White House Task Force briefed the public on Omicron for the first time.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, Dr. Fauci himself spoke from the White House briefing room.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we heard from the President on supply chains and COVID yesterday as well.\par \par \uc2 So it does certainly look as if the White House is trying to take the reins on this COVID response be more front-facing if you will. But does this mean they`re going to engage in the political fight over vaccine mandates?\par \par \uc2 Because as we noted earlier in the week, the President is at least up until this point, losing the vaccine mandate messaging fight.\par \par \uc2 And the resistance to those mandates from a handful of conservatives on the right has actually put us on the brink of a potential government shutdown tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now ahead of the President`s remarks is NBC`s Monica Alba from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Joining us from California, where the country`s first Omicron case was identified just yesterday during this hour, is San Francisco`s Health Director, Dr. Grant Colfax.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we have our own in-house expert, MSNBC Medical Contributor and Infectious Disease Physician, Dr. Nahid Bhadelia.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Monica, let me start with you, though. Look, this is a White House that -- it looks as if they are -- is this -- are we officially saying they`re now back to all hands on deck with COVID in that west wing?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: I think that`s absolutely accurate, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 If you think back to August with the Delta surge, the White House unveiled a six-prong plan and a detailed strategy to combat that.\par \par \uc2 Today, we`re going to hear from the President on a nine-prong plan, so the supersize version, given this Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 And really what we can expect is there`s no blockbuster announcement, it`s not like there`s some major new policy or tool that he`s going to unveil today or talk about.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it`s really going to be an amplification of what is existing right now but where there are some trouble spots when it comes to testing nationwide and surveillance. Something that the administration acknowledges they really need to ramp up.\par \par \uc2 When it gets to boosters, some people are having a hard time still finding appointments or understanding whether they`re eligible or not.\par \par \uc2 So the President is going to try to make some clarity when it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 But again, these are things that have existed for some time that the White House is saying we`re going to fully ramp them up because they are more essential than ever.\par \par \uc2 They`re very much aware that also there is the pressure when it comes to this question of travelers, people entering the United States when it came to Delta, why didn`t they put some of these requirements in place back then?\par \par \uc2 Now they`re saying yes, that anyone entering the U.S., American Citizens included will have to seek a negative test 24 hours before travel which is in and of itself a bit of a logistical complication.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But that`s a big step that this White House is taking that they hadn`t yet before. So they are now using everything that had been on the table.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing that stood out to us, Chuck, in the White House briefing just earlier this afternoon, Press Secretary Jen Psaki said when it comes to domestic travel, everything is still being considered and we could see tighter restrictions there too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you read my mind, Monica. I was about to follow up with that. There`s a -- I -- we`re -- I think we`re all running into a logic circle here.\par \par \uc2 Omicron is in California, Omicron is in Minnesota, it may be in New York, yet we`re banning travel from countries that have had no cases yet.\par \par \uc2 It seems domestic air travel with so many different parts of the country, having different vaccination rates, it almost seems essential at this point. It sounds like they want to get there, but are they afraid of the politics of it?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: I think that that is a major component. But then also, it`s again, the logistical question of this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re there once.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Right now, it`s hard sometimes to find a place you can get a PCR test and results within 24 hours. And so it`s asking a lot of the private sector and of these Airlines to necessarily agree with, implement that.\par \par \uc2 We`ve obviously seen some partners in terms of \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  with this White House, working hand in glove in terms of getting vaccination requirements up. But it`s not like everybody would necessarily get on board. So that`s a little bit of the hesitation.\par \par \uc2 But I think what you`re going to see another thing that the President`s going to talk about today is the fact that he is extending the mask mandate for public transportation like airlines and buses and trains.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: That`s something that TSA agreed to extend through mid-March. Now, it was going to expire in January.\par \par \uc2 So again, these conversations are happening very much on the table not being ruled out. Whereas a few months ago, the White House was saying we`re really not considering that we don`t think that`s necessary. So there is a shift here.\par \par \uc2 And yes, absolutely, the politics is at play, given the fact that the Biden administration`s nationwide vaccine mandate is so tied up in courts and legal battles right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and look, the airlines are having a hard enough time dealing with -- dealing with passengers that are unruly about the mask, they would be at the front lines of having to execute this plan.\par \par \uc2 So I imagine the airlines are a bit hesitant to add to their burden already.\par \par \uc2 Monica, I appreciate you getting us started.\par \par \uc2 I want to dig deeper into what we know about Omicron and what we think about this new COVID plan.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Colfax, I want to start with you. Tell us about a little bit more about the -- this was a vaccinated person, tell us more about what you learned from Omicron?\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a breakthrough case, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 DR. GRANT COLFAX, DIRECTOR OF HEALTH, SAN FRANCISCO CITY & COUNTY: Well, I think it`s really important to step back a minute and realize that this was expected by it is I think he said earlier than show Omicron is here in front of the flood.\par \par \uc2 However, you say it is here, it`s in the country and we knew that it will be detected relatively soon.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco because of our skin Department of Public Health team and our collaboration with \uc2 UCSF\uc2  and most importantly, that the source -- the source patient, that person upon return from South Africa upon travel, develop symptoms got tested -- tested positive received a text from the health department about her test result contacted the health department and share her travel history.\par \par \uc2 That allowed us to collaborate with \uc2 UCSF\uc2  to quickly sequence this virus and discover that it indeed, it`s this variant. But this is not unexpected. I think we need to remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLFAX: But here in San Francisco, we have a very high vaccination rates. 81 percent of people in San Francisco are fully vaccinated at this time. So I think we`re locally well positioned to respond.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you share whether the person who had the variant had gotten a booster yet?\par \par \uc2 COLFAX: The person was fully -- had received two doses of the mRNA vaccine, Moderna. And actually, the time had elapsed jab for this person to be eligible for, administered yet.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s really important to emphasize that boosters are key right now. As you know, the CDC recently changed recommendations for everyone who`s eligible given the timeline from when their first initial series occurred to get a booster.\par \par \uc2 We`re getting boosters into arms in San Francisco. 50 percent of our -- of our population, 65 and over has already received a booster shot and we have about 5000 boosters going to arms every day in our city.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Dr. Bhadelia, the two Omicron cases are breakthrough cases but it looks like their pre-booster cases. Does that give you some sense of hope here that we -- you know, we have the two -- tools are in hand here and that you know it may evade the -- it may evade vaccines if the vaccine is waning in you but if you`re boosted it will not?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 DR. NAHID BHADELIA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: You know, actually the case in Minnesota was boosted, received three doses and the booster was that in November.\par \par \uc2 But I still think that there is you know -- I think people -- two things, I want to step back and just talk a little bit about what this case and his connection to New York means.\par \par \uc2 I think that what it means is, as we expected there are Omicrons in this country, but also that it`s probably been in this country longer than we expected because we`re seeing what we call community transmission.\par \par \uc2 That means somebody got it from somebody else in a community here in the United States, which is why your points about domestic travel are so important.\par \par \uc2 But two, I think people want to read into both the mild illnesses or the vaccine status and say, well, this has something about the severity of disease and, or even look at hospitalizations in South Africa.\par \par \uc2 All in all to say that all this data so far is anecdotal, so we don`t know much about the severity of the disease.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that you are seeing infections in people who are vaccinated, I`d say is also expected because looking at these mutations, we expected that there will be some reduction in prevention of infections, but you`re going to still have very good protection against severe disease.\par \par \uc2 And if you get a booster, your protection against infection is probably going to be higher than if you weren`t vaccinated, or if you got the two doses. So those principles still remain.\par \par \uc2 But the other part of this is that if you are seeing breakthrough infections in vaccinated people, even though they`re protected in a sense against, you know, severe disease, it means that we can`t just rely on vaccines.\par \par \uc2 They`re critical, they are the center stone, you know, the cornerstone of how we grow this, but this winter, we need to layer our strategies with the masking, with the testing, which is what the President`s plan is trying to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to point out something here, Dr. Bhadalia from the CDC`s European counterpart, essentially, the European Center for Disease Control and Prevention.\par \par \uc2 They are hypothesizing that the Omicron variant is expected to cause over half of all COVID infections in the EU within the next few months.\par \par \uc2 Seem pretty declarative there, why do you believe they are so sure that`s going to happen, Dr. Bhadelia?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: I think that you know, they`re facing some of those in the growth rate that they`re seeing in South Africa.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think there`s the numbers there are worrisome you know, they`ve even actually seen an increase in 65 percent in their hospitalizations, but you know, they`re under-vaccinated Country.\par \par \uc2 They`re only 30 percent fully vaccinated, but they`re looking at just the increase in cases that they`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 They`re also looking at potentially mutations that are on this virus that potentially could confer it with both the properties through the immune system and transmissibility.\par \par \uc2 I would say it is -- it`s good to be cautious. I don`t think that you can be as declarative, but it`s good to sort of suppose that this could be a variant that`s more transmissible.\par \par \uc2 And even if it doesn`t change in severity, Chuck, what we`ve found from Delta is even if the diseased part remains the same, if something is more transmissible, it can get around faster to more places and reach vulnerable people and unvaccinated people, and my cause -- might cause increased cases of hospitalizations from that perspective.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Colfax, what are you seeing right now in San Francisco when it comes to COVID cases and hospitalizations, or how many vaccinated how many unvaccinated are ending up hospitalized?\par \par \uc2 COLFAX: So the vast majority of people who have been hospitalized in San Francisco have been unvaccinated. We have very few people right now in the hospital because of our high vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 So in our city, over 800,000 people -- excuse me, we have a total of 21 people in our hospital. In fact, in our public hospitals, Zuckerberg, San Francisco General, we have just one person with COVID in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 So it really I think reinforces the importance of these vaccines and these boosters.\par \par \uc2 And getting back to the point about, you know, vaccines and initial vaccines and boosters can`t be the only strategy, we have really reinforced, and our Mayor, London Breed, has reinforced the importance of masking and testing.\par \par \uc2 And we have built a system of testing across the city that I think is really an important component to our -- has been a very important component to our response.\par \par \uc2 I`m happy to see that the Biden plan is expanding testing and expanding opportunities for people to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco, we have nearly a hundred vaccine sites. San Franciscans are within a five to 10-minute walk of most of these sites.\par \par \uc2 We have drop-ins, we have community sites, we have sites in public schools for people to get vaccinated and also for people to get tested.\par \par \uc2 And, of course -- of course, it`s important to emphasize that we have a vaccine requirement for indoors where people are dining and see people drink.\par \par \uc2 And we`re continuing with the indoor masking requirement for in public spaces.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a combination of these efforts that I think it`s gotten us to where we are today, to be relatively well-positioned to respond to this new variant.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Quite a few people are hitting their six-month -- have already hit their six-month anniversary from their second dose, Dr. Colfax.\par \par \uc2 Have you seen an increased amount of two-dose vaccinated people getting breakthrough cases as they get to this six-month mark? Is that anything that`s quantifiable yet?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 COLFAX: Yes. We`ve actually been very aggressive in getting those boosters into people who are most vulnerable so we haven`t seen a significant trend in that.\par \par \uc2 Again, we actually moved aggressively to recommend boosters for people early on, as we saw the data about waning immunity at the -- at the national and other international level.\par \par \uc2 But we have not actually seen a significant increase in those -- in breakthrough infections on six month day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got you. Dr. Bhadelia, you`re looking at this on such a national level, do we have enough evidence that we`re starting to see that?\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you the anecdote that -- the anecdotes I keep saying, it does -- if you just look at the sports world, the amount of COVID positives we`re seeing among supposedly vaccinated athletes.\par \par \uc2 I say supposedly because there`s been some rumors about fake Vax cards, things like that.\par \par \uc2 But I wondering, is that a coincidence that we`re about at the six month mark for so many people?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: It`s not. And, Chuck, you know, a lot of this data was actually presented at the FDA and the CDC meetings when they approved the boosters for a larger population. Because what you`re seeing is -- you`re seeing sort of waning immunity against infection.\par \par \uc2 I want to, you know, put that aside. People are seeing infections or potentially seeing mild infections, they might, you know, even feel crappy when they get the infection as an outpatient.\par \par \uc2 But they`re also seeing -- but that same data, what they showed is that in old people who are over 50, you might also see some decrease in protection.\par \par \uc2 This is all in the Delta era, right? We haven`t even gotten to Omicron yet.\par \par \uc2 There was some decrease in hospitalizations and that`s why those boosters are important.\par \par \uc2 But New York City actually just released a study that showed that even in the time of Delta, with two doses, you have -- in young people under 50, you still had very good protection against hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 So we were seeing -- people should remember that they`re coming up to their boosters.\par \par \uc2 Omicron or Delta, boosters are still going to be important to increase protection against infection, which will be important if there`s a lot of people around you who might be infected. You don`t want to be one of the areas that might give it to other people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Bhadelia, you`ve been very good about not looking at your e-mail, I`ve had to look at a piece of breaking news we just got. I was about to close that segment.\par \par \uc2 But the South Africa -- South Africa`s essentially their version of the CDC, NIH, however you want to look at it, their National Institute for communicable diseases.\par \par \uc2 They just put out -- Reuters just put out a quick wire update that said their latest finding provides epidemiological evidence that Omicron has the ability to evade immunity from prior infection. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes, and I think this was expected. Again, I`m not surprised looking at two things.\par \par \uc2 One, just the mutations that we`re seeing, but also South Africa, because it`s -- you know, because they went through a lot of just, there`s as low levels of infection, you saw a huge percentage of the population who probably got COVID in the past, you know, they`ve gone through other variants like Beta variant and other variants that were highly immune invasive as well.\par \par \uc2 And so this is -- this is worrisome because it shows that for people in our own country that are sort of, you know, on the fence saying, well, I got an infection before, do I want to get a vaccine or not?\par \par \uc2 But now is your time. I mean, this data, this potential, you know, we`ll have to sort of follow it up and see how much of this plays out in other scenarios and other countries.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that they`re potentially seeing infections in people who have been previously infected, they`re seeing reinfections, goes to show you that in the setting of Omicron, there might be a signal that if you`ve got an -- gotten an infection in the past, now is the time for you to get that vaccine and get boosted here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Colfax, Dr. Bhadelia, appreciate both of you providing your expertise on what we`re dealing with right now. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COLFAX: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And as we see you`re expecting to hear from the President. He`ll be speaking at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  on his administration`s plans to fight the Omicron variant later this hour. Obviously, we`ll bring you those remarks live when they happen.\par \par \uc2 Up next, congressional Republicans appear to me, moving full steam ahead towards threatening a government shutdown tomorrow in protest of the vaccine mandates, yet, another sign of the extreme dysfunction in this \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Colourise Claude Rains shocked but dysfunction is threatening key deadlines that are fast approaching in Congress.\par \par \uc2 With government funding set to expire tomorrow, the House will soon pass legislation that funds the government through mid-February.\par \par \uc2 But it may take some time to make it through the Senate where Republican Senator Roger Marshall plans on blocking quick consideration of the bill.\par \par \uc2 It`s simply a means of protesting President Biden`s employer vaccine soft mandate, which appears likely to lead to at least a short term or could lead to a short term government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 And that`s not all. An essential Defense Funding bill was blocked last night when Florida Republican Senator Marco Rubio objected to moving forward because one of his own bills was not included as an amend.\par \par \uc2 And with less than two weeks before we`re projected to hit the debt ceiling, conversations between Chuck Schumer and Mitch McConnell on raising it appear to have gone nowhere.\par \par \uc2 But it`s all three of these issues are pieces of common-sense legislation that rational lawmakers know they have to pass. The consequences of missing these deadlines have a real impact.\par \par \uc2 We are flirting with awfully close to these deadlines. We may be numb to it.\par \par \uc2 This is not how government is supposed to work. I know we`ve said it this year, last year, five years ago. It`s like government dysfunction.\par \par \uc2 You think it`s at 11, 12, 13, 14? How much higher is the Spinal Tap Amp go?\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali joins us now from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, we have a framework to avoid the government shutdown. The political theatrics of this Senate, what`s the timeline here to get through the -- to get through what some of these senators are trying to -- are trying to do? I guess it`s for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  mentions or something.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Well, it is in the name of the conservative ideology that people like Senators, Mike Lee, and Ted Cruz, and Roger Marshall, espouse.\par \par \uc2 This is something that would shut down the government, if it were able to go forward for a brief period of time, basically over the weekend, potentially into early next week.\par \par \uc2 Just because if these conservative Senators don`t get what they want, which is an amendment vote simple majority on this vaccine mandate funding, then they say they won`t agree to speed up this process in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And just on the timing of this, a government shutdown would be the casualty of Senate procedure going as it normally would without anything in it to speed it up.\par \par \uc2 You talk about where we are on the anvil -- on the amp scale of dysfunction, this is it.\par \par \uc2 It`s really just a point way of saying. This is a time management thing.\par \par \uc2 We knew all of these deadlines were coming at the end of September, the last time that we did all of these news cycles around the debt ceiling and around government funding on the Defense Authorization Act.\par \par \uc2 This is something that comes up every year that the Senate has known that they have to deal with for weeks.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So all of these things we`re seeing in the future, we`re journalists, we know deadlines, we have a love-hate relationship with them, clearly, that`s the same thing in Congress.\par \par \uc2 But when you talk about what the actual implication of this is, again, we have never defaulted on our debt in this country.\par \par \uc2 That`s a deadline. That`s just under a week and a half away on government funding.\par \par \uc2 I have covered these things out in America talking to people who aren`t getting their paychecks, who are canceling their kid`s birthday parties, who are already in debt because of health crises.\par \par \uc2 Those are the things that are the fallout of these political gambits.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really important to keep that in mind, even if it`s just a shutdown for the next few days, there is an implication to that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A little bit of this is there`s sort of this exhaustion from sky is falling coverage, right?\par \par \uc2 And I understand it, like -- look, I roll my eyes at this, everyone oh, my God, this is like, no, we know, you guys, even if you flirt with it, you`re not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 Do they realize though, they`re just diminishing the entire institution and themselves? I mean, what political benefit is any of them getting out of this?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, that`s exactly right. If you talk to almost every other Republican and Democrat on this, and I`ve been spending that time on the Hill going back and forth between lawmakers, everyone agrees that it doesn`t benefit anyone politically right now to shut down the government.\par \par \uc2 What it does do, and you`re right on this, is lend to the idea of dysfunctions.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Pelosi was talking about this earlier. This is silly, the idea that they -- that Republicans could shut down the government even briefly over vaccine mandates, but at the same time, it could happen.\par \par \uc2 And Americans don`t necessarily care how the government is funded, they don`t care about raising or suspending a debt limit, they just care that it happens because they`re busy going about their lives.\par \par \uc2 What this does is it lends to the idea of dysfunction. And also going into a midterm year, you have Democrats in control of both Houses of Congress.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t benefit Republicans to do this but it definitely doesn`t benefit Democrats either, as they`re trying to show that they are the party that`s in charge here.\par \par \uc2 They do have the majorities in these both Houses of Congress plus the White House, and this is still happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you suggesting this is a -- this is a sabotage strategy, just make the Democrats look even more dysfunctional? Is that -- Is that an actual Republican strategy out there?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I`m not sure that it`s the strategy of the full party because you talk to Republicans and they agree that politically for them, there`s no appetite on this, either.\par \par \uc2 They would rather make the messaging points about inflation and about the economy.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, if this is one of the unintended consequences of it, or for the small minority here that are forcing this shutdown issue, that it`s an unintended consequence, sure, all the better.\par \par \uc2 But again, this all leads to. This is going to be the last vestige of 2021 politically that people have in their mouths as you turned the calendar page into 2022.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s our broken incentive structure system, the way the propagandist media works on the right, the incentive is to do political theater.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Destruction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anyway, Ali Vitali, appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We were just showing you some live pictures of the Senate floor there while Ali was talking, Senator Chris Murphy speaking right now about a bill on preventing gun violence, which he`s hoping to get a vote on soon.\par \par \uc2 Up next, I`m going to speak with Republican Senator Mike Braun of Indiana. He has been trying to convince his Republican colleagues to vote for his plan to undo the Biden vaccine mandate. He is not for shutting down the whole federal government to do it. We`ll talk with him about what he`s trying to do next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS HOST: Just to put a fine point. You`re saying you can avert a shutdown or not, sir?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Yes, we`re not going to shut the government down. That makes no sense for anyone. Almost no one on either side thinks that`s a good idea.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was the Senate Republican leader Mitch McConnell saying almost no one thinks a government shutdown is a good idea. I was going to say almost no one because we may still be staring up one down as a few Republican senators are threatening one over the employee soft vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Indiana Republican Senator Mike Braun. He has signed a letter saying he would not vote for any legislation that funds the enforcement of an employer vaccine mandate. But I believe he had stopped short of wanting to use the government funding bill as a way to threatening a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But, Senator, I don`t want to put words in your mouth. Let me ask you, I know you would like to see a defunding of the federal government`s push for the private vaccine mandate. Are you advocating a government shutdown to do that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE BRAUN (R-IN): I`m going to agree with you, Chuck. And what I just heard the leader say as well. We always get, you know, we`re holding the bag on the government shutdown deal. We get blamed for it regardless of the merits of the case. That`s why I chose to get on the CRA version of it. But when I look at some that are wanting to it`s probably due to frustration. Frustrated me since I`ve been here a little under three years.\par \par \uc2 We don`t do budgets any more. No regular order. And remember, this is spending that should have been flushed out before September 30th. It`s for the prior year. CRs, and I guess the only consolation is there are so much money wanting to be spent with a new appropriations regimen that CRs are actually beneficial.\par \par \uc2 But no, shutting government down is another part of the dysfunction here because we don`t do budgets, we don`t mind the business of the biggest business in the world for all the people that depend upon it, and we spend too much money, we borrow too much money. Shutting the government down is not the way to solve anything.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let`s talk about the Congressional Review Act. You know, we can talk about the merits of the idea in a second. But you are -- you are using what one would call a proper channel to address your concerns about what the executive branch is doing. Explain why you want to use the Congressional Review Act to do this.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: First of all, my, I guess, street sense, gut feel was that I could get all Republicans on it once we initiated it and had everyone on it I believe before the Thanksgiving break on our side. I`m optimistic we`ll get a few Democrats because the underlying issue there gets down to, regardless what you think of the vaccines, I`ve been very clear, lucky we have them, take the disease seriously.\par \par \uc2 But when you couch it in either get the vaccine or lose your job, Axios, 14 percent of the public, that means two-thirds of Democrats don`t agree with that. Courts are weighing in. It`s falling apart because it`s a bad idea. But this is the right way to say that you think it`s a bad idea. Every congressman, every senator will be on record, and that at least we hand up with being able to say you`re for it or against it in the only formal way we can do it.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask about the substance, the vaccine mandate. How you described is actually not how the mandate would work. It is either get the vaccine or testing once a week. That`s a very soft mandate, one that`s -- look, we`ll see what the Supreme Court does. But they`ve been pretty -- they`ve upheld pretty much every mandate that`s come before them so far. We`ll find out how this goes. But that doesn`t seem to be that difficult.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think it`s so difficult for a private business to either have testing to protect its workers or a vaccine?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: For whatever that reason might be, and I still think that`s a personal decision between a doctor and the individual, but when you go into the weeds there`s in some places among federal employees at one of our prisons in Indiana, 20 percent didn`t want to get tested or take the vaccine. It sounds like there`s flexibility there or an option. But, Chuck, the reality is through many parts of the country -- and it may be misguided in terms of that point of view -- we`re going to lose 10 percent to 20 percent that don`t like either option there.\par \par \uc2 And especially when it`s in the form of a top-down mandate so I think that you might be able to interpret that as giving flexibility. But the reality is that some places aren`t going to like either option.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s the -- where is the evidence that there is actually going to be 20 percent not participating? The evidence so far, Tysons has been like 97 percent, 98 percent. \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 . I mean, we`ve not -- all of -- there`s been this fear that there will be a whole bunch of people walking away. That hasn`t happened. You know, look.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: I got --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: America, at the end of the day, they`re going to take their paycheck.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: So the explanation for that is very clear. Larger corporate America has been behind, I think, the vaccine mandate from the get-go. I`m taking the pulse of main street America, in places like southern Indiana, throughout my state, in businesses that aren`t large corporations. This isn`t going to impact between 100 and 500 employees, 93,000 businesses that aren`t big corporations, that are -- were defined as small businesses because we were helping them keep employees up to 500.\par \par \uc2 Now we want to force them away down to 100. It`s a bad decision to be pushing that upon places like where I come from. It`s not corporate America. It`s main street.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, I do have a bunch more questions for you but the president has begun his speech in his new COVID plan so I have to stop our interview there. Thank you for being here. I appreciate it. Here`s the president.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The incredible work you`ve done during the pandemic. And developing vaccines, saving lives, giving us hope. Also, also continuing to give me advice on the developments as they occur. I`ve seen more of Dr. Fauci than I have my wife. We kid each other. But look, who`s president? Fauci? But all kidding aside. I sincerely mean it.\par \par \uc2 Today, I`m back to announce our action plan to battle COVID-19 this winter. Not that any of us are surprised of the news because as the combined advice from all of you that we developed this plan. And it doesn`t include shutdowns or lockdowns but widespread vaccinations and boosters and testing and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 There are five key actions that I want to see us take this winter. First of all, first is expanding the nationwide booster campaign with more outreach, more appointments, more hours, more times and sites to walk in, providing boosters, shots for up to 110 million Americans who are eligible for boosters.\par \par \uc2 Now you might want to know parenthetically, I was talking to one of my folks who does polling and national strategy. And he said there is some evidence in one poll, I won`t mention it because I`m not positive of the number. I was told this as I was leaving the White House. That there is an expectation that 30 percent of the non-vaxxers said under no circumstances would I get a vaccination because of the new variant are now saying I`m going to get a vaccination.\par \par \uc2 So we hope that`s true. I hope that`s true. But the second point is that launching new family vaccination clinics to make it easier for children and parents and whole families to get vaccinated in one place and new policies to keep our children in school instead of quarantining them at home. I`ll talk about the detail of each of these in a moment. The third piece of this is making free at-home tests more available than ever before and having them covered by your private insurance plans, available in thousands of locations and available at community health centers and other sites for the uninsured who don`t have insurance.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:12]\par \par \uc2 Four, increasing our surge response team that are our doctors, our nurses. I know people in this audience know incredibly well what a surge team is. But medical staff in the communities with rising cases and overburdened hospitals and short on personnel. And by the way they make a gigantic difference. The governors, Republican governors as well as Democratic governors contact me when I go into their states talk about thank you for these surge teams because it really makes a difference in some communities that are hit so much harder than others.\par \par \uc2 They just -- you know, they just can`t make it without -- what we`re going to do I`ll speak to this in more detail in a second. The fifth thing we`re doing we`re going to accelerate our efforts to vaccinate the rest of the world and strengthen the international travel rules for people coming to the United States. I plan to announce -- my plan I`m announcing today pulls no punches in the fight against COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 It is a plan that I think should unite us. I know COVID-19 has been very divisive in this country, it`s become a political issue, which is a sad, sad commentary. It shouldn`t be. But it has been. Now as we move into the winter and face the challenges of this new variant this is a moment we can put the divisiveness behind us, I hope. This is a moment we can do what we haven`t been able to do enough of through this whole pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Get the nation to come together. Unite the nation in a common purpose. To fight this virus, to protect one another, to protect our economic recovery and to think of it in terms of literally a patriotic responsibility rather than somehow you`re denying people their basic rights.\par \par \uc2 The plan I`m announcing today is a plan our scientists and COVID teams have recommended. And while my existing federal vaccination requirements are being reviewed by the courts this plan does not expand or add to those mandates. A plan that all Americans hopefully can rally around. And it should be -- and should get bipartisan support in my humble opinion. It should unite us, not continue to separate us.\par \par \uc2 Parenthetically you all know that there are literally as it relates to whether or not we`re going to pay the federal debt, whether we`re going to in fact have a continued resolution, et cetera, some of my friends on the other team are arguing that if I don`t commit that there will never be any more mandates they`re going to let us default.\par \par \uc2 In the neighborhood -- I can`t remember they would look at me and say, go figure. Go figure. But before I explain each new action in more detail, I want to -- and I`m not going to embarrass you, Doc, but I want to thank Francis Collins. You know, you`ve done an incredible job, Doc, here at NIH. One of the most important scientists in my time, in my view. No, I`m not being solicitous. I think I`m not exaggerating a bit.\par \par \uc2 After I was elected president Dr. Collins was one of the first calls. So I think you`re the second call I made to ask you if you`d stay on. The second call I made. It`s because when I was vice-president I got to know Dr. Collins really well when I was given the opportunity to manage the Cancer Moon Shot initiative. And Dr. Collins is an incredible resource for our nation. And I`m grateful. I mean this sincerely. I`m grateful for everything he`s done for this pandemic and advancing all kinds of medical breakthroughs including mapping the human genome.\par \par \uc2 He recently announced he`s going to step down as director of the NIH this month after a truly consequential tenure. But the good news really is Dr. Collins is going to return to the lab, to the National Genome Research Institute. And we look forward to his unmatched ability and unlocked possibilities that are within our reach. And Doc, the bad news for you is you ain`t getting rid of me, man. I`m going to keep calling you all the time because there`s a lot of other things we can do, you can do to help me to get done. You just tell me, point me in the direction and I`ll follow.\par \par \uc2 I just received a briefing from Dr. Collins and Fauci and Dr. Lawrence Tibek, as well as Dr. Julie Ledgerwood. And I appreciate it very much. My only regret was it was a short meeting because I had to come and speak, I think we`d all benefit more if we`d made my speech short and spend more time with these docs. But, you know, as they study the Omicron variant, that we have just two cases reported here in the United States. But as I explained on Monday, this new variant is cause for concern but not panic.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 We know there`d be -- we knew there`d be cases of this -- of Omicron here in the United States. And it`s here. But we have the best tools, the best vaccines in the world, and the best medicine and the best scientists in the world. We`re going to fight this variants with science and speed, not chaos and confusion. Just like we beat back COVID-19 in the spring and the more powerful Delta variant in the summer and fall. As a result, we entered this winter from a position of strength compared to where America was last winter.\par \par \uc2 Last Christmas, fewer than 1 percent of American adults were fully vaccinated. This Christmas, that number will be 72 percent, including more than 86 percent of seniors, the most vulnerable population. Last Christmas our children were at risk without a COVID-19 vaccine. This Christmas, we have safe, effective vaccines for children age 5 and older with 20 million children and counting now vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the majority of our schools were closed Christmas time in that area. Now we`re 99 percent of our schools are open. But I pledged to always be straight and give you this straight from the shoulder as president of the United States. Tell the American people exactly where we are. So here - - here it is. Experts say the COVID-19 cases will continue to rise in the weeks ahead in this winter. So we need to be ready.\par \par \uc2 You can read the whole plan we`re talking about here at WhiteHouse.gov. Go to WhiteHouse.gov and it`s all laid out in detail. For now here are the five key points I want to expand on slightly. We`re expanding our national booster campaign to provide booster shots to all eligible adults. Our docs and the scientists believe that people who get a booster shot are more protected than ever from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 I was just told which is a question I got to ask I`ve been meaning to ask a long time, whether or not, not just it increases the resistance of the variant that is being dealt with, but it also is -- it is stronger. It not only just raises the total, but it`s a stronger, makes things more powerful in terms of resisting. And the Center for Disease Control and Prevention, the CDC, the Food and Drug Administration, the FDA and our top public officials recommend all adults, all adults get a booster shot when it`s time.\par \par \uc2 But here is the deal. More than about 100 million are eligible for boosters. But haven`t gotten the booster shot yet. Folks, if you`re over the age of 18 and you got vaccinated before June the 2nd, six months has gone by. Go get your booster now. Go get it now. Booster shots continue to be free. We`ve already made available 80,000 locations coast to coast in the United States of America. 80,000 locations to get the booster.\par \par \uc2 And if you want to know exactly where to go, text your zip code to 438829 to find where you can get your booster shot now. Now. And starting today we are making it easier than ever to get a booster shot. I`m calling on pharmacies and they`ve been cooperative, big and small, to offer more appointments, more walk-in hours, including on weeknights and weekends. So you can get vaccinated at a time that works best for you and your family.\par \par \uc2 Pharmacies will send millions of texts and e-mails to remind their customers to return for the booster shots because they know who got the booster shot. At CDC they`ve agreed they`re going to send text to that particular person when the time is up, when they`ve met their -- if it`s \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna, it`s six months. If it`s J&J, two months. And to, you know, to reach our seniors we`re also collaborating with the \uc2 American Association of Retired Persons\uc2 , AARP, who`ll reaching out to their 38 million members.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to make an aggressive effort. They`re going to be hosting virtual townhalls to answer questions and even arrange rides for seniors to get their booster shots. And so that will all be coming. My administration is also contacting the 64 -- will contact the 64 million people on Medicare to remind them to get their booster shots. We have to ability to do that from the federal level. And just like I did to make it easier for folks to get their first and second shots, I am providing paid off time for any federal employee who goes to get their booster.\par \par \uc2 The only time they can get the booster where they are, if it`s such and such a day in the middle of the day, they can go. All they have to do is demonstrate that`s where they went or they took their son, daughter, husband, wife, mom, dad, they get paid. They don`t get docked pay.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 And I`m asking other employers in the private sector to do the same thing. Now I don`t want you to have to choose between a paycheck and getting an additional protection for a booster shot. Now I want to reiterate, Dr. Fauci and Dr. Collins believe if you`re worried about the Omicron variant, the best thing to do is get fully vaccinated and then get your booster shot when you`re eligible.\par \par \uc2 Excuse me. We don`t yet believe. Excuse me. That additional measures will be needed. But so that we`re prepared, if needed, my team is already working with officials at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Moderna, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , to develop contingency plans for other vaccines or boosters. I`ll also direct the FDA and the CDC to use the fastest process available without cutting corners for safety, to get such vaccines renewed, reviewed and approved if they`re needed.\par \par \uc2 Second, we`re expanding our efforts to vaccinate children ages 5 and up. For any parent worried about Omicron variant or the Delta variant, get your child vaccinated at one of the 35,000 locations in the country including doctors` offices, pharmacies, children`s hospitals, and 9,000 pop-up clinics at schools. If you`re wondering where to go, again, visit vaccines.com.\par \par \uc2 And today I`m announcing that we`re going to launch hundreds of new family vaccination clinics across the country. These sites are going to offer vaccinations for the whole family, one-stop. One-stop. Children can get vaccinated. Parents can get vaccinated, and get their first or second shots or their booster shots. Family vaccination clinics will be held in community health centers and other trusted locations.\par \par \uc2 Some will be mobile to reach further into hard-to-reach communities. It`s booster shots for adults, vaccinations for kids, all at the same place, at the same time. Now, you know, when we first announced, I said the logistical problem, it wasn`t just we didn`t have enough vaccines when we got into office, but once we got the vaccine, the logistical effort to get all communities in all areas vaccinated was a gigantic logistical undertaking that would make any military proud of being able to do it.\par \par \uc2 But because of the incredible talent, including our military, we got that done. And we`re going to do the same thing now as we continue to expand. We know parents of children under the age of 5 are wondering when the vaccine will be available for little ones. That`s the question I most get often asked now at functions. We had a function celebrating Hanukkah and Christmas and various things at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The parents come up to me, they`re saying, I have a 3-year-old, is there going to be a time that that -- am I going to be OK, is she going to be OK, or is he going to be OK? And let me say this. I strongly support the independent scientific review of vaccine usage for children under 5. We can`t take shortcuts with that scientific work. But I`ll do everything in my power to support the FDA to do this safety and quickly as possible when we get to that round-up point.\par \par \uc2 Vaccinating our children is also critical to keeping our schools open. But while over 99 percent of our schools are open now, we need to make sure that we keep that throughout the winter, this winter. The CDC is now reviewing pioneering approaches like what we call test to stay. Test to stay policies, which could allow students to stay in the classroom and be tested frequently when a positive case in that classroom popped up that wasn`t them.\par \par \uc2 Up to now you go home and are quarantined. But rather than being sent home and be quarantined, they`ll be able to stay because the tests will be available, and regularly. The CDC will be releasing the latest science and other findings in the coming weeks so that other schools can learn from the impediments -- excuse me, if there`s any impediments in this practice, they can learn to implement exactly what the best way to do this is. There`s a process.\par \par \uc2 We want our children in school and we`re going to take new steps to make sure it stays that way. But again, the best step is to vaccinate your children, get them vaccinated. The third, this winter we`re going to make free at-home tests more available to more Americans than ever before. To better detect and control the Delta variant, I made testing more available, affordable, and convenient.\par \par \uc2 I used the Defense Production Act to increase production of rapid tests, including at-home tests. When I came into office, none of these tests were on the market. Thanks to our actions and the work of all of you, we now have at least eight at-home testing options and prices for those tests are coming down.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 But it still isn`t good enough, in my view. That`s why I`m announcing that health insurers must cover the cost of at-home testing, so that if you`re one of the 150 million Americans with private health insurance, next month your plan will cover at-home tests. Private insurers already cover the expensive PCR test that you get in the doctor`s office. And now they will cover at-home tests as well.\par \par \uc2 Now for those not covered by private insurance, we`re going to make it available, free tests, at thousands of convenient locations for folks to pick them up and take a test kit home. The bottom line, this winter, you`ll be able to test for free in the comfort of your home and have some peace of mind. This is on top of the 20,000 sites already around the country like pharmacies where you can go in and get tested for free. We have to keep this going.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, we`re going to continue to help communities that experience rising cases this winter and improve the care for those who get COVID-19. Since this summer, we`ve worked with Republican and Democratic governors, as many Republican governors as Democratic governors, to deploy what we call surge response teams. These teams work. They provide needed staff for staff overruns, badly needed staff where overrun hospitals are handling more patients than they can handle for their emergency rooms and intensive care units, who don`t have the personnel available.\par \par \uc2 They help provide life-saving treatments in communities in need like monoclonal antibody treatments. We have over 20 teams deployed now. Today I`m announcing that we`re going to triple that, more than double. We`re going to get to 60 teams, ready to be deployed to states expecting a surge in cases over the course of this winter.\par \par \uc2 Just the governor in Minnesota who`s raving about the positive impact it`s had on his state. But there`s other states the same, in the same circumstance. Additionally, we`re increasing the availability of new medications recommended by real doctors, not conspiracy theorists, OK? For example, monoclonal antibody treatments have been shown to reduce the risk of hospitalization by up to 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 And for unvaccinated people at risk of developing severe disease. We`ve already distributed over three million courses of this treatment to save lives and reduce the strain on hospitals. And we have promising new arriving pill, excuse me, antiviral pills on the horizon. They could help prevent hospitalizations and deaths for people infected by COVID-19. We`ve secured enough supply of these pills already and early indications are that these treatments will remain effective in the face of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know that for sure yet but that`s the hope and expectation. And like with the distribution of vaccines, we will ensure that these medicines will be available to the hardest hit communities in America as well.\par \par \uc2 Fifth and finally, as we see with COVID-19 and the Delta variant and now with the Omicron variant, all that emerged elsewhere, it all came from somewhere else. And all of them -- to beat this pandemic, we need to go to where it came from in the rest of the world. We also need to vaccinate the rest of the world. America has in my view continues to lead in that effort. We ship for free more vaccines around the world than all other countries in the world combined. Every other country combined.\par \par \uc2 Over 280 million vaccines so far to 110 countries including South Africa where we delivered all the doses they requested. Now, today I`m announcing that we`ll accelerate the delivery of more vaccines to countries that need it. We pledge to deliver 200 million more doses within the next hundred days on our way to delivering more than 1,200, 000,000 doses for the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 Let me be clear. There`s not a single vaccine dose that America ever sends to the rest of the world that will ever come at the expense of any American. I`ll always make sure our people are protected first. But vaccinating the world is not just a moral obligation that we have in my view. It`s how we protect Americans, as we`ve seen with this new variant. America is doing our part, and we`ll do more.\par \par \uc2 But this is a global pandemic. And everyone needs to fight it together. And that includes countries we`re helping that aren`t particularly friendly toward us.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211203ehc200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC REPORTS for November 26, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Katy Tur, Raf Sanchez, Josh Lederman, Irwin Redlener, Jo Ling  Kent, Ellison Barber, Jonathan Allen, Erin McLaughlin, Carmen Best, Emilie  Ikeda \par \uc2 8,172 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: There will be some safety protocols in place. But Jose, we are inching towards normalcy, back to you.\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, MSNBC HOST: Emilie Ikeda, thank you very much. There`s just -- for the kids that are watching, there`s the professional Santa, he`s being affected. The real one, unaffected, he`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 That wraps up the hour for me I`m Jose Diaz-Balart. I`ll see you tomorrow night on NBC NIGHTLY NEWS SATURDAY. Thank you for the privilege of your time. Katy Tur picks up with more news right now.\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: And good to be with you, I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 As we come on the air this Friday, there is growing alarm here at home and around the world, with word of a new potentially even more contagious variant of the Coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 That discovery comes from Africa triggering a flood of countries to suspend travel from about half a dozen Southern African nations.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. was one of the first countries to initiate a travel ban followed quickly by the 27 nation \uc2 European Union\uc2  moving to contain the variants, taking no chances before it spreads around the world.\par \par \uc2 The ripple effect has been swift with emergency meetings of health officials. And the stock market plunging, the Dow down 800 points at the open, 900 now.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 , meanwhile, is urging caution on travel bans, and U.S. health officials say while a ban is possible, it is too soon to know.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Well, I mean, obviously as soon as we find out more information, we`ll make a decision as quickly as we possibly can.\par \par \uc2 You always put these things on the table, but you don`t want to say you`re going to do it until you have some scientific reason to do it.\par \par \uc2 That`s the reason why we`re rushing now to get that scientific data to try and make an informed decision about something like that.\par \par \uc2 You`re prepared to do everything you need to do to protect the American public, but you want to make sure there`s a basis for doing that and that`s what we`re doing right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUR: Joining me now is NBC News Foreign Correspondent Raf Sanchez in Tel Aviv, NBC News Correspondent Josh Lederman, who was with President Biden in Nantucket, MSNBC Medical Contributor Dr. Irwin Redlener, and Politico Chief Economic Correspondent Ben White, everybody, welcome and thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, Raf, you`re in Israel, the first cases were found outside of Africa, in Israel, and in Hong Kong, and Belgium. I know Tel Aviv and Israel is shutting down travel from South Africa. What else is happening there?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, MSNBC FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Katy, Israel`s government is moving more aggressively than any other so far, they are stopping travel from pretty much every country in Africa with only a handful of exceptions.\par \par \uc2 This comes after one confirmed case two suspected cases of that new variant, the confirmed case in a traveler who returned recently from Malawi.\par \par \uc2 Now, all three of those people are in isolation, but the Israeli Prime Minister, Naftali Bennett, is preparing the public here for further spread. He is telling people that Israel is at the threshold of an emergency situation.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen similarly, the UK and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , moving to restrict travel slightly, less drastically but from South Africa, and from five other Southern African nations.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen other travel restrictions coming into place in Japan, in Singapore, so far. As you heard from Dr. Fauci, there`s no indication yet that the U.S. is planning to bring in a similar travel ban.\par \par \uc2 And there is some debate among medical professionals in Israel and around the world about whether these travel bans actually work to stop the variants from spreading.\par \par \uc2 Whether they serve a medical purpose, or if it`s more about the government being seen to do something in the face of an alarming new variant like this one emerging, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So, the variant has just been named Omicron by the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 . I apologize if I`m butchering that, but that`s the name.\par \par \uc2 Raf, Israel has also been very skittish when it comes to this virus. They close their borders down for quite a lot of time. Is there any chance that they`re going to be closing their borders down again to travelers beyond South Africa?\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ: I think the Israeli government hasn`t ruled anything out and the tone coming from the Israeli Prime Minister today was not looking to calm people, he was looking to really get their attention.\par \par \uc2 Things here in Israel have a pretty normal feel to them right now. Most, you know businesses, restaurants, shops, cafes are open. This is a highly vaccinated country. Boosters were rolled out here much sooner than everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 But despite those levels of protection you know, much higher rates of vaccination than what you`re seeing in the U.S., the Israeli government is really trying to sound the alarm that this is serious, the virus is already in Israel, it may spread further.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli Prime Minister saying it does appear from what he knows that this variant may end up being more transmissible than the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of outstanding questions about how vaccines will stand up to it whether or not this variant causes more severe illness. But right now, the Israeli government is not taking anything off the table. Katy.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TUR: Oh, gosh. Josh, let`s talk about what`s happening here. I know that we have been slow to shut down our borders, even when other countries have done.\par \par \uc2 So how is the President weighing this right now? If this is a more contagious variant another trying to figure out more about it, why wait to shut down the border to South Africa?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: But Katy, I`ve been posing that very question to White House officials, as they`re watching very closely, that WHO meeting today about this variant, they want to see what comes out of that.\par \par \uc2 But at this point, there are no indications that the U.S. is on the verge of enacting new travel restrictions in response to this Variant.\par \par \uc2 The White House really wants to avoid a knee-jerk response. They feel like it has been their message from the get-go that any response to COVID needs to be science-based. It needs to be based on data and facts. Frankly, dating back to the time when they were campaigning against President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and tried to draw that contrast with him.\par \par \uc2 And so they don`t want to take policy measures that are based on people`s emotional concerns about this variant, legitimate as they may be.\par \par \uc2 The White House is really watching two particular concerns when it comes to this Variant. Transmissibility, meaning is this more infectious than the Delta variant even or the previous strains of this virus, and then, of course, immune response, is this something that the vaccines are going to be effective against?\par \par \uc2 At this point, the White House has largely been deferring to Dr. Fauci who, as you were showing a few minutes ago is saying look, we need more science, we need more data before we can draw those kinds of conclusions.\par \par \uc2 So the White House at this point in time, not ready to really change the current plan for fighting COVID in response to this variant until they have those kinds of answers that our scientists are just starting to get as they look at this virus in much more detail.\par \par \uc2 But of course, the other big concern here, Katy, is the profound implications this is having on the economy, on the markets with people, once again, unsettled by this news of potentially yet another variant that could change the game with COVID.\par \par \uc2 The White House is tracking that economic shift very closely while trying to encourage people to take a deep breath and wait until there`s a little bit more information about exactly what this means before they start altering their economic behavior or trying to predict what could happen 1, 2, 3 months into the future.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Remember, the Dow and Wall Street, these are just snapshots -- the stock market just snapshots in time, we often see these big plummets only to see massive rises again a couple of days later, so hold your breath on that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll get to more of the economic fallout from this in just a moment, but I do want to focus on what we do know about this variant.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Redlener, Josh was just mentioning science and data and facts. And that`s what the White House says they`re going to be relying on. What do we know concretely about this variant so far?\par \par \uc2 DR. IRWIN REDLENER, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, hi, Katy. Yes, so this is a very interesting and potentially alarming but not yet alarming situation because there are several things we need to watch about the virus.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it`s a variant that`s definitely been identified has its own number 1.1.529. It doesn`t yet have a name and the W -- the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  will help determine that.\par \par \uc2 But the things that we need to watch here now are number one, will this be more transmissible, as Josh was saying? Will it be some ways resistant to the vaccines that are out there?\par \par \uc2 But the third thing is, will it be actually more dangerous? Will this particular variant of SARS Cov-2 cause more hospitalizations and more fatalities?\par \par \uc2 All of these factors are being watched very, very carefully. But those of - - those of us who have heard about the spike protein with this virus, we`ll understand that this is a -- the new variant has caused a lot of mutations on that particularly critical protein called the spike protein, couple of dozen new mutations, which is why we don`t understand yet what the trajectory is going to be of this new threat.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I`m calling it a threat, but I want to even keep that on, you know, on check a bit until we have more information, really start following this the way it needs to be.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, this is not the end of new mutations and variants more to come, unfortunately, in the future.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, we`ve been told that the more people get infected, the more the virus does mutate. It`s trying to survive as we`re trying to escape it.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TUR: Just to be clear, the WHO has named it now just moments ago, Dr. Redlener, you might have missed it. Omicron is what it`s called, named after Greek letters of the alphabet. This is the 15th letter of the Greek alphabet, Omicron.\par \par \uc2 And if I`m not saying that correctly, I`m so sorry. I`m so sorry to my Greek family members who will be upset by my mispronunciation but hopefully, I am.\par \par \uc2 Ben White, let`s talk about the economic impact. We`re seeing the stock market go down quite a bit. Are you taking a lot of stock in that reaction? No pun intended.\par \par \uc2 BEN WHITE, CHIEF ECONOMIC CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: I`m selling all my stock. No, obviously, I don`t do that. Hello, Katy, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 This was always going to happen. This was always the risk to this economy and to the stock market. I don`t know how many times on your show and in stories and elsewhere when people ask about the trajectory of these markets in the economy.\par \par \uc2 What is the big risk? The big risk is future Delta or future COVID variants that are resistant to our vaccines and that can spread quickly.\par \par \uc2 And I think is Dr. Redlener -- Dr. Fauci have correctly pointed out there is so much we don`t know, there is no reason to panic yet, there is no reason to be to -- be terribly concerned about this as of yet, but it is the thing that was always going to knock markets off their record pace.\par \par \uc2 Like, Katy, when we hit the, you know, bottom of COVID, we rocketed up in the stock market to record levels, we`ve been in record levels, so we were now just slightly below those. So we`re still very much up on the stock market.\par \par \uc2 But if there is something that`s going to slow the recovery down, get people scared, not spending, not taking jobs, not moving the economy forward, it was always going to be the direction of the virus and new variants like this that could threaten us.\par \par \uc2 But I think taking the cautious approach, the scientific approach, let`s see how bad it is, let`s see how are vaccines hold up to it, let`s see if it comes to these shores should give people some comfort that we`re not at the point to panic or dump all your stocks, the economy`s going to fall apart yet.\par \par \uc2 This is negative news. It`s going to hit the market for a day or two. Wait for details, see where we are. But everybody on Wall Street will tell you, this is the story they`ve been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Can you explain to me why we would be watching the stock market in these moments. And I don`t mean that to be dismissive.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TUR: But we`ve seen this over and over again, where the stock market crashes, plummets down, 900 points, a thousand points, and a few days later, it skyrockets once again.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes. No, it`s a good -- it`s a very fair point not to take any move like this you know, out of context.\par \par \uc2 These moves of 900 points aren`t what they used to be since we`re at 34,000 on the Dow, I like to say that don`t really start to freak out unless we`re at 5 percent, 6 percent, 7 percent movement.\par \par \uc2 This is something like 2 percent, it`s not that much. I mean, it`s -- what you would expect when traders wake up and read in their papers, that there is a new variant in Africa, the Israelis are thinking of shutting down borders, that many in Europe are shutting down borders. These are the headlines that scare traders and so they start to sell some stock.\par \par \uc2 But they`re also taking off a lot of profit before the holidays. You know, markets made a lot of money, people`s 401(k) have done very well, so put it in context.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it`s not the biggest concern in the world what the market does. The biggest concern in the world obviously is beating this virus so I would pay a lot more attention to that.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Ben, because it is Black Friday, we`re going to get our money`s worth out of view. You`re sticking around for the next block.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Raf Sanchez, Josh Lederman, and Dr. Irwin Redlener, gentlemen, thank you so much for joining us today and helping us understand what`s going on\par \par \uc2 Coming up the investigations into the uptick in the smash and grab robberies. You`ve seen this crazy video. Could your online purchases be a part of an organized crime syndicate across the country? It`s something that I would like to know.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Black Friday, as we`ve been saying is in full swing. What`s by now, what`s a weight on, and with demand and prices high, also supply low, and the holiday season upon us, what you can expect?\par \par \uc2 Also later, Help Wanted in the North Pole, The Jolly Old Elf no one can seem to find. This is MSNBC REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TUR: As I`m sure you`re well aware by now, it is Black Friday. And this year, Americans are expected to return to in-person shopping. But when you get there the sales won`t be as deep. Historic inflation and low inventory mean prices aren`t quite as good as years past.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC News Business and Tech Correspondent \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  from Alexandria, Virginia, NBC News Correspondent Ellison Barber from Atlanta, and back with me is Politico Chief Economic Correspondent Ben White, everybody welcome.\par \par \uc2 Jo, I shudder to think the number of planes you`ve been on in the past few days, a mother with small children, but I`m happy to see you. I`m happy to hear what you have for us from Alexandria, Virginia --\par \par \uc2 JO KENT, MSNBC BUSINESS & THE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, to you too.\par \par \uc2 TUR: -- What should I know?\par \par \uc2 KENT: Well, what you need to know is right now, online shopping is very much going strong, $5.1 billion, according to \uc2 Adobe\uc2  was spent yesterday on Thanksgiving Day alone.\par \par \uc2 Now, behind me, you see there`s quite a bit of buzz here at the \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  in Alexandria. But you know the line coming in this morning, those typical door-busting lines, we`re pretty sure, and that was also the case out in Mall of America and you can start to see the impact of inflation, the high cost of living impacting the way that people may be shopping.\par \par \uc2 And so that has individuals shopping online more shopping earlier trying to capture those discounts.\par \par \uc2 And as you think about what that means for Black Friday and the entire holiday season, it definitely has caught the -- caught the attention of Wall Street and the CEOs of these companies.\par \par \uc2 In fact, an executive here at \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  telling me they`re completely shifting the way they do business.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to take these giant stores like this one I`m standing in right now, and transform a lot of them and shrink down the floor size and actually add warehousing to get you stuff faster. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JO KENT, MSNBC BUSINESS & THE CORRESPONDENT: How much faster can you get orders to customers now?\par \par \uc2 DAMIEN HARMON, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT OF BEST BUY: I`d say 10 times faster because only focused on taking your product and getting it out the back door.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENT: Now, that`s Damien Harmon, Executive Vice President of Best Buy. And he is going around the country transforming these \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  stores shrinking them by more than half, adding a warehouse to get packages out quickly because right now we`ve become so accustomed to fast delivery.\par \par \uc2 That`s how a store like Best Buy`s trying to compete with an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or target or your Neighborhood electronic store.\par \par \uc2 But very much all about immediate gratis -- instant gratification, because our patience is worn thin and also, we`re dealing with this massive supply chain crisis, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I miss my Neighborhood electronics store. You tell me where I can find a Neighborhood electronics store and I`ll happily go there.\par \par \uc2 Jo, let me ask you this, does this mean that if you go to a \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  and you`re trying to get I don`t know, printer, TV, that you`re going to have to order it when you get there, and then it will be shipped to you?\par \par \uc2 Because I`ve been to some stores lately that do that and I got to tell you, it is the most frustrating thing. I want to walk in and walk out with what I`m purchasing.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Yes, I have the same frustration as well. It really depends on the product that you`re buying. If you`re buying something that they have in stock, obviously, it`s going to be maybe a less popular item, you can walk out with it.\par \par \uc2 But I asked Damien Harmon, that exact same question about gaming consoles, for example, if you`re looking for a PlayStation 5, or an Xbox, though, they`re very hard to find they sell out mostly online.\par \par \uc2 And so the question is, will there be more stocks in time for Christmas? That`s really the question that a lot of people should be asking right now.\par \par \uc2 And that`s not necessarily going to be a yes. And the issue is because of all of the clogged, ports of LA and Long Beach, not being able to get the stuff over from Asia in time for the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 So my advice and the advice from a lot of shopping experts out there is, go ahead and have a plan B, and also have a Plan C because if you can`t get it now, it`s only a few weeks away, but those few weeks mean a much shorter time, compared to last year or the year before. It`s a totally new game that we`re playing out here for retail, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Very interesting. Ellison, you`re down there in Georgia, What`s it like there?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, this Mall is very busy. There were a decent amount of lines outside when it opened up at 7 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Santa behind me, they use the amount of photos taken -- paid photos -- Santa photos to kind of get a barometer at this mall, in terms of what their holiday season traffic is going to be like.\par \par \uc2 And Santa has been here a little over a week and they tell us that the amount of Santa photos sold this first week is more than the amount they sold the first week he was here before the pandemic back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 So people are coming to this mall. And we`ve spoken to a lot of people who said they are opting to shop in person, in part because they are nervous about shipping delays. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: We hear so much with people talking about supply chain issues, labor shortages, delays, bottlenecking, all that sort of stuff. Is that impacting how you shop this year?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE1: Yes. Yes, for real, because like it`s the shortage of everything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE2: I don`t feel like it`s been affecting me because the store is full. Oh, everything looks open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A lot of deals weren`t -- it weren`t there, we actually went to two stores already in one of them, we came out with basically two things. And basically, there`s nothing on sale at this moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE3: Usually, I would spend a lot more than I do. And the Christmas presents this year, it`s not going to be as much due to the fact that, you know, everything`s increase and you`re not saving as much, which is quite sad. But you got to do what you got to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: I`m speaking with the general manager of this particular mall, she thinks that the supply chain issues could actually help brick and mortar stores because you will have more people in her view, who just don`t want to risk getting some sort of delay or getting a product that they didn`t necessarily want or it was different than they thought and sending it back and then waiting even more for it to come.\par \par \uc2 So she thinks that all of that will factor in on more people showing up in shopping in person this year. And again, they seem like they`re on track here, they see levels that are above what they saw pre-pandemic, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Ben, maybe I`m just getting grumpier in my old age. I hate Black Friday. I hate -- I hate the entire concept of Black Friday. I hate the rampant consumerism of it.\par \par \uc2 I hate that everybody goes out and spends the day at a mall instead of time with their family. I hate that all these Big-box stores are replacing all of our local stores.\par \par \uc2 Main Street, I like walking out of my house and going to a local shop and getting a vacuum cleaner if I need one or I don`t know, a baby monitor if I need one.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Are we -- are we just moving past that entirely? Are those days gone? Is everything going to be either online or at a Big-box store from now on?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Right. No, I don`t think so. I think we`ve moved in that direction largely over recent decades to Big-box and getting rid of Mom-and-pop stores and smaller venues.\par \par \uc2 But I think they`re still a niche market. I was just in like a brand-new general store in Hastings-on-Hudson the other day, there`s still a desire for people to be able to shop in smaller stores.\par \par \uc2 And maybe in a way, all of these delays and slowdowns and problems with Big-box stores and problems with all these orders will push people to maybe shop a little more local.\par \par \uc2 The larger trend and it would be a good idea this year in particular, shop local, shop local, shop local. There are places around you that have cool stuff that are small businesses where you can get gifts, might not be an Oculus, might not be an iPad, but something cool that you know you or your loved ones might like.\par \par \uc2 But overall, you know, this is one funny anecdote. You know, I`m in the car driving my younger kid home from a playdate the other day, he`s 12 years old, he`s very organized, he has his full list of holiday Hanukkah Christmas items and he says Dad, you know, their supply chain disruptions and their port delays, so you might want to get on this quickly.\par \par \uc2 I`m like, huh, you`re 12 like how do you know this stuff? I mean he has -- he hears me talk about it. But he was very well aware and eager for me to order sooner, which I did not do, which I have to do after we get off of this call and the windows open on my computer are things to order.\par \par \uc2 But yes, there could be a move back to some more local shopping, I would highly recommend it this year in particular, both help out your local business and not deal with all of these supply chains.\par \par \uc2 But overall, just to put the big picture on Black Friday and the holiday season, we should do better. It should be a record. You know, we spend something close to a trillion dollars on holiday sales this year, which will be a record.\par \par \uc2 There is a higher volume in stores not that much higher but a little higher than last year and not as big a jump in online sales, it although a job.\par \par \uc2 So people are going back to the stores. But as your reporters noted, it`s not necessarily the giant. Everybody, kill yourself, knock yourselves down lines, which you know amen for that because nobody needs to get trampled on Black Friday.\par \par \uc2 But getting them all with you, you know, let`s find the local you know, electronic store and local craft store and you know, buy a couple of nice things then go have some hot chocolate, hang out with family.\par \par \uc2 TUR: If you want your neighborhood to stay nice, if you want to enjoy the people around you, shop local.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Out of some.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Pick a book at the bookstore, not on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHITE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: You can walk down the street and get it at the bookstore.\par \par \uc2 WHITE: This is a year to do it.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I`m going to stop ranting because like, I can feel my executive producer in the control room telling me to shut up even though she`s not saying it in my ear yet. I can feel her doing it.\par \par \uc2 Ben White, thank you so much. Ellison Barber, she just said Correct. \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2 , thank you. And Happy, Happy Black Friday, guys. I hope you get some time with your families eventually after this is all over.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the President faces his lowest approval rating, record inflation, and a massive not a short thing spending plan. What is the White House doing to try to overcome it?\par \par \uc2 But it is not just the White House, Congress has a lot to get done and not a lot of time. Big functional bills that need to get passed for the government to keep working. You`re watching MSNBC REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:44]\par \par \uc2 TUR: When President Biden gets back to Washington after the holiday, he is going to have a lot waiting for him. His Build Back Better plan is now with the Senate where it`s not exactly a sure thing, and his low approval isn`t helping anything either.\par \par \uc2 So what does that mean for 2024? Well, nothing yet. "The Washington Post" reports that those close to the president are reassuring his allies that he will run for reelection in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC News digital senior political reporter Jonathan Allen and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " political reporter Jeremy Peters.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, really great to have you. So, Jonathan, let`s talk about the president`s agenda, this Build Back Better plan, is the second part of his infrastructure push. The social part of it. Does he have a good chance of getting it to the Senate without Joe Manchin going in and tinkering away?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS DIGITAL SENIOR POLITICAL REPORTER: I mean, you can`t get it through the Senate without Joe Manchin doing what he will with it, because he needs those 50 votes even in this reconciliation, the expedited version of the legislation that he`s trying to do Build Back Better under. Joe Manchin is the 50th vote and he needs that 50th vote in order for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president, to be able to break a tie.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin has, I think, you know, signaled some bearishness about the current state of the economy and there are provisions in the House passed version that Manchin is uncomfortable with. So we`ll have to see when the Senate comes back exactly where Senator Manchin stands. But it doesn`t look good for the White House moving quickly on this.\par \par \uc2 TUR: He doesn`t want to move quickly. So when it gets to the Senate, what sort of expectation should the White House have, Jeremy, on what this bill is going to look like?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY PETERS, THE NEW YORK TIMES POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, I think that there`s practically zero chance that it looks like what the House passed. And that`s going to be quite disappointing to a lot of progressives, but also it doesn`t necessarily spell doom for a very progressive reform package that will have sweeping impacts on everything from the way people raise their children to what the future of our country looks like in terms of climate.\par \par \uc2 So we just don`t know what Manchin is going to want to change. He is quite mercurial and seems to really enjoy his time in the spotlight and the, you know, king-making role that he`s been playing. And it really going forward, it`s all, as Jon said, going to be about what Joe Manchin wants.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, yes, OK. So there`s two things that are going to get raised here. At least on my reading of it. One is paid family leave. Joe Manchin has said he`s not into paid family leave. He thinks people should get paid for working, not paid for not working. There`s a month of paid family leave in the Build Back Better version that the House passed.\par \par \uc2 This is not just for mothers and fathers or adoptive parents or parents in general. It`s for any family member who needs to take care of another family member who has gotten sick. It`s a month paid. There`s also the child tax credit, Jonathan Allen, that might be in the crosshairs to make it a little bit less generous or a little bit less easy for a wide swath of Americans to receive.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:07]\par \par \uc2 Those are two big deal points for the Democratic agenda, and two big deal points that Democrats campaigned on to get to where they are back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Yes, absolutely, Katy. Those are huge ticket items. I don`t think the bill would have passed through the House as easily as it did. It wasn`t easy, but it wouldn`t have passed through the House as easily without that paid family leave, and that there were a lot of objections to try to move it without that. So it was put back in. They passed it. But Senator Manchin is not a big fan, as you note, of that.\par \par \uc2 And he`s also questioned extending the expanded child tax credit that we already saw earlier this year from the Biden administration and Congress. I think he is interested in reigning in the overall spending and drawing different lines on who is defined as middle class, who needs that assistance than certainly President Biden wants.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Let`s talk about reelection. Jeremy, the president is privately assuring his allies that he will be running again in 2024 according to "The Washington Post."\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Yes. That`s exactly right. And I don`t think that`s necessarily surprising even though there`s been some, you know, parlor game speculation going on in political circles, will he, won`t he? I mean, as far as he is concerned, he`s the only one who has proven that he`s capable of beating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And as it looks increasingly likely that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will run again, that`s Biden`s case that he`ll be making.\par \par \uc2 I think the question is what kind of campaign 2024 looks like. It`s going to be much different, because the coronavirus pandemic really limited what Biden had to do publicly. And if he`s out there every day campaigning as one would expect if the world is returning to normal, that kind of opens up the question of whether or not he can withstand the scrutiny. I mean, he`s always been somebody who puts his foot in his mouth on occasion in pretty damaging ways.\par \par \uc2 And the difference this time will be a much less forgiving, to put it mildly, Republican Party and like the Republican nominee who are just out for blood and out to embarrass and humiliate Biden and exaggerate every misstep he makes. So there`s really, you know, not a whole lot of room for error here.\par \par \uc2 TUR: It`s going to be a tough election year.\par \par \uc2 Jeremy, you also have another book coming out or a new book coming out. "Insurgency: How Republicans Lost Their Party and Got Everything They Ever Wanted." When can I purchase this book?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: You can purchase it now. Go anywhere online. Your favorite book seller, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Barnes & Nobles\uc2 , and it`s preordered that way. And it will be delivered to you February 8th. That would be a great Black Friday move for those of you who are not venturing out into the malls.\par \par \uc2 TUR: February 8th. Congratulations, Jeremy. I know how hard it is to finish a book.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: You do.\par \par \uc2 TUR: My hat is off to you, sir. Jonathan knows as well. They`re tough, painful.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, California takes aim at the growing trend of smash-and- grab robberies. Who law enforcement says is really behind it all. Plus even the North Pole isn`t immune to labor shortages. Why Santa may not be coming to a mall near you.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:49]\par \par \uc2 TUR: In California a string of coordinated thefts have shoppers and retail workers on high alert. The robberies are being carried out by mobs of looters. Some armed with hammers and crowbars. They smash their way into high-end stores and leave with tens of thousands of dollars of stuff.\par \par \uc2 NBC News correspondent Erin McLaughlin has the story.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Katy, we`re seeing so-called smash-and-grab robberies happening here in California from San Francisco to Los Angeles. And it`s a problem that law enforcement officials say is only getting worse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): Amidst the Black Friday frenzy, retail stores and shoppers across California on edge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So it is kind of scary. And the fact that this has happened now makes me wonder if I should even walk in there.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Following a string of so-called smash-and-grab robberies, striking retail stores across the state. Wednesday night outside Los Angeles, suspects attacked a department store security guard. Snatching $25,000 worth of high-end purses.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They attacked him with some kind of a caustic chemical. After the initial suspect attacked the security guard, additional suspects entered the location and removed a number of high value -- high-dollar purses.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Criminals seen using similar tactics in the heart of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hands up. Hands up there.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: And in a nearby suburb. Organized shoplifting crews breaking into high-end retailers, grabbing merchandise and fleeing as a cluster of get away cars block traffic. An estimated $200,000 worth of merchandise gone in a matter of minutes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I saw people running down the street. I probably saw 50 to 80 people in like ski masks, crowbars, like a bunch of weapons.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Many of the robberies orchestrated authorities believe by criminal masterminds who stay far away from their crimes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re paying the individuals to do the robberies. They collect the goods, and then fence them and move them to other parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: And the situation is getting worse. In 2020 the state saw more than five times the number of investigations into organized retail crime compared to 2019. Designer clothes and handbags now among the top targets.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 According to law enforcement officials, California is now home to three of the nation`s top 10 cities for organized retail theft.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): You`re a holiday shopper in California. How concerned should you be about this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think people need to keep their wits about them. Don`t flash cash. Keep your purse in two hands or close to your body under your coat. At the sign of trouble, loud noise, cars where they don`t belong, get off the sidewalk, get off the street.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: We don`t know why it`s happened here and now. Some are pointing to legislation that downgrades the penalty for this kind of crime. Others are pointing to copycat attacks and the Internet. Many of these goods end up online for sale on fringe Web sites -- Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Erin McLaughlin, Erin, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And with me now is retired Seattle police chief and MSNBC law enforcement analyst Carmen Best.\par \par \uc2 Carmen, it`s really great to have you. California in particular did change the laws to make low level crimes like stealing not as -- the time for them not as long or the punishment not as hard. Part of the reason was because there`s overcrowding in jails. Do you see this as the issue that`s fueling the rise in the smash-and-grab thefts?\par \par \uc2 CARMEN BEST, RETIRED SEATTLE POLICE CHIEF: Well, Katy, there`s a lot of things to consider here. We know, for example, that each year about $65 billion worth of merchandise is stolen through criminal organized gangs. So this is not necessarily new, but now we are seeing this trend where these flash criminal mobs are coming through as well. And we do know that there are ways that we can combat this, you know, now that we`re seeing this trend, not only in California but it`s also happened in Chicago and other places in the country.\par \par \uc2 That there`s a combined effort for law enforcement to work with retailers to ensure that they have better safety mechanisms in place, that they provide undercover officers in these high-end retail stores, that they do intelligence, that they gather information because it is organized. And that they`re able to, you know, get people in custody further up the chain, if you will, than the young folks that they have out there doing the smash- and-grabs.\par \par \uc2 And prosecution is a part of that. I will say prosecution is a big part of that. If they don`t feel like they`re going to be prosecuted, then they`re more inclined to continue.\par \par \uc2 TUR: If you don`t feel like the punishment is that strong or that stiff, you don`t feel like you`re risking as much.\par \par \uc2 BEST: Right.\par \par \uc2 TUR: That being said, this is an organized crime issue, and how do you get it to stop? What is the solution? I mean, it sounds like if it`s organized crime, that we`re going to be dealing with this for quite a while.\par \par \uc2 BEST: It could be. Once we get people in custody and start getting some of the, you know, masterminds behind it in custody, obviously that will have an impact on what`s occurring here. A lot of ways to do that. Like I said, gathering the intelligence, tracking cell phones, tracking vehicles and other mechanisms to get additional information to get further up the chain, so to speak, of where these houses are occurring and who is masterminding the plot.\par \par \uc2 TUR: We talk about the black market as a place where folks are selling these items. It`s not just the black market, though. It`s sellers that are, I mean, define the black market also, number one, but there`s also talk that a lot of this stuff ends up on popular sites that you and I use all the time. Not necessarily just a guy on a street corner selling stuff that came out of a warehouse or the back of a truck.\par \par \uc2 BEST: Yes. That`s true. Some of these products end up, there`s a proliferation of places where they are figuring out how to sell them. Sometimes unwittingly people purchase these products. So there has to be, you know, again a coordinated effort through law enforcement retailers to go after the folks who are masterminding these thefts.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Certainly scary to be there when this stuff happens. Scary to work in one of those stores as well.\par \par \uc2 BEST: It is.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Chief Carmen Best, Chief, thank you so much for joining us. We appreciate it. Happy holidays to you.\par \par \uc2 BEST: Thank you. You too. Happy Holidays.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next, help wanted. Red suit and white beard required.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:51]\par \par \uc2 TUR: If you are hitting the mall today, you might notice something big is missing. Frankly if you`re hitting the mall any time this holiday season, no red suit, no long white beard, no ho-ho-hos. Santa is missing.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Emilie Ikeda explains.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there. Well, you know it`s the holiday season when you start seeing Santas pop up at your local malls. Here at Glendale Galleria, customers will have the chance to sit near St. Nick but not on his knee. Others, though, across the country may not be so lucky as a Santa shortage fuels empty chairs nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m Santa Claus. Oh, you don`t believe that, do you?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): From the silver screen --\par \par \uc2 WILL FERRELL, ACTOR: Santa. Oh, my god. I know him.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: To holiday jingles. Santa Claus is synonymous with the joy of Christmas and time with him a tried-and-true childhood tradition.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you like to sit by Santa?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: But this year St. Nick may not be coming to town. The U.S. labor shortage stretching as far as the North Pole.\par \par \uc2 MITCH ALLEN, HEAD, HIRESANTA: We`ve never seen the demand or the shortage of Santas like we`re doing this year.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Mitch Allen is head of HireSanta, a company that provides Christmas characters to stores and event nationwide. He says while demand for Santas has surged more than 120 percent, the number of available Kris-Kringle has dropped by double digits.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Talk to me about why we`re seeing less available Santas.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Number one is you can say imagine that Santa Claus entertainers are at high risk for negative consequences if they catch COVID.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Darrick Betzenderfer is among the Santas sitting out because of temporary health issues.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): So I know you have to stay home this year. Are you excited to get back at it next year?\par \par \uc2 DARRICK BETZENDERFER, CHRISTMAS SANTA: I want it so bad. I miss it. The kids need Santa. Especially this time of year.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: With Santas in short supply, some families are making reservations weeks in advance to see him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a family tradition. We believe in Santa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We didn`t get to do it last year because of COVID. So we were excited to experience it with him, finally, this year.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: When visiting Santa`s Village you`ll likely find COVID safety protocols.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell me what you wanted for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A "Star Wars" Lego set.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A "Star Wars" Lego set.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: At the Glendale Galleria outside of Los Angeles, Santa is vaccinated. Kids can sit six feet away or next to him but not on his knee. And masks are required for those over 2 years old. Exceptions can be made for the picture but that`s up to the customer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Safety is the number one thing for our guests first and foremost. Their level of comfort and how they feel about the Santa experience is what matters the most.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: For this 3 1/2-year-old Dominic Lapore (PH), this was the holiday highlight.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Dominic, was this your first time meeting Santa?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: What did you think?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like it.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): The pandemic reshaping holiday celebrations. But Santa`s message steadfast.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What is your Christmas wish for this year?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would love people to be happy. And to be safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Industry experts say if you`re having trouble pinning down Santa for your event, consider hosting Mrs. Claus or elves to bring that festive spirit. And if you`re set on seeing Santa in the mall, a lot of locations offer online reservations.\par \par \uc2 Katy, just be sure to book yours early.\par \par \uc2 TUR: It is worth it. And I`ll show you why it`s worth is. Look what I found in a box in my basement. That`s me on the right. My husband on the left. And then one day you`ll have these photos and you`ll be able to put them up in your house. They`re cute. I think they look like they were meant to be.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, coming up in the next hour of MSNBC REPORTS, crunch time at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 . As our friends at "Punchbowl" put it, rest up for an absolute mess of a December in Washington, oh, joy. And we`ll explore the Buy Nothing Project. I love this. Millions of people have joined this group. What are they? And why are they so popular?\par \par \uc2 That`s all coming up in the next hour. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020211127ehbq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for November 22, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Maura Barrett, Tom Winter, \par \uc2 8,791 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday we`re continuing our coverage of the tragedy in Wisconsin after an SUV plowed into a holiday parade, killing five people and injuring dozens more.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll head to the courtroom in Brunswick, Georgia as closing arguments continue in the trial of three men charged with killing Ahmaud Arbery in February of 2020.\par \par \uc2 And later, President Biden hopes to send the message of financial continuity in the face of surging inflation and a supply chain mess, as he nominates Jerome Powell for a second term as the nation`s Fed chair. We expect to hear from the President on that in this hour.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with the latest out of Wisconsin where we now know the name of the person of interest in custody and connection to yesterday`s deadly incident at a holiday parade in the city of Waukesha.\par \par \uc2 We want to warn you that you may find the video you`ll see of this incident disturbing. Five law enforcement officials tell NBC News that police are now questioning 39-year-old Darrell Brooks, he`s not been charged with any crimes and police are not calling him a suspect.\par \par \uc2 We`re expecting an update on the investigation about an hour from now and we`ll bring that live when it happens.\par \par \uc2 As for the incident itself, police now say five people were killed and more than 40 others were hurt when a red SUV plowed into a crowd during the parade in this Milwaukee suburb. One parade goer describe the moment he knew something wasn`t right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS KLUKA, WITNESS: We were sitting in our normal spot we sit at every year for the past at least 14 years of her life, and then I -- we`ve been coming since 1998 when we moved here, and I noticed him coming through the crowd and I did -- I noticed something was not right.\par \par \uc2 And then I`ve seen a kind of like just people flying as I stood up. I`m like, oh no, my daughter stood up. I threw her out of the way and then I basically yelled, get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 And my wife got out of the way and by the time she did the car came right past me within at least two feet. I could have touched the car going by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Four now, I`m joined by NBC`s Maura Barrett, who`s along the parade route in Waukesha, and also with me is Tom Winter, who`s covering the investigation for NBC News.\par \par \uc2 So Maura, what`s the mood like? What`s the feeling like and Waukesha? How are folks just doing there?\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, MSNBC REPORTER: Well, Garrett, as you can imagine, this is an incredibly difficult day for residents here. This is supposed to be a joyous celebration of heart a start to the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 This parade is a decade`s long tradition here in this suburb of Milwaukee and yesterday was just met with that tragedy. Those videos we`ve been seeing are just incredibly disturbing.\par \par \uc2 This parade route here was full of people excited to get back to it after the last year was canceled due to COVID and it went all down the main street of Waukesha.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you a map because this is what`s interesting is when we were thinking about how this SUV ended up on the parade route, we understand that this suspect might have been fleeing an incident involving a knife fight in a nearby park, I`ll let my colleague, Tom get more into the investigation details.\par \par \uc2 But witnesses telling NBC that they saw the red SUV flying down the parade route 40 to 50 miles an hour, but you can see that there are parallel streets next to the parade route that he could have been taking whether he was fleeing or not, and hitting so many people in his way.\par \par \uc2 Lots of witnesses telling NBC a very similar story that you just heard from that man there, talking about throwing children and family members out of the way and unfortunately, a lot of children are the among those that are injured in that count that you just gave, five people dead and 40 people -- more than 40 people injured.\par \par \uc2 We just got an update from the local children`s hospital about 18 children in the hospital, 10 of them in the ICU and so obviously a very difficult -- a tragic day for this community.\par \par \uc2 And the police are giving us up -- are expected to give us an update in the next hour and they had warned us that these numbers we`ve been reporting might be expected to climb, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Meantime, Tom, you`ve been working on this overnight. What do we know about this person of interest, Darryl Brooks?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: He`s 39 years old, Garrett from Wisconsin, has a significant criminal history not just in the -- in Wisconsin, but in several other states as well.\par \par \uc2 We just got some new documents into our Chicago Bureau and they paint a troubling picture of his recent criminal history including an arrest earlier this month, where a woman, who apparently he has a child with.\par \par \uc2 According to the charging documents, he was involved in domestic violence in a dispute with her. He was charged with five counts including several accounts tied to that domestic violence.\par \par \uc2 According to officers when they arrived on the scene and spoke with a woman, they noticed that she had blood on her face, a swollen lip, and apparently, he had driven over her in a gas station parking lot in that area, the -- she still had the tire marks on her pants on her left leg.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 So we`re seeing somebody who has a history of violence, at least according to what he`s been charged within the past.\par \par \uc2 This is somebody who was out on just $1,000 bail in the Milwaukee County District Attorney`s Office, Garrett is out with a statement saying that they`re reviewing this cash bail recommendation for the incident, which was "inappropriately" low in light of the nature of the recent charges -- in the pending charges against Mr. Brooks.\par \par \uc2 So obviously a lot more to know and find out as to why this individual was out on the streets after being facing such serious charges.\par \par \uc2 And obviously an investigation into a prior incident of domestic assault that did involve a vehicle in him driving over an individual at least that`s what he was hit with, in those charges in those charging documents say. So that`s what we know about this individual.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, it`s important to understand the tracking of this, it started out as an investigation or it started out, we`re told by law enforcement officials is some sort of a fight, perhaps a domestic dispute is well in a local park that involved a knife, a potential stabbing, from there, he fled in then went on to this parade route and obviously the tragic consequences that Maura was talking about and you were speaking about, we`ve seen what occurred there.\par \par \uc2 The question I have is why -- I mean, we`re looking at a map, we`re just looking at on-screen of what happened here. That`s obviously not the only Street in this town.\par \par \uc2 So why did he choose this? Why did he decide to start to go down this street in obviously, if he was trying to flee from a crime but not hurt anybody else, he would have noticed these marching bands and frankly, young kids that were in his way?\par \par \uc2 So there`s obviously some additional questions that need to be answered by law enforcement. We hope to hear more, as you said, coming up within the hour when we get a press briefing from local police officials.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Tom, really quick, I thought it was notable that you said that statement from the DA`s office talked about pending charges. This is about someone who`s not even officially been named as a suspect yet.\par \par \uc2 From your experience, what are we looking at here on a timeline if indeed they think this is, you know, if this is their man?\par \par \uc2 Is it unusual that we`re talking about pending charges for someone who`s not a suspect and who`s seen -- has been in their custody for quite some time now?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: I`m so glad you asked that question. They were referring to the pending charges that he has against him for prior incidents in the last year or two, not specific to what happened yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He still -- as you said at the beginning of the program still has not been named a suspect in yesterday`s incident, is still not been charged with anything tied to yesterday`s incident.\par \par \uc2 What we`ve reported so far is that this is an individual who is a person of interest who was taken into custody yesterday, and is somebody who they were trying to question and speak to.\par \par \uc2 As far as where the investigation stands, we don`t know what statements he may have made. We don`t know whether or not any search warrants were executed.\par \par \uc2 So in other words, did they find anything at the home, on his social media, and his various cell phone devices, if he has any, they could help them out with motive, etcetera or help them figure out why he made the decisions that he made yesterday, which obviously had very tragic consequences and likely severe legal consequences for him.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I appreciate that clarification. Tom and Maura, thank you both. I`m sure we`ll be coming back to you when we do get that update expected in the next hour or so.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in now Wisconsin State Representative Sara Rodriguez, who was actually walking in yesterday`s parade, and whose district includes part of that county.\par \par \uc2 So if you wouldn`t mind, can you just kind of describe for us the scene what it was 