hose remarks when they happen.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:08]\par \par \uc2 And his decision to re nominate Powell also comes as the president hits what may -- what may be the halfway point in getting his sweeping domestic agenda through Congress, with the House passing his Build Back Better Bill on Friday to 220 to 213, with just one Democrat voting no.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the House Democrats who voted for the Bill Back Better Bill and pushed forward quite hard behind the scenes, New York Democratic Hakeem Jeffries. He`s the chair of the House Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 So, Congressman, thank you for being here. And I`m curious up front here. What`s your reaction to the President`s decision to reappoint Jerome Powell? Are you OK with keeping Trump`s Fed nominee on through this recovery?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Well, I think Chairperson Powell has done a very good job in helping to oversee monetary policy during the pandemic, and ensuring that there`s an economic recovery, that it is as fair and as equitable as possible, and at this stability during a very uncertain time.\par \par \uc2 And I think I strongly support President Biden`s decision. It`s within his prerogative, and certainly hopeful that in a second term, Chairman Powell will focus on some of the inflationary issues that we`re seeing, so he can drive down costs for everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are there specific steps you`d like to see him take in that regard to control inflation?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, you know, I want to hear from Chairman Powell in the context of what his ideas are as it relates to dealing with the dynamics that we confront. On our side, in terms of fiscal policy -- and this is one of the reasons why the Build Back Better Act was so important -- we`re going to lower childcare costs, lower health care costs, lower housing costs, lower education costs, and drive down the high cost of life saving prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 So