mic has been relentless.\par \par \uc2 And since Biden tried to declare independence from it on July 4, more than 160,000 Americans have been added to our death toll despite vaccines being widely available.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, most of those recent deaths are all of the unvaccinated. So the folks with blood on their hands there are the miss informers, particularly in right-wing media.\par \par \uc2 More than 770,000 have died in total, 365,000 of them since January 23 this year. And meanwhile, the administration this week has suspended its work on enforcing OSHA`s vaccine mandate for private-sector workers due to some legal issues thanks to the Fifth Circuit.\par \par \uc2 And right now, the White House does not seem anxious to focus more time and energy, and political capital to fight the politicization of this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The unfortunate dark reality is they are fighting some powerful political forces of defiance and disinformation, which have only worsened the partisan gap in human suffering.\par \par \uc2 According to data from the New York Times and the CDC, let me show you this, 19 of the 20 counties with the highest COVID death rates per capita right now are all in deep red America.\par \par \uc2 They all went for Trump. If only the former President would convince these folks to get vaccinated, but he did not.\par \par \uc2 This is the same trend when it comes to the counties with the highest COVID hospitalization rates. It`s predominantly in red America as well.\par \par \uc2 The 10-most vaccinated states, on the other hand, are in a sea of blue. All went for Biden in 2020, every one of them. And every one of the least vaccinated states in America wins for Trump with the exception of one the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 This next fraught phase of the President`s battle against the pandemic is where we start our show today.\par \par \uc2 But before we dig more into COVID, we can report that there is a verdict in the Rittenhouse trial and so we are going to going to take you there to Kenosha right now.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille Brewster is with us. So Shaq, after three and a half days of deliberations, and the families are being brought into the courtroom certainly created a lot of thoughts that something was up, and indeed it appears we now have a verdict.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Chuck. The judge just took the bench and he announced that the jury has reached a verdict in this case. It seems as if he`s waiting for the jury to be brought down.\par \par \uc2 That part wasn`t clear across the feed but this is all happening just in literally the past couple of seconds as you were starting the show. There`s not much happening on the feed right now.\par \par \uc2 But you mentioned this is day four now of deliberations. The jury has been out since Tuesday. Tuesday morning is when they started. Of course, we know that they`re deliberating and going over those five felony counts that Kyle Rittenhouse is facing not only are those are -- not only are there those five felony counts but there are also the lesser included charges in two of those counts. So it`s a lot of material that they`ve been going through and deliberating through.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We do know -- and we haven`t gotten many indications from the jury over the course of the deliberations. We do know, for example, that it was earlier this week that they asked for more video.\par \par \uc2 They wanted to see the video evidence. They want an access to that video evidence, of course. We knew that video was going to be a crucial part of this case, much of both instances. Both shootings that are being litigated in this trial were captured on video so they asked for that video evidence.\par \par \uc2 There was also a period in time in which the judge cleared out the courtroom so that they can come down to the courtroom and watch on the bigger screens, one of the drone videos that was a piece of evidence.\par \par \uc2 And we also knew last night right before jurors were dismissed, and when they went home, one of the jurors asked if she can bring back her jury instructions. That`s that 36-page document that even the attorneys and judge admitted were complicated.\par \par \uc2 And, Chuck, I`m going to stop right now because it looks as if the jury is in the room based on everyone being seated. And I`ll just let you go ahead and listen to what the judge has to say assuming he will be talking imminently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, let`s see if we have audio right now. And if they`ve opened it up, we obviously have a visual, but let`s see if we have the audio. And look like anybody speaking.\par \par \uc2 Yes. Shaq, let`s remind people of the -- of the two counts --\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- that the jury could go with lesser charges on them. Walk us through that.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes, and those are the -- so cover analysis facing five different counts. And you know, the highest one carries that life sentence and it`s that the highest -- it`s count four, so that kind of goes out of order. But count four is that first-degree intentional homicide and that carries the life sentence. That`s for the death of Anthony Huber.\par \par \uc2 Now, the two lower counts within those are the first-degree reckless homicide and second-degree intentional homicide. Outside of those lower counts, you also have Kyle Rittenhouse facing and -- sorry, the judge --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I`m going to -- let`s go listen now.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: The judge is speaking right now so let`s go to that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, let`s pause that a second. Thanks, Shaq. And of course, he turned his mic off. Our timing is impeccable these days, Shaq. All right, there -- Shaq, we -- yes, we don`t have any audio right now. He is talking.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: I`ll just go and I`ll let you stop it, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You keep, I will stop you. Go ahead. I will stop you. Walk us through the catch.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: All right. so let`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This --\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes, let`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is an important part of this that people need to be prepared for.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Exactly. Let`s just start from the top. Let`s start with the five counts, the five felony counts that Kyle Rittenhouse is facing.\par \par \uc2 The first one of course, is that First-Degree Reckless Homicide. That carries a maximum penalty of 60 years.\par \par \uc2 Count number two, First-Degree Reckless Endangering A Safety. That`s for the shooting of Mr. McGinnis. He was one -- or not the shooting but putting the prosecution`s arguing that he was shot at, and that carries 12 and a half years in prison.\par \par \uc2 There`s a third-degree -- or a Count 3 as a First-Degree Reckless Endangering Of Safety. That`s how the man who they call him the jump kick man, the man who appeared to kick Kyle Rittenhouse in the face, he also was shot at, over the course of this incident.\par \par \uc2 And then you have Count 4, it`s the First-Degree Intentional Homicide. That is the biggest count that Kyle Rittenhouse is facing. It carries that life sentence.\par \par \uc2 In other states, that`s essentially called First-Degree Murder but Wisconsin has different statutes, so it`s called that intentional homicide here.\par \par \uc2 And then count number five is that First-Degree -- Attempted First-Degree Intentional Homicide, and that carries the 60-year penalty. For both of those counts, there are two counts under them that the jury can also consider.\par \par \uc2 So essentially, they`re going through, and they had about seven counts that they could -- that they were deliberating in going through.\par \par \uc2 Now, a big part of this case, and let`s just remind our viewers what both sides are arguing. You have the defense representing Kyle Rittenhouse.\par \par \uc2 They`re saying that this was a 17-year-old who was acting in self-defense. They say he was being chased. They say that he was being threatened, he feared for his life and his safety and he was simply responding to the threat.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Rittenhouse took the stand in this trial, there was that moment where he broke down on the stand and that became somewhat of a controversial moment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Shaq.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Let`s listen to the judge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think we`re hearing some.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRUCE SCHROEDER, CIRCUIT COURT JUDGE, KENOSHA, WISCONSIN: No, he doesn`t need to be. There can`t be any reaction at all, no matter how strongly you may feel, and misunderstood that many people do have strong feelings, but we can`t permit -- we can`t some permit any kind of a reaction to the verdict.\par \par \uc2 And as you can see, there`s quite a bit of law enforcement here and you will be whisked out of here if there is any, so just be aware.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (voiceover): Hello?\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Would you go now, please?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (voiceover): Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shaq, when you go back and we know the only -- so you said they asked for more video to see that. And when you look at that, that -- I mean, it impacts every one of the counts that they`re debating. So we can`t really even say, oh, that means they were really needed more information on X. Is that -- is that fair to say?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Exactly. It`s hard to read into what the jury is asking for. I mean, when a video was such a crucial part of this case for both sides for the defense and prosecution, it -- you can`t really read too much into what the jury was thinking.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s fair to assume that they wanted to see the video as they were going through those conversations as they were locked away in that room for nearly 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And I mentioned what the defense has been saying that this is an act of self-defense. The prosecution has been saying that Kyle Rittenhouse put himself in his position. You heard them in the closing arguments saying that you can`t claim self-defense for something that you created, for a situation that you created.\par \par \uc2 They say that he came from Antioch, Illinois, across state lines. That`s become a phrase that you heard over and over though he`s -- Kyle Rittenhouse said he has family ties to Kenosha, but that he came here with that semi-automatic weapon.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Shaq. All right, the jury is that -- it looks like they`ve been waiting for the jury. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 54.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE SCHROEDER, CIRCUIT COURT JUDGE, KENOSHA, WISCONSIN: 54? And has the jury reached a verdict as to each count of the information?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, we have now.\par \par \uc2 SCHRODER: One verdict and one verdict only.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Would you hand all the paperwork to the bailiff, please.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, it`s the one that you didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Everything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Yes, thanks. Yes, see that two faces down (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All right, here sir.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Thank you. The defendant will rise. He`s faced the jury to hearken to its verdicts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: State of Wisconsin Versus Kyle Rittenhouse. As the first count of the information, Joseph Rosenbaum, we, the jury, find the defendant Kyle H. Rittenhouse not guilty.\par \par \uc2 As to the second kind of the information Richard McGinnis, we, the jury, find the defendant Kyle H. Rittenhouse not guilty.\par \par \uc2 As to the third kind of the information, Unknown Male, we, the jury, find the defendant Kyle H. Rittenhouse not guilty.\par \par \uc2 As to the fourth count of the information Anthony Huber, we, the jury, find the defendant Kyle H. Rittenhouse not guilty.\par \par \uc2 As to the fifth count of the information, Gaige Grosskreutz, we the jury find the defendant Kyle Abran -- Kyle H Rittenhouse not guilty.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Members of the jury, these your unanimous verdicts? Is there anyone who does not agree with the verdict as read? Would you wish the jury, Paul?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: OK. OK, folks, your job is done and we started just about three weeks ago. And I caught -- I told you it could last two weeks and two days. This is tweaked -- this was three weeks.\par \par \uc2 You were a wonderful jury to work with. You were punctual, you were accountive, the -- I forgot the six over here who had a very difficult job of keeping from discussing the case during the time that they were sequestered as well.\par \par \uc2 All of you, when you just -- I couldn`t have asked for a better jury to work with and it has truly been my pleasure.\par \par \uc2 I think without commenting on your verdict, the verdicts themselves, just in terms of your -- the attentiveness and the cooperation that you gave to us justifies the confidence that the founders of our country placed in you.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 So I dismiss you at this time. You`re never under any obligation to discuss any aspect of this case with anyone. You`re welcome to do so as little or as much as you want.\par \par \uc2 The media have requested -- a number of media sources have requested the ability to talk to you and they have been allowed to present -- presentations to you that you`ll get in writing. And it`s entirely up to you whether you want to contact -- to contact them, they are not to contact you.\par \par \uc2 If anyone does contact you, and just tell them you`re not interested in discussing, and 