ottesville, Virginia, and the fight continues.\par \par \uc2 We know the truth. And one thing about the truth, the truth will make you free. We`re not satisfied. We`re not going to be pacified. And we`re not going to accept this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, that is not -- that is not Jacob Blake`s uncle, we got some bad information to us and when we turn that camera on, my apologies for that.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, I want to go back. Let you finish what you`re going to say. But I want to -- I think it`s going to be pretty clear, what you were bringing up was actually feeding into a question I had for you, which is the prosecution is now going to be backseat driven by a lot of people here.\par \par \uc2 The decisions they made, what they decided -- what counts they brought obviously they`re going to sit claim that there were some pieces of evidence they didn`t get to present that they think would strengthen their case. There`ll be some of that.\par \par \uc2 But it`s interesting when you say that those -- the deceased`s family didn`t feel like anybody wasn`t speaking for them. That feels like an indirect -- an indirect critique of the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: It could be, Chuck. And you know, I mean, it really -- it`s hard to go back in and analyze that because there are so many factors at play here.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, one thing that made this trial unique and is different from some of the other trials is sometimes you have the prosecution and defense try to separate what is being litigated from the cultural undercurrents of the time.\par \par \uc2 I had -- I wish I had a better phrase for that. But what I mean by that is we talked about the idea of vigilantism, we talked about the idea of self- defense, of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution and defense leaned into those themes as they were laying out their case. The prosecution called Kyle Rittenhouse a vigilante said that he was here for no reason, that he was here looking for trouble.\par \par \uc2 The defense at one point in the closing arguments tried to justify -- or justified Kyle Rittenhouse`s actions by saying that the shooting of Jacob Blake was ruled a justified shooting.\par \par \uc2 So they really leaned into the divides that you had. The -- what you hear outside the courthouse, people wrapping this in the social justice movement, they really connect that into the prosecution and to the defense.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the prosecution expressed a lot of frustration. They got yelled at by the judge in open court many, many times. They were not happy. There was that tension there that you -- that existed.\par \par \uc2 So yes, they probably will argue that they were not able to present the best case that they thought they had. There was evidence that they believed was crucial to the case that the judge ruled against.\par \par \uc2 But part of that is what you have with any trial. You have that -- you have those negotiations and those debates with any trial that is faced.\par \par \uc2 But I think the sentiment that you heard from the families of those who are deceased is that in the prosecution presenting what they thought was their best case at the time, and what they were able to present, there was no one talking about their loved ones in a personal manner.\par \par \uc2 And I think you know, that just goes to the pain that this is a tragedy. Anyone acknowledges this is a tragedy and that just goes to the added pain that you have there.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned that Jacob Blake`s uncle was at the courthouse steps involved, we didn`t hear. I think we got to that feed a little bit late.\par \par \uc2 Some of the things that we heard from his uncle, according to the producer on the steps is that he said we are not satisfied.\par \par \uc2 He said we saw the video, we saw three people shot. He said this case shouldn`t have -- shouldn`t have been about the black and white, it`s about right and wrong. And he says in spite of the verdict, we know what we saw.\par \par \uc2 So there`s that frustration there. There will be that reaction that we have there. And you know, there are many people who have been anticipating this verdict and this verdict has now come and it results in the acquittal of Kyle Rittenhouse.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Shaq, stick around, I got Joyce Vance and Danny Cevallos with me. Joyce Vance, a former Prosecutor, Danny Cevallos a longtime Defense Attorney.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, was this a well-defended case or poorly prosecuted case, or a little bit of both?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, there was some of everything not to like in this case from the way the judge conducted it to some of the strategic decisions the prosecution made.\par \par \uc2 The defense, you know, as we say, was good enough. And the key point of the defense was arguing self-defense.\par \par \uc2 As a former prosecutor, this strikes me as an odd situation for self- defense. It`s something akin to saying that if you go into a bank and rob it and people are trying to apprehend you, you can then shoot your way out and claim self-defense.\par \par \uc2 You know, that`s a little bit of what Kyle Rittenhouse did, he created this problem, and then he proceeded to threaten people to get out.\par \par \uc2 Typically, you can`t claim self-defense in a situation where you`ve provoked the incident. But the defense did a good enough job here of convincing jurors. This was a unanimous verdict on all counts. This jury was thoroughly convinced.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Danny, I would -- same question to you. Was this an easy defense, poor prosecution, friendly judge, tough? What`s your sense?\par \par \uc2 DANNY CEVALLOS, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: There is no such thing as an easy defense. And it`s not so much that it was a poor prosecution, but really poor facts for the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, the prosecution had to disprove self-defense, all the elements or any one of them beyond a reasonable doubt in addition to proving their case.\par \par \uc2 Now at the last minute, they almost snatched victory from the jaws of defeat when they got the provocation instruction because that could completely upend the self-defense defense.\par \par \uc2 But to win on prosecution, they needed to convince the jury that -- it to initiate that confrontation with Joseph Rosenbaum, that Kyle Rittenhouse was the one who started it.\par \par \uc2 And the only evidence they ever had of that was this grainy pixelated video imagery that showed up mid-trial.\par \par \uc2 And as the defense correctly pointed out, provocation was so important to the prosecution, that they mentioned it in their opening statement zero point zero times.\par \par \uc2 So here, the prosecution really -- it can`t be said that they weren`t aggressive. In fact, it`s their aggressiveness that almost got them in a lot of trouble. They almost crossed several different lines.\par \par \uc2 So in this case, prosecutions did their best, but they didn`t have particularly good facts, the defense did a very good job. Because an acquittal on all counts is a good job no matter how you paint it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Joyce, it seems so that on the charges they brought, you know, if you just -- they didn`t have that -- they didn`t have that charge that possibly, and we`re not going to know until we get an idea of where the jurors heads were, right?\par \par \uc2 But you could just see it in the way various people would react to this trial, which was, what was he doing there? Gosh, why was that happen? At the same time, this -- that there was that -- did they miss out?\par \par \uc2 Should they -- should they have found another lesser charge to put in there that would have been an easier case to prove?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, I`m not sure that that`s the issue here. The prosecution`s real problems began when one of their key witnesses, the victim who survived took the witness stand, and he essentially on cross-examination conceded self-defense, talked about the fact that there was a gun in his hand.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution tried to argue their way out of that situation but I think the die was cast in a significant way at that point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Danny, now obviously, it looks like putting Kyle Rittenhouse on the stand was a great move. Did they need to do it?\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: It`s so easy now to say oh, brilliant move and I -- be -- I will jump right in and say yes, it was. Why? Because there was an acquittal.\par \par \uc2 Arguably they didn`t need it because there was so much video evidence they could have rested on the prosecution`s burden. But at the same time, it was a gambit that is rarely taken.\par \par \uc2 Don`t be fooled by the Ahmaud Arbery, the fact that we`ve seen two defendants testify in about a week. This is a very rare occurrence.\par \par \uc2 But in this case, it didn`t hurt them. Obviously, we`ve measured it by the fact that there was an acquittal.\par \par \uc2 Could they have gotten to that same place without him testifying? Yes, I believe so. This is not a case where it was desperation. We got to put up our client or else we got nothing.\par \par \uc2 They had stuff, the defense, they made a strategic decision that the jury might identify with Kyle Rittenhouse. Maybe they were right, we may find out in the coming days but for now, an acquittal isn`t a -- is a win however you take it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joyce, how do you feel about cameras in the courtroom?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think we absolutely need to have more of it.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 The important thing about our criminal justice system is it only works if people have confidence in it. And we only fix problems if people are aware of them. So a big part of the country has been engaged in a significant number of criminal trials in the last year, and that`s because they`ve become more available through court mechanisms during COVID. State courts do a lot better job in this area than the federal courts do. And I think it`s time for the federal courts to follow the state court`s lead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Danny, same question to you. I mean, I sit there, there`s part of me that loves the transparency, and there`s part of me that wonders, is it good for the country to have 330 million backseat lawyers and backseat judges.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: I`m with Joyce on this one. I mean, the courts are supposed to be public. Now when we first came up with that concept of the courts being open to the public, all you had to communicate was, you know, a scroll, maybe a pen that looked like a feather, and somebody shouting in the town square. It just so happens the technology has advanced to the point where we can live stream everything, anytime, anywhere from the comfort of our phone.\par \par \uc2 So with the idea that the court should still be public, I don`t think anything has changed just because technology has moved on from the time of the framers. So I`m with Joyce. I think cameras in the courtroom are a good thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: Does it mean that attorneys sometimes play up to an audience instead of the way they should to the courtroom? Maybe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: But here I think the benefits outweigh the burdens.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in David Henderson, a civil rights attorney, here. And David, if you`re the family of the dead victims here, what`s your next -- do you have another place to go? What would Mr. Huber`s and Mr. Rosenbaum`s family go? Do they go to a civil suit? Is it against the city? Is it against Mr. Rittenhouse? What -- do they have another avenue here for justice in their minds?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HENDERSON, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Chuck, I think the way to answer that question is to tell you what I would tell them if they walked into my office. If they walked into my office, I would tell them no. Is it possible that you can find a lawyer to file a civil suit against Kyle Rittenhouse? Sure it is. I don`t think that that would be an effective lawsuit. I think it would just lead to more frustration.\par \par \uc2 This goes back to something you stressed earlier when I was first joining. And that is, it does represent a failure on the part of the prosecution. And I don`t like to say negative things about other lawyers unless I have to, but here, as a former special crimes prosecutor, you`ve got laws to ensure certain protections and rights for the victims of violent crimes, and that extends to family members.\par \par \uc2 If they feel dissatisfied with the prosecution, part of what you ultimately have to look at is the fact that those prosecutors did not reassure them that they were going to do their best to make sure justice was served. And that doesn`t mean you get a guilty verdict. That just means that you help the family understand that you did everything you could in the courtroom, because that`s the only way that people can get closure under circumstances like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, backseat drive a little bit. What would you have done differently? Obviously you think the prosecution didn`t deal with the families very well. What would you have done differently in the courtroom?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: So, Chuck, let`s start off by saying this. Wisconsin doesn`t have what you`d call a true manslaughter statute. They just got different versions of murder charges. Some people think their equivalent of manslaughter is effectively second-degree intentional homicide. I would argue it`s their version of third-degree reckless homicide. With that in mind, if I were to just ask the question, the first shooting that Kyle Rittenhouse committed, what do you think it is? A murder or a manslaughter? Almost everyone I pose that question to says it`s murder. Of course. Look at the way they presented the case.\par \par \uc2 It`s not. They essentially charged him with manslaughter based on that first crime and then murder based on the second person that he shot, the young man who tried to stop him by hitting him with a skateboard. That makes for a very disconnected case in the terms of the way that you outlay your facts during the presentation of evidence. It makes it extremely confusing for the jury when they go back there and they deliberate.\par \par \uc2 A more effective approach would have been to charge Rittenhouse with second-degree intentional homicide for all of the shootings that he committed and the attempt. It makes it easier for you to present evidence and then move into your argument during jury deliberations. Also the jury was not given any instruction about how to actually work through this charge which is critical when you want them to reach a conviction.\par \par \uc2 Part of what`s not intuitive as a trial lawyer is that to be effective you have to be a teacher. You have to teach the jury what the law is and you have to lay out how they`re supposed to follow that law during the course of the trial. Part of what we saw here is the fundamental lack of understanding about how you tell a story in the context of a criminal trial. And if I had to put it on one thing, that`s ultimately where the prosecution failed here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, the judge was mercurial. And I want to get all of your opinions on this.\par \par \uc2 Joyce Vance, knowing that, and I would argue that the people that should have known the most about this judge`s habits, shall we call them, are the folks in Kenosha.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you, in my observation, Joyce, it seems as if the prosecution seemed to be surprised by some of the judge`s actions. The more I read about this judge they shouldn`t have been. Did they mishandle it?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: One of the things you know as the prosecution is that you don`t get a second shot at the trial. If you get an acquittal like prosecutors did here, the prosecution doesn`t get to take an appeal and ask a court to reverse the trial judge for misconduct. Your only shot is in that first trial. So for prosecutors to not have anticipated that this judge would pull some of the antics that he`s infamous for locally is likely a misstep.\par \par \uc2 They did look surprised at times. And we`ve talked a little bit, Danny mentioned this real misstep prosecutors made where during cross examination of the defendant they straight out of the box dangerously veered towards commenting on the defendant`s failure to speak with police when he was in custody early on. This gave the judge the opportunity to go full boar on them. It likely created some bad impressions with the jury.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Their handling of the judge throughout was problematic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Danny, you know, it did, obviously when you`re the defense lawyer and you see that the prosecution is in the judge`s crosshairs, you just stay out of the way, I assume.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: Well, yes, but 90 percent of the time, I mean, maybe this is just the defense attorney in me. If anyone thought that the judge was biased against the prosecution, it feels like to me it`s 90 percent of the time the other way. But again, I`m biased. And you know, it may be true that in federal court you don`t see this kind of behavior from a judge. But let`s face it. You know, when you get to state court and judges are elected, I know there are defense attorneys and local DAs out there who are watching this saying, yes, you think that judge was crazy? You should see so and so in part whatever. That guy is out of his mind.\par \par \uc2 When you have elected judges, you get a lot more random folks who end up on the bench. You get a lot of good jurists but you get a lot of very interesting folks who end up there. Eccentricities, absolutely. Was this judge someone who would likely -- that you would likely find at the Supreme Court house? No. But is he someone that you might see in a state courthouse or municipal courthouse on a random Tuesday? Absolutely. These judges are out there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I -- you know, one of the things that I can`t explain in our democracy is how you can have a judge face the voters. I don`t get it, but that`s me. It doesn`t make any sense. I don`t know how you can expect the judge to be impartial if they have to face voters and win election or reelection.\par \par \uc2 David Henderson, how would you have managed this judge?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: You know, I agree with what both Joyce and Danny are saying. When you`re in court, you have to play the hand that you`re dealt. And if getting a good judge is what justice depends on, we`re in trouble when it comes to state court. 90 percent of the time they would be yelling at the defense, not at the prosecution. Most of the time that`s true. In a case like this, the roles are often reversed.\par \par \uc2 And so I think a fundamental aspect of managing this judge was managing the presentation of the evidence. I thought the state went down roads, they didn`t need to and should not have with regard to making this case more politically charged than it needed to be. This should have been a theme based primarily on safety. The message should have been hey, folks, look, despite all the background noise here, all we`re trying to do is keep people safe.\par \par \uc2 And you can`t keep people safe when you`ve got somebody running around and shooting people and putting other people at risk. That`s what we are focused on. And I don`t care what your politics are. You`ve got to admit that having two people shot and killed at a protest, and a third person nearly shot, and all the people that are placed in harm`s way is something that we can`t take for granted, and that`s what justice is.\par \par \uc2 And I think that if you do that, it helps you manage the judge because the things that set the judge off had more to do with the politics underlying the way this case was tried.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: I`m not saying it would have been perfect, but you could have avoided a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that was well put and well said. I mean, you know, I`m not a lawyer, but watching the prosecution here, it just seemed as if you want to say read the room.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to Gabe Gutierrez who`s in the crowd outside of the courthouse. And Gabe, what are you seeing, what are you hearing? What are people talking to you about?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, there, Chuck. Well, just to give you some sense, there`s a lot of big media presence here. You can see to my right, your left, the crowd gathered around. This is the uncle of Jacob Blake. He`s just wrapping up speaking to reporters here. And we`ve seen people here on both sides of the issue.\par \par \uc2 But I`m speaking here with a man who -- you came from pretty far off to be here. Tell me, what was your reaction to the verdict? In your opinion, what does this mean for the Second Amendment?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m very happy about the verdict, and my young hero, Kyle, 1,000-year-old right of self-defense was vindicated, but I`m also sad for the two gentlemen that died and the third that was injured.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: What did you make of the prosecution`s argument that you shouldn`t be able to provoke the danger and then claim self-defense?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is a correct statement of the law. In Washington state we call it the initial aggressor doctrine. You can`t --\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But that`s what the prosecution said. The jury did not agree.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Exactly. But that they could have. They could have found provocation but they didn`t. So I think that`s a victory for the thousand- year-old right of self-defense and our young hero Kyle, but again we don`t want to celebrate too much because two people died and a third was seriously wounded.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you very much for speaking to us, sir.\par \par \uc2 So, Chuck, you have people on both sides of the issue. Again, the uncle of Jacob Blake is over there. Such a complicated case. Some days the limits of self-defense.\par \par \uc2 We have been here throughout this trial. There has been small crowds of demonstrations here out in front of the courthouse, largely peaceful. A few minor skirmishes and a few arrests. After the verdict was read and the moments after, you heard some of what you just heard that man said, that the Second Amendment still stands. Certainly supporters of Kyle Rittenhouse will see it that way.\par \par \uc2 Of course the folks that thought that he should have been found guilty, there is a lot of disappointment here. And we`ll see that play out over the coming hours. The city of Kenosha of course preparing for this moment. Schools in Kenosha had switched to virtual learning in anticipation of the verdict. But so far things seem to be peaceful. Local authorities hope that it stays that way -- Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Cold weather may be helping as well. Anyway, Gabe Gutierrez outside of the courthouse for us. Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go back to Joyce and David here. And David, I think you said it well when you were talking about how the prosecution should have thought about basically doing everything they could to sort of take some of the politics out of their presentation, take some of the politics out of their charges. And I guess ultimately what is this case revealing about us as a society?\par \par \uc2 You know, is this a case of we`ve got a -- we obviously are divided on race. Our gun laws are very polarizing in this country as well. It is interesting to me that you`re going to have folks on one side of the political aisle see this through the prism of race and I think on the other side of the aisle claim they`re seeing it through the prism of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 But I guess the question is going to be, you know, if you`re an elected official in Wisconsin, what are you looking at? Are you looking at your gun laws?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: You know, Chuck, I think the first thing that stands out to me here, and I`m going to go back on something that Joyce said. And that is, if you build on the point that she was making which I agree with, we don`t understand what modern prosecution needs to be. This era has shown we have a fundamental lack of certainty about what it needs to entail.\par \par \uc2 So do you go back and you look at your gun laws? Sure you do. But what you have to look at in this case, if you look at the first charge, the charge against shooting Rosenbaum, it begs the question, why did you charge him with first-degree reckless homicide? And I don`t think there`s any conclusion aside from the fact that they didn`t have a clear view of exactly what happened. It seemed like that part of the fact pattern they were reevaluating as they went along.\par \par \uc2 Now I know that`s specific to the trial, to your questions with regard to gun laws. At some point you`re going to have to ask the question, what does it mean to have open carry and stand your ground laws under circumstances where people are going to be taking guns into volatile situations? When I look at this, I think of the movie "Tombstone." And even in the movie about the Wild West, they understood you can own a gun, you can carry a gun, you can`t carry your gun in town.\par \par \uc2 And basically they were saying, under situations where emotions are going to be high, you might want to back off having a weapon in that situation because when things are getting heated, and Rittenhouse just put his gun in the back of someone`s trunk, two people would still be alive and one would not be permanently disabled, with a bunch of other people being threatened in the process. And it`s hard to say that reasonably that`s not what should have happened here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, Joyce, look, there`s a obvious trend that`s taking place across the country over the last decade. Whether it`s enshrining the stand your ground laws in different localities. whether state law or not. And I think you`re just seeing a more pervasive, you know, this was a jury of the Kenosha public here. A -- more of an acceptance that a lot of people are going to -- there`s a lot of guns in a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re going to have a lot more of these trials, I think, where self- defense is going to be getting taken more seriously by more juries.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think that that`s absolutely right. And I`m reminded as David mentioned "Tombstone" that in Alabama when our open carry laws were passed, I was still the U.S. attorney here, and I fielded a lot of phone calls from sheriffs who were concerned about people taking guns, for instance, into football games or into other venues where there were a lot of people and the prospect of animated tempers.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 And so a lot of thought was put into how they would keep guns out of those venues. But, of course, those conversations are happening very quietly because in our political context, in a certain segment of the country, it`s become popular to promote the Second Amendment at the expense of commonsense public safety sort of concerns, interestingly the Supreme Court has a case on its docket that will consider some of those issues.\par \par \uc2 The New York case that determines whether everyone is entitled to a concealed carry permit, and in that oral argument, some of the Supreme Court justices seemed to play a little bit against type. Justice Amy Coney Barrett for instance questioned whether it wouldn`t be OK to require people to relinquish their guns in certain high-risk situations. For instance, in Times Square on New Year`s Eve.\par \par \uc2 The country is in the middle of this conversation. But the question is whether commonsense private-public safety concerns can animate themselves in the face of the Second Amendment politicized issues.\par \par \uc2 TODD: David, have you thought about different ways you`ve got to deal with when you`re dealing with certain civil rights cases and this stand your ground law?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And how to go at this differently? Yes. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Absolutely, Chuck. But, you know, when you asked me about how I`m going to dee with certain cases in the stand your ground law, I think what people need to keep in mind is there is a Kyle Rittenhouse at every major protest in the country after George Floyd`s murder. Here in Dallas you represented several people who are wrongfully arrested for peacefully protesting and several journalists sent us in photographs of what was going on out there. And me in my ignorance, at first, I was thinking, well, maybe these are off-duty special ops people or some type of specialized unit with these assault rifles out walking around.\par \par \uc2 It didn`t occur to me that they were citizens out there I guess protecting the public from peaceful protesters. And so as a civil rights lawyer, when I look at this, what I notice is when "The New York Times" was first reporting on the Kyle Rittenhouse story and they were interviewing people which you can see in their documentary, a couple of protesters took guns out. The same way Grosskreutz did saying they wouldn`t normally do it, but given the tensions that were building they felt like they had to.\par \par \uc2 So when you`ve got protesters doing that, and then you have other people who take vigilantism into their own hands by carrying their AR-15s, you`re creating a volatile situation at a time when the two major areas for civil rights moving forward are police reform and voting rights. You`re not going to see progress without activism. And when you`re seeing that activism now, for me as a civil rights lawyer, I have to be afraid that people are going to be there with guns and they`re going to get hurt over discussions that we should be able to have peacefully.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. And the thing is that you`re going to see others who have been uncomfortable owning a gun deciding they may need to for their own public safety. And it sort of becomes a nod, a pretty uncomfortable spiral.\par \par \uc2 Gabe Gutierrez, I think you just spoke to Jacob Blake`s uncle. I know you did a few minutes. What did you learn from him? What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Chuck -- hey there, Chuck. I see, yes, Justin Blake, the uncle of Jacob Blake, is right over there. We spoke with him. It`s a bit of a crowded situation over here. I want to see if I can catch up with him right here. But what he had told me he`s very disappointed with this verdict. He called Kyle Rittenhouse a provocateur and said that he was furious at this verdict. He`s been speaking to reporters here. You can see we`re going to get a microphone in here and speak with him very quickly. It`s a little tight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re not going to stop. We`re going to use peace.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: I`ll tell you what, Chuck, I`m going to send it back to you really quickly. I am not sure if we can hear what`s going on there right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Jacob Blake`s family. Of course Jacob Blake was the man who was shot and paralyzed who touched off the unrest right before --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Racism and slavery, and it still goes on in Kenosha, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Just spoke with his uncle right now. Not sure if we can turn some of that (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Does it shock you?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Over here. See if I can throw him a question live on MSNBC. Now one second.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. So not at all. I mean, if they didn`t convict the police officer with his dirty eyes that shot my nephew in the back several times, we had hopes because this is a jury of 12, but when you have the guy who is presiding over the whole thing puts his hands on the scale and allows this young man literally to walk out, he gave him a pass. He didn`t allow evidence in. If somebody said they were going to kill somebody with an AR-15 30, 40 days ago and they actually do it 40 days later, that`s good evidence.\par \par \uc2 If somebody before that shows the cowboy sign and goes into a bar at 17 and throws it up again, you know his ideology. They talk about harming African- Americans. They talk about harming minorities. And he acted it out. Now, you tell us why he got a free ride. His bail money was raised by the Proud Boys, the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2 , the Nazis, the skinheads.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 Are they plugged up in this courtroom? Do we know the history of this judge? I heard he`s been racist since (INAUDIBLE) these people in Kenosha for many years. Do we know his background?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Mr. Blake --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: How does that Blake family (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Listen, we got thick skin. We`ve been, my great- grandfather, my grandfather, we`ve been fighting this for losing father. Now we`re going to keep fighting until we win.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re not going to quit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE) to the red, black and blue.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Mr. Blake, we`re live on MSNBC. What did you think of Mr. Rittenhouse`s argument of self-defense?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There was no self-defense. Self-defense is when you`re protecting your home, you`re protecting your family. He in a very bastardly and dastardly way used the law. (INAUDIBLE). There was absolutely no self- defense.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: So, Chuck, there you have it. Reporter: Justin Blake, Jacob Blake`s uncle, speaking to reporters back there. He is obviously very disappointed in this verdict, furious as what he sees as a miscarriage of justice.\par \par \uc2 We just, you know, on the other side of these steps, we spoke to supporters of Kyle Rittenhouse who see this as a victory of the Second Amendment. Emotions running high here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But we can`t say it`s been very peaceful here and the hope is by local authorities that Kenosha stays peaceful as we head into to tonight. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you, Gabe. Thank you for that effort.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to Shaquille Brewster, and Shaq, I think you`ve got the statement from the Huber family that we just got into the newsroom. I think you can share that with us. What does it say?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Yes, Chuck, this is just coming in, this is extensive statement so I`m just going to read a bit of it. But it starts by saying, "We are heartbroken and angry that Kyle Rittenhouse was acquitted in this criminal trial for the murder of our son Anthony Huber. There was no justice today for Anthony or for Mr. Rittenhouse`s other victims, Joseph Rosenbaum and Gaige Grosskreutz. We did not attend the trial because we could not bear to sit in the courtroom and repeatedly watch videos of our son`s murder and because we have been subjected to many hurtful and nasty comments in the past year but we watched the trial closely hoping it would bring us closure." Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 And the final thing I`ll read from it is, they say that did not happen. "Today`s verdict means there is no accountability for the person who murdered our son. It sends an unacceptable message that armed civilians can show up in any town, incite violence, and then use the danger that they`ve created to justify shooting people in the street."\par \par \uc2 That`s, again, from the family of Anthony Huber, the 26-year-old. And one piece of context for this is Anthony Huber was the man reflected in the fourth count of this case, the most significant count, and it was that first-degree intentional homicide. Again, what is considered first-degree murder in pretty much any other state. I think it underscores the pain that you have from the families of those who were shot and killed by Mr. Rittenhouse, by Kyle Rittenhouse.\par \par \uc2 Of course the jury is saying that was justified, they acquitted him on the counts that he faced. But that coming in from the family of Anthony Huber right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shaquille Brewster with that for us. Shaq, thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know, Joyce Vance, this is the uncomfortable -- the Huber statement brings up something uncomfortable. Two people died, another one near almost died. And nobody`s paying any penalty. Now, some could argue that the trial itself was a form of penalty for Mr. Rittenhouse in the near, you know, what was facing him. But how does the Huber family get closure? I don`t know if they ever will.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, it`s a really difficult issue. And I`ll speak to it personally because my father-in-law who was a federal judge was murdered because of his judicial service. And ultimately the man who was responsible for killing him was convicted at trial in both state and federal settings. And that fact of conviction does give families an enormous amount of closure. And something that I`ve always been acutely aware of as a prosecutor is that there are a number of things that are important to victims.\par \par \uc2 But among them is learning the truth and having people accept responsibility and be held accountable. And so in this setting, where, yes, to some extent the family does know the truth, and they`ve been able in some cases to see video, as grainy as it is, yet the notion that Kyle Rittenhouse, who for instance when he was released pretrial, hung out with members of the Proud Boys and flashed white power signs, and now will not face any form of accountability for his acts.\par \par \uc2 That`s extraordinarily difficult for family members. One of the goals of our system has to be restorative justice, helping everyone involved in these situations, from defendants, perpetrators, to family members, and victims, find a path forward. And this trial in Kenosha, Wisconsin, has not done that for the families of the victims and the victims themselves.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, David Henderson, essentially, and you talked about this earlier, this should have been presented by the prosecution as a failure in public safety and should have been focused on public safety. The state failed. The state failed to create an environment that was safe. The state failed to prove who did make that period unsafe. And now we`re in no man`s land here as a society, where nobody`s going to pay a price for these two dead Americans.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: That`s right, Chuck. And if there`s responsibility that lawyers have here, and I know people would laugh at this because it seems like our profession is so far away from this at this point, but part of what we have a responsibility to do, and this is especially true when you`re a prosecutor, is help people heal. And so building on what Joyce was just saying, one of the things that I did when I was in special crimes is, I would always tell people, look, I cannot promise you an outcome and any lawyer who says they can is lying to you.\par \par \uc2 But what I hope I can promise you is that when you look back, you won`t feel like you could have had a smarter lawyer who fought harder for you. In the same way we make that promise to individuals, we need to make that promise to the country that we represent. And that is by letting them see the arguments that we make in court, how they can lead to healing for our society, we`re at a point where we are bursting at the seams and if we don`t start doing more of that, I`m afraid of where things may go.\par \par \uc2 And so the argument that I`m making with regard to public safety stems from that context, just the need to help restore our society to a point where people have hope for the future and being able to come together better as a country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Joyce, the public safety umbrella might be the best way to change some of this conversation on the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think David makes a really excellent point here that this case should have always been about public safety and what the jury should have been considering from opening statement, maybe from voir dire when they were selected onward, was how they would have felt if they were in that crowd with Kyle Rittenhouse free to stalk through the crowd with his gun in hand and then claim self-defense for whatever he did.\par \par \uc2 Because that narrative was absent here, I think that much of the outcome that we saw today was attributable to that failure of narrative by the prosecution. And this is an important conversation to have nationwide, as we think about not just healing communities, but making them safer, preparing them for the next incident, because there will be more protests. There will obviously be more Kyle Rittenhouses out in these crowds.\par \par \uc2 The question that we now have to face is, are we safer after this verdict? Are we safe if we let someone like Kyle Rittenhouse kill people and then claim self-defense? Do we need better laws that protect us better? And the answer is clearly yes today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have to tell you, and to listen that way Joyce just framed that, David, when she said, are we safer today, you know, it`s pretty obvious to me, more people, the next time there`s a protest of some sort, and it may get politicized, that gun owners with a certain ideology may feel incentivized now, may feel even emboldened. And that does seem to be an uncomfortable message that may be received by some.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: I think that`s the obvious message to take away from this, Chuck. I always try to look for the silver lining when I can. But here, let`s think about what happened. A kid had an AR-15 strapped to his body, and he argued that on the theory that I may face lethal force in the future because a person may -- who never touched him -- get his hands on my gun and wrestle it away and then once he`s wrestled away, turn and kill me, based on that theory of a potential fear in the future, the jury found that he was justified in shooting and killing someone in the present.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that, yes, if you take your gun to a protest in the future or any heated situation, I think you`re inclined to feel that you`re within your rights to use it, and that`s very dangerous for all of us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joyce Vance and David Henderson, you guys have been terrific. Thank you for helping us get through and understand this verdict, understand the impact of it where we could be headed next.\par \par \uc2 To summarize, and obviously our coverage is going to continue here, Kyle Rittenhouse has been acquitted on all five counts here. We have reaction coming in. We have reporters on the ground in Kenosha. And my friend Katy Tur is going to take the baton now and lead our coverage going forward.\par \par \uc2 Katy, take it away.\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Great analysis there from David Henderson.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with breaking news. After four days of deliberation, a jury of his peers found Kyle Rittenhouse not guilty on all counts. Rittenhouse collapsed in the Kenosha, Wisconsin, courtroom as the final not guilty verdict was read.\par \par \uc2 Rittenhouse himself took the stand in his own defense, a risky move that did prove successful for the defense. Now the conversation will turn to what is going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211120ehbj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word for November 18, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Kavita Patel \par \uc2 7,770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:41]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 And I have a theory of what Kevin McCarthy is doing. I think he saw that you for the first time tonight exhibited some optimism about this bill actually passing, and Kevin McCarthy thought, I`ll show Rachel Maddow, I`ll show her. I`ll add a couple of hours to this process.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": What it is is that he knows that I got up extra early this morning to go fishing and so I`m going to have a hard time staying up extra late to do coverage if it goes until like 4:00 in 