ducing prescription drug costs --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman?\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: -- as well as many other areas.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, react if you would to their demand for a CBO score. What`s the problem with that? Why not know what the ultimate impact may be on the economy before you sign off on something? Do you see this as a delay tactic?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: The White House and the House already supply the offsets which prove that it is paid for. The CBO is just --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Not the same as the CBO score though.\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Another --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Not the same as a CBO score though. You concede.\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Are we trust in the White House? I trust the White House to be accurate in this assessment.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the CBO score for the Infrastructure bill actually adds to our overall deficit. Doesn`t take away from it the way the way the Build Back Better Act does.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve passed hundreds of bills, hundreds, out of the House without a CBO score. And finally, there are lobbyists who are trying to gut and kill the Build Back Better Act.\par \par \uc2 So, to your point of a delay tactic, we will see if it stays where it is right now, what 1.75 and off of 1.75, off -- it continues to be gutted.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it came to us at 3.5 trillion. We worked it in the House at 3.5 trillion. And then it started to be cut because fossil fuel companies, pharmaceutical companies, they don`t want to be held accountable, businesses and some wealthy do not want to contribute their fair share, and the Build Back Better Act makes sure all those things get done as we move towards a more equitable democracy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman, I`m sure you saw that earlier this week, Senator Manchin expressed some real concerns about inflation, those new inflation numbers, the highest in some 30 years, essentially signaling he`s skeptical about passing another sweeping 