uffered some big defeats all across the country on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you, do you regret holding up the passage of the Infrastructure bill? Do you bear any responsibility for those losses?\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Well, I don`t connect the losses on Tuesday to our, you know, strategy of the way we`ve been negotiating over the last several months.\par \par \uc2 We have to do a better job as a party of putting boots on the ground and communicating with voters who are normally disengaged, and making sure they are part of the party and part of the process.\par \par \uc2 We need to be stronger and clearer in our communications. We need to listen more and get a better understanding of what the American people are asking of us. We need to engage in communities that we historically do not engage in. And that will help us to win elections in all parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 And the candidate always matters, you know. How well is the candidate doing their job in responding to voters? As opposed to a talking point here, a talking point there, those some are the dysfunction in Washington as somebody described it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman, I hear you on the messaging. But I have to tell you, I spoke to voters out on the campaign trail in swing areas, in Virginia, who said they are fed up with the inaction in Washington, isn`t it also your responsibility to show results and Democrats failed to do that before the midterm elections last week?\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Well, we pass the American Rescue Plan which submitted $130 billion to K-12 Public Schools, provided rent relief, made to child -- to increase the child tax credits at the end of the year lifting 50 percent of children in poverty out of poverty and we dramatically increase vaccines both here and abroad and save probably millions of lives as a result.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So, we delivered with the American rescue plan, and the bipartisan bill was ready to move. It was 