\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by \uc2 John Podesta\uc2 , former White House chief of staff, now the founder and chair of the Board of Directors for the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining me. Appreciate it. So just weigh in right there, if you would, on the fact that we are seeing really the resolution of this COP26 summit seems to be leaning toward a weaker final resolution that won`t require scaling back fossil fuels and coal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PODESTA\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, it`s good to see you again, Kristen. And I would say that, you know, from the moment that text hit the table on Wednesday, fossil fuel interests have been trying to weaken it. And it`s important that we push back. One big difference at this COP is the United States came prepared to work, starting with President Biden. We saw former President Obama there, Secretary Kerry has been dogged all year.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`ve seen momentum coming from a lot of different sectors on methane, on deforestation, on finance. But again when you get down to that negotiating text with the governments, countries like Saudi Arabia, Russia, are trying to weaken the text. And quite frankly, even if they get everything they want in the text, we still have to go further and we still have to go faster.\par \par \uc2 The pledges that are coming forward will get us within reach of what the Paris agreement might have called for, about two degrees, 2.4 degrees, but we`ve got to do better than that. We`ve got to go to the so-called net zero world that President Biden has pledged. And to do that, we need some critical legislation passed in the upcoming weeks in Congress.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, and John, to that point, the other big issue that we`re watching at COP26 is funding for smaller countries on climate initiatives. Larger countries including the U.S. so far have failed to reach the level of support that they initially promised to 