ntly, reduce family expenditures in places like child care, prescription drugs, lower the cost of energy.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s important that the country move onto this bill and get it done. And I think, as I said, I think had they be delinked earlier in the summer, I don`t think we`d be talking about whether this bill would be passed because it`d be long dead.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right.\par \par \uc2 PODESTA: So I actually think that, you know, it has its political consequences, but the biggest political consequence is delivering for the American people, keeping your pledges, and that`s what I think the White House and the leadership in Congress is trying to do.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. \uc2 John Podesta\uc2 , thank you so much. Great conversation. Always great to have your perspective. Really appreciate it. And wonderful to see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the great resignation, unprecedented challenges for the American workforce are changing the landscape of U.S. labor. Secretary Marty Walsh joins us live from the White House. That`s right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:06]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back. We learned today that the month of September saw a record number of Americans quit their jobs. According to the Labor Department, 4.4 million Americans quit their jobs in September as the Delta variant of the coronavirus surged in many parts of the country. That`s about 3 percent of the workforce. All told, there were more than 10 million job openings in September with more than seven million Americans unemployed.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss this from the White House is the Secretary of Labor, Marty Walsh.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, thank you for joining me this afternoon. Let`s dive right in. According to your department, 4.4 million people quit their jobs in September. The question is why, and is that really a sign of a recovery?\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH, LABOR SECRETARY: Well, I think, number one, the Delta variant, as you said in the opening here, was a main reason for it. And two, quite honestly, coronavirus. I think still the fact that we are still living in a coronavirus time, we`re going to see a lot of ups and downs when it comes to jobs. We`re seeing certainly last month we had a very good job report here in the White House, with the administration.\par \par \uc2 About 5.6 million jobs have been added. But since I`ve been Labor secretary since March, if you go back to President Biden since he took office, we added 5.6 million jobs but if you look at a graph where we`ve seen dips in high layoffs or high people leaving their jobs or good job reports, it runs parallel for the most part with the virus.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: How would you characterize the strength of the recovery given that you have 4.4 million people who quit their jobs?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: I think I feel personally we are living really in unprecedented times. And certainly, all of us would love to see more people back to work and more of these jobs filled, but I think the president has laid down some plans here. One is the American Rescue Plan that really begins to deal with the pandemic in the beginning and getting shots into people`s arms and getting money to PPE, the cities and towns all across America.\par \par \uc2 We saw the unemployment benefits expire in September. We`re seeing more and more people start to look for jobs and get jobs. Now we might not be where we need to be, but we`re definitely seeing more people involved in the job market. And as we continue to move forward here, I think the outcome -- I feel that we`re going to have a really strong few months here moving forward.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And speaking of the president`s plans, obviously he`s now focused on getting his Build Back Better spending and climate plan passed. And it comes against the backdrop of inflation, new numbers which show it`s soaring to its highest level in over 30 years. As you well know, some moderates, Joe Manchin, say they are concerned that another sweeping plan will only make the economic climate worse.\par \par \uc2 So what is your response? Why is more spending the answer to this increased inflation?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Well, certainly the president acknowledges the fact that inflation and he`s taking a lot of action in the short term on supply chain work, on supply chain. And certainly on long term in the Build Back Better bill, there`s money in there for child care. There`s money in there for transportation. There`s money in there for prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 And those are the challenges that Americans face every single day with rising costs. And a lot of that will help us fight the inflation thing. And I just want to say one other thing about inflation. As a former mayor of Boston, pre-pandemic even though the inflation numbers were down, there were too many American families in this country that couldn`t afford goods and services to bring in to their homes because they weren`t making the money to bring it in.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s plan really is about creating opportunities again to people, the middle class, and where people having the ability to access and get better jobs, better paying jobs, they`ll be able to afford and raise their families. And all of this at the end of the day will be positive for our country.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Secretary Walsh, critics within the Democratic Party would argue that Americans are looking for relief now, and passing another sweeping plan might have an impact over years, but wouldn`t necessarily bring Americans the relief they need now. How do you answer that charge?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: My recommendation is let`s get Build Back Better passed. And let`s make those investments now into people whether it`s into job training, into child care, universal prekindergarten into all of the programs that are in that bill.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 Let`s pass the bill. The longer we talk about it and the more we let it go, the less time on the other side we have to make these investments.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You`re heading into a Cabinet meeting in just a short time from now. What are the tangibles you`re looking for to come out of that? Is this going to be anything more than a sales pitch meeting?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: No, it`s going to be more. Our cabinet meetings have been certainly a lot more than sales pitches. I`m sure there`s going to be a wide range of topics on the table. The president is very -- makes sure we all feel part of the different Cabinets. So I`m going to talk a little bit about what I`m experiencing moving forward and I`m sure that we`re going to have a great meeting today about moving forward, and certainly the issues that we`re talking about right now, inflation and job creation and job growth and job gain and openings. All of those conversations are generally spoken about at that meeting.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. And Secretary Walsh, you obviously oversee OSHA as well. A three-judge panel on the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals has temporarily halted OSHA`s new federal vaccine or test rule for big employers. What happens now and how are you going to respond?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Well, certainly I think the most important thing about the Emergency Temporary Standard is that we get Americans vaccinated. Everything that you and I spoke about right up to this point is about the pandemic. If we didn`t have a pandemic, we might not be talking about job openings, we might not be talking about inflation, and what the pandemic has done to the American public and the American people and the American workforce is quite honestly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And we want to make sure that we get people that go to work in a safe environment, where they know that their colleagues are either vaccinated or being tested on a weekly basis. This is not about to be burdensome to businesses. This is about protecting and having a safe workspace for employees.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You`ll appeal this decision? Do you anticipate you`ll be successful ultimately?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: If I knew that, I`d be in a different job. So we`ll see. We`ll take it a step at a time.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. Secretary Marty Walsh, really appreciate your time this afternoon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And coming up next, an unusual political development in this day and age. A Republican who`s willing to go up against a Trump-backed challenger.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:47]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back. Some 2022 midterm news today. Alaska Republican Senator Lisa Murkowski made it official this morning, announcing she is running for another term in office. Murkowski has been targeted by pro- Trump forces over her vote to convict in the impeachment trial earlier this year. The former president has already endorsed one of her primary challengers but in her announcement video, Murkowski says she will not be cowed by out of state forces. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): In this election, Lower 48 outsiders are going to try to grab Alaska`s Senate seat for their partisan agendas. My commitment to Alaska is the same one I made during my first campaign. I will work with anyone from either party to advance Alaska`s priorities.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, it`s worth noting that Murkowski`s independent streak has saved her at the ballot box before. In 2010, she lost the Republican primary to a Tea Party candidate but won in a write-in campaign. And it also should be noted that Murkowski is the only Republican senator who voted to convict the former president, who is running for reelection next year.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new COVID challenges all across the country. We`ll talk to the top health official in one surprising hotspot.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:10]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back. It`s been one week since children age 5 through 11 became eligible for Pfizer`s pediatric COVID vaccine. And according to the White House, nearly one million kids have already received their first shots. And boosters available to millions of adults the number of shots being administered every day has now reached its highest point since mid- June. But the latest vaccine push comes as cases here and abroad have taken a turn.\par \par \uc2 Following a month of steep decline, the daily U.S. case average has plateaued and is up 5 percent in just two weeks. States seeing the biggest increases are up north, from Michigan and Minnesota, to the upper New England states of New Hampshire and Vermont where the weather is already cooler.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk about what`s ahead for the pandemic and a potential winter surge is the director of Los Angeles County`s Department of Public Health, Dr. Barbara Ferrer.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ferrer, thanks so much for joining me. Let`s dive into what`s happening in California. Of course the governor warned residents this week that COVID would not be taking a break for winter. So how concerned are you about another potential wave in Los Angeles?\par \par \uc2 DR. BARBARA FERRER, DIRECTOR, L.A. COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH: Yes. Thanks so much, Kristen, for having me. And you know, I share the same concern as, I think, everybody in the country. We had a devastating winter last year, there`s no getting around that. And with cases rising both in Europe, other parts of the world, and now in different parts of the United States, we all need to take a pause and remember how important it`s going to be to get through the winter months by doing every single thing we can to protect ourselves and everybody that we love.\par \par \uc2 The good news, I think, for all of us is that we do have vaccines. They`re super powerful. You know, here in L.A. County, people who are unvaccinated are seven times more likely to become infected. About 44 times more likely to end up in the hospital. And sadly, 60 times more likely than vaccinated people to die. So the power of the vaccine is clear, well-established.\par \par \uc2 With the ability now to vaccinate, as you noted, children ages 5 and older, we`ve added here in L.A. County 900,000 children who can now get that protection that helps keep them safe. But also everybody around them. So I think we need to be cautious. I think we need to be vigilant. But I think we have tools at hand that should make a very big difference, as long as we take advantage of the tools that we have and we`re using them.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Doctor Ferrer, why are we seeing these spikes, given where we are in this battle, given that kids are now getting vaccinated? I think a lot of people are concerned by that.\par \par \uc2 FERRER: As long as you have a reservoir of unvaccinated people, it`s pretty easy for the virus to take hold. You know, here in L.A. County, we have about 81 percent of residents with at least one dose and I think 73 percent fully vaccinated. But that leaves almost 2.5 million people not yet vaccinated. So you know the Delta variant, that`s the most dominant strain that`s circulating, that actually is pretty easily transmitted, particularly amongst unvaccinated, but also then can affect those of us who may be fully vaccinated as well.\par \par \uc2 We start seeing people coming indoors. We have a lot more intermingling. We have the Thanksgiving holiday coming up, the winter holiday is ahead of us. And those are all conditions where this virus thrives.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. And Dr. Ferrer, California, interestingly, has actually just opened up booster eligibility to all vaccinated adults. And that`s actually a step further than the CDC`s own guidance. Is that the right move? And frankly, should other states be following suit?\par \par \uc2 FERRER: Yes. I think, you know, it may be semantics in what we`re doing in California. If you look at how the CDC described -- and this really applies to those people who are six months out of their second dose and received either the Moderna or the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. If you look at how CDC described that eligibility, they cast a very wide net. And just about everybody who got vaccinated at least six months ago is going to meet that criteria.\par \par \uc2 You`re older, you have underlying health conditions, you know, you`re 18 and older and you have exposures either at work or an institutional setting. And remember, we were still operating with some scarcity so we were prioritizing who was getting vaccine six months ago still. And you know, we`re talking about our teachers, our health care workers, people who live in congregate living.\par \par \uc2 So I think we`re really -- we`re really I think aligned, just we`re making it easy for people to understand that if you`re six months out from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna, please come and get your booster, two months out from \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , 18 and older, come in and get your booster.\par \par \uc2 [14:00:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Dr. Ferrer, such important information on this Friday. We really appreciate your joining us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FERRER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And thank you for being with us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS" on NBC. MSNBC coverage continues right now with my friend and colleague Chris Jansing.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211113ehbc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for November 9, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Steve Patterson, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Antonia Hylton,  Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 8,628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Morgan Chesky, thank you so much, so many questions to answer. And that does it for this edition of ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS, remember, follow us online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @mitchellreports. Chuck Todd with "MTP DAILY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday, COVID cases have begun to slowly rise across the U.S. Is another surge coming? Misinformation about the vaccine is spreading in new ways.\par \par \uc2 And we`re following troubling developments in Colorado, where cases and hospitalizations are now nearing winter 2020 levels and experts simply aren`t sure why.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new subpoenas in the investigation to the January 6 insurrection, where is this investigation headed and when will it get there? We`re live on Capitol Hill with those details ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, can Biden`s infrastructure agenda rescue Democrats politically? We`ll speak with a House Democrat whose district is on the frontlines of the fight over control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 It`s getting colder. Coronavirus infections rates appear to be rising. Colorado is suddenly battling a mysterious surge in cases.\par \par \uc2 Vaccine misinformation is back in the headlines, thanks to Aaron Rodgers. And overall, experts say the conditions are ripe for a winter surge across the entire northern hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I know it`s the last thing any of us want to hear. Just trust me. But you got to pay attention to these numbers today.\par \par \uc2 It all leads to this question. Are we on the cusp of another spike in infections, hospitalizations, and sadly, deaths here in the United States? We hope not. Well, we don`t know for sure and we don`t have that COVID pill in this country just yet.\par \par \uc2 What we do know for sure is that infection rates across the country had stopped declining. As you can see, we`ve hit a plateau that sits at roughly between 70 and 75,000 new cases every day, and now it`s starting to climb.\par \par \uc2 We sort of flat-lined at around 71,000 for about a week, now it`s inching back up in fact, infection rates have gone up 5 percent in the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 1200 Americans are dying every day and models suggest a death toll exceeding 1 million by the spring. That`s 250,000 additional deaths over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the whole Aaron Rodgers saga has trust the issue of vaccine misinformation back into the spotlight. He is a walking misinformation machine after the famous quarterback used anti-vaccination rhetoric to defend his decision not to get the shot as he tried to evade NFL protocols and public scrutiny and then ended up testing positive.\par \par \uc2 It`s a reminder of just how widespread the misinformation pandemic has spread particularly on fertility claims. He hung a lot of his decision based on misinformation he`d gotten on fertility.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to a new Kaiser poll, nearly eight of 10 Americans believe or seemed open to some form of COVID misinformation, whether it`s falsehoods about the vaccine causing infertility or conspiracy theories that the government is exaggerating, the death toll.\par \par \uc2 The poll also found that Republicans are especially vulnerable to this misinformation because of the closed ecosystem -- new information ecosystem that they live in. And as the New York Times notes, tragically, the death toll in America has gotten redder.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is a -- this is data that we first shared with you a few months ago that we noticed this trend. Well, now it`s gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 They found that on October 25, out of every 100,000 residents of heavily Trump counties died from COVID. That is more than three times higher than the rate in heavily Biden counties.\par \par \uc2 What it all means for this country`s ongoing and at times demoralizing fight against the pandemic? And the forces fueling it is where we`re going to begin our show today.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by our own Steve Patterson, he`s in Colorado trying to get a handle on the state`s mysterious surge in infections.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got \uc2 Dr. Michael Osterholm\uc2  here. He`s the Director for the Center for Infectious Disease Research and Policy at the University of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 And for more on the challenges of combating mis and disinformation, I`m also joined by New York Times Reporter, Emily Anthes. So, who`s out with some new reporting on Aaron Rodgers in the fight against vaccine misinformation?\par \par \uc2 Suzanne Spaulding, a Senior Advisor at the Center for Strategic International Studies, with an expertise in cyber threats, including disinformation is also here.\par \par \uc2 But let me start with Steve in Colorado. And Steve, the Colorado issue is sort of even as we were watching the entire country decline about a month ago, Colorado was seeing this uptick.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was basically, the vaccinated versus the unvaccinated. So, what is -- what is the mystery? What -- why do they feel as if they don`t understand this current surge?\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, a few things. I mean, the million-dollar question is, is this current spike, the Delta spike that many other states have already seen, is -- you know, almost a look in the past, or is this the new spike that other states are going to soon be facing?\par \par \uc2 And it`s so troubling because as you said, you know, most other states that have had these problems -- that also had problems vaccinating populations.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 This is not a state that is ignored science. This is not a governor`s office that has ignored the science. More than 60 percent of Colorado citizens are fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Those numbers could be better, but those numbers are rising. And the infection rate was just a few weeks ago, generally pretty low.\par \par \uc2 Now it is suddenly shot up and nobody really has a good answer as to why. Every time I asked that there`s a list of reasons, right, there`s obviously it`s colder now.\par \par \uc2 So you know, maybe there`s some seasonality to the virus, or maybe that just impacts the fact that people are inside, they`re closer together, a lot of the mask mandates, a lot of the, you know, different mandates throughout the state have relaxed a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And maybe people are just taking this a little bit less seriously so they`ve relaxed and the virus is able to spread.\par \par \uc2 I was told that there are certain pockets of Colorado that are more vaccinated than other pockets. And those vaccinated pockets are doing better but they`re infiltrated by the places that aren`t, almost as if a Petri dish is sort of a lead-out across to the rest of the state.\par \par \uc2 Nobody really has a good answer why, but I can tell you that the hospital systems, no matter what the reasons are suffering.\par \par \uc2 We`re now in a tier-three designation, it`s the highest designation in the state of Colorado basically means that they can more easily transfer patients using a statewide system.\par \par \uc2 Colorado, so far, you know, throughout all of these spikes, has never ever had to do that, and know that they are. And there`s also, you know, there`s some worry that people are delaying care further than they have to.\par \par \uc2 They`ve had to delay care in the -- in the past because elective surgeries have been postponed. And people have been not going to the doctor`s office or not going into the hospitals.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, you have a colonoscopy that could turn into a polyp removal that could turn into cancer because nobody is doing what they`re supposed to be doing.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to a doctor about that here, an ER doctor. He expressed a little bit more about what`s happening in the -- inside the hospitals. Here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD ZANE, CHIEF INNOVATIVE OFFICER, UCHEALTH: What you`re seeing is healthcare breaking, hospitals are full, full stop. Therefore, the ICUs are full, the regular patients that I`m saying regular, like heart attacks, and strokes, and infection are all sicker, and they`re all coming at the same time.\par \par \uc2 So, our Emergency Department volumes are up 30 percent across the country and in Colorado. Combine that with the incredible paralyzing number of COVID patients in the hospital, the vast majority of whom are unvaccinated and it`s almost the perfect storm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: And again, that`s the messaging from the hospital system here, get vaccinated. Vast majority of people in the hospital are unvaccinated, much more when it comes to people who are using obviously the ICUs which there are now less than 100 in the state, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, it`s so weird Steve, to hear the stories we`re hearing out of Colorado. They feel like the stories we heard out of basically every state in August and September.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s why it`s so strange to see it on this in the moment that we`re in. Steve Patterson, getting started in Colorado, Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, let`s go to somebody who could have some answers to the Colorado mystery, and it`s \uc2 Dr. Michael Osterholm\uc2 . Dr. Osterholm, let me start there.\par \par \uc2 What it -- what`s your thesis? Are they -- did Delta skip them and Delta found, as you like to say, wood to burn in Colorado? Or is this the beginning of a final wave that we`re going to feel here?\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL OSTERHOLM\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CENTER FOR INFECTIOUS DISEASE RESEARCH & POLICY, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MINNESOTA\uc2 : Well, the first thing we have to do is answer with humility and say we don`t know. First of all, it`s not Colorado. Right now, the whole entire Four Corners area of the United States has seen this major increase.\par \par \uc2 The entire upper Mid-West, we`re seeing some unprecedented numbers right here in Minnesota, Wisconsin, Iowa, the Dakotas. And we`re also seeing that in the far northeast, and in New Hampshire and Vermont.\par \par \uc2 And what really is the -- really challenge for us is, understanding why do in some cases, the virus suddenly surge and then suddenly reduce its numbers after that surge happens?\par \par \uc2 We know the vaccine can make a big difference in how big that surge is. But why it starts and stop? We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Look right now what`s happening in Europe, in countries much more highly vaccinated than even the United States.\par \par \uc2 Germany today is reporting out its highest number of cases on record during the pandemic. We`re seeing that in other countries.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you have Africa, which has very, very low rates of vaccination, less than 6 percent of the population, and they almost have no activity. Now, they will tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But right now, we don`t understand what makes this virus start and stop when it does these surges in these areas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What you`re seeing in Europe, do you think some of that is vac -- the waning vaccine efficacy?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, some of it might be but in fact, it`s also again, anytime you have less than 100 percent of your population protected either through having been vaccinated or previously having had an infection and some immunity from that, you`re vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 This virus is different than flu viruses. This virus is different than most respiratory viruses, where it does not stop transmitting until virtually everyone is protected.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So, having 70, 80 percent vaccination rates, doesn`t guarantee it`ll stop transmitting, it`ll find those last 10 to 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 As you and I have discussed before, you can`t run the game clock out on this one, it will find you and that`s what it`s doing. And why it`s doing it? We don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 Explain to me why LA and New York, both areas with large unvaccinated populations have not seen any of the Delta surge yet. It`s going to happen, it will happen. But why it isn`t happening now and why it`ll happen later? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the -- is the answer going to be the COVID pill? What the UK approved, assuming we get it approved here and there`s certainly some optimism that it will.\par \par \uc2 Is that the point where we go to endemic and we sort of acknowledge we`re not going to get -- the vaccine wars have been lost, we`re going to plateau at about 70 to 80 percent of the country max, is the COVID pill our last best hope?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, we can`t give up on the vaccine war yet, I surely agree with you that the pill is going to be important.\par \par \uc2 But remember, if you`re vaccinated, you have a six-fold lower risk of actually getting infected. If you`re vaccinated, you have an 11 fold lower risk of getting seriously ill and dying. So, the vaccines will continue to be a very critical part of our response.\par \par \uc2 But now adding on the pill for those individuals who have early symptoms that can be confirmed by a laboratory test, taking these pills will be a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s going to be both. And in that regard, hopefully, because we have both, we will be able to see in another few months, a very different perspective on this pandemic than we have now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before I get to misinform my misinformation guests, Michael, I`m just curious. Do you think this winter surge -- are you bracing for something that we saw this summer or is this next surge going to be a bit more manageable?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, let me just give you the absolute answer, that`s the correct one, OK. We`ll have somewhere between almost no activity and we`re going to have a major surge.\par \par \uc2 And as it`s come we`re in between those two, and I`m quite certain I`m right. Beyond that, anybody that tells you that they know exactly what`s going to happen --\par \par \uc2 TODD: How fair enough you know.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: -- be careful because they probably have a bridge to sell you to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know. I know. We all want our answer right now, Michael, you know that. Anyway, \uc2 Dr. Michael Osterholm\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know with the -- with the -- with the blunt truth about the virus. Dr. Osterholm, as always sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now let`s talk a little misinformation here. I`ll admit, as a fan of the Packers, it pains me to admit it`s the quarterback of my favorite NFL team that is now becoming the leading -- one of the leading misinformers here.\par \par \uc2 Emily Anthes, and Suzanne Spaulding, in your own ways, I`d like it to handle this question.\par \par \uc2 Emily, let me start with you. What -- it was sort of the rhetoric that Rodgers was using and explaining his decision not to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 There was something very familiar about it to me in various folks under the age of 40 in my life, where I`ve had this discussion or I hear the words` fertility come up, and this -- or you know, belief that it might harm a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 That seems to be among the most powerful pieces of misinformation that is persuasive to people under 40 these days. What are folks trying to do about this?\par \par \uc2 Well, you`re right that that`s a piece of misinformation that comes up again, and again, it started coming up the instant the vaccines were available even before then.\par \par \uc2 EMILY ANTHES, HEALTH & SCIENCE REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: And there`s really no evidence that these vaccines have any effect on fertility, male or female fertility, pregnancy outcomes and in fact, if you`re really worried about having a healthy pregnancy, COVID-19 is the far bigger threat.\par \par \uc2 And so, I understand why people are concerned about fertility. It`s a major issue to the public as it should be. But I think we have our danger here exactly backward. It`s the virus that`s the threat and not the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Emily, one of the problems perhaps that the NFL may have is that there are plenty of NFL players in the past who will tell you, they don`t trust medical advice they get from the NFL and they`re -- and they can cite for you a bunch of reasons why.\par \par \uc2 Back in the day, doc -- you know, maybe the teams were looking for doctors to get players back on the field and overlook certain things. So, I think we have to deal with player skepticism of the NFL medical community.\par \par \uc2 We have to at least understand where it comes from. It`s not just made up. Does the NFL have any plans and how to deal with this misinformation though?\par \par \uc2 ANTHES: You know, I`m a science reporter, not an NFL reporter, so I can`t speak to what their plans are.\par \par \uc2 But it`s a little frustrating when you see someone like Aaron Rodgers rejecting sort of the best medical information because he has resources, he`s an intelligent guy, he could certainly -- if he wanted second, and third and fourth and fifth opinions, he could find no shortage of respected public health experts to give it to him.\par \par \uc2 And yet, he still went off on this sort of bizarre pseudo-scientific path that is really far afield from mainstream medicine.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Suzanne, you deal with mis and disinformation when it comes -- and it gets weaponized digitally here. It does seem as if there are -- as Emily pointed out, you know, as soon as we got the vaccines came out, you started to hear some level of misinformation. And now, that kids are in line to get the vaccine it feels like -- feels like there`s like a new round of circulating going on there.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE SPAULDING, CENTER FOR STRATEGIC DISEASE & INTERNATIONAL STUDIES: Yes. And Todd, your point about mistrust of the sources of information, whether it`s the NFL medical profession or the broader medical profession, or government or mainstream media, I mean, a huge part of the problem here is that we have this growing mistrust of traditional sources of information makes it very hard to message broadly to the American public.\par \par \uc2 Our Surgeon General talked about in July that this misinformation is an urgent threat to public health, as you`ve been discussing.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that`s why we saw the guidance that we saw today, the Surgeon General came out with a toolkit for individuals to talk to individuals. We are -- we are now having to go to this very retail person-to-person in- person conversations and empower those conversations.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So does this mean the trusted -- the idea of I trusted messenger? You know, find people that are trusted messengers, I guess that approaches because the minute there is a trusted measured -- person, there`s literally a campaign pops up to delegitimize them. Anthony Fauci comes to mind.\par \par \uc2 SPAULDING: Yes. So, those -- we`ve always said in this kind of public crisis communications, you got to find those trusted messengers and it`s increasingly difficult.\par \par \uc2 So yes, those broad messengers are not going to have the success with a broad segment of the population. And it really is going to come down to trust based on personal relationships.\par \par \uc2 So pediatricians having conversations with their patients, doctors having conversations with their patients, clergy, on, you know, conversations with their parishioners, and family with family, children with parents`, parents` with children.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Emily, when you go -- bring back to the Aaron Rodgers situation, my son absolutely worships him or did, he doesn`t now. I mean, he has decided, oh, my God, I didn`t know he was an idiot. That`s the way my 14-year-old put it.\par \par \uc2 But he may not, you know, there`s a lot of other kids that may look at Aaron Rodgers and say, I don`t -- I mean, I don`t want the vaccine or tell their parents that they don`t want it, or 18-year-olds or 19-year-old. I`m sure plenty of experts you`ve talked to are concerned about this. Is there a good way to combat it?\par \par \uc2 ANTHES: Yes. I mean, you`re right, that that was one of the first things that came up. When I started talking to experts yesterday was just like the really, you know, there`s never a good time to spread misinformation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHES: But right now when we`re really trying to get younger kids vaccinated when that campaign is just rolling out, it`s a particularly unfortunate timing.\par \par \uc2 And I think it really is, you know, it is hard -- to get back to the trusted messenger question, it`s hard to change minds at scale.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s not going to be national ad campaigns. It really is these one-on-one conversations with -- you know, when it comes to kids with the family physician, the pediatrician that families have had relationships with for years, with other people in their communities who people know and trust. That`s what`s I think going to end up making the difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You would think common sense would also work its way out but apparently, that has gotten weaponized on social media as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Emily Anthes, Suzanne Spaulding, appreciate both of you on this topic before that \uc2 Michael Osterholm\uc2  and Steve Patterson. Appreciate all of you getting started on a lead none of us want to have to do but COVID is not done with us yet.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new subpoenas in the January 6 investigation, as a Federal Judge blocks former President Trump`s latest efforts to chill backing and stop by the committee.\par \par \uc2 And later, we`re going to go live to Kenosha, Wisconsin for the very latest on the Kyle Rittenhouse trial as the medical examiner takes the stand. And Rittenhouse, himself, could soon be next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Committee on the January 6 insurrection is now turning its focus to the Trump campaign itself, issuing six new subpoenas yesterday to officials associated with the reelection campaign including former Campaign Manager Bill Stepien and former National Security Adviser Michael Flynn.\par \par \uc2 According to the Committee, Trump campaign aide set up a "war room" to strategize how to stop the counting of electoral votes in the days before the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Now, the six people subpoenaed had said whether they will cooperate, although one of them, former NYPD Police Commissioner Bernard Kerik, released a statement calling this a partisan stunt.\par \par \uc2 And the Justice Department is still deciding whether to prosecute Steve Bannon, who vowed not to cooperate with the Committee, signing executive privilege. The House voted to send a criminal referral to the DOJ last month.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann Caldwell joins me now from Capitol Hill. And Leigh Ann, we think we`re going to see more subpoenas today. In fact, I think we thought we would have them by now of what today`s round would look like.\par \par \uc2 But I am curious, how concerned is the Committee about its subpoenas right now, considering that justice has not moved yet on the Bannon complaint?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Well, Chuck, the Committee is put forward -- putting forward a face of confidence that they`re not concerned about it. They say that Steve Bannon is the only person who has not cooperated, that others are cooperating.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know at what level that cooperation is, but they say that it`s enough to keep them from referring another criminal contempt charge.\par \par \uc2 Now, these subpoenas today though, this is a unique group. These are people who were extremely close with the former President.\par \par \uc2 And while the previous subpoenas had to do with people who were organizing or perhaps financing to stop the steel rallies, riling up a Trump MAGA supporters, these group of people.\par \par \uc2 They were instrumental and trying to persuade local election officials to overturn the election results and even trying to persuade the former Vice President, Mike Pence, to overturn the Electoral College count in Congress on January 6.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So, this is a really interesting and really important group of subpoenas. We have no indication yet if this group is going to comply with those subpoenas but we also know that the Committee, in addition, the subpoenas are who they think are not going to cooperate. That`s who these subpoenas are for.\par \par \uc2 They also say there`s 150 people that they have already spoken to not just in DC, but around the country and so they pointed that as the fact that they are getting a lot of cooperation. They`re getting thousands and thousands of pages of documents.\par \par \uc2 The question is, is do they need these people to fill in the gaps and to really tell a more complete story, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So Leigh Ann, I mean, obviously, you see this at the Trump campaign officials and you think is the Committee`s hypothesis that the Trump campaign itself was helping to orchestrate all of the events that led to the insurrection, or they`re seeing if that was the case, right?\par \par \uc2 Do they already have evidence which led them to subpoenas or are they subpoenaing to find out?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I think a mixture of both. A lot of what they know is from reporting from books that have been written and from journalistic accounts of what has happened and so, they are subpoenaing to find out more.\par \par \uc2 They -- it`s interesting with the Trump campaign officials because it would be much harder for them to say that they are protected under executive privilege. They did not work in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, White House officials that could be a much stronger case, even though our colleague, Pete Williams, says it might not even be that strong.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the Trump campaign, according to the Committee had a very significant role and the lead up to the January 6 and trying to undercut the election results, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill for us. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden says he`s optimistic that the -- his Build Back Better agenda will make it through Congress.\par \par \uc2 But that`s just one of the major hurdles Democrats will have to overcome in a very long to-do list before the end of this calendar year. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Yes, we intend. That is our plan to pass the bills the week of November 15th as is indicated in our statements that were made at the time of passing the infrastructure bill and we`re very proud of that.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was Speaker Pelosi earlier in Glasgow saying it`s still her intention to pass President Biden`s Build Back Better agenda next we`re. That sweeping plan is just one thing on a long congressional to-do list as Democrats are trying to turn the page on last week`s election night disappointments. Beyond the president`s domestic agenda, Congress still has to raise the debt ceiling, fund the government and authorize military spending just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is Texas Democratic Congressman Colin Allred.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to see you. Let me start with a question I imagined you get from constituents. What do you tell your constituents that`s in Build Back Better and what do you tell them is going to be in it when it gets passed?\par \par \uc2 REP. COLIN ALLRED (D-TX): Yes. Well, I`d tell them it`s a historic and necessary bill. This is going to lower your health care costs, lower the cost of prescription drugs and lower cost for families allowing women to get back to work, get through pre-K, with paid leave hopefully capping the cost of child care, that it`s going to help us combat climate change which is something that we`ve seen in Texas, we had a freeze that took out power for a week, and that it is something that`s going to create jobs and help us get back our economy going. We had 500,000 jobs created last month. That`s great. But we can do better with this bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you find it`s difficult to convince somebody that one bill has all those things in it?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: You know, I think -- I try to be honest with my constituents and tell them that we have to do it this way because the Senate is broken and because we can`t get past the filibuster. And so this is the only way for us to be able to pass significant legislation. And it shouldn`t be incumbent upon us to have to do it just through this kind of a mechanism. But that`s where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think the people expect us to deliver on what the president campaigned on when he talked about, when he addressed the Congress, the joint session, these are all things that we`ve been talking about for some time. These are not, you know, surprises. These are Democratic priorities that we know will help our economy, will help people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This wasn`t your decision but do you wish you could be voting on these individually? Maybe at the end of the day it`s all going to be passed under one giant umbrella bill. But it`s certainly I think would help you explain it to voters and explain the process to voters if you were voting on these things one at a time, and say, OK, final passage is going to be over here. Look, that`s what Congress does.\par \par \uc2 We know Decembers are for making your members of Congress family`s lives miserable during the holidays but then people would at least know what`s in it, oh, it did paid leave this week. Oh, they did the prescription drug bill this week. Oh. That to me is I feel as if has made it that much more difficult for you to explain it to your constituents.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, hopefully folks feel it. And you know, we`ve done some really big legislation over the last couple of years to, you know, combat this virus and we`ve kept the economy going. We`ve sent direct checks to people. With the child tax credit, we are literally cutting child poverty in half in this country. And yes, you know, of course, universal pre-K is a huge priority for someone like me.\par \par \uc2 I was the first member of Congress to take paternity leave. So yes, paid leave is a huge priority for me. I was raised by a single mother. So yeas, child care I understand how expensive that is for families but I hope we can talk about it in a way that`s comprehensive and that folks feel it more importantly because they`ll feel that they have more money in their pockets that is easier for them to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had over two million women drop out of the work force during this pandemic. This is going to allow women in particular to get back to work. It`s the right bill for families, it`s right for our country. Yes, the process is messy. I wish that we didn`t have to go through this. I think we should banish the word reconciliation from everyone`s lexicon. But this is where we are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The issue of paid leave, whether it`s going to be in this bill or not and, you know, two months ago a pledge was made to you guys in Congress.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:07]\par \par \uc2 You weren`t going to have to vote on something that wasn`t going to be able to pass the Senate. Now it appears you`re going to be voting on something that may not pass the Senate. How comfortable are you all with that?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, I think we`re 95 percent, 96 percent, 97 percent pretty confident here, Chuck, and they haven`t even gone through vote-a-rama and all things we know that they`re going to do in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: So it is certainly going to change on their side. And we understand that. But I think it`s important to push for paid family leave because of the importance for our economy and because we are the only major developed country that doesn`t have it because we have basically every single Democrat in Washington, D.C. who wants to do it with one exception, and so yes, I think that also has a potential to be bipartisan because, you know, I have spent time working with the Trump White House on this issue. So I think there can be a lot of cross appeal here.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we`ve worked really hard to get in line with the Senate in terms of what they want and what can past there and get all 50 votes. Maybe not every single thing is going to be there but the vast, vast majority is going to be preconference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What lesson did you take away from last Thursday as you prepare for reelection in `22?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, first of all, we`ve created an atmosphere here that we have to move on from in terms of people wondering whether or not Democrats can govern? And we have to show that we can. And, you know, for me, I`m extremely concerned about the state of our democracy. I told my colleagues that, you know, as much as our individual issues or individual concerns are all important, the most important thing to show is our democracy still works, that we`re governing and you don`t have to go outside of the system and that you can trust us when we`re in power to deliver for you and your family.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I think we have not shown as well as we could have while we`re going through this long, drawn-out process. I think we also have to recognize that we have a lot of work to do to still beat this virus and to help folks feel like they`re getting back to normal. I think we felt like we`re getting there then the Delta variant came along and really changed our lives and sent us back into this pandemic, and I think folks are concerned.\par \par \uc2 They`re concerned about their kids. They`re concerned about their families. They`re concerned about their jobs. But we can address that. And we`re the only party that`s really trying to address that and we have to make sure that we do in the bills that we`re passing and the way that we communicate about them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you could make one ask the of the White House now as they`re thinking about this current, you know, the public`s current disappointment, whatever you want to look at it, there is no doubt the numbers are soft for the president nationally. If there is something that you`d like him to do, say, speak to, what would that be?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: I think we have to talk about the economy. We have to talk about the fact that we did create 500,000 jobs last month, that this president has created over 5 million jobs, the most of any president in their first year in office that the economy is coming back, but yes, we`re seeing some inflation but that is because of the pandemic that we`ve been going through, the supply chain and workforce issues that we have.\par \par \uc2 And that this is how we`re addressing those issues systematically, step by step. It`s going to take some time. Be honest with the American people about that but then the economy is not like a light switch. You can`t just turn it off and back on and that we are working every single day on the economy so that you and your family will be better off than you were the day before. A lot of what`s in this bill and I mean, again, I know it`s -- we need to talk about this more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Is about the economy and getting folks back to work. It`s not just pie in the sky stuff. This is about allowing mothers, allowing folks who had to go home to take care of their family.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: To get back into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds like you`re saying it`s not enough to pass some of these bills, sometimes you`ve got to let people know every day, hey, well, here are the new resources you have. You know, if you don`t tell people about it, they might forget.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Allred, Democrat from Texas, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: OK. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, a major blow to the Republicans` hope of taking back control of the Senate and not surprisingly, it`s all about Donald Trump. He claims his first victim in the Senate recruiting wars.\par \par \uc2 But before we go to break, we have sad news to report. Former Democratic senator from Georgia and Veterans Affairs administrator Max Cleland has passed away. Cleland was in a wheelchair most of his adult life after losing three of his limbs while serving in the Vietnam War. The former Army captain was one of 