ision as some of the current school board members seemed to be concerned in community members` views that some of the folks who wanted Whitfield to be fired would hold them accountable, push for them to lose their spots, bring in new candidates if this action wasn`t taken.\par \par \uc2 To give context to what happened last night this building behind me here was packed with parents, young students and residents, the majority of whom were there in support of Dr. Whitfield and expressed outrage, pain, worry that this was going to cast the entire community in a really bad light, as most of the parents there last night believed that this is racially motivated.\par \par \uc2 The district maintains that they actually agree Dr. Whitfield never taught or promoted critical race theory in the district but that they`ve moved forward with his termination due to deficiencies in his performance as principal. Dr. Whitfield maintains that he was promoted twice in three years, and that this all began over this past summer in the wake of George Floyd`s murder and community criticism over the way he spoke out about all of this.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to my exclusive conversation with him last night after the news broke.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WHITFIELD: Educators are fighting this battle and really it`s a battle that has been manufactured because not a single teacher is teaching critical race theory in schools but what they`ve termed to be critical race theory, which is diverse books. There are so many people that are going through experiences based on the legislation that`s come out in certain states and, you know, groups of parents or groups of community members.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I`m hopeful that we can use this to move forward and to progress, right, and get some true meaningful change.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Dr. Whitfield does not know now what he`ll do next or what his next job in education might look like, but he told me that his focus is really on other educators and the fact that his story may strike fear in people who are continuing to do this work and that already he is hearing that around Texas educators are fearful now for their jobs. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Antonia, I`m going to ask you what I think you and I discussed before, the school district, they`ve used deficiencies in his job but what were they? Is it simply sending out that e-mail saying that what happened to George Floyd impacted him a lot? I mean, is that the deficiency?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: You know, Chuck, it`s very complicated and it`s continued to be very confusing for all the reporters who have covered this. They`ve listed a couple different reasons. Deficiencies in his performance as principal, referencing, you know, some personnel interactions and e-mail exchanges with parents that they haven`t given complete context on but they feel he could have been a better communicator on.\par \par \uc2 Some of what they`ve also cited are behaviors or comments that he has made in the wake of the accusations that have been lodged against him. So while they`re saying they`re not firing him because of critical race theory, they are pointing to things that he said to defend himself, to try to repair his reputation, to say that those are part of the reasons they are moving forward with his termination.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, it`s kind of tough to figure out what`s fully transpired here and Dr. Whitfield and the school maintain that they both have agreed to disagree now but the conflict is still here in the community.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. And he`s got children that go to the school, if I remember, right across the street from the high school. Anyway, Antonia Hylton in Grapevine, Texas.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Antonia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 You can watch our entire deep dive on James Whitfield`s story and the textbook wars any time you want. All of the past episodes of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" are available right now on demand on Peacock. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ve got some more key testimony today on the Kyle Rittenhouse trial, as Rittenhouse himself could soon take the stand. We`ll get the latest from Kenosha, Wisconsin, right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:58:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Moments ago in Kenosha, Wisconsin, the prosecution rested the case in the trial of Kyle Rittenhouse. The 18-year-old trial faced with first-degree homicide charges for killing two protesters and injuring another during clashes between armed civilians and protesters in Kenosha last year. The defense is currently presenting their case.\par \par \uc2 Gabe Gutierrez is following the trial for us from Kenosha.\par \par \uc2 And Gabe, in trials like this, you rarely hear that you`re going to -- that the defendant is going to take the stand but we think that`s possible here, correct?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chuck. During its opening statement, the defense indicated that the jury would hear from Kyle Rittenhouse. It`s still an open question on when that might happen, but yes, as you mentioned, the defense now taking over the case, presenting its case in front of those jurors. So it`s possible we could hear from Kyle Rittenhouse later on this week.\par \par \uc2 But, Chuck, a couple of developments just in the last few minutes. As you mentioned, the prosecution resting its case but also the judge has actually just dismissed one of the charges against Kyle Rittenhouse, that of a curfew violation. Basically the prosecution saying that they had presented evidence for that in the sense that they asked a police officer whether it was a curfew in effect.\par \par \uc2 The defense asked to dismiss the charge saying that there wasn`t enough evidence presented for that. The judge agreed with the defense. So now Kyle Rittenhouse is facing six counts, not seven. That curfew violation charge was dismissed. A critical piece of testimony, though, came yesterday with the man Kyle Rittenhouse shot and wounded. The prosecution tried to pain Rittenhouse as a vigilante who was out looking for trouble. The defense coming back and as we speak presenting its case that it was all self- defense.\par \par \uc2 Again, we don`t know exactly when Kyle Rittenhouse might testify. It could happen, though, later this week, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the trial has moved with -- at a pretty crisp pace ever since it got started.\par \par \uc2 [14:00:02]\par \par \uc2 Gabe Gutierrez on the ground for us. Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with my friend Chris Jansing right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211110ehb900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for November 4, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Ali Vitali, Josh Lederman, Dan De  Luce \par \uc2 9,077 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Chuck Todd with MTP DAILY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday, Democrats try to hit the gas on President Biden`s agenda in the wake of Tuesday`s disastrous electoral results. For the Democrats, will it be enough?\par \par \uc2 I`ll speak with DCCC Chair, Congressman Sean Patrick Maloney about what went wrong and where his party goes next.\par \par \uc2 Plus, parents and school boards across America are thrust into the political spotlight as Republicans seize on issues tied to COVID and culture in the classroom. What it means for schools and education, ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, after Biden`s big pledge to fight climate change, NBC sits down for an exclusive interview with Special Presidential Climate Envoy, John Kerry on the sidelines of the UN Climate Summit in Glasgow.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 After Tuesday`s night`s election disaster for Democrats, most everyone in the party agrees that they`re not in a good place right now. And they need to do something to avoid something worse next year and beyond.\par \par \uc2 But there are serious disagreements about what exactly that something is. But for right now, Democratic leaders in Washington are just simply trying to hit the gas on President Biden`s Build Back Better agenda, which President Biden told reporters last night, should have been passed before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether it would have made a difference in this week`s elections, on that score he wasn`t so sure. Still, a source familiar with the matter tells NBC News that Speaker Pelosi told House Democrats this morning that she is hopeful they will see votes on the President`s two-track agenda in the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what she told my NBC colleague, Garrett Haake when asked about the political consequences of the stalled agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: What effect do you think having not passed these bills had on Tuesday`s election results?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI, (D-CA): Well, I -- let me say it a different way. I think that getting the job done, producing results for the American people is always very positive.\par \par \uc2 I do think if the when -- as the American people learn more about what we are doing in this legislation for families, for children, for women in the workforce to save our planet at best, it will be very positive. You can`t deny that it would be very positive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But stop us if you`ve heard this one before. The next steps are going to be a dance of adding and subtracting things from the bill to assuage key members and to maneuver around a legislative roadblock in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But just because there`s more urgency doesn`t mean there are fewer landmines for Democrats, in fact, far from it.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, Democrats want something -- anything to get passed on both of these tracks as soon as possible to turn the page from this week`s elections, which they`re still trying to wrap their own arms around. NBC News can now project that Democrats will hold on in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 But according to The Cook Political Reports House expert, David Wasserman, if Republicans replicate nationally in House races, the on-the-ground success they had locally, really both in New Jersey and Virginia, they would wipe out more than 40 Democratic House seats next year and as many as the mid-50s.\par \par \uc2 So, where do Democrats go from here? In the long term in this conversation we will try and tackle in-depth later this hour because the party faces challenges with messaging, tactics, and yes, demographics to name a few.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with the latest on where the party goes now, like meaning today. So joining us is our Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker, and our Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, let me start with you. And let`s focus on this acceleration of trying to get something past. What role is the White House playing? Or is there -- are they basically, right now, waiting to see what Pelosi can pull off?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think that the President is trying to twist some arms behind the scenes while letting House Speaker Nancy Pelosi take the lead on this, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 He`s here at the White House. He has no events on his public schedule at this point in time that we are aware of. No plans to either go to Capitol Hill or to have lawmakers come here.\par \par \uc2 And so, the action is really on Capitol Hill. And I think what you are seeing from this President, from this White House is an attempt to try to ramp up the pressure on Congress to get something passed.\par \par \uc2 You saw that yesterday when I pressed him about the election results. And I asked him what if any responsibility he bears for what happened in Virginia and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Those losses, that very close race in New Jersey, and he essentially deflected any blame and instead said, look, I think it underscores the need to get the legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that that`s the strategy to try to use those losses to energize Congress to act but there is a lot of finger-pointing going on, Chuck, behind the scenes and also soul searching.\par \par \uc2 Where did they go from here? I think one of the challenges the administration would acknowledge is that the public doesn`t understand all of the individual pieces that they are trying to pass, pieces which "quite well."\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But they would acknowledge that a lot of the American public doesn`t exactly know what it is that is going to be encompassed in this final version of the Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 And so, I wouldn`t be surprised, based on my conversations, if you saw this President increase his outreach, both in terms of going out on the road and trying to explain this to people, I think there`s going to be a ramped up pressure to do that.\par \par \uc2 But I spoke with one official here today when I was trying to get a sense of the mood behind the scenes. And this person said, look, it`s divided. There are some people who are quite glum, others putting their head down and focusing on the work, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Kristen, yesterday, it seemed -- yesterday morning, it seemed the White House was prepared to say, hey, look, Terry McAuliffe didn`t run a good race and that`s why Virginia went south.\par \par \uc2 And then the results from New Jersey got confirmed, and it seems like I`ve noticed they`ve pulled back on their finger-pointing at McAuliffe a bit because they know it`s harder for them to explain away their environment. I take it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I think that`s exactly right. It`s much more difficult to explain the environment and what we witnessed in New Jersey. And that`s why I think you saw President Biden pivot very quickly yesterday from talking about the race in Virginia to the need to get his legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, and Ali`s going to delve into this more deeply, there`s a divide on Capitol Hill about the message that voters were actually sending.\par \par \uc2 Do they want these big pieces of legislation or do they want something smaller, something more piecemeal?\par \par \uc2 That`s part of the debate that`s unfolding right now and what might hold up passage of this legislation if it does encounter more hurdles along the way, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, Kristen, you did set up Ali very well here. And Ali, I do want to shift the conversation towards your side of Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 But I want to begin with playing Joe Manchin`s reaction on paid leave being put back in to Build Back Better, which of course, the President just a week ago had said was not going to be in it, and then, Speaker Pelosi`s response to it. Here it is for viewers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN BERMAN, ANCHOR, CNN: Family leave, OK. The Democrats in the House are putting it back in the bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2 , (D-WV): Yes.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: That changed your view on it at all?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: John, I don`t think it belongs in the bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI, (D-CA): With all the respect in the world for the point of view he represents, I disagree. I think that this is appropriate for this legislation. It fits very comfortably with childcare, health care, home care, family medical leave, and it has the full support of our Caucus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Ali, this is where I`m confused by the strategy now of House Democrats. A month ago, there was no way they were going to pass anything. Vote on anything that they thought would get stripped out in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Now, it appears her strategy is whatever it takes to get this bill passed and make the Senate deal with it. Is that the change now in strategy in trying to get this passed out of the House?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: It certainly seems like it. And Democrats privately will admit that there are several things that are in their version of this Build Back Better bill that are messaging points for them, but that likely will get stripped out of the Senate bill. Paid leave is a really prime example of that.\par \par \uc2 I was the person who broke the news yesterday to Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , that Speaker Pelosi and Democrats had put it back in the bill and what he said to me was that it was very challenging for him to see it in there. And that House Democrats were aware of those parameters.\par \par \uc2 Manchin has been consistent on this on things like paid leave, as well as the point that he doesn`t want to rush this. So, while House Democrats are at a point, and this is talking to Moderates, as well as Progressives, the mood of the Caucus at this point is that they would just like to move on this.\par \par \uc2 That is not where Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is. He`s been fine with taking a little bit more time to get this right. In fact, those are the words that he uses repeatedly when we asked him about each new twist and turn in these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 I think the interesting thing here, though, is what Pelosi is saying publicly versus privately because publicly in that news conference that she gave in just the last hour or so, she wasn`t saying when the House was going to vote.\par \par \uc2 But privately meeting with her Caucus this morning, she did say that she could see a world in which they voted on the Build Back Better social infrastructure bill this evening and then moved on the Infrastructure bill on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Voting on that first certainly ensures that Progressives continue to say --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: -- that they were able to have these bills linked. But make no mistake, Chuck, once they move on the Bipartisan Infrastructure bill that Bill has done. It can go to the President`s desk.\par \par \uc2 But the reality of the social spending package is that that`s going to go to the Senate where a bunch of things are going to be stripped out of it, it`s going to have to go through a vote-a-Rama, bunch of amendments are going to get attached to it, it`s going to come back to the House looking very, very different.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: And so, the entire idea that this was on a two-track process the whole time has sort of fallen apart or will fall apart as soon as we start seeing the House vote on either of these two bills.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and that`s what`s interesting, and it sounds like Progressives are OK now with this strategy. It was a strategy they would not accept a month ago.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Kristen Welker, Ali Vitali, thank you both for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 So, let me turn now to the man that is tasked with electing and re-electing Democrats to the House of Representatives next year, it`s New York Congressman, and DCCC Chairman, Sean Patrick Maloney. Congressman Maloney, I appreciate you coming up.\par \par \uc2 REP. SEAN PATRICK MALONEY, (D-NY): My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, let me just start with what you`re taking away from Tuesday night first before we get into the details there. Which is, on one hand, you could look at Virginia and you could -- we could -- you could put Virginia in a box if you wanted to.\par \par \uc2 But then when you see what happened in New Jersey, and I think about the fact -- you represent the Hudson Valley, and my guess is what is -- you would see here or see similar sentiments in your district that we saw in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 But do you see it that way? And what did particularly New Jersey tell you about the current environment?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: What it tells me is that people are frustrated and they want -- they want us to fix the problems that are plaguing our economy, plaguing our public health. They want the economy to grow, the pandemic to be over. We have a plan to do that and we are this close to putting it in place.\par \par \uc2 The other side, of course, has a ploy to exploit these frustrations for political gain. What we`re doing is hard, Chuck, and no one should be confused about that.\par \par \uc2 We`re doing it with very, very little margin to spare in terms of the majorities on the Hill and we`re tackling the most important problems.\par \par \uc2 You know, the cost that families incur in their health care, lowering prescription drugs, helping them take care of their kids with the child tax cut, huge middle-class tax cut but also with cheaper childcare, also, with home care for aging parents.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re making the most important investments in our infrastructure since the Interstate Highway System. And we`re also tackling the climate crisis with massive investments in the technologies that will create jobs for the future.\par \par \uc2 These are hard things. And we`re not done yet. But the plan is a real plan. And we`re unfinished, but if we`re close. And I just -- I just hope people understand that this is going to -- this is going to affect the frustrations that the other side is seeking to exploit.\par \par \uc2 And that`s where we are. We`re not -- we`re not finished yet but we`re darn close to putting this plan in place.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this, you know, history`s -- there`s obviously a lot less than in history. 1993, 2005, 2009, 2017, and it were all off-year elections where the party out of power did really well and it did -- and it didn`t seem like you could stop that train for the following midterm election.\par \par \uc2 And indeed, those ones that I -- that I outline, they`re all seem to foreshadow an actual party switch either in the House or the Senate. How do you essentially buck history here?\par \par \uc2 Right, you`re dealing with it looks like we see a shocking off-year result. How do you present -- prevent it?\par \par \uc2 Because here`s the cycle of doom that you got to avoid, your own side sees it -- sees, oh, my God, this is going to be terrible. Maybe I won`t run again.\par \par \uc2 Their side suddenly has more candidates wanting to file for elections that they never thought. How do you prevent good from getting better for them and bad for getting worse for you?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, the way to buck history is to make history. And we`re about to make history in terms of creating jobs, developing the technologies in the industries of the future, preparing our country and our workforce, help lowering costs for our families, we`re going to make history, I think that`s fair to say.\par \par \uc2 With the scope and the scale of these -- of this plan that people will feel right away in their family budget, in ways that matter to the working and middle-class families, like the one I grew up in.\par \par \uc2 And there is no plan except to grow this economy and this pandemic and make sure people feel it. We have to tell them what we`ve done. We have to explain to them the nature of this plan because not everything will happen right away, but we need to tell them we`ve done it.\par \par \uc2 We need to remind them that the other side is not behaving responsibly, is fighting every -- fighting us every step of the way in our efforts to grow the economy and the pandemic, because that`s a ploy to get back to power. And that`s how you`re going to defeat that typical pattern.\par \par \uc2 But let`s also put Tuesday in context. I think -- I think it`s fair to say you`d agree that by any measure, it was not as bad as what President Obama faced after his first year in office. It was not as bad as what President Trump faced after his first year in office. It was not as bad as what President Clinton faced after his first year in office.\par \par \uc2 And so, the truth is, it was a split decision on Virginia and New Jersey and we`re taking it seriously and we have things to learn.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no predicting what`s going to happen in 2022 until we put this full plan in place, we create millions of jobs, grow this economy, and the pandemic, and make sure people know what we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me, I`m not going to get into specific members who may retire and may not retire. I`m not doing the name thing, because I know, no, you`re not going to want to address that.\par \par \uc2 But what is -- tell me your pitch to a member of Congress who comes to you and says, look, I`m thinking about not running and you need them to run. Tell me your pitch to convince them to run.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Right. Well, I`ll tell you what, it`s actually quite easy. And the reason is because the stakes are enormous right now. And those of us who lived through January 6 and really had to stare in the face the vision of violence and anger that the other side is offering, I think we understand that there`s much more riding on this than who wins the next election.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about the future of American democracy, we`re talking about whether we can find our way through these current troubles to a future that is more prosperous and more inclusive than we have ever known, and we have real opponents in that.\par \par \uc2 And we have people who are playing a game to try to win power, we have people who are willing to traffic in dangerous conspiracy theories that would only prolong the pandemic and endanger us all.\par \par \uc2 And so the stakes are high. And so my pitch to future candidates is, your country needs you and I can`t guarantee you success but that`s what makes the work important.\par \par \uc2 My mom used to say everything worth doing is hard. And this work is worth doing right now. And yes, it`s hard. You`re seeing that play out.\par \par \uc2 But my goodness, if you care about your country, if you care about the future for your kids, now`s the time to fight for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting you brought up January 6, there`s a debate going on among Democratic strategists about how effective is Trump at getting Democrats out? One could argue talking about Trump seem to get out the Trump voters out.\par \par \uc2 Look, where are you on this? I mean, you know, it did work in Northern Virginia, didn`t seem to work in other parts of Virginia. Is there -- is there Trump fatigue in the messaging or do you still think there`s a -- there`s a way to use it?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I think there`s no substitute for having a record of results. We have to do the work that people care about. Grow this economy and the pandemic, have a real plan for the future. But you bet, the contrast is important too.\par \par \uc2 So, my answer to your question is it`s very important to know that Kevin McCarthy and the House Republicans have just put themselves in service of Donald Trump`s ego to the point where they won`t cooperate with finding the facts about the insurrection in the attack in the Capitol, where they`ll be -- where they`ll spread conspiracy theories or bad information about the pandemic, spread the big lie about who won the election, those things matter in the contrast.\par \par \uc2 So -- but it`s not sufficient. It`s important to understand how irresponsible the House Republicans have become.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, that`s very different with a candidate like Glenn Youngkin, who can -- who can start from a -- from a -- from a clean point and be all things to all people.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy has made this decision long ago. But what I`m telling you is that I don`t confuse it with the -- with the primary job of having a record of results. And we`re proud of that record. We`re going to -- we`re going to fix these tough problems that the other side seeks to exploit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Sean Patrick Maloney, Democrat, like I said, representing part of the Hudson Valley, from New York, DCCC Chair, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective, sir.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, so what is the right path forward for Democrats after Tuesday`s election night setback? Obviously depends on who you ask as you just heard a little bit there.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to talk to top Democratic strategists about if Democrats can turn things around in time and how they could do it.\par \par \uc2 And later, Dr. Fauci and the CDC Director Rochelle Walensky testified before the Senate today as the Biden administration sets its vaccination deadline for the private sector for early January. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I was talking to Terry to congratulate him today. He got 600,000 more votes than any Democrat ever has gotten. We brought out every Democrat about there was more votes than ever had been cast for a Democratic incumbent. I`m not an incumbent Democrat running for governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden pointing out harsh reality for Democrats from Tuesday`s elections. Terry McAuliffe scored record votes in the Virginia gubernatorial election. He also ran up big margins and Virginia`s largest count.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats still came up short. Thanks to gains in Republican turnout across the board, but particularly in rural areas.\par \par \uc2 So, what does that mean for Democrats? Means that their messaging problems with rural voters and white men pose a major political challenge ahead of the midterms because if you write off both of those constituencies, then you got to win somewhere between 65 and 70 percent of everyone else available.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone who knows a little bit about democratic messaging, former White House Communications Director, and Hillary Clinton Campaign Advisor, Jennifer Palmieri. Jen, it is nice to see you. And I know you`re in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Oh, let me begin with something James Carville said yesterday. And I want to get your reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Ron was just stupid wokeness, right? Don`t just look at Virginia in New Jersey, look at Long Island, look at Buffalo, Look at Minneapolis. Even look at Seattle, Washington.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just defund the police lunacy, this takes Abraham Lincoln`s name off of schools. I mean that -- people see that. And it`s just really a have a suppressive effect all across the country.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, some of these people need to go to a Woke Detox Center or something. I mean, their expression language that people just don`t use, and there`s a backlash and a frustration at that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: First of all, Jen, I love the fact that Carville said all that on PBS, right? That`s a -- he`s a hot -- he`s a hot voice, right, for our friends at "News Hour" there.\par \par \uc2 But this is not the first time James has said something like this. What do you make of that issue because it does seem to have a problem with a certain section of voters that you should have in your column?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER PALMIERI, FORMER OBAMA WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Honestly, Chuck, it is wrong. I mean, it`s wrong to continue to obsess about this.\par \par \uc2 The cultural issues, you know, things like CRT, those things take hold in - - with voters in the face of inaction when there is dysfunction, right?\par \par \uc2 And that is when people can fall prey to, you know, like, sort of race- based arguments like that. And, you know, that was why I think, you know, that`s among the reasons why it`s really important that Democrats not just get something done, right?\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk about the bill and to see how to get something. But you have to make sure that people understand the connection in their lives.\par \par \uc2 I`m in Asbury Park, New Jersey, you know, where Phil Murphy ought to win here. And when I talked to him about it, he thinks that the backstop -- because you know, I mean, historically, these are -- these elections are really hard. And the midterms are going to be really hard.\par \par \uc2 But there were things that you can do that make a difference at the margins and that is why Phil Murphy is going to be (INAUDIBLE) New Jersey next year.\par \par \uc2 And he said, you know, he passed pre-K here. They do have paid leave in New Jersey. They did pass an Infrastructure bill. They did do things on child care. So, a lot of the stuff that`s in the Build Back Better Bill, they have in New Jersey and he feels like that was the backstop for him.\par \par \uc2 So, does it mean that Democrats are going to have great midterms if they pass Build Back Better and do a good job of selling it? No, not necessarily but there are things that you can do to affect these historic trends.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s just true that `21 was not as bad as mine. I`m impressed, just wasn`t. For (INAUDIBLE) New Jersey governor lost in 2009. And the Democrat in Virginia in 2009, lost by 17 points. And Terry had a very good race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I understand that --\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: But that cultural stuff --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: -- as that what James was talking about? That -- when that takes hold with voters and frustrates voters on either side, it`s because you`re not getting your job done, right?\par \par \uc2 The fundamental job in terms of how I think Democrats deal with Critical Race Theory when it comes up as issues in the midterm say, hey, you know, parents should be partners in schools, just as they always have.\par \par \uc2 But my job, my job is to make sure that you`re -- that you`re -- that we be infrastructure, so that your kids have a great school to go to so that it`s wired for the best kind of learning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: My job is to make sure that there is pre-K so that your kid can go to school, right? And if you deliver on these things, I think that the other stuff (AUDIO GAP) will, just doesn`t have the hole that it could otherwise.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How about the tactical issue that -- there`s a debate among Democratic strategists about how much time do you spend in areas that you`re not going to do well in? Like we`re all in for the Democrat, that`s rural America, for Republicans to be urban America?\par \par \uc2 Is it worth shaving your margins by three to five points in those areas -- it takes a lot of work, right? You got to show up, takes time away from there.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at what Youngkin pulled off, it really has made the democratic math that much harder. If rural -- if the rural midterm turnout looks like a presidential turnout, you guys got to win 70 percent of everything else that remains. That`s pretty -- that`s a pretty tall task.\par \par \uc2 But I understand the time issue. How much time do you spend in rural America? How would you divide it?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: You have to spend time in rural America. I really think, Jim -- I really, really believe Democrats have to do that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, first of all, look at the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and look how from in 2010, when we had 60 seats -- Democrats had 60 seats, and represented state, you know, had Democratic Senators and states like Montana, and Missouri, and North Dakota that we don`t have today that you need so big.\par \par \uc2 You have to do it not just to win world states but everything is going to be at the margins come November 2022. And you can`t write off any population in your state.\par \par \uc2 Also, it`s a bad way to leave. It fosters depression, it fosters more dysfunction and pulls the nation if you`re not talking to everyone.\par \par \uc2 So yes, it`s a lot of work. But you know, by then -- and I know that Murphy`s team did try to do everywhere in New Jersey, and they did -- they did work a lot on an early vote and they worked a lot on the vote by mail.\par \par \uc2 And you know, they just barely you know, they -- he may win with like a -- you know, a few points in the end when everything`s counted.\par \par \uc2 But like it is, you know, he`s his lesson. -- there`s a good lesson here in New Jersey about how hard it`s going to be but also what you can do to affect the outcome.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jennifer Palmieri in Asbury Park, say hello and salute Asbury Park, I guess if I get it, by getting Bruce`s stuff right which I don`t you will give me grief about. Anyway --\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: No, you did it, your grief.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ms. Palmieri, thanks for coming.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Pleading to Asbury Park (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, pleading for Asbury Park. That was the word I was missing.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, well, greetings from Washington, D.C. Thank you, Jen. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And a quick programming note. Be sure to tune in to the latest episode of our streaming show, "Meet The Press Reports" as our magazine show where "Meet The Midterms" and a deeper dive into this week`s election results, what they mean for next November. Streaming at 9 p.m. Eastern on NBC News Now tonight, available on-demand starting tomorrow on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one of the biggest storylines from this week is how voters took their frustrations with education to the ballot box. Of course, education, kind of an umbrella for a lot of different issues, how COVID and the curriculum shaped these races, and what it means for schools across the country.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. One of the lessons from this week`s election is that schools are becoming increasingly a political battleground. Our NBC News exit poll of Virginia voters showed that the issue of education, whatever that meant to the respondent, was the second most important issue just behind the economy. Obviously that ended up helping Glenn Youngkin who made schools a focus of his campaigns, with parents rights, things like that, issues what kids are learning in schools.\par \par \uc2 The most powerful outside the state, too, new political action committee focused on electing conservative candidates to school boards. They won three-fourths of 58 races they targeted in seven states. Now this politicizing of schools has caught the attention of Arne Duncan who served as education secretary in the Obama administration, was also essentially a school superintendent in Chicago. He and Phil Bryant, a former governor, Republican governor of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 They just put out -- outlined five ways that they believe they can put aside partisan politics, try to take partisanship out of education.\par \par \uc2 Arne Duncan, it`s good to see you. Let me put up your five -- I want to note the five sort of bullet points. Set national standards, fund early education, renew our approach to some infrastructure investment in schools that are badly needed of new technology, things like that. Foster accountability. And then the fifth one, keep politics out of the classroom.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with 5 first. I know you don`t, but I will. Because it`s how do you even define what politics is, right, when it comes to the classroom? You know, I think back to my own AP government teacher I thought was terrific, and he encouraged us to register to vote when we turned 18. And I knew parents who thought that was bringing politics into the classroom. So, like, how do you even define what the word "politics" in the classroom means to a parent?\par \par \uc2 ARNE DUNCAN, FORMER SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: Well, that`s a great question. That`s a hard question, Chuck. And it`s the right question to start with. I think if I listened to you earlier, Jen Palmieri is a good friend, I think she really nailed it. If we`re delivering on some of these other things, then the political tension, the wedge issues, the political divisiveness drops. So if we have access to high quality or early childhood education, which I think is the best investment we can make.\par \par \uc2 If we have strong national standards so that young people when they graduate from high school can go to college and not have to take remedial classes. We help to rebuild our schools and add air conditioner, heating, or whatever it might be. If we have real accountability, the goal can`t just be greater investment, the goal has to be better outcomes, better results, higher graduation rates, less dropout rates.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:07]\par \par \uc2 And I believe if we do those things, and very importantly, Chuck, do those things together, for me and Governor Bryant, a strong conservative from Mississippi, to come together and if we have these principles, I think it`s pretty significant. And I think if we do those four -- those first four things well, that fifth one, I don`t want to say it becomes easy, but it becomes much easier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I look at it, the fact that there was a political action committee who decided to do for school board candidates what`s normally reserved for state legislative candidates, congressional candidates, and it`s pretty one sided, right? There wasn`t an equivalent yet on the left but there will be now. That feels like a spiral that`s troubling because if we know the partisan identity of every school board member in America, how does that keep politics out of the classroom?\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Yes, it`s hard, and being a school board members, one of the most thankless jobs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Just hard work, stress, pressure, I always (INAUDIBLE) public schools we had a school board meeting every month where you spent all day listening to the public and it could be wearing and tiresome and sometimes stressful but you actually got some great ideas from parents. You heard some things you needed to hear. So whatever you do to get more folks running for school boards across the political spectrum that`s a really healthy thing.\par \par \uc2 Look at, for example, President Biden`s education agenda. He`s talking about making universal access to pre-K a reality, so that our babies aren`t starting kindergarten a year, 18 months behind, which happens in far too many communities across the country. And talking about a high school diploma is not enough, making free community college a reality. So it`s moving from a K to 12 system which we`ve had for the past hundred years to pre-K to 14 system.\par \par \uc2 It`s a historic investment, you know, with just a very, very different vision of what education has to be today. That`s the right vision. That`s not a Republican vision or Democratic vision. It`s the right vision. And what`s so interesting to me, who made free community college a reality? A Republican. Governor Haslam of Tennessee. He`s a good friend, one of the most important part of his legacy, what he`s most proud of.\par \par \uc2 So let`s support more candidates, having more people vote, run, I think that`s healthy. It might be stressful, it might be tough, but it`s healthy. Let`s think about what`s our education vision. What do we want for our children? We know today if you drop out of high school you cannot get a good job in a (INAUDIBLE) economy. If you just have a high school diploma, that`s not enough in most communities. So starting earlier, going beyond high school, that has to be something that we all, across the political spectrum, rally behind.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you are still a school superintendent and you`re dealing with a group of parents that don`t like how race is being taught in the classroom, and I want to put it that way, I don`t want to use the whole moniker of what people want to describe it, how would you go about getting buy in on everybody? How would you go about sort of defusing that situation?\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Chuck, you know better than I that trust is so low these days. And it`s really a sad thing. The only way you rebuild trust is to listen. So whatever the issue is, whether it`s around race, whether it`s around cleanliness of buildings, whether it`s around graduation rates, there`s a million issues that parents are rightly concerned about, you can`t spend enough time as a school superintendent, as a principal, as a teacher, listening to parents, hearing them, bringing them in and making them part of the process.\par \par \uc2 I think, and I always advise this to superintendents now, too often they try and present everything as polished and done and everything is rosy. I want parents to see our struggles. I want them to see, you know, the tough decisions we`re making and the issues we`re grappling with, and that we`re not going to make every, you know, decision perfectly. And here`s what we`re trying to think about and most importantly here are our values.\par \par \uc2 And when you do that, when you`re less worried about the polish and more worried about authentic, trusting relationships, over time, you might not agree with everything with parents, they may not agree with you, but you build that relationship that is so critically important to helping everybody move forward and move forward together.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So to sum up, don`t dictate the curriculum, have a public meeting outlining the new curriculum and get buy-in from the public?\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: On everything. Again, that`s just one issue, Chuck. Talking about being transparent. What are your high school graduation rates? What are your drop-out rates? What are your college going rates? What percentage of your kindergartners are ready for school when they start, not starting behind? Be just absolutely transparent on every issue. On every issue. And here`s our goals and here`s how we`re going to put it together.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That sounds like also respect the fact that these people showed up even if you don`t agree with what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Have to. Have to. You want parents buy-in. You want parents engaged.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: You want engaged parents. That`s a good thing. That`s a healthy thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, every child with an engaged parent usually has a better outcome, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Arne Duncan, longtime former education secretary, former Chicago schools superintendent, really appreciate you coming on sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Thanks so much, Chuck. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, we have some new developments in the fight against the coronavirus and the pandemic, as young kids ages 5 to 11 are now getting the shots from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 And a new COVID-19 pill gets approved for the first time overseas.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Let`s turn to the latest coronavirus facts that we believe you should know today. Dr. Fauci and the CDC director Rochelle Walensky has been testifying on the Hill today about the future of the COVID-19 response telling the Senate Health Committee that controlling the pandemic long term will depend heavily on vaccination rates both here and abroad. Their testimony came hours after the president released details on the administration`s vaccine mandates for private employers.\par \par \uc2 Companies with 100 employees or more will have to ensure their workforce is fully vaccinated by January 4th. Unvaccinated workers who opt out of the requirement will have to get tested every week and wear a mask in the workplace. The new rules come from OSHA and cover 84 million workers nationwide according to the White House.\par \par \uc2 And in news overseas on the pandemic, the U.K. just became the first country to approve Merck`s COVID-19 pill. This antiviral is the first authorized remedy of its kind, kind of like the flu, Theraflu there that we use. It`s an oral treatment that COVID patients can take from home. Early data showed taking four pills twice a day for five days cut the rate of deaths and hospitalizations in half when given to the unvaccinated high- risk adults with mild to moderate COVID-19. Merck has not publicly released data on the potential side effects but an FDA advisory committee will evaluate the pill`s safety for use in the U.S. at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 And here at home, COVID is surging in Colorado where hospitals are now rationing care. The governor warned residents this week that the Delta variant has made going out to eat and shopping in Colorado a bit more dangerous than ever before. Nearly 80 percent of eligible Coloradoans are at least partially vaccinated. But according to state health officials it`s the remaining one million unvaccinated folks that are the ones getting sick and crowding the hospitals.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 Nationally, though, there are still hopeful signs the pandemic has turned a corner especially as the vaccine rollouts for tens of millions of children between the ages of 5 and 11. Thousands got their first kids-size shots yesterday. And luckily they didn`t have to do it alone. From D.C. to Texas comfort dogs were seen working hard to keep kids calm as they rolled up their sleeves.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an exclusive one-on-one with the U.S. climate envoy John Kerry as President Biden makes some ambitious promises in Glasgow.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, President Joe Biden might be back stateside after his time at the United Nations Climate Summit in Glasgow, but there are a lot of other people there. Biden of course joined other leaders in committing to significantly reducing methane production, unveiled a new EPA regulation to accomplish that goal. The summit is still ongoing. Biden is still dealing with climate concerns at home as negotiations continue around climate infrastructure and a Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 Our own Josh Lederman, though, sat down with U.S. climate envoy John Kerry in Glasgow earlier today. He joins me now from Scotland.\par \par \uc2 So, everything you read, Josh, that John Kerry is this one-man whirlwind rep there for the United States, what did you learn?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, you know, before the summit here in COP26, Chuck, Kerry, the climate envoy, said that failure to pass significant climate legislation would be like President Trump pulling out of Paris for a second time.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 So he`s kind of eating his words a little bit now, trying to downplay that given that we don`t yet have climate legislation, saying it`s not affecting President Biden`s credibility and that everyone here in Glasgow understands that the U.S. is back in the game and serious. But I also asked in our interview about the results from the election in Virginia. You know, 2024 not that far away. What happens if a Republican wins the White House? Could all of the work he`s been done, you know, be swept away by a Republican president? Here`s what he said about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KERRY, U.S. CLIMATE ENVOY: Because of those trillions of dollars of commitment, because Ford and GM have made the decision that by 2035 they`re not going to produce internal combustion engine cars. They`re going to only do electric. Those kinds of decisions can`t be undone by a politician. Those kinds of decisions are so locked in, and three years from now, 3 1/2 years, if there were to be the election of somebody from the other party, let me tell you something. Their supporters are going to be as committed to this as anybody else.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: So, you know, we`ll see. I mean, that might be a little bit of a rosy prediction. But on the other hand, John Kerry also said he thinks that Republican voters are actually shifting on this issue. This is what he said is the reason why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KERRY: I believe lots of Republicans around the country understand what`s happening. Ask farmers in Iowa and Minnesota, and Oklahoma. Ask people who have been flooded out or heated out. Look at the numbers of people that died in Texas during the winter. You know, this is not some made-up political thing. This has nothing to do with politics. It has nothing to do with ideology or shouldn`t. This is about mathematics and physics. That`s what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: And Chuck, he also said there are more than 50 members of Congress who are expected to come right here to Glasgow for the second week of the COP26 conference next week. Of course the vast majority of those are Democrats. So another thing to remember, if the timing of this legislation, this vote in the Congress, slips to next week, you`re going to have a lot of Democrats who have to make the choice between staying back so that they can actually pass that bill or coming to the summit they`ve all wanted to come for.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Josh, I`m curious, it does seem that one of the challenges that I`ve noticed out of this conference, and it sort of fits in with an interview I had with the prime minister of New Zealand earlier this week, is that every country seems to -- well, if somebody else doesn`t do enough, then -- everybody wants to sort of pull back a bit. It feels like everybody wants to make all the right pledges but is nervous about putting their money where their pledges are. Is that turning into a problem at this conference or not?\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: I think so. I think there`s been a lot of lost trust over the last many years, as countries have made these pledges, particularly these ones that are decades out, you know, oh, we`re going to go net zero by 2050, 2060, 2070, but not actually doing anything now. And the other diversion that we`re seeing here is the fact that the developing countries and the richer countries like the U.S. still not on the same page.\par \par \uc2 Those poorer countries wanting the wealthier countries to actually pay for them to deal with climate change, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Josh Lederman in Glasgow for us, Josh, thank you. Terrific interview there with John Kerry as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an honest mistake? That is what the Pentagon probe has concluded over the botched drone strike that killed seven children and three adults in Afghanistan as a response to the terrorist attack at the Bagram -- at the HKI airport. More details after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:56:54]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The review into the U.S. drone strike that killed innocent civilians including seven children in Kabul, Afghanistan earlier this year has concluded that no illegal action occurred in the lead up to the strike and recommended no actions on those in fault.\par \par \uc2 NBC national security reporter Dan De Luce joins me now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 OK, so then what went wrong? If they call it an honest mistake, never mind the word -- why you put the word "honest" there, but was it bad intelligence, bad targeting, or what?\par \par \uc2 DAN DE LUCE, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: All of the above. They`re saying that there was basically what they call confirmation bias which is seeing what you want to see, maybe not what you should have been more skeptical about. So they had eminent threats that ISIS was going to attack again. There had been a terrible attack on the airport that killed 13 service members.\par \par \uc2 They see this white Toyota Corolla, which happens to be the most common vehicle in Afghanistan, but they think this is suspicious, look how this vehicle is moving. And they start following it. And this person was stopped somewhere, had a back computer bag that seemed to be suspicious, moving possibly explosives into the car. Of course now we know it was an aid worker, it was all benign, the water cans were not explosive, because they were water cans.\par \par \uc2 And the review just basically concluded the intentions were good, there was time pressure, there were communication breakdowns, but no violation of laws of war. And it begs the question, you know, was there negligence to the point where it was a violation of war?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and is there something missing in the system? I mean, what happens, when they make a decision to target somebody with a drone, is there any sort of, OK, let me check one more time, let`s do this, let`s do that? Let`s -- is there something missing in the system?\par \par \uc2 DE LUCE: I think that`s a good question because in the past they said that they have checks, that they have ways of vetting before they carry out a strike, and they have elaborate procedures to avoid civilian casualties. But clearly something went terribly wrong. And it`s not the first time. And so I think this is going to come under more scrutiny in Congress. And you`ve had human rights groups saying for a long time that there`s something wrong in the process, that it`s just somehow too easy to drop a bomb or fire a missile without proper checks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This gets to whether there`s any morality to a drone strike. Right? I mean, what we`re getting at is, can you ever get to a point where you can create a system that can make a drone strike not fail-safe but 99 percent fail-safe?\par \par \uc2 DE LUCE: This shows it`s not possible to be fail-safe. You always take risks. And then the question is, should there be more accountability if there is a terrible mistake. And there won`t be a court-martial in this case. There could very likely be disciplinary measures.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How many people would be involved in that chain of command there? Do we know? Did they release that information?\par \par \uc2 DE LUCE: They did not. It`s quite a number. And one of the problems was sharing information among all the different actors, intelligence agencies send people at different locations. That was also a problem. But considering how many years that we`ve been carrying out strikes, you would have hoped for a better result.\par \par \uc2 [14:00:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this feels like an incomplete investigation.\par \par \uc2 DE LUCE: That`s what a lot of people are saying.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DE LUCE: They say it was very thorough. But you have Adam Schiff saying I didn`t see enough here, that this isn`t enough accountability.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This feels like congressional review is necessary.\par \par \uc2 Dan De Luce, I`m running late on time, I appreciate the report.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. MSNBC`s coverage continues with my friend Chris Jansing right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211105ehb400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Hears Arguments on Texas Abortion Law; World Leaders Gather for U.N. Climate Summit; 22,000 Plus New York City Workers Still Unvaccinated by Deadline, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Pete Williams, Tali Farhadian Weinstein, Kathy  Park, Kelly O`Donnell, Heidi Przybyla, Mark Murray \par \uc2 8,395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday, it`s the final day of campaigning in Virginia before tomorrow`s election. The governor`s race is a dead heat and that signals big trouble for Democrats in Virginia and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden`s standing takes a major hit in our new NBC News poll as Democrats are still searching for a final deal on his legislative agenda. And Biden`s global leadership is in the spotlight in Europe.\par \par \uc2 And later, we`re following a historic day at the Supreme Court where justices today heard arguments over Texas`s controversial, near-total ban on abortion, with the entire hearing broadcast live.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Just a few days ago, President Biden huddled with Democrats on Capitol Hill behind closed doors warning them that this week couldn`t make or break his presidency. Boy, it`s looking like he might be right.\par \par \uc2 With less than 24 hours until Election Day, Democrats are bracing for a potential upset in the Virginia governor`s race. It is a dead heat in a state Democrats won by 10 points in the last presidential contest and by nine points in the last governor`s race. And what happens in Virginia doesn`t typically stay in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 The governor`s race there has been a remarkably good indicator for the party in power, meaning what Democrats do well in Virginia, they`ve historically done well nationally in the following year`s midterms, and when they`ve struggled in Virginia, they`ve struggled nationally. And right now, they are struggling everywhere.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s approval ratings have fallen to 42 percent in our new NBC News poll, that`s 12 points underwater, which is a big decline from where it was just a few months ago. Simply put, the public is souring on the promise of the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 Fewer than three in 10 adults give him good marks on his ability to unite the country. That`s fallen 12 points this year. Same goes for voters who see him as knowledgeable and experienced to handle the presidency, that`s fallen by 10 points. The public is also less confident in his ability to handle a crisis.\par \par \uc2 To put things in perspective for Biden, his overall favorability weight ratings are well underwater and just about the same as Donald Trump`s right now.\par \par \uc2 Yes, really. It is a remarkable stat considering Biden ran his campaign on protecting democracy from what he said was the existential threat of Trump.\par \par \uc2 But right now the country as a whole doesn`t view either of them very favorably. There is no sugarcoating it for Democrats. It is ugly out there.\par \par \uc2 And joining us to break it all down, now-Senior White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell is in Edinburgh, Scotland after the first full day of the UN Climate Summit there, Mark Murray is here to break down the latest NBC poll numbers for us, Heidi Przybyla is in Virginia Beach previewing tomorrow`s election, and I`m also joined by the Richmond Times-Dispatch`s Jeff Shapiro.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly, with all of that said, and the President`s agenda still stalled back here at home, his numbers underwater, what`s the White House feeling on the situation that the President is staring at right now? He said he doesn`t worry about polls. His White House does not have that luxury.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, that is true. And the President is trying to focus on specifics that he can try to accomplish here to project confidence in what he is doing on the world stage, knowing that that may be able to have an impact at home, not in the immediate 24 hours, but over time and trying to deliver on the promises he made as a candidate.\par \par \uc2 The President saying that polls go up and go down, he was asked specifically about some of the polling as reflected in the numbers you ran through that are, in fact, very damaging to the President and challenging for his immediate political future, a snapshot in time, but certainly, a dire and dismal picture that many Americans were questioned in this poll suggests about his performance so far.\par \par \uc2 And the President says that he is not running on his poll numbers, that he is here (AUDIO GAP) at would candidate part of that is delivering on some of the promises that are in the framework that is still sort of cooking back in Washington, that the President says he hopes will be passed by the end of the week with his infrastructure and as he describes it, the Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 And then, here, in Scotland and just coming from Italy, where he had the G20, there have been a number of achievable things that he has been able to do, making some significant steps forward with the EU on some economic things, the global minimum tax, pulling back on some of the tariffs that have an impact at on the home economy, taking steps on the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Those are things that are nitty-gritty in the economy, international in importance, and of course, the climate discussion is still going.\par \par \uc2 So, the President is sort of living in two worlds right now, the domestic political environment and the world stage. And trying to focus on what`s in front of him here while certainly the White House and the staff who remained back in the U.S. is dealing with that more dire picture of domestic politics back home, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kelly O`Donnell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, Heidi, the President could return home later in this week with deliverables from this climate summit, he could even see Congress vote on his two big bills later this week but none of that`s going to happen in time to help Terry McAuliffe.\par \par \uc2 The polling now shows Glenn Youngkin, with all the momentum in Virginia, what are you hearing from the voters you`re talking to in Virginia Beach?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI PRZYBYLA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, I came here to Virginia Beach because it is a quintessential swing area. Biden won this by several points. Before that, Trump won by several points.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, if Youngkin`s going to win, this is where we`re going to feel it. And being here, it`s hard not to feel the cross -- the heavy crosswinds that are being faced by Terry McAuliffe.\par \par \uc2 Now, we spoke with a number of voters at a couple of different diners, I`m outside Anchor Allie`s here, where Youngkin had a breakfast event earlier, and to a person, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 All of these voters, the Republicans and there were much more of them willing to talk than the Democrats wanted to talk about the issue of education, wanted to talk about their belief that this critical race theory notion is being taught in schools and that they`re very upset about it. Take a listen to a couple who we talked to earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENNIS DORSMAN, VIRGINIA VOTER: They`re trying to teach our children that they should feel bad about it. You are privileged, you have an upper leg, you are better. You think you`re better and you should feel something else.\par \par \uc2 BRENDA DORSMAN, VIRGINIA VOTER: That`s given them the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS DORSMAN: Splitting everything.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI PRZYBYLA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Is there any possibility that that is not happening in schools, and you`re just being told that it is?\par \par \uc2 BRENDA DORSMAN: Well, I`m not in school with them. You know what I mean? I`m not there daily and I don`t know what the kids are coming home and tell to their parents.\par \par \uc2 You know, I can`t really speak for that. I believe that is happening. Let`s put it that way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: Now, Garrett, Democrats are all saying that this is all just one big made-up dog whistle. And what I -- pressed many of these individuals for specifics, none of them was able to tell me specifically what the curriculum is being taught that is critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 There was one woman who said that her grandchild came home and asked her if she was bad because she`s white. That was the only sense of evidence and that`s not evidence of curriculum being taught because we don`t know what prompted that.\par \par \uc2 Garrett, I cover the 2017 gubernatorial election, and that was really marked by such fervor and enthusiasm on the left of suburban women waiting in long lines out in the rain to vote for Democrats in order to cast a referendum vote against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And here, there really is all about -- it really is all about the enthusiasm of Republicans and Democrats who are concerned about some of their base groups just not turning out, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, I remember the same thing being out in the rain in Fairfax County watching folks line up to cast their first opportunity to vote against Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, to me, the Trump factor in this is fascinating because, you know, the Youngkin message about education is either a dog whistle or a Rorschach test or an effective message we`ll see tomorrow night when the polls close.\par \par \uc2 But for Terry McAuliffe, he has tried really hard to make Glenn Youngkin an extension of Donald Trump. A Republican source of mine in Virginia said that`s not going to work. Democrats in Virginia are exhausted by fighting Trump because they`ve been on the front lines of it since 2017.\par \par \uc2 How do you think that McAuliffe -- you know Youngkin-Trump connecting strategy is playing in that state?\par \par \uc2 JEFF SCHAPIRO, POLITICS COLUMNIST, RICHMOND TIMES-DISPATCH: Well, keep in mind that Youngkin has not forsaken Trump politics.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No.\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: He`s wrapped them up in his fleece vest and his khaki vest. Now, as Terry McAuliffe is fond of saying, and it`s a legitimate issue, what is the connection here? What can Virginians expect?\par \par \uc2 One wonders if part of the problem, with that message for Democrats, is the messenger. Anyone who knows Terry McAuliffe knows he has all the subtlety of a carnival barker and maybe those high-decibel rants just fall on deaf ears.\par \par \uc2 That said, in Virginia, a very Different Virginia than the one in which Republicans won last a stay -- statewide office in 2009 is a place in which there are structural advantages for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: And these are advantages on which Democrats now hang their hopes that there are more voters who identify as Democrats, that the suburbs are more reliably if not reflexively democratic.\par \par \uc2 We have 45 days of uninterrupted, unrestricted early voting. The numbers out of those democratic jurisdictions have been impressive.\par \par \uc2 However, one cannot say with any certainty, whether those are all or disproportionately Democratic votes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So Mark, this is a state that Biden won by 10. As we said, the governor`s race was not ultimately closed in 2017. The fact that we are talking about this race at all a day before the polls close, how concerning should it be for Democrats nationally, and those in the Old Dominion especially?\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, Garrett, there are two forces really at play here. One is the energy that was Heidi was just talking about. That Republicans seem to be, have like all the energy behind them.\par \par \uc2 Our own national NBC News poll shows that nationally -- just not in Virginia, but nationally, Republicans have an 11 point interest and enthusiasm advantage when it looks going into the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 So, the energies on the Republican side, the math is on the Democrat side, and how those two kinds of forces play out.\par \par \uc2 The McAuliffe campaign is still banking on, that there are 450,000 more Biden voters in Virginia than there are Trump voters.\par \par \uc2 And they don`t necessarily believe that there are a lot of Ralph Northam- Joe Biden voting. Glenn Youngkin voters that are out there and so, they just have to turn their people out.\par \par \uc2 And it really starts becoming a game on, can you knock on enough doors to be able to get those folks out.\par \par \uc2 But right now, if they end up losing, you have to look back that energy, just nine months into the Biden presidency, a year after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Jeff, to that point, what do you make of how the McAuliffe campaign has used President Biden and use their big surrogates?\par \par \uc2 We didn`t see Joe Biden go to Richmond or to Virginia Beach or to any of the swinger parts of the state, we saw him very narrowly focused up here, just across the Potomac in Northern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 SCHAPIRO: Well, you know, it`s entirely possible McAuliffe could have run from Joe Biden, he didn`t. And of course, they use him in the bluest areas of the state.\par \par \uc2 But you know, we`ve had one boldface name after another through Virginia. Barack Obama, Stacey Abrams, the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t know that it`s possible in contemporary politics, even in the upper South Mid-Atlantic States like Virginia, hard by Washington, DC, to put up with anything but increasingly nationalized state politics.\par \par \uc2 To Youngkin`s credit, he has tried to tailor this to Virginia and a lot of this has a cultural bent. What`s going on in the schools?\par \par \uc2 This notion of critical race theory, that somehow the public schools are hotbeds of, you know, transgender, depravity, the situation in Loudoun County just outside of DC, which is bid at full boil and has really captured the imagination, particularly of conservative media.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Mark, in the time we`ve got left, you frame this as looking a lot like 2014, where Mark Warner scraped by with a winless than a point.\par \par \uc2 If this ends up being a lot like 2014 and Terry McAuliffe narrowly wins this race tomorrow night, what should Democrats take away from it?\par \par \uc2 Is there anything they can do to control this environment and improve their chances going into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: So, Garrett, one, they can say a win is a win, and actually who is in control of Governor`s mansions? Who is an Attorney General? Who`s the Lieutenant Governor? All matters in our politics. And who --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`ve all seen --\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: -- Wait, who controls the House of Delegates. So, who wins is who ends up winning. But also, you know, I think if you`re -- if you`re a Democratic strategist, if you`re the Biden White House, you want our poll that just came out on Sunday to be rock bottom.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The low point.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: That is the absolute low point, get beyond what`s playing out on Capitol Hill. Really smart Democrats are telling me this whole reconciliation debate is happening.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s a communication error or not, is it`s the process of trying to throw so much into it, makes it almost impossible to communicate.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you`re a Democrat, you want it in this thing now to basically -- then start talking about the message you want to communicate into the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s very easy to see how this gets a lot worse for Democrats or potentially a lot better. McAuliffe wins, they start passing things out of the House. All of a sudden their gears are turning.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Anyway we got to leave it there for now. Mark Murray, thank you. Jeff and Heidi and Kelly O`Donnell, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 And be sure to tune into our Meet the Press election night special tomorrow night hosted by Chuck and by Kristen Welker starting at 9 p.m. Eastern, streaming on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, much more ahead on the road ahead for Democrats, I`ll speak with one top House Democrat close to leadership about the state of the Biden agenda on Capitol Hill and beyond.\par \par \uc2 And later, New York City`s vaccine mandate for city workers is now in effect. What this could mean for safety and sanitation around the city, as workers are placed on unpaid leave until they get the jab? You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As President Biden talks climate in Scotland and as Democrats sweated out in Virginia, Congress is still hard at work in Washington finalizing a major piece of President Biden`s domestic agenda.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell NBC News that negotiations on the reconciliation package will extend beyond tomorrow as prescription drug pricing and immigration among other programs are being evaluated for inclusion.\par \par \uc2 And they`ve still not gotten the formal thumbs up from Joe Manchin. Democrats are hopeful that they`ll get a win on these issues but they`ve not given a firm indication of when that win could come.\par \par \uc2 A lot of Democrats are looking at the President`s sinking poll numbers now and wanting that win yesterday. Joining me now is Democratic Congressman from Michigan, Debbie Dingell.\par \par \uc2 So, Congressman, give me a sense of the kind of the hold up here on this bill. What`s standing between the House and a vote on either the Build Back Better bill or the Infrastructure bill tonight?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): Well, first of all, let`s just be honest, and there`s a reality of writing legislative language. So, discussions have been going on a weekend, it is going to take time for that language to be finished, and then many members have expressed wanting 72 hours to look at the language.\par \par \uc2 I am hopeful. I think most are hopeful that we will be able to do something by the end of the week. But I think it`s more important to do it right. I do believe the time is here to land this plane and I`m -- think we will land the plane this week.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Every member I talked to tells me there`s some other issue that they`re interested in that they still want to see included. How open do you think negotiations still are on prescription drugs or immigration or paid family leave? Like, how finalized are we looking at in terms of a framework becoming a bill?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: You know, here`s the reality, the President laid out his framework, there are other issues we all care deeply about.\par \par \uc2 When these bills are passed, we`ll get up the next day and start working on a lot of them. And quite frankly, some of the discussions like unpaid family leave have probably given it more impetus.\par \par \uc2 The time is now, we`ve got -- we`ve discussed it`s important that everybody have input, it`s important -- but we`ve reached agreement on a tremendous number of things.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be making major changes in everything from fixing our roads and bridges, which by the way, equal third world countries, we`re going to get the land out of Piper and build EP in chargers.\par \par \uc2 And I would like to say I`m sick and tired of everybody in the media saying we haven`t gotten stuff down in the global climate.\par \par \uc2 When I stood with the President and he made a historic announcement at the White House, in August, that we`ll reduce existing emissions from automobiles by 60 percent by the year 2030.\par \par \uc2 There`s lots of things that have been done. I`m tired of the media being negative on everything. We have accomplished a lot, we`re going to get a lot done and we got to start talking about what we`re getting done.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, look, if you get the multi-trillion-dollar package across the finish line, there is a lot of doing in that package. So, I think your concerns might be addressed if and when the President`s pen hits those two bills.\par \par \uc2 I`m really interested in what has changed over the weekend here in terms of Progressive support for a vote this week. You know, we left Friday, Pramila Jayapal was still saying we want to wait and see, you know, a signal from the two Senators who`ve been kind of holding up a lot of this process.\par \par \uc2 That single didn`t come over the weekend but you guys did have a long meeting yesterday. I know that Congresswoman Jayapal met with Kyrsten Sinema, late last week before she left.\par \par \uc2 Do you know anything about how this has become unstuck among progressives? You all see ready to go.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So, I think that -- I hate these labels, too, by the way, Garrett. I`m a member of Progressive Caucus (INAUDIBLE)--\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know you do. But the Progressive Caucus specifically here is an actual block of votes, right? So, how did things become unstuck among that block?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I think that there has been a great deal of concern that if one bill went with the other bill, get concert.\par \par \uc2 I think the President was clear when he spoke to the Caucus on Thursday that we were going to get both.\par \par \uc2 He has -- had numerous conversations, has told people he`s going to deliver both of these. There have been many one-on-one conversations by many members with Senators.\par \par \uc2 And the times come to get this done. We need to make sure we`re getting both of these bills done. There`s a tremendous thing in it.\par \par \uc2 People have to have more confidence in speaking to people -- Joe Manchin`s never going to publicly give us word that this is -- anybody who knows Joe Manchin should know that that`s not going to happen. But there`s a lot of confidence that he -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But what did we know -- what changed between Friday and today if we didn`t -- if we knew that about Joe Manchin --\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Well, I think what you -- Joe Manchin was never going to do 3.5. It wasn`t clear where it was some 1.75. He signed that piece of paper.\par \par \uc2 But I think there`s been enough indications that the Senate understands as much as the House does. We had to get this down for the American people.\par \par \uc2 President Biden campaigned on this, there`s so many things that we need to get in this so that we get our economy back on the road again, and that people are finding that trust between each other that, you know what, Congress means coming together, getting it done, and our job is to get it done and the time to get it done is now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Congressman, you`ve always -- Congresswoman, you`ve always had a pretty keen sense, I think of how Democrats communicate and how you think they should communicate.\par \par \uc2 Our new poll has 71 percent of people thinking that the country is on the wrong track, rather than on the right direction in this country. How do you explain a number that bad? And what should Democrats do to address it?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So, first of all, you know me and polls. I was the one that predicted Donald Trump would win when you all tell me I was crazy. So, a poll is a poll.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I remember. I didn`t say crazy.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: A lot of your colleagues did. But having said that, I think people are sick of COVID, they`re frustrated, they`re tired of the chaos, they`re tired and it`s a reflection of people`s fatigue and that, when they actually see us start to get stuff done, we -- most people, if you look at your poll what I did, people don`t even know what`s in the Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: We got to talk -- we got to bring the supply chain back to this country. There are a lot of things supply chain resiliency is in this bill. That`s going to help lower prices, lower the inflation cost.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of things that when -- women can get back to work because we`re actually doing something about childcare.\par \par \uc2 We -- that there are a number of things in here, they`re going to help the economy, they`re going to make a difference in everybody`s lives, every single day.\par \par \uc2 Then, people are going to start to feel, OK, we can get our job done we, can come together and get this done. And we got to talk about it. What we have to do is talk about the specifics of this bill, both bills.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, getting these bills to the President`s desk, then telling people what you have done for them seems to be the Debbie Dingell way out of this polling problem. Congressman, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up, a historic hearing at the Supreme Court as the justices hear oral arguments over Texas`s near-total abortion ban, and the public hears the arguments live many for the first time ever. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. It`s been a historic day at the Supreme Court where the Court is hearing two challenges to Texas`s restrictive new Abortion Law.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to the historic nature of the substance of the arguments, we are also, for the first time, hearing those arguments live from inside the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 In the first challenge of the Texas Law which bans nearly all abortions, the lawyer representing Texas abortion clinics focuses not on restrictions to procedures but on the aspect of the law that allows private citizens to suit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: Texas delegated enforcement to literally any person anywhere except its own state officials. The only conceivable reason for doing so was to evade Federal Court review under Ex Parte Young.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Meanwhile, Texas solicitor general argue that federal courts were not the right venue to argue this case in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDD E. STONE, TEXAS SOLICITOR GENERAL: An injunction against SB-8, the law itself, they can`t receive that because federal courts don`t issue injunctions against laws but against officials enforcing laws. No Texas executive official enforces SB-8 either, and so no Texas executive official may be enjoined.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The Justice Department meanwhile is arguing that the Texas law violates a woman`s constitutional right to an abortion. The court`s not expected to issue a decision on either case until the spring. Until then Texas law will remain in effect. And the court is also scheduled to take up a challenge to the landmark Roe v. Wade case in December.\par \par \uc2 We`ll break down today`s arguments and their consequences in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. You`re about to be looking live at the Supreme Court after justices heard arguments challenging Texas` restrictive new abortion law. Arguments we all heard as they happened. There she is.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Pete Williams who covers the Supreme Court and the Justice Department for NBC News. Also with me is Katie Benner, Justice Department reporter for the "New York Times" and NBC News contributor. And we also have Tali Farhadian Weinstein, a former New York state and federal prosecutor who clerked for former Supreme Court Justice Sandra Day O`Connor. She`s now an NBC News legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Pete, let`s start with this argument that this law would lead to a deluge of lawsuits. Justice Sotomayor asked a question about this, and we can actually play it thanks to the new rules here. So let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: You believe that the way this SB-8 is structured that what the chilling effect is the very multiplicity of lawsuits that are threatened against you?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 HEARRON: Yes, your honor. That`s exactly right. It is the fact -- there`s a combination of various ways that this state has created special roles applicable only to SB-8 to make state courts a tool that can be used to nullify constitutional rights that have been recognized by this court.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So much of this seemed to be about the ways in which this law could be applied elsewhere. The structure of it. You know, I think for people who were tuning in and hearing the Supreme Court arguments for the first time, myself among them, dealing with the ways in which this could be applied elsewhere, walk me through what we heard there and what we understand from the argument?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I`d say bottom line this was not a good day for Texas. It seemed to me that a majority of the justices including some of the conservatives are willing to say that a lawsuit filed by abortion providers in Texas and federal court can go ahead. I`m not sure that they`ll say the same thing about the federal case, but, you know, for abortion providers in Texas, either one would do the job for them.\par \par \uc2 The chilling effect thing that you`re hearing from Justice Sotomayor is the argument made by Mark Hearron representing the \uc2 Center for Constitutional Rights\uc2  on behalf of the abortion providers, they say, you know, the prospect of getting sued and having to fork over $10,000 and being repeatedly sued in every county in Texas is just too daunting. That`s the chilling effect, and that`s what she said is different.\par \par \uc2 But the point you made about some of the justices being concerned that other states could replicate this to try to limit other constitutional rights, one of the most powerful Friend of Court Briefs in this case was filed by a gun rights supporters in Texas who said, boy, you know, they sided with the abortion providers. And this is a point that \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  picked up on saying, you know, some other state could say OK, you can sue for a million dollars, anybody that has an AR-15.\par \par \uc2 So I think the Supreme Court is simply not going to buy the structure that Texas has tried to come up with here very cleverly nonetheless to try to evade court challenge.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Katie, is the structure of the Texas law too cute by half here at the end of the day? I mean, so much of the Texas law was designed to take the onus off of Texas officials and spread out responsibility for enforcement. Is that where Texas is going to get into trouble?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: Yes, that seemed to be the -- you know, the feeling of justices. Everybody from \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett to Elena Kagan and Sotomayor. They were saying, listen, this really just doesn`t work. What it does is it allows any state to come up with a law to thwart constitutional rights. They`re currently extended to citizens but that state just doesn`t like, and especially justices like Kavanaugh of course in that gun example can see how this could be used by other states to restrict access to services or to goods or to activities that the state has decided is not appropriate.\par \par \uc2 That is probably the best hope that, you know, people who support the idea of abortion who want access to abortion in the state of Texas have in thwarting this law.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Tali, there was another moment that stuck out where \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  asked Stone about what would be considered an injury under SB-8. This is important. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: What would that injury be in this -- under SB-8 if it`s an injury, in fact?\par \par \uc2 STONE: One example could be akin to the injury suffered in the tort of outrage where an individual becomes aware of an uncompiled abortion and they suffer the sort of same extreme emotional harm, that would ground an Article 3 injury for purposes of Texas law.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That seemed like sort of a weak argument, did it not? I mean, how would you go about proving this kind of extreme moral or psychological harm to yourself as a third party to all of this?\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN, NBC NEWS ANALYST: It was weak, and it was also shocking because what they were exploring was, theoretically, well, who could come to federal court? Because Texas`s position throughout was that the abortion providers could not bring the suit that they brought against state judges and against state clerks in federal court. And that also the United States could not sue Texas in federal court as it has.\par \par \uc2 And this was a kind of strange digression into what would count as an injury which is generally a requirement to have standing to be able to bring a case. And it didn`t really get much pickup. It didn`t get any momentum for Texas. And I agree with Pete that Texas was really on its back foot, particularly by the time we got to the second argument. The argument in the case that the United States had an initiated.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. Katie, let`s talk about that a little bit. The Justice Department`s argument here. How is this different from what we heard this morning?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: You know, the --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We may have lost Katie. So I`ll give the question to Pete instead.\par \par \uc2 Pete, the Justice Department`s argument is totally different here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, it`s very similar in the sense that they both say we ought to be -- people ought to be able to file these lawsuits. We ought to be -- there are two sort of sticking points here.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 One is for the federal government, when you are suing a state, does it have to meet some higher threshold?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think there may be problems there. The second problem for both lawsuits is OK, Texas is right. Texas officials don`t do anything to enforce this law. So who do you sue? And what the plaintiffs in these cases have said is OK, we`ll sue state court judges and state court clerks who are the ones that have to do something to allow these lawsuits to go forward. It did seem to me that a majority of the court is not going to go there on judges but they may be willing to go there on clerks because of previous Supreme Court decisions that suggest that you can`t sue state court judges. Now the plaintiffs are both saying we need to be creative here because this is a sneaky Texas law.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So let`s think outside the box here a little bit.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: For all of those new Supreme Court watchers today, we all heard about Ex Parte Young a lot. Can you explain that, please?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, Ex Parte Young basically says that if a state -- this is the whole thing about you don`t enjoin a law. You enjoin state officials.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Who are enforcing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And so normally speaking the federal government can`t enjoin state court officials. But what Ex Parte Young says is well, if they`re doing something that`s against the law, then the legal fiction is they`re not carrying out an official responsibility, so, yes, you can sue them. But it also seems to say you can`t sue judges. So the question is, is there a little wiggle room?\par \par \uc2 And I should point out one other thing here. I thought one of the most interesting things in the argument was where the justices said now, what are you asking us to do here? Because I`m not exactly sure what happens if -- it seemed pretty likely the Supreme Court is going to say the abortion provider lawsuit can go forward. Does that mean SB-8 gets to put on hold? And you heard Mark Hearron from the \uc2 Center for Constitutional Rights\uc2  say, well, I`m not sure we have that request but let me just make it now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Fascinating. Tali, I`m curious what your take is on the fact that we all heard this live today, and whether the way in which the court is allowing people to see how its work gets done might change anything about either the process or, in your mind, how we laymen all understand it.\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: You know, I -- it`s interesting. This really was unprecedented. I had missed hearing Supreme Court arguments. It used to be you had to go down to D.C., stand in line and be physically present to hear an argument. And there had always been a concern that if you opened up the courtroom, it might make the justices conduct themselves differently to really ham it up for the camera, if you will.\par \par \uc2 I did not see that at all, or in this case, just for the audio, of course. What I saw was very serious Supreme Court argument that was incredibly beneficial to the public because while these were cases ultimately about the right to have an abortion in Texas and how that constitutional right has been curtailed, we barely ever heard about abortion. I think the words Roe v. Wade were hardly spoken. And so it was really a window and a massive education, I think, to see that that`s really not what`s at stake here.\par \par \uc2 You know, the -- both of the petitioners had gone in alongside with Friends of the Court with the argument that this isn`t just about abortion. No matter what you think about abortion, it`s really destabilizing to the country and an affront to the Supreme Court`s authority if states can create these kinds of regimes that insulate an unconstitutional law from judicial review, and we really got to see over the course of two hours that it was really all about the details of that scheme that they had created which could be air lifted and imposed onto another area of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I thought it was fascinating even if I have to admit I understood much less of it than I would like.\par \par \uc2 Tali and Pete, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden`s credibility tested on the world stage as he tries to convince world leaders that the U.S. can lead on climate even though Congress hasn`t yet signed off on his climate agenda back in Washington.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:50]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In an age where this pandemic is made so painfully clear that no nation can wall itself off from borderless threats, we know that none of us can escape the worst that is yet to come if we fail to seize this moment. But ladies and gentlemen, within the growing catastrophe, I believe there`s an incredible opportunity. Not just for the United States, but for all of us. We`re standing in an inflection point in world history. We have the ability to invest in ourselves and build an equitable clean energy future.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. That was President Biden with a message of urgency and opportunity as he joined other world leaders at the U.N. Climate Conference in Glasgow, Scotland this morning. But Biden did not come with his own climate legislation to show off as negotiations continue back here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 For more on what we can expect out of this summit, I`m joined by Leah Stokes, professor of climate policy at the University of California Santa Barbara.\par \par \uc2 Professor, I`m just curious. What do you think would be a successful summit here for the world and for the United States when the president lands back in the U.S. later this week?\par \par \uc2 LEAH STOKES, PROFESSOR OF CLIMATE POLICY, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA SANTA BARBARA: Well, you know, countries have gathered in Glasgow to try to make serious progress on the climate challenge. We are behind on cutting carbon pollution. Countries around the world really need to step up and put forward plans that commit to limiting warming to 1.5 degrees.\par \par \uc2 And so are we going to see countries cooperating and putting forward their own national plans for how they`re going to do it? I think that that`s what everybody is really looking for out of this summit.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How much does it undercut the possible success of this summit that China is just not participating?\par \par \uc2 STOKES: Well, I think that`s quite disappointing and President Biden certainly commented on that in recent days. You know, China is currently the number one emitter globally. The United States is of course the largest emitter historically and still the number two in the world in terms of carbon pollution. So both of those countries are absolutely critical. And during the Obama administration, they were able to do pretty good bilateral negotiations between China and the United States.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve got to hopefully see that again start to develop even if it doesn`t happen at this particular summit.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Obviously the president was disappointed to not have his climate legislation signed into law by the time he got to this conference. As you look at what`s in the framework now, what might be the legislation that ultimately gets to his desk here on climate, are you satisfied at what you see potentially becoming law, the menu of tax credits and so forth that have survived this long process to maybe be in the final bill?\par \par \uc2 STOKES: Well, I think that the climate movement has worked very hard to get lawmakers in Congress and the Biden administration to act boldly on the climate crisis. It`s clear that Americans understand that the climate crisis is happening now. There were $22 billion weather disasters last year that cost the country almost half a trillion dollars. And so these investments from Congress are sorely needed and very popular.\par \par \uc2 Now they`re both the biggest climate investment we will ever have seen, and insufficient to tackle the crisis. So this is going to be a really critical down payment on the taking on the climate crisis and we`re going to need to see Congress and the White House continue to press on climate action.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Continuing to press has been a problem. I mean, the Paris climate accords in 2015, we`re not -- we have not been holding up our end of the bargain there. I mean, I find myself feeling a little bit cynical as we report on this sort of thing because there`s big commitments made and then the follow-through tends to lack. What should we be looking for to make Glasgow feel like an accomplishment, not almost like a dirty word, the Paris climate accords, those things we didn`t do?\par \par \uc2 STOKES: Well, as much as the president has not managed to get the Build Back Better Act onto his desk yet, he`s very close. Last week he went to Congress and he said, here`s the framework for moving forward. And thankfully the Congressional Progressive Caucus has taken that up and said, you know what, if we can get agreement on this from the Senate we are willing to move forward on this this week.\par \par \uc2 So we`re really right on the precipice right now of very transformative climate investments. The fact is that climate is in some ways the biggest part of the Build Back Better Act, and that`s amazing because it didn`t start out that way. So it`s clear that both Congress and the White House understand the climate crisis and know they have to act on it. And I think that that is going to influence the world stage at Glasgow and hopefully we`ll see countries really stepping up to start taking on this crisis at the scale that`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: To that end, how can the United States, how can our allies at this conference put pressure on a Russia or a China to say, hey, this is your problem, too?\par \par \uc2 STOKES: Well, the way that I think about this is actually a lot to do with technology and innovation. If the United States invests in these new technologies like wind and solar and batteries, they`re going to become cheaper. And that`s also going to create millions of good-paying jobs in the United States through all these manufacturing industries.\par \par \uc2 As those technologies get cheaper and the United States becomes a global leader in manufacturing, those technologies can be sold to countries around the world, shared with countries around the world. And that will help everybody have the affordable technology that we need to really cut carbon pollution. So yes, international negotiations are really important. But domestic action that starts to invest in bringing down the cost of clean energy technology is absolutely critical.\par \par \uc2 And that is what Congress and the White House have really worked hard to try to deliver through President Biden`s Build Back Better Act.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Professor Leah Stokes, thank you. We`ll be watching the rest of it closely.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new concerns in New York as vaccine mandate for city workers goes into effect, leaving thousands of unvaccinated essential workers facing unpaid leave.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:30]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. New York City`s vaccine mandate is officially in effect for the entire city workforce. And according to the latest numbers, more than 22,000 employees are still unvaccinated. The mayor says 9,000 of them are on unpaid leave starting today. And the city`s first responders are some of the least vaccinated departments. One-fifth of the entire FDNY still hasn`t gotten at least one shot by the city`s Friday deadline.\par \par \uc2 Now union heads are sounding the alarm about what staffing shortages could mean for quickly putting out fires or for saving lives.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with the latest on the ground in New York City is NBC`s Kathy Park.\par \par \uc2 So, Kathy, I`m curious we got more than 20,000 unvaccinated, 9,000 not showing up for work today. What`s the effect on staffing, firehouses, trash collection, which can very quickly become a problem in the city?\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Garrett, yes, so union leaders for days now have been warning of the potential staffing crisis come Monday when this vaccine mandate officially takes hold. But the mayor has assured New Yorkers that things are moving ahead without any sort of issues. They`ve always had contingencies in place, so they had asked for mandatory overtime and extra shifts to fill in any sort of gaps.\par \par \uc2 This morning he said that there aren`t any problems that they`re noticing. And he also touted the vaccination numbers. Over the weekend thousands of more people got that first dose of the vaccine. And across the board, across city agencies, we saw the vaccination numbers go up. But as you mentioned, there is certainly resistance. Some holdouts, especially among first responders, specifically among firefighters. And they are continuing to push back. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO, PRESIDENT, FDNY FIREFIGHTERS ASSOCIATION: The haphazard way this was thrown together in a nine-day deadline. Members are being told if they get vaccinated they can just show up at the door of the firehouse at 9:00 in the morning and they`ll be permitted to work if they have a vaccination card. You know, this is not a way to run a public service, a lifesaving -- you know, a lifesaving public service in such a haphazard way.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARK: And Garrett, we should point out that ever since Friday`s 5:00 p.m. deadline, more than 2,000 firefighters have called in sick. And the FDNY commissioner has blasted some firefighters who apparently called in sick out of protest. But union leaders have said, look, you know, a lot of them got their COVID-19 vaccine and the aftereffects are flu-like symptoms so they were legitimately sick. So there are still a lot of back and forth and a lot of resistance among firefighters, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 [14:00:07]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kathy Park, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And a quick programming reminder, be sure to tune in to our "MEET THE PRESS" election night special tomorrow night hosted by Chuck and Kristen Welker at 9:00 Eastern streaming on NBC News Now. MSNBC coverage continues with Chris Jansing live in Virginia right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211101ehb100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 21, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali, Kristen Welker, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 9,462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Dr. King said, of all the forms of inequity, injustice in healthcare is the most shocking and most inhumane.\par \par \uc2 This is a once-in-a-century pandemic that hits this country hard and especially the African-American community. It`s like you`ve all lost someone to the virus or know someone who has lost a loved one. One in 600, black Americans have died from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 That`s been reported, the black children more than twice as likely as white children who lost a parent or a caregiver to COVID-19, to have to experience the trauma and loss.\par \par \uc2 Many of my colleagues in the Congress are working on, we have to now work on even more fervently, and that is mental health care. Helping people through the difficult periods we had. It`s been devastating but we can find purpose in pain, we can find purpose in this pain.\par \par \uc2 Equity is the center of my administration`s COVID-19 response. The vaccination rates, among black adults, is now essentially on par with white adults. In the midst of this pandemic, we`re building the Affordable Care Act to extend coverage to lower health care costs for millions of black families.\par \par \uc2 We`re also working on low prescription drug costs by giving Medicare the power to negotiate lower prices. And how do you know the plan to work? Because if drug companies are spending millions of bucks to try to stop it, that`s how you know.\par \par \uc2 Together, making health care right, not a privilege in this nation. Now for the millions, the millions of you who feel financially squeezed in raising a child while caring for an aging parent, a so-called sandwich generation, want to make eldercare affordable and accessible, so your aging loved ones can live with greater independence and dignity.\par \par \uc2 We also want to make sure childcare costs for most families are cut at least in half. No working family if we -- if we get what you all helped me get done. No working family in America will pay more than 7 percent of their income on childcare for any child under five.\par \par \uc2 Want to give raises to millions of care workers at home workers so they can increase their capacity, increase their knowledge, increase their opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Health workers and childcare workers are disproportionately women. Women of color, and immigrants, and workers, like the ones Dr. King stood for when he marched and gave his life.\par \par \uc2 Look, folks, just imagine, instead of consigning millions of our children to under-resourced schools, we gave every single child in America access to education at age three, and age four, quality preschool. We can afford to do this. We can`t afford not to do it.\par \par \uc2 And we do know no matter what the background or circumstance the child comes from, when given that opportunity, they have a better than 58 percent chance to make it all the way through 12 years without getting themselves in trouble and maybe gone beyond that. This will change lives forever.\par \par \uc2 So, historic investments in higher education significantly impede -- increasing Pell Grants to help millions of black students in lower-income families attend community colleges and four years schools.\par \par \uc2 I tell you, let me be clear, in the shadow of the Morehouse men, I hear a lot about that, guys, I will, Howard, I look. I keep making the case of excuse that point of personal privilege we stand in Senate. The best HBCU in the country is Delaware State. That`s where I got started. Come on.\par \par \uc2 But here`s what we`ve done, in addition, putting the President of Delaware State who used to work for me as a doctor, in charge of all of this, we`re committed to nearly $5 billion this year in historic investments, with more historically black colleges and universities to make every single student. Give them a shot to get good-paying jobs. You all know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 But for anybody watching this, one of the problems is black students in college just have every single capability any other student does. And guess what? Because they don`t have great endowments, they can`t compete for those government contracts that are out there, that the big schools are able to go out and get.\par \par \uc2 Cyber security, for example, starting salaries $100,000, $125,000, but you don`t get to get that contract unless you have laboratories, unless you have the facilities you can be in fact trained on.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We also know this about the promise of American. Economic injustice also means delivering environmental justice to communities, on fenceline communities, dividing homes and toxic areas.\par \par \uc2 My state is one of the highest cancer rates in history in America because I lived in a fenceline community called Claymont, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 We used to get up in the morning, not a joke, and I get driven to the law school and went up the street, turned on the windshield wiper in the fall and the fall of the first frost, and literally there`ll be an oil slick in the window. Not a joke, an oil slick in the window.\par \par \uc2 So, I awful lot of us, including me have bronchial asthma. It means reducing pollutions so our children can develop and avoid these consequences.\par \par \uc2 Every one of you have an alley in your state, we call it cancer alley in our state going down Route-30. Look, it means building up our resilience to the climate crisis for the next extreme weather events. And these have been of biblical proportions -- biblical proportions.\par \par \uc2 178 mile top winds in a hurricane down Louisiana, more people dying in Queens in their basements because 20 inches of rain, they flooded and couldn`t get out of the bases, they drowned. Superstorms, droughts, wildfire, hurricanes, this is the promise for America, urban and rural and all across America, not just for any one area.\par \par \uc2 And as we fight for economic just to fulfill the promise America -- for all Americans, the work continues on delivering equal justice under the law.\par \par \uc2 Look, I know the frustration we all feel, that more than one year after George Floyd`s murder, and the conviction of his murder about six months ago, meaningful police reform in George`s name is still not passed Congress.\par \par \uc2 I remember many times meeting with his little daughter, and she said to me, my daddy`s changing history is going to change history. But we haven`t fulfilled that yet.\par \par \uc2 I understand you got to keep fighting but let me be clear, though. We`re going to continue to fight for real police reform legislation and the fights are not anywhere near over.\par \par \uc2 Despite Republican obstruction, my administration is active. We`ve already announced changes in the federal law enforcement policies, a ban on chock holes, restriction on no-knock warrants, requirements that federal agents wear and activate body cameras.\par \par \uc2 And then the Department of Justice used of private prisons rescinding the previous administration`s guidance to U.S. attorneys to require the harshest of penalties.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is open to pattern and practice investigation of systematic police misconduct in police departments in Phoenix, Louisville, Minneapolis, just because we can`t get it done in the States, we are not standing back. We have much more to do.\par \par \uc2 And there are these important steps my administration also wants to advance the meaningful police reform that includes additional executive actions to live up to America`s promise of equal justice under the law.\par \par \uc2 Our work continues to create a safer, and stronger communities in critical ways with my American rescue plan, and thank you in Congress for supporting it. Everybody kind of forgets that was $1.9 billion -- trillion dollars.\par \par \uc2 We got a hell of a heck of a lot done to that. That it did so well, people don`t even know where it came from. No, I`m serious. Think about it. Like what do you do for me lately, but we had $1.9 billion we took care.\par \par \uc2 Well, we made historic investments in community police in violence intervention programs and were shown to reduce -- some of these programs reduced violence by 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 We`re expanding this summer programs and job opportunities in services support to keep young people safe and out of trouble. We`re helping formerly incarcerated people successfully run on their communities, in the past you get $25 in the bucket -- and a bus ticket. You go back right into the bridge, you just were there before.\par \par \uc2 You should have access to Pell Grants, you should have access to the park - - the housing, you should have access to all the things you paid your price. And we shouldn`t put you back in a spot where you have no options.\par \par \uc2 We`re also working to stem the flow of firearms from rogue gun dealers, to curb the academic -- the epidemic of gun violence. I know why I got criticized, me and the guy that passed the assault weapons ban. I`m proud of having passed the assault weapons ban.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the deal. We heard Dr. King paraphrase Mika. He said give us the ballot and we will place judges on the benches of the South who will do justly and love mercy.\par \par \uc2 Well, just nine months, we`ve appointed more black women to the Federal Circuit courts and more former public defenders of vets than any administration in all American history, because of you. We`re going to change it.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We did in a record time and we`re just getting started because all of you in the audience here, you been the engine behind all this.\par \par \uc2 But now we also know this, to make grill of the full promise, America, we have to protect that fundamental right, the right to vote, the sacred right to vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s democracy`s threshold liberty. With it, anything is possible. Without it, nothing is. Today, the right to vote and the rule of law are under unrelenting assault from Republican governors, Attorneys General, Secretary of State, state legislators, and they`re following my predecessor, our last President. I know a deep, deep black hole one of this.\par \par \uc2 Now, I really mean and think about it. It`s what got me involved in civil rights as a kid when I was 26 years old. It gave me that I never plan -- I love reading about how Biden knew he was going to run for President. Hell, I didn`t know I was even going to be able to run for the county council, I didn`t even want to.\par \par \uc2 But look, this struggle is no longer just over who gets to vote, or making it easier for eligible people to vote, it`s about who gets to count the votes. Where they should count at all?\par \par \uc2 Jim Crow in the 21st century is now a sinister combination of voter suppression and elective some -- elections some version. My fellow Americans, I thought at one point, that I had been able to do something good as chairman of the Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 I was able to get every member of the committee, including some of the most conservative members that ever served, clearly who had racist backgrounds, to vote to extend the Voting Rights Act for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 I thought, whoa, and the proudest things I ever did as a Senator. But guess what? This means that some state legislators want to make it harder for you to vote. And if you do vote, they want to be able to tell you whether or not your vote counts. That`s not had before.\par \par \uc2 They want -- the ability to reject the final vote and ignore the will of the people. If their preferred candidate is black or white or Asian, or Latino, doesn`t matter, if that their candidate doesn`t win. And they`re targeting not just voters of color, as I said, but every voter who doesn`t vote the way they want.\par \par \uc2 I have to admit to you, I have been as a Senator, my whole 36 year-career involved in, I`ve worked with a lot of folks out here in civil rights issues. And I thought, man, you can`t turn his back, that you could defeat hate.\par \par \uc2 I thought we can actually defeat hate. But the most un-American thing that any of us can imagine, the most undemocratic and the most unpatriotic, and yet sadly, not unprecedented, time and again, we`d win the threats to the right to vote, and free and fair elections come to fruition. Each time we fought back. And we`ve got to continue to fight back today.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank \uc2 Martin Luther King III\uc2  for leading marches on voting rights during the anniversary of the march on Washington on August 28. Vice President and I and our colleagues here have spent our careers doing this work. It`s central to our administration.\par \par \uc2 On the anniversary of Bloody Sunday, I directed each and every federal agency to promote access to voting from each agency heeding that call.\par \par \uc2 For example, the Department of Veterans Affairs, I asked them to make it easier for veterans and their families to register and to vote at VA facilities, so be open. In addition, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  has doubled the voting rights, enforcement staff.\par \par \uc2 We got a long way to go through. It`s using authorities to challenge the onslaught of state laws, undermining voting rights, whether in old or new ways. To something like 20 percent of the repair -- half the Republicans are registered Republicans, I am not your President, Donald Trump is still your President. As we Catholics say, oh my God.\par \par \uc2 Hello. The focus is going to remain on discrimination and racially discriminatory laws. Georgia`s various new anti-voting laws.\par \par \uc2 And let`s be clear about Georgia. Dr. King`s home state and the home state of someone who has literally stood in his shoes as -- I think some of you guys knew that`s was -- next line was coming that`s why I had to just come on, stood in his shoes as a Morehouse man.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And so I keep getting from Cedric, oh anyway. And as a preacher in the pulpit of Ebenezer, United States Senator Raphael Warnock, the first black Senator in Georgia`s history.\par \par \uc2 Senator Warnock won his election in the battle of ideas. He earned the trust and confidence in the broad coalition of voters in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 In response of, Republicans of Georgia, what was it? It`s not to try winning on the merits and ideas, it`s by changing the rules to make it harder for people to vote, deny the franchise.\par \par \uc2 The Vice President my lean our administration`s efforts and we supported Democrats pressing to enact critical voting rights bills since day one of this administration, making sure we have unanimous support.\par \par \uc2 But each and every time the Senate Republicans blocked it by refusing even to talk about it. They`re afraid to even just debate the bills in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . As they did again yesterday, even on a bill that includes provisions that they`ve traditionally supported. It`s unfair, it`s unconscionable, it`s un-American.\par \par \uc2 This battle is far from over. The door has not been closed. John Lewis Voting Right gab will soon come up for a vote, named after our dear friend, whom we still miss dearly but whose voice we hear every day in our hearts and our conscience.\par \par \uc2 It`s a law that helped lead the reauthorization, as I said for 25 years that I served on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  expanding the Voting Rights Act, traditionally received bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 We have to keep up the fight and get it done. And I know the moment we`re in, you know, the moment we`re in. I know the stakes, you know the stakes. This is far from over.\par \par \uc2 And finally, we`re confronting the stains of what remains a deep stain in the soul of the nation, hate and white supremacy. You know, there`s a tough through-line of subjugation and slave people from our earliest days, to the reigns of radicalized terror, the KKK that Dr. King been assassinated.\par \par \uc2 And through that model -- though that line continues to be the torches emerging from dark shadows in Charlottesville, carrying out Nazi banners and chanting anti-Semitic file and \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  flags. In the violent, deadly insurrection on the Capitol, nine months ago, it was about white supremacy, in my view.\par \par \uc2 With the rise of hate crimes against Asian-Americans during the pandemic and the rise of anti-Semitism here in America and around the world, the through-line is that hate never goes away.\par \par \uc2 It never -- I thought, and all the years I`ve been involved, I thought, once we got through it, it will go away but it doesn`t. It only hides -- it only hides until some seemingly legitimate person breathes some oxygen under the rocks where they`re hiding, and gives us some breath.\par \par \uc2 I`ve said it before, and all my colleagues here know it. The court in the United States intelligence community, domestic terrorism from white supremacists is the most lethal terrorist threat in the homeland.\par \par \uc2 To that end, our administration is carrying out the first-ever comprehensive effort to tackle the threat. It`s passed by domestic -- foes by domestic terrorism, including white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 We`re doing so by taking action to reduce online radicalism and recruitment to violence. We`re also disrupting networks that inspire violence and domestic terrorists by providing resources to communities to build resilience. We cannot and must not give hate any safe harbor -- any safe harbor.\par \par \uc2 My fellow Americans, standing here remind me of the goal the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, its Dr. King lead, and I quote.\par \par \uc2 He said his goal was to "redeem the soul of America." That`s what`s at stake here. The soul of America and we know that it`s not the work of a single day, or a single administration, or even a single generation.\par \par \uc2 But here we stand with Dr. King, to show out of struggle, there`s progress. Out of despair, there`s hope. From the promise of equality and opportunity of jobs, justice, and freedom, we see black excellence, American excellence, black history as American history, and the defining source of the might of this nation.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we`re here today to renew our own courage in the shadow and a light on the shoulders of Dr. King, Coretta Scott King, and all those known and unknown who gave their whole souls to this wok.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 The courage to confront wrong and to try to do right, the courage to heal the broken places as a nation, the courage to see America whole, to acknowledge where we fall short, to devote ourselves of the perfection of the union that we love, and we must protect.\par \par \uc2 If we can summon the courage to do these things, we`ll have done our duty, honored our commitments, brought the dream of Dr. King on just a little bit closer to reality, it`s the highest of callings, it`s the most sacred of charges, and it`s what? With the help of God, we can do now.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s go forth from this sacred place. Tumid and turmoil with hope and promise of a nation always seeking, always thriving, always keeping the faith because folks, you know, I know my colleagues in the Senate used always kid me for quoting Irish poets on the floor.\par \par \uc2 They thought I did it because I was Irish. It`s not the reason, they`re just the best poets in the world. There`s a line from -- and I believe this to be true.\par \par \uc2 There`s a line from a poem, Cure of Troy, it says, once in a lifetime, that tidal wave of justice rises up and hope and history rhyme. It`s not the whole quote that won`t bore you at all, but hope in history rhyme.\par \par \uc2 I believe the American people, the vast majority are with us. I think they see much more clearly what you`ve all been fighting for. Your whole life now is in stark relief.\par \par \uc2 The bad news, we had a President who appealed to the prejudice. The good news is he took them -- he ripped the band-aid off, made it absolutely clear what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 And I think the American people will follow us. But guess what, whether they will or not, we have no choice, we have to continue to fight. God bless you all. May God protect our troops.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: And welcome to an abbreviate edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been watching the President and the Vice President speak at the 10th Anniversary of the dedication of Dr. Martin -- of the Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Memorial here in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be honest with you, I can`t believe it`s been 10 years since that Memorial feels -- still feels like one of the newest memorials and it obviously still is.\par \par \uc2 And what was interesting about the President`s remarks is how he used about half of his remarks to talk about what we`re going to talk about next, which is saving yet his agenda pass.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to turn now to the latest action on Capitol Hill and the White House. It is another day of some brutal sausage-making for Democrats here in Washington, as they try to cobble together a multi-trillion-dollar legislative package with razor-thin majorities in both chambers. The sausage-making isn`t pretty. It rarely is, and Democrats around the country are losing patience.\par \par \uc2 And then a cascade of flashing red lights for the party, from Biden`s declining approval rating to the dead heat in Virginia, to Trump`s resurgence on the Right ahead of the midterms. That frustration was pretty well summed up by Governor Gavin Newsom when I spoke with him yesterday in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): I mean, this political death march of, who`s up, who`s down, Manchin, or Sinema? I can`t take it anymore. No one can take anymore. I just -- enough, stop, just get something done, and it looks like they`re finally going to get something done.\par \par \uc2 And watch, miraculously, numbers will start going up, they actually delivered, now you`d go out and say what you did, even -- don`t even remembers what was in the original $1 trillion package and all the good infrastructure investment.\par \par \uc2 Now, they get to talk about that again, not just this $3.5 trillion reconciliation that`s over 10 years, people don`t want to know it`s 10 years, don`t even know what a trillion is, don`t know what reconciliation is, there is no -- no one`s getting a damn thing done and it`s driving them crazy. And of course, they`re going to blame who`s ever in the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That pretty much sums up how many Democrats feel these days. You can catch more of my interview, by the way, with Governor Newsom on MEET THE PRESS this Sunday, we`ll have another excerpt action later in this shows well.\par \par \uc2 Democrats still have a lot of difficult work ahead of them to actually deliver something to President Biden`s desk, something big, and to do it quickly.\par \par \uc2 But then arguably comes the hard part, selling it to the public, because they haven`t known what exactly they`re selling and that might be the biggest consequence of all the squabbling over this legislation.\par \par \uc2 Once it does get done, assuming it does, Democrats are going to have to convince the public that all of these concessions they may have read about was actually worth it.\par \par \uc2 In other words, they`re going to have to say that while they didn`t like how the sausage was made, it`s still the best damn meal you`ve ever eaten by a Democratic Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now, it might be a tough week considering all the frustrations brewing over this deal, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , apparently floated leaving the party and he confirmed that yes, if -- that he was -- might think about being an independent but he would stick caucusing with the Democrats if things got a bit overheated.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s been no secret that Manchin is worried this legislation is simply just too big. But Progressives are frustrated because they think it`s getting too small. They`re going to have to jettison a bunch of their priorities, on climate change, community college, childcare, and so on.\par \par \uc2 And whatever the price tag, whether it`s a $1.75 trillion bill, a $2 trillion bill, Democrats may have to go back to the drawing board in terms of how they`re going to pay for it because Kyrsten Sinema remains opposed to raising taxes on the wealthy and corporations according to aides familiar with her position.\par \par \uc2 And so, even if they agree on this top-line number, they still got a lot of work to do to figure out how to reinforce their pledge that there is zero cost in this legislation.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill for us, Kristen Welker is at the White House, and also is -- also with us, is Patrick Gaspard. He`s a former White House Political Director under President Obama, and he`s currently the President of the Center for American Progress. He`s going to be tasked with selling this plan among others.\par \par \uc2 But Ali, I want to start with you. I got some sources that tell -- that tell me this, Ali, that maybe they`re close to a deal when it comes to the top-line number. But this same source says, there`s nowhere near close to a deal on how it`s going to get paid for, and frankly, are they`re going to - - and how many of these one-year pilot projects are going to be allowed to be in the deal?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and I think those are a lot of the outstanding questions. As much as it`s felt like there`s been more momentum appear behind actually piecing together what could go in this bill, how they`ll get to a top-line number that somewhere between 1.75 and 1.9, it has at times felt like a game of whack a mole.\par \par \uc2 Because just when you think that there`s optimism, at least on the Senate side, about getting some kind of framework about this by the end of the week, this week, which, now 24 hours away, seems really difficult, there`s this new thing popping up where Kyrsten Sinema is once again, reigniting the concerns that she had about raising taxes on corporations and the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 I asked her about that because right now, the reporting is just coming from lobbyists and aides who have spoken to her. When I asked her about that this morning, she kept with the theme of not commenting on much except for the fact that she liked my blouse.\par \par \uc2 And so, that kind of speaks to the vibe that Democrats have had from her this entire time, which is that she`s not negotiating really necessarily with them, she`s speaking to the White House.\par \par \uc2 But for a lot of people that I`ve spoken to appear, they don`t necessarily know where she stands. But at the same time, she is one of those people who keeps popping up issues that now Democrats have to go back to the drawing board and solve.\par \par \uc2 In terms though of the way that this is going to be the message, and I think that you`re right, that it has to be sold, much of the concept that Democrats have been talking about is that this will be once in a lifetime chance for Democrats in the majority in this building. They just want to see big progress made.\par \par \uc2 I do think at the end of the day, a lot of those one-year projects will allow them to say that they`ve ticked off a lot of the items that will allow them to look at the pandemic through the lens of intersectionality, and helping women climb out of this recession.\par \par \uc2 All of those things are really important but nevertheless, there are still key lawmakers here who even if they get those priorities, it`s not enough for them.\par \par \uc2 And so, the balancing act is really still continuing, but I have to say, more optimism here than we`ve heard in a while but at the same time that can dissipate pretty quickly as we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, exactly, which is why I think a lot of people may empathize with the remarks there from Governor Newsom, who just sort of said I can`t take it anymore.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Kristen Welker. Kristen, it was interesting to me, what the President said at the MLK tribute just now because he talked about one issue that I`m not 100 percent convinced is going to be in this final bill. And that`s prescript -- that`s negotiating the prices on prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 So, where are we as far as the White House is concerned? Because it seems as if, like I said, there does seem like the number is close but the details of what encompasses -- what that number encompasses is not.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It`s not close, and there`s not a clear understanding of what specifically would be included if that piece makes it into the final bill, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 And I think what you saw, the President tried to accomplish with the speech that he just delivered was to lay out a set of principles.\par \par \uc2 And essentially what he hopes to achieve with this, and he uses, of course, \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  as a backdrop for the principles that he says, is really guiding this process.\par \par \uc2 And that`s something that`s being echoed here behind the scenes at the White House with officials saying look, this is going to be a big change, as Ali just said, that`s going to be their talking point.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at the numbers, this is nearly half the package that the White House -- the Democrats initially set out to pass.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 We know that, at this point in time, the President has made it clear it`s not going to include tuition-free community college, that that child tax credit will be scaled back, and so will be a key climate provision. And you already have some Democrats coming out and saying, hey, wait a permanent, we`re not going to back cutting the child tax credit. That`s something that we really care about. So how do you get to the finish line?\par \par \uc2 And of course you have overnight the White House insisting that the price tag for this will be zero, but what are the details for that, Chuck? Now, President Biden for his part is going to be out trying to sell this tonight at a town hall. I`m told not to rule out meeting with lawmakers today. But tough to sell it when the details are still so murky, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sure is. All right, Kristen Welker and Ali Vitali, getting us started. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Patrick Gaspard. He is -- like I said, he`s got a lot of experiences under his belt. He was political director there in the first term, President Obama, was an ambassador to South Africa and now is president of the Center for American Progress. Perhaps the most influential think tank in Democratic politics today.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, it`s good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK GASPARD, FORMER WHITE HOUSE POLITICAL DIRECTOR UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: Thank you for having me on, Chuck, in this important moment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with this. If you and I spoke on January 7th, and I`m not trying to invoke the January 6th issue, but January 7th was the day after we found out for sure that Democrats were going to have control of the Senate. Barely but they were going to get it. 50-50. And on that day, we were going, OK, what is this going to mean by the end of the year?\par \par \uc2 Hey, you`re going to get $1.9 trillion in COVID relief, you`re going to get a bipartisan infrastructure deal, anywhere from half a trillion to a trillion, and you are going to get about $1.8 trillion in efforts to tackle climate change and inequality in this country.\par \par \uc2 Would you have said that was a victory for progressive politics?\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: That is all going to be a victory for progressive politics and let`s be really, really clear, Chuck. You asked me to go to January 7th. Let`s go to January 5th, where it wasn`t altogether clear that Democrats would have an opportunity to even have these debates until we took those seats. So you are right that on healthcare, on infrastructure, on climate change, on what we are doing to center women in this economy in a profound way, that`s all historic changes.\par \par \uc2 Some of us have been saying for weeks that there`s no way we can get a $3.5 trillion package. That`s disappointing for some including me, last few left on table, but extraordinary change that`s going to accrue to the benefit of average Americans. We were going to invest in working people and we`re going to invest in our planet. This is altogether good. And that`s something we can sell.\par \par \uc2 Let me also say, Chuck, that the President Joe Biden that we just saw speaking at the MLK Memorial, that`s a guy who -- there is a reason why 80 million Americans voted for him. That president. We spent far too much time talking about his predecessor. Let`s recognize, 80 million people voted for that man, that vision, that energy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: And that fierce urgency of now. And he`s going to be able to sell this bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look. It`s a tough period right now I think for the president. The Senate majority pact on the Democratic side that handles tough races for the party in the Senate side, they did a presentation on Senate battlegrounds. They had the president`s approval rating at 41 percent in the Senate battlegrounds. CNBC national poll which is the same pollster we use at NBC. Seems to also have the president`s job rating at 41 percent.\par \par \uc2 Look, there is a truism in American politics, Patrick. Whenever you`re bogged down with a congressional issue, the majority party is going to take it on the chin, but how do you bring everybody back? There are some hard feelings here, and I`m going to -- let`s assume the best here, you`re going to get this deal. There seems to be a lot of hard feelings. And, you know, the attacks on Sinema and Manchin seem to be super personal.\par \par \uc2 Like I said, your job is to keep the whole coalition together. How do you do it?\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: You know, it`s a broad tent. You get these kinds of robust debates. I think that they`re all healthy debates. And let`s recognize that right now. But, Chuck, we have 96 percent consensus amongst House senates, Democrats in the House and Senate, about the measures in this bill and you have Democrats far reaching, Bernie Sanders and Manchin that are agreeing on the rolling back of the Trump tax cuts, and censoring climate, and Medicare expansion in (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to be easy I think to keep the party together. You know, you just described the sausage making, that`s democracy. It`s a tough thing. Hard things are hard. But at the end of the day, progressives, moderates, centers in the party are going to be able to go back to their home states and talk about the immediate benefit in the lives of average Americans who don`t really care at the end of the day about the challenges, and this personality and the other or the ongoing debates. They want to know, what are you delivering for us now to make sure we don`t have the insecurity that we all experienced during COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think the, do you think all is fair in politics with Sinema and Manchin?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Or do you think some in the party have gone too far? Do you think there`s been a little too heat on the both of them?\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: You know, Chuck, you mentioned some of my biography. I did have the privilege of leading the DNC for a period. And I have knocked on doors in Arizona and in West Virginia, and I recognize that when they`re campaigning in West Virginia and trying to hold your community together, it`s very different than when you`re campaigning in California and New York and in other spaces.\par \par \uc2 I think that Senator Manchin and Senator Sinema are being as thoughtful as they can about what they need to deliver for their constituency while keeping an eye on the greater Democratic enterprise that we`re all engaged in. And by that, I mean small D democracy in keeping this country together.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: So I think that we all do need to be mindful of their circumstances even as we demand that they appreciate the challenges that faced by Americans across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was in my interview with Governor Newsom, we were talking about. there are so many in many ways existential threats. We`ve got -- we`re concerned about the future of this democracy that seems pretty serious. Climate change. You know, it`s no longer something in the way off. It is here. It feels like an existential threat. And inequality could create a moment where that becomes existential. And it`s sort of like, and you`re trying to tackle them all at the same time. And you`re trying to figure out what to prioritize. And he said --\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: By ourselves without any support from the other party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that. And he said something that I thought was interesting. He said the president needs to remember, and everybody needs to remember, he was elected for one reason, to get us behind COVID. So do you think that everything should be about COVID first and everything else should come second?\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: No, absolutely not. I`m going to disagree with my friend Governor Newsom. President Biden ran on an agenda of centering working class Americans, building out union strength again in this country, making sure that we closed the whole on Medicaid that keeps millions of Americans from getting access to healthcare, and he ran on the fundamental, protecting the fundamental integrity of our democracy that had been sabotaged by Donald Trump and his administration.\par \par \uc2 He ran on all those issues and said, we can get all of these big things done as we bend the curve on COVID. Americans have that expectation. 80 million Americans voted for that vision. And it`s not enough just to get us back to where we were before the pandemic arrived on our shores. We`ve got to do better than that. We have an obligation to future generations on climate and on the economy. And the president is going to see that through.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Patrick Gaspard, the president of the Center for American Politics, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 GASPARD: Thank you so much, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good to see you. Up next, a key senator is going to have to sign off on all this. Every senator is key when it`s 50-50. But this one is pretty key, too. Senator Jeff Merkley.\par \par \uc2 And later, an exclusive look at an all-new "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." We`re digging into the growing textbook wars across this nation as Americans remain divided over how to teach about race in this country and the most painful parts of our country`s history.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:18]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This week, we`ve been learning a bit more about what the Biden administration is willing to sacrifice in order to get more of its human infrastructure bill passed. And a big chunk of the president`s climate agenda seems like it`s landed on the cutting room floor which has a lot of progressives have been uncomfortable particularly those that represent states along the Pacific Coast. And Democrat still need to have a hard conversation around how they pay for it.\par \par \uc2 So I`m joined by Democratic senator from Oregon, Jeff Merkley. And to say the state of Oregon has been on the front lines of climate change with the fires, et cetera, is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, let me start there. If I could sum up what the larger -- what this larger infrastructure or human infrastructure bill is going to be about, I can argue two giant categories, climate and inequality. Right? And there were derivatives down from there. How concerned are you that the climate piece has gotten too small?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JEFF MERKLEY (D-OR): Well, I`m very concerned. On the family investment side, we`re going to have investments in daycare. It`s going to be huge when preschool is going to be huge, investments in housing is so important for families. Each one of those by themselves will be an enormous victory. On the climate side, we cannot miss this opportunity. We can think back to the first President Bush who ran on climate when the Democrat, the caucus, ran on coal.\par \par \uc2 We`ve missed that opportunity then and we`ve missed every single opportunity since, and now we are in deep, deep trouble. My state is burning up. Our snow pack in the cascades is disappearing. The acidity of the Pacific Ocean is affecting our shellfish. The warmth of our streams is affecting our salmon and trout. And so we have to act and we have to lead the world and we can`t miss this opportunity.\par \par \uc2 I personally, if we don`t have a clean electricity payment plan, fine, but tell me how we are going to be on track to cut carbon by 50 percent by the end of this decade. I don`t care, there are many paths. It has to be a viable path. Work it out. Lock everybody up over the weekend. Put this kind of nightmare to rest, come back with a strong set of programs. It won`t be all of them on family investments. And a strong set of strategies on climate that \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  can live with, and let`s get it done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it`s interesting. You`re obviously, you`ve said it, it doesn`t have to be the program that was originally in there. You`ve just said that on climate. What is an alternative path that could cut our carbon emissions by 50 percent in the timeframe? Is it a carbon tax which apparently suddenly that doesn`t have support from not just Manchin and Sinema, but John Tester said that would be tough to support.\par \par \uc2 MERKLEY: Well, a methane theme may very well be part of the solution. Methane is a very big deal leaking out of our natural gas system and our system of pipes. Then you throw in incentives for energy efficiency in manufacturing, you know, transition toward the vision of this, electrify everything with renewable energy. So you put everything you can on incentives to go to electricity and then incentives to move everything you can in terms of putting renewable energy on the grid.\par \par \uc2 That would be almost all on the carrot side and if that`s the way we need to do it. Let`s do it in that fashion. But we have to have people going, yes, in the next three years, I want to put solar panels on my roof. We`re going to have utilities saying this is the future America wants. We`re going to deliver. It may not be a mandate. It may not be a payment plan but our customers are getting subsidized to do it. They want to do it. We have to create that set of incentives to get it done.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you do -- a member of the Congress, Jared Hoffman, on the House side said, why not simply create a carveout for West Virginia? You know, which reminds me of how healthcare was put together, when suddenly there was a carveout for Nebraska or a carveout for -- you know, in order to get certain votes. But there actually may be something to that. A carveout for West Virginia and Wyoming, who have economies that are pretty, you know, pretty heavily linked to coal right now. Is that viable?\par \par \uc2 MERKLEY: You know, if the president can sit down and strike a deal with \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  in whatever strategy, if it puts us on the path of 50 percent reduction, I`d be for it. I think that the -- we all recognize that for states with strong coal industries, it`s a hard transition. And we`re very supportive of every feasible strategy of supporting those families who are in those communities that have been dependent upon coal jobs.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take care of them. That`s the right thing to do. And this transition shouldn`t fall on the backs of fossil fuel workers. Let`s be there with them in powerful ways. But we can`t afford to remain trapped in the fossil fuel economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You earlier in our interview I caught the use of the word nightmare. Obviously, you`re being an elected member of Congress these days, and being a Democrat means you`ve probably been hearing from friends, family, constituents, going, what the heck is going on? What is taking so long, all of this thing? Is this -- I mean, you referred to it as a nightmare. Does this feel harder than previous -- and you`ve been here a while. Does this seem worse than ever?\par \par \uc2 MERKLEY: Well, it takes me back to the Affordable Care Act when Senator Bacchus insisted on a year-long negotiation with three Republicans who publicly said they were not going to get to a deal. And just the energy started evaporating from the process. The momentum just came to a stop. And it was like, there was moment in which you have to wrap it up. And we`re at that moment now.\par \par \uc2 Folks back home are like, what is this all about? What is wrong with you? You have 10 wonderful things you`ve proposed. And if you`re doing one by one, we`d cheering you on with each one, you`d be building momentum. We`d say, wow, we finally got daycare, we finally got pre-school, we finally got big investments in housing. We finally got -- but the way it`s being done now is just, it`s completely taking the air out of the balloon for the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 It`s hurting Biden. It`s hurting the Democrats. It`s undermining the vision of all the accomplishments we`re going to have as being highly significant. The frustration is people`s heads are blowing off. And they should be. It has to come to an end. And I don`t know if soap opera nightmare or a nightmare soap opera. It`s the right worry, but we`re in big trouble right now with this extended getting nowhere negotiation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Jeff Merkley, Democrat from Oregon. I appreciate you coming on, sharing your perspective with us. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 MERKLEY: You`re welcome. Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we`re doing a deep dive into the escalating fight over how we teach about race in America and what we`re not teaching. It`s the subject of our latest episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." We got a sneak peek right after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:38]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Across the country, some school boards and state legislatures have descended into full-on culture war over a simple question of how we teach the history of race in America. For some parents, teachers, and lawmakers, the answer is to whitewash some of America`s most important and most painful parts of its history.\par \par \uc2 Texas has taken some of the most stringent steps to control how schools teach race, a very top-down approach, already passing two laws aimed at curbing the teaching of so-called critical race theory which contends that historical patterns of racism are ingrained in U.S. institutions. Mind you, they`re banning something that has not been happening, so we don`t forget.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Antonia Hylton has been following these education wars as they evolve first in her podcast "South Lake" and now with her latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." Investigating the ramifications felt in one Texas district. Here`s a sneak preview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): State Senator Brian Hughes is the author of Senate Bill 3, the second and most stringent of the anti-CRT laws passed in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): When you look at some of what`s happening around the state right now, you don`t think that any of this has gone off the rails?\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. BRIAN HUGHES (R-TX): I think folks need to focus on what`s in the bill, and not what`s in other states or what they`ve heard or things like that. And if we were to tell a little white children that they are inherently oppressors, that`s not good. If we tell little children of color they are inherently victims, neither one of those is good.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Was that the primary motivation for these laws?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: To make sure that white kids don`t feel guilty?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: So the bill is pretty clear. It would be wrong to tell white kids or children of color that they limited based on the color of their skin or that they are guilty because of what people of their race did in the past.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What about the teachers who say they are closing their classroom libraries or Dr. Whitfield, the principal here who`s about to lose his job?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: I`ll just ask folks to look at the words of the bill. The words of the bill matter, not the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  memes.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: But do you have a message to them? I mean, is there something that you can clarify at a statewide level?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: So what we`ve said, I want to make sure this is clear. So what we do not teach in Texas public schools is that one race is inherently superior or inferior.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: I understand that, but I want to know what you think of the current lives people are living as a result of this entire movement.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Well, I can`t speak to the national movement about CRT, what other states are doing. All I can tell you is what`s in Senate Bill 3.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON (voice-over): A group of Colleyville Heritage High students have led classroom walkouts and spoken out at school board meetings, holding out hope they`ll get their principal back.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA ZELLING, COLLEYVILLE HERITAGE HIGH SCHOOL SENIOR: They fired the best principal that any of us have ever had in our entire life. Just because he`s the first black principal and they could get away with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the reporter behind this great piece will join me next, right after this break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:25]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we showed you before the break, our new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" is taking a deep dive into this escalating education wars in this country and intense fight around how we teach race. NBC News correspondent Antonia Hylton has been covering the impact on school districts in Texas, she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And Antonia, I can`t thank you enough for your reporting for this episode, for everything on here. And I was able to just speak earlier on a podcast with the principal that you highlight here. So let`s start with that back and forth you had with that lawmaker because he keeps saying what`s in the bill, and he keeps saying we`re going to prevent kids from feeling like they`re oppressed or they`re oppressors.\par \par \uc2 Where was this happening? Like, this has been sort of -- where I keep it, did somebody actually teach this anywhere in school? Because I haven`t found any evidence of it and you`ve been working at this a long time. Have you?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Well, I asked him that question, actually, directly, Chuck. And what he told me was we were hearing reports, we got phone calls from parents. But I have spent the entirety of this year in Texas, going back and forth in the north Texas area, reporting on what we`re seeing happen in that community and other communities nearby, and I have not been able to find one verifiable piece of evidence that critical race theory made its way into Texas schools or other schools throughout the United States for that matter.\par \par \uc2 It`s a graduate level concept, really a law school course. It`s not taught in K-12. And so what I think is so important in that conversation with Senator Hughes there is that, you know, I`m asking him to grapple with, right, the tangible impacts that a law that he`s just authored that has been passed is now having on people`s lives. And he`s repeating language in the bill and repeating beliefs about what he thinks is happening in the classroom and, I`m trying to contrast that, right, with, well, here`s what`s actually happening.\par \par \uc2 Here is what teachers are saying they`re experiencing. Here`s an educator about to lose his job. And it`s not clear that what you`re describing ever happened in Colleyville, the community at hand or in any other part of Texas, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And he keeps saying don`t look at other parts of the country. But wasn`t this cookie cutter legislation?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: It was. And these bills, if you look at the language of the bills in states all over the country, they are very closely aligned. I mean, there`s very much copycat language saying, you know, to make sure that students don`t end up feeling guilt or anguish. We see that in Texas, and then in the next-door state of Oklahoma. And actually in the piece tonight, you`ll not only meet Dr. Whitfield and see exclusive access to his family and what his life has become since all these accusations have transpired.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211022ehal00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 19, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Andrea Mitchell, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Pete Williams,  Shannon Pettypiece, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 8,513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 FRED GUTTENBERG, GUN SAFETY ADVOCATE: The penalty faced for me is a really important part of our ability to move forward as families.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Well, our thoughts and prayers are with you tomorrow, as with the other parents, and the other relatives. Thank you, Fred.\par \par \uc2 GUTTENBERG: Andrea, thank you for everything always.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And we`re going to leave it there. That does it for today for ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS. Follow us online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @mitchellreports. Chuck Todd in MTP DAILY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday, can the January 6 Committee break through the delay tactics of Trump and his allies? The panel, gearing up for its first contempt vote later today when Steve Bannon defies the Committee`s subpoenas, and Trump takes the Committee to Court.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new reporting suggests the FDA is poised to allow a mix-and-match approach for COVID booster shots as early as this week as debates continue over which shot offers the best protection. This is the biggest news for \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  recipients.\par \par \uc2 And later, Moderates and Progressives are headed to the White House today to meet with President Biden separately amid a flurry of new talks inside the Democratic Caucus, as it continues to struggle to unite around the size, scope, and urgency of the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 We now have yet another example of the Republican Party`s willingness to overlook the former President`s ongoing attacks on our democracy.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the former President publicly called for the 2020 election results to be decertified in Arizona, and for him to be declared the winner, he also actually said if that doesn`t happen, there should be a new election.\par \par \uc2 He`s saying this on a constant daily basis by the way. If social media existed, you`d probably hear about it more. But there has been virtually no reaction from Republican leaders. None, zilch, zip.\par \par \uc2 The level of numbness to Trump`s attacks on our democracy is in a word, remarkable. As one producer said to me it`s numb and number these days on the right as the Republican Party`s apparent willingness to let Trump try to run off the clock on the House`s investigation into his role surrounded the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Trump is now done to this Committee what he`s done in every lawsuit, or investigation he`s been involved with. He filed a big old lawsuit against both the Committee and the National Archives, he`s simply seeking to delay, but in this case, the specific is to block access to his presidential records and documents. But it`s all about delay, delay, delay, delay, delay.\par \par \uc2 The White House, of course, responded to this suit this morning, saying. "As President Biden determined, the constitutional protections of executive privilege should not be used to shield information that reflects a clear and apparent effort to subvert the Constitution itself."\par \par \uc2 So, how quickly will this legal issue be resolved? We don`t know, which is exactly what Trump wants, be prepared, you know this. Delay tactics are Trump`s loan go-to move, it`s what he does.\par \par \uc2 They`ve worked for him sadly, so many times before. From bankruptcy filings to impeachment probes and they might work again here because of the clock issue. Why? What clock am I referring to? The one on this Congress.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans win back the House next year, they are likely to shut down this House investigation into the insurrection. So, there isn`t a heck of a lot of time for the investigation to do its work.\par \par \uc2 And if we have to go to a Court fight to find out if they get access to the President`s records, that isn`t going to take days, you`re looking at weeks or months.\par \par \uc2 This is why, this afternoon, the Committee will try to make an example of Steve Bannon for defying the Committee`s request for documents and testimony.\par \par \uc2 They`ll be voting to advance a criminal contempt charge, which after a full vote in the House will then be referred to the Justice Department for potential prosecution.\par \par \uc2 If the Justice Department takes it to a Grand Jury, which appears more likely than not, Bannon could be faced with a simple choice, start cooperating with the Committee or go to jail. By the way, if he sits in jail for a year, he actually could still avoid ever talking to the Committee.\par \par \uc2 So what is next? Where does the roadmap for the January 6 Committee`s probe actually lead us? As a political matter, as a legal matter, as a national matter, and as a democracy, and more importantly, how long will it take us to get there, and can we get to the end of this investigation before the end of this Congress?\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill for us, also joining me is Robert Tsai, he`s a Professor of Constitutional Law at the \uc2 Boston University\uc2  Law School, and The Washington Post`s Robert Costa, he`s author of the new book Peril with Bob Woodward. He`ll be joined by -- I`m also joined by Justice Correspondent Pete Williams in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But let me go to Capitol Hill where the action is taking place today. Leigh Ann, we know Bannon`s folks try to get a one-week delay, the Committee said no, so this vote`s going to happen today. How quickly will a full House vote to send this referral to the Justice Department?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Speaker Pelosi hasn`t said specifically when the full House is going to vote yet, but everyone is under the impression that it`s going to happen very quickly.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And the reason is, is because the Committee has actually moved very quickly for this vote with Steve Bannon.\par \par \uc2 This is -- we all know Congress, and we know how slowly things usually work around here. And the fact that the Committee is moving, which that`s -- which sets -- with such speed on this vote is a signal that that`s how they`re going to operate throughout this entire process. So, we should expect a vote perhaps as early as this week.\par \par \uc2 We also know that the Committee is going to vote -- to refer this -- this Bannon contempt issue tonight unanimously, that`s what we expect to happen.\par \par \uc2 The Committee is united, even the two Republicans on that Committee, and we expect the Chair, Cheney -- Co-Chair Liz Cheney and Bennie Thompson to really speak with one voice on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And like you said, Chuck, they`re trying to make an example of Steve Bannon because they say ultimately, they want people to comply. They want everyone to comply, including Steve Bannon.\par \par \uc2 And they remind me that there`s -- these are high profile people, but there`s a lot of other people who they have subpoenaed as well, who are going to be just as important as people like Steve Bannon and Mark Meadows, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, here`s the question I have. They know what the Trump tactics are, which is to delay all these things. And given the clock is the calendar of this Congress is at the end of `22 and you have to, look, we -- you have to prepare for the possibility that this investigation isn`t going to be able to -- they`re -- we`re on a finite timeline, does the Committee start 