hose are still open questions that are working with those three and so there hasn`t been many big developments publicly yet anyway, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete Williams, the Trump lawsuit here, where -- so, the judge that he got may not be a very friendly judge to him, at least that seems to be some early coverage of that.\par \par \uc2 But layout a potential timeline here on the resolution of this, because obviously, that matters and what and when or if the January 6 Committee gets its hands on some of this material.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, it`s about the documents so far, not about the testimony that you`ve been talking about up till this point. This is the two requests that the Committee made to Archives in March and August archives, which is the custodian of all the former President`s papers.\par \par \uc2 And the way it works under the federal law is if somebody asks in the Committee-- the archives goes to the White House, and to the person -- the former President says what do you want us to do? As you know, here, Trump has said, well, I want to assert executive privilege. And Biden says that`s not legitimate here, we think it`s more important for this to go to Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, that weakens the President`s position somewhat, but it doesn`t take it away. So, what he`s basically suing is he`s making two points about the Committee.\par \par \uc2 Number one, he says, it doesn`t have the right to ask for these at all anyway because its only authority to gather information is for legislating, not for investigating. That`s an argument Trump is used in the past and it hasn`t been very successful.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The second one is the executive privilege one, and that`s going to have to be worked out. And that could take, you know -- that could take -- you know if it goes to the Supreme Court, it could take a year, two years, before we finally get an answer on whether President Trump can assert executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 And then, if whether he wins or loses, he had a look determine how much can be turned over to the Committee. So, if this executive privilege thing has any legs, and at this point, it appears it has some, it could take that law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robert Tsai, I saw a quote from somebody else that sort of shares your academic credentials said, you know, when it comes to this issue of executive privilege for former President, that it`s a pretty empty roadmap -- legal roadmap. There have been hints at where the courts may fall, but we don`t -- this hasn`t been fully litigated.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT TSAI, LAW PROFESSOR, BOSTON UNIVERSITY: Well, I think that`s right, Chuck. You know, it`s a sort of situation where I think the Select Committee is doing the smart thing by going after Bannon. He`s sort of the low-hanging fruit among the people who are acting in a recalcitrant way.\par \par \uc2 He was not acting as a government official with respect to the documents, the testimony that they`re seeking, and, you know, on top of that, the sort of subject that they`re investigating does not appear to have to do with any official functions that the -- that the President was involved in.\par \par \uc2 So, his, you know, his position legally is the weakest of the bunch. So, I guess what I would say is, is that he`s got part of a leg to stand on, and it`s very wobbly because there is a little bit of case law in some language that suggests that Presidents have been successful at times.\par \par \uc2 And they`ve certainly tried to claim privilege with respect to some conversations with people who are in -- who are private parties at that point in order to fulfill the public functions. But a lot of those questions have not yet been fully litigated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you know, one of the asks that the President`s legal team has here, Robert, is this idea that, at a minimum, they want to go through everything that they may be asking for, and help decide whether it`s worthy or not.\par \par \uc2 The point being is that felt like a Plan C for delay tactics. Meaning -- and I could see a court saying, yes, maybe there should be some sort of special legal team that decides what belongs, what`s -- what belongs in privilege and what doesn`t, which to me sounds like it could also take a long time to decide that which of course, could successfully run up the clock on the Democrats in this investigation. Is that a likely scenario here?\par \par \uc2 TSAI: It certainly is because this is how most of these kinds of disputes are resolved through some sort of negotiation. One of the things is that it`s going to be different this time around, of course, is the political dimension.\par \par \uc2 That is to say, that the Biden administration, as folks have already pointed out, have indicated that at least with respect to most of the documents in the hands of the of the National Archives, they`re not going to assert some sort of blanket executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to put pressure on Bannon and certainly the others to try to come up with some sort of workable solution. So, we don`t know yet of course, how committed he is in terms of wanting to spend time in prison, potentially up to -- up to a year if --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TSAI: -- if he sort of holds out despite a judicial resolution on this. But yes, most of the time, these kinds of things get resolved. And I would suspect that if they get a negative decision, that is a Bannon loses in any of these judicial cases, then you`ll see the other sort of rollover pretty fast and work out someday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robert Costa, let`s talk about the political element here. We`re in this weird place for this investigation because Senate Republicans refused to put their stamp on this investigation.\par \par \uc2 They did not want to give this, you know, they wanted to appease Trump by not giving it a bipartisan stamp of approval. Obviously, Liz Cheney and Adam Kinzinger did their best to try to improve the prospects of that. But that was a decision.\par \par \uc2 I want to -- I want you to listen to this exchange between one of our reporters, Julie Tsirkin, and Republican Senator Kevin Cramer. Because the former President has been being out there on the big lie and in a more aggressive way the last 10 days and frankly, we`ve seen in a long time. And listen to the dismissiveness of Kevin Cramer here as this being a serious issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIE TSIRKIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Does that concern you at all? I mean, as somebody who could potentially be a nominee again in 2024, you voted for him?\par \par \uc2 SEN. KEVIN CRAMER, (R-ND): Yes. No, it doesn`t concern me again. What concerns me more is when we start canceling out people who have a different view than, you know, than certain other people.\par \par \uc2 I`m very concerned by the cancel culture going on in this country, by the shutting down of views that, you know, Mark Zuckerberg disagrees with, or somebody else.\par \par \uc2 The last time I checked, Joe Biden was sworn in, he`s in the Oval Office, screwing up this country in a massive way, there`s plenty of things to criticize Joe Biden for because he won the election.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not an area that I spend a lot of my time hanging out, it`s not an area very many people do, quite honestly, and I frankly, find the media`s obsession with Donald Trump to be unhealthy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: The media`s obsession. This is a new sort of trick by Republicans now. They try to duck responsibility for what their party is not willing to do with the democracy and somehow blamed the press.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, as a reporter, you just go back to the facts here. Republicans can have their opinion, but the facts are very clear, President Trump was intimately involved with planning the effort and coordinating the effort to block Biden`s certification to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Many players around him, in his inner circle, Dan Scavino, Steve Bannon, were working with him to try to block Biden`s certification.\par \par \uc2 Our book, Peril, is cited in the subpoena documents detailing the scenes of Bannon and Trump on the phone, Scavino in the Oval Office on January 5.\par \par \uc2 Whether you`re a Republican or Democrat, you might choose to look away, but the fact still exists. And another point here is that executive privilege is not some kind of constitutional guarantee, it`s a relatively modern phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at U.S. vs. Nixon, during Watergate, Presidents have been proven in the past to not have this kind of wholesales say over whether their documents or tapes are protected under the law.\par \par \uc2 And it`s going to be really interesting to see what the Supreme Court does. Is an insurrection at the U.S. Capitol considered some kind of criminal activity that demands documents and other material to be shared?\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to go back to the issue of as I use -- the phrase I used earlier from one of my producers, I just thought it was a brilliant phrase, that the GOP is numb and number on this now. And I`m curious, Robert, do you think Glenn Youngkin losing because of this issue?\par \par \uc2 Which I would argue if he loses by more than three or four points, it`s going to be clear that invoking Donald Trump and the thread of him to the democracy does have to resonate with swing voters, at least in Virginia. If that is the case, is that going to breakthrough? Does that get Mitch McConnell?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I know he pretend -- he doesn`t even have any regrets, but he`s single-handedly made sure this investigation got watered down by his decision to stand in the way of this. Does this at all break the Republican silence on Trump?\par \par \uc2 COSTA: The issue here is