ts in arms for millions more Americans within days, especially for folks that have immunocompromises, you know, you`d -- how many more pieces of evidence do you need that if you`re battling other ailments, two doses may not be enough, anyway.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a new reporting from "The New York Times" which says the FDA is also planning to greenlight the mix-and-matching of some booster shots.\par \par \uc2 This comes after an independent panel reviewed an NIH study as part of last week`s two-day FDA Advisory Committee meeting.\par \par \uc2 The data showed that mix-and-matching booster shots, as in boosting fully vaccinated folks with a different shot from what they already received, was safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 And apparently regardless of whether it is a viral vector vaccine, like \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 , or a mRNA vaccine, like the Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now to answer some of these questions is the former Acting Director of the CDC himself, under President Obama, \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 . He`s also currently the President and CEO of the \uc2 Robert Wood Johnson Foundation\uc2 , a Philanthropy, dedicated to improving health and health equity.\par \par \uc2 So, Dr. Besser, I want to start with there. Let me ask this. Is mixing and matching better? Or just an alternative to getting the booster shot or not -- is this -- is this just another alternative? Or could it actually be better?\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD BESSER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING CDC DIRECTOR: Yes. You know, Chuck, I`ll be looking forward to what FDA has to say about this because it`s a limited amount of data that they`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 But the data from NIH that they looked at last week, what it suggests is that regardless of what you`ve got for your first round of vaccination, getting an mRNA vaccine so the Moderna or the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine as your booster, is the best way to go.\par \par \uc2 Our foundation was founded with dollars from the Johnson fortune and we have stock in J&J. But it`s clear that individuals who got a Moderna or \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  booster after a J&J, got a much higher bump in their -- in their antibody titers than those who got a booster with the J&J following a J&J.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, right now, it`s really confusing because you have approvals for only boosters for people who`ve had the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine or those who are immunocompromised.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, though, they`ll come forward with something that makes it much easier for people to go in, roll up their sleeve regardless of what they got and get their boosters.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, kudos to you Dr. Besser for explaining some potential conflicts or appeared -- appearances that you think some might say is a conflict there, so kudos to you for doing that.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious, you`re a doctor and I`m coming to you, I`m going to get a booster, which one should I get? At the end of the day, do you -- do you think people should stick with what they`ve had? Or does it really depend on whether they`re immunocompromised or not? Or are there other factors you would -- you would consider before making your recommendation?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Yes. Well, it`s important I think, to not include in the -- in their conversations around boosters, people who have immun0 -- immune disorders, who are immunocompromised, because what it appears there is that two doses are not enough to give you an initial high boost.\par \par \uc2 For people who don`t have immune disorders, you get a very high level of protection with your first round, whether it`s a one-dose or the two doses.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to boosters, right now, the recommendations aren`t for everyone to get a booster. There`s a recommendation that people who are 65 and older, people who have serious medical conditions that put them at greater risk, people who work in professions that put them at risk of the increased exposure that they go ahead and get -- and get boosters.\par \par \uc2 But I expect we will get to the point where the -- where boosters are recommended for just about everybody at some point since the data are showing that over time, there is some drop off in levels of protection.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, you know, most of that is in terms of mild infection. But over time, especially for those who are elderly, getting that booster shot is a good idea.\par \par \uc2 But it shouldn`t come at the expense, Chuck, of increasing vaccine supply globally because that will have a much greater impact in terms of saving lives, that will have a much greater impact in terms of getting the pandemic under control, and reducing the emergence of new variants.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I -- General Colin Powell, we lost tragically yesterday and it was to a battle with COVID, he was fully vaccinated, but he was also immunocompromised. He was battling cancer, he was battling Parkinson`s.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder, do we need to have a better -- I mean, we`ve said immunocompromised, people got to get the third shot. Do you think there needs to be more than that?\par \par \uc2 Do you think pretty much any cancer patient at this point should be wearing a mask? Is there -- is this a case where maybe cancer patients are going to need booster shots every six months or something?\par \par \uc2 I mean, do we need a little more focus on this? Because it does seem as if the breakthrough cases that turned deadly, usually are connected to another -- to a co-morbidity?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: What a loss this is for our country, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: And I hope people don`t take the wrong lesson away from this. The lesson to me isn`t that vaccines aren`t working. Well, the lesson to me is that if you decide not to get vaccinated, you`re not only putting yourself at risk, you are putting at risk those around you who may have immune disorders where even if they got fully vaccinated, they`re not going to have a full level of protection.\par \par \uc2 And how can you justify doing that? You know, we were part of communities, and one of the things that we can do is look out for each other.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not a big deal to vaccinate yourself to protect those people around you whether it`s young children who can`t get vaccinated yet, or people like you know, General Colin Powell who got vaccinated but have a particular type of cancer that really hits the immune system hard. Multiple myeloma, it really reduces your ability to react.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope some people look at this and say, you know what, I`m going to get vaccinated because I can`t tell walking down the street, if I`m walking by someone who is at increased risk. And I just don`t want to put anyone else at risk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hope -- I hope if anybody`s watching that hasn`t been vaccinated and is persuadable, I hope they heard what you just said, Dr. Besser. Anyway, as always sir, thank you for coming on and sharing your expertise with us.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, another day, another series of meetings between Democrats over the reconciliation bill. Both Progressives and Moderates are set to meet with President Biden, but not together. They`re still meeting separately, shuttle diplomacy continues. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:18]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. In the seemingly never ending back and forth negotiations over the larger Democratic Biden agenda bill, also known as reconciliation in Congress speak, the president is set to meet with two separate groups of lawmakers. One on the moderate side of things. One on the progressive side. And both of those meetings will be this afternoon. They`re meeting separately, so we`re a ways away.\par \par \uc2 Biden met with Manchin in person this morning, and apparently as well as 