exactly selling because we have no idea right now what is on the table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: What is off the table. And the White House isn`t giving any indication for that. So it puts him in a difficult position and it`s certainly one I know White House officials don`t want to be in very long.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In Connecticut last Friday, Shannon, he talked about free community college but had to preface it, saying, "I`m not sure it`s going to be in the bill."\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Yes. And a couple of the things like prescription drugs. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that is -- was the definition of this awkwardness.\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Yes. And prescription drugs have been one of the big things the White House is selling. And now, you know, the indication that might -- the Medicare negotiation might get kicked out. So it`s a real hard sales pitch for them at the moment, it appears.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s hard to explain what`s in this bill when they can`t tell you yet what`s in this bill.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Pettypiece, outside the White House. Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to stick with the executive branch side of things. Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen is going to be a key part of these White House meetings with progressives at the top of the hour. And she just wrapped up a sit-down with my colleague Stephanie Ruhle. We`ve got the first look at their conversation, up next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watch MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Among the Biden administration officials involved in today`s negotiations on reconciliation is the Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen. Before heading to the White House, Yellen told NBC`s Stephanie Ruhle what she hopes is in this ultimate package.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: You`re about to head over to the White House. What is the one thing you`d like to tell lawmakers that must be in this reconciliation bill? What do you think is the most important thing?\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN, TREASURY SECRETARY: Well, there are many things that are very important. Climate change is very high on my list. It`s high on President Biden`s list.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:03]\par \par \uc2 There are policies to address climate change in the infrastructure package and in reconciliation, and certainly a range of policies that will address the needs of families and children, to make sure that children have the investments and the opportunities that they need to thrive. There is a lot in this package. And want to see an agreement reached that will take advantage of an opportunity we have to have healthier economic growth and healthier family life.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Anything you think we could wait on?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: You know, not going to negotiate about that here. This is for members of Congress to sort through and see where they can get. I feel very hopeful that we will get a good reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This week?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, I don`t know what the timetable is going to be.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Stephanie Ruhle joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s interesting, Stephanie, to hear her say climate change because she`s also apparently among the leading advocates for a carbon tax. And a carbon tax was off the table, we thought, but it appears to be back on the table as they, A, look for different ways to pay for things, like Kyrsten Sinema will sign off on, and they need different climate ideas now that Manchin has shut it down. Is that where we`re headed?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Chuck, I mean, Secretary Yellen is at the White House right now. I was surprised that she put climate front and center. I was expecting that she would be pushing the most for making the expanded child tax credit permanent and the money devoted to child care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So I was surprised by climate being first, but my other takeaway was, she is still saying everything should be in this thing. And if the answer is everything is going to be in this thing, then what are you going to do? Make it really, really short term? Well, if that`s the case, you know we could end up losing most of it if in the next election Republicans win.\par \par \uc2 Look at the Expanded Child Tax Credit. They put it in the American Rescue Plan. And unless this reconciliation bill goes through and the child tax credit is in it, it`ll be gone by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So I thought that was interesting. And the other thing that I found interesting, we talked a lot about the labor shortage, the labor shift. You know, she thinks it`s a good thing that workers are now getting paid more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And I said, what are you going to do about the fact that the Amazons of the world can pay people a whole lot more and offer bonuses? And she said, small businesses are just going to have to find a way to compete. I don`t know how that`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Stephanie, I know you probably did this because I`d be tempted to do it, too. She`s a former Fed chair. And I`m guessing she`s going to be hesitant to give her advice. But if she were at the Fed right now dealing with this -- look, we can all agree we have an inflation issue. We disagree about whether it`s long term or short term, but the Fed has the tools here. What would we she want to see the Fed do?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, she argues that it is going to work itself out in the next few months, that it`s short term. So, you know, what we went through, the major shock to our system because of the pandemic, of course we`re going to have the inflationary environment that we`re in right now. She thinks it`s going to solve itself in the coming year.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing I ask you, Chuck. Yes, prices are a lot higher and we don`t like it. But here`s the dirty secret, we`re paying it. Consumer demand is up. Consumer spending is up. So if you, Chuck Todd, ran a business right now, would you be incentivized to lower your prices? Why would you?\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. And more importantly, you`re looking for extra revenue to hire more people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Your labor cost are up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s the other issue right. So you may actually raise prices even more. I mean, that, to me, is the danger here. I understand they claim it`s temporary, but inflation is one of those sticky things. It can build on itself.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Absolutely. And think about what this is like. I go back to small businesses. Again, the president took action at the ports in California last week, and that`s a good thing. But remember, the biggest American businesses out there, they`re going to work out their supply chain issues. I assure you. \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  and Amazon, they can get their own cargo ships. They can figure this out. But for tiny, small businesses, right?\par \par \uc2 For a toy store in Bethesda, Maryland, right now, they`re not going to be getting the supplies they need by Christmas. So it`s not just labor, it`s goods. I don`t know how they solve for it. And she didn`t have an answer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, every -- you name it, every recession or every economic angst we`ve had, big gets bigger. And your point on Amazon, I think, is a very important one here at all.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Stephanie, can`t wait to see the whole thing tomorrow morning. Don`t miss it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can catch much more of that interview at 9:00 a.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we have some breaking news involving -- here`s a name from the recent past -- Russian oligarch Oleg Deripaska. You might remember him. He`s the Putin ally. He`s been sanctioned by the United States for his financial ties to Trump campaign manager Paul Manafort. They were scrutinized as part of Manafort`s criminal trial and a big part of the Mueller investigation.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? This morning, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents swarmed Deripaska`s Washington home to conduct what we`re told is some kind of law enforcement activity, according to an agency`s spokesperson. The spokesperson would not provide details on the scope, target or substance of the investigation, but two officials tell NBC News that these search warrants are the result of an investigation being led by federal investigators in New York City.\par \par \uc2 OK. And as we know, that in itself could also be something that is related to the larger, you know, we have Lev Parnas. There`s a lot going on in New York City-led investigations involving Giuliani and a lot of other people.\par \par \uc2 And with that, we`ll be keeping our eyes for more developments as we get them. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did we end up here? The lies. The division. It starts when we give room for hate to grow.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: You also have very fine people, on both sides.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And for some, they embrace it.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN, VIRGINIA REPUBLICAN GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: I was honored to receive President Trump`s endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was part of Virginia Democratic gubernatorial nominee Terry McAuliffe`s latest ad tying the Republican nominee and his opponent, Glenn Youngkin, to former President Trump. And this ad invoked the memories of not just January 6th but the 2017 march in Charlottesville, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 We are two weeks out from election day and this ad is just further proof that McAuliffe is going all in on Trump. He believes that nationalizing this race is his best bet to win. Talked to some Republicans in the last 24 hours who think that the Bannon-Trump event may have completely stalled Youngkin`s momentum. But we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 The Virginia governor`s race is just one of the races we`re watching on election day. A slew of American major cities are going to be electing new mayors. In Boston, city counselor Michelle Wu holds a commanding over fellow counselor Anissa Essaibi George in a new poll from our NBC affiliate NBC 10. Whoever wins the election will make history as both the first woman and the first person of color to be elected mayor of Boston.\par \par \uc2 In Minneapolis, Mayor Jacob Frey. Remember him during the George Floyd protest? Well, he`s facing 16 challenges. It`s an election that`s taken on not surprisingly a theme of public safety in the wake of George Floyd`s murder. There`s also a key referendum on the police force on the ballot there. Voters get to rank three choices for mayor and Minneapolis Congresswoman Ilhan Omar told supporters yesterday not to rank the incumbent mayor while endorsing two challengers.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of endorsements, the Democratic nominee for mayor of Buffalo in New York is not yet getting any support from the state`s top elected Democrats. Why is that, you might ask. Well, yesterday Senator Chuck Schumer declined to endorse the self-identified socialist nominee India Walton. Governor Kathy Hochul also has refrained from backing her today, but Walton does have the backing of Bernie Sanders and Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.\par \par \uc2 The incumbent mayor is waging a write-in campaign. Remember, he lost the primary. Didn`t really pay a lot of attention to this primary. He lost it. He`s running as a write-in in the general election.\par \par \uc2 Folks, local elections do matter. We saw it during COVID, we saw it during the social justice protest. Local leadership matters. Do yourself a favor. Look up what`s on your ballot in two weeks, not just if it`s the statewide race in Virginia. Look at all of it, your city councilmembers, your mayors. Because guess what? As you learn when there is a pandemic, these people matter more to your lives than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, we`ve got a bit of election 2022 news to share. In the last week, three incumbent Democratic members of Congress have announced they will not be seeking re-election. All of them longtime members of this conference. Pennsylvania`s Mike Doyle, the dean of the Pennsylvania delegation for Democrats. North Carolina`s David Price. He`s been in Congress there for decades. Kentucky`s John Yarmuth, all say this term will be their last.\par \par \uc2 All veteran lawmakers, all have plumb committee assignments. It`s one of those things where one you can explain, three starts to look like a trend. A year out, but retirements from veteran lawmakers in relatively safe seats are often an early warning sign that certainly the majority party doesn`t think they`re going to be in the majority next year.\par \par \uc2 Keep an eye on this. This list does appear to keep growing and we`re entering that last season to announce whether or not you`re going to run.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, as America`s standing in relationships with our allies warm back up, our relationship with China keeps getting a bit chillier, like Cold War chilly. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:56:14]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. A little bit of positive news today for the White House on the global stage. According to a Gallup poll of 46 countries and territories, the median approval of U.S. leadership now stands at 49 percent. That is up nearly 20 point from the end of the Trump presidency. But at the same time, our relationship with China continues to deteriorate as that country continues its own version of saber-rattling.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2 . He`s president of \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 , author of the new book, "The World: A Brief Introduction."\par \par \uc2 And Richard, look, we`ve got a lot of challenges here on the Biden foreign policy plate. But I want to specifically look at China because -- obviously we`re in some version of a cold war here, we can debate the way, you know, from the previous cold war that we`re all have a memory of. But this one is going to be more difficult, but we`re so -- because we`re -- once so intertwined with China economically, and yet here we are about to start to face off more militarily.\par \par \uc2 Can we continue to work with them on climate change and the economy, and be this antagonistic on other things?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 , PRESIDENT, \uc2 COUNCIL ON FOREIGN RELATIONS\uc2 : What you`ve captured, Chuck, is the challenge to foreign policy or diplomacy or state craft. How do we compete with China? How do we push back where we have to, even militarily? But how do we protect selected areas of actual or potential cooperation, say on dealing with North Korea or Afghanistan, which China borders on, or on climate change? So that`s the foreign policy challenge for the United States going forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is China up to right now? You know, Xi is not leaving the country. You know, they do seem to be wanting to send the message that they`re a global superpower now.\par \par \uc2 HAASS: Well, they`re a global power. The world`s second largest economy. By many measure, they are the world`s second largest military in terms of spending. Their model is out there for all to see. Their \uc2 Belt and Road initiative\uc2  spans the world in terms of building infrastructure. They`re now the largest emitter of greenhouse gases on climate change.\par \par \uc2 So China is a heavyweight. That`s simply a fact of life. I think the real question for them is how they use their growing power, and the challenge for us is to try to shape that. Not to prevent China`s rise, but to get them to act responsibly as we see it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much of the saber-rattling is masking domestic problems? I mean, you have parts of the country can only get power a couple of days a week. You`ve got the issue with the real estate giant that could create a housing crisis there we dealt with in our country, could be worse. Part of it, I can`t help but look at some of saber-rattling and think this is about domestic politics, too.\par \par \uc2 HAASS: You may well be right. I don`t know how to say wag the dog in Mandarin, but you`ve got a slowing economy, you`ve got demographic problems, you`ve got environmental problems, you`ve got public health problems. The idea that Xi Jinping wants to abolish term limits and now go for a third term, as shall we say somewhat controversial, so it`s quite possible that he and others see increasingly that their legitimacy and public support will have to come from foreign policy rather than from things domestic, rather than from economic growth. That`s a dangerous possibility if it turns out to be real.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look at how Putin has operated that way now for over a decade and that has not improved U.S.-Russia relations. Let`s just say that.\par \par \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2 , I always wish we had more time, but it`s great to get your expertise on, sir. Thanks for visiting.\par \par \uc2 HAASS: Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We appreciate it. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue this very moment with my friend Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: It is great to be with you. I am Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211020ehaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 18, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Andrea Mitchell, Courtney Kube, Pete Williams, Uche  Blackstock, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Shannon Pettypiece, Tom Winter \par \uc2 9,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: General Powell wrote in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post, you touched my soul and reminded me about what this country is all about and why it is so great. Let`s stop screaming at each other. Let`s just take care of each other. You made my day.\par \par \uc2 My deepest condolences to General Powell`s beloved wife, Alma, his children, his grandchildren, and his devoted longtime colleague, Peggy Cifrino. "MEET THE PRESS DAILY" starts right now with Garrett Haake, in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake. We continue to follow reaction here at home and all over the world to the death of General Colin Powell, the former Secretary of State, Military Leader, two-time recipient of the Presidential Medal of Freedom, and a political icon too many, passed away this morning at Walter Reed Hospital due to complications from COVID-19. According to his family, he was 84 years old and fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Powell had been suffering from multiple myeloma type of blood cancer that can weaken the body`s ability to fight infections.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, President Biden put out a statement remembering Powell`s life and legacy saying that he "embodied the highest ideals of both warrior and diplomat."\par \par \uc2 In the last hour, we heard from Secretary of State Anthony Blinken, we heard him speak about Powell`s lasting impact on American diplomats stationed around the world.\par \par \uc2 And this morning, Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin spoke in personal terms after learning of the loss of his mentor, and friend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLYOD AUSTIN, DEFENSE SECRETARY: I feel as if I have a hole in my heart, just learning of this just recently.\par \par \uc2 First African-American Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, first African-American Secretary of State, a man who was respected around the globe, and who will be, quite frankly, it is not possible to replace a Colin Powell. We will miss him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`ve also heard from former President Bush, he called Powell, a family man, and a friend. Former Vice President Cheney praise Powell`s commitment to the armed forces.\par \par \uc2 We expect more reaction from those who served with Powell from those inside the White House, from members of Congress, and from others as the day continues.\par \par \uc2 Powell`s death in many ways is about much more though than the loss of an elder statesman or a political icon. It`s a reminder of a more civil, less partisan, and imperfect, yes, but a bygone era in American politics, and in Republican politics, especially.\par \par \uc2 As a Republican, his endorsement of Barack Obama`s presidential campaign was a major moment in American politics, an endorsement he made here on MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLIN POWELL, FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: I think back to my Army career, we`ve got two individuals, either one of them, could be a good President. But which is the President that we need now? Which is the individual that serves the needs of the nation for the next period of time?\par \par \uc2 And I come to the conclusion that because of his ability to inspire, because of the inclusive nature of his campaign, because he is reaching out all across America, because of who he is, and his rhetorical abilities, we have to take that into account, as well as his substance, he has both style and substance. He has met the standard of being a successful President, being an exceptional President.\par \par \uc2 I think he is a transformational figure. He is a new generation coming into the world -- onto the world stage, on the American stage. And for that reason, I`ll be voting for Senator Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Now that`s an endorsement. At the same time, Powell`s life serves as a complicated reminder of the mistakes made by U.S. leadership, which included his prominent role in helping to convince the U.N. that Saddam Hussein had weapons of mass destruction when he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- those failures of leadership that arguably fueled or at least set in motion on the generation of political turmoil and cynicism in America, the fallout from which we`re still struggling to wrap our arms around to this day.\par \par \uc2 With me now to talk about it all is NBC News Chief Foreign Affairs Correspondent Andrea Mitchell, Courtney Kube, who covers the Pentagon for NBC News, and NBC News Justice Correspondent Pete Williams, who served as a Pentagon spokesman during George H. W. Bush`s administration. And in a moment, we`ll speak with \uc2 Andy Card\uc2  who was White House Chief of Staff during the George W. Bush administration.\par \par \uc2 So, Andrea, I think people have different knowledge of Colin Powell based on when they first became aware of him in the various roles that he played throughout our government and throughout modern American history. As a diplomat, how do you think he`ll be remembered and what will his legacy be in how the State Department conducts its business?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, I think it goes well beyond that, frankly. When I got to First Amendment was in reshaping the Reagan administration after the Iran- Contra scandal at the National Security Council, he was deputy and then- National Security Adviser.\par \par \uc2 And he always like to be called General, not Mr. Secretary in his afterlife, leaving public life -- at least official public life, because he was proudest of his role as an infantryman as Joint Chiefs Chairman.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Memorably elevated as people remembers to that role by Dick Cheney to make the point largely that he was the first black Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, but he was elevated over several people of higher rank and more experience.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that he helped reshape the American military in the post- Vietnam era, and then, he argued against the war -- against the second Iraq war, the second Gulf War internally, but felt that he was misled and regretted always that he was misled.\par \par \uc2 Despite doing his homework at the Pentagon at -- excuse me, the CIA that weekend, and trying to scrub his testimony to get the U. N., he felt that his copybook his Secretary of State was always spoiled by that presentation about weapons of mass destruction that did not exist.\par \par \uc2 And so, there was a falling out with Dick Cheney and Don Rumsfeld, who had argued so forcefully for the war and had believed in the WMD analysis, which proved to be wrong, tragically.\par \par \uc2 That said, I think his largest legacy is in human rights and minority rights. And in the arguments that he made against the indignities of the Trump era, the indignities of social media -- the excesses of social media, just that he believed so strongly, in what he did in supporting historically black colleges and establishing the Colin Powell Institute at CCNY, which was his alma mater, in helping immigrants such as his family`s -- Jamaican family in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 And for everything he`s suffered in the Jim Crow South as an infantryman, and when he was first courting his wife, Alma, in Alabama, and had to drive from the Bronx down to Alabama and back and get through that terrible time in the 50s.\par \par \uc2 And go through everything they did as a black man in the segregated south, and in the segregated military, really de facto segregated military. So, all that he did for the military, and for education with America`s Promise, that, I think is the largest legacy.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Pete, Andrea, teed it up here a little bit the role that Dick Cheney played in Clubhouse rise. You were right there with Secretary -- then-Chairman Powell, I guess working with him every day in your -- in your previous life at the Defense Department, tackle about what you saw during his rise and what he was like as a colleague.\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Very accessible, and you know, a lot of military people are sort of afraid of the press. He never was.\par \par \uc2 I remember the first day he came into office, we sat down and he said, do we have a hotline? Meaning, you press a button on your desk and the phone rings on the other person`s desk. And I said, no, your predecessor didn`t want one. He said I`ll take care of that. He put it in the next day.\par \par \uc2 He was, of course, a wonderful, briefer, he, I think, exuded a lot of confidence, and that was an important thing during the Gulf War. Remember the famous thing he said of the Powell doctrine, and also that he said the pottery barn analogy --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You break it, you buy it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- you break it you buy it about Iraq. But let me just give you one small example of something that I think is kind of illuminating because Powell once said that he thought his greatest skill was as a problem solver.\par \par \uc2 1982, Bob Hope, wants to do another USO show. And his people call up and I talked to Mr. Hope many times myself, they said that he had never been given an honorary four-star general designation. So, I talked to General Powell about this and he said, you know, we don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Even for somebody like Bob Hope, the army doesn`t give people honorary four-star Generals.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s incredible.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, this went back and forth, and it was getting pretty tense, and finally Powell came up with a solution, to Bob Hope, our four-star hero.\par \par \uc2 And you know, that was the kind of skill he had. He was just -- he was just a great guy, great sense of humor, wonderful person to work with.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s fantastic. Courtney, at the Pentagon now, what is General Powell`s legacy there? I mean, Pete alluded to the Powell doctrine, the idea that if you`re going to go in, you`re doing it with overwhelming force, the pottery barn rule, which has a good role, really in all of life. But what is -- what is the modern impact of General Powell on the modern Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So, there`s two main things that I`ve been hearing about today, since we`ve got the news that Colin Powell had passed away.\par \par \uc2 One is his legacy as a trailblazer in the military. You know, we`ve heard he was the first African-American Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, he was also the youngest at the time, it`s just 52 when he got his four-star and took over as chairman.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, even before that, he blazed a trail for African-American men and women, and that leads to the second most -- the second thing I`ve been hearing the most about today and that is his role as a mentor, and a counselor, and an adviser to men and women, still today.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 KUBE: You know, we heard from Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin, who called him his mentor and someone who is still called on. We hear the same from Secretary of State Antony Blinken, earlier in his remarks, saying that he was someone that -- he would still reach out to Colin Powell and ask for his advice.\par \par \uc2 Well, people now -- I`m hearing more and more who are retired, who are still in the military now, people who have been out for a number of years actually, who still said they would reach out to Colin Powell, even in the more recent days, asking his advice and counsel.\par \par \uc2 And to a person, they keep telling me how he was someone who is very generous with his time, and who was willing to help even when someone was a more -- much more junior rank than he was in the military.\par \par \uc2 He will be remembered, according to people I`ve been speaking to, a number of people now who are pour -- attributes are pouring into him, saying that he was someone who not only showed them what was possible for a young African-American man or woman in the military, if we remember he came in during Vietnam, a tough time for this military, but also as someone who knew how to mentor younger men and women and was willing to put that time in for the next generation of military leaders, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And \uc2 Andy Card\uc2 , if we have you now, as a four-star General coming into a White House, how did his experience -- how did -- how did a person of his stature crossing over?\par \par \uc2 I mean, he was sort of the biggest military to political crossover since Dwight Eisenhower, how did that shape his role as the Bush administration side in your government and the role he played behind the scenes in that White House?\par \par \uc2 ANDY CARD\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF DURING GEORGE W. BUSH ADMINISTRATION: Well, I was pleased to have met Colin Powell all the way back in 1983, when I worked in the Reagan White House, and he was working there. And worked with him in George H. W Bush`s administration, when I was Secretary of Transportation, he was Chairman of the Joint Chiefs.\par \par \uc2 We had a remarkable experience. And I can tell you that when he became Secretary of State, he already had a commanding presence on the world stage, having been the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs.\par \par \uc2 And that commanding presence made a big difference. In terms of the White House, he was always candid with his words, he was not trying to be part of any movement, instead, he was trying to make sure the President had wise counsel.\par \par \uc2 So, it was frequently that Colin Powell would offer contrarian advice in meetings. And he would do it in such a way that he wasn`t argumentative, but he just caused you to open your eyes and your ears, listen a bit -- a little bit better, and then kind of look to see what other solutions we should talk about.\par \par \uc2 He was -- he was a very strong-willed person, but he did not bully his way in any meetings. We had strong views, but he was comfortable.\par \par \uc2 In terms of my relationship with them, we had a wonderful relationship. I had tough conversations with him, and he always appreciated it, and understood what I was saying and when I was saying it, but he was -- he was a joy to work with.\par \par \uc2 I happen to think that one of the greatest legacies of Colin Powell`s life was how much compassion he had for other people. We remember what he did with the little red wagon campaign that helped young people all across the country. He was truly a bright point of light in terms of making a difference for the world.\par \par \uc2 And he did mentor people well, but he also cared deeply about people who had AIDS, and he helped get us AIDS Relief to people in Africa.\par \par \uc2 He did an awful lot in the United States. I`ve worked closely with him when he went to Haiti after the first boatlift of Haitians, when he had left the administration of George H. W. Bush, and when -- and was asked by President Clinton to help make sure that Haiti could be reopened and accepting of the people that had escaped Haiti and gone to Guantanamo when they were brought back to Haiti. So, I`m a witness to how much compassion he had.\par \par \uc2 I`m also a witness to the great relationship that he and Alma Powell have. There were a husband and wife team that made a tremendous difference for a lot of people, and they were a great complement with an E to each other.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Pete, I want to give you the last question here, it`s the toughest. But how should we view the Iraq War -- the second Iraq war, the famous U.N. speech that Colin Powell gave us as part of his legacy here? I mean, you cannot talk about him without that speech.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think what he would say and in fact, what he has said about it is that he had his doubts, he had his internal differences, but that was the decision the administration made, and he was working and a loyal employee of the administration, a loyal servant to the President, and that was his job. That was his mission. So, I think that`s the way -- that I think that`s the way he would say it that he was -- he was doing his job.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Andy, anything you want to add on that point?\par \par \uc2 CARD: Well, you know, it was a very tough time for Colin Powell when -- and I remember the preparation that he put into going up to the United Nations before that address. And he was very skeptical of the information. He challenged the CIA, and the \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2 , and the other intelligence community, people who were helping to draft his remarks, and he ended up being comfortable with it, but I could tell deep down inside, he was skeptical.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 CARD: And he did it, he then recognized that it wasn`t his leadership that would drive the day, it was the President`s decisions. And he said that he would help the President implement that challenge.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that`s what a great soldier does. And he was candid with his advice and counsel, yes, he challenged people very hard, but if the President made a decision, he would help make sure that to the best of his ability, that decision would work out to be the right decision.\par \par \uc2 So, he worked hard to correct problems after they showed up. And the Iraq War, it was tough, I mean, those were tough issues of dealing with those intelligence community recommendations.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Pete, Courtney, Andy, and Andrea, thank you all. And as we said, Powell died from complications of COVID, but it`s really not just that simple.\par \par \uc2 Let`s now bring in MSNBC Medical Contributor Dr. Uche Blackstock. And Doctor, Powell is obviously fully vaccinated against COVID, but he had underlying conditions that left him vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 As a doctor, what do you want people to know and to understand about the risk factors that were in play here, and what General Powell actually died of?\par \par \uc2 DR. UCHE BLACKSTOCK, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Right. Thank you so much for having me on, Garrett. So, I think it`s important for people to understand that on Colin Powell`s situation was quite unique.\par \par \uc2 He was immunocompromised, meaning he had a weakened immune system, he had multiple myeloma, which is a blood cancer that actually affects the blood cells that are responsible for making antibodies. And so, these patients are at baseline at risk for infections, they usually actually die from infections.\par \par \uc2 And so, he was especially vulnerable because of this immunocompromised status, and because of his advanced age. So, even though he was fully vaccinated, he still was at elevated risk for contracting the infection and dying from it.\par \par \uc2 That`s very important for people to understand that the vaccines are still very effective, but in certain populations, you still are at risk, which is so important to understand why we have to take advantage of other strategies in addition to vaccination, to mitigate the spread of this virus.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I feel like this is a classic example of the community elements to this entire pandemic, right?\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: That may.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I mean, Powell did his part to protect himself, but there`s only so much anyone individual can do. Can you kind of, put that into perspective here as we think about vaccination rates and levels continuing to climb across this country but the importance of unvaccinated people still continuing to get those shots to protect others?\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: And that is such an important point, Garrett, that, you know, are immunocompromised community members or members of advanced age can be fully vaccinated and still at risk, which is why we need a collective response for a public health emergency and that is what we`re in right now.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we do need as many people as possible to get vaccinated and I think that`s why vaccine outreach and education is still incredibly important, especially those folks who still have concerns or skepticism about the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And also to recognize that we still need those other strategies, masking, physical distancing, testing, ventilation, filtration, until we can get the transmission levels down across the country.\par \par \uc2 Rates are still incredibly high. We had 13,000 deaths last week alone, from COVID-19, it was the number one leading cause of death. And so, this pandemic is nowhere near over and that`s why we need everyone to play a part in helping to end it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So Doc, a patient says to you, but you know, Colin Powell was vaccinated, his vaccine didn`t work. What do you saying?\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Right. So, I would like people to understand that the vaccines are still highly effective, they may not be 100 percent effective, but they still are highly effective.\par \par \uc2 We`ve given vaccines to about 200 million Americans, we have only about 7000 breakthroughs that, and so that is like point .ooo3 percent. It`s a very, very small to the fraction of a percentage.\par \par \uc2 However, every death is tragic, especially a preventable death. And so, we still need people to get vaccinated because vaccines work at a population level.\par \par \uc2 The more people that are vaccinated, the more effective they are, and the - - and the more -- and the greater our ability to end this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Dr. Blackstock, thank you very much for your perspective on this.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And before we go, we should note that moments ago, President Biden said that flags will be flown at half-staff at the White House and other government facilities through Friday in memory of General Powell.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the clock is ticking for Democrats to strike a deal on President Biden`s agenda. We`ve got the latest from the White House and the Hill next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And welcome back. Turning now to the latest from Capitol Hill where Democrats find themselves at a rip the band-aid off moment in the ongoing talks over President Biden`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 It`s been a difficult couple of days for the party following reports that Joe Manchin has taken positions against Progressives on two priorities that they see as vital to their agenda.\par \par \uc2 President Biden met with Congressional Progressive Caucus Chair Pramila Jayapal this morning, she`ll eventually have to sign off on any deal the White House makes to get Moderates like Manchin on board.\par \par \uc2 And according to the New York Times, White House staffers are now rewriting the legislation after Manchin told them he would not support a clean electricity plan, which was a pillar of the White House`s climate agenda.\par \par \uc2 It`s also something by the way, that Manchin has been telling anyone who would listen to, for weeks, that he was against. According to Axios, Manchin has also told the White House that he wants major eligibility restrictions on the child tax credit, which was a pillar of the White House`s working families agenda.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line here is that something is -- the timeline is shrinking here now and something is going to have to get done if Democrats want to give Terry McAuliffe a boost in the upcoming governor`s race in Virginia, and for so much more of the Democratic agenda.\par \par \uc2 And if Democrats didn`t know exactly how much they were going to have to cut out of the reconciliation bill to get it to actually pass, they are, at least, if you want a silver lining here, getting a better idea now.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Shannon Pettypiece is outside the White House for us and NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell`s on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So Leigh Ann, obviously, really tough news for Democrats on all of these reports about what Joe Manchin is against. But is the fact that we at least now know what Joe Manchin is against a form of progress for congressional Democrats.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I suppose Garrett. I mean, the reality is I could have gone to the moon for two weeks and come back. And so not that much has changed in this storyline, we still have Progressives and Moderates on opposite sides of the coin, you still have people fighting with Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that has changed is that the fight with Joe Manchin has escalated. You have Senator Bernie Sanders writing an op-ed in a West -- the biggest West Virginia newspaper calling out Joe Manchin saying that the Democrats` plan would help West Virginians.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, we have a few more details specifically of what Joe Manchin will or will not do. So -- but we`ve known for a long time that he wants to create work requirements on and means-test. A lot of these government safety programs, we`ve known 