rate Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema because they want different things.\par \par \uc2 There`s reporting out there that Senator Manchin doesn`t necessarily love the paid family leave component of the bill. And I was told a couple of weeks ago by people close to Senator Sinema that that is one of the things she does like in the bill.\par \par \uc2 Also, she supposedly likes the climate provisions in the bill, something that Senator Manchin does not. But Senator Sinema doesn`t like the tax increases in the bill. And that`s how Democrats are going to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, like I said before, they have to make some very difficult decisions and see if they can keep their party and their members in line.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Shannon, really quickly, how much are the various deadlines here weighing on the White House? Be they the end of October deadline, be day, the Virginia election where Terry McAuliffe is out there screaming at congressional or Washington Democrats broadly to get something done here, how much is the White House aware of all the time on the clock here to get this bill together and passed?\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: I mean, there have been so many deadlines, though. I remember there was a memorial day deadline, there was a July 4 deadline, there was a you know, back from Labor Day, and now there`s a Halloween deadline, I guess these all have holidays in common.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Soon there will be a Christmas and a New Year`s Eve deadline. You know, I think certainly the end of the year is really seen as a deadline because there is that recognition that going into 2022 things will really fundamentally change in an election year. So I think maybe that`s the only real firm deadline. You know, the White House, they has been pretty content at this point to continue pushing deadlines back from one holiday to the next as it seems.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maybe they`re operating on a Lunar calendar? I`m sorry, I`m trying to draw the astronaut space thing in here together and it`s just not working for me.\par \par \uc2 Shannon and Leigh Ann, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, I`ll speak with a Republican governor who`s clashing with members of his party over some vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As the fight over vaccines becomes even more political, even moderate Republican governors who support vaccinations are facing questions about vaccine mandates. Unlike Texas`s Gregg Abbott or Florida`s Ron DeSantis, who oppose any type of mandate, New Hampshire`s Chris Sununu says he supports private company`s rights to impose mandates but Sununu told CNBC this morning that New Hampshire will be joining a lawsuit challenging the Biden`s administration`s federal vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire boasts one of the highest vaccination rates in the country. More than 62 percent of eligible residents are fully vaccinated. And the number of new COVID cases statewide have plateaued there as well.\par \par \uc2 Governor Chris Sununu joins me now.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 And I guess, Governor, the first question is, why support private businesses making mandates but not a federal government saying that the private businesses have to mandate a vaccine or a test?\par \par \uc2 GOV. CHRIS SUNUNU (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE: Well, again, that`s the line between the public and the private sector. And really appreciating and respecting the individual rights of those businesses. You know, again, we want everyone to get vaccinated and I`m as pro-vaccine as they come in that sense. But when Joe Biden said I`m just going to just create some new rules and force a mandate into private businesses, that`s not appropriate.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to join that lawsuit with other states and push back because those businesses, again, have to have the flexibility to do what they want to do. On the same side, you can`t have it both ways. The other side of that coin is you see some Republicans out there saying, well, we`re going to have an anti-vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 How about this? How about we stay to Republican conservative values and saying it`s not the government`s place to decide who private businesses hire and fire. We`re not talking about a protected class here. So even though I might want everyone to be vaccinated, and we push that as hard as we can, the executives, the governors, the president, we`re the ones that have the biggest responsibility to knowing the limitations of that power.\par \par \uc2 And, again, just like I don`t think the president can do a pro-vaccine mandate, I don`t think governors should be doing anti-vaccine mandates. Businesses need the flexibility to manage their business how they see fit and not let government intervene.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are you seeing evidence that business-based vaccines in New Hampshire are encouraging vaccinations, that they`re working?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: A little bit here and there. There`s different businesses, different sectors. I think the larger discussion not just in New Hampshire but across the country is what you`re seeing in hospitals. And again hospitals are private entities. They can decide to do that if they want to. We don`t want to see any nurses get fired. Nobody wants to see that across the country. We have a very fragile health care system based on staffing.\par \par \uc2 And so I`ve talked to a lot of the CEOs of hospitals and said, look, it`s your right to do so, but please take into consideration the ramifications of that. What it could mean to, you know, pushing folks out the door. We don`t have a statewide mandate in any way. We don`t have a statewide mandate on our employees, but private entities such as a hospital or Fred`s flower shop, it doesn`t matter. Those folks need the ability to make those decisions for themselves.\par \par \uc2 As soon as you open that Pandora`s Box where government is saying who you can hire and who should fire, whether you`re Republican or Democrat on one side or the other, that box can`t be closed. Now you`re going to have governors and other administrations using all these other flexibilities that they deem necessary in the moment to really control business. And that`s very un-American.\par \par \uc2 It`s very not Republican, not conservative. I think it`s very un-American. And so that`s where I`m pushing back saying we need to be consistent, if anything.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is that a betrayal of conservative principles for a governor like Greg Abbott or Ron DeSantis to tell a business what they can or can`t do in terms of protecting their employees?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I don`t know if it`s a betrayal. I think they`re caught up in the moment. I think they`re caught up in the moment with trying to -- you know, I believe and I think they agree with me that we need to push back on the Biden vaccine mandate using these OSHA rules that haven`t even been created. We`re just seeing the draft versions of them now. And so their quick reaction is well, we`re going to do it on our side to fight back without realizing it`s not about the fight.\par \par \uc2 That`s about an infringement on business. I ran a business. I had 800 employees. And I can`t believe that -- if the state government or the federal government would have come to tell me, sir, you have to hire that person. You can`t fire that person.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: That`s just a big mess. And so I think it`s more of a reaction. I think it`s not appropriate. And so I`m just trying to push back a little bit and say, as governors, we have a responsibility to know the limitations of our power.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Would business owner Chris Sununu have a mandate, too, that his employees be vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. Probably not. Again, I really believe it`s people`s individual choice. It might mean I have to manage differently or have to create different situations to make sure everyone is safe and all that, but that`s something I would probably just choose to manage around. It`s not about -- you know, getting a vaccine is a medical, a very personal medical choice. And so that`s where I think there is just a line we want to encourage you. We want everyone to be vaccinated and get your boosters. You really need that part, too, but at the same time, it is a personal choice, and we need to be supportive of that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Really quickly, have you made a decision yet or when do you think you will announce a decision on whether or not you will be running for Senate?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Do I put your suggestion in now? I`m taking all suggestions, Senate, governor, private sector.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, I know you`re getting a lot of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know there`s a lot of Senate Republicans who are asking you and talking to you about it. Do you have a timeline for making a decision?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I said, you know, I had the luxury and responsibility of waiting a little bit because look, New Hampshire, we get elected every two years. So, you know, the state doesn`t need another giant campaign. I know Senator Hassett is already running tens of millions of dollars of ads here. And look, the people of our state need a little time off, and so I have the responsibility of not jumping into a race if that`s what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 I`m really -- I`m busy managing the state. But I`d probably make a decision sooner than later. I said I`d make a decision sometime in the winter or in the early winter, and I`m still on that timeframe. I haven`t really decided yet. I`m kind of taking a lot of advice and talking to my family. And look, I`m an executive, I`m a manager. I like to design systems and create solutions.\par \par \uc2 Senate and Congress don`t really do that. Right? They do two things. They vote up and down a policy. They vote up and down funding. And then they kind of go on to the next thing. And 80 percent of them are really tied up of being into more fighting with each other than actually getting stuff done.\par \par \uc2 I like to get stuff done. So I got to make sure that my skill set fits Washington, and frankly, Washington could handle me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: And so, you know, it has to be the right fit for everyone.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Governor Sununu, thanks. Rolling deadline is a bit of a theme of our last two blocks here. Thank you for coming on, Governor.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, after repeated delays, former President Trump answered questions today under oath as at least 10 civil cases including ones alleging fraud and incitement of violence are pending against him. We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Former President Donald Trump answered questions under oath this morning. Now this is not for anything related to the January 6th attack on the Capitol or even anything related to his presidency, really. But for a deposition in a lawsuit stemming from his first presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 The suit stems from a 2015 incident where a group of individuals say they were assaulted by members of Trump`s security staff outside Trump Tower while they were protesting the then candidate and his statements on immigration. They want Trump held personally liable for the incident.\par \par \uc2 The deposition today was ordered by a judge after Trump and his lawyers cancelled or delayed the deposition several times already, which just goes to show how long it can sometimes take to force testimony from someone who doesn`t want to give it. That will be a theme of our reporting this week.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Tom Winter has more details on this case.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, the former president testifying this morning in this case. Can you give us just a little bit more background on this suit and what his testimony means here or what it doesn`t mean?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, it`s exactly as you said, Garrett. At the end of the day it boils down to a group of protesters that say they were assaulted by Trump`s body guard, including Keith Schiller, Trump`s personal body guard and kind of right-hand man, leading up to the point that he got Secret Service protection, then obviously he stayed on with the president.\par \par \uc2 The allegations in this incident comes on an important day in then candidate Trump`s candidacy when he came forward and said that he promised that he would not run as a third-party candidate. There was a ton of media outside of Trump Tower that day. And that`s one of the things that`s going to make this lawsuit so difficult for some of the people named it specifically the body guards because a lot of this alleged incident and these assaults were actually captured on camera.\par \par \uc2 So basically what the six individuals are looking for today, they`re looking for damages for their ongoing medical bills that they say that they have for the pain and suffering, but they`re also looking for punitive damages. And that`s where this could potentially become a costly problem for the former president.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Now, Tom, this doesn`t seem to me like the most potentially damaging or potentially troublesome lawsuit for the former president. He`s got a bunch coming down the pipe. Right? Related to any number of other issues from before and during his presidency. How do you see the legal landscape in front of the former president over the next couple of months?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Yes, I mean, I think you`re right. I think it does become a little bit more difficult for him because there are some pretty serious lawsuits that he faces. One, from Democratic lawmakers and members of the U.S. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  tied to January 6th, and his statements that he made in that rally leading up to the now infamous Capitol riot and assault that occurred on that day. The president says that was just him utilizing his free speech as the president at the time. But that`s a lawsuit that has some potential issues for him.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s two others involving two women, Summer Zervos and E. Jean Carroll. Both raising allegations of defamation against the president that he smeared them when he denied that the president -- that he kissed her also without her consent. And then the allegations brought by Jean Carroll, even more serious when the president said that he would never have raped a woman that looked like that essentially was what he said and how he responded to her claims that she was raped by Donald Trump before he was a candidate back in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 So those are just three of 10 lawsuits, and he`s also filed his own against two "Times" reporters and Mary Trump for an article that the "Times" did based on his taxes. So a lot of legal work ahead in Donald Trump`s future. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: A lot of potential depositions. And we`ll be grateful to have you covering it for us.\par \par \uc2 Tom Winter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And up next, an update on strike-tober.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. What could have been the biggest entertainment labor strike since World War II has been averted. At least for now. The International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees which represents camera operators, makeup artists, editors and more, announced a tentative agreement with the major production studios this weekend. According to "Variety," some members aren`t satisfied with the agreements made for working conditions on set and may yet reject the deal when it comes up for a vote.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:03]\par \par \uc2 And that`s just one instance of ongoing turmoil in the labor market. After nearly two years of pandemic, millions of Americans have decided not to return to their high-stress, low-paying jobs. And many of those who are staying are demanding better pay and working conditions. And if their demands aren`t being met, well, they`re headed to the picket line.\par \par \uc2 And there have been more than 180 union strikes this year. Over 24,000 health care workers at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  in Oregon and California are preparing to strike. And over 10,000 John Deere workers went on strike on Thursday. Tomorrow Kellogg`s workers will enter into the third week of their strike over pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a deep dive into how we teach our nation`s history and some states and school districts try to whitewash parts of the painful pasts of this country.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. This country is in the midst of what is arguably the most important debate about school curricula in decades. The debate over how race is taught in American classrooms.\par \par \uc2 The fight is raging in school boards and in state legislatures across the country as more and more states take steps to restrict how the history of racism and slavery is taught in classrooms. In many cases just whitewashing some of the most painful parts of American history right out. Just last week teachers in a Texas school district were directed to provide differing perspectives on topics like slavery or the holocaust in their curriculum.\par \par \uc2 In a new documentary "CIVIL WAR" premiering this Sunday on MSNBC, we`re taking a deep dive into how we teach perhaps the most divisive chapter of American history. The film explores how geography, race and tradition shaped the historic narratives we tell and the ones we don`t tell about the Civil War and how that impacts race in America today. Here`s a preview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID BLIGHT, STERLING PROFESSOR OF AMERICAN HISTORY, YALE UNIVERSITY: We`ve never really had a racial reckoning. The problem started first immediately after the war.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 If you want North and South to get together and get along again, you don`t talk about causes and consequences. You talk about the mutual valor on that battlefield.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I`m joined now by Keith Mayes, a professor of African-American Studies at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, Professor, I want your reaction to that clip there from Dr. Blight, talking about how in the immediate aftermath of the Civil War the narrative changed what we`re going to talk about and how that affects the way we talk about that period in history today.\par \par \uc2 PROF. KEITH MAYES, UNIVERSITY OF MINNESOTA PROFESSOR OF AFRICAN-AMERICAN STUDIES: Thanks for having me on, Garrett. Well, I think that part of the problem that we have in schools and in politics is that we can`t seem to grasp why it is important for us to tell the history as it occurred, that people have to understand that the Civil War was a major watershed moment in American history.\par \par \uc2 And yes, it represented the moment and the era of black emancipation and black freedom. But it also meant that the South lost something. What did it lose? It lost a way of life. It lost its economy. It lost its social structure in many ways. And I think that it`s hard for people who lived in that time of culture to reconcile what happened. Hence that`s why you have various names to the event.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks in the South, you know, saw it as a lost cause or they saw it as the war of northern aggression. These kinds of interpretations mean that there are folks who are still wedded to various narratives that shaped their reality in many ways, and still does.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I mean, when you look at things like, you know, the Texas law here which shows how far people are willing to go to preserve that narrative, what`s your takeaway from lawmakers trying to kind of encode this kind of type of thinking into legislatures across the country? Is it just driven from a place of fear? And I guess to me the question is, fear of what?\par \par \uc2 MAYES: Fear of progress. Fear of racial progress. Fear of a kind of demographic --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But what does that mean? Fear that your African-American neighbor is going to be as well off as you? I mean, it`s sort of embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 MAYES: It is embarrassing. But it`s a fear that the way in which we imagine the racial state means that the level of equality as it first represents this demographic shift in the country is something that lawmakers have to pay attention to, have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 But I think we can`t forget, Garrett, that this is also about politics. So the rewriting of southern history was a civil rights era phenomenon. The rewriting of that southern history that`s occurring today is also part and parcel of the moment that we live in now, with the rise of the conservative right. So I think that history and politics have always been married in that way. And these are not well-meaning politicians who have decided that they want to pass these laws.\par \par \uc2 This is about the current state of our political environment right now. And it has less to do with what actually happened in history, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right, if you can gin up people to fight about a culture war issue, you don`t have to talk about other uncomfortable facts that you may not want.\par \par \uc2 I`m super curious, as a professor, now granted I imagine the students who select your class, there`s a self-selection here of people who are already interested in African-American issues when they come into your class. But what do you find when you take kids who are fresh out of high school that they know or that they don`t know? What presents to you from the high school students of America when they come before you in terms of the gaps in their knowledge or in their understanding of this country`s racial past?\par \par \uc2 MAYES: So they understand the current reality because they`ve lived it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: They lived it. Sure.\par \par \uc2 MAYES: And they may have participated in many of the social movements that are occurring today in society. But they may not necessarily know what happened in the 19th Century. They`ve heard of the civil war. They may have actually heard of the Civil Rights Movement. But they have come to the class wanting to connect the dots.\par \par \uc2 How do we get from 1861 and 1865 to let`s say 1965? What are the things that sort of -- why do we have to revisit things like voting rights? Why do we have to revisit questions of American citizenship? And so they know that something is fundamentally wrong because we still are fighting for the same kinds of things that many of the ancestors fought for over 150 some odd years ago.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s a fascinating topic. Professor Keith Mayes, thank you for joining us. And you can catch the full documentary, "Civil War" this Sunday at 10 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. That`s going to do it for us for this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211019ehai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 15, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Mike Memoli, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Morgan Radford, Jose  Diaz-Balart \par \uc2 9,709 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Follow the show online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @MitchellReports. Chuck Todd with "MTP DAILY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, President Biden hits the road. He`s about to deliver remarks on the importance of passing his agenda as the White House pushes Congress to reach a deal and do it soon. Because they want to have something to sell voters, not just a next year`s midterms, but in this year`s November elections.\par \par \uc2 Plus, an FDA advisory panel is about to hold a key vote on whether or not to approve a booster shot for \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  vaccine recipients, as it also examines the potential benefits of the mixing-and-matching vaccine doses in limited circumstances.\par \par \uc2 And later, it`s the first big test of the polls before next year`s made terms. Can Democrats hang on in Virginia? Terry McAuliffe is trying to turn the race for governor into a referendum on Trump. We`ll show you how and why he wants to do that. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 President Biden once again is hitting the road in an effort to build some public support for his agenda, it comes as the White House is signaling a growing impatience with the state of negotiations among Democrats in Congress to actually pass it.\par \par \uc2 President is gearing up to deliver remarks later this hour in Connecticut to highlight his agenda`s potential benefits on the issue of childcare.\par \par \uc2 It`s just a critical piece of a multi-trillion-dollar agenda that a lot of Democrats see is vital for working family`s lives, and vital for their own political fortunes, as the party tries to hang on to those narrow congressional majorities in next year`s midterms.\par \par \uc2 And in a very real way, those midterms unofficially begin in just over two weeks in the state of Virginia, and that gubernatorial race.\par \par \uc2 It`s a race, which is often really been a pretty good foreshadower of you - - of sorts of what the following year`s midterm environment is going to look like for both parties. And right now, it`s a tight race in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 So, for the White House, for Democrats, and for the Democratic gubernatorial candidate, Terry McAuliffe, there`s an added sense of urgency to have these talks wrapped up by the end of this month, just as voters are casting their ballots.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE (D-VA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Let`s get everybody in a room, lock the door, what do you need? What do you need? And let`s get this thing done. You know, all these folks up here, they only love to go out and do their press conferences. Do your job, vote, and get this done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The White House is also signaling its impatience with the talks on Capitol Hill, and that, frankly, is a bit curious because it makes it look as if they`re in the backseat in these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 And it introduces a strange question for us to ask, but it is this. Who`s in charge of the talks right now, over Biden`s agenda, if it`s not the White House and the President?\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there are very few indications out there that a deal is close. Senators, Manchin and Sinema still have fundamental disagreements with the size and scope of the plan. They don`t even agree with each other, let alone the rest of the Democratic Conference.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , he`s still pushing for the $3.5 trillion top- line number, even though the President, Speaker of the House, pretty much everybody else has acknowledged it`s going to actually be -- have to be lowered to actually pass. But he hasn`t given up on expanding Medicare benefits, which is extraordinarily costly, but that is a sticking point.\par \par \uc2 And we still haven`t seen them all in the same room to try to hammer out their differences. And, frankly, until you see that, it`s hard to see a deal coming together any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli is following the President in Hartford, Connecticut for us as he tries to sell his agenda there, Leigh Ann Caldwell is on duty on Capitol Hill, and also with us is somebody who`s had his share of back and forth with Congress, former Obama White House Press Secretary Robert Gibbs.\par \par \uc2 Mike, let me start with you. You know, this has been an interesting week here in Washington because the President largely had the town to himself, right?\par \par \uc2 He had the Senate, it was out. The House was marginally in session. And let`s just say I thought he`d be more of a dominant presence this week than he was, but he is out there today selling his agenda.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chuck. We`ve heard more urgent language from the White House podium. Jen Psaki saying yesterday it`s time to move forward with these negotiations the day before. She said that time is not unlimited. But we haven`t necessarily seen that same urgency in the President`s schedule by any means.\par \par \uc2 We have been hearing for the last few weeks, how the President was going to be hitting the road more to speak to the American people about what was in his agenda and why it was so important to get it through.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is just the second event focused on Build Back Better in the last two weeks. Last week, he was in Michigan, obviously, then there was an also that canceled event that they`ve postponed -- rescheduled on vaccines. And so, we haven`t seen that same urgency.\par \par \uc2 But the White House is staring some real and some self-imposed deadlines in the face now, and there is, I think increased urgency and that`s why we are hearing from the White House that it`s time to maybe knock a few heads as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: And Chuck, one of those informal deadlines is the -- frankly the Virginia election, and I think Terry McAuliffe has probably been on the phone with Gavin Newsom.\par \par \uc2 And what did we see ahead of the California recall, President Biden, the administration took some more urgent and some people thought overdue stricter actions as it relates to COVID and vaccine requirements in the run-up to that election.\par \par \uc2 And we`re now seeing the increased urgency from the White House in response to that urgency being conveyed from Terry McAuliffe as he looks at his campaign.\par \par \uc2 That -- one of the other big deadlines coming up, Chuck, is the Glasgow Climate Summit. And --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. He wants some --\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: -- the President wants to be able to go to that summit --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: -- saying, I`ve signed, we`ve had -- we`ve taken these actions, and now we want you to do the same. And so, beyond the political focus here at home, he also doesn`t want to go empty-handed on the world stage, especially given what we`ve seen in terms of his foreign policy record over the last few months as well, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike, why Connecticut? Does this for Rosa DeLauro, who obviously is a key player and House Democrat? Is this something else going on? Why Connecticut?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: It might be more about Chris Dodd, his good friend from the Senate who is dedicating the Dodd Center at the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2  today --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: -- but what do you see childcare on the agenda, you know, also Rosa DeLauro, who has been the real champion of this for decades --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: -- is part of that as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got you. The Chris Dodd event, that does help explain the travel schedule a little bit there. Mike Memoli, on the road with the President in Hartford, Connecticut, we`ll see you when we hear from the President. Thank you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 All right, Leigh Ann, I have to tell you, it was a little jarring yesterday to find out the White House was not happy with the state of negotiations, which means, is Congress in charge of the negotiations? And if so, who`s running it?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s a good question, Chuck, and I`m not really sure. So, usually, Speaker Pelosi runs these negotiations, right? But, we haven`t really seen any public meetings, there hasn`t been any public statement, and we`re not really sure where things are landing.\par \par \uc2 We do know that because there was a report on "The New York Times" yesterday that Senator Kyrsten Sinema was in Europe, fundraising for the Democratic Party, that -- we received a statement from her office, it did not confirm that she was in Europe, but it did say that phones are everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And that just this week, Senator Sinema has spoken to President Biden, Leader Schumer, and Congressional Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Notice, she didn`t mention Speaker Pelosi in that conversation or in that statement, but Congressional Democrats are the ones, the Moderates, that she speaks to a lot including people like Stephanie Murphy, and Kathleen Rice, and as -- and others in that group. But there are seem to be no very for -- you know, public negotiations happening at this stage.\par \par \uc2 Senator Schumer put out a statement yesterday to his colleagues that was mostly focused on voting rights. That they`re going to vote on next week.\par \par \uc2 He did reiterate once again, that they hope to come to an agreement by the end of the month on this multi-trillion-dollar bill, but he said that over and over again. This is just another way that he said it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, yesterday, the White House -- we heard that the Biden -- that President Biden seems to now want to make sure that the bipartisan infrastructure Bill is passed by the end of this month, regardless of where the rest of the negotiations are. Is that -- has that taken hold at all on Capitol Hill, or is that just aspirational hope?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, the Progressives have not said that that`s OK, and they have enough votes to block whatever happens, and that`s what they did last time, and so it`s unclear that he has to work that out with the Progressives, and the Progressives have not publicly said that they are OK with that, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, we`ll find out, Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill for us. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, let me bring in Robert Gibbs. And, Robert, I don`t know about you, but I`m having a lot of 2009, 2010 flashbacks where, you know, think about all the deadlines. You guys are going to get healthcare done by the Fourth of July, before the August recess, you remember, March 2010.\par \par \uc2 What is the -- how important do you think it is at a minimum that there is some progress made, some things pass, maybe the infrastructure plan does get passed and signed before Virginia? And is Virginia deadline they ought to pay attention to?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: I do think it is important, Chuck. Look, as you mentioned, the Biden administration is way ahead of where we were on healthcare. So, let`s give them some credit for that.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know whether that`s giving them credit or taking it away from us. But look, I think that I take former Governor Terry McAuliffe on this.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I think he clearly wants to be able to show the democratic base, his base, that something is happening in a positive way in Washington.\par \par \uc2 I think -- look, I think the Virginia race is a little different in that -- that Washington media market is dominant in that electorate, unlike it might be in other places in the country. So, I have no doubt that people are turning on their local news and seeing what`s happening on Capitol Hill differently than they may be in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I think it is -- it is important, I think it`s getting the base excited. It`s quite frankly showing. And I think this is why this whole debate is important. It`s showing what can be delivered with a democratic majority.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I think in the end, failure really isn`t an option here. It has something has to get done, whether it`s before the Virginia gubernatorial election, or by the end of the year. Something has to get done to set up both a change in policy, but also a political message for 2022.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The President is the leader of the party, the White House, is the number one convening place you can craft, right? If you need to convene a meeting can mean. So there -- this is sort of an awkward way of saying.\par \par \uc2 I know, ultimately, the White House is going to be in charge of these negotiations, but it does seem as if they`re trying to have a hands-off approach. How long can they keep this hands-off approach before it sort of gets out of hand? Or is it already too late?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: No, I don`t think it`s already too late, Chuck. My guess is not unlike healthcare, there`s a lot of conversations, meetings, and phone calls that are happening that we don`t know about.\par \par \uc2 I think one thing that was obvious, both in healthcare, and I think in the last month on Capitol Hill, is public negotiation isn`t necessarily moving us toward the end of this game.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s important that those lines of communication happen. I think Chuck Schumer and Nancy Pelosi are going to be in charge of getting the votes.\par \par \uc2 But to your point, there`s only one person involved in this equation that can bring this process to an end, and that`s the President. I`m sure he`s got some senatorial tendencies after having spent a lot of time in that body.\par \par \uc2 But I think both Speaker Pelosi last week, and then President Biden through Press Secretary Jen Psaki, are showing their angst in meeting this process to come to its natural end. And I think that`s the beginning of that moving toward that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to react to something Charlie Cook wrote this week to pretty grim picture he`s drawing right now of the Democrats. This is what he writes.\par \par \uc2 Parties taking over have two basic dangers. The first is overreaching, going further than those voters in the middle can stomach. The second danger is appearing to be ineffective or even incompetent. Democrats push for way too much without having the political capital to make it stick. It`s killing them on overreach and it is killing them on competence. If misreading a mandate is a sin in politics, pretending that you have one when you don`t is a mortal sin.\par \par \uc2 Like I said, it`s a pretty harsh assessment. How -- what has to happen for that assessment not to stick in your mind?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Two things I think are important. One is, I think, people are going to have to come to the understanding that it isn`t going to be a $6 trillion bill, it isn`t going to be a $3.5trillion bill, it`s going to be a $1.5 to $2 trillion bill, right?\par \par \uc2 So -- and I think that`s essentially what Pelosi and Biden have been signaling. We have to understand that we`re not going to get what somebody might want in a perfect world.\par \par \uc2 And secondly, I think, you know, having both the President continue to be out there, but members of Congress have to talk about not just how much but, what.\par \par \uc2 We know from the polling that many aspects of the Build Back Better agenda are highly popular. But not that many people understand that`s what Democrats are fighting for. Because if they are paying any attention, they`re just seeing this top-line debate around 3.5 or 1.5 or 2.5 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it isn`t the problem --\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: it`s time to get into the middle of this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Look, this has been one of the issues that I understand that eventually, you have to pass all this via reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 But for the life of me, Robert, I don`t understand why they didn`t try to at least look like they were tackling a piece of the agenda, a different piece of it every week.\par \par \uc2 So, you could do childcare, one week. You could do the pre-K one week. You do community college, one week. And you if you do tell me, how would you sell it?\par \par \uc2 What do you pick when you`re throwing everything in the bucket? I do think that it`s, in some ways, it`s too big to sell at times.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, it certainly -- I think, that`s going to be one of the challenges, but I think you`re going to have to continue to do events like the President`s doing now.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think everybody`s in the same boat and they`ve got a row in the same direction. We`ve got to get -- every member of Congress has got to get out there talking about what`s important to their district and to the families in their districts or in their states.\par \par \uc2 I think -- I think if Democrats wait for 10 weeks before the 2022 elections to think that TV and digital advertising is going to solve this, it`s not. It -- everyone needs to get to an agreement so that we can all get on the same page and singing from that hymnal.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robert Gibbs, former Press Secretary during the Obama years, and obviously a veteran of these, sometimes these Democratic intraparty negotiations, we`ll keep it at that. We won`t call it any --\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Go Braves, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- Well, good luck to them. It`ll least be fun, and I love me some Freddie Freeman. I`ll give you that. Thank you, Robert.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to some breaking news involving the Justice Department`s ongoing criminal prosecution of those tied to the January 6 insurrection, for the first time in these cases, a U.S. Capitol Police officer has been arrested.\par \par \uc2 Officer Michael Riley has been charged with obstruction of justice for allegedly communicating with a rioter and advising him not to post or to delete images on social media from the day of the riot.\par \par \uc2 Court documents say that Officer Riley pictured here in 2011, deleted \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages between himself and a rioter after the rioter was arrested in an effort to conceal the communication.\par \par \uc2 Officer Riley is making his initial appearance before a judge this hour. My NBC colleague Pete Williams will join us for the latest later in the show.\par \par \uc2 And then, some very disturbing news of political violence from overseas today, Police have now confirmed that British lawmaker David Amess was stabbed to death today while holding regular meetings with constituents.\par \par \uc2 The Conservative member of Parliament was attacked at a Methodist Church in a coastal town east of London. Police say Amess was treated by medical officials but died at the scene. Police also say they`ve arrested the 25- year-old suspect who had a knife. Amess was 69 years old. We`re bringing more developments to that story as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an update from the FDA as an advisory panel is voting for the second day in a row on the issue of booster shots. Yesterday was Moderna`s, today, \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And later, the big changes in the Hispanic electorate. We`re going to dig into what they are and what they mean, up ahead. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. An FDA Advisory Committee is set to vote any minute now on whether or not Johnson and Johnson`s booster shot should be recommended for the approximately 15 million Americans who`ve already received the single-dose vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So, we told you yesterday, there are actually a number of red flags in the \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  data, and the independent panel of experts is discussing those issues virtually right now.\par \par \uc2 Chief among them are the small sample sizes, and apparently some faulty test results that may have rendered some of the immunity data on usable.\par \par \uc2 Panel will then review a study from the National Institute of Health that demonstrates mix and matching booster doses is safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 This is day two of the meeting. Yesterday ended in a unanimous vote in favor of Moderna`s booster shot. Moderna`s third shot was recommended for the same groups of people already eligible for the third shot of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , that is people in the group over the age of 65, and those who have immune deficiencies, high-risk adults, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 The FDA gets the final say on Emergency Use Authorization for both manufacturers, but it usually follows the advice of its Advisory Committee, and the CDC must give one final seal of approval before more boosters roll out, which, could all happen within days. The CDC, of course, has its own Advisory Committee that will meet next week as part of this approval process.\par \par \uc2 Dr. William Schaffner is one of the doctors that advises that Committee. He`s the Medical Director of the National Foundation for Infectious Diseases, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You know, Dr. Schaffner, I`m going to add -- word the -- word the issue this way, here`s what it looks like with this \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  data to us here in our non-scientific minds.\par \par \uc2 It looks like a lot of people agree that a second -- a booster shot is needed, but it looks like Johnson and Johnson`s data isn`t very good. And so, if that`s the issue, what do you do?\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, MEDICAL DIRECTOR, \uc2 NATIONAL FOUNDATION FOR INFECTIOUS DISEASES\uc2 : Well, actually, I think the concept of mixing and matching, Chuck, looks very attractive to many of us. Namely, although you`ve started with \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 , your second dose, which would be your booster, could be either \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Moderna.\par \par \uc2 And I think that study you just alluded to, that they`re going to be looking at this afternoon, actually reinforces that because the data showed that after that mix-and-match, you get a very large antibody rise.\par \par \uc2 So, that looks very attractive to many of us. And I think a lot of people out there who received J&J have heard about this, and are looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, why is it that these -- the mixing and matching is effective? Considering these are -- you know, if you told me that Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  mixing-and-matching were effective, you`d be like, OK, they`re both mRNA vaccines. Why is mixing-and-matching this sort of viral vector -- vaccine with an mRNA, why is it effective?\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: It looks to be because the immune stimulus is slightly different. You get a very large immune response, and you get a diverse immune response, one that will cover the various variants quite effectively.\par \par \uc2 They`re have been doing this really in effect in Europe for quite some time. Their \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine is very similar to J&J, and they`ve been doing mixing-and-matching to good effect.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you about the doses now for these vaccines, particularly as we`re about to open up vaccines for kids under the age of 12. We know some of -- some European countries have decided to do one dose instead of two because of the issue of heart inflammation risk, particularly in young adolescent boys.\par \par \uc2 What`s your sense on this? And do you think there needs to be further investigation here by CDC and FDA? Because it does seem as if we`re -- we don`t have the research yet to know for sure.\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: Well, first of all, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  who`s first in line with their vaccine for children aged five to 11, has indeed reduced the dose in their vaccine. It`s one-third of the dose that was required for adolescents and young adults, and they get a very good immune response, and their immediate safety profile is quite comparable. So, that looks really pretty good.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, we -- although we don`t know everything we would like to know about this Myocarditis, fortunately, for the most part, it gets better really, very quickly in the vast majority of people who have it. And what we have to remember is, the Myocarditis associated with COVID is much more frequent and is a much greater danger to the children.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s the point here is that with COVID is worse than any risk that you might get with the vaccine. Finally -- there`s finally -- and it looks like there`s got to be a nominee for FDA, considering we`re in the middle of a pandemic, and we`re nine months into this administration nearly 10, and we`re just now getting a nominee.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:12]\par \par \uc2 TODD: First, Robert -- Dr. Califf, been there before, how much confidence do you have? There`s some lawmakers are concerned about his ties to the pharmaceutical industry, should they be?\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: I really don`t think so. He`s been there before, he`s been vetted, he has experience, and I would look forward to his taking over at the FDA. I think it`s time that we had a full-time complete director.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, how much of an impact has this been on the FDA not having a full-time director?\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: Actually, I don`t think very much. Because Doctor \uc2 Janet Woodcock\uc2 , who`s also been there for a very long time, and Dr. Peter Marks, who has overseen the vaccine development part, they`ve been working very, very hard.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think the lack of the director has really held things up at all as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But getting one, obviously, will only make things improve. Dr. Schaffner, always good to get your expertise on the show. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next. It`s a tight race for governor in Virginia, and the Democrat, Terry McAuliffe, is going all-in on his Trump message. I`m going to show you why, next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:01]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Glenn Youngkin is a great gentleman. I hope Glenn gets in there. And he`ll do all of the things that we want a governor to do.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R), VIRGINIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I was honored to receive President Trump`s endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Well, that`s part of a new ad that the Virginia Democratic gubernatorial candidate Terry McAuliffe is currently airing aiming to capitalize on some remarks former president Trump made this week in support of Glenn Youngkin, the Republican nominee. Youngkin didn`t sanction the event that the former president spoke at but he`s caught in the political bind many Republicans find themselves in these days, needing support, as Youngkin likes to put it, from the forever Trumpers and the never Trumpers in order to win, especially in an increasingly blue state like Virginia.\par \par \uc2 McAuliffe has already run that ad that I just showed you 30 times in northern Virginia, as it seems he thinks his best chance at winning lies in galvanizing voters who turned out against the former president in 