y of Hispanic Heritage Month, we`re going to take a deep dive in how this key voting bloc isn`t really a bloc anymore, and what the changing Hispanic electorate means for the future of our elections.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Today marks the end of National Hispanic Heritage Month. Hispanics and Latinos make up more than 18 percent of the U.S. population according to the latest Census data and increasingly Hispanics aren`t voting as a bloc. In deep blue states like New York and California, Hispanics did vote overwhelmingly for Joe Biden in last year`s election.\par \par \uc2 But in Texas and Florida, states that Donald Trump won, Biden`s margin of victory among Hispanics was much smaller and Donald Trump got north of 40 percent in both of those states. Then there`s a state like Georgia, which Biden flipped last year. And in that case Hispanics were instrumental in determining control of the Senate. It`s a growing population segment in Georgia in general and they did vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 Democrats Raphael Warnock and Jon Ossoff won their Senate races and giving control of the Senate to the Democrats in large part due to the growth of the Hispanic population and the fact that they won Hispanics by a nearly two to one margin.\par \par \uc2 In fact our own Morgan Radford is in Atlanta with a group focused on turning out the Hispanic vote. Also with me is my longtime colleague and friend from Miami, Jose Diaz-Balart who is newly back in the MSNBC lineup as host of the 10:00 a.m. show, and we are glad to have him back on this side of things.\par \par \uc2 But, Morgan, let me start with you because that`s sort of the point where you`ve been trying to make here is this is not a voting bloc. These are voting blocs and this is looking -- the Hispanic vote is looking more like a tapestry.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s right. And, in fact, Chuck, let me give you just the big picture because we`re talking about the state of Georgia where the Latino population grew by 32 percent, and that was just since the last census. So as you mentioned, the group we`re walking with now, GLAHR Mijente, they are canvassing as we speak going door to door talking to Latino voters because this is such an important voting bloc. In fact they knocked on every single household of every single eligible Latino voter back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And as you saw, eventually the state did turn blue. They say they think this could be largely due to their outreach efforts with the Latino community. In fact, I want to introduce you to Rafael Navar, he is the co- founder of the Mijente, which is the organization we`re with.\par \par \uc2 Raphael, what is it that you are doing differently this time around?\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL NAVAR, GEORGIA LATINO ALLIANCE HUMAN RIGHTS ACTION NETWORK: I think we`re building on what we`ve already done in the past elections, which we speak to each community in their language, to their issues. And we invite our membership across the country that is from those communities to speak to them. It`s much more powerful when you have Puerto Ricans who`s talking to Puertorriquenos and Mejicanos talking to Mejicanos.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Can you talk a little bit about that? Because something Chuck and I were talking about is that, you know, people kind of treat the Latino community as if this is one big massive voting bloc. But when you`re talking to Venezolanos or you`re talking to (INAUDIBLE), you`re talking different issues. So what are the differences in those issues? How do you tailor it?\par \par \uc2 NAVAR: That`s right. I mean I think it`s clear now past this 2020 election that we`re not a monolith. We have many subgroups, and core issues, for example, Puerto Ricans in Georgia were impacted by Hurricane Maria. That`s not the same issue that impacted Mejicanos.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NAVAR: And so Mexicans also have to deal with, for example, much more, unlike Puerto Ricans, with immigration issues.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NAVAR: That are mixed families, mixed status. So you have to actually speak to those core issues and who`s going to deliver on those issues.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: And so, Chuck, they`re doing really smart things like microtargeting with their data to make sure they can get that message out there, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, and Morgan, you`ve been around the country. I`m curious, the Georgia Latino voter versus the California Latino voter versus the Texas Latino voter. How would you differentiate and what would you -- how would you explain it?\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: Chuck, I can`t tell you how happy I am that you asked that question because if you look at the data, for example, urban Latino voters, so we`re talking about, for example, Dominicans in New York, they`re caring more, the data shows, about health care issues, for example. But if you`re talking to rural Georgia, perhaps Mexican immigrants, they may care more about immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 And so then you go to Cubans. You know, Cubans care about the protest that happened, the Libertad, the protests that happened on the island this summer. And so if you talk to Puertorriquenos, they`re still concerned about, for example, Hurricane Maria. So these are the competing issues. But organizers here say that you can actually speak to this group as a whole if, in fact, you are talking about progress for this community as a block, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Morgan Radford on the ground for us in Georgia. Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Jose Diaz-Balart. And, you know, Jose, this is where -- you know, when I look at the state of Florida and somebody would ask me who is the swing vote? I`d actually say it`s a younger Cuban in south Florida. That this is probably the person that might be on the fence, the 50-50 voter. But that isn`t the voter I would identify in Texas. That isn`t the voter I`d identify in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 How would you classify the differences between the Hispanic South Florida swing voter versus Texas versus Georgia?\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, MSNBC HOST, "JOSE DIAZ-BALART REPORTS": That`s such an interesting question, Chuck. And I think that the best way to kind of define it is that there is no Latino vote. There are just Latino voters. And depending on where they live and where their parents are from, they have a sensitivity and a perspective that may be different. For example, the mostly Mexican American voter, for example, in California, can think one thing about immigration.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican-American voter that is in the border towns of Texas may see immigration as a much more directly impacting thing in their lives and have a different perspective on it. South Florida is a key issue. Right? I think that what kind of has changed in South Florida is that the younger Cuban American vote and the older Cuban American vote seem to coalesce more recently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I saw that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Morgan Radford was speaking about those 11th of July protest in the island when hundreds of thousands of people took to the streets asking for one thing, for libertad, for freedom. And I think that it is to the peril of politicians to ignore that reaction, and to think that the United States simply shouldn`t deal with that issue because it`s not an important issue.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about Haitian Americans in South Florida, Chuck, that you know so well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: A community that is vibrant and contributes so much to our country. What we`re seeing in the Del Rio, Texas, thing last month were 7,000 Haitian migrants who were expeditedly sent back to the island. Many of whom hadn`t been there since 2010 for the earthquake.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Also I think plays a part.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting. You know, in your beat, you know this so well, which is, you know, we use the expression all politics is local. And for different Hispanic voters, I think there is a version of that but it`s -- in this way, and I`d love you to explain. What`s happening in Cuba and what`s happening in Venezuela and what`s happening in Colombia can impact their vote and how they`ll vote D and R in South Florida, but you don`t see that with Mexican voters. Mexican Americans. They don`t seem to be as motivated by the politics of what`s happening in Mexico. Why is that? How would you explain that?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: That`s a good point. I think that there`s a difference between immigrants and exiles. Right? But -- and this is a great conversation, Chuck. I think that the impact is -- I mean, Mexican Americans and I have been serving the community for, you know, over 20 years in Telemundo. They`re very concerned with what`s going on in Mexico, and they care about President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador and what he is or isn`t doing. Many of them voted from the United States for the presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 But it`s what`s going on in Cuba, those 62 years of dictatorship, that continue to cause pain for the Cuban people. As we speak, people are still taking to inner tubes to try and get out of that island nation, as we speak, Chuck. And so it`s a very real, vivid.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Open wound.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Jose, you know what I`m up against here. Our producers already told me. We got to go to the president. Great conversation.\par \par \uc2 Here`s President Biden in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- 27 years old. But when I got elected to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and I was 29, I wasn`t old enough to be sworn in yet, and between the time I got elected and 