ke, is an Afghan war veteran. We`re talking about all the work he`s done with the former governor of Delaware, Jack Markell, now who`s placing Afghan refugees coming out of Afghanistan. And we`re continuing to get people out. Thank you for what you do. I really mean it.\par \par \uc2 And Richard Blumenthal, who was, back in those days, the attorney general when my son Beau was still alive and he was attorney general. And not a joke, but he looked to Richard for help, and he gave it. And thanks for the way you took him under your wing. I really mean it, Richard. You were -- it made a difference. You know what he thought of you.\par \par \uc2 And Chris Murphy, who has been not only a real soldier, but he has stood up and stuck up for me. And, Chris, you know, it matters. It matters when things are tight and you stand up and make the case, and I do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And John and I -- John Larson and I go back a long way.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, Joe, you can`t deny me. There`s no way out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Rosa DeLauro, who is -- I don`t have time. I don`t want to keep you, but the first time I came up this way, I was -- my son was going to Yale Law School, and her mother was a committeeman. Was it committeeman?\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): Alderwoman.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Alderwoman. And I was up on a ladder helping him paint the place he had just rented, and I -- this knock on the door and this lovely woman comes and says, "Where`s Biden? Where`s Joe Biden?" And I was up on a ladder and I had paint all over me, and I was a sitting U.S. senator, and I said, "I`m here." She said, "No. Where is Biden?"\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: "Where is Biden?" And she brought the chief of police over to let him know that everything was going to be taken care of, and I`m going to -- but your mother was something else. But that old expression, the apple doesn`t fall far from the tree.\par \par \uc2 Rosie, you`ve been an incredible leader in all things having to do with the health and wellbeing of children and women. And we would not have had the legislation we`re now trying to continue were it not for you.\par \par \uc2 And, Jim, you`re --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You`re the real deal. As well as, you know, Jahana. My -- the comment I got from Jahana I get from everybody, "Where`s Jill?"\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: "Where`s Jill?" I am Jill Biden`s husband. She is now in -- I think she`s either in New Jersey or Virginia, I`m not sure, after teaching 15 credits this week at the community college. And she is out there making the case.\par \par \uc2 I`m here today to talk about what`s fundamentally at stake right now, in my view, for the families not only of Connecticut, because you`re ahead of the curve in some of what you`ve done on your own, but for our country. For a long time, America set the pace across the globe. For most of the 21st century, we literally led the world by a significant margin in the investments we invested in our own people. In our people. Not only our roads, our highways, our bridges, but on our people and on our families.\par \par \uc2 And we didn`t just build the Interstate Highway System and invest to win the space race. We also were among the first to provide access to free education beginning back at the turn of the 20th century. It was a distinction and a direct -- a decision to invest in our children and our families, and it`s a major reason why we were able to lead the world in the 21st century. One of the few nations in the world that had universal education for everyone, beginning in -- what was then first grade.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:06]\par \par \uc2 But somehow along the way, we sort of stopped investing in our people. Our infrastructure has fallen from the best in the world. According to the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2 , our infrastructure ranks 13th in the world, roads, bridges, highways, a whole range of things. But just as important is the Organization for Economic Opportunity and Cooperation now ranks America 35 out of 37 major countries when it comes to investing in early childhood education and care. Say another way, the world is catching up and beginning to pass us.\par \par \uc2 Jill has my community college -- Dr. Biden has an expression she`s used for real. Any country that out-educates us will outcompete us. Any country that out-educates us will outcompete us. We cannot be competitive in the 21st century in this global economy if we fail to invest. That`s why I proposed two critical pieces of legislation being debated in Washington right now. They`re both bills -- they`re not about left versus right, they`re not about, you know, moderate versus progressive, or anything else that pits one American against another.\par \par \uc2 These bills, in my view, are literally about competitiveness versus complacency, about opportunity versus decay, and about leading the world or continue to let the world move by us. Folks, a lot of folks know what`s at stake in the infrastructure bill. It`s about rebuilding the arteries of our economy, putting people to work in good-paying jobs. The estimate from Wall Street it`d created up to 16 million new jobs over time. Good-paying jobs, union jobs. Not five bucks an hour or $7.50, but $40, $50 an hour. You know, a prevailing wage you can raise a family on, you can live with some dignity and pride.\par \par \uc2 Bringing our roads and bridges up to speed. Replacing lead water pipes. There`s over 40,000 schools across America where you got to be worried when you go to the water fountain whether there`s lead in the water and the children are being poisoned. You can turn on the faucet so that every place in America you ensure the water is clean and able to be drunk. Laying transmission lines for a modern and resilient energy grid.\par \par \uc2 Making highspeed Internet affordable and available everywhere in America, from urban, suburban, rural. There`s parts of the country that are just being left behind. And there are parts of the country in states that are economically prosperous that are being left behind. Meeting the moment on the climate crisis and, in the process, creating millions of good-paying jobs. You know, I`ve had a couple conferences already held. I`m going to COP26 in Scotland shortly. And what I had -- I guess I had 71 heads of state on the first one I did in the White House.\par \par \uc2 And I said, and people are starting to talk about it now, not about me, about the idea. When I think climate, I think jobs, good-paying jobs, union jobs. This is an opportunity. We`re the only country in the world that has consistently turned difficulty into opportunity. We have a chance to not only make this world more livable, but to actually create greater opportunity for people -- making landmark investments in public transit and rail, and increasing efficiency, reducing emissions.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s millions of kids getting on a school, but diesel school buses and inhaling air, getting asthma. We could -- they should be electric buses, electric buses. That`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So, look, I`m going to -- I have a tendency to say more than I need to say, because you all understand it, but the bottom line is I wanted to come here today because too many folks in Washington still don`t realize it isn`t enough just to invest in our physical infrastructure. We also have to invest in our people. And that`s what the second bill does. The Build Back Better initiative.\par \par \uc2 Seeing children and educators here at this center is a perfect reminder of what our families need for -- and our economy needs so badly to be able to thrive. You know, you all know the statistics, particularly the teachers here. A child coming out of a single-parent house where there`s real difficulty will hear, literally, a million fewer words spoken -- not different words -- spoken, than the child coming out of a middle-class household.\par \par \uc2 And so no matter what you say, you start them at the same age, at age 6 or 7, 5 or 6 in school they`re already behind the curve, already behind the curve. How can we compete in a world if millions of America`s parents, especially moms, can`t be part of the workforce because they can`t afford the cost of childcare or eldercare, I might add? Eldercare, they`re the sandwich generation getting crushed.\par \par \uc2 Here in Connecticut, the average annual cost to bring your toddler to a quality childcare center is about $16,000 a year. That`s what it is around the country, some places more, some places a little less. A lot of money.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 That`s $16,000 after taxes. After you pay your taxes. So the average two- parent family with two young kids spends 26 percent of their income on childcare every year. My Build Back Better plan is going to change that. It`s going to cut the cost of childcare for most Connecticut families literally in half. No middle-class family will pay more than 7 percent of their income on childcare. None. Period.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And that`s going to help parents get back into the workforce and make ends meet, or maybe care for that -- we have to do the same, I`m not going to talk about it today, but the other piece of it you all know, it deals with eldercare. You got 80,000 people waiting to qualify under Medicaid and there`s no spaces. We can afford to do this. But at any rate, we also have to provide businesses with tax credits to build onsite care facilities.\par \par \uc2 Look, you, in the legislature, were way ahead of the curve. You decided that for people working in the legislature, there should be a place for their children. Well, you know, what we want to do is make sure that we encourage businesses to do the same, to get a significant tax cut to be able, if they have an onsite facility, to take of their workers` children when they show up. So you go to work with your child and you have a serious facility on site.\par \par \uc2 Well, studies show that when you have onsite care for -- a children`s center, businesses, businesses, the business itself, have less employee turnover, less absenteeism, and higher productivity. We can show you all those studies. It`s real. We can`t afford to lag behind other countries when they`re investing in.\par \par \uc2 When America made 12 years of public education universal more than a century ago, it gave the best-educated, best-prepared workforce in the world to the rest of the world. But if we`re designing public education today, if we all of the sudden we had none, we said, OK, what are we going to do? We need free public education. Does anybody think we`d think 12 years was enough, in the second quarter of the 21st century?\par \par \uc2 The fact is today only about half of 3- and 4-year-olds in America are enrolled in early education childhood education, like you`re doing here. In Germany, France, and the U.K., even Latvia, the number of children in those country enrolled is 90 percent. 90 percent. My plan gets us back on track. It provides two years of high-quality preschool for every child in America. It also makes investments in higher education by increasing Pell Grants.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know that I can get it done, but I also had proposed free community college, like you`ve done here in the state of Connecticut to help students from lower-income families attend community colleges and four-year schools. It invests in Historically Black Colleges and Universities to make sure young people from every neighborhood have a shot at good-paying jobs in the future. And we also extend this lady`s Child Tax Credit.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Which -- which is finally a tax cut for the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Now, look, my friends on the other side never had any problem for adding $2 trillion in tax cuts for the very wealthy. Look, I don`t think you shouldn`t be able to make a million or a billion dollars. I`m a capitalist. But guess what? I`m also listed for 36 years as the poorest man in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But I make big money now. I`m the president.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But all kidding aside, I don`t think we should punish anybody. But just pay your fair share. Just pay your fair share.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, the issue that has been championed by Rosa for years, in the past, if you paid your taxes and had an income high enough that you were able to take a $2,000 per child deduction, you could actually write it off your taxes. But how many families do you know of cops and firefighters and schoolteachers and the like that don`t have -- pay that much in tax because they pay a tax, but there`s nothing -- you say you`re going to get $4,000 back for your kids. Well, you know, it`s not refundable. It either comes off your tax bill, or you don`t get it all.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan, which these folks voted for as well, and I`m very proud of it, it`s a real gamechanger, started our economy moving again, recognizes that people with lower incomes don`t get the benefit of that tax rate because they don`t have that much to deduct, so we make it refundable. We make it permanently refundable. So you get that back over the years. You get -- if you didn`t have -- if you only had a $1,000 in taxes and you had three kids, you`d end up in a situation where you`d get $5,000 refundable to you.\par \par \uc2 They would pay you, the government would pay you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211016ehaf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Moderna, J&J Boosters Could Roll Out Within Weeks; Anti-Trump Republicans Announce Support for Democrats; What`s Happening to Evangelicals in the Post-Trump Era, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Anne Thompson, Sahil Kapur, Pete Williams, Monica  Alba \par \uc2 8,799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- him and I are doing everything we can. But I`m calling on more businesses to step up. I`m calling on more parents to get their children vaccinated when they are eligible. And I`m asking everyone, everyone who hasn`t gotten vaccinated, please get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 That`s how we put this pandemic behind us and accelerate our economic recovery. We can do this, I said many times. God bless you all, and may God protect our troops. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: You just watched President Biden there do an update on the vaccination program selling the mandates.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, he wants to keep encourage more mandates. We know the federal government is going to be writing its rules soon. He wanted to reassure that there were plenty of shots as well. And obviously he didn`t take any questions.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have more on the pandemic itself coming up, and the latest on the booster shot situation.\par \par \uc2 But right now, on the other end of Pennsylvania Avenue, we`re watching a number of subpoena deadlines come and possibly go.\par \par \uc2 And today is that deadline. It`s the House`s January 6 Committee that seeks a trove of witness, testimony, and documents from Trump allies tied to the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 The former President has told them all not to cooperate, so the Committee is not holding its breath for members of the former President`s inner circle, including Steve Bannon, Mark Meadows, Dan Scavino, and Kash Patel.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure if they were going to comply with subpoena deadlines today and tomorrow for testimony. And that`s just the tip of the iceberg in terms of who the Committee is targeting.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Committee have crisscrossed the airwaves in the halls of Capitol Hill in recent days who are warning Trump allies that if they don`t cooperate, there will be consequences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): If witnesses don`t appear when they`re supposed to if they don`t produce the documents, we intend to move very quickly to have a vote in the House, to hold them in criminal contempt or for it to the Justice Department for prosecution.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHANIE MURPHY, (D-FL): I would recommend the full extent of consequences. Jail time, fines, we need to make sure that these people understand that this is not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN, (D-MD): Americans across the country should ask themselves what they would do if they had a subpoena to turn over documents or materials were to appear in Court. They would go where they understand that the marshals would show up at their door the next day.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): Every single person on the Committee recognizes how important it is for us to make sure that we ensure -- we enforce our subpoenas and that we do so expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For what it`s worth not complying, only raises the specter of what do you got to hide? But will they go after them in a big way?\par \par \uc2 It`s all big talk right now, but to make good on most of those threats, the January 6 Committee is going to need the blessing of Biden`s Justice Department and the Attorney General Merrick Garland because any criminal prosecution for stonewalling a subpoena would come from federal law enforcement, not Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, the big question is, is Garland on board with this? Democrats on the Committee are basically saying he`s going to have to be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): When Bill Barr was Attorney General, there was no way he was going to prosecute someone who was covering up for Donald Trump. He viewed the Justice Department as essentially Trump`s criminal defense firm.\par \par \uc2 It`s different now, different Attorney General, one that believes in the rule of law. So, we intend to enforce these subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 I think if someone thumbs or knows at the law doesn`t comply is prosecuted, it will be a powerful message to others that they darn well better comply.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHANIE MURPHY, (D-FL): I fully expect this Department of Justice to uphold and enforce that subpoena because I think this Department of Justice believes that nobody is above the law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: NBC`s Sahil Kapur is on Capitol Hill, and with me set, our Justice Correspondent Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 Sahil, the deadlines are today. Steve Bannon was holding a rally in Virginia, so he was close. He`s within driving distance of showing up today. What do we know? And when do -- when does the Committee decide they haven`t complied? Is it five o`clock?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Chuck, there is a growing likelihood that this criminy -- that this Committee is going to put out criminal referrals to the Justice Department to prosecute these individuals.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon is the first to make abundantly clear in a letter from his legal team that he`s not going to comply.\par \par \uc2 He is citing executive privilege claims made by former President Trump which the White House does not accept, which this Committee does not accept.\par \par \uc2 Now, you heard what those Democrats are saying, they`re making it abundantly clear that they`re going this route and that they`re going to send a message to everyone that they need to comply.\par \par \uc2 Their word is going to be tested here because it`s not just the Committee, it`s not just the House that has to do it, but as you point out, the Justice Department, a U.S. Attorney in DC has to make those prosecutions happen, has to issue those fines, those jail times to send that message.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, Bannon`s reason for refusing to comply has nothing to do with the proximity to Washington D.C. He can show up here and he can talk to the Committee if he wants to. He feels like he doesn`t need to, and he feels like he can get away with it, it`s going to be up to the Committee and the DOJ to prove him wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mark Meadows, Sahil, is a former member of Congress, Kash Patel worked on Congressional Committees since, been on the other end of oversight requests.\par \par \uc2 All of the conversations around those two has been they`re talking. What is -- are they try -- are they trying to find a compromise to comply?\par \par \uc2 (13:05:00)\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It is notable, Chuck, that those two men, Kash Patel and Mark Meadows have been described by the January 6 Committee as engaging.\par \par \uc2 Now, that could mean a lot of things. They haven`t clarified whether that means fully cooperating. They haven`t said whether they intend to -- whether they have provided those documents that they had a deadline of doing last week.\par \par \uc2 Patel is supposed to testify today, the Committee hasn`t said whether he is showing up, Meadows is expected to testify tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Meadows of course, yes, a member of Congress, he knows these halls very well, he`s walked them for many years, but he is also now firmly on the side of Donald Trump. Put up -- hoop -- by the way, put out a statement last night lavishing praise on --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I saw all that.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: -- Meadows as Chief of Staff. I don`t want to try to get in the President`s head here, but it seems unlikely to me that Donald Trump would do that if he thought Mark Meadows was going to comply. So, a lot of this happening at the same time, and the pressure increasing here on this January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You know, that had the feel of the boss putting his arm around Mark Meadows, remember who you work for. I saw that same release and had the same reaction.\par \par \uc2 Sahil Kapur on Capitol Hill for us, Sahil, I know you`ve got some more reporting to run to and I`ll let you go.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Pete Williams here. All right, Pete, we know when they -- it looks like they`re going to have at least one criminal referral for Steve Bannon, possibly more. So, walk us to the next step. Now it goes to where`s the U.S. Attorney of the District of Columbia, technically?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, Congress votes to find someone in contempt. There`s a statute that was passed in 1857 that says when Congress does that, the U.S. Attorney in Washington shall present the matter to a Grand Jury.\par \par \uc2 Now, since 1984, the Justice Department`s position has been what you can`t make us do anything, we`ll consider that it`s a matter of our own discretion. The fact is, the Justice Department has never ref -- never prosecuted anybody who cites executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 But this is a complicated case because this is a former official of a former President, whose assertion of executive privilege, at least in one instance, has already been rejected by the current President, and the Supreme Court has said the current President is very influential in this process, doesn`t -- isn`t totally in control --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, it is. The office of the presidency gets executive privilege, not the individuals who were President, right?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But we know from the Nixon case in 1977, when he sued the archives over this, that -- a former President has some right to assert executive privilege. But when the current President goes the other way and undercuts -- doesn`t completely eliminate their claim, but it undercuts them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Steve Bannon hasn`t worked for -- didn`t work in the White House for almost three years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. So, it seems to me that the executive privilege on that is very hard to defend. I don`t think he`s got much of a case. So, what happens next? Is this going to court, they arrest him, Grand Jury indicts him, what is the process?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, you know, the Justice Department`s view of course -- of course, when any prosecution is, well, we`ll take it case by case. If I had to guess, directly a guess, I would think this is a pretty good likelihood that the Justice Department, in fact, will file charges here.\par \par \uc2 Because look, what they`ve done so far, they`ve already waived executive privilege on their own, former officials testifying before Congress.\par \par \uc2 We know what the Biden`s view is on the latest assertion of executive privilege over documents. So, yes, he would be indicted by a grand jury and he would go to trial.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems Mark Meadows being those --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And by the way, if he`s convicted, you get -- you can serve up to a year and -- in jail.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meadows is being the actual Chief of Staff at the time of all of this. He seems to potentially have a stronger executive privilege case than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Remember, the executive privilege claim is the President`s and not the -- not the officials so much, it alls -- it`s all about him, his right to receive candid advice without fear that it`ll become public.\par \par \uc2 But it`s -- you know, you`re -- the further away you get from a sitting President and the President himself in his inner circle, the weaker the claim becomes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a chance it would go to trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM: I mean, the last person to be cited for contempt of Congress was Rita Lavelle during the Reagan administration over the whole EPA Superfund thing. She was tried and she was acquitted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that trial would be in the District of Columbia?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, DC -- federal court here in DC.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`d be a -- it`d be a jury trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, most likely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, one would assume this thing isn`t going to go to trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, if he`s -- if he`s charged, I mean, he has a pretty serious choice then of deep to plead guilty or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This also takes up time. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, how much time are we talking here of something like this? I mean, is this month? Before a resolution?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For sure, but months at least.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that makes -- that also --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: By the way, it just occurred to me. Supposed he`s charged with contempt of Congress, and he`s -- he pleads guilty or he`s found guilty, I`m not sure that that means he still has to testify.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Then what? I was just going to say he serves time. Does he end up stolen never have an existent file?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don`t feel the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, it`s possible he can avoid testifying if he`s willing to sit in jail for a year?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I mean, the -- I guess the problem is, every time he refuses to testify, he can get charged all over again and becomes a new offense. And it would just -- I can`t imagine that it would just go on forever like that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How fast do you expect the Justice Department to act here?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh, I think pretty promptly. I mean, they`ll get the referral from Congress here. I would think Congress will act very quickly, and then the U.S. Attorney will act within. I would think a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Pete Williams, explaining how this could work. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in somebody who`s familiar with the investigation into the January 6 attack. It`s Pennsylvania Congresswoman Madeline Dean.\par \par \uc2 She`s Vice-Chair of the House Judiciary Committee. She was an impeachment manager for this year`s Senate trial of former President Trump. Yes, the second impeachment happened this calendar year for those with sort of COVID calendar brain.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I`m sure you have that too. Stuff either happened yesterday or three years ago, nothing in between. How quickly will Congress refer this as a criminal complaint to the Justice Department in your view?\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELINE DEAN (D-PA): I`m confident it will be a quick referral, and I`m certain the Committee is fully prepared for that. We have seen this stonewalling by some of these actors over the course of the many -- last few years under the former administration.\par \par \uc2 But it is a new day, and peaches (PH) laid it out. This is a different Department of Justice, this is a different administration, thanks to that good lord.\par \par \uc2 And I believe the referral will come quickly. I think a vote on the floor could come quickly. I think that this will be met with much more urgency and swiftness of response.\par \par \uc2 After all, this is about the subpoena power of an equal branch of government. The former administration has some notion that he and he alone was the preeminent branch of government. Well, he`s got a few other things coming through on whether he learns it or not.\par \par \uc2 We are co-equal branch, we do have legal subpoena authority, and those like Bannon who will try to skip town on it, good luck. We will get you now\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you could -- speaking of Bannon, are you concerned, Congresswoman, that he almost it appears to be embracing this fight, that he wants to be a martyr and frankly, sadly, there`s an entire ecosystem that will cheer him on and frankly, fun to his fight, if you will for this?\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve gotten in such a bizarre place, obviously, in our toxic politics, that there is a growing wing of one party that would cheer something like this on. How much does that complicate things?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: To me, Chuck, I see it as a sign of extraordinary insecurity and weakness. Any one of these people shouldn`t need a subpoena at all, as an American, as a patriot, as a citizen, who suffered the insurrection that was not an attack on Democrats in Congress. It was an attack on the entire government, including the Republican sitting Vice President.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, it`s just a sign of their weakness, obviously, to your question earlier on, what on God`s name are they hiding? After all, this insurrection was spontaneous. They must have had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you`ve read some of the Senate Judiciary reports about the desperate measures that the President was taking in the days leading up to the insurrection, we will know that many of these actors know much, much more, and we will learn what their responsibility was for an attack on our government, by Americans holding, and wielding, and beating people with the American flag and with the Trump flag.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m glad you brought up the Senate Judiciary report because I`ve been thinking about this quite a bit. We discussed it on the Sunday show with a Senator who`s on the Judiciary Committee, who was a part of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 You are on the Judiciary Committee in the House, part of the impeachment investigation. If you had had Jeffrey Rosen`s testimony in February, and that was part of your -- I mean, you guys had plenty of evidence but this is even more damning than ever now, the six days between January 1 and January 6, and all of his actions that we now have, had you had all that? Do you think -- do you think it would have improved your chances of winning a (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Sadly, I think we had plenty of facts to reach all 100 Senators. Sure, I wish we had had more of the information of the literally 3-hour meeting in the Oval Office that took place on January 3rd, three days before the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And then the following day, the meeting with Attorney Eastman, with the Vice President, with the six-page memo trying desperately on the part of Donald Trump to overturn the full fair results of an election, sure it would have been good to have.\par \par \uc2 But now, we will have it on the record through the Senate, through the Select Committee, and the American public will know it.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DEAN: But you know, if you look back to that impeachment trial that I was honored, solidly honored to be a part of, we had Senators who we showed them the evidence.\par \par \uc2 The evidence was compelling, and it was not refuted by Donald Trump parties. I don`t know what more we could have brought in there that would have changed the result of the 57-43 vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, certainly now where this party is, I don`t know whether anything would have moved the quest. They were perhaps a bit more movable back in February.\par \par \uc2 Let me turn quickly to sort of the ongoing debate over the President`s agenda. I don`t know if you caught Terry McAuliffe`s sort of, exasperation at Congress.\par \par \uc2 He obviously is on the ballot in neighboring Virginia, he`s experienced this before, he almost lost his first race for governor because of sort of Congressional dysfunction, if you will, or the appearance of it.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about this, impacting that race and therefore creating this sort of domino effect? And do you think we`re going to get closer before the end of this month?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, I honor Terry McAuliffe, in his run to become governor again. And I certainly understand his frustration, and the timing is coming close on his election, certainly.\par \par \uc2 But I would like to push back on this notion of total dysfunction. I was literally at the White House yesterday with the President in a bipartisan way. We had a meeting and had the chance to talk a little bit about Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 I want people to recognize we have two incredibly important transformative bills that are on a dual track. The bipartisan infrastructure bill, which I am confident, will come forward, and we will pass, and it will be meaningful to state after state after state.\par \par \uc2 But we also have the chance in a much more transformative way with Build Back Better. And the substance of that bill is what we should be talking about, not the process, not the date, not the top line number.\par \par \uc2 Think of how different our world will be in terms of educational opportunity and economic opportunity if we put those things in place, and I have a confidence we`re going to do both, that will reflect well on Democrats, while all of the big lie and what has going on in the past reflects seemingly on Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The problem, Congresswoman, is on the -- on this larger bill, the reconciliation bill, we don`t know what`s in it. And there seems to be a debate of, OK, we can`t have every program that we want to do.\par \par \uc2 Generally, it certainly looks like there is a larger chunk of the party that says if you`ve got to pick, then pick the things that help children, pick the things that help young parents, and that Medicare expansion may be a bit too expensive. Is that your sense of where things are headed?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, Chuck, I`m in an interesting spot, and I`ve spoken to Leadership about this. I`m both on the Progressive Caucus and the new Democratic Caucus, and so I`ve had the opportunity to be a part of both of those kinds of conversations.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you what I keep telling including the Speaker this week. What I care about is making a difference for the children in terms of education. So, it`ll be in negotiations, that we decide which programs and for how long these will be funded.\par \par \uc2 But I want to remind the American people, we actually have a plan to pay for it. So, don`t fall for this notion of oh, the Democrats just want to run up a deficit. We actually can pay for this.\par \par \uc2 And then, for example, I just want to feature a couple. Universal pre-K, what an engine that will be for educational opportunity and equity for all of America`s children?\par \par \uc2 Paid family leave, we`ve already seen that make an extraordinary difference in people`s economies, in people`s ability to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 Childcare, affordable childcare, and then one of my real beliefs is free community college. I believe that will be an economic engine. It`s called - - the package was called America`s College Promise.\par \par \uc2 So, when we do things that have to do with the education and economic opportunities around families, and we make those programs last, we will be transforming our economy and the futures for our children.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman Madeline Dean, Democrat from the Judiciary Committee, appreciate your coming on sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 And you`re only reinforcing this notion, I think -- I think we know where the focus of this bill is likely to be. Congresswoman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we`re going to dig into those remarks we just heard from the President over the Coronavirus pandemic, as the FDA meets today to debate whether or not to approve more booster shots. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We just heard from President Biden, and he gave some remarks in the state of the pandemic highlighting recent dropping cases, deaths, and hospitalizations nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Is that quite the Independence Day-like speech on this one? I think everybody is being cautious right now about this current situation. It looks good, let`s hope it continues.\par \par \uc2 The President also touted some encouraging data from private companies. Many have seen employee vaccination rates increased by more than 20 points as a result of new vaccine requirements that are coming, and have already come in some places.\par \par \uc2 But there`s still 66 million eligible Americans that have not yet received even their first shot, and infection rates still remain far too high to say definitively that we`ve turned the corner.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as an FDA Advisory Committee meets today to consider recommending Moderna booster shots for many of the nearly 70 million Americans who already received the first parts of those two-dose vaccines.\par \par \uc2 If authorized, Moderna boosters could roll out for those over 65, and high- risk adults with underlying conditions or certain frontline jobs, the same groups of people already eligible for the third \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shot.\par \par \uc2 Day two of the meeting continues tomorrow to consider boosters from \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  and mix and matching vaccine doses. And questions have already been raised about the data presented by both manufacturers here.\par \par \uc2 Briefing documents made available prior to the meeting show the FDA`s mixed opinion on whether extra shots are necessary and if the data is sufficient.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a new NIH study suggests that \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  recipients may be better off getting a \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Madonna booster, not another J&J anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot to get into with my guest today. We got Monica Alba at the White House for us, also joining us as vaccine expert, Dr. Peter Hotez.\par \par \uc2 He, of course, directs the Center for Vaccine Development at Texas Children`s Hospital, and he`s the Dean of the National School of Tropical Medicine at Baylor College.\par \par \uc2 Monica, I want to start with you. It was interesting to see that the White House -- that the President chose to come out today to talk today about this. Do you have some further insight as to why they thought today was a good day for this?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And usually these speeches, these COVID updates, Chuck, do come tied to some more administrative action or a bigger message that they really want to push.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ALBA: You remember just a couple of weeks and months ago when the President unveiled his six-prong strategy, or they wanted to give that initial rollout of the boosters, today`s was more of an overarching message, a progress report, a check-in, and a warning where the President really wanted to make sure people hear that, yes, things are absolutely trending in the right direction but this is not the time to lit up.\par \par \uc2 So, even though we`re heading into a fall with a lot of new tools in the toolkit, the President is essentially reminding Americans that we want to make sure that people are taking advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 So, he did say, for anybody who`s already eligible for a booster, make sure you`re going to get that. For anyone who might soon be a parent of a youngster aged five to 11, if they do indeed get approved by the FDA, which could happen in the next few weeks, the President said -- he said, we want parents to be ready and planning for that possibility.\par \par \uc2 And then as you just mentioned, this potential J&J and Moderna booster question mark of what may be best, will there be a mix and match, he said he wanted people again, to be vigilant and say, once there is a green light, do be sure you go out there and get it.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President also wanting to pull back and talk about this Department of Labor emergency rule. Remember when we talked about this, we said, how`s this going to work? How`s this going to be enforced? What if businesses don`t comply?\par \par \uc2 We now at least know the rule has been written. We haven`t seen the text of it, but that`s one step closer and it could come as soon as next week, and then we`ll really know whether businesses will be fined or how that`s going to work.\par \par \uc2 So, the President talking about what he sees as the effectiveness of the vaccine mandates so far, but of course, there still is economic woes. Everything we talked about yesterday, with the pandemic, still a major concern.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Monica, is basically the vaccine mandate from the federal government about the only plan the Biden team has left to try to get vaccine resistors to take it?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: I think that`s their most large lever of government that they have left, yes. And they feel that they are prepared for the legal battles and challenges that are already coming.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that, remember, months ago, the President said he was hesitant to do these kinds of requirements and mandates. Now, they`re saying this is actually, maybe exactly to your point, the only thing that will move some people off the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Monica, at the White House for us, Monica, thank you. Let me bring in Dr. Peter Hotez. And Dr. Hotez, I`m just going to put up here just the various quotes from some red flags and an FDA briefing document about the J&J booster in particular, because there`s a lot of booster confusion this morning -- this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Data sets were not submitted in sufficient time for FDA to conduct an independent review to verify the sponsors` analyses, the FDA is not independently verified the data from the final efficacy, these analyses were limited by small sample sizes, PsVNA assay used for the immunogenicity analysis is not validated or not qualified.\par \par \uc2 What is all of that tell you Dr. Hotez? Are they not going to approve this booster?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER HOTEZ, DIRECTOR, CENTER FOR VACCINE DEVELOPMENT, TEXAS CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: Well, there`s a possibility. And in fairness to J&J, you know, for the Pfizer BioNTech vaccine, we had the advantage of all of that data for vaccine effectiveness coming out of Israel and the U.K.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what really, I think, helped push this over the edge with the FDA. And the Moderna and J&J vaccines, are not being used in those two countries, and we just haven`t really collected vaccine effectiveness data as much -- as much as we`d like.\par \par \uc2 I do think it is a good vaccine, and -- but there are some deficiencies in the packet. And I think there`s also some misunderstanding about the paper that came out yesterday in terms of J&J as a booster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. You`ve seen there was a report this morning that perhaps it`s -- you`re better off getting a another -- getting one of the mRNA vaccines as a booster if you took J&J, explain why that is. What that could give?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Well, so that`s what I was -- so that`s exactly what I was referring to. So, this was a preprint that came out yesterday that showed that it looked at all the different permutations and combinations of the three vaccines, the two mRNA vaccines from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , BioNTech, and Moderna, and the J&J vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And what it showed is that as a booster, the mRNA vaccines tended to give a much more robust level of virus-neutralizing antibody compared to the J&J vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But what I was referring to before was the fact that you know, with the kinetics of the antibody response of the J&J vaccine, are quite different.\par \par \uc2 If you looked at the phase, one phase two data, you know, the antibody levels don`t peak till around 60, 70, 80 days afterward. And this study cut it shorter on 30 days.\par \par \uc2 So, it kind of stacked the deck against looking at the effectiveness of the J&J vaccine. So, I think that has to be considered.\par \par \uc2 Also, the J&J vaccine in two doses gives you also a different type of immune response on the CDA positive cells that are very strong.\par \par \uc2 So, put on -- all in that together in the phase one, phase two data, I still think the J&J vaccine as a two-dose vaccine is still a very good vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How comfortable are you -- look, I know that \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Moderna, J&J have pretty good scientific reputations. That said, how comfortable are you that we`re relying on their data sets, their collection of data?\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Shouldn`t this be done independently? Shouldn`t we be asking maybe it`s you to run an independent -- you know, maybe it`s another academic institution? But shouldn`t we be collecting the data on these vaccines that way?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Well, typically it would be a combination of the two. There would be independent studies and, you know, this is why \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 -BioNTech had the advantage of the data collected by the Ministry of Health of Israel and a cadre of Israeli scientists. Same with the U.K. So you are right. I think, you know, this has been a problem that we`ve had with CDC that we`ve not really collected vaccine effectiveness data here in the U.S. at the level that we should have. And so that`s not working to the disadvantages of the companies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And one other question about -- as we`re getting closer to seeing emergency authorization for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  at least for kids 5 to 11, I know overseas some countries have just approved only one dose for kids 5 to 11. There are quite a few doctors that have brought up the idea that could two dose cause an increased risk of heart issues for younger folks during development stages. Where are you on this issue?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: So, you know, the myocarditis does increase in frequency. It`s still a very rare event in the adolescents compared to the young adults. And so the concern is that as you get down to younger ages the rate of myocarditis will subsequently increase. We have no evidence of that. You know, I think one of the issues is the data packet being submitted to the FDA has about 3,000 kids in it, which would be not enough to really assess that myocarditis safety signal.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the problem is a single dose of the vaccine is very modest in terms of its protective effect.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Some saying only 30 percent against the Delta variant. And remember what we`re trying to do here, Chuck. We`re also trying to prevent not only hospitalizations but long COVID and neurological deterioration. And I think that`s the big unknown is how extensive does this filter down to kids?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: And how important is it to both prevent hospitalizations as well as long COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. And is long COVID a worse long-term risk frankly than the threat of myocarditis.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Dr. Peter Hotez, as always, sir, it`s great to get your expertise on the show. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, from John Deere to the film industry, some major labor pains are being felt across the United States right now as thousands of union workers take to the picket lines or threaten to. Workers have all the leverage these days. We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If the last week has shown us anything about the current state of the labor market is that workers may have more leverage over their employers now than at any time in recent history. The Labor Department reported this week that 4.3 million people, nearly 3 percent of the workforce, quit their jobs in August with the prospects of better and higher paying jobs available.\par \par \uc2 And workers in unions have also used the current climate to push for better conditions for themselves. Hollywood crews are threatening to strike next Monday if no deal is reached with studios over better hours, safer conditions and better benefits. About 1400 Kellogg employees who want better pay and benefits have been on strike for more than a week. And about 10,000 John Deere employees went on strike this morning after rejecting an offer from the company last night. That`s the largest public sector strike in two years.\par \par \uc2 Each of these strikes is very different with workers having very different demands. But they all point to the same thing. With a nationwide labor shortage, workers have the upper hand over management. And right now in this environment, they`re not afraid to use it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, is it time for America to establish a third political party? I`ll speak with a former Republican governor about the future of the GOP as she calls on moderate members of her party to team up with Democrats for now.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:44]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. A little bit of breaking news. The January 6 Select Committee has just announced it will meet Tuesday to vote on adopting a contempt report against Steve Bannon after the Trump ally failed to comply with his subpoenas. The announcement only mentioned Bannon, not Mark Meadows, Kash Patel or Dan Scavino, who also face similar deadlines. This would set up a vote in the full House, then refer the matter to the Justice Department for potential indictment and prosecution. We will continue to monitor those developments with the committee as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Staying on this topic, for Republicans opposed to Donald Trump now would seem to be an opportunity to start a third party. But some prominent Republicans say the key to eradicating Trump from their party is to support Democrats.\par \par \uc2 It`s a fascinating debate as former New Jersey governor Christine Todd Whitman and former Trump administration official Miles Taylor wrote in the "New York Times" this week. "Rational Republicans are losing the party`s civil war. And the only near-term way to battle pro-Trump extremists is for all of us to team up on key races and overarching political goals with our longtime political opponents, the Democrats."\par \par \uc2 Well, the organization Whitman and Taylor are helping to lead, Renew America Movement, took the next step in that pledge, endorsing 11 moderate Democrats ahead of next year`s midterms and I believe 10 moderate Republicans, all of them on the House side, voted to impeach the president.\par \par \uc2 Christine Todd Whitman joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Governor Whitman, it`s always a pleasure to talk with you. Look, I want you to respond to -- this is an interesting debate. Jonah Goldberg wrote the following about this debate. He`s part of the never Trump movement as well on "The Dispatch." And he said this, "Asking right of center voters to vote for Democratic senators and representatives who take the opposite positions on abortion, guns, foreign policy and tax and spending issues for democracy`s sake is a heavy lift, even if you can convince them that the democracy is really at stake. Biden has made that lift far heavier by making it clear that he wants right of center voters to compromise on everything while he compromises on little or nothing."\par \par \uc2 So what do you say to that part of this debate in this small tent of never Trumpers?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE TODD WHITMAN (R), FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: Well, we know it`s going to be a difficult battle. But I have to tell you, Chuck, and by the way, it`s always good to see you. Since we announced or put out that op-ed, I`ve been amazed at the number of Democrats that have reached out to say, we`re ready for something like this. We`re worried about how far to the left our party is going. And if you look at registrations across the country, you have 50 percent of registered voters who are now registered independents or unaffiliated.\par \par \uc2 And a large majority of those I am willing to bet are Republicans. And if you look at the numbers of Republican registration versus Democrats, you`ll see that the Republicans have been losing more. So I think the time is ripe to tell people look, what we`re after is supporting those candidates who will stand up to the big lie, who are not part of this concerted effort to undermine the public`s confidence in our electoral process, who will -- they`re not going to agree on everything and we`re not going to agree with them.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to agree with them, Miles won`t agree with them, he and I won`t agree on everything. But we agree on enough that has to do with our core principles, our respect for the rule of law and our respect for the Constitution to actually make this happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s clear to me that everybody has the same goal here, which is eradicate Trump from the Republican Party. And I guess the question is, what is the best way to do it? And why do you believe the best way -- it sounds like you think the best way to do it is starve the Republicans of power and maybe they`ll reform themselves? Is that the hope?\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, we`re doing both. We are supporting those Republicans -- incumbent Republicans right now. In the list that we released it`s an even number of incumbent Democrats and incumbent Republicans that we`re supporting because they are all people of principle, who have stood up to the big lie on both sides.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve been strategic in picking those races where it`s going to be close, where an endorsement and -- you know, bringing them to the attention of the voting public as people who are principled, who are willing to work for both sides and to get things done, willing to work for the American people, putting aside their party affiliations. Most of the public does that anyway. People split their ballots.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 They are ready now to see something different happen so that they can feel that we`re actually getting these problems solved and not ending up in this continuous loggerheads that we find ourselves with Congress. We can`t seem to get anything major done for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, right. It does seem as if there`s a reward structure for dysfunction. There`s a reward structure for obstruction. There`s not a reward structure for success right now. I mean, look at --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, that`s what we`re trying to provide.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, there is more money to be made by Steve Bannon by not cooperating than cooperating. Like that is what has broken our system.\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: I don`t disagree with you. But what we`re saying is, OK, now is the time to stand up and support those who are willing to take that risk of stand on principle, knowing that they`re going to be attacked. I mean, these incumbents that we have endorsed are all going to be in tight races because they`re in those districts that are swing districts or that are, you know, marginal districts in their favor.\par \par \uc2 But they are the kind of people that we want to see in Congress, we believe. And the public can look at these lists and give us some information as to who they should support and where, if they have the financial ability to do so. They can provide some financial support as well because you`re right, nobody has had the backs of those who are willing to stand up to leadership. Frankly, in neither party.\par \par \uc2 Because what leadership can do both through their PAC and just through the power they have and rules of the House and the Senate is to basically emasculate anybody who stands up to them. And that`s what these representatives have been willing to -- been willing to take that chance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Do you want --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: And that`s something we should support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you hope that your willingness to essentially say, look, Republicans want to support Democrats here right now because of this threat of Trump, threat of Trumpism, threat to the democracy? In turn, do you want the Democratic Party to run on that issue, make that more front and center than anything else?\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, it`s an important issue but we`re also asking Democrats to cross and support Republicans. If a Democrat has the choice between a far- left Democrat candidate versus a centrist Republican, we`re saying, vote for the centrist Republican. It`s not just on the Republicans. It`s also on the Democrats so that we can start to get a balance, the kind of balance that we need.\par \par \uc2 Because, you know, what`s happening, Chuck, and you`ve seen it and you know about it and you`ve talked about it is a concerted effort right now by the Trump faction to undermine the public`s confidence in our electoral system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: And that is just an idea to make it easier to overturn the 2022 and the 2024 elections, although having said that and the president is apparently telling Republicans not to vote, I don`t understand where that thinking is. But, you know, be that as it may, people are going to vote. We want to send a strong message.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Christine Todd Whitman, longtime Republican, former governor of New Jersey, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective on this fascinating debate taking place between Republicans --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: It`s not over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you help the Democrats or do you start a third party. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a sneak peek at something pretty exciting coming soon from us here at MEET THE PRESS. Stay with us to learn more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:49]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back here on MEET THE PRETTY DAILY. We are happy to announce the "MEET THE PRESS FILM FESTIVAL." The AFI Fest is returning for its fifth year on November 11th with in-person screenings this year in Los Angeles along with virtual screenings online. The festival, which you normally have here, we`ve got some COVID issues so we`re very excited to be doing it in L.A. The festival will showcase more than 15 short documentary films. Early bird passes are available right now at fest.afi.com.\par \par \uc2 Tickets to in-person and virtual screenings will be available to the general public beginning Wednesday, October 20th at fest.afi.com. You won`t want to miss it. We have a tremendous slate of films again. And as you know, what you`ll see here, you`ll eventually see nominated for Oscars. It`s always a really good slate and AFI is a great partner.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what`s happening to evangelical voters in the post-Trump era? Our latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" digs into just that and we`ve got the details next of what`s a growing divide.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:52]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it`s Thursday, it is time for yet another episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." This week we are looking at specific cohort of Republican votes, white evangelicals. My colleague Anne Thompson asks the burning question, what`s happening to evangelicals in the post-Trump era and found out very quicky at one church that there is no post-Trump era. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Peters is leading one side of the battle for the soul of the evangelical church.\par \par \uc2 KEN PETERS, SENIOR PASTOR, PATRIOT CHURCH: Some of the evangelical church I think it is soft. I think they`re cowardly and they`re trying to ride the fence between the left and the right. And so that`s kind of where the divide is.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Here in the heart of the bible belt.\par \par \uc2 DR. PHIL NORDSTROM, LEAD PASTOR, LIFE CHURCH: I would say the person of faith who`s been around church all my life that the bible belt is unbuckling.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Across town, Pastor Phil Nordstrom leads the Life Church.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What do you mean that the bible belt is unbuckling?\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The branding of Christianity has suffered. I think that our association with political extremism has especially turned off a younger generation toward evangelicalism. So one of the challenges we face right now is, who are we, what does it mean to be an evangelical?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Nordstrom is decidedly old school, a pro-life pastor pointedly staying away from partisan politics on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The funny thing is I`m probably personally pretty conservative. So I don`t come across as like I`m a liberal evangelical. But people get the spirit -- get the feeling pretty quick that we`re pretty inclusive church, that everyone is welcome, that we`re trying to not fight the culture wars from the pulpit.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Nordstrom builds his church with passengers he meets driving for Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: You have a wonderful day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: All right. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Under his soft-spoken demeanor, a growing anger that the things he loves are misconstrued.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: I think the easy thing to do would be to say, I`m no longer an evangelical or like I`m no longer a patriot. No, I`m a patriot and I`m an evangelical. And I`m proud of it. I still believe in a personal relationship with Jesus Christ which is what an evangelical in my view is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anne Thompson is here with more.\par \par \uc2 And Anne, it`s been a fascinating dive that you and I have been taking on this. And the stat that I think is most explanatory of this conundrum evangelicals are in, more people identify at white evangelical today and fewer go to church.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Yes, because I think what increasingly evangelicalism has become is not so much a religious movement. It`s become a political movement. And it`s a political movement that is certainly at home and very much in the camp of the Republican Party and not just the Republican Party, but the extreme right wing of the Republican Party. It is becoming a movement of Donald Trump. And that you see at the Patriot Church.\par \par \uc2 Why Donald Trump? I mean, they admit he is an imperfect messenger. He`s not the kind of Christian that they all hope to be. But they very much support him because of his positions. His pro-life position, his position on illegal immigration. And they want to go a step further, they believe there are only two genders, there is no transgender and they are very much anti- gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting. Russell Moore told me for an interview for our show that`s going tonight, he said, in many ways Donald Trump is actually very much like a Jimmy Swaggart or a Jim and Tammy Faye, that he comes from that sort of lineage of the movement that he admits has always been a part of the movement that has been uncomfortable for the more devout evangelicals.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: He does. He embraces the prosperity gospel and in many ways he would seem to be the personification of the prosperity gospel. And the one thing Donald Trump has always been is he`s been an extraordinary salesman, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anne Thompson, it`s terrific work. Folks are not going to want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211014ehae0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 13, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Monica Alba, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Morgan Chesky, Jacob  Ward, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 8,703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MARC MORIAL, PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 NATIONAL URBAN LEAGUE\uc2 : But we will not give up, we will not give in. We will continue to fight. And thank you for having me always, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: You bet. Well, we will not leave it there, we will continue. And that does it for today`s edition, at least, of Andrea Mitchell Reports.\par \par \uc2 Remember to follow us online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @MitchellReports. Chuck Todd in "MTP DAILY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday, President Biden confronts a major economic headwind, the nation`s growing supply chain backlogs, it`s just one of many headaches plaguing the White House. COVID, the border, our toxic politics, the return of Trump, and none of them are going away anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Plus, at 90, William Shatner becomes the oldest person ever to reach space. A new frontier for Mr. Shatner as Blue Origin launches its second manned rocket, plunging the U.S. deeper into a modern-day private-sector space race, that isn`t just about joy rides for famous people and billionaires, or is it still?\par \par \uc2 And the White House tells U.S. Governors to get ready to vaccinate kids by early November, a deadline that is now just weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Look, every day, we try to tell you what`s hot -- what`s urgent in American politics today. But big picture time. There`s kind of a fog or a, there I say, malaise over Washington right now in the electorate, the pandemic, a sluggish recovery, the supply chain issues, the toxic politics, the return of Trump.\par \par \uc2 There`s just simply a slog of urgent issues facing the White House and Congress, and there are no easy answers. And by the way, control of Congress is up for grab in just a year.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has killed more than 700,000 Americans and daily life remains marred with fights over mandates, vaccine resistance, a parade of grip milestones, and on, and on.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to the pandemic, we have an economic slog that continues as well. Weaker than expected jobs numbers, this inflation issue, that`s really probably being triggered more so by the big thing, supply chain bottlenecks.\par \par \uc2 The President today is meeting with advisors and business leaders to discuss the plan to try to ease those backlogs which have thrown parts of the global supply chain into gridlock that may take months if not longer to unravel. We`re going to have much more on this story later in the show.\par \par \uc2 There`s also the ongoing situation at the border. That`s not going away anytime soon. We`ve got the ongoing crisis of our toxic politics. Sadly, that`s not going away anytime either. And then there`s the former President leading a movement that is essentially all about undermining the democracy.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, there`s the President`s current semi-stalled two-track agenda in Congress and divisions within his own party that have no signs of a breakthrough that the -- they`re either at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 And even if Democrats pass what they want, they`re in for a long slog to implement this agenda, and then, of course, an attempt to get credit for it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s President Biden getting credit for those -- for that -- for those $1900 checks. Folks, to borrow a phrase from Billy Joel, President Biden, and Congressional Democrats, they didn`t start many of these fires, but they were elected to fight them and to put them out.\par \par \uc2 And the political reality facing Democrats right now is that none of these problems are going away anytime soon, leaving their own political future in the hands of an exhausted and frustrated electorate.\par \par \uc2 Monica Alba is at the White House. Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So Monica, this fog, this haze, however you -- we want to describe it here. There`s no doubt the White House gets it, that`s why they`re trying to do a public event trying to figure out the supply chain problem.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s get specific here. You know, is this something the White House can do something about? Or is this something that they can say they want to do something, but they just got to hope for the best?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, they`ve seen this problem coming for months, Chuck. We`ve seen those images out of these ports and the cargo ships being so delayed. Of course, that causing great concern among White House officials, and that`s why back in July, they established a task force to try to look at these supply chain issues, to look at the backlog, and see what they can do.\par \par \uc2 So, today, that`s why the President is meeting with companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , UPS, \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , to talk about what they can do and shifting in many cases to these 24/7 operations, so working overnight around the clock to see how they can unload cargo more quickly.\par \par \uc2 But what the Biden administration is now also talking about and has realized is there`s a major issue when it comes to trucking in this country and having enough people being able to move these goods physically.\par \par \uc2 So, you have just a massive gargantuan challenge that yes, the White House has admitted they`re going to try to put a dent in.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 ALBA: But many experts, when you`re talking about the timeline here, a lot of these gifts are going to potentially be problematic, when we`re talking about Christmas, and that`s why so many are urging people to get ahead of it. But the issue is now there is a very tight runway to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House is acutely aware of it. And it follows a pattern that we`ve seen when they`ve been dealing with other kinds of things like vaccine mandates, and airlines for employees for instance.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the White House gathering all of the leaders of those companies here and they`re going to kind of try to model that with the supply chain issue and say we`re bringing the greatest minds they believe together to try to put our heads together, and then figure out a plan and put it in place.\par \par \uc2 But many people are saying that`s not likely going to be resolved for many weeks and weeks and months, but at least the White House can say, we put together some kind of plan, and now we`re going to try to enact it. So, the President will be speaking on this.\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you, Chuck, it is something that he has been asked about -- that the White House Press Secretary has been asked about, for the last four or five months, it`s been building to this point, and so it finally seemed like they felt they needed to take more drastic action, whether it`s going to really have a significant impact is unclear.\par \par \uc2 But the White House here has been dealing with this whack a mole kind of situation of things that come up, even though they would much rather be talking about other things.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why later in the week, the President is going to go to Connecticut to continue to try to sell his Build Back Better agenda and those two massive currently stalled bills that -- yes, they have very precious time to be working on.\par \par \uc2 So much of that is taking place behind the scenes, well outwardly, the focus today is this supply chain challenge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Monica Alba with sort of the White House perspective.\par \par \uc2 So Leigh Ann, let me go to you. It`s interesting to me to hear the President wants to go and sell a plan. What plan is there to sell?\par \par \uc2 And I say this, I know generally what they want to do but these specifics are getting harder and harder to nail down and there are -- if it goes in one direction, there`ll be more things to campaign on, if that goes into another direction, there`ll be fewer things.\par \par \uc2 Where are we? And what are we supposed to make of Kyrsten Sinema making it clear she only speaks to the White House, that`s it, no other member of Congress?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Chuck, you took the words right out of my mouth. That`s exactly what I was thinking when Monica was speaking about the President trying to sell his Build Back Better agenda.\par \par \uc2 They have nothing to sell yet. They have no legislation. And Speaker Pelosi yesterday in her press conference blamed the media in part for the general public not knowing what is in the Build Back Better agenda.\par \par \uc2 Well, we can`t really talk about what`s in the bill because there is no bill yet, and that is where Democrats are. They`re trying to figure out what is in this legislation. And there`s nothing to sell. They need to come to an agreement first.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator Sinema, this is are -- here`s what she -- what she put out today is what I`ve been told, for the past few weeks that she is not negotiating with anyone. She`s not negotiating with a press that she`s barely negotiating with leader Schumer. She is talking to the President and that the President knows exactly where she stands. And now, that has led to additional questions of where is the White House and where`s the President in these negotiations?\par \par \uc2 House Moderate, a staffer, told me just today, that what they`re -- how the President and the White House are dealing with this reconciliation, multi- trillion-dollar bill, is much different than how they dealt with the bipartisan infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 There was meeting after meeting, these members traipse down to the White House to meet with President Biden, and it feels different to a lot of these members who haven`t heard from the White House on this multi-trillion dollar bill, and they`re wondering when these negotiations are going to start in earnest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, there`s, you know, I`ve used the theme of fog. There`s a real fog over -- I assume for most members of Congress, they don`t know where things stand. Feels like only a handful are involved in this. Is that right, Leigh Ann?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, that`s right. It seems to be some Committee Chairs, the Speaker, the Leader of the Senate, and the President. But even that, they still have a dismay decision, and there`s been a lot of frustration that Speaker Pelosi hasn`t been talking enough to her members to get where they are.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot of, fog is a great word, people aren`t knowing which way`s right, and which way`s left, and which way`s moving forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes -- no, I go back to what Debbie Dingell said. She`s like, there are a lot of people that want to have the President`s back, tell us what to support, tell us what she wants us to support. Monica Alba, Leigh Ann Caldwell, thank you both for getting started.\par \par \uc2 We are lucky enough to have somebody who has a fog lamp, I think because she is one of the key players that is involved in these negotiations over the President`s agenda in Congress. It`s Democratic Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal. She`s, of course, Chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus.\par \par \uc2 So, Congresswoman, I want to start with something you said last night that perked up the ears of a lot of people. There`s a lot of -- you push back on the notion that something is better than nothing. And explain your concern, why you`re not in the camp that says, isn`t something better than nothing. Explain.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): Well, Chuck, it`s great to see you, I`m going to have my fog lamp every time I come to be with you from now on.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that is something -- this phrase, something is better than nothing, don`t let the perfect be the enemy of the good, some of the things that are said, those phrases are normally used at the end of a negotiation.\par \par \uc2 They are used when you really have like the final details and you`re putting the thing on, and you say, you know, don`t let one thing hold you up.\par \par \uc2 That is not the situation here. The situation here is that we are fighting for the President`s Build Back Better agenda, 85 percent of the entire presidential agenda that he laid out to us before Congress is in this Build Back Better Act. And we are saying don`t leave people behind.\par \par \uc2 Don`t leave behind people -- families who need childcare, families who need paid leave, don`t leave behind climate action, don`t leave behind housing, don`t leave by immigrants, that is what we are saying.\par \par \uc2 And 98 percent of all of us in Congress agree, and so, I would disagree a little bit about there`s nothing to sell here because we have a Build Back Better Act.\par \par \uc2 It`s already been created, it went through Committees in the House, and still, there hasn`t been a lot of coverage about what is in the Build Back Better Act.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is what the Speaker is saying, that is what the President is saying, without really telling the American people what`s in the bill. We keep talking about some top-line numbers.\par \par \uc2 The top line number is kind of incidental in my view, and of course, I don`t mean that naively, it -- there`s a number it`s associated with, but it depends on what programs you put in. And for us, we`ve been very clear that 98 percent of all Democrats agree.\par \par \uc2 So, this idea that somehow Democrats are in disarray, and there`s terrible disagreement, it`s not true, we`re waiting for two people to tell us what they agree on, first of all, because they don`t agree with each other.\par \par \uc2 And then we`d like them to tell us what they want us to consider that is different from the act that is on the table that the President agrees with, that 90 percent of Democrats agree with, that 70 percent of the American people agree with if they know what`s in it.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s how I think about this. And I think it`s just important to recognize that there is a very specific agenda here that we`re talking about, and we need people to understand, it`s 12 weeks of paid leave so that we can finally get the United States back up to the standards that other pure countries are at.\par \par \uc2 It`s universal childcare, so no one has to pay more than 7 percent of their income. It`s housing for everybody across the country to ease the numbers of unhoused that are here in the richest country in the world.\par \par \uc2 It is making sure that seniors have access to dental vision and hearing benefits at the very time when they need it the most. It`s real action on climate change. And by that, I mean reducing carbon emissions so that the President can go to Cop26 in a couple of weeks and say we are leaders on climate.\par \par \uc2 And of course, it`s lifting up immigrants. That`s the agenda. We want people to know that`s what we`re fighting for. We`re fighting for them to have transformation in their lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the President has already brought the number down, right? He`s already -- he`s already said it`s not going to be that. So, you`re not going to be able to have all of these things you just described.\par \par \uc2 This is what makes, I think covering, OK, what`s in the bill? Aspirationally, that is what you described. But there isn`t a bill yet, and the President is not fighting for the full bill anymore. He seems to be trying to figure out what a compromise is that can keep everybody at the table.\par \par \uc2 So, I am asking you this, that compromise, it is not going to have everything that you just described in there, there`s going to be something that gives. Is means-testing something you`re open to?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Well, I would just say actually, we winnow down our priorities. We don`t have a big laundry list of 8000 things. We have five things that I mentioned. And those things I do think will be in this bill. And I think that they will be in this bill, but probably for a shorter periods of time -- funded for shorter periods of time, and that is the important piece that we`re getting at.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve had -- I think you and I have had this means-testing conversation. So much of the means-testing we`ve done in America has been absolutely, you know, really not constructed in a way that allows people -- that the very most vulnerable people to participate.\par \par \uc2 Incredibly complex, drives people away, the research shows that that the most vulnerable are actually the most affected when you put a whole bunch of restrictions on, and by the way, it costs a lot of money administratively to get somebody to actually go through a 23-page form. So, we think there are much more simple ways of doing this.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: For example, a 7 percent cap on income -- of income for childcare would be a way of saying if -- you have to spend 7 percent of your income.\par \par \uc2 So, guess what, if you`re a millionaire, it`s -- you`re never going to get a government subsidy because it`s -- childcare is never going to cost that much.\par \par \uc2 But the school teacher, that`s earning $68,000 in Seattle, will get a childcare subsidy from the government to help her be able to get back to work. And that`s, I think, the kind of smart policy that we need to put in place.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s a question I have that in this process, part of it, that I think a lot of ranks and file members of Congress would also agree of. Why isn`t there -- why aren`t you, and Manchin, and Sinema, and the President, and the Leaders, why aren`t you guys in the same room? Why isn`t there more of a back and forth?\par \par \uc2 I feel like there`s -- you know, you guys complain about nego -- you don`t want to negotiate through the press, but in many ways, it seems to be the only time Manchin and Bernie speak to each other, feels like the only time you Sinema`s -- but you know, at -- Senator Sinema said she`s only going to speak to the President. It feels like the process itself is semi-broken here.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Well, I think it just depends, right? The bipartisan bill was different because they brought a group of Senators together, and those people kind of crafted the bill together, and that was the bill. But it left out a heck of a lot of us that weren`t at the table for that.\par \par \uc2 So, in this situation, this Build Back Better Act has been crafted by 98 percent of Democrats. I think that`s a pretty good process, actually.\par \par \uc2 But now, there are two people that still need to come on board, and the President is in many ways, in my view, the best person to be talking to them.\par \par \uc2 I have had a conversation with Senator Sinema and Senator Manchin and I also, you know -- I`ve suggested that I`m happy to sit down with him, he suggested that he`s happy to sit down with me, so maybe that`ll happen.\par \par \uc2 But the real negotiation here is with the President because this is the President`s agenda. This isn`t some, you know, agenda of 10 Senators that was developed separately from everyone else. No, this is the President`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s completely appropriate that those two holdout Senators would talk to the President about what they believe. Now, I, at the same time, I`m also talking to the President, to the White House, to others about what we need just so that we don`t get into a situation where something gets negotiated, that we simply are not going to be able to deliver the votes for because we have thin margins in the House as well.\par \par \uc2 And as one Senator said to me yesterday, it is important that we get all 50 senators on board, not just two. So, that is also another consideration here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you made it pretty clear in this last answer, whose job this is to wrangle all of you together. It is the leader of the party, the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal, the Chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus, great to have you on and get your perspective. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, we`re going to dig more into the nation`s growing supply chain problem as places across the West Coast are having to make major changes, just in an effort to get goods moving again. We`re going to be live in California at the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 . That`s just ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, some major developments in the modern-day Space Race, as Blue Origin launches its second manned rocket into orbit. We`ll have more on that, next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- Two, one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Has clear the tower, she is in her space with the second human spaceflight crew. What a launch. We are on our way to max cue the first milestone here on his flight to space.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SHATNER, ACTOR: That was unlike anything they described.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, one of the heroes of science fiction is now part of actual science history. Earlier today, 90-year-old William Shatner, Captain Kirk himself, became the oldest person ever to reach space as part of Blue Origin`s second all civilian crew.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SHATNER, ACTOR: Everybody in the world needs to view this. Everybody needs this thing. The little thing that`s what`s in this mission, but to see the blue color whip by now, and now you`re staring into blackness, that`s the thing.\par \par \uc2 I`m so filled with emotion about what just happened. I just -- it`s extraordinary, extraordinary. I hope I never recover from this. I hope that I can maintain what I feel now. I don`t want to lose it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If that man`s 90, I will have what he`s having. My gosh. He actually can see if Lucy is in the Sky with Diamonds.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, the crew experienced about three minutes of weightlessness during this 11-minute flight. This was the sixth spaceflight this year carrying civilian astronauts. There could be as many as nine more before the year ends.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Chesky joins me now from Van Horn, Texas. Also with me is retired Colonel Chris Hadfield, Retired Astronaut, Former Commander of the International Space Station, and the first Canadian to walk in space. He`s also the author of a new novel called the Apollo Murders.\par \par \uc2 Morgan, I -- look, I`ve just sort of I just can`t get over. Like I said, William Shatner at 90, I mean, it -- you know, he just looks Spry and he`s got so much energy. But the fact that this is now becoming, we`re getting the point of semi-commonplace here.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, you know, this -- Blue Origin was quick to say that they had 17 consecutive successful flights with the Blue Origin rocket, that new Shepard rocket, and this now makes the 18th consecutive successful flight, the second successful manned flight after we watched that rocket liftoff from the West Texas desert back in late July.\par \par \uc2 And you have to think how more frequently is this going to start happening now that they`re seeing some consistent success here? And not just in the test action mode, but with actual crew members here.\par \par \uc2 And you saw the reaction from William Shatner there after he walked outside of the capsule, and what was so striking to me was prior to this, he said that he looked forward to looking outside of the window and being entranced by the blue orb, that is Earth, and only the way William Shatner could say.\par \par \uc2 But then to see that raw emotion on the other side of this trip, face to face with Jeff Bezos saying that he wanted to grab ahold of it and hold on to it as long as he could, it was very evident to see just the impact that this had on him.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that really does capture a part of the motivation, at least, behind what Jeff Bezos is trying to do here, and that is increasing the frequency of these flights build a "road to space" and hopes of making it more accessible for at least the rest of us who can`t currently afford that quarter-million Dollar estimated price tag for a seat on board, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Morgan, what are the next -- what are the next phases of what Blue Origin wants to do here? Do they hope to dock with a space station? Do they hope to orbit the Earth for a while? Do they want to do overnight flights? What is the next phase here? You know, these are these short flights. What do they hope to start doing next?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Well, I think what we`re going to start to see is more consistency with the flights that have already taken place. I wouldn`t take off -- I wouldn`t take anything off the table when it comes to Jeff Bezos and his goals for, you know, in-space travel, as far as going around the Earth`s orbit. Those are the next natural steps, of course.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, we still just have two manned flights here that taking up four people each time. You know, that capsule can hold up to six people, and we do know that there is already a very long waiting list of people who are more than willing to pay handsomely to take that ride.\par \par \uc2 So, we will start to see Van Horn, Texas become more and more prevalent as this launch site becomes used more by Blue Origin.\par \par \uc2 That as of now is able to reuse the capsule -- reuse that thruster, which I can tell you is pretty wild to see return after going into the sky, come back and land on a launchpad not too far away from where it took off.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact that they`re able to reuse these tools is only a positive sign of what`s to come, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s for sure. Morgan Chesky in Texas for us, Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Colonel Hadfield here. What do you believe these private space flights are accomplishing? And what do you say to folks who say, this feels like a whole bunch of rich guys just trying to show off? Let`s say if.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HADFIELD, RETIRED NASA ASTRONAUT: You know, I think everybody think - - and not only was Morgan excited, listening to him there, but everybody should take a couple of minutes and just listen to the outpouring of emotion from Bill Shatner when he saw the world the way that he saw it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s just a truly honest and profound human experience that he was doing his best to express. It`s worth taking a moment to listening to that today. So, the human experience is the first answer to your question, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 But the second piece is this is really just a demonstration of the technology that we have just now perfected. It opens up access to space for a business like we`ve never seen before.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have GPS satellites, and communications, and remote sensing, and all the other stuff that`s in orbit, photographing the world, understanding it, but the cheaper you can make it and the simpler and safer, it just opens up businesses like trains, or cars, or airplanes.\par \par \uc2 And faster than everybody thinks, we are going to have an earth-moon economic system built. And that`s, you know, it`s great to see bill fly today, and it`s exciting. But to me, that`s what this really signifies is the technological opening of a whole new capability.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Look, you are sort of shared a perspective that Jessica Meir had with me last week. We did a whole special on this sort of the new space race here. Let me play a clip of what she said about these public-private partnerships.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MEIR, NASA ASTRONAUT: 4 percent of GDP was spent on the Apollo missions. Now, the entire NASA budget`s only about .04 percent, that`s not just human spaceflight, that`s all of NASA, so we need this.\par \par \uc2 We need these partnerships with private companies. We need these international partnerships like we`ve been doing on the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 By leveraging these partnerships with commercial partners like \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to concentrate on what we call near-Earth orbit, low Earth orbit for the International Space Station, we can use our more limited NASA assets to go toward the next destination.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Colonel Hadfield, here`s the one question that I have that I`m nervous about because we`re seeing this what happened in the world of social media, we create -- we hurry up and build something and then we realize, oh, we didn`t put in any restrictions on X or Y, and the rules of space. Right now there are no rules, right?\par \par \uc2 It`s basically only the technology -- the science is your limitation right now. Are you nervous about this, that the private sector could go up there and privatized space at a time when it shouldn`t be privatized?\par \par \uc2 HADFIELD: Well think what chaos New York was in in about 1910 when cars started replacing horses. And the infrastructure, and the regulation, and stop signs, and streetlights, and driver`s licenses, those took a little while to get into place, same thing with airplanes in the 1920s.\par \par \uc2 There was just, gosh, we had this new technology and had to figure out how to fit it into and form an FAA. And now, the FAA is sort of dealing with the same thing with spaceflight. It`s not an unsolvable problem, but there`s a transitional phase and the technology has got to push forward. That`s just fundamental human nature.\par \par \uc2 And yes, we need to get it properly integrated into the culture and into society into the rules, all of that. I`m not super worried about it, but it`s bound to happen. But to me, it opens up so much possibility, just like standard human creativity. And I very much agree with Jessica that this model, where the cost has come down so much, there`s a partnership between NASA exploring the universe and then businesses able now to take advantage of the real high ground of orbit, it`s a natural long-term progression, and we`ll get it sorted out pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I spend a lot of time covering Washington, covering the fog of politics, nothing broke through the fog today like William Shatner. This is what`s so exciting about today. Whatever we think of this long term, you are so right. Just spend a minute listening to Shatner today and it`ll put you in a good mood.\par \par \uc2 Colonel Hadfield, thanks for spending some time with us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HADFIELD: Thanks, sir.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, we`ve got the latest shift from the White House on the pandemic as the United States opens its borders for vaccinated visitors from our neighbors Canada and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Let`s turn now to the latest coronavirus facts that you need to know today starting with news for travelers.\par \par \uc2 If you planning to come to the United States from Canada or Mexico by land, you`ll need to get fully vaccinated before crossing the border. The Biden administration announced the new requirement today as an expansion of the vaccine for foreign nationals arriving by air. Nonessential land travelers will have to prove they`re fully vaccinated starting in November while essential travelers like truck drivers will have until January.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 And so more news from the administration on the COVID front. The White House told governors to get ready to vaccinate children as young as 5 by early next month. This comes as the FDA and CDC are set to review Pfizer`s vaccine for kids age 5 to 11 in the coming weeks. Final approval could come as soon as November. And when it does, the administration says they`ve already purchased enough shots for nearly 30 million children that would be eligible.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes amid the hopeful news that the pandemic is still trending in the right direction here at home. White House COVID Response Team highlighted nationwide decreases in cases, deaths and hospitalizations in today`s press briefing. But, again, the nation`s top doctors also reminded folks the pandemic is very much ongoing and our levels are still not great despite the recent good news.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Fauci said we still must do better to get the pandemic under control and keep it that way through vaccinations and masks. And all of us are fearing another variant especially thanks to so many large unvaccinated pockets around the world.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to go live to the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 , taking a deep dive on what`s behind the supply chain crunch that`s certainly having a huge impact on inflation and the economy as President Biden plans to deliver his remarks on his plans to try to break the bottlenecks impacting millions of businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The White House is anticipating more economic woes and a tough midterm fight which comes as President Biden is about to meet with senior officials and executives from companies like \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2 , UPS and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  in an attempt to address the massive bottlenecks in the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 In the next hour the president is going to publicly address the issue, unveil a plan that he believes will help ease those bottlenecks which includes a 90-day sprint in order to help alleviate them. Back up to key U.S. ports like those in California had doubled the time it takes for some products to hit store shelves.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 It`s a major issue impacting consumers, businesses, the economy and the administration. And new data released today shows that inflation rose in September as well, which is yet another economic headwind facing the White House that isn`t expected to go away anytime soon. There`s money out there, not enough things to buy so prices go up. So this supply chain issue really does have a direct correlation with the inflation issue.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss this more is NBC News correspondent Jacob Ward from the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  as well as senior business correspondent Stephani Ruhle.\par \par \uc2 You know, Jacob, I happened to be out in Southern California last weekend. And just looking out the plane window when you leave LAX you can see the backup at Long Beach. It`s one of those things you read about it, it`s another thing to see it. Tell us what you see in Oakland.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is extraordinary, Chuck, to be standing in a place that is as backed up as the West Coast is. We in fact took a tour of the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  where I am standing and back in April to see how they were sort of, you know, considering the future. And let me just try and put it in perspective for you. I want you to listen to the executive director at the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  who at that time was staring down the barrel of what he knew was an incredible crush coming his way. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANNY WAN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PORT OF OAKLAND: We are busier than ever, and the pandemic has changed people`s habits in terms of buying stuff. Repairing their home, getting more furnishing, appliances, so all that stuff is coming through the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  for this region and we are seeing a boom of people buying and shipping through Asia to the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WARD: So just to talk about what exactly that then means here on the ground, if you think about the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 , you know, since then they basically like so many California ports had to -- they basically had shippers turning away because the lines were too long. In August their numbers were actually down because ships simply weren`t arriving here. Since then they have added shifts, they put in some world class cranes. They`ve truly had to transform their operations to get it going.\par \par \uc2 But now we`re still facing the problem of what you do once things come off the boat. You at that point need trucks to carry things away. There`s a huge shortage in truckers themselves. We have a rail car shortage. I mean, the gaps go on and on. And that is why, Chuck, so many companies have now stood up with the White House or expected to stand up today and say we will start moving to a broader schedule. That \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , UPS, \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , as you mentioned, are moved to a 24/7 operation in off-peak hours.\par \par \uc2 All of that being this extraordinary effort on the part of the White House to orchestrate what used to be the free hand of the market and that just hasn`t been doing it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Stephanie, as a convening entity the White House can do a lot and they`re convening all these CEOs. Is there something the government can do here short term to help alleviate, or is what he`s doing the best he can do?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Listen, it is the free market. They`re bringing these businesses together to try to solve for this but we have to remember, Chuck, the moment that we`re in. Right? Jamie Dimond, the CEO of JPMorgan said it earlier. Right? Almost two years ago we were looking at possibly facing a great depression. We had a global pandemic. We were in a terrible situation.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re coming out of it and we are in a good consumer-based economy. The economy is strong. And even though we are facing inflation and these shortages, consumers are still spending and this is dampening consumer spending, but it`s not reversing where we are. And these supply chain issues and these labor issues will work themselves out over time. And what the White House can do is sort of create more buffers in the system, boost the system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re seeing the Social Security assessment for next year that the adjustment for cost of living, they`re going to have the greatest increase they`ve had since the `80s to account for the fact that groceries cost more, gas costs more. But when you talk to CEOs or economists, they`re saying this is transitory, and there`s not a business leader out there that`s not trying to solve for this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand this. But, Stephanie, define the timeline on this. I mean, you know, on one hand, you know, the political calendar is not necessarily going to line up with our eventual ability to sort of cycle out of this issue. And it certainly looks like -- I`m with you, I see what they did on Social Security, but it may feel like a net zero to a Social Security recipient because it does look like inflation is here to stay at least temporarily.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Chuck, you`re not wrong. And you know, we talk about, hey, look at these wage increases. Your wage increases are worth zip or zilch when everything else costs a whole lot more. So to your point, this doesn`t help the Biden administration in terms of the political calendar, and the fact that they are trying to pass two very big infrastructure bills that are important long term, but in the short term the things that people are complaining about every night at the dinner table, none of this solves for that.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Jacob, if they could have one extra asset at the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 , what would it be? Is it truck drivers or is it more people to unload these boats?\par \par \uc2 WARD: I think that the -- you know, obviously we`ve learned that the supply chain was not built for resiliency. Right? It was built to be as cheap as possible, and that`s why it is now turning out to be so fragile. But certainly I think if the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  had its way -- I don`t want to speak for them, but they would certainly point out trucking is a huge problem. You know, a report from the Major Trucking Industry Association found that we`re going to need another million truck drivers in the next 10 years. Right?\par \par \uc2 People are falling out of that industry like crazy. And as a result once things come off the boats you see behind me there`s nowhere necessarily for them to go if they can`t get them to the warehouses and beyond. So the supply chain, you know, is very, very amazing, and we live in an extraordinary globalized world, but, whoa, does it turn out to be fragile, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Truck drivers, just when people were probably thinking that industry was going to get automated. It may but not yet.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Ward, Stephanie Ruhle, thank you --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Go ahead, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We saw scores of truckers -- remember we saw scores of truckers retire last year or shift. It`s not necessarily a labor shortage, it`s but a shift. Remember Amazon has hired millions of people to their distribution centers in the last year as have other big box stores and retailers. And that`s where a lot of those truckers have gone.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. No. There`s competition for that labor group. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Ward, Stephanie Ruhle, appreciate both of you.\par \par \uc2 For more on this issue facing the White House Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg will join my colleague Geoff Bennett for a one-on-one discussion on this issue in a few moments so be sure to stick around.\par \par \uc2 But first, coming up, an update on the closely watched gubernatorial race in Virginia as Democrats have less than three weeks to hang on to an incredibly tight lead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:40]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. An update now in that closely watched race for Virginia governor which remains for the Democrats, they would argue, uncomfortable tight less than three weeks to go from election night. A new poll from our friends over at TBS News has the Democrat Terry McAuliffe with a narrow lead over Republican Glenn Youngkin. It`s way inside the poll margin error. For what it`s worth, Republicans have now claimed that their internal polling actually has Youngkin ahead.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s an off-cycle election, and the thing about all of these polls you`ve got to be careful is, it`s all about the turnout projection in the poll. Trust me, you can have a correct poll that gives you multiple results depending on how you think who`s going to show up.\par \par \uc2 Both campaigns are looking for ways to boost enthusiasm in the final stretch. McAuliffe, it means bringing in as many Democratic Party heavyweights that he can, like Barack Obama, Joe Biden, Stacey Abrams, First Lady Jill Biden to campaign for him. You`re going to hear a lot of the T word from all of those folks. For Youngkin, it means hammering the issue of education with new ads after McAuliffe stumbled on questions of how involved parents should be in school curriculums.\par \par \uc2 Circle your calendars, folks. November 2nd is going to be a big night whenever the outcome is going to tell us a lot about what the landscape for `22 will look like as well and we`re going to be there.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a deep dive into how some states like South Dakota have become global tax havens and how billionaires paying millions to hide trillions.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. What does Wrigley chewing gum, the Hyatt Hotels, and a Brazilian orange juice baron, and the former vice president of the Dominican Republic all have in common? They all reportedly have substantial wealth hidden away in a global tax haven and it`s right here in the United States. That`s right. Not in Panama, not in Switzerland. South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 According to the Pandora Papers, that bombshell report released 10 days ago detailing nearly 12 million confidential documents, South Dakota is among a number of U.S. states most popular these days among the ultrarich for stashing billions with little oversight. And today South Dakota is home to the country`s largest number of trusts. That`s how this money is hidden. So much so that according to "The Washington Post," the state`s trust system has quadrupled in value in the last decade and now holds $367 billion in assets, all of it untaxable and largely anonymous, at least until now.\par \par \uc2 The kicker? It`s all legal. Thanks to a number of tax loopholes and state laws. So I know what you`re wondering because it`s what I`m wondering. How did this happen and how did South Dakota become the place?\par \par \uc2 Chuck Collins has written extensively about this issue, about how the global rich hides their wealth, and he has known about the South Dakota tax haven status for years and in fact he was trying to sound the alarm on this. He himself was once the heir to the Oscar Mayer Wiener fortune but gave his wealth away to focus on inequality. He is now a program director at the Institute for Policy Studies, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you, Chuck, I believe it was just in the last week that we got more countries signing on to sort of a global flat tax of -- at least a baseline agreement. And what I was thinking about is here we`re trying -- you have the Treasury secretary trying to get other countries to sort of not become a tax giveaway and here we are in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Give us a history lesson here. How did South Dakota become essentially the world`s -- one of the world`s great tax havens?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK COLLINS, DIRECTOR, PROGRAM ON INEQUALITY AND THE COMMON GOOD, INSTITUTE FOR POLICY STUDIES: Well, South Dakota`s secret sauce is that they don`t disclose who the beneficiaries are of the trust. Their secrecy. Secondly, they don`t charge any taxes, income tax or taxes on the trust assets. And their third important ingredient is you could have a perpetual trust in South Dakota. So starting in 1983, the trust industry went to the state and they changed the laws to eliminate a provision of property law that basically says a trust has to wind down within a generation or two.\par \par \uc2 And so those three components basically make South Dakota a real destination and attraction for what we call dynasty trusts, people who want to park and sequester billions of dollars forever.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What I can`t figure out here is what benefit an average resident of South Dakota gets from this.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Probably not much of a benefit at all. I mean, in a small state there are probably 500 people who work in the trust industry. So those are pretty good-paying jobs, I assume those people pay some income tax. But otherwise there`s not a lot of benefits to the people of South Dakota. And in some ways there`s a vulnerability that they`re exposing themselves now to criminal wealth from around the world because they have become essentially accomplices to that wealth heist, if you will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So how do we -- and I know you`re making this argument that we`re trying to, on the one hand, as I just noted at the beginning, we`re trying to be a leader in trying to not have a race to the bottom on corporate taxation policy around the world. At the same time, when it comes to wealth inequality, you know, are we, you know -- are we putting our money where our mouth is. Do we need to change our laws on the federal level to prevent states from doing this?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, I think, Chuck, that`s probably the direction to go. I mean, this is a result of the federalist system where states have a lot of autonomy. And Alaska and Delaware and now probably half the states in the United States are in the chase to attract this trust money, create these dynasty trusts. But the federal government can do two things right away. One is they can have a federal definition of trusts that limits their lifespan, particularly as it relates to the estate tax and gift tax.\par \par \uc2 And the other is, we did a year ago pass something called the Corporate Transparency Act, that requires limited liability companies to disclose who their real beneficial owners are. And we could expand that to include trusts. So those are two things that would sort of limit that race to the bottom between states, that competition between states, and bring us in alignment with a lot of the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I`m a bit short on time, but what`s the likelihood -- I mean, who pushes hardest against this that has sway over politicians?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: You know, there`s a powerful wealth defense industry, the tax lawyers, the wealth managers, they have a vested interest in this.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211014ehad00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Trump Directs Former Aides to Ignore Subpoenas; Deep Dive into the Modern Space Race; School Mask Debate Heats Up in Tennessee County; January 6th Committee Threatens Criminal Referral to DOJ; Biden Administration to Restore National Monuments, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Jacob Ward, Cal Perry, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Kelly  O`Donnell,, Mark Murray \par \uc2 8,576 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- bridges, ports, et cetera. We`re among the first in the world to guarantee access to universal education back at the turn of the 20th century.\par \par \uc2 Now, the organization of Economic Cooperation and Development, catch this, ranks America 35 out of 37 major economies when it comes to investing in an early childhood education as a percentage of GDP. 35 out of 37.\par \par \uc2 On all these investments that fuel strong economy, we`ve taken -- we`ve taken our foot off the gas, and the world has taken notice, including our adversaries. And now they`re closing the gap.\par \par \uc2 Look, so it`s essential that we have to regain the momentum we lost. As my wife says all the time, a professional she is, any country that out- educates us is going to out-compete us.\par \par \uc2 The work of our time is to prepare ourselves for, to be competitive to win the fast-changing 21st-century global economy. That`s why I proposed two critical pieces of legislation being debated here in Washington right now.\par \par \uc2 One focus on the investments we need to make in the physical infrastructure in America, roads, bridges, ports, etcetera. The second focus is on the investments we needed to make in the American people, to make us more competitive.\par \par \uc2 I know this is my legislation, and I feel strongly about it but the people who have the most at stake are the American people, so we need to stay focused on what these bills will mean to the people who are just looking for a little bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 A fair chance to build a decent middle-class life to succeed and thrive is better than just hanging on by their fingernails, read to keep an eye, an eye on what`s fundamentally at stake for our country, the ability to compete and win the race of the 21st century as we did the 20th century, a race that other countries are doing everything they can to win.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, China has spent around three times as much on infrastructure. Three times as much as a share of its economy the United States has.\par \par \uc2 Our infrastructure bill makes investments we need to rebuild the arteries of our economy, the roads, the highways, the bridges, the ports, the airports, the rail, and were going to allow us to replace lead water pipes, which are poisoning our children and families. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 Build a modern energy grid that can withstand storms and carry renewable energy across America. Make high-speed internet affordable and available to everywhere in America and create good union jobs in the process of putting the debt together.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to make the largest investment of public transit in American history and we`re going to make the most important investments on our rail since the creation of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  50 years ago, but isn`t enough just to invest in our physical infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to lead the world like we used to. If we`re going to do that we also have to invest in our people. That`s what my second bill does, the Build Back Better plan. That`s what it does.\par \par \uc2 Today, only about half of the three and four-year-olds in America are enrolled in early childhood education -- early childhood education. In Germany, France, the UK, Latvia, that number`s more than 90 percent, we`re falling behind. It`s not just early education.\par \par \uc2 According to one study, America ranks, catch this, America ranks 33rd out of the 44 advanced economies when it comes to the percentage of our young people who have attained a post-high school degree. The United States, 33rd out of 44?\par \par \uc2 My build back plan -- Build Back Better plan gets us back on track to making four additional years of public education available for every person in America.\par \par \uc2 Two years of high-quality preschool in the front end, which indicates that over 56 percent of the children are able to go through all 12 years and beyond without any interruption. And investments in community college, so our students can gain the skills they need. Carve out a piece -- a place for themselves in the 21st-century economy.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to help build families, and we`re going to help them afford to care for their new baby, a child, an elderly relative. It`s going to extend the tax credit for families with children.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to help us meet the moment on climate change and become a global leader in the fast-growing clean energy industries like solar and wind power.\par \par \uc2 The whole world knows that the future of the auto industry is electric. Electric and battery technology. We need to make sure America builds that future, instead of falling behind. We should build those vehicles, and the batteries that go into them, and the charging stations they`re going to need, the 500,000 we`re going to build across America.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Here in the United States, we should be doing this. And look, for getting this done, we`re going to breathe new life into our economy and our workforce, and we`re going to breathe cleaner air at the same time.\par \par \uc2 These are the kinds of investments that will get America back in the game, and give our workers a chance, a fighting chance. Economists left, right, and center agreed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Moody`s on Wall Street projected, at the investments in these bills will bring us a higher GDP and additional 2 million jobs per year and lower unemployment.\par \par \uc2 These bills are not about left versus right, or moderate versus progressive, or anything else that pits Americans against one another. These bills are about competitiveness versus complacency -- competitiveness versus complacency, opportunity versus decay.\par \par \uc2 For about leading the world, our weather going to let the world passes by. The American people understand what`s at stake here. They understand that when workers and families have a better shot, America has a better shot. Given half a chance to the American people have never, never, ever, ever left their country down.\par \par \uc2 Today, I receive more evidence of the progress we`re making and I know we can make a lot more in the days ahead. I want to thank you, and God bless you all, and may God protect our troops. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in Washington, in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been watching President Biden deliver remarks following weaker than expected jobs report again, using the bully pulpit to also push his agenda on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 This is the second month in a row the job growth has been way below expectations. And his remarks come as some Democrats have grown a bit uneasy with the President`s declining approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 The President tried to put a good spin on the report saying it`s a sign of continued progress, talking about all the jobs added since he began his administration. He also took the opportunity to make a pitch for his package of sweeping legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 But speaking of his agenda, Democrats are now caught in a classic Catch-22. They need the President to be popular to help pass his agenda, but at the same time, they`ve got to pass his agenda so that he can be more popular. That`s the dilemma for Democrats in a nutshell.\par \par \uc2 One thing they probably can all agree on is that a weak President won`t help them solve the problem, and weak job numbers don`t help either.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Democrats and Republicans are trying to figure out just how much or how little political capital the President actually has. In poll after poll, his approval ratings have been sliding.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic candidate for Virginia Governor Terry McAuliffe has told supporters that the President is actually a political head win for his campaign. And that`s saying something.\par \par \uc2 If a guy like Biden can help a guy like Terry McAuliffe win in a state like Virginia, that`s a huge alarm bell for the Democratic Party everywhere. And polls now have that race within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 It`s possible, maybe even likely, that things will start breaking Biden`s way. After all, there has been slow progress among Democrats on his agenda. The worst of the Delta COVID surge appears over, vaccines for younger kids could be available soon, and there will be no default at least this month. The economy could also start exceeding expectations, you never know.\par \par \uc2 But right now, at this moment, the problem facing the White House and Democrats is that they all agree where they need to go. They just don`t know if the party or the White House has the political muscle to get them there.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk about all of it is NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell at the White House, NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill, with me on set is NBC News Senior Political Editor Mark Murray and Democratic Strategist Cornell Belcher joins us as well.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly, I`ll start with you. After this another tumultuous week, the White House has managed to buy themselves two months of breathing room on the debt ceiling, on the domestic agenda, no government shutdown coming. How motivated, how focused is this White House to absolutely maximize that time to get something done?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And breathing room, Garrett, is a phrase the President often likes to use, and one that he is living with right now, giving himself some political space as these big issues giving him -- are unfolding, giving him a chance to try to explain them more directly to the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, what we just saw the White House do was take what was in many ways a frustrating jobs report and tease out some of the things within it that are in fact, hopeful for their agenda.\par \par \uc2 And for the President, which we typically don`t hear in these remarks talking about even when the data was collected, that it helps to give people a mental picture of well if you can recall a couple of weeks ago, what was the sort of state of COVID in the country and that kind of thing. That`s usually not the way they lay out a speech like this.\par \par \uc2 So, the President using what he could from this jobs report as a way to spray poured into some of the areas that he is more anxious to talk about.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And the chicken and egg dilemma you talked about with his own popularity and the fate of his own programs, and how will that play out.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a moment where the President has been hearing the voices of fellow Democrats about him needing to lead, even more, using a bit of a, you know, set of remarks on a Friday afternoon. It`s kind of a pleasant, but maybe a little cloudy gray day here and that seems to be the kind of set dressing around this picture for him.\par \par \uc2 Some things to talk about that he thinks are a roadmap for the way to go forward, and acknowledging that this isn`t exactly what they were looking for.\par \par \uc2 So, for the President, these are challenging times, efforts, and work being done here, an idea is not enough when you have such a narrow margin, and you have outside events and forces that determine so much of the way this goes, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And they`re out -- they`re out from underneath those outside events, at least for now, but these things have a funny way of cropping back up.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, we`ve gone through just nuts last couple of weeks on Capitol Hill. Democrats got this temporary win on the debt ceiling last night, they`re talking about the breathing room that Kelly and I were just talking about. But how are things going on the reconciliation package?\par \par \uc2 You`ve had \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Joe Manchin, you know, have dropped the pretense that they were talking to separate audiences, they`re kind of talking at each other through dueling press conferences. That`s not exactly where you want to be when you need both of those men on the same page to pass something.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely not. The fact that Senator Sanders has held two press conferences in as many days focus mostly on Senator Manchin, and to a lesser extent, Senator Sinema is really telling about how these negotiations are going on the President`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 And these job numbers put a lot of pressure on Democrats, additional pressure, especially as unemployment benefits, the Federal unemployment benefits expired last month, and so if the jobs do not keep pace, then you have people with less money in their pockets, and that`s going to be a huge problem for Democrats moving forward, especially as this is supposed to be a post-pandemic economic boom, one that is not quite there yet.\par \par \uc2 But that`s why you have a lot of Democrats on Capitol Hill who are arguing the necessity and the urgency of the Build Back Better plan, this multi- trillion dollar bill.\par \par \uc2 You saw a statement from Leader Hoyer in the House of Representatives today who focused on women, the fact that women are not returning to the workforce, he says is tied directly to the need to more federal assistance for child care. So, they are really trying to use this opportunity as a way to push the members, get their members united in order to pass something.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll say, Garrett, I do talk to a lot of Moderates, especially in the House of Representatives in these swing districts who are worried about it and are worried about President Biden`s polling numbers. They say that it makes it a lot more difficult to pass his agenda when he is unpopular.\par \par \uc2 Now, Speaker Pelosi counters that argument saying look, if we are a divided partly -- party, then you are definitely going to lose, so everyone needs to stick together. But they have a really difficult path ahead of them, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We saw this last night, when the party that is united in one of these standoffs, almost always tends to be the one that comes out on top.\par \par \uc2 Cornell, let`s talk about those presidential approval numbers. We`ve got some recent polling we can put up on screen. President`s numbers are down across the board.\par \par \uc2 Their numbers aren`t terrible, but they`re sure not great, especially as we`re kind of peeking around the corner at Virginia. What`s your take on why we`re seeing this slide for the President? He seems to be taking a hit on almost every issue.\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, that could -- they QUINNIPIAC poll says a bit a little of an outlier, let`s just say that, but no, the President`s job pool ratings are not great.\par \par \uc2 But guess what? How could they be great when you have you know, nearly, you know, see over here, 7000 Americans dying of COVID and you still have a pandemic raging of the unvaccinated as we -- as we try to get people -- more Americans vaccinated, and you are going from manufactured crisis to manufactured crisis?\par \par \uc2 What I don`t think is a big enough part of this story is how successful Mitch McConnell is being, quite frankly, at driving this political -- driving a political narrative.\par \par \uc2 I`ve seen this play before in 2009 right, going in 2010 where Mitch McConnell, and the Republicans, you know, create as much chaos, don`t put the, you know, aren`t for governing at all, and block -- and try to block everything, right? And I think the debt ceiling piece for this --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But Cornell, Mitch McConnell is not responsible for, you know, the Afghanistan situation, he`s not responsible for the border situation. I mean, Biden`s getting here across the board here.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, but let`s talk the -- let`s talk about what he is responsible for. And right now, what is it panic through Americans, quite frankly, to the -- to the global marketplace? It`s a debt ceiling main -- debt ceiling crisis, which is our official, you know, manufactured crisis.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: And you know what we`re going to have again in the next couple of weeks? We`re going to have this manufactured crisis all over again, right?\par \par \uc2 So, it`s -- so you know, Washington has chaos, Washington isn`t working, and who did they ultimately blame? They blame the people who are -- who are -- who are in charge, and that happens to be -- that happens to be Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the flip side of this, you know, I do think Democrats are going to pass the infrastructure, I do think they`re going to pass that major parts of Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 And the question becomes, can they sell that to the American people and connect that with the values and needs of the American people while at the same -- while at the same time pivoting and making Mitch McConnell and Republicans pay some price for blocking everything? We were not able to do that in 2010, is it a challenge to see if Democrats are going to be able to do it in the next midterm?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s selling the accomplishment is a key part of this. So, Mark, we`ve talked about the polling here as the catch-22, you know, you can`t be popular until you pass something, you can`t pass something until you`re popular. How did the Democrats break out of it? Does it involve even going back and doing more salesmanship on the stuff they`ve already gotten done?\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: It`s winning, Garrett. You know, this is a President that hasn`t actually had a win since his first 100 days in office, and so we`re seeing him in his remarks today, trying to take credit for a disappointing jobs report saying, hey, we have legislation right around the corner that`s going to help you. That still hasn`t passed yet.\par \par \uc2 And I also think it`s important to look at the whole COVID situation, too that`s been dragging down the President. You know, this summer, and fall, was supposed to be the time where everyone was going to be traveling, everyone`s excited.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we`re a more normal place than we were a year ago, but certainly not to the expectation that many people had, and Delta surge really did kind of impact that.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is a White House that needs some wins, that needs to get the COVID situation under control, and then you know, it needs to keep its party together. But governing is always really hard.\par \par \uc2 You know, every administration that I`ve covered, Garrett, going back to George W. Bush, to the Barack Obama, the Donald Trump, like when they get to the governing phase, in the Capitol Hill back and forth, and the crisis overseas, it gets really hard. And the thing is you want though be as popular as you can before you start going into those elections.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, that`s right. Kelly, is COVID still job one for this White House? Is that where everything starts and ends here? If they start getting better COVID numbers, is that the thing that maybe unlocks the kind of momentum that makes the rest of these things possible?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, you can argue that that is certainly their top priority in terms of the forces and attention of the administration. And as we saw, when Delta variant became worse, how that hurt the administration.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, one would expect that as children get vaccinated, and we`ve seen a dip in some of the COVID numbers, that that might give a benefit to the administration.\par \par \uc2 And they certainly argue that their decision, the President said he was hesitating to go with the vaccine mandate, but has done that now, and we`re seeing examples of that in all kinds of workplaces around the country, his argument that vaccination, does improve the economy, and helps not only those in the workplace but the consumer piece of that as well.\par \par \uc2 So, if you consider COVID, not only the life and death issue, but the economic issue, the President`s journey through this has, in fact, gone in some ways as the virus has gone. And if he`s going to get any benefit from a downturn, they certainly would want to seize upon that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Leigh Ann, let`s go back to what we`ve been seeing on Capitol Hill last night. Democrats had this sort of victory on the debt ceiling part of it, but there`s already some concern that the way they`ve handled it might cause them some more problems going forward.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking about Joe Manchin and the frustration he and other Senators had after Chuck Schumer seem to spike the ball, maybe a little bit, before getting into the end zone last night on the debt ceiling extension. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): You can`t be playing politics, you don`t understand, on both sides, OK? And both sides have been very guilty of this and the frustration was built up. And I`m sure Chuck`s frustration was built, but that was not a way to take it out. We disagree. We`ve done a different look.\par \par \uc2 I`m just saying it`s just civility is gone, OK, and I`m not going to be part of getting rid of it. I`m going to try to bring it back and I speak out when I see someone who`s (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: A politics` not beanbag. But if people that the Democrats need to get things done like Joe Manchin, who`s needed for basically everything are upset with Chuck Schumer, how much more challenging does that make things in the Senate, and what`s the dynamic now are they -- is this one of those situations where the Senators desperately need the recess week to just get away from each other for a little while?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That`s absolutely true. They need to get away from each other. I`m also told that staff are exhausted as well. They have just had it and they need a break. So, perhaps this recess week could be good for everyone involved. It`s been really intense around here.\par \par \uc2 But, Garrett, you bring up such a good point because Senator Joe Manchin and even Senator Sinema, they`re critical to the Democrats` agenda. They need both of those to pass anything.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And I`m hearing that they are feeling a lot of pressure on every single issue. And this whole debt limit issue and talk about carving out the filibuster, either doing that or having the weight of the nation`s economy on their shoulders, really took a lot out of them.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, pushing aside and pushing away these two Senators from Senator Schumer and from the Democrats can be risky, it can make it much more difficult to bring them into the fold.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s going to be very tricky. They`re trying to keep this party united, and there`s members of this party who aren`t thrilled with the Democratic strategy at this point.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: One of the people who`s been not thrilled with the way that Democrats are operating here in Washington has been Terry McAuliffe, the Democratic nominee for governor in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Cornell, what does McAuliffe need from Democrats on this side of the Potomac if he`s going to win in a blue-ish state, but that`s always competitive in these off-year elections?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes. Now, it`s a good question. And look, sometimes -- and I`m guilty of this, too, we can read too many things into off-year elections. And we look back, you know, Terry didn`t blow the doors off last time, I think Terry will, in fact, win but it`s not like he`s going to win by large, large margins. You know, Virginia, still a tough -- a tough state, especially, in off years.\par \par \uc2 I think what Terry needs is what Democrats need sort of nationally, going into the-- going into midterms. They need some wins, and they need for middle-American not to think that Washington is in chaos, and more importantly, and that I think this is really key here is, we need our base to be as fired up and as energized and ready to be mobilized as Republican basis. And what we`ve seen time and time after time in going to these off your election, these midterm elections is they just aren`t.\par \par \uc2 And I think if Terry struggles in Virginia, he`s not going to -- he`s not going to struggle because of swing voters, he`s going to struggle because our Progressive base in Virginia, are not fired up, and thinking that Democrats are fighting for the things that are most important for them.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at justice and policing, and when you look at voting rights, those are the two top issues for Progressive voters, especially the Progressive voters of color going into the last presidential election, and right now Democrats need to get something done on them.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And those are the issues that are probably the most stuck in Washington. But Mark every other election we`ve seen in the last couple of years, in 2017 when Ralph Northam won, in 2018 when Democrats picked up more seats, and of course, in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Trump was either on the ballot or this looming figure, nothing unites Democrats like the specter of Donald Trump, and you`re seeing them do that same thing in the Virginia race painting, Glenn Youngkin, as Donald Trump with the jump shot basically. Is that going to be how Democrats are brought to coalesce here in the closing months of that campaign?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: We saw that earlier in the race. In a lot of ways the vaccine politics and the mandates that Terry McAuliffe has talked about and Glenn Youngkin has been opposing the vaccine mandates, sometimes it`s been more of a proxy for Donald Trump without even mentioning his name.\par \par \uc2 But look, this governor`s race is always lets us know how the political winds are blowing. I don`t think it`s going to predict like how the midterms are going to look and certainly, it`s hard to actually see you know, who`s going to end up winning next month in Virginia, but it`s our good friend, Dave Wasserman from the Cook Political Report says, watch the margins.\par \par \uc2 Like, if Terry McAuliffe is able to win by two or three points, it would too or too be a really good win for Democrats. Joe Biden won the state by 10 points. And that seven-point margin was the same thing that we saw in 2017 when Ralph Northam won Virginia by nine points after Hillary Clinton had won it by five points --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Huge warning shot.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: -- so again, like who wins and losses, that always matters but the margins matter as well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s going to be fascinating to watch. All right, Mark Murray, Cornell Belcher, Kelly and Leigh Ann, the whole crew, thank you all for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 And up next, four key Trump administration officials all ignoring subpoenas from the Congressional Committee investigating the January 6 attack. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Trump administration officials routinely defied House Democrats subpoenas while Trump was in the White House and that posture does not seems to have changed with Trump no longer in office.\par \par \uc2 Four Trump allies who are subpoenaed by the January 6 Committee missed yesterday`s deadline to submit documents, and there`s no indication, any of them will agree to be deposed next week or anytime soon either.\par \par \uc2 And as a source confirms NBC News Politico`s reporting, that Trump has told former aides to defy the Committee`s subpoenas outright.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a spokesperson for Trump`s Political Action Committee tells NBC News that the former President plans on asserting executive privilege to prevent the January 6 Committee from obtaining information.\par \par \uc2 And The Washington Post is now reporting the Trump confidant, Steve Bannon, has already rebuffed the Committee`s subpoena citing the Trump team`s claim of executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former U.S. Attorney Harry Litman. And Harry, these subpoena deadlines have come and gone for these four individuals, what happens now? Congress doesn`t have a lot of great options to do that.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: It doesn`t, Garrett. They are starting to sort of rattle sabers and say everything is on the table, but everything consists of three not very good possibilities.\par \par \uc2 And the basic problem here is if Trump is able to maneuver things to get to court to make his claims as spurious and meritless, as they may be, it ties things up.\par \par \uc2 They -- there`s a real disconnect between the 18 to 24 months it takes to run through the federal courts and the time that the January 6 committee needs to do its work in.\par \par \uc2 His claims here are particularly bogus. It`s 100 percent clear he has no right, no right at all to assert executive privilege that belongs only to the occupant of the White House, Joe Biden, nevertheless, he can assert it, wait for courts to reject it, and by then, the game is over. At least that`s the hope.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Let`s put a pin in the executive privilege part of it. I`m -- you touched on this as the -- as the attorney -- as the legal expert here, the Trump case is weak. Is the whole point of this, just to run out the clock and hope that Republicans are in charge of the House come next year, in 2023 and say, you know, it never mind with this whole thing?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yes. That I could say more, but really that is it. And the timing is of course everyone knows that the Committee`s, to finish it to work by saying next summer, and this is the strategy that`s already proven effective when they had bad claims before but at least he was the President.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it`s particularly an opposite for Steve Bannon, who a note who wasn`t even in the executive branch. But it`s just that, go to court, anyone can sue for anything and it`s going to take some time.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Congress could repair this, this disconnect between trying things in federal court and complying with congressional subpoenas but to date they have not.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And you touched on this as well. But I feel like we`re going to hear a lot about it over the next couple of weeks. Executive privilege is the privilege of the current executive. Right? I mean, if the Trump administration -- Trump administration. If former President Trump says he wants to exert privilege over something, doesn`t the Biden administration then have to say yes, you can or no, you can`t? Is that a -- do they have to proactively approve things? How exactly will that work?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: One hundred percent. If Trump says he wants it, could be the same as Clinton saying he wants it or you and me. He`s a citizen. He has no authority whatsoever to assert it. So that`s right. It goes to the Biden administration. They`ve already indicated that they won`t assert it here. And that is the big news.\par \par \uc2 There`s one other point about the Biden administration, though, Garrett. Adam Schiff, Bennie Thompson and others are saying here`s what we`ll do this time. We will refer it to the Department of Justice for criminal charges. That would work, except the Department of Justice has a policy of not prosecuting criminal contempt charges against former or current members of the executive branch. And those two things are going to ram right into each other, and we know Merrick Garland has been loathed to just throw policies out the window. I think that`s what it`s probably coming down to.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So of what value are the subpoenas then other than a very nice invitation to come testify if you feel like it?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: You tell me. But there are at least other people. I mean, a subpoena is a subpoena. It used to mean something, and there are other witnesses. For example, in the interim report from yesterday, we had important people from the Department of Justice who will testify. The \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower who will testify. But these subpoenas against these four people if they`re going to go to court even with worthless claims as a practical matter, might be worthless.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: As a political matter, then, I think we`re looking at a committee that`s going to have to rely more on voluntary interviews and widening that circle. Right? I mean, not everyone has the same resources that the former president has or some of his closest aids will have to fight these subpoenas as a political matter then or if you were building a case as a U.S. attorney, is that how you would look at this? Sort of start on the outside and work your way back in while those on the inner circle continue to fight these subpoenas?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: That`s exactly right. And they`ve done that in large degree. I think we have the basic outline, but they haven`t shaded it in. They may not be able to. So, of course, there`s a political gamble or risk that Trump and company are willing to take here which is to have the claims that are pretty damning be unanswered. So that will happen. But, of course, they were willing to do that in the impeachments, that I think pretty well established the charges, but he just folded his arms and said no way and expressed contempt. And at least for his base, which is the only lookout for him, it seems to be, that continues to matter.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Harry Litman, we`re going to probably be checking in again. A lot of work ahead for that committee and a lot of legal analysis will be needed.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new kind of space race. We know that space has become a playground for the rich, but apparently it`s also become a way for people to get rich. We`ll have more on that.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:37:12]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. If it`s Friday, that means there`s a new episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" available on demand on Peacock. This week we`re taking a deep dive into the modern-day space race. It`s not just about billionaires sending themselves into orbit. Private businesses are now leveraging new technology to do things that only NASA used to be able to do and they`re making money while they`re at it.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Jacob Ward has more -- Jacob.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, I know it`s strange to be with you talking about space from where I am, San Francisco, but the truth is in the same way that this place has become a, you know, basically a growing bed of activity when it comes to startups and all the other spaces, it is also a startup hub for space. There is, in fact, an operation center, the kind of thing that you would only see at NASA a few decades ago, located just a few blocks from where I standing. It belongs to a company that now sells data from a fleet of satellites that it maintains in space. And we went inside to have a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WARD (voice-over): Why is data from space so valuable? Well, think about it this way. If you`re an agricultural company looking to make farms more productive, you could put a drone and scan a couple hundred acres to see what plants are dry, sure, or you could buy imagery from a satellite that can see 700 million acres for about 100 times less money.\par \par \uc2 Affordable daily satellite imagery is also a vital weapon against climate change. Why? Because until now, we just didn`t have enough data to see the change happening in real time.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM MARSHALL, CEO, PLANET LABS: We can`t stop deforestation if we`re only measuring it every year if the deforestation happens on days. Same with emission. Same with agriculture and same with all of these things. You need data on the time scale of change to take care of it.\par \par \uc2 WARD: The principle of planet is that anyone aside from terrorist organizations and embargoed nations can buy these images. Clients can even task a handful of these larger satellites with special jobs and keep the footage exclusive for a period of time. Academics and private sector analysts using these satellites have pulled huge geopolitical threats into the public eye that previously only an intelligence agency would have known about.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: We found a set of about 100 nuclear weapon silos in China that are previously undisclosed. That`s important, you know, deal, of course. We`ve actually found them in our scanner fleet and then took a closer look with these.\par \par \uc2 WARD (on-camera): And that was you selling that imagery to --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: In this case think tanks that were taking a look at it. So no, not the government.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Not even the government was buying it?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WARD: Now, Garrett, if you`re looking at a world in which suddenly these private corporations are not only going to space but determining what we get to see from there, what role does NASA have? Well, NASA is going to continue it says to have this exploratory mission, and there are all sorts of things that they will do that a private company would never do.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 In fact, they announced this week that next month they`re going to be launching a whole mission to look at the possibility of knocking deadly asteroids off course. Asteroids that might otherwise reach us. Nothing is currently threatening us but that`s the experiment they`ll be doing. But of course, how are they going to get that experiment up there? Well, it`s going to go up on a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Falcon 9 rocket. We`re seeing this new world in which data, rocket themselves, and the science are all blending together in this public and private world. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Asteroids, the arcade game now in real life. Jacob Ward there.\par \par \uc2 We can see more of Jacob`s reporting plus Chuck`s conversation with the NASA administrator and an astronaut on the latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." That`s available right now on demand on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the White House is moving to restore protections to national monuments that were stripped away by the Trump administration, and we were expecting to hear from President Biden on that second time now in this hour, but as is often the case, things are running behind schedule at the White House so we`ll be ready for those remarks if and when they happen.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The president is expected to make more news from the White House today with remarks coming this afternoon. President Biden will announce that he`s restored protections to three national monuments that were previously stripped away by the Trump administration. Bears Ears and Grand Staircase-Escalante both in southern Utah each lost more than a million acres of protected land during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Former president Trump also lifted restrictions for Northeast Canyons and Seamounts national monuments located just south of Cape Cod to allow for commercial fishing. But all that changes today with President Biden who is restoring the original boundaries of all three monuments. The White House says this is part of the administration`s effort to better protect and conserve land and was a decision made in close consultation with Interior Secretary Deb Haaland.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 In a statement Secretary Haaland said, quote, "The historic connection between indigenous peoples and the land surrounding Bears Ears and the power of the landscape to the tribal and local communities is undeniable. This action will allow us to protect our sacred native sites and cherish these monuments for generations to come."\par \par \uc2 Up next, as millions of parents wait anxiously to find out when their kids can get vaccinated, others are wary for their children to be first in line and others still are still pushing back on vaccines and masks. We`ll cover it all.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. We`re hearing some cautious optimism about the state of the pandemic from many of the country`s top doctors. That`s because every important metric cases, hospitalizations, deaths, and vaccinations are trending in the right direction nationwide. And now that \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  has asked the FDA to approve its vaccine for kids, an estimated 30 million children age 5 through 11 could be just weeks away from getting shots into the arm.\par \par \uc2 The director of the National Institute of Health called the drop in cases encouraging this morning but warned against complacency. And U.S. Surgeon General Vivek Murthy told my colleagues on "MORNING JOE" that there`s still no clear consensus as to exactly why this situation has improved but that good news also doesn`t mean people should stop getting vaccinated or to take their masks off quite yet.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC correspondent Cal Perry. He attended a heated school board meeting in Knox County, Tennessee, where the community is divided over masks in schools.\par \par \uc2 And Cal, the school board meetings I feel like have become the new battle ground in the pandemic. We`re now two months into the school year in Knox County. There`s been an executive order, at least one major lawsuit, and multiple policy changes over masks in schools down there. Where do we stand and what are you hearing from people who are caught up in one of these kind of fights that I think we`re seeing play out all across the country?\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Look, I think the confusion that you`re laying out is what is really getting parents here on both sides of the issue. This is a large school district, 59,000 students in this school district. And as you said, the school board originally saying that they would have masks, changing their minds. You have the governor`s executive order that has now been overruled by a court. Four families sued under the ADA, that is the Americans with Disabilities Act saying that their children were at a higher risk of getting sick and having a bad outcome. The judge agreed.\par \par \uc2 So now in this county you have a mandated order for masks to be worn. And as you said, the politics of this are very divisive. It is overwhelming. These school board meetings -- I was at a meeting on Wednesday that saw more than 60 speakers and masks weren`t even on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen first to a parent who wants masks and then some of that school board meetings. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What`s really upsetting to me is that I took extraordinary measures last year to keep my family safe. And the school board failed to follow up on their promise to follow CDC guidelines and my son got COVID from school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I actually quit my job in November because I was sick and tired of having to wear a mask and being told that I had to enforce masks on these children.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is no longer an isolation room. It is no longer a segregation. As my son puts it, it is a concentration camp that he goes to eight hours a day every morning.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: The situation was so confusing for this board and this school district that they actually canceled school a week ago on Monday to try to figure out how to stay in accordance with that new court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Caught in the middle, Garrett, you have 59,000 students trying to get an education. Some of them feel like they shouldn`t have to wear a mask. And they should still be able to receive that education. Again, confusion reigns here in eastern Tennessee. We do expect, by the way, this case to move up the court so maybe some clarity down the road but for now, it`s a very political, very divisive, and a very confusing situation here, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. Some pretty wildly hyperbolic stuff in that sound you played there.\par \par \uc2 Cal Perry, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And we`ll be right back with some breaking news from Capitol Hill on the January 6th subpoenas. Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:20]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. We`re following breaking news out of Capitol Hill where the chairs of the January 6th committee have just released a statement in regards to those subpoenas we were discussing earlier of four former Trump allies.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to my colleague Leigh Ann Caldwell on the Hill. And Leigh Ann, my apologies if I have to cut you off for the return of President Biden in this hour. What are we learning?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Hey, Garrett. So we are learning how the committee in an update from the subpoena request. Of course documents were requested via subpoenas for four former Trump officials including his former chief of staff Mark Meadows. And what the committee is saying is that -- a little surprisingly actually -- Mr. Meadows and Mr. Patel are so far engaging with the select committee.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bannon, referring to Steve Bannon, has indicated that he will try to hide behind vague references to privileges of the former president. The select committee fully expects all of these witnesses to comply with our demands for both documents and deposition testimony. Interestingly enough, the committee does not reference Dan Scavino, another Trump associate who we don`t know what is happening with him. And also --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Leigh Ann, I`m sorry to do this to you, I`ve got to cut you off. President Biden is back for that national monuments event here at the White House. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL CLIMATE ADVISER: Good afternoon, everybody. Please be seated. We`re here for a celebration.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: We`re here to celebrate the latest step that President Biden is taking to protect, to conserve, and to restore the lands and the waters that all of us cherish. Today, President Biden is restoring protections for three magnificent national monuments. And this announcement follows on consultations with a wide variety of stakeholders and it fulfills a key promise to the American people.\par \par \uc2 Restoring protections for these national monuments is part of this administration`s broader commitments to protect our natural and our cultural resources, to honor tribal sovereignty, and to advance environmental justice. President Biden`s conservation agenda is also a critical part of how we`re tackling the climate crisis by protecting our ecosystems. We strengthen the power of our soils, our grasses, and our trees to trap carbon pollution.\par \par \uc2 And healthy natural systems, build up our resilience against the climate impacts that we know we are already facing. Tapping into these natural climate solutions will protect public health. They will protect us against climate impacts. They will promote biodiversity. And yes, they will grow our economy. That`s worth a clap.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: That`s why President Biden, through his Build Back Agenda, has also proposed creating -- are you ready for it -- a new civilian climate corps.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Which will partner with our unions in putting to work a new generation that looks like America, receiving good benefits and good pay, to restore the health of our public lands, our coasts, our waters, and our forests, and to advance environmental justice and help communities to better prepare for the impacts of a changing climate.\par \par \uc2 And across our administration, we`re taking a whole of government approach to conservation and to climate with the agencies that steward so many of our lands and waters like the Department of the Interior and Agriculture and Commerce, all working together to advance wind and solar, to promote climate-smart agriculture and forestry and create good paying union jobs all along the way in implementing these innovative climate solutions.\par \par \uc2 So as we celebrate today`s restoration of these three national monuments, we`re also committed to Building Back Better as we tackle our climate crisis. And with that, I am so honored to introduce my good friend, Brenda Mallory, the chair of the White House Council on Environmental Quality, who is leading on our conservation and environmental justice efforts. Brenda?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BRENDA MALLORY, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL ON ENVIRONMENTAL QUALITY CHAIR: Thank you, Gina. It is so great to see all of you in person. I can`t tell you how exciting it is to be in person on this day and this event. I want to welcome you to the White House. And I also want to thank you, each and every one of you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211008eha80002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 MTP DAILY, October 5, 2021 MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Cal Perry, Garrett Haake, Jacob Ward \par \uc2 8,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday, the fallout intensifies for \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The whistle-blower, Frances Haugen, opens up to Congress, claiming the social media company chooses profits over people. What it means for \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , big tech, Congress and consumers all ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus: President Biden hits the road to sell his infrastructure agenda, as he warns of an economic catastrophe if the U.S. defaults on its debt. It`s set to happen in less than two weeks, unless Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 And it`s one of the most intriguing revelations in the Pandora Papers, why some of the world`s biggest billionaires are sheltering their money in, of all places, South Dakota, the Cayman Islands of the continental United States?\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to begin with the latest developments from Capitol Hill, and this has nothing to do with the debt ceiling and that ridiculousness.\par \par \uc2 This is about \uc2 Facebook\uc2  where Frances Haugen, the former \uc2 Facebook\uc2  employee turned whistleblower, is testifying right now before Congress for the first time after going public on Sunday with internal company documents she says, shed light on the dangers her former employer poses to children, to our democracy, and to our society at large. That`s all.\par \par \uc2 Today`s testimony comes three weeks after The Wall Street Journal first reported on much of this \uc2 Facebook\uc2  internal research, which it received we now know from Haugen, and two days after she revealed her identity in an interview in 60 Minutes.\par \par \uc2 Today, she`s telling lawmakers directly about her concerns over Facebook`s disregard for safety and the spread of divisive content and she called on Congress to address the dangers of the Social media giant`s outsized power and influence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, WHISTLEBLOWER, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : I`m here today because I believe Facebook`s products harm children, stoke division, and weaken our democracy. The company`s leadership knows how to make \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  safer, but won`t make the necessary changes because they have put their astronomical profits before people. Congressional actions needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Today`s hearing also comes a day after \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , as well as, its platforms, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, were out of service for five hours, an outage that caused widespread disruption well beyond just Social media, because of global disruption for many people who use both WhatsApp and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for retail sales.\par \par \uc2 Big Question facing lawmakers at today`s hearing is what to do about the dangers that are posed by the social media giant? Haugen suggested in her testimony that regulators, lawmakers, and the public first need a better understanding of how the company actually operates before it figures out how to regulate it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, WHISTLEBLOWER, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : No one truly understands the destructive choices made by \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , except \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . We can afford nothing less than full transparency. As long as \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is operating in the shadows, hiding its research from public scrutiny, it is unaccountable. Until the incentives change, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  will not change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: In terms of what comes next, several Committee members are comparing \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to Big Tobacco, and there`s plenty of bipartisan anger toward the company, which means there`s an appetite for change to do something. The question is what is that something? Because it`s not clear what Congress can and will do to address this problem.\par \par \uc2 But we want to dig into it a little bit, I`m joined now by our Senior Capitol Hill Correspondent for NBC News, Garrett Haake, Jacob Ward is outside of Facebook`s headquarters in Menlo Park on the West Coast for us, and Mike Isaac is a Technology Reporter for The New York Times.\par \par \uc2 Garrett, let me start with you, at this hearing. Look, this is one of those rare days where there -- this isn`t -- where it`s not a political argument taking place via the questioning but actually, members of Congress seeming to actually want to figure out what can we learn from this whistleblower and what more do we need to know to decide how to -- how to tackle this regulatory challenge?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, I think one of the things they`re learning is that they need to know a lot more than they do. This has been the problem for Congress as it`s trying to figure out how to regulate Big Tech and these Social media platforms, in general, if they just couldn`t get their arms around it.\par \par \uc2 They were behind in the technology, they were behind on the cultural significance of these platforms, and I think having a whistleblower here, not a company official, not someone with any agenda other than exposing what they know, took some of the steam out of what, you know, we sometimes have devolved into this kind of more partisan hearings where people are trying to score political points. This was very much a fact-finding hearing.\par \par \uc2 And I think that point from Haugen that you played in the opening here about opening up Facebook`s data will be key. Lawmakers have tried to come at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in a couple of different ways, whether they are on an anti- trust basis or on a data protection basis, or whether it`s content management, which Republicans have talked about as a form of censorship.\par \par \uc2 None of those things have exactly worked, and the solution here may be trying to require \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to share a little bit more of how the special sauce, the algorithm works.\par \par \uc2 They don`t have competitors functionally. I mean, they`re a legal monopoly in so many ways. And a lot of the questioning focused around trying to understand how \uc2 Facebook\uc2  shows us what it chooses to show us before they can even start trying to regulate it, and I think the scope of that problem is in many ways revealed today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, Garrett, I almost think that the whistleblower is going to have to be hired as a legal aid on Congress -- by Congress when they figure out who to subpoena and what to subpoena? Is that what we`re learning here? I mean, in some ways it`s just got to turn into a job interview for her to come work with Congress to figure this out?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Look, it might. You know, I mean, I know you`re being a little bit facetious --\par \par \uc2 TODD: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: -- but this is little balance here, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I mean, Tech companies have run circles around regulators. They have the talent, they can pay for the talent, they can pay for the lawyers, they can pay for the lobbyists, who, in many cases used to be on staff for the same lawmakers, or for this or any previous administration. And that is really hamstrung Congress`s ability to even keep up.\par \par \uc2 Having someone with technical expertise and inside knowledge about how these companies work, I think will be a necessary step to effective regulation. And again, I think we`re only just getting started in that conversation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Garrett Haake on the point of view from Congress. Let`s go to Menlo Park where Jacob Ward is. And Jacob, I`m curious, I mean, I`ve been on the receiving end a bit of what \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is how they`re trying to respond to the whistleblower, what they believe is fair game, what they believe isn`t like they really -- they really cringe at the Tobacco Company comparisons?\par \par \uc2 But I`m curious, how are they treating her, the individual, Miss Haugen? I noticed that they`re trying to question what roles she played at the company, but that can be dangerous territory with a whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: It`s been very interesting to watch their reaction evolve over time, Chuck. Yesterday, they were still sticking to a policy of not really naming her or addressing her directly but today, you had Vice President of Communications, Amy stone, tweeting out that Miss Haugen did not work on the teams responsible for kids and teens, and thereby insinuating that somehow she`s not qualified to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 She seeming to predict this or anticipate this, actually said in her testimony, I did not work on these teams but the documents that I took out of the company were documents that everybody inside the company at a certain level had access to. I thought that was very interesting.\par \par \uc2 I also think it`s very interesting, you know, both you and Garrett are making a good point that the Congress has been behind, lawmakers have been behind the ball when it came to Tech regulation in the past, but I think this is the most sophisticated questioning I have seen yet, this, and some of the hearing with Antigone Davis.\par \par \uc2 And if you really just follow what Frances Haugen lays out, she lays out a very clear roadmap for what she thinks should be effective regulation.\par \par \uc2 She says, open up the company`s data to researchers outside of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  so that they are no longer keeping it all inside and grading their own homework, and regulate not the content but the algorithms, which she, again and again, made the point are responsible for curating of things that she says leads to bullying, leads to politicians taking positions that make no sense but that are spread widely on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and leads even to terrible crimes in countries like Ethiopia, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Jacob, the other thing that happened yesterday, it turned out to be a coincidence but in some ways, I think may have unintentionally made the argument that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is a potential monopoly and that is the fact that it had a server problem that knocked \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and WhatsApp all offline, which had a global impact.\par \par \uc2 And again, I think it sent the message at the wrong time to Congress of just how powerful this one company is. Literally, they can pull the plug on Commerce, for billions of people.\par \par \uc2 WARD: It is extraordinary, I mean, one of the things that I think traditional law-making has struggled with when it comes to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is defining its scope, right? Defining its influence in our lives, and done that in monetary terms or in any other terms.\par \par \uc2 Well, we saw it pretty clearly yesterday during the five hours of a total blackout on all these products, because you`re talking not just about losing the opportunity to, you know, waste time on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , you`re talking about family members not able to communicate across borders on WhatsApp, it`s one of the primary ways to communities -- communicate across countries. You had, you know, small businesses on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , totally unable to do what they do.\par \par \uc2 And so, any argument that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  may have made in the past about we are not an infrastructure, we do not have that kind of monopoly, it sure looked like a big chunk of infrastructure, a big almost utility knocked down for that five hour period, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s sure it will. I guess that then Mark Zuckerberg has wanted to say \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has utility. OK, but utilities are heavily, heavily regulated. Jacob Ward, Garrett Haake, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Mike Isaac, Technology Reporter here for The New York Times. And I want to play one more example of -- I think that`s being laid out here that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has a lot of power that government needs to take a look at, and that is this exchange on January 6 between Senator Klobuchar and Miss Haugen. Take a listen\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR, (D-MN): And you know that the insurrection occurred on January 6, do you think that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  turned off the safeguards because they weren`t costing the company money because it was reducing profits?\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, WHISTLEBLOWER, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has been emphasizing a false choice, they`ve said, the safeguards that were in place before the election implicated free speech.\par \par \uc2 The choices that were happening on the platform were really about how reactive and twitchy was the platform, right? Like how viral was the platform? And \uc2 Facebook\uc2  changed those safety defaults in the run-up to the election because they knew they were dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And because they wanted that growth back, they wanted the acceleration on the platform back after the election, they return to their original defaults.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike, this gets at another issue that another source of mine once said about three years ago, which is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  figured out how to get rid of child pornography, for the most part, root that out, they know how -- some of the things they claim they can`t touch they`ve -- it`s been proven they can, they can regulate some speech, if they choose to. They did for a period of time.\par \par \uc2 They do control -- they do have a monopoly on global communications that appears when you look at WhatsApp and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and what happens when they go down. Does this not basically open of -- style red carpet for government regulation now?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ISAAC, TECHNOLOGY REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: You know, I thought the thing that Miss Haugen was saying was super interesting and very -- sort of an indictment of how \uc2 Facebook\uc2  fundamentally works at its core, right? Like you said, they can go in and make these changes whenever they want to. And they can say, oh, this is an extraordinary time around the election, it`s very dangerous, we need to take a heavier hand than we normally would.\par \par \uc2 But it`s just sort of underscores how quickly things can spread across the platform, how, you know, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  likes to say they`re a mirror to society, but they`re more of an accelerant to society and can pour fuel on any fire that`s quickly spreading across the platform.\par \par \uc2 So, I think they`re -- typically they don`t like taking a heavy hand in interacting with how -- what`s spreading across the platform, and how. But the reason the January 6 example is so crucial is because it shows that this is normally how the platform works, and they can and do sometimes step in to stop it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and it`s also an acknowledgment on their part that they`re playing a role. I mean, that`s the other part of this. The fact that they can write, that they can turn the volume up or down is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious what your take was on -- I think she`s laying, the whistleblower, Miss Haugen, is laying out an argument that the current set of regulators in the \uc2 United States government\uc2  isn`t equipped to regulate \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . That almost makes this seem like an even larger problem to tackle, the -- whenever the government creates agencies lately hadn`t gone well.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC: That`s right. I mean, if you remember back to 2018, the Cambridge analytical revelations, how that was leading the news for weeks, if not months, and no real substantive bills have come out of that, that have at least gotten through Congress, no real changes have been enacted.\par \par \uc2 I think, to give, you know, Capitol Hill a bit more credit, the questions have evolved over the years since those first hearings we had, if you remember that sound bite, you know, Senator, we run ads when asked, you know how \uc2 Facebook\uc2  works. So, this hearing has definitely gone very differently compared to the past.\par \par \uc2 But she really is laying out a case that there is -- there is serious regulation to be had here. The number of lobbyists on Capitol Hill, who sort of sway -- from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , who sway their opinions and votes essentially is a veritable phalanx of people influencing and more folks in different governments, not just the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  should be able to step in intervening. And she`s going to do that when she starts speaking abroad, in different conferences in Europe, in the coming weeks, actually.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike, what are you hearing from the other technology companies that on one hand, are loving the fact that it`s \uc2 Facebook\uc2  taking the arrows, but on the other hand, maybe they`re afraid that the government regulating could hurt them whether -- I`m referring to Snap, TikTok, and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in particular?\par \par \uc2 ISAAC: Yes. No, totally. It`s a double-edged sword I think for them. For a long time, companies -- especially \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  were very content with \uc2 Facebook\uc2  being the sort of punching bag for Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I remember hearing a few years ago where \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  were all sort of present to testify, and it was basically like the two other companies were hiding behind the guy at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  waiting to take the hits.\par \par \uc2 I think, they, you know, there`s probably a sort of a bit of shot in Freud on seeing this sort of powerful reckoning around \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but they`re totally nervous, you know.\par \par \uc2 I think constantly folks at \uc2 YouTube\uc2  have been waiting for the other shoe to drop and for folks to go after \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and really rein them in, but I think \uc2 YouTube\uc2  is even probably harder to get folks in mind around just because the people who do the regulating don`t use \uc2 YouTube\uc2  at -- on the same level that they do Facebook`s properties.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s not -- I think it`s interesting, I think \uc2 YouTube\uc2  certainly doing what it did on COVID last week tells you they`re trying to not get the government`s attention too quickly. They`re doing what they can to try to, oh, pay no attention to what we`re doing right now, please. Anyway, Mike Isaac --\par \par \uc2 ISAAC: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- yes. Really appreciate having you on and your expertise with us on this beat. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC: Thanks, Chuck. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Later this hour, we`re going to talk to one of the members of Congress who is trying to figure out just what does regulating Big Tech looks like.\par \par \uc2 But first, President Biden is heading to Michigan to sell his legislative agenda. We`ve noticed Michigan and Pennsylvania are probably his two most frequently stop states. What do those states have in common even as he`s still trying to get members of his own party to buy in?\par \par \uc2 The top Biden whisperer in the Democratic Party, Jim Clyburn, joins me next on these Democratic divisions, and how the party is going to avert this economic calamity.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, another day, another meeting between President Biden and a group of Democratic lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Today`s meeting was with some Democrats who faced the toughest reelection fights next year, and it came just before President Biden left for Michigan to pitch his agenda in a swing district that is home to Democratic Congresswoman Elise Slotkin, who of course -- Elissa Slotkin, who won a seat in the 2018 midterms, slipped a Republican seat. Yesterday, Biden met with some Progressive House members.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell NBC News that he discussed the Reconciliation package priced around $2 trillion. And our sources also tell us those progressives informed the President they`d support whatever top-line number he could get from the moderate Democrats. All this comes with less than two weeks to go before the debt limit is breached.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans are standing by their pledge to do nothing in the face of this potential fiscal calamity, leaving it to the Democrats to act, and they have very few options to do this.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now is the House Democratic Whip, and on again, off, again, Biden whisperer, as you might say, Congressman Jim Clyburn of South Carolina. Congressman, it`s good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC), HOUSE MAJORITY WHIP: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, look, I know how excited all members of Congress are to negotiate in public on television. But the $2 trillion number I -- in what Progressive said in our reporting, is that your sense that, you know, the President is brought Progressives along there, and that if you can get to that number, everybody`s going to be on board?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: We know, I have been saying for quite some time now that I thought that there was a sweet stop -- spot, somewhere between Manchin`s 1.5, and Biden`s or the Senate`s 3.5, and it seems as if there`s a lot of work being done to find that sweet spot.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, what will we do, and for whom? The debate that`s going on in our party is focusing on family. And there are some people who want the focus to be towards seniors, and I`m one --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: I am a senior, and I know mine the focus, but without means test, things like Medicare. And therefore, you got a lot of seniors who are pretty well for people who will be getting this benefit. I`m most interested in right now and getting people started on life`s journey. And these low-income families that need Medicaid to be expanded, to me is where some of the focus ought to be.\par \par \uc2 So, there should be some balance here. So, that`s what the debate is going on. A lot of people say there`s infighting, there`s no infighting, it`s just, people trying to make their case as it were the emphasis ought to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sort of, read between the lines, you`re basically saying, look, there`s a -- there`s a lot of eldercare ideas that are out there and trust me, there`s a lot of us of a certain age are in the -- are in that sweet spot of elderly parents and kids under 18, right?\par \par \uc2 So, you have the squeeze and I know that you want to try to address that. But then there`s a big chunk of whether it`s pre -- universal pre-K, free community college, which is helping families get a start, helping people get started up the economic ladder. It sounds like you`re saying if you have to make a choice, spend more money on pre-K and the younger stuff first.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, that`s where my emphasis is getting young people started on life`s journey. I think that when you leave children out of the equation, we`ve got people trying to go to post-secondary education, very seldom say college, because I do believe that there`s a lot of good living to be done which post-secondary education there may not be a liberal arts college.\par \par \uc2 We have to get people -- get them on with your life`s journey in such a way that they will be productive, and we`ll maintain that pursuit until they finish.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When it comes to the debt limit issue, I understand the frustration, the Republicans have no interest in helping, they`re trying to cause chaos. It`s pretty clear what the strategy is.\par \par \uc2 Why isn`t the reaction fine, all right, Mitch McConnell, you want to do it this way, this is the last time you ever get a chance to use the weapon again and we`re going to change the rules of the debt limit, make it takes a veto-proof majority, the two-thirds ID or something? If you`re going to be forced to act alone, why not take the eye -- make sure it`s never held hostage again?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, that`s the way I feel about a lot of things. I feel that way about the debt limit, I`ve been advocated it ever since I`ve been in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 To that, make the debt limit, it`s a funny thing. I believe he might be the only country left this gap that, we need to get rid of that. And we also need to get rid of the filibuster, especially on constitutional issues.\par \par \uc2 So, I do believe that 50 percent plus one ought to carry the day with the debt limit, as well as with people`s civil and constitutional rights. We have some archaic rules that we need to just get rid of. This debt limit business is archaic and needs to be thrown to the trash pile.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have been, I would argue, probably one of the most important advice -- unofficial Political Advisors, or soothsayers to the party, and certainly to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You see the current situation that he`s in right now. What would be -- what`s your advice to him on how to navigate what is clearly a tough time for him and the Democrats politically?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: To stay true to his principles. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran on the campaign of bringing people together, he`s made those kinds of proposals. The American Rescue Act is a very, very popular piece of legislation that we are now implementing -- I should call it a plan because it`s no longer an act.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re dealing with some pretty popular issues when it comes to infrastructure, as well as when they become so-called family infrastructure. These are very popular programs.\par \par \uc2 But popular programs with the voting public, is not the same thing that he was trying to satisfy partisan -- Republicans because what is more unnecessary than putting the full faith and credit of the United States at peril? But they`re perfectly willing to do that because they think they get some partisan advantage for doing it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats need to stay in close touch with the American people and put in place policies and procedures in the process that allows us to do our work with that 50 plus -- percent plus one, and not worry about all the supermajority stuff that the filibuster allows. It`s up for us to get beyond that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Jim Clyburn, number three in the House Leadership, Democrat from South Carolina, always appreciate getting your perspective on things. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up ahead, the latest COVID confusion. As the CDC now removes its guidance against in-person family gatherings for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 Plus the disturbing rise in violence against medical staff and hospitals across the country. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Here are today`s coronavirus facts you should know starting with the latest on vaccines.\par \par \uc2 As was expected, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  announced this morning that it has officially asked the FDA to authorize its vaccine booster under emergency use. Nearly 15 million Americans did receive the single shot vaccine and now they may be eligible for a second dose once this is approved. That approval could come as soon as October 15th and that`s when an FDA advisory board committee is set to review the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  application.\par \par \uc2 And one of the nation`s top doctors has announced his departure from the National Institute of Health. A pretty familiar face. After 30 years at the agency, Dr. Francis Collins, truly one of the great gentlemen in science, has served under three presidents and is the longest serving NIH director ever. Collins will leave by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 And this just in from the CDC, their holiday guidance page is now under construction. Yesterday during hour we told you that the CDC published new safety recommendation for the upcoming holiday season. Well, those recommendations have since been removed from the Web site. The agency is expected to come out with updated guidance in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Don`t worry, Thanksgiving still in November and Christmas is still in December.\par \par \uc2 And in Missouri, at least one hospital will arm nurses and staff with panic buttons on their badges to help deal with a surge in workplace violence. This comes after a recent study found a majority of nurses who treated COVID-19 patients during a five-month period in 2020 experienced physical abuse at work at least once.\par \par \uc2 This is just a terrible, terrible trend we`re seeing, harassment of that. We`ve seen the harassment of school board members. We`re getting into a dangerous place here. Social media seems to make people think they can just behave any way they want and the actual person, too.\par \par \uc2 All right, NBC correspondent Cal Perry has been following the story of this increased violence against health care workers and he joins me now from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So, Cal, as I was saying it feels like there`s not a walk of life that hasn`t seen increased tension and the dangers of physical interactions whether it`s flight attendants, members of Congress, members of the media and clearly with COVID health care staff.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And look, I think social media as you`re pointing out is a pretty good place to start. There is this disinformation war that is happening in America and in talking to doctors and administrators the masks and the vaccines are causing a lot of friction at these hospitals. The other thing that is happening is the nature of this virus. Anyone who has a loved one and has taken that loved one to the hospital knows it`s an impossible thing to imagine being separated from the person.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve had 700,000 Americans die and a lot of them unfortunately have died alone. That is the nature of this virus. That is causing a standoff a lot of times between patients, patients` families, and doctors. It is having a devastating effect on those who work inside this industry.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to what emergency room doctor told me yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAK DAVE, NORTHEAST, GEORGIA MEDICAL CENTER CHIEF OF EMERGENCY SERVICES: There`s fewer and fewer people that feel that they can bear the risk to come to work. The risk of being exposed to COVID, the risk of, you know, verbal, physical, sexual assault. Those are real risks to a lot of folks. It`s absolutely gotten worse. This is a phenomenon that predates far before the pandemic began. It`s certainly become worse over the last 18 months.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , Chuck, now says you`re five times more likely to be injured in the workplace if you work in health care services. I was talking to some nurses yesterday who told me that before they go to the supermarket they`re taking off their badges. I cannot kind of wrap my head around that. I was thinking 20 years ago, sort of clapping for firefighters and police officers, and 20 years later we have health care workers who are afraid to wear their identifications in the public places, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this is only leading to the staff shortage that we have. We`re short on nurses anyway and now the job becomes less appealing. Anyway not a good situation.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 Cal Perry on the ground for us. Cal, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And up next, I`m going to talk to a member of Congress who says \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is a monopoly, not seen since the era of oil barons and river tycoons essentially when two industries came up out of nowhere. But is there anything to be done about it?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): In the past weeks and days, parents have contacted me with their stories, heartbreaking, and spine-chilling stories, about children pushed into eating disorders, bullying online, self-injury of the most disturbing kind, and sometimes even taking their lives because of social media.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): It is clear that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  prioritizes profit over the well-being of children and all users.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We know there`s bipartisan anger towards \uc2 Facebook\uc2  but what`s still not clear is whether Congress can turn the rhetoric into actual regulation.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Congressman David Cicilline, he`s a Democrat from Rhode Island, he`s chair of the House Judiciary`s Anti-Trust Subcommittee. He`s been deeply involved with the issue of big tech and regulation.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman, to me the most fascinating aspect of Miss Haugen`s testimony is I think she`s making the case -- and I`m curious if you concur with her -- that there currently is not a government entity or agency that is prepared to regulate this industry with which \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is the leader in. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 REP. DAVID CICILLINE (D-RI): No, look, I think -- first of all, thank you for having me. I think we learned from her testimony what we learned during the course of the investigation we conducted for 16 months, that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is a monopoly, it has monopoly power, it`s too big and too powerful to care. And it, in fact, puts profits over everything else. We have legislation we`ve already passed out of the Judiciary Committee in a bipartisan way to produce more competition, to restore competition in the digital marketplace.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:05]\par \par \uc2 That`s part of the solution, give people other choices than \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . But in addition we have to, in fact, pass legislation that will make \uc2 Facebook\uc2  accountable for amplifying toxic and dangerous content. They rely on a business model that allows them and other platforms to extract all of this personal data from folks and then use it to generate revenue in ads, and in doing so, they have a business model that recognizes that the most outrageous, most untrue claims tend to engage people more deeply so they make more money.\par \par \uc2 So they have no incentive to curate what`s on their platform. They can`t be permitted to regulate themselves because they won`t do it. It`s Congress` responsibility, the DOJ, the FTC have some tools, but Congress also has the responsibility to update and modernize our antitrust laws and prevent the kind of conduct that the whistleblower spoke about today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, you`re supposed to protect us as consumers, and we`re all consumers in this case. And so that is what I`m curious of. It sounds like you think you could do this through the FTC, perhaps you could do it through some of the consumer laws that are out there, but I`m curious, what specifically -- you know, let me throw it this way, I think the issue of algorithm safety is something that`s very hard for us, you and I, to explain but you may get where I`m going, which is to find out if an algorithm has been written and if it can be abused or weaponized that somebody needs to sort of look at this from an outside.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it`s not a government internet person, maybe it`s an outside board, you know, maybe it`s the -- ala the Motion Picture Association, but with more teeth or something. Who is going to look and handle that? Because these algorithms are what make these companies go.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Yes, I think there`s a robust discussion under way right now about Section 230 and the use of algorithms in terms of whether or not immunity from liability should attach to everything. There are some people who think, for example, when you post something on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that`s one action, but then \uc2 Facebook\uc2  takes that posting and it applies a complicated set of algorithms to maximize its profits.\par \par \uc2 That`s a set of business decisions. So one idea is you could actually make \uc2 Facebook\uc2  liable. You could reform Section 230 and make them liable for those business decisions that amplify and spread toxic or dangerous information. So I think Section 230 reform is part of it. Using all of the resources of the FTC, the DOJ and then Congress updating and modernizing our antitrust laws to inject more competition into this place.\par \par \uc2 Part of the problem is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has no competitors and so they -- and they`re so large, they can basically do what they want. So restoring competition, prohibiting certain behaviors and then holding them accountable and responsible for their business decision to amplify toxic or dangerous material.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this, if \uc2 Boeing\uc2  comes up with a new way of using an automatic pilot mode in their airplane and they believe it`s a proprietary invention, they still have to disclose everything this invention does and how it works to the FAA to get that approval. It seems as if we don`t -- if \uc2 Facebook\uc2  comes up with a proprietary algorithm that enhances their business capabilities, they don`t seem to think they have to share that. How do you make them share that?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Well, I mean, there`s a number of ways. You can require algorithmic audits to be sure they`re doing what they claim they do. You can require greater transparency for algorithms. You can, you know, permit an agency responsible for looking at their practices, either its impact on competition or on fairness to the consumers are two obvious places. But that`s only part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the problem is, because their business model allows them just to collect incessantly data and then use that data to construct advertising and again take that same information and use it as a basis to connect people whatever the content, whether it`s toxic or dangerous or untrue, and so part of this is, again, gets back to the fact they`re just too big. They`re too big to care, they have no competition, they have no rules.\par \par \uc2 And Congress has a role to play, so do our agencies and the FTC and so does the Department of Justice. But, you know, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is arguing, this is just -- there`s nothing we can do about it. That`s just not true. They will spend billions and billions and billions of dollars because they want to protect this ecosystem that has generated profits never seen in the history of the world. We have got to get control of this. This is dangerous for our democracy, it`s dangerous for young people, it`s dangerous for our public health, and we have a responsibility to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 We`ve already done that work in the Antitrust Subcommittee and more work remains to be done for sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The outage yesterday, the outage that took down WhatsApp, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , doesn`t that make the case that, I mean, literally one company can pull the plug on global commerce for millions or billions of people. I know we don`t worry about monopolies outside the United States, but did that not just make the case they`re a monopoly?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Absolutely. And as we`ve known, you know, the history of our country is a battle between democracy and monopoly and, you know, that made the case perfectly. This company and many of the other large technology platforms are just too big to care and they have been allowed to acquire and grow really without any constraint, any serious review, and that period has finally ended and in a bipartisan way, we`ve taken on the monopoly power of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and these other large technology giants and we`re going to rein in their power once and for all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`ve been promised a digital bill of rights for Americans` privacy for some time. At some point I think you`re going to see those demands glow as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Congressman David Cicilline, who has been on the frontlines of this issue, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, a state of emergency is declared in California as cleanup effort intensify and investigators zero in on a possible cause of the West Coast oil spill that`s impacting particularly Orange County, California.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Governor Gavin Newsom has now declared a state of the emergency for Orange County where cleanup continues following a massive oil spill off the Southern California coast. It`s an estimated 144,000 gallons of crude oil have now been pouring into the Pacific Ocean. The CEO of Amplify Energy which owns the pipeline said in a press conference Monday there`s no longer an active leak, but he also addressed the theory that a ship`s anchor could be to blame for the disaster calling it, quote, "a distinct possibility."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, local officials remain worried about the potential environmental and ecological impact of the spill, and experts warn it could be weeks before the full extent of this damage is known. As people on the Gulf Coast know about oil spills, people in the Alaska know about oil spills, it can take months if not years before you see the remnants of the oil spill go away.\par \par \uc2 Up next, South Dakota, it`s home to Mount Rushmore, the badlands, and according to a new trove of leaked documents, vast amounts of money that super rich people are trying to hide. To sort of quote the movie "Fletch," the story is South Dakota, Frank. Trust me. It`s South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. A leak of nearly 12 million financial documents, these are called the Pandora Papers, have revealed where many world leaders and other wealthy figures are allegedly hiding their billions from tax authorities. And believe it or not, one of those places is right here in the United States. It`s not an island, it`s South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 The documents were given to the \uc2 International Consortium of Investigative Journalists\uc2 . And they`ve been combed through by 150 separate news organizations.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 NBC is not one of them and cannot independently confirm this reporting. "The Washington Post" is one of these entities, for what it`s worth. And when you think of taxing for the ultra-rich, first two places you`re probably thinking Swiss Bank, Switzerland, Cayman Islands, maybe even Bermuda. But South Dakota is the big story in this one because according to the Pandora Papers, it is one of the big places to hide money.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota`s laws have allowed it to become a prime place for international billionaires to shelter their assets. In some cases South Dakota can promise even more privacy and secrecy than other overseas tax havens.\par \par \uc2 Now you might ask, how the heck can they do that? Well, here`s somebody who`s going to answer me that question. I`m joined now by one of the "Washington Post" reporters on this project, Debbie Cenziper.\par \par \uc2 So, Debbie, let`s start right there. South Dakota can protect money from the eyes of even the U.S. federal government, this IRS. How?\par \par \uc2 DEBBIE CENZIPER, THE WASHINGTON POST INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: South Dakota like you said has become a go-to destination for foreign wealth and that is because it offers its customers in some cases more secrecy, more privacy, more discretion than the more traditional tax havens that you just talked about, in the Cayman Islands and other places. And they have been able to do this through laws that were passed year after year after year that made it so customer friendly that this state was able to draw, you know, billions and billions of dollars in assets from millionaires and billionaires, including those from overseas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do they gain from it, though, if they don`t get to tax it? They don`t -- I mean, they`re just housing it. Are they making a fee? I mean, you know, is there like a 1 percent tax they`re getting for the ability to use South Dakota`s state legislature as their personal hideaway?\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: That was the very first question we asked when I started working on this with my colleagues at the \uc2 International Consortium of Investigative Journalists\uc2  which obtained these records and shared them with the "Washington Post" and other media outlets. And so in states like South Dakota, Alaska, Nevada, New Hampshire, where they have these thriving trust industries, they essentially promised to create good, clean jobs and new revenue for the state fees.\par \par \uc2 And what we`ve learned in this reporting is that, yes, some jobs were created but really not a lot. The fees are very, very small. And so what they`ve, I guess, risked by doing this is having potentially bad actors from overseas breaching the American financial system, and that is one of the findings of the Pandora Papers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So are we seeing people who have been sanctioned by the United States, perhaps, you know, they know that their money was ill-begotten and we perhaps have flagged them in some way? Maybe because of work they`ve done with a foreign government or foreign entity. Can they successfully hide their money right here in the United States of America in Sioux Falls, South Dakota?\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: I know. You would never expect Sioux Falls, South Dakota. Potentially. What we found in the Pandora Papers, which was, as you said, it was based on the leak of about 11.9 million financial records from 14 providers of offshore services, including a trust company with a location in South Dakota. And so while the leak was unprecedented in its size, it still doesn`t tell us everything. It`s just 14 providers.\par \par \uc2 But what we found from this leak is the number of people from overseas who have been credibly accused in their own countries of crimes, including corruption and bribery or of other offenses like human rights abuses and labor exploitation, and they or their family members moved their assets into Sioux Falls.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there --\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: Often from, you know, a lot of established tax havens in the British Virgin Islands and the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Is it possible, though, that this is in violation of federal law? I mean, you get to a point you can`t pass a state law that undermines the federal government. Right? There is a line there. Is it possible South Dakota`s law has crossed that?\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: You know, I can`t answer that question. I wish I could. You know, it`s not illegal to move money offshore unless you`re doing it to avoid paying taxes, right, or to hide the proceeds of criminal activities. And so our documents showed us who had accounts in South Dakota and in other places. In fact, we identified 29 or so thousand offshore accounts, or onshore accounts in this case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: But the documents didn`t often tell us why they moved the money here or where the money came from. And so that`s really a question potentially for investigators.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Only definition of offshore when you`re talking about the Missouri River and South Dakota. Anyway, Debbie Cenziper, tremendous reporting by this collective and by "The Post" for participating. Thank you for coming on and sharing what you`ve learned.\par \par \uc2 CENZIPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`re going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211005eha50002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for September 20, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Mike Memoli, Jonathan Lemire,  Julia Ainsley, Courtney Kube \par \uc2 8,916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday.\par \par \uc2 The President returns to the White House to face a mounting series of challenges and crises at home and abroad, as newly infighting between Progressive and moderate Democrats threatens to derail his entire legislative agenda on Capitol Hill, this, as we inch towards a government shutdown and an economic default. But hey, no pressure.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  announces today that their COVID vaccine is safe and likely effective in children ages five to 11. What that means and what it doesn`t? We`ll talk to two of the top doctors working alongside the FDA and the CDC coming up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest on the humanitarian crisis at the southern border as DHS Secretary Mayorkas prepares to answer reporters` questions in Del Rio, Texas moments from now, amid the administration scramble to process or deport thousands of migrants huddled under a bridge in squalid conditions.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd on a very busy Monday.\par \par \uc2 We expect to hear from Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas in Del Rio, Texas on the influx of Haitian migrants at the southern border. We will bring that to you, live, when it happens.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we also got the first step in the news so many people have been waiting for. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  says its COVID vaccine is safe and effective for children ages five to 11 and says it will submit its data to the FDA by the end of the month. This is welcome news for parents, and we`ll have much more on that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 It`s also a development that the White House and Democrats in Congress would love to be celebrating today, but they find themselves in a web of interwoven standoffs where nobody`s blinking. I`m thinking like Reservoir Dogs.\par \par \uc2 And if they don`t figure a way out, the entire Biden legislative agenda could fall apart because deadlines to avoid a government shutdown and a potential default on government debt are right around the corner. So, as Congress returns to Capitol Hill, here`s where we are right now starting with the House.\par \par \uc2 One week from today is this self-imposed deadline that Speaker Pelosi set for the House to take up the Bipartisan Infrastructure Deal but the Progressive Caucus is adamant that they won`t vote for it unless there`s a vote on that sweeping $3.5 trillion Budget Resolution first. But, but, but then there are a group of those nine House moderates who say they`ll jump ship if that happens.\par \par \uc2 And adding it another level of chaos on top of this whole thing, the Reconciliation package likely won`t even be ready by Monday with Senate Committees still working on the bottom lines. You still with me?\par \par \uc2 Well, the Senate parliamentarian has also handled -- handed Democrats a crushing defeat this weekend saying that a pathway to citizenship is not allowed in the budget reconciliation process. Democrats say they have a backup plan but it`s not entirely clear what that is.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, tied up in all of these legislative standoffs and setbacks are two impending deadlines. Government funding runs out on September 30 and the debt ceiling limit is set to be hit sometime in October.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they won`t help at all with the debt ceiling, which leaves the heavy lifting the Democrats, which would add yet another wrinkle into their not yet ready reconciliation package, which might then also derail the Bipartisan Infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Look, folks, there is no way out of these series of standoff that will make every last member of the Democratic caucus happy. But if they don`t get action on at least some of these matters, it`s not just the Biden agenda that`s imperiled, the government won`t be able to keep the lights on in a matter of days, and the economy could be had for something much, much worse after all that.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now with all that happy news behind us is NBC News White House Correspondent Mike Memoli in Delaware, NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell`s on Capitol Hill, and also with me is Jonathan Lemire, White House Reporter for the AP and an MSNBC Political Analysts.\par \par \uc2 So, Leigh Ann, we`ve got both chambers back in session today for the first time since July. Senator Mark Warner just called this situation a Rubik`s Cube on steroids, which is a pretty colorful metaphor. Where do Democrats even start now that they`re all back?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, it`s extremely complicated. And every day that passes, it just gets more and more difficult for Democrats because the news keeps getting worse instead of better. The decision by the parliamentarian last night was another blow to their plans to pass what the Biden agenda is, in just one piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Now, the moderates and the progressives are staring at each other and daring the other side to blink first. These are the types of situations that we usually see between Democrats and Republicans, but this is happening within the party and it comes down to two pieces of legislation, that Bipartisan Bill, which is scheduled, as you mentioned, to be voted on next week, where the moderates are demanding a vote on that. They`re saying that this -- the Speaker and leadership are not yet even negotiating with them on the Reconciliation Bill.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: They are saying that those two bills should be de-linked, and they would not be sad if, in fact, the bipartisan bill moves and there`s a lot more time left for this Reconciliation Bill. They say it has to be done right.\par \par \uc2 They are the ones who are politically vulnerable here and they do not want to vote on legislation that is bad legislation, and they also don`t want to vote on legislation over and over again.\par \par \uc2 They just want one vote and that is why they are demanding that there must be an agreement with the Senate, including Senators Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema before they go down that plank.\par \par \uc2 So this is extremely difficult for Speaker Pelosi, Leader Schumer, and whether this vote takes place next week on the Bipartisan bill, it`s also going to depend on do they -- are they going to need Republicans in order to pass it because if that vote moves forward without the final reconciliation, that Human Infrastructure $3.5 trillion bill, it`s up to progressives on if they`re going to vote for it with the promise of a vote later on on the Human Infrastructure bill, or if they`re going to call the moderate`s bluff, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Leigh Ann, is this kind of boil down to a game of chicken here between moderates and progressives? I mean, they both have taken someone else`s bill hostage.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me like given the order of operations here, and what`s ready to go, is the easiest solution here for Pelosi and democratic leaders to kind of try to force progressives to take what they can get right now and start with their promise they made to the moderates and vote for the infrastructure deal next week?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It`s absolutely a game of chicken, and that perhaps is the easiest path, but are progressives willing to do it? Progressives continuously say that they are a bigger chunk of the Democratic Party than the moderates are.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m speaking to some moderates who say that they are pretty confident that the Bipartisan bill will pass even if some progressives defect next week. They think that they have between 10 and 20 Republicans to help them along.\par \par \uc2 But progressives have little trust and little faith in the moderates in this Party, they are tired of the moderates trying to control the agenda, they think that this is their shot, this is the progressive shot, and the Democratic shot and President Biden shot to get something really big and bold done, and they say that that was part of the deal that these two bills go in tandem.\par \par \uc2 And so it doesn`t look like tandem is going to happen, especially because these recons -- this human infrastructure bill has so many details that still need to be work out -- worked out because Democrats are not all on the same page, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, it turns out it`s a pretty complicated process. So, Mike, I can`t tell you how many times, you know, when Leigh Ann and I asked Speaker Pelosi about how they navigate situations like this, she talks about the value of having a Democratic President in office.\par \par \uc2 Now we know late last week, Biden spoke to Senators, Manchin, and Sinema, we didn`t get much of a readout from those meetings, which I think is telling in its own way, right? If those meetings go really well, the White House tends to shout it from the rooftops.\par \par \uc2 So, what do we know about exactly how involved the President is in trying to solve all these kind of legislative logjams and, and how involved he intends to be going forward?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Garrett, obviously, as you and Leigh Ann know well, there are some genuine policy differences in the Democratic caucus that was always going to make this process a complicated one, as we head towards reconciliation votes here.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that far too early from the White House`s perspective, midterm politics are also starting to get into the equation. And the bully pulpit that a popular President, a President like this President whose approval rating was in the mid to high 50s just a few months ago, is now much more diminished when you`re looking at it President Biden, whose approval rating has sunk into the low to mid-40s, depending on which poll you`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s made it that much harder for this administration to really get Democrats on board with a maybe imperfect vehicle here, but one that they say a popular President will have your back selling when the time comes.\par \par \uc2 It`s clear, especially, among the Moderates that they`re looking for opportunities to hedge their bets to demonstrate some independence from the party that will serve them, even though most of them don`t frankly know what their districts will look like, we haven`t even gotten into the redistricting process in full swing just yet. And so, that`s really what the White House is wrestling with.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately for the White House, this is a President who`s really in foreign policy mode for most of this week. There are some gaps in the President`s schedule, and they are planning for him to be very engaged in more meetings, more phone calls, in addition to the ones that his legislative team is happening.\par \par \uc2 But what`s going to be happening as well, Garrett, behind the scenes according to why Officials I`ve been speaking with is they`re going to be doing everything they can to be making the case both internally, but also increasingly to the public at large that if sold correctly, what -- the core of what the President and what Democrats are trying to do here is very popular if you can get it out to the public and get out of the sort of inside sausage-making at the moment.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: But the problem is, it`s not clear just yet, how much the President`s going to be able to be engaged in making the kind of sales pitches he was doing on the campaign trail, as it were, the public events he was doing like those in Colorado last week, there was hopes to do more of it, but his schedule is increasingly crowded, and his politics just aren`t what they used to be, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Jonathan, first of all, I`m glad to have you on so we can prove to people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that you and I are not the same person.\par \par \uc2 But the White House has to be celebrating some of this news on \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  today, but how do they kind of handle the challenges that Mike was just laying out here, right?\par \par \uc2 The COVID is everything for this White House, beating COVID is everything on the messaging side of it, but they wanted to be talking about so many other things in August and in September, how are they viewing this salesmanship they`ll have to do going forward, as Mike laid out, everything is the campaign trail when it comes to trying to pass big pieces of legislation?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, ASSOCIATED PRESS: Garrett, first of all, I`ve always been flattered by the comparison. This is a lot on the White House`s plate right now. Certainly, they do.\par \par \uc2 COVID is everything. And that`s what the poll number that the White House has pointed to me is the one they`d been the most anxious about in recent weeks, the management of the pandemic had dropped approval for the President`s position. And they were Delta blow on Friday.\par \par \uc2 They had hoped that the FAA board would recommend booster shots for all Americans 16 and up, it didn`t do that, just 65 and up, and those with sort of frontlines jobs or extraordinarily dangerous situations.\par \par \uc2 So, there will be some Americans who are getting that third shot in upcoming weeks, but not everyone has the White House had hoped.\par \par \uc2 Today, certainly, though, good news with the children, there`s some belief that there could be shots in arms for kids five to 12 by about Halloween, which would indeed be a treat.\par \par \uc2 But it`s as far from the only thing on the President`s plate as Mike and Leigh Ann just outlined, there`s the domestic agenda, of course, now seems imperiled by members of his own party.\par \par \uc2 But we`re also dealing with this in the last few days, the revelations from the Pentagon about the drone strike that killed civilians, including seven children, rather than a pair of ISIS-K terrorists as first believed.\par \par \uc2 We even have this ongoing feud with France, about submarines, and certainly, the President has got to spend some energy this week talking to his French counterpart Macron to smooth that over, as well as, rallying at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  efforts for a vaccine summit, and meeting with more international partners in the Indo Pacific, so there`s a lot here right now, and there is a fear about where the legislative agenda could go.\par \par \uc2 But just even this morning, Senior White House Aides tell me they know there`s sweaty palms, but they believe that the other day they`ll get this deal done.\par \par \uc2 Maybe not precisely the timing they want, maybe not precisely the size they want but they have a hard time believing there`d be a Democrat who would cast the vote to submarine the agenda of a President from their own party.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Submarines making a big appearance in this segment. Jonathan, Senate Democrats say they have a backup plan on this reconciliation immigration piece.\par \par \uc2 Does the White House have a backup plan if they just keep hitting the wall here and immigration cannot be dealt with in a Reconciliation bill, it`s not there`s nothing you can do on immigration, that`s going to get 60 votes in the Senate right now?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: No, there isn`t, thanks for noticing the submarine pan. The -- you know, this is something that had there been some hopes that perhaps slightly far-fetched that it could have been done on reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 It is something that the White House wants to tackle but I think that and this is to the anger of some progressive activists. It`s not a front-burner issue at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, there`s a lot that this White House is grappling with, as we`ve just been outlining, and we haven`t even mentioned things like immigration until now, and voting rights, which is certainly many Democrats feel like should be issue number one, that that`s the existential threat to Democrats and their efforts to hold on to power during the midterm elections, beyond of course, just defending a sacred constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 The filibuster looms is an obstacle to all of this Garrett, as you know, better than just about anybody. Right now, the White House, their eyes are on different balls, they`ll come back to that when they can.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Leigh Ann Caldwell, I only got about 30 seconds left here but we mentioned the idea that there`s so much on the plate, and they have to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 Very quickly, what is the Democratic strategy as best we know to make sure government funding passes and we don`t stumble into a shutdown?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, well, Democratic leaders haven`t even been clear about how that`s going to happen because it`s all tied in, perhaps to raising the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Now, every indication is that Democrats are going to tie this debt limit to government funding legislation, something that is not going to pass the Senate, and I actually have sources telling me, Garrett, that it might not even pass the House. And so this is another big staring blinking contest between this time the two Parties, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Leigh Ann, Mike, Jonathan, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve got Department of Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas is speaking right now in Del Rio, Texas. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DHS SECRETARY: Since January 20, it has been doing an extraordinary job. We are in the midst of a pandemic and a critical migration challenge. We continue to exercise the Centers for Disease control`s Title 42 authority.\par \par \uc2 Title 42 is not an Immigration Authority, but a Public Health Authority to protect the American public to protecting the communities along the border, and to protect the migrants themselves.\par \par \uc2 I want to share with you some of the steps we are taking to address the current situation here in Del Rio. CBP has surged 600 agents, officers, and DHS volunteer force personnel to the Del Rio sector to enhance our operational capabilities. If additional staff is needed, more will be sent.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol is coordinating with ICE and the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  to move individuals from Del Rio to other processing locations, including approximately 3500 over the last few days and 3000 today in order to ensure that migrants are swiftly taken into custody, processed, and removed from the United States, consistent with our laws and policies.\par \par \uc2 We, in DHS, are securing additional transportation to accelerate the pace and increase the capacity of removal flights to Haiti and other destinations in the Western Hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 We are working to increase the capacity of return flights to Haiti and other destinations. We anticipate at least one to three flights per day.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is working with source and transit countries in the region to accept individuals who previously resided in those countries.\par \par \uc2 We are undertaking urgent humanitarian actions with other relevant federal, state and local partners to reduce crowding and improve conditions for migrants on United States soil.\par \par \uc2 DHS has already taken a number of steps to ensure the safety and security of individuals as they await processing, including having Border Patrol emergency medical technicians on hand and providing water, towels, and portable toilets.\par \par \uc2 This is all part of an all of government and whole of community effort. We have brought in personnel from the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to address medical needs and supplies.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also worked with the \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  to bring in supplies and much-needed resources to the population. We have worked with a World Central Kitchen to bring in meals for the migrants.\par \par \uc2 Not only do we leverage the resources and capabilities of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , we look across the federal government and in partnership with civil society, local resources, to see what we can do to bring more capabilities to bear to meet the challenge, and that is indeed what we are doing here.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the White House has directed appropriate U.S. agencies to work with the Haitian and other regional governments to provide assistance and support to returnees. The majority of migrants continue to be expelled under CDC`s Title 42 authority.\par \par \uc2 Those who cannot be expelled under that authority, and do not have a legal basis to remain will be placed in expedited removal proceedings. DHS is conducting regular expulsion and removal flights to Haiti, Mexico, Ecuador, and northern triangle countries.\par \par \uc2 We are very concerned that Haitians who are taking this irregular migration path are receiving false information that the border is open or that temporary protected status is available.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that it is known that this is not the way to come to the United States. That is false information. Irregular migration poses a serious security risk to the migrants themselves.\par \par \uc2 Trying to enter the United States illegally is not worth the tragedy, the money or the effort. As we have said consistently since we published the federal register notice officially designating Haiti for temporary protected status or TPS, only Haitians living in the United States before July 29 are eligible for temporary protected status.\par \par \uc2 We have reiterated that our borders are not open, and people should not make the dangerous journey. Individuals and Families are subject to border restrictions, including expulsion.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Irregular migration poses a significant threat to the health and welfare of border communities, and to the lives of the migrants themselves, and should not be attempted.\par \par \uc2 If you come to the United States illegally, you will be returned, your journey will not succeed, and you will be endangering your life and your family`s lives.\par \par \uc2 This administration is committed to developing safe, orderly, and humane pathways for migration, but this is not the way to do it. Thank you. It is my pleasure to introduce Commissioner Troy Miller of the United States Border Protection.\par \par \uc2 TROY MILLER, ACTING COMMISSIONER, U.S. CUSTOMS AND BORDER PROTECTION: Good afternoon. Thank you, Mr. Secretary. Thank you for joining us here today. And importantly, thank you for what you`ve done over the last several days to elevate the work that we`re doing so this group of -- since this group of migrants began arriving in Del Rio.\par \par \uc2 Your leadership has ensured that DHS could count on a whole of government approach, as not just a buzzword. It means that the federal government is engaged and mobilizing the necessary resources to process. The migrants arriving here, I tell real quickly, humanely, and maintain the safety of everyone involved.\par \par \uc2 But I can`t say thank you enough to the state local and NGO partners that are also here with us. Once again, everybody`s come together to assist in addressing the needs of the local communities while also addressing the challenging situations we face together, I thank you for everyone`s hard work.\par \par \uc2 I also want to thank my colleague, Border Patrol Chief Ortiz. Chief took the helm a few short weeks ago, and just in time to lead this important effort in his hometown. The Chief will give additional operational details in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 And I want to thank the CBP workforce who continue to rally to every situation and challenge we face. I am proud to work alongside the dedicated and resilient men and women of CBP every single day, who have served this country with relentless, determination, and integrity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`ve just heard from DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas and we`re going to go back to him when he starts taking questions.\par \par \uc2 But for the moment, I want to bring in NBC Julia Ainsley who`s been covering the Department of Homeland Security`s response to this crisis.\par \par \uc2 So, Julia, we heard from the Secretary there, about 3000 migrants moved out, he said, we might see anywhere between one and three flights a day, walk us through how we got to where we are in this situation in Del Rio in particular, and even the fact that the DHS Secretary is down there and addressing it publicly, is notable the response the administration is now taking?\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, MSNBC DHS CORRESPONDENT: Well, and I will say, Garrett, that our own reporting shows that later this week, we`re going to see the number of these deportations rise exponentially. You`re talking about one to three today. Now, we expect that to be somewhere between six and eight per day later this week as they ramp up. So, that aside, how we got here? This really did take DHS by surprise in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 Usually, when we talk about a caravan of migrants, you already know you have intelligence about people who are meeting in a different country, often Honduras or El Salvador, and they are planning out their route to the United States. That can take two to three weeks, sometimes a month, usually by their usual travel patterns.\par \par \uc2 In this case, so many Haitians ended up on the Guatemala-Mexico border, that in a lot of ways Mexico couldn`t stop it and they ended up coming through many times on buses, which completely expedited that travel time.\par \par \uc2 So, you saw the number of that Brit -- under that bridge go from 5000 on Wednesday, and we`re just starting to hear about these numbers to 15,000 as of yesterday. You did just hear though, from Secretary Mayorkas that they`ve been able to move a lot of people out.\par \par \uc2 When he talks about moving that 3000 number, that doesn`t mean 3000 people are taken from under that bridge and deported back to Haiti, it means they`re moving them to other places, often along the U.S. border, sometimes they`ll do what they call a lateral flight where they take someone from one sector and move them to another, simply because they`re overwhelmed in this one sector that can`t handle them all at once.\par \par \uc2 And so you`ll see more of these people move to other places in the United States before they may be eventually deported, or allowed to stay here and seek asylum.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve talked to people on the ground, Garrett, people who have been processing and responding to crises in the past, and they say this is nothing like anything they`ve seen in their decades of service.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Conservatives have been hammering the Biden administration over this, saying that is a result of administration policy, but listening to the DHS secretary, it also sounds like -- some degree it`s a result of a misunderstanding of administration policy. You heard him say, you know, this idea that Haitians receiving temporary protected status.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s only if you were already here. This doesn`t mean if you were a Haitian who hasn`t lived in Haiti in a decade, but you are in Guatemala, or Mexico, or so forth, that you should come to the U.S., how much of a problem is the messaging from the administration, as you saw the Secretary there trying to really make it abundantly clear the border is not open, you`re not welcome to come here illegally, how challenging is the messaging here for the administration with what they are trying to do for Haitians who are already in the country?\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Yes, unfortunately, messaging is just a big part of this job here. But we saw after DACA was passed years ago, there were more children who started to come, even though they wouldn`t be eligible for DACA.\par \par \uc2 The same thing often happens with temporary protected status. When you say, people from a certain country who were here before the time of whatever event it was, can stay, you`ll start to see more people from that country come, that does happen.\par \par \uc2 The one thing he didn`t say, though, that I do want to point out is that the administration stopped doing deportation flights to Haiti in early September.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t announce it, but we`re did get out that because of that August 14 earthquake, they were not doing deportation flights. That could also be contributing to what we`re seeing now, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Julia, thank you. We`re going to keep one ear on Mayorkas and bring you back if there`s any news when he starts to answer questions.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, up next new signs of optimism in the fight against Coronavirus as the U.S. announces new ways to potentially ease restrictions for foreign travelers, and as \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  says its vaccine is safe for young kids. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As we mentioned at the top of the show, we just got some of the most hopeful news on the pandemic front in a while.\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  released top-line results from its vaccine trial for children aged five to 11 this morning, and it says that their two dose-vaccine was "safe, well-tolerated, and produced an immune response about equal to what we`ve seen in older populations."\par \par \uc2 The company added that results for children younger than five could also come later this year. And while the company released limited data to back up its claims, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  says it has enough to apply for FDA emergency approval by the end of the month, meaning that millions of children could start getting shots in the arms before the new year.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as the Biden administration struggles to get a grip on the pandemic. Today the White House announced that starting in November all foreign travelers flying to the U.S. will be required to get fully vaccinated and tested prior to travel.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention this week was supposed to kick off the administration`s massive booster shot rollout. But in a controversial meeting on Friday, the FDA Advisory Committee voted not to recommend a third shot for the general population. Instead voted to make boosters available only for vulnerable groups such as those over 65 and folks with underlying medical conditions.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is an expert who advises the CDC panel responsible for determining exactly who will receive those booster shots and how, Dr. William Schaffner. And also joining us is Dr. Paul Offit, a pediatrician and one of the 18 doctors that voted in Friday`s FDA committee meeting. The very best people to break this down.\par \par \uc2 So, Doctors, I want to get both of your reactions to the top lines we got this morning from Pfizer`s vaccine trial for children. This is the two-dose vaccine, 21 days apart, containing about a third of the dose given to older folks. And they say the results are comparable to what they`ve seen in other populations.\par \par \uc2 So how promising is this news? How optimistic should viewers be? How soon do they think they might get shots in their children`s arms?\par \par \uc2 Dr. Schaffner, I`ll start with you.\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, DIRECTOR, \uc2 NATIONAL FOUNDATION FOR INFECTIOUS DISEASES\uc2 : Well, I`m very optimistic, Garrett. I think that the data are likely to support the announcement. And I think that Paul and his group will look at those data and the FDA will approve it. So I`m optimistic about that. Down the road, perhaps by late fall, we can start vaccinating children 5 to 11 years of age.\par \par \uc2 This is terribly important because this Delta variant is reaching