aving the vaccine isn`t good enough. You actually have to give it. We have a vaccine for 12 to 15-year-olds and we still continue to see children in our hospital over 12 years of age who aren`t vaccinated and because of that they`re being hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, let`s dig in on that a little bit further, Dr. Offit. My colleague Savannah Guthrie spoke to Dr. Gruber from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  about the lack of efficacy data in this release they put out this morning. Listen to some of this conversation here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM GRUBER, PFIZER SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT OF VACCINE CLINICAL RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT: We`re hopeful and expectant that the FDA will approve the vaccine based on the data we have. It makes sense because the level of antibody the vaccine is generating in children 5 to 11 matches the level of antibody that we know provides protection for individuals 16 years of age and older. And the FDA set the guidance along those lines. That`s their expectation as well. And we believe we`ve met that expectation.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So the whole country has become amateur vaccine experts over the last year. But you`re an actual vaccine expert. So does this comparable antibody response mean that the level of protection, the immune response is the same? Can you kind of walk us through the difference there?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 You know, obviously we don`t have the data. But the difference between the immune response and how well this thing works for kids.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Who are you -- me or --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Doctor Offit, please.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: All right. OK. Right. So when you do, for example, as \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  did, a 40,000-person where they gave 20,000 people a vaccine, 20,000 people got placebo, and so how effective that vaccine was, you then try to correlate that effectiveness with an antibody response, and hopefully you`ll have what`s so-called immunological correlate which in this case would be high - - for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is that they feel that they have met -- bridging studies. That they have a high enough antibody response to be able to say we think that this not only is immunogenic but likely to be protective.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what he`s saying. But again, you know, the FDA and CDC ultimately need to look closely at this data to see what they can see.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Dr. Offit, looking back on Friday`s vote, the booster shot vote here, you voiced concern over the lack of data for younger folks or, you know, folks younger than I guess 65. Specifically that the data that was presented didn`t clearly show the benefits outweighed the risks for people older than 16. So I guess my questions are, what are your concerns about risks around a booster shot and what additional data would you need to see to give a green light for a third dose going forward? We heard Dr. Fauci say he thinks that`s all but inevitable.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Yes. It was a tough discussion on Friday. I think that the goal of this vaccine, and certainly Rochelle Walensky at the CDC has consistently said this is the goal, protect -- exactly that. We have a high level of protection, including Delta, including all age groups. There was a study in Israel that was at variance with those studies. The question was why was it a variance? But I think the answer to the question, are you protected if you`ve gotten two doses of MRNA vaccines, I think it`s yes, you are protected against serious disease. And I worry that this whole booster discussion is going to make a lot of people out there think they`re not protected but they are against serious disease.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: The other thing, though, quickly, is that while it is true that these kinds of vaccines over time you`ll see an erosion in neutralizing antibodies, and with that you`ll see an erosion in protection, say, against asymptomatic infection or mildly symptomatic infection where you still can be contagious. The notion then is that if you then give a booster, then you`ll have less contagiousness out there then and get some hold on the pandemic, although I still think if you really want to get ahold of this pandemic, we`re going to have to find a way to vaccinate people who are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. I think every public health expert, every doctor I`ve interviewed agrees that first and second shots are still the priority over third shots.\par \par \uc2 But, Dr. Schaffner, I want to get back over to you here. Because after the FDA makes its final recommendation, the booster ball goes back into your court. The CDC`s court. The advisory committee is supposed to meet on Wednesday and Thursday to hash out some specifics like exactly who is eligible for a third shot, how it rolls out. You advise that committee.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think should be included within the vulnerable groups outlined by the FDA? Is this a if you feel like you`re vulnerable or is this going to be really kind of specific criterion about who goes in and gets that third shot?\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: Garrett, I think the CDC committee has already laid out a road map in the direction it`s going. And interestingly enough, it`s very similar to what the FDA committee has suggested. People age 65 and older, and they will define then people under age 65 with underlying illnesses that are likely to make them more subject to severe disease. People with heart disease, lung disease, diabetes and the like.\par \par \uc2 And then some folks who are vaccinated very early who continue to be at high risk will include health care workers and Peter Marks of the FDA wanted teachers included into that group. I can understand why that is. And so I think the CDC committee is going to very closely -- this is my crystal ball -- mirror what the FDA is suggesting.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. I appreciate the look in the crystal ball.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Schaffner, Dr. Offit, thank you both for your expertise.\par \par \uc2 And today, Dr. Anthony Fauci joins Hallie Jackson live to discuss the latest news on COVID and vaccinations. You can watch "HALLIE JACKSON REPORTS" at its new time today at 3:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, an update on the criminal case involving Trump Organization`s longtime CFO whose lawyer told the court today he believes more indictments are on the way. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:18]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Turning now to some new developments in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  saga. Allen Weisselberg, longtime CFO of former President Trump`s family business, was back for a hearing in a New York City courtroom today. This marks the first public proceeding since the Manhattan DA`s office indicted Weisselberg and the company in July for tax fraud and other crimes.\par \par \uc2 And Weisselberg`s attorney told the court in part today, quote, "We have strong reason to believe there could be other indictments coming." Weisselberg has pleaded not guilty to the allegations and former President Trump has bashed the indictments claiming the investigation is politically motivated. We will continue to monitor this and bring you anymore developments.\par \par \uc2 Up next, more on the legislative log jam for the White House and Democrats on Capitol Hill that could derail virtually the entire Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:15]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As we mentioned at the top of our show, Congress has a number of issues to take up as it returns to Capitol Hill today. The bipartisan infrastructure deal which Speaker Nancy Pelosi has vowed to vote on by next Monday, keeping the government from shutting down at the end of the month and raising the debt ceiling to prevent the government from defaulting on its debt sometime next month.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Congressman Raja Krishnamoorthi, Democrat from Illinois.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman, how do you think the House should be approaching these priorities right now? What`s the first step you need to be taking to check all of these boxes as you hope you`ll be able to do?\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (D-IL): Well, I think Speaker Pelosi does a masterful job of balancing all these competing priorities. Obviously we actually have to raise our debt ceiling. We cannot possibly put the full faith and credit of the United States ever at risk. Secondly, we`re going to have a spending package in time for the September 30th end of year. And then we`re going to have to pass infrastructure, and get on with it with the reconciliation process. It`s a lot to juggle, but we`ve done it before and we`re going to have to do it again here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you think there`s any possibility that the reconciliation package is done and ready before that deadline to vote on the infrastructure bill a week from today?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: It`s a little challenging. I think that there`s a lot of work that has been --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Understatement. Understatement.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: There`s a lot of work that has to be done. Obviously the Senate needs to write its own provisions. We need to pass our version. And then the two sides have to come together and hash out any disagreements. But I am confident that it`s going to be done in short order, and you know, failure is not an option. We must do this reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Talk to me about the infrastructure package. Obviously the vote is supposed to happen by next Monday. If the speaker does indeed put a vote on that infrastructure bill on the floor next Monday before reconciliation is done, will you vote for it?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: I would vote for it. I think that a lot of my colleagues are going to vote for it. I think at the end of the day, this particular piece of legislation is too important not to vote on. Especially given the tremendous progress that we`re making on the reconciliation bill. I think that we have to put a little bit of faith in the process and in each other. I am seeing a lot of progress being made, and I`m confident that it`s going to be done in short order just as an infrastructure package is going to pass as well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You talk about, you know, putting a little bit of trust in each other. To me that seems to be what so much this comes down to. A lot of Democrats from different wings of the party who don`t entirely trust their colleagues, that everybody is rowing in the same direction.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 Can you take me inside the conversation within the caucus about things like, you know, the red lines that progressives are drawing on the infrastructure bill or that moderates are drawing on the reconciliation bill? What are those discussions like, when it`s just you guys and not the cameras?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: Well, you know, it reminds me of an Indian wedding. You know, there`s a lot of posturing, there`s a lot of animated discussion, but at the end of the day, everyone comes together and we have dessert when the bride and groom get married.\par \par \uc2 I think in this particular instance, honestly, the only people who would be cheering for progressives and others in this party and our caucus not getting along is the Chinese Communist Party because they don`t want to see us get this infrastructure bill passed, and Donald Trump, who wants to see it all come down regardless of whether it`s good for the country or not.\par \par \uc2 And we can`t allow those forces to prevail. And that`s why I think we are going to get something done. We`re going to do it in a fine manner and the American people are going to be proud.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you think you`ll get Republican votes for the infrastructure bill? They got 19 in the Senate. But obviously the former president`s hold on House Republicans is much stronger than it is on Senate Republicans. What`s your sense of the level of Republican support that measure might get?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: I don`t know, Garrett. I think that at this point the ghost of Trump lives on and basically my colleagues on the other side have to decide, are they going to please Trump or are they going to please their constituents? I hope they choose the latter because I`ve got to tell you, Republicans and Democrats are demanding many of the components of not only the infrastructure package, but also the reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 I was just with some people about an hour ago who were telling me how many folks across the board really do benefit from that child tax credit which is being direct deposited into their accounts. And it cuts across party lines. And so let`s do the right thing. Let`s get this done regardless of what our colleagues on the other side do.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How would you rate the way that your party is selling this reconciliation bill? I feel like we talked about it, and some media criticism is certainly fair here if you want to go down that road. It`s been talked about so much in terms of its price tag. But I think it`s been very hard for any Americans to look at individual provisions and how they might affect them.\par \par \uc2 Do you worry that even if you do get that passed, Democrats aren`t going to get much credit for it because they don`t really understand yet what`s in it?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: Well, that`s our challenge, Garrett. A, we have to execute the provisions of that reconciliation package properly. We have to make sure that universal pre-K is available to folks, that two years of community college after high school are available to folks, and that the child tax credit as well as childcare tax credits are available. So that`s up to us in terms of an implementation standpoint.\par \par \uc2 And then we have to communicate about what this is doing and what a difference it`s making in the lives of so many people. And I`m very hopeful we can do that. But obviously we have to take a couple of steps before that, and that is passing the bill and implementing it properly.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Congressman, I only have about 30 seconds left. There`s been some reporting by Axios that Joe Manchin wants to kick this bill into 2022. Can you pass if that happens? Quickly, if you don`t mind.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: No. I think it has to be done now. I mean, I`ll just give you one example, I`m trying to get a global vaccination element of that bill called "No Vid." It`s a play on words no more COVID, which helps us to spearhead a vaccination program for 60 percent of the world`s 92 poorest countries. We can`t put that off until next year. We have to end this pandemic right now. And so that`s why we have to get a lot of these elements done ASAP.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Congressman Krishnamoorthi, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNAMOORTHI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I want to turn now to fallout from the Pentagon. The family of innocent civilians killed in a drone strike in Kabul is now demanding more than just an apology from the Pentagon. Relatives say the U.S. should offer compensation for their losses and help relocate them out of Kabul. Defense officials admitted on Friday that the drone strike intended to kill ISIS-K fighters, instead killed 10 civilians including children.\par \par \uc2 General Mark Milley, the nation`s top military commander, will likely face questions about the mistake when he testifies before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee next week.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Courtney Kube has more from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, over the weekend NBC News communicated with the family of Zemari Ahmadi. He was the Afghan man who was targeted and killed in a drone strike in Kabul in late August. His family is saying that they have not yet heard from the military with any kind of apology and they are asking for compensation, what the military calls solatia payments or condolence payments, and they`ve become relatively common over two decades of war in Iraq and Afghanistan. But the family has not yet heard from the military.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 What happened we found out late Friday from the head of U.S. Central Command is military intelligence showed that a white Toyota Corolla was likely being driven by an ISIS-K operative and that that individual may be planning to target Kabul airport. U.S. drones followed the vehicle around for about eight hours. Ultimately, when it pulled into its compound late in the day, they fired a Hellfire missile at it, killing Mr. Ahmadi and nine other individuals, nine other civilians including seven children.\par \par \uc2 Well, we found out after a U.S. Military investigation that in fact they had been following the wrong vehicle all along. Mr. Ahmadi was in fact a humanitarian aid worker who had been driving around, picking up and dropping off colleagues, and the large canisters, the containers in his vehicle that the military believed to be explosive were in fact bottles of water. And the secondary explosion that the military had been pointing to as evidence that there were explosives in the vehicle was actually from a nearby propane tank.\par \par \uc2 So as you said, Garrett, Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  and chairman of the Joint Chiefs, General Mark Milley, will be up on Capitol Hill next week testifying about the chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan. Now this drone strike and this ensuing investigation is likely to be among the questions that they get, and one of the major lines of topics of questioning that they will get, including the review that Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  has announced and whether anyone will be held accountable for this tragic mistake. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Courtney, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210921eh9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for September 13, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Shaquille Brewster, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Guad  Venegas \par \uc2 8,583 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday. As the nationwide COVID crisis rages, the latest in the political battle between the White House and Republicans over the President`s sweeping new vaccine mandates. We`ll talk to the governor of Kentucky, a Democratic Governor in a red state as hospitals there are being overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Secretary of State Anthony Blinken is about to face what will likely be a major grilling on Afghanistan from the House Foreign Affairs Committee. I`ll speak with a member of that committee about that testimony, coming up.\par \par \uc2 And later, President Biden heads West hoping to address a climate crisis and avoid a potential political crisis. He`ll meet with the nation`s Top Fire Safety Officials in Idaho, then rally in California on the eve of the recall vote for Governor Gavin Newsom.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake, in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials including the Surgeon General, the CDC, Director, the Education Secretary, and the Commerce Secretary have flooded the airwaves over the past 24 hours as the White House forges ahead with the President`s sweeping new requirements, including vaccine mandates on all businesses with more than a hundred employees.\par \par \uc2 Republican governors have redoubled their opposition to those mandates, vowing to use the full legal authority and political power to resist them. South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster says he will fight the mandates "to the gates of hell," OK.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, infections nationally are showing signs of tapering off, although they remain at extremely high levels. And to make it all, many more kids are going back to school today, including roughly a million kids in New York City, the largest school district in the country.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, COVID deaths continue at a grim pace of roughly 1700 people every single day. That`s the highest level since early March in the immediate aftermath of the winter`s record-setting surges, and before vaccines were widely available as they are now.\par \par \uc2 Amid the legal, political, and cultural fight over these new COVID restrictions, there are certainly a lot of questions facing the administration right now, like, when will this new employer vaccine mandate actually keep it -- kick in? How will it be enforced? What`s going to happen to states and companies that refuse to comply?\par \par \uc2 The answers to those questions are murky at best as is the road ahead for the country. This is amid a pandemic that has so far killed more than 663,000 Americans.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, our guests from COVID hotspots across the country. NBC`s Shaq Brewster is in Idaho, where hospitalizations have reached such a critical level in rural areas that patients are waiting up to a hundred hours for basic care. I`m also joined by Kentucky Governor Andy Beshear and Hawaii`s Lieutenant Governor who also happens to be a Practicing Emergency Room Physician, Josh green. Both of these states are -- COVID hospitalizations hitting record highs in recent days. So, thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 And Shaq. I`ll start with you. Idaho`s hospitals were forced to start rationing care last week, and then this crippling surge, talk to me about the latest on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Garrett. Do you have the authorization to lower the standard of care to really deal with the influx of patients that you have here? I mean, last week, we heard from state officials who warned residents that they should be extra careful when driving a car or riding a bike because you don`t want to end up in the emergency room and take away resources that are already there and with the lines that you`re seeing for the emergency rooms. And that`s what you`re seeing at these hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Many of them converting hallways, converting conference rooms into extra beds for patient care, they`re having to lower the standards in a way that they say is frustrating. And ideas, not only do you have that high positivity rate here where you have one in four people going to get that Coronavirus test coming back with a positive result.\par \par \uc2 But you have a situation where there are not those mitigation efforts that medical experts are pleading the public for the -- there`s resistance to masks, there`s a resistance to social distancing, but you also have a situation where there`s a low vaccination rate.\par \par \uc2 In this community, only about 40 percent of the total population have received even just one shot of that vaccine. So, you combine all those issues, and you have doctors saying well, the situation right now is incredibly bad, they are just fearing that it`ll only get worse. I want you to listen to what an official from the health department here told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE HOYER, COMMUNICATIONS OFFICER, PANHANDLE HEALTH: Right now, it`s uncomfortable thinking what might happen if we reach a level where we can`t supply someone a bed or someone waits in the ED too long and they can`t be seen by a doctor. That`s, I mean, it`s just -- it`s heartbreaking and it`s the worst scenario that we could possibly be in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:05:18]\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: You hear her talk about that heartbreaking scenario. And again, it is worth repeating the idea that this is a situation -- this surge is targeting the unvaccinated population.\par \par \uc2 You look at state data and the number of new cases, 90 percent of those cases are among non-vaccinated, you look at hospitalizations, deaths, also about 90 percent of the population.\par \par \uc2 You combine the fact that you have schools that have started last week, you have students returning to schools, most of them not wearing masks or no mask mandates there, it`s really a situation that has doctors and medical experts really stressed out about what the next few weeks will look like, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Shaq, thanks. So, Governor Beshear, you`re also dealing with this record rise in hospitalizations and a lagging vaccination rate. You`ve got hospitals with 10 times as many COVID patients today as they had two months ago, your daily vaccinations have doubled in that same time period, but clearly, not enough to slow Delta spread. So, my question to you is a pretty straightforward one. Do you support the vaccine mandates laid out by the President?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR, (D-KY): Well, I think like you started the broadcast, there are still a lot of questions that have to be answered about what that truly means. I think it`s even called the wrong name. When you read a little bit about it, what it is, is it`s a testing requirement, that unvaccinated individuals are going to have to get tested once per week unless they get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of that from the science makes sense. If you are unvaccinated, you are more likely to spread the virus. And so, while we all want to learn a little bit more about it, at least he`s trying to do something.\par \par \uc2 We are sitting here right now with the Delta variant burning through America, certainly burning through Kentucky with a lot of people who just put up their hands and say, well, if people would just go in and get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President is trying to take action, and these decisions are tough. You know, when you are ultimately the Commander in Chief, when you are the captain of the ship, you got to make those tough decisions. And he`s stepping up and making them when others are just sitting on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, to that end, I want to play for you something that Arkansas` Republican Governor Asa Hutchinson said yesterday on MEET THE PRESS about this very issue. Listen to this, Governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON, (R-AR): And let`s think about this for a second. We have some very aggressive governors from blue states, but I`m not aware of any governor from any state, they said we want to mandate businesses to require vaccinations. And so, the state`s, none of them have done that, and yet the federal government steps in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, to that end, Governor, I mean, you praise the President for taking action, why did we not see a strong state action from Kentucky?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, we`ve taken action, certainly in certain settings like congregate care settings, where in our presence, if you are an unvaccinated Corrections Officer, you have to get tested once per week. That`s similar to what`s in the President`s plan.\par \par \uc2 We do that amount of testing in our long-term care facilities and our nursing homes. We worked with our entire medical community, where just about every major hospital system came out and said, we need all of our employees to get vaccinated, or they`re going to have to be tested a specific amount or move to a non-clinical setting.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think we still need a lot of questions answered about the President`s plan. But it`s really easy to sit back if you`re not taking that bold step and trying to get people vaccinated and criticize others who are.\par \par \uc2 Again, we`re in the midst of a war that has killed so many, we have lost 8000 Kentuckians, now we`ve got to get people vaccinated. And I`m going to look carefully at any plan to make it happen, but I`m certainly not going to criticize anyone who`s stepping up and trying to get it done.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is calling it a testing mandate the way you sell this to people who you know, would just reject the idea of a mandate to put a needle in their arm?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, I think it`s more accurate because you satisfy the criteria if you decide to get tested once per week, instead of, get the vaccine. It`s all about lessening the spread of the virus and it`s all based on the science that we`ve learned about it.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I`m concerned that we`ve used the word mandate, whether it`s with masks or others since early in the pandemic, and I`m worried that we describe things oftentimes to simply, to folks out there who want all of the details.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I know. There are two things that can help us defeat this variant. Number one are vaccinations, and number two are masks.\par \par \uc2 We shouldn`t oppose either of them, and we should look for every way we can. First to encourage people to do the right thing, but then encouraging businesses to do the right thing as well. Remember, this is their workforce --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: -- you like the NFL and they`ve shown you that to them, COVID isn`t red or blue, it`s green.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:04]\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: If you don`t have your players on the field, if you can`t play the games, you`d lose productivity, you lose profit. They`re showing how this is an economic issue for that industry, for that company, and it is for the economy all over America.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know at least one business owner in North Texas who was very glad to hear about this mandate because it allows him to do what he wanted to do and requires his employees to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Governor Green, I want to switch to you. In Hawaii, you`re clearly doing something right. It`s the state with the highest percentage of people who have at least one shot in their arms, something like 88 percent of Hawaiians has gotten that first shot, so you`re doing something right there. But you`ve also got a third of your eligible residents who aren`t fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, my guess -- my question is, how big of an impact are you seeing from the unvaccinated on your hospitals? And if you could, what do you think you`re doing in Hawaii to get those shots in the arms that might be exportable to a place like Kentucky? You`ve got a fellow Democrat here who I`m sure would love to have some ideas to convince more folks to get shots in arms.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GREEN, LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR, HAWAII: Sure. Well, what we are seeing is though, even we`re -- even though we`re very well vaccinated, we still are seeing a ton of cases in our hospitals, almost exclusively among those who are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 83 percent of all of our hospitalizations are people who are unvaccinated, and 98 percent of our deaths are people who are unvaccinated, so my heart breaks as an emergency room physician when I see that.\par \par \uc2 What I would say is this, we have to take the politics out of this. We absolutely have to, and we have to unite as a country. I say shame on those governors who are discouraging vaccination. They are killing their constituents when they do that.\par \par \uc2 And that is not what we`re supposed to do as governors or lieutenant governors. I commend Governor Beshear who`s fighting the fight when he`s getting dragged into this politically.\par \par \uc2 Overall, this should not be a political issue. We`ve been vaccinating our children and ourselves throughout history, it`s the right thing to do, it`s a safe thing to do. The previous administration set this up as a political fight and that is very bad for America.\par \par \uc2 So, all I can say is that in Hawaii, we`ve just worked very hard day in and day out to get people vaccinated, and we`ve tried to minimize the conflict. It`s not easy because you got the internet, you got people watching the wrong news stations, which send all kinds of misinformation out there. This thing is pretty darn safe, and it will save lives.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You also have a bunch of other mandates in place in Hawaii. But I`m curious about the mandate that`s being proposed here.\par \par \uc2 You hear Governor Beshear call it a testing mandate, but for our purposes, we`ll talk about it as a vaccine mandate. For businesses, we crunched the numbers, and it could impact something like half -- roughly half of Hawaii`s workforce, how do you perceive that mandate to working both in terms of vaccinations and what it might do to your economy?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: We had quite a wild roller coaster ride on the economy because when the pandemic broke out, we shut down travel to Hawaii and that decreased travel by 99.6 percent. So, you can see, as you know, Hawaii is almost entirely tourism, so that crushed us --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: -- and gradually, I was able to open this up again. It really won`t affect us that much because 86.4 percent of all of those who are eligible in our state are vaccinated or at least have initiated vaccination. So, they`ll be able to go about their lives. It can be a little divisive, really just trying to encourage people to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I think people can come together over this and dismiss the politics, but obviously, the governors are fighting it out, everyone`s pushing back. I do want to say that Governor Beshear is right. It is actually a mandate to get tested because businesses over a hundred people, they have to either get vaccinated or have a weekly test. So, if you`re allowed to use the weekly test, that`s the common ground.\par \par \uc2 Let`s find some common ground here because I`ll tell you when I see a patient in the hospital, I don`t know if they`re Republican, or Democrat, or Independent, or from Mars.\par \par \uc2 I know that they`ve got COVID, and they`re about to get intubated and they`re going to die likely if they`re on the ventilator. So, all of the politics are just really bogus, and we really have to move on as one country.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are you worried about being able to enforce this mandate, either for testing or for vaccinations? I mean, you`ve got Washington DC making a decision that is going to be very hard for them to enforce across the ocean out in Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes. In Hawaii, we don`t listen to Washington a lot of the time, but that`s OK, we respect the process. I really -- you know, I think it`s going to be business to business, they`re just going to assess it. We have a lot of travelers here now.\par \par \uc2 We set up what`s called the Safe Travels Program so that anyone who travels to Hawaii has to, either be vaccinated or have a free test, which means they`ve already met the standard when they`re travelers here to our businesses and most of our businesses or hotels and restaurants. So, we should be OK.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the whole country should realize, anyone who`s making a good faith effort to get people vaccinated or to wear masks, they`re saving lives. The rest of it is really suspect.\par \par \uc2 We should focus on science -- look, you`ve become a life-threatening rod if you promote science these days, which is kind of ironic on the internet but don`t take your health information from the Internet. Call your doctor or Nurse Practitioner or physician assistant and ask for their opinion and then go forward.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:04]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, we`ve got to leave it there. Governor Beshear, Lieutenant Governor Josh Green of Hawaii, thank you both. I think we`ll probably hear a lot more discussion about this as a testing mandate. I`m getting that pretty clear from both of you all.\par \par \uc2 Before we get to break, \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  arrested a California man overnight for a possession of prohibited weapons. Around midnight, Capitol Police spotted a pickup truck without a license plate that had White Supremacist symbols painted on it. This was just outside DNC headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Inside, they found a machete and a bayonet, both of which are banned in DC. Police say the driver said he was on patrol and began talking about -- talking using White Supremacist ideology.\par \par \uc2 This arrest came as top Democratic and Republican leaders are being briefed today on Saturday`s upcoming Justice for J6 rally planned by supporters of those arrested for the January attack on the Capitol. We will have much more on that in the days to come.\par \par \uc2 But up next, we`re tracking other major developments on the Hill as Secretary of State Blinken prepares to go before the House Foreign Affairs Committee over the fallout in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Plus, growing Democratic divisions as the party faces a number of key deadlines, with government funding set to run out by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 And later, we`ll head out west where President Biden is headed right now. He`s expected to land in Idaho later this hour. Plus, we`ve got all the details on his stuff in California ahead of tomorrow`s big recall election.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. That jammed-pack September in Washington, chock full of looming deadlines that we`ve been telling you is coming, well, it`s here. The next two weeks on Capitol Hill could be chaotic.\par \par \uc2 Government funding runs out on September 30, and Congress will need to pass some sort of legislation just to keep the lights on. U.S. is also expected to hit the debt ceiling shortly after that requiring congressional action that Republicans say they won`t support.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:02]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And as for the Democrats` agenda, well, Wednesday is the deadline for the House Committee Chairs to have their budget reconciliation plans in for consideration. Their homework is due and they`re still attempting to get reconciliation done before a self-imposed September 27 deadline to pass the Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill.\par \par \uc2 You got all this? Well, internal Democratic fighting over the price tag for the reconciliation package continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: You could support this $3.5 trillion plan --\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2 , (D-WV): No, I will not support $3.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, ANCHOR, CNN: He wants to see something more in the ballpark of 1.5 trillion. Is that acceptable to you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS, (I-VT): No, it`s absolutely not acceptable to me. I don`t think it`s acceptable for the President, to the American people, or the overwhelming majority of the people in the Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`ve said this, if I can`t go home and explain it, I can`t vote for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And right now, you don`t think you can explain?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I can`t explain what we`re doing now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But before Democrats resolve that impasse, the focus on the Hill today is on Afghanistan with Secretary of State Anthony Blinken slated to appear before the House Foreign Affairs Committee in just a few minutes, and before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on Capitol Hill is NBC`s, Leigh Ann Caldwell. So, Leigh Ann, on Reconciliation, there`s math problems, calendar problems, policy problems, just how far are Democrats from coming to a consensus that they can actually bring to the floor in either chamber?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Garrett. So, there`s a couple of things, Democrats are plowing forward on crafting this $3.5 trillion bill and the key is they`re still crafting a $3.5 trillion bill.\par \par \uc2 But once they`re done with that, which their expectation is -- supposedly their goal, I should say, is to be done with the crafting of that this week, then they have to start negotiating that because as Senator Manchin has said over and over again, that`s not a number he could support, and that`s not something that he`s going to vote for. And his vote is extremely necessary.\par \par \uc2 And so, negotiations are going to commence because there`s no way at this point that $3.5 trillion is going to pass. They`re going to have to work with Manchin, Senator Sinema of Arizona, and other moderates as well, who are uncomfortable with that high price tag.\par \par \uc2 And so, they were really only in the first step of this process of drafting this legislation, and then negotiations begin. And what`s going to be extremely critical is how much their revenue raisers cost? How much it`s going to be paid for?\par \par \uc2 If it comes out at a trillion-dollar, $1.5trillion, $2 trillion, whatever it is, that`s very likely going to be the closer number of what the actual cost of the bill will be.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Leigh Ann, we got a little peek at that math today from the House Ways and Means Committee, what did we learn about the tax changes, Democrats want to make to try to get to some number of what they can actually pay for?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, well, they`re fulfilling their promise of increasing court - - taxes on corporations and the wealthy, and that`s been a big priority of President Biden. And Democrats are trying to redistribute, readjust the economy so that the wealthy have to pay more and distribute those benefits to the middle and lower class. And that`s what this $3.5 trillion bill is meant to do.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to raise the corporate tax rate to 26.5 percent ultimately, and then they raise the highest income tax earners rate to 39.6 percent. And those are key goals of theirs and ones that they anticipate it to move forward, except for the fact, when it comes to Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , who once again has said that 26.5 percent corporate rate is probably too high for him.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We know a lot about what Manchin is against in this bill, but much less about what he`s for. And Leigh Ann, finally, obviously the big event on Capitol Hill today is this testimony from Secretary Blinken on the House side, what can we expect to hear today?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, Secretary Blinken is going to defend the chaotic exit from Afghanistan and those final few days and weeks in the country. But he`s going to receive a lot of tough questions not just by Republicans, who many of them have called for Secretary Blinken to resign over the Afghanistan exit but, also some Democrats as well, who do have a lot of questions and concerns, including the number of Americans left in Afghanistan, how they are going to resettle the Afghans who have left and also the diplomacy in the negotiations with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be -- could be anyway, an extremely tense hearing. And this is just as first of two days being on Capitol Hill. Tomorrow, he`s going to testify before the Senate, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, a rough start of the week possible for the Secretary of State. Leigh Ann Caldwell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is California Democratic Congressman Ami Bera. He`s a member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee. He will be questioning Secretary Blinken later this afternoon. And Congressman, first question, what do you want to hear from the Secretary today? What are you going to be asking him about?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:05]\par \par \uc2 REP. AMI BERA (D-CA): Well, Garrett, thanks for having me on. You know, I think nobody`s questioning the President`s decision to withdraw but we are questioning, you know, how rapidly the Afghan government, the security forces lay down arms, and how they didn`t even fight the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 That, you know, what kind of information was the Secretary getting? You know, did they see this coming in? And if they saw it coming, you know, why didn`t we get people out sooner? So, yes, I think it`s hard to defend kind of the chaos that we saw at the end of August.\par \par \uc2 I`m also going to focus on you know, we`ve submitted over 10,000 names of American citizens, visa holders, family members of Afghans who have ties to my district in Sacramento County. Many of those folks are still in the country, including children that are American citizens. What`s our plan to get them out?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: There, you`ve been focusing on the SIV issue quite a bit. Secretary Blinken talks about this in his opening statement, and he actually puts the blame on Congress for delays in processing SIVs. He writes, when we first took office, we inherited a program with a 14-step process based on statutory framework enacted by Congress and involving multiple government agencies a backlog of more than 17,000 SIV applicants.\par \par \uc2 He said, there had not been a single interview of an SIV applicant in Kabul in nine months, going back to March of 2020. The program, he says or will say, was basically in a dead stall. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BERA: You know, I don`t think that`s quite fair because Congress`s authorized multiple thousands of visas that went unused. So, you can criticize the Trump administration for basically shutting the program down, and then the pandemic comes top of that for slowing the process down.\par \par \uc2 But as soon as they came into office, you know, we reached out along with other members of Congress in a bipartisan way to say let`s get through this backlog of tens of thousands of individuals that have already started the process.\par \par \uc2 Once the withdrawal decisions were made, we knew that one of the possible outcomes was a collapse of the Afghan government and that there was an importance of speeding up the process. It really wasn`t until July, but the administration started to work through this backlog and get folks out.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think the criticism of Congress is truly fair, I think the process was burdensome. If they had suggestions on unburdening the process, they should have come to us.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: On the plot itself, you laid this out a little bit in your first answer. But when we hear the administration defend what happened in Afghanistan, they oftentimes defend the decision to leave not necessarily how it was done.\par \par \uc2 But the other day, your own Chairman, Chairman Meeks talked about the pullout. He said was the evacuation perfect? Absolutely not. But if you talk about it in its entirety, and what the results were, you`ll have to say it was a successful operation.\par \par \uc2 So, not talking about the decision to leave Afghanistan, merely how the pullout was executed, do you agree with the Chairman, and to some extent with the administration that that was a successful operation?\par \par \uc2 BERA: Yes. I mean, the airlift of that magnitude over, you know, a period of two weeks, and to get 120,000 plus individuals out fairly quickly, fairly safely, I think was a monumental logistical success. That said, we shouldn`t have found ourselves in that situation.\par \par \uc2 And again, did we just totally miscalculate the Afghan government`s ability to hold on to territory to defend itself to take a stance? And obviously, we saw the end result.\par \par \uc2 I`d like to know, like, why did we have such bad information? Or if the administration knew this collapse was coming, we should have been doing this months ago.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, I`m very curious to see what answers we get from the Secretary today. I want to ask you about the Democratic agenda here, this big Reconciliation Bill working its way through the committee`s, I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 You saw over the weekend, your Senate colleagues, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , says he won`t accept a $3.5 trillion bill, Bernie Sanders says that`s already as low as he will go. How do you see this getting worked out? Where do you think this final Bill ends up being if we ever see a final version of it?\par \par \uc2 BERA: Yes, I think you`ll see the House put together a $3.5 trillion Reconciliation Bill. We`ve -- we obviously need Senator Manchin`s vote, Senator Sinema`s vote. I think then you will start seeing negotiations, and hopefully, some of that is taking place as we speak.\par \par \uc2 You know, I hope we can find a middle ground here because the American people expect us to enact an agenda that does invest in the social capital of people here. I think we can come up with a fair back feed, and then let`s also pass the Bipartisan Infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Those two pieces of legislation, we can get this done, really do set us forward to make investments in the next generation that we haven`t done for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I went through all the transcripts of Joe Manchin`s Sunday show interviews yesterday, and really the only thing he was very clear about in terms of what he doesn`t like in this plan are the Clean Energy provisions.\par \par \uc2 You`re in the House Science Committee. You`ve got a lot of climate hawks in the Democratic caucus. If the climate provisions were to come out or to shrink dramatically, can a bill without those key planks in it pass?\par \par \uc2 REP. AMI BERA (D-CA): It`d be tough. Like, we`re seeing horrific wildfires, we`ve seen Hurricane Ida and the damage it`s done. And I consider \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  a friend. I`d go to Senator Manchin and say, what we ought to do is take those coal miners and those workers whose jobs are going away, we ought to invest in job training, we ought to invest in creating jobs so those folks in West Virginia can find clean energy jobs that will pay them more, that are more secure, and give them and their children a career track. So I think there`s a way to make this palatable to Senator Manchin.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Congressman, let me know what he says when you have that conversation. For now, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, new alarming developments from Pyongyang that suggest North Korea could have just developed a new kind of long-range missile in what might be the country`s first such weapon with nuclear capability.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: There are new signs from North Korea today that the country continues to build up its weapons program. State media announced overnight that that country successfully launched a newly developed long-range cruise missiles. It`s the regime`s first missile test in six months, and analysts say this could be North Korea`s first weapon of this kind with nuclear capability.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:07]\par \par \uc2 North Korea`s state-run news agency said the missiles are, quote, "a strategic weapon of great significance," and that they flew 930 miles before hitting their targets and falling into the country`s territorial waters.\par \par \uc2 The agency also claims the weapons have been in development for two years. The tests appear to signal progress in Pyongyang`s weapons program development. Talks aimed at dismantling North Korea`s nuclear and ballistic missile programs have been pretty much stalled since 2019.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Military`s Indo-Pacific Command says it`s aware of the test and is working with our allies like Japan and South Korea to monitor the situation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re down to the final hours of campaigning in the California recall election. As Republicans seem to be relying on President Trump`s 2020 playbook, President Biden is on his way to lend Governor Newsom his support.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. President Biden`s first trip to California as president will include a stop in Long Beach to give a last-minute boost to Governor Gavin Newsom ahead of the recall election tomorrow. Millions of people have already voted and Democrats have returned far more ballots than have Republicans. And despite concern early in the summer that Democratic disinterest could give Republicans a path to victory, recent poll suggests the governor will survive this recall effort.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans are already alleging voter fraud despite no evidence to support those claims. And speaking to NBC News today, Larry Elder, the leading Republican candidate, would not commit to accepting the results of the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Again, whether or not you win or lose, will you accept the results of the election tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER (R), CALIFORNIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I think we all ought to be looking at election integrity. No matter whether you`re a Democrat, an independent or a Republican. Let`s all make sure that the election is a fair election. So let`s all work together,