y to blow it up and say what it does about -- what it says about national politics. So I`ll ask you as the California expert, what if anything will this election tell us that we can apply nationally?\par \par \uc2 MARINUCCI: I mean, I think Gavin Newsom`s response to COVID is something that Democrats nationally may take as a road map going forward to 2020. Overwhelmingly Californians say this is the number one issue for them. The Delta variant and the rise of the Delta variant and his aggressive response to it has been a key to what looks like this landslide, could be a landslide victory tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Certainly Democrats all over the country may be watching that, and certainly President Biden is watching that, as he tries to institute tougher vaccine and testing mandates. So going forward, yes, this could be the template of what we`re seeing here in California, for what Democrats may want to do all around the country in 2022.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Aggressively fighting COVID is good politics, it seems.\par \par \uc2 All right, Guad Venegas, Carla Marinucci, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And before we go to break, we`re seeing the president on his way now landing in Boise, Idaho. This is for the fires briefing that he`ll have today on his way to California.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, we turn back to some of those big questions about the vaccine mandates. When will it happen, how will it be enforced. I`ll talk to the former head of the agency President Biden is tapping to make these mandates happen, next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:25]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Now that President Biden has taken the step of announcing his new vaccine mandates on federal employees and companies of more than 100 employees or testing mandate as we heard earlier in the show, the real work begins now to implement them. Much of that work falls to the Department of Labor, specifically the Occupational Health and Safety Administration also known as OSHA.\par \par \uc2 They are expected to be the administration`s workhorse in terms of crafting the language behind these new mandates and figuring out how to enforce them. As of today, the White House has provided very few detail about its approach on either of those things.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to break down the potential next steps for those mandates is someone who ran OSHA for eight years, former administrator David Michaels, who`s also an epidemiologist, which makes him very well qualified to have this discussion.\par \par \uc2 So, Doc, the Department of Labor hasn`t even formally crafted this rule yet. We obviously know the parameters that the White House laid out, so what now? How does this actually get written and how long will it likely take before this rollout is like a real rule?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MICHAELS, FORMER OSHA ADMINISTRATOR: Well, Garrett, the staff of the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  are pedal to the metal, they`re writing right now exactly what President Biden has asked them to do, and this rule that they will put out, which will take some weeks to do, will not require vaccines. That`s a really important thing. It`s sort of been, you know, misstated.\par \par \uc2 What this regulation will do will be to tell employers they have to keep their workplace safe. And an important way to do that in this pandemic is to make sure that people who could be infectious are not coming into the workplace and spreading the virus. And so the rule will tell employers you`ve got to figure out who is vaccinated, who has tested negative, and they can be allowed in, and people who aren`t can`t come into the workplace.\par \par \uc2 They can have alternative work arrangements, there are things you can do, but your job as an employer under this new OSHA rule is to keep your workplace safe.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think that framing is so interesting and so important. I mean, you heard -- I mean, perhaps you were not dialed in, but at the top of the show, we had a Democratic governor and lieutenant governor both describing this as a testing mandate, not a vaccination mandate, so you`re seeing kind of that same framing here.\par \par \uc2 Can the system in this country handle even a testing mandate at this point? I mean, it`s still in a lot of places kind of challenging to go get tested on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 MICHAELS: You know, it`s absolutely challenging. We`ve got to work it, we`ve got to, you know, increase the amount of tests available, but it`s certainly doable to have every employer work out a system for themselves, for their employees, to make sure we know that they`re not infectious.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MICHAELS: And over time, this will make a difference.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So then why stop at, you know, employers with 100 or more employees? Why not include everybody?\par \par \uc2 MICHAELS: I absolutely agree with you. I think the White House probably decided it`s much harder to involve small employers. Maybe it`ll them longer. But I think there`s no reason to separate it. OSHA has never separated large employers with smaller employers when there is a life- threatening hazard. Every workers should be safe whether they work at a small facility or a large one.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s interesting. So, I think there`s a lot of conversation about this, about the idea as we`re watching the president come down off Air Force One here in Boise, Idaho, while we`re discussing this. A lot of discussion about how these mandates, whether for testing or for vaccines, could be enforced. You had a really interesting \uc2 Twitter\uc2  thread on the idea you don`t have to enforce 100 million cases of these. Can you kind of explain how enforcement works in a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 MICHAELS: That`s right. You know, government regulations for the most part are self-enforced in that every employer and certainly large employers, they have HR staffs, they have attorneys who tell them, this is what the law says and this is what you have to do. So most employers even before OSHA issues this rule will already be working on what do we need to do to make sure that we`re in compliance with the law because that`s what they do.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, OSHA will start doing some infections. You know, workers will complain, and there will be plenty of employers where workers will send in complains. It`s not happening as they expected to happen. And then OSHA can send out inspectors. You know, OSHA does lots of inspections for other things, and every time an OSHA inspector goes out, they`re going to ask, let me see your records, how are you keeping track of who`s vaccinated, who`s tested, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 That process I think will lead to widespread compliance, and I think it`s going to stop a lot of workplace spread of the virus.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How much of this do you think gets done on the front end before this rule even goes into place. I mentioned, you know, someone I know is a business owner in north Texas who said, great, you know, now if there`s going to be a mandate, I can make my workforce get vaccinated when they were hesitant to do so, without any kind of guidance or rule from the federal government. How much do you think will get done on the front end from companies who`ve been waiting for an opportunity to make the federal government the heavy and start trying to get their employees vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 MICHAELS: That is going to be happen all over the country especially in those states where the governors have said you have no right to require a vaccine, you can`t force people to test, et cetera. It was the federal government trumps all of these state rules. When OSHA says this is what must happen in this workplace, if that workplace is covered by federal OSHA, a state law that says you can`t ask about vaccination, for example, doesn`t apply. It`s the supremacy of the federal government. It`s in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 So many, many employers are extremely relieved. They have wanted to do this, they want to make sure their workers feel comfortable coming back to work, and there are so many workers now who are hesitant to come back in because they`re worried about being exposed. But I think it will be something that most businesses will want to do right now even before the rule officially goes into place.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, David Michaels, who knew we could have such an interesting conversation about OSHA and workplace safety regulations. I really appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Thank you. And thank you all for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with my friend Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: It is good to be with you. I`m Geoff Bennett, and right now, you are looking live inside a hearing room in the Rayburn House Office Building at the U.S. Capitol. Let`s see if we can get that shot. There it is.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210914eh9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for September 3, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kelly O`Donnell, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Shaquille  Brewster, Yasmin Vossoughian, Kerry Sanders \par \uc2 8,760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, not much relief in sight for the White House after a rash of rough headlines, and now face a one-two punch of declining poll numbers in a Democratic Party infighting over strategy with Joe Manchin threatening one major agenda item, and \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  threatening the other.\par \par \uc2 All of this as the President heads to New Orleans where he`ll tour the damage from Hurricane Ida. We`ll also get an update on how the storm`s floodwaters have totally ravaged parts of the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 And later, it is jobs day and the August numbers prove the financial burden caused by that pandemic is far from over. We`ve got the details on today`s lackluster COVID infused report and President Biden`s reaction to it with a member of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 . All that`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I am Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Happy Friday and Labor Day weekend coming up to everybody. And it`s been a brutal summer of headlines, particularly in the month of August for the Biden administration. And the developments of the last 24 hours, suggest it`s not going to get much easier in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 The Delta surge is impacting the economy, particularly hiring. We learned from today`s report that job growth slowed dramatically in August compared to the prior two months. Although wages were up and the overall employment- -unemployment rate fell, but it is clear that Delta`s disruption is what made this report not so good.\par \par \uc2 The President addressed the disappointing economic numbers, acknowledging that Delta is having a huge impact and that the administration needs to do more to address it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s no question that Delta variant is why today`s job report isn`t stronger. I know people were looking, and I was hoping for a higher number. But next week, I`ll lay out the next steps that are going to -- we`re going to do to combat the Delta variant, to address some of those fears and concerns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, this comes as the President`s approval rating has been declining for a few weeks now, either a little or a lot depending on the poll you want to look at, but the trend line is definitely down. How far down it will go is not yet clear. Are we at the bottom in this low to mid- 40s?\par \par \uc2 And there`s new infighting among Democrats. Senator Joe Manchin has reiterated that he is not willing to support the Democrats currently proposed $3.5 trillion Reconciliation Bill as the bill that is tied right now to just about every other major legislative priority on the White House agenda.\par \par \uc2 Manchin says that amid inflation, debt, and the inevitability of future crises, Congress needs to take a strategic pause. In response, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  reiterated the progressive position, that there will be no Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill without the Reconciliation Bill.\par \par \uc2 A standoff between Democratic moderates and progressive is clear, and how it gets resolved is not. And it all leaves some Americans and lawmakers feeling a crippling sense of uncertainty about the road ahead politically, and economically.\par \par \uc2 And if you have to think this is a moment when this White House could really benefit from the optics of a big bill signing, especially on something like infrastructure, as Biden tours the damage from Ida, and oh, by the way, spending on infrastructure in Louisiana seem to work, and we need some more infrastructure help in the northeast, don`t we now? I`m not saying, I`m just saying.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House, Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly, let me start with you. You know, it`s interesting that the President is going to be touring Louisiana. Ida`s impacts, in some ways, as it connects to the Bill he`s been pushing with infrastructure could really be used in his home turf of the North East, how much do we expect him to tie his push for infrastructure with the -- with climate, and what he`s doing today in Louisiana?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Considerably. This is an opportunity for the President to bring back talk of his domestic agenda after we`ve been through this intense period with the exit from Afghanistan, and all of the crises that were a part of that, and some remain, but this is an opportunity for the President to try to talk about his domestic agenda when it comes to the combination of infrastructure spending, support for the emergencies that people are experiencing from Louisiana, where he`ll be today tour in both on the ground and from an aerial perspective, all the way through the Northeast, where there is an aging infrastructure that certainly took a beating with the unexpected flooding and the severity of that, and the climate piece that is also a part of the President`s larger agenda, so we do expect him to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 I was in the State Dining room today when the President made his remarks about the job`s numbers, and I thought it was notable that he did acknowledge it at more than one point during his remarks running 20 minutes or so about his concern that the numbers weren`t better.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A real acknowledgment that this is not the picture they were hoping for, and could that be used as a lever to try to get that divide between progressives and moderates in his own party? He did not respond to a question about Joe Manchin, he did take one question related to the Texas Abortion Law.\par \par \uc2 So, the President will have a chance today to speak more, perhaps sometimes, as you know, from your days covering the White House, when you`re on the ground with the President in a disaster area, sometimes your close up and there`s an opportunity for more conversation about this so we may hear him point to that.\par \par \uc2 But trying to get money to where it`s needed, is something that benefits the President politically, as well as his larger legacy issues, and we know what the tension points are in trying to get that done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kelly, I`m curious about something the President claimed he was going to layout next week. What is that next week? Is it a new plan in combating COVID? Is it a new -- what is it specifically the -- that he`s hinting at that he`s going to be rolling out next week?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, we haven`t been read in on the plan, but I think part of what you`re seeing is the longer-term push for. Now, if this delta variant is not receding, if there is still a push to try to get more people vaccinated, how can they try to do that?\par \par \uc2 They`ve tried incentives, they`ve tried mandates, where else can they go with that? And there`s a certain quality of living with COVID that we`ve all begun to experience when -- we began with 15 days to slow the spread, and now we`re into 15, 16, 17 months of people`s lives being disrupted to some degree by what has happened both economically, and in health terms, and concerns about children in education.\par \par \uc2 So, one of the things as you -- as you follow President Biden, he is often doing his own teases, where he`ll say, I`m going to talk about that important issue next week --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- And then -- and then we have to follow that and see, does he come through and give us something new on that?\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, that maybe it`s something he would like to do and maybe the stuff pulls him back. I can tell you this, Kelly, from talking to public health officials on the ground on the state and local level, all of them are quietly, well, they`re relieved, that it is not what they were dealing with the Trump White House.\par \par \uc2 They`re not pleased with the way this COVID task force works, and think it needs a little bit of a jumpstart, a refresh because people aren`t listening. So, what that look like? I think we`re all wondering this point. Kelly O`Donnell at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me move over to the action on Capitol Hill. So, Leigh Ann, it`s sort of Joe Manchin left a gift for the congressional Democrats for Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Look, there`s a lot to -- there`s a lot to his op-ed, we could unpack, we could parse, what it sounded like to me when you read it is that this was a trial balloon to see how many other Democrats agree with Joe Manchin, so that`s my question to you, Leigh Ann. How many other Democrats agree with Joe Manchin`s idea of let`s take a pause?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chuck. So, the thing about Senator Manchin is he doesn`t always say anything necessarily new when he dropped these bombshells on these democratic negotiations, but he likes to remind his colleagues that he is here and that he is going to be a thorn in their side, and he uses that to his negotiating benefit.\par \par \uc2 Now, what Senator Manchin said in his op-ed yesterday saying that take a pause, let`s hold off on this $3.5 trillion bill. Well, it`s not that different than what Senator Sinema of Arizona has said previously said that $3.5 trillion is too much, and that is something a number that she is not going to support.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a lot of conversations going on among Capitol Hill -- among Democrats about what that final number is going to be. Is it going to be $3.5 trillion, or are they going to have to lower that number to satisfy people like Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema?\par \par \uc2 And the House Democrats are very clear. They know that the challenges are mostly in the Senate. And Senator Manchin, whether people like it or not, he actually does have a lot of power here in this situation, as we know that every vote counts. And so, there is some recognition privately anyway, that perhaps that $3.5 trillion number is going to be reduced.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You know, Leigh Ann, it was interesting, you know, when you tried to read the Manchin tea leaves right at the end of the op-ed, he made a pledge, I`m not going to add to the deficit through this process the way the Republicans added to the deficit.\par \par \uc2 So, does that -- I -- if you take that to its logical conclusion, he will support any spending that`s paid for, which feels like the number that`s somewhere around 2 trillion. Is that -- is that where this realistic line is going to end up being drawn?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, coincidentally, perhaps, the Office of Senator Ron Wyden, the head of the Finance Committee dropped a set of proposals of how this $3 trillion bill is going to pay -- be paid for. Nothing is set in stone at all, but these are things they are discussing, and I think that the timing is really critical here.\par \par \uc2 It`s the day after this Manchin op-ed pointing out exactly what you did. So, this is their effort to show, look, we are not going to add $3 trillion to the debt, we are going to try to make sure that this is paid for and those are conversations that are happening.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:25]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kelly O`Donnell, Leigh Ann Caldwell, thank you both for getting us started on this much busier Friday before Labor Day weekend. Then we`re all used to, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the leaders of the Progressive Caucus. Democrat from California, Congressman Ro Khanna. Congressman, it`s good to have you on. So, let me start with your reaction to Joe Manchin`s op-ed.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA, (D-CA): Well, Chuck, I believe Senator Manchin will get there, he always supports the President`s agenda at the end.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the issue. I mean, the centrists say that it is urgent by September 27 to have our roads and bridges invested in, I agree. But it is equally urgent to get the other part of the President`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 It is urgent that we have electric vehicle infrastructure and not let the Chinese win, it is urgent that we have childcare so that women can get back to work, it is urgent that we get lead pipes to fix so that people, young kids, and communities of color aren`t drinking poisoned water.\par \par \uc2 The progressives actually have the President`s back. We`re the ones who are pushing the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. When you -- like I said, when you read between the lines in Senator Manchin, I don`t disagree with you, I think at the end of the day, he`s going to want to figure out how to get to yes. Just like you guys want to get him to yes, in various ways.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think he`s going to be alone, though, among some Democrats in wanting to try to not deficit spent. As you know, there are at least three or four that have -- Senators that are being that column.\par \par \uc2 Where are you on that? And if that`s where we are, every proposal I`ve seen indicates that the most amount of tax revenue being raised here that`s being talked about is in the $2 trillion range, if that`s where this bill ends up, is that something you can support?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: First of all, we can raise I believe, taxes on big corporations, on the wealthy, and on estates in a way that would generate more than 2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, even Paul Krugman, many economists have said that if you have deficit spending for productive investments, investments that are going to lead your long-term returns, that is fine. So, I believe that those concerns can be answered.\par \par \uc2 On the inflation concern, look at what Lawrence Summers has said. He said it`s a monetary phenomenon. If you really care about inflation, and we can debate whether that exists, talk to the Fed about quantitative easing, it has nothing to do with this 3.5 trillion number. And one point on the number, it`s a 10-year number, it`s 350 billion per year.\par \par \uc2 When they talk about defense increases, they always use the one-year number, 23 billion. Well, that`s -- they never use the 10-year number, and yet, for something like social investments, they always use the 10-year number. Let`s talk about the actual one-year numbers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, fair point, it`s something the White House keeps bringing up behind the scenes, they are concerned that the number itself has almost become too famous.\par \par \uc2 And is that a concern of yours that in many ways and whether it`s how you guys talk about on Capitol Hill, how we end up talking about in the media? We talk about, hey, it`s the $3.5 trillion reconciliation bill. It`s not the -- what the Biden White House wants to call it bill back better plan or what you guys want to call it. Some of that -- some of this sometimes is his messaging.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I agree with that. It should be the bill banner plan, we should say it`s a few percent increase in the GDP per year, and it is -- what does it do?\par \par \uc2 It`s for getting people childcare, it`s making sure seniors can have dental benefits, it`s making sure people can attend community college, it`s making sure that we deal with climate, with the IDA storm.\par \par \uc2 People want to know that we actually have a plan for clean energy, that we have a plan for the electrification of the smart grid. I think if we sell the policies and don`t wobble or argue over the large number itself, we will succeed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have to say, as a -- as an observer here, I have not understood the motivation of holding back the infrastructure bill. You look at it, this President could use a bill signing, pretty badly right now, if you look at his poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 And if the only reason this is being held back is because the two sides don`t trust each other, then I might look at this and say well, that you`re never going to get to yes, and neither these because of if this trust is at the center of this strategy, I don`t know how you get over just trust.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think we will get to yes. I think the point is that there are a lot of concerns with the bipartisan infrastructure bill, particularly on climate.\par \par \uc2 People say OK, we`re fine building more highways, but you can`t just build more highways if there`s not going to be some concern about the climate impact. So, let`s have electric vehicles, let`s have a civilian corps, Let`s have some retrofitting, let`s have some clean energy standard, and if you do that, then we`re going to build modern infrastructure with the climate there.\par \par \uc2 But to just pass, OK, we want to have more highways and roads and not have the climate art, people, including our House Transportation Chair, has concerned with that, but I believe we will get to a yes on both of them.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:19]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you on the -- on the larger philosophy you have on this bill, which is, your -- look, even at 3.5 trillion, you don`t get to do everything on your wish list. If things get smaller, is your philosophy that you include a little bit of everything and just cut down the size, or do you end up lopping off big programs and do a few things big and right.\par \par \uc2 Yes, sir, I`m going here, you know, do you do a little bit of pre-K, or do you say, you know, what we`re going to do well this pre-K? We`re not going to be able to do affordable housing this route or something in that. What is your philosophy, a little bit of everything, or a couple of things big and incorrect?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, Chuck, I don`t want to negotiate on MEET THE PRESS when our position is the 3.5 trillion, which is actually the Biden agenda. But I will say this, the progressives have always been willing to have back and forth, we have never drawn a red line.\par \par \uc2 And if the question is, what is the priority, I think the priority is investing in the key needs of the American people in childcare, in dental, in vision being covered in Medicare, in community college being free, and we can look at how long those investments should last and the scope of those investments.\par \par \uc2 I believe 3.5 trillion was already the compromise, but we have never, look, Chuck, we never did something that those 10 centrists did.\par \par \uc2 Name me a time where the progressives have actually threatened to hold up the agenda. And the irony here is that the progressives are at the President`s back. We`re the ones who`ve been on TV on cable news defending the withdrawal. We`re the ones who are saying let`s get the Biden agenda passed. His strongest allies. I`ve actually been the progressives in Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Ro Khanna, Democrat from Silicon Valley, actually not just anywhere in California. Thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir, and enjoy the long weekend as best as you can.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you. Happy Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Up next, we`re going to go live in New Orleans where President Biden is expected to tour Hurricane Ida`s storm damage later this afternoon as people across the state continue to struggle for basic necessities.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Coronavirus death toll is hitting a record high in Florida. Hospital beds are running low, and the messy debate over wearing masks in schools sadly continues. We`re live in Fort Lauderdale up ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:39]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Biden is now on his way to Louisiana, where he`ll see firsthand the devastation Hurricane Ida caused across Louisiana, but also how the levees held? It`s an important reminder, infrastructure investments worked in Louisiana, folks.\par \par \uc2 Still, there are more than 700,000 customers without power nearly a week after it first made landfall, so we still have some electric grid issues. And it means no air conditioning as temperatures are still in the 80s and 90s. down south.\par \par \uc2 Experts warn that situations like that are some of the reasons that the days and weeks after Hurricane strikes can often be even more deadlier than the storm itself.\par \par \uc2 And as the President tours the devastation in the southeast, many residents in the northeast are having to assess the damage Ida caused to their own homes.\par \par \uc2 Floods that they had never had before, basements get water in it that had never gotten water in it before. Much of Manville New Jersey, for instance, remain underwater after it dropped 10 inches of rain in the area in just three hours.\par \par \uc2 Ida is now being blamed for at least 49 deaths in the northeast, more than half of them in New Jersey. Much larger death toll than anything we`re seeing in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille Brewster joins me now from New Orleans, Yasmin Vossoughian is live for us in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, let me go to you. Look, like I said, the good news has been the basic infrastructure the levees held. That`s the big picture good news story, but power and some of those basics -- \uc2 Entergy\uc2 , the local power company seems to be struggling getting everything going. What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, it can be hard understanding folks struggle because you don`t see as many houses that have been damaged by this storm. You don`t see the hundreds and thousands of people that are, you know, on top of roofs getting rescued because of flooding.\par \par \uc2 But people are dealing with these what feels like triple-digit temperatures without electricity, without that AC, and -- which is a dangerous condition in of itself.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you something that the President will see and can`t avoid seeing are these lines that are like the ones behind me. This line goes on for over an hour here.\par \par \uc2 This is a \uc2 Costco\uc2 , a gas station, but you not only have them at gas stations, you not only have them outside of restaurants, you also have them as people are going in to get ice for example. So, this is becoming part of people`s daily routine.\par \par \uc2 Now, we did get an update from the power company today, \uc2 Entergy\uc2 , they`re saying that there`s still more than 700,000 people without power right now, but they did give a timeline of when the restoration of power will happen, at least here in the Greater New Orleans area.\par \par \uc2 And they`re saying that can take place by the middle of next week for most customers, which is a positive sign. For the folks I talked to earlier today, it`s still a long ways away. Listen to what they told me about how these lines are becoming part of their daily routines.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I go to the gas station every day.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Every day? How long you`re waiting every day?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today is not bad. Two days ago, I was in line for seven hours.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The day before that. I was hit twice, two hours each time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The help that came through was fast and immediate, but this is something new for us for the electricity to be out. We need to get more generators on the ground when stuff like this happens so we won`t be in the heat for so long.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: So, I think bottom line, Chuck, all of these lines are based on the lack of power and we`re hearing that the power will be restored for most people here in this area by the middle of next week. Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, well, that`s going to make for a very, very long weekend, though. That`s for sure. Shaquille Brewster in Louisiana for us. Shaq, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go over to Yasmin, where, again, the story of Ida now in the northeast, particularly New Jersey, this is a case as we talked about yesterday, overwhelmed infrastructure, people experiencing floods that have never seen water like this before.\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: Yes. I mean, I was in Westchester County yesterday, and this might be the worst, Chuck. If I was standing here around 5 a.m. after that storm, the water would have covered my head. I would have been underwater at that point. And we are seeing complete and utter destruction to a lot of these homes.\par \par \uc2 Just around the corner from here, two homes were set on fire, blew up because of gas lines obviously, getting wet. Down the street, you had a convention hall completely blow as well. It`s hard to really navigate this.\par \par \uc2 And I was actually told by the Superintendent of the fire department who essentially told me when there`s flooding here because they do have some flooding here, this place basically becomes an island. It`s surrounded on three sides by rivers.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:01]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: The issue this time though is, as we have heard over the last 48 hours, nothing like this has ever happened before. They knew a storm was coming but was it going to be this bad, they didn`t anticipate that.\par \par \uc2 Let me just show you a little bit of what I`m seeing here as we walk towards this house. Now, Hyman is a renter of this home. He`s obviously incredibly emotionally distraught having now lost all of his belongings alongside his wife and his five-year-old child so he didn`t necessarily want to talk to us, but he is allowing us to look inside his home.\par \par \uc2 And if you just look inside here -- sorry Hyman, I don`t mean to interrupt what you`re doing here. I mean, it is completely destroyed here, Chuck. The water is still waist level, right.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, in Westchester County, a lot of the places that I`ve been seeing, the water was beginning to recede. Here, we`re still seeing a pretty significant amount of water.\par \par \uc2 There was a highway just around the corner as well, that is still completely flooded. So, it is receding but nonetheless the cleanup is still very much happening.\par \par \uc2 And let me just paint a picture for you, Chuck, because this really stuck with me as a parent. Hyman and his wife, have a five-year-old child, they`re awoken around 2 a.m. because of the flooding. They said, we got to get out, right?\par \par \uc2 At 3 a.m. is when they finally made their way out. The water was up to his neck. At this point. He had to carry his kid on his head to walk across the street all the way across the street Tim`s -- swing around here to show Chuck where that`s -- where that fence is, that white fence. He had to get all the way over there in order to seek safety and the water continued to go up to head level for me.\par \par \uc2 So really, the question for these folks is, as the governor is saying, we got to talk about climate change, this is the long game right? This is going to be continued conversation as you and I have been having.\par \par \uc2 For Hyman -- Hyman, his family, it`s, where do I go? Where do I live? And how do I take back what I`ve lost? Thank god they`re safe, but their belongings, gone.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And again, as we discussed yesterday, water can end up condemning these houses. They may become inhabitable, we don`t know yet, because of mold and things like that. So, this is both a long-term problem, obviously in figuring out how to get that community ready for the new form of whether we get but also short-term, a lot of pain as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Shaq and Yasmin, thank you both as the southeast and Northeast continue to recover from Ida.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, out west California is still dealing with historic wildfires, two of which had been burning for more than three weeks straight now. They`ve become the first fires to burn from one side of the Sierra Nevada to the other.\par \par \uc2 The Caldor fire, which is near Lake Tahoe is already burned more than 330 square miles, is destroyed more than 650 homes, and it is just 29 percent contained. Evacuation orders remain in place for thousands of people, but officials are optimistic that the weather conditions are finally going to be on their side and could help to contain the fire, and they`ve been done giving some residents the green light to return home.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Dixie fire. Yes, that fire that has already just destroyed many towns, that continues to burn nearly two months after it was first sparked. It`s burned more than 1300 square miles. For a little comparison, that is bigger than the state of Rhode Island. The fire is more than 50 percent contained.\par \par \uc2 Wildfires in California have already burned more than 2800 square miles this year alone.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re going to talk to one of the President`s Top Economic Advisors as COVID uncertainty takes its toll on the August job numbers and threatens the White House agenda on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What we`re seeing is a recovery that`s durable and strong. The Biden plan is working. We`re getting results.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden earlier today defending a jobs report that showed a dramatic drop off in hiring last month, although there were some silver linings in the report itself particularly in the form of rising wages. The unemployment rate also fell to 5.2 percent but only 235,000 jobs were added in August compared to more than a million in July, and an estimate of about 750,000.\par \par \uc2 The president of course pinned the blame on the Delta variant. He`s not alone blaming Delta for this one. It all comes, though, as nearly 10 million people still out of work, who`ve relied on federal unemployment assistance, will lost those benefits when they expire this Monday which is Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now from the White House is Jared Bernstein. He is a member of the president`s Council of Economic Advisers. He of course was Biden`s chief economic adviser when Biden was vice president. He`s somebody a very familiar face around here.\par \par \uc2 Jared, thanks for coming on. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me just start with this job`s report. It`s clear Delta is the biggest impact here and whether it`s people holding back in some hiring decisions because they`re going to hold back opening offices, right. We know in some areas return to work has been delayed. That could have a cascading effect. You know, how uncertain does COVID make economic forecasting these days?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, we`ve had to deal with this uncertainly since we`ve got here and I think the record speaks volumes about the effectiveness of the president`s policies in this regard. You know, I was out here a month ago. I did the same thing I`m about to do with you. I didn`t just focus on the one million jobs in July. I focused on the three-month average because one month is not a new trend make.\par \par \uc2 And over the past three months, we`ve added 750,000 per month. Now this month`s downside miss is included in that afternoon. This president has a historical record by adding over four million jobs since he`s gotten here. There is no other presidential job record that matches that. So we have very strong momentum, tail winds at our back. But yes, headwinds from Delta are out there, too, much as you`ve described, then we see them in this data certainly as we`ve described particularly in some of the in-person services, leisure hospitality, restaurants.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it seems that this economy on one hand we know there`s a lot of pent-up demand. There is a lot of people with extra money ready to spent. Then there`s the other side of this pandemic, right, in this economy where you have people that are on the paycheck to paycheck, have been on unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Where do you -- do you still think we`re poised for some sort of post-COVID economic boom or should we not assume that that`s coming since we may be in an endemic?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So you raise a lot of important questions there. Let me try to unpack them. There`s no question that we`ve seen already booming growth in the first half of this year. In fact the growth rate of GDP in the first half of this year was the fastest in 40 years. And if you look at forecast going forward, even those that have been marked down from Delta, and I would say appropriately so, they`re still well above trend.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate as you just pointed out came down to 5.2 percent in August. That`s the lowest it`s been over this pandemic. So strong momentum pushing in the right direction, solid underlying pace of job growth. But you also raised the issue of what I think of is inequality and the fact that you can cite GDP and stock markets all day. The president made this point today in some comments.\par \par \uc2 But you have to make sure that these benefits are reaching folks in the bottom half of the income scale. And that`s where some of the measures like the child tax credit come in. This has helped reduced child poverty by half this year. That is a historic accomplishment and one that we have to keep going which takes us right to the reconciliation or Build Back Better bill, further investments to build on what we`ve -- the successes we`ve seen thus far.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you, does the top line number as much as getting some of those policies? You know, look, the $3.5 trillion numbers are a lot for some Democrats, particularly in the Senate and a few in the House to support. They don`t want to necessarily deficit spend and they don`t want to raise $3 trillion in taxes. Can you build back better with $2.5 trillion?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Look, I think the building back better agenda really speaks for itself. And I think it`s probably much more informative for people who are trying to evaluate this to put aside the trillions for a second, and think about what they pay for. So investments in childcare, in elder care, reducing the costs to families who face those costs and in many cases have a real barrier to getting into the job market because they can`t find accessible and affordable childcare.\par \par \uc2 Paying for universal pre-K of high quality. Two years of community college, free. Making sure that this child tax credit I mentioned, which is a big tax cut for families with kids, continues on after it`s rescue plan extension. Investing in climate, investing in battery storage technology, making sure that we`re investing in pushing back on the kind of climate change that`s so demonstrably hurting our economy and our communities.\par \par \uc2 These are all what those dollars go for. And I think that`s the way to understand these investments.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the issue of inflation. I accept the idea that this is a -- some of this is supply chain driven. Some of this is pent-up cash driven. There is a lot of factors in the inflation issue. But here`s a fact, Jared. Things are more expensive right now. I get that maybe this will work itself out in the next 18 months. But how do you deal with people who are feeling the effects of inflation right now? How do you answer those critiques?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It`s a totally fair question. And I know exactly what you mean, and I have certainly seen the polling we`ve done, some of it internally. One of the best things you can do is to make sure that this robust economy continues apace. And by the way, that really takes you to the health care agenda. Getting shots in arms and checks in pockets got us to where we are. And the fact is that even with the headwinds from Delta upon the land, we`re still seeing solid job creation and the decline in the unemployment rate down to 5.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 We have to continue to press on our public health agenda. At the same time, we have to do everything we can to help break these supply chain constraints. One area that we`ve been talking about just this past week is around housing where you`ve seen some very steep increases not just our own home prices but in rentals.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And we just put out a plan, which by the way is, again, this is in the reconciliation plan that will increase the number of housing units we predict by over two million. That is directly responsive to that supply constraint.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it, though, Jared? I mean, I don`t mean to push back on that part of it. I understand what you`re saying but just because you`re proposing more housing doesn`t produce more lumber. Right? I mean, the real issue here is the actual supplies to get these houses built now, I mean, or within reason.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, that is our -- that`s the inventory issue. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK, so let me say this, Chuck. We`re not going to stand up, you know, Joe Biden`s sawmill, right? I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: What we are going to do, and I talked about the agenda for Building Back Better just because that`s so important on the docket right now. What I didn`t mention was a set of much more fast-acting administrative actions that we also articulated.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:03]\par \par \uc2 I helped -- myself and many colleagues from the CEA wrote a blog on this, which you can find on our site, where we talked about measures that we think in the near term, over the next 12 to 18 months, can add over 100,000 units both in owned housing but especially in multifamily or rental units. That`s one of the things. But we`re also working on the ports. We`re working on some of the food price constraints.\par \par \uc2 So this is something that we are trying to do everything we can, you know, on the supply side of the economy. It`s not always the -- you know, again, we don`t produce these goods.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So that`s another constraint we have to be mindful of.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, there`s only -- some of these is limited and some of these you can argue it`s in the hands of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Jared Bernstein, a lot to unpack today. Appreciate having you on and sharing the administration`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: My pleasure, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we got the latest in the coronavirus situation in hard-hit Florida. Hospitals are filled to the max, thousands of nurses are in need of both help, and we need the staff.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This summer, the coronavirus surge in Florida has hit a tragic new milestone. On average 325 Floridians are now dying from COVID each day. 325 a day. It`s now a record high. Deaths of course are a lagging indicator. Another sign showed the state may finally be turning a corner on this fourth wave of COVID surge. The weeks of what was exponential growth new cases in the Sunshine State are finally decreasing.\par \par \uc2 Hospitalizations among adults, too, are finally on their way down. Now Florida hospitals are raising the alarm over the number of children, shockingly high number, that they are admitting for COVID just weeks after most schools reopened their door.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now for the state of COVID in Florida is, in Fort Lauderdale is our own Kerry Sanders.\par \par \uc2 So, Kerry, I know Florida does this an odd way that they release their numbers, their death numbers on COVID. They delay it, I guess they think it`s better for their PR, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 But this latest report we got showed just how devastating this surge has been.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, the release of the numbers is counter to what we see in other states. So, you know, the comparison becomes one of what you just said, 325 on average a day. But the number is shocking. It gets people`s attention, and really at this point Florida is looking at, as you noted, a slight decline but a real problem especially at the hospitals, and even among the pediatric cases which have really alarmed doctors.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to take a look at Palm Beach County. Over all right now, Palm Beach County has a deficit of hospital beds, 210 bed deficit. That is significant where they have already expanded where they can take in patients and treat them because remember you can`t just put somebody on a gurney in a hallway. You have to have the equipment there to monitor them so they have stretched themselves to the limit.\par \par \uc2 In the pediatric areas where they would have 42 beds specifically for pediatric patients right now there are 31 of those beds occupied. And so there is great concern about how this is all going to be handled. At the same time, as we see these numbers in the state of Florida, we also have, as we have seen elsewhere but certainly going on here in Florida, an exhausted medical staff, doctors and nurses.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Listen to what the mayor of Palm Beach County had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DAVE KERNER (D), PALM BEACH COUNTY, FLORIDA: The hospitals are very much full. As we sit here today in Florida, the \uc2 Florida Hospital Association\uc2  reports that there is a deficit of 8,000 nurses in the state. Again that`s a confluence of increased demand and burnout.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And to underscore, in Palm Beach County, 87 percent of the patients in the COVID wards are unvaccinated. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sadly not a shocking statistic these days as we`ve seen across the country during the surge.\par \par \uc2 Kerry Sanders, down in Fort Lauderdale for us. Kerry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, some cautious optimism for Democrats in California now after a lot of hand wringing, a lot of concern about lackluster early turnout, Governor Gavin Newsom may be turning a corner with less than two weeks to go until the ballots are all due in the recall election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As you see there the president just landed in New Orleans. That appears to be Senator Cassidy if I am looking at the back of his head correctly there. But the president just landing in New Orleans and we know he is going to get a tour of what the devastation has wreaked there.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now out west to California in their recall election. It is under way and after what was clearly a slow start that had Democrats spooked both in the state and nationally. The latest numbers seemed to signal that the Democratic governor there, Gavin Newsom, may be able to avoid getting ousted. "L.A. Times" analysis of voter data from Wednesday reported that nearly 4.7 million voters had already returned their ballots.\par \par \uc2 Voting in the recall is simple. Because of the pandemic every single registered voter in the state was mailed a ballot. So the breakdown of those ballots right now seemed to favor Newsom. 2.5 million Democrats have returned ballots while Republicans have sent back just over a million. And in recent polling we`ve seen one poll, \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 , only 39 percent of Californians now say they support the effort to recall Newsom compared to 58 percent who do not.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by "L.A. Times" and our what we like to refer to is our California politics guru, it`s Mark Barabak.\par \par \uc2 So, Mark, is this a simple case of Gavin Newsom found an opponent, that opponent was Larry Elder, and suddenly it engaged the California Democratic electorate? Is it that simple?\par \par \uc2 MARK BARABAK, LOS ANGELES TIMES COLUMNIST: Well, yes and no. You know, this was a slow-moving election in that, you know, take a place over the summer. Take a while for people to get engaged in it. But yes, I think the elevation, the rise of Larry Elder, the conservative talk radio host, as a foil, if you will, for Gavin, you know, helped him. I think Democrats are also going to get engaged. Like I said, this is a weird election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: It is taking place in September. In an off-year. So it`s going to take a while for people to pay attention. As you suggest, the poll this week, I mean, it was like if you get a case of whiplash, it was induced right around 9:00 Wednesday night, 12:00 Eastern, when the PPIC released this poll.\par \par \uc2 As you suggest, up and down the narrative is, boy, is Gavin in a lot of trouble, Democrats aren`t engaged, people aren`t paying attention, Republicans, recall voters were much more likely to turn out. You know, that poll has changed and again, as you know, we`ve got to be careful, we`re not going to say what`s going to happen, we don`t know what`s going to happen, but it definitely changed the narrative to use the popular word.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it did seem as if Newsom`s campaign plan at first was all about using Donald Trump as the bogeyman and that didn`t seem to move the needle. Larry Elder has. Any thoughts on why Elder has and Trump didn`t?\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: Well, again, I think, to some extent it`s a matter of timing and folks getting more engaged. I mean, Donald Trump has receded in the rearview mirror. They`re still referring to this as that, you know, referring to Larry Elder as a Trump Republican so it`s not as if he`s just gone away. I just think a lot of the Trump stuff is baked in to the calculation. You know, Larry Elder gave him an opportunity to go after a foil, somebody who is out there and to raise the prospect.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people in California don`t like Donald Trump, or at least a lot of them do not. Donald Trump is not going to be our next governor whatever happens on September 14th. There is a prospect that Larry Elder could be elected governor if the recall passes.\par \par \uc2 You know, for folks who (INAUDIBLE) the weird thing about this is that Gavin Newsom could get 49.9 percent of the California electorate wanting him to stay in office, if he falls short of 50 percent, whoever gets the most votes, and Gavin Newsom cannot be on the second half of the ballot, whoever gets the most votes would be governor.\par \par \uc2 Right now Larry Elder is 26 percent of that PPIC vote, so we want to take that to be the final result, we could have a governor, if the recall were to pass, elected by 26 percent of the vote. I mean, a fraction of a fraction of a fraction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Let`s project out.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 Let`s assume Gavin Newsom survives this recall. Is he stronger or weaker inside the state, inside the California Democratic Party? You know, does he made him look -- has he taken a few dings and it makes him look gettable or does him surviving make him stronger?\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: I think, you know, I did a column to this effect. I mean, you can argue that if he barely skates by, he looks weaker, but if you look at the history of California, we don`t very often turn out our incumbents. And realistically, what you`re going to look at is, let`s say again we`re all hopping on the supposition he survives, you know, if he does, it`s really tough to see how any Republican beats him.\par \par \uc2 You know, Republicans with the exception of Arnold Schwarzenegger haven`t won any political office in California in a couple of decades. So, you know, Newsom survives, you know, doesn`t face a real Republican threat. Republicans can`t beat him under these circumstances, it`s hard to see how they beat him next year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: The question then, does some Democrat run? You know, California has no shortage of egos, no shortage of rich people who think they should be governor, so will Gavin Newsom get a Democratic challenger? Not unlikely. Will that person prevail?\par \par \uc2 Really tough to see how a Democratic governor is defeated in a Democratic state. We should point out, this is a recall that is driven largely, not entirely but largely by Republicans, by conservatives, and Gavin Newsom has pretty decent recall numbers among Democrats. So, you know, if he survived this, and you`re a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: And you like the guy, you know, why not?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mark Barabak, a longtime columnist, a reporter and columnist for the "L.A. Times" and a longtime friend of mine in the political world. Mark, as always, sir, thanks for your expertise here.\par \par \uc2 BARABAK: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We will be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. It`s a big college football weekend for some of us. Let`s hope I`m in a good mood on Sunday morning. And speaking of Sunday morning, don`t miss MEET THE PRESS" on your local NBC station. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break. Go Canes. Roll back that tide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210903eh9300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for August 27, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Mike Memoli, Courtney Kube, Andrea Mitchell, Richard  Engel \par \uc2 8,502 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:18]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Later this hour, we expect White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki to hold her daily briefing as a situation in -- a situation in Afghanistan and the risks that are spiraling on the White House watch continue after the Pentagon publicly warned more specific credible threats along with the expectation of another potential attack.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon`s warning comes as a White House official tells NBC News that President Biden`s National Security Team has provided him with a similar assessment this morning in the Situation Room that yes, another terror attack in Kabul before we evacuate is likely.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon now says it believes it was a single suicide bomber yesterday, not two separate bombs that killed 13 U.S. service members. The attack has left 113 Afghans dead by the way, as well, according to the health ministry there, and many more were injured.\par \par \uc2 ISIS-K, a terrorist group based in Afghanistan, that region essentially claimed responsibility for the bombing as President Biden has vowed to exact revenge on those responsible.\par \par \uc2 And minutes ago, while meeting with the Israeli Prime Minister in the Oval Office, the President weighed in again, on yesterday`s horrific attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The mission they`re being performed is dangerous and is now, it`s come with a significant loss of American personnel. And whether it`s a worthy mission because they continue to evacuate folks out of that region, out of the airport. Evacuated more than 12,000 additional people out of the airport in the last 24 hours. I met with my commanders this morning and first thing in the morning, we got a detailed briefing by yesterday`s attacks and the measures they`re taking to protect our forces and complete the mission.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, the Pentagon says the U.S. military, as well as coalition forces, help evacuate more than 12,000 people yesterday even after that attack. More than 100,000 in total have been evacuated from the country since August 14.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s get the latest from what we know. I`m joined by Mike Memoli at the White House, Courtney Kube is at the Pentagon, Richard Engel is in Doha, Andrea Mitchell is here in our Washington bureau.\par \par \uc2 Mike, let me start with you. We know the President got another warning today. Do you get a sense that essentially, the goal is now to almost speed up the evacuation process at this point, or are we going to -- are we still going to try to get other Afghan allies out, or are we just really focused on Americans only right now?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, it`s been clear as you talk about speeding up the evacuations at this point that the conversation that we`ve been having for the most part over the past week about the consequences potentially, should America extend that August 31 deadline would be on the back end and as a result of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  but all along if you look closely at the comments from the President from other officials over the course of the last week, it`s actually been the scene that played out yesterday that had been their concern all along and why they had been so rapidly accelerating the pace leading up to yesterday.\par \par \uc2 It was the concern that the longer our presence was there, very much a conspicuous target at one location, or in several locations around one bigger location, the higher the risk of this attack was. And on Sunday, the President was explicit in saying just that.\par \par \uc2 And so, the idea now of getting as many Americans certainly, and that number has drawn down from the estimate of 6000 from the State Department earlier this week, indications it has now dropped under 1000 and perhaps even lower is the priority. But what I think we`re also going to be asking in the days ahead is, and the White House opened the door to this yesterday is, what does a non-military evacuation operation look like? Because the President said this, and the White House has indicated this as well, that they will be continuing efforts to get both Americans and Afghan partners out of Afghanistan for as long as it takes but they continue to be insistent that the military deadline of August 31 proceeds.\par \par \uc2 So that`s I think the biggest question as we really get close to what would be the end of the evacuation flights, if you -- especially that somebody who`s been listening closely to Courtney`s reporting all week, know that August 31 is the deadline but you have to backdate that time from there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you know, you set up Courtney quite well, Mike so I`m just going to pass that baton to Courtney. Courtney, explain what would the Pentagon`s role be in a non-military lead evacuation post-August 31? I got to assume they would have a hand in this.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: That`s actually the big question. But it`s funny when we talk to people here about it, they are pointing right over to the State Department and saying if there is a non- military evacuation or non-military efforts, then this would be more in the diplomatic realm. And I think that the -- there are several different ways that this could go, Chuck. The U.S. could work with allies, one that they`ve been taught using -- working with a lot -- with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has been the Qataris, as you well know, or there could be some efforts to work with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  directly on this.\par \par \uc2 There is now a precedent of the U.S. coordinating with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  on the ground there. Right now, it`s sort of a military-to-military level where we have these U.S. military commanders on the ground who are essentially dealing with their counterparts in the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  for safe passage of Americans getting to Kabul airport. So, there already is that precedent of working with them on the ground to get Americans safely out.\par \par \uc2 The question is, would the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  be open to any kind of continued coordination or cooperation after August 31, and is that safe for the U.S. to even try to undertake? And those are the kinds of things that government -- the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is having to deal with those questions right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:58]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have to say it, it almost looks comical the way we`re -- they want to pass the buck to each -- to each other, and I have to pass the baton to another. All right, Andrea, answered from the State Department`s point of view. So, if it`s a non-military-led evacuation starting on September 1, what does that look like? Does that mean State hires military contractors to help them with evacuations?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: There are no military contractors. I mean, the military will be gone so any extractions that are now taking place are being done by the military by helicopter. They can`t get people to the airport, the buses are being told to turn back with lists of people, many of whom are accepted at risk, identified Afghans, lawyers, judges, journalists, but they can`t get there, especially after the bombings because they`ve expanded the perimeter.\par \par \uc2 So, the State Department can only collect lists, turn them over to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , which has been going on because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  -- the military have actually been turning those names over as Courtney well knows. And there was some criticism of that but how else were you going to handle it if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is partnering in this unprecedented partnership outside the gates on getting people to come in, then the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are in charge? It`s their country now at least, it`s their city of Kabul.\par \par \uc2 If they don`t have total control over the country because there are some rivals -- there are some resistance groups in several of the provinces, and now the rival terrorist groups as well, but right now they`re in charge and they`ve brought in the Haqqani leader is, you know, who`s a leader of one of the worst of these rival terror groups, and he`s running security so that`s an unholy alliance. As far as an American -- the American perspective, there`s a $5 million head -- bounty on his head by the State Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as among the -- and a wanted list, can we have Haqqani.\par \par \uc2 But that said, it`s going to be up to them to help get people out if they think it`s in their interest. We already heard a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leader complaining about the brain-drain of losing the best and the brightest, but then saying to Richard Engel well, but we will let them go if they want to go. Well, will that happen after the U.S. leaves? And hopefully, the State Department is going to be left. State Department diplomats who have been stationed there want to stay. We don`t yet have a policy decision from the White House, from the National Security Council, talking about passing the baton as to whether they`ll be allowed to stay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Two weeks ago, I think Secretary Blinken seemed to indicate there was always going to be a presence there. I am curious to see what that answer is.\par \par \uc2 Richard, let me go over to you because I am curious what we`re watching here with this ISIS-K threat. And I saw your reporting earlier, you`re saying, you know, that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  now, that they are taking security a bit more -- maybe more seriously now, expanded the perimeter a bit. Is this the beginning of a new phase of a civil war inside of Afghanistan that word -- that we`re sort of collateral damage of because they`re -- if this is about bragging rights of who gets to say they kick the Americans out literally?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think the second part of your question is, is what we`re seeing? The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fought for 20 years against the United States and they got very good at guerrilla warfare, you and I saw them recently. They hold their weapons professionally, they know how to fight, and I think if you talk to the soldiers who have fought against them, they are -- they are ware and remain a very serious adversary.\par \par \uc2 ISIS is -- has always been a much smaller group in Afghanistan, it was always a much more vicious group. They were the ones who attacked civilians, they attacked hotels, wedding halls, they would carry out mass- casualty attacks. They really have no base of support among the population in Afghanistan. ISIS after all is an organization that is not indigenous to Afghanistan. It came from Syria, it came from Iraq and set up an offshoot over there when some extremely radical -- \uc2 Taliban\uc2  members thought that this was -- this was a good idea.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:11]\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is not going to tolerate their presence. They do not want a rival organization that could bring international condemnation, that could bring drone strikes, that could bring complications as they are now trying to move forward and set up their Islamic Emirate so they will be hunting them down. Whether they are able to do that, I think they probably can. they -- they`re very effective, they`re massive numbers in Kabul, they have good intelligence about the city, they`ve been operating in it for a long time.\par \par \uc2 A bigger problem, perhaps, is the number of foreign fighters who are coming in. There are a lot of foreign fighters who are seeing what happened, seeing this \uc2 Taliban\uc2  victory, seeing the Americans leaving now under fire, and they want to be part of it. And this was happening over the last several months where extremists have been coming in from Pakistan, from Uzbekistan, because they want to witness the final departure of the Americans.\par \par \uc2 And while the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  say they don`t want to accept foreign fighters, they don`t want ISIS, they particularly don`t want ISIS now stealing credit for their war, are they actually going to be able to recognize these people? Is a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighter who greets someone -- an Islamist coming over the border from Pakistan really get to see that person as a threat, or is he going to see him as a brother in the struggle?\par \par \uc2 So, while they say they`re going to stop these foreign fighters, I -- it will -- it will be difficult to know if they really recognize how extreme and dangerous some of the people coming in actually are, and do they see them as a threat as the U.S?\par \par \uc2 The other part about these civilian evacuations, it all about how that continues after the 31st. It all hinges on the airport. There is an effort to get the airport underway in Qatar and in -- and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would like the Turks to take over that airport. Turkey is having some reservations about it. So, there are ongoing negotiations to get that civilian airport open.\par \par \uc2 Once the civilian airport is open, then you just got to get people visas and then you have to get them safe passage. And the Taliban spokesman promised me, he said, if the people have visas, we`re going to give them passports, they can go. So, some of that evacuation process might be a visa issuing process. And some diplomatic pressures saying \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , you want to be recognized internationally? Then you got to allow people to travel.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll see, I would be -- I would be not surprised but it would be a very brave move but -- maybe a smart move for this State Department to leave some people behind if they want to establish a new chapter of relations and they want to maintain influence. But that`s up to the State Department security people to decide.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, Mike and Courtney, I know these are tough, dark days at the White House and the Pentagon, respectively. You`re both there right now. Mike, how would you describe morale and the mode?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: This is, I mean, been a really a punch to the gut certainly yesterday. I mean, you talk about a President who described himself yesterday as both heartbroken and outraged. You saw him -- this is a President who describes himself as a military parent and somebody who buried a son who he thinks died of cancer that was in part -- a consequence of his service in Iraq. So, he feels this on a personal level, they know the stakes politically as well, and that is why every move going forward in this question of military strikes, potentially against ISIS-K is being handled so delicately at this point, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Courtney, same question for you. Has -- I know that emotions sometimes don`t break through our stoic military leaders, but what do you seeing?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Now, there`s a lot of tired eyes here today and there`s a lot of -- there`s a much more solemn and somber tone. It`s been a very difficult couple of weeks here at the Pentagon. There`s no question about that. Morale has been low about the -- overall because of the reality that many of the people, these military interpreters who`ve been supporting the military for two decades in Afghanistan are not going to get out safely. And they have friends, in some cases, they consider them their brothers here in the building.\par \par \uc2 If -- at senior levels, there has been a lot of frustration in this building in the last few weeks, and yesterday was -- it was almost like the straw that broke the camel`s back. Things were -- it -- there is definitely a somber tone here today, Chuck, and I -- it`s even at the most senior levels.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I get a sense in a couple of months that we may see some people decide that this was too much for them. We`ll see these are -- these are tough moments for any individual to go through when you have this kind of responsibility. Mike, Richard, Courtney, and Andrea, thank you all for getting us started, and thank you for all your work this week. Richard, stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. Leaders in Congress way their roles in tackling the Afghanistan Fallout as our allies around the world are watching. I`ll speak with the Chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee next.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And later, the latest on the raging pandemic as the U.S. reports 2000 Coronavirus-related deaths in a single day. It`s the first time we`ve hit that bleak Mark since early March as the fourth wave of Delta continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The White House`s briefing all 100 senators today in the current situation in Afghanistan and committees for both Houses of Congress had vowed to conduct various investigations into the U.S. withdrawal and that was before what we saw happen yesterday. Among them is the House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman, Gregory Meeks. He joins me now. He`s actually called on the Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin, and Secretary of State to testify before his committee sooner, obviously rather than later.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to have you on. And I`ve been thinking about the different after-action reports and the oversight that the legislative branch needs to be conducting here, where do you begin? Because on one hand, the withdrawal and focusing on this, it`s a singular -- there`s a singular sort of decision-making process and how all that went, but there are so many other reasons why we were in a -- in a place where our only options were bad, bad and worse. So, how do you go about an oversight -- an oversight investigation with your committee, and where do you begin?\par \par \uc2 REP. GREGORY MEEKS (D-NY): Well, I think that we begin -- first we begin after we keep all of our American citizens, and SIVs, and (INAUDIBLE) from Afghanistan who supported us -- we keep our word. So, first, we do that. Then once we have finished that, I think that we`ve got to look at across the board and many through -- many administrations. Where you, know, what happened after we got Osama bin Laden? What did we do? Why do we stay? What we were looking to accomplish? And all the way up from the Obama administration through the Trump administration, and now through the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: I think we got to explore and look at all of those things from those times and the connection between -- you might even want to go back to the Bush administration also because we went into Afghanistan, we left, we went into Iraq, and we came back into Afghanistan, you know, what are the circumstances that lead one thing to the other, and what did we learn from it? I think that at some point that we`ve got to examine all of that, as well as the role that Congress should have or could have played, and that is looking at the authorization of the use of military force. We`re going to have to explore that also.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve been looking at as opposed to President`s utilizing one authorization for several different periods of time without having to come back to Congress, we are a separate and equal branch of government so we have to look at that also. There will be a lot of work to be done and our committee will start rather soon. You know, as I --as you indicated, we sent an invite to Secretary Austin, as well as, to state of -- the Secretary of State, Blinken and we got to look to start that sometime soon but we first want to make sure that we bring all of our folks all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have a hypothesis of how we sort of ended up in this race against time and these last 10 days. I mean, I think when you look at the last 10 days, and the sped-up evacuation process, here`s what it looks like. While it looks like we made a bunch of progress getting a lot of people out, it was a slapdash plan. You know, it was within the last 10 days that they`re reaching out to domestic airlines to acquire planes. What does that tell me is that it looks to me as if they didn`t have a contingency plan for the worst-case scenario. Is that where you begin?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: No. I think that would be quick took place, for me, was the surprise. I think everybody has indicated they were surprised how quick the Afghan security forces fell -- gave in literally. How quick it took for the President of the Afghan government, Mr. Ghani to turn away? And I could recall talking to him when he visited the United States just in June. And one of the questions that were asked of him was, would he stay? And would the Afghan security forces fight? And he assured us in Congress, the meeting that we had, that he would. So, it was not anticipated.\par \par \uc2 I think that timeliness, how quick that they -- that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  entered into Kabul and took over from province after province and so, therefore, that sped up a lot of other things, of which people thought that we had time to do. So, should we have anticipated the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  taking over as quickly as they did? Well, that`s a question that we need to ask a number of different individuals. But, you know, for me, it is important that in the beginning, we were talking about evacuating 60 to 70,000 people. That`s what we thought that we had to do. It is of note that we`re now well over 100,000, and that`s the goal. The goal to get our folks out and to keep our word.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your level of concern with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in charge? Not necessarily, I guess it`s clear, not fully in control but in charge of Afghanistan. What`s your level of concern about the stability and future of Pakistan now? And the reason I asked is, for obvious reasons, that`s a country that has nuclear weapons and I don`t think anybody wants to see that fall into \uc2 Taliban\uc2  hands.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: I think that`s a situation that we must keep our eye on. It`s a very serious consideration. It`s questions that have been asked and will continue to be asked. I know that it is something that members of my committee have talked about even visiting Pakistan once the situation is stable in Afghanistan. It`s time for Pakistan, and we need Pakistan to step up.\par \par \uc2 And I know that there are also security measures that are put in place by our Department of Defense. I know that there`s a number of individuals in the State Department are also concerned. So, something that is not -- that we`re not blindsided by. And there are dialogues, conversations, and plans that`s been made in that regard but we`ve got a hope that the Pakistanis -- but the first thing is that make sure that Pakistanis will be able to stand up and so that dialogue and conversation must take place immediately.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about diplomacy with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . First of all, do you think every effort should be made to leave a diplomatic corps in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: Yes. I think that we`ve got to continue to engage. We`re not just completely deserted Afghanistan, we`re saying that the military option after 20 years, after a trillion dollars being spent, that`s not going to give us any kind of outcome of work, anything, that will be different for the relationship between our two countries.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:10]\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: It`s clearly that they would not -- that the Afghan security forces for not stand and fight for themselves. So now, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is end. They made certain commitments. We don`t trust them, I don`t trust them, they say they`re going to be different, or they are different from where they were 20 years ago with reference to women and girls, I think that they want to see that they have some sense of the credibility of something of that nature, you know, dependent upon of foreign countries as far as their economy is concerned, so we still will have some leverage on them. So, we`ll see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m reminded some when I look at it to date, you know, we didn`t like the government that was moving into Vietnam some years -- many years ago but still now today, we have a good relationship with that -- with that government, we doing trade and having other you know, other kinds of business with them. My hope, I don`t know, but I continue to hope that we can -- in the future have a better -- have a better cause for the -- for us, for our relationship in Afghanistan, and for the Afghanistan people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Gregory Meeks, the chair of one of the more powerful and important Committees in Congress, the House Foreign Affairs Committee, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective and we look forward to those hearings, sir.\par \par \uc2 Up next. We`re going to continue this conversation on Afghanistan and dig into the National Security dangers the U.S. now faces, as well as mistakes that many administrations may have made. I`ve got our former Defense Secretary Leon Panetta and a former National Security Adviser and Ambassador to the United Nations, John Bolton. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:19]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Security concerns are reaching a fever pitch as the fallout continues over what was in many ways President Biden`s darkest day so far in office. In remarks yesterday, he acknowledged that the Afghanistan withdrawal was always a risky highly dangerous mission. White House officials say he was warned by his National Security Team again this morning of another potential attack in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 A least a lot of folks are wondering about America`s current quagmire in the region. What went wrong or what happens next. I`m going to have a little bit of both of those conversations here.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone who helped oversee the mission to get Osama bin Laden, former secretary of Defense and former CIA director under President Obama, Leon Panetta.\par \par \uc2 Leon, it`s good to see you. So let me start with this. I`m guessing you`ve been thinking about this a lot, even more so over the last 24 hours. When you look back, over the last 10 years, basically since we got bin Laden, what do you think you`ve gotten wrong about the situation in Afghanistan -- and I say you, you and others -- that maybe you thought was going to go a certain way that didn`t.\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA, FORMER SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Well, Chuck, you know, we got into Afghanistan for two basic missions. Obviously one was to go after bin Laden and al Qaeda. Those that were involved in the attack on 9/11. And the second was trying to prevent Afghanistan from becoming a haven for terrorism again. And we were successful at the bin Laden mission. And I`m proud of having been a part of that effort.\par \par \uc2 I think once we got bin laden and once we undermined al Qaeda, we probably should have taken a step back and looked at just exactly what our mission should be. We needed a clearer mission, I think we needed a clearer strategy, and we needed an exit strategy as well. But we never really focused on that. We just basically tried to continue what was kind of a hit and miss effort to try to get Afghanistan to be able to secure and govern themselves and that obviously didn`t work very well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. It felt like -- let me ask you this, do you think it was -- do you think we ended up in this sort of de facto containment strategy which is all, it seemed like, looking back, is what it was, which is let`s just keep the lid on things because nobody wanted to be the president, whether it was Obama, Trump, and Biden, that they all got talked out of it or wanted to keep punting the final decision because it was inevitable it would be a chaotic end and nobody wanted to be the one to make that call?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Well, it`s a tough call to make and, you know, here we had Afghanistan. We`d spent a tremendous amount of time fighting in Afghanistan. Lives were lost in Afghanistan. I deployed intelligence officers there, I deployed men and women in uniform there. Obviously we thought some progress was being made with regards to Afghanistan. The military did put up some pretty good fights, but they needed U.S. support in order for that to happen.\par \par \uc2 So no one really took the time very frankly to consider what a transition would look like under the circumstances. How would we be able to kind of maintain stability, or containment, if you will, in Afghanistan, and at the same time be able to draw down so that we wouldn`t just simply get stuck there? That formula, that chemistry that was so important, never really came together and that`s what led to the chaos that we saw these last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s talk about the near term here. We`re going to get a real test of President Biden`s theory that you can basically deal with terrorist outposts in Afghanistan over the horizon, and I guess similar to what we`ve been doing in Syria and in parts of Africa, how logistically possible is that with Afghanistan? With Syria and in Africa, we`ve got plenty of staging areas that can get you within close proximity. Can you say the same for Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: The bottom line is this, you know, we can leave a battlefield of the war on terrorism, but frankly we cannot leave the war on terrorism. We still face terrorists around the world, whether it`s al Qaeda, whether it`s \uc2 Boko Haram\uc2 , whether it`s Al-Shabbat. And we`re going to have to deal with them hopefully with using counterterrorism operations. And the same thing, frankly, is true for Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 You know, we can`t just say the hell with Afghanistan at this point because the fact is the president yesterday said we have to go after ISIS. We have to go after those who are involved in this attack. That`s going to require counterterrorism operations to go after them.\par \par \uc2 The same thing is going to be true with al Qaeda. I think the likelihood is that al Qaeda will reestablish itself with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And we`re going to have to go after al Qaeda. So the work in Afghanistan is not finished. We are going to have to find ways to go after terrorists wherever they are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that, but what is -- I would guess compare the challenge of that in Afghanistan to how we`re able to do operations in Syria. I mean, what is extra challenge in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: The biggest challenge in Afghanistan is that it`s over the horizon issue where we need to have bases nearby that give us the opportunity to be able to deploy into Afghanistan. That`s not easy. We`re having a hell of a time trying to find bases from which to operate. Secondly, the ability to be able to go in and hit a target, you need good intelligence on the ground.\par \par \uc2 When we were operating in Afghanistan, we had great intelligence. We were working with the Afghans. We have good military intelligence cooperation and the counterterrorism operations. They were working very effectively. But to do this from over the horizon, outside of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , is going to be very difficult.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s the line when you`re dealing with somebody like ISIS-K who seems to be as intent -- you know, when is a terrorist organization a threat to the United States and when is it us getting involved in a potential civil war? What is that line?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: It`s pretty simple. When terrorists attack and kill our people. When they threaten to attack our country, they are an enemy, they`re an adversary, and we`ve got to stop them. And ISIS-K is an adversary that is intent on attacking Americans and intent on attacking our country. And for that reason that we have to strike back at them.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, ISIS almost captured Baghdad when President Obama said we had to go back in and work with the Iraqis to basically stop them from establishing a caliphate in Iraq. We did. And so we`re going to have to continue to go after ISIS. We can`t just sit back and pretend that that problem is over. It remains a real threat to our national security and the president of the United States, his first responsibility as commander-in- chief, is to protect our country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leon Panetta, former Defense secretary and former head of the CIA, thank you.\par \par \uc2 By the way we expect the White House daily briefing to begin at any moment. We`re going to take you there live as soon as the press secretary Jen Psaki starts taking questions there. But when we come back, we`re going to talk with John Bolton, former National Security adviser to Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We expect White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki to brief reporters in a few minutes where of course she`s expected to field a number of questions about the current situation in Afghanistan. But I want to bring in another National Security expert, somebody else who`s been involved in various policy decisions over this war over -- off and on over the last 20 years. It`s John Bolton. He of course was the National Security adviser at one time to President Trump and he was the ambassador to the U.N. during the Bush administration.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Bolton, thanks for coming on. Let me start with basically the same question I asked Leon Panetta. Have you thought about what you may have gotten wrong about Afghanistan over the last 20 years?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOLTON, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, I think one of the principal mistakes that clearly has been made is that political leaders did not explain adequately to the American people why it was in our national security interest to have a continuing U.S. Military presence in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 As an insurance policy to make sure the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  did not come back to power and provide safe haven for ISIS, al Qaeda, or many other terrorist groups, and also threaten the stability of Pakistan next door with the risk of a terrorist government there taking control and gaining access to that country`s nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 For that strategic reason it has been valid for 20 years since the day that al Qaeda was first overthrown. But I think it got lost in the noise of that nation building in Afghanistan. And now people say after 20 years and billions and billions of dollars spent that nation building didn`t succeed, so we have no strategic interests and that`s simply false. We had a presence in Afghanistan to protect American civilians. Not to do charity work for the Afghans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So in hindsight the original sin maybe then invading Iraq? Without the war in Iraq, wouldn`t we have had a more principled focus on Afghanistan and perhaps the political will to do what you suggested?\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: Well, there were a lot of administrations involved in making mistakes in both Afghanistan and Iraq. I don`t think Iraq distracted from Afghanistan. I think in fact it was the Obama administration`s decision to get out of Iraq in 2011, a decision which I fear may look very much like the Biden decision -- the Trump-Biden decision to get out of Afghanistan in 2021, which brought ISIS into such prominence in Syria and Iraq, requiring the Obama administration to go back into Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Look, the comparison among these difference situations, I think in a very short interview, obscures more than illuminates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: But the central question that you keep -- you have to keep asking is what is America`s strategic interest at any given time? And if you ask about Afghanistan today, are we safe with this withdrawal underway than we were beforehand? The answer, I think, is unambiguously no.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I understand your hesitance to -- you don`t want to conflate Iraq and Afghanistan. But the reason I ask you is this. We can debate a lot of things about Iraq, but the fact of the matter is, our reasoning for going into Iraq turned out not to be true, and it created a credibility problem with the American problem. And the credibility problem, I might argue, is what made them sour and have a lack of patience in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I guess that`s what I -- it`s like, can you really sort of sit there and just look at a tactical decision in Iraq and not look at the basic premise for why we went being wrong and that that ended up impacting the credibility with the American public about Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 Whatever you want to say about President Trump and President Biden about this decision, they both were reflecting the will of the country.\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: I don`t agree with that point but let me address the first point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: The mistake made in saying that we had a nation building, democracy building mission, in both Iraq and Afghanistan was a mistake. Both countries enjoyed a lot of collateral benefits because of our presence there for strategic reasons, but I think what the American people believe in and understand and would support is the deployment of American force to advance clear American national interests.\par \par \uc2 Not for what some have called international nannyism. So to the extent our mission shifted or was explained as having shifted to those objectives, I think it was a mistake. Now let me come back to what the American opinion is. Number one in foreign policy and national security, I think that leaders have to lead the public opinion polls, not follow them, so what the polls say doesn`t much determine for me in any case.\par \par \uc2 But just recently as this has been happening, there was a Politico-Morning Consult poll that came out on August 16th having been conducted the weekend that the Afghan army was collapsing, and it asked the question, do you support withdrawal? 49 percent said yes, down significantly from their prior poll in April. But 49 percent said yes. Then they asked the question, and if that means \uc2 Taliban\uc2  comes back to power, what`s your view? Support for withdrawal fell to 38 percent, opposition rose to 45 percent.\par \par \uc2 Then they asked, well, what happens if our withdrawal means al Qaeda could reestablish itself in Afghanistan? And support for withdrawal fell to 35 percent, opposition rose to 48 percent. It was only one poll. But the insight is clear. If you ask the kind of freebie question, hey, do you want to get out of Afghanistan, it`s an easy yes. If you say, and here are the costs to that decision, at least as reflected in that poll, the American people made the correct strategic choice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. Look, Ambassador, I think there`s no question that we`ve had -- I`d argue four presidents in a row that have not -- certainly the three previous have not articulated a rationale in Afghanistan like that.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this final question in terms of President Trump. When you came on board, a lot of people were like, wait a minute, you`re hawkish, Trump is a dove, and I remember you saying, look, you`re going to be there to give strategic advice to him. What advice did you give him about how to withdraw? He wanted a withdrawal, you didn`t. Did you give him advice on how you would do it even though you were against it?\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: No, I gave him advice on how to maintain a minimal military presence. Below what I would have preferred but enough to hold on to support a lot of other activities that were also being undertaken in Afghanistan. In order to avoid what I thought would have been the catastrophic consequences of complete withdrawal. I do think there`s a distinction between the decision to withdraw and the way the withdrawal is being executed. I don`t think there`s any question.\par \par \uc2 But I think fundamentally at bottom here what the Trump administration did, the Trump-Pompeo negotiating model, delegitimized the Afghan government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: The negotiations were conducted essentially only with \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , and by delegitimizing the Afghan government, in effect almost by derecognizing it, you told the army, which was supposed to protect that government that its efforts were futile, so there`s no real surprise to me that the army collapsed when Biden finally pulled the plug.\par \par \uc2 TODD: John Bolton, former National Security adviser to former President Trump and former ambassador to the United Nations, thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 BOLTON: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As we await the start of today`s White House briefing, up next we have some major COVID deja vu. We`ve got an update on some alarming new virus, coronavirus related cases and deaths are spiking nationwide but there are some good news. The rate of growth is slowing. Maybe that`s a sign that maybe we`re peeking. We shall see. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Well, the largest school districts in Florida who`ve been fighting Governor Ron DeSantis` mandate against mask mandates got a pretty big win this morning as a state judge ruled that Governor DeSantis cannot punish school districts for enacting their own localized mask mandates.\par \par \uc2 This news comes as the virus continues to surge in Florida and across the U.S. In fact, we`ve reported our deadliest single day of COVID in nearly six months yesterday with more than 2,000 deaths. The last time that we had a 2,000 plus death day on a single day was back in March.\par \par \uc2 So to lay all this out, this day in the pandemic is worse by every single metric we track than it was one year ago before 170 million people were fully vaccinated and of course the millions who have chosen not to get vaccinated even though it`s (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 With that we`ve got the White House press briefing with Jen Psaki, considering what we have going on today, we think we ought to take a listen to some of the questions and answers that she has going on. So let`s take you to the briefing.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: --million shots in arms, 1.1 million is the highest single day total shots since July 3rd which is a good sign. So far in August, we have gotten 12.5 million first shots into arms. That`s already two million more first shots than in all of July with several days still left to go to add to that total.\par \par \uc2 Importantly, we`ve now hit a major milestone in our effort to vaccinate adolescents at a particularly important time as people go back to school. Fifty percent of 12 to 17-year-olds now have at least their first shot. Obviously more work to do, but a positive step forward.\par \par \uc2 Also, I wanted to note that today the Department of Health and Human Services Secretary Javier Becerra announced $85 million in funding to improve mental health services for our nation`s children and youth which is of course a priority for the Biden-Harris administration as a part of our push to get kids back to school safely and address the impact of the pandemic on them.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, he announced the \uc2 Health Resources and Services Administration\uc2  is making 24 awards, totaling $10.7 million in funds from the president`s American Rescue Plan to expand pediatric mental health care access by integrating telehealth services, for example actions like technical assistance for pediatric care providers to diagnose, treat and refer children and youth with mental health conditions and substance use disorders.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:25]\par \par \uc2 And this will also -- the \uc2 Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration\uc2  is also granting 29 awards totaling $74.1 million to enhance mental health services for school-aged youth. So as an example of that, \uc2 Project AWARE\uc2 , which stands for Advancing Wellness and Resilience in Education will use its funding to help raise awareness with mental health issues for young people and train school personnel.\par \par \uc2 Also I wanted to note that today we`re announcing that President Biden has directed the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to serve as the lead federal agency coordinating across the federal government to relocate evacuated Afghans to the United States. Already DHS has been working closely with agencies across government including our military, diplomats, intelligence community and law enforcement professionals and many others to ensure that all Afghans are screened and vetted prior to being allowed into the United States.\par \par \uc2 Additionally all evacuees will continue to undergo extensive COVID-19 and public health precautions including immediate COVID-19 and offered vaccines upon arrival. DHS will also coordinate with numerous other federal agencies to ensure that relocated Afghans have access to medical care and sufficient support to enable their successful resettlement in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The president is grateful for the critical role the dedicated workforces across the federal government including our military servicemen and women, diplomats, our intelligence and law enforcement professionals and many others are in implementing this effort.\par \par \uc2 Finally this afternoon, the president and FEMA administrator Deanne Criswell will host a call with the governors of Louisiana, Alabama and Mississippi to discuss ongoing preparations for Tropical Storm Ida which is expected to make landfall on the Gulf Coast as a major hurricane on Sunday. As some of you may know, Sunday is the 16th anniversary of Hurricane Katrina which caused widespread devastation, claimed more than 1800 lives and displaced so many families.\par \par \uc2 The FEMA administrator was in Louisiana yesterday. She met with Governor Bel Edwards and the state emergency management director to discuss how they are mobilizing to prepare for what could be a very dangerous storm hitting a region that has been heavily impacted by the Delta variant. And the president will be closely tracking this and receive updates throughout the course of the weekend. And with that, we`ll get questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: You put out a statement not too long, (INAUDIBLE), the question is pretty sober language and (INAUDIBLE) president talking (INAUDIBLE) his national security adviser that the impact is likely. I know you`re limited, but is there any more specificity you can offer on what that threat is, and secondly, what does it mean for the evacuation? Is the intel in anyway also? And changes that need to be made to limit or restrict the ability to continue or gets much done while the mission through August 30th?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure. Well, I think many of you may have seen the statement I put out this morning, but just to highlight the reference I believe Armar was making, what I conveyed is the statement was that the National Security Team and the president met with this morning, advised the president and vice president that another terror attack in Kabul is likely, and they are taking maximum force protection measures at the Kabul airport and in the surrounding areas with our forces as a result.\par \par \uc2 I will note that the Department of Defense also just gave a briefing this morning and I will echo some of the descriptions that they offered. The threat is ongoing. And it is active. Our troops are still in danger. That continues to be the case everyday that they are there. This is the most dangerous part of the mission. This is the retrograde period of the mission. And what that means is that this is the period of time when the military, commanders on the ground and forces begin to move not just troops home, but also equipment home.\par \par \uc2 And that is often a very dangerous part of any mission, but in this case they`re also doing that while there is an ongoing and acute threat from ISIS-K. So that is what they are facing. I would note, since you gave me the opportunity that the military made clear to the president that they are committed to continuing this mission, to getting -- to saving lives, to evacuating more people from the country over the coming days and completing their mission by the 31st.\par \par \uc2 What it will also mean as they move to this retrograde phase is that there will be a reduction of numbers over the next couple of days and we`ve been putting those numbers out to all of you twice a day so that you can see how that -- how we`re evacuating people out of the country. Those numbers will go down in the next couple of days and you should anticipate that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210828eh8r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for August 26, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Andrea Mitchell, Richard Engel, Kelly O`Donnell,  Courtney Kube, Raf Sanchez, Jack Jacobs, Clint Watts \par \uc2 10,992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:07]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Well, don`t worry, Andrea, you`re not going very far. We`re going to come back to you in a moment. Welcome back to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd and we`re continuing following the breaking news out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 A pair of explosions have rocked Kabul and thrown the already chaotic U.S. evacuation efforts into some turmoil just five days before President Biden agreed to a deadline to get all Americans out. U.S. officials tell NBC News that at least three Marines were wounded in this attack just outside the Kabul airport. Hospital officials in Kabul tell us they`re treating about 60 people injured and that at least six people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 It was Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby, who Tweeted that there were two blasts and what he calls a complex attack. One of those explosions was at the Abbey gate of the airport, the other was nearby at a hotel. Kirby was supposed to address reporters this morning at the Pentagon`s daily briefing. Obviously, that briefing was postponed by the events in the ground, as was the White House -- White House`s COVID Task Force briefing.\par \par \uc2 The President`s meeting with Israeli Prime Minister Naftali Bennett has been delayed but not yet canceled. The administration says the President has been briefed on the situation. It`s our understanding right now. He`s in the Oval Office after going in and out of the Situation Room.\par \par \uc2 A Taliban spokesman is telling NBC News that at least 13 people were killed including some children. And while it`s unclear who was responsible for the attack to U.S. intelligence officials, say they are operating on the assumption that it was the Afghan affiliate of ISIS that is behind it. And of course, that is what our own intelligence officials had been warning about and worrying about.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to go to the Pentagon, check in with our own Courtney Kube in just a moment. Richard Engel joins me now though, from Doha, Qatar.\par \par \uc2 So, Richard, this was exactly -- the threat was out there for a few days. This specific threat from ISIS-K was clearly something the administration was worried about, publicly warned about and here it is. What it is -- this obviously is setting back evacuation efforts. Could this essentially end them?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: No, it couldn`t end the evacuation. It shouldn`t end the evacuation. It is not an attack of that kind of magnitude. It didn`t impact the runways, didn`t impact the planes. There are three main gates into the airbase. What this was, was a horrific attention-grabbing suicide attack, a complex attack, at least one suicide attack, and another bomb right outside the gate. And all indications are that this was the work of ISIS, or at least of a member of ISIS.\par \par \uc2 And as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rolled into Kabul, they set free a lot of prisoners or prisoners in the chaos were able to set themselves free, break out of jail, and these are enemies of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . And for the last week or so, they have been roaming free, they`ve had no place to go and it seems that at least one or possibly more attackers took the opportunity today to carry out the attack. And what witnesses are saying and security sources in Kabul are saying is that 13 people were killed, including several \uc2 Taliban\uc2  guards who were outside the gate managing the flow of traffic, and these coordinates reporting three U.S. Marines who were injured.\par \par \uc2 Just to sort of bear down a little bit on the -- of the geography of where this took place because the area around military bases is sometimes quite complicated. You have the airbase, we describe it as the military side of the Kabul airport, it is a military base. It is surrounded by walls, there are three main gates into it.\par \par \uc2 By one of the gates, there is a hotel called the Baron hotel. We`ve stayed there, other journalists stayed there, we were just there a few days ago. The Baron hotel was built primarily for contractors because it is so close to the airport. That way they could get on and off of the airport quickly, safely.\par \par \uc2 From the Baron hotel, there was about a 200-yard corridor that leads to the Abbey gate, one of the three gates onto the base, and that corridor is flanked with high concrete barriers. And within the corridor, there is a deep sewage canal -- drainage canal. And perhaps using that training -- drainage canal, a suicide bomber was able to get into that corridor and detonate an explosive.\par \par \uc2 And the images that we have seen on social media are absolutely horrific. People -- bodies thrown into the sewage canal, some of them -- some of the dead bodies, some people who are clearly gravely injured, other people pulling them out of the -- of the wastewater and then ambulances coming and being brought in to take the wounded away and to try and treat them.\par \par \uc2 So, it will slow down the pace of the evacuations because one gate, I wouldn`t say it`s totally operation -- dis -- totally taken out of operation but it is certainly caused a degree of chaos, they`ll be trying to step up security. But they did have a specific threat and it seems like that specific threat was carried out. And if it was ISIS, it`s ISIS trying to put themselves back on the map to do exactly what we`re doing right now, to get them back into the conversation.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:22]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I know. Make them -- make them a major cog when perhaps they shouldn`t be treated as one. But let me ask you this, Richard. We know that the U.S. military is in charge of securing the airport, they`re sort of the base part of the airport. Does that security perimeter -- so is this within the U.S. security perimeter, or is the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in charge of security closer to the hotel?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  do all the security. They control Afghanistan, it is now the Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan. It is the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  country except for a tiny little mountainous area that is putting up some say heroic, some say a doomed resistance movement, but they -- the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  control the country. They are patrolling the outer perimeter. They are controlling checkpoints throughout the city. They are limiting people to get to the airport. They are trying to find ISIS fighters because they don`t want this kind of thing to happen because they want the process to move smoothly, and Americans to leave.\par \par \uc2 At the gate itself, there is a handover and we saw that ourselves. I was at that airport earlier this morning. You drive -- and it is a very bizarre thing to witness. We saw \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters in their guns, in their -- in their traditional dress, some in American-style uniforms, you pass them, a few yards later, we saw troops from the 82nd Airborne and it is a stark contrast. And I must say the troops I saw if you can see the expressions on their face, this was before the attack, they were not pleased with the situation. They were not pleased to be doing this evacuation, working a few yards away from their former enemies, evacuating the people who worked with American troops and their allies, and getting them out of the country.\par \par \uc2 So, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  control the outer perimeter, Americans in the inner perimeter and the Americans are now trying to get everybody out. And then this disruption today, this horrific -- horrible incident at one of the gates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Richard Engel in Doha for us. Richard, thank you. Let me go over to Kelly O`Donnell at the White House. We`ve got Raf Sanchez in London., Andrea Mitchell, as I promised you, has stuck around is here in the Bureau with me. NBC News Military Analyst Colonel Jack Jacobs is with me. And National Security Analyst Clint Watts.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, I want to go over to you for a second. We know this is basically canceled all -- anything else today, or this is the focus of the administration. This is a disruption in our evacuation plans, obviously and I`m sure we don`t have the answer to this, but is essentially this National Security meeting -- constant meeting that`s happening with the President, it`s about now what? Like, what do we do now? Can we still meet this deadline? Do we try to extend? How do we secure the area? Do you have any insight of what the discussions are that are going on right now?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, the word of the day here so far is fluid, that everything related to the President is fluid. Meaning who he`s meeting with, what his schedule looks like, will we see him publicly? All of that. As to your question about the calculation the President needs to make.\par \par \uc2 An event like what is playing out with all its glory deadly awfulness is exactly one of the factors that the President said needed to be part of his decision-making process about evacuations. The possibility, which at that time was just a threat stream of warnings, could that disrupt evacuations? And what would they need to do to complete the mission of securing all Americans who want to leave Afghanistan? The number still at this point is somewhere, roughly a thousand, not all of them determined to be American citizens who actually want to leave but that`s the latest numbers we have there. And then how does that all factor into the deadline of August 31?\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve been told the President has been briefed on the contingency plans that had already taken place before today`s events. He`s spent an extensive amount of time in the Situation Room with his Defense Secretary, his Secretary of State, the National Security team. We`re told he`s in the Oval Office now. And the real challenge for the President is when this began, this evacuation began and there was the initial chaos when the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over and Kabul fell, have civilian deaths at the beginning, then the administration was very proud of the ramp-up of evacuations that were being done safely. The President even noting, no one had died.\par \par \uc2 And then today, the concern about the threats being there, urging Americans to leave and then the events that have unfolded where there are clearly lots of injuries, at least three Americans included in the injury tally, we don`t know how much further that will go and if the worst -- the worst news could come.\par \par \uc2 So, now we`re back to a situation where the President now has a different dynamic. Does he need additional force protection? Can they work with the troops that are on the ground? And can he evacuate all the embassy personnel and all of the others in time? We don`t have the answers but imagine having to work through those decisions with the contingencies and with the very difficult situation right now. The President has a real burden on his plate. He made some of these choices, and some of these are being controlled by outside events. Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:39]\par \par