ybody out, who`s remained there, particularly the troops.\par \par \uc2 As you withdraw troops, the force on the ground gets smaller and smaller and less capable of defending itself. Which means that the detachment left and contact, those soldiers, sailors, airmen, and Marines who were left behind at the very end who go out on the last plane are most at risk, how you`re going to protect them? Protect them with airstrikes and Overwatch and all the rest of that.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, some credence is going to have to be given to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to make sure that they protect our troops. What`s been demonstrated today is that they can`t do it. They`re certainly not going to be able to guarantee safety. Now, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would surely like to have us out as quickly as possible. They also need money.\par \par \uc2 There`s an argument -- and we would like to have a little bit more time to make sure that we get as many people out as we possibly can. There`s an argument that says that there is a deal here. For like Andrea says, maybe an extension of a couple of days to make sure that we get as many people out as we possibly can and the exchanges, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  get some of the money or all the money that`s currently locked up and sequestered by a Western financial institution.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not that`s actually going to happen, remains to be seen. But at the end of the day, some Americans got to turn the lights out on the American presence there and that`s going to be the most dangerous time.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know. It sounds like we`re having to pay a ransom to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , right? I mean, that`s what -- that sounds like, and it`s an uncomfortable position for any American President. Is it -- no matter what you`re trying to do here.\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: No, I think we`ve already -- we`ve already paid a ransom to them -- we`ve already paid a ransom to them in agreeing that 31 August is 