 so they`re in charge but not necessarily in control.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Chuck, that`s part of it. I think that we have the sense that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  suddenly magically took over all of Kabul and are in control, and not meeting any resistance.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 Remember most of the people in Kabul did not expect the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to take over that quickly and so they have done this in such a short period of time. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  don`t have probably as good of sensors or nose or intelligence capabilities right now inside Kabul as they do other parts of the country. So they`re really, you know, probably trying to figure out what`s going on. They`re trying to secure as many areas as possible.\par \par \uc2 This is, as you can see right there from the footage, this is not the same as the American military presence that was there in Kabul and now to Bagram Air Force Base. And so we`re relying on a group that might say, I want to protect the air base, and we`ll let you go, just get out by the 31st, but maybe has no capability to do that. You know, some things to think about is, remember when the U.S. took Baghdad back in 2003.\par \par \uc2 There was a brief lull but it wasn`t long after that that U.S. forces could not control all of Baghdad. They did not have intelligence. You start seeing insurgencies pop up. I think you`ll see a similar phenomenon in a lot of these Afghan cities, where the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have varying degrees of control, they understand the networks better or worse. And in fact it might be our own intelligence capabilities and contacts or our Western allies, that Five Eyes partners as they would say like the U.K. who still have really good sensors, really good human intelligence in a lot of these networks, will have to rely on a combination of factors.\par \par \uc2 And so we can`t just take at face value the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  saying they`ll do it, they have alternative motives. But let`s also be honest about their capability to rule and govern and know everything going on in a town they just came into two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kelly O`Donnell, I want to go back, you know, to the president`s own words which I know we`re going to get played for a lot of folks, on what he said last week. But this is complicated. I mean, on one hand, our operation was disrupted. American service members are dead because of this. Yet there is no identifiable, quote-unquote, "enemy" here. What are the calculations the White House is dealing with here?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, certainly the president has to deal with how many American lives have been put at risk and taken by this attack, how do you respond to that, so certainly dealing with the immediate concern of force protection and those evacuees who are in the process. How to deal with that. That`s one set of concerns. And then the president has said ISIS-K is a sworn enemy of the United States and \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and so how to strike back at that?\par \par \uc2 It is not lost on any of us that when Americans died 20 years ago, it was U.S. forces that went into Afghanistan in search of Osama bin Laden and al Qaeda. This is an attack of a much smaller scale but certainly it`s impactful and it`s going to change the lives of a number of American families and it could alter sort of the dynamic on the ground there. So these are complicated issues.\par \par \uc2 What the president has been clear about is that he did not believe it was in the long-term national interest of the United States to be with a military presence in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So now the question is, do you strike back in some targeted way? We know that of course there are CIA assets, there are special operators in the region, there has to be some intelligence as you mentioned earlier. This intelligence appeared to be spot on, so much so they were giving Americans through phone calls and e-mails and warnings, and our allied partners saying get away from those gates.\par \par \uc2 And then of course many Afghan civilians may not have been aware of that or may have been willing to take the risk trying to get out. So a lot of complicated issues for the president on how to respond, how protect those still there, and what happens with the remaining evacuation operations. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kelly O`Donnell, Andrea Mitchell, Raf Sanchez, Clint Watts, Colonel Jack Jacobs, really appreciate having you all on to help us navigate what is just a terrible day here. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 As we learn more about these attacks, I want to bring in someone who`s been in contact with some senior military officials over in Kabul. Democratic congressman from California, senior member of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 , longtime member of Congress, John Garamendi is joining me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Congressman, play reporter for us. What do you know? What more details can you fill in that we haven`t been able to report?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN GARAMENDI (D-CA): You`ve covered almost every detail that I have available to me. We do know that American service members have lost their lives. Tragic, heartbreaking. But not unexpected. This is extremely dangerous operation from the very moment it began. The collapse of the Afghan government, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  taking control or really not taking control of the city. It`s extremely dangerous.\par \par \uc2 For all of us, we need to take a short pause here, gather the information, and then let the president and the military and the intelligence make a determination on the best step forward.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 Right now, we can all take a guess, we can all that pretend that we know what`s going on. The fact is we don`t. But we do know it`s a dangerous situation. The president will make a decision in due course here. Whether the extension -- deadline is extended or not remains to be seen. Where are those Americans? In many places, we simply don`t know where they are and they may or may not even exist.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me ask you this. When it comes to trusting the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , and I guess that`s a strong word, they`ve got to earn it over time, but do you think we have no other choice but to give the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  some opportunity here to show that they can get control? Do we use the frozen assets as a way for them to get tough, and not allow an al Qaeda or an ISIS to sort of get safe harbor? Clearly this ISIS-K has some safe harbor here.\par \par \uc2 How much do we attempt to work with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  here versus against them?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: Well, we really will have no choice but to work with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , as long as they are in apparent control of Kabul and other major cities. Now what leverage do we have? I suspect there are many and it may very well be best for the United States to work through an international organization like the United Nations and to recognize that we`re not the only player in this game.\par \par \uc2 Clearly the countries that are surrounding Afghanistan have a major and a critical stake in what the future will hold and will undoubtedly be in touch with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  as we are trying to sort out what needs to be done there. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  face an awesome and very, very difficult and dangerous situation of their own. They`re not loved by everybody. In fact, they were hated and they were involved in fights, revenge killings and power conflicts are going on as we speak.\par \par \uc2 All of those things create a very unstable situation. So right now for the United States, it`s to get all of our people out as the president says, those who want to get out, and as many of those people that helped us over the last 20 years. That`s our goal. And then to extract the American military safely. Obviously American military personnel have lost their life and so the safety issue is even more on the minds of all of us, so it`s going to be complex.\par \par \uc2 The president needs all of our support as he works through a very difficult situation that actually began long before he became president and the stage was set for tragedy. And here we are, the tragedy that all of us feared, the president very clearly feared this happening, and now where do we go from here? We`ll be as safe as we possibly can for military and try to get the Americans out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Colonel Jack Jacobs earlier was on and he sort of floated that one of the potential ideas that the president is being offered, you know, I said put yourself in the shoes of General Milley, Secretary Austin, what are the various options he`s being given. And that is use the frozen assets as leverage to basically get a commitment from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  that look, we need to go longer now. It`s going to take us maybe one more week, maybe 10 more days. It`s in your interest and here`s how. Are you comfortable with that type of negotiating?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: There will be -- the negotiations are under way, have been under way from the very outset of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover of Kabul and Afghanistan. The CIA director was there. We don`t know what was in that discussion but we do know that there was a negotiation that presumably led to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  attempting to provide security outside of the airport.\par \par \uc2 Are there ongoing discussions now? There certainly are with regard to military to military, such as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has a military organization. Are there quid pro quos? Of course there are. Afghanistan is in deep, deep economic, financial, and social chaos. They need -- the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and the Afghan people are going to need significant international support. Everything from food to economic assets, all of those things are on the table.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 Are there some that are more imminent that would lead to an extension of the American presence? I suspect that there are. It doesn`t do much good for me or I guess for any of us to try to figure it all out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: We do know that it is in the process of being figured out by the American military, by our diplomats, and by the intelligence agencies. Will it lead to an extension? Possibly. Will it lead to better access for Americans to the base? Hopefully. These things are under discussion. Right now, the White House is holed up in the situation room going through all the things that I`m sitting here guessing about, and I just assume leave it to the president.\par \par \uc2 He understands the weight of this decision is going to be enormous, but my opinion is, he has made the proper decisions along the way, given the hand that he was dealt. He came into this situation with a May 1st deadline for American soldiers all out. That`s three months -- four months after he took office. And so what is he to do? He extended the deadline, giving more time for Americans to get out, for more stability for the Afghan government.\par \par \uc2 Did any of us, and I say any of us -- the Afghan government was at the White House and in the Congress meeting with us two weeks before they simply fled Afghanistan, giving us assurances that they would fight to the death, just give us the money, they said, give us the support, aerial support, all of which we gave them, and then they up and left and abandoned the country, leaving a vacuum that was immediately filled, partially and unsuccessfully thus far, by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is -- there`s going to be a lot of after-action hearings. If there is one --\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If there`s one big thing that you want to get an answer to, you know, once we get this behind us and to sort of unpack how the withdrawal process worked, what is the one issue you want to drill down on the most?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: I want to look to the future. I want whatever hearings take place to provide a foundation of information about what America, what the world needs to do to deal with Afghanistan. I`d like to know what the surrounding countries want to do, what their interests are, how we can engage with others in providing as much stability as possible in Afghanistan, providing the humanitarian support that Afghan is desperately going to need, and also how we and others can deal with international terrorism that could reoccur in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 So those are the interests that I have, as to who`s responsible for what, when, and how, I have no interest in that, except that it could provide information about what we need going forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: John Garamendi, senior member of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 , a veteran here, on both coasts, in government and politics.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And your input here.\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in Admiral James Stavridis who, of course, was in charge of the NATO command.\par \par \uc2 Jim, walk us through what the president, what the options that are being put forth on him, put forth before him right now of dealing with this current security mess that we have at that airport. It`s obviously much harder to get people into the airport. It`s not going to get easier. It`s going to get harder. What are the various realistic options in order to finish this evacuation?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JAMES STAVRIDIS (RET), FORMER NATO COMMANDER: Option one will be wrap it up right now. Stop taking anybody else and get our military out of there. I don`t think he`ll select that. Smack too much of the Lebanon response to the terrorist bombing there where we effectively decamped immediately.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Option two, Chuck, will be continue the mission with a very firm eye toward getting out of here as currently postulated on Tuesday, try to push the security perimeter out further, try to work with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to get better security, but effectively option two is continue the mission.\par \par \uc2 Option three would be to extend the mission, open it more broadly, perhaps conduct strikes against ISIS-K at their camps and so forth. I don`t think the president is going to go there. I suspect he`ll land on some variant of option two.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there -- are there realistic targets for ISIS-K that are identifiable right now or is that a bit complicated given that -- I mean, look, our intelligence seemed to be solid enough that we were forewarned about this attack? So what is your sense of how quickly we could acquire targets if we wanted to?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: My sense is we could in fact find and exploit targets. It would be a big mission, a long-range mission, we`d have to coordinate it. We wouldn`t have to, but we would probably need to coordinate it with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . I think it`s very unlikely that we`re going to want to do it, it would be low bang for the buck. Think about those Tomahawks we launched, Chuck against bin Laden way back in, you know, the 1990s that almost got him and got his training camp. Really doesn`t have much effect.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I doubt we`ll go that way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pull back a minute here. The NATO alliance, we know some of our allies in Europe are not happy with how this has played out. I assume it is -- these are disputes that can heal over time, but what is your feeling on the impact on NATO going forward?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I think it is a speed bump if you will. There are much bigger issues for the alliance going forward including working together to counterterrorism. As we know that`s not going away, to deter Russia, to look to the Arctic, to work on cybersecurity, to continue to stabilize the Balkans. NATO is not going anywhere in the immediate future.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there are some grousing I would say from some of the allies about this, but I would say even there, Chuck, over the last three days, you`ve seen the Biden administration work very hard to heal those, convening the G7, talking very consistently to the allies. I think that one you can kind of put behind us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. And, you know, one thing, I`d -- were there really any of our allies arguing for us to stay indefinitely when it came to Afghanistan? I mean, did -- you know, were the Brits making that case behind the scenes? I mean, I didn`t see a ton of evidence of that? But was there any of our allies that were wanting us to stay?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: No, I think that our allies would have been willing if we have wanted to continue to have a presence, you know, in the, say, U.S. kind of the 3,000 to 5,000 range, probably could have gotten allies to match that one to one. You probably could have built a continuing NATO mission around 10,000 troops. I think they would have stayed. I think when the U.S. made the determination to pull out, they were on board with that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Admiral James Stavridis, it`s a tough day losing American service members in a mission, but I know they all knew how dangerous this mission was, but it doesn`t mean we take these deaths any lightly.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Yes. Think of their families today. Think of their families today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I`m thinking of all the families who haven`t gotten the call yet, who are wondering, am I going to get that call, too? So thinking of all of them these days.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Jim, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 According to new estimates by "The New York Times," there are at least 250,000 Afghans in Afghanistan who worked for the United States who haven`t been evacuated yet. So how will this latest attack on the ground impact evacuation efforts and the future for these Afghan refugees?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Mark Hetfield. He`s the president and CEO of HIAS, who is focused on this issue of Afghan refugees right now, to discuss this.\par \par \uc2 So, Mark, first of all I know you`ve got a lot of contacts on the ground. What are you hearing about safety and security and the ability to get out right now?\par \par \uc2 MARK HETFIELD, PRESIDENT AND CEO, HIAS: Well, Chuck, there is no safety and security, and there is no ability to get out right now. Even for people who are U.S. citizens, even for people who are (INAUDIBLE) residence or a special immigrant visa holders, let alone the many vulnerable people, religious minorities, activists, people associated with U.S. media or U.S. NGOs. They can`t get out right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s 250,000 Afghans, we know there are some Afghan-Americans that can get out now, but they can`t bring their families with them. Explain that struggle. Is this more having to do with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  or more having to do with our SIV process?\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: It has everything to do with our SIV process. We had 20 years to prepare for this not unlikely outcome, and it wasn`t done. And so as a result, we are now scrambling, and there is a cascading effect. Because we`re scrambling to help these SIVs who laid their lives down for American soldiers, we now are not paying enough attention to the other people who are vulnerable and need assistance. So this is an absolute disaster. Refugees have to stop being an afterthought in U.S. foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 It should be in the front of everybody`s minds when we go into a situation like this 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In fact, when we hear the president say, look, we`re getting every American who wants to get out to get out, the ones that say they don`t want to get out, it`s your understanding, are most of them Afghan Americans who are not ready to leave because they can`t bring their family with them? They want to get out. They just can`t get out by themselves. Is that a better way to put it?\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Exactly. And most SIVs hightailed it out of Afghanistan as soon as they got the status to do so. I mean, the SIV process, it`s a 14-step process, and God knows if you were associated with the U.S. Military, you`re not going to leave your family behind. So that`s exactly it.\par \par \uc2 There`s just no leadership on this issue right now, Chuck. There is no special envoy who`s overseeing the humanitarian evacuation, the SIV process and the refugee process. There`s nobody at the U.N. who reports to the secretary-general looking at this stuff, either. I don`t understand why. But there has to be some leadership on this issue, there has to be somebody with authority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, walk us through the scope of what you expect in the next year when it comes to Afghan refugees. I mean, we`re talking about -- so we have 250,000 Afghans who have a direct connection to the United States operation who deserve some sort of special status, but we`re going to have a couple million Afghans who want to -- you know, who are the next level down, who can`t get that special, you know, can`t qualify for the special visa, but certainly aren`t going to feel as if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are friendly to them. How many do you expect to try to get out and where do you expect them to try to go?\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Well, I mean, there needs to be a humanitarian corridor established to get people out, whether that`s an air corridor or a land corridor, but it`s very tough to leave Afghanistan in the winter. It`s almost impossible by land. So people are going to be trapped there. There needs -- and that needs to be a multinational force, and if the U.S. is going to leave at the end of the month, I don`t know how that`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve seen this -- we`ve seen this show before unfortunately, and we know that there is going to be hundreds and thousands or more, fleeing for Pakistan and Tajikistan and Iran, and if you get to Iran, good luck trying to get to United States from Iran.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously, the rise in this nationalism particularly in Eastern and Central Europe has made these refugee and migrant flows even more complicated. Are you having a hard -- I mean, even the politics in our country, you want to know why we don`t have a czar in refugees, I might point to our current politics when it comes to what`s happened on this issue, particularly on the right side of our political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 Are you having more trouble with Western nations being open to taking in these refugees?\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Yes, absolutely. I mean, that`s the problem is we have refugees that are -- well, they`re not refugees yet because they`re stuck in Afghanistan. They`re trying to get out. They have flights lined up, they have planes lined up, but they have no place to go, so people aren`t flying out. So absolutely, they have absolutely no place to go, whether it`s the United States or a country in Europe or in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 There`s just -- it`s a St. Louis situation, but people aren`t even able to leave Afghanistan. And when I say St. Louis I mean the --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know what you mean, yes.\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: The shape of geography (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Jewish-German refugees turned away before World War II. Let me ask you this, who do you deal with in the administration, and who would you like to get some more Facetime with? I mean, it does sound like you`re a bit at wits ends here.\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Well, the thing with the refugee program, one of the flaws of its accountability spread across multiple agencies. The State Department, Health and Human Services, Homeland Security, then multiple vetting agencies, law enforcement agencies, and intelligent agencies, and then you need the Department of Defense to implement right now. Right? And so you have -- there is no one person. There needs to be somebody who reports to the president who can coordinate all of these different pieces. I mean, that`s what`s missing here. So the person I should be talking to doesn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And so -- and that`s basically what you`re arguing for is that there needs to be almost a single person put in charge right now because this is turning into a bureaucratic calamity is what you`re describing.\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Absolutely. Who reports to the president and can really make things happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Mark Hetfield, the head of HIAS, who`s working entirely on this refugee situation -- in some ways we hope becomes a refugee situation because it means these folks were able to actually get out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thanks for coming on and expressing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 HETFIELD: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that does it for me this hour. We`re obviously going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage is going to continue without breaks right now. Here`s my friend, Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 Geoff, take it away.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Chuck. It`s good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 And as await further updates from the White House and the Pentagon, we want to bring you up to the minute with what we know.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210826eh8q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell August 19, 2021 MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, John Heilemann \par \uc2 7,001 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:36]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Have you noticed anything different about my TV show this week?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: No.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNEL: Good, you`re not supposed to.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Other than the fact that you are achieving new levels of perfection every day. You`re always ascending, right?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So here`s the thing. I`m coming from a different undisclosed location this week.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Really?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It`s supposed to look like all of my unclosed locations. So, you know, it`s pretty close. You`re not supposed to guess. This one is a couple thousand miles away from my last undisclosed location.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, here`s the thing. Here`s the thing. It`s the first time -- I`m not going to say where I am. It`s the first time in a year and a half that you and I have been doing our shows in the same state. So, we can just kind of leave it at that and --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You`re in Narnia too?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`re so close. Rachel, we haven`t been this close since 2020. It`s like, February, first week of march 2020.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It takes me back. I totally thought that I had locked up the wardrobe and I was the only one who could broadcast from Narnia. But now that I know you`re here, I`m going to come find you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The same state. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, compadre. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, as of tonight, and this could change because things could take a dramatic turn for the worst at any moment. But as of tonight, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is managing the most successful American military evacuation from a war that America lost. As of tonight, the Biden administration and the American military have managed to evacuate 7,000 people from Afghanistan after the capitol city of Kabul came under the control of the people who won the war.\par \par \uc2 In Vietnam in 1975 after Saigon, then the capital city of south Vietnam fell under the complete control of the north Vietnamese army, Republican President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  was able to get absolutely no one out of Vietnam, no one. In those final days before the last American helicopter left Saigon, leaving thousands of our allies behind, the Ford administration and the American military were able to evacuate a total of 7,000 people.\par \par \uc2 The Biden-run evacuation is well on its way to evacuating many more people than the American military was able to evacuate from Vietnam in the final days. In Vietnam, the Americans were being driven out of the country at gunpoint. In Afghanistan so far, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have not fired a shot at the American military. In both desperate last-minute evacuations, the military did its very best.\par \par \uc2 The final days in both are filled with stories of American heroism, but they are also demonstrations of the American`s continuing ability to invade a country, spend 20 years losing a war, and then evacuate in a way that meets with the approval of an American news media filled with people who think they know how to do what the American military has never been able to do. What you`re not hearing in any of the critical analysis of how the Biden administration and the American military have handled the evacuation of Afghanistan is the example of who has done this sort of thing better.\par \par \uc2 No one is telling you that the Russians did a much better job of invading Afghanistan, losing the war there, and then evacuating. No one is holding up the Russian evacuation of Afghanistan as the model. No one is holding up the evacuation of any defeated army from a foreign country as the model. And of course no one is holding up the American evacuation of Vietnam as the model of how to do this because the American evacuation of Vietnam was much, much worse in every way, by every measure.\par \par \uc2 Every war produces a limitless flow of tragic individual stories. The end of every war produces a similar flow of tragic, individual stories.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But no individual story can tell you what your war policy should be or should have been. If this is your first experience with watching people left behind in war, then use it to decide whether you will support the next American war.\par \par \uc2 But if you use it as an example of something you know how to do better than the American military, then you are making the mistake of believing that the madness of war can be managed. The American military does not know how to manage the madness of war.\par \par \uc2 Catch-22 is a phrase that has entered our daily vocabulary to describe impossible situations. Sixty years ago, Joseph Heller put that phrase in our vocabulary through the title of his war novel "Catch-22," which captures the clumsy and inefficient processes of the American military, which he experienced first hand serving in World War II. Armies are not efficient machines. Armies are not run like automobile affects when the CEO of Ford became the secretary of defense during the Vietnam War. That organizational master, Robert McNamara was lost in that job, as lost in that job as any of the other secretaries of defense during the Vietnam War.\par \par \uc2 The American military is a massive bureaucracy that does some things well. But the thing that it does not know how to do is the same thing that no military in the world knows how to do, and that is organize a dignified and honorable retreat and full evacuation from a war that we lost in a foreign country without leaving anyone behind. When someone tells you that the evacuation from Afghanistan could have been run better, ask them why that has never happened before in history. Ask them why this should be the very first time in history that a desperate, last-minute military evacuation from a lost war should not be chaotic and messy.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is now being criticized for saying he knew it would be chaotic. President Biden was a United States senator when he watched the chaotic exit from Vietnam. Of course, he knew this was going to be chaotic. And of course he could not say that publicly before the chaos developed because such a statement by the president would have immediately created the chaos.\par \par \uc2 The American news media is very good at telling you the tragic individual stories of war, and it is very good at forgetting those stories. So far, in all the tragic stories being presented to us about people struggling to get out of Afghanistan, people who deserve our full sympathy and support, there is no story quite like the story of two friends who were left behind in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Gorgeous, gorgeous blue eyes. Long eyelashes of which I was very envious. That`s how Darwin Judge`s sister remembered him. Darwin Judge was a 19- year-old lance corporal in the Marine Corps from Marshalltown, Iowa, who spent less than two months in Vietnam before he was killed. He did not die alone.\par \par \uc2 His 21-year-old marine corporal buddy, Charlie McMahon, from a suburb of Boston, was right besides him when they were both killed in a rocket attack. That was Charlie McMahon`s 11th day in Vietnam. Charles McMahon and Darwin Judge became the answer to John Kerry`s family question in his Senate testimony as a Vietnam veteran against the war, how do you ask a man to be the last man to die for a mistake?\par \par \uc2 It turns out, there were two men, Charles McMahon and Darwin Judge, hit by the same rocket attack. They were in Vietnam to help manage the final desperate exit. They were there to protect the people trying to get on those final helicopters. They might have been on that final helicopter on April 30th, 1975, if they had lived just one more day.\par \par \uc2 The day after Charlie McMahon and Darwin judge were killed in action, the last American helicopter left Saigon and left their bodies behind, in a hospital in Saigon. It was a mistake. It was the kind of mistake the American military can make in a situation like that.\par \par \uc2 The American military did not just leave behind friendly interpreters in Vietnam, they did not just leave behind interpreters who helped reporters in Vietnam. They did not just leave behind friendly allies who helped the military and worked on American military bases. They also left behind hundreds of babies and children who were fathered by American soldiers over the 18 years of the American military involvement in Vietnam. And they left behind the last two men to die for the mistake of the Vietnam War. They left Charles McMahon and Darwin Judge behind.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts Senator \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  went to work for his constituents, the McMahon family, and a year later he managed to secure the return of the bodies of Charles McMahon and Darwin Judge. Senator Kennedy never criticized President Ford for leaving them behind. Senator Kennedy never said he had a better idea about how to manage the evacuation of American troops and American allies from a war that we lost.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats did not attack Republican President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  for how he handled the exit from a war lost by Democratic and Republican presidents. The governor of Georgia, who was an Annapolis graduate and a navy veteran, was running for president on the day that the last American helicopter left Vietnam, and he did not criticize how Republican President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  managed that evacuation, because \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  knew there was no good version of an evacuation from a lost war.\par \par \uc2 No one was surprised by the chaos and the dishonor of the American final exit from Vietnam. The world was not surprised. Every day of the Vietnam War was chaos, including the final day.\par \par \uc2 Just a year after the evacuation from Vietnam, at the Democratic National Convention in his acceptance speech for his presidential nomination, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I`ve never had more faith in America than I do today. We have an America that involved Dylan`s phrase, is busy being born, not busy dying.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In that speech, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  did not criticize Gerald`s management of the evacuation from Vietnam. None of the anti-war protesters that demand a withdrawal from Vietnam attacked his management of the final withdrawal from Vietnam. No one in the anti-war movement, including the Vietnam veterans, believed that they had a better idea about how to evacuate a lost war or that the American military was capable of doing a better job of evacuating from a war that they lost.\par \par \uc2 The lesson of the Vietnam War and the war in Afghanistan, including the last days of those wars is not that we have to teach West Point classes of future generals how to safely and honorably evacuate from our lost wars without leaving anyone behind. The lesson for a country that has not won a war since 1945 is, stop launching wars of dubious legality and unclear moral purpose that we do not know how to win.\par \par \uc2 The people who own the American exit from the Afghanistan war are the people who advocated launching that war and, more importantly, the people who never learned, the people who never stopped advocating for continuing that war.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is someone who was there in the thick of our politics in the immediate aftermath of the Vietnam War, James Fallows a speechwriter for presidential candidate \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and then speechwriter for President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . He`s now a staff writer for the "Atlantic."\par \par \uc2 Jim, thank you very much for joining us tonight. I find discussions that do not involve memories that range as far back at 1975 this week to be rather frustrating, so I`m glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 One thing I`m fascinated by is how the evacuation of Vietnam did not become the political football that the Afghanistan evacuation has become instantaneously.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FALLOWS, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: Let me say first, Lawrence. Thank you for the setup you did. I agree with everything you were presenting about the horrors of that war generations ago, and of course there are horrors now, but when you start a war, you buy into the end of that war.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 And I agree the solution -- \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , his proudest achievement was that he did not go to war in four years that he was in office.\par \par \uc2 As you probably recall, as a very young man then, the mid-1970s -- there were horrors on every front. The first American president in history to resign, Richard Nixon resigned, there was the Watergate scandal -- there were -- after the fall of Saigon, there were, again, the horrors of the Khmer Rouge onslaught in Cambodia. That part of Carter`s campaign as part of the Democratic nomination and against \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  was to talk about the tragedy of Vietnam and Cambodia as opposed to the tactically failure of the Ford administration, those final days, and that the United States needed not to put itself in that situation again and to learn from that tragedy and to do better as opposed to, you know, what we have been seeing in the last week, as you pointed out.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have a polling now saying that 62 percent now believe that - - or as of a couple days ago anyway that fighting the Afghanistan war was not worth it. It`s hard to imagine, you know, support for the Afghanistan war going up from there at this point.\par \par \uc2 And so, the president is doing something that 62 percent of the country thinks should be done. When you say not worth fighting, that means they`re saying it should`ve been done years ago.\par \par \uc2 FALLOWS: Should have been done. I think there`s a whole separate debate about the date when the war in Afghanistan was lost, and I think that was in December of 2001 when the leaders of the Bush administration decided o stop concentrating on Afghanistan and began preparing for the invasion of Iraq. Everything since then has been a sequel from that time.\par \par \uc2 But I think there has been -- what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in his remarkably blunt speech earlier this week was that he realized that he was not going to be - - he was not going to pass this war onto a fifth president, it having been on the watch of four presidents, including him. So I think the polling and just they suggest most people in the U.S. even recognizing the tragedies that are happening right now, that even recognizing that there are -- this was not -- not a wise use of American power.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to use both of your hats. As someone who worked in the White House and as someone who worked in the news media, because the news media that many in the Washington press corps have been relishing in the gotcha moment with the president and they think they got a gotcha moment because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying after the fact that he`s not surprised that there`s chaos. He obviously is old enough to have seen the Vietnam chaos, he`s not surprised by the chaos. He didn`t say that before the fact.\par \par \uc2 Now, I made the point that a president of the United States could never say before the fact that there will be chaos possibly or likely in a situation like this.\par \par \uc2 What is it like for you as a member of the press when you watch a president kind of trapped in the presidency, as it were, and not being able to say things as certain points that he can say as events develop, which is what we`re watching here, and then the news media`s complete inability to process what`s actually happening in that, the news media`s feigned naivete about, oh, you know, we got him in this huge contradiction with himself?\par \par \uc2 FALLOWS: I think a very astute point you made earlier on is about what would have happened that is something we`ve been inured to in the past couple years. We had a president that could say anything and had no sense of the gravity attached to normal presidents words. Normal presidents of any party and any inclination he knows as his eight years as vice president, which is that there`s great consequence to what a normal president says. You can imagine just the additional chaos apart from what we`re seeing if Biden had said what many people in the press want him to have said now beginning a week ago a month ago, or whatever. So yes, I believe this is a kind of kabuki.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: James Fallows, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Your perspective on this is invaluable. Your experience in 1975 with the presidential campaign at that time is an invaluable window for us. Thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 FALLOWS: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, once again, a Trump supporter terrorized the Capitol, this time it`s a guy with a pickup truck and said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to be reinstated as president.\par \par \uc2 Congress is not in session today. When the House returns next week, they will take up the infrastructure bill. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:24:16]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here`s something the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  did not do today. A Trump supporter terrorized Capitol Hill saying he had a bomb. Forty-nine-year-old Floyd Ray Roseberry of North Carolina drove a pickup truck onto the sidewalk in front of the library of Congress. Multiple buildings were evacuated, including the Cannon House Office Building.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Roseberry told police he had a bomb and surrendered after officers negotiated with him for about five hours. Officers collected, quote, possible bomb-making materials.\par \par \uc2 During the standoff, Floyd Ray Roseberry lived streams streamed on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  from inside the truck, "\uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2 " reports in the live extremes the man demanded that president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democratic politicians step down, accusing them of killing America.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:05]\par \par \uc2 He identified himself as a Southerner and called on other patriots and Southerners to join him on Capitol Hill to begin a revolution.\par \par \uc2 "Talking Points Memo" reports that in a video posted yesterday, Floyd Ray Roseberry says Trump will be reinstated as president once \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is driven from office. When members of the House of Representatives returned return to next week, as long as there are no demented Trump supporters threatening to blow up the capitol, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  plans to have the house begin voting on the Biden infrastructure legislation.\par \par \uc2 Joining our decision now, Norm Ornstein, congressional historian and emeritus scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . And John Heilemann, MSNBC national affairs analyst, and host of the "Hell and High Water" podcast from "The Recount".\par \par \uc2 John, let me start with you. The infrastructure bill in the form of the budget resolution is one of the things that`s supposed to be cued up for the House`s return next week, but that`s pending how many pickup trucks of Trump supporters might be negotiating with cops about exactly when they`re going to get out of their trucks or blow up the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HEILEMANN, MSNBC NATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: Yeah, Lawrence, that`s a very optimistic, rosy picture you paint there. But unfortunately, seems to be opposite, seems to be right on. You don`t get cases that are clear than this one.\par \par \uc2 We all understand what the threat is that the post-January 6th threat and we`ve now seen multiple attacks on the Capitol. We`ve now seen multiple people who want to do damage to the capitol. The Capitol has become a prime target for the MAGA right and the white grievance crowd that are out there. And, you know, I think there`s no reason to think that it`s going to stop anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 I think -- as you know, I`m predicting a new era of political balance, unfortunately, for a while. And today is just one more piece of evidence we`re not getting further away from that but we`re getting closer to it. In fact, I think, you know -- I am bracing for worse ahead, not only on the domestic legislative front but people keep coming back to that building and trying to do damage to it, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Norm Ornstein, one thing that could help would be for House Leader Kevin McCarthy next week or sooner to get out of his Republican members together to make a statement that President Trump is not going to be reinstated ever. He`s allowed to run for the office again, but there`s no such thing as reinstatement.\par \par \uc2 Now, what are the chances of Kevin McCarthy actually doing that, and are they equal to or less than the chances of Kevin McCarthy supporting the Biden infrastructure legislation?\par \par \uc2 NORM ORNSTEIN, CONGRESSIONAL HISTORIAN: It`s about the same, which is a big fat zero. And we should note here that the Republican Representative Mo Brooks issued a statement pretty much supporting this violence that was threatened by this bomber, somebody who had been involved in helping to instigate the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And what I would also like to see Kevin McCarthy do, which is equally unlikely, is to denounce his own member for, in effect, giving imprimatur to violence. That`s where we are now in those politics. You know, to segue to the infrastructure plan, one would hope that these nine Democrats who have said they`re not going to support anything involving that larger ambitious infrastructure package, the $3.5 trillion one, unless and until they get first the bipartisan package, it would be very nice if they made a statement that really was against this kind of insurrection and said we`re going to support legislation that improves America.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi`s putting them on the spot by packaging these things together and saying you wanted to vote on your package? We`re going to put it together with the other one and we`ll see if they respond.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: John Heilemann, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has been so good at navigating these kinds of legislative courses in the past that it feels to me like there`s an awful lot of confidence about, oh, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will put it all together next week in the House with the nine Democrats who want a different procedure from all the rest of the Democrats and make peace between them and the more progressive Democrats and -- I don`t know. I have that feeling, but in any other world in the past, if we had nine Democrats taking a stand like this, we would say, wow, this thing`s in trouble.\par \par \uc2 HEILEMANN: Well, right. More than that, Lawrence, I think, you know, we`ve -- part of the confidence, I think, people had, I think people still have the confidence you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:10\par \par \uc2 And and I think they have good reason to have the confidence \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , and again, I`ll observe for the 400th time, the kind of incredible lockstep, well-organized, well-orchestrated legislative juggernaut that so far has been the Biden White House and its staff with all of that experience that it has. The Ron Klain`s and Steve Ricchetti`s of the world working hand in glove with Nancy Pelosi`s office, working hand in glove with Chuck Schumer`s office, have been the only reason why this whole high- wire act that you and I have observed all along -- we haven`t seen anything like it. There`s never been anything this complicated or anything this costly.\par \par \uc2 The combination of these two pieces of legislation historically off the charts in terms of dollar values and then the complexity of trying to get them through, trying to land the two planes at the same time without that kind of coordination and that kind of skill and experience, they would have no chance.\par \par \uc2 And instead, we all sit back and say, well, of course, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will pull it off.\par \par \uc2 And again, as I said, there`s good reason to think that, but the reality is right now that all of that complexity just got magnified or multiplied by the fact that moderates suddenly woke up in the House and realized they had as much leverage in terms of trying to extort differences both in process and in outcome as progressives had all along. That should be -- you`re correct, it is like, oh, my god, this thing was already this hard to do, it`s now impossible, and yet I think somehow the stakes are so high that I think the combination of Pelosi, Schumer, and Biden and what would happen, the cost of failure, the political cost of failure in addition to the substantive cost of failure, it might just make this thing all too big and too complex in some ways to fail.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST, THE LAST WORD: Norm, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 NORM ORNSTEIN, AUTHOR, "IT`S EVEN WORSE THAN IT LOOKS": You know, I don`t think it`s out of the question that this could go down on a vote when it first comes up next week. I think it would be really bad given the fact that so many Democrats have criticized \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- your statement to begin this program was very powerful -- over what`s happened in Afghanistan. It would not be a great thing to happen.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe that Pelosi has a backup plan if that happens, and we might come back with something that we talked about earlier in the week. If what they want is a stand-alone vote on just that one narrower package, bring it up with just one small change so that if they vote against that a second time, they really are looking like nothing, but people going for extortion and being obstructionists.\par \par \uc2 But if you do that, then there`s time at least to go through with the larger infrastructure package, and of course, this also would mean that the John Lewis Voting Rights Act would go down if they vote against it.\par \par \uc2 She`s added on a lot of pressure on these members, and ultimately I just have to believe that Pelosi is going to find a way to prevail, but don`t be surprised if it takes a little bit longer than next week.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: All right. We need you both on standby for next week. Norm Ornstein and John Heilemann, thank you both very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 ORNSTEIN: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  was the Chairman of President Obama`s Council of Economic Advisers, and he is seeing things in the Biden economic recovery that he`s never seen before. His one-word description of the economic picture is, quote, "wonderful." Professor \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:37:38]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here is something you don`t hear economists say very often. No, wait. Wait a second. Here`s something you`ve never heard an economist say. Quote, "I have yet to find a blemish in this Jobs report. I`ve never before seen such a wonderful set of economic data." That`s what Harvard Professor \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  said about the July Jobs report, and know the employment picture is looking even better.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , former Chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers for President Obama. He is currently Professor of the Practice of Economic Policy at Harvard`s Kennedy School.\par \par \uc2 Professor Furman, I have to say, in all my hours in classroom with economics professors, I don`t ever remember the word "wonderful" being used for anything. It`s called the dismal science for a reason. What is going on here?\par \par \uc2 JASON FURMAN\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN OF THE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS FOR PRESIDENT OBAMA: You know, 8:30 a.m., the jobs numbers come out. I open them the second they come out. The first number I saw, 943,000 jobs. That`s just a really high number. I, then went and checked about 11 other numbers, the labor force participation rate, the black unemployment rate, the wage number, the number of people working part time.\par \par \uc2 Every one of those numbers improved in the month of July, all moving in the direction we want them to move.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And we have unemployment -- go ahead. Sorry, the unemployment number now, while we`re still in the pandemic, it`s just extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes. I mean, if you would have asked me a year ago, if you have had asked me six months ago, I would have thought it would take a lot longer to where we are now. We`re definitely not all the way there. We have a lot to go on the unemployment rate right now, it is 5.4 percent, prior to the pandemic it was 3.5 percent. There is no reason we can`t get back there.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully we can get a lot of the way back there by the end of this year, but after the damage the economy went through last year, it`s healing more quickly than we`ve seen in previous recessions, many of which weren`t as bad as this recession was.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And there is more fuel coming to the economy if the infrastructure package gets through the Congress in whole or in part.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:05]\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes, absolutely. And I think, you know, the President says build back better and he says build back better for a reason. If we could return the economy to exactly where it was in February 2020, that would be pretty good. But it wouldn`t be good enough. That was an economy that had a lot of inequality, that had not enough opportunities for, you know, people to work, too many children not in preschool and, of course, an economy that wasn`t green enough.\par \par \uc2 So all those things need to change. We don`t just need to repair the damage. A lot of what`s coming next is to build something better.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And of course, in the nature of infrastructure spending, it`s not -- it`s not a one-year spending program. You know, this kind of building goes on for many years. The building that will be authorized by the infrastructure bill, and then the economic benefits of that work go on for years after the construction itself is completed.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes. That`s exactly right. And it`s such a great opportunity right now. A lot of this is being paid for. The President has proposed tax increases on high-income households and some rollback of the corporate rate reduction under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But also, frankly, I think we can borrow. The Federal government can borrow at a 1.3 percent rate right now. That`s actually lower than the inflation rate. And so when you`re making an investment, the question is, is that investment going to pay off over time? Is it going to create economic growth? Is it worth the cost to that borrowing, and it`s an awful lot of investments that pass that test.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And another thing built into the cost of this infrastructure bill is there haven`t been infrastructure bills for several years. So, if you`ve neglected the maintenance, if you`ve neglected the upkeep, of course it`s then going to be more expensive than doing regular maintenance.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes. That`s exactly right. I mean, again, you look at the Federal debt. The Federal debt doesn`t include deferred maintenance. It doesn`t include the cost of not investing in children and children that don`t go to preschool, don`t grow up, and succeed, and have the earnings they would otherwise have.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a lot of costs out there and part of this is doing is recognizing them, addressing them sooner, and that`s a smarter, more cost- effective thing to do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Coming up, the delta variant has upended our previous thinking about the coronavirus and how long we will be living with it and how many shots of vaccine we will need to get through it. Up next, a Harvard epidemiologist who has been studying this will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:47:13]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: C.D.C. reported 157,694 new cases of coronavirus today in the United States, three of those cases are senators. Republican Senator Roger Wicker from Mississippi, Independent Senator Angus King from Maine, Democratic Senator John Hickenlooper from Colorado, South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  also tested positive this month. All four senators had been fully vaccinated before they were infected.\par \par \uc2 Alabama, the state with the lowest vaccination rate, has run out of ICU beds due to the surge in coronavirus infections in the state. The \uc2 Alabama Hospital Association\uc2  said there were 29 people in the state on Wednesday being forced to wait in emergency rooms for an open ICU bed.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Mississippi Department of Health\uc2  says there are only seven ICU beds available in the state and 96 patients need them. Yesterday, the Biden administration announced continued effectiveness of the vaccines will now depend on getting a third booster shot for the Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. There is no guidance yet on a booster shot for the recipients of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  single-dose vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. Michael Mina, epidemiologist with the Harvard school of Public Health. Dr. Mina, thank you very much for joining us tonight. The issue on the vaccine booster shot raises the question of just how protected vaccinated people are.\par \par \uc2 I, for example, have never stopped wearing masks indoors, at stores. I now know that in November, I`ll eight months away from my second shot of Moderna. So, the effectiveness of this vaccine is kind of on a downward slope during that period of time, apparently. That`s my understanding of the science.\par \par \uc2 So what should vaccinated people be doing in protecting themselves as we head toward our third shot, our booster shot?\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL MINA, EPIDEMIOLOGIST, \uc2 HARVARD SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH\uc2 : That`s a great question. Vaccinated people right now should on the one hand recognize that just getting the vaccine at all that see has given you a massive leg up to ensure if you do get this virus and infected with it, you will not end up in the hospital. It`s the most likely scenario.\par \par \uc2 However, it really scales with age. As you say, as we move further and further away from the date that you got your vaccine, unfortunately, the oldest individuals in our society are going to be likely the quickest to have their protection start to wane, which is why the boosters are starting to be recommended. And also why it`s become controversial about whether or not everyone needs it or just the older individuals and immunocompromised, recognizing that the vaccines may not be doing what they -- may not be sufficient to prevent transmission is important.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:11]\par \par \uc2 MINA: We have to recognize that the virus is able to transmit in some people who are vaccinated and we have to be aware of that as we move around in what our expectations are for what benefit we are getting. It is really a medical benefit to help us not end up in the hospital, but it is becoming less and less effective against delta in terms of limiting spread.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is breakthrough the right word for describing vaccinated people who get infected? We call it a breakthrough infection like we should be surprised or shocked that somehow the virus got through the vaccine wall.\par \par \uc2 MINA: I don`t think that breakthrough is the right word for somebody who becomes asymptomatically infected who is detected on a test. The vaccines were not indicated or designed to stop transmission, but when people become symptomatic, that would be defined as a breakthrough infection in my book because that`s really the indication that these vaccines have. That`s what the trials measured.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that none of this is surprising. We expected, or at least many people expected that these vaccines will wane over time. The tremendous efficacy of 90 to 95 percent that we saw was right after people got vaccinated. That`s when you have the most antibodies circulating. And naturally, just like in any vaccine or infection, waning is an expected outcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, if we were not developing, let`s just go to a hypothetical universe where we are not developing new variants and the vaccine was still fighting the same old variant it began fighting in say, February, would that that have anything to do with the length of effectiveness of the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 MINA: It would for sure. The mutations that are rising that have now led to delta and will inevitably lead the further variants down the road are certainly giving the virus a leg up to both transmit more readily, but also appear to be giving it an ability to start to chip away at our immune protection, and enable it to infect individuals a little easier.\par \par \uc2 So, if we were still dealing with version one of this virus, for example, and that really matched the vaccines well, whereas now the vaccines that`s we`ve gotten and including the boosters, unfortunately, are going to be -- I don`t want to say outdated, but they are based around the genomic sequence of a virus that is no longer actually in circulation. We`ve now moved on to delta and that`s a different -- a slightly different sequence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dr. Michael Mina, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MINA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:57:11]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Throughout this process, my north star has been remembering what it was like when my late son, Beau, was deployed to Iraq. How proud he was to serve his country, how insistent he was to deploy with his unit, and the impact it had on him. And all of us at home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It has been seven decades since we have had a war-time President who knows what it feels like to have a son or daughter serving in a war zone. During World War II, President Franklin Delano Roosevelt had four sons serving in the war at the same time. Incidentally, Winston Churchill`s son serves in combat with the British Army in World War II. President \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2  who presided over the final months of the Korean War in his first year as President had been the General and Commander of Allied Forces in Europe during World War II, his son served in that war and the Korean War.\par \par \uc2 Presidents Lyndon Johnson and Richard Nixon each had two daughters. Presidents Johnson and Nixon presided over the disastrous Vietnam war while their sons-in-law were serving in that war.\par \par \uc2 Now Presidents who`s launched our biggest foreign wars did not end them. President Roosevelt served admirably as Commander-in-Chief during World War II. He died in office at age 62 the month before Germany surrendered and five months before Japan surrendered.\par \par \uc2 When President Nixon was forced to resign and hand the title of Commander- in-Chief to his Vice President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2 , it became President Ford`s duty in 1975 to stare helplessly at America`s defeat in Vietnam and our chaotic and dishonorable exit from Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 And now, President Biden is ending a war that he did start. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the fourth President to be the Commander-in-Chief of our forces in Afghanistan and he decided there should not be a fifth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What`s happening now could just as easily happen five years ago or 15 years in the future. We have to be honest. Our mission in Afghanistan has taken many missteps, made many missteps over the last two decades. I am now the fourth American President to preside over the war in Afghanistan. Two Democrats and two Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I will not pass this responsibility on to a fifth President.\par \par \uc2 I made a commitment to the American people when I ran for President that I would bring America`s military involvement in Afghanistan to an end. While it has been hard and messy, and yes, far from perfect, I`ve honored that commitment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President Biden gets tonight`s last word. "The 11th Hour" with Brian Williams starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:05]\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WILLIAMS, MSNBC HOST: Well, good evening once again. Day 212 of the Biden administration. There`s mounting pressure to speed up the air lift to get people out of Afghanistan as new reports indicate the White House was warned about the disaster that would quickly unfold after the official U.S. withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:20]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210820eh8j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press Daily for August 13, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Josh Lederman, Courtney Kube, Kelly Cobiella \par \uc2 8,818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: I`ll speak with one of the Democratic Party`s leaders of the redistricting fight, and also happens to be a former Attorney General. Eric Holder is coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with the rapidly deteriorating situation in Afghanistan, a crisis that appears to be worsening, literally by the hour. Overnight, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took control of Kandahar and Herat, Afghanistan`s second and third largest cities, it has now taken over at least nine provincial capitals in just the last week. All of it as Sign officials say that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has far better military capabilities than they expected. That means the capital city of Kabul could fall in a matter of weeks, and some fear, believe it or not even days.\par \par \uc2 This map shows Afghan territory controlled by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in red back in April. Today, as you can see, nearly two-thirds of that map of Afghanistan is now red for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . The Taliban`s stunning advance has led the U.S. to have to deploy an additional 3000 U.S. troops back to Kabul temporarily, not to assist in any military offensive to keep the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  out of Kabul, but simply to help secure the safe withdrawal of the remaining staff from the U.S. Embassy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: Our first responsibility has always been protecting the safety and the security of our citizens serving in Afghanistan and around the world. As we`ve said all along, the increased tempo of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  military engagement and the resulting increase in violence and instability across Afghanistan is of grave concern.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We believe this is the prudent thing to do given the rapidly deteriorating security situation in and around Kabul.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We do expect another update, at least through the prism of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  about the operation in the next hour when the Pentagon holds a press briefing. Meanwhile, the White House is facing headlines like these, say President Biden stands by his decision to withdraw all U.S. troops from Afghanistan by the 20th anniversary of the September 11 attacks.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now from Wilmington Delaware, where President Biden is about to depart for Camp David is NBC News Correspondent Josh Lederman, Courtney Kube is at the Pentagon for us, and Kelly Cobiella is on the ground in Kabul, Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Josh, let me just start with you. Is the President headed to Camp David, is this going to be a working weekend, and how much of his national security staff is joining him?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: It certainly will be a working weekend for him. The plan -- President planning to continue to get briefed throughout the weekend on the situation effectively in real-time but there are no indications that the President is going to be plotting any kind of change in course here even despite the growing calls, especially on Capitol Hill, for the President to reconsider his decision and his plan on Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 What we`re hearing from White House officials is that, yes, they are surprised by the pace of the Taliban`s takeover of large swaths of Afghanistan, but they`re not particularly surprised about the fact that it`s happening because even though the White House has held out hope for some type of a diplomatic outcome and negotiated settlement between the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and the Afghan government, they continue to push that message, continue to try to tell the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  they`re going to have no international legitimacy. Jeopardize potential future, U.S. assistance if they do take over by force.\par \par \uc2 Everyone sort of knows that that was not going to be a very likely outcome, particularly given the way the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  has been acting. And so, the President standing firm behind his decision, and really focused on this very narrow mission set at this point which Courtney will elaborate on to try to get American civilians out of there safely. With White House Spokeswoman Jen Psaki saying today that the President is firmly focused on getting out American civilians, doing that in efficient safe matter, and when it comes to his decision, saying there are no regrets.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Josh, I want to play something the President said on July 8, when somebody asked him whether this is -- there`s going to be a Saigon moment like 1975. Here was his response nearly a month ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is not the South -- the North Vietnamese Army. They`re not -- they`re not remotely comparable in terms of capability. There`s going to be no circumstance where you see people being lifted off the roof of the embassy in the of the United States from Afghanistan. It is not at all comfortable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Josh, I know that the team around the White House, they don`t like these comparisons, they`re upset about the media coverage. But boy, that quote is not aging well.\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Well, look, I mean, part of the reason that they`re sending these 3000 troops in right now is precisely to avoid that dire of a scenario. That`s four miles from the embassy to the airport. And so, by relocating a lot of these people to the airport, making sure the airport is protected, they hope to be able to get them out of the country. If need, be on airplanes instead of on helicopters landing within the embassy grounds.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter as we`re seeing play out, and really, a vivid- terms here, Chuck, is there`s no way to evacuate your personnel in a quick timeframe from a country that is being taken over by an insurgent group and have it look victorious. You just can`t. There`s no way to do that without it looking like defeat, whether that looks like what happened in Saigon or what`s going to happen in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:41]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney, walk us through when these 3000 troops are getting there, and what is there -- I mean, is their mission simply to get the safe return of anybody that can get on an airplane? What exactly is their mission?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: I mean, the answer is now. So, the first elements are literally heading in there right now. We`ll see all 3000 of them there by the end of this weekend. And it`s important to point out, the Pentagon is very, very quick to say that this is a specific, tailored, and temporary mission. And what that means is they want to make it clear that these 3000 marines and soldiers are specifically there to secure the airport, and to help with the evacuation of the Americans assigned to the embassy, and the end -- to aid the Special Immigrant Visa candidates who may need to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 What -- the reason that this is all accelerated so much in just the last 48 to 72 hours is, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have militarily behaved pretty masterfully here. They have been able to isolate Kabul much faster and more effectively than even some of the most dire assessments have -- indicated that they might be able to do. And they are right on Kabul`s doorstep right now. They are threatening Logar province today as we speak, they`ve taken Ghazni, they`ve -- I mean, they are -- they are literally right on the doorstep of Kabul.\par \par \uc2 And the biggest concern here, really the worst-case scenario that the military and this -- the government -- the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is concerned about is that if they start to threaten Kabul, and they`re able to march in that they will head straight for the airport. And once they -- if they are able to take the airport than any Americans who are in that -- any Afghans who are eligible for the SIV program, they will be stuck there, they will not be able to get out. So, that`s why these troops are going in there.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just these 3000 going into Kabul, there`s another 3500 to 4000 of an immediate reaction force were going forward to Kuwait, and they`ll be waiting there in case the security situation in Kabul, particularly around the airport really starts to deteriorate, they`ll be sent -- to deteriorate, they will send them in very quickly, and help to secure the path for these individuals so they can get out of the country.\par \par \uc2 But the Defense Officials I speak with here, they want to make it clear that in fact, these troops are not going in because they`re going to be fighting the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and other troops of the country, they are there for this mission.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, let`s go to Kelly, you`re on the ground. Kelly, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , is this a better military, or are the Afghan forces really not even putting up a fight in many of these provinces?\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think it`s a little bit complicated, Chuck. I mean, part of the problem is that in some of these areas, you have sort of pockets of small forces -- small numbers of forces who weren`t resupplied by the central government, that`s according to the local officials on the ground so they really had no ability or very limited ability to fight back.\par \par \uc2 There`s also an element here of very low morale among Afghan forces. Their problems with low pay, problems with supplies problems, really with the organization of the defense forces themselves. And they`re -- and also a problem with just risk to personal safety concerns that if they don`t lay down their arms, and join the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , or surrender, that they will be killed. And we have seen these reports from the United Nations and from other areas that there have been extrajudicial killings of troops that have surrendered -- of Afghan forces have surrendered. There`s a deep fear among a lot of these forces, that they weren`t going to be able to carry out the fight against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and partly because they weren`t supplied properly. And so, they lay down their arms.\par \par \uc2 There were cases in the country, and still are very, you know, small examples of places where forces did fight and fought for quite a long time. I mean, you had that as an example in Kandahar, which just fell in the past 12 hours or so. There was heavy fighting in Helmand Province as well and frankly, in Kunduz for a while in the North. But essentially it got to the point and we`ve heard lots of stories from different sources, from politicians, and people on the ground, about, it`s just sort of, a lack of organization, not a good command structure within the forces, and also a lack of will to fight for a government that they sort of saw as not in it for them. A government that they didn`t feel loyal to or feel like that they should fight for.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kelly, is there any reason to believe that the Afghan security forces are just better, that are the closer you get to Kabul, as just a case where they`ve been well paid, they`ve been taken care of, they`ve been resupplied, do we have any reason to believe that?\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: That`s very likely. The concentration that -- we haven`t yet heard a strategy basically from the central government on how all of this was going to play out but there have been sort of hints that the basic thought was to protect the big population centers, protect the cities like Kabul, of course, like Mazar-i-Sharif in the north, Herat was supposed to be one of those cities, and support the supply lines, then sort of give up the rest of the area, allow the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to take over these smaller provincial capitals because they didn`t matter so much.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, that strategy hasn`t quite played out the way it was intended if in fact, that was the government strategy. But this also plays into the idea that Kabul is the center of the world here and particularly with this government. It seems as though the centralized government has put all of the resources within Kabul and surrounding Kabul. So, you know, potentially, there is a much better chance that security forces will be able to defend the Capitol than some of these outlying provinces they were able to do because they didn`t get supplies. But again, this is -- this is something we`re going to have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 Like I said, Chuck, we haven`t -- we haven`t really heard from the central government, we haven`t heard from President Ghani, really at all in the past week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me -- final question to you, Courtney. The commanders you talked to, and your sources, do they think that the defense of Kabul will be a lot more robust than what we saw in Kandahar?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, it`s -- that -- it`s going to depend on a number of factors. And it`s important to point out, you know -- you know, Kelly`s absolutely right, that there are -- there`s different levels of the Afghan military, and their ability to defend, and their ability to fight, and there will probably to fight too. I mean, Kandahar actually has the 2 of 5 core, which is one of the stronger, more capable military elements of the Afghan military. That`s the conventional guys who are down there and they weren`t able to fend off the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, there are reports that they were overrun, sometimes the reports say they were -- they were folding, but not just that. The Afghan commandos, the Special Forces, they were sent to some of these bigger cities to try to defend them, to shore them up and they still fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in some cases. And that was a combination of reasons as Kelly was saying that they -- problems with resupply, but more than anything else, they were just exhausted, they wore them down, they made them fight, the Afghans made them fight in so many different places for so long that they just ran them down. So, that`s one of the big problems here is that these more capable fighters are just tired and worn out.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re talking about Kabul, yes, there are some of the more capable fighters there, and it`s not just that, they`re also layered. So, there`s not just the normal core and the Special Forces guys, but there`s also other elements, sort of like you see, you know, here in DC, you have different levels of Defense`s that you would have.\par \par \uc2 But the big question is, just because they have those capable fighters in Kabul, will they stand, or will they fold? That is a huge question. And one of the -- one of the factors that we need to look into is, is the central government still going to be standing? Will they still have a government in Kabul that, you know, that is talking to the people, that is leading the people? Or will they flee when the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are really coming down on the city? And these are all the questions and it`s hard to know.\par \par \uc2 And because of that, the assessment of when Kabul may fall is really all over the map at this point, Chuck. It is an hour-to-hour situation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we have had a civilian partnership problem in Afghanistan pretty much from day one. Anyway, Josh, Lederman, Courtney Kube, and Kelly Cobiella, all on this story for us. And Kelly, of course, please stay safe while you`re on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, to get a little better sense of why this training mission just never succeeded. I have a retired Army Colonel Mike Jason, he commanded combat units in Iraq and Afghanistan. He helped recruit and train Afghan police units and commandos, and he just wrote a piece in The Atlantic about his assessment of the U.S. training of Afghan security forces, and he titled what we got wrong in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Colonel Mike, Jason, I appreciate you spending a little time with us. Look, you basically make the case here that, you know, which you use the phrase, when we went through this training process, we checked the box. OK. It`s clear our training didn`t work. Why?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:23]\par \par \uc2 MIKE JASON, RETIRED COLONEL, U.S. ARMY: Well. Chuck, thanks for -- thanks for having me to really get a chance to talk about the last two decades of efforts in Afghanistan. You know, building these forces was a complex operation but I think you have to go back to the history and we went in light, we sort of didn`t know what we were doing, we kind of tried to figure it out on the fly, and then Iraq came along and that took our attention off the ball, and then we started rolling through it. But throughout this whole process, there are three fundamental components of the Afghan military that we need to look at.\par \par \uc2 One is, we heard a lot from Courtney and crew on the Special Forces and the commandos, which I had the chance to work with, they were heavily recruited and trained by our own special forces. So, lots of resources, lots of training, lots of mentorship, lots of assets.\par \par \uc2 Then you had the conventional army. Well, now that gets a little -- a little more interesting because we don`t really have or what do we see over 20 years. We never really have a strategy to do it. We never have a solid plan. We have what I called an alphabet soup of efforts, each a different chapter in a book without a plot.\par \par \uc2 And then the police forces, that`s an even bigger kind of problem because as you know, America does not have a national police force. So how are we going to stand up a nationalized law enforcement force to handle the mess of security?\par \par \uc2 So, we handled it -- we handed it back to us, to the army, we brought in some retired cops to kind of try and take a look. But our own cops know how to do, I don`t know, handcuffing and fingerprinting and some investigations, but not building institutions. And so, all of us, our allies, our partners, the army, we`re all kind of frustrated and really muddled along for about two decades.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds like we were trying to build an institution that maybe the Afghans didn`t either want or know that they needed and that just seems like a giant disconnect. We said we had to stand up this army because we thought that`s what should be done. Is that part of the problem that wasn`t -- there wasn`t just sort of full buy-in?\par \par \uc2 JASON: Well, I think that`s a great question. And what did they want, and who wanted it? Are we listening to the elites, you know, were we dealing with a lot of Expat refugees that came back from Europe, the United States and arrived in Afghanistan and became the power base in Kabul, is that warlords? But we certainly went and started.\par \par \uc2 But on the cheap, on the fly, we started an army that we generally want to look like us. Technologically based, educated, using equipment that had never really been used in Afghanistan, intelligence, surveillance, aviation, stuff that was not -- had not been an area. And we did the same kind of stuff in Iraq as well. And tried to sort of model ourselves, our noncommissioned officer corps, our leadership style, to an army that was engaged in an active counterinsurgency on the ground.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, again, I have to go back to the large domestic law enforcement apparatus that we also were building from the ground up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. How about the hearts and minds issue? I mean, how much of this do you think are these Afghan security forces just not having the capability to fight the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ? How much of it is fear, and why they`re surrendering? And how much of it is that they`re not sure where their loyalties lie?\par \par \uc2 JASON: Well, momentum has a -- has a -- has a speed of its own right? So, they`re seeing it, they`re watching it, they`re listening to our political pronouncements, whether we had previous administration to propose the timeline, or we said that we were leaving.\par \par \uc2 You know, from 2001 on, we never really established, here`s our end state, here`s where we`re going to go, here`s our commitment to you. And so, for 20 years, these forces, whether it`s Colonel Bashir, my friend Toralei, they`re looking at us like, Mike, are you guys staying? Should I put my family in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 These are also the interpreters that work with us. They`re making these calculated decisions on the safety of themselves and their families on a six-month, nine-month, or annual basis based on what American or what advisor is coming into town and making whatever promises and what they`re seeing on the news. And so, without that they`re making -- they`re making these calls. And so, I`m an Afghan soldier, I`m a commander, this are -- and there`s a lot of empathy here. And what do you do and how do you -- how do you -- how do you make the case for continuing to fight or for your safety?\par \par \uc2 I`m texting today with my war college classmate who is an Afghan officer, his brother has been captured by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and his family has fled his home and he`s trying to figure out like going to work or to like figure out how to get my family out of the country. And these are decisions that they`ve had to make daily for decades.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And how our credibility now with a -- with the -- with a sort of non- \uc2 Taliban\uc2  supporting Afghans is? How low?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JASON: Well, I -- you know, I can only imagine -- I can only imagine that it`s really tough. They`re looking at us and they look at the exit signs, and they look at all the promises we`ve made along the years. And everyone is watching, the world is watching and it`s got to be just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Retired Colonel Mike Jason, it was a very enlightening piece that you wrote in The Atlantic. It`s definitely worth folks` time if they haven`t checked it out already. Mike, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JASON: Thanks for having me, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to continue to keep a close eye on Afghanistan as the situation there clearly is changing sometimes by the hour. It`s going to be a big chunk of the time we spend on MEET THE PRESS on Sunday for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 Up next, with Democrats currently holding on to a razor-thin majority in Congress, when head over to the big board use the studio, as they say, to break down the brand-new data. It`s going to be critical in the fight over who controls Congress, who controls all the state legislatures, not just this year, potentially for the next decade?\par \par \uc2 And later, a standoff is developing between moderate Democrats and progressives could threaten the passage of President Biden`s entire legislative agenda. But will it?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It is manna for data nerds like me, the redistricting fight is officially kicked off, thanks to the release of the district level and county level census data which came out yesterday afternoon. So why is this data so important politically? Well, I think you know by now. The Democratic portrait of America could determine control of Congress not just for `22 but basically for the next decade. Really, all of the state legislatures around the country. So. let`s look at the big picture here.\par \par \uc2 The biggest thing here, America`s getting more diverse, less white. You can see here I mean, the Hispanic population from 2000 to 2020 has basically jumped 50 percent. The aging population in this country is basically almost gone up 100 percent. You see where the growth is, the white population has dropped here a good -- more than 10 percentage points so that obviously -- that`s one of the top lines.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Topline number two is the -- the other big story is the darker areas here in brown is basically a rural area. Well, guess what? That is where the population is declining.\par \par \uc2 All of the green on this map, and I know it`s a weird color green, evergreen for the screen, whatever you want to use, many of those areas are all have a growing population. So, all of the population growth was in these green areas, these all coincide with metro areas. The other big story is the depopulating if you will of rural America. This coincides with what we`ve seen in this new polarization between urban, suburban, and rural American population moving there, coupled with what we`re seeing in rural America, coupled with where the two parties are going, you can see this is going to perhaps create an ugly short-term clash of geographic polarization.\par \par \uc2 And then look here, just to reinforce this point, if you look all of the population growth is in the cities, New York City is up LA, up, Chicago, you name it. You know, there was a big movement 20 years ago, where it was the city populations were flat, and it was suburban and x-urban that was growing, that is not the case right now, this is where you`re seeing. And it`s going to cause be some interesting mapmaking challenges, which we`re going to discuss here coming up.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the one place where Republicans have the advantages, who draws the map? Their advantage is not as great as it was 10 years ago for what it`s worth. There was a big movement over the last decade into increasing the number of independent Commission to draw maps. And as you can see, in the number of actual Congressional districts, that`s more than doubled. 173 will be drawn by the commission, Lion`s share, that in California. Republicans controlled a lot more of the process 10 years ago than they do now but they still have a pretty good handle on this. Democrats basically are flat and their ability, I went back there and too quickly, and it`s a mixed bag for just a handful.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to dig in further and I just gave you a preview of what state I want to start with in terms of where redistricting and reapportionment is going with our friend Dave Wasserman. He`s of course the House Editor at the Cook Political Report, very into this topic. How he can do it? His \uc2 Twitter\uc2  handle is at redistricting. That`s how we ended this topic is. He doesn`t -- he wants redistricting to be his brand, not Wasserman.\par \par \uc2 All right, Dave. So, the big story being the population shifts and the geographic bias towards cities and suburban areas, which brings us to the challenge of Republicans draw the map in Texas for two new seats but all the population growth is in Democratic areas. How are they going to pull this off?\par \par \uc2 DAVE WASSERMAN, HOUSE EDITOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Yes, Chuck. The numbers yesterday, there`s just explosive growth along the I-35 corridor between Austin and San Antonio. Republicans currently hold 23 of the 36 congressional seats in Texas, but the state`s gaining two seats and nine of the 23 Republican seats gave Biden more than 47 percent of the vote. So, Republicans may have no choice but to draw at least one new democratic seat likely in Austin to shore up their own incumbents and that limits their ability to make big gains in Texas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, one of the other things on in Texas, you know, 10 years ago, they did a -- they did a hub and spoke in Austin, right? Which is they tried to take a Democratic district and essentially divided it into a bunch of Republican districts, divided up Austin, Utah did a similar thing with Salt Lake City, why does that not work for them this time?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: The growth in Democratic votes in the Austin area in particular, and Dallas, and Houston as well, is just too hot for Republicans to be able to protect all of their current members and get greedy and add a lot more new seats. So, I think a 25 to 13 breakdown in Texas is the most likely scenario with Republicans shoring up their own incumbents and grabbing two new seats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s go to a state where the Democrats control the process and where there is a loss of seat, and that is New York State. What`s fascinating here, Dave, and John Reese, executive producer, he`s a New York native, and I -- we`re talking about this. Not only did New York lose a district but non-New York City may lose another district. Meaning, New York City`s population growth demands another new Congressional district even as the state overall lost power. How are they going to pull this off?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: Yes, look. This is the story that no one`s talking about, Chuck. We always talked about how Republicans have a big gerrymandering advantage in Texas, Florida, Georgia, and North Carolina. New York is Democrat`s biggest redistricting weapon, even though it`s losing one seat. The growth in New York City exceeded expectations. Hakeem Jeffries, who Democrats believe is their speaker in waiting, and Sean Patrick Maloney the Chair of the D Triple C, they`re both from New York and they know they probably can`t hold on to the house majority without gerrymandering the state.\par \par \uc2 Look, they could purge as many as five of the eight Republicans in the U.S. delegation. They can get rid of one on Staten Island, one on Long Island, and three Upstate. And so, look, New York is a huge deal for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And finally, I want -- our best state as an example where a commission is in charge and we can talk about the politics of this later with Eric Holder about whether Democrats are tying it up. They would control this process under the rules of most other states but because they do a commission, this means a new swing state in Colorado, does it not?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: That`s right. And look, Colorado is one of three states with new commissions, independent commissions, to handle redistricting. Michigan and Virginia are the other two. And in Colorado competitiveness is a criteria. And the likely outcome in Colorado is a new district, somewhere in the Denver suburbs that is somewhat swingy. Probably would have voted for Trump in `16 but Biden in 2020. Given the trend line in the Denver suburbs, though, I like Democrats` chances of picking up a seat there for a 5-3 advantage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wasserman, big picture, Republicans are fighting one of these Democratic ideas in Congress that would basically create -- try to make it a commission everywhere to draw congressional districts. Given what you saw in the census, in 10 years, are Republicans going to regret not signing up for that?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: Well, Republicans already regret some of the choices they made in the last redistricting round. We saw in 2018 a number of these suburban seats that started off the decade as safe Republican districts. They --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Georgia 6th, Georgia 7th.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: They fell to Democrats. Right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: And so Republicans want to draw seats that are going to be able to withstand bad cycles in let`s say 2026 or 2028, and in order to do that they`re going to have to draw a lot of their suburban incumbents into rural areas. Republicans, the consultants I talked to, they are afraid of one word, density. Anywhere where there`s population density they are losing ground. And they know it. They need to move their districts into the countryside in order to shore them up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have a feeling there`s going to be a whole bunch of Republicans wishing they were in on fair districts. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Dave Wasserman, again, follow him at @redistrict. This is your mana from heaven. Enjoy map making for the next 18 months, sir.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: Happy Census Week, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, did you get enough? Because I got more. Former attorney general Eric Holder is going to be here to talk about the battle lines being drawn literally and figuratively. He`s essentially the guy in charge of the redistricting fight on behalf of Democrats when it comes to these maps. We`ll talk to him after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This redistricting fight is going to be a major political and legal battle. A lot of courts could decide what our maps look like. All of it could affect the balance of power here in Washington and in state capitals around the country. And before we even got our hands on the new Census data yesterday, Democratic groups are already launching legal challenges in some key places. So joining me now is the chair of one of those groups. It`s former attorney general Eric Holder. He`s from the National Democratic Redistricting Committee and he has been focused on this problem before it was cool.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Holder, it`s good to see you. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Good to see you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with, you know, in 1920 after the pandemic, we had a lot of problems with that census and they basically punted that census. Before we get to the census itself, why do you think President Biden and the Democratic-controlled Congress accepted this data and did not push for a -- basically go get it done right? I mean, are there going to be -- look, cities gained as it is, but there is a lot of concern there was a massive under count here. A lot of estimating. Why was this census accepted and should it have been?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, well, you know the Census had to deal with the pandemic. The Census had to deal with an administration that was not supportive, the Trump administration was not supportive of it. I think that, you know, the Census Bureau professionals did the best that they could under the circumstances. And I suspect that there is an under count, especially in the Hispanic community.\par \par \uc2 There have been historic undercounts of people of color in the census counts so I think that, you know, the count that we got is a pretty good one, but there`s always going to be an asterisk next to it, you know, not knowing given what the Trump administration tried to do with it to subvert the census count, given the pandemic, whether or not it is as accurate as it could have been.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I wonder if you saw what Mike Duggan, and he`s the mayor of Detroit, I mean, he`s really upset. He believes Detroit itself as a city was undercounted by 10 percent. And that stuff matters. It matters when it comes to federal funding. It matters when it comes to school fundings and things like that. Does he have any recourse?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, you know, there`s not much you can do when it comes to reapportionment and when it comes to redistricting. There are ways in which you can try to affect the count that will have an impact on the amount of federal funds that will go to the cities and to the counties later on, but in terms of pure politics and in terms of the determinations that have to be made over the next, you know, 12 months or so, there is not an awful lot of recourse there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, let`s talk about the idea of partisan power versus fairness. I put up a quote from June. It`s Gerry Connolly. He`s a Democrat from Fairfax County, and he says this. "We Democrats are cursed with this blindness about good government. In rabid partisan states that are controlled by Republicans, they`re carving up left and right, and we`re kind of unilaterally disarming. But having said that I still come down on the side of reforming this process because it`s got to start somewhere."\par \par \uc2 Are Democrats tying one arm behind their back? I mean, you look at Colorado, the example we did. You know, and they have partisan control. They could probably guarantee Democrats maybe not just the new district but maybe one more seat. We know if Republicans had that shot they`d be doing it, too. What is the right outcome here in your mind?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, I think fairness is still the talisman. That`s the thing that we should be following. I mean, I thought that Dave Wasserman`s, you know, analysis was an interesting one. The only thing that he didn`t inject -- and I`m not faulting him with this -- was to bring in the notion of fairness. If you put fairness into the equation it means that in New York, yes, there should be a decrease in the number of Republican representatives because there has been a loss of Republican leaning voters and an increase in the number of Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 If you look at Texas and you want fairness, you`ve seen an increase in the Hispanic population by about 50 percent, an increase in the non-white population by almost 90 percent, all which is Democrat leaning.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:11]\par \par \uc2 And if you want fairness there that would lead to an increase in the number of congressmen and state legislatures of Democratic congressmen and Democratic state legislatures in Texas. So there`s not a tension between fairness and Democrats doing better. In fact if it`s fair, Democrats will do far better. Democrats don`t have to cheat. We don`t have to gerrymander. Republicans do in order to deal with a nation that is more diverse, that is younger, that is more urban, that is more suburban, and that people need to focus on is less, less rural.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not every state, though, has the, you know, Colorado has a fairness sort of statute. Iowa does as well. And it`s unclear what the ballot initiative in Florida means. And in fact, let me focus on Florida. Do you think that is a fairness standard that you`re going to have to go to court to uphold?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, I suspect we probably will as we did in the last decade. There is a constitutional provision in Florida that requires that redistricting be done in a fair way. It is something that had to be taken to the Florida Supreme Court. The last time Democrats, progressives won you`re going to have a -- you`re going to face now a more conservative Florida Supreme Court. My hope would be that these judges on the Supreme Court will focus on precedent, will focus on following the law, following the constitution there, and come up with a fair result.\par \par \uc2 I don`t expect that we`re going to see from Republicans and the state legislature anything close to fairness. And so I suspect it will probably in the court in Florida.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You told Rachel Maddow in an interview earlier this week that you had some high confidence that there would be a carve out on voting rights in the Senate. And I guess the implications means that they would do this, you know, essentially suspend the filibuster issue if you were to pass this. Joe Manchin didn`t see it carve out. What gives you this optimism?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, I`d say I`m cautiously optimistic. You know, I`ve had conversations with Senator Schumer, with other people in the Senate. They have a process that they want to go through to try to convince those who at this point are supportive of the filibuster as it exists. So that we get to a point where when you have to make that choice between supporting the filibuster or supporting our democracy, and trying to push back against those threats to our democracy, the Democrats end up on the right side.\par \par \uc2 So as I said I`m cautiously optimistic that we can end up in the right place. We`ll see, you know, when they come back, and when as Senator Schumer says, the first thing they consider is this new voting legislation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, considering what we did find in the census, and that the suburban and urban are getting all the population, I do think a lot of Republicans are going to regret missing this fair district moment. Do you think you can find some Republicans who end up supporting this -- the sort of the fair district national option here because of the reality that`s going to hit them in the face in about a decade?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: I have been searching for Republican partners in this fight for fairness in the redistricting process since January 2017 when we formed this. Arnold Schwarzenegger has stood up. Former Senator John Danforth has helped. I don`t think we`re going to find any Republican help here. This is in their DNA now. They don`t have confidence in their ability to win fair elections or to win a fair redistricting process.\par \par \uc2 They think that they`ve got to put their hands, put their thumb on the scale. So I suspect we`re not going to find any Republican allies in this fight for fairness.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Former Attorney General Eric Holder, like I said, his organization, it`s going to be probably at the forefront of all of these battles when they head to court.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Holder, good to see you. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up ahead, we`re going to keep our eyes right now on a meeting of independent advisors to the CDC. They`re voting on whether to recommend that third dose of the vaccine for some Americans. We know there is a list of Americans that are going to be recommended for it. The question is how big will that list get? That`s what we`re waiting to find out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:18]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The CDC`s Vaccine Advisory Committee is voting as we speak on whether to recommend that third dose of an MRNA COVID vaccine meaning essentially Moderna or \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , for those with compromised immune systems. Now this vote comes after the FDA amended its emergency guidance for the vaccines last time to include a third shot for specific immune compromised groups.\par \par \uc2 So according to this advisory committee, only about seven million Americans or just under 3 percent of the country, would fall under the new FDA and CDC guidance. Some of those qualifications appear to include people with immunosuppressive diseases, cancer patients, and those who are taking drugs that suppressed their immune system. They don`t have a specific group yet or a line that they`ve drawn. We will keep you updated, though, on that vote when it occurs.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a possible Democratic deadlock leaves President Biden`s agenda potentially hanging in the balance. Will either moderates or progressives drop their demands on which bill needs to be voted on first and if not, is either bill going anywhere?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:59]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. There is more pressure on House Speaker Nancy Pelosi after nine moderate Democrats have signed a new letter stating their refusal to vote for the party`s $3.5 trillion budget blueprint. That vote will be this month by the way until the House passes the bipartisan infrastructure legislation. Progressive Democrats are demanding the opposite order and refusing to vote for the bipartisan bill until the budget reconciliation bill gets passed.\par \par \uc2 Now the speaker has been clear that she was supporting the position that puts them both together, which is the progressive position here. But the new demand from the moderates could complicate matters. It`s all about if people are going to make threats and keep their threats or not.\par \par \uc2 So back with me today, after being interrupted by President Biden yesterday is the chair of the House Progressive Caucus, Washington Democratic Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal.\par \par \uc2 I went through an entire question and then had to interrupt you so -- but I`ll ask the same question I was asking then. What`s wrong with doing the bipartisan bill now?\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): Well, Chuck, it`s good to see you and I`m always happy to be interrupted by the president when he`s talking about lowering prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Which of course is in the Build Back Better reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well done.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: So listen, the deal has always been the Democratic agenda. President Biden`s agenda that he`d laid out to the House in February and that we all ran on and won on. And so what we have always said is, progressives are absolutely committed to delivering on the entire agenda, because if we create jobs through an infrastructure package, that`s great. But if we don`t allow women to get childcare, they don`t get to get those jobs.\par \par \uc2 If we don`t allow people to get free community college and trade school education, they don`t get to get those jobs. So if we are going to build back better, we have to do all of these things at the same time because the urgency is so real. The need for childcare, the need for paid leave, health care and of course addressing climate change, given the IPCC`s report that just came out, this is not something that can wait around. So we want to deliver both bills to the president in September and have him sign them into law so we deliver for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a trust issue here? I mean, do you fear that if somehow the bipartisan deal, let`s say -- look, let`s say Speaker Pelosi on August 23rd, you come back, she says, all right, I`ll bring it up, let`s go, do you somehow fear moderates are going to walk away from you on the bigger bill?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Yes, Chuck, you know how it works in D.C. works. Everything works on timelines and everything works on making sure people have urgency and momentum to move quickly. The best way to keep the urgency and momentum up is to do both bills together, and to make sure that everyone understands that whatever their priority is, whether it`s in the infrastructure bill or whether it`s in the Build Back Better reconciliation package, the way we get them done is to move them at the same time to the president`s desk.\par \par \uc2 Without that, you`re going to have a lot of people trying to push for one piece and not voting for another piece. And that`s true of progressives, but it`s also true of the Democrats who might not want to.\par \par \uc2 One more thing, Chuck, I`ve got to say, you know, we can`t call people moderate Democrats who vote against childcare, paid leave and addressing climate change. That is a Democratic agenda, it is the president`s agenda, and it`s what we promised.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it sounds like if these Democrats decide to essentially not to support the framework in August and they follow through on this threat, it sounds like relationships inside the conference would break apart pretty badly. Is that your sense?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: It would be a very bad breach of trust because of course this is what the president said. This is what Senator Schumer said. This is what Nancy Pelosi said from the beginning is we are delivering on all of these things. Progressives have been very clear for three months, nobody should be surprised that we did not oppose the bipartisan bill moving forward just on a tiny piece of jobs because we knew that there was a commitment to doing the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Now, the other folks, need to be willing to go along with the team, and deliver on the reconciliation package so that we can deliver the entire package. So that -- and this is just a really important point, Chuck. We are talking about people feeling differently about their lives, their livelihoods and their opportunities, knowing that they can get childcare, paid leave, health care, go to community college and address the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: This is absolutely critical.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman, you were not here during the health care battle.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: True.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I`m watching a phenomenon develop with the reconciliation bill that`s similar to health care which is the parts are popular. The individual ideas and reconciliation are very popular. They poll very well. But the overall package doesn`t poll as well. Perhaps it`s the price tag, perhaps it`s -- it`s confusion about what`s in it. How do you avoid that similar political problem that Democrats entered with health care? Eventually they were right. The parts were popular. We are where we are today 10 years later but it caused a lot of political pain in that process. How do you avoid that here?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: You know, Chuck, I just looked at polling this morning, the most recent polling is that the reconciliation package, the Build Back Better package, is just as -- almost just as popular. It`s a few points less than the jobs package but it`s quite remarkable and people do not mind, they`re not put off by the price tag, and I`ll tell you something else. We`re talking about $3.5 trillion for this reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, of course the wealthiest individuals, the billionaires and millionaires and the biggest corporations are going to pay their fair share, and the actual amount that is spent on this package is going to be -- if we had our ways -- zero, and I think it`s going to be very, very small.\par \par \uc2 So don`t forget that Americans, the popularity of this package gets even higher when we are clear that we`re going to make wealthy pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s what campaigns are about, that`s what messaging is about. We`ll be watching as the fall develops. And I guess we`re going to see guys in August, a little bit sooner.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Jayapal, I`m glad we got the entire interview. So thank you and thank you for coming back.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Me, too. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. If it`s Sunday, MEET THE PRESS in your local NBC station. As I said, Afghanistan is going to be a big focus for us on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage continues with Garrett Haake right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210813eh8d0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for August 11, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Kerry Sanders, Dasha Burns, Leigh Ann Caldwell,  Garrett Haake, Kelly Cobiella, Yasmin Vossoughian, Monica Alba \par \uc2 8,764 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:16]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: If it`s Wednesday. We`ve got a major Delta dilemma hospitalization soaring across the South, two levels higher than anything we`ve seen in the pandemic, and state and local leaders cannot agree on how to keep kids safe in schools.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with COVID raging, President Biden will deliver remarks at the White House this hour as he tries to keep the pressure on Congress for trillions of dollars in new spending. We`ll bring you his remarks coming up.\par \par \uc2 And later, as the U.S. finalizes its withdrawal, new warnings from U.S. officials that Afghanistan could fall to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  potentially in a matter of weeks much earlier than feared.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Andrea Mitchell in for just a few moments for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 But right now, within 18 months into this pandemic, and several months after vaccines became widely available in this country, states are breaking grim pandemic records, breaking them. The crisis is most acute in the South amid a raging debate about masking in schools.\par \par \uc2 States from Texas to Missouri, Arkansas, Louisiana, and Florida are all seeing hospitalizations surge to record or near-record heights. The number of people hospitalized in Florida is now larger than any state has ever seen during this pandemic surpassing the surge in New York last year. That is a remarkable statistic because 50 percent of Florida has been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 That New York number came at the start of the pandemic long before the vaccine was created. That gives you a sense of just how contagious this Delta variant is. Thankfully, though, fewer people are dying because many vulnerable Americans have been vaccinated. Still, the dangers are very real.\par \par \uc2 The federal government is sending hundreds of ventilators to Florida as its hospital system is pushed to its limits. The state has now also broken its record of pediatric hospitalizations. That scary statistic comes as Governor Ron DeSantis has gone to great lengths to oppose mask mandates in the state, even threatening to withhold salaries of Public Schools Officials who defy him.\par \par \uc2 Some local officials, superintendents, and mayors are defying the governor and imposing mask mandates in schools to keep children safe. We`re going to speak with one of those mayors in just a moment. And that comes as new polling suggests a majority of parents support school masking policies. The \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  found that 63 percent of parents favor requiring unvaccinated students and staff to wear masks.\par \par \uc2 What`s happening in Florida in terms of cases and hospitalizations, and in terms of kids and controversy over masks has become a microcosm of the crisis from other parts of this -- other parts of the country just across the United States as we enter yet another dark chapter in this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kerry Sanders is in Riviera Beach, Florida. I know where you are, Kerry, and I know you know where you are. That`s one of the places where the school district is requiring masks. Also with us, Broward County Mayor Steve Geller, and NBC`S Dasha Burns is in Johnson City, Tennessee where children are heading back to school without mask requirements. Another place for cases among kids are rising.\par \par \uc2 Kerry, I think first to you in Riviera, Florida. Talk to us about what`s going on there.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And let`s talk a little bit about the schools and what`s going on here. So first of all, just across the state, there`s been a 24 percent increase in the last week of children testing positive for Coronavirus. So, that is significant.\par \par \uc2 Then wrap in, and I just want to go back to the statistic that you shared at the top that Florida now has more people in hospitals with Coronavirus than at the peak in New York. So, that underscores how much this has changed and shifted to the new Epicenter here in Florida with the backdrop of kids going back to school this week here in Palm Beach County, other counties, Broward and Dade later this month, and so what they`re looking at is potentially kids coming to school and spreading the virus.\par \par \uc2 We see that percentage in the hospitals. I mean, the video that we have here inside Joe DiMaggio is heartbreaking to see children in the ICU and in the emergency room here who are fighting Coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 The governor says he will not only withhold the salaries of School Superintendents and School Board Members if they defy his order of no mask mandate, but he will also restrict the state money that goes to counties. That`s about $9,000 per student in a year. This county here, 179,000 students. We`re talking about some serious money and we`re just didn`t talking about Palm Beach County.\par \par \uc2 So, here they`ve decided kids must wear masks but the parents can sign something and they can opt out. In Broward County, the decision will be there`s no opting out, you must. You must go to school with a mask when school starts later this month.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what some of the parents had to say about the government telling them what their children must do for safety purposes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:24]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY FRY, PARENT: I want them to be protected when they go back to school. I don`t want to send them into an unsafe environment and we know that masks will keep kids safe.\par \par \uc2 KELLY DOYLE, PARENT: If people want to wear masks, it`s absolutely up to their choice to do that but as a parent who does not want their kid wearing a mask, we should have a choice as well. It shouldn`t just be one-sided.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: This is very much a political debate in the state of Florida and I think the best way to reflect that here on MEET THE PRESS DAILY is to take a look at the counties that have pushed back against the governor and look at how those counties voted in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The counties that are coming up with either opt-out or defying directly that there will be absolute mask mandates, those counties voted for Biden.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Andrea, as you know, Florida is now more of a red state, go voting for Trump, but in these counties that are pushing back against the Republican Governor Ron DeSantis, those who are the ones that voted for Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day here in Palm Beach County, and we`re going to see this elsewhere, one of the biggest challenges is going to be for teachers themselves that then become police officers in the classroom of this one`s not wearing a mask, this one is wearing a mask. Should this one that is not wearing a mask, should they be wearing it?\par \par \uc2 The teachers here in Palm Beach County tell me they don`t get a list of the kids that have opted out so how are they then supposed to enforce the rules? And we know eventually, Andrea, kids are kids, they`re going to get into fights over things like why am I having to wear a mask? Why is he not wearing a mask? Back and forth, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Of course, kids are kids and adults should be adults. Kerry Sanders, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Geller, last night the Broward School Board voted to keep masks mandatory in schools and that`s defying Governor DeSantis` order. You`re the only county in South Florida to do that so far. So far, you know, what`s your reaction to what`s going on? What he said today?\par \par \uc2 STEVE GELLER, MAYOR OF BROWARD COUNTY, FLORIDA: Well, understand that that is the school board which is separately elected from the county commission. Although I agree with what they`ve done, you`ve talked about setting records.\par \par \uc2 I remember we -- it was only about two weeks ago that Florida set a record of 10,389 new cases -- new hospitalizations. Today, as you said we`re over 15,000. Not only is that a new record, it`s 50 percent higher than the old records which were at only a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the number of new cases per 100,000 which is one of the major ways of measuring, it should be under 10 over 10-year in the red zone. June 15, we were at six, July one we were at 14, July 15 we`re at 37, July 30 we were at 85, today we`re at 118 new cases per 100,000. Again, that should be under 10. That numbers are horrible.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned children at Joe DiMaggio Children`s Hospital, which I think Kerry mentioned or somebody mentioned, if you look at just the number of children that were admitted, excuse me that went to the emergency room - - the pediatric emergency room just the Joe DiMaggio Children`s Hospital, as of -- as of earlier in June, that number was -- in June, it was 36, July 27 it was 190, by the end of the month, four days later, it was 240. That`s children that are going to the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 If nothing else is going to get you to wear a mask, if you`re not going to wear a mask to protect yourself, your friends, your family, your community, your country, wear it to protect the kids.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: It doesn`t make a lot of sense. But mayor, the governor doubled down today, he said there`s actually positive signs in Florida`s numbers that you could be turning the corner soon, you`re not seeing any indication of that and the data you`ve been citing?\par \par \uc2 GELLER: Yes, there -- if you look at the day today, today may be lower than yesterday, yesterday may be lower than the day before, I haven`t checked but if you look at the week to week, every week it`s going up. The only silver lining and it`s a small one is this is also caused an increase in vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 We had been at our peak, we were at almost 175,000 vaccinations per week, we have gone under 10,000, now the vaccinations are going back up. Three weeks ago, it was 16,667, two weeks ago is 26,434, last week it was 28,245. So, the tiny, tiny dim silver lining in this horrible expansion that we`re having is it`s causing people to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Again, I do not understand why people won`t protect their friends, won`t do the patriotic thing, and get vaccinated to protect their country. And if you won`t do it for any other reason, do it to protect the children.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:44]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And is the governor is messaging hampering your efforts to get the virus under control in your county?\par \par \uc2 GELLER: Yes, ma`am. I mean, one of the things that bother us, you know, I`ve -- I was in the legislature for 20 years, I always have a great deal of respect for the Office of the governor but we`ve heard the governor and the legislature consistently tell Washington, Washington, please give local governments the opportunity because what works in New York, doesn`t work in North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Well, when I tell the governor and the legislature, what works in Bradford County, a population of 28,000 doesn`t work in Broward County with a population of 2 million, crickets. I mean, they don`t respond and we`re just asking them to be consistent. The same way that they asked the federal government not to exert total control over the states. We in local government would like the ability to respond to local conditions. And again, if they`re not prepared to do with the statewide mask mandate, let us do it locally.\par \par \uc2 I promise you we`re not talking about doing another economic shutdown, we`re not planning on closing businesses, we want to protect our people and the business community. In many cases, we would require masks.\par \par \uc2 I keep hearing about, oh, we have freedom. You do but there are limits. You may have the freedom to drink, but you don`t have the freedom to drive while drunk. And that`s what we`re trying to explain to the anti-maskers that yes, you have certain rights, whatever you want to do when you`re home but when you`re running the risk of infecting the general public, it`s no different than driving while intoxicated. You`re endangering the public.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And does it make any sense of -- returning to schools for a moment, to have mask mandates in schools without vaccination mandates for teachers?\par \par \uc2 GELLER: I would support vaccination mandates for teachers while understanding, again, the same issue that we have in local government which is we all have union contracts and you have to negotiate with the unions. We think before you`re able to mandate that but that`s a school board issue.\par \par \uc2 What doesn`t make sense to me not as a school board member because I`m not but again, if you are letting some people wear masks, and some people not, you need to remember masks are protecting other people.\par \par \uc2 So, you`re not wearing a mask, or your child not wearing a mask is certainly endangering the other students in the school if your children have COVID and that`s one of the reasons why everybody needs to really be wearing masks. In order for that to work, we have already mandated in all county buildings, you must wear masks.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Mayor Geller in Broward County, Florida, thank you very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 GELLER: Thank you, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And turning to Dasha in Washington County, Tennessee, where schools have been open for a week without mask mandates. You`re in an Appalachian part of Tennessee, a state that fired its Health Commissioner not too long ago. So, what are you seeing as the challenges there?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC REPORTER: Andrea, I have spent a lot of time in the Appalachian Highlands over the last year, spending a lot of time with health workers here who have not only been dealing with the crisis of COVID-19 but also a crisis of misinformation and politicization around this virus.\par \par \uc2 They say it has led members of their community not to take those steps that are necessary to protect themselves and to protect their neighbors, and now they say it`s the kids that they`re most worried about valid health though -- one of the larger hospital systems in this region yesterday reported that 29 percent of positive COVID cases in their system were among those under the age of 18.\par \par \uc2 And Andrea, Washington County Schools reopened here just over a week ago. They have already reported 50 positive cases among students and staff, and 194 are in quarantine and despite these numbers, there is no mask requirement here, there are no social distancing requirements, and there are no strict protocols around quarantining students who have been exposed at school.\par \par \uc2 And Andrea, these trends are terrifying the health workers that I`ve been speaking with, they`re scared of what`s coming in the next couple of weeks. I want you to hear from the CEO of the valid health Niswonger Children`s Hospital here, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:33]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA CARTER, CEO, NISWONGER CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: I think the hardest part for me personally is trying to get people to understand, you know, I stood in the pediatric intensive care unit last week and watched everything that was unfolding and it was just heartbreaking. It`s important that we try to relay that message to the community, again, to counter some of that misinformation and to counter some of the things out there that just aren`t true. This is affecting children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Andrea, I`ve spoken to some parents who are scared to send their kids to school, one mom with a son who`s immunocompromised, she feels like she is putting a mask on him. She is making the choice to give him a mask but she doesn`t know what happens once he walks through those doors. I spoke to the superintendent here who says that they`re re-evaluating the situation every day but he says that at the end of the day, it`s going to be a local decision and personal choice is an important factor here, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Dasha Burns, thank you so much in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 And this Friday at 11 a.m. Eastern, MSNBC is going to have a special hour devoted to answering your back-to-school Coronavirus pandemic questions. So, submit your questions on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  using #MSNBCanswers or email, talk@msnbc.com.\par \par \uc2 And coming up here next, we`re expecting to hear from President Biden on the next steps for his domestic agenda as the Senate voted last night to start work on the Democrats massive $3.5 trillion, that`s trillion with a T plan that some of Biden`s biggest legislative priorities is in. We`ll bring you live those remarks when they happen.\par \par \uc2 Plus, thousands of firefighters continuing to battle the monster Dixie wildfire burning in California. Weather conditions are worsening, it`s possibly another town that could be burned off the map.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:32]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I welcome you -- welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We`re expecting to hear from President Biden in just the next few minutes, a day after the Senate passed a Bipartisan Infrastructure bill and hours after they began working on a sweeping $3.5 trillion budget resolution. The fate of essentially the entire White House`s domestic agenda is now tied up in both these bills, so it`s going to take an all-hands-on-deck effort to sell them, especially on that budget resolution where Senators, Manchin, and Sinema are already bristling about its price tag.\par \par \uc2 While Senate Committees have more than a month to decide what makes it into the final resolution, the early blueprint contains about a decade`s worth of domestic priorities, many taken straight from President Biden`s campaign platform.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, Majority Leader Schumer said that everything is on the table when it comes to the reconciliation package. But as long as the details are in flux, the message is in flux as well.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill, and also with us, New York Times Chief White House Correspondent Peter Baker, who was also, of course, an MSNBC Political Analyst.\par \par \uc2 Peter, President Biden is about to make his first sell speech on reconciliation and many are just seeing the price tag on this signature bill. So, how is he going to sell this, especially to those Senate moderates and actually the moderates in the House, whereas we know, Nancy Pelosi only has a three-seat margin?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Well, that`s exactly right. You know, basically, President Biden has lumped his entire domestic agenda practically into this one package, right. As it set were doing the entire Great Society all with one bill and he`s doing -- as you point out on correctly, climate, immigration, Medicare, universal pre- K, Community College for free, all these big priorities wrapped into one very big expensive package.\par \par \uc2 We`re back to this sort of kind of defining debate that Washington used to have over the size and scale of government, right. Very, very familiar to those of us who covered this going back decades in which Democrats advocate for a more robust view, and Republicans said this is tax and spending gone amok.\par \par \uc2 And the problem for President Biden, as you point out, rightly, is he barely has a margin in the House and has only a 50-50 Senate with no votes to spare in the Senate. That means it`s going to be a big dance.\par \par \uc2 You already heard as you rightly point out Senators, Sinema and Manchin say that even though they voted to advance the package blueprint earlier today, that they`re not happy with the bottom lines. Too expensive, they`re going to have to pull it back.\par \par \uc2 Schumer is trying to balance those feelings on the part of the moderates with Senator Bernie Sanders, AOC wing of the party, which is saying, this is our one chance to get a lot of this stuff done, let`s not be anything other than the most ambitious we can be. And that`s something where you don`t know how it`s going to fall because it can take weeks, if not months to play this out.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Leigh Ann, the story for the next couple of months, it`s going to be that price tag the scope of moving on this Senate bill, that reconciliation that was just passed. So where are they starting, they must be exhausted, they were up all night?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Andrea, the Senate was up all night and so was our Capitol Hill team as well, covering all of this. But as far as moving forward, the price tag at this moment is $3.5 trillion. Now, that is the ceiling, not the floor.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator Schumer in a press conference just in the past hour, he said that the Party is united, that they want to do more than just this traditional Bipartisan Infrastructure bill but, how much more is the question, how big are Democrats willing to go?\par \par \uc2 He also said as Peter pointed out, that some members wanted more than $3.5 trillion, and then we heard from Senator Joe Manchin this morning in a statement saying that $3.5 trillion is too much.\par \par \uc2 Well, another thing that Senator Schumer said at his press conference is, he did not rule out coming below 3.5 trillion if he has to if that is going to be what it takes to get all of his members together. It`s a very complicated dance that he and House Speaker Nancy Pelosi are starting on.\par \par \uc2 And we also are just told about two Democratic sources who were on a conference call with Speaker Pelosi that she doubled down on her timeline of passing these two infrastructure bills, that she is going to hold off on this bipartisan bill until there`s a passage in the Senate from this big reconciliation package. So, that timeline does not seem to be shifting in the House of Representatives at this point, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, well, that gives her some, I guess, leverage in both sides, but is it all or nothing? Leigh Ann?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:05]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, it -- we`ll see what is happening. I mean, so, there was a group of moderates who have said that they want to vote on this Bipartisan bill immediately and she`s having to weigh that against the progressive.\par \par \uc2 There seem to be a lot more of the progressives who are saying, wait a minute, no, we need to see that this reconciliation, this 3.5 trillion Human Infrastructure bill is able to pass the Senate before we are willing to give the Republicans a bipartisan win. And that is the side that Speaker Pelosi is taking at this moment, and that`s the prevailing position in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 And she can also count votes, she knows that for the time being there are more people on that side of the position than there are of the moderates who want a vote immediately. So, she is insistent that she is doing the right thing and getting her party together. And as we all know that Speaker Pelosi has been masterful before in unifying her conference. We`ll see if she`s able to do it again, Andrea?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No, I would never underestimate her, never have. Peter Baker, how active is the President going to be because a lot of people are saying that that first bipartisan package would never have passed if he had not -- he had not been out campaigning for it?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I think that`s right. But the challenge for him is different this time, right. The challenge before was proven that he could reach across the aisle and bring Republicans on board. He succeeded in doing that, at least in the Senate so far with this physical infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 The question now on the Human Infrastructure bill to use a term they use is, can he bring his own party behind, can he bring the progressives into the full, can he bring the moderates into the pool? Because they don`t plan to get any Republican votes, which means they need almost every single Democrat that he has out there and that`s going to be the big challenge for the President now.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Leigh Ann, what about the Senate Republicans? They voted for the bipartisan bill. Where do you think that they are absolutely against this next bill -- the Reconciliation bill?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Absolutely against this Reconciliation bill. They are willing to go home to this bipartisan -- take this Bipartisan bill back to their states and show the transportation projects that are about to be -- that could in the near future if this reaches the President`s desk. This started, and they are also very happy with the Democrats taking all of the heat from them on this $3.5 trillion bill that they say is going to be bad for the economy, that they say is going to increase more inflation, and they think that that is going to be a political loser. And so, they feel very comfortable where they stand.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell sided with the Democrats and 18 other Republicans on this bipartisan bill. And, you know, Senator Roy Blunt, a member of his leadership team said McConnell`s, you could call him an infrastructure Republican.\par \par \uc2 He knows that infrastructure is good for his members, he knows it`s good for the country, but that is where the bipartisanship stops for Leader McConnell especially on this big major Human Infrastructure proposal, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, thanks to Leigh Ann, and thanks to Peter. And Leigh Ann, I hope you get some rest at least for the first part of this recess.\par \par \uc2 And as all of you who watch this hour, you know, I am not usually here at 1:30. Chuck is having some technical issues with his studio and as we continue to work on them, I`m going to hand it off to Garrett Haake, right here after the break. I`ll be heading over to the State Department for today`s daily briefing. More on that later on.\par \par \uc2 And coming up. Garrett will have the new reporting today that the Biden administration is preparing for Kabul, possibly falling in just a few months as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  continues its raging advance. Now seizing control of three more provincial capitals. Stay with us this is MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:42]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hi, everybody. Welcome back. Thank you for your patience with MSNBC musical chairs. I`m Garret Haake picking up MSNBC`s coverage.\par \par \uc2 And with the U.S. set to complete its withdrawal from Afghanistan a month from now, the country may look a lot like it did before U.S. troops first arrived 20 years ago. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  continues to gain territory by the day, capturing seven provincial capitals just since Friday. The "Washington Post" is reporting that U.S. officials warned that the collapse of the Afghan capital of Kabul could come sooner than expected, possibly within a mere matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 To give you a sense of just how quickly the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is regaining control, the Foundation for the Defense of Democracy`s long war journal posted a timelapse video showing the Taliban`s advances since April. Take a look at this. Areas under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  control are in red. Areas under government control are in gray. And you can see just how quickly that map transforms from gray to red.\par \par \uc2 Our own Kelly Cobiella joins me now from Kabul.\par \par \uc2 And Kelly, you`ve been on the ground there for a little bit now. Tell us what you`re seeing in Afghanistan`s capital and how they are watching what`s happening in the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Well, you really see the impact of that rapid advance by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  when you go to some of these parks in Kabul, and talk to the people who have been displaced because all of them say that they came with next to nothing, and these are people who are educated.\par \par \uc2 They had good government jobs. They had homes in their hometowns and villages and cities, and they had to leave literally in the dead of night at times. With a moment`s notice. And make a very harrowing journey down to Kabul.\par \par \uc2 A lot of them are talking about what the situation was like when the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rolled in. In some cases, government forces, they say, completely gave up. In others, they actually did put up a fight and quite a long fight.\par \par \uc2 What`s happening now is, that because so much fighting is ongoing in different parts of the country, as people continue to flee to Kabul, this is sort of the safe space in the country, and thousands of people are coming in to the city. They`re coming with nothing. They`re homeless, and it`s becoming a real problem and a potential humanitarian catastrophe. Today the vice president ordered the Minister of Hajj to open the mosques in order to allow these displaced people to at least have a roof over their heads and something to eat and drink at night.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:07]\par \par \uc2 A lot of children. A lot of women. We spoke to one student today whose family is from the north, and he said that his father is still there but they sent the women and children first to make sure that they could get out of harm`s way before the men left. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kelly, Kabul is still I think as secure as it relatively has been over time. Is there a sense that the capital at least is protected or that it`s just a matter of time until the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  controls all of Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: I mean, when you talk to people here there`s definitely that worry. People are terrified, frankly. They see what`s happening in other parts of the country, and they`re really, really worried that it is only a matter of time. We spoke to a teacher today who said, you know, I don`t understand how this is happening. How could we be in this position again? And she said that she fears for her life, she fears for the lives of the young female students who she`s been teaching now for more than 10 years.\par \par \uc2 On the flip side, the government is definitely not saying that they`re giving up, and you wouldn`t expect them to. There was a shake-up in the army today, and in addition to that, a number of warlords have been called in to muster their militias and join the fight. In fact, one, a former vice president, left for the north -- from the capital to the north, late last night. His men are with him, and he -- his spokesperson is telling us that he`s actually going to the lead the charge tonight.\par \par \uc2 They`re going to take on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in the city of -- or in the surrounds of Mazar-i-Sharif. That`s the biggest city in the north. A city of about 500,000 people. So the fighting is going to intensify.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Stunning that after 20 years of training the Afghan army we are calling on warlords here at the end of the war to back up government forces.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And as we await those remarks from President Biden, coming up, the latest on the political fall of Andrew Cuomo as New York state is about to meet its first female governor in history. We`ll be live in Albany, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:26]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Following New York Governor Andrew Cuomo`s stunning resignation announcement yesterday where he continued to dispute allegations of harassment and unwanted touching, the biggest questions now are about, what happens next? What`s next for the impeachment inquiry into his conduct? For the criminal complaint filed by one of his accusers and for the other ongoing investigations? And what about what`s next for the state of New York itself as it faces a resurgent virus on top of the current political turmoil, as New Yorkers just want to move forward?\par \par \uc2 Many of those questions are now going to be asked of Lieutenant Governor Kathy Hochul, who was about to become New York`s first female governor ever. We`re expected to hear from her for the first time since Cuomo`s resignation announcement in just about 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from Albany is MSNBC`s Yasmin Vossoughian.\par \par \uc2 And Yasmin, what are you hearing on the ground there about how local lawmakers are reacting, about what`s next for Governor Cuomo? He`ll still be the governor for another two weeks.\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. You`ve got a lot of what`s next in that intro, so I`m going to try to answer as many of those questions as I can.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Take your time.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: In the two minutes or so that I`m usually allotted as you well know. Let`s talk impeachment first. Right? That`s the big question. Are they going to continue to pursue impeachment? What I`m hearing overall from those before both Republicans and Democrats speaking to is that resignation does not necessarily satisfy accountability. I`ve heard that from a New York state senator, who said that to me specifically.\par \par \uc2 We heard from the Judiciary chair, Lavine, on Monday, who said even if this governor does resign we`re going to continue on with this impeachment investigation. And then there`s Mary Beth Walsh, who is a member of the Judiciary, who I actually spoke to a little bit earlier who echoes that sentiment. A part of the Republican caucus here. Here`s what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY BETH WALSH (R), NEW YORK STATE ASSEMBLYWOMAN: People are calling my office and saying, I lost a loved one to COVID in a nursing home, and a I don`t want -- if the governor did something wrong if he fudged the data or if he rewrote the report, or whatever choices that he did, I want him held accountable for that. But I worry that the political will to actually go through with the impeachment is starting to wane on the majority side.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So you got to remember the impeachment investigation was not just about these sexual harassment claims, Garrett, right? It was about a controversy surrounding nursing homes during the pandemic in the early spring. It was about the Mario Cuomo bridge.\par \par \uc2 It was about the governor`s book deals. So it seems as if at this point those investigations are continuing on. Right? We`ve got a couple of days that we`ve got to look for. One is Friday. That is when the deadline for the governor to submit his counter material for the impeachment investigation and then there`s Monday when the Judiciary meets. We`re going to get more information then.\par \par \uc2 You`re talking about the criminal investigations in five separate areas of New York state. The criminal complaint filed in Albany, that`s going to continues on. This resignation doesn`t affect that. The criminal investigations in those five separate areas of New York, those are continuing on as well. This resignation does not affect this.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And then you asked, what is ahead for New York state, well, you mentioned --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yasmin --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And that is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yasmin, we`re going to have to set aside --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`re going to have to set aside New York state for a moment as the president of the United States is at the podium. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- seventh largest month of job creation in U.S. history, making the administration the first ever to add four million jobs in the first six months in office. And then in the past 24 hours, we`ve seen the Senate advance two key pieces of my economic agenda. The Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill and the Budget Resolution that is the framework for more Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 Today I`m pleased to share more good news with the American people. The latest report on consumer prices show that we`ve expanded some easing -- excuse me, the expected easing we thought was going to come has increased, that we`ve seen a good monthly report. Monthly Core Consumer Price Index is down by nearly two-thirds from its pace over the past three months. And when you take out the goods directly impacted by the pandemic, like cars and airplane tickets, and the monthly core consumer price index that is less than two-tenths of 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 So here`s where we stand. Jobs are up, and monthly price increases have come down. Economic growth is up, the fastest in 40 years, and unemployment is coming down. So I would argue the Biden economic plan is working. And historic investments are on the way as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 This isn`t accidental. It`s a result of our strategy to get shots in arms, grow the economy from the bottom up and the middle out and it`s the rest of the result of the American rescue plan and everything else that we`ve done. And it`s a result of the grit and determination and really hard work of the American people.\par \par \uc2 But even with all these progress, a lot of families are still feeling the pinch. Family budgets remain tight, and paychecks don`t go as far as they need to. That has been the reality in the millions of households all across America for too long. That`s why I want to talk today about what we`re going to do to try to ease the burden on families right now, and what we need to do to help them succeed over the longer term.\par \par \uc2 First, for millions of families, help is on the way right now thanks to the American Rescue Plan. On Friday, about 40 million families will receive their second monthly payment as part of our tax cut for families with children. $300 for each child under the age of 6, and $250 for every child 6 through the age of 17. That`s money for diapers, food, rent, school supplies, fees and equipment for the child to join sports teams and dance class. Most of all, as my dad used to say, it just gives a parent a little bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 The money is a game changer. And so I would argue for some it`s really a life saver. Economists also tell us that those kind of tax cuts boost test scores, college attendance and lifetime earnings for children. A win-win, and that`s why I`m glad that early this morning Congress took an important step to make sure that this tax cut for families with children does not expire next year. So let`s keep this tax cut going. And not raise taxes on middle-class families.\par \par \uc2 The second point I`d like to make is we`re talking about taking action that alleviates global supply chain challenges that keep prices higher than they should be. For example, we`re tracking congestion at the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach. The largest ports in the United States, where increased shipping traffic and the challenges of operating safely in a pandemic are creating disruptions. Those disruptions impact everything from how much our food costs to when deliveries arrive.\par \par \uc2 So my administration is bringing together port operators, shipping lines, labor unions, trucking companies, railroads and others to speed up the ports` operations. Right now our experts believe the major independent forecasters agree as well that these bottlenecks and price spikes will reduce as our economy continues to heal. Well, today`s consumer price report points in that direction. We will keep a careful eye on inflation each month, and trust the Fed to take appropriate action, if and when it`s needed.\par \par \uc2 Third, I`ve directed my administration to crack down on what some major players are doing in the economy that are keeping prices higher than they need be. Take your grocery bill. When big agriculture operations consolidate, they put a squeeze on small and family farms, making them pay more for seed, paying them less for what they produce, and raising prices on what your groceries -- what you pay for your groceries at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 My executive order opens up competition in the agricultural business. Gives more farmers a chance to compete, which will give Americans more food choices at lower cost.\par \par \uc2 Fourthly, we are taking action to address gas prices as well. Today gas prices are lower than they were earlier in this decade, but they`re still high enough to create a pinch on working families. One key thing about the infrastructure bill that just passed the Senate is there are no gas tax increases. No gas tax increases. I made that absolutely clear that I would not raise gas taxes, and I`m glad everyone in the Senate seemed to agree with that, but that`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 Recently we`ve seen the price of oil companies pay for a barrel of oil begin to fall. But the cost of gasolines at the pump for more American people hasn`t fallen. That`s not what you`d expect in a competitive market. I want to make sure that nothing stands in the way of oil price declines leading to lower prices for consumers. So today my director of the Economic -- National Economic Council has asked the chair of the Federal Trade Commission to use every available tool to monitor the U.S. gasoline market and address any illegal conduct that might be contributing to price increases at the pump while the cost of barrel prices is going down.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:17]\par \par \uc2 We also made clear to OPEC, a major oil exporting nations of the world that the production cuts made during the pandemic should be reversed as the global economy recovers in order to lower prices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 The Child Tax Credit stepping in to address the supply chain challenges. My competition orders the FTC investigation in the price gouging. These are some of the immediate steps we`re taking to put more money in your pocket and make that money go further. We also need to do more, and bring down the costs that are squeezing families month after month and year after year. We need to make this economy work better for working families in the long run. These challenges are with us long before the pandemic and before I took office.\par \par \uc2 But as we recover from this crisis now is the moment to put in place the long-term plant to build back America better. A plan that will increase opportunities with better jobs and with higher wages. A plan that will lower the everyday costs that strain our budgets and our nation`s families today, and long into the future. It starts with making investments that we know will make the economy more productive and lead to more growth over the long run.\par \par \uc2 Bringing down the cost -- everyday costs that have been taking a bigger and bigger bite out of the middle-class families` incomes, the expenses that keep parents up at night, and rob seniors of their dignity, health care, prescription drug cost, child care, education, housing or caring for an elderly relative or a loved one.\par \par \uc2 For those who get their health insurance through the Affordable Care Act, the American Rescue Plan allowed us to cover more people at lower premiums and lower premiums by an average of 40 percent. In my Build Back Better plan, we want to build on that progress or prescription drugs. Right now, we pay the highest prescription drug cost prices of any developed nation in the world, the highest.\par \par \uc2 My Build Back Better plan is going to lower the prescription drug costs by finally giving Medicare the power to negotiate the price of the drugs they purchase for the American people, saving Americans hundreds of billions of dollars. On top of that, my plan would add hearing, dental and vision benefits to Medicare. Right now, there are hundreds of thousands of Americans who need home and community-based care services. And my plan expands home care for older Americans and people with disabilities while improving jobs and the pay for the workers who care for them.\par \par \uc2 My plan will also provide access to quality affordable child care with new and upgraded child care facilities all across the country. Middle-class families will pay no more than 7 percent of their income for highly qualified care for children up to the age of 5. The most hard-pressed working families will not pay a dime.\par \par \uc2 Today, my Council of Economic Advisers and the Office of Management and Budget released a report showing clearly how my Build Back Better plan will lower out of pocket expenses for families. For example, a family with two parents who together earn $85,000 a year, they have an adult daughter who lives with them and attends a community college. They care for an elderly parent who needs arthritis medicine which costs $5500 out of pocket each year. An eye exam to get a new pair of glasses.\par \par \uc2 Under our Build Back Better plan, their daughter will be eligible for two years of community college free. That will save them $2400 a year. That`s like a $2400 tax credit. In addition, and I should say tax cut, not just credit. In addition, my plan would cap out of pocket expenses and cost for their mom`s and dad`s prescription drugs saving that family another $2400 a year, and the new vision benefit under Medicare would pay for that eye exam and new glasses and lenses saving $450 a year. All told, my plan would save that family making $85,000 a year, $5,250.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:08]\par \par \uc2 And the Build Back Better plan will save your family a lot as well. There`s been a lot of misleading talk which is no surprise, I guess, about what I`m proposing in my Build Back Better agenda. It is not a short-term stimulus, it`s a long-term investment in American families. Our Republican colleagues have argued that long-term investments and physical infrastructure will grow the economy and reduce inflationary pressures. And I thank them for that. They are exactly right. We agree on that.\par \par \uc2 In the same time, it`s true that long term investments that bring down the biggest cost that families face, housing, child care, education and health care, these investments will lower out of pocket expenses not raise them. They will spare more people who work by easing the burdens of child care and senior care, that parents especially mother`s bear, keeping them out of the job market.\par \par \uc2 And they`ll spread out over the decade. They`ll make a huge difference for families. But they`ll only make up around one percent of the economy`s each year over the next decade. And they are going to be fully paid for. This is not going to be anything like my predecessor whose unpaid tax cuts and other spending added nearly $8 trillion in his four years to the national debt. $8 trillion. They didn`t even try to pay for the tax cuts which went straight to the largest corporations and the wealthiest Americans.\par \par \uc2 The investments I am proposing will be fully paid for over the long term by having the largest corporations, including the 55 corporations that paid zero federal tax last year, and the super wealthy begin to pay their fair share. They`ll still make a lot of money, but pay their fair share. That means that we`ll actually reduce the national debt, improve our fiscal position over the long run.\par \par \uc2 My Build Back Better agenda is fiscally responsible. The fiscally responsible way to reduce the cost for families. In fact, you are hearing economists across the board confirm this. Moody`s has said that, quote, "Worries that the plan will ignite," and this is Moody`s now, "Will ignite undesirably high inflation and overheating economy is overdone."\par \par \uc2 Nobel Prize winning economy Joseph Stiglitz said my plan, and I quote, "There is no conceivable way that it will have any significant way on inflation. \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  of \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  has said, quote, "I don`t think the infrastructure bill or the reconstruction plan would materially impact inflation over the next decade. So if your primary right now is the cost of living, you should support this plan, not oppose it. Because a vote against this plan is a vote against lowering the cost of health care, housing, child care, elder care, and prescription drugs for American families.\par \par \uc2 So let me close at this. We brought this economy back from a cold start. And there is going to be -- there are going to be some ups and downs. But I am committed to making sure that our historic economy recovery reaches everyone. This time reaches everyone. And eases the burden on working families not just this year, but for the years to come. So thank you, God bless you and may God protect our troops. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. You`ve been listening to President Biden speaking from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go outside the White House and NBC News White House correspondent, Monica Alba who`s been listening in.\par \par \uc2 And Monica, I think we just heard kind of the classic of the genre of President Biden. Not a lot of jargon, not a lot of technical terminology. Talking about what his agenda would do and I also got the sense of White House that is keenly aware of the criticism here about inflation or rising prices. The president trying to get ahead of that.\par \par \uc2 What did you hear today?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Exactly that, Garrett. This was a president who did try to speak directly to the American people, saying he knows that families are feeling the pinch, the effects even though the economy has had some slow and steady progress in the last six or seven months of this administration, he said he knows that it`s not the case for everybody that things are obviously not back to normal, given the coronavirus pandemic, and he did say that the White House and in particular his administration is keeping a close eye on inflation, but he used that as a way to make the argument for his economic investments, not just the bipartisan infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210811eh8b0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for August 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Vaughn Hillyard, Shannon Pettypiece, Morgan Chesky,  Irwin Redlener, Sahil Kapur, Mark Murray \par \uc2 8,965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:28]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Monday. It`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Today the United States is passing another grim Coronavirus milestone, more than 35 million cases since the pandemic began, as infections now surge in all 50 states. The epicenter of that surge in Florida, where daily infections and hospitalizations are now at all-time highs. You heard that right. We`ll have more on that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as the White House has just now said we have hit President Biden`s Fourth of July vaccination goal but nearly a month late. 100 million Americans are still unvaccinated and health officials are warning that the situation will get worse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT: We`re looking not I believe to lockdown but we`re looking to some pain and suffering in the future because we`re seeing the cases go up which is the reason why we keep saying over and over again, the solution to this is get vaccinated and this would not be happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: To fight the Delta variant crisis, some areas are now taking steps backward, reimplementing mask mandates but not vaccine requirements, though some companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and \uc2 Disney\uc2  have announced vaccine requirements for many of their employees.\par \par \uc2 For its part, the White House seems a bit unsure of what to do. They`re trying to walk a balance beam between reinsuring the vaccinated that they`re safe from the virus` worst consequences, but also sounding the alarm for everyone because of the evidence that breakthrough infections might be contributing to the more dangerous surge that exists among the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS COLLINS, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF HEALTH: This is the moment to step aside from all of the misinformation and frankly disinformation that`s been put out there, look at the data, and then roll up your sleeve. Protect yourself, protect your family, be part of the answer. You`re a part of the winning team. You can`t count on the vaccinated people to save you. You got to become one of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: This afternoon, the White House Coronavirus Response Team will also hold its first on-camera briefings since the CDC updated its mask guidance last week, a lot of interest in that. In a moment we`ll speak with NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard from Jacksonville, Florida where Coronavirus hospitalizations are surging but first NBC`s Shannon Pettypiece is at the White House where the President will huddle privately with his Coronavirus Response Team in just a few minutes, and Morgan Chesky is in Baton Rouge, Louisiana where the governor is expected to give an update on masking later this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 So, Shannon, the White House has hit this goal now, 70 percent, I think it`s pretty safe to say. They didn`t expect the vaccination rate to just fall off the table after the spring peak the way it did. What other tools do they still have in their kit? We`ve got this new Federal Workers vaccine requirement, what else can the White House even do at this point?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Well, they got a little bit of a boost on the vaccination effort front, sadly, from the Delta variant`s surging cases, they said that the areas where they have seen vaccinations going up the most, or the area`s getting hit hardest by the Delta variant, so there has been some uptick in the number of vaccines driven by that.\par \par \uc2 You know, at the same time, though they`ve got this paradigm they`re struggling with what you touched on a little bit a moment ago, Garrett --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Where is the largest three to four inches?\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: -- where you have these variants out there and the number of breakthrough cases increasing but at the same time, they need to reassure people that the vaccines do work. And there is the concern we saw on some internal CDC documents last week that these increasing reports of breakthrough infections could make those who weren`t vaccinated yet think the vaccines don`t work, they don`t need to get them why to go out there and get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a message the White House is trying to juggle as they try and calm concerns about these breakthrough vaccinations, to try and keep that momentum going on the vaccination front.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I guess we`ve got another 2020 call back this afternoon in the form of a big COVID press conference at the White House this afternoon. Shannon, I know the White House is reportedly upset with the press for how we`ve -- they say we`ve missed the moment in the coverage of this current surge, but did they grasp the degree in which their message particularly starting, you know, middle of last week has gotten so muddled up to this point?\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Right, and that the way the CDC was really driving most of that messaging on this new mask recommendation for the vaccinated and the White House really did appear to be playing catch up on that.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing them come out again with a briefing that was added to the schedule, the President is getting briefed today as a way to get out ahead of this and of course, we also heard from the President late last week discussing this. But it is a complicated message because at the same time they are reassuring the vaccinated by telling them your risk of getting severely ill is low, the risk of a breakthrough infection is rare they continue to say, but at the same time telling everyone they should wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 And part of it is a data problem they are up against because there is limited data out of the U.S. on the cases of breakthrough infections in those who are vaccinated. They`re having to rely on data from other countries, which is making it difficult for them to make their case to the vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:23]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But here we are talking about fractions of percentage points when you talk about low risk, I think that`s an important point that we should continue to emphasize.\par \par \uc2 Morgan, you`re down in Louisiana where they`re suffering one of the worst surges in the country right now, we`re seeing this quite widespread across the southeast, you`ve got new cases of something like 240 percent in the last two weeks, now you`ve got an \uc2 HHS\uc2  team of dozens of medical workers coming, I believe in the hospital you`re standing in front of they just arrived. What`s the situation that these medical workers are walking into down there?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s becoming more dire by the day Garrett. Our Lady of the Lake Medical Center is the largest hospital in the state, they have never been in this situation since this pandemic began. What`s wild is that they have the bed space but they simply do not have the staff to handle this onslaught of new COVID cases.\par \par \uc2 I walked on the ICU floor yesterday, a week ago, they only had one or two COVID patients there, today, every single room is full and that`s why this federal team is absolutely vital to handle these patients coming in by the day. We do know that this federal team is comprised of doctors, nurses, paramedics, and other positions that can help shore up the staffing here at this hospital, and unfortunately, they see the number is only going up in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 One of the key problems that staff here tells me is that they`re battling misinformation, they have people that are being intubated, as we speak, who have a loved ones who refuse to get the vaccine because they simply don`t trust it yet, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: On the vaccine front, Louisiana, I think it`s the fifth-lowest in the nation when it comes to vaccinations but the numbers have been ticking up, I mean, Shannon kind of alluded to this, the idea that fear is potentially a powerful motivating factor there. What are they seeing down there in terms of vaccination numbers, and is there anything that local officials feel like they have in their tool chest to try to increase those numbers?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Well, that is interesting. For the third week running the states with the highest COVID counts are also seeing the highest vaccination rates, Louisiana, one of those states and so, on that front, they are encouraging that more people are getting vaccinated but they have a long way to go especially if they want to try to curb some of these numbers as far as what can be done locally.\par \par \uc2 The Chief Medical Officer here at Our Lady of Lake told me that she almost took it personally, that she felt like it was her own fault that people were not trusting of the vaccine. She says that studies have shown people wanted to hear from their physicians and she felt like there was some sort of a disconnect that they could have done a better job with, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Shannon, and Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard. Vaughn is in Florida, which is now unquestionably the epicenter of this fourth wave of the virus in the United States. And Vaughn, how is the hospital? You`re in Jacksonville, how is the county holding up right now?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC REPORTER: Exactly. Just take into account here, Garrett, the city of Jacksonville compared to the previous hospitalization record here, they`re already 60 percent over what their previous record here is, and who are these people, they`re the unvaccinated. 96 percent of the Florida hospitals, the patients with COVID inside of them, they are unvaccinated individuals but these are also younger individuals.\par \par \uc2 I actually was just inside here, this Jacksonville hospital, U.F. Health here in Jacksonville, where they went from 14 individuals in their COVID units back in June, to now more than 220, and we were just inside and we`re going to turn that video as fast as we can for folks.\par \par \uc2 But we were just talking with two of the nurses in there, Debbie and Sabrina, who said in their unit, you know this is a moment for them where they are left with nothing else which to tell these individuals. They can`t say here`s a COVID shot because that`s not going to save you right now, and essentially it is now coming down to a, we hope you get better. I want to let you listen to part of our conversation with Sabrina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABRINA OETTERER, NURSE: In June, Oh, I think we were down to one COVID patient, that one nurse a day had a COVID patient, the rest of us it was just business as usual, and now it -- we don`t even ask if they`re COVID positive, we know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: They said, 20-year-olds, 30-year-olds, 40-year-olds, and I said so they`re sick enough to come to the hospital? They said, not just sick enough, they`re getting sicker, and there`s only so much they can do here is literally just -- when we were inside there, they moved another 40-year- old individual from a unit that -- and took them over to the ICU unit. Again, it`s just a staggering increase here. When you look at where this is heading, there`s no sign that this is going to slow down at all here in the area.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Vaughn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with his medical perspective is MSNBC Public Health Analyst, Dr. Irwin Redlener. He`s the Founding Director of The National Center for Disaster Preparedness at Columbia University. And Doc, we just heard from Morgan in Louisiana, where there`s this dramatic surge that has also brought in an increase of hospitalizations among kids, Vaughn was saying the same thing.\par \par \uc2 There`s also been news out of the New York Times about an increase of cases of RSV among kids, can you talk a little bit about RSV is in terms of what it means for the pandemic and what we need to know about protecting kids better, they of course, still cannot get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 IRWIN REDLENER, FOUNDING DIRECTOR, NATIONAL CENTER FOR DISASTER PREPAREDNESS AT \uc2 COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Yes. Well, children are at risk Garrett, and they will be continuing to be at risk and may be a greater risk than they are right now. So, RSV is a virus that causes -- a very common virus that causes respiratory illness in children, and before the pandemic, we would see a lot of this in very young children.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re seeing a rise in the RSV, it`s called Respiratory Syncytial Virus, we`re seeing a rise in that along with a rise in COVID cases among children, this does not portend very well for what`s going to happen when we start opening schools and have, of course, children all over the country going back into the classroom without necessarily even mandating that teachers and other adult workers in schools get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I`m afraid that we haven`t really done enough to protect kids in schools, hopefully, we will. Children absolutely need to be back in the classroom but we have to make sure that we`re not putting them at risk from COVID, Garret.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Doc, this seems to me like a case where the politics and the public health are at a little bit of odds. I don`t think any serious Public Health Official and you can stop me if you disagree, thinks we can mask our way out of the pandemic, right? The solution is, was always going to be vaccines. From a purely public health perspective, wouldn`t vaccine mandates by the much more effective way to address this surge than the mask mandates? Does it make sense to have one and not the other?\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Yes, well, I don`t think you could find a legitimate Public Health Expert that would refute what you just said, Garrett, and the fact is that we absolutely need everybody to get vaccinated. You know, I`m affiliated at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , I got a -- I got a warning that if I`m not vaccinated, and cannot prove them I`m vaccinated, I will not be allowed back on -- back on campus, and they might withhold pay or have other penalties.\par \par \uc2 So, what this is boiling down to is the fact that you know, the Federal government can`t mandate vaccines, that`s up to states, local organizations, etcetera, and that`s not happening consistently, either.\par \par \uc2 We have governors in Florida, and Texas, and so on, that won`t mandate anything, even mask words necessary. So, I think this is going to be up to individual businesses, organizations, people run events, universities, and schools that say, you know, you want to come back to work, you want to go to school, you have to prove that you`ve been vaccinated. I think it`s going to take sort of thousand points of mandate to get us where we need to go but it`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 If I just add another point quickly, the convincing people who are currently not vaccinated to get the vaccine is no easy task and it`s in some ways out of the realm of Public Health Experts because we have now the full entry of vaccine resistance into crazy town here there.\par \par \uc2 People are not getting vaccinated, they`re afraid that people are going to be injecting microchips in their body or make them magnetic, I mean, it`s, it`s out of control, and you know, just the argument that you won`t die if you get vaccinated may not be enough.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. I mean, there are different varieties of vaccine resistances I think we`ve all been learning over the last couple of weeks. I want to ask you about this CDC data that finally came out end of last week that backed up their decision to recommend an indoor mask.\par \par \uc2 We saw these leaked internal reports from the post of other news organizations about the Delta variant, what information that you found there was new, and what was most useful to you? And do you think the CDC kind of botched it by not leading with the data instead of leading with the recommendations?\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Well, let me say this, first of all, that I think the White House, and the CDC, and the other agencies need to get their messaging act together. It is not been good Garrett, and I think it`s causing public confusion, not that the policy recommendations are wrong.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the reason that we went from, you know, if you`re vaccinated, you don`t need a mask indoors, to yes, you will maybe you do, especially in areas where there`s a lot of communication of the virus -- l0t of spread the virus.\par \par \uc2 The problem now is that between May when the CDC said you know if you`re vaccinated don`t worry, you don`t need a mask indoors, and now we have this extraordinary outbreak of the Delta variant, 83 percent of people with COVID cases have Delta. Delta is transmitted extremely rapidly, multiple times -- multiple times faster than the original version of the SARS COVID- 2 verse, that`s why they change, and they should change -- they should have changed but they have not prepared the public for the coming changes in policies which will be inevitable.\par \par \uc2 And the White House just this morning, issued a statement that the press and experts were overhyping the issue about the vaccinated people being subject to getting infected, which they are.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Doctor?\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Yes?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:24]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Doctor, we got to leave it there, we got to pay some bills here, but Dr. Irwin Redlener, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re seeing a lot of finger-pointing among Democrats and the White House as pandemic-related eviction protections set to expire for millions of Americans. It`s just one item on a very busy Capitol Hill agenda where priority number one right now is the President`s infrastructure bill. I`ll talk to the number two Senate Democrats, Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  about all of that next.\par \par \uc2 And then later as we look ahead to the upcoming midterm elections, what a couple of special primary elections tomorrow could tell us about the current state of both political parties. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Turning to Capitol Hill, some unfinished business along with a little Democratic infighting to start the week in Congress. It took what seemed like forever but the Bipartisan Infrastructure Framework is now a bill, an honest to God bill and it`s finally the pending business on the Senate floor. The current version clocks in around 2700 pages and that`s before what could be a lengthy amendment process.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want a final vote by the end of this week, so the caucus can then turn its attention to that multi-trillion-dollar budget reconciliation package. Remember, House Democrats insist they will not pass the bipartisan agreement without it. But one thing Congress apparently won`t be pursuing this week is a renewal for the COVID eviction moratorium after the White House put the onus on Congress at the last minute to extend the expired moratorium late last week.\par \par \uc2 House Democratic leaders are now trying to put the ball back in the White House`s court, they said the opposition to the extension in the Senate means that any action needs to come from the White House even though the Supreme Court signaled it was Congress`s job. Confused yet? Well, it gets weirder. The Houses left town which is leaving some progressives furious about the inaction.\par \par \uc2 Progressive Congresswoman Cori Bush has spent the last three consecutive nights sleeping on the Capitol steps to urge Congress and the White House, somebody, anybody to act.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Sahil Kapur is on Capitol Hill now with more on all of this, and Sahil, we`ve got the bipartisan bill text, we`re expecting a CBO score to come later today, negotiators want this voted on before they go home, what needs to happen to make that possible this week?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:39]\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: That`s right, Garret. Infrastructure week is finally here in the Senate after weeks, really months, actually years, of talking about it and it is becoming a running joke. They have text 200 -- 2700 pages of what appears to be a $555 billion Infrastructure Bill.\par \par \uc2 Of course, as you mentioned, we`re still waiting on the final score and in the meantime, Senators are trying to figure out an amendment process to get the ball rolling here, and in that regard, there`s some shadowboxing going on between Chuck Schumer, the Democratic Majority Leader, and Mitch McConnell, the Republican Minority Leader sure is in a hurry to get this done.\par \par \uc2 He wants to wrap this up, move on to the separate budget resolution for that multitrillion-dollar bill and let Senators go home for the August recess. McConnell was on the floor just some time ago saying he`s in no rush that there should be no timetable, some -- no sort of artificial deadlines as he described them on this and there should be a robust amendment process.\par \par \uc2 In reality, the Senate can do whatever it wants if it has consented. There`s likely to be a wrench or two thrown in from the right, from Senators who don`t want to spend this much money and have a tendency to make a fuss about doing a bill like this quickly, but at the end of the day, Garrett, Democrats hold the high cards on this because the longer the senate stays in, the more time Senators get cut out of their August recess, and they tend to like to go home for that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It is amazing how the prospect of a shortened vacation can clarify minds in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Sahil, the Bipartisan Bill is on I guess a relative glide path, it could be slow, it could be fast, but we know which way this is going but the needle slats to get threaded on reconciliation. We heard from \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  over the weekend, we`ve heard from other moderate Senators, they`re still probably not as on board as my next guess would like. Where are we right now on the reconciliation part of this package?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: So, two steps on that Garrett, of course, there`s the budget resolution that is likely to come up right after the bipartisan infrastructure deal is completed in the Senate, Democrats shouldn`t have too much trouble getting all 50 members on board for that, that is just the vehicle and nobody has drawn red lines on the -- on just the kind of -- the budget resolution itself, which of course is non-binding.\par \par \uc2 The second process is going to be the very tough one and they`re going to work through the August recess. Democrats are, I`m told to try to move this along, but getting all 50 Senate Democrats on board and all but three House Democrats which is their margin for error, on a multitrillion-dollar piece of legislation is going to be a long and treacherous journey. It`s going to have probably multiple near-death experiences, there`re going to be the demands that are made, some of them are going to be kept, some of them are going to be walked back.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be very, very difficult for Democrats to pull off and the best thing it`s got going for it is, this is apart from the infrastructure bill and what`s done so far, the be-all and the end-all of President Biden`s economic agenda. This is probably the last train leaving the station Garrett, and Democrats want to get big things done on health care, on child care, on climate change, on so many other things, this is really the only opportunity to get it done any time in the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Sahil, I`ve got time for one more quick question. This eviction moratorium story is particularly interesting because we haven`t seen the House and the White House have a -- have a row like this in public. It`s quite unusual from the Congressional perspective. Is there a way out of this that Congress can take or is it -- do you sort of line up with the Congressional folks that this does have to be something the White House is going to solve?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It`s probably not something that`s going to get handled and Congress, Garrett, they don`t have the votes in the House to pass it with even just Democratic support and even if they do, if it comes here in the Senate, you, of course, need 60 to break a filibuster, so it`s unlikely they`ll get out of this and Democrats appear to have gotten caught flat- footed on this. They waited till the end, and now they`ve been kind of passing it around like a hot potato.\par \par \uc2 The House saying, the White House should do it, the White House saying, well, we can`t do it because Supreme Court stopped us and some in the House saying, even if we do, do it, it`s jammed up in the Senate. Meanwhile, you`ve got folks like Representative Cori Bush, who feel very, very strongly about it, and are not willing to go quietly on it, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, an unusual thing to see when one party controls all of Washington. Sahil, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And as we mentioned, clearing the Senate is just one hurdle for the Democrats to track pathway on infrastructure, and on all those other priorities. Over the weekend, we heard House Progressives say they will not consider the Bipartisan Infrastructure Deal until they have the larger Reconciliation Bill in hand. While at the same time Senate moderates have not actually committed to this specific sudden reconciliation. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): If there is not a reconciliation bill on the House, and if the Senate does not pass the reconciliation bill, we will uphold our end of the bargain and not pass the bipartisan bill until we get all of these investments in.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, ANCHOR, CNN: Can you guarantee her that the reconciliation package will pass?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2 , (D-WV): I can`t really guarantee anybody you know, and I have not guaranteed anybody on any of these pieces of legislation, we would like to do more. Yes, you can do what you can pay for. This is pay-for, our infrastructure bills are all paid for. We don`t have a debt, we`re going to incur more debt and throwing onto it, and on the other as far as reconciliation bill should be looked at the same that`s why I said we`re going to get to a budget resolution, let`s start the process and let`s see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:40]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Joining me now is the Senate`s number two Democrat, Illinois Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , he`s the man who counts the votes. So, Senator, are you on track on both of these pieces of legislation, or I guess only one of them is an actual piece of legislation so far?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN\uc2  (D-IL): Well, "the Beach Boys", I`m getting good vibrations on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: -- But it only applies to the infrastructure bill. We`re going to get that done properly before we really engage on the budget resolution.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know you`re only counting the votes on the Senate side, but you may have a House problem with the infrastructure bill. The committee -- Transportation Committee Chairman, Peter DeFazio, who called the bipartisan deal crap, his words, not mine continues to be less than a fan. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETER DEFAZIO, (D-OR): It set out to bring us into the 21st century with transportation. This, to me looks pretty much like the status quo highway-centric bill, we can`t -- we can`t perpetuate that. If we`re continuing mid-20th century policies, it would be a disservice to the American people, also be a disservice to the planet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And not exactly a ringing endorsement from a fellow Democrat. It is an unusual question about a bill that`s going to spend a trillion dollars over its lifetime, but are you confident that a bipartisan bill does enough to get through both chambers and ultimately satisfy even more progressive critics?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I`m going to put my good friend Pete DeFazio in the undecided column at this point, but I would like to have a moment to speak to him because I really -- all of us respect his knowledge of this whole infrastructure field. But I might say to him, the investment in transit is historic. It`s not just highways, and bridges, and tunnels, we`re putting money into transit.\par \par \uc2 Would do we have liked to have gone further when it comes to electric vehicles are responding with sustainable renewable energy? Yes. But we have a 50-50 Senate, we can only take the Republicans so far, and we took what we can get. Now come budget resolution and reconciliation, there may be an opening of a new discussion.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Let`s come back to that in a second here. What do you think the timelines are on these two tracks? I obviously -- Chuck Schumer has been very clear, you guys will not go home until both pieces of legislation are done enough. When do you see that happening?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: There is nothing like a looming recess deadline to focus the attention of members of the Senate and I think Chuck Schumer is doing this right. He stayed in through the weekend, he`s been very focused on making sure that this bill was written and on available to members on both sides of the aisle, he is asking for bipartisanship so that we have a reasonable limit to amendments to be considered, and he`s told people we`re going to stay here till we get it done. That means infrastructure and the budget resolution.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When it comes to the reconciliation package, are you more worried about getting the 50 votes in the Senate either to start or finish that process or to get the majority in the House when you ultimately do? I want to play for you something that Congresswoman Ocasio-Cortez said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): If there is not a Reconciliation Bill in the House, and if the Senate does not pass the Reconciliation Bill, we will uphold our end of the bargain and not pass the Bipartisan Bill until we get all of these investments in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you take that as a threat? I have a hard time imagining the House progressives really sort of standing thwart the Biden agenda in that way but I wonder how you manage that as you get to work on the reconciliation piece.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: My job, I was elected to do is to count votes in the Senate. Now, as a Senator and someone who has intent on getting things done in the Senate, I think about the House as well. It`s a thin margin, three votes, for goodness` sake.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Three people don`t show up, you lose so I take it very seriously. We`re going to have to sit down and have meaningful discussions on a bipartisan basis when it comes to reconciliation, but we start with the reality that we have 50 votes, we need every one of them, and Kamala Harris once again riding to the rescue, so we don`t have a lot of flexibility here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are you confident that you`ll have \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Kyrsten Sinema and perhaps some of these other moderates for anything as close to as big as a $3.5 trillion package when you get that far?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I`m not going to predict where they`re going to be on what number but I will tell you that both of them have shown a real interest in working to achieve a goal here, the most significant infrastructure package in all -- of all time.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And both of them have been very open in their conversations with the Democratic caucus. We want to keep that constructive relationship with them. There`s going to be give and take, I know it, and you know it as well. But that`s the nature of legislation.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to ask you about this eviction moratorium. And it seems like this kind of got dropped back in the Congress` lapse just in the last few days. I wonder if you think it was a mistake for the White House to wait or whether there needs to be more congressional action on this? I mean obviously I know you have appropriated the money already. It`s slow going out. What other steps can or should the Senate take right now?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I don`t want to point a finger of blame. I don`t think it`s constructive. But someone dropped the ball. It could have been on our end in Pennsylvania Avenue or in the president`s end. The reality is that millions of Americans face eviction. A cruel, devastating experience for many families which we ought to do our best to avoid.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned there is money on the table. For some reason that money isn`t flowing to landlords and others to keep people in their homes and in their apartments during this period of time. It`s a high priority for me and I`m sure it is for our Democratic side of the aisle here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And a good reminder how challenging it is to get this money out on any of these projects even after the legislation is written and passed.\par \par \uc2 Senator Durbin, we`ve got to leave it there. Thank you very much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up, how the nation`s southern states are now battling coronavirus surges has GOP leaders continue pushing their resistance to more restrictions. The Democratic governor of Kentucky joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:16]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Among the southern states dealing with a surge in recent COVID cases is Kentucky which has seen its own spike in infections. A little more than 45 percent of the state`s population is now vaccinated. And in fact Kentucky reported more than 1600 new cases on Friday alone. That`s the single biggest one-day total since February. More than 350 of those new cases were in children under the age of 18 and it comes as some classes in Kentucky and some schools are set to go back as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Kentucky`s governor, Democrat Andy Beshear.\par \par \uc2 And, Governor, I don`t know if you heard my interview with Irwin Redlener earlier today but he talked about the power that local officials have, state level officials have. Will you consider mask mandates or vaccine mandates in any way in Kentucky?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR (D-KY): Well, as we see the Delta variant causing new surges in cases, I think it`s important that we don`t take anything off the table. But right now what we`re trying to do is to set the right example. First, encouraging vaccinations. Now when we look at first doses our numbers are a little bit better than the ones that you quoted but we still have a long ways to go, including with younger folks\par \par \uc2 But what we are seeing is a significant increase in the last week or week in a half. We had about 34,000 vaccinations occur in the last three days. We`ve been hitting 2,000 or 3,000 on average before now. We also reinstituted a masking requirement in state office buildings. And we`re seeing different private sector entities do the same.\par \par \uc2 My kids went back to school today. They`re one of the earliest ones and their school district is requiring masks. All that while we continue to provide incentives like our "Shot at a Million" or free higher education for getting vaccinated. When we look at any of those restrictions that you think, just remember its effectiveness that`s important. And that is restriction times the number of people who will or won`t follow it, equal that effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How concerned are you about the spike in cases among kids specifically? I mean, you mentioned your kids going back to school. Obviously most young people can`t get the vaccine even if they want to.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, I`m a dad, I`m a dad of a 12-year-old who is now fully vaccinated. I believe so much in these vaccines. I took my child that is old enough, that I love more than life itself, who I would give my life for any minute, any moment of any day if necessary. But I also have an 11-year- old who is not old enough, so I am concerned. I`m concerned both about how many days they will have in-person learning.\par \par \uc2 I`m concerned if enough children get the Delta variant, how many of them have a bad outcome. But I`m also worried about our workforce because if we get the large-scale quarantines, and we saw some of this coming out of a Georgia school, it won`t just be the kids that are home in distanced learning, which we don`t want to have to do, but it`s going to be the parents that are going to be home, too. That`s going to be lost productivity.\par \par \uc2 And I got an economy on fire right now. We announced 1500 new jobs in the last week and a half, and we`ve got to have people out there working those. And the answer here is really simple. Get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is that the ticket out of this? I mean, you talk about the economy on fire? Would you like to see big businesses, even small businesses in the state try and push vaccine mandates or lean on their employees in some way to get vaccinated? Your fellow Democratic governor, Andrew Cuomo, said this about that topic today. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO (D-NY): Private businesses, I am asking them and suggesting to them, go to vaccine-only admission. Go to vaccine-only admission. I believe it`s in your best business interests.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there a good old fashioned capitalistic solution to this if it keeps your workers healthy, if it keeps your customers, to have your big employers be the ones to say we need you to be vaccinated, to participate?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Well, I think that there is certainly a push by the private sector which we`re seeing, we`re seeing here in Kentucky, would significantly help. I think when you look at the NFL, and how they`re going about it. You know, COVID to them isn`t red or blue, it`s green.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: It`s going to impact their profit. It`s going to impact what they have on the field. It`s the same for so many different private sector entities. When we went back to masking in state office buildings, I was looking at it as an employer, right?\par \par \uc2 I`ve got 20,000 plus employees that inspect bridges, that process unemployment, that do so many critical things. And I can`t have an outbreak that prevents them from having person-to-person contact. So I think that there is a compelling interest in the private sector both for current productivity but also we`re seeing so much investment.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:08]\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing the United States take off. I`m seeing Kentucky take off in ways that I never thought possible. And we don`t want to stop that. So if everybody could get vaccinated we could move from an era of tragedy to a historic moment of prosperity. Let`s all try to be a part of that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Governor Andy Beshear, we`ve got to leave it there. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up more cases, more problems. And for some cities that means more mask mandates. I`ll ask the mayor of Kansas City about the challenges ahead next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. In a sign of how serious this latest spike in COVID cases is in Missouri, Kansas City has reimposed a mask mandate which takes effect today. From now until August 28th masks must be worn in public indoor space. New cases are up 49 percent over the past two weeks in Missouri. And yet despite that spike, the state`s attorney general says he`ll sue to overturn the Kansas City mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 Quinton Lucas is the mayor of Kansas City, Missouri. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Mayor, you`ve got your mask mandate going into effect today. You`re the second locality in Missouri to do so after St. Louis. I wonder why not go a step further even and institute a vaccine mandate of some kind? Do you think you have the power as mayor to do that for city employees like the fire department or the Kansas City Police Department?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR QUINTON LUCAS (D), KANSAS CITY, MISSOURI: Well, it is our view that we do have the power to issue a vaccine mandate. We are trying to right now do more carrot than stick. Trying to get people to recognize that vaccines important, response rate vaccination events. But we continue to think every day about mandates on vaccines. We see more jurisdictions, private and public, do more vaccination mandate work. We`re not there yet.\par \par \uc2 We hope that masking works. We hope that letting people know the way we get out of this crisis is through vaccinations, will be the sort of thing that helps us change these terrible numbers we have right now here in Kansas City and throughout the entire state of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:11]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What would it take to get you to switch from carrot to stick?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: Well, you know what, I think the trend line we`re on right now actually speaks to that, which is that we have increasing infection rates here in Missouri, although our state government is calling them field hospitals we have field hospitals, at least eight field hospitals in Southwest Missouri. And I think ultimately when you look at deaths that will follow from these increased hospitalizations those are very real concerns.\par \par \uc2 I hope we do not get to that point. So I continue to hope that some of our interventions now, masking, increased testing, and certainly encouragement of vaccines for our fire department, our city employees, and others, will be something that makes a world of difference. But look, I will not just sit by if we go through the types of infection rates and deaths that we were seeing at least last year and then we would probably look to have some stronger change and a vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are you talking to any of the big employers out there about either mandating or doing something more with vaccines? I used to live in Kansas City and I can`t imagine if an \uc2 H&R Block\uc2  or a Cerner or a Hallmark decides to start putting vaccine mandates in place for their employees. That would matter.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: You know we talked to our employers a decent amount. I visited with some the other day. Everybody was thinking that -- persuading people, kind of encouraging them, would be enough to suggest that we can make some level of difference. Unfortunately it has not been. But I think that we keep those conversations ongoing. I`ll step up for our bars have actually imposed some vaccine requirements upon entry. Have an internally vaccinated staff. And then requiring proof of vaccination to come in.\par \par \uc2 I think that is something that`s actually getting people`s attention. So it`s not just big employers. A lot of our smaller employers, a lot of our mom-and-pops and others are really stepping up and saying how important vaccination is.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`re seeing a similar dynamic in Missouri. Some of these other states where you`ve got blue or cities and counties trying to put in place your own restrictions. A Republican-controlled state pushing back. What`s your response to the attorney general trying to overturn your mask order?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: Well, look, I mean, it shows a lack of seriousness in terms of what he is actually trying to do with its job himself. Last week we announced that we would have a mask order come back. The attorney general immediately, tweeted, "I`m going to file suit." One would ask what he would file suit on since there wasn`t even an order that had been entered yet.\par \par \uc2 Look, it is theatrics, it is performative, it is not something that`s serious about the biggest challenge Missouri has right now, which is not keeping lawyers employed. Our biggest challenge is the spread of COVID-19. I have received communications from a convention that says they`ve may consider pulling their meeting out of Missouri and Kansas City coming up in a few months before of the very real concerns that they have.\par \par \uc2 This is a serious issue that is being used as a political ploy from statewide electors in Missouri, in Florida, Texas, and lots of other places. And it is exceedingly unfortunate in my view. But look, we`ll keep doing the right thing. I reached out.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to the attorney general`s office and said if you wanted to have a meeting with us, we`d be happy to. He has not responded but he has found time since then to tweet about the mayor of D.C. which is only about a thousand -- a thousand miles from Missouri. So I hope he comes back to focusing on things that we`re concerned with.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Mayor Quinton Lucas of KC, Mo, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Up next, former President Trump may be fundraising more than any other Republican ahead of the 2022 midterms, but his kingmaker status is about to face yet another critical test.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:26]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Former president Trump is making a major statement with his latest fundraising haul. New figures showed Trump`s political action committee entered July with more than $100 million in cash, an unprecedented amount at this stage of the election cycle for a former president. It comes as Trump weighs another presidential run in 2024 and doubles down on his baseless claims that the 2020 election was somehow rigged against him.\par \par \uc2 The numbers suggest he remains the favorite to lead the party at least among Republican donors by far despite losing the last election. The war chest could also be pivotal in the upcoming midterms elections as the GOP looks to retake Congress. So let`s talk about a few of those congressional fights.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, we`ve got our eye on a couple of special primary elections tomorrow that could shed a light on the current state of both parties. It`s an establishment versus progressive fight in a Democratic primary to fill HUD Secretary Marcia Fudge`s Cleveland, Akron, Ohio, seat. The race between Cuyahoga County and Democratic Party chair Shontel Brown and former Bernie Sanders national co-chair Nina Turner has been one of the most vitriolic Democratic primaries in recent memory especially over the airwaves.\par \par \uc2 And down the road near Columbus, we`re watching an 11-way Republican primary field that includes a candidate backed by former president Donald Trump and another backed by the seat`s most recent holder, Steven Stivers. After his pick lost a special election in Texas last week, another defeat could deal a pretty serious blow to the former president who`s been using primary threats as his primary means of keeping influence over his party.\par \par \uc2 According to the "Washington Post," a super PAC run by Trump allies made a six-figure buy last week on behalf of Trump`s chosen candidate there in Ohio, Mike Kerry.\par \par \uc2 NBC News senior political editor Mark Murray is with us now.\par \par \uc2 And Mark, some big named surrogates came to town for Brown and Turner over the weekend. What should we be watching for in that Ohio 11th special primary tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, that Democratic race, Garrett, has actually given me PTSD from the Bernie Sanders versus Democratic establishment wars that we saw play out in 2016 as well as 2020. And as you mentioned over the weekend, we saw Bernie Sanders and a lot of his allies stump for Nina Turner who was co-chair of Bernie Sanders` 2020 presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 And then on the other side, you ended up having Jim Clyburn who ended up campaigning for Shontel Brown, and really -- I mentioned the 2016 and 2020 races, because a lot of the fault lines have centered on those same battles that we saw four and a year ago, and that`s over the $15 minimum wage, Medicare for all, as well as who would actually be the better Democrat and the better ally for Joe Biden in Congress.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 And so strategists on both sides tell me this is a really close race. Nina Turner had the early lead, but we`re going to have to wait and see what ends up happening tomorrow, and I think what`s going to be a really close and very closely watched race.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, you called it one of the nastiest Democrat-on-Democrat primaries since Obama-Clinton. I want to ask you about the Republican race, too, across the state, this special primary tomorrow with a Trump-backed candidate, as Steve Stivers-backed candidate in a local race. Does the local endorsement maybe carry more weight? We just saw a Rick Perry-backed candidate beat a Trump-backed candidate down in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, and, Garrett, I think that`s going to be a really great question. And as we were just talking about in that Democratic race, it`s really been the national forces at play more than local, but in this particular race in Ohio, on the Republican side in Ohio 15, we`re going to see what is actually more powerful, local forces or national forces. And of course, the national forces are trying to propel the Trump-backed candidate Mike Carey. He`s a coal lobbyist who has close ties to Trump world. He ended up getting Trump`s endorsement.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand you have Steve Stivers actually kind of make a more localized decision, ended up backing a candidate by the name of Jeff Beret and so trying to propel him on local issues being the best candidate here. And who ends up winning tomorrow again, a really, really tough contest, a lot on the line, though, for the former president who, you know, probably doesn`t want to go 0-2 in these types of endorsed races when it comes to a congressional race.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, you have to wonder if he goes 0-2 does that affect his ability to beat the people he really wants to beat like Adam Kinzinger or Liz Cheney down the line. We`ll be watching those races closely tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And thank you for being with us for this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And MSNBC coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210803eh8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for July 13, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kasie Hunt, Priscilla Thompson, Mike Memoli, Sahil Kapur, Henry  Gomez \par \uc2 8,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:18]\par \par \uc2 KASIE HUNT, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Tuesday, it`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Kasie Hunt in for Chuck Todd on a very busy day of developments surrounding Voting Rights.\par \par \uc2 In just a few moments, President Biden will be leaving the White House to deliver a major address on that issue from the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2  in Philadelphia. It comes as Texas Democrats are ramping up pressure on the President to outline a clear strategy to get Federal Voting Rights legislation through Congress.\par \par \uc2 More than 50 elected Democrats fled Texas last night as part of an effort to block the passage of new voting restrictions in their state. The Texas House actually just voted to authorize the State`s Sergeant at Arms to find those lawmakers and potentially have them arrested.\par \par \uc2 They are now here in Washington, where they held a press conference this morning at the Capitol begging Democrats in Congress and the President for help.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TURNER, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, TEXAS: Our intent is to stay out and kill this bill, this session. And use the intervening time, I think 24 or 25 days now, before the end of this session, to implore the folks in this building behind us to pass Federal Voting Rights legislation to protect voters in Texas and across the country.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking forward to seeing what the President says today in his speech on voting rights in Philadelphia. And we hope what we`ll hear is an even stronger commitment and a clear plan of action as to how we can break the gridlock in the Senate and pass federal legislation immediately this summer. We can`t hold this tied back forever. We`re buying some time. We need Congress and all of our federal leaders to use that time wisely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The problem facing the President and Democrats in Washington is that they just don`t have the votes to pass new voting rights legislation. And they don`t have the votes to make changes to the Filibuster either. Moderate Democrats like \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Kyrsten Sinema have been adamant about that.\par \par \uc2 What that means is that Texas Democrats have now cast a huge spotlight not just on Republicans, but on their fellow Democrats and the President without a clear path forward and the clock is ticking. Texas Democrats have made it clear that time is not on their side. The State`s Republican Governor Greg Abbott has redoubled his commitment to get these new voting restrictions passed one way or another.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREGORY ABBOTT, STATE REPUBLICAN GOVERNOR, TEXAS: I can and I will continue to call a special session, after special session, after special session, all the way up until Election next year. And so, if these people want to be hanging out, wherever they`re hanging out on this taxpayer-paid junket, they`re going to have to be prepared to do it for well over a year.\par \par \uc2 As soon as they come back in the State of Texas, they will be arrested, they will be cabined inside the Texas Capitol until they get their job done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, NBC`s Mike Memoli is in Philadelphia ahead of President Biden`s remarks, and NBC`s Priscilla Thompson is in Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 And Priscilla, let me actually start with you just for the latest on the delegation and what`s being done to try and get Texas Democrats back into the state. What have you heard on the ground there? How is this playing out in Texas? And what`s next for these legislators who fled?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Kasie. At this moment, the lawmakers on the House side who did show up this morning are cabined inside of the House. The doors have been locked. They are not being allowed out at the moment. And that comes of course after that vote earlier today which fell along party lines with 76 Republican House members voting in favor, four Democrats present voting against allowing the Sergeant of Arms the permission to send for those missing lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 And so now the Sergeant of Arms does have that power. He can also appoint any other officer in the state to send for those lawmakers who are absent without an excuse and arrest them if necessary in order to bring them back here. But of course, that jurisdiction only falls within the state.\par \par \uc2 We know that Democratic lawmakers in Washington D.C. have messaged that they do not plan on coming back during this special session. And I had an opportunity to actually speak with one of those Republican lawmakers before he went inside to cast that ballot. And take a listen to what he had to say about the Democrats` decision to leave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACEY JETTON, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, TEXAS: I think that most of the voters across Texas, hardworking people, have regular jobs and recognize that when the job gets hard, you don`t get to not show up and you definitely don`t get to flee to another state to avoid having to do your job. And so, I think that the voters -- their voters in 2022 will ultimately decide whether or not that was the right decision or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And Republicans here are saying that Democrats should have stayed. They should have tried to continue to legislate this bill, that there were still sort of opportunities for them here. But of course, Democrats don`t think that their voters are going to feel that way.\par \par \uc2 Some of them telling me that they`ve been receiving e-mails and phone calls from their constituents telling them to leave, to not stay, and to try to do everything they can to block these new voting bills, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:11]\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Mike Memoli, this puts some pressure on the White House, certainly a spotlight on voting rights and what`s going on in state legislature, not just in Texas, but across the country. What`s the response been behind the scenes to that pressure? And how does it play into the speech that the President is such to give today?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Kasie, this is a speech that the President has been talking about giving and which progressives had voting rights advocates have been wanting him to give for some time. I think the question is, is the substance of what he`s going to say here today going to satisfy those in the party who, including some of his closest allies, by the way, who think he really hasn`t done enough on an issue which they feel is a matter of true urgency at this moment?\par \par \uc2 What White House officials are telling me is the President wants to make a number of points today. First is, to really criticize that his predecessor to talk about the big lie, as he puts it, which is at the root of some of these voting changes that are happening across the country. He`s going to praise sort of the institutions which have held firm thus far. He`ll point to the 2020 election as actually an example of something that the country should be proud of in terms of the outpouring of voters despite COVID, despite all the other adversity that they faced in order to do so.\par \par \uc2 And also, though, to really push back to elevate this issue to raise the stakes for a moderate middle of this country that hasn`t necessarily been as engaged in this fight, as so much of both parties` basis at the moment. He`s going to be very stark in describing what he sees as the very real threat to election integrity that these voting changes represent.\par \par \uc2 What we aren`t going to hear the President do though, is talk about the Filibuster. And ultimately any path towards the kind of legislation that advocates want to see the For The People Act and the John Lewis Voting Rights Act are probably going to require either a breaking of the Filibuster or some sort of carve-out in order for that to take place.\par \par \uc2 The President sees this as a longer-term fight. He recognizes that there aren`t the votes to change the filibuster now. And so, he wants to make sure it`s clear in next year`s midterm elections, but also in some of the state elections to come in between now and then, that there are political consequences for the Republicans who are pursuing these kinds of changes.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Mike, the conversation on the Hill around the Filibuster today and kind of over the past few days, has been about potential ways to adjust it. Not get rid of it necessarily, but potentially change the way that it operates in order to make it more politically painful to implement one, perhaps, carve out some sort of exception the way budget resolutions aren`t subject to a Filibuster, maybe define constitutional rights as something that is exempt from the Filibuster. Right now, we don`t necessarily think that there is anyone who`s ready to take that step, but there are some conversations going on about it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what is the White House said about that? And as someone who`s you know, listened and watched to Joe Biden for so, so many years, I mean, what`s your sense on whether those might be some things that he ultimately could take a public stand in favor of?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, where there`s frustration on the part of the White House and on the President specifically about the pressure he`s facing from his party, is that they don`t necessarily think there`s an easy solution here other than waving a magic wand. The President has talked about that. And so, what the President has been doing, and I talked to former Congressman Cedric Richmond, about this, just in the last few days, is working quietly and speaking very often with the likes of Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , who ultimately is going to be the key decider of with and when they can be a carve-out of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 The President has said he supports this. He said, obviously a person who spent 36 years in the Senate has revered the institution, but feels that there should be a little bit of political pain here for the Republicans who continue to stand in the way of this.\par \par \uc2 But what they`re also doing and I was reminded that the Voting Rights Act, the John Lewis Voting Rights Act has not actually been written yet. So, there are also conversations happening there about what could be included in there that might attract a broader support. Richmond telling me that our goal is still to sign legislation from this Congress in this year, if possible, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Priscilla Thompson, what`s ultimately the end game here for House -- the Texas House Democrats, I mean, I talked to one of them this morning who says they plan to stay out of the state until the special session runs out. But at the end -- at the end of the day, it`s unlikely that they can stop this bill from becoming law. No?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Right, you`re exactly right. This is a GOP-controlled legislature and as we, you know, you just played the soundbite from Governor Greg Abbott, he does plan to call another special session and he will continue to do that. And the reality is Democrats cannot leave the state for another month every time that that happens.\par \par \uc2 Now, what Democrats have said is that they are hopeful that this may have a bigger impact. They went down a few weeks ago, about 20 of them, now it`s more than 50 of them that are they`re going to be lobbying and speaking with senators about this. But the reality is, when they come back here, they face a GOP-controlled legislature intent on getting these bills passed into law, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:24]\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right. Priscilla Thompson, Mike Memoli, thank you both very much for getting us started this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And we do now want to bring in one of the Texas Democrats who fled Austin last night, State Representative Trey Martinez Fischer. He represents the San Antonio area.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Representative, thank you so much for being with us today. We really appreciate it. And let me just start with kind of the latest twist here, which is this authorization for the Sergeant at Arms to come and track all of you down and try to bring you back to the state. I mean, what powers do they actually have to do that? And what is your plan over the course of the next week as they try to figure out how to bring you all back to Texas to get this session started again?\par \par \uc2 TREY MARTINEZ FISCHER, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, TEXAS: Well, thank you, Kasie. Well, first of all, those Sergeant at Arms does have the ability if ordered by the House to make arrangements for our return and our possible detention by our state police, that would be the Department of Public Safety. They`ll have to do some paperwork such as a warrant, have some sort of civil demand for our return to Austin, and then they can go pursue those requests like investigations.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, they`re going to be limited to the state of Texas. So, this has been tried before in 2003, troopers tried to enforce this paperwork in the state of Oklahoma, they were turned away. Of course, the same troopers then tried to, you know, prosecute or use these demands to bring back senators from New Mexico, they were denied by the State of New Mexico. And I guess if they want to try to do this here in the District of Columbia, well, we will wish them luck with that.\par \par \uc2 But we are here to talk about voting rights, we are here to stand with the nation, we are here to rally the nation, and we want to wake up the Senate so they can hear us and understand just why we need a national standard when it comes to voting in America.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, it`s an uphill battle obviously here in Washington to pass Federal Voting Rights legislation. How have Democrats that your group has talked to today, Senate Democrats, here explain to you what the hurdles are and whether or not they have reason to be optimistic?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, you know, Kasie, politics is hard in general. It doesn`t matter whether you`re talking about voting rights, or, you know, you`re talking about scheduling, you know, I guess the caucus lunch. I mean, if the goal of Republicans is to object, and object, and object, and object, you`re not going to find any bipartisan solution, you will not find a pragmatic response when it comes to fundamental Voting Rights. And those rights, many of which will determine the winners and losers of elections.\par \par \uc2 This is, you know, this is a full context for, you know, politics. And I think we just have to, you know, really, really understand what`s important here. The important thing here is our democracy. What`s important here is our constitutional rights to vote. You know, I`m in the legislature, we have house rules, and we have house practice. But those rules and practices do not Trump our constitutional rights. A very good example, we have a right to be in that house chamber by rule, but we have a constitutional right that says we can leave in a night quorum and shut down the business of the legislature. And that`s what we did. We should not let a Senate tradition infringe on somebody`s constitutional rights to bring Voting \uc2 Rights and Democracy\uc2  in our country.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: I completely understand your argument. I just want to kind of flip it around for one second, though, which is, that the Filibuster is designed to protect the rights of the minority, and there`s likely to be a time probably even in the not-so-distant future that perhaps Republicans are in the majority in the Senate. Do you see any contradiction between using essentially what is a tool of the minority to block legislation in order to come to Washington to protest the tool that the minority has to block the passage of legislation, if you understand what I mean?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Yes, I understand completely what you mean. And listen, there was, you know, there was no disregard or not a lot of debate with Mitch McConnell, when he wanted to add an extra Supreme Court Justice to the Supreme Court, or when he wants to deny President Obama the right to pick a successor to the court under his term. People will use the rules to that advantage when they`re in power and that`s just the nature of this beast. People will use the rules of breaking quorum if they find that it suits them. The fact is that you have the right to use them.\par \par \uc2 Democrats need to use every tool in their bucket in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And that means use the rules they have. And when they don`t work, well, then you make rules that will work and allow this country to have a democracy.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, how confident are you that this issue is going to be one that is galvanizing enough to actually change the status quo here? I mean, it does seem and clearly the way you guys are using the spotlight here, is to try and change public opinion because we know that right now things are pretty calcified in the Senate. What are the chances you think that act -- that that actually happens? And if you can`t pull it off and actually make the change at the federal level, what do you need to do on the ground as activists to try and counteract what you`re opposing here?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:19]\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Sure. Great, great question. If we did nothing today, there`s a 100 percent chance that we`re going to lose. If we do something, there is a chance that we can prevail. And that`s something right now is to rally this nation, to wake America up, to understand the stakes. The president of the United States the most -- the busiest man in the world is going to spend today to talk about voting rights. That ought to mean something to America. And we as Americans need to rally and we need to rise to that occasion. And right now, there seems to be a juggernaut in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it is our job to make sure that the Senate is hearing us. It is our job to make sure that they are going to act and that action needs to be the passage of the For The People Act. While we speed things up on the other federal voting rights legislation, the focus is on For The People Act. We need to get it done. It`s a now or never moment for our country.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Texas State Representative Trey Martinez Fischer, thank you very much for spending some time with us today. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And tonight, will be a clear reminder that the politics of voting rights goes way beyond the ballot box and the Capitol Complex. The fight has even affected our national pastime, baseball. Major League Baseball`s All-Star Game will be played tonight in Denver at Coors Field. Not in Atlanta, where it was originally scheduled to be played. The MLB changed the venue in response to Georgia`s sweeping voting restrictions. And the National Republican Senatorial Campaign Committee is expected to air an ad during the game tonight attacking the "radical left woke crowd".\par \par \uc2 OK, coming up, while President Biden plans to speak about voting rights, many in his own party say it`s the action that`s needed, especially when it comes to changing the filibuster rules as we`ve been discussing.\par \par \uc2 And later, what Dr. Fauci just told my colleague, Andrea Mitchell, as concerns grow over the surge in Delta variant. And the FDA issues another warning surrounding the \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  vaccine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:2o]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENFRONIA THOMPSON, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, TEXAS: I`m not up here to take a vacation in Washington D.C. When I look at the African-American Museum, I thought about the struggle of my people who fought in this country to get the right to vote. And that right is sacred to my constituents that I represent back in Houston, Texas. I`m not going to be a hostage that my voter -- my constituents` right to be stripped from them. We have fought too long and too hard in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. That was Texas State Representative Senfronia Thompson at the Capitol today, the longest-serving woman and African-American in the Texas State Legislature, laying out what could be at stake for her community as she tries to delay passage of restrictive Voting Rights Bills in Texas. Those Texas Democrats are meeting with Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer later this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 As we said they`re calling on congressional Democrats to pass the two voting rights bills that are currently stalled in Congress. But Democrats in both Texas and Washington D.C. are up against a wall wanting to enact voting rights changes but without the votes to do it.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by NBC`s Sahil Kapur, who has the latest on where the Voting Rights legislation stands on Capitol Hill, and the NAACP Legal Defense and Educational Funds Janai Nelson. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Sahil, let me start with you just in terms of the state of play. I know you have a new story out on NBCnews.com, kind of outlining where we are here. We talked a little bit about it with Mike Memoli in terms of what President Joe Biden may or may not be willing to do. But where is the conversation on Capitol Hill right now about the Filibuster and Voting Rights?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Kasie, the conversation on Capitol Hill is quite simply that every debate on Voting Rights is ultimately a debate about the Filibuster. Democrats have the votes in the House and the Senate to pass major Voting Rights legislation with a simple majority. It`s not going to be exactly the For The People Act. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , the Senator from West Virginia does not support that as is, but he supports major components of it.\par \par \uc2 So, the Democrats have a path to getting a lot of it through, but they don`t have the 50 votes necessary in the Senate to abolish or weaken the Filibuster sufficiently to the point where it can pass the Senate where the simple majority. It requires 10 Republican votes at a minimum to pass. And the Venn diagram overlap between the two parties on the issue of voting rights is vanishingly small.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, for the most part, say the Federal Government should not be stepping in at all to this issue, and states should run their elections as they see fit, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Sahil, briefly, there have been some discussions about OK, perhaps if we`re not going to eliminate the Filibuster, we could at least make people pay a political price for Filibustering an issue like voting rights? My sense is Republicans would be happy to go and publicly Filibuster this. What have you been hearing from senators at the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Kasie, I think that`s exactly right. The idea of talking Filibuster is something that Senator Manchin has expressed openness to, something that President Biden has called for, is the kind of thing that might get certain pieces of legislation through where the minority party can be shamed into, you know, ultimately surrendering the floor and letting something pass. That is not the case with voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell, the Republican leader has made it his top priority to kill this Democrats -- this Democratic voting and election bill. This is not the type of issue, as you correctly point out, that Republicans are going to back away from. They can take turns holding the floor for as long as it takes to keep this block.\par \par \uc2 The only way to really get this done is to nuke the Filibuster. Either create a carve-out entirely for voting rights and allow it to come down to 50 votes, which again, doesn`t appear to be in the cards right now, or to nuke it entirely, which is even more of an uphill climb. We do know that Senator Manchin is meeting with these Texas Democratic legislators.\par \par \uc2 Our colleague, Frank Thorp asked him just moments ago, whether he supports a Filibuster carve out for voting rights. He did not say yes, he didn`t say no, he just said he`s anxious to talk to them. But of course, Kasie, he has expressed skepticism in the past about a carve-out on any issue for the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: For sure. So, Janai Nelson, what -- you`ve been in -- your organization has been in some of these meetings with the White House. There`s obviously been a lot of pressure brought to bear on President Biden. So, let`s start with him since we`re about to hear the speech from him in just a little while. What do you hope he says today in that address? And then we`ll discuss what`s going on on Capitol Hill. But how far do you want the President to go today in saying that he supports this and call us -- calling for action?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NELSON, ASSOCIATE DIRECTOR-COUNSEL, \uc2 NAACP LEGAL DEFENSE AND EDUCATIONAL FUND\uc2 : Well, we hope that he sounds the alarm that our democracy is in great peril. That our right to vote is fundamental, it is preservative of every other right that we hold dear. And to the extent that it`s being used as a political football, it is threatening to undermine the safety and national security of our country.\par \par \uc2 If we want to live in a Representative Democracy, we need to ensure that every eligible voter is able to cast a ballot that will be counted, without impediment, without barriers, and without discrimination.\par \par \uc2 He must emphasize that the Congress is charged with protecting the right to vote. Congress has been invited by the Supreme Court to enact new legislation that will protect the Right to Vote. And it is high time that Congress acted. And if that means that we need to change the filibuster to create an exception for democracy-expanding legislation, then that`s what it takes. That is -- I believe our democracy is worth it. And that is a necessary measure that he should endorse.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:43]\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, the reality is to get that done what you just outlined, you have to move as we were talking about with Sahil, you got to move \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  on this issue. And he clearly is feeling the pressure. I mean in conversations, you can tell he knows that this issue is going to come to a head in some way, that there is going to be some other attempts to pass a major bill. And that he`s thinking very seriously about OK, what sort of potential changes to the Filibuster might he be willing to support even if he is firm in that 60-vote threshold question. Do you think that there is a scenario where you can bring enough pressure to bear that he would be willing to make this carve-out for Constitutional Rights when he has been so definitive here?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: I think there is. He`s got to see the chaos that this is creating. The fact that legislators in Texas had to take a stand and leave their state and are willing to stake out time in D.C. until this special session that Governor Abbott called in order to pass what is clearly racially discriminatory voter suppression legislation, tells you that people are willing to make dear sacrifices to preserve the right to vote. And if they are willing to do so, our Congress, our elected Congress members should be willing to do so as well.\par \par \uc2 And right now, he`s standing between the protection of the right to vote that we need at the federal level and what will continue to be a very difficult and chaotic scenario at the state level if that legislation has not passed. So, it`s incumbent upon him to respond to what is happening and unfolding across the country.\par \par \uc2 When we look at the wave of voter suppression bills that have been passed, it is an absolute demand for federal legislation. Restoration of the Voting Rights Act through the Voting Rights Advancement Act, and also a passage of the For The People Act.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So very briefly, the John Lewis Rights Act, which Manchin has expressed some support for, it does need to be tweaked to take into account the recent Supreme Court decision about Arizona restrictions. How confident are you that that particular piece of legislation can be adequately rewritten and passed?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: I`m fairly confident. That legislation can do two things. One, it can respond to the Shelby County versus holder decision from 2013 which rendered significant provision of the Voting Rights Act inoperative. And as I mentioned earlier, in the course of that decision, Chief Justice Roberts invited Congress to enact legislation to modernize the standard on the Voting Rights Act. And that is exactly what the Voting Rights Advancement Act does.\par \par \uc2 The latest decision that the Supreme Court issued on the Voting Rights Act just a couple of weeks ago, the Brnovich case, actually confuses how we can interpret the remainder of the Voting Rights Act, the main provision that protects the right to vote in that section two. And so, that is an invitation for Congress to clarify what it means. To make it clear that the way in which the Supreme Court interpreted section two was actually erroneous.\par \par \uc2 And this is an opportunity for Congress to be unequivocal and to issue a direct mandate that the voting rights of Americans must be protected against racial discrimination, and that we can use the tools at our disposal, Congress`s enforcement powers to do so.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Janai Nelson, Sahil Kapur, thank you both very much for this important conversation today. We really appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, after thousands of Cubans took to the streets in the largest anti-government protests in decades, the communist government has reportedly beefed up policing and shut down social media. Now President Biden is facing a lot of questions about his administration`s Cuba policy or lack thereof.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:13]\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. Cuban authorities appear to be blocking internet access in an effort to prevent activists from organizing more protests like the ones that erupted on Sunday. Cuba`s president is accusing citizens of using social media to coordinate the biggest anti-government demonstrations the communist country has seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports the Cuban police are out in force, patrolling the streets in a country where dissent is not tolerated.\par \par \uc2 Frances Robles is a foreign correspondent for "New York Times" who`s covered Cuba and the Caribbean for years and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Frances, it`s lovely to see you. Thank you so much for being with us. Can you talk a little bit about this latest development, the cutting off of internet? We have these reports that say that all of the -- essentially there are disruptions to WhatsApp, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , Telegram, a lot of the services that the Cuban people are using to communicate with each other.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES ROBLES, NEW YORK TIMES FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: There`s no question that internet is the oxygen that gives this movement breath. And so without internet, the government is going to really be able to succeed in putting a stop to it because when people see those videos of their neighbors and people in other cities around the country attending these protests, they say well, I`m motivated, I`m going to do it, too. Became like a domino effect. And so the government shut that down. Makes it very difficult for people -- impossible for people to be sending \uc2 Facebook\uc2  live videos, WhatsApp videos and things of that nature.\par \par \uc2 It`s not impossible to get a phone call through, but it`s pretty difficult to get a video live streams on the internet.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Frances, what is your sense of how the Biden administration is handling this? I mean, I think that we have a little bit of sound we can play of President Biden on Cuba. This was him talking yesterday. Let`s take a look at what he had to say, and then I want to ask you about it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Cuban people are demanding their freedom from an authoritarian regime and I don`t think we have seen anything like this protest in a long, long time, if quite frankly ever. And the United States stands firmly with the people of Cuba as they assert their universal rights.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So he calls them universal rights, he says the U.S. stands firmly with them. What do you hear as someone who`s covered the region a long time? And what are the protesters hearing from the Biden administration right now?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Well, they`re not hearing much. I mean, they`re hearing support, but the question is what does that mean, what is support? One of the things that you see, the big change that happens depending on whether there`s a Republican or a Democrat occupying the White House is the kinds of restrictions that Cubans have in terms of receiving money, in terms of receiving visitors, flights, and things of that nature.\par \par \uc2 And right now, the restrictions that are in place are the ones that President Trump put in, and it`s really notable that President Biden did not as a first order of business take his pen and reverse that, and put it back to how it had been when he was the vice president in the Obama administration. And so what does that silence mean? Does that mean that he supports these sanctions? That`s very unclear.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, I mean, what is your sense of what the most effective way to -- I mean, if the goal here is to give more voice to the Cuban people, does lifting those sanctions -- I understand obviously that it gives more leeway to people in Cuba, but I mean, talk to people like Senator Bob Menendez, who`s really a regime hawk, he seems to think that perhaps lifting them ultimately keeps the regime in power by lessening the pressure on them. I mean, what`s your sense from the region?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: I mean, this is the big question. These are two very divided camps on this issue that either -- you know, under the Obama administration, they were saying, oh, let`s visit more, let`s get more money in there, let`s get people in there in Cuba opening businesses, and then when they opened their eyes and see how wonderful it is to live a capitalist life, that`s how freedom is going to come to Cuba. And then the other camp, the other complete opposite opinion, is that you have to put them in a chokehold, cut off the money. And cutting off the money cuts off the regime and brings it to its knees. And that`s what you`re seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 Right now, between the restrictions and COVID, you know, restrictions which keeps people from traveling to Cuba, and if people can`t travel to Cuba, that means you don`t have couriers going over there with cash to give out to their families or to their neighbors. And without that cash, you have people who are hungry, you have people who can`t -- there`s no medicine to buy, and that`s where we have the situation that we`re in now. The people are starting to realize, wait a second.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: This is an unsustainable situation.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes. So before I let you go, I want to ask about your latest reporting out of Haiti. I know you cover the region broadly. This investigation into the assassination of the Haitian president and the involvement of a Florida doctor, it`s a little bit hard to wrap your head around. I mean, what is your latest reporting?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: You know, I interviewed some people last night who attended meetings with him earlier this year where they said that he was an impressive person who seemed really motivated, he wanted to be a transitional leader, to bring democracy to Cuba, and that never in a million years did they dream that he would do something like this. And I want to be clear that I`m not saying that he did it. You know, it`s what the government is saying, and let`s find out -- the investigation will find out whether that bears true, but the people who know him say they had absolutely no hint of it.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right. Frances Robles, thank you very much for all of your reporting. We really appreciate you spending some time with us this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And coming up next here, coronavirus cases are now on the rise in more than half the country, and in some places, hospitals are starting to get overwhelmed again. We`re going to go live to the state where health officials say they`re getting nervous.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:21]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: People need to understand that pharmaceutical companies don`t make the decisions. This becomes a regulatory issue with the FDA and a public health recommendation with the CDC and their advisory committee on immunization practices.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. That was Dr. Anthony Fauci talking to my colleague Andrea Mitchell this afternoon, a day after a meeting where \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  presented the Biden administration with data about the potential need for a vaccine booster shot, which health officials continue to say is not necessary right now. Dr. Fauci called it a, quote, "courtesy meeting." But health officials are continuing to push for more people to get their first vaccination as the Delta variant continues to spread especially in communities with low vaccination rates. Nearly all the COVID hospitalizations and deaths are now occurring among the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is infectious disease physician, Dr. Dana Hawkinson. He is the medical director of Infection Prevention and Control with the University of Kansas Health System.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hawkinson, thank you so much for spending some time with us today. And I know your community is right on the line between Kansas and Missouri, outside of Kansas City there. What are you seeing right now in your hospitals with COVID infections? How bad is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. DANA HAWKINSON, INFECTIOUS DISEASE PHYSICIAN: Yes. Right now in the metro areas, in the urban areas, it`s not too bad, specifically in the Kansas City area. It`s not bad. But we know that our numbers in all the -- all the hospitals around us are increasing. We have taken some transfers from those hard-hit areas of Missouri like Springfield, Missouri, as well. But overall, the numbers in our hospitals over the past week to 10 days have been increasing with those acute infections.\par \par \uc2 And I should add, the majority, 75 percent to 80 percent of those people that are coming to the hospital are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So that of course means that a handful of people likely are vaccinated. Can you explain -- do those people tend to have underlying conditions or what might prompt a breakthrough infection that would land you in the hospital if you do have the shot?\par \par \uc2 HAWKINSON: Yes, absolutely. I think that`s the other second point to understand about this. You know, all the vaccines are not 100 percent. But these vaccines that we currently have still do significantly protect you from that severe disease and hospitalization and death. Those patients that we do see in the hospital, right now I`m treating a couple in the ICU and on the regular medical floor, do have significant comorbidities. They have underlying heart disease. They have lung disease. There`s a couple that have active cancer, undergoing chemotherapy or active hematologic malignancy where they don`t have that immune system to work correctly.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:10]\par \par \uc2 So that is a very important point to continue to address is that the people that have gotten vaccinated that we have seen in our hospital do have those significant comorbidities. And even if they aren`t specifically ill from COVID itself, that disease process of COVID may tip them over the line as far as their other conditions go, like their heart disease or their underlying lung disease.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So bottom line, get vaccinated, obviously. I mean, what kinds of reasons --\par \par \uc2 HAWKINSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: I mean, do you encounter people regularly in your job who are not vaccinated who talk to you about the concerns around vaccination or even other health care workers in your hospital? I mean, what are the reasons people are giving you now after all of this evidence that the vaccine works so well is out in public?\par \par \uc2 HAWKINSON: Yes. I mean, I had a friend text me today who still doesn`t really believe in it. Unfortunately. It is very difficult. I have patients in my infectious disease clinic who have declined to get the vaccine. They still believe that the infection itself is not bad or that it is nothing more than a flu-type illness, or that it`s a government hoax. There is that continued concern that with vaccination we are being subject to experiments and we are like guinea pigs. There are a myriad of questions around this. There are myriad of reasons. There still is that misinformation about the vaccines causing infertility.\par \par \uc2 So we have to address these head on and we do try to address those on a daily basis and listen and provide thoughtful answers. But you also have to understand, there are people who just want to continue to be obstinate for one reason or another. There is that division there. You know, we look at this as a pure health issue and we are addressing it as a pure health issue. Politics do not matter, ethnicity, race, other concerns do not matter. We are addressing this as a health issue.\par \par \uc2 Our goal is to keep you healthy, to keep those people that you love and are around healthy, and really keep our whole community healthy.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right. Dr. Dana Hawkinson, of the University of Kansas Health System, thank you very much for taking some time to be with us today. We really appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, as we keep getting new reporting about Trump`s 2020 election lies, Republicans keep demonstrating their loyalty to both him and those election lies. We are going to dive into how that commitment to Trumpism is already affecting some key midterm races, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:17]\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. Even though it`s been eight months since Donald Trump lost his bid for reelection, his power over the Republican Party doesn`t seem to be waning and in fact may even be getting stronger. For many Republicans, the 2022 midterm elections are turning into a Trump loyalty test. In what is normally a highly unusual move, incumbent Senator Lisa Murkowski of Alaska just lost the support of her own state`s Republican Party. GOP leaders there endorsed Murkowski`s pro-Trump primary challenger.\par \par \uc2 And Murkowski isn`t alone. Senator James Lankford faces a similar situation in Oklahoma. And in Ohio, the crowded primary field for the open Senate seat is becoming a race to be the Trumpiest candidate. Altogether it is clear that the Republican Party is betting big on Trumpism in 2022. The question now, whether it will pay off or whether it will scare off independent voters.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Henry Gomez is a national political reporter based in the Midwest. He joins me from the suburbs of Cleveland, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Henry, it is lovely to see you. I have -- in fact you and I have spoken many times in the state of Ohio and across the rest of the country on the campaign trail. So there`s no one I`d rather talk to about let`s start with the critical Ohio Senate race because it`s a pretty good example of how Republicans are falling all over themselves to get in line behind Trump.\par \par \uc2 I know you interviewed J.D. Vance. You talked to Sherrod Brown. Brown`s description of what`s happening there is pretty evocative. What do you think we need to know right now about what`s happening in this race?\par \par \uc2 HENRY GOMEZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: I mean, Sherrod Brown, the Democratic senator who`s not on the ballot in 2022, he sums it up as, you know, five candidates are basically on the playground or schoolyard saying Donald Trump likes me better than he likes you. Really, you know, I mean, it`s partisan but it`s a distillation of what this GOP primary has become in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 You have five announced candidates, all of whom have gone to varying lengths to demonstrate their loyalty to former President Trump, whether it`s -- when they endorsed him, how early they endorsed him, whether they (INAUDIBLE) to campaign in 2016, whether they said something nice about him, whether they raised money for him, whether they (INAUDIBLE) with him. Like there`s no tie that`s too small to flaunt in this race if you`re a Republican running for the seat.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Pretty remarkable. And when you talked to Vance -- he of course is the author of "Hillbilly Elegy," former venture capitalist, someone who really aggressively criticized Trump. He of course said well, I`ve got to suck it up and support if I want to win this race. But you also pressed him on whether Joe Biden was the legitimate nominee and whether there was room in the party for people who believe that Biden legitimately won the election. I thought his answers were pretty interesting.\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: You know, Vance did not directly answer the question. He basically said, I`m just asking questions, and there are irregularities that need to be pointed out and we need to ask questions about, and I`m all for getting to the bottom of that, which really sidesteps, you know, around the direct question of, do you believe Joe Biden is a legitimate president? And you know, actually that`s a milder approach than some of the other candidates in this race have taken.\par \par \uc2 Josh Mandel, a former state treasurer, Jane Timken, a former state party chair, have both basically said there was election fraud on a level that it deprived Trump from winning. And of course, these are things that are not true or cannot be substantiated. And Vance says, you know, rather than tell the truth and say that, you know, Joe Biden won, Donald Trump lost, he`s sort of nibbling around the edges with his answer.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what some Republicans who aren`t willing to fully commit to the lie are trying to do. They`re trying to circle the square there. And it`s interesting to hear -- you know, you have to listen really closely to the answers to find out what they`re saying and what they`re not saying.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 So, Henry, big picture here. We`re obviously heading into the midterms the Senate is going to be on the line for sure. It`s 50-50. But there does seem to be a little bit more confidence among Democrats that they`re going to be able to figure out a way to hang onto the chamber. It`s really the House where things are going to potentially get really dicey. And that of course has to do with redistricting. But there are also questions about the messaging and how each party is positioning itself what they`re doing in Congress and also what President Joe Biden is doing.\par \par \uc2 And I`m just curious your sense, there are a lot of districts across the Midwest where, you know, we talked a lot heading into 2020 about the blue wall that the president needed to rebuild across the Midwest. And he did it and it gave him the White House along with of course the wins in Georgia and other places that are turning blue.\par \par \uc2 But I`m curious to your sense of where things stand right now in some of those really tricky House races. What are you hearing from voters that you talk to about things like policing and crime and voting rights, and whether or not they believe or are buying into Republican arguments that Democrats are going too far or whether they don`t like all of the Trumpism that we just outlined?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: Well, there`s a combination of that. And it`s, you know, it`s hard to put anybody, any voters especially in the Midwest into one box. I mean, when you have a message that says, you know, explicit as defund the police, you know, it takes a couple of conversations at least to explain to a voter what that means and what that doesn`t mean. And so when you get into the more nuances of things, you can have a conversation with these voters, maybe have an interesting debate.\par \par \uc2 But getting, you know, headlines and you know, a couple of snippets here and there, especially in these TV ads, what I`ll tell you both parties are really worried about or watching going into 2022 especially with redistricting is how these suburbs are carved out, what districts they go in, how that affects the makeup of the map. Because the suburbs were big for Biden in 2020. And they`re hoping -- Democrats hope they are again in 2022.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. All right, Henry Gomez, thank you very much for your reporting today. We really appreciate it. It`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to all of you for being with us this hour. I will be back tomorrow with much more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And you can also catch me weekday mornings 5:00 a.m. on "WAY TOO EARLY." But don`t go anywhere right now, MSNBC coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210714eh7d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet The Press Daily for July 9, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Carlos Curbelo, Monica Alba, Courtney Kube \par \uc2 8,755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:56]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, the Republican reckoning. Six months after January 6th, the party`s increasing radicalization and unflinching loyalty to Donald Trump may win primaries but it may mean losses in the long term.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the new CDC masks guidance for kids in school. As \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  pushes for a vaccine booster shot, the CDC and FDA say not so fast. We`ll talk to a governor struggling to get his own state vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And later, after a new wave of arrests in the assassination of Haiti`s President, some are questioning who`s really responsible for the murder? And the White House just announced they`re sending \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials to help with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Friday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd here in Washington where the fence surrounding the Capitol that was put up after pro-Trump extremists stormed the building on January 6 is finally coming down. The dismantling of the fence is arguably the most symbolic change on Capitol Hill since the insurrection six months ago. But perhaps the more substantive change on Capitol Hill in that -- in the last six months since the deadly attack on the Capitol, the Republican Party is arguably not more radical, and more committed to Trumpism, not less.\par \par \uc2 On January 6, North Carolina Senator Richard Burr said in a statement, the President bears responsibility for today`s events. He`d go on to convict him in the impeachment trial.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator Lindsey Graham, a key Trump ally said on the Senate floor that night, count me out, enough is enough. That lasted about 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And education secretary Betsy DeVos resigned from Trump`s cabinet writing, this is an inflection point for me. That day did prove to be an inflection point for many in the Republican Party, but the turn they took was towards Trump rather than away.\par \par \uc2 Just look at the primary as already shaping up for next year`s midterm elections. Let`s take Oklahoma. Trump loyalists including the state party chair are backing a primary challenge to reliably conservative sitting Senator James Lankford, who`s also a practicing pastor, by the way. His sin, he refused to reject the electoral count on January six.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, the Republican primary, to succeed retiring Senator Rob Portman has devolved into something of a race to the conspiratorial right and a contest to hug Trump the hardest.\par \par \uc2 Candidate Josh Mendell repeatedly echoes Trump`s lie that November -- the November election was somehow stolen. And he uses his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed to traffic in Mega-friendly issues like calling sitting Republican Governor Mike DeWine, a tyrant for his COVID leadership and burning a mask on camera.\par \par \uc2 A newly announced candidate J.D. Vance had to completely backtrack on his past comments that were critical of Trump. Even telling Time Magazine, Trump is the leader of this movement. And if I actually care about these people and the things I say I care about, I need to just suck it up and support them.\par \par \uc2 And it`s important to note, blind loyalty to Trump wasn`t a seeming prerequisite to run for election as a Republican. Senator Portman would have likely run for another term.\par \par \uc2 But folks to get elected, you don`t have to just win a primary, you`ve got to win a general election in November too. For Republicans running to the far right, they could win votes in the primary but it could also lose votes in the general.\par \par \uc2 And in a lot of places, if you can at least be competitive with the independents, folks that actually do sit in the middle of this electorate, you just can`t win, period. Not in a place like Ohio, not in Texas. You might be able to pull it off in Oklahoma, but it takes a very specific set of circumstances to give Democrats a shot at any open seat in some of these places, Ohio, Oklahoma you name it. That Republicans may actually be doing their best to create those opportunities for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, losing elections may be the only thing that makes Republicans reexamine what they`ve allowed themselves to become.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former Florida Republican Congressman Carlos Curbelo. He`s also an MSNBC Political Analyst. And Nathan Gonzales, he`s Editor and Publisher of Inside Elections who`s already knee deep in the midterm elections handicapping all of these primary races.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: But Carlos, let me get some big picture here from you. We`re six months and three days from that interaction. Where did you think the Republican Party would be today? Did you really think it would be even Trumpier? Because that`s where we are and I don`t think a lot of us thought that on January 7.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, that`s right Chuck. A lot of us thought that perhaps Republicans were going to wake up, turn the corner, turn the page. A lot of them did seem to make that decision on that day, you pointed out some examples. But since then, really, it`s gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 You think about the fact that 70 something House Republicans voted to certify or to accept the election results on the sixth of January, and then only 35, about half voted for a commission to discover the truth, the full truth on what happened on the sixth of January.\par \par \uc2 Senators who voted almost unanimously. Mitch McConnell only lost I think, seven or eight on the sixth of January, have also drifted back to Trumpism. You get Kevin McCarthy every once in a while, trying to steer the party on climate change and other issues, but as soon as one of these Trump themes comes up, whether it`s the election of 2020, immigration, you see Republicans snap back. And that`s troubling for the party looking at `22 when they should be successful if you look at history.\par \par \uc2 But Mitch McConnell knows this because he`s probably short four or five Senate seats over the last decade, missed opportunities for Republicans, it could be another big year of missed opportunities for Republicans if they don`t turn the page.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nathan, is there a competitive primary on the Republican side of the aisle for a major statewide office that doesn`t revolve around Trump right now?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN GONZALES, EDITOR & PUBLISHER, INSIDE POLITICS: Well, the short answer is no. I mean, in some places, you know, the President -- the former President has already made his decision and made an endorsement in other places such as Ohio. Even though he just went to Ohio, he is yet to back a candidate.\par \par \uc2 But I think that -- is the -- right now the Republican Party, -- there are a few Republicans who like how President Trump acts and what he does and what he says. But for elected officials and for -- and for these potential candidates, I think they see how popular he remains with the voters, that they want his voters either want them to get into the office or keep them to remain in office, and that`s why he still exerts so much influence.\par \par \uc2 And one of the downsides for the Republican Party in dismissing the 2020 election results is that there`s no need to In their minds to reach across the aisle or do to try to reach out to moderate voters because they see the coalition as -- the Trump coalition as being sufficient. When in reality the Trump coalition was not enough to hold the White House, to keep the Senate, or to regain control of the House. And so that`s where the Republican Party is caught.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Here Carlos, where did this lack of understanding of math come from on the Republican side of the aisle? To build on Nathan`s point, I mean, Donald Trump performed quite the trifecta. He was head of the party. And in a two-year period, Lost the House, the Senate, and the White House. You know, American history is littered with ex-presidents who are asked to no longer even show up at the convention if they perform like that as party leaders. And instead, it`s this blind loyalty the other way.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: That`s right, Chuck. And look, this race to win the support and the approval of Donald Trump is a race to the bottom of a bottomless pit. I mean, you can`t lie enough, you can`t be dishonest enough, you can`t ignore the truth enough, and that`s going to get these candidates into big trouble come general election time. So, the Republicans understand in their minds that this is a losing proposition. But their instincts are wrong.\par \par \uc2 You know, in their hearts, they continue pursuing this goal of being smiled upon by Donald Trump. And while that may help you win a primary, it`s going to make it very hard to win a general, it`s going to make it very hard to win the Presidency, and to hold meaningful durable majorities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Nathan, I want to bring up Virginia. I wasn`t going to bring up Virginia in our conversation today, but the former President decided to put out a second endorsement of Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Youngkin is the Republican nominee. He`s been blasting the airwaves with ads that don`t say the word Republican, desperately tried to say, hey, politics, what just happened? Can`t we, you know, -- he`s desperately trying to shake the Republican and Trump label.\par \par \uc2 And then here`s what Trump put out this morning. Wow, the numbers are really looking good for Glenn Youngkin in this race against \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  for governor of Virginia. Glenn has been an incredible success and will truly make Virginia great again.\par \par \uc2 Could \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  have gotten a better gift than that statement today?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:18]\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: I think if we were -- if we were closer to the general election, they probably just take that statement and just put it on the air, put a few thousand points of television behind an ad and just put that out because, you know, if this race is -- if this gubernatorial race is about, you know, do you support or do you like President Trump or not, then \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is going to win easily. I mean, Virginia is not a swinger battleground circumstance -- a battleground state under normal circumstances.\par \par \uc2 What I do think the Virginia race will be interesting because I don`t -- I think that in the short term, the Republican Party is not going to become a bigger tent or more moderate because of what Republicans are going to do, but they could become a bigger tent because of what Democrats do.\par \par \uc2 That, if Democrats are viewed as going too far and too fast on either policy or cultural issues, that could drive some moderates who have been voting Democratic in the past few elections, it could drive them into the Republican tent, in spite of President Trump and despite of what other Republican elected officials doing the same.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Carlos, what is it, James Lankford, do? This is not a moderate Senator that`s been representing Oklahoma. He is, I would say, a very principled conservative. He is you know, he`s a -- he`s as ethical I think as they come. He`s a practicing pastor. I think he`s one of just two in the Senate, Senator Raphael Warnock being the other. And his sin is essentially abiding by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 How do you beat back a primary challenge like that when you`re -- the state party chair has decided you committed a sin? They`re going to censure him and Jim Inhofe for this. I`m not talking about RINOs here, Lankford and Inhofe.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Chuck, the best strategy if you`re in James Lankford`s first position is the strategy a lot of House Republicans who I`ve been following, who did vote to certify the elections, who did vote for a January 6 Commission. What they`ve done is gone out and sold the truth in a very compelling way to their voters.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of people get upset. But you really got to stand up there at these town halls and say, hey, this is why I voted for the commission because I stand with our Capitol Police officers who were attacked that day. They deserve to know the truth. This is why I voted to certify the election because these election results were valid and because every single court that heard a challenge rejected the challenges of the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 You cannot run away from the truth. You can`t try to have it both ways because that`s when you really get in trouble. You lose everyone. So, James Lankford and everyone else has to come out, sell the truth hard, own their votes, own their positions, and try to convince their voters that what they`re saying is true.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nathan, it seems like the other issue that`s been created by Donald Trump`s decision to involve himself in all of these primaries is in some key places, you know, you`re not getting the candidates that Mitch McConnell would want.\par \par \uc2 Doug Ducey is not going to be a candidate for Senate. In another world, he would have been in Arizona. Herschel Walker has cleared the field even as Georgia Republicans are going, but we can`t beat him, but he might be the weakest nominee to have. He`s not behind Eric Greitens, but Eric Greitens kind of -- is sort of pulled this off so far it looks like in Missouri. This has really impacted Senate recruiting, has it not?\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: Yes, I think there`s a difference between the goals, what`s on -- at the top of President Trump`s mind and what`s on the top of Republicans who are trying to win back the House and the Senate. You mentioned -- the key races, Arizona and Georgia being two of the biggest ones.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting, though, is that President Trump`s influence in the races where he has endorsed or even has shown a preferred candidate such as Georgia hasn`t cleared the fields entirely. You know, he is -- he`s endorsed Congressman Mo Brooks in Alabama. There still going to be a primary there. He`s endorsed Congressman Ted Budd in North Carolina. There still going to be a primary there. I mean, I think the candidates want to have Trump but it`s not a definite to clear the field situation.\par \par \uc2 I would also say when we`re looking at the House and the Senate, it`s possible that Republicans do well and win the majorities without broadening their tent. I mean, if there is a disproportionately lower turnout on the Democratic side, maybe they`re just not as excited because President Trump isn`t in the office and hasn`t been defeated, then Republicans, they just need a single Senate seat. And they just need five seats in the House and they might be able to get almost all of those through redistricting alone.\par \par \uc2 And so, and that -- that would embolden Republicans to continue on this path if they can win the majorities without changing, you know, the scope of their party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Nathan, let me -- let me -- but right now if I were a betting person I would bet the Democrats pick up Senate seats and the Republicans pick control of the House. How would you -- how would you deal with that scenario?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:08]\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: I -- I`m not -- I`m not there yet on the specifics. I`m not confident. If I were a Democratic strategist, I would not be confident in holding this. I mean, every -- literally every seat is going to matter, you know. And if Republicans get someone like Governor Chris Sununu in New Hampshire, you know, that`s a big get for them on that side.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we`re talking about Democrats are holding Senate seats in Arizona, in Georgia, where sure Biden won but it was narrow. And so, again, if that Democratic turnout dips a little lower than what Republican turnout is going to be, then those are -- you know -- then those become Republican states in a midterm environment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, that`s for sure. Carlos Curbelo, Nathan Gonzales, I appreciate you both on this Friday getting a start with quite the change in the Republican Party from January 6 and not the change many folks expected.\par \par \uc2 Let me change gears here a little bit. We have a bit of breaking news from the White House where the Press office just announced that President Biden spoke with the Russian leader Vladimir Putin today and they talked about the ongoing ransomware attacks that appear to be emanating from Russia. Let`s now bring in Monica Alba at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And Monique, I know this is early. They`ve made this part of it public. Is this part -- is this a phone call to say be prepared, what I said I was going to do, I`m going to do? Do we have any more details on what the converse -- what happened in the conversation?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, this is something where the President just a couple of days ago, Chuck, was asked what are you prepared to do in response to this latest ransomware attack? How will you speak with President Putin? And he said I will deliver that message to him. I`m not going to read it out for you guys right now. Well, that call has now taken place. We know it lasted about 60 minutes earlier today.\par \par \uc2 And I want to read you the portion specifically that talks about this cyberattack and concerns where the President and the White House are saying, he underscored the need for Russia to take action to disrupt ransomware groups operating in the country and emphasized he`s committed to continuing engagement on the broader threat posed by ransomware.\par \par \uc2 But another very key line here where the readout says that President Biden reiterated the U.S. will take any necessary action to defend its people and its critical infrastructure in the face of this continuing challenge. So, in effect, a very similar message to what was delivered in person in Geneva just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the White House gave Russia that list of things that were essentially don`t touch this or there were going to be serious consequences. So, this is something where we know the White House is going to continue to be engaged.\par \par \uc2 The Press Secretary Jen Psaki is briefing right now. She was asked whether President Putin offered any assurances that he was going to tackle cybercriminals within his own country, and she said, I`m not going to read out on behalf of the Kremlin, what he committed to or didn`t commit to. We will let them do that.\par \par \uc2 So, we haven`t gotten the readout from that side of things yet. But this is something that there was certainly a lot of pressure on President Biden to do because he said specifically when he was at that Switzerland summit that if this continued, he was going to call out the Russian President and that there would absolutely be consequences.\par \par \uc2 In fact I think the words were, the U.S. will respond in kind. So, this is the first step it seems of that action in response, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, they don`t like the -- they don`t like the phrase red line, but clearly this was a red line that was drawn, it appears Putin has crossed it, and it sounds like this red line is going to hold. We`ll find out. Monica Alba at the White House for us, Monica, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, coronavirus cases are spiking in states with low vaccination rates. And it`s the red states getting hit the hardest right now particularly in the deep South.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to talk to a Republican governor whose state is in a race against the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: CDC announced this morning that children who are fully vaccinated will not have to wear masks in the classroom this year. That`s good news to the ears of my two kids. Currently, the vaccine is only approved for children over 12, leaving a large number of younger students out of the guidance.\par \par \uc2 In this, cases of the Delta variant rise across the United States, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  has announced that they`re planning to seek emergency use authorization for a third dose of the vaccine, what they`re calling a booster shot, to protect against the highly contagious strain.\par \par \uc2 But so far, the FDA and CDC released a joint statement saying, pushing back on the necessity of a booster. Saying right now, Americans who have been fully vaccinated do not need a booster at this time. We continue to review any new data as it becomes available and will keep the public informed. We are prepared for booster doses if and when the science demonstrates that they are needed.\par \par \uc2 Experts say though, the immediate focus needs not to be on a booster for those that have already been vaccinated, but it`s dealing with the unvaccinated. Some states are still struggling to convince residents to get their first dose of the vaccine and that is the population most at risk right now.\par \par \uc2 According to the CDC, 19 states still have a vaccination rate below 50 percent. And many of the lowest rates are clustered in the southeast of this country. Some of the states are also seeing a sharp rise in new COVID cases. Yesterday, Arkansas had their second consecutive day with more than a thousand cases.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by somebody who`s been working really hard on this in his state, and it`s Republican Governor Asa Hutchinson. Governor Hutchinson, it`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`ve got family in Arkansas. I`ve got family that live in a county that`s less than 30 percent vaccinated right now. And, you know, the anecdotal conversations they have -- there is -- I`m sure you`ve heard them -- they`re very frustrating conversations.\par \par \uc2 Have you found anything that has penetrated this sort of wall of hesitancy, particularly in your smaller counties?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON, (R-AR): What`s a tough wall of hesitancy that we see, and we`ve been hit very hard in the sense that we have the Delta variant that came, and then our vaccine rate -- vaccination rate is not as high as it should have been. A month ago, we were in good shape, and all of a sudden, these two factors that came into play. And so, we`re mounting a -- again, another significant campaign going to the communities, listening to them, trying to elicit different strategies to encourage everyone to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And it`s the misinformation that`s out there. Everything from the vaccination will lead to mind control, to misinformation in terms of consequences in fertility. And we want everybody to go to their trusted physician, go to health care advisor, get good advice on this. We believe that will lead to vaccination. What we`re seeing, though, is that, as the risk increase, vaccination rates will go up.\par \par \uc2 And so, it`s sad to say, but as we see more cases, more hospitalizations, that could be the impact.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s what happened in South Texas. I mean, sadly, it took a bunch of deaths that suddenly they`d improve their vaccination rate. I`m curious, you know, are you running into problems with even pastors who want to be helpful, that feel as if they`re so exhausted from the masked mandate, debates that took place in churches that they don`t want it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, in many of these communities, they`re the most trusted person. Not you, not the elected official, you know, the church pastor. And you know, there are some stories here naturally indicated that these pastors are exhausted from these fights.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:26]\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, there is an exhaustion across the board. I mean, 18 months of dealing with this pandemic, and then you feel like you`ve got it whipped, you feel like you`re going in the right direction, and it reverses on you. And so, it`s tough.\par \par \uc2 You got to summon courage, and our healthcare providers are doing that. Pastors are trusted. And I think they`ve been a very great partner particularly in the minority community where they`ve been key to our outreach, key to our vaccination programs where we continue to enhance that, but there is a weariness out there.\par \par \uc2 But more than the weariness, I`m concerned about the misinformation and that`s what we`re really trying to counter right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What would -- who would help the most here? I mean, is it -- is it -- folks at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  being more aggressive? Because a lot of this I know -- my anecdotal with friends and family, it`s always something they saw on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , right, and then it -- sort of circulates. Is there something they could do? Would you like to see the former President do more on this front too? I mean, these are a lot of these folks are supporters of his.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I don`t think the answer is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  trying to control the speeds that are on their platform in this regard, but it`s up to people out there to respond, and educate, and compete on those platforms.\par \par \uc2 You know, in terms of the former President, you know, I`ve heard numerous times of ads and public service announcements that include his voice encourage people to get the vaccine. And so, it`s actually ironic that some of the strongest Trump supporters do not get the vaccine, but Trump himself is saying, let`s get it and recognize the importance of it. So, we have to have that unified voice. And we`re going to continue to magnify that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are some -- you know, you`re taking over the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 , you`ve been a governor a while, you`ve got these friendships, you know, professional friendship. What are some best practices that you think do work? That -- you know, you said, in the minority communities, the religious outreaches work. That`s not obviously working in all the rural communities. What are -- been some best practices?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: You know, that`s what`s unique about the states is that what works in one might not work in another. We have different obstacles and challenges. But there are some best practices that we see and that`s one of the great roles of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And first, it was the accessibility, it was the equity that we practice to make sure we get it into all of the areas of our state. It is the advertising campaign and trusted advisors. All of these work in different ways across the states. We`re trying something different Arkansas which is the -- what I call community COVID conversations where I`m going into each many different communities, listening to them.\par \par \uc2 And so, every state might try something different, but the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2  is very proud of our colleagues and sharing the information that works. We try things. If it didn`t work, we move on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. You`ve said getting the full FDA approval will help build some confidence and I don`t think you`re wrong there. I think a lot of people think that there is a chunk of these folks that that will -- that will motivate them.\par \par \uc2 We have a vaccine mandate for students in school to go to public schools. At some point, is that going to be necessary? Do you think for 12 and over, if it`s fully approved, that there ought to be a public-school mandate at least for those students there, and that may then essentially trickle up, if you will?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I think that is worthy of discussion but let`s first get that final approval from the FDA. I don`t think that you`re going to see that kind of mandate across the board for utilizing this vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Once it`s fully approved, there is precedent for it obviously. But in Arkansas, the legislature passed a law that prohibited vaccine mandates of that nature. And so, that`s not going to happen here. It`s not going to happen in many states. And the sad thing here --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, do you not need measles, so you can go to Arkansas public schools now without getting vaccinated for the measles, the mumps, or polio or any of that stuff?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: No. That`s -- no we do have requirements for vaccinations but I`m saying that sadly even once we get final approval for the COVID vaccine, we`re prohibited from mandating that in the schools and making that a requirement. And so maybe that is unique in Arkansas, but that was the will of the legislature and so we`re not going to go down that path, and that`s why we have to have the responsibility of the parents and the schools in our education to accomplish the same result.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor Asa Hutchinson, the newly I guess minted -- I don`t know if you`re elected but I guess you`re the newly minted chair of the National Governor`s Association. I imagine we`ll be talking again soon, good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Good to be with you, Chuck. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, some new arrests in the assassination of Haiti`s president are spurring new questions about that investigation as the U.S. announces they`ll be sending a delegation to Haiti that includes some \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials to help.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Now an update in Haiti. Fifteen people are now under arrest in Haiti after President Moise was brazenly killed by what the Haitian government says were former mercenaries posing as DEA agents. Haitian authorities held a dramatic news conference yesterday parading these handcuffed suspected behind tables of machetes, guns, and passports. Authorities say nine more suspects remain at large and four others were killed in a gun battle.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 But there are many questions over who these suspects are, how authorities found them so quickly, and whether these are even the real perpetrators.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is a former "New York Times" reporter and he`s the founder and publisher of the "Haitian Times," Garry Pierre-Pierre.\par \par \uc2 All right, Garry, this -- yesterday, it`s hard to look at all of this and suddenly they found all these suspects. They have this big press conference, sort of almost saying, OK, case closed. None of this seems -- it almost seemed to be a little too clean, a little too quick. There`s just a lot of unanswered questions here. What more do you know?\par \par \uc2 GARRY PIERRE-PIERRE, FOUNDER AND PUBLISHER, THE HAITIAN TIMES: You`re absolutely right, Chuck. This is so Hollywood. So quaint. I have never seen the Haitian police been so efficient in their history. And I have never seen mercenaries to be so incompetent. So it doesn`t add up. OK. This is all show we`re watching. And I think we need to get to the bottom of this, and I think that the Haitian government should reach out to Washington and ask the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to help investigate this.\par \par \uc2 This is too serious. We should not play around. We should not be having these fake videos that they`re parading the people. This is unnecessary. These men, whether they`re innocent or not, should not be in cuff and paraded like this. This is not how a civilized society operate and we shouldn`t operate that way. We should get to the bottom of it. They try to use us in the media, and let`s not go for it. I know we`re not going to go for it. We are too smart to go for this obvious and really absurd explanation.\par \par \uc2 When you have the leading -- one of the leading journalists in Haiti, Jean Dominique, who was assassinated in front of his radio station 20 years ago, today we still don`t have the culprit. But all of a sudden --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE-PIERRE: No one was shot at the president`s house, but they have all of the suspects already lined up for us to watch?\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you hate to say it, you look at it and I don`t think anybody wants to jump to conclusions, but there are people that are now in power or have claimed power that are telling people to go back to work, everything is in hand, we`ve got this. It`s hard not to look at the people benefitting right now with this power vacuum and not want to ask a bunch of questions.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE-PIERRE: The biggest problem is right now to me is what will the gangs do? Chuck, because the gangs have been controlling Haiti for well over a couple -- two years, where -- with their military precision, where they have cut off the artery of the capital city. They`re making it inaccessible to the provinces so therefore there is no movement whatsoever. And so, they have been in charged. The police are no match for them.\par \par \uc2 And now if they`re deciding that they want to fill that power vacuum, you know, it`s going to be bad, and we have to be careful not to let that happen. That`s the biggest worry I have right now. Forget about the little theater that they`re feeding us, but they`re a real concern on the ground in Haiti. What will the gangs do because they have been massacring people left and the right in the last two years?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE-PIERRE: And the place has been really volatile.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the U.N. recognized Prime Minister Joseph yesterday and gave him legitimacy. Ariel Henry is still claiming the prime ministership. Where is this headed?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE-PIERRE: Well, since the U.N. has given its stamp of approval, I think he has a little bit more legitimacy and support. Hopefully they will organize elections, but September is really out of the question. I think they should push for November, stabilize things, and have people go out and vote. Hopefully if the situation remains stable.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, Chuck, the Delta variant of COVID has been ravaging Haiti as we speak. There is no vaccine whatsoever in the country. And to get a COVID test cost $80 U.S. The average Haitian makes about that much in a month so there are a lot of pressing issue facing Haiti while, you know, they are showing a movie.\par \par \uc2 TODD: An excellent point and considering how close we are and the importance of us as an ally to Haiti, that is also on the COVID front, something that we ought to be getting involved in and helping out.\par \par \uc2 Garry Pierre-Pierre, appreciate you coming on and I imagine we`ll be checking in with you against next week, sir. So thank you. And thank you for your work.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE-PIERRE: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, President Biden defending his decision to leave Afghanistan and leave the fight against the Taliban to the Afghan army. But can they really do it?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:44]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Do I trust the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ? No, but I trust the capacity of the Afghan Military who is better trained, better equipped and more competent in terms of conducting war.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden yesterday insisting that he does trust Afghanistan`s military to take over operations in the country as the U.S. Military withdraws from the country. But despite that trust, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is continuing to battle Afghan troops even gaining territory in northern Afghanistan, inching ever closer to the capital of Kabul. They`ve even been sending diplomatic teams to Moscow and other neighboring countries, essentially reassuring them that they`re ready perhaps if they end up taking complete control.\par \par \uc2 Courtney Kube covers the Pentagon for NBC News. She`s also been covering the Afghan Military`s training for years and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So let`s just unpack that one statement from President Biden. How many people in that five-walled building there that you work at would say the same thing about the Afghan Security Forces?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So you kind of have to unpack it into different areas, right? The Afghan commandos are actually pretty proficient. They have been doing operations on their own for some time. They still have some logistical support from the U.S. and coalition forces but they operate pretty independently. The Afghan Air Force literally move -- came up from nothing.\par \par \uc2 And they`re conducting some air strikes. They`re doing OK. They`re going to really have a difficult time with maintenance once the U.S. and coalition and contractors leave specifically. But as far as operating they do OK. Where they are having -- the Afghan Security Forces have an issue is the more rural areas, and the local police and the local soldiers. And that`s because the further you get away from the central government in Kabul the less support they have. They may not have the same level of training.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  there -- it`s much easier for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to move into these areas and kind of pick them off. We`re already hearing reports of some of them that are just fleeing or they`re laying down their weapons and they`re handing them over to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . So that`s where the real vulnerability is.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and the NATO coalition have been training up these Afghan Security Forces for more than a decade. They built them up to about 352,000 but it`s more than just the size of the forces that matter, the sheer number. It`s the capabilities and that`s where officials are concerned.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about the idea of leaving Bagram? I mean, you know, when you think about the various threats that the U.S. has to worry about, an unstable Pakistan that has nuclear weapons next door, Afghanistan is also next door to Iran, next door to Russia, you could make a strategic, you know, case that geographically Bagram was a pretty important base of operations.\par \par \uc2 Are there a lot -- are there people concerned in the Pentagon that we`ve left a perfectly base that may have been very helpful with other things that we have to worry about when it comes to our national security?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Yes. It`s not just an important location but it was also a base where they could fly enormous cargo planes, too. So it was a very valuable asset there. When people talked in the past about the U.S. maintaining some sort of an enduring, real enduring military presence in Afghanistan, Bagram was always the base that they talked about keeping open.\par \par \uc2 But I am not surprised that it closed. We`ve been hearing for weeks now that the U.S. was going to hand it over late June, early July for operational security concerns. That`s been kept very quiet. People have not really been reporting on it. But I`m not at all surprised. The way that the Biden administration, they have been saying they want to move -- you know, keep moving ahead with this draw down and it is virtually complete right now, Chuck. More than 90 percent of U.S. troops and equipment are out at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a Bagram plan B -- excuse me, a Kabul plan B, meaning if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  gets Kabul, then what?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: If the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  gets Kabul then we should expect to see the U.S. embassy presence there to go away. And that will be because obviously it won`t be a safe place for them but they also won`t have a safe airport. You need to have an airport nearby if you`re going to have a diplomatic presence.\par \par \uc2 We should also expect the Afghan Security Forces. The U.S. and NATO coalition are not going to keep funding them. If the forces don`t dissolve right away, if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  were to take over, we should expect that funding to dry up, and that is one of the things that`s really keeping the Afghanistan Security Forces alive right now and it`s coalition and international funding. And right now it`s promised to them for several years. I can`t imagine that the U.S. is going to keep funding them if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is running the government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s for sure. Courtney Kube, at the Pentagon for us. Courtney, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And I believe we are perhaps seconds away we got the two-minute warning on President Biden`s remarks. We expect him to talk about an executive order on competition. I think we`re going to try to sneak in a quick break. But the minute he starts speaking we will break out of it. So we`ll do that and we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president is speaking now. This is an executive order on competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And because our successful vaccination program strategy has been working and the immediate relief through the American Rescue Plan has brought back our economy from the worst economic crisis in nearly a century, America is now on track. We`re now on track for the highest economic growth in 40 years. And we`re in the highest growth records on record.\par \par \uc2 We designed our economic strategy to be durable through the ups and downs that come with recovery. There are ups and downs. That`s why the American Rescue Plan was designed to help people not just all at once but over the course of a full year, so we can continue to support families, small businesses, state and local budgets to help them weather those ups and downs. And now that the economy is back on track we`re making progress on the second phase of our strategy, ensuring long-term growth.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 That`s what my Build Back Better agenda, including my American`s Family Plan, and the bipartisan infrastructure agreement we reached last month. That`s what they`re all about, long term. But to keep the country moving we have to take another step as well. And I know you`re all tired of hearing me during the campaign and since I have been elected president talk about it. And that`s bringing fair competition back to the economy.\par \par \uc2 That`s why today I`m going to be signing shortly the executive order promoting competition to lower prices, to increase wages, and to take another critical step toward an economy that works for everybody.\par \par \uc2 The heart of American capitalism is a simple idea. Open and fair competition. That means that if your companies want to win your business they have to go out and they have to up their game, better prices and services, new ideas and products. That competition keeps the economy moving and keeps it growing. Fair competition is why capitalism has been the world`s greatest force in prosperity and growth.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, competitive economy means companies must do all they do -- everything they do to compete for workers. Offering higher wages, more flexible hours, better benefits, but what we`ve seen over the past few decades is less competition and more concentration that holds our economy back. We`ve seen it in big agriculture and big tech and big pharma, the list goes on. Rather than competing for consumers, they are consuming their competitors.\par \par \uc2 Rather than competing for workers, they`re finding ways to gain the upper hand on labor. And too often the government has actually made it harder for new companies to break in and compete. Look at what that means for family budgets. Take prescription drugs. Just a handful of companies control the market for many vital medicines, giving them leverage over everyone else to charge whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 As a result, Americans pay 2 1/2 times more for prescription drugs than in any other leading company, and nearly one in four Americans struggle to afford the medication. Another example, hearing aids. Right now, if you need a hearing aid, you can`t just walk into a pharmacy and pick one up over the counter. You have to get it from a doctor or a specialist.\par \par \uc2 Not only does that make getting hearing aids inconvenient, it makes them considerably more expensive. And it makes it harder for new companies to compete, innovate and sell hearing aids at lower prices. As a result, a pair of hearing aids can cost thousands of dollars. That`s a big reason why just 1 in 7 Americans with hearing loss actually use a hearing aid.\par \par \uc2 Another example, internet services. There are more than 65 million Americans live in a place with only one high speed internet provider. Research shows when you have a limited internet operation, you pay up to five times more on average than families in places with more choices. That`s what a lack of competition does. It raises the prices you pay.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just consumes getting hurt. Big ag is putting a squeeze on farmers, small and family farms, first-time farmers, like veterans coming home and black and Latino and indigenous farmers. They`re seeing price hikes for seed, lopsided contracts, shrinking profits and growing debt.\par \par \uc2 Lack of competition hurts workers as well. In many communities there are only a handful of employers left competing for workers. Think of the company towns across Appalachian and other parts of the country where one big corporation runs the show. When corporations have that kind of leverage over workers, it pushes down -- it pushes down advertised wages up to 17 percent.\par \par \uc2 And as this competition decreases, businesses don`t feel the pressure to innovate or invest in their workforce. That hurts working families and hurts our economy. All told, between rising prices and lowering wages, lack of choice cost the median American household $5,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m a proud capitalist. I spent most of my career representing the corporate state of Delaware. I know America can`t succeed unless American business succeeds, and let me be very clear, capitalism without competition isn`t capitalism, it`s exploitation. Without healthy competition, big players can change and charge whatever they want and treat you however they want.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:08]\par \par \uc2 And for too Americans, that means accepting a bad deal for things that you can`t go without. So, we know we`ve got a problem, a major problem, and we also have an incredible opportunity. We can bring back more competition to more of the country, helping entrepreneurs and small businesses get in the game. Helping workers get a better deal, helping families save money every month.\par \par \uc2 The good news is we`ve done it before. The early 1900s President Teddy Roosevelt saw an economy dominated by giants like Standard Oil and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , railroads. He took them on and he won. He gave the little guy a fighting chance. Decades later, during the Great Depression, his cousin Franklin Roosevelt saw a wave of corporate mergers that wiped out scores of small business crushing competition and innovation. So he ramped up antitrust enforcement, eightfold in just two years. Saving families billions in today`s dollars and helping set the course for sustained economic growth after World War II.\par \par \uc2 He also called for an economic bill of rights including, quote, "The right of every businessman large and small to trade in an atmosphere of freedom from unfair competition and domination by monopolies," end of quote. Between them, the two Roosevelts established an American tradition. An anti-trust tradition. It is how we ensure that our economy isn`t about people working for capitalism. It`s about capitalism working for people. But over time we`ve lost the fundamental American idea that true capitalism depends on fair and open competition.\par \par \uc2 Forty years ago we chose the wrong path in my view, following the misguided philosophy of people like Robert Bork, and pulled back on enforcing the laws to promote competition. We are now 40 years into the experiment of letting giant corporations accumulate more and more power. And what have we gotten from it? Less growth, weakened investment, fewer small businesses. Too many Americans who feel left behind, too many people who are poorer than their parents.\par \par \uc2 I believe the experiment failed. We have to get back to an economy that grows from the bottom-up and the middle-out. The executive order I`m soon going to be signing commits the federal government to full and aggressive enforcement of our anti-trust laws. No more tolerance for abuse of actions by monopolies. No more bad mergers that lead to mass layoffs, higher prices, fewer options for workers and consumers alike.\par \par \uc2 My executive order includes 72 specific actions. I expect the federal agencies, and they know this, to help restore competition. So that we have lower prices, higher wages, more money, more options, and more convenience for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Today, I want to focus on three specific actions. First, the FDA, the Food and Drug Administration. They`re going to work with the states and tribes to safely import prescription drugs from Canada. That`s just one of many actions in the executive order that will lower prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 Second, the FDA is going to issue rules so that hearing aids can be sold over the counter. That`s something the last administration supposed to have done but didn`t do. We`re going to get it done. After these rules gone to effect, the pair of hearing aids would cost hundreds of dollars, not thousands, and you`ll be able to pick them up at your local drugstore.\par \par \uc2 Third, we`re going to improve competition for workers. I`ve talked a lot about noncompete agreements, contracts that say you can`t take another job in your field even if you get a better deal. I made a speech I was just reminiscing with my staff back in 2018 at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  where I talked about the non-compete clause that were just -- I found to be absolutely ridiculous but how prevalent they were throughout industries.\par \par \uc2 At least one in three businesses require their workers to sign a noncompete agreement. These aren`t just high-paid executives or assigned to hold secret formulas for \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  so Pepsi can`t get their hands on it. The recent study found 1 in 5 workers, without a college education, they`re subject to noncompete agreements like construction workers, hotel workers, disproportionately women and women of color.\par \par \uc2 Think of the 26-year-old employee at a company. She`s a star worker, but she hasn`t been treated right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210710eh7900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet The Press Daily for July 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Vin Gupta, Ellison Barber, Richard Engel \par \uc2 8,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Search for survivors and answers as the death toll continues to rise in Surfside, Florida. I`ll speak with the top structural engineer brought in to investigate the collapse as concerns grow for other buildings in the area.\par \par \uc2 And later, just days ahead of the 4th of July, the last remaining U.S. troops have officially left Bagram Air Force Base in Afghanistan marking the end of a two-decade presence for American military power in the region. We have a live report from Kabul, just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Friday, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. America is on the move but so is the Delta variant. We got a bit of an ominous briefing yesterday from public health officials as folks hit airports and roads this weekend and are set to break pandemic travel records.\par \par \uc2 The highly transmissible COVID variant is fueling a rise in infections in communication -- communities where vaccinations lag which is just too many places right now. This morning while delivering remarks about the COVID recovery, President Biden laid out his concerns, as the U.S. has sadly set to fall short of his vaccination goal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am concerned that people who have not gotten vaccinated have the capacity to catch the variant and spread the variant to other people who have not been vaccinated. I am not concerned there`s going to be a major outbreak in other words, that we`re going to have another epidemic nationwide, but I`m concerned lives will be lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a lot of lives have been and will be lost, and in many cases right now completely unnecessarily. Last month, we lost about 10,000 Americans from this virus. It`s really tragic because probably somewhere around 99 percent of them would be alive today had they been vaccinated. That`s based on recent CDC data.\par \par \uc2 The uncomfortable truth is that when it comes to vaccinations, there are two Americas. Of the 19 states in the District of Columbia that have already surpassed the White House`s vaccination goal, Joe Biden carried every single one of those states in 2020. And of the 17 states that have yet to surpass even 60 percent of adults vaccinated, Donald Trump won all of them except one, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I just spoke to the President`s Chief Medical Adviser Dr. Anthony Fauci, for an in-depth interview that`s going to air this Sunday on MEET THE PRESS, and he specifically brought up this kind of divide. Here`s a piece of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about the Delta variant. It is clearly the most transmissible variant we`ve dealt with yet. Is it more lethal? And how concerned are you that it is -- that it could cause another spike in this country?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: I don`t think you`re going to be seeing anything nationwide because fortunately, we have a substantial proportion of the population vaccinated so it`s going to be regional. And that`s the thing that will be confusing when people look at what we do. We`re going to see and I`ve said almost two types of America. You know, those regions of America which are highly vaccinated, and we have a low level of dynamics of infection, and in some places, some states, some cities, some areas with a level of vaccination is low, and the level of virus dissemination is high. That`s where you`re going to see the spikes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again, you can catch my full interview with Dr. Fauci this Sunday on MEET THE PRESS where we`re going to focus on a deep dive on how COVID has changed our lives, and still changing our lives.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for more on these developments is Ed Yong, he`s a Staff Writer at the Atlantic. He won a Pulitzer for his reporting on the pandemic. And Dr. Vin Gupta is an NBC News medical contributor. He`s a Pulmonologist and a Pandemics Consultant.\par \par \uc2 Ed, I want to dive into to -- you really frame this so well this week in the Atlantic, basically, a race between our vaccines and the variants. The good news is the vaccines are still beating the variants. The bad news is the variants have probably a lot more running room right now because the vaccines are not widespread enough.\par \par \uc2 ED YONG, STAFF WRITER, "THE ATLANTIC": Yes, that`s right. The vaccines are still holding up to the lofty expectations that we had for them. They are still working in the main against the variants, but the variants are pummeling unvaccinated communities.\par \par \uc2 And critically, the more that second dynamic is allowed to continue, the less our vaccine shield is likely to hold. Firstly, because the vaccines are imperfect, right? They`re very, very good, but they`re not 100 percent which means that in -- if community transmission is very high, then there`s going to be more breakthrough cases. But also the more -- the more the virus test relay in the unvaccinated communities, the more chance it then has to evolve into even -- into newer variants which have a chance of further edging into the protection that the vaccines have afforded us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play what Dr. Walensky said yesterday, Dr. Gupta, because then I want you to-- I imagine you will jump off of her comments but let me play what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: We continue to see overall low numbers of cases, hospitalizations, and deaths. However, working state by state, and county by county, it is clear that communities where people remain unvaccinated are communities that remain vulnerable. As the Delta variant continues to spread across the country, we expect to see increased transmissions in these communities unless we can vaccinate more people now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Dr. Gupta, I have to say it`s a bit disconcerting. You know, the fact that we had 10 -- basically 10,000 Americans die in the month of June, and you sit there and you`re like, they were completely preventable, completely. We could talk about what happened in the first 599,000 deaths that we have, and many of them we could -- you know, we could have a debate about how many of them were preventable deaths.\par \par \uc2 But we know right now, in the month of June, these vaccines were available for everybody, and basically, 99 percent of these deaths were unavoidable and somehow, we`re still having these deaths. It is beyond frustrating, it`s beyond alarming. It`s also a bit -- it`s just sad.\par \par \uc2 VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Chuck, good afternoon. Good to see you. I -- what I`ll say, I mean, it`s frustrating as a clinician that has to care for a 30-year-old that has the rest of their life in front of them but that`s the paradigm we`re seeing, young people unvaccinated coming into hospitals, in my case, in intensive care. And that is what we`re seeing throughout the course of now the middle of 2021.\par \par \uc2 It`s this notion that the threat that existed in late 2019, 2020, you know, we had normed, Chuck. You and I`ve talked about this that well, if you`re vulnerable, or if you`ll say 70 or 80 years old or with a pre-existing condition, you`re more likely to end up on a ventilator.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, that`s how a residual effect on how young people especially think about their -- the threat perception of COVID which is why if so, we need to we need -- maybe we need to label it something different but this has changed, the message hasn`t cut through.\par \par \uc2 You`re seeing places like Washington County and Georgia, 20 percent to 30percent levels of a vaccine uptake at this moment, I was just talking to colleagues out in Nashville, they`re seeing spikes 700 percent increase in ICU hospitalizations for COVID. Southwest Missouri (INAUDIBLE), a similar type of paradigm. Seasonality, Chuck, once we get into colder, drier air, the variants and low vaccine uptake will cause regional surges as Dr. Fauci mentioned.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Ed, you were talking with -- in your piece, you talked with quite a few folks who basically we`re going to have a little bit of a hint this summer in the south on just how vulnerable we are with this Delta variant and perhaps, as you hauntingly put it, there may be an Omega variant that we have -- we don`t know of yet that could also start to do damage in the fall.\par \par \uc2 But what do -- what do we expect to learn this summer in the South given these vaccination rates?\par \par \uc2 YONG: I think we saw last year that the virus, even though you know, vanilla original variants of the virus will try and transmit quite happily in the fall if given the opportunity. And if that happens in the South this year, that gives us some worrying indications of what we might expect in the fall and winter as, you know, perhaps protections -- other protections ease off even further as Delta and other future variants continue to spread. I do also really want to push back against this idea that, you know, this is entirely a fault of people not taking the opportunity to, you know, to take part in the protections that vaccines offer.\par \par \uc2 A lot of what we`re seeing in terms of low vaccination rates is also still to do with her difficulties of access, people not being able to take the time to either get their shots or to work through the side effects of getting those shots.\par \par \uc2 We sort of wrongly portraying it is entirely a question of personal responsibility. This idea that because people are eligible for the vaccine, they can get it. There are lots of complicated issues here and I don`t think we should turn our backs on people who are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Now we should extend the full measure of our empathy and try and get them better access to these shots, and better trust in the medical system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, look, I hear you, Ed. I don`t think that`s -- and I`m sorry that you see it portrayed that way. I wasn`t -- I was certainly wasn`t attempting to do that because I think a lot of these folks are being misled, and I think that`s, you know, part of it. We have a massive misinformation problem that is done this. We know this. we know what \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has done, right, the social media impact on anti-vaccine behavior.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there have been attempts to go more local with the shot to do these things, but you`ve had many employers, in some cases, perhaps they`re too hesitant to help out with this. I mean, what would improve the access issue in some of these communities, Ed?\par \par \uc2 YONG: I think just -- you know, some -- a measure as simple as paid sick leave, a thing that we`ve talked about for much of last year and giving people the opportunity to not only take time off from low-income jobs to get their shots but also to recover from the variant, from this side effects, especially from the mRNA vaccines.\par \par \uc2 All these social measures are really important for giving people the option of like, of taking advantage of these -- of the incredible protections that the vaccines provide.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Gupta, do you think we could do -- you`d mentioned the issue of young people and perhaps, they were -- took comfort in the fact that this was preying on an older, vulnerable -- in an older, vulnerable population, and that there were some perhaps immunities that they thought they would have from it or would be able to recover easily. What other -- what other ideas do you think we`re not pursuing, and that the Biden administration is not pursuing that we should?\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Well, so I think the Biden administration is thinking about all the things and is trying to address the inequities that I just mentioned, so that -- I mean, this is a constantly moving effort and whether it`s mobile clinics trying to reach out to rural, extra-urban areas.\par \par \uc2 Personally, I have been involved with, Chuck, in an effort to answer questions. I think most young people, since we`re talking about that younger demographic, are reachable, at least in my experience in just talking to frontline workers across a range of organizations. They just have questions, and they don`t want to get a finger-wagged to. They want to be -- they want to have a discussion.\par \par \uc2 It`s tough to scale one on one conversations to millions of young people, but that`s why engagement with their medical provider, people they trust is vital. I will say just quickly with the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  information that was released yesterday, that we need to start more clearly discussing and defining to at least the American people since we have an excess of options here in this country, what do we mean by what is fully vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 You know, 6 months ago, and I was at the top of saying this, from the top of my lungs, get the first vaccine available to you will keep from the hospital, that still remains true. And we have encouraging early data about \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  versus the Delta variant and averting serious illness.\par \par \uc2 But the question I get from young people, especially on parents is, well, what happens if I go unmask because the CDC says I can go unmask now if I`m fully vaccinated, and I come back home and remain unmask.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to acknowledge that there`s likely a difference in the ability to transmit the virus if you get two mRNA shots than if you got one \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  shot, especially with a changing threat paradigm with all these new variants.\par \par \uc2 And people are picking up on that and these are the questions we`re getting asked. So, we need to -- we need to delve into that detail.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Ed, very quickly. I know we talked about doing everything we can to care it -- you know, make it care to try to get people to vaccinate. Would a vaccine mandate a little bit of a stick here, help close this gap a bit faster?\par \par \uc2 YONG: Yes, it`s a really good question. I don`t know the answer to that. I actually think that this issue of trust is so important. And rather than using the sick, like actually trying to build trust where it has been lacking. Not just in terms of like misinformation, but in long-brewing historical distrust in the medical establishment especially among minority groups who are now skeptical and hesitant about the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 I think we`re going to do a lot better to improve vaccination rates by rebuilding that trust in communities that have many of them have good reasons to lack it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, there are unfortunately though, some people who are doing misinformation because, perhaps, they think it`s good ratings, or for whatever reason, or take an advantage of people, and that, sadly, is putting people`s lives at risk as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Ed Yong and Dr. Vin Gupta, I appreciate both of you in leading us off in this discussion.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of other issues that could put lives in danger, we`re just 32 days into hurricane season and yet we already have reached our 5th named storm. Tropical Storm Elsa strengthened into the first hurricane of 2021 Atlantic storm season putting Eastern Caribbean Islands on high alert. Hurricane conditions are already underway in Barbados. And warnings have also been issued for St. Lucia, St. Vincent, and the Grenadines. Forecasters predict that Elsa could hit Florida sometime early next week potentially complicating search and rescue efforts in Surfside.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the entire State of Florida sounds a bit of the panhandle is in the current cone of uncertainty. We`ll obviously keep a close eye on the storm, and we`ll bring you updates as we get them throughout the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, back to Surfside. What we now know on day nine of the search for survivors and answers in Florida after that condo collapse. We`ll be live in Surfside with a full report. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 And later, what we just heard from President Biden about June`s job report and what the numbers could mean for his hefty-priced infrastructure package on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 Senator Angus King of Maine, the independent, will be here on our Independence Day weekend show. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As search and rescue operations continue in Surfside, Florida, crews are battling time and Mother Nature in their hopes of finding any survivors. Just a short time ago officials announced that the death toll has now risen officially to 20 and now 128 people are still unaccounted for. Among the two victims recovered last night, the 7-year-old daughter of a Miami firefighter.\par \par \uc2 Complicating search efforts, hurricane Elsa, that`s barreling toward the Florida coast. Regardless of the storm strength, or what happens, officials say the remaining structure will have to come down at some point. Nobody wants to see it come down without being in a controlled way.\par \par \uc2 Our own Ellison Barber joins me now from Surfside. So, Ellison, we know that yesterday, of all days when the President visited, they really had -- they had to shut down search and rescue efforts. What`s the situation today, and how much is the weather not cooperating?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, they`re out there working. This is probably the only day in the last, you know, six, seven days however long it is that we`ve been here, where it has not been raining at some point. But obviously, we know what is likely just around the corner at this point.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned them wanting to remove the rest of that remaining structure, obviously because they want to make sure that there is not a situation or a scenario like what happened yesterday where workers are unable to keep searching for people or recover bodies that may be underneath that rubble because of structural concerns. But taking down that building, that will take time, weeks, most likely. And obviously, we could feel the effects of Hurricane Elsa as early as Sunday night if it stays in the path where it is right now.\par \par \uc2 I`m told that we actually could start to see equipment moving out of the area here any minute and that initially when people start to see things move, it is equipment leaving, but it doesn`t mean that rescue efforts are stopping.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to a firefighter with Miami-Dade Urban Search and Rescue team or Florida Task Force One and she said that what`s leaving first are things that are not being used in the active search and rescue efforts.\par \par \uc2 She said that rescuers with hand tools, they will keep searching until they are forced to leave that pile of rubble and if they are told to go, they will be back as soon as it is safe, as soon as the storm passes, and they are able to return to that mount they say they`ll be the first ones back on site.\par \par \uc2 Again, if they are forced to leave because of Hurricane Elsa approaching and they wanted to make sure that the families of those still missing knew that if they go, they`re going to come back. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE CASTRO, MIAMI-DADE FIRE RESCUE: We`re not going anywhere, and we express that to them today during their family meeting, explain to them that as soon as the green light is given, that every piece of equipment can come back, and rescuers can be allowed back on the pile, even without the rest of the equipment being here, we will be back to work. We will be the last ones to leave, and we will be the first ones to be back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: 20 lives lost in this building collapse. 128 people still unaccounted for. Chuck, this is the 9th day of rescue efforts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Ellison Barber on the ground force in Surfside. Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I`m now joined by the man who`s in charge -- who has been put in charge of the Surfside investigation of the building collapse, it`s Allyn Kilsheimer.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kilsheimer, thank you for coming on. First, let me start with the issue of dealing with the remaining part of the building. It obviously will have to come down. How does that process work? And how does that fit into your investigation?\par \par \uc2 ALLYN KILSHEIMER, STRUCTURAL ENGINEER: When the rest of the building -- two parts to the building. One is the part that is already severely damaged, and you see all the debris, and then there`s more building to the south of that that you -- from the outside don`t see anything.\par \par \uc2 So, I believe the building is going to be, when it`s taken down, to be taken down to three pieces. The bottom line is that either before it`s taken down, which we`ve been trying, we`ve been working over the last couple of days to try and work it out. Either before it`s taken down, or after it`s taken down, the debris will be taken to a secure site and we`ll be able to be looking at the debris to try and learn things about the materials and how they`re put together and everything.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I got to think with this hurricane coming and look, there`s, -- look, I`m a South Floridian. We all know these things can take a lot of weird turns. But if South Florida is going to get a version of this, I mean, that -- what -- that this can`t be the debris itself could become problematic for the area. What`s the best way to secure the site in preparing for this storm?\par \par \uc2 KILSHEIMER: Well, we`re doing some computer-based models now that we should have done by tonight or tomorrow, that in -- that we are proving to ourselves what wind load, we believe the building can sustain given the conditions it`s in.\par \par \uc2 We -- the issue appears, if we`re going to get those wind loads, you`re not going to be able to do anything to shore up this building, certainly not in the timeframe, and you -- not in a very long-time frame.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be to get the rescue people off of that debris pile so they can`t be hurt if parts or bigger parts of this building are blown down by the hurricane. That`s the only thing that you can do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, let`s talk about your investigation. Obviously, this is -- I`m guessing this is something that`s going to take months. You`re going to want to look at, I assume, everything that you can of that building, and how much do you want to -- how much of your investigation do you want to conduct before the rest of that building is brought down?\par \par \uc2 How much is there to learn do you believe in your investigation before you make the decision to have that control -- just sort of that controlled demolition for the rest of that building?\par \par \uc2 KILSHEIMER: We started the investigation last Friday, and we were looking at drawings looking for information we could find, we`re listening to what people have to say, understanding what they saw, or thought they saw and heard and all that kind of stuff and all that`s being put together.\par \par \uc2 But when we do these investigations, we start with what are the possible causes that could make this happen, and you end up with 20, or 30, or 40, or whatever number of absolutely possible causes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KILSHEIMER: And then you essentially attempt to eliminate them one at a time based on the information that you`re developing. The idea of this is you`re looking for the trigger mechanism. This building sat there for 40 years, it didn`t come down, it came down. So, you`re trying to find the trigger mechanism. At the same time, you`re looking at the original design drawings, material tests that we`ll be doing, foundation tests we`re be doing, and then you say, OK, so this thing wasn`t perfect, no building we`ve ever been involved in is perfect, and either design mistakes sometimes during construction.\par \par \uc2 And then what happens is, you say if, this was the trigger or possible triggers, if that one thing would have been perfect, would it still have happened? So, you go through this huge Chinese puzzle kind of thing of throwing four or five puzzles up in the air and mix them all up to get something trying to figure it out. And that`s the process we are going through.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You toured the North building, obviously you have similar plans, what did you learn? And I`m curious, would you -- the Mayor of Surfside said he would not sleep in that building, would you?\par \par \uc2 KLISHEIMER: Yes, I would. I think more important than that is, would I allow my kids or grandkids to sleep in that building because I`m 80 years old? The answer is yes, I would let my kids based on what I`ve seen on the exposed and visible conditions, which is mainly the garage, I would let my family stay in that building. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What did you learn from that building that you think will help you understand what happened in the south building?\par \par \uc2 KILSHEIMER: I`m not sure that there`s anything that we have learned yet from that building that will help us in the south building. I just could see the condition of the exposed and visible conditions.\par \par \uc2 Now we may be doing some non-destructive or even partially destructive testing in the north building to test those materials, and then we`ll compare that to what we find for the materials that we will look at in the debris.\par \par \uc2 So, we might use it as part of a science experiment, if you want to call it that, to understand what the north building is, and then see how that compares to what we`re going to find out the south building was.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`ll tell you this, I was going to ask if you had a running hypothesis and you basically said there are already 40 potential hypotheses so with that, I imagine I`ll be checking -- we`ll be checking in with you quite a bit as your investigation continues. Mr. Kilsheimer, we really appreciate you spending a little time with us.\par \par \uc2 KILSHEIMER: Sure, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. U.S. troop withdrawal from Afghanistan is nearly complete as we`re an inch closer to our Independence Day. We got the latest and what this all means for the Middle East live from Kabul. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Afghanistan`s Bagram Airbase is now back in the hands of the Afghan military. U.S. troops vacated the base overnight, ending control over the Afghan National Security and Defense Force. Bagram has been an integral part of the U.S. and coalition operations of the last 20 years. More than 100,000 American troops have passed through its gates.\par \par \uc2 Today`s withdrawal is a major step toward President Biden`s stated goal of withdrawing nearly all U.S. troops from Afghanistan by September. This symbolically feels like we are moving a lot faster than that.\par \par \uc2 Our chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel is in Kabul and has been witnessing what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 So I`d tell you, symbolically leaving Bagram feels like we`ve left. So after this how many more U.S. troops are there before we are totally gone?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: So it is more than a symbol. This is a major base. It was the center for a lot of operations in and around Kabul. It was one of the first bases established after 9/11 when U.S. troops came here to look for Osama bin Laden, root out al Qaeda, hunt down the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . So it is a major milestone.\par \par \uc2 How many more troops are left is something of a military secret. The U.S. is pulling out its troops here very quietly, citing security concerns. They are not saying how long this is going to take, they are not saying where the troops are coming from, where the next withdrawals will be, how many have already been taken out. They`re not saying when it is going to be completed. There had been some reporting that it could be completed over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 I`m getting some guidance that it might not be done over the next several days, but it`s really just guidance. We don`t know because the military is trying to keep this very quiet. And that is understandable because there is still the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  here and there is a surge risk to American forces being fired on as they`re leaving. But the other side of it is as American troops are leaving after 20 years and not really projecting what they`re doing it creates a lot of uncertainty, a lot of anxiety among Afghans who worry that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are going to take over, that the Americans are leaving them in the dead of night, without really saying a proper goodbye.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are making inroads. They have been capturing district centers. Some of the district centers they`re taking without even fighting as Afghan security forces, police and soldiers are handing them over, handing over their weapons, and collapsing as their life support, the American troops, are leaving.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, can the Afghan -- do you expect the Afghanistan Security Forces to at least be able to hold on to Bagram?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Holding on to Bagram, potentially, but the Afghan Security Forces are already starting to fracture. And what we`re seeing now is the warlords in Afghanistan, and there are many warlords regionally based predominantly, are trying to form a coalition to back up the Afghan Security Forces. So a lot of the old warlords from before the American presence here are starting to reemerge and bring their guns out.\par \par \uc2 And it is going to be very difficult to create unity among the old warlords and the weakened Afghan Security Forces as they fight against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . So you have a dynamic where you have the Americans leaving and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  per se loose coalition of everybody else. Some people say it`s a formula for civil war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, well, everything old is sadly new again, it looks like, in the country that is known as Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Richard Engel, on the ground for us, at Bagram Airbase. Richard, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how President Biden is reacting to these new jobs report and how the job numbers are being received over on the Hill. The independent Senator Angus King of Maine joins me for our annual Independence Day conversation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The last time the economy grew at this rate was in 1984 and Ronald Reagan was telling us it`s morning in America. Well, it`s getting close to afternoon here, the sun is coming out. Put simply, our economy is on the move, and we have COVID-19 on the run.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden this morning discussing the new jobs numbers. The economy added 850,000 jobs in June, beating expectations from economists. The unemployment rate remained relatively unchanged, still rounding there at 5.9 percent. But we still have 9.5 million Americans out of work and the economy still is transforming amid the coronavirus pandemic. We still have a bunch of people that have not gotten back into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 This is the economic climate as Congress debates infrastructure measures that will put hundreds of billions, perhaps even trillions of dollars into this economy.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is a member of the bipartisan working group on infrastructure who has that fragile bipartisan deal with President Biden. It`s Maine`s independent senator, Angus King, who caucuses with the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Independence Day weekend, Senator, you and I have this made this a somewhat I think nearly a decade long annual tradition. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ANGUS KING (I-ME): Yes. Good to see you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me just start with, look, our economy appears to be on the move, in the words of President Biden, and that has led to a debate about just how much more stimulus, how much more investment from the government is necessary. I know where you guys on this bipartisan infrastructure deal, but what is a moving economy do to the reconciliation debate that`s taking place inside the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, I think we have to take account of what is happening in the economy, and I would separate the infrastructure discussion from a stimulus. Infrastructure is stimulus but the real intent is to shore up the basic building blocks of our economy, of roads, bridges, highways, rail and I have to add broadband. To me broadband is the most important part of the infrastructure package. It will stimulate jobs, it will stimulate the economy, but this is -- this is a one-in-a-generation sort of paying down of deferred maintenance, if you will, in many ways.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s -- getting the infrastructure package done is the first job. And then we can talk about what additional stimulus or indeed, is it stimulus or is it simply shoring up some places that really need help like homecare, childcare, and those kinds of things?\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons, Chuck, people aren`t going back to work is a lack of childcare. If you`ve got a 7-year-old and a 9-year-old at home then you can`t get anybody to take care of them, you can`t go to work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much deficit spending are you comfortable with? I mean, it appears there`s going to be some pay-fors in this infrastructure package, some pay-fors in the larger, let`s call it the sort of human infrastructure package that the Democrats-only bill is being worked on. There`s going to be some pay-fors there. But it`s pretty clear there`s going to be some additions to the deficit there. How much are you comfortable with?\par \par \uc2 KING: I`m not comfortable with very much because we`re already in such a deep hole. And to me the only way to get out of a hole is to stop digging. So I`ve been pushing for pay-fors both in the infrastructure, I got off a call 15 minutes ago on that, and the last thing we were talking about was pay-fors. And I think whatever, going forward, should be pretty close to deficit neutral simply because we`re in this hole and our kids are going to have to bail us out of it.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that`s right. The worry about the deficit, Chuck, isn`t an abstract concern. It`s interest rates. We`re at an all-time low in interest rates. If interest rates go back to anything close to normal we`ll have over $1 trillion a year of just debt service payments by the federal government. That`s almost equal to the whole discretionary budget. So that`s the thing that everybody, progressives, conservatives, everybody ought to be worried about that. And that`s why, as I say, the best role for me is if you`re in a hole, stop digging.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So would you not support a bill if -- would you not support a bill if, say, it`s a $3 trillion reconciliation and they have $2 trillion in pay-fors but it adds a trillion to the deficit over 10 years? That too much for you?\par \par \uc2 KING: Todd, I`ve learned a couple things, one is to not negotiate over television. So I`m not going to draw a line right now. I`ll see what the circumstances are at the time. What`s being recommended. But, you know, my inclination is to want it to be close to being paid for. You have to define close.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, let`s -- fair enough. I`m trying to get you to define close. I hear you, it`s all subjective and nobody wants to show their cards. I had Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, I said, well, how much was the number and she said, the number is not important, it`s what`s in it. All of you have all learned this lesson, whether you`re new or a veteran.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about the issue of the broad center in American politics. Let me show you this most recent NBC poll. Favorable ratings of the two parties plus the two presidents. Both former and the current. The Republican Party, Democrat Party, both under 40 percent favorable. Donald Trump at 32 percent favorable. Joe Biden at a 50 percent favorable rating.\par \par \uc2 His coalition is bigger than the Democratic Party. It includes people like yourself in this broad center. Is it a strong enough center to sort of stick or are we just going to continue to see this vacillation between the two parties?\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, I think you put your finger on a real problem. And I have not seen a lot written about this, but the real danger of the political situation we`re in right now is the fact that compromising is seen as a politically dangerous or deadly thing to do. You know, this is 4th of July weekend. It`s all about the Constitution, the founding of the country. And this country was founded on compromise.\par \par \uc2 If those folks at that convention in Philadelphia in 1787 hadn`t compromised on a whole lot of things, one of which was the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  by the way, we wouldn`t have a country or we certainly wouldn`t have a constitution that we have. So the real danger now, Chuck, is particularly with these gerrymandered congressional districts on both sides, if you`re viewed as a person who will listen to the other side and try to find a compromise, you can lose your seat just for that. Not because your position on an issue. That`s really dangerous in our ability to get something done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much of this is a communications problem? Because, you know, I see plenty of polling work that shows there is a broad center that wants this consensus. But the conversation and those with the mega phones are the two political bases. And they denounce those efforts. And we see what happens in primaries. It feels like we are really disconnected here between what sort of a majority of Americans want and what a majority of Americans hear.\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, I think the problem is that that broad center that you talk about doesn`t vote in primaries. If you`ve got a gerrymandered congressional district that`s 20 points Republican or 20 points Democrat, it doesn`t matter what the center said. You have to be furthest to the left or furthest to right in order to get win that primary. And the center gets left out. That`s one of the problems -- I mean, the primary system was invented to open up democracy, control over who we nominate, but the problem is, principally, the activist vote.\par \par \uc2 So the center doesn`t have a constituency in the primary. And by the time the primary, again if it`s a district that`s 20 percent Republican, when you win the Republican primary, you`ve won the seat. And there`s no incentive for you to compromise with the other side. In fact all the incentives to not do it, to not talk to the other side, to not be viewed as somebody who`s trying to be reasonable.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a very conservative Republican senator who is facing a right- wing primary. I said what are they going to charge you with? You`re really conservative. He said they`re going to charge me with being reasonable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KING: Man, we`re in trouble when that`s a deadly sin in politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You said it very well.\par \par \uc2 Senator Angus King, the independent from Maine, who yes, does caucus with the Democrats, Happy Independence Day, yourself, sir.\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, Chuck. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, I`m going to speak with another past Democratic presidential candidate about lessons learned and where the Democratic Party is headed in the future.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Can you name them? Could you try to name as many as you can remember?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, good grief.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, good lord.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hickenlooper.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Florida? Who was the -- there was -- no. Gibberd or Gibbard?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Kamala Harris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Elizabeth Warren, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , the guy from Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, Beto O`Rourke.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m trying to think of a "Saturday Night Live" skit when they were all on the stand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . Yes. That`s what I was thinking. Bernie and Biden because they were kind of neck and neck.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. So there was a Texan. The Texas guy. The twin brothers, whatever their names are.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Anyway the Texas guy with the twin brother is of course former HUD secretary Julian Castro. He is next up in our MEET THE PRESS DAILY series where we`re talking to some of the former 2020 candidates about the current state of the Democratic Party and its future. And there`s perhaps no one better candidate to have that conversation with than Julian Castro.\par \par \uc2 That`s because we have some brand-new Pew numbers showing that Biden`s win in November was not driven by the young diverse voters that many progressive candidates like Castro was trying to appeal to. In fact compared to 2016, Biden made some of the biggest gains with married men and suburban voters. Trump meanwhile actually improved with Hispanics.\par \par \uc2 Just want to talk a little bit about this and lessons learned from the campaign. Julian Castro joins me now to get into those numbers, discuss the party`s future.\par \par \uc2 Julian, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JULIAN CASTRO (D), FORMER HUD SECRETARY: Hey, great to be with you, Chuck. Thanks for that walk down memory lane.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I thought it would be fun. Hey, you know, something stuck with me that you said to me, and I don`t know if you remember saying this to me. I want to say about two years before the campaign even started, and you telling you were looking, you were researching the various presidential campaigns where there were open nomination battles on the Democratic side, `76, `92 and `08 were the three you really dove into.\par \par \uc2 And you said, you know, I think this is a lot like `76 and you know what, you were more prescient perhaps than you realized because one way to look at what happened in `76 is the country went with the person whose temperament was the most opposite of what they didn`t want. And in that case, I would say Biden was the opposite temperament of Donald Trump. Do you think you ended up stumbling on the correct answer?\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Well, I mean, you point out something that`s obviously very true. And I think that the number one lesson for me after that 2020 campaign was that the person who wins the nomination and then ultimately the presidency is the person who has the most experience. And Joe Biden, for many reasons, was the person that (INAUDIBLE), including, as you say, people had tired completely of Donald Trump`s chaos, his antics, his dishonesty, lack of experience and (INAUDIBLE) with COVID and many other ways.\par \par \uc2 And they wanted somebody that they thought was going to be the adult at the Oval Office. They wanted somebody that they knew somebody that`s been there before, somebody that, you know, had experience to do the job well and had a good temperament and that was Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, look, you`re not alone in making the argument that you believe there`s a coalition here that the Democrats can build on, of younger voters particularly Latino voters, and yet, we saw Latino voters, number one, turnout was mixed, was a bit middling. That`s one. And two, those Latinos who did turn out Donald Trump made some inroads here.\par \par \uc2 What have you taken away from the general election? What have you learned from that?\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: What I see is that the Democratic Party is still the big tent party. When you boil all of the numbers down, and the \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  report that came out in the last couple of days was fantastic to be able to get that insight, sure, in some places Donald Trump made gains with people of color, with Latinos. I saw that in my home state of Texas, for instance, but still, almost two-thirds of Latinos overall in the country supported Donald Trump. There still -- supported Joe Biden against Donald Trump. There still is a strong coalition of Latinos, of African-Americans, of Asian American voters, and also suburban whites who make up this base of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s promising for the future. Having said that, as a party, we cannot take Latinos or other groups for granted. We cannot assume that they`re going to vote just in one direction, because they`ve traditionally done that, and in that sense, 2020 was a wake-up call. I said that before the election, I said that in the weeks after the election. We have to invest in voter registration. We have to invest in turnout. And we`ve got to do it more than three months, six months before the election. It has to be a 365-day year, full-cycle effort throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, and this has been time and time again, you`re not alone here, every cycle you hear this, you can`t just show up in the election year. Look, I`m a little short on time, are you going to be on a ballot in 2022?\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: You know, I haven`t taken that completely off the table, but it`s unlikely right now. I feel like I just ran this marathon in 2019 and early 2020, and I`m happy supporting the progressive candidates that are running in the 2022 cycle. It`s unlikely right now, but if I change my mind, I`ll be sure to let you know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`d appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Former HUD secretary, former mayor of San Antonio, and somebody that`s been very frequent on this program, Julian Castro. It`s good to see you. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your memories and perspective with us. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Good to be with you again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. Please enjoy your holiday weekend. Please, if you haven`t been vaccinated, please get vaccinated. If you know somebody that hasn`t, please find a way to convince them in any way possible.\par \par \uc2 And if it`s Sunday be sure to tune in on MEET THE PRESS in your local NBC station where we`re going to take a deep dive on COVID at this 4th of July mark.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage continues with my friend Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210703eh7200001\par }\par \b \uc2 S